/* Context Engineer practice · 20261126-ce-r1 */
globalThis.CE_BANK = {"schema_version":1,"id":"context-engineer-20261126","title":"Context Engineer 合格演習室","edition":20261126,"description":"提供された模試1〜4、各45問。英語の原文と日本語訳・解説。","exams":[{"id":"CE-PRACTICE-1","title":"Context Engineer 模試1","minutes":90,"questions":[{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-001","number":1,"form":1,"stem":"A Databricks agent handles high-volume, low-latency FAQ lookups (selecting/rephrasing a pre-approved answer) and infrequent, complex reconciliation tasks requiring multi-step reasoning over large retrieved ledger data. The team wants to control token cost and latency without sacrificing quality on the harder task.\n\nWhich reasoning-mode configuration is most appropriate?","options":["Enable extended thinking with a large thinking-token budget for every request, regardless of task type, to guarantee consistent quality.","Use standard (near-instant) mode for the FAQ lookups, and enable extended thinking with a bounded thinking-token budget only for the reconciliation task.","Always use reduced thinking to minimize token costs, relying on additional retrieval passes to compensate for shallower reasoning on the reconciliation task.","Route both task types through extended thinking, but cap the overall context window instead of the thinking-token budget to control cost."],"correct":1,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"タスク別の推論予算","concept":"ce-f1-q001","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Use standard (near-instant) mode for the FAQ lookups, and enable extended thinking with a bounded thinking-token budget only for the reconciliation task.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe workload contains two substantially different task classes:\n\n- FAQ lookups are frequent, predictable, and low complexity. The agent mainly selects or rephrases an approved answer.\n\n- Ledger reconciliation is infrequent and requires multi-step analysis over a large amount of retrieved data.\n\nUsing the same reasoning configuration for both would either waste tokens and increase latency on simple requests or under-allocate reasoning capacity to the difficult task. The best configuration therefore routes requests by complexity: fast standard processing for FAQ questions and bounded extended thinking for reconciliation.\n\nDatabricks-hosted hybrid reasoning models support fast responses and deeper extended thinking, while reasoning budgets can be controlled to balance quality, latency, and token usage.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption B applies reasoning effort only where it is likely to improve answer quality.\n\nFor FAQ lookups, extended reasoning offers little benefit because the expected behavior is constrained:\n\n- Retrieve or identify the approved answer.\n\n- Rephrase it without changing its meaning.\n\n- Return it quickly.\n\nStandard or near-instant mode minimizes response latency and avoids consuming reasoning tokens on a routine task.\n\nThe reconciliation task is different. It may require the model to:\n\n- Compare multiple ledger records.\n\n- Identify unmatched transactions.\n\n- Follow dependencies across retrieved data.\n\n- Test competing explanations.\n\n- Perform several reasoning steps before reaching a conclusion.\n\nExtended thinking is therefore appropriate for this task. A bounded thinking-token budget provides sufficient reasoning capacity while preventing unpredictable token usage, cost, and latency.\n\nFor supported Claude hybrid reasoning models on Databricks, extended thinking can be enabled with the `thinking` configuration, while `budget_tokens` limits the number of tokens available for internal reasoning. Databricks notes that larger reasoning budgets can improve complex-task quality but increase token usage.\n\nThe exact control depends on the selected model:\n\n- Hybrid Claude models can use `thinking` and `budget_tokens`.\n\n- Supported GPT-5 reasoning models use `reasoning_effort`, where higher effort can improve complex reasoning but can also increase latency and token usage.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Enable extended thinking with a large thinking-token budget for every request, regardless of task type, to guarantee consistent quality.\n\nThis configuration would allocate expensive reasoning capacity to high-volume FAQ requests that do not require substantial analysis.\n\nIt would likely increase:\n\n- Per-request token consumption.\n\n- Response latency.\n\n- Serving cost.\n\n- Capacity requirements during peak FAQ traffic.\n\nIt also does not “guarantee” quality. Model output must still be evaluated, and Databricks explicitly notes that models can make mistakes even when they have advanced reasoning capabilities.\n\nExtended thinking should be reserved for tasks whose complexity justifies its additional cost.\n\nC. Always use reduced thinking to minimize token costs, relying on additional retrieval passes to compensate for shallower reasoning on the reconciliation task.\n\nRetrieval and reasoning solve different problems:\n\n- Retrieval provides relevant ledger data.\n\n- Reasoning analyzes relationships among those records.\n\nAdditional retrieval passes do not necessarily compensate for insufficient reasoning. They may instead add more context, latency, and retrieval cost without helping the model reconcile conflicting or dependent transactions.\n\nReduced reasoning may be appropriate for the FAQ workload, but applying it to the complex reconciliation workflow risks sacrificing the quality that the scenario explicitly requires.\n\nD. Route both task types through extended thinking, but cap the overall context window instead of the thinking-token budget to control cost.\n\nThe context window and the reasoning-token budget are separate controls.\n\nCapping the context window limits how much prompt, history, and retrieved ledger data the model can receive. It does not directly limit how many tokens the model uses for internal reasoning.\n\nThis could be especially harmful for reconciliation because the task may require substantial retrieved ledger evidence. Reducing the context limit could remove necessary records while still allowing unnecessary reasoning expenditure on FAQ requests.\n\nThe correct approach is to:\n\n- Manage retrieved context according to task requirements.\n\n- Independently control reasoning effort or the thinking-token budget.\n\n- Enable deeper reasoning only for the task that benefits from it.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main distractor is A because extended thinking appears to offer universally higher quality.\n\nThe exam principle is that reasoning effort should be proportional to task complexity. Applying maximum reasoning to every request wastes resources and undermines the latency objective without providing meaningful benefit for simple FAQ lookups.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production agent should classify or route requests before model invocation.\n\nFor example:\n\n- FAQ intent → standard mode, small output limit, approved-answer retrieval.\n\n- Reconciliation intent → extended thinking, bounded reasoning budget, larger relevant context allowance, and additional validation.\n\n- Uncertain intent → conservative routing or a lightweight classification step.\n\nThe team should empirically tune the reconciliation budget rather than automatically selecting the largest permitted value. Evaluation should compare:\n\n- Reconciliation accuracy.\n\n- Unsupported conclusions.\n\n- End-to-end latency.\n\n- Input, output, and reasoning-token consumption.\n\n- Cost per successful task.\n\nThe model-specific API must also be considered. A Claude hybrid model may expose `thinking` and `budget_tokens`, whereas another reasoning model may use a control such as `reasoning_effort`.\n\nFor the certification exam, the expected principle is adaptive reasoning allocation, making option B the best answer.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Query reasoning models\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/model-serving/query-reason-models](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/model-serving/query-reason-models)\n\n- Databricks-hosted foundation models available in Foundation Model APIs\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/foundation-model-apis/supported-models](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/foundation-model-apis/supported-models)\n\n- Databricks Certified Context Engineer Associate\n[https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate](https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate)","source_option_explanations":["A. Enable extended thinking with a large thinking-token budget for every request, regardless of task type, to guarantee consistent quality.\n\nThis configuration would allocate expensive reasoning capacity to high-volume FAQ requests that do not require substantial analysis.\n\nIt would likely increase:\n\n- Per-request token consumption.\n\n- Response latency.\n\n- Serving cost.\n\n- Capacity requirements during peak FAQ traffic.\n\nIt also does not “guarantee” quality. Model output must still be evaluated, and Databricks explicitly notes that models can make mistakes even when they have advanced reasoning capabilities.\n\nExtended thinking should be reserved for tasks whose complexity justifies its additional cost.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Use standard (near-instant) mode for the FAQ lookups, and enable extended thinking with a bounded thinking-token budget only for the reconciliation task.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe workload contains two substantially different task classes:\n\n- FAQ lookups are frequent, predictable, and low complexity. The agent mainly selects or rephrases an approved answer.\n\n- Ledger reconciliation is infrequent and requires multi-step analysis over a large amount of retrieved data.\n\nUsing the same reasoning configuration for both would either waste tokens and increase latency on simple requests or under-allocate reasoning capacity to the difficult task. The best configuration therefore routes requests by complexity: fast standard processing for FAQ questions and bounded extended thinking for reconciliation.\n\nDatabricks-hosted hybrid reasoning models support fast responses and deeper extended thinking, while reasoning budgets can be controlled to balance quality, latency, and token usage.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption B applies reasoning effort only where it is likely to improve answer quality.\n\nFor FAQ lookups, extended reasoning offers little benefit because the expected behavior is constrained:\n\n- Retrieve or identify the approved answer.\n\n- Rephrase it without changing its meaning.\n\n- Return it quickly.\n\nStandard or near-instant mode minimizes response latency and avoids consuming reasoning tokens on a routine task.\n\nThe reconciliation task is different. It may require the model to:\n\n- Compare multiple ledger records.\n\n- Identify unmatched transactions.\n\n- Follow dependencies across retrieved data.\n\n- Test competing explanations.\n\n- Perform several reasoning steps before reaching a conclusion.\n\nExtended thinking is therefore appropriate for this task. A bounded thinking-token budget provides sufficient reasoning capacity while preventing unpredictable token usage, cost, and latency.\n\nFor supported Claude hybrid reasoning models on Databricks, extended thinking can be enabled with the `thinking` configuration, while `budget_tokens` limits the number of tokens available for internal reasoning. Databricks notes that larger reasoning budgets can improve complex-task quality but increase token usage.\n\nThe exact control depends on the selected model:\n\n- Hybrid Claude models can use `thinking` and `budget_tokens`.\n\n- Supported GPT-5 reasoning models use `reasoning_effort`, where higher effort can improve complex reasoning but can also increase latency and token usage.","C. Always use reduced thinking to minimize token costs, relying on additional retrieval passes to compensate for shallower reasoning on the reconciliation task.\n\nRetrieval and reasoning solve different problems:\n\n- Retrieval provides relevant ledger data.\n\n- Reasoning analyzes relationships among those records.\n\nAdditional retrieval passes do not necessarily compensate for insufficient reasoning. They may instead add more context, latency, and retrieval cost without helping the model reconcile conflicting or dependent transactions.\n\nReduced reasoning may be appropriate for the FAQ workload, but applying it to the complex reconciliation workflow risks sacrificing the quality that the scenario explicitly requires.","D. Route both task types through extended thinking, but cap the overall context window instead of the thinking-token budget to control cost.\n\nThe context window and the reasoning-token budget are separate controls.\n\nCapping the context window limits how much prompt, history, and retrieved ledger data the model can receive. It does not directly limit how many tokens the model uses for internal reasoning.\n\nThis could be especially harmful for reconciliation because the task may require substantial retrieved ledger evidence. Reducing the context limit could remove necessary records while still allowing unnecessary reasoning expenditure on FAQ requests.\n\nThe correct approach is to:\n\n- Manage retrieved context according to task requirements.\n\n- Independently control reasoning effort or the thinking-token budget.\n\n- Enable deeper reasoning only for the task that benefits from it."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/model-serving/query-reason-models","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/foundation-model-apis/supported-models","https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate"],"source_hash":"4f2c9f99ab2e57fba1c92cb71d446c0764f89850ff7fad5fc8c41f453ef55cfb","stem_ja":"Databricksのエージェントは、大量の低遅延FAQ検索（事前承認済みの回答を選択・言い換えする処理）と、取得した大量の台帳データを使って多段階の推論を行う、頻度の低い複雑な照合作業を処理しています。チームは難しい作業の品質を落とさず、トークン費用と遅延を抑えたいと考えています。\n\n最も適切な推論モードの設定はどれですか？","options_ja":["品質の一貫性を保証するため、作業の種類にかかわらず、すべての要求で大きな思考トークン予算を指定して拡張思考を有効にする。","FAQ検索には標準のほぼ即時のモードを使い、照合作業にだけ上限付きの思考トークン予算で拡張思考を有効にする。","常に思考を減らしたモードでトークン費用を最小化し、照合作業で推論が浅くなる分は追加の検索処理で補う。","両方の作業を拡張思考に振り分け、費用を抑えるため、思考トークン予算ではなくコンテキストウィンドウ全体に上限を設ける。"],"explanation_ja":"簡単な検索と複雑な照合では、必要な推論量が異なります。承認済み回答の選択は速いモード、依存関係を追う照合は上限付きの拡張思考に振り分けます。「タスクの複雑さに推論予算を合わせる」と覚えましょう。入力できるコンテキスト量と、内部の思考量は別の制御です。","distractors_ja":["簡単なFAQにも高価な推論を使うため、遅延と費用が増えます。拡張思考を有効にしても品質が保証されるわけではありません。","簡単な作業の速さと、難しい作業に必要な推論能力を両立し、上限によって費用も管理できます。","検索は必要な情報を集める処理です。取得回数を増やしても、記録同士を照合する推論の不足は解消できません。","入力上限を削ると照合に必要な証拠が欠けるおそれがあります。入力の長さだけでは内部思考の費用を直接管理できません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-002","number":2,"form":1,"stem":"A team is building a fraud-investigation agent on Databricks that must resume long-running, multi-day investigation threads exactly where an analyst left off. Thread state must support sub-second reads/writes, atomic updates, and resumability by any application replica. The team orchestrates with LangGraph and needs to register a checkpointer.\n\nWhich Databricks component should back this checkpointer?","options":["Lakebase Postgres, using a Postgres-compatible checkpointer to persist thread-scoped state with low-latency, ACID-compliant reads and writes.","A Unity Catalog managed Delta table, queried directly on each turn to reconstruct thread state.","MLflow 3's tracing and experiment tracking store, used to log and replay the sequence of tool calls for each thread.","An MCP server that exposes the investigation state as a tool resource, refreshed each turn from object storage."],"correct":0,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"スレッド状態の永続化","concept":"ce-f1-q002","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Lakebase Postgres, using a Postgres-compatible checkpointer to persist thread-scoped state with low-latency, ACID-compliant reads and writes.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirements describe an operational state store for a production LangGraph agent:\n\n- Investigations can continue for multiple days.\n\n- The agent must resume from the exact graph state where execution stopped.\n\n- Reads and writes must have sub-second latency.\n\n- Updates must be atomic.\n\n- State cannot reside in one application replica’s memory.\n\n- LangGraph requires a registered checkpointer.\n\nLangGraph checkpointers persist graph-state snapshots for a specific thread and enable interruption recovery, human-in-the-loop workflows, fault tolerance, and conversation resumption. Databricks specifically positions Lakebase as a fully managed Postgres backend for agent state and supports LangGraph checkpointers for short-term, thread-scoped memory.\n\nTherefore, Lakebase Postgres with a Postgres-compatible LangGraph checkpointer is the most direct match.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA LangGraph checkpointer saves the graph state at execution checkpoints. Each investigation can use a unique `thread_id`, allowing the application to retrieve the current or historical state and continue from the correct checkpoint.\n\nLakebase provides the characteristics required by the scenario:\n\n- Durability: State survives application restarts and redeployments.\n\n- Shared accessibility: Any application replica can connect to the same database and resume the thread.\n\n- Transactional behavior: Postgres transactions support atomic and consistent state updates.\n\n- Low latency: Lakebase is intended for transactional applications and operational state rather than analytical scans.\n\n- LangGraph compatibility: A Postgres-backed saver can be registered when compiling the graph.\n\n- Checkpoint-based resumption: Databricks documents Lakebase-backed checkpoints for resuming or forking agent conversations from saved checkpoints.\n\nA typical design would conceptually register a Postgres saver with the graph:\n\n```\nfrom langgraph.checkpoint.postgres import PostgresSaver checkpointer = PostgresSaver.from_conn_string(    \"postgresql://...\")checkpointer.setup() graph = builder.compile(checkpointer=checkpointer)\n```\n\nThe application then supplies the investigation’s thread identifier when invoking the graph:\n\n```\nconfig = {    \"configurable\": {        \"thread_id\": investigation_id    }}\n```\n\nLangGraph’s official documentation identifies `PostgresSaver` as a persistent production checkpointer and distinguishes it from in-memory savers, whose checkpoints are lost when the process restarts.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. A Unity Catalog managed Delta table, queried directly on each turn to reconstruct thread state.\n\nDelta tables provide durable storage and ACID transactions, but directly querying a managed Delta table on every agent turn is not the best operational checkpointer design.\n\nDelta Lake is primarily optimized for lakehouse analytics, large-scale data processing, history, and batch or streaming workloads. Reconstructing graph state by scanning or querying event records introduces avoidable latency and requires the team to implement custom checkpoint serialization, concurrency control, checkpoint lookup, and replay logic.\n\nA Delta table could be useful for:\n\n- Investigation audit history.\n\n- Offline analytics.\n\n- Compliance reporting.\n\n- Long-term archival.\n\n- Training and evaluation datasets.\n\nHowever, the question asks which component should back the LangGraph checkpointer for low-latency transactional access. Lakebase is explicitly designed for that operational workload.\n\nC. MLflow 3's tracing and experiment tracking store, used to log and replay the sequence of tool calls for each thread.\n\nMLflow tracing is intended for observability, evaluation, debugging, and recording agent execution details. A trace can show prompts, model calls, tool invocations, latency, inputs, outputs, and errors.\n\nHowever, a trace is not equivalent to a LangGraph checkpoint. Replaying logged tool calls would not reliably reconstruct the exact mutable graph state and could repeat external side effects, such as:\n\n- Creating duplicate fraud cases.\n\n- Re-running database updates.\n\n- Reissuing alerts.\n\n- Repeating API calls.\n\n- Producing different results from nondeterministic tools.\n\nMLflow should complement the checkpointer by providing observability and auditability, but it should not serve as the transactional runtime state store.\n\nD. An MCP server that exposes the investigation state as a tool resource, refreshed each turn from object storage.\n\nMCP standardizes how an agent discovers and accesses tools, resources, and prompts. It is an integration protocol, not a transactional database or native LangGraph checkpoint backend.\n\nRefreshing state from object storage through an MCP resource would add unnecessary architectural layers and would not inherently provide:\n\n- Atomic checkpoint updates.\n\n- Efficient thread-state indexing.\n\n- Transaction isolation.\n\n- Native LangGraph checkpoint semantics.\n\n- Safe concurrent access by multiple replicas.\n\nAn MCP server could expose investigation evidence or external fraud-analysis tools, but durable graph execution state should remain in a transactional checkpointer backend.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe primary distractor is B because Delta tables provide both durability and ACID guarantees.\n\nThe overlooked distinction is between:\n\n- Analytical lakehouse storage, where Delta excels.\n\n- Low-latency operational state, where Lakebase Postgres is the appropriate component.\n\nThe phrase “register a checkpointer” is also a strong signal that the answer should use a database backend supported by LangGraph’s checkpoint persistence model.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nFor a production fraud-investigation system, use Lakebase Postgres for active LangGraph checkpoints and assign every investigation a stable, unique `thread_id`. All application replicas should connect to the same Lakebase database rather than maintaining replica-local state.\n\nA complete architecture could use:\n\n- Lakebase Postgres: Current thread state and LangGraph checkpoints.\n\n- MLflow Tracing: Execution observability, debugging, quality evaluation, and latency analysis.\n\n- Delta tables: Immutable investigation events, historical audit data, analytics, and compliance retention.\n\n- Unity Catalog: Governance and access control for associated data assets.\n\n- MCP servers: Controlled access to fraud-detection tools, case systems, and evidence sources.\n\nOperationally, the team should also establish checkpoint-retention rules because long-running threads can accumulate many historical checkpoints. Database credentials should be managed securely, connections should use pooling, and update operations should be designed to prevent concurrent analysts or replicas from overwriting one another.\n\nFor the certification question, however, the required component is unambiguously Lakebase Postgres.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent state and memory – Databricks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-managemen)\n\n- Lakebase Postgres – Databricks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/)\n\n- LangGraph Persistence\n[https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/persistence](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/persistence)\n\n- LangGraph Checkpointer Integrations\n[https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/integrations/checkpointers](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/integrations/checkpointers)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Lakebase Postgres, using a Postgres-compatible checkpointer to persist thread-scoped state with low-latency, ACID-compliant reads and writes.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirements describe an operational state store for a production LangGraph agent:\n\n- Investigations can continue for multiple days.\n\n- The agent must resume from the exact graph state where execution stopped.\n\n- Reads and writes must have sub-second latency.\n\n- Updates must be atomic.\n\n- State cannot reside in one application replica’s memory.\n\n- LangGraph requires a registered checkpointer.\n\nLangGraph checkpointers persist graph-state snapshots for a specific thread and enable interruption recovery, human-in-the-loop workflows, fault tolerance, and conversation resumption. Databricks specifically positions Lakebase as a fully managed Postgres backend for agent state and supports LangGraph checkpointers for short-term, thread-scoped memory.\n\nTherefore, Lakebase Postgres with a Postgres-compatible LangGraph checkpointer is the most direct match.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA LangGraph checkpointer saves the graph state at execution checkpoints. Each investigation can use a unique `thread_id`, allowing the application to retrieve the current or historical state and continue from the correct checkpoint.\n\nLakebase provides the characteristics required by the scenario:\n\n- Durability: State survives application restarts and redeployments.\n\n- Shared accessibility: Any application replica can connect to the same database and resume the thread.\n\n- Transactional behavior: Postgres transactions support atomic and consistent state updates.\n\n- Low latency: Lakebase is intended for transactional applications and operational state rather than analytical scans.\n\n- LangGraph compatibility: A Postgres-backed saver can be registered when compiling the graph.\n\n- Checkpoint-based resumption: Databricks documents Lakebase-backed checkpoints for resuming or forking agent conversations from saved checkpoints.\n\nA typical design would conceptually register a Postgres saver with the graph:\n\n```\nfrom langgraph.checkpoint.postgres import PostgresSaver checkpointer = PostgresSaver.from_conn_string(    \"postgresql://...\")checkpointer.setup() graph = builder.compile(checkpointer=checkpointer)\n```\n\nThe application then supplies the investigation’s thread identifier when invoking the graph:\n\n```\nconfig = {    \"configurable\": {        \"thread_id\": investigation_id    }}\n```\n\nLangGraph’s official documentation identifies `PostgresSaver` as a persistent production checkpointer and distinguishes it from in-memory savers, whose checkpoints are lost when the process restarts.","B. A Unity Catalog managed Delta table, queried directly on each turn to reconstruct thread state.\n\nDelta tables provide durable storage and ACID transactions, but directly querying a managed Delta table on every agent turn is not the best operational checkpointer design.\n\nDelta Lake is primarily optimized for lakehouse analytics, large-scale data processing, history, and batch or streaming workloads. Reconstructing graph state by scanning or querying event records introduces avoidable latency and requires the team to implement custom checkpoint serialization, concurrency control, checkpoint lookup, and replay logic.\n\nA Delta table could be useful for:\n\n- Investigation audit history.\n\n- Offline analytics.\n\n- Compliance reporting.\n\n- Long-term archival.\n\n- Training and evaluation datasets.\n\nHowever, the question asks which component should back the LangGraph checkpointer for low-latency transactional access. Lakebase is explicitly designed for that operational workload.","C. MLflow 3's tracing and experiment tracking store, used to log and replay the sequence of tool calls for each thread.\n\nMLflow tracing is intended for observability, evaluation, debugging, and recording agent execution details. A trace can show prompts, model calls, tool invocations, latency, inputs, outputs, and errors.\n\nHowever, a trace is not equivalent to a LangGraph checkpoint. Replaying logged tool calls would not reliably reconstruct the exact mutable graph state and could repeat external side effects, such as:\n\n- Creating duplicate fraud cases.\n\n- Re-running database updates.\n\n- Reissuing alerts.\n\n- Repeating API calls.\n\n- Producing different results from nondeterministic tools.\n\nMLflow should complement the checkpointer by providing observability and auditability, but it should not serve as the transactional runtime state store.","D. An MCP server that exposes the investigation state as a tool resource, refreshed each turn from object storage.\n\nMCP standardizes how an agent discovers and accesses tools, resources, and prompts. It is an integration protocol, not a transactional database or native LangGraph checkpoint backend.\n\nRefreshing state from object storage through an MCP resource would add unnecessary architectural layers and would not inherently provide:\n\n- Atomic checkpoint updates.\n\n- Efficient thread-state indexing.\n\n- Transaction isolation.\n\n- Native LangGraph checkpoint semantics.\n\n- Safe concurrent access by multiple replicas.\n\nAn MCP server could expose investigation evidence or external fraud-analysis tools, but durable graph execution state should remain in a transactional checkpointer backend."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-managemen","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/persistence","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/integrations/checkpointers"],"source_hash":"f0f18056f17184a158f45edfdbd1b9bbd2024f3bf3eeb35285f68f383a39e490","stem_ja":"チームはDatabricks上で不正調査エージェントを構築しています。数日にわたる長期の調査スレッドを、担当者が中断した正確な時点から再開できる必要があります。スレッド状態には、1秒未満での読み書き、原子的な更新、どのアプリケーションレプリカからでも再開できることが求められます。オーケストレーションにはLangGraphを使い、checkpointerを登録する必要があります。\n\nこのcheckpointerの保存先には、Databricksのどの構成要素を使うべきですか？","options_ja":["Lakebase Postgres。Postgres互換のcheckpointerを使い、スレッド単位の状態を低遅延かつACIDに準拠した読み書きで永続化する。","Unity Catalogの管理対象Deltaテーブル。各ターンで直接問い合わせ、スレッド状態を再構成する。","MLflow 3のトレーシングおよび実験追跡用ストア。各スレッドのツール呼び出し列を記録・再生する。","調査状態をツールリソースとして公開し、各ターンでオブジェクトストレージから更新するMCPサーバー。"],"explanation_ja":"求められているのは「同じスレッドの実行状態を保存し、どのレプリカでも続きを再開する」仕組みです。Lakebase Postgresを保存先とするcheckpointerが、低遅延のトランザクション処理に適します。追跡ログを保存するMLflowと、実行を再開するための状態保存を区別しましょう。","distractors_ja":["低遅延の読み書きとトランザクションを備えるPostgresを、LangGraphのスレッド状態の保存先として利用できます。","Deltaは耐久性のあるデータ保存に使えますが、ターンごとの低遅延な状態更新とcheckpointer連携にはPostgresが適します。","トレースは診断や評価の記録です。ログを再生するだけでは、checkpointerの原子的な状態保存の代わりになりません。","MCPはツールを公開する仕組みです。それ自体は、スレッド状態を原子的に永続化するデータベースではありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-003","number":3,"form":1,"stem":"A context engineer is running a coding agent on a large, multi-hour refactor spanning dozens of files in a single continuous session. Early on, the user establishes hard constraints (a naming convention, a deprecated API the agent must never call, a target Python version). Hundreds of turns later, even though these constraints are still technically present in the transcript, the agent begins violating them. The session has not yet reached the point where compaction would normally trigger.\n\nWhich context management technique most directly addresses this failure?","options":["Enable context compaction to summarize the full conversation into a shorter form.","Increase the underlying model's context window so more of the transcript fits without truncation.","Externalize the established constraints into a structured, persistent note that is kept close to the most recent turn, rather than relying on the model to retrieve them from deep in the transcript.","Disable the agent's tool calls temporarily to reduce the rate of token accumulation."],"correct":2,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"長い履歴から制約を救い出す","concept":"ce-f1-q003","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Externalize the established constraints into a structured, persistent note that is kept close to the most recent turn, rather than relying on the model to retrieve them from deep in the transcript.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe decisive clue is that the constraints are still present in the transcript, but the agent begins ignoring them after hundreds of turns. Therefore, the problem is not truncation or loss caused by exceeding the context window. It is an attention and context-positioning failure: important instructions have become buried beneath a large amount of newer interaction history.\n\nThe question also states that compaction would not normally trigger yet. This eliminates a reactive capacity-management answer and points toward a proactive context-management technique.\n\nOption C directly addresses the failure by:\n\n- Extracting durable constraints from the historical conversation\n\n- Representing them in a concise, structured form\n\n- Keeping them in a high-salience location near the agent’s current working context\n\n- Avoiding repeated dependence on retrieval from deep conversation history\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nLong context does not guarantee uniform attention to every token. As a session expands, older instructions can receive less effective attention even though they remain technically available to the model. This can cause an agent to overlook naming rules, compatibility requirements, exclusions, and prior decisions.\n\nA structured constraint note functions as a form of working memory or in-context scratchpad. For this refactoring task, it could contain entries such as:\n\n```\nActive project constraints:- Follow the established naming convention.- Never call the deprecated API.- All generated code must support the specified Python version.\n```\n\nThe note should be maintained as constraints change and injected near the active portion of the context. This improves salience without waiting for context-window pressure to become severe.\n\nThis answer also aligns closely with the official exam outline, which expects candidates to:\n\n- Identify the context-management technique that most directly resolves a described failure\n\n- Recognize elements consuming or losing the model’s effective attention budget\n\n- Apply proactive context management before compaction becomes necessary\n\n- Preserve constraints, exclusions, and user preferences during long-running workflows\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Enable context compaction to summarize the full conversation into a shorter form.\n\nCompaction can help when context pressure is high, but the question explicitly says the normal compaction point has not been reached. Enabling compaction prematurely is broader and more disruptive than necessary.\n\nCompaction also introduces a risk that important details will be omitted unless the compaction prompt is specifically designed to preserve constraints, decisions, exclusions, and preferences. The immediate problem can be addressed more precisely by extracting only the critical constraints and keeping them prominent.\n\nB. Increase the underlying model's context window so more of the transcript fits without truncation.\n\nThe transcript already fits, and the constraints have not been truncated. A larger context window therefore does not directly address the failure.\n\nIncreasing capacity may allow even more information to accumulate while leaving the constraints buried. Context-window size and effective attention are related but are not identical; having access to more tokens does not ensure that the model consistently prioritizes older instructions.\n\nD. Disable the agent's tool calls temporarily to reduce the rate of token accumulation.\n\nThis reduces future token growth but does not restore the salience of the constraints already buried in the transcript. It could also prevent the coding agent from performing necessary file inspection, search, testing, and editing operations.\n\nTool-call control is useful when verbose or unnecessary tool outputs are causing context pressure, but there is no indication that tool calls are the direct cause of this specific constraint-violation failure.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe likely trap is assuming that every long-session problem requires either compaction or a larger context window.\n\nThe scenario is testing the distinction between:\n\n- Information being absent from the context\n\n- Information being present but receiving insufficient attention\n\nBecause the constraints remain present and compaction is not yet required, the best response is to make the important information concise, structured, and recent.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nFor a production coding agent, maintain a compact project-state or constraint ledger that records:\n\n- Non-negotiable requirements\n\n- Prohibited APIs or actions\n\n- Runtime and compatibility targets\n\n- Architectural decisions\n\n- Naming and formatting conventions\n\n- Outstanding tasks and accepted decisions\n\nRefresh or re-inject this state during long-running work, particularly before major code-generation or modification steps.\n\nIn a real implementation, this note might be held in session state, an in-context scratchpad, or a durable state store when recovery across interruptions or sessions is required. The exam answer is specifically about improving the salience of constraints within the ongoing session; cross-session persistence would introduce additional storage, retrieval, governance, and update considerations.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks Certified Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n[https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-07/databricks-certified-context-engineer-associate-exam-guide.pdf](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-07/databricks-certified-context-engineer-associate-exam-guide.pdf)\n\n- Databricks Certified Context Engineer Associate\n[https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate](https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate)\n\n- Agent memory — Databricks documentation\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)\n\n- Managed agent memory — Databricks documentation\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/managed-memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/managed-memory)","source_option_explanations":["A. Enable context compaction to summarize the full conversation into a shorter form.\n\nCompaction can help when context pressure is high, but the question explicitly says the normal compaction point has not been reached. Enabling compaction prematurely is broader and more disruptive than necessary.\n\nCompaction also introduces a risk that important details will be omitted unless the compaction prompt is specifically designed to preserve constraints, decisions, exclusions, and preferences. The immediate problem can be addressed more precisely by extracting only the critical constraints and keeping them prominent.","B. Increase the underlying model's context window so more of the transcript fits without truncation.\n\nThe transcript already fits, and the constraints have not been truncated. A larger context window therefore does not directly address the failure.\n\nIncreasing capacity may allow even more information to accumulate while leaving the constraints buried. Context-window size and effective attention are related but are not identical; having access to more tokens does not ensure that the model consistently prioritizes older instructions.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Externalize the established constraints into a structured, persistent note that is kept close to the most recent turn, rather than relying on the model to retrieve them from deep in the transcript.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe decisive clue is that the constraints are still present in the transcript, but the agent begins ignoring them after hundreds of turns. Therefore, the problem is not truncation or loss caused by exceeding the context window. It is an attention and context-positioning failure: important instructions have become buried beneath a large amount of newer interaction history.\n\nThe question also states that compaction would not normally trigger yet. This eliminates a reactive capacity-management answer and points toward a proactive context-management technique.\n\nOption C directly addresses the failure by:\n\n- Extracting durable constraints from the historical conversation\n\n- Representing them in a concise, structured form\n\n- Keeping them in a high-salience location near the agent’s current working context\n\n- Avoiding repeated dependence on retrieval from deep conversation history\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nLong context does not guarantee uniform attention to every token. As a session expands, older instructions can receive less effective attention even though they remain technically available to the model. This can cause an agent to overlook naming rules, compatibility requirements, exclusions, and prior decisions.\n\nA structured constraint note functions as a form of working memory or in-context scratchpad. For this refactoring task, it could contain entries such as:\n\n```\nActive project constraints:- Follow the established naming convention.- Never call the deprecated API.- All generated code must support the specified Python version.\n```\n\nThe note should be maintained as constraints change and injected near the active portion of the context. This improves salience without waiting for context-window pressure to become severe.\n\nThis answer also aligns closely with the official exam outline, which expects candidates to:\n\n- Identify the context-management technique that most directly resolves a described failure\n\n- Recognize elements consuming or losing the model’s effective attention budget\n\n- Apply proactive context management before compaction becomes necessary\n\n- Preserve constraints, exclusions, and user preferences during long-running workflows","D. Disable the agent's tool calls temporarily to reduce the rate of token accumulation.\n\nThis reduces future token growth but does not restore the salience of the constraints already buried in the transcript. It could also prevent the coding agent from performing necessary file inspection, search, testing, and editing operations.\n\nTool-call control is useful when verbose or unnecessary tool outputs are causing context pressure, but there is no indication that tool calls are the direct cause of this specific constraint-violation failure."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-07/databricks-certified-context-engineer-associate-exam-guide.pdf","https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/managed-memory"],"source_hash":"1b7133de367e0f0b1f723d25cde156ef224599db52a580d43fb9f47894142d30","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアは、数十のファイルにまたがる大規模なリファクタリングを、数時間続く1つのセッションでコーディングエージェントに実行させています。ユーザーは初期段階で、命名規則、絶対に呼び出してはいけない非推奨API、対象のPythonバージョンという厳守事項を指定しました。数百ターン後、それらの制約は会話記録には残っているのに、エージェントが違反し始めました。セッションは、通常コンパクションが起動する時点にはまだ達していません。\n\nこの失敗に最も直接対処するコンテキスト管理手法はどれですか？","options_ja":["コンテキストのコンパクションを有効にし、会話全体を短い形に要約する。","基盤モデルのコンテキストウィンドウを拡大し、より多くの会話記録が切り捨てられずに収まるようにする。","確定した制約を構造化した永続メモに外出しし、会話記録の奥からモデルが探し出すことに頼らず、直近ターンの近くに配置しておく。","一時的にエージェントのツール呼び出しを無効にし、トークンが蓄積する速度を下げる。"],"explanation_ja":"「履歴に存在する」と「推論時に確実に参照される」は別です。重要な制約が長い履歴に埋もれているので、構造化した制約メモを外部に保持し、現在の入力に近い位置へ再提示します。容量不足だけを疑わず、必要な情報の位置と目立ちやすさを見ます。","distractors_ja":["履歴の整理には役立ちますが、厳守事項を確実に残し、直近で参照させる設計を直接指定した選択肢のほうが適切です。","制約は既に履歴に残っています。入力容量を増やすだけでは、埋もれた制約が参照されない問題を解決できません。","重要制約を独立したメモにし、現在の判断に使いやすい位置へ置くことで、過去の制約の見落としを直接防ぎます。","蓄積速度を下げても、既に埋もれた制約は復元されません。必要な作業能力まで制限してしまいます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-004","number":4,"form":1,"stem":"A context engineer is designing a Databricks agent with access to a Unity Catalog tool registry of 50 functions spanning finance, HR, and IT, though the task only ever needs 6 finance tools. On every turn, full JSON schemas for all 50 tools load into the system prompt, and each tool call returns its complete raw output — internal IDs, timestamps, pagination metadata — even though only two or three fields are ever used downstream. Sessions run under 15 turns, so compaction hasn't triggered.\n\nWhich combination of proactive strategies would most directly reduce context window pressure before compaction becomes necessary?","options":["Scope the agent's tool access to only the 6 relevant finance tools, and post-process each tool result so only the fields the agent needs are returned.","Increase the model's context window so all 50 schemas and full raw outputs fit without truncation.","Leave the full tool registry in place, but enable conversation compaction after every third turn.","Move the tool registry from Unity Catalog into the system prompt as static text so it no longer loads dynamically each turn."],"correct":0,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"ツール集合と返却内容の絞り込み","concept":"ce-f1-q004","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Scope the agent's tool access to only the 6 relevant finance tools, and post-process each tool result so only the fields the agent needs are returned.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question identifies two separate sources of unnecessary context consumption:\n\n- The full schemas for 50 tools are included even though the task requires only 6 finance tools.\n\n- Every tool call returns a large raw response containing metadata and fields that are never used downstream.\n\nThe most direct proactive solution must address both sources before compaction becomes necessary:\n\n- Reduce the baseline tool-definition cost by exposing only the relevant tools.\n\n- Reduce the per-call result cost by returning only the information required for subsequent reasoning.\n\nOption A applies the two techniques explicitly targeted by the exam domain: minimal tool sets and tool-result scoping.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nEvery tool exposed to an agent introduces context overhead through its name, description, parameter definitions, and schema. Providing 50 tools when only 6 are relevant wastes tokens and can also make tool selection more difficult because the model must distinguish among many unrelated capabilities.\n\nRestricting the agent to the six required finance tools provides:\n\n- Lower baseline context usage\n\n- Clearer tool selection\n\n- Less risk of choosing an unrelated HR or IT function\n\n- A smaller governed capability surface\n\nThe second part of the answer applies tool-result scoping. Instead of returning internal IDs, timestamps, pagination information, and unused attributes, the tool wrapper or post-processing layer should return only the two or three fields required by the agent.\n\nFor example, rather than returning:\n\n```\n{  \"internal_record_id\": \"FIN-93483\",  \"created_at\": \"2026-08-01T10:42:17Z\",  \"pagination\": {    \"page\": 1,    \"page_size\": 100,    \"next_token\": null  },  \"employee_id\": \"E1024\",  \"expense_total\": 7425.50,  \"approval_status\": \"Approved\"}\n```\n\nthe agent might receive only:\n\n```\n{  \"expense_total\": 7425.50,  \"approval_status\": \"Approved\"}\n```\n\nThis preserves the information required for reasoning while avoiding unnecessary token accumulation. It is more targeted than compaction because it prevents irrelevant context from entering the window in the first place.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Increase the model's context window so all 50 schemas and full raw outputs fit without truncation.\n\nA larger context window increases capacity but does not reduce unnecessary context consumption. All 50 schemas and verbose outputs would still be loaded, creating avoidable token cost and potentially distracting the model with unrelated tools and metadata.\n\nThe question asks for strategies that reduce context-window pressure, not merely accommodate it. Increasing capacity treats the symptom rather than removing the cause.\n\nC. Leave the full tool registry in place, but enable conversation compaction after every third turn.\n\nThis is a reactive approach. It allows irrelevant tool schemas and verbose results to enter the context and then attempts to compress the accumulated conversation.\n\nIt is also unnecessarily aggressive because sessions contain fewer than 15 turns and compaction has not naturally become necessary. Frequent compaction may introduce additional processing cost and could discard details that remain useful.\n\nThe better approach is to prevent low-value content from entering the context through minimal tool exposure and scoped outputs.\n\nD. Move the tool registry from Unity Catalog into the system prompt as static text so it no longer loads dynamically each turn.\n\nMoving the definitions does not eliminate their context cost. The static text would still form part of the system prompt supplied to the model, and all 50 tool descriptions and schemas would continue consuming tokens.\n\nThis option could also create maintenance and governance problems because the static definitions might become inconsistent with the actual Unity Catalog functions. It neither restricts the agent to the six relevant tools nor reduces verbose tool results.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main distractor is confusing more context capacity or earlier compaction with good proactive context management.\n\nThe exam tests whether the candidate recognizes that unnecessary information should be prevented from entering the context through:\n\n- Minimal tool exposure\n\n- Progressive or scoped tool discovery\n\n- Tool-result filtering\n\nCompaction should not be the first response when the context is being wastefully populated from the beginning.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nIn a production Databricks agent, tool access should be designed around the agent’s actual responsibilities rather than exposing every function available in the enterprise registry.\n\nA practical implementation could:\n\n- Grant the finance agent access only to approved finance functions.\n\n- Organize tools by domain or use staged discovery to load additional tools only when needed.\n\n- Create wrapper functions that return compact, task-specific objects.\n\n- Remove pagination data, transport metadata, internal identifiers, and audit fields unless the agent needs them.\n\n- Preserve full raw results outside the model context for tracing, auditing, debugging, or MLflow evaluation.\n\nThe exam answer is to minimize the available tool set and scope tool results. In a real implementation, the organization should also evaluate authorization, auditability, schema evolution, error handling, and whether removed metadata is required for downstream debugging or compliance.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks Certified Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-07/databricks-certified-context-engineer-associate-exam-guide.pdf)\n\n- [Create Agent Tools Using Unity Catalog Functions](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool)\n\n- [Connect Agents to Tools](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/agent-tool)\n\n- [Databricks Certified Context Engineer Associate](https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Scope the agent's tool access to only the 6 relevant finance tools, and post-process each tool result so only the fields the agent needs are returned.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question identifies two separate sources of unnecessary context consumption:\n\n- The full schemas for 50 tools are included even though the task requires only 6 finance tools.\n\n- Every tool call returns a large raw response containing metadata and fields that are never used downstream.\n\nThe most direct proactive solution must address both sources before compaction becomes necessary:\n\n- Reduce the baseline tool-definition cost by exposing only the relevant tools.\n\n- Reduce the per-call result cost by returning only the information required for subsequent reasoning.\n\nOption A applies the two techniques explicitly targeted by the exam domain: minimal tool sets and tool-result scoping.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nEvery tool exposed to an agent introduces context overhead through its name, description, parameter definitions, and schema. Providing 50 tools when only 6 are relevant wastes tokens and can also make tool selection more difficult because the model must distinguish among many unrelated capabilities.\n\nRestricting the agent to the six required finance tools provides:\n\n- Lower baseline context usage\n\n- Clearer tool selection\n\n- Less risk of choosing an unrelated HR or IT function\n\n- A smaller governed capability surface\n\nThe second part of the answer applies tool-result scoping. Instead of returning internal IDs, timestamps, pagination information, and unused attributes, the tool wrapper or post-processing layer should return only the two or three fields required by the agent.\n\nFor example, rather than returning:\n\n```\n{  \"internal_record_id\": \"FIN-93483\",  \"created_at\": \"2026-08-01T10:42:17Z\",  \"pagination\": {    \"page\": 1,    \"page_size\": 100,    \"next_token\": null  },  \"employee_id\": \"E1024\",  \"expense_total\": 7425.50,  \"approval_status\": \"Approved\"}\n```\n\nthe agent might receive only:\n\n```\n{  \"expense_total\": 7425.50,  \"approval_status\": \"Approved\"}\n```\n\nThis preserves the information required for reasoning while avoiding unnecessary token accumulation. It is more targeted than compaction because it prevents irrelevant context from entering the window in the first place.","B. Increase the model's context window so all 50 schemas and full raw outputs fit without truncation.\n\nA larger context window increases capacity but does not reduce unnecessary context consumption. All 50 schemas and verbose outputs would still be loaded, creating avoidable token cost and potentially distracting the model with unrelated tools and metadata.\n\nThe question asks for strategies that reduce context-window pressure, not merely accommodate it. Increasing capacity treats the symptom rather than removing the cause.","C. Leave the full tool registry in place, but enable conversation compaction after every third turn.\n\nThis is a reactive approach. It allows irrelevant tool schemas and verbose results to enter the context and then attempts to compress the accumulated conversation.\n\nIt is also unnecessarily aggressive because sessions contain fewer than 15 turns and compaction has not naturally become necessary. Frequent compaction may introduce additional processing cost and could discard details that remain useful.\n\nThe better approach is to prevent low-value content from entering the context through minimal tool exposure and scoped outputs.","D. Move the tool registry from Unity Catalog into the system prompt as static text so it no longer loads dynamically each turn.\n\nMoving the definitions does not eliminate their context cost. The static text would still form part of the system prompt supplied to the model, and all 50 tool descriptions and schemas would continue consuming tokens.\n\nThis option could also create maintenance and governance problems because the static definitions might become inconsistent with the actual Unity Catalog functions. It neither restricts the agent to the six relevant tools nor reduces verbose tool results."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-07/databricks-certified-context-engineer-associate-exam-guide.pdf","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/agent-tool","https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate"],"source_hash":"31b40cbfa8ce0e5d6d40d6b37cca7345570740e4503931d3157e82baecc471ae","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアは、財務・人事・ITの50個の関数を含むUnity CatalogのツールレジストリにアクセスするDatabricksエージェントを設計しています。ただし、この作業で必要なのは財務の6ツールだけです。毎ターン、全50ツールの完全なJSONスキーマをシステムプロンプトに読み込みます。また、ツール呼び出しは内部ID、タイムスタンプ、ページ送り用メタデータなどの生の出力をすべて返しますが、後続処理が使うのは2〜3個のフィールドだけです。セッションは15ターン未満なので、コンパクションは起動していません。\n\nコンパクションが必要になる前にコンテキストウィンドウへの負荷を最も直接減らす、予防的な方策の組み合わせはどれですか？","options_ja":["エージェントが利用できるツールを関連する財務の6ツールに限定し、各ツール結果を後処理して必要なフィールドだけを返す。","全50ツールのスキーマと完全な生の出力が切り捨てられずに収まるよう、モデルのコンテキストウィンドウを拡大する。","ツールレジストリ全体はそのままにし、3ターンごとに会話のコンパクションを有効にする。","ツールレジストリをUnity Catalogからシステムプロンプト内の静的なテキストへ移し、各ターンで動的に読み込まれないようにする。"],"explanation_ja":"増えすぎているのは、使わないツールのスキーマと、後続処理が読まない返却フィールドです。読み込むツールと返す情報を両方絞れば、必要な機能を保ちながら無駄な入力を発生源で減らせます。「後で要約する前に、最初から不要な情報を入れない」が判断の軸です。","distractors_ja":["必要な6ツールと必要な結果フィールドに絞り、スキーマと結果という2つの無駄を直接削減します。","容量は増やせても、入力の無駄や注意の分散は残ります。コンテキスト圧迫の原因を取り除く方法ではありません。","履歴を事後的に圧縮する対処であり、毎回投入される不要なツールスキーマや結果を予防的に減らせません。","静的な文章として置いてもモデルに毎回送るならトークンを消費します。保存場所の変更だけでは情報量は減りません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-005","number":5,"form":1,"stem":"A context engineer reviews a trace from an e-commerce support agent. A retrieval call returns two passages for a return-window question: an FAQ snippet stating 30 days (last updated 14 months ago, present due to a missed re-crawl), and a current policy snippet stating 60 days (published last month). Both land in the same context window. The agent's final answer tells the customer they have \"30 days to return the item, but our extended holiday policy also allows returns within 60 days\" — inventing a policy name that appears in neither passage.\n\nWhich context failure mode does this trace exhibit?","options":["Context poisoning, because a hallucinated policy detail was introduced and is now being treated as an established fact.","Context distraction, because the accumulated passages grew large enough that the model over-relied on pattern-matching instead of trained knowledge of retail policy.","Context confusion, because an irrelevant document unrelated to return policy was surfaced and used to generate a low-quality response.","Context clash, because two retrieved passages contain contradictory information, and the response reflects an attempt to reconcile conflicting values rather than favoring the authoritative source."],"correct":3,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"矛盾する根拠によるcontext clash","concept":"ce-f1-q005","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Context clash, because two retrieved passages contain contradictory information, and the response reflects an attempt to reconcile conflicting values rather than favoring the authoritative source.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe decisive fact is that the context window contains two passages about the same return policy that directly contradict each other:\n\n- An outdated FAQ says 30 days.\n\n- The current policy says 60 days.\n\nThe agent does not ignore one passage or use unrelated information. Instead, it tries to make both values simultaneously true by inventing an “extended holiday policy.” This is characteristic of context clash: conflicting statements remain in the active context and cause the model to produce an inconsistent or fabricated reconciliation.\n\nThe current Databricks exam guide explicitly includes diagnosing context poisoning, distraction, confusion, and clash from agent traces. It also emphasizes controlling retrieval so that agents use authoritative sources rather than conflicting or derived assets.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA context clash occurs when two or more pieces of active context provide incompatible information about the same subject.\n\nHere, both passages are relevant to the customer’s question, but they disagree on the return period. The model is therefore forced to choose between:\n\n- 30 days from a stale FAQ.\n\n- 60 days from the newer, authoritative policy.\n\nInstead of prioritizing the current source, the model attempts to harmonize the contradiction by inventing an unsupported explanation. The fabricated “extended holiday policy” is a symptom of the clash, not the primary context failure mode.\n\nThe Databricks exam outline specifically expects candidates to recognize conflicting information within agent context and to distinguish it from superfluous or irrelevant context. It also expects retrieval designs to constrain agents to authoritative sources.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Context poisoning, because a hallucinated policy detail was introduced and is now being treated as an established fact.\n\nThe agent did hallucinate the phrase “extended holiday policy,” but the trace does not show that this hallucination was saved into memory, inserted into later context, or repeatedly relied upon as an established fact.\n\nContext poisoning generally involves an error or hallucination entering persistent or accumulated context and then influencing subsequent reasoning. In this scenario, the underlying cause of the bad answer is the contradiction already present between the retrieved 30-day and 60-day passages.\n\nIf the invented holiday policy were stored in agent memory and reused in later customer interactions, the system could then exhibit context poisoning.\n\nB. Context distraction, because the accumulated passages grew large enough that the model over-relied on pattern-matching instead of trained knowledge of retail policy.\n\nNothing indicates that the context window is excessively long or that accumulated history has overwhelmed the model’s attention.\n\nOnly two short, directly relevant passages are described. The problem is not context volume or attention-budget exhaustion; it is that the two passages contain incompatible policy values.\n\nThe model should also rely on the organization’s retrieved policy rather than its general trained knowledge of retail practices.\n\nC. Context confusion, because an irrelevant document unrelated to return policy was surfaced and used to generate a low-quality response.\n\nNeither document is unrelated to the question. Both passages address the return window.\n\nContext confusion would be a better diagnosis if, for example, the retriever surfaced a shipping-policy document, warranty terms, or another customer’s conversation and the model incorporated that irrelevant material. Here, the retrieved content is relevant but mutually contradictory, which makes the failure a clash rather than confusion.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe distractor is A, because the final answer does contain a hallucinated phrase.\n\nHowever, candidates must diagnose the source of the failure, not merely identify a symptom in the output. The hallucination arose while the model attempted to reconcile contradictory retrieved evidence. Without evidence that the invented policy entered persistent context and was repeatedly reused, this is not primarily context poisoning.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nIn production, the retrieval pipeline should prevent stale and current policy versions from being presented as equally authoritative. Appropriate controls include:\n\n- Synchronizing the search index after policy updates.\n\n- Removing, expiring, or de-prioritizing superseded documents.\n\n- Storing publication date, effective date, document status, and authority level as metadata.\n\n- Applying filters so only currently effective policies are retrieved.\n\n- Deduplicating overlapping policy versions.\n\n- Instructing the agent to prefer the most recent authoritative source and explicitly report unresolved conflicts rather than inventing a reconciliation.\n\nDatabricks AI Search indexes configured with continuous synchronization update automatically as the source Delta table changes, whereas triggered indexes must be explicitly synchronized. MLflow evaluation and tracing can then be used to detect ungrounded answers and verify that retrieval changes reduce failures.\n\nThe expected exam answer is context clash. In a real implementation, the deeper remediation belongs primarily in retrieval freshness, metadata governance, source-authority ranking, and groundedness evaluation.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks Certified Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-07/databricks-certified-context-engineer-associate-exam-guide.pdf)\n\n- [Databricks Certified Context Engineer Associate](https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate)\n\n- [Create AI Search Endpoints and Indexes](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search)\n\n- [RetrievalGroundedness Judge](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/is_grounded)","source_option_explanations":["A. Context poisoning, because a hallucinated policy detail was introduced and is now being treated as an established fact.\n\nThe agent did hallucinate the phrase “extended holiday policy,” but the trace does not show that this hallucination was saved into memory, inserted into later context, or repeatedly relied upon as an established fact.\n\nContext poisoning generally involves an error or hallucination entering persistent or accumulated context and then influencing subsequent reasoning. In this scenario, the underlying cause of the bad answer is the contradiction already present between the retrieved 30-day and 60-day passages.\n\nIf the invented holiday policy were stored in agent memory and reused in later customer interactions, the system could then exhibit context poisoning.","B. Context distraction, because the accumulated passages grew large enough that the model over-relied on pattern-matching instead of trained knowledge of retail policy.\n\nNothing indicates that the context window is excessively long or that accumulated history has overwhelmed the model’s attention.\n\nOnly two short, directly relevant passages are described. The problem is not context volume or attention-budget exhaustion; it is that the two passages contain incompatible policy values.\n\nThe model should also rely on the organization’s retrieved policy rather than its general trained knowledge of retail practices.","C. Context confusion, because an irrelevant document unrelated to return policy was surfaced and used to generate a low-quality response.\n\nNeither document is unrelated to the question. Both passages address the return window.\n\nContext confusion would be a better diagnosis if, for example, the retriever surfaced a shipping-policy document, warranty terms, or another customer’s conversation and the model incorporated that irrelevant material. Here, the retrieved content is relevant but mutually contradictory, which makes the failure a clash rather than confusion.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Context clash, because two retrieved passages contain contradictory information, and the response reflects an attempt to reconcile conflicting values rather than favoring the authoritative source.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe decisive fact is that the context window contains two passages about the same return policy that directly contradict each other:\n\n- An outdated FAQ says 30 days.\n\n- The current policy says 60 days.\n\nThe agent does not ignore one passage or use unrelated information. Instead, it tries to make both values simultaneously true by inventing an “extended holiday policy.” This is characteristic of context clash: conflicting statements remain in the active context and cause the model to produce an inconsistent or fabricated reconciliation.\n\nThe current Databricks exam guide explicitly includes diagnosing context poisoning, distraction, confusion, and clash from agent traces. It also emphasizes controlling retrieval so that agents use authoritative sources rather than conflicting or derived assets.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA context clash occurs when two or more pieces of active context provide incompatible information about the same subject.\n\nHere, both passages are relevant to the customer’s question, but they disagree on the return period. The model is therefore forced to choose between:\n\n- 30 days from a stale FAQ.\n\n- 60 days from the newer, authoritative policy.\n\nInstead of prioritizing the current source, the model attempts to harmonize the contradiction by inventing an unsupported explanation. The fabricated “extended holiday policy” is a symptom of the clash, not the primary context failure mode.\n\nThe Databricks exam outline specifically expects candidates to recognize conflicting information within agent context and to distinguish it from superfluous or irrelevant context. It also expects retrieval designs to constrain agents to authoritative sources."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-07/databricks-certified-context-engineer-associate-exam-guide.pdf","https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/is_grounded"],"source_hash":"ac7003ff56304c79f692bfe91f09b227cdc8abc40f0d44533582ccf58ccdf2b2","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが電子商取引のサポートエージェントのトレースを調べています。返品期限の質問に対して、検索は2つの文章を返しました。1つは再クロール漏れで残っていた、14か月前に更新された「30日」とするFAQ断片です。もう1つは先月公開された、現在の「60日」とする方針の断片です。両方が同じコンテキストウィンドウに入りました。最終回答は「返品期限は30日ですが、年末年始の延長方針では60日以内も認められます」と説明し、どちらの文章にもない方針名を作り出しています。\n\nこのトレースが示しているコンテキストの失敗モードはどれですか？","options_ja":["Context poisoning。幻覚による方針の詳細が持ち込まれ、確定した事実として扱われているため。","Context distraction。蓄積した文章が大きくなり、モデルが学習済みの小売方針の知識ではなくパターン照合に頼りすぎたため。","Context confusion。返品方針と関係のない文書が検索され、低品質な回答の生成に使われたため。","Context clash。検索された2つの文章が矛盾しており、回答が正当な根拠を優先する代わりに、矛盾する値をつじつま合わせしようとしているため。"],"explanation_ja":"原因は「30日」と「60日」という根拠同士の矛盾です。エージェントは現在の正式方針を優先せず、架空の例外を作って両方を両立させました。入力の矛盾が主因ならcontext clashです。出力に幻覚があるという結果だけでpoisoningと判断しないことが重要です。","distractors_ja":["幻覚は出力されていますが、誤った事実が後続の文脈へ入り込み増幅される経過より、今回の直接原因である根拠の対立に着目します。","文章量や注意散漫が原因だとは示されていません。短い根拠であっても、内容が対立すればこの失敗は起こります。","両方とも返品期限に関係しています。無関係な文書が紛れ込んだのではなく、関連文書同士の値が矛盾しています。","現行方針と古いFAQの矛盾を不適切につじつま合わせした現象で、context clashに最も直接対応します。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-006","number":6,"form":1,"stem":"An agent's system prompt includes a 3,000-token legal disclaimer repeated verbatim every turn — needed only for a downstream logging system, not for the model's own reasoning — plus a front-loaded set of 40 few-shot examples. The task-relevant retrieved passage is typically only ~200 tokens per turn. As sessions progress, the agent's attention to that small retrieved passage measurably degrades.\n\nWhich change most directly improves model focus without sacrificing needed capability?","options":["Increase the model's context window so the disclaimer and few-shot block no longer compete with the retrieved passage.","Repeat the retrieved passage twice in the prompt so the model attends to it more strongly relative to the surrounding text.","Remove the disclaimer text from the model-facing prompt (routing it only to the downstream logging system), and trim the few-shot set down to the handful of examples that cover genuinely novel or ambiguous cases.","Switch the agent's reasoning mode from standard to extended thinking so the model has more computation to sift through the large prompt."],"correct":2,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"不要な常設指示による注意の分散","concept":"ce-f1-q006","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Remove the disclaimer text from the model-facing prompt (routing it only to the downstream logging system), and trim the few-shot set down to the handful of examples that cover genuinely novel or ambiguous cases.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe central problem is not that the model has reached its maximum context-window limit. The problem is that the model-facing context contains a large amount of information that is either:\n\n- Completely irrelevant to the model’s reasoning, such as the 3,000-token legal disclaimer.\n\n- Excessive and repetitive, such as 40 front-loaded few-shot examples.\n\n- Disproportionately larger than the approximately 200-token retrieved passage that actually answers the current question.\n\nThe strongest clue is that the model’s attention to the relevant retrieved passage degrades as sessions progress. This indicates context distraction and poor allocation of the model’s limited attention across the supplied tokens.\n\nOption C addresses the source of the problem. It removes content that the model does not need and retains only the few-shot examples that materially improve behavior.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA model should receive only the instructions, examples, conversation history, retrieved evidence, and tool information necessary for the current task.\n\nThe legal disclaimer is required by a downstream logging system, but the scenario explicitly states that it is not required for model reasoning. Therefore, it should be handled outside the model-facing prompt. Continuing to send it to the model:\n\n- Consumes tokens on every request.\n\n- Increases latency and inference cost.\n\n- Dilutes the prominence of the retrieved passage.\n\n- Creates additional irrelevant context through which the model must attend.\n\nSimilarly, 40 few-shot examples are unlikely to be equally valuable on every turn. Few-shot examples are useful when they demonstrate behavior that is difficult to express through instructions alone, but unnecessary or redundant examples add context without adding proportional capability.\n\nKeeping only examples that represent genuinely novel, difficult, or ambiguous cases preserves the behavioral benefit of few-shot prompting while reducing distraction.\n\nThis change therefore improves the signal-to-noise ratio of the prompt:\n\n- The retrieved passage becomes more prominent.\n\n- The model has fewer irrelevant tokens to process.\n\n- Required logging behavior is preserved outside the inference prompt.\n\n- Important few-shot capabilities remain available through a smaller, curated example set.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Increase the model's context window so the disclaimer and few-shot block no longer compete with the retrieved passage.\n\nA larger context window increases the amount of text the model can technically accept, but it does not make irrelevant text useful.\n\nThe existing problem is not described as prompt truncation or a context-limit error. The model is already receiving the retrieved passage; it is simply paying decreasing attention to it relative to the surrounding material.\n\nIncreasing the context window would also retain the underlying inefficiencies:\n\n- The unnecessary disclaimer would still be processed every turn.\n\n- All 40 examples would remain in every request.\n\n- Token usage, cost, and latency could remain unnecessarily high.\n\n- The relevant 200-token passage would still be surrounded by much larger blocks of competing context.\n\nA larger context window may be appropriate when all supplied information is necessary, but it is not the most direct solution when much of the prompt is unnecessary.\n\nB. Repeat the retrieved passage twice in the prompt so the model attends to it more strongly relative to the surrounding text.\n\nRepeating the passage attempts to compensate for excessive context by adding even more context.\n\nAlthough repetition can occasionally emphasize an instruction, it is not a sound primary remediation for poor context design. It:\n\n- Increases token consumption.\n\n- Duplicates information unnecessarily.\n\n- Does not remove the irrelevant disclaimer or redundant examples.\n\n- Can create maintenance problems if one copy is updated but another is not.\n\n- May unintentionally cause the model to over-weight or repeat information from the passage.\n\nThe better solution is to eliminate unnecessary competing content rather than duplicate the relevant content.\n\nD. Switch the agent's reasoning mode from standard to extended thinking so the model has more computation to sift through the large prompt.\n\nAdditional reasoning computation does not correct poor context composition.\n\nExtended reasoning may help with genuinely complex analysis, planning, or multi-step problem solving. However, it does not make a logging-only disclaimer relevant, nor does it justify supplying dozens of unnecessary examples.\n\nThis option may also increase latency and cost while leaving the actual failure mode untouched. The model would receive more computation but still operate on the same low-signal, high-noise context.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main distractor is A because candidates may assume that every context-related degradation should be solved by choosing a model with a larger context window.\n\nThe exam distinction is between:\n\n- Context capacity: Whether the prompt fits within the supported token limit.\n\n- Context quality: Whether the supplied tokens are relevant, concise, and useful.\n\nThe prompt already fits. The issue is excessive irrelevant and redundant content, so the correct action is to reduce and route context appropriately.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nIn a production agent, every piece of information should be routed to the component that actually requires it.\n\nA better implementation would separate:\n\n- Model context: Current user request, essential instructions, selected examples, relevant conversation state, and retrieved evidence.\n\n- Logging metadata: Legal notices, compliance identifiers, request metadata, and auditing fields that do not influence generation.\n\n- Long-term records: Full traces and historical interactions stored through an observability or persistence system rather than replayed into every prompt.\n\nThe team should also evaluate which few-shot examples produce measurable quality improvements. Rather than permanently including all examples, it can:\n\n- Retain a small representative set.\n\n- Dynamically select examples based on the current task.\n\n- Version and evaluate prompt variants.\n\n- Measure groundedness, relevance, latency, token usage, and cost before and after optimization.\n\nFor the certification exam, the key principle is: remove irrelevant context and retain only the minimum context required to preserve the agent’s capabilities.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Agent system design patterns — Development guidance](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- [AI Search retrieval quality guide](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality)\n\n- [Evaluate and compare prompt versions](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts)\n\n- [Create and edit prompts](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/create-and-edit-prompts)","source_option_explanations":["A. Increase the model's context window so the disclaimer and few-shot block no longer compete with the retrieved passage.\n\nA larger context window increases the amount of text the model can technically accept, but it does not make irrelevant text useful.\n\nThe existing problem is not described as prompt truncation or a context-limit error. The model is already receiving the retrieved passage; it is simply paying decreasing attention to it relative to the surrounding material.\n\nIncreasing the context window would also retain the underlying inefficiencies:\n\n- The unnecessary disclaimer would still be processed every turn.\n\n- All 40 examples would remain in every request.\n\n- Token usage, cost, and latency could remain unnecessarily high.\n\n- The relevant 200-token passage would still be surrounded by much larger blocks of competing context.\n\nA larger context window may be appropriate when all supplied information is necessary, but it is not the most direct solution when much of the prompt is unnecessary.","B. Repeat the retrieved passage twice in the prompt so the model attends to it more strongly relative to the surrounding text.\n\nRepeating the passage attempts to compensate for excessive context by adding even more context.\n\nAlthough repetition can occasionally emphasize an instruction, it is not a sound primary remediation for poor context design. It:\n\n- Increases token consumption.\n\n- Duplicates information unnecessarily.\n\n- Does not remove the irrelevant disclaimer or redundant examples.\n\n- Can create maintenance problems if one copy is updated but another is not.\n\n- May unintentionally cause the model to over-weight or repeat information from the passage.\n\nThe better solution is to eliminate unnecessary competing content rather than duplicate the relevant content.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Remove the disclaimer text from the model-facing prompt (routing it only to the downstream logging system), and trim the few-shot set down to the handful of examples that cover genuinely novel or ambiguous cases.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe central problem is not that the model has reached its maximum context-window limit. The problem is that the model-facing context contains a large amount of information that is either:\n\n- Completely irrelevant to the model’s reasoning, such as the 3,000-token legal disclaimer.\n\n- Excessive and repetitive, such as 40 front-loaded few-shot examples.\n\n- Disproportionately larger than the approximately 200-token retrieved passage that actually answers the current question.\n\nThe strongest clue is that the model’s attention to the relevant retrieved passage degrades as sessions progress. This indicates context distraction and poor allocation of the model’s limited attention across the supplied tokens.\n\nOption C addresses the source of the problem. It removes content that the model does not need and retains only the few-shot examples that materially improve behavior.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA model should receive only the instructions, examples, conversation history, retrieved evidence, and tool information necessary for the current task.\n\nThe legal disclaimer is required by a downstream logging system, but the scenario explicitly states that it is not required for model reasoning. Therefore, it should be handled outside the model-facing prompt. Continuing to send it to the model:\n\n- Consumes tokens on every request.\n\n- Increases latency and inference cost.\n\n- Dilutes the prominence of the retrieved passage.\n\n- Creates additional irrelevant context through which the model must attend.\n\nSimilarly, 40 few-shot examples are unlikely to be equally valuable on every turn. Few-shot examples are useful when they demonstrate behavior that is difficult to express through instructions alone, but unnecessary or redundant examples add context without adding proportional capability.\n\nKeeping only examples that represent genuinely novel, difficult, or ambiguous cases preserves the behavioral benefit of few-shot prompting while reducing distraction.\n\nThis change therefore improves the signal-to-noise ratio of the prompt:\n\n- The retrieved passage becomes more prominent.\n\n- The model has fewer irrelevant tokens to process.\n\n- Required logging behavior is preserved outside the inference prompt.\n\n- Important few-shot capabilities remain available through a smaller, curated example set.","D. Switch the agent's reasoning mode from standard to extended thinking so the model has more computation to sift through the large prompt.\n\nAdditional reasoning computation does not correct poor context composition.\n\nExtended reasoning may help with genuinely complex analysis, planning, or multi-step problem solving. However, it does not make a logging-only disclaimer relevant, nor does it justify supplying dozens of unnecessary examples.\n\nThis option may also increase latency and cost while leaving the actual failure mode untouched. The model would receive more computation but still operate on the same low-signal, high-noise context."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/create-and-edit-prompts"],"source_hash":"f20154728128379afe339988473162ffa63bda3591e3cad8cb8e6a09bb481091","stem_ja":"エージェントのシステムプロンプトには、各ターンで一字一句繰り返される3,000トークンの法的免責事項と、先頭に配置した40個のfew-shot例が含まれています。免責事項が必要なのは後続のログシステムだけで、モデル自身の推論には不要です。作業に関係する検索文章は、通常1ターンあたり約200トークンにすぎません。セッションが進むにつれ、その短い検索文章に対するエージェントの注意が測定上低下しています。\n\n必要な能力を損なわずにモデルの集中を最も直接改善する変更はどれですか？","options_ja":["モデルのコンテキストウィンドウを拡大し、免責事項とfew-shotブロックが検索文章と競合しなくなるようにする。","プロンプト中で検索文章を2回繰り返し、周囲の文章より強く注意を向けさせる。","免責事項をモデル向けプロンプトから除き、後続のログシステムだけに渡す。また、few-shot例を、本当に新しい事例や曖昧な事例をカバーする少数に絞る。","推論モードを標準から拡張思考に切り替え、大きなプロンプトを読み解く計算量を増やす。"],"explanation_ja":"モデルに不要な3,000トークンと、価値の低い例が重要な200トークンを埋もれさせています。免責事項は必要なログ経路へ分離し、例は新しい判断に役立つものだけ残します。必要な情報を増幅するより、不要な情報を取り除くほうが、費用と注意の両面で直接的です。","distractors_ja":["入力容量を増やしても不要な文章の占める注意や費用は消えません。まずモデルが読む必要のない情報を分離します。","重複によって入力が増えます。大量の不要な免責事項と例が残るため、根本的な改善になりません。","ログだけに必要な文章を推論入力から外し、少数の有用な例を残すため、必要機能を保ちながら雑音を減らせます。","追加の計算で不要な入力を処理する対処です。入力の雑音を減らすより費用と遅延が増えやすくなります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-007","number":7,"form":1,"stem":"An internal engineering-assistant agent answers monorepo questions via sequential code-search and file-read tool calls in a single session. MLflow 3 traces show that once a session passes ~60% of the context window — driven almost entirely by raw file contents skimmed once and never referenced again — the agent begins citing wrong function definitions and contradicting file paths it named earlier. The team wants long sessions preserved without discarding facts still needed, like the bug description and the two or three confirmed-relevant files.\n\nWhich intervention most directly restores retrieval accuracy and reasoning quality?","options":["Raise the model's context window limit so accumulated file contents no longer approach the 60% threshold.","Truncate the oldest messages on a fixed schedule, regardless of whether they contain the bug description or confirmed-relevant file paths.","Disable the file-read tool once the session exceeds 60% of the context window, forcing the agent to reason only from what it has already retrieved.","Apply compaction that clears low-value raw tool outputs (e.g., full file contents only briefly scanned) while preserving a summary of key facts established so far, such as the bug description and confirmed-relevant files."],"correct":3,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"価値の低いツール出力の圧縮","concept":"ce-f1-q007","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Apply compaction that clears low-value raw tool outputs (e.g., full file contents only briefly scanned) while preserving a summary of key facts established so far, such as the bug description and confirmed-relevant files.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe trace identifies a clear relationship between context growth and declining agent quality:\n\n- Raw file contents dominate the context window.\n\n- Most files were inspected once and never used again.\n\n- Important working facts remain relatively small: the bug description, relevant paths, confirmed findings, and unresolved hypotheses.\n\n- After context usage exceeds roughly 60%, the agent begins confusing definitions and contradicting previously identified paths.\n\nThe best intervention is therefore selective compaction. It removes or offloads low-value raw tool output while retaining a concise summary of the facts required for continued debugging.\n\nThis preserves the long-running session without forcing the model to repeatedly process every file it has ever inspected.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nCompaction reduces the active conversation size by replacing older, verbose content with a smaller representation of what remains important.\n\nIn this scenario, the compaction process should preserve information such as:\n\n- The original bug description and reproduction steps.\n\n- The repository branch or commit being investigated.\n\n- The two or three files confirmed to be relevant.\n\n- Important function names and verified definitions.\n\n- Fixes already attempted and their results.\n\n- Hypotheses confirmed or ruled out.\n\n- The next planned search or code change.\n\nLow-value content can be removed from the active model context, including:\n\n- Full contents of files that were only skimmed.\n\n- Repeated code-search results.\n\n- Unrelated function definitions.\n\n- Large tool outputs whose useful conclusions are already summarized.\n\nLangChain’s context-engineering guidance describes summarization as persistently replacing old message history with a compact summary. Its Deep Agents guidance also supports offloading large tool results while keeping a reference or preview so the agent can retrieve them again if needed.\n\nThis directly improves the context’s signal-to-noise ratio. Relevant repository facts remain prominent, while stale code no longer competes with current evidence for the model’s attention.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Raise the model's context window limit so accumulated file contents no longer approach the 60% threshold.\n\nA larger window increases capacity but does not correct the poor composition of the context.\n\nThe agent would continue carrying large quantities of stale and irrelevant source code. This could postpone the observed threshold, but it would not necessarily restore accurate retrieval or reasoning. Models can still become distracted by stale or off-topic content even when the entire conversation technically fits within the context window.\n\nIt could also increase input-token cost and latency. Increasing capacity is appropriate when all retained content is necessary; here, most raw file contents are explicitly described as low-value after their initial inspection.\n\nB. Truncate the oldest messages on a fixed schedule, regardless of whether they contain the bug description or confirmed-relevant file paths.\n\nFixed truncation is not relevance-aware.\n\nIt could delete foundational information merely because it appeared early in the session, including:\n\n- The original failure symptoms.\n\n- Constraints supplied by the developer.\n\n- Confirmed-relevant files.\n\n- Earlier experiments that ruled out a proposed fix.\n\nThe question specifically requires preserving facts that remain useful. Selective compaction is superior because it condenses old information according to value rather than deleting it according to age. Official LangChain guidance notes that simple trimming can lose needed information, whereas summarization can retain important conversational context.\n\nC. Disable the file-read tool once the session exceeds 60% of the context window, forcing the agent to reason only from what it has already retrieved.\n\nDisabling the tool would prevent further context growth, but it would also remove an essential debugging capability.\n\nThe agent may need to:\n\n- Inspect a newly discovered file.\n\n- Re-read the canonical definition of a function.\n\n- Verify whether a file has changed.\n\n- Compare an earlier assumption with the actual source.\n\n- Follow imports or call chains into another module.\n\nForcing the agent to rely on its already-confused context could reinforce incorrect function definitions and file paths. The correct response is to manage accumulated tool output, not prevent the agent from obtaining new authoritative evidence.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe primary distractor is A because the failure appears after the session reaches a context-usage threshold.\n\nHowever, the problem is not simply that the model lacks sufficient maximum capacity. The context is filled with low-value raw tool output. Enlarging the window preserves that noise, while compaction removes it and retains the important working state.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production coding agent should separate the active reasoning context from the complete repository artifacts it has inspected.\n\nA practical compaction summary could contain:\n\n```\nCurrent objective:Resolve intermittent null records in the billing aggregation pipeline. Confirmed-relevant files:- pipelines/billing/aggregate.py- pipelines/billing/normalizer.py- tests/billing/test_aggregate.py Verified findings:- aggregate_invoices() calls normalize_record() before grouping.- normalize_record() can return None for legacy records.- The failing test uses a shared fixture modified by another test. Ruled-out hypotheses:- Database query ordering is not responsible.- Increasing the test timeout did not change the failure rate. Next action:Inspect fixture isolation in test_aggregate.py.\n```\n\nThe complete raw files can remain in repository storage or an external scratch workspace and be re-read through tools when required. Deep Agents documentation describes this pattern of offloading large tool responses and summarizing older messages to keep agents effective during extended sessions.\n\nMLflow Tracing remains valuable for confirming that context growth correlates with failures because it records application inputs, outputs, tool calls, and intermediate execution steps. It diagnoses and measures the problem, while compaction is the context-management intervention that resolves it.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Context engineering in Deep Agents\n[https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/deepagents/context-engineering](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/deepagents/context-engineering)\n\n- Context engineering in agents\n[https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/context-engineering](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/context-engineering)\n\n- Short-term memory\n[https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/short-term-memory](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/short-term-memory)\n\n- MLflow Tracing – GenAI observability\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/)","source_option_explanations":["A. Raise the model's context window limit so accumulated file contents no longer approach the 60% threshold.\n\nA larger window increases capacity but does not correct the poor composition of the context.\n\nThe agent would continue carrying large quantities of stale and irrelevant source code. This could postpone the observed threshold, but it would not necessarily restore accurate retrieval or reasoning. Models can still become distracted by stale or off-topic content even when the entire conversation technically fits within the context window.\n\nIt could also increase input-token cost and latency. Increasing capacity is appropriate when all retained content is necessary; here, most raw file contents are explicitly described as low-value after their initial inspection.","B. Truncate the oldest messages on a fixed schedule, regardless of whether they contain the bug description or confirmed-relevant file paths.\n\nFixed truncation is not relevance-aware.\n\nIt could delete foundational information merely because it appeared early in the session, including:\n\n- The original failure symptoms.\n\n- Constraints supplied by the developer.\n\n- Confirmed-relevant files.\n\n- Earlier experiments that ruled out a proposed fix.\n\nThe question specifically requires preserving facts that remain useful. Selective compaction is superior because it condenses old information according to value rather than deleting it according to age. Official LangChain guidance notes that simple trimming can lose needed information, whereas summarization can retain important conversational context.","C. Disable the file-read tool once the session exceeds 60% of the context window, forcing the agent to reason only from what it has already retrieved.\n\nDisabling the tool would prevent further context growth, but it would also remove an essential debugging capability.\n\nThe agent may need to:\n\n- Inspect a newly discovered file.\n\n- Re-read the canonical definition of a function.\n\n- Verify whether a file has changed.\n\n- Compare an earlier assumption with the actual source.\n\n- Follow imports or call chains into another module.\n\nForcing the agent to rely on its already-confused context could reinforce incorrect function definitions and file paths. The correct response is to manage accumulated tool output, not prevent the agent from obtaining new authoritative evidence.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Apply compaction that clears low-value raw tool outputs (e.g., full file contents only briefly scanned) while preserving a summary of key facts established so far, such as the bug description and confirmed-relevant files.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe trace identifies a clear relationship between context growth and declining agent quality:\n\n- Raw file contents dominate the context window.\n\n- Most files were inspected once and never used again.\n\n- Important working facts remain relatively small: the bug description, relevant paths, confirmed findings, and unresolved hypotheses.\n\n- After context usage exceeds roughly 60%, the agent begins confusing definitions and contradicting previously identified paths.\n\nThe best intervention is therefore selective compaction. It removes or offloads low-value raw tool output while retaining a concise summary of the facts required for continued debugging.\n\nThis preserves the long-running session without forcing the model to repeatedly process every file it has ever inspected.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nCompaction reduces the active conversation size by replacing older, verbose content with a smaller representation of what remains important.\n\nIn this scenario, the compaction process should preserve information such as:\n\n- The original bug description and reproduction steps.\n\n- The repository branch or commit being investigated.\n\n- The two or three files confirmed to be relevant.\n\n- Important function names and verified definitions.\n\n- Fixes already attempted and their results.\n\n- Hypotheses confirmed or ruled out.\n\n- The next planned search or code change.\n\nLow-value content can be removed from the active model context, including:\n\n- Full contents of files that were only skimmed.\n\n- Repeated code-search results.\n\n- Unrelated function definitions.\n\n- Large tool outputs whose useful conclusions are already summarized.\n\nLangChain’s context-engineering guidance describes summarization as persistently replacing old message history with a compact summary. Its Deep Agents guidance also supports offloading large tool results while keeping a reference or preview so the agent can retrieve them again if needed.\n\nThis directly improves the context’s signal-to-noise ratio. Relevant repository facts remain prominent, while stale code no longer competes with current evidence for the model’s attention."],"references":["https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/deepagents/context-engineering","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/context-engineering","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/short-term-memory","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/"],"source_hash":"7b2d4079f979fdd9c193b9281ca8160fad44cc527dbd00c3f8ae2530c75ee08a","stem_ja":"社内の技術支援エージェントは、1つのセッション内でコード検索とファイル読み取りを順に呼び出し、モノレポに関する質問へ答えています。MLflow 3のトレースでは、セッションがコンテキストウィンドウの約60％を超えると、誤った関数定義を引用したり、以前に示したファイルパスと矛盾したりし始めます。蓄積量のほとんどは、一度ざっと読んだ後に再参照されないファイルの生の内容です。チームは、バグの説明や関連が確認された2〜3個のファイルなど、まだ必要な事実を捨てずに長いセッションを維持したいと考えています。\n\n検索の正確さと推論品質を最も直接回復する介入はどれですか？","options_ja":["モデルのコンテキストウィンドウ上限を引き上げ、蓄積したファイル内容が60％のしきい値に近づかないようにする。","バグの説明や関連が確認されたファイルパスを含むかにかかわらず、固定の間隔で最古のメッセージを切り捨てる。","セッションがコンテキストウィンドウの60％を超えたらファイル読み取りツールを無効にし、取得済みの情報だけで推論させる。","短時間しか参照しなかったファイル全体などの価値の低い生のツール出力を消し、バグの説明や関連が確認されたファイルなど、確定した重要事実の要約を保つコンパクションを適用する。"],"explanation_ja":"履歴の生データを全部残すことより、次の判断に必要な事実を残すことが重要です。一度読んだだけのファイル出力を整理し、バグの定義や確認済みパスを要約として維持します。60％はこの事例の観測値であり、すべてのモデルに共通する固定の限界ではありません。","distractors_ja":["容量拡大は先送りになります。使われない生のファイル内容が増え続けるという原因はそのままです。","古いという理由だけで削ると、バグの説明など後続でも必要な情報を失います。保持価値を見て整理する必要があります。","追加の証拠を調べる能力を失い、既にある不要な情報も残ります。必要な検索の正確さを回復する方法ではありません。","不要な生の出力を削り、確定事項を残すため、長い作業を継続しながら入力の密度と注意を改善できます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-008","number":8,"form":1,"stem":"A data analyst is preparing a Genie space for a retail merchandising team before company-wide rollout. Day-to-day questions fall into two buckets: (1) a long tail of loosely-phrased, exploratory questions about margins, markdowns, and category performance, and (2) a small, fixed set of 8 recurring, high-stakes questions (e.g., \"weekly sell-through rate by region\") reviewed every Monday by leadership, requiring an exact, auditable calculation every time.\n\nWhich approach best closes the gap to production readiness?","options":["Write trusted SQL assets for every question the team might plausibly ask, including the long-tail exploratory questions, so all responses are labeled Trusted.","Write a trusted SQL asset for each of the 8 recurring leadership questions so they always return an exact, auditable result, and add general instructions plus sample questions that cover the terminology and phrasing patterns of the exploratory long-tail questions.","Rely entirely on general instructions to define margin, markdown, and sell-through logic, and skip trusted assets, since the sample questions alone will teach Genie how to interpret the leadership questions correctly.","Add only sample questions that mirror the 8 leadership questions verbatim, without instructions or trusted assets, so Genie learns the correct SQL pattern purely from the example phrasing."],"correct":1,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"Genieの指示・例・信頼済みSQL","concept":"ce-f1-q008","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Write a trusted SQL asset for each of the 8 recurring leadership questions so they always return an exact, auditable result, and add general instructions plus sample questions that cover the terminology and phrasing patterns of the exploratory long-tail questions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe two question categories require different levels of determinism:\n\n- The eight recurring leadership questions are fixed, high stakes, and reviewed regularly. They need verified business logic and reproducible results.\n\n- The exploratory long-tail questions vary in wording and analytical intent. Attempting to pre-author trusted SQL for every possible variation would be impractical.\n\nThe appropriate production-readiness strategy is therefore to use trusted SQL assets for the small, predictable, high-risk question set, while using curated instructions, terminology, examples, metadata, synonyms, and business-semantic definitions to help Genie interpret the broader exploratory workload.\n\nDatabricks defines trusted assets as parameterized example SQL queries or Unity Catalog SQL functions whose logic has been verified by the agent author. When Genie answers with a trusted asset, it provides a verified answer, giving users additional confidence in the result.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nFor the eight leadership questions, the calculations must be exact and auditable. A trusted asset allows the analyst to encode and review the required SQL logic in advance, including:\n\n- Sell-through-rate formulas.\n\n- Time-period boundaries.\n\n- Regional grouping logic.\n\n- Product eligibility filters.\n\n- Treatment of returns or cancelled sales.\n\n- Required joins and aggregations.\n\nA parameterized trusted query can also support controlled variation, such as a different week, region, or category, while retaining the verified calculation structure. Databricks states that parameterized example queries reuse a defined query structure with runtime values and can produce verified responses when Genie uses their exact SQL.\n\nFor exploratory questions, Genie needs enough domain context to recognize loosely phrased business language such as:\n\n- “Which categories are losing margin?”\n\n- “Where are markdowns getting worse?”\n\n- “How did premium apparel perform?”\n\n- “Show slow-moving categories.”\n\nCurated metadata, synonyms, SQL expressions, representative examples, and focused instructions help Genie connect this informal language to the correct tables, columns, metrics, and filters. Databricks recommends using SQL expressions for common business concepts, example SQL for common or ambiguous query patterns, and concise text instructions for globally applicable guidance.\n\nThus, option B applies deterministic assets where precision is mandatory while preserving Genie's flexible natural-language exploration for the long tail.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Write trusted SQL assets for every question the team might plausibly ask, including the long-tail exploratory questions, so all responses are labeled Trusted.\n\nThis is unnecessarily broad and difficult to maintain.\n\nThe long-tail questions are loosely phrased and potentially open-ended. The analyst cannot realistically anticipate every combination of category, period, region, metric, comparison, and follow-up question.\n\nTrusted assets should be concentrated on predictable questions whose calculation logic must remain fixed. Trying to encode every exploratory possibility would create excessive authoring and maintenance effort and could produce many overlapping or conflicting examples.\n\nIt also ignores the intended flexibility of Genie, which can use curated semantics and examples to generate SQL for related questions that were not written in advance.\n\nC. Rely entirely on general instructions to define margin, markdown, and sell-through logic, and skip trusted assets, since the sample questions alone will teach Genie how to interpret the leadership questions correctly.\n\nGeneral instructions are not the strongest mechanism for enforcing exact metric calculations.\n\nNatural-language instructions must be interpreted by Genie and therefore do not provide the same deterministic assurance as verified SQL logic. Small differences in generated filters, joins, date ranges, or aggregations could change leadership results from one query to another.\n\nDatabricks recommends structured SQL definitions and examples over plain-text instructions for business semantics. The eight high-stakes recurring questions specifically justify trusted assets.\n\nD. Add only sample questions that mirror the 8 leadership questions verbatim, without instructions or trusted assets, so Genie learns the correct SQL pattern purely from the example phrasing.\n\nA question’s wording does not define its calculation logic.\n\nFor example, showing “weekly sell-through rate by region” does not establish:\n\n- The numerator and denominator.\n\n- Whether inventory is beginning, ending, or average inventory.\n\n- Which sales statuses count.\n\n- How returns are treated.\n\n- Which date defines the week.\n\n- Which regional hierarchy is authoritative.\n\nWithout trusted SQL, SQL expressions, or other semantic guidance, Genie must infer these decisions. That is inappropriate for high-stakes, recurring leadership reporting.\n\nAlso, strictly speaking, UI sample questions primarily help users begin asking questions. They should not be confused with verified example SQL queries or trusted assets.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main distractor is A because trusted assets sound desirable for every question.\n\nThe exam tests whether candidates can match the configuration method to the workload:\n\n- Use trusted assets for known, recurring, high-value questions requiring verified calculations.\n\n- Use curated semantics and examples for variable exploratory questions.\n\nProduction readiness does not mean converting every possible natural-language question into a prewritten trusted query.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nFor an actual retail Genie Agent, the analyst should implement the eight leadership questions as parameterized example queries or Unity Catalog SQL functions and carefully validate them against existing reports.\n\nFor the exploratory workload, the analyst should additionally configure:\n\n- SQL expressions for margin, markdown rate, sell-through rate, and other reusable metrics.\n\n- Synonyms for retailer-specific terminology.\n\n- Clearly defined table and column metadata.\n\n- Example SQL for representative ambiguous or complex questions.\n\n- Focused general instructions for global business rules.\n\n- Join relationships and relevant filters.\n\n- Benchmarks covering both the eight leadership questions and representative long-tail questions.\n\nDatabricks recommends testing with realistic business questions, inspecting generated SQL, correcting inaccurate responses, and using benchmarks to measure accuracy before broad rollout.\n\nTherefore, B is the expected certification answer, although a mature production implementation should supplement its general instructions and sample questions with structured SQL expressions, metadata, synonyms, and benchmark testing.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Tune Genie Agent quality\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality)\n\n- Curate an effective Genie Agent\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices)\n\n- Test and monitor a Genie Agent\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/monitor](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/monitor)\n\n- Genie Agents concepts\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/concepts](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/concepts)","source_option_explanations":["A. Write trusted SQL assets for every question the team might plausibly ask, including the long-tail exploratory questions, so all responses are labeled Trusted.\n\nThis is unnecessarily broad and difficult to maintain.\n\nThe long-tail questions are loosely phrased and potentially open-ended. The analyst cannot realistically anticipate every combination of category, period, region, metric, comparison, and follow-up question.\n\nTrusted assets should be concentrated on predictable questions whose calculation logic must remain fixed. Trying to encode every exploratory possibility would create excessive authoring and maintenance effort and could produce many overlapping or conflicting examples.\n\nIt also ignores the intended flexibility of Genie, which can use curated semantics and examples to generate SQL for related questions that were not written in advance.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Write a trusted SQL asset for each of the 8 recurring leadership questions so they always return an exact, auditable result, and add general instructions plus sample questions that cover the terminology and phrasing patterns of the exploratory long-tail questions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe two question categories require different levels of determinism:\n\n- The eight recurring leadership questions are fixed, high stakes, and reviewed regularly. They need verified business logic and reproducible results.\n\n- The exploratory long-tail questions vary in wording and analytical intent. Attempting to pre-author trusted SQL for every possible variation would be impractical.\n\nThe appropriate production-readiness strategy is therefore to use trusted SQL assets for the small, predictable, high-risk question set, while using curated instructions, terminology, examples, metadata, synonyms, and business-semantic definitions to help Genie interpret the broader exploratory workload.\n\nDatabricks defines trusted assets as parameterized example SQL queries or Unity Catalog SQL functions whose logic has been verified by the agent author. When Genie answers with a trusted asset, it provides a verified answer, giving users additional confidence in the result.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nFor the eight leadership questions, the calculations must be exact and auditable. A trusted asset allows the analyst to encode and review the required SQL logic in advance, including:\n\n- Sell-through-rate formulas.\n\n- Time-period boundaries.\n\n- Regional grouping logic.\n\n- Product eligibility filters.\n\n- Treatment of returns or cancelled sales.\n\n- Required joins and aggregations.\n\nA parameterized trusted query can also support controlled variation, such as a different week, region, or category, while retaining the verified calculation structure. Databricks states that parameterized example queries reuse a defined query structure with runtime values and can produce verified responses when Genie uses their exact SQL.\n\nFor exploratory questions, Genie needs enough domain context to recognize loosely phrased business language such as:\n\n- “Which categories are losing margin?”\n\n- “Where are markdowns getting worse?”\n\n- “How did premium apparel perform?”\n\n- “Show slow-moving categories.”\n\nCurated metadata, synonyms, SQL expressions, representative examples, and focused instructions help Genie connect this informal language to the correct tables, columns, metrics, and filters. Databricks recommends using SQL expressions for common business concepts, example SQL for common or ambiguous query patterns, and concise text instructions for globally applicable guidance.\n\nThus, option B applies deterministic assets where precision is mandatory while preserving Genie's flexible natural-language exploration for the long tail.","C. Rely entirely on general instructions to define margin, markdown, and sell-through logic, and skip trusted assets, since the sample questions alone will teach Genie how to interpret the leadership questions correctly.\n\nGeneral instructions are not the strongest mechanism for enforcing exact metric calculations.\n\nNatural-language instructions must be interpreted by Genie and therefore do not provide the same deterministic assurance as verified SQL logic. Small differences in generated filters, joins, date ranges, or aggregations could change leadership results from one query to another.\n\nDatabricks recommends structured SQL definitions and examples over plain-text instructions for business semantics. The eight high-stakes recurring questions specifically justify trusted assets.","D. Add only sample questions that mirror the 8 leadership questions verbatim, without instructions or trusted assets, so Genie learns the correct SQL pattern purely from the example phrasing.\n\nA question’s wording does not define its calculation logic.\n\nFor example, showing “weekly sell-through rate by region” does not establish:\n\n- The numerator and denominator.\n\n- Whether inventory is beginning, ending, or average inventory.\n\n- Which sales statuses count.\n\n- How returns are treated.\n\n- Which date defines the week.\n\n- Which regional hierarchy is authoritative.\n\nWithout trusted SQL, SQL expressions, or other semantic guidance, Genie must infer these decisions. That is inappropriate for high-stakes, recurring leadership reporting.\n\nAlso, strictly speaking, UI sample questions primarily help users begin asking questions. They should not be confused with verified example SQL queries or trusted assets."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/monitor","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/concepts"],"source_hash":"961fd7084b9176bd07e3159ba30305673656e2c85d34a4d9747c25a9a16f5d5c","stem_ja":"データアナリストは、全社展開に向け、小売の商品企画チーム用のGenie spaceを準備しています。日常の質問は2種類あります。1つ目は、利益率、値下げ、商品カテゴリの成績について、曖昧な表現を含む多種多様な探索的質問です。2つ目は、経営陣が毎週月曜に確認する、例えば「地域別の週間消化率」のような、少数で固定された8種類の重要な定例質問です。後者には、毎回正確で監査可能な計算が必要です。\n\n本番運用に必要な不足を最も適切に埋める方法はどれですか？","options_ja":["探索的な質問も含め、チームが尋ねそうなすべての質問に信頼済みSQLアセットを作り、すべての回答にTrustedと表示させる。","経営陣の8種類の定例質問それぞれに信頼済みSQLアセットを作り、常に正確で監査可能な結果を返すようにする。探索的な質問に現れる用語や表現のパターンは、一般指示とサンプル質問で補う。","一般指示だけで利益率、値下げ、消化率のロジックを定義し、信頼済みアセットは使わない。サンプル質問だけでGenieが経営陣の質問の正しい解釈を学べるため。","指示や信頼済みアセットは使わず、経営陣の8種類の質問を一字一句まねたサンプル質問だけを追加し、その表現から正しいSQLパターンを学ばせる。"],"explanation_ja":"固定された重要計算には、レビュー済みの信頼済みSQLを使います。一方、無数にある探索的質問は一般指示とサンプルで意味や用語を補います。「定例の厳密さは信頼済みSQL、自由な質問の理解は指示と例」と役割を分けると、保守可能な本番設計になります。","distractors_ja":["探索的な質問をすべて事前定義することは難しく、保守負担が大きくなります。重要な固定質問に信頼済みSQLを集中させます。","厳密さが必要な定例質問と、幅広い探索的質問で手段を分けており、正確さと保守性の両方に合います。","自然言語の定義だけでは、重要計算を毎回同じ承認済みSQLで実施する要件を満たしにくくなります。","サンプルは質問の表現を教えますが、それだけで監査可能な計算を固定する仕組みにはなりません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-009","number":9,"form":1,"stem":"A context engineer is building a Databricks agent that classifies and routes IT support tickets, with six candidate few-shot examples and a limited token budget. The agent already handles unambiguous tickets at high accuracy without demonstration. Failures cluster around tickets mentioning two plausible teams at once, and around a recently added output field, priority_justification, requiring a structured format the agent has never been shown.\n\nWhich combination of few-shot examples would contribute the most marginal value?","options":["One example showing a straightforward, single-team ticket being correctly classified and assigned, plus one example demonstrating the list_teams tool call in isolation.","Six examples, one for each ticket category the taxonomy defines, regardless of whether the agent already classifies that category correctly.","One example demonstrating how to reason through and resolve a dual-team ambiguous ticket, plus one example showing the exact structured format expected for priority_justification.","One example per available tool, showing each tool called successfully in a simple, unambiguous scenario."],"correct":2,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"few-shot例の限界価値","concept":"ce-f1-q009","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. One example demonstrating how to reason through and resolve a dual-team ambiguous ticket, plus one example showing the exact structured format expected for `priority_justification`.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question asks which examples provide the greatest marginal value under a limited token budget.\n\nThe agent already performs well on straightforward tickets, so examples that repeat behavior it has already mastered add little value. Its observed failures are concentrated in two areas:\n\n- Tickets that plausibly belong to two teams.\n\n- A new structured output field, `priority_justification`, whose required format has never been demonstrated.\n\nOption C targets both gaps directly. One example teaches the agent how to disambiguate overlapping team ownership, while the other demonstrates the exact output contract for the unfamiliar field.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nFew-shot examples are most valuable when they demonstrate behavior the model does not already perform reliably.\n\nThe dual-team example addresses an actual decision-boundary failure. A useful demonstration could show the agent how to:\n\n- Identify evidence supporting each candidate team.\n\n- Apply routing precedence or ownership rules.\n\n- Select the primary destination.\n\n- Explain why the competing team was not selected.\n\n- Escalate or request clarification when the evidence is insufficient.\n\nThe second example teaches a genuinely new output requirement by showing the exact structure expected for `priority_justification`. This is especially valuable because verbal instructions alone may not fully communicate details such as:\n\n- Required keys.\n\n- Allowed values.\n\n- Field ordering or nesting.\n\n- Expected justification length.\n\n- How the justification connects to the assigned priority.\n\nThese two examples cover the model’s measured failure clusters while consuming far fewer tokens than six broad or tool-oriented demonstrations.\n\nFor production, a schema-enforced structured response should also be used when supported. The few-shot example improves the model’s semantic use of the field, while schema validation ensures that the returned object conforms to the required format.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. One example showing a straightforward, single-team ticket being correctly classified and assigned, plus one example demonstrating the `list_teams` tool call in isolation.\n\nThe straightforward classification example provides limited additional value because the agent already handles unambiguous tickets accurately.\n\nThe isolated tool-call example also does not address either observed failure:\n\n- It does not teach the agent how to resolve a ticket involving two plausible teams.\n\n- It does not demonstrate the required `priority_justification` structure.\n\nA tool-use example would be valuable only if traces showed that the agent was selecting, invoking, or formatting arguments for `list_teams` incorrectly.\n\nB. Six examples, one for each ticket category the taxonomy defines, regardless of whether the agent already classifies that category correctly.\n\nThis uses the limited token budget inefficiently.\n\nProviding equal example coverage for every category ignores the evaluation evidence showing that most categories are already handled correctly. Several examples would merely reinforce mastered behavior while leaving less room for demonstrations of ambiguous routing and the new output field.\n\nTaxonomy coverage can be useful during initial development, but once failure data is available, examples should be selected according to observed weaknesses and important decision boundaries.\n\nD. One example per available tool, showing each tool called successfully in a simple, unambiguous scenario.\n\nThe number of tools is not the relevant organizing principle for few-shot selection.\n\nSimple successful tool calls would be useful if the agent had tool-selection or argument-construction failures. The scenario instead identifies classification ambiguity and structured-output formatting as the problems.\n\nTool demonstrations that do not address those failures would add prompt length without providing comparable quality improvement.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe likely distractor is B because broad category coverage appears comprehensive.\n\nThe exam tests whether examples should be selected for coverage alone or for their incremental contribution to known weaknesses. Under a limited token budget, difficult boundary cases and unfamiliar output formats provide more value than redundant examples of behavior the model already performs correctly.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nFew-shot selection should be driven by trace and evaluation data rather than by a desire to represent every category or tool equally.\n\nA production workflow should:\n\n- Build an evaluation set containing dual-team and other boundary cases.\n\n- Add a gold-standard example for the most common ambiguity pattern.\n\n- Define `priority_justification` through an explicit JSON schema or typed response model.\n\n- Include a representative example showing the field’s expected meaning and content.\n\n- Re-evaluate accuracy, formatting compliance, latency, and token usage.\n\n- Remove examples that do not produce measurable improvement.\n\nThe exam answer is C. In a real implementation, the structured-output example should complement—not replace—schema validation.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Improve RAG chain quality – Prompt augmentation](https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain)\n\n- [Generate structured outputs](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/generative-ai/foundation-models/structured-outputs)\n\n- [MLflow Prompt Optimization](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/automatically-optimize-prompts)\n\n- [Agent system design patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)","source_option_explanations":["A. One example showing a straightforward, single-team ticket being correctly classified and assigned, plus one example demonstrating the `list_teams` tool call in isolation.\n\nThe straightforward classification example provides limited additional value because the agent already handles unambiguous tickets accurately.\n\nThe isolated tool-call example also does not address either observed failure:\n\n- It does not teach the agent how to resolve a ticket involving two plausible teams.\n\n- It does not demonstrate the required `priority_justification` structure.\n\nA tool-use example would be valuable only if traces showed that the agent was selecting, invoking, or formatting arguments for `list_teams` incorrectly.","B. Six examples, one for each ticket category the taxonomy defines, regardless of whether the agent already classifies that category correctly.\n\nThis uses the limited token budget inefficiently.\n\nProviding equal example coverage for every category ignores the evaluation evidence showing that most categories are already handled correctly. Several examples would merely reinforce mastered behavior while leaving less room for demonstrations of ambiguous routing and the new output field.\n\nTaxonomy coverage can be useful during initial development, but once failure data is available, examples should be selected according to observed weaknesses and important decision boundaries.","Correct Answer\n\nC. One example demonstrating how to reason through and resolve a dual-team ambiguous ticket, plus one example showing the exact structured format expected for `priority_justification`.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question asks which examples provide the greatest marginal value under a limited token budget.\n\nThe agent already performs well on straightforward tickets, so examples that repeat behavior it has already mastered add little value. Its observed failures are concentrated in two areas:\n\n- Tickets that plausibly belong to two teams.\n\n- A new structured output field, `priority_justification`, whose required format has never been demonstrated.\n\nOption C targets both gaps directly. One example teaches the agent how to disambiguate overlapping team ownership, while the other demonstrates the exact output contract for the unfamiliar field.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nFew-shot examples are most valuable when they demonstrate behavior the model does not already perform reliably.\n\nThe dual-team example addresses an actual decision-boundary failure. A useful demonstration could show the agent how to:\n\n- Identify evidence supporting each candidate team.\n\n- Apply routing precedence or ownership rules.\n\n- Select the primary destination.\n\n- Explain why the competing team was not selected.\n\n- Escalate or request clarification when the evidence is insufficient.\n\nThe second example teaches a genuinely new output requirement by showing the exact structure expected for `priority_justification`. This is especially valuable because verbal instructions alone may not fully communicate details such as:\n\n- Required keys.\n\n- Allowed values.\n\n- Field ordering or nesting.\n\n- Expected justification length.\n\n- How the justification connects to the assigned priority.\n\nThese two examples cover the model’s measured failure clusters while consuming far fewer tokens than six broad or tool-oriented demonstrations.\n\nFor production, a schema-enforced structured response should also be used when supported. The few-shot example improves the model’s semantic use of the field, while schema validation ensures that the returned object conforms to the required format.","D. One example per available tool, showing each tool called successfully in a simple, unambiguous scenario.\n\nThe number of tools is not the relevant organizing principle for few-shot selection.\n\nSimple successful tool calls would be useful if the agent had tool-selection or argument-construction failures. The scenario instead identifies classification ambiguity and structured-output formatting as the problems.\n\nTool demonstrations that do not address those failures would add prompt length without providing comparable quality improvement."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/generative-ai/foundation-models/structured-outputs","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/automatically-optimize-prompts","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns"],"source_hash":"04b1d0ef024f2eaaeae3a9f325b379564f9eaf2f6572b01448054f4de8393f31","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアは、ITサポートチケットを分類して担当先へ振り分けるDatabricksエージェントを構築しています。few-shot例の候補は6つで、トークン予算には制約があります。明確なチケットは、例を示さなくても既に高精度で処理できます。失敗は、担当候補の2チームが同時に言及されるチケットと、最近追加した出力フィールドpriority_justificationに集中しています。このフィールドには、まだ一度も例示していない構造化形式が必要です。\n\n追加による効果が最も大きいfew-shot例の組み合わせはどれですか？","options_ja":["単純で担当チームが1つだけのチケットを正しく分類・割り当てする例を1つと、list_teamsツール呼び出しだけを単独で示す例を1つ。","エージェントが既にその分類を正しく行えるかにかかわらず、分類体系で定義された各カテゴリに1つずつ、計6つの例。","担当候補が2チームある曖昧なチケットを推論して解決する例を1つと、priority_justificationに期待する正確な構造化形式を示す例を1つ。","利用可能なツールごとに、単純で曖昧さのない場面で呼び出しが成功する例を1つずつ。"],"explanation_ja":"限られた例は「既にできること」の再確認より、「できていない判断と出力形式」の補強に使います。今回は担当の曖昧さの解消とpriority_justificationの書式が弱点です。例を均等に並べるより、失敗パターンを新たにカバーする例の限界価値を重視します。","distractors_ja":["既に高精度な明確な分類を示しても効果は小さく、優先すべき曖昧な担当判断と新しい出力形式を教えられません。","網羅的に見えても、既にできる分類に予算を使います。現在の失敗を解消する情報を優先すべきです。","2つの実際の失敗原因をそれぞれ直接例示するため、少ないトークンで最も大きい改善を期待できます。","単純なツール実行の成功例だけでは、担当が重なるときの判断や新しい構造化出力の失敗は改善しません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-010","number":10,"form":1,"stem":"A Databricks agent's system prompt instructs it to \"always be thorough and consider all relevant information before answering.\" In production, the agent frequently pads simple, single-fact lookup answers with lengthy caveats and unrelated background, inflating response length and token cost, even though retrieval and reasoning are accurate. The team wants to fix verbosity without substantially increasing prompt length or adding new maintenance overhead.\n\nWhich revision strategy most directly resolves this with the least increase in token cost and maintenance burden?","options":["Replace the vague \"always be thorough\" instruction with a concise, conditional instruction: give a direct, minimal answer for straightforward single-fact questions, and reserve caveats for genuinely ambiguous or multi-part questions.","Add a second LLM call after the agent's response that edits the answer down to a shorter length before returning it to the user.","Append ten additional few-shot examples of concise answers to the system prompt to demonstrate the desired brevity through examples alone.","Increase the agent's temperature parameter so its responses vary and occasionally happen to be shorter."],"correct":0,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"曖昧な指示の最小修正","concept":"ce-f1-q010","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Replace the vague \"always be thorough\" instruction with a concise, conditional instruction: give a direct, minimal answer for straightforward single-fact questions, and reserve caveats for genuinely ambiguous or multi-part questions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe excessive verbosity is caused by the system prompt itself. The instruction to “always be thorough and consider all relevant information” applies indiscriminately to every request, including simple fact lookups.\n\nThe desired behavior is conditional:\n\n- Simple, single-fact question → direct, minimal answer.\n\n- Ambiguous, complex, or multi-part question → additional explanation and relevant caveats.\n\nOption A fixes the instruction at its source with only a small prompt change. It adds negligible token overhead, requires no extra model call, and avoids maintaining a large set of demonstrations.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nPrompt instructions should state the desired response behavior precisely. “Always be thorough” encourages the model to expand every answer because it does not distinguish between tasks that need explanation and tasks that do not.\n\nA better system instruction would be:\n\nFor straightforward single-fact questions, answer directly and briefly. Include caveats or background only when the question is ambiguous, risk-sensitive, or requires multi-step explanation.\n\nThis revision:\n\n- Removes the instruction that causes unconditional verbosity.\n\n- Defines when additional detail is appropriate.\n\n- Preserves thoroughness for genuinely complex questions.\n\n- Adds very few prompt tokens.\n\n- Requires only one prompt change to maintain.\n\n- Avoids additional inference latency and cost.\n\nDatabricks guidance recommends explicitly specifying the desired level of detail and using concise, well-structured prompts to control token usage. It also supports setting output-token limits as an additional safeguard, although such a limit alone would not correct the behavioral instruction.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Add a second LLM call after the agent's response that edits the answer down to a shorter length before returning it to the user.\n\nA second model call could shorten the output, but it introduces unnecessary operational overhead:\n\n- Additional input and output tokens.\n\n- Increased end-to-end latency.\n\n- Another model invocation to monitor and evaluate.\n\n- A possibility that the editor removes important qualifications.\n\n- Additional prompts, traces, failure modes, and deployment logic.\n\nSince the verbosity originates in one vague system instruction, correcting that instruction is simpler and more cost-effective.\n\nC. Append ten additional few-shot examples of concise answers to the system prompt to demonstrate the desired brevity through examples alone.\n\nFew-shot examples can demonstrate style, but ten additional examples would significantly increase the prompt’s recurring token cost.\n\nThey would also create maintenance overhead because the examples would need to remain representative as the agent’s question types evolve. Examples alone might not clearly express the conditional rule that simple questions should be brief while complex questions may require detail.\n\nA concise explicit instruction communicates that distinction more efficiently.\n\nD. Increase the agent's temperature parameter so its responses vary and occasionally happen to be shorter.\n\nTemperature controls output variability, not dependable conciseness.\n\nIncreasing it would make responses less predictable and could produce more creative, inconsistent, or verbose answers. It would not reliably override the system instruction to “always be thorough.”\n\nProduction behavior should be governed by a clear instruction rather than hoping sampling variation occasionally generates a shorter response.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main distractor is B because a post-processing model could technically shorten the answers.\n\nHowever, the question asks for the solution with the least token cost and maintenance burden. Correcting the small but overly broad system instruction addresses the root cause without adding another model call or workflow component.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nPrompt instructions should be conditional when the appropriate response style depends on task complexity. Avoid absolute wording such as:\n\n- “Always be thorough.”\n\n- “Explain everything in detail.”\n\n- “Include all potentially relevant information.”\n\nInstead, define observable decision rules:\n\nAnswer simple factual questions in one or two sentences. Add background, assumptions, or caveats only when they materially affect correctness or when the user requests detail.\n\nThe team should evaluate the revised prompt against both simple and complex questions to ensure that brevity improves without suppressing necessary qualifications. A reasonable `max_tokens` limit can provide an additional safety boundary, but it should complement rather than replace clear prompt instructions.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Tips to improve Genie Code responses — Be specific about response detail and structure](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/tips)\n\n- [Foundation Model APIs limits and quotas — Optimize token usage](https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/machine-learning/foundation-model-apis/limits)\n\n- [MLflow Prompt Optimization](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/automatically-optimize-prompts)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Replace the vague \"always be thorough\" instruction with a concise, conditional instruction: give a direct, minimal answer for straightforward single-fact questions, and reserve caveats for genuinely ambiguous or multi-part questions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe excessive verbosity is caused by the system prompt itself. The instruction to “always be thorough and consider all relevant information” applies indiscriminately to every request, including simple fact lookups.\n\nThe desired behavior is conditional:\n\n- Simple, single-fact question → direct, minimal answer.\n\n- Ambiguous, complex, or multi-part question → additional explanation and relevant caveats.\n\nOption A fixes the instruction at its source with only a small prompt change. It adds negligible token overhead, requires no extra model call, and avoids maintaining a large set of demonstrations.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nPrompt instructions should state the desired response behavior precisely. “Always be thorough” encourages the model to expand every answer because it does not distinguish between tasks that need explanation and tasks that do not.\n\nA better system instruction would be:\n\nFor straightforward single-fact questions, answer directly and briefly. Include caveats or background only when the question is ambiguous, risk-sensitive, or requires multi-step explanation.\n\nThis revision:\n\n- Removes the instruction that causes unconditional verbosity.\n\n- Defines when additional detail is appropriate.\n\n- Preserves thoroughness for genuinely complex questions.\n\n- Adds very few prompt tokens.\n\n- Requires only one prompt change to maintain.\n\n- Avoids additional inference latency and cost.\n\nDatabricks guidance recommends explicitly specifying the desired level of detail and using concise, well-structured prompts to control token usage. It also supports setting output-token limits as an additional safeguard, although such a limit alone would not correct the behavioral instruction.","B. Add a second LLM call after the agent's response that edits the answer down to a shorter length before returning it to the user.\n\nA second model call could shorten the output, but it introduces unnecessary operational overhead:\n\n- Additional input and output tokens.\n\n- Increased end-to-end latency.\n\n- Another model invocation to monitor and evaluate.\n\n- A possibility that the editor removes important qualifications.\n\n- Additional prompts, traces, failure modes, and deployment logic.\n\nSince the verbosity originates in one vague system instruction, correcting that instruction is simpler and more cost-effective.","C. Append ten additional few-shot examples of concise answers to the system prompt to demonstrate the desired brevity through examples alone.\n\nFew-shot examples can demonstrate style, but ten additional examples would significantly increase the prompt’s recurring token cost.\n\nThey would also create maintenance overhead because the examples would need to remain representative as the agent’s question types evolve. Examples alone might not clearly express the conditional rule that simple questions should be brief while complex questions may require detail.\n\nA concise explicit instruction communicates that distinction more efficiently.","D. Increase the agent's temperature parameter so its responses vary and occasionally happen to be shorter.\n\nTemperature controls output variability, not dependable conciseness.\n\nIncreasing it would make responses less predictable and could produce more creative, inconsistent, or verbose answers. It would not reliably override the system instruction to “always be thorough.”\n\nProduction behavior should be governed by a clear instruction rather than hoping sampling variation occasionally generates a shorter response."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/tips","https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/machine-learning/foundation-model-apis/limits","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/automatically-optimize-prompts"],"source_hash":"9514beaadfd512e44db45cd6bba364b7573586985e0e5311ea4551f5731fa99a","stem_ja":"Databricksエージェントのシステムプロンプトには「常に十分詳しく、回答前に関連情報をすべて検討すること」とあります。本番では、検索と推論は正確なのに、単純な1つの事実を尋ねる回答にも長い留保や無関係な背景を付け、回答の長さとトークン費用を増やしています。チームは、プロンプトを大幅に長くしたり、新しい保守負担を増やしたりせず、冗長さを修正したいと考えています。\n\nトークン費用と保守負担の増加を最小限にして、最も直接解決する指示の修正方法はどれですか？","options_ja":["曖昧な「常に十分詳しく」という指示を、短い条件付きの指示に置き換える。単純な単一事実の質問には直接的で最小限の回答を返し、本当に曖昧な質問や複数要素の質問にだけ留保を付ける。","エージェントが回答した後に2回目のLLM呼び出しを追加し、ユーザーへ返す前に短く編集する。","望む簡潔さを例だけで示すため、簡潔な回答のfew-shot例をさらに10個、システムプロンプトへ追加する。","エージェントのtemperatureを上げて回答に変化を持たせ、時々短い回答が出るようにする。"],"explanation_ja":"検索も推論も正しいので、修正すべきなのは曖昧な「詳しく」という指示です。質問の複雑さに応じて回答の長さを変える短い条件に置き換えます。「新しい仕組みを足す前に、問題を生む指示を直す」と考えると、費用・保守の制約にも合います。","distractors_ja":["問題を生む指示を直接具体化し、単純な質問と複雑な質問で必要な詳しさを分けるため、追加負担が小さく済みます。","新しいモデル呼び出しの費用・遅延・保守が必要です。元の指示を短く直せる今回の最小解決ではありません。","毎回の入力と保守対象が増えます。10例を追加するより、冗長さを生む指示を具体化するほうが直接的です。","出力の揺らぎを増やすだけで、簡潔さを安定して制御できません。正しい検索結果の一貫性まで損なう可能性があります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-011","number":11,"form":1,"stem":"Using MLflow 3, a context engineer logs two runs for the same support agent. Config 1's system prompt is 800 tokens. Config 2 is 2,400 tokens: identical, plus twelve additional few-shot examples and a 500-token \"company voice and tone\" style guide. Across 200 traces, answer_correctness and faithfulness are statistically indistinguishable, but Config 2's responses are judged noticeably more on-brand in tone. Token cost per request for Config 2 is roughly three times Config 1's.\n\nWhich conclusion and next step is best supported by this evidence?","options":["The higher-token configuration is justified outright, since any measurable quality improvement, including tone, justifies the added cost regardless of which prompt element produced it.","The higher-token configuration is unjustified on correctness and faithfulness grounds, since those metrics are unchanged; if the tone improvement matters to the business, isolate the style guide — which plausibly drives the tone gain — by testing it independently of the twelve additional few-shot examples, rather than keeping the full 2,400-token prompt.","The higher-token configuration should be discarded entirely, since correctness and faithfulness did not improve, without further testing of the individual elements.","The twelve additional few-shot examples must be the cause of the tone improvement, since few-shot examples are generally the most influential prompt element, so they alone should be kept and the style guide dropped."],"correct":1,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"複数変更の効果を切り分ける","concept":"ce-f1-q011","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. The higher-token configuration is unjustified on correctness and faithfulness grounds, since those metrics are unchanged; if the tone improvement matters to the business, isolate the style guide — which plausibly drives the tone gain — by testing it independently of the twelve additional few-shot examples, rather than keeping the full 2,400-token prompt.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe experiment establishes three important facts:\n\n- Config 2 costs approximately three times as many prompt tokens.\n\n- It provides no statistically distinguishable improvement in answer correctness or faithfulness.\n\n- It produces a measurable improvement in brand tone.\n\nHowever, Config 2 changes two variables simultaneously:\n\n- It adds twelve few-shot examples.\n\n- It adds a 500-token style guide.\n\nTherefore, the traces show that the combined prompt improves tone, but they do not establish which added component caused that improvement. Keeping the entire prompt would preserve a substantial recurring cost without evidence that all of its added content is useful.\n\nThe best next step is an ablation test: add the style guide to Config 1 without the twelve examples and evaluate whether the tone improvement remains.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe evidence does not support using Config 2 for correctness or faithfulness because those measures are statistically indistinguishable from Config 1. This does not prove that the configurations are perfectly identical; it means the evaluation did not detect a meaningful improvement on those dimensions.\n\nThe tone improvement may still have legitimate business value. A support agent may need to communicate consistently with the company’s approved voice, even when its factual quality is unchanged. The team should therefore not discard the tone result automatically.\n\nHowever, the experiment is confounded because two prompt components were introduced together. The style guide is a plausible source of the improvement because it directly describes voice and tone, but that causal conclusion has not yet been demonstrated.\n\nThe next evaluation should compare configurations such as:\n\n- Baseline: Config 1.\n\n- Style-only: Config 1 plus the 500-token style guide.\n\n- Examples-only: Config 1 plus the twelve few-shot examples.\n\n- Combined: Existing Config 2, if needed for comparison.\n\nAll versions should be evaluated against the same traces or evaluation dataset using:\n\n- Answer correctness.\n\n- Faithfulness or groundedness.\n\n- A brand-tone scorer.\n\n- Input and output token usage.\n\n- Latency.\n\n- Cost per request.\n\nIf the style-only version preserves the tone improvement, the twelve examples can be removed, producing nearly the same business outcome at substantially lower recurring cost.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. The higher-token configuration is justified outright, since any measurable quality improvement, including tone, justifies the added cost regardless of which prompt element produced it.\n\nA measurable improvement does not automatically justify any associated cost.\n\nThe business must determine whether the tone gain is valuable enough to offset approximately three times the token cost. More importantly, the current experiment does not show that the entire additional 1,600 tokens are necessary.\n\nKeeping both additions without isolating their effects could result in paying for twelve examples that provide no incremental benefit.\n\nC. The higher-token configuration should be discarded entirely, since correctness and faithfulness did not improve, without further testing of the individual elements.\n\nThis ignores the observed tone improvement.\n\nIf brand consistency matters to the support organization, the result represents a potentially valuable quality gain. Discarding Config 2 immediately would prevent the team from determining whether a smaller, cheaper prompt could preserve that benefit.\n\nThe appropriate response is further isolation and testing, not automatic rejection.\n\nD. The twelve additional few-shot examples must be the cause of the tone improvement, since few-shot examples are generally the most influential prompt element, so they alone should be kept and the style guide dropped.\n\nThe data does not establish that the examples caused the improvement.\n\nBoth the examples and style guide were added simultaneously. Moreover, the style guide is explicitly designed to affect voice and tone, making it at least as plausible a cause as the examples.\n\nAssuming causation based on a general belief about few-shot prompting would be methodologically unsound. The components must be tested independently.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is treating an aggregate A/B result as proof that every added prompt element contributed value.\n\nThe evaluation demonstrates that the combined configuration improved tone. It does not reveal whether the improvement came from the style guide, the few-shot examples, or an interaction between them. The correct next step is controlled ablation, not an unsupported causal conclusion.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nPrompt optimization should evaluate both quality and efficiency. A longer prompt should earn its recurring token, latency, and cost overhead through measurable improvement on a business-relevant criterion.\n\nA practical next test would be:\n\n- Create a new prompt version containing Config 1 plus only the style guide.\n\n- Evaluate it against the same 200 traces.\n\n- Add a custom brand-tone scorer alongside correctness and faithfulness.\n\n- Compare confidence intervals, token consumption, latency, and cost.\n\n- Retain the smallest prompt that meets the required quality thresholds.\n\nIf the style guide alone achieves the desired tone, remove the twelve examples. If not, test a small, targeted subset of examples rather than retaining all twelve by default.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Evaluate and compare prompt versions — Databricks](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts)\n\n- [Scorers and LLM judges — Databricks](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/scorers)\n\n- [Building MLflow evaluation datasets — Databricks](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset)\n\n- [MLflow Prompt Registry — Databricks](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/)","source_option_explanations":["A. The higher-token configuration is justified outright, since any measurable quality improvement, including tone, justifies the added cost regardless of which prompt element produced it.\n\nA measurable improvement does not automatically justify any associated cost.\n\nThe business must determine whether the tone gain is valuable enough to offset approximately three times the token cost. More importantly, the current experiment does not show that the entire additional 1,600 tokens are necessary.\n\nKeeping both additions without isolating their effects could result in paying for twelve examples that provide no incremental benefit.","Correct Answer\n\nB. The higher-token configuration is unjustified on correctness and faithfulness grounds, since those metrics are unchanged; if the tone improvement matters to the business, isolate the style guide — which plausibly drives the tone gain — by testing it independently of the twelve additional few-shot examples, rather than keeping the full 2,400-token prompt.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe experiment establishes three important facts:\n\n- Config 2 costs approximately three times as many prompt tokens.\n\n- It provides no statistically distinguishable improvement in answer correctness or faithfulness.\n\n- It produces a measurable improvement in brand tone.\n\nHowever, Config 2 changes two variables simultaneously:\n\n- It adds twelve few-shot examples.\n\n- It adds a 500-token style guide.\n\nTherefore, the traces show that the combined prompt improves tone, but they do not establish which added component caused that improvement. Keeping the entire prompt would preserve a substantial recurring cost without evidence that all of its added content is useful.\n\nThe best next step is an ablation test: add the style guide to Config 1 without the twelve examples and evaluate whether the tone improvement remains.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe evidence does not support using Config 2 for correctness or faithfulness because those measures are statistically indistinguishable from Config 1. This does not prove that the configurations are perfectly identical; it means the evaluation did not detect a meaningful improvement on those dimensions.\n\nThe tone improvement may still have legitimate business value. A support agent may need to communicate consistently with the company’s approved voice, even when its factual quality is unchanged. The team should therefore not discard the tone result automatically.\n\nHowever, the experiment is confounded because two prompt components were introduced together. The style guide is a plausible source of the improvement because it directly describes voice and tone, but that causal conclusion has not yet been demonstrated.\n\nThe next evaluation should compare configurations such as:\n\n- Baseline: Config 1.\n\n- Style-only: Config 1 plus the 500-token style guide.\n\n- Examples-only: Config 1 plus the twelve few-shot examples.\n\n- Combined: Existing Config 2, if needed for comparison.\n\nAll versions should be evaluated against the same traces or evaluation dataset using:\n\n- Answer correctness.\n\n- Faithfulness or groundedness.\n\n- A brand-tone scorer.\n\n- Input and output token usage.\n\n- Latency.\n\n- Cost per request.\n\nIf the style-only version preserves the tone improvement, the twelve examples can be removed, producing nearly the same business outcome at substantially lower recurring cost.","C. The higher-token configuration should be discarded entirely, since correctness and faithfulness did not improve, without further testing of the individual elements.\n\nThis ignores the observed tone improvement.\n\nIf brand consistency matters to the support organization, the result represents a potentially valuable quality gain. Discarding Config 2 immediately would prevent the team from determining whether a smaller, cheaper prompt could preserve that benefit.\n\nThe appropriate response is further isolation and testing, not automatic rejection.","D. The twelve additional few-shot examples must be the cause of the tone improvement, since few-shot examples are generally the most influential prompt element, so they alone should be kept and the style guide dropped.\n\nThe data does not establish that the examples caused the improvement.\n\nBoth the examples and style guide were added simultaneously. Moreover, the style guide is explicitly designed to affect voice and tone, making it at least as plausible a cause as the examples.\n\nAssuming causation based on a general belief about few-shot prompting would be methodologically unsound. The components must be tested independently."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/scorers","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/"],"source_hash":"b973d61f570e039a97e163e40ccdea66ce83e3eae0deef376c5e0b122ba09d57","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアは、MLflow 3で同じサポートエージェントの2つの実行を記録しています。構成1のシステムプロンプトは800トークンです。構成2は2,400トークンで、同じ内容に12個のfew-shot例と500トークンの「自社らしい語り口・口調」のスタイルガイドを追加しています。200件のトレースではanswer_correctnessとfaithfulnessに統計上の差はありませんが、構成2は口調が明らかにブランドに合っていると評価されました。構成2の要求あたりトークン費用は、構成1の約3倍です。\n\nこの証拠から最も適切に導ける結論と次の行動はどれですか？","options_ja":["口調を含め、測定可能な品質改善が少しでもあれば、どのプロンプト要素が改善を生んだかにかかわらず追加費用を正当化できるため、長い構成は無条件に妥当である。","正確さと根拠への忠実さは変わらないため、それらを理由に長い構成は正当化できない。口調の改善に業務上の価値があるなら、2,400トークンの全体を残す代わりに、改善原因として考えられるスタイルガイドを12個の追加few-shot例から切り離し、単独で試験する。","正確さと根拠への忠実さが改善していないので、個別要素を追加検証せず、長い構成を完全に破棄すべきである。","few-shot例は一般に最も強い影響を与える要素なので、口調の改善原因は必ず12個の追加例である。例だけを残し、スタイルガイドは削除する。"],"explanation_ja":"一度に例とスタイルガイドを追加したので、どちらが口調を改善したかは分離できません。正確さは変わらず費用だけ3倍なので、業務上必要な口調の効果を少ない入力で得られるか、要素を分けて比較します。「差が出た指標」と「原因と証明できる要素」を混同しないことが重要です。","distractors_ja":["どんな小さな改善でも費用を無条件に正当化できるわけではありません。改善の価値と、費用を生む不要要素を比較します。","観測された効果だけを認め、原因は追加実験で切り分けるため、正確さ・口調・費用の情報を適切に扱っています。","口調に業務価値がある可能性を捨てています。必要な改善だけを低費用で残せるか検証する余地があります。","例とスタイルガイドを同時に変えたため、例が原因と断定できません。要素を分けた比較が必要です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-012","number":12,"form":1,"stem":"A Genie space for a subscription-billing team returns incorrect join results when analysts ask about \"active subscribers by plan tier.\" Reviewing the underlying Unity Catalog metadata, the engineer finds: the subscriptions table's status column has no comment describing its possible values, the plan_tier column comment incorrectly states it is unique per customer even though customers can hold multiple concurrent plans, and there are no column-level synonyms or SQL expressions mapping the business term \"active subscriber\" to the underlying filter logic (status = 'ACTIVE' AND end_date IS NULL). Table names and data types are otherwise well documented, and the AI Search index and trusted assets are unrelated to this issue.\n\nWhich configuration change will have the highest impact on closing this accuracy gap?","options":["Correct the plan_tier column comment to accurately reflect that customers can hold multiple concurrent plans, add a description of status's possible values, and add a synonym/SQL expression mapping \"active subscriber\" to the correct filter logic.","Increase the number of tables added to the Genie space so it has more context to reason from.","Switch the underlying SQL warehouse from a pro warehouse to a serverless warehouse to improve query latency.","Add a general text instruction stating \"always return accurate results for subscriber questions,\" without changing any column-level metadata."],"correct":0,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"Genieの列メタデータと業務用語","concept":"ce-f1-q012","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Correct the `plan_tier` column comment to accurately reflect that customers can hold multiple concurrent plans, add a description of `status`'s possible values, and add a synonym/SQL expression mapping \"active subscriber\" to the correct filter logic.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe accuracy problem is caused by missing and incorrect business-semantic metadata:\n\n- The incorrect `plan_tier` comment can lead Genie to assume a one-plan-per-customer relationship, producing incorrect joins, grouping, or counts.\n\n- The undocumented `status` values make it harder for Genie to select the correct filter value.\n\n- The business phrase “active subscriber” is not mapped to the required logic:\n\n```\nstatus = 'ACTIVE' AND end_date IS NULL\n```\n\nOption A corrects all three sources of ambiguity. It gives Genie accurate cardinality context, valid status semantics, and an explicit reusable definition for the business concept being queried.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nGenie uses table and column descriptions, synonyms, join relationships, and SQL expressions to translate business questions into SQL.\n\nThe current metadata actively encourages incorrect reasoning:\n\n- Describing `plan_tier` as unique per customer may cause Genie to assume that each customer contributes only one plan record.\n\n- Failing to document valid `status` values leaves Genie to infer how active records are represented.\n\n- Omitting an explicit definition for “active subscriber” leaves Genie to guess whether active means only `status = 'ACTIVE'`, only `end_date IS NULL`, or both.\n\nThe strongest correction is to improve the semantic layer:\n\n- Correct the inaccurate `plan_tier` description.\n\n- Document the meaning and valid values of `status`.\n\n- Add “active subscriber” as relevant business terminology.\n\n- Define the exact filter as a reusable SQL expression, such as:\n\n```\nstatus = 'ACTIVE' AND end_date IS NULL\n```\n\nA synonym helps Genie associate user terminology with the relevant data concept, while the SQL expression provides the precise logic Genie should consistently apply.\n\nGiven the stated facts, this is the highest-impact intervention because it directly fixes the information Genie uses to construct the query.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Increase the number of tables added to the Genie space so it has more context to reason from.\n\nAdding more tables does not correct the inaccurate or missing semantics in the existing tables.\n\nIt can make the problem worse by introducing:\n\n- More candidate join paths.\n\n- Overlapping columns.\n\n- Additional ambiguity.\n\n- Greater context consumption.\n\n- More opportunities for Genie to select the wrong data source.\n\nGenie Agents should generally remain focused on the minimum set of tables required for the intended questions. The problem here is metadata quality, not insufficient table quantity.\n\nC. Switch the underlying SQL warehouse from a pro warehouse to a serverless warehouse to improve query latency.\n\nChanging the warehouse can affect query startup time, scalability, or execution performance, but it does not change the SQL logic Genie generates.\n\nA faster warehouse would simply execute the incorrectly generated join or filter more quickly. The scenario describes an accuracy issue, not a latency or compute-capacity issue.\n\nD. Add a general text instruction stating \"always return accurate results for subscriber questions,\" without changing any column-level metadata.\n\nThis instruction expresses an objective but provides no actionable business logic.\n\nIt does not tell Genie:\n\n- Which `status` value represents active.\n\n- Whether `end_date` must also be null.\n\n- That customers can hold multiple concurrent plans.\n\n- How subscriptions should be joined or counted.\n\n- How to interpret “active subscriber.”\n\nDatabricks recommends using SQL expressions for metrics, filters, and dimensions that can be represented precisely in SQL. A generic instruction to be accurate cannot replace correct metadata and an explicit business definition.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe likely distractor is D because adding instructions is a common Genie-tuning technique.\n\nHowever, the issue is not a missing behavioral instruction. It is missing and incorrect data semantics. The correct fix is to encode those semantics in descriptions, synonyms, and SQL expressions rather than ask Genie abstractly to be accurate.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nFor production use, business concepts should be defined in a way that is both understandable and executable.\n\nA robust configuration might define:\n\n- `status`: Subscription lifecycle state, with documented values such as `ACTIVE`, `CANCELLED`, and `EXPIRED`.\n\n- `plan_tier`: The tier associated with one subscription record; not unique by customer because a customer can hold multiple concurrent subscriptions.\n\n- `active_subscriber`: A reusable filter expression applying:\n\n```\nstatus = 'ACTIVE' AND end_date IS NULL\n```\n\nThe engineer should then create benchmark questions such as:\n\n- “Active subscribers by plan tier”\n\n- “How many currently subscribed customers are in each tier?”\n\n- “Count live subscriptions by plan”\n\n- “Show customers with more than one active plan”\n\nThe generated SQL should also be inspected to confirm whether the business wants to count:\n\n- Active subscription records.\n\n- Distinct customers with at least one active subscription.\n\n- Distinct customer-and-plan combinations.\n\nThat distinction matters in a multi-plan subscription model. For this exam scenario, however, A most directly closes the identified semantic accuracy gap.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Curate an effective Genie Agent](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices)\n\n- [Tune Genie Agent quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality)\n\n- [Troubleshoot Genie Agents](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/troubleshooting)\n\n- [Genie Agents concepts](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/concepts)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Correct the `plan_tier` column comment to accurately reflect that customers can hold multiple concurrent plans, add a description of `status`'s possible values, and add a synonym/SQL expression mapping \"active subscriber\" to the correct filter logic.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe accuracy problem is caused by missing and incorrect business-semantic metadata:\n\n- The incorrect `plan_tier` comment can lead Genie to assume a one-plan-per-customer relationship, producing incorrect joins, grouping, or counts.\n\n- The undocumented `status` values make it harder for Genie to select the correct filter value.\n\n- The business phrase “active subscriber” is not mapped to the required logic:\n\n```\nstatus = 'ACTIVE' AND end_date IS NULL\n```\n\nOption A corrects all three sources of ambiguity. It gives Genie accurate cardinality context, valid status semantics, and an explicit reusable definition for the business concept being queried.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nGenie uses table and column descriptions, synonyms, join relationships, and SQL expressions to translate business questions into SQL.\n\nThe current metadata actively encourages incorrect reasoning:\n\n- Describing `plan_tier` as unique per customer may cause Genie to assume that each customer contributes only one plan record.\n\n- Failing to document valid `status` values leaves Genie to infer how active records are represented.\n\n- Omitting an explicit definition for “active subscriber” leaves Genie to guess whether active means only `status = 'ACTIVE'`, only `end_date IS NULL`, or both.\n\nThe strongest correction is to improve the semantic layer:\n\n- Correct the inaccurate `plan_tier` description.\n\n- Document the meaning and valid values of `status`.\n\n- Add “active subscriber” as relevant business terminology.\n\n- Define the exact filter as a reusable SQL expression, such as:\n\n```\nstatus = 'ACTIVE' AND end_date IS NULL\n```\n\nA synonym helps Genie associate user terminology with the relevant data concept, while the SQL expression provides the precise logic Genie should consistently apply.\n\nGiven the stated facts, this is the highest-impact intervention because it directly fixes the information Genie uses to construct the query.","B. Increase the number of tables added to the Genie space so it has more context to reason from.\n\nAdding more tables does not correct the inaccurate or missing semantics in the existing tables.\n\nIt can make the problem worse by introducing:\n\n- More candidate join paths.\n\n- Overlapping columns.\n\n- Additional ambiguity.\n\n- Greater context consumption.\n\n- More opportunities for Genie to select the wrong data source.\n\nGenie Agents should generally remain focused on the minimum set of tables required for the intended questions. The problem here is metadata quality, not insufficient table quantity.","C. Switch the underlying SQL warehouse from a pro warehouse to a serverless warehouse to improve query latency.\n\nChanging the warehouse can affect query startup time, scalability, or execution performance, but it does not change the SQL logic Genie generates.\n\nA faster warehouse would simply execute the incorrectly generated join or filter more quickly. The scenario describes an accuracy issue, not a latency or compute-capacity issue.","D. Add a general text instruction stating \"always return accurate results for subscriber questions,\" without changing any column-level metadata.\n\nThis instruction expresses an objective but provides no actionable business logic.\n\nIt does not tell Genie:\n\n- Which `status` value represents active.\n\n- Whether `end_date` must also be null.\n\n- That customers can hold multiple concurrent plans.\n\n- How subscriptions should be joined or counted.\n\n- How to interpret “active subscriber.”\n\nDatabricks recommends using SQL expressions for metrics, filters, and dimensions that can be represented precisely in SQL. A generic instruction to be accurate cannot replace correct metadata and an explicit business definition."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/troubleshooting","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/concepts"],"source_hash":"20c036f44af37a20a5e93c67fa2f5ef56843dff7f149ec7c3d01b66d0c56e59f","stem_ja":"サブスクリプション請求チームのGenie spaceは、アナリストが「プラン階層別の有効な契約者数」を尋ねると、誤った結合結果を返します。基になるUnity Catalogメタデータを調べると、subscriptionsテーブルのstatus列には、取り得る値を説明するコメントがありません。plan_tier列のコメントは、顧客が複数のプランを同時に持てるにもかかわらず、顧客ごとに一意と誤記しています。また、業務用語「有効な契約者」を基の条件（status = 'ACTIVE' AND end_date IS NULL）に対応付ける列単位の同義語やSQL式がありません。テーブル名とデータ型の説明は十分で、AI Searchのインデックスや信頼済みアセットはこの問題に関係しません。\n\n正確さの不足を埋めるうえで最も効果が大きい設定変更はどれですか？","options_ja":["plan_tier列のコメントを、顧客が複数のプランを同時に持てることが正しく伝わる内容へ修正する。statusの取り得る値を説明し、「有効な契約者」を正しいフィルタ条件へ対応付ける同義語・SQL式を追加する。","Genieが推論に使う情報を増やすため、Genie spaceへ追加するテーブル数を増やす。","クエリ遅延を改善するため、基盤のSQLウェアハウスをProからサーバーレスへ切り替える。","列単位のメタデータは変えず、「契約者に関する質問には常に正確に回答すること」という一般的な文章の指示を追加する。"],"explanation_ja":"誤った列説明と、業務用語からSQL条件への対応の欠落が、Genieの意味解釈を誤らせています。列の意味・値・多重度を正し、業務上の「有効」を正確な条件へ対応付けます。「説明があるか」だけでなく「その説明が正しいか」を確認しましょう。計算速度の変更では意味の誤りは直りません。","distractors_ja":["問題文で特定された3つの欠陥を直接修正し、正しい結合関係とフィルタ条件をGenieへ伝えられます。","誤った説明が残ったまま情報だけ増えます。関係のないテーブル追加は意味の解釈をさらに難しくする場合があります。","実行速度や処理基盤の変更です。列の意味や有効契約の条件を誤解する問題とは別です。","「正確に」という抽象的な要求だけでは、statusの値やプランの多重度、必要なSQL条件を教えられません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-013","number":13,"form":1,"stem":"A data analyst is building a Genie space for a supply-chain operations team. The Unity Catalog environment for this domain includes: a large, wide raw_shipments_bronze table ingested directly from a legacy ERP feed with cryptic column names and no cleansing; a curated shipments_gold view that joins and cleans the bronze data into well-named, business-ready columns; a parameterized SQL query that calculates on-time delivery rate by carrier and date range; and a Unity Catalog SQL function that looks up the current shipping-lane cost given an origin and destination.\n\nWhich combination of these objects should the analyst curate into the Genie space for this domain?","options":["Curate only the raw_shipments_bronze table, since Genie should always work from the most granular, unprocessed source data available to avoid losing information during transformation.","Curate all four objects with equal weight and no distinction between them, letting Genie's SQL generation decide at query time which table or function best matches each question.","Curate only the parameterized SQL query and the SQL function, excluding both the bronze table and the gold view entirely, since trusted assets alone are sufficient for a production Genie space.","Curate the shipments_gold view as the primary data source, register the parameterized on-time-delivery query as a trusted asset, and register the shipping-lane-cost function as a trusted asset, so both recurring calculations return verified results."],"correct":3,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"Genieの業務データと信頼済み資産","concept":"ce-f1-q013","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Curate the `shipments_gold` view as the primary data source, register the parameterized on-time-delivery query as a trusted asset, and register the shipping-lane-cost function as a trusted asset, so both recurring calculations return verified results.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scenario presents three different types of useful Genie context:\n\n- A business-ready Gold view for flexible, natural-language exploration.\n\n- A parameterized SQL query implementing a recurring, verified KPI calculation.\n\n- A Unity Catalog SQL function implementing reusable shipping-cost lookup logic.\n\nThe raw Bronze table is wide, uncleansed, and uses cryptic column names. Adding it would make it harder for Genie to select the correct fields and generate understandable SQL. Databricks recommends focused Genie configurations built on simplified, well-documented datasets rather than unnecessarily exposing raw or confusing structures.\n\nThe Gold view should therefore serve as the primary data source, while the parameterized query and SQL function should be included as trusted assets for questions that must use verified business logic.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe `shipments_gold` view is the appropriate primary data source because it:\n\n- Contains cleaned and joined shipment information.\n\n- Uses meaningful, business-oriented column names.\n\n- Encapsulates transformation and joining logic.\n\n- Presents a simplified interface for supply-chain analysts.\n\n- Reduces the likelihood that Genie will select cryptic or inappropriate raw fields.\n\nDatabricks recommends providing Genie with concise, well-documented datasets and simplifying data through prejoined or denormalized views. It also recommends limiting the included data to objects and columns relevant to the agent’s domain.\n\nThe parameterized on-time-delivery query is suitable as a trusted asset because its approved calculation can be reused with different carrier and date-range values. When Genie uses the exact parameterized query, the resulting answer is treated as verified.\n\nThe shipping-lane-cost function is also an appropriate trusted asset. Unity Catalog SQL functions can encapsulate approved logic and accept user-provided parameters such as origin and destination. Genie can call the function without modifying or exposing its internal implementation.\n\nTherefore, option D provides the correct separation of responsibilities:\n\n- Gold view: General domain exploration and generated queries.\n\n- Parameterized query: Verified on-time-delivery calculation.\n\n- SQL function: Verified shipping-lane-cost lookup.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Curate only the `raw_shipments_bronze` table, since Genie should always work from the most granular, unprocessed source data available to avoid losing information during transformation.\n\nThis is incorrect.\n\nBronze data is intended primarily for raw ingestion and preservation. It commonly contains unvalidated, uncleaned, and source-oriented data. Gold-layer objects are designed for business intelligence, operational reporting, and business users.\n\nThe cryptic column names and lack of cleansing would reduce Genie's ability to interpret business questions accurately. Genie does not need the most granular raw source merely because it contains more information. It needs the most relevant, understandable, and reliable data representation for the defined domain.\n\nThis option also omits the verified KPI query and shipping-cost function.\n\nB. Curate all four objects with equal weight and no distinction between them, letting Genie's SQL generation decide at query time which table or function best matches each question.\n\nThis is not the best design.\n\nAlthough Genie can use multiple tables, views, queries, and functions, more context is not automatically better. Databricks recommends starting small, staying focused, limiting unnecessary tables and columns, and avoiding confusing data structures.\n\nIncluding both the raw Bronze table and the curated Gold view introduces overlapping representations of the same business domain. Genie could select cryptic Bronze columns or generate logic that bypasses the validated Gold transformation.\n\nAdditionally, the objects should not be treated as interchangeable:\n\n- The Gold view is a queryable data source.\n\n- The parameterized query is approved example logic.\n\n- The SQL function is reusable encapsulated logic.\n\n- The raw Bronze table is an ingestion-layer source that is unnecessary for this business-facing space.\n\nC. Curate only the parameterized SQL query and the SQL function, excluding both the Bronze table and the Gold view entirely, since trusted assets alone are sufficient for a production Genie space.\n\nThis is incomplete.\n\nTrusted assets provide verified answers for anticipated questions, but they do not replace the general-purpose dataset needed for broader natural-language analysis.\n\nThe parameterized query can answer variations of the on-time-delivery question, and the function can retrieve lane costs. However, the operations team might also ask questions such as:\n\n- Which destinations have the highest shipment volume?\n\n- Which carriers have the most delayed shipments?\n\n- How have shipment volumes changed by region?\n\n- Which products experience the longest delivery times?\n\nThe trusted assets alone cannot necessarily support these broader questions. The `shipments_gold` view gives Genie the domain data needed to generate additional SQL while the trusted assets preserve verified logic for recurring calculations.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is assuming either:\n\n- Genie should always use the most granular raw data, or\n\n- Trusted assets completely replace curated tables and views.\n\nA well-designed Genie space combines a focused, business-ready dataset for flexible exploration with trusted assets for calculations that must follow verified logic.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nIn a real implementation, expose the smallest useful semantic surface to Genie.\n\nUse a cleaned Gold view or metric view with:\n\n- Clear table and column descriptions.\n\n- Business-friendly names.\n\n- Only relevant columns.\n\n- Documented units, status definitions, and date semantics.\n\n- Hidden technical identifiers where they do not help users.\n\nAdd parameterized trusted queries for important organizational metrics whose definitions must remain consistent, such as on-time delivery rate. Add Unity Catalog SQL functions when the underlying logic is complex, reusable, sensitive, or should not be modified by generated SQL.\n\nUsers must also have the required access to the underlying data, and they need `EXECUTE` permission for SQL functions used as trusted assets.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Curate an effective Genie Agent\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices)\n\n- Tune Genie Agent quality – Example queries, parameters, and trusted assets\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality)\n\n- Create and manage a Genie Agent\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/set-up](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/set-up)\n\n- What is the medallion lakehouse architecture?\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/medallion](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/medallion)","source_option_explanations":["A. Curate only the `raw_shipments_bronze` table, since Genie should always work from the most granular, unprocessed source data available to avoid losing information during transformation.\n\nThis is incorrect.\n\nBronze data is intended primarily for raw ingestion and preservation. It commonly contains unvalidated, uncleaned, and source-oriented data. Gold-layer objects are designed for business intelligence, operational reporting, and business users.\n\nThe cryptic column names and lack of cleansing would reduce Genie's ability to interpret business questions accurately. Genie does not need the most granular raw source merely because it contains more information. It needs the most relevant, understandable, and reliable data representation for the defined domain.\n\nThis option also omits the verified KPI query and shipping-cost function.","B. Curate all four objects with equal weight and no distinction between them, letting Genie's SQL generation decide at query time which table or function best matches each question.\n\nThis is not the best design.\n\nAlthough Genie can use multiple tables, views, queries, and functions, more context is not automatically better. Databricks recommends starting small, staying focused, limiting unnecessary tables and columns, and avoiding confusing data structures.\n\nIncluding both the raw Bronze table and the curated Gold view introduces overlapping representations of the same business domain. Genie could select cryptic Bronze columns or generate logic that bypasses the validated Gold transformation.\n\nAdditionally, the objects should not be treated as interchangeable:\n\n- The Gold view is a queryable data source.\n\n- The parameterized query is approved example logic.\n\n- The SQL function is reusable encapsulated logic.\n\n- The raw Bronze table is an ingestion-layer source that is unnecessary for this business-facing space.","C. Curate only the parameterized SQL query and the SQL function, excluding both the Bronze table and the Gold view entirely, since trusted assets alone are sufficient for a production Genie space.\n\nThis is incomplete.\n\nTrusted assets provide verified answers for anticipated questions, but they do not replace the general-purpose dataset needed for broader natural-language analysis.\n\nThe parameterized query can answer variations of the on-time-delivery question, and the function can retrieve lane costs. However, the operations team might also ask questions such as:\n\n- Which destinations have the highest shipment volume?\n\n- Which carriers have the most delayed shipments?\n\n- How have shipment volumes changed by region?\n\n- Which products experience the longest delivery times?\n\nThe trusted assets alone cannot necessarily support these broader questions. The `shipments_gold` view gives Genie the domain data needed to generate additional SQL while the trusted assets preserve verified logic for recurring calculations.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Curate the `shipments_gold` view as the primary data source, register the parameterized on-time-delivery query as a trusted asset, and register the shipping-lane-cost function as a trusted asset, so both recurring calculations return verified results.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scenario presents three different types of useful Genie context:\n\n- A business-ready Gold view for flexible, natural-language exploration.\n\n- A parameterized SQL query implementing a recurring, verified KPI calculation.\n\n- A Unity Catalog SQL function implementing reusable shipping-cost lookup logic.\n\nThe raw Bronze table is wide, uncleansed, and uses cryptic column names. Adding it would make it harder for Genie to select the correct fields and generate understandable SQL. Databricks recommends focused Genie configurations built on simplified, well-documented datasets rather than unnecessarily exposing raw or confusing structures.\n\nThe Gold view should therefore serve as the primary data source, while the parameterized query and SQL function should be included as trusted assets for questions that must use verified business logic.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe `shipments_gold` view is the appropriate primary data source because it:\n\n- Contains cleaned and joined shipment information.\n\n- Uses meaningful, business-oriented column names.\n\n- Encapsulates transformation and joining logic.\n\n- Presents a simplified interface for supply-chain analysts.\n\n- Reduces the likelihood that Genie will select cryptic or inappropriate raw fields.\n\nDatabricks recommends providing Genie with concise, well-documented datasets and simplifying data through prejoined or denormalized views. It also recommends limiting the included data to objects and columns relevant to the agent’s domain.\n\nThe parameterized on-time-delivery query is suitable as a trusted asset because its approved calculation can be reused with different carrier and date-range values. When Genie uses the exact parameterized query, the resulting answer is treated as verified.\n\nThe shipping-lane-cost function is also an appropriate trusted asset. Unity Catalog SQL functions can encapsulate approved logic and accept user-provided parameters such as origin and destination. Genie can call the function without modifying or exposing its internal implementation.\n\nTherefore, option D provides the correct separation of responsibilities:\n\n- Gold view: General domain exploration and generated queries.\n\n- Parameterized query: Verified on-time-delivery calculation.\n\n- SQL function: Verified shipping-lane-cost lookup."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/set-up","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/medallion"],"source_hash":"237d91cd2fc598693404027bac5b87f5d64b058736ca8dbb98409fa2a31d3b8b","stem_ja":"データアナリストが、サプライチェーン運用チーム向けのGenieスペースを構築しています。この業務領域のUnity Catalogには、旧ERPのフィードから直接取り込まれ、分かりにくい列名を持ち、クレンジングもされていない大規模で列数の多いraw_shipments_bronzeテーブルがあります。また、Bronzeデータを結合・整備し、業務で使える分かりやすい列名へしたshipments_goldビュー、配送業者と期間を指定して定時配達率を計算するパラメーター付きSQLクエリ、出発地と到着地から現在の配送区間費用を調べるUnity Catalog SQL関数もあります。\n\nこの業務領域のGenieスペースへ整備して組み込むべきオブジェクトの組み合わせはどれですか。","options_ja":["変換で情報を失わないよう、Genieは常に最も細粒度の未加工データで動くべきなので、raw_shipments_bronzeだけを組み込む。","4つのオブジェクトを区別せず同じ重みで組み込み、どのテーブルや関数が質問に合うかは、クエリ時のGenieのSQL生成に任せる。","本番のGenieスペースには信頼済み資産だけで十分なので、パラメーター付きSQLクエリとSQL関数だけを組み込み、BronzeテーブルとGoldビューは両方とも除く。","shipments_goldビューを主データソースとし、パラメーター付きの定時配達率クエリと配送区間費用の関数を、それぞれ信頼済み資産として登録し、繰り返し使う両方の計算で検証済みの結果を返すようにする。"],"explanation_ja":"業務で使いやすい整備済みビューを基本にし、繰り返す重要な計算は検証済みのクエリや関数へ固定します。未加工データを無条件に全部渡すより、列の意味と計算の正しさを揃える方が有効です。覚え方は「自由な分析の土台はGold、反復する計算は信頼済み資産」です。","distractors_ja":["未整備で分かりにくい列を主な入口にすると、意味の取り違えやSQLの誤生成を招きます。","信頼度と用途の異なる情報を区別せず渡すと、未加工データの誤利用を防げません。","定型計算は再利用できますが、業務データへの柔軟な質問に答える主データソースまで除いてしまいます。","整備済みの業務データと、検証済みの反復計算を組み合わせるため、正確さと利用範囲を両立できます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-014","number":14,"form":1,"stem":"A Databricks agent for an electronics retailer's support team retrieves troubleshooting articles from a Databricks AI Search index built from the company's knowledge base. Support engineers report that when customers ask about a specific model number (e.g., \"error code E-4471 on the XR-2200\"), the agent frequently retrieves generic articles about unrelated error codes or products, even though an article specifically addressing \"E-4471\" and \"XR-2200\" exists in the underlying Delta table and is confirmed to be correctly synced into the index. The index currently uses ANN (approximate nearest neighbor) similarity search over embeddings only, with no other retrieval mode enabled.\n\nWhich AI Search configuration change most directly improves the signal quality of retrieved context for this failure?","options":["Rebuild the index using CONTINUOUS pipeline_type instead of TRIGGERED, so newly published articles sync within seconds.","Enable hybrid (keyword + vector) search on the index, so exact terms like the model number and error code are matched directly rather than relying solely on semantic similarity in embedding space.","Switch the index from DELTA_SYNC to DIRECT_ACCESS and add programmatic upsert calls to the ingestion workflow.","Increase num_results so more candidate articles are returned per query, giving the agent more chances to find the right one."],"correct":1,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"型番・エラーコードをハイブリッド検索で照合","concept":"ce-f1-q014","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Enable hybrid (keyword + vector) search on the index, so exact terms like the model number and error code are matched directly rather than relying solely on semantic similarity in embedding space.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe key failure is retrieval relevance, not missing or stale data:\n\n- The exact article exists in the Delta table.\n\n- It has been successfully synchronized into the AI Search index.\n\n- The query contains highly specific identifiers: `E-4471` and `XR-2200`.\n\n- The index currently performs only ANN vector similarity search.\n\nEmbeddings are effective for finding semantically related content, but exact alphanumeric identifiers such as model numbers, SKUs, part numbers, and error codes may not produce a strong semantic signal. Hybrid search addresses this by combining semantic vector search with literal keyword matching.\n\nDatabricks specifically recommends hybrid search when users search for product codes or technical terms, when exact matching is important, or when semantic retrieval misses obvious keyword matches.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nHybrid search combines two complementary retrieval signals:\n\n- ANN vector search retrieves content with similar semantic meaning.\n\n- Keyword search rewards documents containing exact query terms such as `E-4471` and `XR-2200`.\n\nThis is the most direct improvement because the failure concerns exact identifiers being overshadowed by semantically similar but incorrect articles. Keyword matching strengthens the ranking signal for the article containing those identifiers, while vector similarity still supports broader natural-language phrases such as “device fails during startup.”\n\nDatabricks describes hybrid search as suitable for queries containing domain-specific keywords and recommends it for improving both recall and precision when exact terms matter. Enabling it is a query configuration change, such as setting `query_type=\"HYBRID\"`, rather than redesigning the ingestion architecture.\n\nTherefore, option B fixes the actual retrieval problem: insufficient lexical matching for exact product and error identifiers.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Rebuild the index using CONTINUOUS `pipeline_type` instead of TRIGGERED, so newly published articles sync within seconds.\n\nThis addresses data freshness, not search relevance.\n\nA Continuous Delta Sync index automatically processes source-table changes, whereas a Triggered index is updated when synchronization is explicitly initiated. However, the question confirms that the required article is already correctly synchronized into the index.\n\nChanging the synchronization mode would not improve how the existing indexed article is ranked for `E-4471` or `XR-2200`. It would only change how quickly future source updates become searchable.\n\nC. Switch the index from DELTA_SYNC to DIRECT_ACCESS and add programmatic upsert calls to the ingestion workflow.\n\nThis changes the index’s data-maintenance model, not its retrieval algorithm.\n\nA Delta Sync index automatically derives and updates indexed data from a source Delta table. A Direct Vector Access index requires the application to manage insertions, updates, and deletions through APIs or SDK calls.\n\nBecause the article already exists and is synchronized correctly, replacing automated synchronization with programmatic upserts introduces unnecessary operational complexity without improving exact-term matching or ranking quality.\n\nD. Increase `num_results` so more candidate articles are returned per query, giving the agent more chances to find the right one.\n\nIncreasing `num_results` might cause the correct article to appear somewhere deeper in the result set, but it does not improve the relevance signal used to rank the documents.\n\nIt can also:\n\n- Return more irrelevant articles.\n\n- Consume more of the model’s context window.\n\n- Increase retrieval latency.\n\n- Reduce precision in the context provided to the agent.\n\nDatabricks notes that returning more results increases retrieval work and latency. The scenario asks for the configuration change that most directly improves signal quality, which is hybrid retrieval rather than simply returning a larger set of poorly ranked candidates.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe primary trap is confusing a retrieval-quality problem with an ingestion or synchronization problem.\n\nThe statement that the article is already correctly synchronized eliminates Continuous sync and Direct Access as solutions. Another distractor is increasing result count, which may improve raw recall but does not correct weak ranking for exact identifiers.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nFor support documentation, parts catalogs, device manuals, and operational knowledge bases, pure semantic retrieval is often insufficient because users commonly search using:\n\n- Error codes.\n\n- Product model numbers.\n\n- SKUs and part numbers.\n\n- Version identifiers.\n\n- Ticket or incident numbers.\n\n- Commands and API names.\n\nHybrid search is generally a strong default for these workloads because it preserves semantic understanding while recognizing exact terms.\n\nIn production, additional improvements could include:\n\n- Storing model number and product family as metadata.\n\n- Filtering results by product or model.\n\n- Reranking the hybrid-search candidates.\n\n- Measuring `Precision@k` using representative support queries.\n\n- Keeping the number of returned chunks small enough to avoid irrelevant context.\n\nThose are useful follow-up optimizations, but the expected exam answer is to enable hybrid search first.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- AI Search retrieval quality guide\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality)\n\n- Query an AI Search index\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search)\n\n- AI Search performance guide\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/best-practices](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/best-practices)\n\n- Create AI Search endpoints and indexes\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search)","source_option_explanations":["A. Rebuild the index using CONTINUOUS `pipeline_type` instead of TRIGGERED, so newly published articles sync within seconds.\n\nThis addresses data freshness, not search relevance.\n\nA Continuous Delta Sync index automatically processes source-table changes, whereas a Triggered index is updated when synchronization is explicitly initiated. However, the question confirms that the required article is already correctly synchronized into the index.\n\nChanging the synchronization mode would not improve how the existing indexed article is ranked for `E-4471` or `XR-2200`. It would only change how quickly future source updates become searchable.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Enable hybrid (keyword + vector) search on the index, so exact terms like the model number and error code are matched directly rather than relying solely on semantic similarity in embedding space.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe key failure is retrieval relevance, not missing or stale data:\n\n- The exact article exists in the Delta table.\n\n- It has been successfully synchronized into the AI Search index.\n\n- The query contains highly specific identifiers: `E-4471` and `XR-2200`.\n\n- The index currently performs only ANN vector similarity search.\n\nEmbeddings are effective for finding semantically related content, but exact alphanumeric identifiers such as model numbers, SKUs, part numbers, and error codes may not produce a strong semantic signal. Hybrid search addresses this by combining semantic vector search with literal keyword matching.\n\nDatabricks specifically recommends hybrid search when users search for product codes or technical terms, when exact matching is important, or when semantic retrieval misses obvious keyword matches.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nHybrid search combines two complementary retrieval signals:\n\n- ANN vector search retrieves content with similar semantic meaning.\n\n- Keyword search rewards documents containing exact query terms such as `E-4471` and `XR-2200`.\n\nThis is the most direct improvement because the failure concerns exact identifiers being overshadowed by semantically similar but incorrect articles. Keyword matching strengthens the ranking signal for the article containing those identifiers, while vector similarity still supports broader natural-language phrases such as “device fails during startup.”\n\nDatabricks describes hybrid search as suitable for queries containing domain-specific keywords and recommends it for improving both recall and precision when exact terms matter. Enabling it is a query configuration change, such as setting `query_type=\"HYBRID\"`, rather than redesigning the ingestion architecture.\n\nTherefore, option B fixes the actual retrieval problem: insufficient lexical matching for exact product and error identifiers.","C. Switch the index from DELTA_SYNC to DIRECT_ACCESS and add programmatic upsert calls to the ingestion workflow.\n\nThis changes the index’s data-maintenance model, not its retrieval algorithm.\n\nA Delta Sync index automatically derives and updates indexed data from a source Delta table. A Direct Vector Access index requires the application to manage insertions, updates, and deletions through APIs or SDK calls.\n\nBecause the article already exists and is synchronized correctly, replacing automated synchronization with programmatic upserts introduces unnecessary operational complexity without improving exact-term matching or ranking quality.","D. Increase `num_results` so more candidate articles are returned per query, giving the agent more chances to find the right one.\n\nIncreasing `num_results` might cause the correct article to appear somewhere deeper in the result set, but it does not improve the relevance signal used to rank the documents.\n\nIt can also:\n\n- Return more irrelevant articles.\n\n- Consume more of the model’s context window.\n\n- Increase retrieval latency.\n\n- Reduce precision in the context provided to the agent.\n\nDatabricks notes that returning more results increases retrieval work and latency. The scenario asks for the configuration change that most directly improves signal quality, which is hybrid retrieval rather than simply returning a larger set of poorly ranked candidates."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/best-practices","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search"],"source_hash":"51ecb7cd4cf2c0fc01211b55f1c1305df5198f28fdb50c64fcc2719b4974cc2f","stem_ja":"電子機器小売店のサポートチーム向けDatabricksエージェントが、社内ナレッジベースから作ったDatabricks AI Searchインデックスでトラブル対処記事を検索します。サポート担当者は、顧客が「XR-2200でエラーコードE-4471」のように特定の型番を質問すると、エージェントが無関係なエラーコードや製品の一般記事をよく取得すると報告しています。元のDeltaテーブルにはE-4471とXR-2200へ直接対応する記事があり、インデックスにも正しく同期されたことを確認済みです。現在のインデックスは埋め込みのANN（近似最近傍）類似検索だけを使い、ほかの検索モードは有効にしていません。\n\nこの障害で、取得するコンテキストの信号品質を最も直接改善するAI Searchの設定変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["新しく公開した記事が数秒で同期されるよう、pipeline_typeをTRIGGEREDではなくCONTINUOUSにしてインデックスを作り直す。","埋め込み空間の意味的な近さだけに頼らず、型番とエラーコードのような正確な語を直接照合できるよう、ハイブリッド検索（キーワード＋ベクトル）を有効にする。","インデックスをDELTA_SYNCからDIRECT_ACCESSへ変え、取り込みワークフローへプログラムによるupsert呼び出しを追加する。","正しい記事を見つける機会を増やすため、num_resultsを増やし、1クエリで返す候補記事数を多くする。"],"explanation_ja":"型番やエラーコードは意味の近さより、正確な文字列一致が重要です。ハイブリッド検索ならキーワード照合で固有の識別子を捉え、ベクトル検索の意味的な関連性も利用できます。索引は同期済みなので、更新方式の変更は原因に合いません。覚え方は「型番・コードにはキーワードも使う」です。","distractors_ja":["対象記事は正しく同期済みなので、同期の速さを変えても照合方法の問題は直りません。","固有の型番とコードを正確に照合する手段を追加でき、埋め込みだけの弱点を直接補います。","索引へのデータ供給方法を変えても、ANNだけで検索する問題は残ります。","候補を増やすだけでは関連性の低い記事も増え、識別子の正確な一致を保証できません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-015","number":15,"form":1,"stem":"A team is designing a RAG pipeline: source PDFs live in a Unity Catalog Volume with fine-grained ACLs, where some documents are restricted to a Legal group only. The team parses, chunks, embeds, and syncs the results into a Databricks AI Search index queried by a general company-wide chatbot open to all employees.\n\nWhich pipeline design most directly preserves governance while still enabling retrieval?","options":["Sync the full corpus into a single, ungoverned Delta table with no ACLs, since the vector index is a separate object and doesn't inherit source document restrictions.","Grant the chatbot's service principal full USE CATALOG and SELECT privileges on the Legal schema, since the chatbot must be authenticated as an authorized user to access data at all.","Add the source documents' access-control metadata (e.g., an allowed-viewer-group field) as a filterable field in the vector index, and apply a metadata filter at query time matching the querying user's Unity Catalog group membership, so restricted-document chunks are only returned to users authorized to see the source in UC.","Remove the Legal-only documents from the corpus before chunking, and permanently exclude them from the RAG pipeline, so the chatbot never gains awareness of their existence."],"correct":2,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"RAGで元文書の閲覧権限を維持","concept":"ce-f1-q015","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Add the source documents' access-control metadata (e.g., an allowed-viewer-group field) as a filterable field in the vector index, and apply a metadata filter at query time matching the querying user's Unity Catalog group membership, so restricted-document chunks are only returned to users authorized to see the source in UC.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe central requirement is to preserve document-level authorization after the documents are transformed into chunks and synchronized to an AI Search index.\n\nThe important clues are:\n\n- The source documents have different access permissions.\n\n- Legal documents must remain accessible only to the Legal group.\n\n- The chatbot is available company-wide.\n\n- Authorized Legal users should still be able to retrieve Legal content.\n\n- The retrieval layer must not expose restricted chunks to other employees.\n\nUnity Catalog permissions on the source Volume do not, by themselves, automatically become per-document authorization rules inside the derived AI Search index. Therefore, the pipeline must propagate the source authorization information into the chunk metadata and enforce it during every retrieval request.\n\nAI Search supports metadata columns and query filters, making option C the most direct design for security-aware retrieval.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe pipeline should associate every chunk with authorization metadata inherited from its source document, such as:\n\n- `allowed_group = \"Legal\"`\n\n- `access_level = \"restricted\"`\n\n- `department = \"Legal\"`\n\n- A normalized list or mapping of permitted principals\n\nWhen the chatbot receives a request, the application should determine the authenticated user’s authorized Unity Catalog groups and construct a mandatory AI Search filter. A non-Legal employee’s retrieval request must exclude Legal-only chunks before those chunks are returned to the agent or placed in the model prompt.\n\nThis approach preserves both objectives:\n\n- Governance: Unauthorized users do not retrieve restricted context.\n\n- Retrieval: Authorized Legal users can still search the restricted documents.\n\nDatabricks AI Search supports filters based on columns synchronized from the source Delta table. Databricks also describes metadata filtering as a retrieval mechanism that requires coordination between metadata extraction in the data pipeline and filtering in the online RAG chain.\n\nThe authorization decision should be enforced in trusted application or retrieval code—not delegated to the LLM. The filter must be derived from the authenticated user’s identity and applied before retrieval results are sent to the model.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Sync the full corpus into a single, ungoverned Delta table with no ACLs, since the vector index is a separate object and doesn't inherit source document restrictions.\n\nThis recognizes that source permissions might not automatically protect individual indexed chunks, but proposes the opposite of the required solution.\n\nRemoving governance from the intermediate Delta table creates additional exposure paths. Anyone with table access could read the extracted text directly, independently of the chatbot.\n\nThe processed document table, embeddings, index, and serving components should remain governed. Source access metadata should be propagated rather than discarded.\n\nB. Grant the chatbot's service principal full `USE CATALOG` and `SELECT` privileges on the Legal schema, since the chatbot must be authenticated as an authorized user to access data at all.\n\nThis violates least privilege and does not preserve per-user authorization.\n\nA service principal used by a company-wide chatbot normally represents the application. Granting it unrestricted access to Legal data means every request could technically access that data, regardless of the employee making the request.\n\nAuthentication of the chatbot service is not equivalent to authorization of the individual user. The retrieval layer must consider the requesting employee’s identity or group membership, not only the service principal’s privileges.\n\nThe service principal may need permission to query the AI Search index, but that alone should not determine which indexed documents each user may retrieve.\n\nD. Remove the Legal-only documents from the corpus before chunking, and permanently exclude them from the RAG pipeline, so the chatbot never gains awareness of their existence.\n\nThis prevents unauthorized disclosure, but it does not satisfy the requirement to preserve governance while still enabling retrieval.\n\nLegal users would lose the ability to retrieve documents they are authorized to access. This is unnecessarily restrictive and creates separate knowledge silos.\n\nA governed retrieval design should return:\n\n- General documents to all authorized employees.\n\n- Legal documents only to authorized Legal users.\n\nOption C provides that selective behavior.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is confusing access to the AI Search index with authorization to every document stored within that index.\n\nIndex-level permissions determine who can query the index. They do not automatically implement the source documents’ varying per-document permissions. Those authorization attributes must be propagated and enforced through retrieval filters.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nFor production permission-aware RAG, treat access-control metadata as part of the document throughout the complete lifecycle:\n\n`Source document → parsed document → chunk → embedding record → search index → retrieval filter`\n\nImportant implementation controls include:\n\n- Derive group membership from authenticated, trusted identity data.\n\n- Never accept authorization groups directly from an untrusted client request.\n\n- Apply filters in deterministic retrieval code, not through an LLM-generated filter.\n\n- Use deny-by-default behavior when access metadata is missing.\n\n- Ensure every chunk retains its source document ID and authorization attributes.\n\n- Keep intermediate Delta tables and AI Search indexes governed in Unity Catalog.\n\n- Test for cross-group leakage using adversarial and negative authorization tests.\n\n- Log the user identity, applied filters, and retrieved document IDs for auditing.\n\nThe exam answer is metadata propagation plus query-time filtering. In a real implementation, this must be combined with least-privilege access, secure identity propagation, auditing, and mandatory server-side enforcement.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks AI Search\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search)\n\n- Query an AI Search index\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search)\n\n- Improve RAG chain quality\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain)\n\n- Row filters and column masks in Unity Catalog\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/)","source_option_explanations":["A. Sync the full corpus into a single, ungoverned Delta table with no ACLs, since the vector index is a separate object and doesn't inherit source document restrictions.\n\nThis recognizes that source permissions might not automatically protect individual indexed chunks, but proposes the opposite of the required solution.\n\nRemoving governance from the intermediate Delta table creates additional exposure paths. Anyone with table access could read the extracted text directly, independently of the chatbot.\n\nThe processed document table, embeddings, index, and serving components should remain governed. Source access metadata should be propagated rather than discarded.","B. Grant the chatbot's service principal full `USE CATALOG` and `SELECT` privileges on the Legal schema, since the chatbot must be authenticated as an authorized user to access data at all.\n\nThis violates least privilege and does not preserve per-user authorization.\n\nA service principal used by a company-wide chatbot normally represents the application. Granting it unrestricted access to Legal data means every request could technically access that data, regardless of the employee making the request.\n\nAuthentication of the chatbot service is not equivalent to authorization of the individual user. The retrieval layer must consider the requesting employee’s identity or group membership, not only the service principal’s privileges.\n\nThe service principal may need permission to query the AI Search index, but that alone should not determine which indexed documents each user may retrieve.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Add the source documents' access-control metadata (e.g., an allowed-viewer-group field) as a filterable field in the vector index, and apply a metadata filter at query time matching the querying user's Unity Catalog group membership, so restricted-document chunks are only returned to users authorized to see the source in UC.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe central requirement is to preserve document-level authorization after the documents are transformed into chunks and synchronized to an AI Search index.\n\nThe important clues are:\n\n- The source documents have different access permissions.\n\n- Legal documents must remain accessible only to the Legal group.\n\n- The chatbot is available company-wide.\n\n- Authorized Legal users should still be able to retrieve Legal content.\n\n- The retrieval layer must not expose restricted chunks to other employees.\n\nUnity Catalog permissions on the source Volume do not, by themselves, automatically become per-document authorization rules inside the derived AI Search index. Therefore, the pipeline must propagate the source authorization information into the chunk metadata and enforce it during every retrieval request.\n\nAI Search supports metadata columns and query filters, making option C the most direct design for security-aware retrieval.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe pipeline should associate every chunk with authorization metadata inherited from its source document, such as:\n\n- `allowed_group = \"Legal\"`\n\n- `access_level = \"restricted\"`\n\n- `department = \"Legal\"`\n\n- A normalized list or mapping of permitted principals\n\nWhen the chatbot receives a request, the application should determine the authenticated user’s authorized Unity Catalog groups and construct a mandatory AI Search filter. A non-Legal employee’s retrieval request must exclude Legal-only chunks before those chunks are returned to the agent or placed in the model prompt.\n\nThis approach preserves both objectives:\n\n- Governance: Unauthorized users do not retrieve restricted context.\n\n- Retrieval: Authorized Legal users can still search the restricted documents.\n\nDatabricks AI Search supports filters based on columns synchronized from the source Delta table. Databricks also describes metadata filtering as a retrieval mechanism that requires coordination between metadata extraction in the data pipeline and filtering in the online RAG chain.\n\nThe authorization decision should be enforced in trusted application or retrieval code—not delegated to the LLM. The filter must be derived from the authenticated user’s identity and applied before retrieval results are sent to the model.","D. Remove the Legal-only documents from the corpus before chunking, and permanently exclude them from the RAG pipeline, so the chatbot never gains awareness of their existence.\n\nThis prevents unauthorized disclosure, but it does not satisfy the requirement to preserve governance while still enabling retrieval.\n\nLegal users would lose the ability to retrieve documents they are authorized to access. This is unnecessarily restrictive and creates separate knowledge silos.\n\nA governed retrieval design should return:\n\n- General documents to all authorized employees.\n\n- Legal documents only to authorized Legal users."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/"],"source_hash":"0b39a9872f54d17353db2cf9d430374dd30087f6d3c66c556b204d5348bae8f3","stem_ja":"チームがRAGパイプラインを設計しています。ソースPDFは細かなACLを持つUnity Catalog Volumeにあり、一部の文書はLegalグループだけに公開されています。文書を解析・分割・埋め込みし、Databricks AI Searchインデックスへ同期します。このインデックスは、全社員が使える全社チャットボットから検索されます。\n\n検索を可能にしつつガバナンスを最も直接維持するパイプライン設計はどれですか。","options_ja":["ベクトルインデックスは別のオブジェクトで元文書の制約を継承しないため、コーパス全文をACLのない、ガバナンスされていない単一Deltaテーブルへ同期する。","チャットボットもデータへアクセスするには認証済みの許可されたユーザーでなければならないため、サービスプリンシパルへLegalスキーマの完全なUSE CATALOGとSELECT権限を与える。","閲覧可能なグループなどの元文書のアクセス制御メタデータを、インデックスのフィルター可能なフィールドに含める。検索時に利用者のUnity Catalogグループ所属に合うメタデータフィルターを適用し、UCで元文書の閲覧を許された利用者だけへ制限文書のチャンクを返す。","Legal専用文書を分割前にコーパスから除き、RAGパイプラインから永久に除外して、チャットボットがその存在を一切認識しないようにする。"],"explanation_ja":"文書をチャンク化しても、誰が読めるかという条件を失わせてはいけません。元のアクセス情報を検索可能な属性として保持し、検証済みの利用者権限に基づくフィルターをアプリ側で必ず適用します。覚え方は「内容と一緒に閲覧条件も運ぶ」。フィルターをモデルや利用者が任意に外せる設計にはしません。","distractors_ja":["元文書の制約が自動継承されないことは、制約を捨ててよい理由にはならず、情報漏えいにつながります。","サービス主体を広く許可しても、質問した社員本人の閲覧権限を区別できません。","元の閲覧条件をチャンクへ持たせ、利用者ごとの条件を検索時に強制すれば、許可された検索を維持できます。","漏えいは避けられても、Legalグループなど正当に読める利用者まで、その文書を検索できなくなります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-016","number":16,"form":1,"stem":"A context engineer is building a RAG agent over a corpus of regulatory compliance contracts. Unlike a general wiki corpus, these documents have a clear, consistent hierarchical structure: numbered sections and clauses (e.g., \"Section 4.2.1\"), where each clause typically states an obligation together with its qualifying conditions and exceptions in the same clause. Analysts most often ask about a specific clause number, but occasionally ask broader questions spanning an entire section.\n\nWhich chunking strategy should the engineer apply, given this document structure?","options":["Chunk along the document's existing clause and section boundaries — each numbered clause becomes one chunk, split further only if a single clause exceeds the embedding model's context length — preserving each clause's obligations and exceptions as an intact unit.","Apply the same 512–1,024 token recursive character splitting with 10–15% overlap used for a general wiki corpus, since a consistent chunk size simplifies the embedding pipeline across corpora.","Use sentence-level chunking, treating each sentence as an independent chunk, to maximize retrieval granularity for narrow clause lookups.","Chunk each contract as a single document, relying on the embedding model's context window to hold the entire document without splitting."],"correct":0,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"契約条項の義務と例外を一緒に分割","concept":"ce-f1-q016","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Chunk along the document's existing clause and section boundaries — each numbered clause becomes one chunk, split further only if a single clause exceeds the embedding model's context length — preserving each clause's obligations and exceptions as an intact unit.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question provides three decisive clues:\n\n- The contracts have a consistent hierarchical structure.\n\n- Each clause normally contains an obligation together with its conditions, qualifications, and exceptions.\n\n- Most questions target a specific clause number, while some questions cover a broader section.\n\nThese characteristics favor document-structure-aware chunking. Each numbered clause is already a natural, semantically complete unit, so the chunking pipeline should preserve that unit rather than impose arbitrary token or sentence boundaries.\n\nClause-level chunks provide precise retrieval for questions such as “What does Section 4.2.1 require?” At the same time, section identifiers and hierarchy metadata can help retrieve multiple related clauses when an analyst asks a section-level question.\n\nDatabricks recommends creating semantically coherent chunks based on document structure, such as sections and topic boundaries, rather than assuming that one fixed-size strategy works for every corpus.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA regulatory clause normally expresses a complete legal proposition. Its meaning may depend on several connected components:\n\n- The primary obligation.\n\n- The party responsible for performing it.\n\n- Preconditions or qualifying circumstances.\n\n- Exceptions and exclusions.\n\n- Deadlines or enforcement consequences.\n\nKeeping these elements in one chunk preserves semantic coherence. If the obligation and its exception are separated, the agent might retrieve only the obligation and produce an incomplete or legally misleading answer.\n\nOption A also aligns chunk granularity with the dominant query pattern. Because analysts usually search by clause number, one chunk per numbered clause provides a direct correspondence between the user’s question and the retrievable unit.\n\nThe instruction to split a clause only when it exceeds the embedding model’s context limit is also appropriate. Embedding inputs must remain within the model’s supported limit, but arbitrary splitting should not occur unless technically necessary. Databricks notes that oversized inputs can be truncated and lose important information.\n\nFor broader questions, the engineer can preserve metadata such as:\n\n- Contract ID.\n\n- Section number.\n\n- Clause number.\n\n- Section heading.\n\n- Parent section.\n\n- Page number.\n\nThis enables the retriever to locate related clauses from the same section or use a parent-child retrieval design. Databricks recommends retaining section headers and document-level context to improve retrieval quality.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Apply the same 512–1,024 token recursive character splitting with 10–15% overlap used for a general wiki corpus, since a consistent chunk size simplifies the embedding pipeline across corpora.\n\nThis is operationally convenient but not the best strategy for this corpus.\n\nFixed or recursive token-based splitting can divide a clause at an arbitrary point, potentially placing an obligation in one chunk and its exception in another. Overlap may reduce this risk, but it does not guarantee that the complete legal meaning remains together.\n\nDatabricks states that arbitrary fixed-size chunking often fails to produce consistently coherent chunks and that chunking decisions should depend on the use case and nature of the documents.\n\nPipeline consistency is less important than retrieval accuracy and preservation of legal meaning.\n\nC. Use sentence-level chunking, treating each sentence as an independent chunk, to maximize retrieval granularity for narrow clause lookups.\n\nThis creates chunks that are too granular.\n\nA clause may contain several sentences, with later sentences defining exceptions, limitations, or conditions that materially change the first sentence. Treating each sentence independently could retrieve only part of the clause and cause the agent to state an obligation without its applicable exception.\n\nSmaller chunks can improve localization, but Databricks warns that they can lose necessary surrounding context. The correct unit should be the smallest unit that remains independently meaningful—in this case, normally the complete clause.\n\nD. Chunk each contract as a single document, relying on the embedding model's context window to hold the entire document without splitting.\n\nThis is too coarse.\n\nEven when an entire contract fits within the embedding model’s technical token limit, representing it with one embedding can weaken retrieval precision. A contract may contain many unrelated sections, and one document-level vector cannot reliably represent every individual clause.\n\nRetrieving the entire contract would also:\n\n- Include substantial irrelevant text.\n\n- Consume more of the generation model’s context window.\n\n- Make it harder to ground answers in a specific clause.\n\n- Increase the risk that the model mixes provisions from different sections.\n\nDatabricks recommends splitting large documents into smaller, semantically concentrated units to minimize distracting or irrelevant retrieved content.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is selecting a familiar, standardized chunk size simply because it works reasonably well for general-purpose text.\n\nChunking is corpus-dependent. When documents already contain meaningful structural boundaries, those boundaries should normally take precedence over arbitrary character, sentence, or token limits.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is clause-aware structural chunking.\n\nIn a production implementation, each clause chunk should retain its hierarchical context, for example:\n\n```\nContract: Vendor Services AgreementSection: 4 — Data ProtectionClause: 4.2.1Parent section: 4.2 — Data RetentionContent: ...\n```\n\nFor section-wide questions, the engineer could additionally implement:\n\n- Metadata filtering by section number.\n\n- Retrieval of adjacent clauses.\n\n- Parent-child chunking, where clauses are searched but the containing section is returned.\n\n- A section summary stored alongside clause-level chunks.\n\n- Hybrid search so exact clause numbers are matched lexically.\n\n- Evaluation datasets containing both clause-specific and section-spanning questions.\n\nWhen an unusually long clause must be split, the pipeline should split at meaningful internal boundaries, retain the same clause number and parent metadata, and use controlled overlap so conditions or exceptions are not lost.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Build an unstructured data pipeline for RAG\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag)\n\n- AI Search retrieval quality guide\n[https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality](https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality)\n\n- `ai_prep_search` function\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_prep_search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_prep_search)\n\n- AI Search foundational embedding model example\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/vector-search-foundation-embedding-model-gte-example](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/vector-search-foundation-embedding-model-gte-example)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Chunk along the document's existing clause and section boundaries — each numbered clause becomes one chunk, split further only if a single clause exceeds the embedding model's context length — preserving each clause's obligations and exceptions as an intact unit.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question provides three decisive clues:\n\n- The contracts have a consistent hierarchical structure.\n\n- Each clause normally contains an obligation together with its conditions, qualifications, and exceptions.\n\n- Most questions target a specific clause number, while some questions cover a broader section.\n\nThese characteristics favor document-structure-aware chunking. Each numbered clause is already a natural, semantically complete unit, so the chunking pipeline should preserve that unit rather than impose arbitrary token or sentence boundaries.\n\nClause-level chunks provide precise retrieval for questions such as “What does Section 4.2.1 require?” At the same time, section identifiers and hierarchy metadata can help retrieve multiple related clauses when an analyst asks a section-level question.\n\nDatabricks recommends creating semantically coherent chunks based on document structure, such as sections and topic boundaries, rather than assuming that one fixed-size strategy works for every corpus.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA regulatory clause normally expresses a complete legal proposition. Its meaning may depend on several connected components:\n\n- The primary obligation.\n\n- The party responsible for performing it.\n\n- Preconditions or qualifying circumstances.\n\n- Exceptions and exclusions.\n\n- Deadlines or enforcement consequences.\n\nKeeping these elements in one chunk preserves semantic coherence. If the obligation and its exception are separated, the agent might retrieve only the obligation and produce an incomplete or legally misleading answer.\n\nOption A also aligns chunk granularity with the dominant query pattern. Because analysts usually search by clause number, one chunk per numbered clause provides a direct correspondence between the user’s question and the retrievable unit.\n\nThe instruction to split a clause only when it exceeds the embedding model’s context limit is also appropriate. Embedding inputs must remain within the model’s supported limit, but arbitrary splitting should not occur unless technically necessary. Databricks notes that oversized inputs can be truncated and lose important information.\n\nFor broader questions, the engineer can preserve metadata such as:\n\n- Contract ID.\n\n- Section number.\n\n- Clause number.\n\n- Section heading.\n\n- Parent section.\n\n- Page number.\n\nThis enables the retriever to locate related clauses from the same section or use a parent-child retrieval design. Databricks recommends retaining section headers and document-level context to improve retrieval quality.","B. Apply the same 512–1,024 token recursive character splitting with 10–15% overlap used for a general wiki corpus, since a consistent chunk size simplifies the embedding pipeline across corpora.\n\nThis is operationally convenient but not the best strategy for this corpus.\n\nFixed or recursive token-based splitting can divide a clause at an arbitrary point, potentially placing an obligation in one chunk and its exception in another. Overlap may reduce this risk, but it does not guarantee that the complete legal meaning remains together.\n\nDatabricks states that arbitrary fixed-size chunking often fails to produce consistently coherent chunks and that chunking decisions should depend on the use case and nature of the documents.\n\nPipeline consistency is less important than retrieval accuracy and preservation of legal meaning.","C. Use sentence-level chunking, treating each sentence as an independent chunk, to maximize retrieval granularity for narrow clause lookups.\n\nThis creates chunks that are too granular.\n\nA clause may contain several sentences, with later sentences defining exceptions, limitations, or conditions that materially change the first sentence. Treating each sentence independently could retrieve only part of the clause and cause the agent to state an obligation without its applicable exception.\n\nSmaller chunks can improve localization, but Databricks warns that they can lose necessary surrounding context. The correct unit should be the smallest unit that remains independently meaningful—in this case, normally the complete clause.","D. Chunk each contract as a single document, relying on the embedding model's context window to hold the entire document without splitting.\n\nThis is too coarse.\n\nEven when an entire contract fits within the embedding model’s technical token limit, representing it with one embedding can weaken retrieval precision. A contract may contain many unrelated sections, and one document-level vector cannot reliably represent every individual clause.\n\nRetrieving the entire contract would also:\n\n- Include substantial irrelevant text.\n\n- Consume more of the generation model’s context window.\n\n- Make it harder to ground answers in a specific clause.\n\n- Increase the risk that the model mixes provisions from different sections.\n\nDatabricks recommends splitting large documents into smaller, semantically concentrated units to minimize distracting or irrelevant retrieved content."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag","https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_prep_search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/vector-search-foundation-embedding-model-gte-example"],"source_hash":"bdfcf16395099dba275c9951becb6a80b796a405bf64715fbdace26e5beceacf","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、規制遵守に関する契約書のコーパスを対象にRAGエージェントを構築しています。一般的なWikiとは異なり、この文書には「Section 4.2.1」などの番号付き節・条項による、明確で一貫した階層構造があります。通常、各条項には義務と、その限定条件・例外が一緒に書かれています。アナリストの質問の大半は特定の条項番号についてですが、ときどき節全体にまたがる広い質問もあります。\n\nこの文書構造に対して、どのチャンク分割戦略を採るべきですか。","options_ja":["文書がすでに持つ条項・節の境界で分割する。各番号付き条項を1チャンクとし、1条項が埋め込みモデルのコンテキスト長を超える場合だけさらに分割して、義務と例外を一体として保持する。","コーパス全体で一定のチャンクサイズなら埋め込みパイプラインが簡単になるため、一般のWikiで使う512～1,024トークン、10～15％の重複による再帰的な文字列分割を同じように適用する。","狭い条項検索で取得粒度を最大化するため、1文ずつを独立したチャンクとする文単位分割を使う。","埋め込みモデルのコンテキストウィンドウへ文書全体が収まることに期待し、各契約書を分割せず1つのチャンクにする。"],"explanation_ja":"契約の義務だけを取り、例外や限定条件を落とすと意味が変わります。番号付き条項という既存構造を使い、関連する義務・条件・例外をまとめて保持するのが適切です。ただし埋め込み上限を超える条項は再分割します。覚え方は「法的な意味の単位を守り、その中で長さを制限する」です。","distractors_ja":["検索される条項単位と一致し、義務の適用条件や例外を一緒に取得できるため、意味の欠落を防げます。","一律の長さを優先すると、文書にある有用な境界を無視し、義務と例外を分断する可能性があります。","細かく分け過ぎると、同じ条項の義務と例外が別々になり、誤った解釈を招きます。","長い契約書は埋め込み上限を超えやすく、条項単位の正確な検索にも不向きです。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-017","number":17,"form":1,"stem":"A Databricks agent approves or escalates employee expense reports. Available data sources include: the requester's role and cost-center, the company's tiered expense-policy thresholds by role, the requester's manager's identity, the historical rate of approvals for that cost-center over the past year, and the current exchange-rate table (reports may be submitted in foreign currencies, while policy thresholds are denominated in USD).\n\nGiven a submitted expense report in EUR, which combination of context elements is required for the agent to correctly scope and execute the approval decision?","options":["The historical rate of approvals for the requester's cost-center over the past year, used to predict whether this report is likely to be approved.","The requester's manager's identity only, since managers are the ultimate decision-makers regardless of policy thresholds.","The tiered policy thresholds by role and the historical approval rate, without needing the exchange rate, since Genie can infer currency conversion automatically from context.","The requester's role, the tiered policy thresholds by role, and the current EUR-to-USD exchange rate, so the reported amount can be normalized and compared against the correct threshold."],"correct":3,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"経費承認で役割別上限と通貨を揃える","concept":"ce-f1-q017","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. The requester's role, the tiered policy thresholds by role, and the current EUR-to-USD exchange rate, so the reported amount can be normalized and compared against the correct threshold.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe approval decision requires three logically necessary context elements:\n\n- Requester’s role — determines which policy tier applies.\n\n- Expense-policy threshold for that role — provides the authoritative approval rule.\n\n- Current EUR-to-USD exchange rate — converts the submitted amount into the currency used by the policy.\n\nWithout any one of these elements, the agent cannot make a correctly scoped comparison.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nSubmitted expense: EUR 900Requester’s role: Senior ConsultantApplicable USD threshold: USD 1,000Current EUR-to-USD rate: 1.12 Normalized expense = 900 × 1.12 = USD 1,008Decision = Escalate\n```\n\nThe historical approval rate is descriptive rather than authoritative. It indicates what happened previously but does not define what the current policy permits.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption D supplies the minimum complete decision context:\n\n- Identity-derived context: the requester’s role.\n\n- Authoritative business-rule context: the role-specific expense threshold.\n\n- Current operational context: the applicable exchange rate.\n\nThe role scopes the policy lookup to the correct tier. The exchange rate then normalizes the EUR amount into USD so the agent can compare equivalent values.\n\nDatabricks agents can query structured data in Unity Catalog tables or external sources through tools. For a known, repeatable operation—such as retrieving a role threshold, looking up an exchange rate, and evaluating a policy rule—Databricks supports deterministic tools implemented with Unity Catalog functions.\n\nThis decision should ideally be implemented as deterministic logic rather than leaving arithmetic and policy interpretation entirely to an unconstrained language model. Databricks describes tools as well-defined operations that agents can call to retrieve structured information or execute specific logic.\n\nThe requester’s manager identity may be needed afterward to route an escalation, but it is not required to determine whether the report exceeds the applicable policy threshold.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. The historical rate of approvals for the requester's cost-center over the past year, used to predict whether this report is likely to be approved.\n\nHistorical approval rates describe previous decisions; they do not define the current expense policy.\n\nUsing the approval rate as the primary decision signal could cause the agent to:\n\n- Approve an expense that violates the current policy.\n\n- Escalate a valid expense merely because the cost-center historically had a low approval rate.\n\n- Reproduce inconsistent or biased past decisions.\n\n- Ignore changes to thresholds or organizational roles.\n\nThe question asks the agent to execute a policy-based decision, not predict how humans historically behaved.\n\nB. The requester's manager's identity only, since managers are the ultimate decision-makers regardless of policy thresholds.\n\nThe manager’s identity does not reveal:\n\n- Which policy tier applies.\n\n- The applicable monetary threshold.\n\n- The USD value of the EUR expense.\n\n- Whether automatic approval or escalation is required.\n\nManager identity can be useful for routing the escalated report, but it cannot independently determine whether escalation is necessary.\n\nC. The tiered policy thresholds by role and the historical approval rate, without needing the exchange rate, since Genie can infer currency conversion automatically from context.\n\nThis option is missing two essential requirements.\n\nFirst, the agent needs the requester’s role to determine which tiered threshold applies. Second, it needs a current exchange rate to compare the EUR expense with a USD-denominated threshold.\n\nAn agent should not infer or invent a current currency conversion rate from general model knowledge. Exchange rates are time-sensitive operational data and must be retrieved from an authoritative table or service.\n\nThe historical approval rate does not compensate for either missing input.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is confusing predictive context with decision-authoritative context.\n\nHistorical approval patterns may appear useful, but the decision must be based on the current policy, the requester’s applicable role tier, and currency-normalized expense amount.\n\nAnother trap is assuming that an agent can safely infer current exchange rates without retrieving authoritative data.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe certification-exam answer is D because it contains the minimum information needed to evaluate the policy correctly.\n\nIn production, the decision could be encapsulated in a governed function similar to:\n\n```\nevaluate_expense(    requester_role,    submitted_amount,    submitted_currency,    submission_date)\n```\n\nThe function would:\n\n- Retrieve the policy version effective on the submission date.\n\n- Retrieve the threshold for the requester’s role.\n\n- Retrieve the appropriate exchange rate.\n\n- Normalize the submitted amount to USD.\n\n- Return `APPROVE`, `ESCALATE`, or `REVIEW_REQUIRED`.\n\n- Record the policy version, rate, calculation, and reason for auditability.\n\nThe requester’s manager identity could then be used as a separate context element to route an escalated report. Other real-world considerations include exchange-rate timestamps, policy-effective dates, rounding rules, missing-rate handling, manual-review fallbacks, least-privilege access, and decision tracing.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Connect agents to structured data\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools)\n\n- Create agent tools using Unity Catalog functions\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool)\n\n- Connect agents to tools\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/agent-tool](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/agent-tool)\n\n- Concepts: AI on Databricks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/concepts/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/concepts/)","source_option_explanations":["A. The historical rate of approvals for the requester's cost-center over the past year, used to predict whether this report is likely to be approved.\n\nHistorical approval rates describe previous decisions; they do not define the current expense policy.\n\nUsing the approval rate as the primary decision signal could cause the agent to:\n\n- Approve an expense that violates the current policy.\n\n- Escalate a valid expense merely because the cost-center historically had a low approval rate.\n\n- Reproduce inconsistent or biased past decisions.\n\n- Ignore changes to thresholds or organizational roles.\n\nThe question asks the agent to execute a policy-based decision, not predict how humans historically behaved.","B. The requester's manager's identity only, since managers are the ultimate decision-makers regardless of policy thresholds.\n\nThe manager’s identity does not reveal:\n\n- Which policy tier applies.\n\n- The applicable monetary threshold.\n\n- The USD value of the EUR expense.\n\n- Whether automatic approval or escalation is required.\n\nManager identity can be useful for routing the escalated report, but it cannot independently determine whether escalation is necessary.","C. The tiered policy thresholds by role and the historical approval rate, without needing the exchange rate, since Genie can infer currency conversion automatically from context.\n\nThis option is missing two essential requirements.\n\nFirst, the agent needs the requester’s role to determine which tiered threshold applies. Second, it needs a current exchange rate to compare the EUR expense with a USD-denominated threshold.\n\nAn agent should not infer or invent a current currency conversion rate from general model knowledge. Exchange rates are time-sensitive operational data and must be retrieved from an authoritative table or service.","Correct Answer\n\nD. The requester's role, the tiered policy thresholds by role, and the current EUR-to-USD exchange rate, so the reported amount can be normalized and compared against the correct threshold.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe approval decision requires three logically necessary context elements:\n\n- Requester’s role — determines which policy tier applies.\n\n- Expense-policy threshold for that role — provides the authoritative approval rule.\n\n- Current EUR-to-USD exchange rate — converts the submitted amount into the currency used by the policy.\n\nWithout any one of these elements, the agent cannot make a correctly scoped comparison.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nSubmitted expense: EUR 900Requester’s role: Senior ConsultantApplicable USD threshold: USD 1,000Current EUR-to-USD rate: 1.12 Normalized expense = 900 × 1.12 = USD 1,008Decision = Escalate\n```\n\nThe historical approval rate is descriptive rather than authoritative. It indicates what happened previously but does not define what the current policy permits.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption D supplies the minimum complete decision context:\n\n- Identity-derived context: the requester’s role.\n\n- Authoritative business-rule context: the role-specific expense threshold.\n\n- Current operational context: the applicable exchange rate.\n\nThe role scopes the policy lookup to the correct tier. The exchange rate then normalizes the EUR amount into USD so the agent can compare equivalent values.\n\nDatabricks agents can query structured data in Unity Catalog tables or external sources through tools. For a known, repeatable operation—such as retrieving a role threshold, looking up an exchange rate, and evaluating a policy rule—Databricks supports deterministic tools implemented with Unity Catalog functions.\n\nThis decision should ideally be implemented as deterministic logic rather than leaving arithmetic and policy interpretation entirely to an unconstrained language model. Databricks describes tools as well-defined operations that agents can call to retrieve structured information or execute specific logic.\n\nThe requester’s manager identity may be needed afterward to route an escalation, but it is not required to determine whether the report exceeds the applicable policy threshold."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/agent-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/concepts/"],"source_hash":"267872381a94b0ae0431e783720e63da1097cca2aef2f67ddeb6959ab07fc4b3","stem_ja":"Databricksのエージェントが、従業員の経費申請を承認するか、上位の判断へ回すかを決めます。利用できるデータには、申請者の職務とコストセンター、職務ごとの段階別経費上限、申請者の上司、過去1年のコストセンター別承認率、現在の為替レート表があります。申請は外貨の場合がありますが、ポリシーの上限は米ドル建てです。\n\nユーロ建てで提出された経費申請について、承認判断の範囲を正しく決めて実行するために必要なコンテキストの組み合わせはどれですか。","options_ja":["今回承認される可能性を予測するために使う、申請者のコストセンターにおける過去1年の承認率。","上限に関係なく最終判断者は上司なので、申請者の上司の情報だけ。","Genieがコンテキストから通貨換算を自動推測できるため、為替レートなしの、職務別の段階的上限と過去の承認率。","申請金額を同じ通貨に換算し正しい上限と比較できるよう、申請者の職務、職務別の段階的上限、現在のEURからUSDへの為替レート。"],"explanation_ja":"承認は過去の傾向の予測ではなく、今回に適用する規則との比較です。申請者の職務から上限を選び、ユーロの申請額を現在のレートで米ドルへ揃えて比較します。覚え方は「適用する規則と、比較する単位を揃える」。無関係な履歴を加えても、通貨や役割の不足は補えません。","distractors_ja":["過去の承認割合は今回のポリシー適合性を決める基準ではなく、為替や職務別上限も不足します。","上司の情報だけでは、今回の金額がどの基準に当てはまるかを比較できません。","外貨と米ドルの上限を比較するには、検証可能な為替情報が必要で、推測には任せられません。","誰に適用する基準かを決め、金額の通貨を一致させるため、判断に必要な情報が揃います。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-018","number":18,"form":1,"stem":"A Databricks team is deciding between two retrieval strategies for two different agents. Agent A answers questions against a static internal wiki of company policies that changes only a few times per quarter, where recall across the full corpus matters — the agent must consider all potentially relevant policy sections before answering. Agent B answers questions about live inventory counts and current order status, which change every few seconds and must always reflect the latest values.\n\nWhich retrieval strategy should be applied to each agent, respectively?","options":["Both agents should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval over a periodically refreshed AI Search index, since embeddings are the standard approach for any RAG use case.","Agent A should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval over a Databricks AI Search index built from the wiki corpus; Agent B should use just-in-time agentic retrieval via tool calls that query the live Delta tables directly at inference time.","Agent A should use just-in-time retrieval via tool calls that re-scan the entire wiki on every question; Agent B should use a periodically refreshed AI Search index rebuilt every few seconds.","Both agents should use just-in-time agentic retrieval exclusively, since JIT retrieval always returns fresher data than any embedding-based approach regardless of source volatility."],"correct":1,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"社内規程の意味検索と最新在庫の直接照会","concept":"ce-f1-q018","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Agent A should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval over a Databricks AI Search index built from the wiki corpus; Agent B should use just-in-time agentic retrieval via tool calls that query the live Delta tables directly at inference time.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe two agents have fundamentally different context requirements:\n\n- Agent A uses a mostly static, unstructured document corpus. Its main concern is retrieving all policy passages that may be relevant to a question.\n\n- Agent B uses rapidly changing, structured operational data. Its main concern is returning the latest committed inventory and order values.\n\nFor Agent A, preprocessing the wiki, splitting it into searchable chunks, generating embeddings, and maintaining a Databricks AI Search index is appropriate. The index can efficiently search the complete corpus at inference time without rescanning every source document.\n\nFor Agent B, embedding indexed values would create a freshness gap between the operational tables and the index. The agent should instead call a governed SQL, Genie, or Unity Catalog function tool that queries the live tables when the question is asked.\n\nTherefore, B correctly matches each retrieval method to the source’s structure, volatility, and freshness requirements.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nFor Agent A, pre-inference retrieval through Databricks AI Search is suitable because:\n\n- The source is an unstructured wiki corpus.\n\n- Policy content changes infrequently.\n\n- Embeddings and indexes can be prepared before user requests.\n\n- Similarity or hybrid search can identify relevant sections efficiently.\n\n- Periodic synchronization is sufficient for a corpus that changes only a few times per quarter.\n\n“Recall across the full corpus” does not mean inserting the entire wiki into every model prompt. It means indexing the complete corpus and configuring retrieval to locate all potentially relevant sections. In practice, recall can be improved through hybrid search, query rewriting, reranking, appropriate chunking, and a sufficiently large initial candidate set.\n\nFor Agent B, just-in-time retrieval is appropriate because inventory counts and order statuses change every few seconds. A tool call can execute a governed query against the current Delta table state during the agent interaction.\n\nThis avoids depending on:\n\n- A potentially stale embedding index.\n\n- An index synchronization interval.\n\n- Embeddings that are poorly suited to exact numeric and transactional lookups.\n\nInventory and order-status questions are also usually structured queries involving exact identifiers, filters, joins, aggregates, and current values. SQL-oriented tools are better suited to these operations than semantic vector retrieval.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Both agents should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval over a periodically refreshed AI Search index, since embeddings are the standard approach for any RAG use case.\n\nThis incorrectly assumes that embedding search is appropriate for every data source.\n\nAI Search is well suited to semantic retrieval over unstructured content, but live inventory and order status are structured and highly volatile. Even a frequently synchronized index introduces possible delay between a source-table change and its availability in the index.\n\nEmbedding search is also not the most reliable mechanism for exact questions such as:\n\n- “How many units of SKU 7821 are currently available?”\n\n- “What is the current status of order 104587?”\n\n- “Which warehouse has this product in stock?”\n\nThese should be answered using direct structured-data queries.\n\nC. Agent A should use just-in-time retrieval via tool calls that re-scan the entire wiki on every question; Agent B should use a periodically refreshed AI Search index rebuilt every few seconds.\n\nThis reverses the appropriate strategies.\n\nRescanning the complete wiki for every request would be inefficient, slow, and unnecessarily expensive. The wiki is stable enough to preprocess and index.\n\nRebuilding or refreshing an AI Search index every few seconds for Agent B would introduce unnecessary operational complexity and still might not guarantee that the response reflects the latest transactional value. Direct table queries are more appropriate for rapidly changing structured records.\n\nD. Both agents should use just-in-time agentic retrieval exclusively, since JIT retrieval always returns fresher data than any embedding-based approach regardless of source volatility.\n\nJust-in-time retrieval can improve freshness, but freshness is not the only design consideration.\n\nFor Agent A, the corpus changes infrequently, and repeatedly scanning or dynamically processing the entire wiki would not provide meaningful freshness benefits. A prepared search index offers more efficient semantic retrieval.\n\nThe term “always” is also a warning sign. The correct retrieval design depends on:\n\n- Data volatility.\n\n- Data structure.\n\n- Query type.\n\n- Latency requirements.\n\n- Recall and precision requirements.\n\n- Index synchronization behavior.\n\n- Operational cost.\n\nA single strategy is not optimal for both agents.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is treating all RAG workloads as embedding-search workloads.\n\nUse indexed semantic retrieval for relatively stable, unstructured corpora. Use inference-time tool queries when answers depend on rapidly changing structured values.\n\nAnother trap is interpreting “recall across the full corpus” as requiring a complete source scan for every question. A properly constructed index covers the complete corpus while retrieving only the most relevant sections.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe expected exam mapping is:\n\nAgentData characteristicsPreferred retrievalAgent AStatic, unstructured policy documentsDatabricks AI SearchAgent BLive, structured operational recordsJust-in-time SQL or tool call\n\nIn production, Agent A could use:\n\n- Structure-aware policy chunking.\n\n- Hybrid keyword and vector retrieval.\n\n- Metadata such as policy section, effective date, and department.\n\n- Reranking and sufficient candidate retrieval to improve recall.\n\n- Index synchronization whenever policies change.\n\nAgent B could use a governed tool such as:\n\n```\nget_inventory(sku, warehouse_id)get_order_status(order_id)\n```\n\nThe tool should query the authoritative tables at request time and return structured results. The implementation should also account for ingestion latency, Delta transaction consistency, SQL warehouse latency, Unity Catalog permissions, tool timeouts, and audit logging.\n\nA combined agent can use both methods: AI Search for explanatory documents and direct tool calls for current transactional facts.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) on Databricks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation)\n\n- Databricks AI Search\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search)\n\n- Connect agents to unstructured data\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/unstructured-retrieval-tools](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/unstructured-retrieval-tools)\n\n- Connect agents to structured data\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools)","source_option_explanations":["A. Both agents should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval over a periodically refreshed AI Search index, since embeddings are the standard approach for any RAG use case.\n\nThis incorrectly assumes that embedding search is appropriate for every data source.\n\nAI Search is well suited to semantic retrieval over unstructured content, but live inventory and order status are structured and highly volatile. Even a frequently synchronized index introduces possible delay between a source-table change and its availability in the index.\n\nEmbedding search is also not the most reliable mechanism for exact questions such as:\n\n- “How many units of SKU 7821 are currently available?”\n\n- “What is the current status of order 104587?”\n\n- “Which warehouse has this product in stock?”\n\nThese should be answered using direct structured-data queries.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Agent A should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval over a Databricks AI Search index built from the wiki corpus; Agent B should use just-in-time agentic retrieval via tool calls that query the live Delta tables directly at inference time.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe two agents have fundamentally different context requirements:\n\n- Agent A uses a mostly static, unstructured document corpus. Its main concern is retrieving all policy passages that may be relevant to a question.\n\n- Agent B uses rapidly changing, structured operational data. Its main concern is returning the latest committed inventory and order values.\n\nFor Agent A, preprocessing the wiki, splitting it into searchable chunks, generating embeddings, and maintaining a Databricks AI Search index is appropriate. The index can efficiently search the complete corpus at inference time without rescanning every source document.\n\nFor Agent B, embedding indexed values would create a freshness gap between the operational tables and the index. The agent should instead call a governed SQL, Genie, or Unity Catalog function tool that queries the live tables when the question is asked.\n\nTherefore, B correctly matches each retrieval method to the source’s structure, volatility, and freshness requirements.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nFor Agent A, pre-inference retrieval through Databricks AI Search is suitable because:\n\n- The source is an unstructured wiki corpus.\n\n- Policy content changes infrequently.\n\n- Embeddings and indexes can be prepared before user requests.\n\n- Similarity or hybrid search can identify relevant sections efficiently.\n\n- Periodic synchronization is sufficient for a corpus that changes only a few times per quarter.\n\n“Recall across the full corpus” does not mean inserting the entire wiki into every model prompt. It means indexing the complete corpus and configuring retrieval to locate all potentially relevant sections. In practice, recall can be improved through hybrid search, query rewriting, reranking, appropriate chunking, and a sufficiently large initial candidate set.\n\nFor Agent B, just-in-time retrieval is appropriate because inventory counts and order statuses change every few seconds. A tool call can execute a governed query against the current Delta table state during the agent interaction.\n\nThis avoids depending on:\n\n- A potentially stale embedding index.\n\n- An index synchronization interval.\n\n- Embeddings that are poorly suited to exact numeric and transactional lookups.\n\nInventory and order-status questions are also usually structured queries involving exact identifiers, filters, joins, aggregates, and current values. SQL-oriented tools are better suited to these operations than semantic vector retrieval.","C. Agent A should use just-in-time retrieval via tool calls that re-scan the entire wiki on every question; Agent B should use a periodically refreshed AI Search index rebuilt every few seconds.\n\nThis reverses the appropriate strategies.\n\nRescanning the complete wiki for every request would be inefficient, slow, and unnecessarily expensive. The wiki is stable enough to preprocess and index.\n\nRebuilding or refreshing an AI Search index every few seconds for Agent B would introduce unnecessary operational complexity and still might not guarantee that the response reflects the latest transactional value. Direct table queries are more appropriate for rapidly changing structured records.","D. Both agents should use just-in-time agentic retrieval exclusively, since JIT retrieval always returns fresher data than any embedding-based approach regardless of source volatility.\n\nJust-in-time retrieval can improve freshness, but freshness is not the only design consideration.\n\nFor Agent A, the corpus changes infrequently, and repeatedly scanning or dynamically processing the entire wiki would not provide meaningful freshness benefits. A prepared search index offers more efficient semantic retrieval.\n\nThe term “always” is also a warning sign. The correct retrieval design depends on:\n\n- Data volatility.\n\n- Data structure.\n\n- Query type.\n\n- Latency requirements.\n\n- Recall and precision requirements.\n\n- Index synchronization behavior.\n\n- Operational cost.\n\nA single strategy is not optimal for both agents."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/unstructured-retrieval-tools","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools"],"source_hash":"f22fd0dc5e5c3a8751d199af35af136e36009b4d848e7a47811434e5cfdf48b0","stem_ja":"Databricksのチームが、2つの異なるエージェントに対する検索戦略を選んでいます。エージェントAは、四半期に数回だけ変わる静的な社内規程Wikiについて答えます。回答前に関連する可能性のある規程の節をすべて考慮する必要があり、コーパス全体からの取り漏れを抑えることが重要です。エージェントBは、数秒ごとに変わる在庫数と注文の現在の状態について答え、常に最新値を反映する必要があります。\n\nそれぞれに適用すべき検索戦略はどれですか。","options_ja":["どのRAGでも埋め込みが標準なので、両エージェントとも定期更新のAI Searchインデックスに対する推論前の埋め込み検索を使う。","AはWikiコーパスから作ったDatabricks AI Searchインデックスに対する推論前の埋め込みベース検索を使い、Bは推論時に稼働中のDeltaテーブルを直接照会するツール呼び出しによる、必要時のエージェント検索を使う。","Aは質問のたびにWiki全体を再走査する必要時のツール検索を使い、Bは数秒ごとに作り直す定期更新のAI Searchインデックスを使う。","元データの変動性に関係なく、JIT検索はどの埋め込み方式より常に新しいデータを返すため、両方とも必要時のエージェント検索だけを使う。"],"explanation_ja":"変化が少ない規程の関連箇所を探す用途では、事前構築した索引を再利用できます。数秒で変わる在庫と注文状態は、推論時に元の表を直接読む方が最新性に合います。覚え方は「安定した文書は索引、動く現在値は直接取得」。鮮度と検索目的に合わせて方法を分けます。","distractors_ja":["数秒ごとの現在値では、定期更新の索引に反映されるまでの遅れが問題になります。","規程の広い意味検索と、最新の構造化状態の取得という異なる条件に合っています。","静的Wikiの毎回の全走査や、最新値のための索引頻繁再構築は、不要な処理負担を増やします。","必要時取得が常に最良とは限らず、静的コーパスでは索引の事前構築と再利用が有効です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-019","number":19,"form":1,"stem":"A context engineer investigates a Databricks agent's declining context_recall scores in MLflow evaluation traces, specific to questions about employee compensation bands. Cross-referencing Unity Catalog metadata, the engineer finds that the compensation table was recently reclassified with an ABAC tag restricting it to the \"HR-Confidential\" attribute group, and the agent's service principal was not added to that group when the reclassification occurred. As a result, the agent's retrieval tool silently returns zero rows for these questions instead of an error, and the agent responds as if no compensation data exists.\n\nWhich Unity Catalog governance action most directly resolves this failure?","options":["Lower the compaction threshold for the agent so it retains more of the eval log history.","Rebuild the AI Search index from scratch to refresh stale embeddings for the compensation table.","Grant the agent's service principal membership in (or an ABAC policy match for) the \"HR-Confidential\" attribute group so its queries against the compensation table are authorized to return rows again.","Increase the agent's num_results parameter so more candidate rows are retrieved per query."],"correct":2,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"権限変更による検索の取り漏れ","concept":"ce-f1-q019","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Grant the agent's service principal membership in (or an ABAC policy match for) the \"HR-Confidential\" attribute group so its queries against the compensation table are authorized to return rows again.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure is caused by an authorization change, not by degraded embeddings, insufficient retrieval depth, or trace retention.\n\nThe decisive sequence is:\n\n- Compensation questions previously returned useful context.\n\n- The compensation table was reclassified with a restrictive ABAC tag.\n\n- The agent’s service principal was not included in the authorized principal group.\n\n- The retrieval tool now returns zero visible rows.\n\n- The missing rows cause the agent’s `context_recall` score to decline.\n\nUnity Catalog ABAC policies are evaluated at query time using the querying principal’s identity, group memberships, and governed tags on the accessed data. Changes to group membership can therefore change which rows the same query returns.\n\nRestoring the service principal’s authorized relationship to the `HR-Confidential` policy directly resolves the root cause.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe retrieval tool is apparently executing under the agent’s service principal. Consequently, Unity Catalog evaluates the compensation-table policy against that service principal—not against the agent’s prompt, MLflow evaluation identity, or expected answer.\n\nThe applicable ABAC policy likely uses one of these mechanisms:\n\n- A `TO` or `EXCEPT` principal list.\n\n- An account-group membership test such as `is_account_group_member()`.\n\n- A row-filter UDF that returns compensation rows only for approved principals.\n\nDatabricks documents that ABAC policy targeting can include users, groups, and service principals. It also recommends using `TO` and `EXCEPT` for straightforward principal targeting and account-group membership functions for conditional policy logic.\n\nService principals can be members of Databricks account groups. Therefore, adding the agent’s service principal to the approved HR group—or updating the policy’s principal condition so that the service principal is authorized—allows its subsequent queries to return the permitted compensation rows.\n\nA terminology clarification is useful: in Unity Catalog, governed tags normally describe securable objects, while users, groups, and service principals are the principals targeted or evaluated by the policy. Thus, the practical action is to correct the service principal’s account-group membership or the policy’s `TO`, `EXCEPT`, or identity-based condition.\n\nThis assumes the service principal still has the necessary baseline Unity Catalog privileges, such as `USE CATALOG`, `USE SCHEMA`, and `SELECT`. Row-filter and column-mask ABAC policies restrict data that an already-authorized principal can see; they do not independently grant ordinary table access.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Lower the compaction threshold for the agent so it retains more of the eval log history.\n\nThis addresses trace or evaluation-log retention, not data authorization.\n\nThe engineer already has sufficient trace evidence to identify the problem: compensation retrieval calls return zero rows after the ABAC reclassification. Keeping additional historical traces would help with diagnosis but would not permit the service principal to see compensation records.\n\nThe retrieval result would remain empty until the Unity Catalog authorization configuration is corrected.\n\nB. Rebuild the AI Search index from scratch to refresh stale embeddings for the compensation table.\n\nThe scenario describes a structured retrieval tool querying a governed compensation table, not an embedding-staleness problem.\n\nEven if an AI Search index existed, rebuilding it would not override Unity Catalog access controls. Moreover, the question explicitly connects the failure to an ABAC reclassification and missing service-principal authorization.\n\nRebuilding embeddings treats the symptom as a relevance problem while leaving the actual authorization failure unchanged.\n\nD. Increase the agent's `num_results` parameter so more candidate rows are retrieved per query.\n\n`num_results` affects the number of results returned after records are eligible for retrieval. It cannot make unauthorized rows visible.\n\nWhen the ABAC policy filters all compensation rows for the service principal, increasing the requested result count still produces zero authorized rows:\n\n```\nEligible rows after policy enforcement: 0Requested result count: 5, 10, or 100Returned rows: 0\n```\n\nRetrieval tuning cannot compensate for a governance denial.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is confusing a low `context_recall` score with a search-quality problem.\n\nEvaluation metrics identify the observable failure—missing relevant context—but do not necessarily identify its cause. Here, the cause is explicitly an identity and governance mismatch, so the solution must restore authorized access rather than tune retrieval parameters.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is C, but production remediation should be controlled carefully because employee compensation is highly sensitive.\n\nThe governance team should:\n\n- Confirm that the agent is genuinely approved to access compensation data.\n\n- Add the service principal to the appropriate account-level group or modify the policy’s principal targeting.\n\n- Avoid broadly exempting the service principal from unrelated HR controls.\n\n- Verify its baseline Unity Catalog privileges.\n\n- Run a direct query as the service principal to confirm expected row visibility.\n\n- Add tests that distinguish “no matching business data” from “data hidden by authorization.”\n\n- Monitor policy, governed-tag, and group-membership changes that can affect agent retrieval.\n\n- Log the effective principal, applied policy, row count, and retrieval outcome for troubleshooting.\n\nBecause ABAC evaluates dynamically, group-membership or tag changes can alter effective results at query time without requiring the agent application itself to be redeployed.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Core concepts for attribute-based access control (ABAC)\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/core-concepts](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/core-concepts)\n\n- Row filter and column mask policy evaluation and runtime behavior\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/policy-evaluation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/policy-evaluation)\n\n- Best practices for ABAC policies\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/best-practices](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/best-practices)\n\n- Manage groups\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/users-groups/manage-groups](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/users-groups/manage-groups)","source_option_explanations":["A. Lower the compaction threshold for the agent so it retains more of the eval log history.\n\nThis addresses trace or evaluation-log retention, not data authorization.\n\nThe engineer already has sufficient trace evidence to identify the problem: compensation retrieval calls return zero rows after the ABAC reclassification. Keeping additional historical traces would help with diagnosis but would not permit the service principal to see compensation records.\n\nThe retrieval result would remain empty until the Unity Catalog authorization configuration is corrected.","B. Rebuild the AI Search index from scratch to refresh stale embeddings for the compensation table.\n\nThe scenario describes a structured retrieval tool querying a governed compensation table, not an embedding-staleness problem.\n\nEven if an AI Search index existed, rebuilding it would not override Unity Catalog access controls. Moreover, the question explicitly connects the failure to an ABAC reclassification and missing service-principal authorization.\n\nRebuilding embeddings treats the symptom as a relevance problem while leaving the actual authorization failure unchanged.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Grant the agent's service principal membership in (or an ABAC policy match for) the \"HR-Confidential\" attribute group so its queries against the compensation table are authorized to return rows again.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure is caused by an authorization change, not by degraded embeddings, insufficient retrieval depth, or trace retention.\n\nThe decisive sequence is:\n\n- Compensation questions previously returned useful context.\n\n- The compensation table was reclassified with a restrictive ABAC tag.\n\n- The agent’s service principal was not included in the authorized principal group.\n\n- The retrieval tool now returns zero visible rows.\n\n- The missing rows cause the agent’s `context_recall` score to decline.\n\nUnity Catalog ABAC policies are evaluated at query time using the querying principal’s identity, group memberships, and governed tags on the accessed data. Changes to group membership can therefore change which rows the same query returns.\n\nRestoring the service principal’s authorized relationship to the `HR-Confidential` policy directly resolves the root cause.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe retrieval tool is apparently executing under the agent’s service principal. Consequently, Unity Catalog evaluates the compensation-table policy against that service principal—not against the agent’s prompt, MLflow evaluation identity, or expected answer.\n\nThe applicable ABAC policy likely uses one of these mechanisms:\n\n- A `TO` or `EXCEPT` principal list.\n\n- An account-group membership test such as `is_account_group_member()`.\n\n- A row-filter UDF that returns compensation rows only for approved principals.\n\nDatabricks documents that ABAC policy targeting can include users, groups, and service principals. It also recommends using `TO` and `EXCEPT` for straightforward principal targeting and account-group membership functions for conditional policy logic.\n\nService principals can be members of Databricks account groups. Therefore, adding the agent’s service principal to the approved HR group—or updating the policy’s principal condition so that the service principal is authorized—allows its subsequent queries to return the permitted compensation rows.\n\nA terminology clarification is useful: in Unity Catalog, governed tags normally describe securable objects, while users, groups, and service principals are the principals targeted or evaluated by the policy. Thus, the practical action is to correct the service principal’s account-group membership or the policy’s `TO`, `EXCEPT`, or identity-based condition.\n\nThis assumes the service principal still has the necessary baseline Unity Catalog privileges, such as `USE CATALOG`, `USE SCHEMA`, and `SELECT`. Row-filter and column-mask ABAC policies restrict data that an already-authorized principal can see; they do not independently grant ordinary table access.","D. Increase the agent's `num_results` parameter so more candidate rows are retrieved per query.\n\n`num_results` affects the number of results returned after records are eligible for retrieval. It cannot make unauthorized rows visible.\n\nWhen the ABAC policy filters all compensation rows for the service principal, increasing the requested result count still produces zero authorized rows:\n\n```\nEligible rows after policy enforcement: 0Requested result count: 5, 10, or 100Returned rows: 0\n```\n\nRetrieval tuning cannot compensate for a governance denial."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/core-concepts","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/policy-evaluation","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/abac/best-practices","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/users-groups/manage-groups"],"source_hash":"6e14c512229bd5da33c431f71b6e39b172823972ba5c23072f982299c2478d54","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、従業員の給与等級に関する質問でDatabricksエージェントのcontext_recallが低下したことを、MLflow評価トレースから調べています。Unity Catalogのメタデータを確認すると、給与テーブルが最近、HR-Confidential属性グループに制限するABACタグで再分類されたのに、エージェントのサービスプリンシパルはそのグループへ追加されていませんでした。その結果、検索ツールはエラーを返す代わりに該当質問で黙って0行を返し、エージェントは給与データ自体が存在しないように回答します。\n\nこの障害を最も直接解決するUnity Catalogのガバナンス対応はどれですか。","options_ja":["評価ログの履歴をより多く残せるよう、エージェントの圧縮閾値を下げる。","給与テーブルの古い埋め込みを更新するため、AI Searchインデックスをゼロから作り直す。","給与テーブルのクエリが再び行を返す権限を持てるよう、エージェントのサービスプリンシパルにHR-Confidential属性グループへの所属、またはABACポリシーへの適合を付与する。","1クエリでより多くの候補行を取得するよう、num_resultsを増やす。"],"explanation_ja":"データがないのではなく、ポリシー変更で実行主体が必要な行を読めなくなっています。承認された利用範囲の中で、サービスプリンシパルの権限・グループ・ポリシー適合を修正するのが原因に合う対策です。覚え方は「検索の取り漏れが増えたら、索引だけでなく権限変更も見る」です。","distractors_ja":["圧縮は会話の情報量を調整するもので、データ取得主体のアクセス許可は修復しません。","元のデータが索引にあっても、権限の不一致が残る限り、取得できる範囲は回復しません。","認可の不一致という直接の原因を修正します。業務上許可された権限範囲で行うことが前提です。","取得件数を増やしても、アクセス制御で見えない行は返りません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-020","number":20,"form":1,"stem":"Before deploying a customer-facing agent, a team reviews the Unity Catalog environment it will retrieve from. It contains a small set of certified, owner-maintained \"gold\" tables that are the single source of truth for pricing and product specifications, and a much larger number of ad hoc, uncertified \"sandbox\" schemas created by individual analysts, some of which contain outdated or exploratory copies of the same pricing data with different values. The team wants the agent's retrieval space, before deployment, to be constrained to only the authoritative sources.\n\nWhich governance strategy achieves this?","options":["Grant the agent's service principal broad SELECT access across both the gold and sandbox catalogs, and rely on the agent's system prompt to instruct it to prefer the gold tables when it notices a conflict.","Leave permissions unchanged, but add a general instruction to the agent's system prompt stating \"always use the most authoritative source,\" without any Unity Catalog access changes.","Grant access to the sandbox schemas only, since analysts' more granular exploratory tables reflect more up-to-date business context than the periodically-certified gold tables.","Register the agent's retrieval tools and AI Search indexes only against the certified \"gold\" schema/catalog, and grant the agent's service principal SELECT privileges scoped to that schema alone, withholding access to the sandbox schemas entirely."],"correct":3,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"正式なGoldデータだけへ検索範囲を制限","concept":"ce-f1-q020","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Register the agent's retrieval tools and AI Search indexes only against the certified \"gold\" schema/catalog, and grant the agent's service principal SELECT privileges scoped to that schema alone, withholding access to the sandbox schemas entirely.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirement is to constrain the agent’s retrieval space before deployment so it can access only authoritative pricing and product information.\n\nThe scenario establishes that:\n\n- The Gold tables are certified, owner-maintained, and authoritative.\n\n- Sandbox schemas contain duplicate, exploratory, and potentially outdated values.\n\n- Conflicting sandbox data could cause inaccurate customer-facing responses.\n\n- The team wants a preventive governance control, not merely guidance about which source the agent should prefer.\n\nThe strongest solution is therefore to apply two complementary restrictions:\n\n- Configure the agent’s retrieval tools and AI Search indexes using only Gold assets.\n\n- Apply least-privilege Unity Catalog permissions so the agent’s service principal cannot access sandbox assets.\n\nThis prevents the sandbox data from entering the agent’s retrievable context in the first place.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nUnity Catalog’s certified status identifies assets that have satisfied internal standards for accuracy, completeness, and trust. This makes the certified Gold assets appropriate sources for a production customer-facing agent.\n\nHowever, certification is primarily a trust and discoverability signal. The team must also enforce access boundaries using Unity Catalog privileges.\n\nUnity Catalog provides fine-grained privileges at the catalog, schema, table, view, function, and other securable-object levels. Permissions granted at a container level can be inherited by current and future child objects, allowing the service principal’s access to be restricted to the approved Gold schema while excluding sandbox schemas.\n\nRestricting both the retrieval configuration and the identity’s permissions provides defense in depth:\n\n- The tools are designed to query only approved Gold assets.\n\n- The AI Search indexes are built only from approved Gold data.\n\n- The service principal cannot bypass those tools and read sandbox tables.\n\n- Prompt wording cannot accidentally expose conflicting exploratory data.\n\n- New sandbox tables remain unavailable to the agent.\n\nFor an actual implementation, the service principal normally requires:\n\n- `USE CATALOG` on the Gold catalog.\n\n- `USE SCHEMA` on the Gold schema.\n\n- `SELECT` on the required tables, views, or schema.\n\n- `SELECT` on any AI Search indexes it queries.\n\n- `EXECUTE` on any approved Unity Catalog functions used as tools.\n\nUnity Catalog requires the applicable usage privileges on parent objects in addition to the operation-specific privilege. AI Search similarly requires `USE CATALOG`, `USE SCHEMA`, and `SELECT` on the index.\n\nTherefore, option D provides the most direct and enforceable governance strategy.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Grant the agent's service principal broad SELECT access across both the gold and sandbox catalogs, and rely on the agent's system prompt to instruct it to prefer the gold tables when it notices a conflict.\n\nThis violates least privilege and leaves unreliable sources within the agent’s accessible retrieval space.\n\nA system prompt is behavioral guidance, not an authorization boundary. The agent could still:\n\n- Select a sandbox table with a plausible name.\n\n- Retrieve outdated pricing.\n\n- Combine conflicting Gold and sandbox values.\n\n- Follow an incorrectly generated SQL query.\n\n- Be influenced by tool descriptions or retrieved metadata.\n\nBroad access unnecessarily increases the likelihood of inaccurate customer-facing answers.\n\nB. Leave permissions unchanged, but add a general instruction to the agent's system prompt stating \"always use the most authoritative source,\" without any Unity Catalog access changes.\n\nThis is weaker than option A because it does not even constrain the configured data sources.\n\nThe phrase “most authoritative source” is ambiguous unless the agent is given deterministic metadata and selection logic. It also does not prevent retrieval from sandbox schemas.\n\nGovernance requirements should be enforced through identity permissions and controlled tool configuration rather than relying entirely on probabilistic prompt compliance.\n\nC. Grant access to the sandbox schemas only, since analysts' more granular exploratory tables reflect more up-to-date business context than the periodically-certified gold tables.\n\nThis contradicts the scenario.\n\nThe question explicitly states that the Gold tables are the single source of truth and that some sandbox tables contain outdated or exploratory pricing values. Granularity does not imply accuracy or authority.\n\nUsing only sandbox data would exclude the organization’s certified sources and expose the agent to precisely the inconsistency the team is trying to prevent.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is treating a system prompt as a governance control.\n\nInstructions such as “prefer certified data” can improve agent behavior, but they do not prevent access to uncertified data. The reliable approach is to remove unauthorized or untrusted sources from both:\n\n- The agent’s configured retrieval surface.\n\n- The service principal’s Unity Catalog permissions.\n\nAnother trap is assuming that a certified tag automatically blocks access to uncertified objects. Certification communicates trust status; Unity Catalog privileges enforce access.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe certification-exam answer is D: constrain both the agent configuration and its service-principal permissions to the certified Gold domain.\n\nA production design should also:\n\n- Mark approved assets as certified and obsolete assets as deprecated.\n\n- Use a dedicated service principal for the production agent.\n\n- Avoid inherited catalog-wide access that includes sandbox schemas.\n\n- Build AI Search indexes only from approved source tables or views.\n\n- Restrict Genie spaces and SQL tools to authoritative datasets.\n\n- Review effective grants before deployment.\n\n- Test whether the service principal can enumerate or query sandbox objects.\n\n- Monitor new grants and schema changes through audit logs.\n\n- Revoke legacy group memberships that might indirectly provide sandbox access.\n\nA prompt may still tell the agent that Gold assets are authoritative, but that should supplement—not replace—enforced Unity Catalog controls.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Flag data as certified or deprecated\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/certify-deprecate-data](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/certify-deprecate-data)\n\n- Unity Catalog permissions model concepts\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/permissions-concepts](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/permissions-concepts)\n\n- Manage privileges in Unity Catalog\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges)\n\n- Query an AI Search index\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search)","source_option_explanations":["A. Grant the agent's service principal broad SELECT access across both the gold and sandbox catalogs, and rely on the agent's system prompt to instruct it to prefer the gold tables when it notices a conflict.\n\nThis violates least privilege and leaves unreliable sources within the agent’s accessible retrieval space.\n\nA system prompt is behavioral guidance, not an authorization boundary. The agent could still:\n\n- Select a sandbox table with a plausible name.\n\n- Retrieve outdated pricing.\n\n- Combine conflicting Gold and sandbox values.\n\n- Follow an incorrectly generated SQL query.\n\n- Be influenced by tool descriptions or retrieved metadata.\n\nBroad access unnecessarily increases the likelihood of inaccurate customer-facing answers.","B. Leave permissions unchanged, but add a general instruction to the agent's system prompt stating \"always use the most authoritative source,\" without any Unity Catalog access changes.\n\nThis is weaker than option A because it does not even constrain the configured data sources.\n\nThe phrase “most authoritative source” is ambiguous unless the agent is given deterministic metadata and selection logic. It also does not prevent retrieval from sandbox schemas.\n\nGovernance requirements should be enforced through identity permissions and controlled tool configuration rather than relying entirely on probabilistic prompt compliance.","C. Grant access to the sandbox schemas only, since analysts' more granular exploratory tables reflect more up-to-date business context than the periodically-certified gold tables.\n\nThis contradicts the scenario.\n\nThe question explicitly states that the Gold tables are the single source of truth and that some sandbox tables contain outdated or exploratory pricing values. Granularity does not imply accuracy or authority.\n\nUsing only sandbox data would exclude the organization’s certified sources and expose the agent to precisely the inconsistency the team is trying to prevent.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Register the agent's retrieval tools and AI Search indexes only against the certified \"gold\" schema/catalog, and grant the agent's service principal SELECT privileges scoped to that schema alone, withholding access to the sandbox schemas entirely.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirement is to constrain the agent’s retrieval space before deployment so it can access only authoritative pricing and product information.\n\nThe scenario establishes that:\n\n- The Gold tables are certified, owner-maintained, and authoritative.\n\n- Sandbox schemas contain duplicate, exploratory, and potentially outdated values.\n\n- Conflicting sandbox data could cause inaccurate customer-facing responses.\n\n- The team wants a preventive governance control, not merely guidance about which source the agent should prefer.\n\nThe strongest solution is therefore to apply two complementary restrictions:\n\n- Configure the agent’s retrieval tools and AI Search indexes using only Gold assets.\n\n- Apply least-privilege Unity Catalog permissions so the agent’s service principal cannot access sandbox assets.\n\nThis prevents the sandbox data from entering the agent’s retrievable context in the first place.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nUnity Catalog’s certified status identifies assets that have satisfied internal standards for accuracy, completeness, and trust. This makes the certified Gold assets appropriate sources for a production customer-facing agent.\n\nHowever, certification is primarily a trust and discoverability signal. The team must also enforce access boundaries using Unity Catalog privileges.\n\nUnity Catalog provides fine-grained privileges at the catalog, schema, table, view, function, and other securable-object levels. Permissions granted at a container level can be inherited by current and future child objects, allowing the service principal’s access to be restricted to the approved Gold schema while excluding sandbox schemas.\n\nRestricting both the retrieval configuration and the identity’s permissions provides defense in depth:\n\n- The tools are designed to query only approved Gold assets.\n\n- The AI Search indexes are built only from approved Gold data.\n\n- The service principal cannot bypass those tools and read sandbox tables.\n\n- Prompt wording cannot accidentally expose conflicting exploratory data.\n\n- New sandbox tables remain unavailable to the agent.\n\nFor an actual implementation, the service principal normally requires:\n\n- `USE CATALOG` on the Gold catalog.\n\n- `USE SCHEMA` on the Gold schema.\n\n- `SELECT` on the required tables, views, or schema.\n\n- `SELECT` on any AI Search indexes it queries.\n\n- `EXECUTE` on any approved Unity Catalog functions used as tools.\n\nUnity Catalog requires the applicable usage privileges on parent objects in addition to the operation-specific privilege. AI Search similarly requires `USE CATALOG`, `USE SCHEMA`, and `SELECT` on the index.\n\nTherefore, option D provides the most direct and enforceable governance strategy."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/certify-deprecate-data","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/permissions-concepts","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/manage-privileges","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search"],"source_hash":"dba6064aa1e2c430abb09116eb9259e40e82551b5e67666d046dc746c14843a1","stem_ja":"顧客向けエージェントのデプロイ前に、チームが検索先のUnity Catalog環境を点検しています。価格と製品仕様の単一の正の情報源である、所有者が保守する少数の認定済みGoldテーブルがあります。一方、個々のアナリストが作った多数の臨時で未認定のsandboxスキーマもあり、異なる価格を持つ古いコピーや調査用コピーが含まれています。チームはデプロイ前に、検索空間を正式な情報源だけへ制約したいと考えています。\n\nこれを実現するガバナンス戦略はどれですか。","options_ja":["サービスプリンシパルへGoldとsandboxの両方の広いSELECT権限を与え、矛盾を見つけたらGoldを優先するよう、システムプロンプトへ指示する。","Unity Catalogのアクセスは変えず、権限をそのままにし、システムプロンプトへ「常に最も正式な情報源を使う」という一般的指示を加える。","アナリストの細粒度の調査テーブルは定期認定のGoldより新しい業務状況を反映するため、sandboxスキーマだけへアクセスを与える。","検索ツールとAI Searchインデックスを認定済みGoldスキーマ／カタログだけに対して登録する。サービスプリンシパルのSELECT権限もそのスキーマに限定し、sandboxへのアクセスを与えない。"],"explanation_ja":"正式な情報だけを使わせたいなら、モデルに選別を頼むより、検索先と実行主体のアクセス範囲を絞ります。認定Goldだけをツール・索引・権限の対象にすれば、古いsandbox価格を取得する経路を防げます。覚え方は「信頼度の低い情報は、優先順位よりアクセス境界で除く」です。","distractors_ja":["矛盾に気づかなかった場合に未認定価格を使える状態が残り、正式情報だけを保証できません。","抽象的な指示では、アクセス可能な誤情報を取得・利用する経路を強制的に閉じられません。","正の情報源と指定されたGoldを除外し、信頼性の低い調査データを採用するため、要求と逆です。","検索設定とデータ権限を両方Goldへ限定し、正式な情報源だけに検索空間を制約できます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-021","number":21,"form":1,"stem":"A customer-service agent is deployed with access to both the current session's in-context conversation history and a Lakebase-backed long-term memory store containing facts from the customer's past sessions. During an active conversation, the customer states \"please ship this to my new address\" and provides the new address a few messages earlier in the same session. When the agent later needs to reference the shipping address, it queries the Lakebase long-term store on every turn (as it does for all fact lookups) and returns the customer's old address on file from a prior session, ignoring the address just stated in the active conversation.\n\nWhat is the mismatch, and what is the correct memory strategy?","options":["The agent needs a larger context window; increasing it will let the current session's turns dominate over irrelevant long-term facts.","The agent is pulling from long-term/cross-session memory for information that already exists in the current session's active context; it should prioritize (or exclusively use) the current session's in-context conversation history for facts stated in this session, reserving the Lakebase-backed long-term store for information that must persist across separate sessions.","The agent is correctly using long-term memory; the fix is to ensure Lakebase syncs more frequently so cross-session facts stay fresh.","The agent should stop using Lakebase entirely and rely only on session-level context for all facts, including customer preferences that must be remembered across future sessions."],"correct":1,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"今回伝えた新住所を優先","concept":"ce-f1-q021","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. The agent is pulling from long-term/cross-session memory for information that already exists in the current session's active context; it should prioritize (or exclusively use) the current session's in-context conversation history for facts stated in this session, reserving the Lakebase-backed long-term store for information that must persist across separate sessions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure is a memory-scope and precedence mismatch.\n\nThe new address was provided earlier in the active conversation, so it belongs to the agent’s short-term session context. The old address came from a previous interaction and belongs to long-term cross-session memory.\n\nWhen the two sources conflict, the agent should prioritize the newer, session-specific fact because it reflects the customer’s current instruction. Querying long-term memory indiscriminately on every turn allows stale historical information to override the active conversation.\n\nDatabricks distinguishes the memory types as follows:\n\n- Short-term memory: Maintains context and earlier turns within one conversation session.\n\n- Long-term memory: Stores reusable information across multiple conversations, such as preferences and historical facts.\n\nTherefore, option B correctly assigns the address supplied in the current conversation to short-term context while retaining long-term memory for future-session continuity.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe active conversation already contains the information required to resolve “my new address.” The correct retrieval order should normally be:\n\n- Explicit facts and instructions from the current turn.\n\n- Relevant facts from earlier turns in the same session.\n\n- Long-term memory only when the required fact is absent from the active session.\n\n- An authoritative customer-profile system when the fact represents operational account data.\n\nDatabricks describes short-term memory as context maintained within a single conversation for follow-up questions. Long-term memory extracts and preserves information across separate conversations.\n\nThe agent should therefore use the address stated in the active session. After the customer confirms that it is a permanent address change, the application may update the appropriate customer system and, where suitable, replace or invalidate the older long-term memory.\n\nThis design prevents stale cross-session facts from overriding more recent customer instructions.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. The agent needs a larger context window; increasing it will let the current session's turns dominate over irrelevant long-term facts.\n\nThe current address is already available within the active context. The problem is not that the conversation has exceeded the model’s context window.\n\nA larger context window also does not guarantee that the model will prioritize the newer address. If the application keeps retrieving and injecting the old address, the model still receives conflicting facts.\n\nThe solution requires explicit memory selection and precedence rules, not merely more tokens.\n\nC. The agent is correctly using long-term memory; the fix is to ensure Lakebase syncs more frequently so cross-session facts stay fresh.\n\nThe agent is not using memory correctly for this lookup.\n\nThe new address was stated during the current session and may not yet have been promoted to persistent memory. Long-term-memory synchronization is therefore not the most direct solution.\n\nEven perfectly synchronized long-term memory requires a policy for deciding whether current-session instructions override previously stored facts. Without that precedence rule, similar conflicts can recur.\n\nD. The agent should stop using Lakebase entirely and rely only on session-level context for all facts, including customer preferences that must be remembered across future sessions.\n\nThis overcorrects the problem.\n\nSession context is appropriate for information needed during the active conversation, but it disappears or becomes unavailable when a separate session begins. Removing long-term memory would prevent the agent from remembering useful cross-session facts such as:\n\n- Communication preferences.\n\n- Previous support decisions.\n\n- Stable customer preferences.\n\n- Relevant facts explicitly approved for future reuse.\n\nThe correct design uses both memory types, with appropriate scope and precedence.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is treating long-term memory as the default source for every factual lookup merely because it is persistent.\n\nPersistence does not make long-term memory more authoritative than the active conversation. For facts updated during the current session, recent session context should take precedence over older cross-session memory.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe certification-exam answer is B.\n\nA production agent should implement deterministic conflict-resolution rules, such as:\n\n```\nCurrent explicit user instruction    > earlier current-session context    > authoritative system-of-record value    > long-term remembered fact\n```\n\nThe exact order between the system of record and session context depends on the operation. For example, a user-provided address may be used for the current shipment only until identity verification and persistence succeed.\n\nA robust implementation should also:\n\n- Timestamp long-term memories.\n\n- Track the source and confidence of each fact.\n\n- Treat “new,” “updated,” and “use this instead” as override signals.\n\n- Avoid retrieving long-term memory when the fact is already resolved in the session.\n\n- Confirm whether an address change is shipment-specific or permanent.\n\n- Update or invalidate stale memories after confirmed changes.\n\n- Keep operational customer data in an authoritative transactional system rather than treating agent memory as the sole system of record.\n\nDatabricks supports using short-term and long-term memory together: session history can be maintained through conversations, framework sessions, or checkpointing, while durable memory supports continuity across conversations.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Self-managed agent memory (Lakebase)\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory)\n\n- Agent state and memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)\n\n- Agent memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)\n\n- Managed agent memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/managed-memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/managed-memory)","source_option_explanations":["A. The agent needs a larger context window; increasing it will let the current session's turns dominate over irrelevant long-term facts.\n\nThe current address is already available within the active context. The problem is not that the conversation has exceeded the model’s context window.\n\nA larger context window also does not guarantee that the model will prioritize the newer address. If the application keeps retrieving and injecting the old address, the model still receives conflicting facts.\n\nThe solution requires explicit memory selection and precedence rules, not merely more tokens.","Correct Answer\n\nB. The agent is pulling from long-term/cross-session memory for information that already exists in the current session's active context; it should prioritize (or exclusively use) the current session's in-context conversation history for facts stated in this session, reserving the Lakebase-backed long-term store for information that must persist across separate sessions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure is a memory-scope and precedence mismatch.\n\nThe new address was provided earlier in the active conversation, so it belongs to the agent’s short-term session context. The old address came from a previous interaction and belongs to long-term cross-session memory.\n\nWhen the two sources conflict, the agent should prioritize the newer, session-specific fact because it reflects the customer’s current instruction. Querying long-term memory indiscriminately on every turn allows stale historical information to override the active conversation.\n\nDatabricks distinguishes the memory types as follows:\n\n- Short-term memory: Maintains context and earlier turns within one conversation session.\n\n- Long-term memory: Stores reusable information across multiple conversations, such as preferences and historical facts.\n\nTherefore, option B correctly assigns the address supplied in the current conversation to short-term context while retaining long-term memory for future-session continuity.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe active conversation already contains the information required to resolve “my new address.” The correct retrieval order should normally be:\n\n- Explicit facts and instructions from the current turn.\n\n- Relevant facts from earlier turns in the same session.\n\n- Long-term memory only when the required fact is absent from the active session.\n\n- An authoritative customer-profile system when the fact represents operational account data.\n\nDatabricks describes short-term memory as context maintained within a single conversation for follow-up questions. Long-term memory extracts and preserves information across separate conversations.\n\nThe agent should therefore use the address stated in the active session. After the customer confirms that it is a permanent address change, the application may update the appropriate customer system and, where suitable, replace or invalidate the older long-term memory.\n\nThis design prevents stale cross-session facts from overriding more recent customer instructions.","C. The agent is correctly using long-term memory; the fix is to ensure Lakebase syncs more frequently so cross-session facts stay fresh.\n\nThe agent is not using memory correctly for this lookup.\n\nThe new address was stated during the current session and may not yet have been promoted to persistent memory. Long-term-memory synchronization is therefore not the most direct solution.\n\nEven perfectly synchronized long-term memory requires a policy for deciding whether current-session instructions override previously stored facts. Without that precedence rule, similar conflicts can recur.","D. The agent should stop using Lakebase entirely and rely only on session-level context for all facts, including customer preferences that must be remembered across future sessions.\n\nThis overcorrects the problem.\n\nSession context is appropriate for information needed during the active conversation, but it disappears or becomes unavailable when a separate session begins. Removing long-term memory would prevent the agent from remembering useful cross-session facts such as:\n\n- Communication preferences.\n\n- Previous support decisions.\n\n- Stable customer preferences.\n\n- Relevant facts explicitly approved for future reuse.\n\nThe correct design uses both memory types, with appropriate scope and precedence."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/managed-memory"],"source_hash":"e9f73538d0d96962ceeebdf06ea46e426d0c4b22f92f638b017c16d3e5af7025","stem_ja":"顧客サービスエージェントは、現在のセッション内の会話履歴と、顧客の過去セッションの事実を保存するLakebaseの長期メモリの両方を利用できます。会話中、顧客は数メッセージ前に「こちらは新しい住所へ送ってください」と、新住所を伝えています。その後、配送先住所を使う際、エージェントはすべての事実検索で行うのと同じように毎ターンLakebaseの長期保存を問い合わせ、今の会話の住所を無視し、過去セッションから保存していた旧住所を返します。\n\nどのような不一致があり、正しいメモリ戦略は何ですか。","options_ja":["モデルにはもっと大きなコンテキストウィンドウが必要で、増やせば現在の会話が無関係な長期情報より優勢になる。","現在の会話にすでにある情報を長期・セッション間メモリから引いているのが不一致である。このセッションで伝えられた事実は現在の会話履歴を優先、またはそれだけを利用し、Lakebaseは別セッション間で永続化する必要のある情報に使う。","長期メモリの使い方は正しく、セッション間の事実が新しくなるよう、Lakebaseの同期頻度を増やすのが修正である。","将来のセッションでも覚える必要がある顧客の好みを含め、すべての事実をセッション内だけへ置き、Lakebaseの使用を完全にやめる。"],"explanation_ja":"今回の明示的な新住所は、以前の保存住所より優先されます。現在の会話に必要な情報があるのに、毎回古い長期記憶で上書きするのが誤りです。覚え方は「今の明示情報が優先、長期記憶は足りない情報を補う」。セッションをまたぐ永続化自体を廃止する必要はありません。","distractors_ja":["容量の不足ではなく、参照するメモリ源と優先順位の選択が誤っています。","現在の会話の明確な更新を優先し、長期保存とは用途を分けるため、不一致を直接解消します。","同期を速めても、今の住所を保存・適用せず古い記憶を優先する設計は変わりません。","長期保存は将来の会話で必要なため、誤った優先順位の対策として全面廃止するのは過剰です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-022","number":22,"form":1,"stem":"A multi-step compliance-review agent processes a large document collection over potentially hundreds of tool calls spanning several hours. The task's progress (documents reviewed, findings so far, open items) must survive process restarts or redeploys without losing work, and different worker processes may pick up different parts of the same job over time.\n\nWhich approach is required for tracking this task's progress?","options":["An in-context scratchpad is sufficient, since the agent's own model calls can always regenerate any lost state by re-reading the original documents from scratch.","Neither approach is needed; the task should rely entirely on the model's parametric knowledge to track progress without external state.","An in-context scratchpad is preferable here because it keeps state changes visible to the model on every turn without requiring any additional read/write calls to external storage.","A Delta-backed state object is required, since the task's progress must survive process restarts, be visible to multiple worker processes, and not depend on any single conversation's context window remaining intact."],"correct":3,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"再起動をまたぐ作業進捗の永続化","concept":"ce-f1-q022","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. A Delta-backed state object is required, since the task's progress must survive process restarts, be visible to multiple worker processes, and not depend on any single conversation's context window remaining intact.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe decisive requirements are:\n\n- The workflow may run for several hours.\n\n- It can involve hundreds of tool calls.\n\n- Progress must survive restarts and redeployments.\n\n- Different worker processes may continue the same job.\n\n- Completed work must not be lost or unnecessarily repeated.\n\nAn in-context scratchpad exists only within the model’s active conversation state. It is constrained by the context window and can be lost when the serving process restarts, the conversation expires, or another worker takes over.\n\nThe task therefore requires a durable, externally stored, shared state object. Among the available options, the Delta-backed state object in option D is the only approach that provides persistent and worker-independent progress tracking.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA persistent state object can store structured progress such as:\n\n- Unique job and document identifiers.\n\n- Documents not yet reviewed.\n\n- Documents currently being processed.\n\n- Completed documents.\n\n- Findings and supporting citations.\n\n- Open compliance issues.\n\n- Retry counts and failure details.\n\n- Checkpoint timestamps.\n\n- Overall workflow status.\n\nBecause this state is stored outside the model process, a replacement worker can reload it and resume from the last committed checkpoint.\n\nA Delta-backed implementation also provides durable storage and transactional behavior. Delta Lake supports ACID guarantees and concurrent access from multiple writers, although the implementation must still handle write conflicts and design updates carefully.\n\nThe essential exam concept is not merely that the model should “remember” previous turns. It is that long-running distributed work requires persisted execution state, independent of the model’s prompt and any individual worker.\n\nA current Databricks implementation may also use Lakebase-backed checkpointing for operational agent state. Databricks documents Lakebase as a durable agent-state backend and provides examples using LangGraph checkpointers and the OpenAI Agents SDK.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. An in-context scratchpad is sufficient, since the agent's own model calls can always regenerate any lost state by re-reading the original documents from scratch.\n\nThis is not sufficient for a long-running workflow.\n\nRe-reading hundreds or thousands of documents after every restart would:\n\n- Repeat expensive model and tool calls.\n\n- Increase latency and cost.\n\n- Risk producing different findings during reprocessing.\n\n- Lose information about completed versus pending work.\n\n- Make safe coordination between workers difficult.\n\nA scratchpad can support reasoning during the current execution, but it should not be the authoritative record of workflow progress.\n\nB. Neither approach is needed; the task should rely entirely on the model's parametric knowledge to track progress without external state.\n\nA model’s parametric knowledge contains information learned during training. It does not persist the runtime progress of a specific compliance-review job.\n\nThe model cannot inherently remember:\n\n- Which customer documents were reviewed.\n\n- Which findings were generated during earlier tool calls.\n\n- Which items failed.\n\n- Which worker completed each task.\n\n- Where execution should resume after a redeployment.\n\nJob-specific progress must be stored explicitly.\n\nC. An in-context scratchpad is preferable here because it keeps state changes visible to the model on every turn without requiring any additional read/write calls to external storage.\n\nAn in-context scratchpad is convenient for temporary reasoning, but it does not satisfy the durability or distributed-access requirements.\n\nIt is tied to:\n\n- A particular conversation or thread.\n\n- The available context-window capacity.\n\n- The process maintaining that context.\n\n- The worker currently executing the agent.\n\nAs the scratchpad grows, older details may be truncated, summarized, or compacted. Another process also cannot safely coordinate through a scratchpad that exists only inside the original agent invocation.\n\nThe system can place a compact summary of the persisted state into the prompt, but the authoritative state must remain in external durable storage.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is confusing reasoning memory with execution state.\n\nAn in-context scratchpad helps the model reason within an active interaction. It is not a durable checkpoint, distributed work queue, or transactional record of completed work.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe certification-exam answer is D because the workflow needs durable, shared state.\n\nA production state record might resemble:\n\n```\njob_id: compliance-review-4821document_id: contract-00982status: completedassigned_worker: worker-7findings_count: 4checkpoint_version: 12updated_at: 2026-08-02T13:10:00+05:30\n```\n\nImportant implementation practices include:\n\n- Assign stable IDs to jobs, documents, findings, and tool calls.\n\n- Checkpoint after meaningful units of work.\n\n- Make tool operations idempotent so retries do not create duplicate findings.\n\n- Use atomic updates or optimistic concurrency controls.\n\n- Distinguish `pending`, `in_progress`, `completed`, `failed`, and `needs_review`.\n\n- Record leases or ownership so two workers do not process the same item simultaneously.\n\n- Store detailed findings separately from the compact state supplied to the model.\n\n- Maintain an audit trail for compliance decisions.\n\nFor low-latency, frequently updated operational state, current Databricks guidance emphasizes Lakebase as a durable agent-state backend. Delta tables remain appropriate for durable lakehouse records, analytical history, and custom checkpoint designs. The exact storage technology can vary, but the non-negotiable requirement is external, persistent, shared state rather than prompt-only memory.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent state and memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)\n\n- Self-managed agent memory with Lakebase\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory)\n\n- What are ACID guarantees on Databricks?\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/acid](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/acid)\n\n- Isolation levels and write conflicts\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/isolation/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/isolation/)","source_option_explanations":["A. An in-context scratchpad is sufficient, since the agent's own model calls can always regenerate any lost state by re-reading the original documents from scratch.\n\nThis is not sufficient for a long-running workflow.\n\nRe-reading hundreds or thousands of documents after every restart would:\n\n- Repeat expensive model and tool calls.\n\n- Increase latency and cost.\n\n- Risk producing different findings during reprocessing.\n\n- Lose information about completed versus pending work.\n\n- Make safe coordination between workers difficult.\n\nA scratchpad can support reasoning during the current execution, but it should not be the authoritative record of workflow progress.","B. Neither approach is needed; the task should rely entirely on the model's parametric knowledge to track progress without external state.\n\nA model’s parametric knowledge contains information learned during training. It does not persist the runtime progress of a specific compliance-review job.\n\nThe model cannot inherently remember:\n\n- Which customer documents were reviewed.\n\n- Which findings were generated during earlier tool calls.\n\n- Which items failed.\n\n- Which worker completed each task.\n\n- Where execution should resume after a redeployment.\n\nJob-specific progress must be stored explicitly.","C. An in-context scratchpad is preferable here because it keeps state changes visible to the model on every turn without requiring any additional read/write calls to external storage.\n\nAn in-context scratchpad is convenient for temporary reasoning, but it does not satisfy the durability or distributed-access requirements.\n\nIt is tied to:\n\n- A particular conversation or thread.\n\n- The available context-window capacity.\n\n- The process maintaining that context.\n\n- The worker currently executing the agent.\n\nAs the scratchpad grows, older details may be truncated, summarized, or compacted. Another process also cannot safely coordinate through a scratchpad that exists only inside the original agent invocation.\n\nThe system can place a compact summary of the persisted state into the prompt, but the authoritative state must remain in external durable storage.","Correct Answer\n\nD. A Delta-backed state object is required, since the task's progress must survive process restarts, be visible to multiple worker processes, and not depend on any single conversation's context window remaining intact.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe decisive requirements are:\n\n- The workflow may run for several hours.\n\n- It can involve hundreds of tool calls.\n\n- Progress must survive restarts and redeployments.\n\n- Different worker processes may continue the same job.\n\n- Completed work must not be lost or unnecessarily repeated.\n\nAn in-context scratchpad exists only within the model’s active conversation state. It is constrained by the context window and can be lost when the serving process restarts, the conversation expires, or another worker takes over.\n\nThe task therefore requires a durable, externally stored, shared state object. Among the available options, the Delta-backed state object in option D is the only approach that provides persistent and worker-independent progress tracking.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA persistent state object can store structured progress such as:\n\n- Unique job and document identifiers.\n\n- Documents not yet reviewed.\n\n- Documents currently being processed.\n\n- Completed documents.\n\n- Findings and supporting citations.\n\n- Open compliance issues.\n\n- Retry counts and failure details.\n\n- Checkpoint timestamps.\n\n- Overall workflow status.\n\nBecause this state is stored outside the model process, a replacement worker can reload it and resume from the last committed checkpoint.\n\nA Delta-backed implementation also provides durable storage and transactional behavior. Delta Lake supports ACID guarantees and concurrent access from multiple writers, although the implementation must still handle write conflicts and design updates carefully.\n\nThe essential exam concept is not merely that the model should “remember” previous turns. It is that long-running distributed work requires persisted execution state, independent of the model’s prompt and any individual worker.\n\nA current Databricks implementation may also use Lakebase-backed checkpointing for operational agent state. Databricks documents Lakebase as a durable agent-state backend and provides examples using LangGraph checkpointers and the OpenAI Agents SDK."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/acid","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/optimizations/isolation/"],"source_hash":"0016fdfd0c1511fc1d1208d83405c98a024d6dba79d544156e23038b984cdc61","stem_ja":"複数段階のコンプライアンス審査エージェントが、数時間にわたって数百回のツール呼び出しを行い、大量の文書を処理します。確認済み文書、それまでの指摘、未処理項目などの進捗は、プロセス再起動や再デプロイでも作業を失わず保持する必要があります。時間がたつ中で、異なるワーカープロセスが同じジョブの別の部分を引き受けることもあります。\n\nこのタスクの進捗を追跡するために必要な方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["エージェントが元の文書を最初から読み直せば、失われた状態を常に再生成できるため、コンテキスト内の作業メモで十分である。","外部状態を使わず、モデルの学習済みパラメーターの知識だけで進捗を追えばよいので、どちらの方法も不要である。","外部ストレージへの追加の読み書きなしで、毎ターン変更をモデルへ見せられるため、コンテキスト内の作業メモが適する。","再起動に耐え、複数ワーカーから見え、単一会話のコンテキストウィンドウが維持されることに依存しない必要があるため、Deltaを基盤とする状態オブジェクトが必要である。"],"explanation_ja":"数時間の進捗を再起動後や別ワーカーへ引き継ぐには、会話の外に永続化された状態が必要です。選択肢ではDelta-backed stateがその条件に合います。覚え方は「再開したい作業は、処理の外へ保存」。作業メモは現在の推論に便利でも、共有可能な永続的進捗の代わりにはなりません。","distractors_ja":["最初から読み直すことは、作業を失わず進捗から再開するという要求を満たしません。","実行中の新しい進捗が、モデルの学習済みパラメーターへ自動的に記憶されるわけではありません。","入出力の呼び出しを減らせても、再起動後や別プロセスでの復旧は保証できません。","実行コンテキストの寿命と独立して、進捗を保存・共有・参照できる設計です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-023","number":23,"form":1,"stem":"An agent stores two kinds of memory in Lakebase: (1) structured records of exact facts like \"user's default shipping address\" and \"user's subscription tier,\" keyed by user ID, and (2) free-form notes summarizing past support conversations in natural language (e.g., \"customer was frustrated about a delayed refund in March, resolved via partial credit\"). At query time, the agent needs to answer (a) \"what is this user's subscription tier?\" and (b) \"has this user had similar complaints before, and how were they resolved?\"\n\nWhich retrieval mechanism is appropriate for each query type?","options":["Query (a) should use a structured SQL query against the Lakebase table by user ID, since the answer is a single known field; query (b) should use Databricks AI Search / semantic retrieval over the free-form conversation-note memories, since it requires matching a described situation to conceptually related past notes rather than an exact key match.","Both queries should use Databricks AI Search over the Lakebase-persisted memory, since semantic search is universally more accurate than direct queries.","Query (a) should use Databricks AI Search since subscription tier is unstructured text; query (b) should use a structured SQL query since past complaints are stored as one row per user.","Both queries should use structured SQL queries only, since Lakebase is a relational Postgres store and does not support any form of semantic retrieval."],"correct":0,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"正確な属性取得と似た相談の検索を分ける","concept":"ce-f1-q023","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Query (a) should use a structured SQL query against the Lakebase table by user ID, since the answer is a single known field; query (b) should use Databricks AI Search / semantic retrieval over the free-form conversation-note memories, since it requires matching a described situation to conceptually related past notes rather than an exact key match.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe two questions require different retrieval patterns:\n\n- “What is this user’s subscription tier?” is an exact lookup against a known user and known field.\n\n- “Has this user had similar complaints before, and how were they resolved?” is a semantic similarity question over natural-language notes.\n\nFor the subscription tier, the agent should issue a deterministic SQL query using the user ID, such as:\n\n```\nSELECT subscription_tierFROM customer_memoryWHERE user_id = :user_id;\n```\n\nFor prior complaints, the agent needs to find notes that are conceptually similar to the current issue even when they do not use identical words. Embedding-based semantic retrieval is therefore appropriate.\n\nDatabricks describes RAG retrieval sources as including SQL databases for structured lookups and vector stores or search systems for semantic document retrieval.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nQuery (a) has a precise access path:\n\n- Known entity: the current user.\n\n- Known key: `user_id`.\n\n- Known attribute: `subscription_tier`.\n\n- Expected result: one exact value.\n\nA structured query is more accurate, faster, and more deterministic than embedding search for this type of lookup. Semantic search could return related text mentioning subscription plans without guaranteeing that it returns the user’s current authoritative tier.\n\nQuery (b) is different. The user might ask about a delayed refund, missing reimbursement, payment delay, or unresolved credit. Past notes could describe similar situations using different wording. Semantic retrieval converts the query and stored notes into embeddings and retrieves records based on conceptual similarity rather than exact keyword or key matching. Databricks AI Search is designed for embedding-based semantic search and RAG workloads.\n\nThe free-form notes can be synchronized into a Databricks AI Search index. Alternatively, where available and suitable, Lakebase Search supports vector and hybrid search directly within Lakebase. The key exam concept remains unchanged: use exact structured retrieval for known fields and semantic retrieval for conceptually related unstructured memories.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Both queries should use Databricks AI Search over the Lakebase-persisted memory, since semantic search is universally more accurate than direct queries.\n\nThis is incorrect because semantic search is not universally more accurate.\n\nFor a known user ID and a known field such as `subscription_tier`, SQL provides an exact, deterministic result. Semantic retrieval introduces unnecessary ambiguity and could retrieve:\n\n- An old support note mentioning a previous tier.\n\n- General documentation about subscription tiers.\n\n- Another semantically similar record.\n\n- A note about a proposed rather than active tier.\n\nSemantic retrieval is appropriate for fuzzy meaning-based questions, not as a replacement for direct key-based access.\n\nC. Query (a) should use Databricks AI Search since subscription tier is unstructured text; query (b) should use a structured SQL query since past complaints are stored as one row per user.\n\nThis reverses the correct retrieval mechanisms.\n\nThe subscription tier is explicitly stored as a structured field keyed by user ID. It should therefore be queried directly.\n\nAlthough conversation notes may occupy relational rows, their content is still unstructured natural language. Storage in a relational table does not automatically make the meaning of the text suitable for exact SQL matching.\n\nA SQL filter can retrieve all notes for a user, but semantic search is better for identifying which notes describe complaints similar to the current issue.\n\nD. Both queries should use structured SQL queries only, since Lakebase is a relational Postgres store and does not support any form of semantic retrieval.\n\nThis is technically inaccurate.\n\nLakebase is PostgreSQL-compatible and supports structured SQL access, but Databricks also provides Lakebase Search capabilities for vector and keyword search. Free-form notes can also be synchronized into Databricks AI Search for semantic retrieval.\n\nSQL alone could retrieve every past note for a user, but it would not inherently rank those notes by conceptual similarity to the current complaint. Keyword conditions such as `LIKE '%refund%'` would also miss semantically related notes using different terminology.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is choosing a retrieval mechanism based only on where the data is stored.\n\nBoth structured facts and free-form notes can reside in Lakebase, but retrieval should be selected based on the question:\n\n- Known key and known field: structured SQL lookup.\n\n- Meaning-based similarity over natural language: semantic retrieval.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is A, but production agents commonly combine both mechanisms.\n\nA practical workflow could be:\n\n- Query the customer’s structured profile by user ID.\n\n- Apply a user-ID filter to restrict memory retrieval to that customer.\n\n- Run semantic search over only that customer’s conversation notes.\n\n- Return the most relevant historical complaints and their resolutions.\n\n- Preserve timestamps so recent memories can be prioritized.\n\n- Keep authoritative fields such as subscription tier separate from model-generated summaries.\n\nThe application should not treat semantic memory as the system of record for exact operational facts. Conversely, it should not expect exact SQL predicates alone to identify conceptually similar situations in large collections of natural-language notes.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) on Databricks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation)\n\n- Databricks AI Search\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search)\n\n- Lakebase Search\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/lakebase-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/lakebase-search)\n\n- Agent state and memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Query (a) should use a structured SQL query against the Lakebase table by user ID, since the answer is a single known field; query (b) should use Databricks AI Search / semantic retrieval over the free-form conversation-note memories, since it requires matching a described situation to conceptually related past notes rather than an exact key match.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe two questions require different retrieval patterns:\n\n- “What is this user’s subscription tier?” is an exact lookup against a known user and known field.\n\n- “Has this user had similar complaints before, and how were they resolved?” is a semantic similarity question over natural-language notes.\n\nFor the subscription tier, the agent should issue a deterministic SQL query using the user ID, such as:\n\n```\nSELECT subscription_tierFROM customer_memoryWHERE user_id = :user_id;\n```\n\nFor prior complaints, the agent needs to find notes that are conceptually similar to the current issue even when they do not use identical words. Embedding-based semantic retrieval is therefore appropriate.\n\nDatabricks describes RAG retrieval sources as including SQL databases for structured lookups and vector stores or search systems for semantic document retrieval.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nQuery (a) has a precise access path:\n\n- Known entity: the current user.\n\n- Known key: `user_id`.\n\n- Known attribute: `subscription_tier`.\n\n- Expected result: one exact value.\n\nA structured query is more accurate, faster, and more deterministic than embedding search for this type of lookup. Semantic search could return related text mentioning subscription plans without guaranteeing that it returns the user’s current authoritative tier.\n\nQuery (b) is different. The user might ask about a delayed refund, missing reimbursement, payment delay, or unresolved credit. Past notes could describe similar situations using different wording. Semantic retrieval converts the query and stored notes into embeddings and retrieves records based on conceptual similarity rather than exact keyword or key matching. Databricks AI Search is designed for embedding-based semantic search and RAG workloads.\n\nThe free-form notes can be synchronized into a Databricks AI Search index. Alternatively, where available and suitable, Lakebase Search supports vector and hybrid search directly within Lakebase. The key exam concept remains unchanged: use exact structured retrieval for known fields and semantic retrieval for conceptually related unstructured memories.","B. Both queries should use Databricks AI Search over the Lakebase-persisted memory, since semantic search is universally more accurate than direct queries.\n\nThis is incorrect because semantic search is not universally more accurate.\n\nFor a known user ID and a known field such as `subscription_tier`, SQL provides an exact, deterministic result. Semantic retrieval introduces unnecessary ambiguity and could retrieve:\n\n- An old support note mentioning a previous tier.\n\n- General documentation about subscription tiers.\n\n- Another semantically similar record.\n\n- A note about a proposed rather than active tier.\n\nSemantic retrieval is appropriate for fuzzy meaning-based questions, not as a replacement for direct key-based access.","C. Query (a) should use Databricks AI Search since subscription tier is unstructured text; query (b) should use a structured SQL query since past complaints are stored as one row per user.\n\nThis reverses the correct retrieval mechanisms.\n\nThe subscription tier is explicitly stored as a structured field keyed by user ID. It should therefore be queried directly.\n\nAlthough conversation notes may occupy relational rows, their content is still unstructured natural language. Storage in a relational table does not automatically make the meaning of the text suitable for exact SQL matching.\n\nA SQL filter can retrieve all notes for a user, but semantic search is better for identifying which notes describe complaints similar to the current issue.","D. Both queries should use structured SQL queries only, since Lakebase is a relational Postgres store and does not support any form of semantic retrieval.\n\nThis is technically inaccurate.\n\nLakebase is PostgreSQL-compatible and supports structured SQL access, but Databricks also provides Lakebase Search capabilities for vector and keyword search. Free-form notes can also be synchronized into Databricks AI Search for semantic retrieval.\n\nSQL alone could retrieve every past note for a user, but it would not inherently rank those notes by conceptual similarity to the current complaint. Keyword conditions such as `LIKE '%refund%'` would also miss semantically related notes using different terminology."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/lakebase-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management"],"source_hash":"bf10af125176ec2fb780b4cb8a9b5ab199d6cb67b105660bb9d6094ef9ed44be","stem_ja":"エージェントがLakebaseへ2種類の記憶を保存しています。(1)ユーザーIDをキーとする「既定の配送先住所」「契約プラン」などの正確な事実の構造化レコードと、(2)「顧客は3月の返金遅延に不満を示したが、一部返金で解決した」など、過去のサポート会話を自然言語で要約した自由記述メモです。質問時には、(a)「このユーザーの契約プランは何か」と、(b)「過去に似た苦情があったか、どう解決したか」へ答える必要があります。\n\nそれぞれに適した取得方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["(a)は既知の1フィールドが答えなので、ユーザーIDでLakebaseテーブルを構造化SQL検索する。(b)は正確なキーの一致ではなく、説明された状況と概念的に近い過去メモを探すため、自由記述の会話メモに対するDatabricks AI Search／意味検索を使う。","意味検索は直接照会より普遍的に正確なので、両方でLakebaseに永続化した記憶に対するDatabricks AI Searchを使う。","契約プランは非構造テキストなので(a)にDatabricks AI Searchを使い、過去の苦情はユーザーごとに1行保存されるので(b)に構造化SQLを使う。","LakebaseはリレーショナルなPostgresストアで、意味検索の仕組みを一切利用できないため、両方で構造化SQLだけを使う。"],"explanation_ja":"IDから決まった契約プランを取るなら、厳密なSQL条件検索が適します。過去の似た苦情では、言葉が違っても意味が近い記録を探す必要があり、意味検索を使います。覚え方は「既知のキーで値を取るならSQL、似た話を探すなら意味検索」。保存先と検索方式を分けて考えます。","distractors_ja":["正確なフィールド照会と、自然言語の意味的な類似探索を適切に使い分けています。","意味検索は関連性を探す方法で、正確なIDに基づく1フィールド取得より常に正確とは限りません。","契約プランは構造化フィールドで、苦情は概念的な類似を探すため、方法が逆です。","Postgresへの保存と意味検索用の索引・機構の利用は両立し、SQLだけに限定する必要はありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-024","number":24,"form":1,"stem":"A context engineer runs four MLflow 3 experiment configurations for a customer-support agent, varying how many prior session summaries are retrieved from Lakebase into context: 0, 3, 10, and all available summaries. Each run logs task_success_rate, response_latency, and hallucination_rate (a measure of context pollution from irrelevant retrieved history). The team wants the configuration that performs reliably across all three metrics, rather than one that scores best on a single metric while lagging badly on another.\n\nThe results show: \"0 summaries\" has the lowest latency and hallucination_rate but the worst task_success_rate. \"All summaries\" has the highest task_success_rate but by far the worst hallucination_rate and latency. \"3 summaries\" and \"10 summaries\" both have task_success_rate close to \"all,\" with hallucination_rate and latency both close to \"0 summaries\" — and \"3 summaries\" is slightly better than \"10\" on hallucination_rate, roughly tied on the others.\n\nWhich approach and configuration should the engineer select as most reliable?","options":["Select the \"all summaries\" configuration, since it achieves the single highest task_success_rate observed across all runs.","Select the \"0 summaries\" configuration, since it minimizes latency and hallucination_rate, and success rate can be addressed later through a separate fix.","Compare the spread across the three metrics for each configuration rather than any single metric in isolation, and select \"3 summaries,\" since it scores near-best on task_success_rate while avoiding the pollution and latency costs of \"10\" and \"all.\"","Average the three raw metrics directly for each configuration and select whichever has the highest arithmetic mean, since combining metrics into one score simplifies the comparison."],"correct":2,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"MLflow実験で成功率・遅延・汚染を併せて比較する","concept":"ce-f1-q024","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Compare the spread across the three metrics for each configuration rather than any single metric in isolation, and select \"3 summaries,\" since it scores near-best on task_success_rate while avoiding the pollution and latency costs of \"10\" and \"all.\"\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe team explicitly wants the configuration that performs reliably across all three metrics, rather than optimizing one metric at the expense of the others.\n\nThe results show two undesirable extremes:\n\n- 0 summaries: Excellent latency and hallucination rate, but unacceptable task success.\n\n- All summaries: Highest task success, but excessive latency and context pollution.\n\nBoth 3 summaries and 10 summaries preserve nearly all the task-success benefit of retrieving long-term memory while remaining close to the no-memory configuration on latency and hallucination rate. However, 3 summaries has a slightly lower hallucination rate and is approximately tied with 10 summaries on the other metrics.\n\nTherefore, 3 summaries provides the strongest balanced operating point.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe engineer should compare each configuration as a multi-metric performance profile:\n\n- `task_success_rate`: Higher is better.\n\n- `response_latency`: Lower is better.\n\n- `hallucination_rate`: Lower is better.\n\nThe 3-summary configuration is near-best on task success while keeping latency and hallucination close to their best observed values. It therefore avoids the major weaknesses of both extreme configurations.\n\nIt also effectively dominates the 10-summary configuration based on the supplied results:\n\n- Approximately equal task success.\n\n- Approximately equal latency.\n\n- Slightly lower hallucination rate.\n\nRetrieving additional summaries beyond three therefore provides no meaningful improvement while introducing more irrelevant historical context.\n\nMLflow evaluation runs can store aggregate metrics and per-example traces, allowing teams to compare application configurations and detect quality or performance regressions across versions. The correct decision is based on the combined quality and performance evidence rather than the highest value of one metric.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Select the \"all summaries\" configuration, since it achieves the single highest task_success_rate observed across all runs.\n\nThis optimizes only one metric.\n\nAlthough all summaries produces the highest task-success rate, the question states that it has by far the worst:\n\n- Hallucination rate.\n\n- Response latency.\n\n- Context pollution.\n\nThe small success-rate improvement does not justify the substantial degradation in the other required dimensions. More retrieved memory is not always better; excessive historical context can distract the agent with irrelevant or conflicting information.\n\nB. Select the \"0 summaries\" configuration, since it minimizes latency and hallucination_rate, and success rate can be addressed later through a separate fix.\n\nThis also optimizes only part of the objective.\n\nThe zero-summary configuration has the worst task-success rate, meaning it frequently lacks historical context required to complete customer-support tasks. Low latency is not valuable when the agent regularly fails to answer correctly.\n\nThe scenario asks for the most reliable configuration now, not one that knowingly sacrifices success in anticipation of an unspecified future improvement.\n\nD. Average the three raw metrics directly for each configuration and select whichever has the highest arithmetic mean, since combining metrics into one score simplifies the comparison.\n\nA direct arithmetic mean is invalid because the metrics:\n\n- Have different units and scales.\n\n- Have different optimization directions.\n\n- May have different business importance.\n\nFor example, task success may be expressed as a proportion, latency in milliseconds, and hallucination rate as another proportion. Averaging these raw values would allow the metric with the largest numerical scale to dominate the result.\n\nA composite score would require normalization, direction alignment, and defensible weights or acceptance thresholds. None of those are specified in the question.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe primary trap is selecting the configuration with the highest value for one attractive metric—usually task success—while ignoring severe regressions in latency and hallucination.\n\nAnother trap is assuming that retrieving more long-term memory always improves the agent. Beyond a useful retrieval depth, additional memories can increase context pollution without materially improving task completion.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe certification-exam answer is 3 summaries because it offers the best observed balance and is slightly better than 10 summaries on hallucination while being approximately tied elsewhere.\n\nIn production, the team should also:\n\n- Establish minimum task-success requirements.\n\n- Define maximum acceptable latency and hallucination thresholds.\n\n- Run each configuration repeatedly to measure variance and confidence intervals.\n\n- Evaluate performance across different customer-query categories.\n\n- Retrieve summaries by relevance and recency rather than taking the latest arbitrary number.\n\n- Apply metadata filters to restrict memories to the current customer and relevant issue type.\n\n- Monitor whether the optimal retrieval count changes as the memory store grows.\n\nThe team could create a normalized, weighted composite score, but only after explicitly defining metric direction, normalization method, business weights, and hard safety constraints.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Evaluate and monitor agents\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor)\n\n- Tutorial: Evaluate and improve a GenAI application\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app)\n\n- Evaluation runs in MLflow\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/evaluation-runs](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/evaluation-runs)\n\n- Compare application versions with traces\n[https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/genai/version-tracking/compare-app-versions/](https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/genai/version-tracking/compare-app-versions/)","source_option_explanations":["A. Select the \"all summaries\" configuration, since it achieves the single highest task_success_rate observed across all runs.\n\nThis optimizes only one metric.\n\nAlthough all summaries produces the highest task-success rate, the question states that it has by far the worst:\n\n- Hallucination rate.\n\n- Response latency.\n\n- Context pollution.\n\nThe small success-rate improvement does not justify the substantial degradation in the other required dimensions. More retrieved memory is not always better; excessive historical context can distract the agent with irrelevant or conflicting information.","B. Select the \"0 summaries\" configuration, since it minimizes latency and hallucination_rate, and success rate can be addressed later through a separate fix.\n\nThis also optimizes only part of the objective.\n\nThe zero-summary configuration has the worst task-success rate, meaning it frequently lacks historical context required to complete customer-support tasks. Low latency is not valuable when the agent regularly fails to answer correctly.\n\nThe scenario asks for the most reliable configuration now, not one that knowingly sacrifices success in anticipation of an unspecified future improvement.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Compare the spread across the three metrics for each configuration rather than any single metric in isolation, and select \"3 summaries,\" since it scores near-best on task_success_rate while avoiding the pollution and latency costs of \"10\" and \"all.\"\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe team explicitly wants the configuration that performs reliably across all three metrics, rather than optimizing one metric at the expense of the others.\n\nThe results show two undesirable extremes:\n\n- 0 summaries: Excellent latency and hallucination rate, but unacceptable task success.\n\n- All summaries: Highest task success, but excessive latency and context pollution.\n\nBoth 3 summaries and 10 summaries preserve nearly all the task-success benefit of retrieving long-term memory while remaining close to the no-memory configuration on latency and hallucination rate. However, 3 summaries has a slightly lower hallucination rate and is approximately tied with 10 summaries on the other metrics.\n\nTherefore, 3 summaries provides the strongest balanced operating point.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe engineer should compare each configuration as a multi-metric performance profile:\n\n- `task_success_rate`: Higher is better.\n\n- `response_latency`: Lower is better.\n\n- `hallucination_rate`: Lower is better.\n\nThe 3-summary configuration is near-best on task success while keeping latency and hallucination close to their best observed values. It therefore avoids the major weaknesses of both extreme configurations.\n\nIt also effectively dominates the 10-summary configuration based on the supplied results:\n\n- Approximately equal task success.\n\n- Approximately equal latency.\n\n- Slightly lower hallucination rate.\n\nRetrieving additional summaries beyond three therefore provides no meaningful improvement while introducing more irrelevant historical context.\n\nMLflow evaluation runs can store aggregate metrics and per-example traces, allowing teams to compare application configurations and detect quality or performance regressions across versions. The correct decision is based on the combined quality and performance evidence rather than the highest value of one metric.","D. Average the three raw metrics directly for each configuration and select whichever has the highest arithmetic mean, since combining metrics into one score simplifies the comparison.\n\nA direct arithmetic mean is invalid because the metrics:\n\n- Have different units and scales.\n\n- Have different optimization directions.\n\n- May have different business importance.\n\nFor example, task success may be expressed as a proportion, latency in milliseconds, and hallucination rate as another proportion. Averaging these raw values would allow the metric with the largest numerical scale to dominate the result.\n\nA composite score would require normalization, direction alignment, and defensible weights or acceptance thresholds. None of those are specified in the question."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/evaluation-runs","https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/genai/version-tracking/compare-app-versions/"],"source_hash":"dce7c4af1190127bb71c9da267386a6232f3199cf6049f78e6be2a81b7e70832","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、カスタマーサポートエージェントについてMLflow 3で4つの実験設定を実行します。Lakebaseからコンテキストへ取得する過去セッションの要約数を、0件、3件、10件、利用可能な全件に変えます。各実行ではtask_success_rate、response_latency、hallucination_rate（無関係な取得履歴によるコンテキスト汚染の指標）を記録します。チームは、1指標では最良でも他で大きく劣る設定ではなく、3指標すべてで信頼できる設定を求めています。\n\n結果は次のとおりです。「0件」は遅延とhallucination_rateが最小ですが、task_success_rateは最悪です。「全件」はtask_success_rateが最高ですが、hallucination_rateと遅延は突出して最悪です。「3件」と「10件」はどちらもtask_success_rateが「全件」に近く、hallucination_rateと遅延は「0件」に近い値です。また「3件」はhallucination_rateが「10件」よりわずかに良く、他の指標はほぼ同等です。\n\n最も信頼できるものとして、どの比較方法と設定を選ぶべきですか。","options_ja":["全実行で単独のtask_success_rateが最も高いため、「全件」の設定を選ぶ。","遅延とhallucination_rateを最小化でき、成功率は後で別の修正によって対処できるため、「0件」の設定を選ぶ。","個々の指標を単独で見るのではなく、各設定の3指標全体にわたる性能を比較し、「3件」を選ぶ。task_success_rateが最良に近く、「10件」や「全件」の汚染と遅延のコストを避けられるため。","比較を簡単にするため、各設定の3つの未加工指標を直接平均し、算術平均が最も高いものを選ぶ。"],"explanation_ja":"成功率は高いほど、遅延と誤生成率は低いほど望ましいため、1つの最高値だけでは判断できません。3件は成功率が全件に近く、残り2指標も良好で、10件に比べても劣らず誤生成率が少し良い設定です。単位と望ましい方向が違う未加工指標を平均するのは不適切です。「必要な記憶は十分に、無関係な履歴は増やさない」が要点です。","distractors_ja":["成功率だけを最大化すると、大きな遅延と誤生成率を受け入れてしまいます。3指標すべてで信頼できるという選定条件に反します。","速度と誤生成率は良くても、成功率が最悪という弱点を残します。別の修正が成功するという未提示の前提には頼れません。","成功率をほぼ維持しながら汚染と遅延を抑え、10件よりも良いか同等です。提示された比較ではバランスの良い選択です。","成功率・誤生成率・時間は単位も改善方向も異なります。未加工の値の平均では、意味のある共通スコアにはなりません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-025","number":25,"form":1,"stem":"A team is choosing between two designs for loading a user's profile (roughly 40 fields of preferences and settings) from Lakebase: static retrieval, which loads the full profile once at the start of a session and holds it in context for the whole session, versus dynamic retrieval, which queries Lakebase on demand each time a specific field is needed. Sessions can run for hours, some profile fields can be updated by other systems mid-session (e.g., the user changes an email preference in a separate mobile app), and only a small fraction of the 40 fields are ever referenced in a typical session.\n\nWhich tradeoff most directly favors dynamic retrieval over static retrieval for this architecture?","options":["Static retrieval guarantees the agent always reflects the most current state of the user's profile, since it is loaded fresh at the start of every session.","Dynamic retrieval avoids loading all ~40 profile fields into context up front when only a few are typically needed, and it naturally reflects mid-session updates made by other systems, at the cost of an extra Lakebase query each time a new field is needed.","Static retrieval is strictly better in every respect, since it eliminates all runtime latency from querying Lakebase during the session.","Dynamic retrieval should be avoided entirely for long sessions, since repeated queries to Lakebase during a multi-hour session are not supported by its low-latency OLTP design."],"correct":1,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"プロフィール取得の鮮度・コンテキスト・クエリ負荷","concept":"ce-f1-q025","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Dynamic retrieval avoids loading all ~40 profile fields into context up front when only a few are typically needed, and it naturally reflects mid-session updates made by other systems, at the cost of an extra Lakebase query each time a new field is needed.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nTwo scenario details favor dynamic retrieval:\n\n- Only a small subset of the approximately 40 profile fields is usually needed.\n\n- Profile values can change while a multi-hour session is still active.\n\nStatic retrieval would place the entire profile in the model context, even though most fields may never be used. It would also create a session-start snapshot that could become stale after another system updates the profile.\n\nDynamic retrieval allows the agent to request only the required field at the time it is needed. Because Lakebase is queried during the active session, the agent can use the latest committed profile value rather than relying on an older copy retained in its prompt.\n\nThe trade-off is additional database-query latency and operational overhead for each previously unloaded field.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nDynamic retrieval is appropriate when context is:\n\n- Relatively large.\n\n- Sparsely accessed.\n\n- Mutable during the session.\n\n- Available through a low-latency operational store.\n\nFor example, the agent might retrieve only:\n\n```\nSELECT email_preferenceFROM user_profilesWHERE user_id = :user_id;\n```\n\nThis is preferable to loading shipping preferences, localization settings, notification channels, subscription details, accessibility settings, and dozens of other unused fields into every model call.\n\nLakebase is a managed PostgreSQL operational database intended for real-time transactional applications and low-latency access. It can therefore serve structured profile fields on demand during agent execution.\n\nDynamic retrieval also improves freshness. Suppose the profile is loaded at 9:00 a.m., but the user changes the email preference in a mobile application at 10:30 a.m. A static copy remains stale for the rest of the session, whereas an on-demand query after the update can retrieve the latest committed value.\n\nThe main cost is that each newly required field can introduce:\n\n- A database round trip.\n\n- Some additional response latency.\n\n- More connection and query-management overhead.\n\n- Potential availability or timeout dependencies.\n\nLakebase is designed for OLTP workloads and agent state or memory use cases, making repeated operational reads supported rather than inherently unsuitable.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Static retrieval guarantees the agent always reflects the most current state of the user's profile, since it is loaded fresh at the start of every session.\n\nStatic retrieval is current only at the moment the profile is loaded.\n\nFor a session lasting several hours, the retained profile becomes a snapshot. Any subsequent update made through another application will not automatically replace the value already held in the model’s context.\n\nTherefore, static retrieval cannot guarantee mid-session freshness.\n\nC. Static retrieval is strictly better in every respect, since it eliminates all runtime latency from querying Lakebase during the session.\n\nStatic retrieval can reduce repeated database lookups, but it introduces other costs:\n\n- More prompt tokens on every relevant model call.\n\n- Context pollution from unused fields.\n\n- Stale values during long sessions.\n\n- Greater risk that the model uses an irrelevant profile attribute.\n\n- Possible displacement of more useful conversation or retrieved context.\n\nIt is therefore not strictly better. It represents a trade-off between upfront loading and runtime retrieval.\n\nD. Dynamic retrieval should be avoided entirely for long sessions, since repeated queries to Lakebase during a multi-hour session are not supported by its low-latency OLTP design.\n\nThis is technically incorrect.\n\nLakebase is designed as a PostgreSQL-compatible OLTP database for operational applications and agents. Repeated low-latency reads are a normal use case.\n\nThe application should still use production database practices such as connection pooling, indexed user identifiers, timeouts, retries, and query observability, but the length of the agent session does not make dynamic retrieval unsupported.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is focusing only on database-query latency and overlooking two major costs of static context:\n\n- Loading many fields that are never needed.\n\n- Retaining stale values throughout a long-running session.\n\nThe question asks which trade-off favors dynamic retrieval, not whether dynamic retrieval has zero cost.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe expected exam answer is dynamic retrieval because the profile is sparsely accessed and can change during the session.\n\nA production design can use a hybrid strategy:\n\n- Dynamically retrieve mutable or rarely used fields.\n\n- Cache frequently used, stable fields for a limited period.\n\n- Invalidate cached values when profile-update events are received.\n\n- Retrieve multiple related fields in one query when the agent is likely to need them together.\n\n- Include version numbers or `updated_at` timestamps.\n\n- Avoid treating prompt-held data as authoritative after it may have changed.\n\nFor highly sensitive attributes, dynamic retrieval also allows authorization to be evaluated at the time of use rather than copying the complete profile into model context unnecessarily.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Lakebase Postgres\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/)\n\n- Agent state and memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)\n\n- Self-managed agent memory with Lakebase\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory)\n\n- AI and ML integration with Lakebase\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/ai-ml-integrations](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/ai-ml-integrations)","source_option_explanations":["A. Static retrieval guarantees the agent always reflects the most current state of the user's profile, since it is loaded fresh at the start of every session.\n\nStatic retrieval is current only at the moment the profile is loaded.\n\nFor a session lasting several hours, the retained profile becomes a snapshot. Any subsequent update made through another application will not automatically replace the value already held in the model’s context.\n\nTherefore, static retrieval cannot guarantee mid-session freshness.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Dynamic retrieval avoids loading all ~40 profile fields into context up front when only a few are typically needed, and it naturally reflects mid-session updates made by other systems, at the cost of an extra Lakebase query each time a new field is needed.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nTwo scenario details favor dynamic retrieval:\n\n- Only a small subset of the approximately 40 profile fields is usually needed.\n\n- Profile values can change while a multi-hour session is still active.\n\nStatic retrieval would place the entire profile in the model context, even though most fields may never be used. It would also create a session-start snapshot that could become stale after another system updates the profile.\n\nDynamic retrieval allows the agent to request only the required field at the time it is needed. Because Lakebase is queried during the active session, the agent can use the latest committed profile value rather than relying on an older copy retained in its prompt.\n\nThe trade-off is additional database-query latency and operational overhead for each previously unloaded field.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nDynamic retrieval is appropriate when context is:\n\n- Relatively large.\n\n- Sparsely accessed.\n\n- Mutable during the session.\n\n- Available through a low-latency operational store.\n\nFor example, the agent might retrieve only:\n\n```\nSELECT email_preferenceFROM user_profilesWHERE user_id = :user_id;\n```\n\nThis is preferable to loading shipping preferences, localization settings, notification channels, subscription details, accessibility settings, and dozens of other unused fields into every model call.\n\nLakebase is a managed PostgreSQL operational database intended for real-time transactional applications and low-latency access. It can therefore serve structured profile fields on demand during agent execution.\n\nDynamic retrieval also improves freshness. Suppose the profile is loaded at 9:00 a.m., but the user changes the email preference in a mobile application at 10:30 a.m. A static copy remains stale for the rest of the session, whereas an on-demand query after the update can retrieve the latest committed value.\n\nThe main cost is that each newly required field can introduce:\n\n- A database round trip.\n\n- Some additional response latency.\n\n- More connection and query-management overhead.\n\n- Potential availability or timeout dependencies.\n\nLakebase is designed for OLTP workloads and agent state or memory use cases, making repeated operational reads supported rather than inherently unsuitable.","C. Static retrieval is strictly better in every respect, since it eliminates all runtime latency from querying Lakebase during the session.\n\nStatic retrieval can reduce repeated database lookups, but it introduces other costs:\n\n- More prompt tokens on every relevant model call.\n\n- Context pollution from unused fields.\n\n- Stale values during long sessions.\n\n- Greater risk that the model uses an irrelevant profile attribute.\n\n- Possible displacement of more useful conversation or retrieved context.\n\nIt is therefore not strictly better. It represents a trade-off between upfront loading and runtime retrieval.","D. Dynamic retrieval should be avoided entirely for long sessions, since repeated queries to Lakebase during a multi-hour session are not supported by its low-latency OLTP design.\n\nThis is technically incorrect.\n\nLakebase is designed as a PostgreSQL-compatible OLTP database for operational applications and agents. Repeated low-latency reads are a normal use case.\n\nThe application should still use production database practices such as connection pooling, indexed user identifiers, timeouts, retries, and query observability, but the length of the agent session does not make dynamic retrieval unsupported."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/ai-ml-integrations"],"source_hash":"28fc3ebd4d970cc05b29a44aa339aeb8546353a7c29ab396fb9c4785864cb34d","stem_ja":"チームが、ユーザーのプロフィール（好みや設定の約40フィールド）をLakebaseから読み込む2つの設計を比較しています。静的取得ではセッション開始時に全プロフィールを一度読み込み、セッション中ずっとコンテキストへ保持します。動的取得では、特定フィールドが必要になるたびにLakebaseへ問い合わせます。セッションは数時間続くことがあり、一部のフィールドはセッション中に他システムによって更新されます。例えば、ユーザーが別のモバイルアプリでメール設定を変更します。通常のセッションで参照するのは、40フィールドのうちごく一部です。\n\nこの構成で、静的取得より動的取得を支持する最も直接的なトレードオフはどれですか。","options_ja":["セッション開始時に新しく読み込むので、静的取得ならユーザープロフィールの最新状態を常に反映することが保証される。","動的取得は、通常必要な少数フィールドのために約40フィールドすべてを最初から載せるのを避け、他システムによるセッション途中の更新も自然に反映できる。ただし新しいフィールドが必要になるたびにLakebaseへの問い合わせが増える。","セッション中のLakebaseへの問い合わせによる実行時遅延をすべて排除できるため、静的取得はあらゆる点で厳密に優れている。","低遅延OLTP設計のLakebaseは数時間のセッション中の反復クエリをサポートしないため、長いセッションでは動的取得を全面的に避けるべきである。"],"explanation_ja":"必要なフィールドが少なく、セッション途中にも更新されるなら、必要時取得がコンテキスト量と鮮度の面で有利です。全件を最初に読む方式は呼び出しを減らせますが、使わない情報も常駐し、外部更新を見落とします。一方、動的取得には都度のDB問い合わせという遅延・負荷があります。「静的は呼び出し節約、動的は必要量と鮮度」を比較します。","distractors_ja":["開始時に最新でも、その後の他システムからの変更は反映されません。数時間続くセッションで常時最新という保証はありません。","使う項目だけを取得でき、取得時点の値を読めます。その代わり必要に応じたクエリ数と遅延が増える、という正しい比較です。","実行時クエリを省ける利点はありますが、不要な40項目の常駐と古い値の使用という欠点を無視しています。普遍的な優位ではありません。","LakebaseのOLTP用途は低遅延の繰り返し読み書きを想定します。長いセッションだから反復クエリできないという制限はありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-026","number":26,"form":1,"stem":"Two variants of a support agent are compared. Variant 1 retrieves the customer's entire multi-year support history — hundreds of past tickets — into context for every new question, and its responses begin drifting off-topic, referencing resolved and irrelevant issues from years ago as though they still apply. Variant 2 retrieves only the single most recent prior ticket regardless of relevance, and fails to recall a clearly relevant issue from six months ago that directly relates to the current question, simply because that ticket isn't the most recent one.\n\nWhich diagnosis and fix best applies?","options":["Variant 1's behavior is actually correct and expected, since more historical context should always improve an agent's ability to answer follow-up questions.","Both variants suffer from the same underlying problem — an under-provisioned context window — so the fix for both is simply increasing the model's context window.","Variant 1 suffers from under-retrieval and Variant 2 suffers from over-retrieval; the fix for Variant 1 is to retrieve even more history, and the fix for Variant 2 is to strictly cap retrieval to only the single most recent ticket.","Variant 1 suffers from over-retrieval / context pollution (too much irrelevant history diluting relevance), and Variant 2 suffers from under-retrieval (missing relevant history that isn't merely the most recent); the fix is retrieval scoped by relevance to the current question, rather than either \"everything\" or \"only the most recent.\""],"correct":3,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"過剰取得と取得不足は関連性で解決する","concept":"ce-f1-q026","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Variant 1 suffers from over-retrieval / context pollution (too much irrelevant history diluting relevance), and Variant 2 suffers from under-retrieval (missing relevant history that isn't merely the most recent); the fix is retrieval scoped by relevance to the current question, rather than either \"everything\" or \"only the most recent.\"\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe two variants fail at opposite ends of the retrieval spectrum:\n\n- Variant 1 retrieves too much: Hundreds of old tickets introduce irrelevant and outdated issues into the prompt. This reduces retrieval precision and causes the agent to treat resolved historical problems as though they are still applicable.\n\n- Variant 2 retrieves too little: Selecting only the most recent ticket uses recency as a substitute for relevance. It misses an older ticket that directly matches the current issue.\n\nThe correct design is not to retrieve everything or mechanically retrieve the newest item. The agent should search the customer’s history using the current question and return a limited set of the most relevant prior tickets.\n\nDatabricks distinguishes recall—the ability to retrieve all relevant information—from precision—the proportion of retrieved results that are actually relevant. A reliable support agent requires an appropriate balance between both.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nVariant 1 has poor precision. Retrieving the complete ticket history increases the amount of irrelevant context supplied to the model. The model must then distinguish current, unresolved, and applicable issues from unrelated or obsolete cases. When irrelevant documents dominate the prompt, responses can drift away from the user’s present question.\n\nVariant 2 has poor recall. A six-month-old ticket can be more relevant than yesterday’s ticket. Recency can be used as a ranking signal, but it should not be the only selection rule.\n\nThe appropriate retrieval process is:\n\n- Restrict the search to the current customer.\n\n- Use the current question as the retrieval query.\n\n- Rank historical tickets by semantic or hybrid relevance.\n\n- Optionally incorporate recency, issue category, product, resolution status, and account metadata.\n\n- Return only the highest-value results that fit within the context budget.\n\nDatabricks recommends improving retrieval through filtering, relevance-based ranking, and reranking. Metadata filtering eliminates irrelevant candidates, while reranking prioritizes the documents most semantically relevant to the query.\n\nMLflow’s `RetrievalRelevance` judge can then evaluate whether each retrieved ticket is relevant to the current request, helping detect the context pollution demonstrated by Variant 1.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Variant 1's behavior is actually correct and expected, since more historical context should always improve an agent's ability to answer follow-up questions.\n\nThis is incorrect because additional context is useful only when it is relevant.\n\nLoading every historical ticket can introduce obsolete addresses, resolved complaints, superseded policies, and unrelated product issues. More context can therefore reduce answer quality rather than improve it.\n\nB. Both variants suffer from the same underlying problem — an under-provisioned context window — so the fix for both is simply increasing the model's context window.\n\nThe context window is not the root problem.\n\nVariant 1 already retrieves too much irrelevant information. A larger window would merely allow even more irrelevant tickets to be included. Variant 2 fails because its retrieval rule selects only the latest ticket, not because the model lacks space for additional results.\n\nRetrieval quality must be corrected before increasing context capacity.\n\nC. Variant 1 suffers from under-retrieval and Variant 2 suffers from over-retrieval; the fix for Variant 1 is to retrieve even more history, and the fix for Variant 2 is to strictly cap retrieval to only the single most recent ticket.\n\nThis reverses both diagnoses.\n\nVariant 1 is already retrieving the entire history and therefore cannot be classified as under-retrieval. Variant 2 retrieves only one ticket and misses relevant historical information, making it an under-retrieval problem.\n\nThe proposed fixes would worsen both failures.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming that retrieval should be based only on quantity or recency.\n\n- “Retrieve everything” maximizes raw coverage but causes context pollution.\n\n- “Retrieve only the newest item” minimizes context but can miss the most relevant history.\n\nThe correct principle is query-relevant retrieval with an appropriate balance of recall and precision.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is D.\n\nA production support-memory query could combine several signals:\n\n```\ncustomer_id = current customersemantic relevance = similarity to current issueproduct/category = matching product or support domainstatus = previously resolved or currently openrecency = secondary ranking boosttop_k = small evaluated result set\n```\n\nA practical implementation might first filter tickets by customer and product category, retrieve a broader candidate set, rerank the candidates against the current question, and provide only the top few tickets to the model.\n\nThe team should evaluate:\n\n- Recall@k: Whether relevant historical tickets are found.\n\n- Precision@k: Whether retrieved tickets are mostly relevant.\n\n- Retrieval relevance: Whether each supplied ticket helps answer the current question.\n\n- Answer relevance: Whether the final response remains focused on the user’s request.\n\nThe exact number of tickets should be established through evaluation rather than fixed arbitrarily at “one” or “all.”\n\nOfficial References\n\n- AI Search retrieval quality guide\n[https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality](https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality)\n\n- Evaluate AI Search retrieval quality\n[https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality-eval](https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality-eval)\n\n- Answer and context relevance judges\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/is_context_relevant](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/is_context_relevant)\n\n- Query an AI Search index\n[https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/query-ai-search](https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/query-ai-search)","source_option_explanations":["A. Variant 1's behavior is actually correct and expected, since more historical context should always improve an agent's ability to answer follow-up questions.\n\nThis is incorrect because additional context is useful only when it is relevant.\n\nLoading every historical ticket can introduce obsolete addresses, resolved complaints, superseded policies, and unrelated product issues. More context can therefore reduce answer quality rather than improve it.","B. Both variants suffer from the same underlying problem — an under-provisioned context window — so the fix for both is simply increasing the model's context window.\n\nThe context window is not the root problem.\n\nVariant 1 already retrieves too much irrelevant information. A larger window would merely allow even more irrelevant tickets to be included. Variant 2 fails because its retrieval rule selects only the latest ticket, not because the model lacks space for additional results.\n\nRetrieval quality must be corrected before increasing context capacity.","C. Variant 1 suffers from under-retrieval and Variant 2 suffers from over-retrieval; the fix for Variant 1 is to retrieve even more history, and the fix for Variant 2 is to strictly cap retrieval to only the single most recent ticket.\n\nThis reverses both diagnoses.\n\nVariant 1 is already retrieving the entire history and therefore cannot be classified as under-retrieval. Variant 2 retrieves only one ticket and misses relevant historical information, making it an under-retrieval problem.\n\nThe proposed fixes would worsen both failures.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Variant 1 suffers from over-retrieval / context pollution (too much irrelevant history diluting relevance), and Variant 2 suffers from under-retrieval (missing relevant history that isn't merely the most recent); the fix is retrieval scoped by relevance to the current question, rather than either \"everything\" or \"only the most recent.\"\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe two variants fail at opposite ends of the retrieval spectrum:\n\n- Variant 1 retrieves too much: Hundreds of old tickets introduce irrelevant and outdated issues into the prompt. This reduces retrieval precision and causes the agent to treat resolved historical problems as though they are still applicable.\n\n- Variant 2 retrieves too little: Selecting only the most recent ticket uses recency as a substitute for relevance. It misses an older ticket that directly matches the current issue.\n\nThe correct design is not to retrieve everything or mechanically retrieve the newest item. The agent should search the customer’s history using the current question and return a limited set of the most relevant prior tickets.\n\nDatabricks distinguishes recall—the ability to retrieve all relevant information—from precision—the proportion of retrieved results that are actually relevant. A reliable support agent requires an appropriate balance between both.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nVariant 1 has poor precision. Retrieving the complete ticket history increases the amount of irrelevant context supplied to the model. The model must then distinguish current, unresolved, and applicable issues from unrelated or obsolete cases. When irrelevant documents dominate the prompt, responses can drift away from the user’s present question.\n\nVariant 2 has poor recall. A six-month-old ticket can be more relevant than yesterday’s ticket. Recency can be used as a ranking signal, but it should not be the only selection rule.\n\nThe appropriate retrieval process is:\n\n- Restrict the search to the current customer.\n\n- Use the current question as the retrieval query.\n\n- Rank historical tickets by semantic or hybrid relevance.\n\n- Optionally incorporate recency, issue category, product, resolution status, and account metadata.\n\n- Return only the highest-value results that fit within the context budget.\n\nDatabricks recommends improving retrieval through filtering, relevance-based ranking, and reranking. Metadata filtering eliminates irrelevant candidates, while reranking prioritizes the documents most semantically relevant to the query.\n\nMLflow’s `RetrievalRelevance` judge can then evaluate whether each retrieved ticket is relevant to the current request, helping detect the context pollution demonstrated by Variant 1."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality-eval","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/is_context_relevant","https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/query-ai-search"],"source_hash":"029e38a017ae04e56361d31bd353f9587b0be1d649c12dac8ab7e13424a8919d","stem_ja":"2種類のサポートエージェントを比較しています。方式1は、新しい質問のたびに顧客の数年分のサポート履歴、すなわち数百件の過去チケットをすべてコンテキストへ取得します。その回答は話題からそれ始め、何年も前に解決した無関係な問題を、今も当てはまるかのように参照します。方式2は、関連性にかかわらず直近の過去チケット1件だけを取得します。そのため現在の質問と直接関係する6か月前の明らかに重要な問題を、最新ではないという理由だけで思い出せません。\n\n最も適切な診断と修正はどれですか。","options_ja":["より多い履歴コンテキストは常に後続質問への回答能力を改善するはずなので、方式1の動作は実際には正しく、期待どおりである。","両方式の根本原因は同じで、コンテキストウィンドウの容量不足である。両方ともモデルのウィンドウを拡大するだけで修正できる。","方式1は取得不足、方式2は取得過多である。方式1はさらに多くの履歴を取得し、方式2は最新チケット1件だけへ厳密に制限するのが修正である。","方式1は取得過多／コンテキスト汚染で、無関係な履歴が関連性を薄めている。方式2は、単に最新ではない重要履歴を欠く取得不足である。「全部」か「最新だけ」ではなく、現在の質問への関連性で取得範囲を決める。"],"explanation_ja":"方式1は必要のない履歴を入れすぎ、方式2は必要な履歴まで切り捨てています。どちらも量だけで選び、現在の質問との関連性を評価していない点が問題です。関連性と対象ユーザーなどの条件で検索を絞り、必要な過去の事実を適量取得します。「多いほど良い」でも「新しいほど良い」でもなく、「今の判断に必要か」を基準にします。","distractors_ja":["無関係な過去の問題を現在へ当てはめるのは誤りです。情報量を増やすと、注意の分散や古い事実の混同が起き得ます。","容量を増やしても無関係な履歴の混入は残り、検索対象から除いた6か月前の問題も戻りません。関連性の設計を直します。","取得過多と不足を逆に診断しています。提案された変更は、すでに起きている両方の問題をさらに強めます。","履歴を入れすぎる汚染と、必要な履歴を落とす不足を正しく区別しています。質問との関連性で範囲を決める対策が適切です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-027","number":27,"form":1,"stem":"A team already has a Lakebase Postgres instance integrated as a LangGraph checkpointer for thread-level (single-conversation) state. They now want a returning user's preferences and summarized conversation history to be available in any new session, not just within the single thread where they were first stated.\n\nWhich additional design decision is needed to support this user-level, cross-session persistent memory, beyond what the existing thread-level checkpointer already provides?","options":["Store the durable, cross-session facts (such as user preferences and conversation summaries) keyed by a stable user identifier in a separate Lakebase table or schema, distinct from the thread-scoped checkpointer state, and read/write from that user-keyed store at the start and end of each new session or thread.","Nothing further is needed; the existing thread-level checkpointer configuration automatically extends to cross-session, user-level memory, since Lakebase is durable Postgres storage.","Increase the thread-level checkpointer's retention window so that old threads are retained indefinitely, since all cross-session memory needs can be satisfied by simply keeping every old thread's checkpoint around.","Move the checkpointer's Postgres connection from Lakebase into a Unity Catalog managed Delta table, since Delta natively supports user-level partitioning that Postgres does not."],"correct":0,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"スレッドのチェックポイントとユーザーの長期記憶","concept":"ce-f1-q027","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Store the durable, cross-session facts (such as user preferences and conversation summaries) keyed by a stable user identifier in a separate Lakebase table or schema, distinct from the thread-scoped checkpointer state, and read/write from that user-keyed store at the start and end of each new session or thread.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe existing LangGraph checkpointer provides short-term, thread-scoped memory. It persists the state of one conversation using a thread identifier, allowing the agent to resume that same thread after a restart.\n\nThe new requirement is different:\n\n- A returning user might begin a completely new thread.\n\n- Preferences and summaries must remain accessible across multiple threads.\n\n- Memory must therefore be associated with the user, not only with a particular conversation ID.\n\nDatabricks distinguishes short-term memory based on thread IDs and checkpointing from long-term memory that stores useful information across multiple conversations.\n\nTherefore, the team needs an additional user-scoped long-term-memory store keyed by a stable user identifier.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe two storage scopes serve different purposes:\n\n```\nCheckpointer key:thread_id → state of one conversation Long-term-memory key:user_id → preferences, summaries, and reusable facts across conversations\n```\n\nThe checkpointer can preserve:\n\n- Conversation messages.\n\n- LangGraph node state.\n\n- Tool-call progress.\n\n- Intermediate state.\n\n- The ability to resume a specific thread.\n\nThe user-level store can preserve:\n\n- Communication preferences.\n\n- Product preferences.\n\n- Important past decisions.\n\n- Summaries of previous conversations.\n\n- Other durable facts approved for reuse.\n\nWhen a new thread begins, the application identifies the authenticated user, retrieves the appropriate user-scoped memories, and supplies only relevant facts to the agent. During or after the session, it can extract new durable facts and update that same user-keyed store.\n\nDatabricks documents self-managed Lakebase memory as supporting both thread-based conversation context and persistent user insights across sessions. It also notes that using a custom schema and direct SQL access requires the developer to manage the database schema, access controls, and retrieval design.\n\nOption A correctly introduces that second persistence scope.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Nothing further is needed; the existing thread-level checkpointer configuration automatically extends to cross-session, user-level memory, since Lakebase is durable Postgres storage.\n\nDurability does not automatically change the scope of stored state.\n\nA checkpoint keyed by `thread_id` remains associated with that thread even when its underlying records are stored durably in Lakebase. A new thread normally has a different thread ID and does not automatically inherit the state of every previous thread belonging to the same user.\n\nThe application must explicitly define a stable user-level namespace, key, or scope for cross-session memory.\n\nC. Increase the thread-level checkpointer's retention window so that old threads are retained indefinitely, since all cross-session memory needs can be satisfied by simply keeping every old thread's checkpoint around.\n\nKeeping old checkpoints preserves historical thread state, but it does not create an effective user-level memory design.\n\nThe agent would still need to:\n\n- Determine which old threads belong to the current user.\n\n- Read potentially large conversation histories.\n\n- Identify which facts are still valid.\n\n- Resolve conflicting or outdated preferences.\n\n- Extract only the information relevant to the new session.\n\nLong-term memory should normally store distilled, reusable information rather than require the agent to replay every previous conversation. Databricks distinguishes episodic interaction history from semantic memory containing reusable facts and insights.\n\nRetention is therefore not a substitute for user-scoped memory.\n\nD. Move the checkpointer's Postgres connection from Lakebase into a Unity Catalog managed Delta table, since Delta natively supports user-level partitioning that Postgres does not.\n\nThis is technically incorrect and unnecessary.\n\nLakebase is already designed to persist operational agent state and memory. PostgreSQL tables can be keyed and indexed by `user_id`, `thread_id`, or any other required identifier.\n\nMoving the checkpointer to Delta does not solve the logical distinction between:\n\n- Thread-scoped short-term state.\n\n- User-scoped cross-session memory.\n\nThe missing component is an appropriate memory schema and keying strategy, not a different storage engine.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is assuming that persistent storage automatically means cross-session memory.\n\nPersistence determines whether data survives restarts. Scope determines where that data is available:\n\n- `thread_id` scope preserves one conversation.\n\n- `user_id` scope makes selected memories available across conversations.\n\nBoth can use the same Lakebase instance, but they require distinct logical storage and retrieval behavior.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe certification-exam answer is A.\n\nA practical Lakebase design could use:\n\n```\nconversation_checkpoints- thread_id- checkpoint_id- graph_state- created_at user_memories- user_id- memory_type- memory_key- memory_value- source_thread_id- created_at- updated_at- valid_until\n```\n\nImportant production considerations include:\n\n- Derive `user_id` from authenticated application identity rather than allowing the model or client to supply an arbitrary user ID.\n\n- Store only information intended to persist.\n\n- Timestamp memories and invalidate obsolete preferences.\n\n- Keep the source thread ID for auditability.\n\n- Retrieve only memories relevant to the current request.\n\n- Resolve conflicts by favoring explicit, recent user instructions.\n\n- Apply least-privilege database permissions.\n\n- Do not use agent memory as the sole system of record for critical operational data.\n\nA separate physical database or schema is not strictly mandatory; the key requirement is a separate logical user-scoped memory store or namespace distinct from thread checkpoints.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Self-managed agent memory with Lakebase\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory)\n\n- Agent state and memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)\n\n- Agent memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)\n\n- Managed agent memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/managed-memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/managed-memory)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Store the durable, cross-session facts (such as user preferences and conversation summaries) keyed by a stable user identifier in a separate Lakebase table or schema, distinct from the thread-scoped checkpointer state, and read/write from that user-keyed store at the start and end of each new session or thread.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe existing LangGraph checkpointer provides short-term, thread-scoped memory. It persists the state of one conversation using a thread identifier, allowing the agent to resume that same thread after a restart.\n\nThe new requirement is different:\n\n- A returning user might begin a completely new thread.\n\n- Preferences and summaries must remain accessible across multiple threads.\n\n- Memory must therefore be associated with the user, not only with a particular conversation ID.\n\nDatabricks distinguishes short-term memory based on thread IDs and checkpointing from long-term memory that stores useful information across multiple conversations.\n\nTherefore, the team needs an additional user-scoped long-term-memory store keyed by a stable user identifier.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe two storage scopes serve different purposes:\n\n```\nCheckpointer key:thread_id → state of one conversation Long-term-memory key:user_id → preferences, summaries, and reusable facts across conversations\n```\n\nThe checkpointer can preserve:\n\n- Conversation messages.\n\n- LangGraph node state.\n\n- Tool-call progress.\n\n- Intermediate state.\n\n- The ability to resume a specific thread.\n\nThe user-level store can preserve:\n\n- Communication preferences.\n\n- Product preferences.\n\n- Important past decisions.\n\n- Summaries of previous conversations.\n\n- Other durable facts approved for reuse.\n\nWhen a new thread begins, the application identifies the authenticated user, retrieves the appropriate user-scoped memories, and supplies only relevant facts to the agent. During or after the session, it can extract new durable facts and update that same user-keyed store.\n\nDatabricks documents self-managed Lakebase memory as supporting both thread-based conversation context and persistent user insights across sessions. It also notes that using a custom schema and direct SQL access requires the developer to manage the database schema, access controls, and retrieval design.\n\nOption A correctly introduces that second persistence scope.","B. Nothing further is needed; the existing thread-level checkpointer configuration automatically extends to cross-session, user-level memory, since Lakebase is durable Postgres storage.\n\nDurability does not automatically change the scope of stored state.\n\nA checkpoint keyed by `thread_id` remains associated with that thread even when its underlying records are stored durably in Lakebase. A new thread normally has a different thread ID and does not automatically inherit the state of every previous thread belonging to the same user.\n\nThe application must explicitly define a stable user-level namespace, key, or scope for cross-session memory.","C. Increase the thread-level checkpointer's retention window so that old threads are retained indefinitely, since all cross-session memory needs can be satisfied by simply keeping every old thread's checkpoint around.\n\nKeeping old checkpoints preserves historical thread state, but it does not create an effective user-level memory design.\n\nThe agent would still need to:\n\n- Determine which old threads belong to the current user.\n\n- Read potentially large conversation histories.\n\n- Identify which facts are still valid.\n\n- Resolve conflicting or outdated preferences.\n\n- Extract only the information relevant to the new session.\n\nLong-term memory should normally store distilled, reusable information rather than require the agent to replay every previous conversation. Databricks distinguishes episodic interaction history from semantic memory containing reusable facts and insights.\n\nRetention is therefore not a substitute for user-scoped memory.","D. Move the checkpointer's Postgres connection from Lakebase into a Unity Catalog managed Delta table, since Delta natively supports user-level partitioning that Postgres does not.\n\nThis is technically incorrect and unnecessary.\n\nLakebase is already designed to persist operational agent state and memory. PostgreSQL tables can be keyed and indexed by `user_id`, `thread_id`, or any other required identifier.\n\nMoving the checkpointer to Delta does not solve the logical distinction between:\n\n- Thread-scoped short-term state.\n\n- User-scoped cross-session memory.\n\nThe missing component is an appropriate memory schema and keying strategy, not a different storage engine."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/managed-memory"],"source_hash":"0f8b2365eb0692c2db38d9dca153c88fbc43025b95cab6687bda8ce9e173e5b7","stem_ja":"チームには、スレッド単位（1つの会話）の状態を保存するLangGraphチェックポインターとして統合済みのLakebase Postgresインスタンスがあります。今度は、リピーターの好みと要約した会話履歴を、最初に述べた単一スレッド内だけでなく、新しいどのセッションでも使えるようにしたいと考えています。\n\n既存のスレッド単位チェックポインターが提供するものに加え、ユーザー単位でセッションをまたぐ永続メモリを支えるために必要な設計判断はどれですか。","options_ja":["好みや会話要約など、セッションをまたいで残す事実を、安定したユーザー識別子をキーとして別のLakebaseテーブルまたはスキーマに保存する。スレッド単位のチェックポイント状態と分離し、新しいセッション・スレッドの開始時と終了時にユーザーキーのストアを読み書きする。","Lakebaseは永続的なPostgresストレージなので、既存のスレッド単位チェックポインターの設定が、ユーザー単位のセッション間メモリへ自動的に拡張される。追加作業は不要である。","過去の全スレッドのチェックポイントを残せば、あらゆるセッション間メモリ要件を満たせるため、保持期間を延ばして古いスレッドを無期限に保存する。","Postgresにはないユーザー単位のパーティションをDeltaはネイティブにサポートするため、チェックポインターのPostgres接続をLakebaseからUnity Catalog管理のDeltaテーブルへ移す。"],"explanation_ja":"永続DBを使っていても、スレッド状態とユーザーの長期記憶は同じではありません。チェックポインターは特定会話の継続に使い、別会話でも必要な好みや要約は安定したユーザーIDで別に管理します。保存だけでなく、新しいセッションで読み、終了時に更新する経路が必要です。「永続化の有無」と「何をキーに読み戻すか」を分けて考えます。","distractors_ja":["スレッドIDではなくユーザーIDで長期の事実を管理し、新しい会話でも明示的に読み戻します。既存チェックポイントに不足する役割を補います。","ストレージが永続でも、自動的に別スレッドの情報が選択・読込されるわけではありません。ユーザー単位の記憶を別途設計します。","保存期間を延ばすだけでは、別スレッドから必要な事実を選んで読み戻せません。全チェックポイントの無期限保持は要件の代わりではありません。","PostgresでもユーザーIDをキーにしたテーブルを作れます。必要なのは記憶のスコープ設計であり、Deltaへの移行ではありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-028","number":28,"form":1,"stem":"An internal HR-policy agent retrieves accurate, correctly cited policy passages, but its answers frequently address a different situation than the one the user meant. For example, a user asks \"can I carry over my PTO?\" without specifying which country's policy applies to them. The agent retrieves and confidently answers using the US PTO policy passage — a real, accurately cited passage — when the user is actually an employee in the UK. Retrieval ran before any attempt was made to establish which country's policy applies.\n\nAt which pipeline stage should this mismatch be resolved, and what design change addresses it?a","options":["This should be resolved during context compaction, by summarizing all countries' PTO policies into a single unified summary before retrieval occurs.","This should be resolved during retrieval itself, by retrieving all countries' PTO policy passages for every question so no country's policy can be missed.","This should be resolved before retrieval, by having the agent first resolve the user's intent (e.g., determine or ask for the employee's country or region) and use that resolved intent to scope the retrieval query, rather than retrieving broadly and hoping the right passage surfaces.","This should be resolved after the response is generated, by adding a disclaimer to every answer noting that policies vary by country."],"correct":2,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"ユーザー意図を解決してから検索範囲を限定する","concept":"ce-f1-q028","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. This should be resolved before retrieval, by having the agent first resolve the user's intent (e.g., determine or ask for the employee's country or region) and use that resolved intent to scope the retrieval query, rather than retrieving broadly and hoping the right passage surfaces.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe retrieved US passage is factually accurate and correctly cited, so this is not primarily a document-quality, citation, or search-ranking failure.\n\nThe failure occurs because the user’s request is underspecified:\n\n- PTO rules vary by country.\n\n- The user did not identify the applicable jurisdiction.\n\n- The agent initiated retrieval before resolving that missing qualifier.\n\n- Retrieval therefore returned a relevant passage for the wrong interpretation of the question.\n\nThe agent must establish the applicable country or employment jurisdiction before searching the policy corpus. It can obtain that information from trusted employee-profile context or ask a clarification question. The resolved value should then scope retrieval through metadata filtering or a jurisdiction-specific retrieval tool.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe correct pipeline sequence is:\n\n- Interpret the user’s request.\n\n- Detect that a required qualifier is missing.\n\n- Resolve the qualifier from trusted context or ask the user.\n\n- Construct a scoped retrieval query.\n\n- Retrieve the applicable policy passage.\n\n- Generate an answer grounded in that passage.\n\nFor example, the agent might resolve:\n\n```\nIntent: Determine PTO carryover eligibilityApplicable jurisdiction: United KingdomPolicy category: Paid time off\n```\n\nIt can then retrieve with a constraint equivalent to:\n\n```\ncountry = \"UK\" AND policy_topic = \"PTO\"\n```\n\nThis prevents a semantically relevant but jurisdictionally incorrect policy from reaching the answer-generation stage.\n\nThe distinction is important: the retrieved US passage is relevant to the words “carry over PTO,” but it is not relevant to the user’s actual situation. Retrieval relevance depends on both semantic similarity and the resolved business context.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. This should be resolved during context compaction, by summarizing all countries' PTO policies into a single unified summary before retrieval occurs.\n\nContext compaction reduces the size of accumulated context. It does not determine which jurisdiction governs the employee.\n\nCombining multiple countries’ policies into one summary could also blur material differences in carryover limits, expiration rules, statutory leave, and employer-specific requirements.\n\nAdditionally, the option is internally inconsistent because compaction ordinarily operates on context that has already been assembled; it cannot reliably resolve an ambiguity before retrieval without first understanding the user’s applicable jurisdiction.\n\nB. This should be resolved during retrieval itself, by retrieving all countries' PTO policy passages for every question so no country's policy can be missed.\n\nRetrieving every country’s policy improves raw coverage but significantly reduces precision.\n\nIt would:\n\n- Add irrelevant policies to the model context.\n\n- Increase context size and latency.\n\n- Create conflicting instructions.\n\n- Force the generation model to guess which country applies.\n\n- Increase the risk of mixing rules from multiple jurisdictions.\n\nRetrieval should be scoped using the resolved country rather than compensating for missing intent by retrieving everything.\n\nD. This should be resolved after the response is generated, by adding a disclaimer to every answer noting that policies vary by country.\n\nA disclaimer does not correct the answer.\n\nThe agent would still provide a confidently stated US policy to a UK employee. A warning that policies vary cannot replace identifying the applicable policy before answering.\n\nThe correct control must prevent the wrong jurisdictional passage from becoming the basis of the response.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is diagnosing this as a retrieval-accuracy problem merely because the wrong document was retrieved.\n\nThe search system retrieved an accurate passage matching the literal question. The actual failure occurred earlier: the agent had not resolved the user’s intended jurisdiction before constructing the retrieval query.\n\nAnother trap is assuming that broader retrieval is always safer. Retrieving all possible policies creates context pollution and transfers an unresolved business decision to the language model.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is C: resolve material ambiguity before retrieval.\n\nA production HR agent should use a hierarchy such as:\n\n- Check whether the user explicitly stated the applicable country.\n\n- Check trusted employee-profile data for employment jurisdiction.\n\n- Distinguish employment jurisdiction from current physical location.\n\n- Ask a clarification question when the jurisdiction remains uncertain.\n\n- Apply the resolved value as a mandatory retrieval filter.\n\n- Return an answer citing only the applicable policy version.\n\nThe policy index should preserve metadata such as:\n\n- Country or jurisdiction.\n\n- Employee classification.\n\n- Policy topic.\n\n- Business unit.\n\n- Effective date.\n\n- Policy version.\n\n- Employment type.\n\nThe system should also avoid guessing from language, IP address, timezone, or prior conversations when an authoritative HR attribute is available.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- AI Search retrieval quality guide\n[https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality](https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/retrieval-qualityThis guidance identifies metadata filtering as a major retrieval-quality mechanism and provides region and issue-category examples.)\n\n- AI Search filtering guide\n[https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/filtering-guide](https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/filtering-guide)\n\n- Improve RAG chain quality\n[https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/](https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain?utm_source=chatgpt.com)[agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain](https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain)\n\n- Curate an effective Genie Agent\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/best-practices](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/best-practices)","source_option_explanations":["A. This should be resolved during context compaction, by summarizing all countries' PTO policies into a single unified summary before retrieval occurs.\n\nContext compaction reduces the size of accumulated context. It does not determine which jurisdiction governs the employee.\n\nCombining multiple countries’ policies into one summary could also blur material differences in carryover limits, expiration rules, statutory leave, and employer-specific requirements.\n\nAdditionally, the option is internally inconsistent because compaction ordinarily operates on context that has already been assembled; it cannot reliably resolve an ambiguity before retrieval without first understanding the user’s applicable jurisdiction.","B. This should be resolved during retrieval itself, by retrieving all countries' PTO policy passages for every question so no country's policy can be missed.\n\nRetrieving every country’s policy improves raw coverage but significantly reduces precision.\n\nIt would:\n\n- Add irrelevant policies to the model context.\n\n- Increase context size and latency.\n\n- Create conflicting instructions.\n\n- Force the generation model to guess which country applies.\n\n- Increase the risk of mixing rules from multiple jurisdictions.\n\nRetrieval should be scoped using the resolved country rather than compensating for missing intent by retrieving everything.","Correct Answer\n\nC. This should be resolved before retrieval, by having the agent first resolve the user's intent (e.g., determine or ask for the employee's country or region) and use that resolved intent to scope the retrieval query, rather than retrieving broadly and hoping the right passage surfaces.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe retrieved US passage is factually accurate and correctly cited, so this is not primarily a document-quality, citation, or search-ranking failure.\n\nThe failure occurs because the user’s request is underspecified:\n\n- PTO rules vary by country.\n\n- The user did not identify the applicable jurisdiction.\n\n- The agent initiated retrieval before resolving that missing qualifier.\n\n- Retrieval therefore returned a relevant passage for the wrong interpretation of the question.\n\nThe agent must establish the applicable country or employment jurisdiction before searching the policy corpus. It can obtain that information from trusted employee-profile context or ask a clarification question. The resolved value should then scope retrieval through metadata filtering or a jurisdiction-specific retrieval tool.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe correct pipeline sequence is:\n\n- Interpret the user’s request.\n\n- Detect that a required qualifier is missing.\n\n- Resolve the qualifier from trusted context or ask the user.\n\n- Construct a scoped retrieval query.\n\n- Retrieve the applicable policy passage.\n\n- Generate an answer grounded in that passage.\n\nFor example, the agent might resolve:\n\n```\nIntent: Determine PTO carryover eligibilityApplicable jurisdiction: United KingdomPolicy category: Paid time off\n```\n\nIt can then retrieve with a constraint equivalent to:\n\n```\ncountry = \"UK\" AND policy_topic = \"PTO\"\n```\n\nThis prevents a semantically relevant but jurisdictionally incorrect policy from reaching the answer-generation stage.\n\nThe distinction is important: the retrieved US passage is relevant to the words “carry over PTO,” but it is not relevant to the user’s actual situation. Retrieval relevance depends on both semantic similarity and the resolved business context.","D. This should be resolved after the response is generated, by adding a disclaimer to every answer noting that policies vary by country.\n\nA disclaimer does not correct the answer.\n\nThe agent would still provide a confidently stated US policy to a UK employee. A warning that policies vary cannot replace identifying the applicable policy before answering.\n\nThe correct control must prevent the wrong jurisdictional passage from becoming the basis of the response."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/retrieval-qualityThis guidance identifies metadata filtering as a major retrieval-quality mechanism and provides region and issue-category examples.","https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/filtering-guide","https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain?utm_source=chatgpt.com","https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/best-practices"],"source_hash":"cea7aaf5af847fac48e7373fbca49bd6679754b51ab98ed4dac9f6a39961fead","stem_ja":"社内の人事ポリシーエージェントは、正確で正しく引用された規程の文章を取得しますが、ユーザーが意図したものとは別の状況に回答することがよくあります。例えばユーザーが、適用される国を示さず「有給休暇（PTO）を繰り越せますか」と尋ねます。実際は英国の従業員ですが、エージェントは米国のPTO規程という実在する正確な引用文章を取得し、それを使って自信を持って回答します。どの国の規程が適用されるかを確かめる前に検索が実行されました。\n\nこの不一致はパイプラインのどの段階で解消し、どの設計変更で対処すべきですか。","options_ja":["検索の前に全国家のPTO規程を1つの共通要約へまとめる、コンテキスト圧縮の段階で解消する。","国別の規程を見落とさないよう、すべての質問で全国家のPTO規程を取得する、検索そのものの段階で解消する。","検索の前に解消する。従業員の国や地域を特定または質問するなど、まずユーザーの意図を確定し、その条件で検索クエリの範囲を限定する。広く取得して正しい文章が偶然現れることに頼らない。","応答生成後に解消する。国によって規程が異なる旨の免責文をすべての回答へ追加する。"],"explanation_ja":"引用が正確でも、適用対象が違えば回答は誤りです。国・地域という判断に不可欠な条件を先に解決し、その条件で取得範囲を限定します。プロフィール等で確定できなければ確認質問を行います。全国家をまとめて取得したり、後から免責文を足したりしても、誤った規程を選ぶ根本原因は残ります。「意図を確定してから検索」が基本です。","distractors_ja":["国ごとの条件を1つに圧縮すると、違いが埋もれるおそれがあります。そもそもどの国が適用されるかを確定する必要があります。","全国家の規程を並べても、どれが本人に適用されるかは分かりません。不要な情報と混同の可能性を増やします。","ユーザーの状況を検索前に解決し、正しい対象へ絞ります。正確な取得結果が別の問いへ使われる不一致を防げます。","免責文は誤った適用対象を修正しません。ユーザーが必要とする国の規程を、回答前に正しく選ぶべきです。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-029","number":29,"form":1,"stem":"An MCP server exposes 200 tools spanning a company's CRM, ticketing, calendar, and internal wiki systems. The current agent harness loads the full JSON schema (name, description, and complete parameter definitions) for all 200 tools into the system prompt on every single turn, regardless of what the user is asking about. Token analysis shows that this static tool-schema block accounts for roughly 70% of total prompt tokens on typical turns, even though a given user request usually only requires 2–4 tools.\n\nWhich progressive-disclosure design would most reduce token usage without limiting which tools the agent can ultimately access?","options":["Reduce the number of registered tools from 200 down to the 20 most commonly used ones, permanently removing agent access to the other 180.","Keep loading full schemas for all 200 tools on every turn, but compress them using a general-purpose text summarization pass before they enter the prompt.","Move the full JSON schemas for all 200 tools into a single large few-shot example demonstrating correct usage, so the model learns the schemas implicitly rather than reading them directly.","Expose a lightweight tool-discovery mechanism (e.g., a searchable tool index or catalog) that returns only the names and short summaries of the small number of tools relevant to the current request, and load the full parameter schema only for the specific tool(s) the agent decides to invoke."],"correct":3,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"MCPの軽量発見と選択後のスキーマ取得","concept":"ce-f1-q029","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Expose a lightweight tool-discovery mechanism (e.g., a searchable tool index or catalog) that returns only the names and short summaries of the small number of tools relevant to the current request, and load the full parameter schema only for the specific tool(s) the agent decides to invoke.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe problem is caused by eagerly loading every tool definition into the model context, even though most requests use only two to four tools.\n\nWith 200 tools, complete definitions containing names, descriptions, and JSON parameter schemas consume most of the prompt before the model processes the user’s request. This increases:\n\n- Input-token consumption.\n\n- Inference latency and cost.\n\n- Pressure on the model’s context window.\n\n- The chance of selecting a similar but inappropriate tool.\n\nThe required design is progressive tool discovery:\n\n- Initially expose only a compact, searchable representation of the available tools.\n\n- Identify the small subset relevant to the user’s request.\n\n- Load complete schemas only for those selected tools.\n\n- Invoke the selected tools normally.\n\nThis preserves ultimate access to all 200 tools while avoiding the cost of placing all 200 complete schemas into every prompt. The official MCP client guidance describes progressive discovery specifically as deferring full tool definitions and retrieving a complete schema only after a likely tool has been identified.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption D separates tool discovery from tool execution.\n\nDuring discovery, the agent needs only enough information to identify potentially useful tools, such as:\n\n```\ncrm_search_customer — Find CRM customerstickets_get_history — Retrieve support-ticket historycalendar_find_availability — Find open meeting timeswiki_search — Search internal documentation\n```\n\nAfter the agent determines that `tickets_get_history` is required, the harness can load its complete definition:\n\n```\n{  \"name\": \"tickets_get_history\",  \"description\": \"Retrieve historical support tickets for a customer\",  \"inputSchema\": {    \"type\": \"object\",    \"properties\": {      \"customer_id\": {        \"type\": \"string\"      },      \"status\": {        \"type\": \"string\"      }    },    \"required\": [\"customer_id\"]  }}\n```\n\nThe model therefore receives the detailed schema only when it needs to construct the call.\n\nMCP defines separate operations for discovering available tools and invoking a selected tool. Tool definitions include names, descriptions, and input schemas, while `tools/call` performs the actual invocation. This enables client implementations to control when full definitions enter the model’s context.\n\nThe approach does not permanently remove any tools. A request about calendars can discover calendar tools, while a later request about CRM records can discover CRM tools. The complete catalog remains available through the discovery layer.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Reduce the number of registered tools from 200 down to the 20 most commonly used ones, permanently removing agent access to the other 180.\n\nThis would reduce token usage, but it violates the requirement that the agent must ultimately retain access to all tools.\n\nAn uncommon request might legitimately require one of the excluded tools. Permanently removing it would convert a context-efficiency problem into a capability limitation.\n\nTool restrictions can be useful for security and least privilege, but they are different from progressive disclosure. Progressive disclosure keeps approved tools available while selectively loading their definitions.\n\nB. Keep loading full schemas for all 200 tools on every turn, but compress them using a general-purpose text summarization pass before they enter the prompt.\n\nThis does not implement true progressive disclosure.\n\nA general-purpose summarizer might omit or alter important schema details, including:\n\n- Required parameters.\n\n- Enumerated values.\n\n- Nested object structures.\n\n- Parameter types.\n\n- Validation constraints.\n\n- Tool-specific behavioral distinctions.\n\nThe model ultimately needs the exact JSON schema to construct a valid invocation. Summarizing all 200 schemas also creates an additional model operation and still places information about every tool into the prompt.\n\nA compact discovery catalog is useful, but it should lead to retrieval of the authoritative full schema for selected tools rather than replacing exact schemas with lossy summaries.\n\nC. Move the full JSON schemas for all 200 tools into a single large few-shot example demonstrating correct usage, so the model learns the schemas implicitly rather than reading them directly.\n\nThis merely relocates the token cost.\n\nThe full schemas would still occupy the prompt, whether they appear in the system-tool block or inside a few-shot example. A very large example could consume even more tokens because it would include schemas, sample arguments, and example outputs.\n\nFew-shot examples also do not replace current authoritative tool definitions. Schemas may change, and the client still needs exact definitions for validation and invocation.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is confusing reducing available tools with reducing tools exposed in the current context.\n\nProgressive disclosure does not permanently remove capabilities. It delays loading expensive schema details until the agent has identified which tools are relevant to the present request.\n\nAnother trap is trying to compress schemas as ordinary prose. Tool parameter schemas are executable interface contracts and should be loaded accurately when needed.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe certification-exam answer is D: use progressive tool discovery and lazy schema loading.\n\nA production implementation could use a two-stage architecture:\n\n```\nStage 1: Tool discoveryUser request → search compact tool catalog → return 2–4 candidate tools Stage 2: Tool executionFetch exact schemas for candidates → model selects tool → validate arguments → call tool\n```\n\nThe discovery index could incorporate:\n\n- Tool name and short description.\n\n- Business domain such as CRM, calendar, or ticketing.\n\n- Read-only versus mutating behavior.\n\n- Required permissions.\n\n- Example user intents.\n\n- Risk or approval classification.\n\nThe full authoritative schema should be fetched before invocation so the model and harness know the exact required parameters.\n\nOther production considerations include:\n\n- Cache tool definitions after discovery.\n\n- Invalidate cached schemas when an MCP server emits a tool-list change notification.\n\n- Preserve authorization checks independently of discovery.\n\n- Require confirmation for high-impact write operations.\n\n- Log which tools were discovered, exposed, selected, and invoked.\n\n- Evaluate tool-selection accuracy as well as token reduction.\n\nDatabricks MCP integrations also support automatic tool discovery and governed access through Unity AI Gateway. Governance controls can limit which tools are exposed or callable, while progressive discovery separately optimizes how approved tool definitions are presented to the model.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- MCP Client Best Practices – Progressive Tool Discovery\n[https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/clients/client-best-practices](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/clients/client-best-practices)\n\n- Model Context Protocol – Tools Specification\n[https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/tools](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/tools)\n\n- Understanding MCP Servers\n[https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/server-concepts](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/server-concepts)\n\n- Connect Agents to Third-Party Tools with MCP Services\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/generative-ai/agent-framework/external-connection-tools](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/generative-ai/agent-framework/external-connection-tools)","source_option_explanations":["A. Reduce the number of registered tools from 200 down to the 20 most commonly used ones, permanently removing agent access to the other 180.\n\nThis would reduce token usage, but it violates the requirement that the agent must ultimately retain access to all tools.\n\nAn uncommon request might legitimately require one of the excluded tools. Permanently removing it would convert a context-efficiency problem into a capability limitation.\n\nTool restrictions can be useful for security and least privilege, but they are different from progressive disclosure. Progressive disclosure keeps approved tools available while selectively loading their definitions.","B. Keep loading full schemas for all 200 tools on every turn, but compress them using a general-purpose text summarization pass before they enter the prompt.\n\nThis does not implement true progressive disclosure.\n\nA general-purpose summarizer might omit or alter important schema details, including:\n\n- Required parameters.\n\n- Enumerated values.\n\n- Nested object structures.\n\n- Parameter types.\n\n- Validation constraints.\n\n- Tool-specific behavioral distinctions.\n\nThe model ultimately needs the exact JSON schema to construct a valid invocation. Summarizing all 200 schemas also creates an additional model operation and still places information about every tool into the prompt.\n\nA compact discovery catalog is useful, but it should lead to retrieval of the authoritative full schema for selected tools rather than replacing exact schemas with lossy summaries.","C. Move the full JSON schemas for all 200 tools into a single large few-shot example demonstrating correct usage, so the model learns the schemas implicitly rather than reading them directly.\n\nThis merely relocates the token cost.\n\nThe full schemas would still occupy the prompt, whether they appear in the system-tool block or inside a few-shot example. A very large example could consume even more tokens because it would include schemas, sample arguments, and example outputs.\n\nFew-shot examples also do not replace current authoritative tool definitions. Schemas may change, and the client still needs exact definitions for validation and invocation.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Expose a lightweight tool-discovery mechanism (e.g., a searchable tool index or catalog) that returns only the names and short summaries of the small number of tools relevant to the current request, and load the full parameter schema only for the specific tool(s) the agent decides to invoke.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe problem is caused by eagerly loading every tool definition into the model context, even though most requests use only two to four tools.\n\nWith 200 tools, complete definitions containing names, descriptions, and JSON parameter schemas consume most of the prompt before the model processes the user’s request. This increases:\n\n- Input-token consumption.\n\n- Inference latency and cost.\n\n- Pressure on the model’s context window.\n\n- The chance of selecting a similar but inappropriate tool.\n\nThe required design is progressive tool discovery:\n\n- Initially expose only a compact, searchable representation of the available tools.\n\n- Identify the small subset relevant to the user’s request.\n\n- Load complete schemas only for those selected tools.\n\n- Invoke the selected tools normally.\n\nThis preserves ultimate access to all 200 tools while avoiding the cost of placing all 200 complete schemas into every prompt. The official MCP client guidance describes progressive discovery specifically as deferring full tool definitions and retrieving a complete schema only after a likely tool has been identified.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption D separates tool discovery from tool execution.\n\nDuring discovery, the agent needs only enough information to identify potentially useful tools, such as:\n\n```\ncrm_search_customer — Find CRM customerstickets_get_history — Retrieve support-ticket historycalendar_find_availability — Find open meeting timeswiki_search — Search internal documentation\n```\n\nAfter the agent determines that `tickets_get_history` is required, the harness can load its complete definition:\n\n```\n{  \"name\": \"tickets_get_history\",  \"description\": \"Retrieve historical support tickets for a customer\",  \"inputSchema\": {    \"type\": \"object\",    \"properties\": {      \"customer_id\": {        \"type\": \"string\"      },      \"status\": {        \"type\": \"string\"      }    },    \"required\": [\"customer_id\"]  }}\n```\n\nThe model therefore receives the detailed schema only when it needs to construct the call.\n\nMCP defines separate operations for discovering available tools and invoking a selected tool. Tool definitions include names, descriptions, and input schemas, while `tools/call` performs the actual invocation. This enables client implementations to control when full definitions enter the model’s context.\n\nThe approach does not permanently remove any tools. A request about calendars can discover calendar tools, while a later request about CRM records can discover CRM tools. The complete catalog remains available through the discovery layer."],"references":["https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/clients/client-best-practices","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/tools","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/server-concepts","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/generative-ai/agent-framework/external-connection-tools"],"source_hash":"023a1dbff7ca30d0726d4ab4a86ae36b9d538f0fe29341eb5ada7675eaeabdf1","stem_ja":"MCPサーバーが、企業のCRM、チケット管理、カレンダー、社内Wikiにまたがる200個のツールを公開しています。現在のエージェント実行基盤は、ユーザーが何を尋ねているかにかかわらず、全200ツールの完全なJSONスキーマ（名前、説明、全パラメーター定義）を毎ターンのシステムプロンプトへ読み込みます。トークン分析では、この静的なツールスキーマ部分が通常のターンでプロンプト全体の約70%を占めていますが、1つの依頼で必要なのは通常2〜4個のツールだけです。\n\nエージェントが最終的にアクセスできるツールを制限せず、トークン使用量を最も削減する段階的開示の設計はどれですか。","options_ja":["登録するツールを200個から最もよく使う20個へ減らし、残り180個へのエージェントのアクセスを恒久的になくす。","全200ツールの完全なスキーマを毎ターン読み込み続けるが、プロンプトへ入れる前に汎用テキスト要約で圧縮する。","全200ツールの完全なJSONスキーマを正しい使用法を示す1つの大きなfew-shot例へ移し、モデルが直接読むのではなく暗黙的に学ぶようにする。","検索可能なツール索引やカタログなどの軽量な発見機能を公開する。現在の依頼に関連する少数ツールの名前と短い要約だけを返し、実際に呼び出すと決めたツールの完全なパラメータースキーマだけを読み込む。"],"explanation_ja":"ツールを使えることと、全定義を毎回モデルへ渡すことは別です。軽量な発見で候補を探し、選んだものだけの詳細スキーマを読むと、200個への到達性を保ちながら2〜4個分の定義に絞れます。スキーマの意味を要約で欠落させたり、未使用機能を永久削除したりする必要はありません。「広く発見し、狭く詳読する」と覚えます。","distractors_ja":["トークンは減りますが、残り180個へアクセスできなくなります。利用できるツールを制限しないという必須条件に反します。","すべてを渡す構造が残り、要約で型や必須項目が失われる可能性もあります。必要な候補だけ詳細を読む方が直接的です。","few-shotへ移しても、その巨大な例はモデルの入力トークンを消費します。全定義を読まずに暗黙学習できるという前提は成り立ちません。","全ツールを発見可能なまま保ち、実行候補の完全な定義だけを読み込みます。機能範囲とトークン節約を両立できます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-030","number":30,"form":1,"stem":"A code-review agent has access to two registered tools:\n\nsearch_code(query: str, repo: str) — description: \"Searches the codebase for files and functions matching a query within a given repository.\"\n\nfind_function(query: str, repo: str) — description: \"Finds functions and files in a repository matching a search query.\"\n\nWhen the agent needs to locate a specific function, it inconsistently alternates between these two tools across otherwise identical requests, and reviewers report no clear pattern predicting which one gets chosen.\n\nWhat is causing the ambiguous tool selection, and which correction most directly resolves it?","options":["The two descriptions describe functionally overlapping capabilities in nearly interchangeable language (\"searches... matching a query\" vs. \"finds... matching a search query\"), giving the model no distinguishing signal between them; the fix is to redefine one tool's scope and description so their capabilities are clearly non-overlapping (e.g., restricting find_function specifically to exact function-name lookups, and search_code to broader free-text code search), or to consolidate them into a single tool.","The tools have identical parameter signatures (query, repo), and identical parameter names alone are sufficient to cause ambiguous selection regardless of the description text; renaming one parameter (e.g., search_term instead of query) would resolve the issue without touching either description.","The agent's temperature setting is too high, causing random tool selection; lowering temperature to 0 will make tool selection deterministic and correct regardless of the description overlap.","Both tools should be renamed to more generic names like tool_1 and tool_2, since specific names are what's confusing the model into treating them as different capabilities."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"重複したツール説明の意味上の境界を作る","concept":"ce-f1-q030","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. The two descriptions describe functionally overlapping capabilities in nearly interchangeable language (\"searches... matching a query\" vs. \"finds... matching a search query\"), giving the model no distinguishing signal between them; the fix is to redefine one tool's scope and description so their capabilities are clearly non-overlapping (e.g., restricting `find_function` specifically to exact function-name lookups, and `search_code` to broader free-text code search), or to consolidate them into a single tool.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe two tools expose nearly identical signals:\n\nToolApparent capability`search_code`Search files and functions using a query`find_function`Find functions and files using a search query\n\nBoth accept the same arguments and claim to find both files and functions. Nothing clearly tells the model:\n\n- When to choose one instead of the other.\n\n- Whether one performs exact matching.\n\n- Whether one searches file contents, symbols, or metadata.\n\n- Whether one is broader, faster, or more precise.\n\nThe inconsistent selection is therefore caused by overlapping tool semantics and insufficiently differentiated descriptions. Databricks recommends clear tool and parameter descriptions so an agent can understand when and how to use each tool.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nTool descriptions are part of the decision context used by the model when selecting an operation. MCP tools are identified by names and include metadata describing their schemas; models use that contextual information to decide which tool to invoke.\n\nThe tools should either be consolidated or given distinct contracts, for example:\n\n```\nfind_functionPurpose: Locate a function by its exact or fully qualified symbol name.Use when: The requested function name is known.Do not use for: Searching arbitrary code text, comments, or filenames. search_codePurpose: Perform broad free-text or semantic search across source code,comments, filenames, and symbols.Use when: The exact function or file name is unknown.Do not use for: Exact symbol lookup when the function name is already known.\n```\n\nThe distinction should also be reflected in the parameter contracts:\n\n```\nfind_function(function_name: str, repo: str)search_code(query: str, repo: str, path_filter: str | None = None)\n```\n\nIf both underlying implementations perform essentially the same search, keeping two separate tools adds ambiguity without adding capability. Consolidating them into one tool would be the cleaner solution.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. The tools have identical parameter signatures (`query`, `repo`), and identical parameter names alone are sufficient to cause ambiguous selection regardless of the description text; renaming one parameter (e.g., `search_term` instead of `query`) would resolve the issue without touching either description.\n\nIdentical signatures contribute little differentiation, but they are not independently the root cause.\n\nTwo tools may legitimately accept the same parameters while performing clearly different operations. For example, `delete_file(path, repo)` and `read_file(path, repo)` remain distinguishable because their names and descriptions define different actions.\n\nRenaming `query` to `search_term` would not explain when the model should use one tool rather than the other. Their capabilities would remain overlapping.\n\nC. The agent's temperature setting is too high, causing random tool selection; lowering temperature to 0 will make tool selection deterministic and correct regardless of the description overlap.\n\nLowering temperature may reduce variation, but it does not correct the ambiguous tool definitions.\n\nA model operating at low temperature still lacks a principled basis for choosing between two tools that appear equivalent. It might choose one more consistently, but consistency does not establish that the selection is semantically correct.\n\nThe proper fix is to improve the tool interface, not conceal the ambiguity by reducing sampling variability.\n\nD. Both tools should be renamed to more generic names like `tool_1` and `tool_2`, since specific names are what's confusing the model into treating them as different capabilities.\n\nGeneric names would remove useful semantic information and make selection even harder.\n\nTool names and descriptions should communicate purpose. Databricks explicitly recommends meaningful descriptions to make tools easier for agents and humans to use, while MCP requires each tool to have a unique identifying name and schema metadata.\n\nNames such as `tool_1` and `tool_2` provide no indication of their behavior.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is attributing inconsistent tool selection to model randomness rather than examining the tool contracts.\n\nTemperature can influence variability, but it cannot supply a distinction that the names and descriptions fail to define. The first correction should be to create mutually distinguishable tool purposes.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nFor reliable tool routing, each tool should document:\n\n- Its unique purpose.\n\n- When it should be selected.\n\n- When it should not be selected.\n\n- Expected inputs and outputs.\n\n- Important limitations.\n\n- How it differs from similar tools.\n\nAfter revising the definitions, the team should evaluate them with repeated examples such as:\n\n- “Find the exact `calculate_tax` function.”\n\n- “Search for code handling tax rounding.”\n\n- “Locate files mentioning `TaxConfiguration`.”\n\n- “Find the definition of `OrderService.submit`.”\n\nMLflow traces can then confirm whether the agent consistently selects `find_function` for exact symbols and `search_code` for broader searches. If the distinction cannot be made meaningful at the implementation level, combine the tools.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Create agent tools using Unity Catalog functions — Improve tool-calling with clear documentation\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool)\n\n- Debug a custom code agent\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/debug-agent](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/debug-agent)\n\n- Model Context Protocol — Tools\n[https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/server/tools](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/server/tools)\n\n- Use Supervisor Agent to create a coordinated multi-agent system\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. The two descriptions describe functionally overlapping capabilities in nearly interchangeable language (\"searches... matching a query\" vs. \"finds... matching a search query\"), giving the model no distinguishing signal between them; the fix is to redefine one tool's scope and description so their capabilities are clearly non-overlapping (e.g., restricting `find_function` specifically to exact function-name lookups, and `search_code` to broader free-text code search), or to consolidate them into a single tool.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe two tools expose nearly identical signals:\n\nToolApparent capability`search_code`Search files and functions using a query`find_function`Find functions and files using a search query\n\nBoth accept the same arguments and claim to find both files and functions. Nothing clearly tells the model:\n\n- When to choose one instead of the other.\n\n- Whether one performs exact matching.\n\n- Whether one searches file contents, symbols, or metadata.\n\n- Whether one is broader, faster, or more precise.\n\nThe inconsistent selection is therefore caused by overlapping tool semantics and insufficiently differentiated descriptions. Databricks recommends clear tool and parameter descriptions so an agent can understand when and how to use each tool.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nTool descriptions are part of the decision context used by the model when selecting an operation. MCP tools are identified by names and include metadata describing their schemas; models use that contextual information to decide which tool to invoke.\n\nThe tools should either be consolidated or given distinct contracts, for example:\n\n```\nfind_functionPurpose: Locate a function by its exact or fully qualified symbol name.Use when: The requested function name is known.Do not use for: Searching arbitrary code text, comments, or filenames. search_codePurpose: Perform broad free-text or semantic search across source code,comments, filenames, and symbols.Use when: The exact function or file name is unknown.Do not use for: Exact symbol lookup when the function name is already known.\n```\n\nThe distinction should also be reflected in the parameter contracts:\n\n```\nfind_function(function_name: str, repo: str)search_code(query: str, repo: str, path_filter: str | None = None)\n```\n\nIf both underlying implementations perform essentially the same search, keeping two separate tools adds ambiguity without adding capability. Consolidating them into one tool would be the cleaner solution.","B. The tools have identical parameter signatures (`query`, `repo`), and identical parameter names alone are sufficient to cause ambiguous selection regardless of the description text; renaming one parameter (e.g., `search_term` instead of `query`) would resolve the issue without touching either description.\n\nIdentical signatures contribute little differentiation, but they are not independently the root cause.\n\nTwo tools may legitimately accept the same parameters while performing clearly different operations. For example, `delete_file(path, repo)` and `read_file(path, repo)` remain distinguishable because their names and descriptions define different actions.\n\nRenaming `query` to `search_term` would not explain when the model should use one tool rather than the other. Their capabilities would remain overlapping.","C. The agent's temperature setting is too high, causing random tool selection; lowering temperature to 0 will make tool selection deterministic and correct regardless of the description overlap.\n\nLowering temperature may reduce variation, but it does not correct the ambiguous tool definitions.\n\nA model operating at low temperature still lacks a principled basis for choosing between two tools that appear equivalent. It might choose one more consistently, but consistency does not establish that the selection is semantically correct.\n\nThe proper fix is to improve the tool interface, not conceal the ambiguity by reducing sampling variability.","D. Both tools should be renamed to more generic names like `tool_1` and `tool_2`, since specific names are what's confusing the model into treating them as different capabilities.\n\nGeneric names would remove useful semantic information and make selection even harder.\n\nTool names and descriptions should communicate purpose. Databricks explicitly recommends meaningful descriptions to make tools easier for agents and humans to use, while MCP requires each tool to have a unique identifying name and schema metadata.\n\nNames such as `tool_1` and `tool_2` provide no indication of their behavior."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/debug-agent","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/server/tools","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor"],"source_hash":"2f7f51886690537efc02eaa64ebc0a3a02b7927051add0cd86a7f690d9ab3b73","stem_ja":"コードレビューエージェントが、次の2つの登録済みツールへアクセスできます。\n\n`search_code(query: str, repo: str)` — 説明：「指定リポジトリ内のコードベースで、クエリに一致するファイルと関数を検索する。」\n\n`find_function(query: str, repo: str)` — 説明：「リポジトリ内で、検索クエリに一致する関数とファイルを見つける。」\n\n特定の関数を探す必要があるとき、ほぼ同じ依頼でもエージェントは一貫せずに両ツールを選び分けます。レビュー担当者にも、どちらが選ばれるかを予測できる明確なパターンがありません。\n\n曖昧なツール選択の原因と、最も直接的な修正はどれですか。","options_ja":["両説明は「クエリに一致するものを検索する」と「検索クエリに一致するものを見つける」のように、ほぼ互換な言葉で機能の重複を示しており、区別する手がかりがない。一方の範囲と説明を見直し、例えばfind_functionを正確な関数名の参照、search_codeを広い自由文のコード検索に限定するなど、明確に重ならない機能にするか、1つのツールへ統合する。","両ツールのパラメータシグネチャ（query、repo）が同じであり、説明文にかかわらず同じ引数名だけで選択は曖昧になる。例えばqueryをsearch_termへ改名すれば、説明を変えなくても解消する。","エージェントのtemperatureが高すぎて無作為に選んでいる。0へ下げれば、説明が重なっていても選択は決定的かつ正しくなる。","具体的な名前こそが別の機能だと誤解させているため、両ツールをtool_1、tool_2のような一般的な名前へ変えるべきである。"],"explanation_ja":"引数の型も説明の機能もほぼ同じで、どちらを使うべきかの意味上の境界がありません。名前だけの微修正ではなく、完全一致の関数名参照と自由文検索のように用途を分けます。実質的に同じ機能なら統合も選択肢です。temperatureを下げても、曖昧な仕様が正しい仕様に変わるわけではありません。「機能の違いを説明する」が対策です。","distractors_ja":["機能境界を明確にして、要求との対応をモデルが判断できるようにします。機能が本当に同じなら、統合して選択自体をなくせます。","同じ引数を持つ別機能は一般的です。引数名を変えるだけでは、どの用途にどちらを使うかという曖昧さを解消しません。","変動が減っても、根拠のない選択が正しくなる保証はありません。まずツールの用途を区別できる情報を与える必要があります。","一般的な名前は機能の手がかりをさらに減らします。明確な用途を表す名前と説明の方が、意図に合う選択を支えます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-031","number":31,"form":1,"stem":"A team is implementing progressive disclosure for an MCP-connected agent with access to a large tool catalog. They evaluate two designs:\n\nDesign 1: On each turn, the agent first receives a compact list of tool names and one-line summaries for the full catalog. Only after the agent selects a candidate tool by name does the harness fetch and inject that tool's full parameter schema, immediately before the agent issues the actual call.\n\nDesign 2: On each turn, the agent receives the full detailed parameter schema (including nested object definitions and enum lists) for every registered tool, regardless of whether the agent ends up calling it.\n\nWhich statement correctly explains why Design 1 consumes fewer tokens than Design 2 across a typical session, without reducing the number of tools the agent can ultimately use?","options":["Design 1 consumes fewer tokens because it permanently restricts the agent to only calling tools it has called before, which shrinks the effective catalog over time.","Design 1 and Design 2 consume the same number of tokens overall, since both designs must eventually expose full schemas to the model before any tool can be executed.","Design 1 consumes fewer tokens because the full parameter schema — the most token-heavy part of a tool definition — is only loaded for the one or few tools actually selected on a given turn, while Design 2 pays that same token cost for every registered tool on every single turn, regardless of relevance.","Design 1 consumes fewer tokens only because it uses a smaller, less capable model for the discovery stage, not because of any difference in how much schema information is loaded."],"correct":2,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"段階的開示が節約する未使用ツールの詳細定義","concept":"ce-f1-q031","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Design 1 consumes fewer tokens because the full parameter schema — the most token-heavy part of a tool definition — is only loaded for the one or few tools actually selected on a given turn, while Design 2 pays that same token cost for every registered tool on every single turn, regardless of relevance.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe important distinction is when and how many complete tool schemas are placed in the model’s context.\n\nUnder Design 1:\n\n- The agent receives a compact catalog covering all available tools.\n\n- It identifies the small subset relevant to the request.\n\n- The harness loads complete schemas only for those selected tools.\n\n- The agent creates and issues the tool call.\n\nUnder Design 2, the complete schema for every tool is loaded on every turn, even though most tools are irrelevant to that turn.\n\nThe official MCP client guidance describes progressive discovery as allowing a model to identify the tools it needs and then load their schemas, minimizing the token cost of tool definitions while keeping context focused on reasoning.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nComplete tool definitions can be token-heavy because they may include:\n\n- Detailed descriptions.\n\n- Required and optional parameters.\n\n- Nested object structures.\n\n- Enum lists.\n\n- Validation constraints.\n\n- Input and output schemas.\n\nMCP tools are identified by name and include metadata describing their schemas. Their input arguments must conform to the tool’s declared `inputSchema`.\n\nSuppose the catalog contains 200 tools, but a request requires only three:\n\n- Design 1 initially exposes a compact representation of all 200 tools, then loads three complete schemas.\n\n- Design 2 loads all 200 complete schemas.\n\nDesign 1 therefore avoids paying the full-schema token cost for the other 197 tools on that turn.\n\nThe agent does not lose access to those tools. A tool omitted from one turn’s detailed context remains discoverable and can have its complete schema loaded when a later request requires it. Progressive disclosure reduces immediate context exposure, not the underlying catalog or the agent’s ultimate capabilities.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Design 1 consumes fewer tokens because it permanently restricts the agent to only calling tools it has called before, which shrinks the effective catalog over time.\n\nThis incorrectly describes progressive disclosure as permanent tool removal.\n\nThe full tool catalog remains discoverable. The agent can select and load a previously unused tool whenever a new request requires it. Design 1 reduces how many detailed schemas are present on a particular turn; it does not progressively eliminate tools.\n\nB. Design 1 and Design 2 consume the same number of tokens overall, since both designs must eventually expose full schemas to the model before any tool can be executed.\n\nBoth designs may need to expose the full schema of a tool that is actually called, but Design 1 does not need to expose the schemas of every unselected tool.\n\nFor a request requiring two tools:\n\n- Design 1 pays for two detailed schemas.\n\n- Design 2 pays for every registered tool’s detailed schema.\n\nTherefore, their total prompt-token consumption is not equivalent across a typical session.\n\nD. Design 1 consumes fewer tokens only because it uses a smaller, less capable model for the discovery stage, not because of any difference in how much schema information is loaded.\n\nThe scenario does not specify a different model for discovery.\n\nProgressive disclosure provides token savings even when the same model performs both discovery and invocation. The savings come from replacing a large collection of complete schemas with a compact catalog and loading detailed schemas only when required.\n\nA smaller discovery model could be an additional optimization, but it is not the reason Design 1 consumes fewer prompt tokens.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is reasoning that because every invoked tool eventually needs its full schema, progressive disclosure cannot save tokens.\n\nOnly the selected tools need their complete schemas. The schemas of hundreds of irrelevant tools do not need to be repeatedly injected into every turn.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is C: discover broadly, disclose detailed schemas selectively.\n\nA production flow could be:\n\n```\nUser request   ↓Search compact tool catalog   ↓Select 2–4 candidate tools   ↓Load exact schemas for those tools   ↓Choose and invoke the required tool\n```\n\nThe harness should preserve the authoritative schema rather than generate a lossy summary of the selected tool’s parameters. It can also cache selected schemas during a multi-turn workflow and invalidate them when the MCP server reports that its tool catalog has changed.\n\nMCP separates tool discovery from tool execution through operations such as `tools/list` and `tools/call`, enabling clients to control how available tools are discovered and invoked.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- MCP Client Best Practices — Progressive Tool Discovery\n[https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/clients/client-best-practices](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/clients/client-best-practices)\n\n- Model Context Protocol — Tools Specification\n[https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/tools](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/tools)\n\n- Model Context Protocol Architecture Overview\n[https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/architecture](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/architecture)\n\n- Understanding MCP Servers\n[https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/server-concepts](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/server-concepts)","source_option_explanations":["A. Design 1 consumes fewer tokens because it permanently restricts the agent to only calling tools it has called before, which shrinks the effective catalog over time.\n\nThis incorrectly describes progressive disclosure as permanent tool removal.\n\nThe full tool catalog remains discoverable. The agent can select and load a previously unused tool whenever a new request requires it. Design 1 reduces how many detailed schemas are present on a particular turn; it does not progressively eliminate tools.","B. Design 1 and Design 2 consume the same number of tokens overall, since both designs must eventually expose full schemas to the model before any tool can be executed.\n\nBoth designs may need to expose the full schema of a tool that is actually called, but Design 1 does not need to expose the schemas of every unselected tool.\n\nFor a request requiring two tools:\n\n- Design 1 pays for two detailed schemas.\n\n- Design 2 pays for every registered tool’s detailed schema.\n\nTherefore, their total prompt-token consumption is not equivalent across a typical session.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Design 1 consumes fewer tokens because the full parameter schema — the most token-heavy part of a tool definition — is only loaded for the one or few tools actually selected on a given turn, while Design 2 pays that same token cost for every registered tool on every single turn, regardless of relevance.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe important distinction is when and how many complete tool schemas are placed in the model’s context.\n\nUnder Design 1:\n\n- The agent receives a compact catalog covering all available tools.\n\n- It identifies the small subset relevant to the request.\n\n- The harness loads complete schemas only for those selected tools.\n\n- The agent creates and issues the tool call.\n\nUnder Design 2, the complete schema for every tool is loaded on every turn, even though most tools are irrelevant to that turn.\n\nThe official MCP client guidance describes progressive discovery as allowing a model to identify the tools it needs and then load their schemas, minimizing the token cost of tool definitions while keeping context focused on reasoning.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nComplete tool definitions can be token-heavy because they may include:\n\n- Detailed descriptions.\n\n- Required and optional parameters.\n\n- Nested object structures.\n\n- Enum lists.\n\n- Validation constraints.\n\n- Input and output schemas.\n\nMCP tools are identified by name and include metadata describing their schemas. Their input arguments must conform to the tool’s declared `inputSchema`.\n\nSuppose the catalog contains 200 tools, but a request requires only three:\n\n- Design 1 initially exposes a compact representation of all 200 tools, then loads three complete schemas.\n\n- Design 2 loads all 200 complete schemas.\n\nDesign 1 therefore avoids paying the full-schema token cost for the other 197 tools on that turn.\n\nThe agent does not lose access to those tools. A tool omitted from one turn’s detailed context remains discoverable and can have its complete schema loaded when a later request requires it. Progressive disclosure reduces immediate context exposure, not the underlying catalog or the agent’s ultimate capabilities.","D. Design 1 consumes fewer tokens only because it uses a smaller, less capable model for the discovery stage, not because of any difference in how much schema information is loaded.\n\nThe scenario does not specify a different model for discovery.\n\nProgressive disclosure provides token savings even when the same model performs both discovery and invocation. The savings come from replacing a large collection of complete schemas with a compact catalog and loading detailed schemas only when required.\n\nA smaller discovery model could be an additional optimization, but it is not the reason Design 1 consumes fewer prompt tokens."],"references":["https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/clients/client-best-practices","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/tools","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/architecture","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/server-concepts"],"source_hash":"b1804ea184577519e4ce894bc058976322a5a1ad52513d60dcd0ba0ab6095a30","stem_ja":"チームが、大きなツールカタログへアクセスできるMCP接続エージェントの段階的開示を実装しています。2つの設計を評価します。\n\n設計1：各ターンで、まず全カタログのツール名と1行の要約からなる簡潔な一覧を受け取る。エージェントが名前で候補を選んだ後にだけ、実際の呼び出し直前に、そのツールの完全なパラメータースキーマを実行基盤が取得・挿入する。\n\n設計2：結局呼び出すかどうかにかかわらず、ネストしたオブジェクト定義やenum一覧を含む全登録ツールの詳しいパラメータースキーマを、各ターンで受け取る。\n\n最終的に使えるツール数を減らさず、通常のセッション全体で設計1の消費トークンが設計2より少なくなる理由を正しく説明したものはどれですか。","options_ja":["設計1は、以前呼び出したツールしか今後呼べないよう恒久的に制限し、時間とともに実効的なカタログを小さくするため、トークンが少なくなる。","実行前には結局完全なスキーマをモデルへ公開する必要があるため、設計1と設計2の全体の消費トークンは同じである。","設計1では、定義の中で最もトークンを使う完全なパラメータースキーマを、そのターンで実際に選んだ1個または少数のツールだけについて読み込む。設計2は関連性にかかわらず、全登録ツールのそのコストを毎ターン払うため。","設計1のトークンが少ないのは、発見段階に小型で能力の低いモデルを使うためだけであり、読み込むスキーマ量の違いによるものではない。"],"explanation_ja":"段階的開示では、使う可能性がある全機能を発見できる状態にしつつ、詳細を使う直前まで読ませません。パラメーターの型、ネスト、enumなどの重い定義は、選ばれた少数分だけ入力されます。使わないツールの詳細コストを毎ターン払わないことが節約源です。実行するツールに必要な完全スキーマを省略する方式ではありません。","distractors_ja":["過去に呼んだツールだけに制限する設計ではありません。初めて必要となるツールも、軽量カタログから発見して使えます。","実行対象に完全スキーマが必要でも、未使用ツールの定義まで読む必要はありません。両設計はこの未使用分の入力コストが違います。","選択した少数の詳細だけを読むので、全機能を毎回詳読するコストを回避できます。機能範囲を狭めずに節約する理由です。","設問にはモデルの変更がありません。同じモデルでも、入力する詳細定義を減らせばトークン消費は減ります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-032","number":32,"form":1,"stem":"An agent's message history is approaching the context window limit after a long research session. The transcript includes: (1) the full raw JSON response from an early web-search call, most of which (headers, pagination tokens, unrelated links) was never referenced again after the agent extracted three relevant facts from it in that same turn; (2) the full text of a document the agent is still actively quoting from and cross-referencing in its most recent three turns; (3) the complete stdout of a data-processing script the agent ran ten turns ago, whose only relevant output (a computed total) was already restated in the agent's own summary right after running it; and (4) the user's original task description from the very first turn.\n\nWhich raw tool outputs are the best candidates for clearing to free up context window space?","options":["The full raw web-search response from item (1) and the complete script stdout from item (3), since their relevant contents were already extracted and restated elsewhere in the conversation, and clearing the raw dumps does not remove that already-captured information.","The user's original task description from item (4), since it is the oldest message in the session and therefore the least relevant to the agent's current step.","The full document text from item (2), since long documents are always the best candidates for removal regardless of whether the agent is still actively using them.","All four items should be cleared equally, since age in the conversation — not current relevance or duplication — is the only factor that should determine what gets cleared."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"必要情報を引き継いだ生ツール出力を削除する","concept":"ce-f1-q032","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. The full raw web-search response from item (1) and the complete script stdout from item (3), since their relevant contents were already extracted and restated elsewhere in the conversation, and clearing the raw dumps does not remove that already-captured information.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe best candidates for tool-result clearing are outputs that are:\n\n- Large and token-intensive.\n\n- No longer actively required.\n\n- Redundant because their useful information has already been extracted.\n\n- Re-fetchable or reproducible if unexpectedly needed later.\n\nBoth the raw web-search response and the script stdout meet these conditions:\n\n- The agent already extracted the three relevant search facts.\n\n- The script’s computed total was preserved in a later summary.\n\n- The remaining headers, pagination data, unrelated links, and verbose logs provide little continuing value.\n\nBy contrast, the document is still being actively quoted and cross-referenced, while the original task description continues to define the agent’s objective and constraints.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nTool outputs become part of the conversation history and continue consuming context-window capacity on subsequent turns. Once an agent has extracted and retained the useful conclusions, keeping the entire original payload often adds cost without adding useful reasoning context.\n\nFor item (1), the meaningful search findings have already been incorporated into the conversation. The unused headers, pagination tokens, and unrelated results are redundant.\n\nFor item (3), the only relevant script result—the computed total—has already been captured in the agent’s summary. The complete stdout may contain progress messages, diagnostic output, and other execution details that are no longer needed.\n\nClearing these raw outputs preserves the meaningful derived information while recovering context space. A robust implementation can retain the tool-call record or a short placeholder indicating that the original result was cleared. If the detailed source is unexpectedly needed again, the tool can often be called again.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. The user's original task description from item (4), since it is the oldest message in the session and therefore the least relevant to the agent's current step.\n\nThis is incorrect because age alone does not determine relevance.\n\nThe original task description may contain:\n\n- The objective of the research.\n\n- Scope limitations.\n\n- Required output format.\n\n- User preferences.\n\n- Success criteria.\n\nRemoving it could cause the agent to lose alignment with the user’s original request. Important instructions should normally be preserved directly or captured faithfully in a compact task-state summary.\n\nC. The full document text from item (2), since long documents are always the best candidates for removal regardless of whether the agent is still actively using them.\n\nThe document is still being quoted and cross-referenced in recent turns. Clearing it now could force repeated retrieval or prevent the agent from verifying quotations and relationships between passages.\n\nContent size is relevant, but current usefulness is more important than size alone. Active working material should generally remain available until that stage of the task is complete.\n\nD. All four items should be cleared equally, since age in the conversation — not current relevance or duplication — is the only factor that should determine what gets cleared.\n\nThis applies an unsafe, purely chronological policy.\n\nTool-result clearing commonly starts with older outputs, but age should be combined with:\n\n- Whether the output is still being used.\n\n- Whether its important information has been preserved.\n\n- Whether it is duplicated elsewhere.\n\n- Whether it can be retrieved again.\n\n- Whether removing it would affect task continuity.\n\nClearing all four items would remove both active evidence and the user’s governing task instructions.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming that the oldest or largest content should always be removed first.\n\nEffective context management is based on relevance, redundancy, recoverability, and current use. Large raw outputs are strong clearing candidates only after their useful information has been extracted or persisted elsewhere.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is A: clear bulky, no-longer-needed tool results while retaining active source material and task-defining instructions.\n\nA practical context-management policy can classify content as follows:\n\nContext typeRecommended treatmentRaw tool output already summarizedClear or replace with a placeholderRe-fetchable historical API responseClear after extracting useful factsActive document being quotedRetain until the active work finishesOriginal task requirementsRetain or preserve in a high-fidelity task summaryImportant derived calculationPreserve the result and relevant assumptionsUnused logs, headers, and pagination dataClear\n\nThe system should also retain provenance—such as a source URL, file path, query, or tool-call identifier—so removed content can be retrieved again if necessary.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Context editing — Tool result clearing \n[https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-editing](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-editing)\n\n- Context windows — Manage context with compaction and tool-result clearing \n[https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-windows](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-windows)\n\n- Context engineering: Memory, compaction, and tool clearing \n[https://platform.claude.com/cookbook/tool-use-context-engineering-context-engineering-tools](https://platform.claude.com/cookbook/tool-use-context-engineering-context-engineering-tools)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. The full raw web-search response from item (1) and the complete script stdout from item (3), since their relevant contents were already extracted and restated elsewhere in the conversation, and clearing the raw dumps does not remove that already-captured information.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe best candidates for tool-result clearing are outputs that are:\n\n- Large and token-intensive.\n\n- No longer actively required.\n\n- Redundant because their useful information has already been extracted.\n\n- Re-fetchable or reproducible if unexpectedly needed later.\n\nBoth the raw web-search response and the script stdout meet these conditions:\n\n- The agent already extracted the three relevant search facts.\n\n- The script’s computed total was preserved in a later summary.\n\n- The remaining headers, pagination data, unrelated links, and verbose logs provide little continuing value.\n\nBy contrast, the document is still being actively quoted and cross-referenced, while the original task description continues to define the agent’s objective and constraints.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nTool outputs become part of the conversation history and continue consuming context-window capacity on subsequent turns. Once an agent has extracted and retained the useful conclusions, keeping the entire original payload often adds cost without adding useful reasoning context.\n\nFor item (1), the meaningful search findings have already been incorporated into the conversation. The unused headers, pagination tokens, and unrelated results are redundant.\n\nFor item (3), the only relevant script result—the computed total—has already been captured in the agent’s summary. The complete stdout may contain progress messages, diagnostic output, and other execution details that are no longer needed.\n\nClearing these raw outputs preserves the meaningful derived information while recovering context space. A robust implementation can retain the tool-call record or a short placeholder indicating that the original result was cleared. If the detailed source is unexpectedly needed again, the tool can often be called again.","B. The user's original task description from item (4), since it is the oldest message in the session and therefore the least relevant to the agent's current step.\n\nThis is incorrect because age alone does not determine relevance.\n\nThe original task description may contain:\n\n- The objective of the research.\n\n- Scope limitations.\n\n- Required output format.\n\n- User preferences.\n\n- Success criteria.\n\nRemoving it could cause the agent to lose alignment with the user’s original request. Important instructions should normally be preserved directly or captured faithfully in a compact task-state summary.","C. The full document text from item (2), since long documents are always the best candidates for removal regardless of whether the agent is still actively using them.\n\nThe document is still being quoted and cross-referenced in recent turns. Clearing it now could force repeated retrieval or prevent the agent from verifying quotations and relationships between passages.\n\nContent size is relevant, but current usefulness is more important than size alone. Active working material should generally remain available until that stage of the task is complete.","D. All four items should be cleared equally, since age in the conversation — not current relevance or duplication — is the only factor that should determine what gets cleared.\n\nThis applies an unsafe, purely chronological policy.\n\nTool-result clearing commonly starts with older outputs, but age should be combined with:\n\n- Whether the output is still being used.\n\n- Whether its important information has been preserved.\n\n- Whether it is duplicated elsewhere.\n\n- Whether it can be retrieved again.\n\n- Whether removing it would affect task continuity.\n\nClearing all four items would remove both active evidence and the user’s governing task instructions."],"references":["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-editing","https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-windows","https://platform.claude.com/cookbook/tool-use-context-engineering-context-engineering-tools"],"source_hash":"3458d13a038f064fbcc32abb2a6d7566463657b62967b32105343fe6d6247376","stem_ja":"長い調査セッションの後、エージェントのメッセージ履歴がコンテキストウィンドウの上限に近づいています。記録には次の内容があります。(1) 初期のWeb検索の完全な生JSON応答。ヘッダー、ページ送りトークン、無関係なリンクなどの大半は、そのターンで3つの関連事実を抽出した後は参照されていない。(2) エージェントが直近3ターンで今も引用し、相互参照している文書の全文。(3) 10ターン前に実行したデータ処理スクリプトの標準出力全文。唯一関連する結果である計算済みの合計値は、実行直後にエージェント自身の要約へ再記載済み。(4) 最初のターンの、ユーザーの元のタスク説明。\n\nコンテキストの空きを増やすために削除する、生のツール出力の最良の候補はどれですか。","options_ja":["(1)のWeb検索の生応答全文と(3)のスクリプトの標準出力全文。関連する内容は会話内の別の場所に抽出・再記載されており、生の大量出力を削っても取得済みの情報は失われないため。","(4)のユーザーの元のタスク説明。セッションで最も古いメッセージなので、現在の段階への関連性が最も低いため。","(2)の文書全文。現在も使っているかにかかわらず、長い文書は常に最良の削除候補であるため。","現在の関連性や重複ではなく、会話内の古さだけを削除の判断基準とすべきなので、4つとも等しく削除する。"],"explanation_ja":"安全に削れるのは、必要情報がすでに抽出され、以後の作業が生出力へ依存していないものです。Web検索の不要な付帯情報と、合計を再記載した標準出力が該当します。現在も引用している文書や、目的と制約を定める元の依頼は古くても重要です。「大きい・古い」より「重複しており今は参照されない」を基準にします。","distractors_ja":["必要な3事実と合計値は別の場所へ残っています。未参照の生出力だけを減らすので、進行中の判断材料を失いにくい候補です。","元のタスク説明は現在の目標や制約を定めます。古さだけで関連性が低いとは判断できず、生のツール出力でもありません。","いま引用・照合している文書を削ると、直接参照する証拠を失います。大きいことだけでは削除の根拠になりません。","履歴の年齢だけで削ると、現在も必要な制約や証拠を落とします。依存関係と、要点が別に残っているかを確認します。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-033","number":33,"form":1,"stem":"A Databricks agent needs to answer: \"What was the average order value for customers in the Northeast region last quarter?\" The following Unity Catalog–registered tools are available:\n\n- get_customer_profile(customer_id: STRING) — returns a single customer's profile fields (region, signup date, loyalty tier). Accepts one customer ID at a time.\n\n- query_orders_aggregate(region: STRING, start_date: DATE, end_date: DATE) — a registered SQL function returning aggregate order metrics (average order value, order count, total revenue) filtered by region and date range.\n\n- list_recent_orders(limit: INT) — returns the most recent N individual order records across all regions, with no region or date-range filtering.\n\n- update_customer_region(customer_id: STRING, new_region: STRING) — a write function that updates a customer's assigned region.\n\nWhich tool is the most appropriate choice for this task, and why?","options":["get_customer_profile, because region is a field on the customer profile, and the agent can loop over every customer's profile individually to compute the average manually.","query_orders_aggregate, because its parameters (region, start_date, end_date) directly match the task's required inputs, and it returns the exact aggregate metric (average order value) needed as output, without requiring the agent to fetch and manually aggregate individual records.","list_recent_orders, because it returns order records that can be filtered and averaged by the agent after retrieval, and \"recent\" most likely includes last quarter's data.","update_customer_region, because region is directly referenced in the task, so a region-related function is the most functionally relevant match."],"correct":1,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"ツールは入力・処理・出力の一致で選ぶ","concept":"ce-f1-q033","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. `query_orders_aggregate`, because its parameters (`region`, `start_date`, `end_date`) directly match the task's required inputs, and it returns the exact aggregate metric (average order value) needed as output, without requiring the agent to fetch and manually aggregate individual records.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe user’s question requires:\n\n- A regional filter: Northeast\n\n- A time filter: last quarter\n\n- An aggregate calculation: average order value\n\nThe `query_orders_aggregate` tool directly accepts all required filtering inputs and returns the requested aggregate metric. It is therefore the most precise and efficient tool for the task.\n\nDatabricks recommends using a Unity Catalog SQL function as a structured retrieval tool when the required query is known in advance and the agent supplies the parameters. This scenario fits that pattern exactly: the aggregation logic is predefined, while the agent provides the region and date range.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe tool signature aligns directly with the question:\n\n```\nquery_orders_aggregate(    region: STRING,    start_date: DATE,    end_date: DATE)\n```\n\nThe agent can resolve:\n\n- `region` → `Northeast`\n\n- `start_date` → first day of the previous quarter\n\n- `end_date` → last day of the previous quarter\n\nThe function then performs the filtering and aggregation in the data layer and returns `average order value` as an output metric.\n\nThis design is preferable because it:\n\n- Pushes filtering and aggregation into the governed SQL function.\n\n- Avoids retrieving unnecessary individual order records.\n\n- Reduces context-window consumption.\n\n- Avoids asking the language model to perform manual arithmetic.\n\n- Produces a deterministic calculation based on the authoritative order data.\n\n- Minimizes the number of required tool calls.\n\nThe tool’s description, parameters, and output are also closely aligned with the user’s intent, which makes correct tool selection more reliable.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. `get_customer_profile`, because region is a field on the customer profile, and the agent can loop over every customer's profile individually to compute the average manually.\n\nThis tool returns profile information for only one customer at a time. It does not return:\n\n- Order values.\n\n- Orders placed during the previous quarter.\n\n- Aggregate sales metrics.\n\n- A list of all customers in the Northeast region.\n\nThe agent would need to discover every customer ID, call the tool repeatedly, retrieve those customers’ orders through another unavailable mechanism, and calculate the average manually. That would be inefficient and still would not be possible using this tool alone.\n\nA field appearing in a tool’s output does not make that tool appropriate when it cannot produce the actual data needed for the calculation.\n\nC. `list_recent_orders`, because it returns order records that can be filtered and averaged by the agent after retrieval, and \"recent\" most likely includes last quarter's data.\n\nThis tool cannot reliably satisfy either required filter:\n\n- It cannot filter by region.\n\n- It cannot filter by an explicit date range.\n\n“Recent” is not equivalent to “last quarter.” Depending on order volume and the specified `limit`, the returned records might cover only the last few hours or days. Alternatively, an extremely large limit would retrieve excessive data and still provide no guarantee of completeness.\n\nThe agent would also have to transfer individual order records into context and perform the aggregation itself. This is less efficient and less reliable than using the purpose-built aggregate function.\n\nD. `update_customer_region`, because region is directly referenced in the task, so a region-related function is the most functionally relevant match.\n\nThis is a write operation that changes customer data. The user asked for an analytical answer, not a data modification.\n\nSelecting a tool merely because its name contains a term from the request is incorrect. The agent must consider the tool’s action and complete purpose.\n\nInvoking this function could also create an unintended and potentially harmful side effect by changing a customer’s assigned region.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is matching tools based on isolated keywords such as region or recent, rather than comparing the complete task requirements with each tool’s inputs, output, and behavior.\n\nThe best tool is the one whose contract most directly matches:\n\n```\nRequired filters + required calculation + required output\n```\n\nAnother trap is selecting a general data-retrieval tool and expecting the agent to perform aggregation manually when a purpose-built aggregate tool already exists.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is B: use the narrowest authoritative tool that directly performs the required structured query.\n\nBefore calling it, the application should translate “last quarter” into deterministic dates. It should clarify whether the organization uses:\n\n- Calendar quarters.\n\n- Fiscal quarters.\n\n- The user’s local timezone.\n\n- A business-specific reporting calendar.\n\nFor example, if calendar quarters apply and the request is made on August 2, 2026, the previous quarter is:\n\n```\nstart_date = 2026-04-01end_date   = 2026-06-30\n```\n\nThe agent should then call the aggregate function once and return the `average_order_value` field. The service principal also requires appropriate Unity Catalog privileges to execute the registered function and access its underlying data.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Connect agents to structured data\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools)\n\n- Create agent tools using Unity Catalog functions\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool)\n\n- SQL and Python user-defined functions in Unity Catalog\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/udf/unity-catalog](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/udf/unity-catalog)","source_option_explanations":["A. `get_customer_profile`, because region is a field on the customer profile, and the agent can loop over every customer's profile individually to compute the average manually.\n\nThis tool returns profile information for only one customer at a time. It does not return:\n\n- Order values.\n\n- Orders placed during the previous quarter.\n\n- Aggregate sales metrics.\n\n- A list of all customers in the Northeast region.\n\nThe agent would need to discover every customer ID, call the tool repeatedly, retrieve those customers’ orders through another unavailable mechanism, and calculate the average manually. That would be inefficient and still would not be possible using this tool alone.\n\nA field appearing in a tool’s output does not make that tool appropriate when it cannot produce the actual data needed for the calculation.","Correct Answer\n\nB. `query_orders_aggregate`, because its parameters (`region`, `start_date`, `end_date`) directly match the task's required inputs, and it returns the exact aggregate metric (average order value) needed as output, without requiring the agent to fetch and manually aggregate individual records.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe user’s question requires:\n\n- A regional filter: Northeast\n\n- A time filter: last quarter\n\n- An aggregate calculation: average order value\n\nThe `query_orders_aggregate` tool directly accepts all required filtering inputs and returns the requested aggregate metric. It is therefore the most precise and efficient tool for the task.\n\nDatabricks recommends using a Unity Catalog SQL function as a structured retrieval tool when the required query is known in advance and the agent supplies the parameters. This scenario fits that pattern exactly: the aggregation logic is predefined, while the agent provides the region and date range.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe tool signature aligns directly with the question:\n\n```\nquery_orders_aggregate(    region: STRING,    start_date: DATE,    end_date: DATE)\n```\n\nThe agent can resolve:\n\n- `region` → `Northeast`\n\n- `start_date` → first day of the previous quarter\n\n- `end_date` → last day of the previous quarter\n\nThe function then performs the filtering and aggregation in the data layer and returns `average order value` as an output metric.\n\nThis design is preferable because it:\n\n- Pushes filtering and aggregation into the governed SQL function.\n\n- Avoids retrieving unnecessary individual order records.\n\n- Reduces context-window consumption.\n\n- Avoids asking the language model to perform manual arithmetic.\n\n- Produces a deterministic calculation based on the authoritative order data.\n\n- Minimizes the number of required tool calls.\n\nThe tool’s description, parameters, and output are also closely aligned with the user’s intent, which makes correct tool selection more reliable.","C. `list_recent_orders`, because it returns order records that can be filtered and averaged by the agent after retrieval, and \"recent\" most likely includes last quarter's data.\n\nThis tool cannot reliably satisfy either required filter:\n\n- It cannot filter by region.\n\n- It cannot filter by an explicit date range.\n\n“Recent” is not equivalent to “last quarter.” Depending on order volume and the specified `limit`, the returned records might cover only the last few hours or days. Alternatively, an extremely large limit would retrieve excessive data and still provide no guarantee of completeness.\n\nThe agent would also have to transfer individual order records into context and perform the aggregation itself. This is less efficient and less reliable than using the purpose-built aggregate function.","D. `update_customer_region`, because region is directly referenced in the task, so a region-related function is the most functionally relevant match.\n\nThis is a write operation that changes customer data. The user asked for an analytical answer, not a data modification.\n\nSelecting a tool merely because its name contains a term from the request is incorrect. The agent must consider the tool’s action and complete purpose.\n\nInvoking this function could also create an unintended and potentially harmful side effect by changing a customer’s assigned region."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/udf/unity-catalog"],"source_hash":"6e66fc0e77de2cf12cadfe48209a25ae0a1acb778aba6b3e60afcc8f8d5c1901","stem_ja":"Databricksエージェントが、「前四半期の北東地域の顧客の平均注文額はいくらだったか」に答える必要があります。次のUnity Catalog登録済みツールを利用できます。\n\n- `get_customer_profile(customer_id: STRING)`：1人の顧客のプロフィール項目（地域、登録日、ロイヤルティ階層）を返す。顧客IDは一度に1つだけ受け取る。\n- `query_orders_aggregate(region: STRING, start_date: DATE, end_date: DATE)`：地域と期間で絞った注文の集計指標（平均注文額、注文数、売上合計）を返す登録済みSQL関数。\n- `list_recent_orders(limit: INT)`：全地域の最新N件の個別注文記録を返す。地域や期間のフィルターはない。\n- `update_customer_region(customer_id: STRING, new_region: STRING)`：顧客に割り当てられた地域を更新する書き込み関数。\n\nこのタスクに最も適したツールと、その理由はどれですか。","options_ja":["get_customer_profile。地域は顧客プロフィールの項目であり、エージェントが全顧客のプロフィールを1人ずつ繰り返し取得して平均を手計算できるため。","query_orders_aggregate。パラメーター（region、start_date、end_date）が要求の入力と直接一致し、必要な集計値である平均注文額をそのまま返すため。個別記録の取得と手作業の集計が不要である。","list_recent_orders。取得後にエージェントが注文記録を絞って平均でき、「最近」にはおそらく前四半期のデータが含まれるため。","update_customer_region。タスクが地域へ直接言及しており、地域に関する関数が機能上最も関連するため。"],"explanation_ja":"ツール選択は名前の単語一致ではなく、入力・処理・出力の一致で決めます。地域と四半期の日付範囲を受け取り、平均注文額を返す集計関数がそのまま要件を満たします。プロフィールには注文金額がなく、最新N件では期間内の全注文を保証できません。問い合わせに不要な書き込みも行いません。「求める集計をデータ側で正確に計算する」が要点です。","distractors_ja":["プロフィールに注文金額がないため、全顧客を取得しても平均注文額は計算できません。不要な大量の個別呼び出しにもなります。","必要な絞り込みを正確に指定でき、目的の集計値を直接返します。コンテキストと計算負荷を増やさず要求に対応します。","最新N件は前四半期全体をカバーする保証がありません。取得後の絞り込みでは、そもそも取得されなかった注文を復元できません。","地域という単語が一致しても、これは顧客情報を変更するツールです。読み取り集計のタスクに書き込みは不要です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-034","number":34,"form":1,"stem":"A Databricks agent's system prompt has grown to include detailed step-by-step instructions for 15 distinct capabilities: the 3 capabilities used in over 90% of requests (checking account balance, checking order status, resetting a password) are described concisely in a few lines each, but the other 12 — covering rare scenarios like initiating a formal dispute, processing a bulk data export, or handling a regulatory data-deletion request — each include multiple paragraphs of detailed procedural instructions, edge cases, and required disclaimers. Every single request pays the token cost of all 15 capabilities' instructions, even though 12 of them are invoked less than once per thousand conversations.\n\nWhich loading strategy for these rarely-invoked capabilities minimizes baseline context cost without harming task success rate?","options":["Delete the instructions for the 12 rare capabilities entirely, since they are invoked so infrequently that supporting them well is not worth any token cost.","Keep all 15 capabilities' full instructions in the system prompt exactly as they are today, since removing any instructions risks the agent failing on a rare-but-important request.","Package each of the 12 rarely-invoked capabilities as a separate Agent Skill that is loaded into context only when the agent's task recognition indicates that specific capability is needed, keeping only the 3 high-frequency capabilities' concise instructions in the always-loaded baseline system prompt.","Move all 15 capabilities, including the 3 frequently used ones, out of the system prompt and into on-demand Agent Skills, so the baseline system prompt contains no capability instructions at all."],"correct":2,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"高頻度の基礎指示と低頻度のAgent Skills","concept":"ce-f1-q034","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Package each of the 12 rarely-invoked capabilities as a separate Agent Skill that is loaded into context only when the agent's task recognition indicates that specific capability is needed, keeping only the 3 high-frequency capabilities' concise instructions in the always-loaded baseline system prompt.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe core issue is baseline context waste. Every request currently includes lengthy procedures for 12 capabilities used in fewer than one in a thousand conversations.\n\nThe appropriate loading strategy is therefore:\n\n- Keep concise instructions for the three capabilities needed in more than 90% of requests in the always-available prompt.\n\n- Move detailed, specialized workflows into narrowly scoped Agent Skills.\n\n- Load a rare skill only when the current request matches its description.\n\nThis preserves support for formal disputes, bulk exports, and regulatory deletion requests without making ordinary balance, order-status, and password-reset requests pay their full token cost.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAgent Skills are designed to package task-specific knowledge and workflows, including:\n\n- Detailed procedures.\n\n- Examples.\n\n- Edge cases.\n\n- Required disclaimers.\n\n- Supporting templates or scripts.\n\nUnlike globally applied instructions, relevant skills can be discovered and loaded only when needed. This reduces baseline prompt size while preserving the agent’s ability to handle rare but important tasks.\n\nOption C also applies an appropriate frequency-based separation:\n\nInstruction typeLoading strategyFrequently needed and conciseAlways loadedRare, detailed, specializedLoaded on demand\n\nFor example, a normal “check my order” request would not need multiple paragraphs about regulatory identity verification, deletion exceptions, retention holds, and audit disclosures. Those instructions become relevant only when the user actually requests data deletion.\n\nEach rare capability should have a clear name and activation description, such as:\n\n```\nregulatory-data-deletion Use when a customer requests deletion, erasure, or removal of theirpersonal information under an applicable privacy regulation.\n```\n\nThis enables reliable task recognition while keeping the full procedural content outside the default context.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Delete the instructions for the 12 rare capabilities entirely, since they are invoked so infrequently that supporting them well is not worth any token cost.\n\nThis reduces tokens but harms task success for rare requests.\n\nSome infrequent capabilities—particularly disputes, privacy requests, and regulatory deletion workflows—may be high-impact or compliance-sensitive. Removing their instructions could cause the agent to:\n\n- Skip mandatory verification.\n\n- Omit required disclosures.\n\n- Take an unauthorized action.\n\n- Mishandle a legal or regulatory request.\n\nThe correct strategy is deferred loading, not permanent removal.\n\nB. Keep all 15 capabilities' full instructions in the system prompt exactly as they are today, since removing any instructions risks the agent failing on a rare-but-important request.\n\nThis preserves capability coverage but does not address the problem.\n\nIt forces every request to carry detailed instructions for tasks that are almost never invoked. This increases:\n\n- Input-token consumption.\n\n- Latency and inference cost.\n\n- Context-window pressure.\n\n- The possibility that unrelated instructions distract or conflict with the current task.\n\nAgent Skills preserve the rare capabilities without keeping their full procedures in the prompt continuously.\n\nD. Move all 15 capabilities, including the 3 frequently used ones, out of the system prompt and into on-demand Agent Skills, so the baseline system prompt contains no capability instructions at all.\n\nThis is an unnecessary overcorrection.\n\nThe three common capabilities have short instructions and are needed in more than 90% of requests. Keeping them in the baseline prompt gives the agent immediate, reliable guidance with relatively little token cost.\n\nMoving them all to on-demand skills could introduce:\n\n- An additional recognition and loading step on nearly every request.\n\n- Greater dependence on correct skill activation.\n\n- Potential failure when a common skill is not selected.\n\n- Little token benefit because those instructions are already concise.\n\nOn-demand loading is most beneficial for instructions that are both large and infrequently relevant.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming there are only two choices:\n\n- Keep every instruction loaded permanently.\n\n- Remove rare capabilities completely.\n\nAgent Skills provide a third option: retain the capabilities while loading their detailed instructions progressively when the current task requires them.\n\nAnother trap is moving everything on demand without considering frequency. Frequently used, concise guidance is often appropriate as baseline context.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is C: maintain a small high-frequency baseline and progressively load specialized skills.\n\nA production design should:\n\n- Create one focused skill per rare workflow.\n\n- Give each skill a clear activation description.\n\n- Avoid overlapping skill scopes.\n\n- Include procedural steps, edge cases, examples, and disclaimers inside the skill.\n\n- Test activation against representative and ambiguous requests.\n\n- Trace which skills were considered and loaded.\n\n- Keep critical global controls—such as security, authorization, privacy, and confirmation requirements—in the baseline prompt when they apply to every capability.\n\nThe team should measure both token savings and task success. A skill that saves context but is frequently missed by task recognition needs a clearer description or deterministic routing rule.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Extend Genie Code with agent skills\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/skills](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/skills)\n\n- Agent skills for AI coding assistants\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agent-skills/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agent-skills/)\n\n- Customize Genie Code with custom instructions\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/instructions](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/instructions)","source_option_explanations":["A. Delete the instructions for the 12 rare capabilities entirely, since they are invoked so infrequently that supporting them well is not worth any token cost.\n\nThis reduces tokens but harms task success for rare requests.\n\nSome infrequent capabilities—particularly disputes, privacy requests, and regulatory deletion workflows—may be high-impact or compliance-sensitive. Removing their instructions could cause the agent to:\n\n- Skip mandatory verification.\n\n- Omit required disclosures.\n\n- Take an unauthorized action.\n\n- Mishandle a legal or regulatory request.\n\nThe correct strategy is deferred loading, not permanent removal.","B. Keep all 15 capabilities' full instructions in the system prompt exactly as they are today, since removing any instructions risks the agent failing on a rare-but-important request.\n\nThis preserves capability coverage but does not address the problem.\n\nIt forces every request to carry detailed instructions for tasks that are almost never invoked. This increases:\n\n- Input-token consumption.\n\n- Latency and inference cost.\n\n- Context-window pressure.\n\n- The possibility that unrelated instructions distract or conflict with the current task.\n\nAgent Skills preserve the rare capabilities without keeping their full procedures in the prompt continuously.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Package each of the 12 rarely-invoked capabilities as a separate Agent Skill that is loaded into context only when the agent's task recognition indicates that specific capability is needed, keeping only the 3 high-frequency capabilities' concise instructions in the always-loaded baseline system prompt.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe core issue is baseline context waste. Every request currently includes lengthy procedures for 12 capabilities used in fewer than one in a thousand conversations.\n\nThe appropriate loading strategy is therefore:\n\n- Keep concise instructions for the three capabilities needed in more than 90% of requests in the always-available prompt.\n\n- Move detailed, specialized workflows into narrowly scoped Agent Skills.\n\n- Load a rare skill only when the current request matches its description.\n\nThis preserves support for formal disputes, bulk exports, and regulatory deletion requests without making ordinary balance, order-status, and password-reset requests pay their full token cost.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAgent Skills are designed to package task-specific knowledge and workflows, including:\n\n- Detailed procedures.\n\n- Examples.\n\n- Edge cases.\n\n- Required disclaimers.\n\n- Supporting templates or scripts.\n\nUnlike globally applied instructions, relevant skills can be discovered and loaded only when needed. This reduces baseline prompt size while preserving the agent’s ability to handle rare but important tasks.\n\nOption C also applies an appropriate frequency-based separation:\n\nInstruction typeLoading strategyFrequently needed and conciseAlways loadedRare, detailed, specializedLoaded on demand\n\nFor example, a normal “check my order” request would not need multiple paragraphs about regulatory identity verification, deletion exceptions, retention holds, and audit disclosures. Those instructions become relevant only when the user actually requests data deletion.\n\nEach rare capability should have a clear name and activation description, such as:\n\n```\nregulatory-data-deletion Use when a customer requests deletion, erasure, or removal of theirpersonal information under an applicable privacy regulation.\n```\n\nThis enables reliable task recognition while keeping the full procedural content outside the default context.","D. Move all 15 capabilities, including the 3 frequently used ones, out of the system prompt and into on-demand Agent Skills, so the baseline system prompt contains no capability instructions at all.\n\nThis is an unnecessary overcorrection.\n\nThe three common capabilities have short instructions and are needed in more than 90% of requests. Keeping them in the baseline prompt gives the agent immediate, reliable guidance with relatively little token cost.\n\nMoving them all to on-demand skills could introduce:\n\n- An additional recognition and loading step on nearly every request.\n\n- Greater dependence on correct skill activation.\n\n- Potential failure when a common skill is not selected.\n\n- Little token benefit because those instructions are already concise.\n\nOn-demand loading is most beneficial for instructions that are both large and infrequently relevant."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/skills","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agent-skills/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/instructions"],"source_hash":"08799e04881070fc1eca1b5b3a1e5450c51782995c209583e902b39cf8ae08c3","stem_ja":"Databricksエージェントのシステムプロンプトに、15種類の機能の詳しい手順が蓄積しています。要求の90%超で使う3機能（口座残高の確認、注文状況の確認、パスワードのリセット）は、それぞれ数行で簡潔に説明されています。しかし残り12機能は、正式な異議申立ての開始、一括データ書き出し、規制に基づくデータ削除要求の処理などのまれな状況を扱い、各機能に複数段落の詳細手順、境界事例、必要な免責文があります。12機能は会話1,000回あたり1回未満しか呼ばれないのに、すべての要求が15機能全体の指示のトークンコストを払っています。\n\nタスク成功率を損なわずに標準のコンテキストコストを最小化するため、まれな機能の指示をどう読み込むべきですか。","options_ja":["呼び出しが非常に少なく、十分に支援する価値がトークンコストに見合わないため、まれな12機能の指示を完全に削除する。","指示を少しでも外すと、まれでも重要な要求で失敗する危険があるため、15機能の完全な指示を現状どおりシステムプロンプトへ保持する。","まれな12機能をそれぞれ別のAgent Skillにまとめ、タスク認識で該当する機能が必要だと分かったときだけ読み込む。常時読み込む基礎プロンプトには、高頻度の3機能の簡潔な指示だけを残す。","高頻度の3機能も含め、全15機能をシステムプロンプトから必要時取得のAgent Skillsへ移し、基礎プロンプトには機能の指示を一切残さない。"],"explanation_ja":"頻繁に使う短い指示は常駐させ、まれに使う長い手順だけをSkillsとして必要時に開示します。これなら機能を捨てずに、ほとんどの要求の基礎コストを下げられます。全指示の永久削除は成功率を損ない、全機能の遅延読込は日常業務にも不要な発見処理を足します。「よく使う要点は基礎、まれな詳細はSkills」と覚えます。","distractors_ja":["低頻度でも、必要になったときの成功は維持しなければなりません。完全削除はコンテキストを節約しても機能要件を壊します。","機能を失わない点は守れますが、ほとんど使わない大量の詳細を毎回読む無駄がそのまま残ります。必要時に載せる設計が可能です。","主要3機能は軽く常駐させ、まれな詳細だけ必要時に開きます。通常コストを下げながら特殊な要求への対応力を保てます。","高頻度で短い3機能まで外すと、通常要求の大半で追加の発見・読み込みが必要になります。頻度と長さに応じて分ける方が適切です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-035","number":35,"form":1,"stem":"A context engineer oversees a long-running data-migration validation agent that iteratively tests different mapping approaches to reconcile a legacy schema with a new target schema, across dozens of tool calls. After a compaction event, the agent begins re-attempting mapping approaches it had already tried and discarded earlier in the same session, each time re-discovering the same failure after several turns before abandoning it again. Inspection of the compacted context shows the compaction prompt collapsed the session into a single summary of the current best-guess mapping and remaining unmapped fields. The compaction prompt currently reads: \"Summarize the current mapping status and the fields still unmapped.\"\n\nWhich revised compaction prompt addresses the root cause of the coherence failure without significantly increasing token cost?","options":["Summarize the current mapping status and append the complete raw tool output from every mapping attempt, successful or failed.","Summarize the current mapping status and include a full chain-of-thought transcript of the agent's reasoning during each attempt.","Summarize the current mapping status and append verbatim copies of every user message from the session.","Summarize the current mapping status, the fields still unmapped, and a deduplicated list of previously attempted mapping approaches that failed, along with the specific reason each one failed."],"correct":3,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"失敗した試行とその理由を圧縮後も残す","concept":"ce-f1-q035","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Summarize the current mapping status, the fields still unmapped, and a deduplicated list of previously attempted mapping approaches that failed, along with the specific reason each one failed.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe compaction event preserved only:\n\n- The current best mapping.\n\n- The fields that remain unmapped.\n\nIt discarded the session’s negative findings—the approaches already tested and the evidence showing why they were rejected. After compaction, those failed approaches no longer appear in the agent’s working context, so the agent treats them as unexplored possibilities and repeats them.\n\nOption D preserves the minimum additional state needed for coherent continuation:\n\n- What is currently believed to work.\n\n- What remains unresolved.\n\n- What has already been tried.\n\n- Why each rejected approach must not be retried.\n\nThis prevents repetitive exploration without retaining large tool outputs or a verbatim transcript.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA long-running iterative task needs more than its latest result. Its compacted state should preserve important decisions and constraints accumulated during the task.\n\nA suitable compacted summary might contain:\n\n```\nCurrent mapping:- legacy_customer_id → target_customer_key- legacy_region_code → target_sales_region Still unmapped:- account_status_flag- legacy_segment_code Rejected approaches:- Direct account_status_flag → is_active mapping:  Failed because values P, S, and C represent three lifecycle states,  not a Boolean active/inactive field. - legacy_segment_code lookup against customer_tier:  Failed because 18% of source codes have no matching target tier. - Deriving segment from annual_revenue alone:  Failed because the target rule also requires employee_count.\n```\n\nThis is compact but operationally useful. It acts as a record of explored paths, allowing the agent to continue from previous findings instead of repeatedly rediscovering them.\n\nDatabricks guidance for long-running agents emphasizes maintaining context and state across extended workflows, and recommends guardrails that prevent an agent from repeatedly attempting the same failing action.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Summarize the current mapping status and append the complete raw tool output from every mapping attempt, successful or failed.\n\nThis would preserve the evidence, but it defeats the purpose of compaction.\n\nRaw tool outputs may contain:\n\n- Full schemas.\n\n- Query logs.\n\n- Repeated error messages.\n\n- Diagnostic metadata.\n\n- Large sample datasets.\n\n- Irrelevant intermediate results.\n\nThe agent needs the failed approach and its decisive failure reason—not every token produced while testing it. Retaining all raw outputs would quickly recreate the original context-window problem.\n\nB. Summarize the current mapping status and include a full chain-of-thought transcript of the agent's reasoning during each attempt.\n\nA full reasoning transcript is unnecessary and highly token-intensive.\n\nThe agent needs concise, externally verifiable conclusions such as:\n\n- Approach attempted.\n\n- Result observed.\n\n- Reason rejected.\n\n- Any condition under which it could be reconsidered.\n\nIt does not need a verbatim record of every internal reasoning step. A structured decision log is more compact, auditable, and useful for continuation.\n\nC. Summarize the current mapping status and append verbatim copies of every user message from the session.\n\nThe user messages may preserve the original objective and constraints, but they do not necessarily capture the mapping experiments conducted by the agent or their results.\n\nThe repeated-work failure is caused by losing the history of rejected approaches, not by losing every user utterance. Copying all user messages also wastes tokens when a concise task-and-constraints summary would be sufficient.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is summarizing only the agent’s current positive conclusion.\n\nFor iterative research and troubleshooting, discarded hypotheses are also important state. Without a compact record of failed approaches and failure reasons, compaction can erase the evidence that prevents the agent from repeating unproductive work.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is D.\n\nA strong compaction prompt for long-running validation tasks would be:\n\nSummarize the current objective, confirmed mappings, unresolved fields, important constraints, and next recommended actions. Include a deduplicated decision log of previously attempted approaches that were rejected, stating the specific evidence or failure reason for each. Preserve identifiers, values, and assumptions needed to avoid repeating completed work. Omit verbose logs and redundant intermediate output.\n\nIn production, the rejected-approach history can also be stored as structured durable state:\n\n```\nattempt_idfield_nameapproachstatusfailure_reasonevidence_referenceretry_conditiontimestamp\n```\n\nThat makes retries, deduplication, auditing, and continuation more reliable than relying exclusively on a free-form conversation summary.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Supervisor Agent long-running tasks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor-long-running-tasks](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor-long-running-tasks)\n\n- Agent state and memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)\n\n- Concepts: Generative AI on Databricks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/concepts/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/concepts/)\n\n- LangChain built-in middleware — Summarization\n[https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/middleware/built-in](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/middleware/built-in)","source_option_explanations":["A. Summarize the current mapping status and append the complete raw tool output from every mapping attempt, successful or failed.\n\nThis would preserve the evidence, but it defeats the purpose of compaction.\n\nRaw tool outputs may contain:\n\n- Full schemas.\n\n- Query logs.\n\n- Repeated error messages.\n\n- Diagnostic metadata.\n\n- Large sample datasets.\n\n- Irrelevant intermediate results.\n\nThe agent needs the failed approach and its decisive failure reason—not every token produced while testing it. Retaining all raw outputs would quickly recreate the original context-window problem.","B. Summarize the current mapping status and include a full chain-of-thought transcript of the agent's reasoning during each attempt.\n\nA full reasoning transcript is unnecessary and highly token-intensive.\n\nThe agent needs concise, externally verifiable conclusions such as:\n\n- Approach attempted.\n\n- Result observed.\n\n- Reason rejected.\n\n- Any condition under which it could be reconsidered.\n\nIt does not need a verbatim record of every internal reasoning step. A structured decision log is more compact, auditable, and useful for continuation.","C. Summarize the current mapping status and append verbatim copies of every user message from the session.\n\nThe user messages may preserve the original objective and constraints, but they do not necessarily capture the mapping experiments conducted by the agent or their results.\n\nThe repeated-work failure is caused by losing the history of rejected approaches, not by losing every user utterance. Copying all user messages also wastes tokens when a concise task-and-constraints summary would be sufficient.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Summarize the current mapping status, the fields still unmapped, and a deduplicated list of previously attempted mapping approaches that failed, along with the specific reason each one failed.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe compaction event preserved only:\n\n- The current best mapping.\n\n- The fields that remain unmapped.\n\nIt discarded the session’s negative findings—the approaches already tested and the evidence showing why they were rejected. After compaction, those failed approaches no longer appear in the agent’s working context, so the agent treats them as unexplored possibilities and repeats them.\n\nOption D preserves the minimum additional state needed for coherent continuation:\n\n- What is currently believed to work.\n\n- What remains unresolved.\n\n- What has already been tried.\n\n- Why each rejected approach must not be retried.\n\nThis prevents repetitive exploration without retaining large tool outputs or a verbatim transcript.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA long-running iterative task needs more than its latest result. Its compacted state should preserve important decisions and constraints accumulated during the task.\n\nA suitable compacted summary might contain:\n\n```\nCurrent mapping:- legacy_customer_id → target_customer_key- legacy_region_code → target_sales_region Still unmapped:- account_status_flag- legacy_segment_code Rejected approaches:- Direct account_status_flag → is_active mapping:  Failed because values P, S, and C represent three lifecycle states,  not a Boolean active/inactive field. - legacy_segment_code lookup against customer_tier:  Failed because 18% of source codes have no matching target tier. - Deriving segment from annual_revenue alone:  Failed because the target rule also requires employee_count.\n```\n\nThis is compact but operationally useful. It acts as a record of explored paths, allowing the agent to continue from previous findings instead of repeatedly rediscovering them.\n\nDatabricks guidance for long-running agents emphasizes maintaining context and state across extended workflows, and recommends guardrails that prevent an agent from repeatedly attempting the same failing action."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor-long-running-tasks","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/concepts/","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/middleware/built-in"],"source_hash":"1b69818687598d90cd0d9847ace06268c779d2899c477d2c4ae60fd67873f68d","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、数十回のツール呼び出しにわたって異なるマッピング方法を反復試験し、旧スキーマと新しい移行先スキーマを整合させる、長時間のデータ移行検証エージェントを管理しています。圧縮後、エージェントは同じセッションの前半ですでに試して却下したマッピング方法を再試行し始めました。そのたびに数ターンかけて同じ失敗を再発見し、また断念します。圧縮後のコンテキストを調べると、現在最もよさそうなマッピングと未対応フィールドだけの単一要約にまとめられていました。現在の圧縮プロンプトは「現在のマッピング状況と、まだマッピングされていないフィールドを要約してください」です。\n\nトークン費用を大きく増やさず、この一貫性の障害の根本原因に対処する改訂プロンプトはどれですか。","options_ja":["現在のマッピング状況を要約し、成功・失敗にかかわらず、すべてのマッピング試行の完全な生ツール出力を付けてください。","現在のマッピング状況を要約し、各試行でのエージェントの推論の完全な思考連鎖トランスクリプトを含めてください。","現在のマッピング状況を要約し、セッションのすべての利用者メッセージを一字一句そのまま付けてください。","現在のマッピング状況、まだマッピングされていないフィールド、過去に試して失敗したマッピング方法の重複を除いた一覧と、それぞれが失敗した具体的な理由を要約してください。"],"explanation_ja":"現在の最善案だけを残すと、「どの案がなぜ失敗したか」という負の知識が消え、同じ探索を繰り返します。却下した方法と理由を重複なく短く記録すれば、全ツール出力や推論履歴を残さずに再試行を防げます。覚え方は「成功した現在地に加え、進んではいけない道も残す」です。","distractors_ja":["全出力なら失敗情報も残りますが、不要な生データが大量に増え、トークンを大きく増やさない条件に合いません。","必要なのは失敗した方法と理由であり、完全な推論履歴ではありません。長大な内部過程の保存は過剰です。","利用者の指示を残しても、ツール試験から得た失敗方法とその理由がなければ、同じ試行を繰り返す原因は残ります。","重複のない失敗一覧と具体的理由が、再試行を避けるための情報を少ないトークンで保持します。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-036","number":36,"form":1,"stem":"A team is tuning the compaction prompt for a customer-support agent's long conversation histories. Their first version aggressively distilled each conversation into a single sentence per topic. After deploying this version, downstream response quality dropped: the agent began missing details it needed to answer accurate follow-up questions, because those details had been compressed away. The team wants to correct course using an appropriate tuning process, without immediately reverting to zero compaction.\n\nWhich iteration approach should the team follow to tune the compaction prompt effectively?","options":["Immediately optimize for precision by trimming the current summary format even further, since a shorter summary is always a safe next step after any observed quality drop.","First revise the compaction prompt to maximize recall by capturing everything plausibly relevant to downstream tasks, even at the cost of some added length, and validate that downstream task success recovers; only then iterate to improve precision by removing content shown through testing to be genuinely superfluous.","Keep the original overly aggressive compaction prompt unchanged, and instead compensate by increasing the model's context window so any missed details matter less.","Skip iterative tuning entirely and disable compaction altogether, since any compaction prompt that has previously caused a quality drop can never be trusted going forward."],"correct":1,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"圧縮調整は情報保持を先に検証する","concept":"ce-f1-q036","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. First revise the compaction prompt to maximize recall by capturing everything plausibly relevant to downstream tasks, even at the cost of some added length, and validate that downstream task success recovers; only then iterate to improve precision by removing content shown through testing to be genuinely superfluous.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe quality drop occurred because the compaction prompt was too aggressive. It produced shorter summaries but removed details required for accurate follow-up answers.\n\nThe correct tuning sequence is:\n\n- Restore sufficient information coverage.\n\n- Verify that downstream task success recovers.\n\n- Gradually remove only content demonstrated to be unnecessary.\n\n- Re-evaluate after every prompt revision.\n\nThis is a recall-first, precision-second approach. When essential information has already been lost, further shortening would make the failure worse. MLflow evaluation is designed to support iterative comparison of prompt and application versions against consistent test data and quality metrics.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA useful compacted summary must retain enough information to support future turns, including:\n\n- Current customer issue and status.\n\n- Important dates, amounts, identifiers, and commitments.\n\n- Actions already performed.\n\n- Failed troubleshooting attempts.\n\n- Unresolved questions.\n\n- Customer preferences and constraints.\n\n- Relevant tool results and their conclusions.\n\n- Decisions that should not be reversed without new evidence.\n\nThe team should first broaden the compaction prompt so it captures everything that could materially affect later support tasks. The resulting summary may initially be longer, but it establishes whether information loss was the cause of the quality regression.\n\nOnce task success has recovered, the team can improve precision by removing repeated, irrelevant, or low-value details through measured experiments. Recall measures whether necessary information is preserved, while precision concerns how much retained context is genuinely useful rather than distracting. Databricks exposes recall- and precision-oriented evaluation scorers for assessing context quality.\n\nA suitable revised prompt could be:\n\nSummarize the conversation while preserving all facts, decisions, actions, identifiers, dates, amounts, customer constraints, unresolved issues, failed attempts, and tool-result conclusions that could affect future responses. Remove greetings, repetition, and details that have no plausible downstream relevance.\n\nAfter validating recovery, the team can progressively tighten this prompt rather than immediately returning to highly lossy one-sentence summaries.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Immediately optimize for precision by trimming the current summary format even further, since a shorter summary is always a safe next step after any observed quality drop.\n\nThis moves in the wrong direction.\n\nThe current failure is missing necessary detail. Further shortening would likely decrease recall even more and cause additional follow-up failures.\n\nShorter context is not automatically better. The goal is the smallest summary that still preserves all information required for downstream success.\n\nC. Keep the original overly aggressive compaction prompt unchanged, and instead compensate by increasing the model's context window so any missed details matter less.\n\nA larger context window cannot recover details that the compaction process has already discarded.\n\nOnce older messages are permanently replaced by an incomplete summary, the model sees only that incomplete representation. The team must correct the summarization behavior itself.\n\nContext-window size and compaction quality are separate concerns:\n\n- A larger window delays when compaction becomes necessary.\n\n- A better compaction prompt determines what information survives after compaction.\n\nD. Skip iterative tuning entirely and disable compaction altogether, since any compaction prompt that has previously caused a quality drop can never be trusted going forward.\n\nThis is an unnecessary overcorrection.\n\nLong customer-support conversations can eventually exceed model context limits or incur excessive latency and token costs. Summarization middleware exists specifically to compress older history while retaining relevant conversational context.\n\nThe failure demonstrates that the current prompt needs refinement—not that compaction is inherently unusable.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is optimizing immediately for the smallest possible summary.\n\nWhen compression has already caused lost information, the first priority is restoring recall and task success. Precision and token reduction should be optimized only after the summary reliably preserves necessary context.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is B: tune compaction iteratively, beginning with a high-recall summary and then carefully improving precision.\n\nA practical evaluation process would be:\n\n- Build a representative dataset of long support conversations and follow-up questions.\n\n- Run the current compaction prompt as the baseline.\n\n- Create a higher-recall version that preserves all potentially consequential facts.\n\n- Measure downstream correctness, task success, token count, and latency.\n\n- Inspect failed traces to identify omitted information.\n\n- Remove one category of apparently unnecessary content at a time.\n\n- Re-run the same evaluation set after every change.\n\n- Deploy only when quality thresholds remain satisfied.\n\nThe team should version the compaction prompts and compare them using the same evaluation dataset. MLflow supports modifying prompts, rerunning evaluations, and comparing resulting quality across application or prompt versions.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Evaluate and monitor AI agents\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor)\n\n- Tutorial: Evaluate and improve a GenAI application\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app)\n\n- Building MLflow evaluation datasets\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset)\n\n- RAGAS scorers\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/third-party-scorers/ragas](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/third-party-scorers/ragas)","source_option_explanations":["A. Immediately optimize for precision by trimming the current summary format even further, since a shorter summary is always a safe next step after any observed quality drop.\n\nThis moves in the wrong direction.\n\nThe current failure is missing necessary detail. Further shortening would likely decrease recall even more and cause additional follow-up failures.\n\nShorter context is not automatically better. The goal is the smallest summary that still preserves all information required for downstream success.","Correct Answer\n\nB. First revise the compaction prompt to maximize recall by capturing everything plausibly relevant to downstream tasks, even at the cost of some added length, and validate that downstream task success recovers; only then iterate to improve precision by removing content shown through testing to be genuinely superfluous.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe quality drop occurred because the compaction prompt was too aggressive. It produced shorter summaries but removed details required for accurate follow-up answers.\n\nThe correct tuning sequence is:\n\n- Restore sufficient information coverage.\n\n- Verify that downstream task success recovers.\n\n- Gradually remove only content demonstrated to be unnecessary.\n\n- Re-evaluate after every prompt revision.\n\nThis is a recall-first, precision-second approach. When essential information has already been lost, further shortening would make the failure worse. MLflow evaluation is designed to support iterative comparison of prompt and application versions against consistent test data and quality metrics.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA useful compacted summary must retain enough information to support future turns, including:\n\n- Current customer issue and status.\n\n- Important dates, amounts, identifiers, and commitments.\n\n- Actions already performed.\n\n- Failed troubleshooting attempts.\n\n- Unresolved questions.\n\n- Customer preferences and constraints.\n\n- Relevant tool results and their conclusions.\n\n- Decisions that should not be reversed without new evidence.\n\nThe team should first broaden the compaction prompt so it captures everything that could materially affect later support tasks. The resulting summary may initially be longer, but it establishes whether information loss was the cause of the quality regression.\n\nOnce task success has recovered, the team can improve precision by removing repeated, irrelevant, or low-value details through measured experiments. Recall measures whether necessary information is preserved, while precision concerns how much retained context is genuinely useful rather than distracting. Databricks exposes recall- and precision-oriented evaluation scorers for assessing context quality.\n\nA suitable revised prompt could be:\n\nSummarize the conversation while preserving all facts, decisions, actions, identifiers, dates, amounts, customer constraints, unresolved issues, failed attempts, and tool-result conclusions that could affect future responses. Remove greetings, repetition, and details that have no plausible downstream relevance.\n\nAfter validating recovery, the team can progressively tighten this prompt rather than immediately returning to highly lossy one-sentence summaries.","C. Keep the original overly aggressive compaction prompt unchanged, and instead compensate by increasing the model's context window so any missed details matter less.\n\nA larger context window cannot recover details that the compaction process has already discarded.\n\nOnce older messages are permanently replaced by an incomplete summary, the model sees only that incomplete representation. The team must correct the summarization behavior itself.\n\nContext-window size and compaction quality are separate concerns:\n\n- A larger window delays when compaction becomes necessary.\n\n- A better compaction prompt determines what information survives after compaction.","D. Skip iterative tuning entirely and disable compaction altogether, since any compaction prompt that has previously caused a quality drop can never be trusted going forward.\n\nThis is an unnecessary overcorrection.\n\nLong customer-support conversations can eventually exceed model context limits or incur excessive latency and token costs. Summarization middleware exists specifically to compress older history while retaining relevant conversational context.\n\nThe failure demonstrates that the current prompt needs refinement—not that compaction is inherently unusable."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/third-party-scorers/ragas"],"source_hash":"c0c1a655bdc38da1b022a0914cedae006e397521342d3c1eef064fdcc9c681e3","stem_ja":"チームが、顧客サポートエージェントの長い会話履歴をまとめる圧縮プロンプトを調整しています。最初の版は各話題を1文に強く凝縮しました。この版を導入すると下流の応答品質が下がり、正確な追加質問へ答えるために必要な詳細が圧縮で失われ、エージェントがそれを見落とし始めました。チームはすぐに圧縮をゼロへ戻すのではなく、適切な調整プロセスで軌道修正したいと考えています。\n\n効果的に圧縮プロンプトを調整するには、どの反復方針に従うべきですか。","options_ja":["品質低下を観測した後でも、短い要約は常に安全な次の手順なので、現在の要約形式をさらに削って適合率を直ちに最適化します。","まず圧縮プロンプトを修正して再現率を最大化し、多少長くても下流タスクに関係しそうな内容をすべて捉え、タスク成功率の回復を検証します。その後に初めて、試験で本当に不要と分かった内容を除き、適合率を改善します。","元の強すぎる圧縮プロンプトは変えず、代わりにモデルのコンテキストウィンドウを拡大し、失われた詳細の影響を小さくします。","一度品質低下を起こした圧縮プロンプトは今後決して信頼できないため、反復調整を省き、圧縮を完全に無効化します。"],"explanation_ja":"品質低下は、必要な情報まで削ったことを示します。まず必要な詳細を残す再現率を優先し、後続タスクが再び成功するか試します。次に不要部分だけを少しずつ削り、毎回検証します。短さを最適化する前に、要約から正しく作業を続けられることが前提です。","distractors_ja":["必要情報の欠落が原因なのにさらに短くすると、失敗を悪化させるおそれがあります。まず保持を回復します。","必要な情報を残して成功を確認した後に余分な内容を削るため、忠実性と効率を順に改善できます。","元のプロンプトが情報を捨てるままでは、受け入れ先の容量が増えても捨てられた詳細は復元されません。","一度の失敗は圧縮全体を放棄する根拠ではありません。代表的な継続タスクで検証しながら調整できます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-037","number":37,"form":1,"stem":"A long-running research agent investigates a scientific question by making sequential searches, and it established a key overarching finding in turn 4 of a 200-turn session that remains critical to every subsequent step. Much of the content added in turns 150–190 consists of dead-end searches and tool errors that turned out to be irrelevant to the final analysis. The team is considering a simple, hard-coded trimming heuristic: drop the oldest N turns whenever the context exceeds a fixed token threshold, keeping only the most recent messages.\n\nGiven this task's information relevance pattern, is the hard-coded recency-based trimming heuristic sufficient, or does the task require a more sophisticated compaction approach?","options":["The heuristic is sufficient, since recency is always the best proxy for relevance in any long-running agent task, regardless of when key findings were established.","The heuristic is sufficient as long as the fixed token threshold is set high enough, since any threshold that is generous enough will naturally avoid dropping anything important.","The heuristic is insufficient, but the fix is simply to never drop any messages at all, regardless of token cost, rather than adopting any more sophisticated approach.","The heuristic is insufficient, because relevance in this task does not correlate with recency — the turn-4 finding remains critical at turn 200, while some of the most recent turns (150–190) are low-value; a more sophisticated, relevance-aware compaction approach that preserves key findings regardless of position while discarding low-value recent content is needed instead."],"correct":3,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"古い重要情報を守る関連性ベースの圧縮","concept":"ce-f1-q037","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. The heuristic is insufficient, because relevance in this task does not correlate with recency — the turn-4 finding remains critical at turn 200, while some of the most recent turns (150–190) are low-value; a more sophisticated, relevance-aware compaction approach that preserves key findings regardless of position while discarding low-value recent content is needed instead.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe proposed heuristic assumes that newer messages are more valuable than older messages. The scenario explicitly disproves that assumption:\n\n- A critical finding from turn 4 remains essential throughout the investigation.\n\n- Many much newer turns contain dead ends and tool failures with little continuing value.\n\n- Dropping the oldest messages first would remove high-value evidence while retaining low-value recent content.\n\nThe agent therefore needs compaction based on semantic and task relevance, not message age alone. The compacted state should preserve important findings, decisions, evidence, unresolved questions, and rejected approaches regardless of where they appeared in the transcript.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nSimple recency trimming can be appropriate when recent conversation turns reliably contain the most useful context. It is not appropriate when long-running research produces durable findings early and irrelevant experimentation later.\n\nA suitable compaction result would preserve information such as:\n\n```\nResearch objective:- Determine whether hypothesis X is supported. Confirmed overarching finding:- Finding established in turn 4, including its evidence and source. Important supporting evidence:- Sources and conclusions that remain relevant. Rejected approaches:- Dead-end searches and why they were rejected. Open questions:- Items that still require investigation. Next actions:- Highest-value searches or analyses to perform.\n```\n\nVerbose errors, redundant searches, and dead-end outputs can be removed or summarized briefly. However, the turn-4 finding must survive compaction because later reasoning depends on it.\n\nLangChain documents trimming and summarization as different context-management strategies and warns that directly removing messages can lose information. It also supports summarization and custom filtering strategies when straightforward trimming is insufficient.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. The heuristic is sufficient, since recency is always the best proxy for relevance in any long-running agent task, regardless of when key findings were established.\n\nRecency is not always correlated with relevance.\n\nIn this scenario, the oldest key finding is more important than many recent turns. A policy that always retains newer content would preserve irrelevant errors while deleting a foundational conclusion.\n\nWords such as “always” and “regardless” are warning signs because context-management strategies must reflect the task’s information pattern.\n\nB. The heuristic is sufficient as long as the fixed token threshold is set high enough, since any threshold that is generous enough will naturally avoid dropping anything important.\n\nA larger threshold only delays the failure.\n\nIn an open-ended research session, the transcript can eventually exceed any fixed context budget. When trimming finally occurs, an oldest-first policy can still delete the critical turn-4 finding.\n\nThe problem is therefore not merely when trimming starts; it is how the system decides what to preserve.\n\nC. The heuristic is insufficient, but the fix is simply to never drop any messages at all, regardless of token cost, rather than adopting any more sophisticated approach.\n\nKeeping everything indefinitely is not viable because model context windows are finite, and growing message histories increase token use and processing cost.\n\nThe correct response is selective compaction: retain durable, decision-relevant information while removing or compressing redundant and low-value content. Summarization middleware exists specifically to replace older history with a compact representation when conversations approach model limits.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is treating recency as equivalent to relevance.\n\nKeeping the latest turns is easy to implement, but chronological position does not indicate whether information remains necessary. Long-running research often contains:\n\n- Early foundational discoveries.\n\n- Important decisions made midway through the task.\n\n- Recent but irrelevant retries, errors, or dead ends.\n\nCompaction must preserve information according to its downstream importance.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is D: use relevance-aware compaction rather than unconditional oldest-first trimming.\n\nA production implementation should preserve:\n\n- The original objective and constraints.\n\n- Confirmed findings and supporting evidence.\n\n- Important source references.\n\n- Decisions and assumptions.\n\n- Rejected hypotheses with concise failure reasons.\n\n- Unresolved questions and next steps.\n\nIt should remove or reduce:\n\n- Repeated tool errors.\n\n- Duplicate searches.\n\n- Raw outputs whose conclusions were already captured.\n\n- Superseded working hypotheses.\n\n- Dead-end details that no longer affect future reasoning.\n\nFor especially important findings, the agent should also persist structured research state outside the prompt. Databricks supports durable agent state and memory through Lakebase, allowing key information to survive context replacement, process restarts, and extended workflows.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- LangChain short-term memory — Trim, delete, and summarize messages\n[https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/short-term-memory](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/short-term-memory)\n\n- LangGraph memory — Message trimming, summarization, and custom strategies\n[https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/add-memory](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/add-memory)\n\n- LangChain context engineering\n[https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/context-engineering](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/context-engineering)\n\n- Databricks agent state and memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)","source_option_explanations":["A. The heuristic is sufficient, since recency is always the best proxy for relevance in any long-running agent task, regardless of when key findings were established.\n\nRecency is not always correlated with relevance.\n\nIn this scenario, the oldest key finding is more important than many recent turns. A policy that always retains newer content would preserve irrelevant errors while deleting a foundational conclusion.\n\nWords such as “always” and “regardless” are warning signs because context-management strategies must reflect the task’s information pattern.","B. The heuristic is sufficient as long as the fixed token threshold is set high enough, since any threshold that is generous enough will naturally avoid dropping anything important.\n\nA larger threshold only delays the failure.\n\nIn an open-ended research session, the transcript can eventually exceed any fixed context budget. When trimming finally occurs, an oldest-first policy can still delete the critical turn-4 finding.\n\nThe problem is therefore not merely when trimming starts; it is how the system decides what to preserve.","C. The heuristic is insufficient, but the fix is simply to never drop any messages at all, regardless of token cost, rather than adopting any more sophisticated approach.\n\nKeeping everything indefinitely is not viable because model context windows are finite, and growing message histories increase token use and processing cost.\n\nThe correct response is selective compaction: retain durable, decision-relevant information while removing or compressing redundant and low-value content. Summarization middleware exists specifically to replace older history with a compact representation when conversations approach model limits.","Correct Answer\n\nD. The heuristic is insufficient, because relevance in this task does not correlate with recency — the turn-4 finding remains critical at turn 200, while some of the most recent turns (150–190) are low-value; a more sophisticated, relevance-aware compaction approach that preserves key findings regardless of position while discarding low-value recent content is needed instead.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe proposed heuristic assumes that newer messages are more valuable than older messages. The scenario explicitly disproves that assumption:\n\n- A critical finding from turn 4 remains essential throughout the investigation.\n\n- Many much newer turns contain dead ends and tool failures with little continuing value.\n\n- Dropping the oldest messages first would remove high-value evidence while retaining low-value recent content.\n\nThe agent therefore needs compaction based on semantic and task relevance, not message age alone. The compacted state should preserve important findings, decisions, evidence, unresolved questions, and rejected approaches regardless of where they appeared in the transcript.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nSimple recency trimming can be appropriate when recent conversation turns reliably contain the most useful context. It is not appropriate when long-running research produces durable findings early and irrelevant experimentation later.\n\nA suitable compaction result would preserve information such as:\n\n```\nResearch objective:- Determine whether hypothesis X is supported. Confirmed overarching finding:- Finding established in turn 4, including its evidence and source. Important supporting evidence:- Sources and conclusions that remain relevant. Rejected approaches:- Dead-end searches and why they were rejected. Open questions:- Items that still require investigation. Next actions:- Highest-value searches or analyses to perform.\n```\n\nVerbose errors, redundant searches, and dead-end outputs can be removed or summarized briefly. However, the turn-4 finding must survive compaction because later reasoning depends on it.\n\nLangChain documents trimming and summarization as different context-management strategies and warns that directly removing messages can lose information. It also supports summarization and custom filtering strategies when straightforward trimming is insufficient."],"references":["https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/short-term-memory","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/add-memory","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/context-engineering","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management"],"source_hash":"6185a041f9f6c1355b12d8b3ebbc463e2cd925f7e41f65c0fa0b41e438db828b","stem_ja":"長時間動作する調査エージェントが、逐次検索によって科学的な問いを調べています。200ターンのセッションの4ターン目に全体を支配する重要な発見があり、それは以後すべての手順で不可欠です。一方、150～190ターン目に追加された内容の多くは、最終分析には無関係と分かった行き詰まりの検索やツールエラーです。チームは、コンテキストが固定トークン閾値を超えるたび、古いNターンを捨て、最近のメッセージだけを残す単純な固定トリミングの規則を検討しています。\n\nこのタスクの情報関連性のパターンを踏まえると、この新しさ基準の固定トリミングで十分ですか。それとも、より高度な圧縮が必要ですか。","options_ja":["十分です。重要な発見がいつ得られたかに関係なく、どんな長期タスクでも新しさが常に関連性の最良の代替指標だからです。","固定トークン閾値を十分高くすれば十分です。余裕のある閾値なら、重要なものを自然にすべて残せるためです。","不十分ですが、修正は高度な方法を導入することでなく、トークン費用によらずメッセージを一切捨てないことです。","不十分です。4ターン目の発見が200ターン目にも不可欠な一方、最近の150～190ターン目には低価値情報があり、関連性は新しさに比例しません。位置によらず重要発見を残し、低価値の最近情報を捨てる、関連性を考慮した高度な圧縮が必要です。"],"explanation_ja":"メッセージの新しさは、将来の仕事への重要性を表しません。全体の前提となる初期の発見は保持し、最近でも不要と分かった探索履歴は除きます。固定閾値を上げるだけでは、さらに会話が長くなると同じ欠落が起きます。必要なのは時刻でなく内容に基づく選別です。","distractors_ja":["設問では古い発見が今も重要で、最近の内容の多くは不要です。新しさと関連性が一致する前提が崩れています。","閾値を増やしても長期的には超過し、初期発見を削る同じ問題が再発します。重要性を識別する仕組みが必要です。","すべて保持すれば容量と費用が増え続けます。重要情報を選んで保持し、低価値情報を除く方法が適切です。","発見の持続的価値と不要な最近情報を区別するため、容量を制限しつつ必要な継続性を保てます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-038","number":38,"form":1,"stem":"A Databricks agent trace being considered for compaction includes: (1) an error message from a failed API call in turn 12, followed immediately in turn 13 by the agent successfully retrying with corrected parameters and receiving a valid response that is still being used; (2) the agent's final synthesized answer to the user's original question, delivered in the most recent turn; (3) the original user question stated in turn 1, which the agent is still actively working to answer; (4) authoritative data retrieved in turn 45 that the agent has relied on in all of its subsequent reasoning.\n\nWhich content is safe to remove during compaction without affecting downstream task execution?","options":["The original user question from turn 1, since it is the oldest message and the agent already has a plan in place.","The turn-12 failed API call's error message and raw failure details, since the issue was fully resolved by the successful retry in turn 13 and no subsequent reasoning depends on the specifics of the original failure.","The agent's final synthesized answer, since once delivered to the user it no longer serves any function in the ongoing trace.","The turn-45 authoritative data, since the agent has stopped needing new information at this stage of the task."],"correct":1,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"解決済みエラーの削除と依存情報の保持","concept":"ce-f1-q038","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. The turn-12 failed API call's error message and raw failure details, since the issue was fully resolved by the successful retry in turn 13 and no subsequent reasoning depends on the specifics of the original failure.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nSafe compaction removes information that is:\n\n- No longer needed for future reasoning.\n\n- Superseded by a successful result.\n\n- Not referenced by subsequent steps.\n\n- Redundant or reproducible.\n\nThe failed API call satisfies all these conditions. The parameters were corrected, the retry succeeded, and the valid result remains available and actively used.\n\nThe other three items remain task-relevant:\n\n- The original question defines the agent’s objective.\n\n- The authoritative data supports subsequent reasoning.\n\n- The synthesized answer records the agent’s conclusion and what was communicated to the user.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe turn-12 error represents a resolved intermediate failure rather than durable task knowledge. Once the successful turn-13 response is retained, preserving verbose exception text, invalid parameters, stack traces, and raw failure metadata provides little downstream value.\n\nThe compacted context can retain a short note when operationally useful:\n\nInitial API request failed because of incorrect parameters; the corrected retry succeeded, and its response is authoritative.\n\nThis preserves relevant execution history without paying the token cost of the complete error dump.\n\nContext-management middleware supports trimming, summarizing, and clearing tool results when conversations approach their limits. The purpose is to preserve useful conversational state while removing redundant details.\n\nA practical caution is that tool messages often have structural relationships: an assistant tool-call message is followed by its corresponding tool-result message. The compaction implementation should remove or replace the complete failed-call sequence consistently rather than leaving an orphaned tool call or result.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. The original user question from turn 1, since it is the oldest message and the agent already has a plan in place.\n\nThis is not safe to remove merely because it is old.\n\nThe original question defines the task’s objective, scope, constraints, and expected outcome. A plan may omit important requirements or may have evolved incorrectly during execution.\n\nIf the original wording must be removed for token reasons, its essential requirements should first be captured faithfully in a compact task summary. Age alone is not a valid measure of relevance.\n\nC. The agent's final synthesized answer, since once delivered to the user it no longer serves any function in the ongoing trace.\n\nThe synthesized answer records the conclusion already reached and communicated. Removing it could cause the agent to:\n\n- Repeat completed work.\n\n- Produce an inconsistent conclusion.\n\n- Forget what was already communicated.\n\n- Mismanage a follow-up that refers to the previous answer.\n\nIt may eventually be summarized, but it should not be discarded without preserving its material conclusions.\n\nD. The turn-45 authoritative data, since the agent has stopped needing new information at this stage of the task.\n\nThe scenario explicitly states that the agent has relied on this data throughout its subsequent reasoning.\n\nThe fact that no new information is currently needed does not make existing supporting evidence irrelevant. Removing it could weaken verification, citations, follow-up analysis, or correction of the synthesized answer.\n\nThe authoritative data should remain available or be replaced with a high-fidelity summary that preserves the exact facts, source, and provenance.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is treating compaction as chronological deletion.\n\nThe oldest content is not automatically expendable, and the most recent content is not automatically essential. Safe removal depends on current relevance, redundancy, whether the information has been superseded, and whether its important conclusions have been preserved elsewhere.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe expected exam answer is B.\n\nA reliable compaction policy should classify trace content into categories:\n\nContentTreatmentResolved tool error superseded by successRemove or summarize brieflySuccessful result still in usePreserveOriginal task requirementsPreserve or summarize faithfullyAuthoritative supporting evidencePreserve with provenanceFinal decisions and communicated conclusionsPreserve in compact formRepeated logs and unused diagnosticsRemove\n\nFor important workflows, the authoritative task state should also be checkpointed outside the prompt. Databricks supports persistent agent state so relevant context can survive long conversations, process restarts, and session continuation.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent state and memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)\n\n- Self-managed agent memory with Lakebase\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory)\n\n- LangGraph memory — Trim, delete, and summarize messages\n[https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/add-memory](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/add-memory)\n\n- LangChain prebuilt middleware — Context editing and summarization\n[https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/middleware/built-in](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/middleware/built-in)","source_option_explanations":["A. The original user question from turn 1, since it is the oldest message and the agent already has a plan in place.\n\nThis is not safe to remove merely because it is old.\n\nThe original question defines the task’s objective, scope, constraints, and expected outcome. A plan may omit important requirements or may have evolved incorrectly during execution.\n\nIf the original wording must be removed for token reasons, its essential requirements should first be captured faithfully in a compact task summary. Age alone is not a valid measure of relevance.","Correct Answer\n\nB. The turn-12 failed API call's error message and raw failure details, since the issue was fully resolved by the successful retry in turn 13 and no subsequent reasoning depends on the specifics of the original failure.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nSafe compaction removes information that is:\n\n- No longer needed for future reasoning.\n\n- Superseded by a successful result.\n\n- Not referenced by subsequent steps.\n\n- Redundant or reproducible.\n\nThe failed API call satisfies all these conditions. The parameters were corrected, the retry succeeded, and the valid result remains available and actively used.\n\nThe other three items remain task-relevant:\n\n- The original question defines the agent’s objective.\n\n- The authoritative data supports subsequent reasoning.\n\n- The synthesized answer records the agent’s conclusion and what was communicated to the user.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe turn-12 error represents a resolved intermediate failure rather than durable task knowledge. Once the successful turn-13 response is retained, preserving verbose exception text, invalid parameters, stack traces, and raw failure metadata provides little downstream value.\n\nThe compacted context can retain a short note when operationally useful:\n\nInitial API request failed because of incorrect parameters; the corrected retry succeeded, and its response is authoritative.\n\nThis preserves relevant execution history without paying the token cost of the complete error dump.\n\nContext-management middleware supports trimming, summarizing, and clearing tool results when conversations approach their limits. The purpose is to preserve useful conversational state while removing redundant details.\n\nA practical caution is that tool messages often have structural relationships: an assistant tool-call message is followed by its corresponding tool-result message. The compaction implementation should remove or replace the complete failed-call sequence consistently rather than leaving an orphaned tool call or result.","C. The agent's final synthesized answer, since once delivered to the user it no longer serves any function in the ongoing trace.\n\nThe synthesized answer records the conclusion already reached and communicated. Removing it could cause the agent to:\n\n- Repeat completed work.\n\n- Produce an inconsistent conclusion.\n\n- Forget what was already communicated.\n\n- Mismanage a follow-up that refers to the previous answer.\n\nIt may eventually be summarized, but it should not be discarded without preserving its material conclusions.","D. The turn-45 authoritative data, since the agent has stopped needing new information at this stage of the task.\n\nThe scenario explicitly states that the agent has relied on this data throughout its subsequent reasoning.\n\nThe fact that no new information is currently needed does not make existing supporting evidence irrelevant. Removing it could weaken verification, citations, follow-up analysis, or correction of the synthesized answer.\n\nThe authoritative data should remain available or be replaced with a high-fidelity summary that preserves the exact facts, source, and provenance."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/add-memory","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/middleware/built-in"],"source_hash":"8afd92aa46821780c2423bd9e7d592d4f72aca33a82b5e491eb0a65c915c81d5","stem_ja":"圧縮を検討しているDatabricksエージェントのトレースには、（1）12ターン目の失敗したAPI呼び出しのエラーと、直後の13ターン目にパラメータを修正して再試行に成功し、現在も使われる有効な応答を得た記録、（2）直近ターンで利用者の元の質問へ返した、最終的に統合した回答、（3）エージェントが現在も回答に取り組んでいる1ターン目の利用者の元の質問、（4）45ターン目に取得し、それ以後のすべての推論で参照してきた正当なデータ、があります。\n\n下流タスクの実行へ影響せず、圧縮時に安全に除ける内容はどれですか。","options_ja":["1ターン目の元の利用者質問。最も古いメッセージで、エージェントにはすでに計画があるためです。","12ターン目の失敗したAPI呼び出しのエラーメッセージと生の失敗詳細。13ターン目の再試行成功で完全に解決しており、その後の推論が元の失敗の具体的内容に依存しないためです。","エージェントの最終統合回答。利用者へ渡した後は、進行中トレースで役割を持たなくなるためです。","45ターン目の正当なデータ。この段階では新しい情報を必要としなくなっているためです。"],"explanation_ja":"解決済みで今後の判断が依存しない生エラーは、削除の有力候補です。一方、元の依頼、現在の回答、継続して使う根拠はタスクの一貫性を支えます。「古い」「一度返した」だけでは削除理由になりません。必要なら再発防止に有用な短い教訓だけを残します。","distractors_ja":["元の依頼は目的と制約の基準です。計画があっても質問自体を捨てると、要求からずれる可能性があります。","修正後の成功結果が残り、以後に生エラーの詳細へ依存しないため、必要な情報を失わずに削減できます。","利用者へ返した結論は、その後の継続や整合性確認に役立ちます。返却済みなら無条件に不要とはいえません。","以後の推論が根拠として依存しているデータです。新規取得が不要なことと、既存の根拠が不要なことは異なります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-039","number":39,"form":1,"stem":"A team operates two different Databricks agents: Agent X is a legal-contract review assistant used to flag subtle risk language in high-value merger agreements, where a missed nuance could have significant financial and legal consequences, and conversations are relatively short (typically under 20 turns) so the token cost of retaining more detail is modest. Agent Y is a casual internal FAQ chatbot answering routine questions about office logistics (parking, badge access, lunch menus) at extremely high volume, where any single conversation's nuance rarely matters and cost efficiency at scale is the primary concern.\n\nWhich compaction posture should be applied to each agent, respectively?","options":["Agent X should use conservative compaction that prioritizes higher fidelity and retains more subtle context, even at higher token cost, given the low volume and high cost of losing nuance; Agent Y should use more aggressive compaction that accepts some risk of losing minor context, given its high volume and low per-conversation stakes.","Agent X should use aggressive compaction to minimize token costs, since legal review conversations are short and therefore don't need much context retained regardless of nuance.","Agent Y should use conservative, high-fidelity compaction, since its extremely high volume means even small quality losses will affect a large number of users in aggregate, making fidelity the top priority regardless of cost.","Both agents should use the same compaction posture, since compaction aggressiveness should be standardized across an organization's agents for consistency rather than tailored to each agent's stakes and volume."],"correct":0,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"高リスクと大量処理で異なる圧縮強度","concept":"ce-f1-q039","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Agent X should use conservative compaction that prioritizes higher fidelity and retains more subtle context, even at higher token cost, given the low volume and high cost of losing nuance; Agent Y should use more aggressive compaction that accepts some risk of losing minor context, given its high volume and low per-conversation stakes.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nCompaction aggressiveness should reflect the application’s risk profile, informational complexity, conversation length, and operating economics.\n\nFor Agent X, the cost of losing a qualification, exception, defined term, or subtle contractual relationship could be substantial. Its conversations are also generally short, making the marginal token savings from aggressive compaction relatively small. The correct posture is therefore conservative: retain more source detail and compact only information that is clearly redundant or no longer relevant.\n\nFor Agent Y, individual interactions are routine and low-stakes, while total request volume is extremely high. Small per-request token savings can become substantial at scale. More aggressive summarization or trimming is therefore appropriate, provided evaluation confirms that the chatbot still answers common FAQ questions correctly.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAgent X requires high-fidelity context preservation. Legal-contract analysis can depend on details such as:\n\n- Exceptions to an obligation.\n\n- Defined terms established earlier.\n\n- Cross-references between clauses.\n\n- Limitations of liability.\n\n- Conditional or qualifying language.\n\n- Differences between “may,” “must,” and “commercially reasonable.”\n\n- Facts supporting why a clause was previously flagged.\n\nAggressive compaction could preserve a clause’s general topic while losing the exact language that creates legal or financial risk. Because the conversations remain under roughly 20 turns, retaining additional detail imposes a relatively modest token cost compared with the potential consequence of losing nuance.\n\nAgent Y supports a different optimization target. Questions about parking, badge access, and lunch menus are generally narrow, repeatable, and low-risk. The agent can usually preserve sufficient state through compact summaries containing the active topic, relevant location, date, and unresolved question. Retaining every conversational detail provides limited additional value.\n\nAt high traffic volume, even small reductions in input tokens and latency can produce meaningful aggregate savings. Databricks recommends evaluating GenAI applications across quality, latency, and cost rather than optimizing one dimension in isolation.\n\nTherefore, the compaction policies should be tailored:\n\n- Agent X: Quality- and fidelity-first.\n\n- Agent Y: Efficiency-first, subject to minimum quality thresholds.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Agent X should use aggressive compaction to minimize token costs, since legal review conversations are short and therefore don't need much context retained regardless of nuance.\n\nThis draws the wrong conclusion from the short conversation length.\n\nShort conversations mean the cost of retaining context is manageable; they do not mean the information is unimportant. In legal review, a small amount of text can contain highly consequential qualifications or exceptions. Aggressive compaction would provide limited savings while creating disproportionate risk.\n\nC. Agent Y should use conservative, high-fidelity compaction, since its extremely high volume means even small quality losses will affect a large number of users in aggregate, making fidelity the top priority regardless of cost.\n\nThe concern about aggregate quality is valid, but this option ignores the scenario’s stated priorities:\n\n- Questions are routine and low-stakes.\n\n- Conversational nuance rarely matters.\n\n- Cost efficiency at scale is the primary concern.\n\nAgent Y should still have quality thresholds and evaluation tests, but it does not require the same fidelity posture as high-value legal analysis. “Regardless of cost” is too absolute and contradicts the business requirement.\n\nD. Both agents should use the same compaction posture, since compaction aggressiveness should be standardized across an organization's agents for consistency rather than tailored to each agent's stakes and volume.\n\nA common implementation framework can be standardized, but the actual compaction settings should not be identical.\n\nThe two agents differ materially in:\n\n- Consequence of information loss.\n\n- Importance of subtle language.\n\n- Typical context length.\n\n- Request volume.\n\n- Latency and cost sensitivity.\n\nUsing one universal configuration would either over-compress Agent X or make Agent Y unnecessarily expensive.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is treating compaction as a purely technical configuration that should be standardized across all agents.\n\nCompaction is a risk-and-economics decision. The acceptable amount of information loss depends on the task’s stakes, the value of contextual nuance, and the cost of retaining additional tokens.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is A, but production teams should validate both policies empirically.\n\nFor Agent X, use a conservative policy that preserves:\n\n- Exact risk-bearing passages.\n\n- Clause identifiers and citations.\n\n- Defined terms and cross-references.\n\n- Qualifications, exceptions, and dependencies.\n\n- Previous findings and their supporting evidence.\n\nFor Agent Y, use a more aggressive summary that retains only:\n\n- The current question.\n\n- Relevant office or location.\n\n- Applicable date or time.\n\n- Actions already completed.\n\n- Any unresolved follow-up.\n\nThe team should compare compaction variants using representative evaluation datasets and monitor answer correctness, omitted-detail failures, latency, and token cost. Summarization replaces older messages with a compressed representation, so its information-retention behavior should be tested for each use case rather than assumed to be universally safe.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- MLflow 3 for GenAI\n\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/)\n\n- Tutorial: Evaluate and improve a GenAI application\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app)\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- LangChain context engineering\n[https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/context-engineering](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/context-engineering)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Agent X should use conservative compaction that prioritizes higher fidelity and retains more subtle context, even at higher token cost, given the low volume and high cost of losing nuance; Agent Y should use more aggressive compaction that accepts some risk of losing minor context, given its high volume and low per-conversation stakes.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nCompaction aggressiveness should reflect the application’s risk profile, informational complexity, conversation length, and operating economics.\n\nFor Agent X, the cost of losing a qualification, exception, defined term, or subtle contractual relationship could be substantial. Its conversations are also generally short, making the marginal token savings from aggressive compaction relatively small. The correct posture is therefore conservative: retain more source detail and compact only information that is clearly redundant or no longer relevant.\n\nFor Agent Y, individual interactions are routine and low-stakes, while total request volume is extremely high. Small per-request token savings can become substantial at scale. More aggressive summarization or trimming is therefore appropriate, provided evaluation confirms that the chatbot still answers common FAQ questions correctly.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAgent X requires high-fidelity context preservation. Legal-contract analysis can depend on details such as:\n\n- Exceptions to an obligation.\n\n- Defined terms established earlier.\n\n- Cross-references between clauses.\n\n- Limitations of liability.\n\n- Conditional or qualifying language.\n\n- Differences between “may,” “must,” and “commercially reasonable.”\n\n- Facts supporting why a clause was previously flagged.\n\nAggressive compaction could preserve a clause’s general topic while losing the exact language that creates legal or financial risk. Because the conversations remain under roughly 20 turns, retaining additional detail imposes a relatively modest token cost compared with the potential consequence of losing nuance.\n\nAgent Y supports a different optimization target. Questions about parking, badge access, and lunch menus are generally narrow, repeatable, and low-risk. The agent can usually preserve sufficient state through compact summaries containing the active topic, relevant location, date, and unresolved question. Retaining every conversational detail provides limited additional value.\n\nAt high traffic volume, even small reductions in input tokens and latency can produce meaningful aggregate savings. Databricks recommends evaluating GenAI applications across quality, latency, and cost rather than optimizing one dimension in isolation.\n\nTherefore, the compaction policies should be tailored:\n\n- Agent X: Quality- and fidelity-first.\n\n- Agent Y: Efficiency-first, subject to minimum quality thresholds.","B. Agent X should use aggressive compaction to minimize token costs, since legal review conversations are short and therefore don't need much context retained regardless of nuance.\n\nThis draws the wrong conclusion from the short conversation length.\n\nShort conversations mean the cost of retaining context is manageable; they do not mean the information is unimportant. In legal review, a small amount of text can contain highly consequential qualifications or exceptions. Aggressive compaction would provide limited savings while creating disproportionate risk.","C. Agent Y should use conservative, high-fidelity compaction, since its extremely high volume means even small quality losses will affect a large number of users in aggregate, making fidelity the top priority regardless of cost.\n\nThe concern about aggregate quality is valid, but this option ignores the scenario’s stated priorities:\n\n- Questions are routine and low-stakes.\n\n- Conversational nuance rarely matters.\n\n- Cost efficiency at scale is the primary concern.\n\nAgent Y should still have quality thresholds and evaluation tests, but it does not require the same fidelity posture as high-value legal analysis. “Regardless of cost” is too absolute and contradicts the business requirement.","D. Both agents should use the same compaction posture, since compaction aggressiveness should be standardized across an organization's agents for consistency rather than tailored to each agent's stakes and volume.\n\nA common implementation framework can be standardized, but the actual compaction settings should not be identical.\n\nThe two agents differ materially in:\n\n- Consequence of information loss.\n\n- Importance of subtle language.\n\n- Typical context length.\n\n- Request volume.\n\n- Latency and cost sensitivity.\n\nUsing one universal configuration would either over-compress Agent X or make Agent Y unnecessarily expensive."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/context-engineering"],"source_hash":"0e2031f22a0bff13f4f32d6ff67f8973e7cfc852a89aa0b03d624a11422f659a","stem_ja":"チームは2種類のDatabricksエージェントを運用しています。Xは高額の合併契約に含まれる微妙なリスク表現を指摘する法務アシスタントで、ニュアンスを見落とすと重大な財務・法的影響が生じます。会話は比較的短く、通常20ターン未満のため、詳細を多く保持してもトークン費用は小さい状況です。Yは駐車場、入館証、昼食メニューなど、事務所の定型質問へ非常に大量に答える気軽な社内FAQボットです。個々の会話の微妙なニュアンスはほとんど重要でなく、規模に応じた費用効率が主な関心です。\n\nそれぞれにどの圧縮方針を適用すべきですか。","options_ja":["Xは処理量が少なくニュアンス喪失の代償が大きいため、トークン費用が増えても高い忠実性を優先して細かな文脈を多く残す保守的圧縮を使います。Yは大量処理で会話単位のリスクが低いため、軽微な文脈喪失の危険を許容する強い圧縮を使います。","Xは法務確認の会話が短く、ニュアンスに関係なく多くの文脈は不要なので、トークン費用を最小化する強い圧縮を使います。","Yは処理量が非常に多く、小さな品質低下でも合計では大勢に影響するため、費用によらず忠実性を最優先し、保守的で高忠実性の圧縮を使います。","エージェントのリスクや量に合わせず、一貫性のため組織全体で圧縮強度を標準化すべきなので、両方に同じ方針を使います。"],"explanation_ja":"圧縮は情報損失の影響と費用を比べて選びます。法務では小さな条件の欠落が結論を変えるため忠実性を優先し、低リスクの定型FAQでは大規模な費用効率を重視します。会話が短いことは重要情報を消してよい理由ではなく、詳しく残す負担が小さいという意味です。","distractors_ja":["用途ごとの情報損失の代償と費用の重要度に合わせ、法務では保持、FAQでは効率を優先しています。","法務の短い会話でも微妙な限定条項は重要です。短いことを理由に削りすぎると、重大な結論変更を招きます。","大量処理の影響は考慮しますが、設問は低リスクと費用効率を優先しています。すべての詳細を守る方針は条件に合いません。","リスクと量が異なるので、一律の方針では一方を過剰処理したり、他方で必要情報を失ったりします。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-040","number":40,"form":1,"stem":"A multi-agent system generates a quarterly business report using three agents: a Sales Agent summarizing sales figures, a Marketing Agent summarizing campaign performance, and a Synthesis Agent that combines both into a final narrative. Each of the Sales and Marketing agents independently pulls the \"reporting period\" (fiscal Q3) from its own separate configuration rather than a shared source. Unbeknownst to the team, the Marketing Agent's config was updated to calendar Q3 (offset by one month from fiscal Q3), while the Sales Agent's config still uses fiscal Q3. The Synthesis Agent receives figures from both with no indication they cover different date ranges, and the final report presents sales and marketing numbers side by side as if for the same period, producing a report that doesn't logically reconcile.\n\nWhich failure mode does this describe, and what's the fix?","options":["This is a hallucination problem within the Synthesis Agent alone, unrelated to the Sales or Marketing agents; the fix is to fine-tune the Synthesis Agent on more report-writing examples.","This is a routine data-quality issue in the source Delta tables, unrelated to the multi-agent architecture; the fix is to re-ingest the raw source data at a higher frequency.","This is a multi-agent failure caused by insufficient shared context — each agent computed its own reporting period from an independent, unsynchronized source rather than a single shared definition; the fix is to establish a single authoritative source for shared parameters like the reporting period and propagate it consistently to every agent.","This is expected behavior, since different departments always define reporting periods slightly differently, and reconciling reporting periods across agents is unnecessary."],"correct":2,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"報告期間を共通の正当な情報源から伝播する","concept":"ce-f1-q040","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. This is a multi-agent failure caused by insufficient shared context — each agent computed its own reporting period from an independent, unsynchronized source rather than a single shared definition; the fix is to establish a single authoritative source for shared parameters like the reporting period and propagate it consistently to every agent.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe individual Sales and Marketing figures may each be internally correct, but they were computed using different date ranges:\n\n- Sales Agent: fiscal Q3.\n\n- Marketing Agent: calendar Q3.\n\n- Synthesis Agent: assumes both outputs cover the same period.\n\nThe failure therefore occurs at the multi-agent coordination and shared-context level. The system has no common, authoritative definition of the reporting period and does not require subagents to return the period used with their results.\n\nThe reporting period should be resolved once—preferably by the orchestrator or a governed configuration service—and passed unchanged to every subagent.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nMulti-agent systems use specialized agents that may have their own prompts, context, and tools. This modularity increases orchestration complexity because independently configured agents can operate under inconsistent assumptions.\n\nShared business parameters must therefore be treated as explicit workflow state rather than independently inferred by each agent. For this report, the orchestrator should establish a canonical context such as:\n\n```\nreporting_period_type: fiscal_quarterfiscal_year: 2026quarter: Q3start_date: 2026-05-01end_date: 2026-07-31timezone: America/New_Yorkcalendar_version: corporate_fiscal_calendar_v4\n```\n\nThe same resolved dates should then be supplied to both the Sales and Marketing agents.\n\nEach subagent should also return provenance with its result:\n\n```\nmetric_domain: marketingperiod_start: 2026-05-01period_end: 2026-07-31period_type: fiscalsource: campaign_performance_gold\n```\n\nBefore combining results, the Synthesis Agent or a deterministic validation step should verify that the reporting periods match. If they do not, synthesis should stop rather than silently presenting incomparable figures together.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. This is a hallucination problem within the Synthesis Agent alone, unrelated to the Sales or Marketing agents; the fix is to fine-tune the Synthesis Agent on more report-writing examples.\n\nThe Synthesis Agent received two outputs without metadata indicating that their periods differed. It could not reliably detect the mismatch from the values alone.\n\nFine-tuning report-writing style would not synchronize the upstream reporting periods. The failure originates before synthesis, when the specialist agents use conflicting configuration sources.\n\nB. This is a routine data-quality issue in the source Delta tables, unrelated to the multi-agent architecture; the fix is to re-ingest the raw source data at a higher frequency.\n\nThe scenario does not indicate stale, incomplete, or incorrect source records.\n\nThe problem is that valid data was queried using different date boundaries. Increasing ingestion frequency would not cause calendar Q3 and fiscal Q3 to become equivalent.\n\nThis is a parameter-consistency problem, not a raw-data freshness problem.\n\nD. This is expected behavior, since different departments always define reporting periods slightly differently, and reconciling reporting periods across agents is unnecessary.\n\nDepartment-specific definitions may exist, but the final report presents the metrics side by side as though they are comparable.\n\nIf different periods are intentionally used, the report must label them explicitly and avoid direct comparison. For a unified quarterly business report, shared time boundaries must be reconciled before calculating and synthesizing metrics.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is blaming the final synthesizer because the inconsistency appears in the final report.\n\nThe deeper failure is that the subagents did not receive a shared, authoritative parameter definition. A synthesis agent cannot safely reconcile results when essential provenance—such as start date, end date, and calendar type—is missing.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is C: centrally resolve and consistently propagate shared context.\n\nFor production workflows:\n\n- Store fiscal-calendar definitions in one governed source.\n\n- Resolve natural-language periods such as “fiscal Q3” once.\n\n- Pass explicit start and end dates to every subagent.\n\n- Require each result to include period and source metadata.\n\n- Validate compatible units, currencies, filters, and reporting periods before synthesis.\n\n- Fail closed or request correction when subagent outputs are not comparable.\n\n- Trace the parameters supplied to every agent through MLflow.\n\nThe authoritative source could be a Unity Catalog table or function, a governed configuration service, or orchestrator-managed workflow state. The essential principle is that shared business parameters must not be independently re-derived from unsynchronized local configurations.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- Build a multi-agent system on Databricks Apps\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/multi-agent-apps](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/multi-agent-apps)\n\n- Use Supervisor Agent to create a coordinated multi-agent system\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor)\n\n- Agent state and memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)","source_option_explanations":["A. This is a hallucination problem within the Synthesis Agent alone, unrelated to the Sales or Marketing agents; the fix is to fine-tune the Synthesis Agent on more report-writing examples.\n\nThe Synthesis Agent received two outputs without metadata indicating that their periods differed. It could not reliably detect the mismatch from the values alone.\n\nFine-tuning report-writing style would not synchronize the upstream reporting periods. The failure originates before synthesis, when the specialist agents use conflicting configuration sources.","B. This is a routine data-quality issue in the source Delta tables, unrelated to the multi-agent architecture; the fix is to re-ingest the raw source data at a higher frequency.\n\nThe scenario does not indicate stale, incomplete, or incorrect source records.\n\nThe problem is that valid data was queried using different date boundaries. Increasing ingestion frequency would not cause calendar Q3 and fiscal Q3 to become equivalent.\n\nThis is a parameter-consistency problem, not a raw-data freshness problem.","Correct Answer\n\nC. This is a multi-agent failure caused by insufficient shared context — each agent computed its own reporting period from an independent, unsynchronized source rather than a single shared definition; the fix is to establish a single authoritative source for shared parameters like the reporting period and propagate it consistently to every agent.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe individual Sales and Marketing figures may each be internally correct, but they were computed using different date ranges:\n\n- Sales Agent: fiscal Q3.\n\n- Marketing Agent: calendar Q3.\n\n- Synthesis Agent: assumes both outputs cover the same period.\n\nThe failure therefore occurs at the multi-agent coordination and shared-context level. The system has no common, authoritative definition of the reporting period and does not require subagents to return the period used with their results.\n\nThe reporting period should be resolved once—preferably by the orchestrator or a governed configuration service—and passed unchanged to every subagent.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nMulti-agent systems use specialized agents that may have their own prompts, context, and tools. This modularity increases orchestration complexity because independently configured agents can operate under inconsistent assumptions.\n\nShared business parameters must therefore be treated as explicit workflow state rather than independently inferred by each agent. For this report, the orchestrator should establish a canonical context such as:\n\n```\nreporting_period_type: fiscal_quarterfiscal_year: 2026quarter: Q3start_date: 2026-05-01end_date: 2026-07-31timezone: America/New_Yorkcalendar_version: corporate_fiscal_calendar_v4\n```\n\nThe same resolved dates should then be supplied to both the Sales and Marketing agents.\n\nEach subagent should also return provenance with its result:\n\n```\nmetric_domain: marketingperiod_start: 2026-05-01period_end: 2026-07-31period_type: fiscalsource: campaign_performance_gold\n```\n\nBefore combining results, the Synthesis Agent or a deterministic validation step should verify that the reporting periods match. If they do not, synthesis should stop rather than silently presenting incomparable figures together.","D. This is expected behavior, since different departments always define reporting periods slightly differently, and reconciling reporting periods across agents is unnecessary.\n\nDepartment-specific definitions may exist, but the final report presents the metrics side by side as though they are comparable.\n\nIf different periods are intentionally used, the report must label them explicitly and avoid direct comparison. For a unified quarterly business report, shared time boundaries must be reconciled before calculating and synthesizing metrics."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/multi-agent-apps","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management"],"source_hash":"1adf4db1f951cc9c6a2d3510ba08ffdf4286f3fab740e57f2ab60b969c69861c","stem_ja":"マルチエージェントシステムが、売上高を要約するSales Agent、施策実績を要約するMarketing Agent、両者を最終文章へまとめるSynthesis Agentの3つで四半期業績報告を作成します。SalesとMarketingは共有元でなく、それぞれ別の設定から「報告期間」（会計上のQ3）を独立に取得します。チームが知らないうちにMarketingの設定が暦年Q3（会計Q3と1か月ずれる）へ更新され、Salesは会計Q3のままです。Synthesisは対象日付範囲が違うという表示なしに双方の数値を受け取り、同じ期間の数値のように並べて、論理的に整合しない報告を作ります。\n\nどの障害で、どう直すべきですか。","options_ja":["Synthesis Agent単独のハルシネーションで、SalesやMarketingとは無関係です。報告書作成例を増やしてSynthesisをファインチューニングします。","元のDeltaテーブルの通常のデータ品質問題で、複数エージェントの構成とは無関係です。元データの再取り込み頻度を増やします。","共有コンテキスト不足による複数エージェントの障害です。各エージェントが共通定義でなく、独立した非同期の情報源から報告期間を決めています。期間などの共有パラメータに単一の正当な情報源を設け、すべてへ一貫して伝播させます。","部門ごとに報告期間が多少違うのは常に普通なので、期待どおりの動作であり、エージェント間で期間をそろえる必要はありません。"],"explanation_ja":"数値が正しくても対象期間が違えば比較できません。期間、単位、集計基準のような共有パラメータを一つの正当な定義から全エージェントへ配布し、結果にも範囲を付けます。これは文書生成モデルや取り込み鮮度ではなく、共通前提の伝播の欠落です。","distractors_ja":["統合前から期間定義がずれています。Synthesisだけを学習しても、各数値の対象期間が伝わらなければ直りません。","元データが正しくても集計する期間が異なると不整合になります。再取り込み頻度では設定のずれを解消できません。","共有する期間定義を中央化して一貫して渡すことで、各担当が同じ範囲を集計し、比較の前提をそろえられます。","異なる期間を同じ期間のように並べると報告の意味が変わります。部門差を認識し、共通の比較条件を定める必要があります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-041","number":41,"form":1,"stem":"A coordinating agent triages incoming support tickets and dispatches each one to a specialist sub-agent (billing, technical, account-security). Each sub-agent currently receives only the raw ticket text as its task message, with no visibility into the coordinator's own classification reasoning or any related tickets from the same customer already reviewed earlier that day. When a customer submits a follow-up ticket related to an issue the coordinator had already partially diagnosed earlier, the dispatched sub-agent repeats diagnostic steps from scratch and occasionally reaches a conclusion that contradicts the coordinator's earlier partial diagnosis, confusing the customer.\n\nWhich configuration resolves this without expanding each sub-agent's context window?","options":["Give each sub-agent the coordinator's full raw multi-ticket session trace in every dispatch, so no relevant detail is ever missed.","Have the coordinator stop dispatching to sub-agents entirely and resolve every ticket itself, since the sub-agent architecture is fundamentally incompatible with cross-ticket continuity.","Attach a compact summary of the coordinator's relevant prior reasoning and any related earlier tickets from the same customer to each dispatched task message, so sub-agents inherit the necessary continuity without receiving the coordinator's entire raw session trace.","Increase each sub-agent's model's context window so it can hold more historical tickets, without changing what's included in the dispatch message itself."],"correct":2,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"委任メッセージへ関連する継続文脈を含める","concept":"ce-f1-q041","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Attach a compact summary of the coordinator's relevant prior reasoning and any related earlier tickets from the same customer to each dispatched task message, so sub-agents inherit the necessary continuity without receiving the coordinator's entire raw session trace.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe problem is not that the specialist agents lack expertise or have insufficient maximum context-window capacity. The problem is an incomplete coordinator-to-subagent handoff.\n\nEach specialist receives only the newest raw ticket, so it cannot see:\n\n- The coordinator’s earlier partial diagnosis.\n\n- Diagnostic steps already completed.\n\n- Relevant facts from related tickets.\n\n- Hypotheses already rejected.\n\n- Commitments or explanations already given to the customer.\n\nBecause every subagent has its own context, it cannot automatically infer or access the coordinator’s private reasoning and earlier ticket history. Databricks describes multi-agent systems as specialized agents with distinct contexts coordinated by a supervisor, and specifically notes that individual agents may need conversation history or domain-specific context.\n\nThe coordinator should therefore construct a concise, task-specific handoff containing only the information necessary for the dispatched specialist to continue the investigation coherently.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption C applies selective context propagation.\n\nA useful dispatch message might include:\n\n```\nCurrent ticket:- Customer reports a second unexpected subscription charge. Related ticket reviewed today:- Ticket T-1042 concerned the same subscription and payment method. Work already completed:- Coordinator confirmed the first charge was the monthly renewal.- A second transaction remains unexplained.- Duplicate-account hypothesis was checked and rejected. Current working diagnosis:- Possible payment retry after an earlier authorization timeout. Specialist task:- Verify the payment-attempt history and determine whether the second  transaction is a duplicate capture.\n```\n\nThis gives the billing specialist enough continuity to avoid repeating completed work and contradicting established evidence.\n\nAt the same time, it excludes irrelevant coordinator messages, unrelated customer tickets, verbose tool outputs, and internal routing discussions. Databricks recommends giving an agent only the tools and context it requires rather than an unbounded amount of irrelevant context.\n\nThe summary can be generated from the coordinator’s active state or retrieved from a durable customer- or case-scoped memory store. Databricks supports short-term and long-term memory for preserving earlier conversational context and reusable insights across interactions.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Give each sub-agent the coordinator's full raw multi-ticket session trace in every dispatch, so no relevant detail is ever missed.\n\nThis would provide continuity, but it is unnecessarily broad and inefficient.\n\nThe complete trace could contain:\n\n- Unrelated tickets from other customers.\n\n- Irrelevant routing decisions.\n\n- Failed tool calls and diagnostic logs.\n\n- Duplicate messages.\n\n- Information outside the specialist’s domain.\n\n- Sensitive information the specialist does not need.\n\nIt would increase token usage and context pollution while making it harder for the subagent to identify the important facts. The correct approach is a focused handoff rather than indiscriminate trace sharing.\n\nB. Have the coordinator stop dispatching to sub-agents entirely and resolve every ticket itself, since the sub-agent architecture is fundamentally incompatible with cross-ticket continuity.\n\nMulti-agent architecture is not inherently incompatible with continuity.\n\nA coordinator can route tasks to specialists while explicitly passing relevant context. Databricks supports multi-agent orchestration in which an orchestrator treats specialist agents as callable subagents.\n\nRemoving specialization would sacrifice the benefits of domain-focused prompts, tools, and expertise without addressing the underlying context-handoff design flaw.\n\nD. Increase each sub-agent's model's context window so it can hold more historical tickets, without changing what's included in the dispatch message itself.\n\nA larger context window cannot help when the relevant history is never included.\n\nThe specialist currently receives only the raw current ticket. Increasing its context capacity changes how much information it could hold, but it does not provide:\n\n- The coordinator’s diagnosis.\n\n- Earlier related tickets.\n\n- Previously completed steps.\n\n- Rejected hypotheses.\n\nThis is an information-delivery problem, not a context-window-size problem.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is confusing available context capacity with actual context propagation.\n\nA specialist agent cannot use information that the coordinator never passes to it. The correct fix is not a larger context window or the entire raw trace, but a concise and relevant handoff summary.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is C: provide each specialist with a structured, case-relevant handoff.\n\nA production handoff should typically preserve:\n\n- Customer and case identifiers.\n\n- The current issue and requested outcome.\n\n- Relevant related-ticket summaries.\n\n- Facts already verified.\n\n- Diagnostic actions already performed.\n\n- Rejected hypotheses and rejection reasons.\n\n- Current working diagnosis.\n\n- Unresolved questions.\n\n- Any commitments already made to the customer.\n\nThe coordinator should avoid sharing its complete internal trace. Instead, it should produce a compact case-state object that both the coordinator and specialist can reference. For cross-ticket continuity, this state can be persisted using customer-, incident-, or case-level keys rather than relying exclusively on a single agent’s conversation history.\n\nThe system should also require specialists to return their findings in a structured form so the coordinator can update the shared case summary after every dispatch.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- Build a multi-agent system on Databricks Apps\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/multi-agent-apps](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/multi-agent-apps)\n\n- Agent state and memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)\n\n- Agent memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/stateful-agents](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/stateful-agents)","source_option_explanations":["A. Give each sub-agent the coordinator's full raw multi-ticket session trace in every dispatch, so no relevant detail is ever missed.\n\nThis would provide continuity, but it is unnecessarily broad and inefficient.\n\nThe complete trace could contain:\n\n- Unrelated tickets from other customers.\n\n- Irrelevant routing decisions.\n\n- Failed tool calls and diagnostic logs.\n\n- Duplicate messages.\n\n- Information outside the specialist’s domain.\n\n- Sensitive information the specialist does not need.\n\nIt would increase token usage and context pollution while making it harder for the subagent to identify the important facts. The correct approach is a focused handoff rather than indiscriminate trace sharing.","B. Have the coordinator stop dispatching to sub-agents entirely and resolve every ticket itself, since the sub-agent architecture is fundamentally incompatible with cross-ticket continuity.\n\nMulti-agent architecture is not inherently incompatible with continuity.\n\nA coordinator can route tasks to specialists while explicitly passing relevant context. Databricks supports multi-agent orchestration in which an orchestrator treats specialist agents as callable subagents.\n\nRemoving specialization would sacrifice the benefits of domain-focused prompts, tools, and expertise without addressing the underlying context-handoff design flaw.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Attach a compact summary of the coordinator's relevant prior reasoning and any related earlier tickets from the same customer to each dispatched task message, so sub-agents inherit the necessary continuity without receiving the coordinator's entire raw session trace.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe problem is not that the specialist agents lack expertise or have insufficient maximum context-window capacity. The problem is an incomplete coordinator-to-subagent handoff.\n\nEach specialist receives only the newest raw ticket, so it cannot see:\n\n- The coordinator’s earlier partial diagnosis.\n\n- Diagnostic steps already completed.\n\n- Relevant facts from related tickets.\n\n- Hypotheses already rejected.\n\n- Commitments or explanations already given to the customer.\n\nBecause every subagent has its own context, it cannot automatically infer or access the coordinator’s private reasoning and earlier ticket history. Databricks describes multi-agent systems as specialized agents with distinct contexts coordinated by a supervisor, and specifically notes that individual agents may need conversation history or domain-specific context.\n\nThe coordinator should therefore construct a concise, task-specific handoff containing only the information necessary for the dispatched specialist to continue the investigation coherently.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption C applies selective context propagation.\n\nA useful dispatch message might include:\n\n```\nCurrent ticket:- Customer reports a second unexpected subscription charge. Related ticket reviewed today:- Ticket T-1042 concerned the same subscription and payment method. Work already completed:- Coordinator confirmed the first charge was the monthly renewal.- A second transaction remains unexplained.- Duplicate-account hypothesis was checked and rejected. Current working diagnosis:- Possible payment retry after an earlier authorization timeout. Specialist task:- Verify the payment-attempt history and determine whether the second  transaction is a duplicate capture.\n```\n\nThis gives the billing specialist enough continuity to avoid repeating completed work and contradicting established evidence.\n\nAt the same time, it excludes irrelevant coordinator messages, unrelated customer tickets, verbose tool outputs, and internal routing discussions. Databricks recommends giving an agent only the tools and context it requires rather than an unbounded amount of irrelevant context.\n\nThe summary can be generated from the coordinator’s active state or retrieved from a durable customer- or case-scoped memory store. Databricks supports short-term and long-term memory for preserving earlier conversational context and reusable insights across interactions.","D. Increase each sub-agent's model's context window so it can hold more historical tickets, without changing what's included in the dispatch message itself.\n\nA larger context window cannot help when the relevant history is never included.\n\nThe specialist currently receives only the raw current ticket. Increasing its context capacity changes how much information it could hold, but it does not provide:\n\n- The coordinator’s diagnosis.\n\n- Earlier related tickets.\n\n- Previously completed steps.\n\n- Rejected hypotheses.\n\nThis is an information-delivery problem, not a context-window-size problem."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/multi-agent-apps","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/stateful-agents"],"source_hash":"be2076f79d936a3bc0b9e6a32f9af4c8ce6689246c911e3a4fcd72f9e4d692c1","stem_ja":"調整エージェントが受信サポートチケットを分類し、請求、技術、アカウントセキュリティの専門サブエージェントへ振り分けます。現在、各サブエージェントが受け取るタスクメッセージは生のチケット本文だけで、調整役の分類判断や、その日にすでに確認した同じ顧客の関連チケットは見えません。顧客が以前に部分診断された問題の追加チケットを送ると、派遣されたサブエージェントは最初から診断し直し、以前の部分診断と矛盾する結論を出すこともあり、顧客を混乱させます。\n\n各サブエージェントのコンテキストウィンドウを広げずに解決する設定はどれですか。","options_ja":["関連する詳細を一切落とさないよう、毎回の委任で調整役の複数チケットにわたる完全な生セッショントレースを渡します。","サブエージェント構成はチケット間の連続性と根本的に両立しないため、委任を全面的にやめ、調整役自身がすべて解決します。","各委任メッセージに、調整役の関連する過去の判断と同一顧客の関連先行チケットの短い要約を付け、調整役の全生履歴を渡さずに必要な継続性を引き継ぎます。","委任メッセージの内容は変えず、各サブエージェントのモデルのウィンドウを広げ、過去チケットを多く保持できるようにします。"],"explanation_ja":"サブエージェントは、渡されていない診断や関連チケットを知りません。委任の際に現在の依頼と関連する既知事実・判断を短く添えれば、不要な再診断と矛盾を減らせます。容量を増やすより先に、必要な情報を実際の入力へ含めることが重要です。","distractors_ja":["継続性は得られても、無関係な多数のチケットまで渡すため、容量を増やさず効率よく継承する方法として過剰です。","委任と継続性は両立できます。必要な前提と関連する過去判断を委任時に渡す構成へ直せばよい問題です。","タスクに必要な先行判断と関連履歴だけを追加し、容量を増やさずに共有前提をそろえられます。","問題の入力が生チケットだけのままでは、容量を増やしても関連チケットや過去診断は自動で入ってきません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-042","number":42,"form":1,"stem":"A multi-agent research pipeline has a Fact-Gathering Agent that researches and extracts claims from multiple sources, and a Writing Agent that composes a final report from those claims. The Fact-Gathering Agent silently discards a source it determines to be low-quality partway through its research, but does not communicate that decision or its reasoning to the Writing Agent — it simply omits the source's claims from its final list. A Fact-Checking Agent, added later, independently re-crawls all original sources referenced anywhere in the pipeline's logs, including the discarded one, and flags a contradiction between the Writing Agent's report (which never mentions the discarded source) and the discarded source's claims, incorrectly concluding the report is unreliable.\n\nWhich context propagation change would most likely prevent this conflict?","options":["Have the Fact-Gathering Agent propagate its source-inclusion decisions (including which sources were excluded and why) to both the Writing Agent and the Fact-Checking Agent, so downstream agents share awareness of which sources were deliberately excluded rather than each independently forming its own view of the source set.","Have the Fact-Gathering Agent stop evaluating source quality entirely, and pass every source's claims through to the Writing Agent unfiltered, so there's nothing left to discard.","Remove the Fact-Checking Agent from the pipeline entirely, since it is the agent surfacing the conflict.","Have the Writing Agent re-run its own independent source-quality evaluation from scratch, without reference to the Fact-Gathering Agent's decisions, so each agent reaches its own conclusion."],"correct":0,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"情報源の除外決定も後続へ伝える","concept":"ce-f1-q042","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Have the Fact-Gathering Agent propagate its source-inclusion decisions (including which sources were excluded and why) to both the Writing Agent and the Fact-Checking Agent, so downstream agents share awareness of which sources were deliberately excluded rather than each independently forming its own view of the source set.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe conflict is caused by incomplete context propagation between agents.\n\nThe Fact-Gathering Agent made a material research decision:\n\n- It evaluated a source.\n\n- It classified the source as low quality.\n\n- It excluded that source’s claims from the evidence set.\n\n- It did not communicate the exclusion or its rationale downstream.\n\nThe Writing Agent therefore used the intended evidence set, but the later Fact-Checking Agent reconstructed a different evidence set from raw logs. Because the fact checker did not know that one source had been intentionally rejected, it treated the rejected source as valid contradictory evidence.\n\nThe fix is to propagate not only accepted claims, but also the source-selection state and provenance decisions governing those claims.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA multi-agent research pipeline needs a shared, explicit evidence manifest. That manifest should distinguish among:\n\n- Sources reviewed and accepted.\n\n- Sources reviewed and excluded.\n\n- Reasons for exclusion.\n\n- Claims extracted from accepted sources.\n\n- Confidence or quality assessments.\n\n- Unresolved source conflicts.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nSource: source-17Status: excludedReason: Unverified secondary source with no primary evidenceAffected claims: Claim C-42Decision owner: Fact-Gathering Agent\n```\n\nBoth downstream agents should receive this information:\n\n- The Writing Agent should know which sources and claims are approved for use.\n\n- The Fact-Checking Agent should validate the report against the approved evidence set and separately verify whether the exclusions were reasonable.\n\nThis establishes a consistent source-of-truth boundary across agents. It also prevents an agent from incorrectly interpreting an intentionally rejected source as authoritative evidence that the report ignored.\n\nThe fact checker should not blindly trust the exclusion. It may audit or challenge the source-quality decision, but it must know that the exclusion was deliberate and understand the rationale before declaring the final report unreliable.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Have the Fact-Gathering Agent stop evaluating source quality entirely, and pass every source's claims through to the Writing Agent unfiltered, so there's nothing left to discard.\n\nThis removes an important quality-control function.\n\nPassing every source through without assessment could allow unreliable, outdated, duplicated, or unsupported claims to influence the report. The problem is not that the Fact-Gathering Agent filtered a source; the problem is that it failed to communicate the filtering decision.\n\nSource evaluation should remain in place, with its decisions made visible and auditable.\n\nC. Remove the Fact-Checking Agent from the pipeline entirely, since it is the agent surfacing the conflict.\n\nThe Fact-Checking Agent is exposing a coordination defect, not creating the underlying defect.\n\nRemoving it would hide inconsistencies rather than resolve them. Independent validation is valuable for a research pipeline, but the validator must receive the intended evidence scope, provenance records, and exclusion decisions.\n\nD. Have the Writing Agent re-run its own independent source-quality evaluation from scratch, without reference to the Fact-Gathering Agent's decisions, so each agent reaches its own conclusion.\n\nThis would increase duplicated work and could produce additional inconsistencies.\n\nThe Writing Agent might classify sources differently from the Fact-Gathering Agent, resulting in:\n\n- Conflicting evidence sets.\n\n- Inconsistent quality standards.\n\n- Repeated crawling and evaluation.\n\n- No authoritative record of which sources governed the final report.\n\nIndependent review can be useful, but it should operate against a shared evidence manifest and explicitly record any disagreement with the original inclusion decision.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is focusing only on the claims passed to the Writing Agent.\n\nIn multi-agent workflows, decisions about omitted information can be as important as the information that is included. If exclusions and their rationales are not propagated, downstream agents may reconstruct incompatible views of the task state.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is A: propagate source-selection decisions and provenance to all downstream agents.\n\nA production research pipeline should maintain a structured evidence registry containing:\n\n```\nsource_idsource_locationquality_statusinclusion_statusexclusion_reasonclaims_extractedsupporting_passagesreviewing_agentdecision_timestampdecision_version\n```\n\nThe Fact-Checking Agent should then perform two separate checks:\n\n- Report verification: Does the report accurately reflect the accepted evidence?\n\n- Evidence-governance verification: Were excluded sources rejected for defensible reasons?\n\nThis preserves independent fact checking without allowing each agent to silently create a different source universe.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Agent system design patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- [Build a multi-agent system on Databricks Apps](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/generative-ai/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps)\n\n- [Agent memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/stateful-agents)\n\n- [Agent state and memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Have the Fact-Gathering Agent propagate its source-inclusion decisions (including which sources were excluded and why) to both the Writing Agent and the Fact-Checking Agent, so downstream agents share awareness of which sources were deliberately excluded rather than each independently forming its own view of the source set.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe conflict is caused by incomplete context propagation between agents.\n\nThe Fact-Gathering Agent made a material research decision:\n\n- It evaluated a source.\n\n- It classified the source as low quality.\n\n- It excluded that source’s claims from the evidence set.\n\n- It did not communicate the exclusion or its rationale downstream.\n\nThe Writing Agent therefore used the intended evidence set, but the later Fact-Checking Agent reconstructed a different evidence set from raw logs. Because the fact checker did not know that one source had been intentionally rejected, it treated the rejected source as valid contradictory evidence.\n\nThe fix is to propagate not only accepted claims, but also the source-selection state and provenance decisions governing those claims.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA multi-agent research pipeline needs a shared, explicit evidence manifest. That manifest should distinguish among:\n\n- Sources reviewed and accepted.\n\n- Sources reviewed and excluded.\n\n- Reasons for exclusion.\n\n- Claims extracted from accepted sources.\n\n- Confidence or quality assessments.\n\n- Unresolved source conflicts.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nSource: source-17Status: excludedReason: Unverified secondary source with no primary evidenceAffected claims: Claim C-42Decision owner: Fact-Gathering Agent\n```\n\nBoth downstream agents should receive this information:\n\n- The Writing Agent should know which sources and claims are approved for use.\n\n- The Fact-Checking Agent should validate the report against the approved evidence set and separately verify whether the exclusions were reasonable.\n\nThis establishes a consistent source-of-truth boundary across agents. It also prevents an agent from incorrectly interpreting an intentionally rejected source as authoritative evidence that the report ignored.\n\nThe fact checker should not blindly trust the exclusion. It may audit or challenge the source-quality decision, but it must know that the exclusion was deliberate and understand the rationale before declaring the final report unreliable.","B. Have the Fact-Gathering Agent stop evaluating source quality entirely, and pass every source's claims through to the Writing Agent unfiltered, so there's nothing left to discard.\n\nThis removes an important quality-control function.\n\nPassing every source through without assessment could allow unreliable, outdated, duplicated, or unsupported claims to influence the report. The problem is not that the Fact-Gathering Agent filtered a source; the problem is that it failed to communicate the filtering decision.\n\nSource evaluation should remain in place, with its decisions made visible and auditable.","C. Remove the Fact-Checking Agent from the pipeline entirely, since it is the agent surfacing the conflict.\n\nThe Fact-Checking Agent is exposing a coordination defect, not creating the underlying defect.\n\nRemoving it would hide inconsistencies rather than resolve them. Independent validation is valuable for a research pipeline, but the validator must receive the intended evidence scope, provenance records, and exclusion decisions.","D. Have the Writing Agent re-run its own independent source-quality evaluation from scratch, without reference to the Fact-Gathering Agent's decisions, so each agent reaches its own conclusion.\n\nThis would increase duplicated work and could produce additional inconsistencies.\n\nThe Writing Agent might classify sources differently from the Fact-Gathering Agent, resulting in:\n\n- Conflicting evidence sets.\n\n- Inconsistent quality standards.\n\n- Repeated crawling and evaluation.\n\n- No authoritative record of which sources governed the final report.\n\nIndependent review can be useful, but it should operate against a shared evidence manifest and explicitly record any disagreement with the original inclusion decision."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/generative-ai/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/stateful-agents","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management"],"source_hash":"1ba99c83cfb2810d0cef96807a350b521caafa0567d3e5e428444ef685e055fb","stem_ja":"複数エージェントの調査パイプラインに、複数の情報源から主張を抽出するFact-Gathering Agentと、それらから最終報告を書くWriting Agentがあります。Fact-Gatheringは調査途中で低品質と判断した情報源を黙って捨てますが、その判断や理由をWritingへ伝えず、最終の主張一覧からその情報源を省くだけです。後から加えたFact-Checking Agentが、ログのどこかに参照のあるすべての元情報源を独立に再クロールします。除外済みの情報源も含め、報告（その情報源に言及なし）と除外元の主張に矛盾を見つけ、報告は信頼できないと誤認します。\n\nこの衝突を防ぐ可能性が最も高いコンテキスト伝播の変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["Fact-Gatheringが情報源の採否判断（除外した情報源とその理由を含む）をWritingとFact-Checkingの両方へ伝え、各担当が独立に情報源集合を決めず、意図的な除外について共通認識を持つようにします。","Fact-Gatheringに品質評価を全面的にやめさせ、全情報源の主張を未選別でWritingへ渡し、捨てるものがなくなるようにします。","矛盾を見つけている担当なので、Fact-Checkingをパイプラインから完全に除きます。","Writingに、Fact-Gatheringの判断を参照せず、独立に最初から情報源品質を再評価させ、各担当が自分の結論を出すようにします。"],"explanation_ja":"最終の主張だけでは、情報が抜けたのか、意図的に除外したのか後続には分かりません。採用・除外した情報源と理由を共有すれば、検証役も同じ基準で報告を評価できます。失敗した案や除外した根拠といった負の判断も、下流の整合性を保つ重要な文脈です。","distractors_ja":["除外の事実と品質判断の理由を共有し、後続が低品質情報源を再び正当な基準へ戻すことを防げます。","低品質の情報源まで通すと、品質管理を放棄することになります。問題は選別自体でなく、選別判断の未共有です。","検証をなくすと別の誤りを見逃します。検証役へ必要な採否判断を伝えることが根本的な改善です。","各担当が別々に採否を決める構造を残すため、共通の情報源認識がないことによる矛盾を解消できません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-043","number":43,"form":1,"stem":"An orchestrating agent coordinates five specialized sub-agents in a document-processing pipeline. Each sub-agent currently returns its full raw working transcript to the orchestrator — every intermediate tool call, every discarded hypothesis, every internal retry — rather than just its final result. As more sub-agents are added, the orchestrator's context window fills primarily with these verbose raw transcripts, most of which the orchestrator never actually needs to make its next coordination decision.\n\nWhich sub-agent output design change would most reduce orchestrator context load without compromising task coherence?","options":["Remove sub-agents from the architecture and have the orchestrator perform every task itself in a single unified context.","Have sub-agents return a concise, structured summary of their final result and any decisions the orchestrator specifically needs to act on, rather than their full raw working transcript, reserving the full transcript for separate logging/debugging storage the orchestrator doesn't need to load.","Increase the orchestrator's context window so it can comfortably hold every sub-agent's full raw transcript without truncation.","Have sub-agents send their full raw transcripts to each other directly, bypassing the orchestrator entirely, so the orchestrator's context load is unaffected regardless of transcript size."],"correct":1,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"生履歴でなく意思決定に必要な成果を返す","concept":"ce-f1-q043","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Have sub-agents return a concise, structured summary of their final result and any decisions the orchestrator specifically needs to act on, rather than their full raw working transcript, reserving the full transcript for separate logging/debugging storage the orchestrator doesn't need to load.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe orchestrator does not need every intermediate action performed by each specialist. It needs only the information required to decide:\n\n- Whether the subtask succeeded.\n\n- What result was produced.\n\n- What evidence or assumptions support it.\n\n- Whether blockers, risks, or unresolved questions remain.\n\n- Which agent or action should run next.\n\nReturning full transcripts mixes execution observability with coordination context. As more sub-agents are added, retries, discarded hypotheses, and verbose tool outputs consume the orchestrator’s context window without improving its next routing or synthesis decision.\n\nOption B separates these concerns:\n\n- Compact structured outputs support orchestration.\n\n- Full traces remain available for debugging, monitoring, and audit purposes.\n\nDatabricks describes multi-agent architectures as modular and scalable but more complex to orchestrate, trace, and debug. MLflow Tracing separately captures complete execution flows—including agent, model, retriever, and tool activity—so verbose diagnostic information does not need to be passed through the orchestrator’s active prompt.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nEach sub-agent should return a well-defined result contract, for example:\n\n```\n{  \"status\": \"completed\",  \"result_summary\": \"Extracted 14 contract obligations and identified 2 conflicts.\",  \"key_findings\": [    \"Section 4.2 requires 30-day notice.\",    \"Section 7.1 permits immediate termination under specified conditions.\"  ],  \"evidence_references\": [\"contract-17:4.2\", \"contract-17:7.1\"],  \"decisions_required\": [    \"Send the two conflicting clauses to the legal-review agent.\"  ],  \"open_items\": [],  \"confidence\": \"high\"}\n```\n\nThis preserves task coherence because the orchestrator receives the final conclusions, provenance, unresolved issues, and required next steps. It does not need to ingest every search query, failed retry, or discarded hypothesis unless one of those details materially affects the next decision.\n\nThe complete working trace should still be retained outside the orchestrator’s active context. MLflow Tracing is intended to capture the full request-response and execution flow for debugging, evaluation, latency analysis, cost monitoring, and production observability.\n\nThis design therefore provides:\n\n- Lower orchestrator token consumption.\n\n- Less context pollution.\n\n- More predictable sub-agent handoffs.\n\n- Easier result validation and synthesis.\n\n- Continued access to full diagnostic history when needed.\n\n- Better scalability as the number of sub-agents increases.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Remove sub-agents from the architecture and have the orchestrator perform every task itself in a single unified context.\n\nThis eliminates specialization rather than correcting the output contract.\n\nMulti-agent systems are useful when separate agents require different prompts, tools, domains, or reasoning strategies. Moving all work into the orchestrator could produce an even larger context, reduce modularity, and make the system harder to maintain.\n\nThe underlying problem is not the existence of sub-agents; it is that they return unnecessarily verbose outputs.\n\nC. Increase the orchestrator's context window so it can comfortably hold every sub-agent's full raw transcript without truncation.\n\nA larger context window treats the symptom rather than the cause.\n\nThe orchestrator still pays the token, latency, and attention cost of processing irrelevant intermediate details. As the number of agents and tool calls grows, the system can eventually reach the new limit as well.\n\nThe correct optimization is to improve what sub-agents return, not continually increase capacity for information the orchestrator does not need.\n\nD. Have sub-agents send their full raw transcripts to each other directly, bypassing the orchestrator entirely, so the orchestrator's context load is unaffected regardless of transcript size.\n\nThis would spread context pollution across the entire multi-agent system.\n\nSub-agents would receive transcripts outside their own responsibilities, increasing token usage, exposing unnecessary information, and making it harder to identify the authoritative result of each stage. It would also weaken centralized coordination because the orchestrator would no longer have a clear, concise view of task status.\n\nAgents should exchange task-relevant results and decisions, not indiscriminately broadcast their internal execution histories.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is assuming that preserving coherence requires sharing every detail of how a sub-agent reached its answer.\n\nCoherence requires preserving material outputs, evidence, decisions, assumptions, blockers, and unresolved items. Raw retries, discarded hypotheses, and verbose tool calls belong in observability traces unless they materially affect downstream action.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is B: use compact, structured sub-agent handoffs and store detailed execution traces separately.\n\nA production sub-agent output schema should typically contain:\n\n- Completion status.\n\n- Final result or finding.\n\n- Supporting evidence and provenance.\n\n- Important assumptions.\n\n- Decisions already made.\n\n- Open questions or blockers.\n\n- Recommended next action.\n\n- Confidence or validation status.\n\n- References to the full trace for debugging.\n\nThe orchestrator can then make routing and synthesis decisions from the compact result. When an error, contradiction, or low-confidence result requires investigation, engineers—or a dedicated diagnostic component—can inspect the associated MLflow trace without injecting that entire trace into every subsequent model call.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- Build a multi-agent system on Databricks Apps\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps)\n\n- Debug and analyze your app with tracing\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces/)\n\n- Trace concepts\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/tracing-101](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/tracing-101)","source_option_explanations":["A. Remove sub-agents from the architecture and have the orchestrator perform every task itself in a single unified context.\n\nThis eliminates specialization rather than correcting the output contract.\n\nMulti-agent systems are useful when separate agents require different prompts, tools, domains, or reasoning strategies. Moving all work into the orchestrator could produce an even larger context, reduce modularity, and make the system harder to maintain.\n\nThe underlying problem is not the existence of sub-agents; it is that they return unnecessarily verbose outputs.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Have sub-agents return a concise, structured summary of their final result and any decisions the orchestrator specifically needs to act on, rather than their full raw working transcript, reserving the full transcript for separate logging/debugging storage the orchestrator doesn't need to load.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe orchestrator does not need every intermediate action performed by each specialist. It needs only the information required to decide:\n\n- Whether the subtask succeeded.\n\n- What result was produced.\n\n- What evidence or assumptions support it.\n\n- Whether blockers, risks, or unresolved questions remain.\n\n- Which agent or action should run next.\n\nReturning full transcripts mixes execution observability with coordination context. As more sub-agents are added, retries, discarded hypotheses, and verbose tool outputs consume the orchestrator’s context window without improving its next routing or synthesis decision.\n\nOption B separates these concerns:\n\n- Compact structured outputs support orchestration.\n\n- Full traces remain available for debugging, monitoring, and audit purposes.\n\nDatabricks describes multi-agent architectures as modular and scalable but more complex to orchestrate, trace, and debug. MLflow Tracing separately captures complete execution flows—including agent, model, retriever, and tool activity—so verbose diagnostic information does not need to be passed through the orchestrator’s active prompt.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nEach sub-agent should return a well-defined result contract, for example:\n\n```\n{  \"status\": \"completed\",  \"result_summary\": \"Extracted 14 contract obligations and identified 2 conflicts.\",  \"key_findings\": [    \"Section 4.2 requires 30-day notice.\",    \"Section 7.1 permits immediate termination under specified conditions.\"  ],  \"evidence_references\": [\"contract-17:4.2\", \"contract-17:7.1\"],  \"decisions_required\": [    \"Send the two conflicting clauses to the legal-review agent.\"  ],  \"open_items\": [],  \"confidence\": \"high\"}\n```\n\nThis preserves task coherence because the orchestrator receives the final conclusions, provenance, unresolved issues, and required next steps. It does not need to ingest every search query, failed retry, or discarded hypothesis unless one of those details materially affects the next decision.\n\nThe complete working trace should still be retained outside the orchestrator’s active context. MLflow Tracing is intended to capture the full request-response and execution flow for debugging, evaluation, latency analysis, cost monitoring, and production observability.\n\nThis design therefore provides:\n\n- Lower orchestrator token consumption.\n\n- Less context pollution.\n\n- More predictable sub-agent handoffs.\n\n- Easier result validation and synthesis.\n\n- Continued access to full diagnostic history when needed.\n\n- Better scalability as the number of sub-agents increases.","C. Increase the orchestrator's context window so it can comfortably hold every sub-agent's full raw transcript without truncation.\n\nA larger context window treats the symptom rather than the cause.\n\nThe orchestrator still pays the token, latency, and attention cost of processing irrelevant intermediate details. As the number of agents and tool calls grows, the system can eventually reach the new limit as well.\n\nThe correct optimization is to improve what sub-agents return, not continually increase capacity for information the orchestrator does not need.","D. Have sub-agents send their full raw transcripts to each other directly, bypassing the orchestrator entirely, so the orchestrator's context load is unaffected regardless of transcript size.\n\nThis would spread context pollution across the entire multi-agent system.\n\nSub-agents would receive transcripts outside their own responsibilities, increasing token usage, exposing unnecessary information, and making it harder to identify the authoritative result of each stage. It would also weaken centralized coordination because the orchestrator would no longer have a clear, concise view of task status.\n\nAgents should exchange task-relevant results and decisions, not indiscriminately broadcast their internal execution histories."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/tracing-101"],"source_hash":"604ebde6ceabfe22cee6a3975027d1142c564a5b4550aada6eac0b0754ee9ea5","stem_ja":"文書処理パイプラインで、オーケストレーターが5つの専門サブエージェントを調整しています。現在、各サブエージェントは最終結果だけでなく、中間の全ツール呼び出し、却下した全仮説、内部の全再試行を含む完全な生作業履歴を返しています。サブエージェントが増えるにつれ、オーケストレーターのウィンドウは、次の調整判断にはほとんど不要な冗長な履歴で埋まっていきます。\n\nタスクの一貫性を損なわず、オーケストレーターの負担を最も減らすサブエージェント出力の変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["構成からサブエージェントを除き、オーケストレーターが単一の統一コンテキストで全タスクを処理します。","完全な生作業履歴でなく、最終結果とオーケストレーターが具体的に対処すべき判断の簡潔な構造化要約を返させます。全履歴は親が読み込まなくてよい別のログ・デバッグ用ストレージへ保存します。","オーケストレーターのウィンドウを広げ、全サブエージェントの完全な生履歴を切り捨てず余裕を持って保持させます。","サブエージェント同士が完全な生履歴を直接送り合い、親を完全に迂回して、履歴の大きさに関係なく親の負担が変わらないようにします。"],"explanation_ja":"親の次の判断に必要な成果と、デバッグ用の詳しい履歴を分けます。結果・重要判断・必要な根拠を短く構造化して返し、詳細ログは外部へ残せば、追跡可能性とコンテキスト効率を両立できます。委任した内部作業をすべて親へ戻すと、分離した利点が失われます。","distractors_ja":["全作業を親へ集中すると、専門化や並列化を失い、文脈負担も集約されます。出力設計を直す方が直接的です。","調整に必要な結果だけを親へ渡し、生履歴は外部に保持するため、一貫性と調査可能性を残して負担を減らせます。","容量を増やしても、不要な生履歴を全件読む費用と重要情報が埋もれる原因は残ります。","生履歴の全員間転送は他の担当の負担と混乱を増やし、親に必要な成果の共有を設計する解決にはなりません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-044","number":44,"form":1,"stem":"A team designs a data-pipeline-debugging multi-agent system with 12 narrowly-scoped agents, each responsible for a single, tiny sub-step (e.g., one agent only checks null counts in one specific column, another only checks a single foreign-key constraint). Every handoff between agents requires compressing the current investigation state into a summary passed to the next agent, and with 12 sequential handoffs per investigation, the team observes that critical nuance about the root cause is repeatedly lost or subtly altered at each compression step. The overall investigation takes far longer than expected due to the cumulative overhead of these handoffs, even though no individual agent's context window is ever close to full.\n\nWhich boundary placement failure is occurring, and what's the general direction of the fix?","options":["The boundaries are drawn too coarse — a single agent is handling too many responsibilities, causing unmanageable context window growth; the fix is to split that agent into many smaller agents.","There is no boundary placement failure here; the slow investigation time is unrelated to the multi-agent architecture and is simply due to the complexity of the underlying data pipeline itself.","The boundaries are correctly placed; the fix is to increase each of the 12 agents' individual context windows, since larger windows always resolve handoff-related information loss.","The boundaries are drawn too fine — too many narrowly-scoped agents create excessive handoff and compression overhead, with cumulative information loss at each transition; the fix is to consolidate some of these tightly related sub-steps into fewer, moderately-scoped agents so fewer handoffs and compressions are needed."],"correct":3,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"細かすぎる委任による情報損失","concept":"ce-f1-q044","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. The boundaries are drawn too fine — too many narrowly-scoped agents create excessive handoff and compression overhead, with cumulative information loss at each transition; the fix is to consolidate some of these tightly related sub-steps into fewer, moderately-scoped agents so fewer handoffs and compressions are needed.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scenario identifies a too-fine agent-boundary problem:\n\n- Twelve agents perform extremely small, closely related checks.\n\n- The investigation state must be summarized at every transition.\n\n- Important diagnostic nuance is repeatedly compressed or altered.\n\n- Coordination takes longer than the underlying checks.\n\n- No agent is approaching its context-window limit.\n\nThis means the system is paying a large handoff tax without receiving a meaningful benefit from specialization.\n\nRelated validation operations—such as null analysis, foreign-key validation, schema comparison, and anomaly inspection—should be grouped into fewer agents that can maintain continuous reasoning across several related steps.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA multi-agent design is most appropriate when agents represent genuinely distinct domains, expertise, prompts, toolsets, security boundaries, or independent contexts. Databricks notes that multi-agent systems provide modularity but are more complex to orchestrate, trace, and debug, and each additional handoff adds routing and coordination overhead.\n\nIn this scenario, checking one column’s null count does not usually require a separate autonomous agent. It is more naturally:\n\n- A tool call made by a broader data-quality agent.\n\n- One step in a deterministic validation workflow.\n\n- Part of a moderately scoped schema-reconciliation agent.\n\nFor example, the 12 agents could be consolidated into three broader specialists:\n\n```\nSchema Compatibility Agent- Data types- Field mappings- Required columns- Naming differences Data Quality Agent- Null counts- Value distributions- Duplicate records- Referential integrity Reconciliation Agent- Source-target count comparison- Transformation validation- Unmapped values- Root-cause synthesis\n```\n\nEach agent would retain the complete context for several related checks, reducing repeated summarization and allowing it to connect evidence across steps.\n\nDatabricks recommends starting with simpler designs, adding multi-agent complexity only for clearly distinct domains or task contexts, and using a single-agent system when tasks remain within a cohesive domain.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. The boundaries are drawn too coarse — a single agent is handling too many responsibilities, causing unmanageable context window growth; the fix is to split that agent into many smaller agents.\n\nThis describes the opposite of the actual problem.\n\nThe system already has 12 extremely narrow agents, and none is close to exhausting its context window. Further splitting would add more:\n\n- Handoffs.\n\n- Summarization steps.\n\n- Routing decisions.\n\n- Latency.\n\n- Opportunities for information distortion.\n\nThe scenario provides no evidence that any individual agent is overloaded.\n\nB. There is no boundary placement failure here; the slow investigation time is unrelated to the multi-agent architecture and is simply due to the complexity of the underlying data pipeline itself.\n\nThe scenario directly attributes the delay and coherence problems to the architecture:\n\n- Twelve sequential handoffs occur per investigation.\n\n- Every handoff requires compaction.\n\n- Nuance is lost at each transition.\n\n- The cumulative overhead makes the process slower than expected.\n\nThe underlying pipeline may be complex, but the agent decomposition is clearly amplifying that complexity.\n\nC. The boundaries are correctly placed; the fix is to increase each of the 12 agents' individual context windows, since larger windows always resolve handoff-related information loss.\n\nA larger context window would not solve this problem.\n\nEach agent already has adequate context capacity. The loss occurs because the state is compressed and handed from one agent to another—not because an agent cannot hold the information it receives.\n\nEven a model with a much larger context window cannot recover information omitted or distorted before the handoff reaches it.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming that greater specialization always improves a multi-agent system.\n\nExtremely narrow agents can turn simple tool-level operations into expensive agent-level boundaries. Every boundary adds coordination, serialization, summarization, latency, and another opportunity for semantic loss.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is D: consolidate tightly coupled tasks into fewer, moderately scoped agents.\n\nA useful boundary test is:\n\nDoes this component require distinct expertise, instructions, tools, permissions, or an independent reasoning context?\n\nWhen the answer is no, the operation may be better represented as a tool or workflow step inside an existing agent.\n\nThe team should measure:\n\n- Number of handoffs per investigation.\n\n- Information lost between handoffs.\n\n- End-to-end latency.\n\n- Token consumption.\n\n- Repeated diagnostic work.\n\n- Root-cause accuracy.\n\n- Routing and coordination failures.\n\nDatabricks describes a single tool-calling agent as a strong default for moderately complex work within one cohesive domain, while multi-agent systems are better suited to genuinely distinct domains or contexts.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- Build a multi-agent system on Databricks Apps\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/multi-agent-apps](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/multi-agent-apps)\n\n- Use Supervisor Agent to create a coordinated multi-agent system\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor)","source_option_explanations":["A. The boundaries are drawn too coarse — a single agent is handling too many responsibilities, causing unmanageable context window growth; the fix is to split that agent into many smaller agents.\n\nThis describes the opposite of the actual problem.\n\nThe system already has 12 extremely narrow agents, and none is close to exhausting its context window. Further splitting would add more:\n\n- Handoffs.\n\n- Summarization steps.\n\n- Routing decisions.\n\n- Latency.\n\n- Opportunities for information distortion.\n\nThe scenario provides no evidence that any individual agent is overloaded.","B. There is no boundary placement failure here; the slow investigation time is unrelated to the multi-agent architecture and is simply due to the complexity of the underlying data pipeline itself.\n\nThe scenario directly attributes the delay and coherence problems to the architecture:\n\n- Twelve sequential handoffs occur per investigation.\n\n- Every handoff requires compaction.\n\n- Nuance is lost at each transition.\n\n- The cumulative overhead makes the process slower than expected.\n\nThe underlying pipeline may be complex, but the agent decomposition is clearly amplifying that complexity.","C. The boundaries are correctly placed; the fix is to increase each of the 12 agents' individual context windows, since larger windows always resolve handoff-related information loss.\n\nA larger context window would not solve this problem.\n\nEach agent already has adequate context capacity. The loss occurs because the state is compressed and handed from one agent to another—not because an agent cannot hold the information it receives.\n\nEven a model with a much larger context window cannot recover information omitted or distorted before the handoff reaches it.","Correct Answer\n\nD. The boundaries are drawn too fine — too many narrowly-scoped agents create excessive handoff and compression overhead, with cumulative information loss at each transition; the fix is to consolidate some of these tightly related sub-steps into fewer, moderately-scoped agents so fewer handoffs and compressions are needed.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scenario identifies a too-fine agent-boundary problem:\n\n- Twelve agents perform extremely small, closely related checks.\n\n- The investigation state must be summarized at every transition.\n\n- Important diagnostic nuance is repeatedly compressed or altered.\n\n- Coordination takes longer than the underlying checks.\n\n- No agent is approaching its context-window limit.\n\nThis means the system is paying a large handoff tax without receiving a meaningful benefit from specialization.\n\nRelated validation operations—such as null analysis, foreign-key validation, schema comparison, and anomaly inspection—should be grouped into fewer agents that can maintain continuous reasoning across several related steps.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA multi-agent design is most appropriate when agents represent genuinely distinct domains, expertise, prompts, toolsets, security boundaries, or independent contexts. Databricks notes that multi-agent systems provide modularity but are more complex to orchestrate, trace, and debug, and each additional handoff adds routing and coordination overhead.\n\nIn this scenario, checking one column’s null count does not usually require a separate autonomous agent. It is more naturally:\n\n- A tool call made by a broader data-quality agent.\n\n- One step in a deterministic validation workflow.\n\n- Part of a moderately scoped schema-reconciliation agent.\n\nFor example, the 12 agents could be consolidated into three broader specialists:\n\n```\nSchema Compatibility Agent- Data types- Field mappings- Required columns- Naming differences Data Quality Agent- Null counts- Value distributions- Duplicate records- Referential integrity Reconciliation Agent- Source-target count comparison- Transformation validation- Unmapped values- Root-cause synthesis\n```\n\nEach agent would retain the complete context for several related checks, reducing repeated summarization and allowing it to connect evidence across steps.\n\nDatabricks recommends starting with simpler designs, adding multi-agent complexity only for clearly distinct domains or task contexts, and using a single-agent system when tasks remain within a cohesive domain."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/multi-agent-apps","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor"],"source_hash":"243851cbee4b751f2ba9f78e388f667ecfc97b3279ca73deea5e594bfacd8c2b","stem_ja":"チームが12個の狭い担当範囲のエージェントからなるデータパイプライン調査システムを設計しました。各担当は、特定列のnull件数だけを確認する、1つの外部キー制約だけを確認するなど、非常に小さい1段階を担当します。引き継ぎのたびに現在の調査状態を次の担当向けに圧縮要約し、1調査で12回の逐次引き継ぎが発生します。チームは、各圧縮で根本原因の重要なニュアンスが繰り返し失われたり少しずつ変わったりすると観測しています。個々のウィンドウには常に余裕があるのに、累積する引き継ぎ負担で調査全体が想定よりはるかに長くかかります。\n\nどの境界設定の障害があり、修正はどちらの方向ですか。","options_ja":["境界が粗すぎます。1担当が多すぎる責任を持ち、制御不能なウィンドウ増大があるため、その担当を多数の小さい担当へ分割します。","境界設定の障害はありません。調査の遅さは複数エージェント構成とは無関係で、元パイプライン自体の複雑さによるものです。","境界は正しいので、12担当それぞれのウィンドウを拡大します。大きなウィンドウは常に引き継ぎ関連の情報損失を解決するためです。","境界が細かすぎます。狭い担当が多すぎて過剰な引き継ぎと圧縮負担が生じ、遷移のたびに情報損失が蓄積しています。密接に関係する段階を、より少数の適度な担当範囲へ統合し、引き継ぎと圧縮を減らします。"],"explanation_ja":"個々のウィンドウに余裕があるため、容量不足ではありません。細かく分けすぎた仕事の境界で、文脈の圧縮・再伝達が何度も起こり、費用と意味のずれが蓄積しています。同じ根拠を使う密接な検証をまとめ、必要な引き継ぎ回数を減らす方向が適切です。","distractors_ja":["症状が逆です。担当はすでに狭すぎ、個々のウィンドウも満杯ではないため、さらに分割すると負担が増えます。","12回の要約による損失と累積負担が明示されており、選んだ境界の細かさと直接関係しています。","問題は容量でなく繰り返す圧縮と引き継ぎです。各ウィンドウを大きくするだけでは原因を取り除けません。","強く関連する検証を同じ担当へまとめれば、同じ文脈を何度も圧縮する回数と、意味が失われる機会を減らせます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B001-045","number":45,"form":1,"stem":"A long-horizon Databricks agent task performs a multi-stage database schema migration: each stage (extract, transform, validate, load, index) strictly depends on the exact, validated output of the immediately preceding stage — for example, indexing cannot begin until the load stage's output schema is confirmed. The team's current strategy dispatches all five stages to run in parallel across independent sub-agents to maximize throughput, each sub-agent starting from an initial best-guess assumption about what the prior stage's output would look like rather than waiting for its actual confirmed output. In production, this produces corrupted migrations whenever a stage's actual output differs even slightly from what the next stage's sub-agent assumed.\n\nWhich long-horizon strategy mismatch is occurring, and what should replace it?","options":["The mismatch is that a sequential execution strategy is being used when a parallel strategy would be faster; the fix is to keep the current parallel design, since faster throughput is always preferable regardless of dependency structure.","The mismatch is unrelated to execution ordering; the real issue is that the underlying model is not powerful enough, and the fix is to switch to a larger model while keeping the parallel fan-out design unchanged.","The mismatch is that a parallel/fan-out execution strategy was applied to a task with strict, sequential step-to-step dependencies, where each stage requires the prior stage's exact confirmed output before it can correctly proceed; the appropriate replacement is a sequential (or dependency-gated) execution strategy, where each stage begins only after the immediately preceding stage's actual output is validated and available.","There is no mismatch; corrupted migrations are an inherent, unavoidable risk of any multi-stage database migration regardless of the orchestration strategy used."],"correct":2,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"依存する工程には検証済み出力を順に渡す","concept":"ce-f1-q045","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. The mismatch is that a parallel/fan-out execution strategy was applied to a task with strict, sequential step-to-step dependencies, where each stage requires the prior stage's exact confirmed output before it can correctly proceed; the appropriate replacement is a sequential (or dependency-gated) execution strategy, where each stage begins only after the immediately preceding stage's actual output is validated and available.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe key phrase is “each stage strictly depends on the exact, validated output of the immediately preceding stage.”\n\nThe migration forms a dependency chain:\n\n```\nExtract → Transform → Validate → Load → Index\n```\n\nThese stages are not independent branches that can safely run simultaneously. For example:\n\n- Transform requires the actual extracted schema and data.\n\n- Validation requires the actual transformed output.\n\n- Load requires the validated schema and records.\n\n- Indexing requires confirmation of the schema and data successfully loaded.\n\nLaunching all stages in parallel forces downstream agents to work from assumptions instead of validated upstream artifacts. When an upstream output differs from the assumption, every dependent stage may produce invalid or corrupted results.\n\nDatabricks workflows support explicit task dependencies so upstream tasks complete before dependent downstream tasks begin. Parallelism should be applied only to tasks whose dependencies have already been satisfied and that can execute independently.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption C correctly matches the orchestration strategy to the task’s dependency structure.\n\nEach stage should produce a concrete, validated artifact or state transition before the next stage starts. For example:\n\n```\nExtract  ↓ Output manifest and source schema confirmedTransform  ↓ Target-compatible schema and transformed dataset confirmedValidate  ↓ Validation checks passedLoad  ↓ Loaded schema, row counts, and transaction status confirmedIndex  ↓ Index built against the actual loaded objects\n```\n\nThe orchestrator should enforce dependency gates such as:\n\n- Do not transform until extraction succeeds.\n\n- Do not load until validation passes.\n\n- Do not index until the load completes and its output schema is confirmed.\n\n- Stop or route to remediation when a validation gate fails.\n\nDatabricks Lakeflow Jobs allows task execution order to be controlled through dependencies and conditional run states such as All succeeded, None failed, and All done. Upstream tasks run before dependent downstream tasks, while tasks without dependencies can still execute in parallel.\n\nThe replacement does not require eliminating all parallelism. The system can still parallelize work within a stage when subtasks are independent. For example, multiple extracted tables might be validated concurrently after transformation is complete. However, the main stage boundaries must remain dependency-gated.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. The mismatch is that a sequential execution strategy is being used when a parallel strategy would be faster; the fix is to keep the current parallel design, since faster throughput is always preferable regardless of dependency structure.\n\nThis reverses the actual problem.\n\nThe current strategy is already parallel, and that parallel execution is causing corruption. Throughput is beneficial only when tasks are independent. A downstream stage cannot safely begin when its required input has not yet been produced or validated.\n\nThe word “always” is also a warning sign. Faster execution does not compensate for invalid outputs.\n\nB. The mismatch is unrelated to execution ordering; the real issue is that the underlying model is not powerful enough, and the fix is to switch to a larger model while keeping the parallel fan-out design unchanged.\n\nA larger model cannot eliminate a missing dependency.\n\nEven a highly capable model cannot know the exact transformed or loaded schema before the upstream stage generates it. It may make a better prediction, but the workflow would still depend on an unverified assumption.\n\nThis is an orchestration and state-management failure, not primarily a model-capability failure.\n\nD. There is no mismatch; corrupted migrations are an inherent, unavoidable risk of any multi-stage database migration regardless of the orchestration strategy used.\n\nDatabase migrations carry risk, but corruption caused by downstream stages running against guessed upstream outputs is avoidable.\n\nExplicit dependencies, validation checkpoints, transactional writes, schema checks, and idempotent retries significantly reduce this risk. Databricks supports sequencing, conditional execution, and dependency-aware control flow for precisely these kinds of multi-stage workflows.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming that maximum parallelism always produces the best long-horizon strategy.\n\nParallel fan-out is appropriate for independent tasks. When one stage requires the exact output of another, execution must be sequential or protected by explicit dependency gates.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe exam answer is C: use dependency-gated execution for strictly dependent migration stages.\n\nA production implementation should pass structured artifacts between stages, including:\n\n- Schema version or schema hash.\n\n- Source and target table identifiers.\n\n- Input and output row counts.\n\n- Validation status.\n\n- Rejected-record counts.\n\n- Data-quality results.\n\n- Output location or transaction identifier.\n\n- Completion timestamp.\n\n- Retry and idempotency keys.\n\nThe orchestrator should fail closed when a prerequisite is missing or validation fails. Independent subtasks can still run concurrently, but only after their common upstream dependency has completed successfully.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nExtract   ↓Transform   ↓Parallel validation checks   ├─ Schema validation   ├─ Row-count reconciliation   ├─ Null and constraint checks   └─ Business-rule validation             ↓         Validation gate             ↓            Load             ↓           Index\n```\n\nThis combines correctness-preserving sequencing with safe parallelism where dependencies genuinely allow it.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Control the flow of tasks within Lakeflow Jobs\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/control-flow](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/control-flow)\n\n- Configure task dependencies\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-if](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-if)\n\n- Lakeflow Jobs\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/)\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)","source_option_explanations":["A. The mismatch is that a sequential execution strategy is being used when a parallel strategy would be faster; the fix is to keep the current parallel design, since faster throughput is always preferable regardless of dependency structure.\n\nThis reverses the actual problem.\n\nThe current strategy is already parallel, and that parallel execution is causing corruption. Throughput is beneficial only when tasks are independent. A downstream stage cannot safely begin when its required input has not yet been produced or validated.\n\nThe word “always” is also a warning sign. Faster execution does not compensate for invalid outputs.","B. The mismatch is unrelated to execution ordering; the real issue is that the underlying model is not powerful enough, and the fix is to switch to a larger model while keeping the parallel fan-out design unchanged.\n\nA larger model cannot eliminate a missing dependency.\n\nEven a highly capable model cannot know the exact transformed or loaded schema before the upstream stage generates it. It may make a better prediction, but the workflow would still depend on an unverified assumption.\n\nThis is an orchestration and state-management failure, not primarily a model-capability failure.","Correct Answer\n\nC. The mismatch is that a parallel/fan-out execution strategy was applied to a task with strict, sequential step-to-step dependencies, where each stage requires the prior stage's exact confirmed output before it can correctly proceed; the appropriate replacement is a sequential (or dependency-gated) execution strategy, where each stage begins only after the immediately preceding stage's actual output is validated and available.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe key phrase is “each stage strictly depends on the exact, validated output of the immediately preceding stage.”\n\nThe migration forms a dependency chain:\n\n```\nExtract → Transform → Validate → Load → Index\n```\n\nThese stages are not independent branches that can safely run simultaneously. For example:\n\n- Transform requires the actual extracted schema and data.\n\n- Validation requires the actual transformed output.\n\n- Load requires the validated schema and records.\n\n- Indexing requires confirmation of the schema and data successfully loaded.\n\nLaunching all stages in parallel forces downstream agents to work from assumptions instead of validated upstream artifacts. When an upstream output differs from the assumption, every dependent stage may produce invalid or corrupted results.\n\nDatabricks workflows support explicit task dependencies so upstream tasks complete before dependent downstream tasks begin. Parallelism should be applied only to tasks whose dependencies have already been satisfied and that can execute independently.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption C correctly matches the orchestration strategy to the task’s dependency structure.\n\nEach stage should produce a concrete, validated artifact or state transition before the next stage starts. For example:\n\n```\nExtract  ↓ Output manifest and source schema confirmedTransform  ↓ Target-compatible schema and transformed dataset confirmedValidate  ↓ Validation checks passedLoad  ↓ Loaded schema, row counts, and transaction status confirmedIndex  ↓ Index built against the actual loaded objects\n```\n\nThe orchestrator should enforce dependency gates such as:\n\n- Do not transform until extraction succeeds.\n\n- Do not load until validation passes.\n\n- Do not index until the load completes and its output schema is confirmed.\n\n- Stop or route to remediation when a validation gate fails.\n\nDatabricks Lakeflow Jobs allows task execution order to be controlled through dependencies and conditional run states such as All succeeded, None failed, and All done. Upstream tasks run before dependent downstream tasks, while tasks without dependencies can still execute in parallel.\n\nThe replacement does not require eliminating all parallelism. The system can still parallelize work within a stage when subtasks are independent. For example, multiple extracted tables might be validated concurrently after transformation is complete. However, the main stage boundaries must remain dependency-gated.","D. There is no mismatch; corrupted migrations are an inherent, unavoidable risk of any multi-stage database migration regardless of the orchestration strategy used.\n\nDatabase migrations carry risk, but corruption caused by downstream stages running against guessed upstream outputs is avoidable.\n\nExplicit dependencies, validation checkpoints, transactional writes, schema checks, and idempotent retries significantly reduce this risk. Databricks supports sequencing, conditional execution, and dependency-aware control flow for precisely these kinds of multi-stage workflows."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/control-flow","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-if","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns"],"source_hash":"e26708182913ec7bb71ef892ff499584ccf403f3837e3a33979eb3e43f7d66a9","stem_ja":"長期のDatabricksエージェントタスクが、多段階のデータベーススキーマ移行を行います。抽出、変換、検証、ロード、索引作成の各段階は、直前の段階の正確で検証済みの出力へ厳密に依存します。例えばロード段階の出力スキーマが確定するまで、索引作成は開始できません。現在の戦略は処理量を最大化するため5段階を独立したサブエージェントで並列実行し、各担当は実際の確認済み出力を待たず、前段の出力について最初の推測を前提に開始します。本番では、実際の出力が次の担当の仮定と少しでも違うと、移行が破損します。\n\nどの長期戦略の不一致があり、何へ置き換えるべきですか。","options_ja":["並列の方が速いのに逐次戦略を使っている不一致です。依存関係によらず高速な処理量が常に優れるので、現在の並列設計を維持します。","実行順序とは無関係な不一致で、モデルの能力不足が本当の問題です。並列のファンアウト設計を変えずに大きなモデルへ置き換えます。","各段階が正しく進むために前段の正確な確認済み出力を必要とする、厳密な逐次依存のタスクへ並列・ファンアウト戦略を適用した不一致です。直前の実出力を検証して利用可能になった後だけ各段階を始める、逐次または依存関係で実行を制御する戦略へ置き換えます。","不一致はありません。多段階データベース移行の破損は、どんなオーケストレーション戦略でも避けられない本質的リスクです。"],"explanation_ja":"前段の出力が次段の入力を決めるため、推測で全段階を並列に進めてはいけません。各段階の実出力を検証し、成功を条件に次段を開始する依存ゲートを設けます。段階内の独立処理は並列化できますが、抽出→変換→検証→ロード→索引の必須依存は守ります。","distractors_ja":["現在はすでに並列実行で、それが不整合を生んでいます。依存を無視した高速化では正しい移行になりません。","大きいモデルでも、前段がまだ生成していない実際の出力を知ることはできません。依存と状態管理の問題です。","確認済みの実出力がそろうまで次段を止めるため、仮定したスキーマと実際のスキーマのずれを防げます。","推測入力による破損は避けられます。明示的な依存、検証、トランザクション、再試行設計で減らすべき問題です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"}]},{"id":"CE-PRACTICE-2","title":"Context Engineer 模試2","minutes":90,"questions":[{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-001","number":1,"form":2,"stem":"A Databricks agent operates in a single continuous session handling two distinct workstreams for the same team: drafting creative marketing copy for a product launch, and auditing that same product's compliance documentation against regulatory requirements. Both workstreams share one long-running conversation thread. Over time, the compliance-audit portions of the agent's output begin adopting the promotional, persuasive tone of the marketing drafts, and on one occasion the agent describes a compliance finding using marketing superlatives inappropriate for an audit report.\n\nWhich context management technique most directly addresses this failure?","options":["Apply context quarantine by isolating the marketing and compliance-audit work into separate agent contexts (e.g., separate threads or sub-agents), so tone and content from one workstream cannot bleed into the other.","Enable extended thinking mode so the agent reasons more carefully before switching between tasks within the same thread.","Reduce the number of tools available to the agent, since fewer tools will naturally prevent tone drift between tasks.","Increase the compaction frequency so the shared thread is summarized more often, preserving both workstreams' tone within the same summary."],"correct":0,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"異なる業務のコンテキスト隔離","concept":"ce-f2-q001","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Apply context quarantine by isolating the marketing and compliance-audit work into separate agent contexts (e.g., separate threads or sub-agents), so tone and content from one workstream cannot bleed into the other.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe agent is performing two workstreams with fundamentally different objectives and behavioral requirements:\n\n- Marketing copy should be creative, persuasive, and promotional.\n\n- Compliance auditing should be objective, evidence-based, restrained, and precise.\n\nBecause both workstreams share the same long-running thread, marketing instructions, examples, vocabulary, and prior outputs remain available when the agent performs compliance work. The appearance of promotional superlatives in an audit finding is evidence of cross-task context leakage.\n\nThe most direct solution is to establish a context boundary between the workstreams. Separate threads or specialized sub-agents prevent marketing history from becoming part of the compliance agent’s working context.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nContext quarantine isolates information belonging to one task, domain, or workstream so that it cannot improperly influence another.\n\nOption A addresses the root cause by giving the workstreams independent contexts:\n\n- The marketing context can contain promotional examples, brand voice, audience information, and creative drafts.\n\n- The compliance context can contain regulatory requirements, evidence, findings, control mappings, and formal reporting instructions.\n\n- Each context can have a separate system prompt, tools, memory scope, evaluation criteria, and output format.\n\n- Only explicitly selected information needs to cross the boundary.\n\nA practical design could use either:\n\n- Separate conversation threads, when the same agent can handle both tasks but should not share their histories.\n\n- Specialized sub-agents, when each workstream needs distinct instructions, tools, tone, permissions, or evaluation criteria.\n\nThe latter could use a coordinator that routes marketing requests to a marketing sub-agent and audit requests to a compliance sub-agent. Each sub-agent receives only the context required for its assigned domain.\n\nThis is more precise than trying to make one increasingly complex prompt manage two incompatible behavioral modes inside the same accumulated conversation.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Enable extended thinking mode so the agent reasons more carefully before switching between tasks within the same thread.\n\nExtended thinking provides additional reasoning capacity, but it does not remove the marketing material from the compliance task’s context.\n\nThe agent would still see both:\n\n- Promotional language and earlier marketing drafts.\n\n- Compliance evidence and audit instructions.\n\nMore reasoning may help with a difficult regulatory analysis, but it does not create an isolation boundary. It could also increase token usage and latency without reliably preventing tone contamination.\n\nThe failure concerns which context the agent receives, not how much computation it performs over that context.\n\nC. Reduce the number of tools available to the agent, since fewer tools will naturally prevent tone drift between tasks.\n\nTool reduction can lower context-window pressure and reduce tool-selection ambiguity, but the failure is not caused by excessive or overlapping tools.\n\nThe inappropriate language comes from conversational history and cross-workstream context. Even an agent with no tools could reproduce the marketing tone if previous promotional drafts remain in its active context.\n\nSeparate tool sets could be useful after creating specialized agents—for example, giving the compliance agent access only to regulatory and evidence-retrieval tools—but merely reducing the total number of tools does not solve the demonstrated problem.\n\nD. Increase the compaction frequency so the shared thread is summarized more often, preserving both workstreams' tone within the same summary.\n\nCompaction reduces the size of a long-running context, but it does not necessarily separate incompatible workstreams.\n\nA shared summary that preserves both marketing and compliance information could continue carrying promotional vocabulary into later audit tasks. It may even make the contamination harder to identify because the two workstreams are blended into a condensed representation.\n\nCompaction is appropriate when context-window growth is the main concern. Here, the more direct requirement is to control context boundaries, not merely context size.\n\nSeparate threads can still be compacted independently if either workstream becomes long-running.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe likely distractor is D because the scenario involves a long-running conversation.\n\nHowever, the observable failure is not simply context-window exhaustion. It is semantic and tonal leakage between incompatible workstreams. Summarizing the shared history does not guarantee isolation; separate contexts do.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nIn production, agent boundaries should reflect meaningful differences in task purpose, risk, tone, tools, and governance.\n\nA strong design would include:\n\n- A marketing agent with promotional style instructions and creative resources.\n\n- A compliance agent with evidence-grounding requirements, restrained language, regulatory sources, and audit-specific output schemas.\n\n- A coordinator that classifies the request and routes it to the correct specialist.\n\n- Separate thread identifiers and memory namespaces for each workstream.\n\n- Explicit handoff schemas that pass only necessary product facts between agents.\n\n- Evaluation rules that flag persuasive language, unsupported superlatives, or marketing claims in audit outputs.\n\nFor the certification exam, context quarantine through separate threads or sub-agents is the most direct answer. In a real implementation, the team should also govern what information may cross the boundary and test each workstream independently.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks Certified Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n[https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-07/databricks-certified-context-engineer-associate-exam-guide.pdf](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-07/databricks-certified-context-engineer-associate-exam-guide.pdf)\n\n- Build a multi-agent system on Databricks Apps\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/multi-agent-apps](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/multi-agent-apps)\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- Context engineering in Deep Agents\n[https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/deepagents/context-engineering](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/deepagents/context-engineering)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Apply context quarantine by isolating the marketing and compliance-audit work into separate agent contexts (e.g., separate threads or sub-agents), so tone and content from one workstream cannot bleed into the other.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe agent is performing two workstreams with fundamentally different objectives and behavioral requirements:\n\n- Marketing copy should be creative, persuasive, and promotional.\n\n- Compliance auditing should be objective, evidence-based, restrained, and precise.\n\nBecause both workstreams share the same long-running thread, marketing instructions, examples, vocabulary, and prior outputs remain available when the agent performs compliance work. The appearance of promotional superlatives in an audit finding is evidence of cross-task context leakage.\n\nThe most direct solution is to establish a context boundary between the workstreams. Separate threads or specialized sub-agents prevent marketing history from becoming part of the compliance agent’s working context.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nContext quarantine isolates information belonging to one task, domain, or workstream so that it cannot improperly influence another.\n\nOption A addresses the root cause by giving the workstreams independent contexts:\n\n- The marketing context can contain promotional examples, brand voice, audience information, and creative drafts.\n\n- The compliance context can contain regulatory requirements, evidence, findings, control mappings, and formal reporting instructions.\n\n- Each context can have a separate system prompt, tools, memory scope, evaluation criteria, and output format.\n\n- Only explicitly selected information needs to cross the boundary.\n\nA practical design could use either:\n\n- Separate conversation threads, when the same agent can handle both tasks but should not share their histories.\n\n- Specialized sub-agents, when each workstream needs distinct instructions, tools, tone, permissions, or evaluation criteria.\n\nThe latter could use a coordinator that routes marketing requests to a marketing sub-agent and audit requests to a compliance sub-agent. Each sub-agent receives only the context required for its assigned domain.\n\nThis is more precise than trying to make one increasingly complex prompt manage two incompatible behavioral modes inside the same accumulated conversation.","B. Enable extended thinking mode so the agent reasons more carefully before switching between tasks within the same thread.\n\nExtended thinking provides additional reasoning capacity, but it does not remove the marketing material from the compliance task’s context.\n\nThe agent would still see both:\n\n- Promotional language and earlier marketing drafts.\n\n- Compliance evidence and audit instructions.\n\nMore reasoning may help with a difficult regulatory analysis, but it does not create an isolation boundary. It could also increase token usage and latency without reliably preventing tone contamination.\n\nThe failure concerns which context the agent receives, not how much computation it performs over that context.","C. Reduce the number of tools available to the agent, since fewer tools will naturally prevent tone drift between tasks.\n\nTool reduction can lower context-window pressure and reduce tool-selection ambiguity, but the failure is not caused by excessive or overlapping tools.\n\nThe inappropriate language comes from conversational history and cross-workstream context. Even an agent with no tools could reproduce the marketing tone if previous promotional drafts remain in its active context.\n\nSeparate tool sets could be useful after creating specialized agents—for example, giving the compliance agent access only to regulatory and evidence-retrieval tools—but merely reducing the total number of tools does not solve the demonstrated problem.","D. Increase the compaction frequency so the shared thread is summarized more often, preserving both workstreams' tone within the same summary.\n\nCompaction reduces the size of a long-running context, but it does not necessarily separate incompatible workstreams.\n\nA shared summary that preserves both marketing and compliance information could continue carrying promotional vocabulary into later audit tasks. It may even make the contamination harder to identify because the two workstreams are blended into a condensed representation.\n\nCompaction is appropriate when context-window growth is the main concern. Here, the more direct requirement is to control context boundaries, not merely context size.\n\nSeparate threads can still be compacted independently if either workstream becomes long-running."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-07/databricks-certified-context-engineer-associate-exam-guide.pdf","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/multi-agent-apps","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/deepagents/context-engineering"],"source_hash":"7bc1757a3b7b054ebe8cb3f6416356bd4d943a16e0e2b6098cc7a2973a2ec3fc","stem_ja":"Databricksのエージェントが、同じチームの2つの異なる業務を、1つの連続したセッションで処理しています。製品発売向けの創造的なマーケティング文案の作成と、同じ製品のコンプライアンス文書を規制要件に照らして監査する業務です。両業務は1つの長時間の会話スレッドを共有しています。時間がたつにつれ、コンプライアンス監査の出力にもマーケティング原稿の宣伝的で説得的な語調が現れ始めました。あるときは、監査報告に不適切なマーケティング上の最上級表現で、コンプライアンス上の指摘を説明しました。\n\nこの障害に最も直接対処するコンテキスト管理手法はどれですか。","options_ja":["マーケティングとコンプライアンス監査を、別スレッドやサブエージェントなどの独立したエージェントコンテキストに分離するコンテキスト隔離を適用し、一方の業務の語調や内容が他方へ混入しないようにする。","拡張思考モードを有効にして、同じスレッド内でタスクを切り替える前に、エージェントがより慎重に推論するようにする。","ツールを減らせばタスク間の語調のずれも自然に防げるため、エージェントが利用できるツール数を減らす。","圧縮の頻度を増やして共有スレッドをより頻繁に要約し、両業務の語調を同じ要約内に保持する。"],"explanation_ja":"原因は容量不足ではなく、異なる目的の会話履歴が混ざることです。宣伝では有効な説得的表現も、監査では不適切です。業務ごとにスレッドやサブエージェントを分け、必要な製品の事実だけを明示して引き渡します。覚え方は「混ざって困る業務は、要約する前に隔離する」です。","distractors_ja":["業務間に境界を設けるため、宣伝の文例や語調が監査の作業コンテキストへ入ることを直接防げます。","推論量を増やしても、宣伝文案が監査のコンテキストに残る構造は変わりません。","今回はツールの多さではなく、異なる業務の会話履歴の共有が原因です。","両業務を一緒に要約すると、宣伝の語調を監査へ持ち越す可能性が残ります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-002","number":2,"form":2,"stem":"A legal-research agent is configured so that, on every turn regardless of the question, the full text of a 40,000-token case-law abstract corpus is loaded into the system prompt, and every retrieval tool call returns complete, unfiltered case documents (headnotes, procedural history, full citations, dissenting opinions) even when the user's question only concerns a single holding. Sessions currently run short, so compaction has not yet triggered, but the team wants to reduce context window pressure proactively.\n\nSelect the two proactive strategies that would most directly reduce context window pressure before compaction becomes necessary.","options":["Enable extended thinking mode so the agent reasons more carefully about which parts of the large corpus to focus on.","Replace the always-loaded full case-law corpus with just-in-time retrieval, so relevant case content is fetched only when a specific question requires it, rather than being loaded on every turn regardless of relevance.","Scope tool results so retrieval calls return only the specific holding or passage relevant to the question, rather than the complete case document with all sections included by default.","Increase the model's context window so the full corpus and complete case documents fit comfortably without needing to trim anything."],"correct":1,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"必要時検索とツール結果の絞り込み","concept":"ce-f2-q002","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Replace the always-loaded full case-law corpus with just-in-time retrieval, so relevant case content is fetched only when a specific question requires it, rather than being loaded on every turn regardless of relevance.\n\nC. Scope tool results so retrieval calls return only the specific holding or passage relevant to the question, rather than the complete case document with all sections included by default.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question asks for two proactive strategies that reduce context-window pressure before compaction becomes necessary.\n\nThere are two direct sources of unnecessary token consumption:\n\n- A 40,000-token corpus is inserted into every prompt regardless of the question.\n\n- Retrieval tools return complete case documents when only a narrow holding or passage is required.\n\nOptions B and C address these sources at the point where context is assembled:\n\n- B prevents irrelevant knowledge from entering every model call.\n\n- C limits each tool response to the smallest useful evidence set.\n\nTogether, they reduce model input size, improve the signal-to-noise ratio, lower latency and token consumption, and postpone the need for compaction.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nB. Replace the always-loaded full case-law corpus with just-in-time retrieval\n\nJust-in-time retrieval keeps the large corpus outside the active prompt and queries it only when the user’s request requires case-law evidence.\n\nThis follows the core retrieval-augmented generation pattern:\n\n- The user submits a question.\n\n- The application searches an external knowledge source.\n\n- Only relevant supporting passages are added to the prompt.\n\n- The model generates an answer grounded in those passages.\n\nThe entire corpus remains available as a capability, but it no longer consumes 40,000 tokens on every turn. This is more efficient than treating the full corpus as static system-prompt content.\n\nC. Scope tool results to the relevant holding or passage\n\nEven after adopting just-in-time retrieval, returning an entire case can still introduce substantial unnecessary context. The tool should return only the portions needed for the current legal question, such as:\n\n- The relevant holding.\n\n- A directly supporting passage.\n\n- Essential jurisdiction and citation metadata.\n\n- Limited surrounding language needed to interpret the holding correctly.\n\nThis does not mean blindly truncating documents. Retrieval must preserve sufficient legal context to avoid misrepresenting the ruling. However, headnotes, full procedural histories, unrelated dissents, and complete citation chains should not be included automatically when they do not help answer the question.\n\nOptions B and C are complementary:\n\n- B controls when information enters the context.\n\n- C controls how much retrieved information enters the context.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Enable extended thinking mode so the agent reasons more carefully about which parts of the large corpus to focus on.\n\nExtended reasoning changes how much computation the model uses, but it does not reduce the number of input tokens.\n\nThe model would still receive:\n\n- The entire 40,000-token corpus on every turn.\n\n- Complete, unfiltered case documents from tool calls.\n\nIt may reason more extensively over the oversized prompt, potentially increasing latency and cost, but the context-window pressure remains. Context composition should be corrected before adding more reasoning effort.\n\nD. Increase the model's context window so the full corpus and complete case documents fit comfortably without needing to trim anything.\n\nA larger context window increases capacity but does not eliminate unnecessary context.\n\nThe scenario does not require every case and every document section for every question. Supplying all available information can still:\n\n- Increase inference cost and latency.\n\n- Reduce the prominence of the relevant passage.\n\n- Introduce unrelated or conflicting legal material.\n\n- Make grounded evaluation and citation selection more difficult.\n\n- Eventually recreate the same capacity problem as more content accumulates.\n\nA larger-window model can be appropriate when all supplied information is genuinely necessary. It should not replace retrieval and result-scoping when most of the content is irrelevant to the current request.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe principal distractor is D. A larger context window may prevent an immediate overflow, but it treats context capacity rather than poor context selection.\n\nThe question asks for proactive context-management strategies, making selective, just-in-time context assembly the expected approach.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production legal-research agent should keep the case-law corpus in an indexed retrieval system rather than embedding it permanently in the system prompt.\n\nA strong implementation would:\n\n- Retrieve cases only when the current question requires them.\n\n- Apply jurisdiction, court, date, authority, and document-type filters.\n\n- Retrieve a small number of highly relevant chunks.\n\n- Rerank results before adding them to the prompt.\n\n- Return the holding plus enough surrounding text to preserve legal meaning.\n\n- Keep full case documents externally accessible through references or follow-up tools.\n\n- Measure retrieval precision, recall, groundedness, latency, and token consumption.\n\nCompaction remains valuable for long-running conversation history. However, it should not be used as a substitute for preventing oversized static prompts and verbose tool outputs from entering the context in the first place.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks Certified Context Engineer Associate](https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate)\n\n- [RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) on Databricks](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation)\n\n- [AI Search Retrieval Quality Guide](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality)\n\n- [Context Engineering in Agents](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/context-engineering)","source_option_explanations":["A. Enable extended thinking mode so the agent reasons more carefully about which parts of the large corpus to focus on.\n\nExtended reasoning changes how much computation the model uses, but it does not reduce the number of input tokens.\n\nThe model would still receive:\n\n- The entire 40,000-token corpus on every turn.\n\n- Complete, unfiltered case documents from tool calls.\n\nIt may reason more extensively over the oversized prompt, potentially increasing latency and cost, but the context-window pressure remains. Context composition should be corrected before adding more reasoning effort.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Replace the always-loaded full case-law corpus with just-in-time retrieval, so relevant case content is fetched only when a specific question requires it, rather than being loaded on every turn regardless of relevance.\n\nC. Scope tool results so retrieval calls return only the specific holding or passage relevant to the question, rather than the complete case document with all sections included by default.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question asks for two proactive strategies that reduce context-window pressure before compaction becomes necessary.\n\nThere are two direct sources of unnecessary token consumption:\n\n- A 40,000-token corpus is inserted into every prompt regardless of the question.\n\n- Retrieval tools return complete case documents when only a narrow holding or passage is required.\n\nOptions B and C address these sources at the point where context is assembled:\n\n- B prevents irrelevant knowledge from entering every model call.\n\n- C limits each tool response to the smallest useful evidence set.\n\nTogether, they reduce model input size, improve the signal-to-noise ratio, lower latency and token consumption, and postpone the need for compaction.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nB. Replace the always-loaded full case-law corpus with just-in-time retrieval\n\nJust-in-time retrieval keeps the large corpus outside the active prompt and queries it only when the user’s request requires case-law evidence.\n\nThis follows the core retrieval-augmented generation pattern:\n\n- The user submits a question.\n\n- The application searches an external knowledge source.\n\n- Only relevant supporting passages are added to the prompt.\n\n- The model generates an answer grounded in those passages.\n\nThe entire corpus remains available as a capability, but it no longer consumes 40,000 tokens on every turn. This is more efficient than treating the full corpus as static system-prompt content.\n\nC. Scope tool results to the relevant holding or passage\n\nEven after adopting just-in-time retrieval, returning an entire case can still introduce substantial unnecessary context. The tool should return only the portions needed for the current legal question, such as:\n\n- The relevant holding.\n\n- A directly supporting passage.\n\n- Essential jurisdiction and citation metadata.\n\n- Limited surrounding language needed to interpret the holding correctly.\n\nThis does not mean blindly truncating documents. Retrieval must preserve sufficient legal context to avoid misrepresenting the ruling. However, headnotes, full procedural histories, unrelated dissents, and complete citation chains should not be included automatically when they do not help answer the question.\n\nOptions B and C are complementary:\n\n- B controls when information enters the context.\n\n- C controls how much retrieved information enters the context.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Replace the always-loaded full case-law corpus with just-in-time retrieval, so relevant case content is fetched only when a specific question requires it, rather than being loaded on every turn regardless of relevance.\n\nC. Scope tool results so retrieval calls return only the specific holding or passage relevant to the question, rather than the complete case document with all sections included by default.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question asks for two proactive strategies that reduce context-window pressure before compaction becomes necessary.\n\nThere are two direct sources of unnecessary token consumption:\n\n- A 40,000-token corpus is inserted into every prompt regardless of the question.\n\n- Retrieval tools return complete case documents when only a narrow holding or passage is required.\n\nOptions B and C address these sources at the point where context is assembled:\n\n- B prevents irrelevant knowledge from entering every model call.\n\n- C limits each tool response to the smallest useful evidence set.\n\nTogether, they reduce model input size, improve the signal-to-noise ratio, lower latency and token consumption, and postpone the need for compaction.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nB. Replace the always-loaded full case-law corpus with just-in-time retrieval\n\nJust-in-time retrieval keeps the large corpus outside the active prompt and queries it only when the user’s request requires case-law evidence.\n\nThis follows the core retrieval-augmented generation pattern:\n\n- The user submits a question.\n\n- The application searches an external knowledge source.\n\n- Only relevant supporting passages are added to the prompt.\n\n- The model generates an answer grounded in those passages.\n\nThe entire corpus remains available as a capability, but it no longer consumes 40,000 tokens on every turn. This is more efficient than treating the full corpus as static system-prompt content.\n\nC. Scope tool results to the relevant holding or passage\n\nEven after adopting just-in-time retrieval, returning an entire case can still introduce substantial unnecessary context. The tool should return only the portions needed for the current legal question, such as:\n\n- The relevant holding.\n\n- A directly supporting passage.\n\n- Essential jurisdiction and citation metadata.\n\n- Limited surrounding language needed to interpret the holding correctly.\n\nThis does not mean blindly truncating documents. Retrieval must preserve sufficient legal context to avoid misrepresenting the ruling. However, headnotes, full procedural histories, unrelated dissents, and complete citation chains should not be included automatically when they do not help answer the question.\n\nOptions B and C are complementary:\n\n- B controls when information enters the context.\n\n- C controls how much retrieved information enters the context.","D. Increase the model's context window so the full corpus and complete case documents fit comfortably without needing to trim anything.\n\nA larger context window increases capacity but does not eliminate unnecessary context.\n\nThe scenario does not require every case and every document section for every question. Supplying all available information can still:\n\n- Increase inference cost and latency.\n\n- Reduce the prominence of the relevant passage.\n\n- Introduce unrelated or conflicting legal material.\n\n- Make grounded evaluation and citation selection more difficult.\n\n- Eventually recreate the same capacity problem as more content accumulates.\n\nA larger-window model can be appropriate when all supplied information is genuinely necessary. It should not replace retrieval and result-scoping when most of the content is irrelevant to the current request."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/context-engineering"],"source_hash":"5fabefdc17a22e0f1ab6641513547af3bee89c93dbbb660b756a81fc0480b6ab","correct_many":[1,2],"stem_ja":"法律調査エージェントは、質問の内容にかかわらず、毎ターン40,000トークンの判例要旨コーパス全文をシステムプロンプトへ読み込む構成です。検索ツールも、ユーザーの質問が1つの判旨だけに関する場合でも、判例要旨、訴訟手続の経緯、引用情報全文、反対意見を含む、絞り込みのない判決文全体を毎回返します。現在のセッションは短いため、まだ圧縮は実行されていませんが、チームはコンテキストウィンドウへの負荷を事前に減らしたいと考えています。\n\n圧縮が必要になる前に、その負荷を最も直接減らす予防的な方策を2つ選んでください。","options_ja":["拡張思考モードを有効にして、大きなコーパスのどの部分に注目すべきか、より慎重に推論させる。","常時読み込む判例コーパス全文を必要時の検索に置き換え、関連性にかかわらず毎ターン読み込むのではなく、個別の質問で必要になった場合にだけ関連する判例内容を取得する。","検索ツールがすべての節を含む判決文全体を既定で返すのではなく、質問に関連する特定の判旨や箇所だけを返すように結果の範囲を絞る。","モデルのコンテキストウィンドウを大きくして、何も切り詰めずにコーパス全文と判決文全体を余裕を持って収める。"],"explanation_ja":"入口で不要な情報を入れないことが、圧縮より先の対策です。必要時検索は「いつ知識を入れるか」、結果の絞り込みは「どれだけ入れるか」を制御します。必要な判旨と、その解釈に必要な引用や前後関係だけを渡せば、検索機能を失わずにトークン負荷を減らせます。","distractors_ja":["思考を深くしても、入力される40,000トークンや判決文全文は減りません。","質問に必要な知識だけをその都度検索することで、無関係なコーパス全文の常時投入をなくせます。","判旨と必要な補足箇所に結果を限定し、ツールが返す不要なトークンを減らせます。","容量は増えますが、不要な情報の投入や、そのコスト・注意の分散を直接解決しません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-003","number":3,"form":2,"stem":"A coding agent is debugging a data pipeline failure. Early in the session, without verifying against the actual database configuration, the agent states: \"Since the database stores all timestamps in UTC, the mismatch must be in the client-side conversion.\" This assumption is incorrect — the database actually stores timestamps in the server's local time zone — but it is never checked or corrected. Over the next 30 turns, the agent repeatedly justifies successive fix attempts by referencing back to its earlier statement about UTC storage, and each attempted fix fails for reasons consistent with the assumption being wrong, though the agent doesn't recognize this. The final patch it proposes is incorrect specifically because it's built on the original faulty assumption.\n\nWhich context failure mode does this trace exhibit?","options":["Context distraction, because the growing conversation history caused the agent to over-focus on accumulated context rather than its trained knowledge of time zone handling.","Context confusion, because superfluous, irrelevant information in the context was used to generate a low-quality response.","Context clash, because the context contained two contradictory statements about the database's time zone handling that the agent could not reconcile.","Context poisoning, because an unverified, incorrect claim entered the context early on and was repeatedly referenced and built upon in later reasoning, compounding into a wrong final result."],"correct":3,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"誤った前提によるコンテキスト汚染","concept":"ce-f2-q003","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Context poisoning, because an unverified, incorrect claim entered the context early on and was repeatedly referenced and built upon in later reasoning, compounding into a wrong final result.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe critical sequence is:\n\n- The agent introduces an incorrect, unverified assumption that the database stores timestamps in UTC.\n\n- The assumption remains in the conversation context without validation or correction.\n\n- The agent repeatedly treats that assumption as an established fact over the next 30 turns.\n\n- Each new diagnosis and proposed fix is built on the faulty premise.\n\n- The error compounds until it directly causes the incorrect final patch.\n\nThis is the defining pattern of context poisoning: erroneous information enters the agent’s active context and subsequently contaminates later reasoning.\n\nThe repeated failures are not merely caused by a long conversation. They result from the agent continually trusting and propagating one false contextual claim.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nContext poisoning occurs when inaccurate or misleading information becomes part of the context and is subsequently treated as reliable evidence.\n\nThe initial UTC statement acts as the poison:\n\n“Since the database stores all timestamps in UTC…”\n\nBecause the agent never verifies this assumption against the database configuration, later reasoning inherits a false foundation. Each unsuccessful attempt then becomes interpreted through that same false premise rather than prompting the agent to re-examine it.\n\nThe strongest indicators are:\n\n- The claim is factually incorrect.\n\n- It enters the context early.\n\n- It is repeatedly referenced as established truth.\n\n- Later decisions depend on it.\n\n- Its influence compounds over multiple turns.\n\n- The final output is wrong specifically because of it.\n\nThis is different from an isolated hallucination. The important feature is the continued reuse and propagation of the erroneous claim through the agent’s subsequent reasoning.\n\nIn this exam context, “context poisoning” refers to contamination of the agent’s inference-time context. It should not be confused with adversarial poisoning of model-training data.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Context distraction, because the growing conversation history caused the agent to over-focus on accumulated context rather than its trained knowledge of time zone handling.\n\nThe session is long, but length alone is not the demonstrated cause of failure.\n\nContext distraction would involve excessive accumulated content consuming attention and causing the model to neglect more relevant information. Here, the trace identifies a specific false claim that repeatedly controls the reasoning.\n\nThe agent is not simply distracted by a large history. It is consistently reasoning from poisoned information contained within that history.\n\nB. Context confusion, because superfluous, irrelevant information in the context was used to generate a low-quality response.\n\nThe incorrect UTC claim is not superfluous or unrelated to the problem. It is directly relevant to timestamp handling, but factually wrong.\n\nContext confusion is more appropriate when irrelevant or mismatched information is included and the model improperly uses it—for example, applying configuration details from a different database environment.\n\nHere, the failure comes from a relevant but false assumption becoming an established premise, which is context poisoning.\n\nC. Context clash, because the context contained two contradictory statements about the database's time zone handling that the agent could not reconcile.\n\nThe trace does not show two conflicting contextual claims.\n\nOnly the incorrect statement that the database stores timestamps in UTC is present in the agent’s context. The actual fact—that timestamps are stored in the server’s local time zone—is external ground truth that the agent never retrieves or verifies.\n\nA context clash would require both claims to be present simultaneously, such as:\n\n- One configuration result stating UTC.\n\n- Another authoritative result stating server-local time.\n\nThe agent would then have to reconcile contradictory evidence. That does not occur here.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe primary distractor is A because the question mentions 30 turns and a growing history.\n\nHowever, the failure is not described as general attention degradation. A specific false claim enters the context and repeatedly shapes later reasoning. The candidate must identify the causal information defect, not merely the length of the session.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production coding agent should distinguish verified facts from working hypotheses.\n\nFor this scenario, the agent should have queried an authoritative source before proposing fixes, such as:\n\n- The database server’s time-zone configuration.\n\n- The relevant column data types and timestamp semantics.\n\n- The session or connection time zone.\n\n- The client driver’s conversion settings.\n\n- A sample stored value compared with its source timestamp.\n\nUseful safeguards include:\n\n- Labeling unverified statements explicitly as assumptions.\n\n- Recording provenance for configuration facts.\n\n- Requiring tool-based verification before foundational assumptions are reused.\n\n- Re-evaluating assumptions after repeated failed attempts.\n\n- Removing or correcting invalid facts in retained thread state.\n\n- Using MLflow Tracing to identify the first incorrect premise and observe how it propagates through later model and tool interactions.\n\nFor the exam, the answer is context poisoning. In a real implementation, the remediation is to validate the premise, repair the active context, and restart the diagnosis from the verified database behavior.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks Certified Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-07/databricks-certified-context-engineer-associate-exam-guide.pdf)\n\n- [Databricks Certified Context Engineer Associate](https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate)\n\n- [MLflow Tracing – GenAI Observability](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/)\n\n- [Evaluate and Monitor AI Agents](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor)","source_option_explanations":["A. Context distraction, because the growing conversation history caused the agent to over-focus on accumulated context rather than its trained knowledge of time zone handling.\n\nThe session is long, but length alone is not the demonstrated cause of failure.\n\nContext distraction would involve excessive accumulated content consuming attention and causing the model to neglect more relevant information. Here, the trace identifies a specific false claim that repeatedly controls the reasoning.\n\nThe agent is not simply distracted by a large history. It is consistently reasoning from poisoned information contained within that history.","B. Context confusion, because superfluous, irrelevant information in the context was used to generate a low-quality response.\n\nThe incorrect UTC claim is not superfluous or unrelated to the problem. It is directly relevant to timestamp handling, but factually wrong.\n\nContext confusion is more appropriate when irrelevant or mismatched information is included and the model improperly uses it—for example, applying configuration details from a different database environment.\n\nHere, the failure comes from a relevant but false assumption becoming an established premise, which is context poisoning.","C. Context clash, because the context contained two contradictory statements about the database's time zone handling that the agent could not reconcile.\n\nThe trace does not show two conflicting contextual claims.\n\nOnly the incorrect statement that the database stores timestamps in UTC is present in the agent’s context. The actual fact—that timestamps are stored in the server’s local time zone—is external ground truth that the agent never retrieves or verifies.\n\nA context clash would require both claims to be present simultaneously, such as:\n\n- One configuration result stating UTC.\n\n- Another authoritative result stating server-local time.\n\nThe agent would then have to reconcile contradictory evidence. That does not occur here.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Context poisoning, because an unverified, incorrect claim entered the context early on and was repeatedly referenced and built upon in later reasoning, compounding into a wrong final result.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe critical sequence is:\n\n- The agent introduces an incorrect, unverified assumption that the database stores timestamps in UTC.\n\n- The assumption remains in the conversation context without validation or correction.\n\n- The agent repeatedly treats that assumption as an established fact over the next 30 turns.\n\n- Each new diagnosis and proposed fix is built on the faulty premise.\n\n- The error compounds until it directly causes the incorrect final patch.\n\nThis is the defining pattern of context poisoning: erroneous information enters the agent’s active context and subsequently contaminates later reasoning.\n\nThe repeated failures are not merely caused by a long conversation. They result from the agent continually trusting and propagating one false contextual claim.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nContext poisoning occurs when inaccurate or misleading information becomes part of the context and is subsequently treated as reliable evidence.\n\nThe initial UTC statement acts as the poison:\n\n“Since the database stores all timestamps in UTC…”\n\nBecause the agent never verifies this assumption against the database configuration, later reasoning inherits a false foundation. Each unsuccessful attempt then becomes interpreted through that same false premise rather than prompting the agent to re-examine it.\n\nThe strongest indicators are:\n\n- The claim is factually incorrect.\n\n- It enters the context early.\n\n- It is repeatedly referenced as established truth.\n\n- Later decisions depend on it.\n\n- Its influence compounds over multiple turns.\n\n- The final output is wrong specifically because of it.\n\nThis is different from an isolated hallucination. The important feature is the continued reuse and propagation of the erroneous claim through the agent’s subsequent reasoning.\n\nIn this exam context, “context poisoning” refers to contamination of the agent’s inference-time context. It should not be confused with adversarial poisoning of model-training data."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-07/databricks-certified-context-engineer-associate-exam-guide.pdf","https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor"],"source_hash":"6c611c4e0d80ed07342406f53da045d10375a265f27d7955a607d256547c5e9f","stem_ja":"コーディングエージェントがデータパイプラインの障害を調査しています。セッションの初期に、実際のデータベース設定を確かめず、「データベースはすべてのタイムスタンプをUTCで保存するため、不一致の原因はクライアント側の変換に違いない」と述べました。この前提は誤りで、実際にはデータベースはサーバーのローカルタイムゾーンで保存していますが、前提が検証・修正されることはありません。その後30ターンにわたり、エージェントはUTC保存という自分の以前の発言を繰り返し根拠として修正を試みます。各修正は、前提が誤りであることと整合する理由で失敗しますが、エージェントは気づきません。最終的なパッチも、最初の誤った前提に基づくため不正確です。\n\nこのトレースが示すコンテキスト障害はどれですか。","options_ja":["会話履歴の増加により、学習済みのタイムゾーン処理の知識よりも蓄積したコンテキストに過度に注目したため、コンテキストの注意逸脱である。","余分で無関係な情報をコンテキストから使って品質の低い回答を生成したため、コンテキストの混乱である。","データベースのタイムゾーン処理に関する矛盾した2つの記述がコンテキストにあり、整合させられなかったため、コンテキストの衝突である。","未検証で誤った主張が初期にコンテキストへ入り、その後の推論で繰り返し参照されて積み重なり、最終結果も誤らせたため、コンテキスト汚染である。"],"explanation_ja":"決め手は「最初の誤情報が、後の推論の根拠として繰り返し使われた」ことです。これはコンテキスト汚染です。単に情報が多い、無関係な情報がある、矛盾する資料があるという問題ではありません。覚え方は「誤った前提が連鎖するなら汚染」。元の設定を検証して誤った記憶を修正する必要があります。","distractors_ja":["履歴の量自体より、特定の誤った主張を事実として再利用していることが中心です。","主張は無関係な雑音ではなく、解決方針を左右する誤情報として作用しています。","実際のローカル時刻設定は検証されておらず、矛盾する2つの確定情報を比較する場面ではありません。","未検証の誤りがコンテキストに入り、後続の判断を継続的に誤らせる流れを正確に表しています。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-004","number":4,"form":2,"stem":"A platform team supports several internal agent frameworks — one team uses LangGraph, another a custom Python harness, a third a no-code agent builder — that all need to call the same external shipping-carrier tracking API. Rather than writing separate, framework-specific integration code for each team, the platform team wants a single, standardized way to expose this external tool so any framework's agent can discover and call it consistently.\n\nWhich Databricks/AI ecosystem component should the platform team use to expose this external tool in a standardized, framework-agnostic way?","options":["MCP (Model Context Protocol), since it provides a standardized protocol for exposing tools and data sources that different agent frameworks can consistently discover and call.","Unity Catalog, since all external tool definitions must be stored as managed tables before any agent framework can call them.","MLflow 3, since its tracing capability is what allows different frameworks to share a single tool implementation.","Lakebase, since its Postgres compatibility allows any framework to query the shipping-carrier API through standard SQL."],"correct":0,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"MCPによるフレームワーク共通のツール公開","concept":"ce-f2-q004","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. MCP (Model Context Protocol), since it provides a standardized protocol for exposing tools and data sources that different agent frameworks can consistently discover and call.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe key requirements are:\n\n- One external shipping-carrier API.\n\n- Several different agent frameworks.\n\n- No framework-specific integration for each implementation.\n\n- Standardized tool discovery and invocation.\n\n- Consistent reuse across LangGraph, custom Python, and no-code agents.\n\nThese requirements point directly to Model Context Protocol. The platform team can implement or host an MCP server that wraps the carrier’s API and exposes operations such as `get_tracking_status`, `get_delivery_estimate`, or `get_shipment_events` as MCP tools.\n\nAny agent framework or builder with MCP client support can then discover the available tool definitions and invoke them through the same protocol rather than requiring a separate native integration.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nMCP defines a common interface between AI applications and external capabilities. An MCP server can publish:\n\n- Tool names and descriptions.\n\n- Input schemas.\n\n- Callable operations.\n\n- Structured results.\n\n- Resources and other contextual data where applicable.\n\nFor this scenario, the shipping integration is implemented once behind an MCP server. Each participating agent framework acts as an MCP client and uses the same exposed tool contract.\n\nWithin Databricks, the team can use an external or custom MCP server and make it available to agents. This provides the required separation:\n\n- The MCP server handles carrier-specific authentication, API requests, error translation, rate limits, and response normalization.\n\n- The agent frameworks handle reasoning and decide when to call the tools.\n\n- The MCP protocol standardizes discovery and invocation between them.\n\nThis is more reusable than creating separate LangGraph tools, Python wrapper functions, and no-code custom actions that each independently implement the carrier API.\n\nAn important real-world qualification is that each framework must have MCP client support, either natively or through an adapter. “Framework-agnostic” means the tool is exposed through a common protocol, not that an agent platform without MCP support can automatically consume it.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Unity Catalog, since all external tool definitions must be stored as managed tables before any agent framework can call them.\n\nUnity Catalog is Databricks’ governance layer for data and AI assets. It can govern assets such as functions, models, agents, and MCP servers, but it does not require external API tool definitions to be stored as managed tables.\n\nA managed table is not the standard interoperability mechanism for discovering and invoking external agent tools. Unity Catalog may complement the solution through permissions, governance, registration, and auditing, but MCP provides the framework-independent tool interface requested by the question.\n\nC. MLflow 3, since its tracing capability is what allows different frameworks to share a single tool implementation.\n\nMLflow Tracing provides observability into agent execution, including model calls, tool calls, inputs, outputs, latency, and errors.\n\nIt can trace agents built with different frameworks, but tracing does not expose a shared tool contract or provide standardized tool discovery and invocation. The teams would still need an integration mechanism for calling the carrier API.\n\nMLflow would be useful for monitoring the shared MCP tool—for example, measuring latency and diagnosing failed calls—but it is not the component that standardizes access to the tool.\n\nD. Lakebase, since its Postgres compatibility allows any framework to query the shipping-carrier API through standard SQL.\n\nLakebase is a managed Postgres database intended for transactional and operational workloads. PostgreSQL compatibility does not automatically convert an external carrier API into a SQL-queryable agent tool.\n\nThe platform team could store cached shipment data in Lakebase, but it would still need application code to:\n\n- Authenticate with the carrier.\n\n- Invoke the external API.\n\n- Refresh tracking information.\n\n- Handle carrier-specific errors and rate limits.\n\n- Expose the operation to agents.\n\nLakebase can support operational state or caching around the integration, but it does not provide the standardized agent-tool protocol requested in the scenario.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe likely distractor is B because Unity Catalog is associated with centralized governance and discoverability.\n\nThe distinction is:\n\n- MCP: Standardizes how agents discover and call tools.\n\n- Unity Catalog: Governs and controls access to supported data and AI assets.\n\n- MLflow: Observes and evaluates agent execution.\n\n- Lakebase: Stores low-latency transactional data and application state.\n\nThe phrase “framework-agnostic way” is the strongest signal for MCP.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe platform team should build a custom MCP server that wraps the carrier API and exposes narrowly scoped tools such as:\n\n- `get_tracking_status`\n\n- `get_tracking_events`\n\n- `get_estimated_delivery`\n\n- `report_delivery_exception`\n\nThe MCP server should centrally handle credentials, input validation, response normalization, timeouts, retries, rate limits, and audit logging. Destructive or sensitive operations should require stronger authorization than read-only tracking calls.\n\nDatabricks governance and observability can then complement MCP:\n\n- Register or govern MCP access through supported Databricks capabilities.\n\n- Restrict which tools each agent may use.\n\n- Trace tool execution with MLflow.\n\n- Store cached operational data in Lakebase when necessary.\n\nFor the certification exam, however, the component that directly provides standardized, framework-independent tool exposure is MCP.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Connect agents to tools — Databricks \n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/agent-tool](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/agent-tool)\n\n- Connect agents to third-party tools with MCP Services — Databricks \n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/mcp-services](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/mcp-services)\n\n- Use MCP servers in agents — Databricks \n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/mcp/use-mcp-in-agents](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/mcp/use-mcp-in-agents)\n\n- MCP Server Tools Specification — Model Context Protocol \n[https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/server/tools](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/server/tools)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. MCP (Model Context Protocol), since it provides a standardized protocol for exposing tools and data sources that different agent frameworks can consistently discover and call.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe key requirements are:\n\n- One external shipping-carrier API.\n\n- Several different agent frameworks.\n\n- No framework-specific integration for each implementation.\n\n- Standardized tool discovery and invocation.\n\n- Consistent reuse across LangGraph, custom Python, and no-code agents.\n\nThese requirements point directly to Model Context Protocol. The platform team can implement or host an MCP server that wraps the carrier’s API and exposes operations such as `get_tracking_status`, `get_delivery_estimate`, or `get_shipment_events` as MCP tools.\n\nAny agent framework or builder with MCP client support can then discover the available tool definitions and invoke them through the same protocol rather than requiring a separate native integration.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nMCP defines a common interface between AI applications and external capabilities. An MCP server can publish:\n\n- Tool names and descriptions.\n\n- Input schemas.\n\n- Callable operations.\n\n- Structured results.\n\n- Resources and other contextual data where applicable.\n\nFor this scenario, the shipping integration is implemented once behind an MCP server. Each participating agent framework acts as an MCP client and uses the same exposed tool contract.\n\nWithin Databricks, the team can use an external or custom MCP server and make it available to agents. This provides the required separation:\n\n- The MCP server handles carrier-specific authentication, API requests, error translation, rate limits, and response normalization.\n\n- The agent frameworks handle reasoning and decide when to call the tools.\n\n- The MCP protocol standardizes discovery and invocation between them.\n\nThis is more reusable than creating separate LangGraph tools, Python wrapper functions, and no-code custom actions that each independently implement the carrier API.\n\nAn important real-world qualification is that each framework must have MCP client support, either natively or through an adapter. “Framework-agnostic” means the tool is exposed through a common protocol, not that an agent platform without MCP support can automatically consume it.","B. Unity Catalog, since all external tool definitions must be stored as managed tables before any agent framework can call them.\n\nUnity Catalog is Databricks’ governance layer for data and AI assets. It can govern assets such as functions, models, agents, and MCP servers, but it does not require external API tool definitions to be stored as managed tables.\n\nA managed table is not the standard interoperability mechanism for discovering and invoking external agent tools. Unity Catalog may complement the solution through permissions, governance, registration, and auditing, but MCP provides the framework-independent tool interface requested by the question.","C. MLflow 3, since its tracing capability is what allows different frameworks to share a single tool implementation.\n\nMLflow Tracing provides observability into agent execution, including model calls, tool calls, inputs, outputs, latency, and errors.\n\nIt can trace agents built with different frameworks, but tracing does not expose a shared tool contract or provide standardized tool discovery and invocation. The teams would still need an integration mechanism for calling the carrier API.\n\nMLflow would be useful for monitoring the shared MCP tool—for example, measuring latency and diagnosing failed calls—but it is not the component that standardizes access to the tool.","D. Lakebase, since its Postgres compatibility allows any framework to query the shipping-carrier API through standard SQL.\n\nLakebase is a managed Postgres database intended for transactional and operational workloads. PostgreSQL compatibility does not automatically convert an external carrier API into a SQL-queryable agent tool.\n\nThe platform team could store cached shipment data in Lakebase, but it would still need application code to:\n\n- Authenticate with the carrier.\n\n- Invoke the external API.\n\n- Refresh tracking information.\n\n- Handle carrier-specific errors and rate limits.\n\n- Expose the operation to agents.\n\nLakebase can support operational state or caching around the integration, but it does not provide the standardized agent-tool protocol requested in the scenario."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/agent-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/mcp-services","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/mcp/use-mcp-in-agents","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/server/tools"],"source_hash":"45e8b51c7abefa86d0b4e4901027440dd65de26916c58948fca276148f4a9e43","stem_ja":"プラットフォームチームは、社内の複数のエージェントフレームワークを支援しています。あるチームはLangGraph、別のチームは独自のPython実行基盤、さらに別のチームはノーコードのエージェントビルダーを使っています。どのチームも、同じ外部配送業者の追跡APIを呼び出す必要があります。フレームワークごとに別の連携コードを書く代わりに、どのフレームワークのエージェントでも一貫して発見・呼び出しできる、単一の標準的な方法でこの外部ツールを公開したいと考えています。\n\nフレームワークに依存せず標準化して外部ツールを公開するには、Databricks／AIエコシステムのどの構成要素を使うべきですか。","options_ja":["異なるエージェントフレームワークが一貫して発見・呼び出しできる、ツールやデータソースの標準公開プロトコルを提供するMCP（Model Context Protocol）。","どのエージェントフレームワークでも外部ツールを呼び出す前に、その定義を管理対象テーブルとして保存しなければならないため、Unity Catalog。","トレース機能によって異なるフレームワークが1つのツール実装を共有できるため、MLflow 3。","Postgres互換性により、どのフレームワークからでも標準SQLで配送業者APIを照会できるため、Lakebase。"],"explanation_ja":"問われているのは保存・監視ではなく、ツールの共通インターフェースです。MCPはツールの発見と呼び出しを標準化し、フレームワークごとの個別連携を減らします。役割を「UC＝管理、MLflow＝追跡・評価、Lakebase＝状態保存、MCP＝接続の標準」と分けると選びやすくなります。","distractors_ja":["ツールの公開・発見・呼び出しの共通プロトコルという要件に直接一致します。","Unity Catalogはガバナンスを担いますが、外部ツール定義を必ず管理対象テーブルにする必要はありません。","トレースは実行の観測機能で、ツール公開の共通プロトコルを置き換えません。","Postgres互換のデータベースであることは、外部APIをSQLから自動で呼べることを意味しません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-005","number":5,"form":2,"stem":"A debugging agent is helping a developer fix a flaky test. After each failed retry, the agent's harness re-pastes the entire 150-line stack trace into the conversation verbatim, even though the stack trace is identical across all 15 retries except for a single differing line number near the bottom. The actual new information on each retry — what changed and what the result was — amounts to only two or three lines each time. As the session progresses, the agent's suggestions become less targeted and it starts re-suggesting fixes it already tried and ruled out.\n\nWhich change most directly improves model focus by addressing disproportionate attention-budget consumption here?","options":["Increase the model's context window so all 15 full stack traces fit without approaching any token limit.","Stop re-pasting the full, largely-identical stack trace on every retry; retain one copy of the stack trace and surface only the small diff — what changed, and the new result — for each subsequent retry.","Switch the agent's reasoning mode to extended thinking so it can more carefully sift through the repeated stack traces.","Ask the developer to manually delete old messages from the conversation before each retry."],"correct":1,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"繰り返しログを差分に置き換える","concept":"ce-f2-q005","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Stop re-pasting the full, largely-identical stack trace on every retry; retain one copy of the stack trace and surface only the small diff — what changed, and the new result — for each subsequent retry.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe problem is caused by disproportionate attention-budget consumption. The agent receives the same 150-line stack trace 15 times, while each retry contributes only two or three lines of genuinely new information.\n\nThis produces a context dominated by redundant failure output rather than useful troubleshooting state, such as:\n\n- Which fixes have already been attempted.\n\n- What changed during each retry.\n\n- Whether the result improved or regressed.\n\n- Which hypotheses have already been ruled out.\n\n- The one line number that changed.\n\nOption B directly removes the duplication while preserving all meaningful information. One canonical stack trace remains available, and subsequent retries add only their differences and outcomes.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe harness should distinguish between stable diagnostic information and incremental information:\n\n- Stable information: The original stack trace, environment, failure type, and relevant code.\n\n- Incremental information: The attempted change, changed stack-trace line, test result, and conclusion.\n\nRe-inserting the complete trace on every retry wastes context on duplicated text. As duplicate traces accumulate, they compete for attention with the small but important changes between attempts.\n\nRetaining one full trace and presenting later retries as concise deltas improves the context’s signal-to-noise ratio. For example:\n\n```\nBaseline failure:Canonical stack trace stored as Retry 1. Retry 2:Change: Increased the network timeout from 5 to 10 seconds.Result: Failed with the same exception.Trace difference: Failure moved from line 128 to line 131.Conclusion: A simple timeout increase did not resolve the issue. Retry 3:Change: Replaced the shared test fixture with a per-test fixture.Result: Test passed 8 of 10 runs.Conclusion: Shared fixture state is a likely contributor.\n```\n\nThis format preserves troubleshooting continuity without repeatedly consuming tokens on identical diagnostic output.\n\nIt also makes previously attempted and rejected fixes easier for the model to identify, reducing the chance that it will recommend the same unsuccessful solution again.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Increase the model's context window so all 15 full stack traces fit without approaching any token limit.\n\nA larger context window addresses capacity, not redundancy.\n\nEven when all traces fit, the model still receives thousands of unnecessary duplicate tokens. The repeated text can continue to reduce the relative prominence of the actual changes and conclusions.\n\nA larger context window may be necessary when all information is unique and relevant. It is not the most direct solution when almost all the consumed context is duplicated.\n\nC. Switch the agent's reasoning mode to extended thinking so it can more carefully sift through the repeated stack traces.\n\nAdditional reasoning computation does not improve the quality of the supplied context.\n\nThe model would still need to process 15 nearly identical stack traces and identify a few small differences buried within them. Extended thinking could increase latency and cost while leaving the duplication problem unchanged.\n\nThe better approach is to structure the context efficiently before asking the model to reason over it.\n\nD. Ask the developer to manually delete old messages from the conversation before each retry.\n\nManual deletion is operationally fragile and places responsibility for context management on the developer.\n\nIt may also remove useful information, such as:\n\n- Earlier attempted fixes.\n\n- Conclusions already reached.\n\n- The original baseline trace.\n\n- Important changes across retries.\n\nThe harness should manage context programmatically by deduplicating repeated outputs and retaining concise retry summaries. This is more reliable, scalable, and reproducible than requiring manual cleanup.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main distractor is A because the scenario describes growing token usage.\n\nHowever, the question asks how to address disproportionate attention-budget consumption, not merely how to prevent context-window overflow. Increasing capacity preserves the waste; deduplicating repeated traces removes it.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nProduction debugging agents should not treat every tool response as equally valuable conversation history.\n\nA stronger harness would retain:\n\n- One canonical copy of the original stack trace.\n\n- A structured record of each attempted fix.\n\n- The result of each retry.\n\n- Only the changed trace lines.\n\n- A list of hypotheses confirmed or ruled out.\n\n- The current best diagnosis.\n\nLarge raw outputs can also be stored outside the active model context and referenced through identifiers. The model-facing context should contain concise summaries and relevant excerpts, while the complete artifacts remain available through a tool when deeper inspection is required.\n\nFor the exam, the expected technique is deduplication and delta-based context updates, represented by option B.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Agent system design patterns — Databricks](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- [Context engineering in agents — LangChain](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/context-engineering)\n\n- [Context engineering in Deep Agents — LangChain](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/deepagents/context-engineering)\n\n- [Short-term memory — LangChain](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/short-term-memory)","source_option_explanations":["A. Increase the model's context window so all 15 full stack traces fit without approaching any token limit.\n\nA larger context window addresses capacity, not redundancy.\n\nEven when all traces fit, the model still receives thousands of unnecessary duplicate tokens. The repeated text can continue to reduce the relative prominence of the actual changes and conclusions.\n\nA larger context window may be necessary when all information is unique and relevant. It is not the most direct solution when almost all the consumed context is duplicated.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Stop re-pasting the full, largely-identical stack trace on every retry; retain one copy of the stack trace and surface only the small diff — what changed, and the new result — for each subsequent retry.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe problem is caused by disproportionate attention-budget consumption. The agent receives the same 150-line stack trace 15 times, while each retry contributes only two or three lines of genuinely new information.\n\nThis produces a context dominated by redundant failure output rather than useful troubleshooting state, such as:\n\n- Which fixes have already been attempted.\n\n- What changed during each retry.\n\n- Whether the result improved or regressed.\n\n- Which hypotheses have already been ruled out.\n\n- The one line number that changed.\n\nOption B directly removes the duplication while preserving all meaningful information. One canonical stack trace remains available, and subsequent retries add only their differences and outcomes.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe harness should distinguish between stable diagnostic information and incremental information:\n\n- Stable information: The original stack trace, environment, failure type, and relevant code.\n\n- Incremental information: The attempted change, changed stack-trace line, test result, and conclusion.\n\nRe-inserting the complete trace on every retry wastes context on duplicated text. As duplicate traces accumulate, they compete for attention with the small but important changes between attempts.\n\nRetaining one full trace and presenting later retries as concise deltas improves the context’s signal-to-noise ratio. For example:\n\n```\nBaseline failure:Canonical stack trace stored as Retry 1. Retry 2:Change: Increased the network timeout from 5 to 10 seconds.Result: Failed with the same exception.Trace difference: Failure moved from line 128 to line 131.Conclusion: A simple timeout increase did not resolve the issue. Retry 3:Change: Replaced the shared test fixture with a per-test fixture.Result: Test passed 8 of 10 runs.Conclusion: Shared fixture state is a likely contributor.\n```\n\nThis format preserves troubleshooting continuity without repeatedly consuming tokens on identical diagnostic output.\n\nIt also makes previously attempted and rejected fixes easier for the model to identify, reducing the chance that it will recommend the same unsuccessful solution again.","C. Switch the agent's reasoning mode to extended thinking so it can more carefully sift through the repeated stack traces.\n\nAdditional reasoning computation does not improve the quality of the supplied context.\n\nThe model would still need to process 15 nearly identical stack traces and identify a few small differences buried within them. Extended thinking could increase latency and cost while leaving the duplication problem unchanged.\n\nThe better approach is to structure the context efficiently before asking the model to reason over it.","D. Ask the developer to manually delete old messages from the conversation before each retry.\n\nManual deletion is operationally fragile and places responsibility for context management on the developer.\n\nIt may also remove useful information, such as:\n\n- Earlier attempted fixes.\n\n- Conclusions already reached.\n\n- The original baseline trace.\n\n- Important changes across retries.\n\nThe harness should manage context programmatically by deduplicating repeated outputs and retaining concise retry summaries. This is more reliable, scalable, and reproducible than requiring manual cleanup."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/context-engineering","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/deepagents/context-engineering","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/short-term-memory"],"source_hash":"0e462567dac5a81cbe15cb7e5fd67d85b37935db2cd114b802995b23ddd700e5","stem_ja":"デバッグエージェントが、不安定なテストの修正を開発者と進めています。再試行が失敗するたび、実行基盤は150行のスタックトレース全体をそのまま会話へ貼り付けます。15回の再試行すべてで、末尾付近の1つの行番号以外は同じトレースです。再試行ごとの実際の新情報である変更内容と結果は、毎回2～3行しかありません。セッションが進むと、エージェントの提案は的を射なくなり、すでに試して除外した修正を再び提案し始めます。\n\nこの過剰な注意予算の消費に対処し、モデルの焦点を最も直接改善する変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["15個の完全なスタックトレースがトークン上限に近づかず収まるよう、モデルのコンテキストウィンドウを大きくする。","ほぼ同じスタックトレース全体を再試行のたびに貼り付けるのをやめる。トレースは1部だけ保持し、それ以降は変更内容と新しい結果という小さな差分だけを示す。","繰り返すスタックトレースをより慎重に読み分けられるよう、推論モードを拡張思考に切り替える。","再試行の前に、開発者が古いメッセージを会話から手作業で削除するよう求める。"],"explanation_ja":"同一ログを何度も渡しても情報は増えず、重要な変更履歴が埋もれます。代表のトレースを1部残し、各再試行の変更点・結果・除外した仮説を差分として保持します。覚え方は「同じログは1回、以降は差分」。容量の拡大や思考量の追加より、入力の重複除去が直接的です。","distractors_ja":["収容容量は増えますが、同じログが注意を消費し、試行の違いを見えにくくする問題は残ります。","元の診断情報を保持しつつ、毎回増えた情報だけを伝えられるため、重複と注意の分散を直接減らせます。","推論の計算量を増やしても、冗長な入力そのものや、そのコストは減りません。","必要な診断履歴を消す危険があり、実行基盤で自動的に差分を扱う方が確実です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-006","number":6,"form":2,"stem":"A Databricks agent powers real-time bid pricing for a programmatic advertising platform, where each request must return a price within roughly 200 milliseconds at very high request volume. The pricing logic itself is a simple, well-defined lookup against a small set of templated rules with minimal branching. The team currently runs every request through extended thinking mode with a substantial thinking-token budget \"to be safe,\" and is now reviewing latency and cost data showing this adds significant token overhead and latency without any corresponding improvement in pricing accuracy.\n\nWhich reasoning-mode adjustment is most appropriate for this task?","options":["Keep extended thinking enabled for all requests, since more reasoning capacity is always safer regardless of task complexity or latency requirements.","Switch to reduced thinking only during off-peak hours, and keep extended thinking during peak traffic, since peak traffic always requires deeper reasoning.","Switch to reduced thinking (minimal reasoning effort) for this task, since the pricing logic is simple and templated, latency is critical at high volume, and the extended thinking budget is adding cost and delay without measurable quality benefit.","Increase the thinking-token budget further, since a larger budget for extended thinking will reduce the observed latency."],"correct":2,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"単純で低遅延な処理の推論量","concept":"ce-f2-q006","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Switch to reduced thinking (minimal reasoning effort) for this task, since the pricing logic is simple and templated, latency is critical at high volume, and the extended thinking budget is adding cost and delay without measurable quality benefit.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe decisive details are:\n\n- Each request must complete in approximately 200 milliseconds.\n\n- The platform handles a very high request volume.\n\n- The pricing task is a simple, deterministic lookup against templated rules.\n\n- Extended thinking produces no measurable accuracy improvement.\n\n- The current configuration increases both token consumption and latency.\n\nReasoning effort should match task complexity. High or extended reasoning is useful when a model must perform complex analysis, resolve ambiguity, plan multiple steps, or evaluate competing possibilities. This scenario requires none of those capabilities.\n\nDatabricks documentation states that higher reasoning effort can increase latency and token usage, while minimal reasoning is intended for the fastest, lowest-latency responses.\n\nTherefore, option C provides the best balance of latency, throughput, cost, and output quality.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nReduced or minimal reasoning is appropriate because the task has a small, well-defined decision space. The agent does not need to explore multiple hypotheses, create a complex plan, or reason through uncertain information.\n\nUsing minimal reasoning provides several benefits:\n\n- Reduces or eliminates reasoning-token overhead.\n\n- Lowers response latency.\n\n- Improves throughput under heavy traffic.\n\n- Reduces inference cost.\n\n- Preserves accuracy because extended reasoning has already shown no measurable benefit.\n\nDatabricks recommends starting with simpler and more deterministic designs for well-defined tasks, adding greater agentic complexity only when it produces a meaningful improvement. More complex reasoning can introduce additional latency and operational complexity.\n\nThis is an example of task-appropriate inference configuration: allocate additional reasoning capacity only when the task’s complexity and measured evaluation results justify it.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Keep extended thinking enabled for all requests, since more reasoning capacity is always safer regardless of task complexity or latency requirements.\n\nThis is incorrect because more reasoning is not automatically safer or more accurate. Here, production measurements show that extended thinking increases latency and token consumption without improving pricing accuracy.\n\nKeeping it enabled would directly conflict with the strict latency target and high-volume cost requirements.\n\nB. Switch to reduced thinking only during off-peak hours, and keep extended thinking during peak traffic, since peak traffic always requires deeper reasoning.\n\nThis reverses the appropriate optimization.\n\nPeak traffic increases the need for:\n\n- Lower per-request latency\n\n- Greater throughput\n\n- Lower token consumption\n\n- Predictable execution time\n\nTraffic volume does not make an individual pricing decision more intellectually complex. During peak traffic, unnecessary extended reasoning would create even greater capacity pressure and cost.\n\nD. Increase the thinking-token budget further, since a larger budget for extended thinking will reduce the observed latency.\n\nThis is technically backwards. Increasing the reasoning-token budget allows the model to spend more tokens on internal reasoning. It does not reduce latency and will ordinarily increase potential processing time and token usage.\n\nA larger budget could be justified for genuinely difficult tasks, but this scenario explicitly describes simple, templated logic with no observed quality benefit from extended reasoning.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main distractor is the belief that more reasoning always produces a better or safer answer.\n\nThe exam is testing whether you can match reasoning effort to:\n\n- Task complexity\n\n- Latency requirements\n\n- Request volume\n\n- Token cost\n\n- Measured quality improvements\n\nReasoning capacity should be treated as a configurable resource, not automatically maximized.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nFor production agents, choose reasoning modes through evaluation and workload characteristics, not intuition alone.\n\nFor this pricing workload, a practical implementation would:\n\n- Use minimal or reduced reasoning for the normal pricing path.\n\n- Measure p50, p95, and p99 latency.\n\n- Track reasoning tokens, total tokens, cost, throughput, and pricing accuracy.\n\n- Enforce deterministic validation around the generated price.\n\n- Use a fallback or escalation path only for genuinely exceptional inputs.\n\nFor an extremely latency-sensitive bidding system, teams should also evaluate whether an LLM is necessary for the core lookup at all. A deterministic rules engine or cached service may provide more predictable sub-200-millisecond behavior. That is an additional real-world architectural consideration; within the answer choices, reduced thinking is clearly the expected exam answer.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Query reasoning models\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/model-serving/query-reason-models](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/model-serving/query-reason-models)\n\n- Foundation Model REST API reference\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/foundation-model-apis/api-reference](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/foundation-model-apis/api-reference)\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)","source_option_explanations":["A. Keep extended thinking enabled for all requests, since more reasoning capacity is always safer regardless of task complexity or latency requirements.\n\nThis is incorrect because more reasoning is not automatically safer or more accurate. Here, production measurements show that extended thinking increases latency and token consumption without improving pricing accuracy.\n\nKeeping it enabled would directly conflict with the strict latency target and high-volume cost requirements.","B. Switch to reduced thinking only during off-peak hours, and keep extended thinking during peak traffic, since peak traffic always requires deeper reasoning.\n\nThis reverses the appropriate optimization.\n\nPeak traffic increases the need for:\n\n- Lower per-request latency\n\n- Greater throughput\n\n- Lower token consumption\n\n- Predictable execution time\n\nTraffic volume does not make an individual pricing decision more intellectually complex. During peak traffic, unnecessary extended reasoning would create even greater capacity pressure and cost.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Switch to reduced thinking (minimal reasoning effort) for this task, since the pricing logic is simple and templated, latency is critical at high volume, and the extended thinking budget is adding cost and delay without measurable quality benefit.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe decisive details are:\n\n- Each request must complete in approximately 200 milliseconds.\n\n- The platform handles a very high request volume.\n\n- The pricing task is a simple, deterministic lookup against templated rules.\n\n- Extended thinking produces no measurable accuracy improvement.\n\n- The current configuration increases both token consumption and latency.\n\nReasoning effort should match task complexity. High or extended reasoning is useful when a model must perform complex analysis, resolve ambiguity, plan multiple steps, or evaluate competing possibilities. This scenario requires none of those capabilities.\n\nDatabricks documentation states that higher reasoning effort can increase latency and token usage, while minimal reasoning is intended for the fastest, lowest-latency responses.\n\nTherefore, option C provides the best balance of latency, throughput, cost, and output quality.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nReduced or minimal reasoning is appropriate because the task has a small, well-defined decision space. The agent does not need to explore multiple hypotheses, create a complex plan, or reason through uncertain information.\n\nUsing minimal reasoning provides several benefits:\n\n- Reduces or eliminates reasoning-token overhead.\n\n- Lowers response latency.\n\n- Improves throughput under heavy traffic.\n\n- Reduces inference cost.\n\n- Preserves accuracy because extended reasoning has already shown no measurable benefit.\n\nDatabricks recommends starting with simpler and more deterministic designs for well-defined tasks, adding greater agentic complexity only when it produces a meaningful improvement. More complex reasoning can introduce additional latency and operational complexity.\n\nThis is an example of task-appropriate inference configuration: allocate additional reasoning capacity only when the task’s complexity and measured evaluation results justify it.","D. Increase the thinking-token budget further, since a larger budget for extended thinking will reduce the observed latency.\n\nThis is technically backwards. Increasing the reasoning-token budget allows the model to spend more tokens on internal reasoning. It does not reduce latency and will ordinarily increase potential processing time and token usage.\n\nA larger budget could be justified for genuinely difficult tasks, but this scenario explicitly describes simple, templated logic with no observed quality benefit from extended reasoning."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/model-serving/query-reason-models","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/foundation-model-apis/api-reference","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns"],"source_hash":"fe6fb0c7efac0d850ec20f0c43365211e3444724973d199827da0a1c174e9c8e","stem_ja":"Databricksのエージェントが、プログラマティック広告プラットフォームのリアルタイム入札価格を決めています。非常に多いリクエストに対し、それぞれ約200ミリ秒以内に価格を返す必要があります。価格決定ロジック自体は、分岐が少ない、少数の定型ルールに対する単純で明確な参照です。現在、チームは「念のため」、十分な思考トークン予算を持つ拡張思考モードで全リクエストを処理しています。遅延と費用のデータを調べたところ、価格の正確さには相応の改善がないのに、トークン負荷と遅延が大きく増えていました。\n\nこのタスクに最も適切な推論モードの調整はどれですか。","options_ja":["タスクの複雑さや遅延要件に関係なく、推論能力が多いほど常に安全なので、全リクエストで拡張思考を有効のままにする。","ピーク時の通信量は常に深い推論を必要とするため、閑散時間だけ思考を抑えるモードに切り替え、ピーク時は拡張思考を維持する。","価格決定は単純で定型的であり、大量処理では低遅延が重要で、拡張思考に測定可能な品質向上がないため、このタスクを思考を抑えるモード（最小限の推論量）に切り替える。","拡張思考の予算を増やせば観測された遅延が減るため、思考トークン予算をさらに増やす。"],"explanation_ja":"推論量は多いほどよいのではなく、タスクの難しさと品質向上に合わせます。単純なルール参照で正確さが変わらず、200ミリ秒の応答要件があるなら、最小限の推論が適切です。覚え方は「単純・大量・低遅延なら思考を絞る」。実務では変更後も正確さと遅延を測って確認します。","distractors_ja":["追加の推論に品質上の効果がなく、費用と遅延だけが増えるという測定結果を無視しています。","通信量と1件の推論の難しさは別です。ピーク時ほど不要な計算を避ける必要があります。","単純な処理と厳しい遅延要件に推論量を合わせ、効果のない追加コストを減らせます。","一般に思考予算の増加は追加のトークンや時間を要し、遅延を減らす対策ではありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-007","number":7,"form":2,"stem":"A financial-advisory agent conducts multi-turn portfolio review conversations. After every single tool call (fetching account balances, calculating allocations, checking risk tolerance), the harness automatically appends the full text of a 400-token standard compliance disclaimer to the conversation, identical every time, regardless of how many tool calls have already occurred earlier in the same session. MLflow traces show that once a session accumulates roughly 20 tool calls, the agent begins misstating the client's previously-confirmed risk tolerance and repeating recommendations it already made earlier in the same conversation. The team wants to keep sessions long enough to support a full portfolio review without losing the specific facts established earlier, such as the client's actual risk tolerance and which recommendations have already been made.\n\nWhich intervention most directly restores reasoning quality here?","options":["Raise the model's context window limit so the repeated disclaimers no longer approach any threshold.","Disable the compliance disclaimer entirely so it never appears in the conversation, regardless of any legal requirement to display it.","Truncate the oldest 10 messages in the session once a fixed token count is reached, regardless of whether they contain the client's risk tolerance or prior recommendations.","Apply compaction that removes the redundant, repeated disclaimer text — retaining it once, or handling its display outside the model-facing context — while preserving the substantive facts established so far, such as the client's risk tolerance and prior recommendations."],"correct":3,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"重要な事実を残して冗長な免責文を圧縮","concept":"ce-f2-q007","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Apply compaction that removes the redundant, repeated disclaimer text — retaining it once, or handling its display outside the model-facing context — while preserving the substantive facts established so far, such as the client's risk tolerance and prior recommendations.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe central problem is not simply that the conversation is approaching a hard token limit. The problem is that the model-facing context has become filled with low-value, duplicated text.\n\nAfter approximately 20 tool calls, the same 400-token disclaimer has been added about 20 times, consuming roughly 8,000 tokens without contributing new information. Meanwhile, the facts that matter—such as the client’s confirmed risk tolerance and recommendations already given—become harder for the model to use reliably.\n\nThe required intervention must therefore accomplish two things:\n\n- Remove redundant context that does not need to be repeated.\n\n- Preserve important conversational state required for future reasoning.\n\nOption D does exactly this through selective context compaction. Databricks describes compaction as deciding what information should remain active and what should be summarized or removed as conversations grow, allowing agents to continue long-running work without being overwhelmed by accumulated context.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nContext compaction reduces the size and noise of a growing conversation while retaining information necessary to continue the task accurately.\n\nIn this scenario, the compliance disclaimer is:\n\n- Identical after every tool call.\n\n- Not new evidence or reasoning input.\n\n- Consuming substantial context space.\n\n- Reducing the relative prominence of client-specific facts.\n\nThe disclaimer does not necessarily need to be removed from the user experience. It can be:\n\n- Retained once in the model-facing instructions.\n\n- Displayed separately in the application interface.\n\n- Attached to the final user-visible response.\n\n- Managed by deterministic application logic rather than repeatedly passed to the model.\n\nAt the same time, compaction should retain a structured record of important state, including:\n\n- The client’s confirmed risk tolerance.\n\n- Current balances and portfolio allocations.\n\n- Recommendations already presented.\n\n- Client decisions and rejected recommendations.\n\n- Outstanding review steps.\n\nThis aligns directly with the certification objective of managing context-window constraints through compaction and trimming without losing essential information.\n\nUnlike arbitrary deletion, good compaction is semantic and importance-aware: it removes duplication and compresses low-value history while preserving high-value facts.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Raise the model's context window limit so the repeated disclaimers no longer approach any threshold.\n\nA larger context window may delay the problem, but it does not correct the underlying context-design failure.\n\nThe harness would continue adding hundreds of duplicate tokens after every tool call. This would:\n\n- Increase token consumption and cost.\n\n- Preserve irrelevant duplication.\n\n- Continue diluting important client-specific information.\n\n- Eventually create the same problem in longer conversations.\n\nThe symptoms can appear before the maximum context-window limit is reached. A model can struggle to identify and consistently use the most relevant facts when its context contains excessive redundant material. Therefore, increasing capacity is less direct and less efficient than removing the duplication.\n\nB. Disable the compliance disclaimer entirely so it never appears in the conversation, regardless of any legal requirement to display it.\n\nThis could reduce context usage, but it ignores the compliance requirement.\n\nThe problem is not necessarily that the disclaimer exists. The problem is that its complete text is repeatedly inserted into the model-facing conversation after every tool call.\n\nA better design separates:\n\n- What the model needs for reasoning.\n\n- What the application must display to satisfy legal or compliance obligations.\n\nRemoving required disclosures entirely could create legal, regulatory, or governance risk. Option D preserves compliance while avoiding unnecessary context repetition.\n\nC. Truncate the oldest 10 messages in the session once a fixed token count is reached, regardless of whether they contain the client's risk tolerance or prior recommendations.\n\nBlind truncation is dangerous because message age does not determine information value.\n\nThe oldest messages might contain:\n\n- The client’s confirmed risk tolerance.\n\n- Investment objectives.\n\n- Time horizon.\n\n- Liquidity requirements.\n\n- Prior recommendations or client decisions.\n\nDeleting those messages could directly cause the behavior the team is trying to prevent. Trimming can be useful, but it should be relevance-aware or combined with summarization and structured state preservation—not applied mechanically without considering message content.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is confusing context capacity with context quality.\n\nA larger context window does not make duplicated, irrelevant content useful. Similarly, arbitrary truncation reduces context size but may remove the most important facts.\n\nThe best intervention optimizes the information composition of the context: remove redundancy while preserving essential state.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nIn a production financial-advisory agent, do not rely exclusively on raw conversation history to preserve critical client facts.\n\nA stronger architecture would maintain structured session state for items such as:\n\n```\nRisk tolerance: ModerateInvestment horizon: 12 yearsLiquidity requirement: ₹500,000Recommended actions:- Reduce technology-sector concentration- Increase fixed-income allocationRejected recommendations:- International equity allocation\n```\n\nThe agent-facing context can then contain:\n\n- One concise compliance instruction.\n\n- A compact conversation summary.\n\n- Structured client state.\n\n- The most recent relevant messages.\n\n- Current tool results.\n\nDatabricks distinguishes short-term memory, which maintains context within a conversation, from longer-term memory that stores important preferences and accumulated information. These mechanisms can help preserve client-specific state without repeatedly replaying the entire raw transcript.\n\nMLflow Tracing should then be used to compare:\n\n- Input-token growth.\n\n- Recall of confirmed risk tolerance.\n\n- Duplicate recommendation frequency.\n\n- Tool-call accuracy.\n\n- Latency and cost.\n\n- Compliance-display behavior.\n\nThe exam answer is D because it most directly addresses the identified context-quality problem. In a real implementation, compaction should be combined with structured state management, testing, and deterministic compliance controls.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- What is an AI Agent Harness? — Memory and Context Management\n[https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness](https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness)\n\n- Databricks Certified Context Engineer Associate\n[https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate](https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate)\n\n- Self-managed Agent Memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory)\n\n- MLflow Tracing — GenAI Observability\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/)","source_option_explanations":["A. Raise the model's context window limit so the repeated disclaimers no longer approach any threshold.\n\nA larger context window may delay the problem, but it does not correct the underlying context-design failure.\n\nThe harness would continue adding hundreds of duplicate tokens after every tool call. This would:\n\n- Increase token consumption and cost.\n\n- Preserve irrelevant duplication.\n\n- Continue diluting important client-specific information.\n\n- Eventually create the same problem in longer conversations.\n\nThe symptoms can appear before the maximum context-window limit is reached. A model can struggle to identify and consistently use the most relevant facts when its context contains excessive redundant material. Therefore, increasing capacity is less direct and less efficient than removing the duplication.","B. Disable the compliance disclaimer entirely so it never appears in the conversation, regardless of any legal requirement to display it.\n\nThis could reduce context usage, but it ignores the compliance requirement.\n\nThe problem is not necessarily that the disclaimer exists. The problem is that its complete text is repeatedly inserted into the model-facing conversation after every tool call.\n\nA better design separates:\n\n- What the model needs for reasoning.\n\n- What the application must display to satisfy legal or compliance obligations.\n\nRemoving required disclosures entirely could create legal, regulatory, or governance risk. Option D preserves compliance while avoiding unnecessary context repetition.","C. Truncate the oldest 10 messages in the session once a fixed token count is reached, regardless of whether they contain the client's risk tolerance or prior recommendations.\n\nBlind truncation is dangerous because message age does not determine information value.\n\nThe oldest messages might contain:\n\n- The client’s confirmed risk tolerance.\n\n- Investment objectives.\n\n- Time horizon.\n\n- Liquidity requirements.\n\n- Prior recommendations or client decisions.\n\nDeleting those messages could directly cause the behavior the team is trying to prevent. Trimming can be useful, but it should be relevance-aware or combined with summarization and structured state preservation—not applied mechanically without considering message content.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Apply compaction that removes the redundant, repeated disclaimer text — retaining it once, or handling its display outside the model-facing context — while preserving the substantive facts established so far, such as the client's risk tolerance and prior recommendations.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe central problem is not simply that the conversation is approaching a hard token limit. The problem is that the model-facing context has become filled with low-value, duplicated text.\n\nAfter approximately 20 tool calls, the same 400-token disclaimer has been added about 20 times, consuming roughly 8,000 tokens without contributing new information. Meanwhile, the facts that matter—such as the client’s confirmed risk tolerance and recommendations already given—become harder for the model to use reliably.\n\nThe required intervention must therefore accomplish two things:\n\n- Remove redundant context that does not need to be repeated.\n\n- Preserve important conversational state required for future reasoning.\n\nOption D does exactly this through selective context compaction. Databricks describes compaction as deciding what information should remain active and what should be summarized or removed as conversations grow, allowing agents to continue long-running work without being overwhelmed by accumulated context.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nContext compaction reduces the size and noise of a growing conversation while retaining information necessary to continue the task accurately.\n\nIn this scenario, the compliance disclaimer is:\n\n- Identical after every tool call.\n\n- Not new evidence or reasoning input.\n\n- Consuming substantial context space.\n\n- Reducing the relative prominence of client-specific facts.\n\nThe disclaimer does not necessarily need to be removed from the user experience. It can be:\n\n- Retained once in the model-facing instructions.\n\n- Displayed separately in the application interface.\n\n- Attached to the final user-visible response.\n\n- Managed by deterministic application logic rather than repeatedly passed to the model.\n\nAt the same time, compaction should retain a structured record of important state, including:\n\n- The client’s confirmed risk tolerance.\n\n- Current balances and portfolio allocations.\n\n- Recommendations already presented.\n\n- Client decisions and rejected recommendations.\n\n- Outstanding review steps.\n\nThis aligns directly with the certification objective of managing context-window constraints through compaction and trimming without losing essential information.\n\nUnlike arbitrary deletion, good compaction is semantic and importance-aware: it removes duplication and compresses low-value history while preserving high-value facts."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness","https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/"],"source_hash":"f2eaeae8d6eaceeed139a6ceee678079a1320cc299729ea6a3170060ed571602","stem_ja":"金融助言エージェントが、複数ターンの会話で顧客のポートフォリオを見直します。口座残高の取得、配分の計算、リスク許容度の確認など、ツールを1回呼ぶたびに、実行基盤は同じ400トークンの標準コンプライアンス免責文全文を自動的に会話へ追加します。セッション内でそれまでに何回ツールを呼んでいても同じです。MLflowのトレースでは、ツール呼び出しが約20回蓄積すると、以前に確定した顧客のリスク許容度を誤って述べたり、同じ会話ですでに行った助言を繰り返したりし始めます。チームは、ポートフォリオ全体を見直せる長さのセッションを維持しつつ、顧客の実際のリスク許容度や、すでに行った助言などの具体的な確定事項を失いたくありません。\n\n推論の品質を最も直接回復する介入はどれですか。","options_ja":["繰り返す免責文が上限に近づかなくなるよう、モデルのコンテキストウィンドウ上限を引き上げる。","表示する法的義務があるかどうかに関係なく、免責文を完全に無効にし、会話へ一切表示しない。","固定のトークン数に達したら、顧客のリスク許容度や以前の助言を含むかどうかに関係なく、最も古い10件のメッセージを切り捨てる。","顧客のリスク許容度や以前の助言など、それまでに確定した重要な事実を保ちながら、重複する免責文を取り除く圧縮を適用する。免責文は1部だけ残すか、モデルに見せるコンテキストの外側で表示する。"],"explanation_ja":"必要なのは会話を短く終えることではなく、重複を除いて判断に必要な事実を残すことです。免責文の表示義務と、同文をモデルへ毎回再投入する必要性は分けて考えます。重複文を1部にし、確定したリスク許容度・既出の助言を保持する圧縮なら、長い会話の一貫性を回復できます。","distractors_ja":["上限到達は遅らせられますが、重複文による注意の消費や重要事実の埋没は解消しません。","モデルへの重複投入を減らすことと、必要な免責文の表示自体を廃止することは別です。","時刻だけで削ると、問題が保持を求めているリスク許容度や過去の助言まで失います。","不要な重複を除き、継続的な判断に必要な事実を残すため、原因と要件の両方に合います。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-008","number":8,"form":2,"stem":"A data analyst configures a Genie space shared by both the Maintenance and Quality teams at a manufacturing plant. Both teams use the term \"downtime,\" but mean different things by it: Maintenance defines downtime as any period a machine is powered off for any reason (scheduled or unscheduled), while Quality defines downtime as only unscheduled stoppages caused by a defect or failure. The underlying table has separate columns for scheduled_stop_minutes and unscheduled_stop_minutes. When a Quality analyst asks \"what was our downtime last week,\" Genie currently sums both columns, matching Maintenance's definition instead of Quality's.\n\nGiven that the same Genie space is shared by both teams, what is the most effective way to resolve this terminology conflict?","options":["Split into two separate Genie spaces, one for each team, each with its own instructions and SQL expression defining \"downtime\" according to that team's specific definition, since the same term genuinely means two different, incompatible things depending on which team is asking.","Add a single SQL expression defining \"downtime\" as the sum of scheduled_stop_minutes and unscheduled_stop_minutes, since a single technical definition should be authoritative across all teams sharing the space.","Add a general instruction telling both teams to stop using the word \"downtime\" and instead always specify \"scheduled\" or \"unscheduled\" explicitly in every question.","Remove the scheduled_stop_minutes and unscheduled_stop_minutes columns from the space entirely, so Genie can no longer generate an incorrect combined total."],"correct":0,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"部署間で異なる業務用語の定義","concept":"ce-f2-q008","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Split into two separate Genie spaces, one for each team, each with its own instructions and SQL expression defining \"downtime\" according to that team's specific definition, since the same term genuinely means two different, incompatible things depending on which team is asking.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe key constraint is that “downtime” has two incompatible business meanings:\n\n- Maintenance: scheduled plus unscheduled stoppages.\n\n- Quality: only unscheduled defect- or failure-related stoppages.\n\nA single shared Genie space applies the same configured semantic context, SQL expressions, examples, and instructions to all users. It cannot safely assign two different meanings to the same unqualified term merely by knowing which department submitted the question.\n\nBecause the correct calculation depends on the user’s business domain, the most reliable design is to create separate domain-specific Genie spaces:\n\n- A Maintenance space defining downtime as scheduled plus unscheduled stop minutes.\n\n- A Quality space defining downtime as unscheduled stop minutes, potentially further filtered to defect- or failure-related events.\n\nDatabricks describes Genie spaces as curated, domain-specific environments where teams configure trusted datasets, business rules, SQL expressions, and terminology.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nGenie must be given an unambiguous semantic definition for frequently used business terms. Databricks recommends defining metrics, filters, and other business concepts through SQL expressions because structured SQL definitions are more reliable than relying only on general text instructions.\n\nIn this scenario, the two teams do not merely use different synonyms. They assign different calculations to the same word:\n\n- Maintenance downtime:\n\n```\nscheduled_stop_minutes + unscheduled_stop_minutes\n```\n\n- Quality downtime:\n\n```\nunscheduled_stop_minutes\n```\n\nPotentially, the Quality definition may also require filtering by a defect or failure reason if the table contains other categories of unscheduled stoppage.\n\nSeparate spaces allow each team to have:\n\n- Its own SQL expression for `downtime`.\n\n- Team-specific instructions and example queries.\n\n- Appropriate datasets, metrics, and terminology.\n\n- Independent testing and refinement.\n\n- A consistent definition within that team’s analytical context.\n\nDatabricks supports cloning an existing Genie space and independently modifying its instructions, SQL examples, tables, and functions, making this separation practical without rebuilding both spaces from scratch.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Add a single SQL expression defining \"downtime\" as the sum of scheduled_stop_minutes and unscheduled_stop_minutes, since a single technical definition should be authoritative across all teams sharing the space.\n\nThis would formalize only the Maintenance definition. It would continue producing an incorrect result for Quality users.\n\nSQL expressions are appropriate for defining business terminology, but the expression must represent the intended business meaning. A single expression cannot represent two incompatible definitions of the same unqualified term in the same semantic context.\n\nThis option incorrectly assumes that one department’s definition should override the other department’s legitimate domain-specific meaning.\n\nC. Add a general instruction telling both teams to stop using the word \"downtime\" and instead always specify \"scheduled\" or \"unscheduled\" explicitly in every question.\n\nThis may reduce ambiguity when users follow the instruction, but it is not the most reliable solution.\n\nIt places the burden on every user to remember special phrasing and does not resolve questions such as:\n\nWhat was our downtime last week?\n\nUsers will naturally continue using familiar business terminology. Additionally, Databricks recommends prioritizing structured SQL expressions and example SQL over plain-text instructions when defining business semantics.\n\nThe question also asks for the most effective resolution given that the term genuinely has different meanings for the two teams. Domain-specific spaces provide stronger and more maintainable semantic separation.\n\nD. Remove the scheduled_stop_minutes and unscheduled_stop_minutes columns from the space entirely, so Genie can no longer generate an incorrect combined total.\n\nRemoving the columns would prevent Genie from answering valid downtime questions for either team.\n\nThe underlying data is not the problem. The problem is the ambiguous semantic interpretation of that data. Appropriate columns should remain available and be mapped to clearly defined, team-specific business metrics.\n\nThis option eliminates analytical capability rather than correcting the terminology conflict.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming that every business term must have one organization-wide definition.\n\nStandardizing terminology is useful when teams can agree on a common meaning. Here, however, the question explicitly establishes that the same term represents two valid but incompatible domain-specific metrics.\n\nA single shared semantic definition would necessarily be wrong for one team.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nFor production Genie implementations, curate spaces around coherent business domains rather than placing unrelated or semantically conflicting audiences into one broad space.\n\nA practical implementation would create:\n\nMaintenance Genie space\n\n```\ndowntime_minutes =scheduled_stop_minutes + unscheduled_stop_minutes\n```\n\nQuality Genie space\n\n```\ndowntime_minutes =unscheduled_stop_minutes\n```\n\nIf unscheduled stops can occur for reasons other than defects or failures, the Quality expression should include the appropriate reason filter.\n\nEach space should then include tested examples such as:\n\n```\n-- Maintenance definitionSELECT  SUM(scheduled_stop_minutes + unscheduled_stop_minutes)FROM machine_eventsWHERE event_date >= CURRENT_DATE() - INTERVAL 7 DAYS;\n```\n\n```\n-- Quality definitionSELECT  SUM(unscheduled_stop_minutes)FROM machine_eventsWHERE event_date >= CURRENT_DATE() - INTERVAL 7 DAYS  AND stop_reason IN ('DEFECT', 'FAILURE');\n```\n\nIn a real organization, teams might also establish explicit metric names such as `maintenance_downtime` and `quality_downtime` in a governed semantic layer. However, because users will continue asking for “downtime” according to their own domain language, separate Genie spaces remain the best answer among the available options.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Curate an effective Genie Agent\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/best-practices](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/best-practices)\n\n- Tune Genie Agent quality\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/tune-quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/tune-quality)\n\n- Create and manage a Genie Agent\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/set-up?utm_source=chatgpt.com)[/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/set-up)[genie/set-up](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/set-up)\n\n- Genie Agents\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Split into two separate Genie spaces, one for each team, each with its own instructions and SQL expression defining \"downtime\" according to that team's specific definition, since the same term genuinely means two different, incompatible things depending on which team is asking.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe key constraint is that “downtime” has two incompatible business meanings:\n\n- Maintenance: scheduled plus unscheduled stoppages.\n\n- Quality: only unscheduled defect- or failure-related stoppages.\n\nA single shared Genie space applies the same configured semantic context, SQL expressions, examples, and instructions to all users. It cannot safely assign two different meanings to the same unqualified term merely by knowing which department submitted the question.\n\nBecause the correct calculation depends on the user’s business domain, the most reliable design is to create separate domain-specific Genie spaces:\n\n- A Maintenance space defining downtime as scheduled plus unscheduled stop minutes.\n\n- A Quality space defining downtime as unscheduled stop minutes, potentially further filtered to defect- or failure-related events.\n\nDatabricks describes Genie spaces as curated, domain-specific environments where teams configure trusted datasets, business rules, SQL expressions, and terminology.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nGenie must be given an unambiguous semantic definition for frequently used business terms. Databricks recommends defining metrics, filters, and other business concepts through SQL expressions because structured SQL definitions are more reliable than relying only on general text instructions.\n\nIn this scenario, the two teams do not merely use different synonyms. They assign different calculations to the same word:\n\n- Maintenance downtime:\n\n```\nscheduled_stop_minutes + unscheduled_stop_minutes\n```\n\n- Quality downtime:\n\n```\nunscheduled_stop_minutes\n```\n\nPotentially, the Quality definition may also require filtering by a defect or failure reason if the table contains other categories of unscheduled stoppage.\n\nSeparate spaces allow each team to have:\n\n- Its own SQL expression for `downtime`.\n\n- Team-specific instructions and example queries.\n\n- Appropriate datasets, metrics, and terminology.\n\n- Independent testing and refinement.\n\n- A consistent definition within that team’s analytical context.\n\nDatabricks supports cloning an existing Genie space and independently modifying its instructions, SQL examples, tables, and functions, making this separation practical without rebuilding both spaces from scratch.","B. Add a single SQL expression defining \"downtime\" as the sum of scheduled_stop_minutes and unscheduled_stop_minutes, since a single technical definition should be authoritative across all teams sharing the space.\n\nThis would formalize only the Maintenance definition. It would continue producing an incorrect result for Quality users.\n\nSQL expressions are appropriate for defining business terminology, but the expression must represent the intended business meaning. A single expression cannot represent two incompatible definitions of the same unqualified term in the same semantic context.\n\nThis option incorrectly assumes that one department’s definition should override the other department’s legitimate domain-specific meaning.","C. Add a general instruction telling both teams to stop using the word \"downtime\" and instead always specify \"scheduled\" or \"unscheduled\" explicitly in every question.\n\nThis may reduce ambiguity when users follow the instruction, but it is not the most reliable solution.\n\nIt places the burden on every user to remember special phrasing and does not resolve questions such as:\n\nWhat was our downtime last week?\n\nUsers will naturally continue using familiar business terminology. Additionally, Databricks recommends prioritizing structured SQL expressions and example SQL over plain-text instructions when defining business semantics.\n\nThe question also asks for the most effective resolution given that the term genuinely has different meanings for the two teams. Domain-specific spaces provide stronger and more maintainable semantic separation.","D. Remove the scheduled_stop_minutes and unscheduled_stop_minutes columns from the space entirely, so Genie can no longer generate an incorrect combined total.\n\nRemoving the columns would prevent Genie from answering valid downtime questions for either team.\n\nThe underlying data is not the problem. The problem is the ambiguous semantic interpretation of that data. Appropriate columns should remain available and be mapped to clearly defined, team-specific business metrics.\n\nThis option eliminates analytical capability rather than correcting the terminology conflict."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/best-practices","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/tune-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/set-up?utm_source=chatgpt.com","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/set-up","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/"],"source_hash":"1f57b714e35b769385712b0a98ceb1797007e8bf5502f0e51fdade07140e678c","stem_ja":"製造工場の保全部門と品質部門が共有するGenieスペースを、データアナリストが設定しています。両部門は「downtime（停止時間）」という用語を使いますが、意味が異なります。保全部門は理由を問わず機械の電源が切れている全期間（計画停止・計画外停止）と定義し、品質部門は不具合や故障による計画外停止だけと定義します。元のテーブルにはscheduled_stop_minutesとunscheduled_stop_minutesが別々の列としてあります。品質部門のアナリストが「先週の停止時間は」と聞くと、現在のGenieは両列を合算し、品質部門ではなく保全部門の定義に従います。\n\n同じGenieスペースを両部門が共有している状況で、この用語の衝突を解消する最も有効な方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["質問する部門によって同じ用語に互いに両立しない2つの意味があるため、部門ごとにGenieスペースを分け、それぞれの指示とSQL式で、その部門における「停止時間」を定義する。","共有スペースでは単一の技術的定義をすべての部門の正とすべきなので、scheduled_stop_minutesとunscheduled_stop_minutesの合計を「停止時間」とするSQL式を1つ追加する。","両部門に「停止時間」という語を使うのをやめ、すべての質問で必ず「計画停止」か「計画外停止」を明示するよう、一般的な指示を追加する。","誤った合計をGenieが生成できなくなるよう、scheduled_stop_minutesとunscheduled_stop_minutesの両列をスペースから完全に削除する。"],"explanation_ja":"この問題では同じ語の意味が部門ごとに異なり、一方の定義を共通の正解にはできません。スペースを分けて、部門の業務定義とSQL式を一致させる方法が最も確実です。覚え方は「同じ言葉でも業務の意味が違うなら、業務コンテキストを分ける」。単なる集計処理の高速化では解決しません。","distractors_ja":["それぞれに正しい定義を設定でき、どの部門の意味を使うかという曖昧さをなくせます。","両列の合計は保全部門の定義であり、品質部門の要求を満たしません。","ユーザーに毎回の言い換えを強制するだけで、部門に合う業務定義を設定できていません。","必要な分析列まで失うため、正しく質問へ答える能力を損ないます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-009","number":9,"form":2,"stem":"A Databricks agent drafts customer-facing incident-status updates and has 8 candidate few-shot examples available under a limited token budget. It already reliably drafts updates for typical \"delayed but on track\" scenarios. Gaps cluster around two situations: communicating a full outage with no known ETA (a tone and structure it has never been shown, and tends to either over-promise a fix time or sound overly alarmed), and a required legal safe-harbor closing line that must appear verbatim in a specific format for regulated-service outages, which the agent currently omits or paraphrases incorrectly.\n\nSelect the two few-shot examples that would contribute the most marginal value given the limited token budget.","options":["An example demonstrating a calm, appropriately-hedged update for a full outage with no known ETA, modeling the tone and structure the agent hasn't been shown.","An example demonstrating a \"delayed but on track\" update, since this is the most common scenario the agent will encounter.","An example showing the exact required legal safe-harbor closing line in its correct verbatim format for a regulated-service outage.","An example demonstrating how to greet the customer at the start of the message, since a friendly opening is important for every type of update."],"correct":0,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"未対応の失敗パターンを補うfew-shot例","concept":"ce-f2-q009","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. An example demonstrating a calm, appropriately-hedged update for a full outage with no known ETA, modeling the tone and structure the agent hasn't been shown.\n\nC. An example showing the exact required legal safe-harbor closing line in its correct verbatim format for a regulated-service outage.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question asks which two examples provide the greatest marginal value under a limited token budget. The best examples are not necessarily those representing the most common requests; they are the examples that address the agent’s most important demonstrated failures.\n\nThe agent already handles ordinary “delayed but on track” incidents reliably. Additional examples of that pattern would mostly duplicate behavior it has already learned.\n\nIts quality gaps are concentrated in two areas:\n\n- A novel, high-risk scenario: a complete outage with no known ETA.\n\n- A strict output requirement: a legal safe-harbor statement that must appear verbatim.\n\nTherefore, examples A and C add the most new and useful information to the prompt.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA addresses an uncovered behavioral scenario. The agent has never been shown how to communicate a full outage when no restoration estimate is available. Its current failures—over-promising a recovery time or using an unnecessarily alarming tone—show that general incident examples are not transferring reliably to this case.\n\nA strong example can demonstrate several requirements simultaneously:\n\n- Clearly acknowledge the outage.\n\n- Avoid inventing or implying an ETA.\n\n- Use calm, factual language.\n\n- Explain what is being investigated.\n\n- Set an appropriate expectation for the next update.\n\nThis example has high marginal value because it fills a meaningful gap rather than reinforcing an already reliable pattern.\n\nC addresses an exact-compliance failure. The safe-harbor line must be reproduced verbatim and in a specific format, but the agent currently omits or paraphrases it. A correct demonstration gives the model a concrete output pattern rather than relying only on an abstract instruction such as “include the required legal wording.”\n\nTogether, A and C cover two distinct deficiencies:\n\n- A: semantic, structural, and tonal behavior for a novel scenario.\n\n- C: exact adherence to a mandatory output constraint.\n\nThis is the most efficient use of the limited few-shot token budget.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. An example demonstrating a \"delayed but on track\" update, since this is the most common scenario the agent will encounter.\n\nThis is a valid incident type, but the agent already handles it reliably. Frequency alone does not make an example valuable.\n\nBecause few-shot examples consume context tokens, repeating a well-performing pattern provides relatively little incremental improvement. The limited budget should instead be allocated to known failure modes and underrepresented scenarios.\n\nD. An example demonstrating how to greet the customer at the start of the message, since a friendly opening is important for every type of update.\n\nA friendly opening may improve presentation, but it does not address either of the important observed failures:\n\n- Inappropriate communication during a complete outage with no ETA.\n\n- Failure to reproduce legally required text exactly.\n\nA greeting is also comparatively simple to specify through a concise instruction or template. It does not justify consuming one of the highest-value few-shot example slots when material operational and regulatory gaps remain.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe primary trap is choosing an example because it represents the most common case rather than because it provides the greatest incremental improvement.\n\nFew-shot examples should prioritize:\n\n- Known failure clusters.\n\n- Unseen or poorly generalized scenarios.\n\n- Important edge cases.\n\n- Strict output formats that the model repeatedly violates.\n\nAnother trap is treating all formatting behaviors as equally important. A legally required verbatim statement has substantially greater value than demonstrating a generic greeting.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nIn production, select few-shot examples using evaluation evidence rather than intuition. Review traces and evaluation results to identify where examples would reduce the largest or highest-risk errors.\n\nA practical example set should be diverse and non-redundant, covering:\n\n- Representative normal cases.\n\n- High-impact edge cases.\n\n- Common failure modes.\n\n- Output structures that are difficult for the model to follow.\n\nFor the legal safe-harbor statement, few-shot prompting can improve adherence, but a regulated production system should not rely solely on probabilistic generation. The safer implementation is to append or validate the required line deterministically in application code and use an exact-match scorer to detect omissions or modifications.\n\nThe expected exam selections remain A and C, because the question specifically asks which candidate few-shot examples have the greatest marginal value.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- MLflow Prompt Optimization\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/automatically-optimize-prompts](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/automatically-optimize-prompts)\n\n- Evaluate and compare prompt versions\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts)\n\n- Scorers and LLM judges\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/scorers](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/scorers)\n\n- Define quality: Evaluation sets\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/evaluate-define-quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/evaluate-define-quality)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. An example demonstrating a calm, appropriately-hedged update for a full outage with no known ETA, modeling the tone and structure the agent hasn't been shown.\n\nC. An example showing the exact required legal safe-harbor closing line in its correct verbatim format for a regulated-service outage.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question asks which two examples provide the greatest marginal value under a limited token budget. The best examples are not necessarily those representing the most common requests; they are the examples that address the agent’s most important demonstrated failures.\n\nThe agent already handles ordinary “delayed but on track” incidents reliably. Additional examples of that pattern would mostly duplicate behavior it has already learned.\n\nIts quality gaps are concentrated in two areas:\n\n- A novel, high-risk scenario: a complete outage with no known ETA.\n\n- A strict output requirement: a legal safe-harbor statement that must appear verbatim.\n\nTherefore, examples A and C add the most new and useful information to the prompt.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA addresses an uncovered behavioral scenario. The agent has never been shown how to communicate a full outage when no restoration estimate is available. Its current failures—over-promising a recovery time or using an unnecessarily alarming tone—show that general incident examples are not transferring reliably to this case.\n\nA strong example can demonstrate several requirements simultaneously:\n\n- Clearly acknowledge the outage.\n\n- Avoid inventing or implying an ETA.\n\n- Use calm, factual language.\n\n- Explain what is being investigated.\n\n- Set an appropriate expectation for the next update.\n\nThis example has high marginal value because it fills a meaningful gap rather than reinforcing an already reliable pattern.\n\nC addresses an exact-compliance failure. The safe-harbor line must be reproduced verbatim and in a specific format, but the agent currently omits or paraphrases it. A correct demonstration gives the model a concrete output pattern rather than relying only on an abstract instruction such as “include the required legal wording.”\n\nTogether, A and C cover two distinct deficiencies:\n\n- A: semantic, structural, and tonal behavior for a novel scenario.\n\n- C: exact adherence to a mandatory output constraint.\n\nThis is the most efficient use of the limited few-shot token budget.","B. An example demonstrating a \"delayed but on track\" update, since this is the most common scenario the agent will encounter.\n\nThis is a valid incident type, but the agent already handles it reliably. Frequency alone does not make an example valuable.\n\nBecause few-shot examples consume context tokens, repeating a well-performing pattern provides relatively little incremental improvement. The limited budget should instead be allocated to known failure modes and underrepresented scenarios.","Correct Answer\n\nA. An example demonstrating a calm, appropriately-hedged update for a full outage with no known ETA, modeling the tone and structure the agent hasn't been shown.\n\nC. An example showing the exact required legal safe-harbor closing line in its correct verbatim format for a regulated-service outage.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question asks which two examples provide the greatest marginal value under a limited token budget. The best examples are not necessarily those representing the most common requests; they are the examples that address the agent’s most important demonstrated failures.\n\nThe agent already handles ordinary “delayed but on track” incidents reliably. Additional examples of that pattern would mostly duplicate behavior it has already learned.\n\nIts quality gaps are concentrated in two areas:\n\n- A novel, high-risk scenario: a complete outage with no known ETA.\n\n- A strict output requirement: a legal safe-harbor statement that must appear verbatim.\n\nTherefore, examples A and C add the most new and useful information to the prompt.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA addresses an uncovered behavioral scenario. The agent has never been shown how to communicate a full outage when no restoration estimate is available. Its current failures—over-promising a recovery time or using an unnecessarily alarming tone—show that general incident examples are not transferring reliably to this case.\n\nA strong example can demonstrate several requirements simultaneously:\n\n- Clearly acknowledge the outage.\n\n- Avoid inventing or implying an ETA.\n\n- Use calm, factual language.\n\n- Explain what is being investigated.\n\n- Set an appropriate expectation for the next update.\n\nThis example has high marginal value because it fills a meaningful gap rather than reinforcing an already reliable pattern.\n\nC addresses an exact-compliance failure. The safe-harbor line must be reproduced verbatim and in a specific format, but the agent currently omits or paraphrases it. A correct demonstration gives the model a concrete output pattern rather than relying only on an abstract instruction such as “include the required legal wording.”\n\nTogether, A and C cover two distinct deficiencies:\n\n- A: semantic, structural, and tonal behavior for a novel scenario.\n\n- C: exact adherence to a mandatory output constraint.\n\nThis is the most efficient use of the limited few-shot token budget.","D. An example demonstrating how to greet the customer at the start of the message, since a friendly opening is important for every type of update.\n\nA friendly opening may improve presentation, but it does not address either of the important observed failures:\n\n- Inappropriate communication during a complete outage with no ETA.\n\n- Failure to reproduce legally required text exactly.\n\nA greeting is also comparatively simple to specify through a concise instruction or template. It does not justify consuming one of the highest-value few-shot example slots when material operational and regulatory gaps remain."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/automatically-optimize-prompts","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/scorers","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/evaluate-define-quality"],"source_hash":"c1f3a1e5c1cbf5df847748cf9f86e10b7c069e8a43e406b39bbc5fc69589de7a","correct_many":[0,2],"stem_ja":"Databricksのエージェントが、顧客向けの障害状況の案内文を作成します。限られたトークン予算の中で、few-shot例の候補が8件あります。「遅延しているが順調に進んでいる」という典型的な状況は、すでに安定して案内できます。不足は2つの状況に集中しています。1つは復旧見込み時刻（ETA）が不明な全面停止の案内で、この語調と構成を例示したことがなく、復旧時刻を過度に約束するか、不必要に不安をあおる表現になりがちです。もう1つは規制対象サービスの障害時に特定の書式で一字一句そのまま記載すべき、法的セーフハーバーの結びの文で、現在は省略したり誤って言い換えたりします。\n\n限られたトークン予算で、追加による価値が最も高いfew-shot例を2つ選んでください。","options_ja":["復旧見込みが不明な全面停止に対し、落ち着いて適切に断定を避けた案内を示し、まだ提示されていない語調と構成を例示するもの。","エージェントが最も頻繁に遭遇するため、「遅延しているが順調」という案内を示すもの。","規制対象サービスの障害時に必要な法的セーフハーバーの結びの文を、正しい書式と正確な文言で示すもの。","どの案内でも親しみやすい冒頭が重要なので、メッセージの始めに顧客へ挨拶する方法を示すもの。"],"explanation_ja":"例の価値は出現頻度だけでなく、今できていない行動をどれだけ補えるかで判断します。すでに得意な遅延案内を重ねるより、復旧時刻不明の案内と、厳密な法的定型文を例示する方が有効です。覚え方は「few-shotは既知の成功を増やすより、未対応の失敗を埋める」です。","distractors_ja":["実際に失敗している語調と構成を、新しい例で直接補えるため、追加の価値が高いです。","すでに安定して処理できる場面なので、限られた予算で優先すべき不足を補いません。","省略・言い換えが許されない出力形式を、正確な文例で直接教えられます。","一般的な挨拶は、明示された2つの失敗パターンを直接改善しません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-010","number":10,"form":2,"stem":"A Databricks agent's system prompt includes the blanket instruction \"never ask the user clarifying questions; always make your best assumption and proceed.\" In production, this causes the agent to silently guess when a request is genuinely ambiguous — for example, a customer has two similarly-named open orders, and the agent picks one to cancel without confirming which the customer meant, occasionally canceling the wrong order. The team wants to fix this without significantly increasing prompt length or adding new infrastructure like a separate confirmation-checking service.\n\nWhich revision strategy most directly resolves this failure with the least increase in token cost and maintenance burden?","options":["Remove the instruction entirely and add no replacement guidance, leaving the agent's default behavior around ambiguity undefined.","Add a separate microservice that intercepts every agent action and requires human approval before execution, regardless of ambiguity.","Keep the instruction unchanged, and instead add ten few-shot examples demonstrating the agent correctly guessing right in ambiguous order-cancellation scenarios.","Replace the blanket instruction with a conditional one: proceed on a best-guess assumption for low-stakes or unambiguous requests, but ask a single clarifying question when a request is genuinely ambiguous and the action is costly or hard to reverse, such as canceling one of multiple similar orders."],"correct":3,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"曖昧で重大な操作に限定した確認","concept":"ce-f2-q010","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Replace the blanket instruction with a conditional one: proceed on a best-guess assumption for low-stakes or unambiguous requests, but ask a single clarifying question when a request is genuinely ambiguous and the action is costly or hard to reverse, such as canceling one of multiple similar orders.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure is caused by an overly broad system instruction that prohibits clarification under every circumstance. It forces the agent to guess even when:\n\n- The user’s intent cannot be determined reliably.\n\n- Multiple valid action targets exist.\n\n- The action is consequential or difficult to reverse.\n\n- A wrong assumption can directly harm the customer.\n\nThe question also requires the solution to avoid:\n\n- Significant prompt growth.\n\n- Multiple few-shot examples.\n\n- New infrastructure.\n\n- Unnecessary human approval for routine requests.\n\nOption D directly replaces the defective absolute rule with a concise, risk-aware decision rule. The agent can continue efficiently for ordinary requests while asking one focused question when ambiguity and action risk occur together.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA conditional instruction aligns agent autonomy with the ambiguity and consequence of the action.\n\nThe revised behavior would be:\n\n- Unambiguous request: Proceed.\n\n- Low-stakes ambiguity: Make a reasonable assumption when safe.\n\n- High-stakes, reversible only with difficulty: Ask one targeted clarification question.\n\n- Multiple plausible action targets: Do not select one silently.\n\nFor example:\n\n“I found two open orders named ‘Office Supplies’: order 4821 and order 4876. Which one would you like me to cancel?”\n\nThis avoids an incorrect cancellation while adding only one short interaction when clarification is actually necessary.\n\nIt also has the lowest operational burden because it changes a single prompt instruction rather than introducing services, approval queues, or many additional examples. Databricks guidance emphasizes carefully constraining agent autonomy, particularly when tools can perform external actions, while keeping the architecture as simple as the use case permits.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Remove the instruction entirely and add no replacement guidance, leaving the agent's default behavior around ambiguity undefined.\n\nRemoving the harmful instruction may stop explicitly forcing the agent to guess, but it does not establish the required safe behavior.\n\nWithout replacement guidance, the agent could:\n\n- Ask unnecessary questions for routine requests.\n\n- Continue guessing in consequential situations.\n\n- Behave inconsistently across models or prompt versions.\n\n- Alternate unpredictably between clarification and execution.\n\nThe failure requires a clear conditional policy, not an undefined default.\n\nB. Add a separate microservice that intercepts every agent action and requires human approval before execution, regardless of ambiguity.\n\nThis could prevent incorrect cancellations, but it is disproportionate to the stated problem and violates the scenario’s constraints.\n\nIt would introduce:\n\n- New infrastructure.\n\n- Additional latency.\n\n- Operational and maintenance overhead.\n\n- Human-review workload for unambiguous, low-risk actions.\n\n- Reduced agent usefulness and autonomy.\n\nHuman approval can be appropriate for particularly sensitive actions, but requiring it for every action is not the least-cost or most direct correction.\n\nC. Keep the instruction unchanged, and instead add ten few-shot examples demonstrating the agent correctly guessing right in ambiguous order-cancellation scenarios.\n\nThis preserves the root cause: the agent is still explicitly prohibited from requesting information it needs.\n\nFew-shot examples of successful guesses would also reinforce unsafe behavior. They cannot guarantee the correct choice when two orders are genuinely indistinguishable from the available context.\n\nThis approach would:\n\n- Increase prompt-token usage substantially.\n\n- Require ongoing example maintenance.\n\n- Fail to cover every future ambiguity.\n\n- Encourage the agent to appear confident despite insufficient information.\n\nExamples should not be used to teach an agent how to guess correctly when the necessary distinguishing fact is unavailable.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe primary trap is treating clarification as universally undesirable because it adds an interaction.\n\nThe correct objective is not to eliminate all clarification. It is to avoid unnecessary clarification while requiring it when:\n\n- Intent is genuinely ambiguous.\n\n- Multiple targets are plausible.\n\n- The consequences of a wrong assumption are significant.\n\nAnother trap is selecting a technically safer but unnecessarily complex human-approval architecture when a concise prompt correction directly addresses the observed failure.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nSystem prompts should avoid unconditional rules such as:\n\n- “Never ask questions.”\n\n- “Always execute immediately.”\n\n- “Always make an assumption.”\n\n- “Never refuse a tool call.”\n\nReplace them with explicit decision boundaries. For example:\n\nProceed without clarification when the request is unambiguous or when a reasonable assumption has low impact and is easy to reverse. Ask one concise clarifying question when multiple interpretations are plausible and choosing incorrectly could cause financial loss, delete or modify data, cancel an order, or otherwise create a costly or difficult-to-reverse outcome.\n\nIn production, the revised prompt should be evaluated against cases covering:\n\n- One clearly identified order.\n\n- Two similarly named orders.\n\n- An already canceled order.\n\n- A request containing a valid order ID.\n\n- Ambiguous read-only requests.\n\n- Ambiguous destructive requests.\n\nThe exam answer is D because it fixes the instruction at its source while adding minimal tokens and no new architecture.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- Curate an effective Genie Agent\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/best-practices](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/best-practices)\n\n- Author an agent and deploy it on Databricks Apps\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/author-agent](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/author-agent)\n\n- Evaluate and compare prompt versions\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts)","source_option_explanations":["A. Remove the instruction entirely and add no replacement guidance, leaving the agent's default behavior around ambiguity undefined.\n\nRemoving the harmful instruction may stop explicitly forcing the agent to guess, but it does not establish the required safe behavior.\n\nWithout replacement guidance, the agent could:\n\n- Ask unnecessary questions for routine requests.\n\n- Continue guessing in consequential situations.\n\n- Behave inconsistently across models or prompt versions.\n\n- Alternate unpredictably between clarification and execution.\n\nThe failure requires a clear conditional policy, not an undefined default.","B. Add a separate microservice that intercepts every agent action and requires human approval before execution, regardless of ambiguity.\n\nThis could prevent incorrect cancellations, but it is disproportionate to the stated problem and violates the scenario’s constraints.\n\nIt would introduce:\n\n- New infrastructure.\n\n- Additional latency.\n\n- Operational and maintenance overhead.\n\n- Human-review workload for unambiguous, low-risk actions.\n\n- Reduced agent usefulness and autonomy.\n\nHuman approval can be appropriate for particularly sensitive actions, but requiring it for every action is not the least-cost or most direct correction.","C. Keep the instruction unchanged, and instead add ten few-shot examples demonstrating the agent correctly guessing right in ambiguous order-cancellation scenarios.\n\nThis preserves the root cause: the agent is still explicitly prohibited from requesting information it needs.\n\nFew-shot examples of successful guesses would also reinforce unsafe behavior. They cannot guarantee the correct choice when two orders are genuinely indistinguishable from the available context.\n\nThis approach would:\n\n- Increase prompt-token usage substantially.\n\n- Require ongoing example maintenance.\n\n- Fail to cover every future ambiguity.\n\n- Encourage the agent to appear confident despite insufficient information.\n\nExamples should not be used to teach an agent how to guess correctly when the necessary distinguishing fact is unavailable.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Replace the blanket instruction with a conditional one: proceed on a best-guess assumption for low-stakes or unambiguous requests, but ask a single clarifying question when a request is genuinely ambiguous and the action is costly or hard to reverse, such as canceling one of multiple similar orders.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure is caused by an overly broad system instruction that prohibits clarification under every circumstance. It forces the agent to guess even when:\n\n- The user’s intent cannot be determined reliably.\n\n- Multiple valid action targets exist.\n\n- The action is consequential or difficult to reverse.\n\n- A wrong assumption can directly harm the customer.\n\nThe question also requires the solution to avoid:\n\n- Significant prompt growth.\n\n- Multiple few-shot examples.\n\n- New infrastructure.\n\n- Unnecessary human approval for routine requests.\n\nOption D directly replaces the defective absolute rule with a concise, risk-aware decision rule. The agent can continue efficiently for ordinary requests while asking one focused question when ambiguity and action risk occur together.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA conditional instruction aligns agent autonomy with the ambiguity and consequence of the action.\n\nThe revised behavior would be:\n\n- Unambiguous request: Proceed.\n\n- Low-stakes ambiguity: Make a reasonable assumption when safe.\n\n- High-stakes, reversible only with difficulty: Ask one targeted clarification question.\n\n- Multiple plausible action targets: Do not select one silently.\n\nFor example:\n\n“I found two open orders named ‘Office Supplies’: order 4821 and order 4876. Which one would you like me to cancel?”\n\nThis avoids an incorrect cancellation while adding only one short interaction when clarification is actually necessary.\n\nIt also has the lowest operational burden because it changes a single prompt instruction rather than introducing services, approval queues, or many additional examples. Databricks guidance emphasizes carefully constraining agent autonomy, particularly when tools can perform external actions, while keeping the architecture as simple as the use case permits."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/best-practices","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/author-agent","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts"],"source_hash":"000084ffef31c4465a81232fcd3c2f3607727c2629e2256d4302fe630104da12","stem_ja":"Databricksのエージェントのシステムプロンプトには、「ユーザーに確認質問を決してしない。常に最善の仮定を置いて進める」という一律の指示があります。本番では、依頼が本当に曖昧でも黙って推測する原因となっています。たとえば、顧客に似た名前の未完了注文が2つある場合、どちらを指すか確かめずに片方を取り消し、ときどき誤った注文をキャンセルします。チームは、プロンプトを大幅に長くしたり、別の確認チェックサービスのような新しい基盤を加えたりせずに直したいと考えています。\n\nトークンコストと保守負担の増加を最小限にして、この障害を最も直接解決する指示の修正はどれですか。","options_ja":["指示を完全に削除して代わりの方針は追加せず、曖昧さへの対応をエージェントの既定動作に任せる。","曖昧かどうかに関係なく、すべてのエージェント操作を横取りし、実行前に人の承認を要求する別のマイクロサービスを追加する。","指示をそのままにして、曖昧な注文キャンセルでエージェントの推測が当たる例をfew-shot例として10件追加する。","一律の指示を条件付きに変える。影響が小さい依頼や明確な依頼は最善の仮定で進めるが、似た注文のどれかを取り消す場合のように、本当に曖昧で、費用が大きいか元に戻しにくい操作では、1つだけ確認質問をする。"],"explanation_ja":"問題の原因は「確認を一律禁止する指示」です。重大で曖昧な操作だけ確認する条件に置き換えれば、短い修正で誤キャンセルを防げます。何でも確認する新基盤も、多数の成功例も不要です。覚え方は「明確・軽微なら進める、曖昧・重大なら確認する」。実装の前に原因の指示を直します。","distractors_ja":["誤った禁止は除けますが、重大で曖昧な依頼をどう扱うかが明示されず、再発を防ぎにくくなります。","基盤と保守負担が増え、明確で軽微な操作にも承認を強いるため、要件に合いません。","例を増やしても、確認を禁じる指示は残り、推測が外れたときの誤操作を防げません。","確認が必要な条件を短く指定し、問題の原因を最小限の変更で修正できます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-011","number":11,"form":2,"stem":"Using MLflow 3, a context engineer compares two system prompt configurations for a technical-support agent. Configuration 1 (600 tokens) is the current baseline. Configuration 2 (1,800 tokens) adds three additional worked examples of multi-step diagnostic reasoning. Across 150 evaluation traces, segmented by question difficulty: on straightforward single-fact questions, answer_correctness is statistically identical between the two configurations. On multi-step diagnostic questions, Configuration 2 shows a substantial, statistically significant improvement over Configuration 1. Token cost per request for Configuration 2 is roughly three times that of Configuration 1.\n\nWhich conclusion and next step is best supported by this evidence?","options":["Blanket adoption of the higher-token configuration is not justified, since it would triple cost even for the straightforward questions where it shows no measurable benefit; the evidence instead supports a conditional design — routing multi-step diagnostic questions to the version with the added worked examples, while keeping the cheaper baseline for straightforward questions — capturing the improvement only where it's actually earned.","The higher-token configuration is unjustified everywhere, since any config that doesn't improve every single question category should be discarded in its entirety.","The higher-token configuration is justified for blanket adoption on all questions, since the observed improvement on multi-step questions proves the added examples are universally beneficial regardless of question type.","The results are inconclusive and no action should be taken until a fourth configuration is tested, since two configurations are never sufficient to draw any conclusion."],"correct":0,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"難易度別にプロンプト追加の効果を評価","concept":"ce-f2-q011","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Blanket adoption of the higher-token configuration is not justified, since it would triple cost even for the straightforward questions where it shows no measurable benefit; the evidence instead supports a conditional design — routing multi-step diagnostic questions to the version with the added worked examples, while keeping the cheaper baseline for straightforward questions — capturing the improvement only where it's actually earned.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe evaluation results show that the benefit of Configuration 2 is conditional on question difficulty:\n\n- For straightforward questions, both configurations perform equally well.\n\n- For multi-step diagnostic questions, Configuration 2 produces a significant improvement.\n\n- Configuration 2 costs approximately three times as much per request.\n\nTherefore, neither blanket rejection nor blanket adoption is supported. The evidence indicates that the additional worked examples are valuable specifically when multi-step reasoning is required.\n\nThe best next step is to use:\n\n- Configuration 1 for straightforward requests.\n\n- Configuration 2 for multi-step diagnostic requests.\n\nThis preserves the quality improvement while avoiding unnecessary token cost for requests that do not benefit from the longer prompt.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nMLflow 3 evaluation supports comparing prompt or application versions against a consistent evaluation dataset and measuring their quality with scorers. Segmenting the traces by question difficulty reveals information that would be hidden by relying only on an overall average score.\n\nThe longer prompt is not universally superior. Its added examples provide measurable value for multi-step diagnostics, but they are redundant for simple questions that the baseline already answers correctly.\n\nA conditional design therefore optimizes the quality-cost trade-off:\n\nRequest categoryAppropriate configurationReasonStraightforward single-fact questionConfiguration 1Same correctness at lower token costMulti-step diagnostic questionConfiguration 2Significant correctness improvement justifies the additional tokens\n\nThis conclusion is based on measured marginal value. The additional 1,200 prompt tokens should be consumed only for the category where they produce a meaningful quality gain.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. The higher-token configuration is unjustified everywhere, since any config that doesn't improve every single question category should be discarded in its entirety.\n\nThis ignores the substantial and statistically significant improvement on multi-step diagnostic questions.\n\nA configuration does not need to improve every category to be valuable. It can still be the correct choice for a particular request segment. Discarding it entirely would sacrifice a demonstrated quality improvement on the more difficult questions.\n\nC. The higher-token configuration is justified for blanket adoption on all questions, since the observed improvement on multi-step questions proves the added examples are universally beneficial regardless of question type.\n\nThis overgeneralizes the segmented result.\n\nThe evaluation explicitly shows no measurable benefit for straightforward questions. Deploying Configuration 2 for every request would therefore triple token cost for a large category without improving correctness.\n\nAn improvement within one segment does not prove that the change is universally beneficial.\n\nD. The results are inconclusive and no action should be taken until a fourth configuration is tested, since two configurations are never sufficient to draw any conclusion.\n\nTwo configurations can be sufficient for a controlled comparison when:\n\n- They are tested against the same evaluation criteria.\n\n- The dataset has useful coverage.\n\n- Results are segmented appropriately.\n\n- Statistical analysis establishes whether observed differences are meaningful.\n\nAdditional configurations might uncover further optimization opportunities, but they are not required to act on the current evidence. The existing evaluation already supports a conditional deployment strategy.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is using only an aggregate winner instead of examining where each configuration adds value.\n\nThe question tests whether you can distinguish between:\n\n- A universally better prompt.\n\n- A prompt that is better only for a specific request category.\n\n- Additional token cost that is justified by measurable quality improvement.\n\n- Additional token cost that provides no marginal benefit.\n\nThe longer prompt should not automatically replace the shorter one merely because it wins on the hardest cases.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nIn production, prompt selection can be treated as a routing problem.\n\nA lightweight router could classify requests as:\n\n- Straightforward fact retrieval.\n\n- Multi-step diagnosis.\n\n- Ambiguous or uncertain.\n\nStraightforward requests would use the baseline prompt, while multi-step cases would use the enhanced prompt. Low-confidence routing decisions could default to Configuration 2 when the cost of an incorrect technical answer exceeds the additional token expense.\n\nBefore deployment, the team should also evaluate:\n\n- Router classification accuracy.\n\n- End-to-end correctness after routing.\n\n- Average and percentile token cost.\n\n- Latency for each request category.\n\n- Whether the extra examples introduce regressions.\n\n- The actual production distribution of simple versus diagnostic questions.\n\nThe exam answer is A because it is the only option that follows the segmented evaluation evidence and captures the quality improvement without paying the higher cost where no benefit was measured.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Evaluate and compare prompt versions\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts)\n\n- Building MLflow evaluation datasets\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset)\n\n- Debug and analyze your app with tracing\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces)\n\n- Tutorial: Evaluate and improve a GenAI application\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Blanket adoption of the higher-token configuration is not justified, since it would triple cost even for the straightforward questions where it shows no measurable benefit; the evidence instead supports a conditional design — routing multi-step diagnostic questions to the version with the added worked examples, while keeping the cheaper baseline for straightforward questions — capturing the improvement only where it's actually earned.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe evaluation results show that the benefit of Configuration 2 is conditional on question difficulty:\n\n- For straightforward questions, both configurations perform equally well.\n\n- For multi-step diagnostic questions, Configuration 2 produces a significant improvement.\n\n- Configuration 2 costs approximately three times as much per request.\n\nTherefore, neither blanket rejection nor blanket adoption is supported. The evidence indicates that the additional worked examples are valuable specifically when multi-step reasoning is required.\n\nThe best next step is to use:\n\n- Configuration 1 for straightforward requests.\n\n- Configuration 2 for multi-step diagnostic requests.\n\nThis preserves the quality improvement while avoiding unnecessary token cost for requests that do not benefit from the longer prompt.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nMLflow 3 evaluation supports comparing prompt or application versions against a consistent evaluation dataset and measuring their quality with scorers. Segmenting the traces by question difficulty reveals information that would be hidden by relying only on an overall average score.\n\nThe longer prompt is not universally superior. Its added examples provide measurable value for multi-step diagnostics, but they are redundant for simple questions that the baseline already answers correctly.\n\nA conditional design therefore optimizes the quality-cost trade-off:\n\nRequest categoryAppropriate configurationReasonStraightforward single-fact questionConfiguration 1Same correctness at lower token costMulti-step diagnostic questionConfiguration 2Significant correctness improvement justifies the additional tokens\n\nThis conclusion is based on measured marginal value. The additional 1,200 prompt tokens should be consumed only for the category where they produce a meaningful quality gain.","B. The higher-token configuration is unjustified everywhere, since any config that doesn't improve every single question category should be discarded in its entirety.\n\nThis ignores the substantial and statistically significant improvement on multi-step diagnostic questions.\n\nA configuration does not need to improve every category to be valuable. It can still be the correct choice for a particular request segment. Discarding it entirely would sacrifice a demonstrated quality improvement on the more difficult questions.","C. The higher-token configuration is justified for blanket adoption on all questions, since the observed improvement on multi-step questions proves the added examples are universally beneficial regardless of question type.\n\nThis overgeneralizes the segmented result.\n\nThe evaluation explicitly shows no measurable benefit for straightforward questions. Deploying Configuration 2 for every request would therefore triple token cost for a large category without improving correctness.\n\nAn improvement within one segment does not prove that the change is universally beneficial.","D. The results are inconclusive and no action should be taken until a fourth configuration is tested, since two configurations are never sufficient to draw any conclusion.\n\nTwo configurations can be sufficient for a controlled comparison when:\n\n- They are tested against the same evaluation criteria.\n\n- The dataset has useful coverage.\n\n- Results are segmented appropriately.\n\n- Statistical analysis establishes whether observed differences are meaningful.\n\nAdditional configurations might uncover further optimization opportunities, but they are not required to act on the current evidence. The existing evaluation already supports a conditional deployment strategy."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app"],"source_hash":"a2e2348917f32f6f1246e9217d1e91145e683ed193c7534562ed24cd5e3c6c7d","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアがMLflow 3を使い、技術サポートエージェントのシステムプロンプトを2構成で比較しています。構成1は600トークンの現行基準です。構成2は1,800トークンで、複数ステップの診断推論の解答付き例を3件追加しています。150件の評価トレースを質問の難しさで分けると、単純な1つの事実を聞く質問では、両構成のanswer_correctnessに統計的な差はありません。複数ステップの診断質問では、構成2が構成1より大きく、統計的に有意に改善しています。構成2の1リクエスト当たりトークンコストは、構成1の約3倍です。\n\nこの証拠から最も支持される結論と次の対応はどれですか。","options_ja":["単純な質問でも効果なく費用が3倍になるため、高トークン構成の一律採用は正当化されない。証拠は条件付き設計を支持しており、複数ステップの診断質問は解答例を追加した版へ、単純な質問は安価な基準版へ振り分け、効果が得られる場面だけ改善を利用する。","すべての質問区分で改善しない構成は全体を捨てるべきなので、高トークン構成はどの場面でも正当化されない。","複数ステップの質問での改善が、質問種別にかかわらず追加例の普遍的な有効性を証明したので、すべての質問で高トークン構成を一律採用すべきである。","2つの構成では結論を出せないため、結果は不確定であり、4つ目の構成を試すまで何も対応すべきではない。"],"explanation_ja":"平均点だけでなく、どの質問で改善したかを見ます。追加例は難しい診断には有効ですが、単純な質問では費用増だけです。難易度に応じて構成を振り分ければ、有効な改善を残しながら不要なコストを避けられます。覚え方は「効果が出た区分にだけ追加のコンテキストを使う」です。","distractors_ja":["改善が測定された難問だけに追加例を使うため、効果とコストの両方を踏まえています。","難しい診断では有意な改善があるため、全面的な破棄は有効な成果まで失います。","単純な質問で改善がないという実測結果と矛盾し、不要な費用を発生させます。","適切に比較した2構成からでも区分別の効果は判断でき、4構成が必須という根拠はありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-012","number":12,"form":2,"stem":"An underperforming Genie space for a hospital's scheduling team returns inflated appointment counts whenever analysts ask \"how many appointments did each provider have last month.\" Investigating the Unity Catalog metadata, the engineer finds the appointments table has no declared primary key, and its join relationship to the appointment_notes table — which stores one or more free-text notes per appointment — is undocumented in Unity Catalog. Genie infers a join on appointment_id, and because a single appointment can have multiple notes, the join fans out and inflates the appointment count by however many notes each appointment has.\n\nWhich configuration change will have the highest impact on closing this accuracy gap?","options":["Declare the appointments table's primary key in Unity Catalog, and either curate a pre-aggregated view that avoids the one-to-many fan-out from appointment_notes, or explicitly document that appointment_notes should not be joined for count-based questions.","Add a general text instruction telling Genie to \"always return accurate appointment counts,\" without changing any table or join metadata.","Increase the SQL warehouse size so the inflated join query executes faster.","Remove the appointment_notes table from the Genie space's list of curated table descriptions only, while leaving it fully joinable and undocumented."],"correct":0,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"1対多結合による件数の水増し","concept":"ce-f2-q012","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Declare the appointments table's primary key in Unity Catalog, and either curate a pre-aggregated view that avoids the one-to-many fan-out from appointment_notes, or explicitly document that appointment_notes should not be joined for count-based questions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe inaccurate count is caused by a data-model and join-grain problem, not by insufficient compute or a vague prompt.\n\nThe relevant facts are:\n\n- `appointments` contains one row per appointment.\n\n- `appointment_notes` contains one or more rows per appointment.\n\n- Genie joins the tables using `appointment_id`.\n\n- Counting rows after this one-to-many join counts each appointment once for every related note.\n\n- Unity Catalog does not identify the primary key or provide the relationship metadata Genie needs to reason about table grain correctly.\n\nThe highest-impact correction is therefore to improve the structured semantic context and provide a safe dataset for appointment-level aggregation. Option A addresses the actual cause by identifying the appointment grain and preventing the unnecessary one-to-many join from affecting counts.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nDeclaring `appointments.appointment_id` as the primary key tells Genie that this column uniquely identifies appointments. Genie uses primary-key and foreign-key metadata from Unity Catalog when translating natural-language questions into SQL and determining table relationships.\n\nHowever, declaring the primary key alone does not automatically make every join safe. Databricks primary-key and foreign-key constraints are informational rather than enforced, and a one-to-many join can still multiply appointment rows.\n\nFor a question such as:\n\n```\nSELECT    provider_id,    COUNT(*) AS appointment_countFROM appointmentsWHERE appointment_date >= DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', CURRENT_DATE()) - INTERVAL 1 MONTH  AND appointment_date < DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', CURRENT_DATE())GROUP BY provider_id;\n```\n\n`appointment_notes` is not needed. A curated appointment-level view can expose exactly the dimensions and measures required for scheduling analysis without introducing note-level fan-out.\n\nDatabricks recommends simplifying Genie datasets through pre-joined, denormalized, aggregated, or metric views when complex joins and aggregations can cause inaccurate results. It also recommends defining join relationships explicitly when Unity Catalog does not contain the required metadata.\n\nTherefore, option A most directly closes the accuracy gap by combining:\n\n- Correct grain metadata.\n\n- Controlled join behavior.\n\n- A simplified appointment-level analytical source.\n\n- Explicit instructions preventing an irrelevant notes join for count questions.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Add a general text instruction telling Genie to \"always return accurate appointment counts,\" without changing any table or join metadata.\n\nThis instruction describes the desired outcome but does not teach Genie how to produce it.\n\nIt does not specify:\n\n- The correct appointment grain.\n\n- Why joining notes causes duplication.\n\n- Whether to use `COUNT(*)` or `COUNT(DISTINCT appointment_id)`.\n\n- When `appointment_notes` should be excluded.\n\n- How appointments and notes are related.\n\nDatabricks recommends structured metadata, SQL expressions, example SQL, and simplified views over vague general instructions. “Always be accurate” is not an executable business or join rule.\n\nC. Increase the SQL warehouse size so the inflated join query executes faster.\n\nA larger warehouse might improve query execution speed, but it does not change the query’s logic.\n\nThe incorrect join would still create multiple rows per appointment, and Genie would return the same inflated result more quickly. Compute scaling addresses performance, not semantic correctness.\n\nD. Remove the appointment_notes table from the Genie space's list of curated table descriptions only, while leaving it fully joinable and undocumented.\n\nRemoving only the description does not reliably prevent Genie from selecting or joining the table if it remains available as a source.\n\nIt can make the situation worse because Genie would have even less information about:\n\n- The table’s note-level grain.\n\n- Its one-to-many relationship with appointments.\n\n- When it should or should not be used.\n\nTo eliminate the risk, the table should be removed or hidden from the agent when it is unnecessary, or its relationship and usage rules should be explicitly curated. Merely deleting its description does neither.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is confusing a query-performance problem with a query-correctness problem.\n\nThe warehouse is successfully executing the SQL that Genie generated. The result is wrong because the SQL joins datasets at incompatible grains before counting rows.\n\nAnother trap is assuming that a vague natural-language instruction is as reliable as primary-key metadata, explicit join definitions, or a curated appointment-level view.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe safest implementation would strengthen option A further:\n\n- Declare `appointments.appointment_id` as the primary key.\n\n- Declare `appointment_notes.appointment_id` as a foreign key referencing the appointment key, or define the relationship in the Genie knowledge store.\n\n- Document that the relationship is one appointment to many notes.\n\n- Create a scheduling-focused view or metric view with one row per appointment.\n\n- Define an appointment-count measure using the correct grain.\n\n- Add benchmark questions that verify counts by provider, department, location, and month.\n\nFor example, when a notes join is genuinely required, counting distinct appointments prevents fan-out:\n\n```\nCOUNT(DISTINCT appointments.appointment_id)\n```\n\nA curated appointment-grain view is generally preferable for common scheduling questions because it simplifies SQL generation and reduces the chance of future join mistakes.\n\nThe expected exam answer is A, although in a production implementation, documenting both the primary key and the corresponding foreign-key or local join relationship would provide even stronger semantic context.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Tune Genie Agent quality\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality)\n\n- Troubleshoot Genie Agents\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/troubleshooting](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/troubleshooting)\n\n- Curate an effective Genie Agent\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices)\n\n- Constraints on Databricks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/constraints](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/constraints)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Declare the appointments table's primary key in Unity Catalog, and either curate a pre-aggregated view that avoids the one-to-many fan-out from appointment_notes, or explicitly document that appointment_notes should not be joined for count-based questions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe inaccurate count is caused by a data-model and join-grain problem, not by insufficient compute or a vague prompt.\n\nThe relevant facts are:\n\n- `appointments` contains one row per appointment.\n\n- `appointment_notes` contains one or more rows per appointment.\n\n- Genie joins the tables using `appointment_id`.\n\n- Counting rows after this one-to-many join counts each appointment once for every related note.\n\n- Unity Catalog does not identify the primary key or provide the relationship metadata Genie needs to reason about table grain correctly.\n\nThe highest-impact correction is therefore to improve the structured semantic context and provide a safe dataset for appointment-level aggregation. Option A addresses the actual cause by identifying the appointment grain and preventing the unnecessary one-to-many join from affecting counts.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nDeclaring `appointments.appointment_id` as the primary key tells Genie that this column uniquely identifies appointments. Genie uses primary-key and foreign-key metadata from Unity Catalog when translating natural-language questions into SQL and determining table relationships.\n\nHowever, declaring the primary key alone does not automatically make every join safe. Databricks primary-key and foreign-key constraints are informational rather than enforced, and a one-to-many join can still multiply appointment rows.\n\nFor a question such as:\n\n```\nSELECT    provider_id,    COUNT(*) AS appointment_countFROM appointmentsWHERE appointment_date >= DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', CURRENT_DATE()) - INTERVAL 1 MONTH  AND appointment_date < DATE_TRUNC('MONTH', CURRENT_DATE())GROUP BY provider_id;\n```\n\n`appointment_notes` is not needed. A curated appointment-level view can expose exactly the dimensions and measures required for scheduling analysis without introducing note-level fan-out.\n\nDatabricks recommends simplifying Genie datasets through pre-joined, denormalized, aggregated, or metric views when complex joins and aggregations can cause inaccurate results. It also recommends defining join relationships explicitly when Unity Catalog does not contain the required metadata.\n\nTherefore, option A most directly closes the accuracy gap by combining:\n\n- Correct grain metadata.\n\n- Controlled join behavior.\n\n- A simplified appointment-level analytical source.\n\n- Explicit instructions preventing an irrelevant notes join for count questions.","B. Add a general text instruction telling Genie to \"always return accurate appointment counts,\" without changing any table or join metadata.\n\nThis instruction describes the desired outcome but does not teach Genie how to produce it.\n\nIt does not specify:\n\n- The correct appointment grain.\n\n- Why joining notes causes duplication.\n\n- Whether to use `COUNT(*)` or `COUNT(DISTINCT appointment_id)`.\n\n- When `appointment_notes` should be excluded.\n\n- How appointments and notes are related.\n\nDatabricks recommends structured metadata, SQL expressions, example SQL, and simplified views over vague general instructions. “Always be accurate” is not an executable business or join rule.","C. Increase the SQL warehouse size so the inflated join query executes faster.\n\nA larger warehouse might improve query execution speed, but it does not change the query’s logic.\n\nThe incorrect join would still create multiple rows per appointment, and Genie would return the same inflated result more quickly. Compute scaling addresses performance, not semantic correctness.","D. Remove the appointment_notes table from the Genie space's list of curated table descriptions only, while leaving it fully joinable and undocumented.\n\nRemoving only the description does not reliably prevent Genie from selecting or joining the table if it remains available as a source.\n\nIt can make the situation worse because Genie would have even less information about:\n\n- The table’s note-level grain.\n\n- Its one-to-many relationship with appointments.\n\n- When it should or should not be used.\n\nTo eliminate the risk, the table should be removed or hidden from the agent when it is unnecessary, or its relationship and usage rules should be explicitly curated. Merely deleting its description does neither."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/troubleshooting","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/constraints"],"source_hash":"a4ce71e42b0e4e5226744f0c32a27798a1b696800e7c6827aa713a5120939f65","stem_ja":"病院の予約管理チーム向けGenieスペースで、「先月、各医療従事者には何件の予約があったか」とアナリストが聞くと、予約数が過大になります。Unity Catalogのメタデータを調べると、appointmentsテーブルに主キーが宣言されておらず、1予約につき1件以上の自由記述メモを保存するappointment_notesテーブルとの結合関係も記録されていません。Genieはappointment_idでの結合を推測します。1予約に複数のメモがあるため、結合で行が増殖し、予約数が各予約のメモ件数に応じて水増しされます。\n\nこの正確さの不足を埋める効果が最も高い設定変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["Unity Catalogでappointmentsの主キーを宣言し、appointment_notesとの1対多結合による行増殖を避ける事前集計ビューを用意するか、件数を数える質問ではappointment_notesを結合すべきでないことを明記する。","テーブルや結合のメタデータを変えずに、「常に正確な予約数を返す」という一般的な文章の指示を追加する。","水増しされた結合クエリが速く実行されるよう、SQLウェアハウスを大きくする。","appointment_notesを結合可能かつ未記録のままにして、Genieスペースの整備済みテーブル説明の一覧からだけ削除する。"],"explanation_ja":"同じ予約が複数のメモと結合され、予約の粒度が崩れています。主キーを示し、件数集計で1対多結合を避けるビューや明確な関係の説明を用意することが直接的です。「正確に数えて」という抽象指示や計算資源の追加では直りません。覚え方は「件数の水増しは、結合と粒度を確認する」です。","distractors_ja":["主キーと結合時の粒度を明確にし、同じ予約が重複して数えられる原因を取り除きます。","正しい件数を求めるだけでは、どの結合が行増殖を起こすかという情報が伝わりません。","計算を速くしても、誤った結合による集計結果は正しくなりません。","結合を選べる状態と不明な関係を残すため、誤ったSQLを防ぐ対策になりません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-013","number":13,"form":2,"stem":"A data analyst is building a Genie space for a marketing analytics team. The Unity Catalog environment includes: a campaign_events_raw table ingested directly from an ad-tech vendor with vendor-specific internal codes and undocumented columns; a curated campaign_performance_gold view joining and cleaning the raw data into well-named, business-ready metrics (impressions, click-through rate, cost-per-acquisition); a parameterized SQL query calculating return-on-ad-spend (ROAS) by campaign and date range, used weekly by leadership; and a personal, uncertified scratch table one analyst created last month for a one-off experiment, containing a since-abandoned alternate ROAS calculation with a since-discovered bug.\n\nSelect the objects that should be curated into the Genie space for this domain.","options":["The campaign_events_raw table, so Genie always has access to the most granular underlying vendor data.","The campaign_performance_gold view, as the primary business-ready data source for the space.","The personal scratch table with the abandoned, buggy alternate ROAS calculation, so analysts have multiple ROAS methodologies to compare.","The parameterized ROAS query, registered as a trusted asset, so leadership's recurring weekly question returns a verified, consistent result."],"correct":1,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"Genieへ信頼できる業務資産を選ぶ","concept":"ce-f2-q013","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. The campaign_performance_gold view, as the primary business-ready data source for the space.\n\nD. The parameterized ROAS query, registered as a trusted asset, so leadership's recurring weekly question returns a verified, consistent result.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question asks which assets provide the most reliable, relevant, and maintainable context for a marketing-focused Genie space.\n\nThe strongest candidates are:\n\n- A curated view containing well-named, business-ready marketing metrics.\n\n- A verified parameterized query implementing the organization’s approved ROAS calculation.\n\nThe raw vendor table contains undocumented columns and internal codes, making it difficult for Genie to interpret accurately. The personal scratch table is even less suitable because it is uncertified, abandoned, and contains a known calculation defect.\n\nTherefore, B and D are the two objects that should be curated into the Genie space.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nB. The campaign_performance_gold view\n\nThe gold view is the best primary data source because it has already transformed the vendor-specific raw data into business concepts understood by the marketing team, including:\n\n- Impressions\n\n- Click-through rate\n\n- Cost per acquisition\n\n- Cleaned campaign attributes\n\n- Meaningful business-oriented column names\n\nGenie performs best when its domain is based on concise, well-documented, simplified datasets. A curated view reduces ambiguity, hides unnecessary implementation details, and minimizes the likelihood that Genie will select incorrect vendor codes or misunderstand raw columns.\n\nIt also supports a focused semantic environment. Rather than forcing Genie to rediscover joins, cleansing logic, and metric definitions, the view presents a reliable analytical structure designed for business consumption.\n\nD. The parameterized ROAS query\n\nThe ROAS query represents verified, recurring business logic already used by leadership. Registering it as a trusted asset allows Genie to reuse the approved calculation while accepting campaign and date-range parameters.\n\nThis is especially valuable because ROAS can be calculated differently depending on how revenue, attribution, refunds, and advertising spend are defined. Providing the validated SQL prevents Genie from inventing an alternative formula.\n\nThe two selected assets complement each other:\n\n- The gold view provides the trusted business-ready dataset.\n\n- The parameterized query provides verified logic for a recurring, important question.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. The campaign_events_raw table, so Genie always has access to the most granular underlying vendor data.\n\nGranularity alone does not make a dataset appropriate for Genie.\n\nThis table contains:\n\n- Vendor-specific internal codes.\n\n- Undocumented columns.\n\n- Raw ingestion structures.\n\n- Data that requires additional cleaning and interpretation.\n\nIncluding it could cause Genie to select unclear fields, misinterpret internal codes, or recreate transformation logic inconsistently.\n\nThe raw table may remain useful for engineering, auditing, or troubleshooting, but it should not be the primary curated source for marketing analysts when a reliable gold view already exists. Genie spaces should include only the data needed for their defined audience and purpose.\n\nC. The personal scratch table with the abandoned, buggy alternate ROAS calculation, so analysts have multiple ROAS methodologies to compare.\n\nThis table should be excluded because it is:\n\n- Uncertified.\n\n- Personally created for a one-time experiment.\n\n- Based on an abandoned methodology.\n\n- Known to contain a calculation bug.\n\n- In conflict with the approved ROAS logic.\n\nAdding it would introduce ambiguous and contradictory business semantics. Genie could select the defective calculation instead of the trusted one, producing inconsistent answers to the same leadership question.\n\nMultiple methodologies should only be exposed when each methodology is valid, clearly named, documented, and intentionally supported. That is not the case here.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is assuming that Genie should receive every available data asset, especially the most granular one.\n\nMore data does not automatically improve accuracy. Undocumented, irrelevant, conflicting, or defective assets can make query generation less reliable.\n\nThe correct approach is to curate the smallest trusted set of business-ready datasets and verified SQL logic required for the domain.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production marketing Genie space should be built around governed analytical assets rather than unrestricted access to every available table.\n\nA practical configuration would include:\n\n- The `campaign_performance_gold` view.\n\n- Clear descriptions for metrics and columns.\n\n- The approved ROAS parameterized query as a trusted asset.\n\n- Example questions using normal marketing terminology.\n\n- Benchmarks validating campaign, date-range, CTR, CPA, and ROAS questions.\n\nThe raw vendor table should remain outside the space unless a specific supported use case requires it. If it must be included, its codes and columns should first be thoroughly documented and unnecessary fields hidden.\n\nThe buggy scratch table should be deprecated, restricted, renamed to indicate that it must not be used, or deleted according to the organization’s governance and retention policies.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Curate an effective Genie Agent\n[https://d](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices?utm_source=chatgpt.com)[o](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices)[cs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices)\n\n- Tune Genie Agent quality\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality)\n\n- Create and manage a Genie Agent\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/set-up](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/set-up)\n\n- Genie Agents concepts\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/concepts](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/concepts)","source_option_explanations":["A. The campaign_events_raw table, so Genie always has access to the most granular underlying vendor data.\n\nGranularity alone does not make a dataset appropriate for Genie.\n\nThis table contains:\n\n- Vendor-specific internal codes.\n\n- Undocumented columns.\n\n- Raw ingestion structures.\n\n- Data that requires additional cleaning and interpretation.\n\nIncluding it could cause Genie to select unclear fields, misinterpret internal codes, or recreate transformation logic inconsistently.\n\nThe raw table may remain useful for engineering, auditing, or troubleshooting, but it should not be the primary curated source for marketing analysts when a reliable gold view already exists. Genie spaces should include only the data needed for their defined audience and purpose.","Correct Answer\n\nB. The campaign_performance_gold view, as the primary business-ready data source for the space.\n\nD. The parameterized ROAS query, registered as a trusted asset, so leadership's recurring weekly question returns a verified, consistent result.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question asks which assets provide the most reliable, relevant, and maintainable context for a marketing-focused Genie space.\n\nThe strongest candidates are:\n\n- A curated view containing well-named, business-ready marketing metrics.\n\n- A verified parameterized query implementing the organization’s approved ROAS calculation.\n\nThe raw vendor table contains undocumented columns and internal codes, making it difficult for Genie to interpret accurately. The personal scratch table is even less suitable because it is uncertified, abandoned, and contains a known calculation defect.\n\nTherefore, B and D are the two objects that should be curated into the Genie space.","C. The personal scratch table with the abandoned, buggy alternate ROAS calculation, so analysts have multiple ROAS methodologies to compare.\n\nThis table should be excluded because it is:\n\n- Uncertified.\n\n- Personally created for a one-time experiment.\n\n- Based on an abandoned methodology.\n\n- Known to contain a calculation bug.\n\n- In conflict with the approved ROAS logic.\n\nAdding it would introduce ambiguous and contradictory business semantics. Genie could select the defective calculation instead of the trusted one, producing inconsistent answers to the same leadership question.\n\nMultiple methodologies should only be exposed when each methodology is valid, clearly named, documented, and intentionally supported. That is not the case here.","Correct Answer\n\nB. The campaign_performance_gold view, as the primary business-ready data source for the space.\n\nD. The parameterized ROAS query, registered as a trusted asset, so leadership's recurring weekly question returns a verified, consistent result.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question asks which assets provide the most reliable, relevant, and maintainable context for a marketing-focused Genie space.\n\nThe strongest candidates are:\n\n- A curated view containing well-named, business-ready marketing metrics.\n\n- A verified parameterized query implementing the organization’s approved ROAS calculation.\n\nThe raw vendor table contains undocumented columns and internal codes, making it difficult for Genie to interpret accurately. The personal scratch table is even less suitable because it is uncertified, abandoned, and contains a known calculation defect.\n\nTherefore, B and D are the two objects that should be curated into the Genie space."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices?utm_source=chatgpt.com","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/set-up","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/concepts"],"source_hash":"f5f25c1fcdc18e6a32d39b0bd65fccaa5faaa4ab4df3daec44d0baffef8de489","correct_many":[1,3],"stem_ja":"マーケティング分析チーム向けのGenieスペースを、データアナリストが構築しています。Unity Catalogには、広告技術ベンダーから直接取り込み、ベンダー固有の内部コードや未説明の列を持つcampaign_events_rawテーブルがあります。また、生データを結合・整備し、インプレッション、クリック率、顧客獲得単価といった分かりやすい業務指標にするcampaign_performance_goldビュー、経営層が毎週使う、キャンペーンと期間を指定して広告費用対効果（ROAS）を計算するパラメーター付きSQLクエリもあります。さらに、あるアナリストが先月の単発実験で作成した、未認定の個人用作業テーブルがあり、そこには後にバグが見つかって使用中止となった別のROAS計算が含まれています。\n\nこの業務領域のGenieスペースへ整備して組み込むべきオブジェクトを選んでください。","options_ja":["Genieが常に最も細粒度のベンダー生データを使えるよう、campaign_events_rawテーブル。","スペースの主な業務向けデータソースとして、campaign_performance_goldビュー。","アナリストが複数のROAS計算手法を比較できるよう、バグのある使用中止済み計算を持つ個人用作業テーブル。","経営層の毎週の定型質問へ検証済みで一貫した結果を返せるよう、信頼済み資産として登録したパラメーター付きROASクエリ。"],"explanation_ja":"Genieへ渡すものは「利用可能な全データ」ではなく、「この業務で正しいと確認された資産」です。整備済みGoldビューを基準にし、反復するROAS質問には検証済みのパラメーター付きクエリを使います。覚え方は「業務向けの正しい入口を絞る」。粒度の細かさや選択肢の多さだけでは品質は上がりません。","distractors_ja":["未説明の列やベンダー固有コードを含み、主な業務質問に対する信頼できる入口としては不十分です。","整備された業務指標を持つため、Genieが正しい意味で分析する土台に適しています。","バグが判明し使用中止となった非認定の計算は、誤答や定義の衝突を持ち込みます。","経営層が繰り返し使う検証済み計算を再利用でき、結果の一貫性を保てます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-014","number":14,"form":2,"stem":"An AI engineer investigates a Databricks agent that answers questions about a corpus of engineering datasheets, many containing dense technical tables (e.g., voltage tolerance ranges by part number). Reviewing retrieved chunks flagged as low-quality in MLflow eval logs, the engineer finds the ingestion pipeline used a generic plain-text PDF extraction step that flattened multi-column tables into a single run of disordered text, scrambling which value belonged to which part number and column header. The Databricks AI Search index is confirmed fully synced and up to date with the source Delta table, and hybrid search is already enabled.\n\nWhich change most directly improves the signal quality of retrieved context for this failure?","options":["Switch pipeline_type from TRIGGERED to CONTINUOUS so the index syncs with lower latency.","Increase num_results so more candidate chunks are returned per query, increasing the odds a correct chunk appears somewhere in the results.","Replace the generic plain-text PDF extraction step with a table-aware document parsing approach (e.g., Databricks' ai_parse_document) that preserves table structure before chunking and embedding, so ingested text correctly associates each value with its part number and column.","Enable hybrid (keyword + vector) search on the index, since exact-match part numbers require keyword matching."],"correct":2,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"表を壊さない文書解析","concept":"ce-f2-q014","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Replace the generic plain-text PDF extraction step with a table-aware document parsing approach (e.g., Databricks' `ai_parse_document`) that preserves table structure before chunking and embedding, so ingested text correctly associates each value with its part number and column.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure originates before retrieval:\n\n- The PDF extractor flattened multi-column tables into disordered text.\n\n- Incorrect part-number, header, and value relationships were stored in the Delta table.\n\n- The corrupted text was then chunked and embedded.\n\n- AI Search correctly retrieved from that low-quality representation.\n\nThe index is already synchronized, and hybrid search is already enabled. Therefore, neither synchronization frequency nor retrieval mode addresses the root cause.\n\nThe highest-impact intervention is to improve the document parsing and ingestion stage, then regenerate the chunks, embeddings, and AI Search index content.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA retrieval system cannot reliably recover relationships that were destroyed during document extraction. If a technical table is flattened incorrectly, the resulting chunk may contain the right words and numbers but associate them with the wrong rows or headers.\n\nA table-aware parser can preserve document structure such as:\n\n- Table rows and columns\n\n- Column headings\n\n- Part-number-to-value relationships\n\n- Page and layout metadata\n\n- Section context\n\nDatabricks’ `ai_parse_document` extracts document text, tables, figures, and layout information into a structured representation. In the current function version, detected tables can be represented structurally rather than flattened into an arbitrary text stream.\n\nAfter parsing, the pipeline should create semantically coherent, embedding-ready chunks. Databricks’ `ai_prep_search` is specifically designed to transform structured `ai_parse_document` output into chunks enriched with information such as titles, section headers, and page references for retrieval workloads.\n\nOption C therefore improves the signal contained in the indexed data, rather than attempting to compensate for corrupted source content during search.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Switch `pipeline_type` from `TRIGGERED` to `CONTINUOUS` so the index syncs with lower latency.\n\nThis addresses data freshness, not data quality.\n\nThe question explicitly confirms that the AI Search index is fully synchronized with the source Delta table. A continuously synchronized index would ingest the same incorrectly parsed chunks more quickly, but the table relationships inside those chunks would remain scrambled.\n\nB. Increase `num_results` so more candidate chunks are returned per query, increasing the odds a correct chunk appears somewhere in the results.\n\nReturning additional results cannot reliably repair information that was incorrectly extracted.\n\nMore results may instead:\n\n- Return more corrupted or ambiguous chunks.\n\n- Increase context-token consumption.\n\n- Add irrelevant information.\n\n- Make it harder for the model to distinguish correct values.\n\n- Increase latency and inference cost.\n\nIncreasing retrieval depth can help when relevant chunks exist but rank too low. Here, the relevant table was structurally corrupted before indexing, so retrieval quantity is not the primary problem.\n\nD. Enable hybrid (keyword + vector) search on the index, since exact-match part numbers require keyword matching.\n\nHybrid search can improve retrieval for exact identifiers such as part numbers by combining lexical and semantic matching. However, the scenario explicitly states that hybrid search is already enabled.\n\nMore importantly, hybrid search can locate a chunk containing the requested part number, but it cannot reconstruct the correct association between that part number, the table heading, and the corresponding voltage value when extraction has already scrambled them.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming every retrieval failure should be fixed by tuning the search index.\n\nRAG quality depends on the entire pipeline:\n\nparsing → normalization → chunking → embedding → indexing → retrieval → generation\n\nHere, the first stage corrupted the source signal. Search tuning cannot reliably compensate for lost table structure.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nFor document-heavy RAG applications, inspect the retrieved content itself before tuning search parameters. If the retrieved chunk contains incorrect or disordered information, correct the ingestion pipeline first.\n\nA practical remediation would be to:\n\n- Reparse the original datasheets using a table- and layout-aware parser.\n\n- Inspect the structured output for representative dense tables.\n\n- Convert each table into self-contained chunks that repeat necessary headers.\n\n- Include metadata such as document name, page number, table title, and part number.\n\n- Regenerate the chunk Delta table and embeddings.\n\n- Resynchronize or rebuild the AI Search index.\n\n- Rerun the MLflow evaluation set containing part-number and tolerance questions.\n\nFor example, an embedding-ready chunk should resemble:\n\n```\nDatasheet: Component Series XTable: Operating voltage tolerancesPart number: X-104Minimum voltage: 4.75 VNominal voltage: 5.00 VMaximum voltage: 5.25 VPage: 8\n```\n\nThis representation preserves the associations needed for accurate retrieval and answer generation.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- `ai_parse_document` function\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_parse_document](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_parse_document)\n\n- Intelligent document processing\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/intelligent-document-processing](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/intelligent-document-processing)\n\n- `ai_prep_search` function\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_prep_search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_prep_search)\n\n- AI Search retrieval quality guide\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality)","source_option_explanations":["A. Switch `pipeline_type` from `TRIGGERED` to `CONTINUOUS` so the index syncs with lower latency.\n\nThis addresses data freshness, not data quality.\n\nThe question explicitly confirms that the AI Search index is fully synchronized with the source Delta table. A continuously synchronized index would ingest the same incorrectly parsed chunks more quickly, but the table relationships inside those chunks would remain scrambled.","B. Increase `num_results` so more candidate chunks are returned per query, increasing the odds a correct chunk appears somewhere in the results.\n\nReturning additional results cannot reliably repair information that was incorrectly extracted.\n\nMore results may instead:\n\n- Return more corrupted or ambiguous chunks.\n\n- Increase context-token consumption.\n\n- Add irrelevant information.\n\n- Make it harder for the model to distinguish correct values.\n\n- Increase latency and inference cost.\n\nIncreasing retrieval depth can help when relevant chunks exist but rank too low. Here, the relevant table was structurally corrupted before indexing, so retrieval quantity is not the primary problem.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Replace the generic plain-text PDF extraction step with a table-aware document parsing approach (e.g., Databricks' `ai_parse_document`) that preserves table structure before chunking and embedding, so ingested text correctly associates each value with its part number and column.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure originates before retrieval:\n\n- The PDF extractor flattened multi-column tables into disordered text.\n\n- Incorrect part-number, header, and value relationships were stored in the Delta table.\n\n- The corrupted text was then chunked and embedded.\n\n- AI Search correctly retrieved from that low-quality representation.\n\nThe index is already synchronized, and hybrid search is already enabled. Therefore, neither synchronization frequency nor retrieval mode addresses the root cause.\n\nThe highest-impact intervention is to improve the document parsing and ingestion stage, then regenerate the chunks, embeddings, and AI Search index content.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA retrieval system cannot reliably recover relationships that were destroyed during document extraction. If a technical table is flattened incorrectly, the resulting chunk may contain the right words and numbers but associate them with the wrong rows or headers.\n\nA table-aware parser can preserve document structure such as:\n\n- Table rows and columns\n\n- Column headings\n\n- Part-number-to-value relationships\n\n- Page and layout metadata\n\n- Section context\n\nDatabricks’ `ai_parse_document` extracts document text, tables, figures, and layout information into a structured representation. In the current function version, detected tables can be represented structurally rather than flattened into an arbitrary text stream.\n\nAfter parsing, the pipeline should create semantically coherent, embedding-ready chunks. Databricks’ `ai_prep_search` is specifically designed to transform structured `ai_parse_document` output into chunks enriched with information such as titles, section headers, and page references for retrieval workloads.\n\nOption C therefore improves the signal contained in the indexed data, rather than attempting to compensate for corrupted source content during search.","D. Enable hybrid (keyword + vector) search on the index, since exact-match part numbers require keyword matching.\n\nHybrid search can improve retrieval for exact identifiers such as part numbers by combining lexical and semantic matching. However, the scenario explicitly states that hybrid search is already enabled.\n\nMore importantly, hybrid search can locate a chunk containing the requested part number, but it cannot reconstruct the correct association between that part number, the table heading, and the corresponding voltage value when extraction has already scrambled them."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_parse_document","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/intelligent-document-processing","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_prep_search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality"],"source_hash":"cae9ef805cb98c842e17fd716e63174f7226dc231451b0d1938516f2b8bb698f","stem_ja":"AIエンジニアが、部品番号ごとの許容電圧範囲など、密な技術表を多く含む仕様書について答えるDatabricksエージェントを調査しています。MLflow評価ログで低品質とされた検索チャンクを確認すると、取り込みパイプラインの汎用的なPDFテキスト抽出が、複数列の表を順序の乱れた1続きの文章へ平坦化し、各値がどの部品番号や列見出しに属するかを壊していました。Databricks AI Searchのインデックスは、ソースDeltaテーブルと完全に同期され最新であることが確認済みで、ハイブリッド検索も有効です。\n\nこの障害で、検索コンテキストの信号品質を最も直接改善する変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["インデックス同期の遅延を短くするため、pipeline_typeをTRIGGEREDからCONTINUOUSへ切り替える。","検索結果のどこかに正しいチャンクが現れる可能性を高めるため、num_resultsを増やす。","チャンク分割と埋め込みの前に表の構造を保ち、各値が正しい部品番号と列に対応するよう、汎用PDFテキスト抽出を、Databricksのai_parse_documentなどの表を認識する文書解析手法に置き換える。","部品番号の完全一致にはキーワード検索が必要なので、インデックスでハイブリッド検索（キーワード＋ベクトル）を有効にする。"],"explanation_ja":"検索の前に、保存した情報自体が壊れています。最新のインデックスや検索件数の増加では、崩れた値と見出しの対応は戻りません。表の行・列関係を維持できる解析に変え、その後に分割・埋め込み・再索引を行います。覚え方は「検索で直せない取り込み品質は、解析段階へ戻る」です。","distractors_ja":["インデックスはすでに最新で、問題は同期の速さではなく抽出後の内容です。","壊れた表から作ったチャンクを増やしても、元の対応関係が正しくなるとは限りません。","表の構造が失われる発生箇所を修正し、埋め込みに渡す内容自体の正確さを改善します。","ハイブリッド検索はすでに有効で、表の抽出崩れを修復する機能でもありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-015","number":15,"form":2,"stem":"A team is designing a RAG pipeline over a Unity Catalog Volume of internal engineering wikis. One proposed design: a script reads files directly from the Volume at query time, chunks and embeds them on the fly with no intermediate storage, and calls an AI Search DIRECT_ACCESS index's insert API immediately before returning results — repeating this from scratch on every single query. An alternative design: parse and chunk the documents once, write the resulting chunks (with source-file lineage) to a Unity Catalog-managed Delta table, and build a DELTA_SYNC AI Search index from that table, refreshed on a schedule as source documents change.\n\nWhich design is more appropriate for a production RAG pipeline over this UC-governed corpus, and why?","options":["The on-the-fly, query-time chunk-and-embed design is preferable, since it guarantees the freshest possible data on every single query with no sync delay.","Both designs are functionally equivalent from a governance standpoint, since both ultimately read from the same source Volume.","The on-the-fly design is preferable specifically because DIRECT_ACCESS indexes are always more cost-effective than DELTA_SYNC indexes, regardless of query volume.","The Delta-table-backed design is preferable, since it produces a governed, queryable, lineage-tracked intermediate asset that can be reused, audited, and incrementally synced, rather than repeating expensive parsing and embedding work from scratch on every query."],"correct":3,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"Deltaを中間資産とするRAG取り込み","concept":"ce-f2-q015","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. The Delta-table-backed design is preferable, since it produces a governed, queryable, lineage-tracked intermediate asset that can be reused, audited, and incrementally synced, rather than repeating expensive parsing and embedding work from scratch on every query.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nA production RAG pipeline should separate two workloads:\n\n- Offline or incremental ingestion — reading, parsing, chunking, enriching, and embedding documents.\n\n- Online retrieval — querying a prepared search index with low latency.\n\nThe first proposal incorrectly performs the entire ingestion workload during every user query. Even when the source files have not changed, it repeatedly parses and embeds the same documents and manually updates a `DIRECT_ACCESS` index.\n\nThe alternative processes documents once, persists reusable chunks and lineage metadata in a governed Delta table, and uses a `DELTA_SYNC` index to synchronize changes. This is more scalable, auditable, maintainable, and appropriate for production.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe Delta-table-backed design establishes a durable knowledge-processing layer between the original files and the search index.\n\nThe chunk table can retain fields such as:\n\n- Unique chunk identifier\n\n- Chunk text\n\n- Source file path\n\n- Document version or modification time\n\n- Section and page information\n\n- Parsing timestamp\n\n- Access-control or classification metadata\n\n- Precomputed embedding, when using self-managed embeddings\n\nBecause the chunks are stored in a Unity Catalog-managed Delta table, they can be queried, inspected, quality-tested, permissioned, and reused by other pipelines. Processing defects can be diagnosed by comparing a retrieved chunk with its source-file metadata.\n\nA `DELTA_SYNC` index automatically and incrementally updates from its source Delta table as table data changes. Depending on freshness requirements, the team can use triggered or continuous synchronization rather than manually inserting every chunk during every query.\n\nThis creates the appropriate production pattern:\n\nUC Volume → parsing and chunking pipeline → governed Delta chunk table → DELTA_SYNC AI Search index → online retrieval\n\nParsing and embedding occur only when documents are initially ingested or changed. User queries then execute against the prepared index instead of rebuilding the retrieval corpus.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. The on-the-fly, query-time chunk-and-embed design is preferable, since it guarantees the freshest possible data on every single query with no sync delay.\n\nThis could provide very recent data, but at an unacceptable operational cost for most production RAG systems.\n\nEvery query would unnecessarily repeat:\n\n- File reads\n\n- Document parsing\n\n- Chunk generation\n\n- Embedding computation\n\n- Index upserts\n\nThis would significantly increase latency, compute usage, embedding cost, API traffic, and failure exposure. Concurrent requests could also repeat the same processing work or attempt overlapping index updates.\n\nWhen near-real-time freshness is required, a continuous or frequently triggered ingestion and Delta Sync process is more appropriate than reconstructing the entire corpus for every request.\n\nB. Both designs are functionally equivalent from a governance standpoint, since both ultimately read from the same source Volume.\n\nReading from the same governed Volume does not make the pipelines equivalent.\n\nThe on-the-fly design has no durable intermediate representation of:\n\n- Which parser and chunking strategy were used\n\n- What exact chunks were indexed\n\n- Which source file produced each chunk\n\n- When each document was processed\n\n- Which document version is represented\n\n- Whether a parsing or embedding change affected retrieval\n\nA managed Delta chunk table makes the derived retrieval corpus visible, queryable, governable, and auditable instead of leaving it as transient query-time processing.\n\nC. The on-the-fly design is preferable specifically because DIRECT_ACCESS indexes are always more cost-effective than DELTA_SYNC indexes, regardless of query volume.\n\nDatabricks does not establish a universal rule that `DIRECT_ACCESS` indexes are always more cost-effective.\n\nThe two index types represent different data-management models:\n\n- `DELTA_SYNC` automatically and incrementally synchronizes from a source Delta table.\n\n- `DIRECT_ACCESS` requires the application to manage vector and metadata inserts, updates, and deletions through APIs.\n\nIn this scenario, manual query-time ingestion would repeatedly incur parsing and embedding costs. Even if a particular index configuration had a favorable infrastructure price, that would not make repeated end-to-end corpus reconstruction cost-effective.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe primary trap is overvaluing absolute freshness while ignoring the distinction between ingestion-time processing and query-time retrieval.\n\nAnother trap is assuming that governance of the original files automatically governs and documents every transient transformation applied to those files. A production RAG system also needs a durable, inspectable representation of the chunks actually supplied to the search layer.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nFor a production implementation, the ingestion pipeline should process only new or changed source files.\n\nA practical design would:\n\n- Record each file’s path, version, modification time, or content hash.\n\n- Reparse only documents that have changed.\n\n- Write stable chunk IDs and source lineage into a Delta table.\n\n- Delete or invalidate chunks belonging to removed or replaced documents.\n\n- Use a `DELTA_SYNC` index with triggered or continuous synchronization based on freshness requirements.\n\n- Evaluate parsing, chunking, and retrieval quality independently.\n\n- Keep the online query path limited to embedding the query, searching the index, and generating the answer.\n\nA `DIRECT_ACCESS` index remains valid for applications where vectors originate outside Delta or where the application deliberately owns all index-update logic. It is not the appropriate justification for rebuilding a stable, UC-governed document corpus on every request.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Build an unstructured data pipeline for RAG\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag)\n\n- Create AI Search endpoints and indexes\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search)\n\n- Databricks AI Search\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search)\n\n- Indexes API — AI Search index types\n[https://docs.databricks.com/api/workspace/vectorsearchindexes](https://docs.databricks.com/api/workspace/vectorsearchindexes)","source_option_explanations":["A. The on-the-fly, query-time chunk-and-embed design is preferable, since it guarantees the freshest possible data on every single query with no sync delay.\n\nThis could provide very recent data, but at an unacceptable operational cost for most production RAG systems.\n\nEvery query would unnecessarily repeat:\n\n- File reads\n\n- Document parsing\n\n- Chunk generation\n\n- Embedding computation\n\n- Index upserts\n\nThis would significantly increase latency, compute usage, embedding cost, API traffic, and failure exposure. Concurrent requests could also repeat the same processing work or attempt overlapping index updates.\n\nWhen near-real-time freshness is required, a continuous or frequently triggered ingestion and Delta Sync process is more appropriate than reconstructing the entire corpus for every request.","B. Both designs are functionally equivalent from a governance standpoint, since both ultimately read from the same source Volume.\n\nReading from the same governed Volume does not make the pipelines equivalent.\n\nThe on-the-fly design has no durable intermediate representation of:\n\n- Which parser and chunking strategy were used\n\n- What exact chunks were indexed\n\n- Which source file produced each chunk\n\n- When each document was processed\n\n- Which document version is represented\n\n- Whether a parsing or embedding change affected retrieval\n\nA managed Delta chunk table makes the derived retrieval corpus visible, queryable, governable, and auditable instead of leaving it as transient query-time processing.","C. The on-the-fly design is preferable specifically because DIRECT_ACCESS indexes are always more cost-effective than DELTA_SYNC indexes, regardless of query volume.\n\nDatabricks does not establish a universal rule that `DIRECT_ACCESS` indexes are always more cost-effective.\n\nThe two index types represent different data-management models:\n\n- `DELTA_SYNC` automatically and incrementally synchronizes from a source Delta table.\n\n- `DIRECT_ACCESS` requires the application to manage vector and metadata inserts, updates, and deletions through APIs.\n\nIn this scenario, manual query-time ingestion would repeatedly incur parsing and embedding costs. Even if a particular index configuration had a favorable infrastructure price, that would not make repeated end-to-end corpus reconstruction cost-effective.","Correct Answer\n\nD. The Delta-table-backed design is preferable, since it produces a governed, queryable, lineage-tracked intermediate asset that can be reused, audited, and incrementally synced, rather than repeating expensive parsing and embedding work from scratch on every query.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nA production RAG pipeline should separate two workloads:\n\n- Offline or incremental ingestion — reading, parsing, chunking, enriching, and embedding documents.\n\n- Online retrieval — querying a prepared search index with low latency.\n\nThe first proposal incorrectly performs the entire ingestion workload during every user query. Even when the source files have not changed, it repeatedly parses and embeds the same documents and manually updates a `DIRECT_ACCESS` index.\n\nThe alternative processes documents once, persists reusable chunks and lineage metadata in a governed Delta table, and uses a `DELTA_SYNC` index to synchronize changes. This is more scalable, auditable, maintainable, and appropriate for production.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe Delta-table-backed design establishes a durable knowledge-processing layer between the original files and the search index.\n\nThe chunk table can retain fields such as:\n\n- Unique chunk identifier\n\n- Chunk text\n\n- Source file path\n\n- Document version or modification time\n\n- Section and page information\n\n- Parsing timestamp\n\n- Access-control or classification metadata\n\n- Precomputed embedding, when using self-managed embeddings\n\nBecause the chunks are stored in a Unity Catalog-managed Delta table, they can be queried, inspected, quality-tested, permissioned, and reused by other pipelines. Processing defects can be diagnosed by comparing a retrieved chunk with its source-file metadata.\n\nA `DELTA_SYNC` index automatically and incrementally updates from its source Delta table as table data changes. Depending on freshness requirements, the team can use triggered or continuous synchronization rather than manually inserting every chunk during every query.\n\nThis creates the appropriate production pattern:\n\nUC Volume → parsing and chunking pipeline → governed Delta chunk table → DELTA_SYNC AI Search index → online retrieval\n\nParsing and embedding occur only when documents are initially ingested or changed. User queries then execute against the prepared index instead of rebuilding the retrieval corpus."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/api/workspace/vectorsearchindexes"],"source_hash":"cc1f11099bd167c3087ccc132822e4bc9f3faf4430a39a1106ec87679f0a9328","stem_ja":"Unity Catalog Volume内の社内技術Wikiを対象に、チームがRAGパイプラインを設計しています。ある案では、クエリ時にスクリプトがVolumeから直接ファイルを読み、途中保存なしでその場で分割・埋め込みを行い、結果を返す直前にAI SearchのDIRECT_ACCESSインデックスの挿入APIを呼びます。この処理を各クエリで毎回ゼロから繰り返します。別案では、文書を一度解析・分割し、ソースファイルのリネージを付けたチャンクをUnity Catalog管理のDeltaテーブルに書き込み、そのテーブルからDELTA_SYNC AI Searchインデックスを作ります。元文書の変更に応じ、スケジュールで更新します。\n\nこのUCで管理されたコーパスの本番RAGパイプラインには、どちらの設計がより適切ですか。その理由も選んでください。","options_ja":["同期遅延なく、各クエリで常に最も新しいデータを保証するため、クエリ時にその場で分割・埋め込みする設計がよい。","最終的には同じソースVolumeから読むので、ガバナンスの観点では両設計の機能は同等である。","クエリ量に関係なくDIRECT_ACCESSインデックスは常にDELTA_SYNCより費用対効果が高いので、その場で処理する設計がよい。","Deltaテーブルを使う設計がよい。各クエリで高価な解析と埋め込みをゼロからやり直す代わりに、管理・照会・リネージ追跡が可能な中間資産を作り、再利用、監査、増分同期に使えるためである。"],"explanation_ja":"同じ文書を問い合わせのたびに解析・埋め込みすると、処理費用と応答遅延が増え、監査や再現も難しくなります。チャンクをUC管理のDeltaに保存すれば、変換結果を再利用・照会し、ソースとの関係を追跡してインデックスへ同期できます。覚え方は「重い取り込みは事前に、検索は問い合わせ時に」です。","distractors_ja":["鮮度だけを優先し、毎回の高価な取り込みや、再利用・監査可能な中間資産という要件を軽視しています。","同じソースを読むことだけでは、変換後の資産の管理、監査、リネージの扱いは同等になりません。","常に安いという一般化は誤りで、費用は更新頻度・クエリ量・処理方式で変わります。","UC管理の再利用可能な中間資産を作り、取り込みと検索を分ける設計が本番要件に合います。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-016","number":16,"form":2,"stem":"A context engineer is building a RAG agent over a corpus of internal API reference documentation, auto-generated from source code docstrings. Each function or class has its own docstring block containing a description, parameter list, return type, and a runnable code example. Docstring blocks range from a few lines to several hundred lines for complex classes. Developers' queries are almost always about a specific function or class by name (e.g., \"what does the retry_with_backoff function do and what are its parameters\").\n\nWhich chunking strategy is most appropriate given this document structure and query pattern?","options":["Chunk by function/class boundary, keeping each docstring block — description, parameters, return type, and code example — together as a single chunk, splitting only the rare oversized class docstring that exceeds the embedding model's context length.","Apply fixed-size chunking at 256 tokens with no regard for function or class boundaries, since a small, consistent chunk size is always the safest default regardless of source structure.","Chunk at the paragraph level within each docstring, separating the description, parameter list, and code example into different chunks each time.","Embed each docstring's code example separately from its description and parameter list, since code and prose require fundamentally different chunking granularity."],"correct":0,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"API文書の関数・クラス単位の分割","concept":"ce-f2-q016","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Chunk by function/class boundary, keeping each docstring block — description, parameters, return type, and code example — together as a single chunk, splitting only the rare oversized class docstring that exceeds the embedding model's context length.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe document structure already provides a strong natural boundary: one docstring represents one function or class. The developers’ queries follow the same boundary because they usually ask about a named function or class and expect its description, parameters, return type, and example together.\n\nTherefore, each function or class docstring is a semantically coherent, independently useful retrieval unit.\n\nOption A aligns:\n\n- The chunk boundary with the source-code entity.\n\n- The indexed content with the developers’ query pattern.\n\n- The description with the parameters and example needed to answer the question.\n\n- Retrieval metadata with an exact function or class name.\n\nThis is more appropriate than applying arbitrary token boundaries or consistently splitting related parts of the same API entry.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA good RAG chunk should represent a coherent unit that can answer a likely user question without requiring the retriever to locate and combine several disconnected fragments.\n\nFor a query such as:\n\nWhat does `retry_with_backoff` do, and what are its parameters?\n\nThe ideal retrieved chunk contains:\n\n- The `retry_with_backoff` name.\n\n- Its purpose and behavior.\n\n- Its parameter definitions.\n\n- Its return type.\n\n- Its runnable example.\n\nKeeping these elements together gives the generation model a complete, grounded API entry. It also avoids situations where the description is retrieved but the parameter list or example is missed.\n\nFunction and class boundaries are better than arbitrary token boundaries because they reflect the semantic structure of the source. Databricks recommends considering document-specific splitting strategies and creating semantically coherent chunks that preserve related information and can stand independently.\n\nFor unusually large class docstrings, the engineer should split at logical internal boundaries such as:\n\n- Class overview\n\n- Constructor parameters\n\n- Public methods\n\n- Usage examples\n\nEach resulting child chunk should retain the class name and relevant headings as metadata or repeated context. Although option A uses the embedding model’s context limit as the explicit splitting threshold, production evaluation might show that an extremely broad class document should be divided earlier to prevent embedding dilution.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Apply fixed-size chunking at 256 tokens with no regard for function or class boundaries, since a small, consistent chunk size is always the safest default regardless of source structure.\n\nFixed-size chunking could split an API entry in the middle of:\n\n- A parameter list.\n\n- A return-type description.\n\n- A code example.\n\n- An exception or usage note.\n\nThe result might require several chunks to answer one simple function-level question. It could also separate the function name from details needed to retrieve the chunk accurately.\n\nA 256-token size can be a useful experimental starting point for some precise fact-retrieval workloads, but it is not universally the safest strategy. Databricks explicitly notes that arbitrary fixed-size chunking often fails to preserve semantic coherence and rarely works well as the sole strategy for production applications.\n\nC. Chunk at the paragraph level within each docstring, separating the description, parameter list, and code example into different chunks each time.\n\nParagraph boundaries can be useful when paragraphs represent independent topics, but consistently separating the components of an API entry is counterproductive for this query pattern.\n\nA developer usually needs several related elements in one answer. Separating them increases the possibility that retrieval returns only:\n\n- The general description but not the parameters.\n\n- The parameters but not their behavioral context.\n\n- The example without the function contract.\n\nThis strategy sacrifices useful local context even though each complete docstring already forms a natural semantic unit.\n\nD. Embed each docstring's code example separately from its description and parameter list, since code and prose require fundamentally different chunking granularity.\n\nCode and prose do not always require separate chunks. In API documentation, the runnable example often explains how the documented function should be called and how its parameters interact.\n\nSeparating the example could weaken both chunks:\n\n- The prose chunk loses practical usage context.\n\n- The code chunk may lack enough descriptive context for accurate retrieval.\n\nSeparate code chunks might be justified when examples are extremely large or when the application supports a distinct code-search use case. The scenario, however, focuses on retrieving complete information about a named function or class, so keeping its example with the corresponding documentation is more useful.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is treating a fixed token size as a universally correct chunking strategy.\n\nChunking should reflect:\n\n- The structure of the source documents.\n\n- The semantic unit users ask about.\n\n- The amount of context required to answer those questions.\n\n- The embedding model’s limits.\n\nHere, the function or class—not an arbitrary token count or individual paragraph—is the natural retrieval unit.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA strong implementation would store one row per normal-sized API entity with metadata such as:\n\n```\nentity_name: retry_with_backoffentity_type: functionmodule: networking.retrychunk_text: description + parameters + return type + examplesource_path: src/networking/retry.py\n```\n\nExact entity names should be available as searchable text or metadata because developers frequently query by identifiers containing underscores or other code-specific syntax.\n\nFor oversized class documentation, use parent-child chunking:\n\n- Index smaller logical child chunks for precise matching.\n\n- Retain the complete class entry or a larger parent section for answer context.\n\n- Repeat the class name, module name, and section heading in every child chunk.\n\nThe exam answer is A because it best aligns chunking granularity with both the corpus structure and the dominant query pattern.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Build an unstructured data pipeline for RAG](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag)\n\n- [AI Search retrieval quality guide](https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality)\n\n- [ai_prep_search function](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_prep_search)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Chunk by function/class boundary, keeping each docstring block — description, parameters, return type, and code example — together as a single chunk, splitting only the rare oversized class docstring that exceeds the embedding model's context length.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe document structure already provides a strong natural boundary: one docstring represents one function or class. The developers’ queries follow the same boundary because they usually ask about a named function or class and expect its description, parameters, return type, and example together.\n\nTherefore, each function or class docstring is a semantically coherent, independently useful retrieval unit.\n\nOption A aligns:\n\n- The chunk boundary with the source-code entity.\n\n- The indexed content with the developers’ query pattern.\n\n- The description with the parameters and example needed to answer the question.\n\n- Retrieval metadata with an exact function or class name.\n\nThis is more appropriate than applying arbitrary token boundaries or consistently splitting related parts of the same API entry.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA good RAG chunk should represent a coherent unit that can answer a likely user question without requiring the retriever to locate and combine several disconnected fragments.\n\nFor a query such as:\n\nWhat does `retry_with_backoff` do, and what are its parameters?\n\nThe ideal retrieved chunk contains:\n\n- The `retry_with_backoff` name.\n\n- Its purpose and behavior.\n\n- Its parameter definitions.\n\n- Its return type.\n\n- Its runnable example.\n\nKeeping these elements together gives the generation model a complete, grounded API entry. It also avoids situations where the description is retrieved but the parameter list or example is missed.\n\nFunction and class boundaries are better than arbitrary token boundaries because they reflect the semantic structure of the source. Databricks recommends considering document-specific splitting strategies and creating semantically coherent chunks that preserve related information and can stand independently.\n\nFor unusually large class docstrings, the engineer should split at logical internal boundaries such as:\n\n- Class overview\n\n- Constructor parameters\n\n- Public methods\n\n- Usage examples\n\nEach resulting child chunk should retain the class name and relevant headings as metadata or repeated context. Although option A uses the embedding model’s context limit as the explicit splitting threshold, production evaluation might show that an extremely broad class document should be divided earlier to prevent embedding dilution.","B. Apply fixed-size chunking at 256 tokens with no regard for function or class boundaries, since a small, consistent chunk size is always the safest default regardless of source structure.\n\nFixed-size chunking could split an API entry in the middle of:\n\n- A parameter list.\n\n- A return-type description.\n\n- A code example.\n\n- An exception or usage note.\n\nThe result might require several chunks to answer one simple function-level question. It could also separate the function name from details needed to retrieve the chunk accurately.\n\nA 256-token size can be a useful experimental starting point for some precise fact-retrieval workloads, but it is not universally the safest strategy. Databricks explicitly notes that arbitrary fixed-size chunking often fails to preserve semantic coherence and rarely works well as the sole strategy for production applications.","C. Chunk at the paragraph level within each docstring, separating the description, parameter list, and code example into different chunks each time.\n\nParagraph boundaries can be useful when paragraphs represent independent topics, but consistently separating the components of an API entry is counterproductive for this query pattern.\n\nA developer usually needs several related elements in one answer. Separating them increases the possibility that retrieval returns only:\n\n- The general description but not the parameters.\n\n- The parameters but not their behavioral context.\n\n- The example without the function contract.\n\nThis strategy sacrifices useful local context even though each complete docstring already forms a natural semantic unit.","D. Embed each docstring's code example separately from its description and parameter list, since code and prose require fundamentally different chunking granularity.\n\nCode and prose do not always require separate chunks. In API documentation, the runnable example often explains how the documented function should be called and how its parameters interact.\n\nSeparating the example could weaken both chunks:\n\n- The prose chunk loses practical usage context.\n\n- The code chunk may lack enough descriptive context for accurate retrieval.\n\nSeparate code chunks might be justified when examples are extremely large or when the application supports a distinct code-search use case. The scenario, however, focuses on retrieving complete information about a named function or class, so keeping its example with the corresponding documentation is more useful."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag","https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_prep_search"],"source_hash":"6b89c2a2311a9101279f2efbfd0078fd15fc547581dbb201930995e5f141ba8d","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、ソースコードのdocstringから自動生成した社内APIリファレンス文書を対象に、RAGエージェントを構築しています。各関数またはクラスには、説明、引数一覧、戻り値の型、実行可能なコード例を含む独立したdocstringブロックがあります。ブロックの長さは数行から、複雑なクラスでは数百行までさまざまです。開発者の質問はほぼ常に、「retry_with_backoff関数は何をし、引数は何か」のように、名前を指定した特定の関数やクラスについてです。\n\nこの文書構造と質問パターンに最も適したチャンク分割戦略はどれですか。","options_ja":["関数・クラスの境界で分割し、説明・引数・戻り値の型・コード例を含む各docstringブロックを1チャンクとしてまとめる。埋め込みモデルのコンテキスト長を超える、まれにある大きなクラスのdocstringだけをさらに分割する。","ソース構造にかかわらず、小さく一定のサイズが常に最も安全な既定値なので、関数やクラスの境界を考慮せず256トークン固定で分割する。","各docstring内を段落単位で分割し、説明・引数一覧・コード例を毎回それぞれ別のチャンクにする。","コードと文章では根本的に必要な分割粒度が異なるため、各docstringのコード例を説明と引数一覧から分けて埋め込む。"],"explanation_ja":"質問の単位が関数やクラスなので、検索チャンクもその意味の単位に合わせます。説明・引数・戻り値・実行例が一緒なら、1回の検索で質問に答える材料が揃います。ただし埋め込み上限を超えるブロックは分割が必要です。覚え方は「検索する名前の単位に、説明と利用条件をまとめる」です。","distractors_ja":["文書の意味的な境界と検索対象が一致し、利用に必要な情報を一緒に取得できます。","固定サイズを優先すると関数の説明と引数を途中で分断し、必要な情報が別々になります。","段落ごとに細分化すると、同じ関数を理解するための関連情報が分散します。","コード例と説明は同じAPIの理解に必要であり、必ず別々にする根拠はありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-017","number":17,"form":2,"stem":"A Databricks agent pages the correct on-call engineer when a production incident is reported. Available data sources include: the current on-call rotation schedule by team, a service-ownership mapping (which team owns which service), the incident's reported severity level, the reporting employee's job title and department, and the building floor plan showing which floor each team sits on.\n\nGiven an incident report naming an affected service and a severity level, which combination of context elements is required for the agent to correctly scope and execute the paging decision?","options":["The reporting employee's job title and department, since only certain job titles are authorized to trigger a page.","The building floor plan, so the agent can page whichever team is physically closest to the incident-response war room.","The service-ownership mapping (to determine which team owns the affected service) and the current on-call rotation schedule for that team (to determine who is on call right now), scoped by the incident's severity level to determine paging urgency.","The current on-call rotation schedule for every team in the company, regardless of which team owns the affected service, so the agent has maximum coverage in case of an error in the ownership mapping."],"correct":2,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"オンコール通知に必要なコンテキスト","concept":"ce-f2-q017","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. The service-ownership mapping (to determine which team owns the affected service) and the current on-call rotation schedule for that team (to determine who is on call right now), scoped by the incident's severity level to determine paging urgency.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe agent must resolve three linked decisions:\n\n- Which team owns the affected service?\nUse the service-ownership mapping.\n\n- Which engineer is currently responsible for that team?\nUse the team’s current on-call rotation schedule.\n\n- How urgently should that engineer be paged?\nUse the reported incident severity.\n\nThis creates the required decision path:\n\nAffected service → owning team → current on-call engineer → severity-based paging action\n\nThe employee’s job title and the building floor plan do not contribute to this decision. Loading every team’s schedule would also add unnecessary context after the owning team has been identified.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe agent requires context that is both sufficient and directly relevant to the paging decision.\n\nThe service name alone does not identify an individual engineer. It must first be mapped to the responsible team. The current rotation for that team then identifies the engineer who should receive the page.\n\nSeverity supplies the operational policy for deciding whether the incident should:\n\n- Trigger an immediate page.\n\n- Generate a lower-priority notification.\n\n- Follow an escalation procedure.\n\n- Be recorded without waking the on-call engineer.\n\nOption C therefore supplies every necessary context element without introducing unrelated organizational data.\n\nIt also follows good context-engineering practice: provide the agent with the smallest complete set of information needed to make and execute the decision.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. The reporting employee's job title and department, since only certain job titles are authorized to trigger a page.\n\nThe scenario does not state that paging authorization depends on the reporter’s job title or department.\n\nThis option invents an additional business rule that is not provided. Even with the reporter’s role, the agent would still not know:\n\n- Which team owns the affected service.\n\n- Which engineer is currently on call.\n\n- How severity affects the paging action.\n\nAuthorization could be an additional real-world control, but it is not the context required by this question.\n\nB. The building floor plan, so the agent can page whichever team is physically closest to the incident-response war room.\n\nPhysical proximity does not determine service responsibility.\n\nThe closest team might have no expertise, permissions, or operational responsibility for the affected service. Incident routing should be based on service ownership and the established on-call rotation, not seating location.\n\nThis is irrelevant context that could distract the agent from the correct routing logic.\n\nD. The current on-call rotation schedule for every team in the company, regardless of which team owns the affected service, so the agent has maximum coverage in case of an error in the ownership mapping.\n\nThe agent does not need every team’s schedule. After determining the owning team, only that team’s current rotation is necessary.\n\nProviding all schedules would:\n\n- Add irrelevant context.\n\n- Increase the chance of selecting the wrong engineer.\n\n- Increase token or tool-result volume.\n\n- Make the decision less deterministic.\n\nIf the ownership mapping might be incorrect, the proper solution is to govern, validate, and update that mapping—not bypass it by exposing every possible on-call engineer.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming that more context provides safer coverage.\n\nThe agent needs complete context, but not unlimited context. The correct approach is to retrieve only the data that participates in the decision chain:\n\nservice ownership + team-specific current rotation + severity policy\n\nUnrelated employee attributes, physical location, and company-wide schedules do not improve the decision.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nIn production, this workflow should preferably be implemented as a constrained sequence:\n\n- Validate the affected service identifier.\n\n- Retrieve its owning team from the authoritative service catalog.\n\n- Retrieve the current primary and secondary on-call engineers for that team.\n\n- Apply a deterministic severity-to-notification policy.\n\n- Execute the page and record the incident, recipient, timestamp, and escalation path.\n\nAdditional production safeguards could include:\n\n- Checking that the rotation data is current.\n\n- Handling missing or conflicting ownership records.\n\n- Paging a secondary engineer when the primary does not acknowledge.\n\n- Requiring confirmation for unusually broad paging actions.\n\n- Recording tool calls and paging outcomes in MLflow traces.\n\nFor the certification question, however, C is the complete and correctly scoped context combination.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent system design patterns \n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- Concepts: AI on Databricks — Tools \n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/concepts/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/concepts/)\n\n- Databricks Certified Context Engineer Associate\n[https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate](https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate)\n\n- Debug and analyze your app with tracing \n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces/)","source_option_explanations":["A. The reporting employee's job title and department, since only certain job titles are authorized to trigger a page.\n\nThe scenario does not state that paging authorization depends on the reporter’s job title or department.\n\nThis option invents an additional business rule that is not provided. Even with the reporter’s role, the agent would still not know:\n\n- Which team owns the affected service.\n\n- Which engineer is currently on call.\n\n- How severity affects the paging action.\n\nAuthorization could be an additional real-world control, but it is not the context required by this question.","B. The building floor plan, so the agent can page whichever team is physically closest to the incident-response war room.\n\nPhysical proximity does not determine service responsibility.\n\nThe closest team might have no expertise, permissions, or operational responsibility for the affected service. Incident routing should be based on service ownership and the established on-call rotation, not seating location.\n\nThis is irrelevant context that could distract the agent from the correct routing logic.","Correct Answer\n\nC. The service-ownership mapping (to determine which team owns the affected service) and the current on-call rotation schedule for that team (to determine who is on call right now), scoped by the incident's severity level to determine paging urgency.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe agent must resolve three linked decisions:\n\n- Which team owns the affected service?\nUse the service-ownership mapping.\n\n- Which engineer is currently responsible for that team?\nUse the team’s current on-call rotation schedule.\n\n- How urgently should that engineer be paged?\nUse the reported incident severity.\n\nThis creates the required decision path:\n\nAffected service → owning team → current on-call engineer → severity-based paging action\n\nThe employee’s job title and the building floor plan do not contribute to this decision. Loading every team’s schedule would also add unnecessary context after the owning team has been identified.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe agent requires context that is both sufficient and directly relevant to the paging decision.\n\nThe service name alone does not identify an individual engineer. It must first be mapped to the responsible team. The current rotation for that team then identifies the engineer who should receive the page.\n\nSeverity supplies the operational policy for deciding whether the incident should:\n\n- Trigger an immediate page.\n\n- Generate a lower-priority notification.\n\n- Follow an escalation procedure.\n\n- Be recorded without waking the on-call engineer.\n\nOption C therefore supplies every necessary context element without introducing unrelated organizational data.\n\nIt also follows good context-engineering practice: provide the agent with the smallest complete set of information needed to make and execute the decision.","D. The current on-call rotation schedule for every team in the company, regardless of which team owns the affected service, so the agent has maximum coverage in case of an error in the ownership mapping.\n\nThe agent does not need every team’s schedule. After determining the owning team, only that team’s current rotation is necessary.\n\nProviding all schedules would:\n\n- Add irrelevant context.\n\n- Increase the chance of selecting the wrong engineer.\n\n- Increase token or tool-result volume.\n\n- Make the decision less deterministic.\n\nIf the ownership mapping might be incorrect, the proper solution is to govern, validate, and update that mapping—not bypass it by exposing every possible on-call engineer."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/concepts/","https://www.databricks.com/learn/certification/context-engineer-associate","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces/"],"source_hash":"7445beaa0614d7fbf0a0b45f2a585a2c04493c4ee00f9fae463dbcadbe634264","stem_ja":"本番障害が報告されると、Databricksのエージェントが適切なオンコールエンジニアを呼び出します。利用できるデータには、チーム別の現在のオンコール当番表、サービスの所有関係（どのチームがどのサービスを担当するか）、障害の報告された重大度、報告した従業員の役職と部署、各チームの座席階を示す建物の平面図があります。\n\n影響を受けたサービス名と重大度を指定した障害報告について、通知先を正しく特定して呼び出すために必要なコンテキストの組み合わせはどれですか。","options_ja":["特定の役職だけが呼び出しを要求できるため、報告した従業員の役職と部署。","障害対応の対策室に物理的に最も近いチームを呼び出せるよう、建物の平面図。","影響を受けるサービスの担当チームを特定する所有関係と、そのチームで今誰が当番かを特定する現在のオンコール当番表を使い、障害の重大度に応じて通知の緊急性を決める。","所有関係に誤りがあっても最大限対応できるよう、影響を受けるサービスの担当に関係なく、会社の全チームの現在のオンコール当番表。"],"explanation_ja":"通知判断には「対象サービス→担当チーム→現在の当番」という関係が必要で、重大度が緊急性を決めます。近くに座る人や報告者の役職では、担当者は特定できません。覚え方は「対象・所有者・現在の担当・優先度」。利用可能な情報を全部入れず、実行判断に必要な関係だけを組み立てます。","distractors_ja":["役職ごとの通知権限という要件は示されておらず、サービス担当者も特定できません。","物理的な距離はサービスの責任者や現在の当番を表していません。","サービス所有者と当番表をつなぎ、重大度で緊急性を判断するため、必要な要素が揃います。","無関係なチームの当番表を大量に加えても、対象サービスの担当特定には不要です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-018","number":18,"form":2,"stem":"A team is deciding between retrieval strategies for two agents. Agent A answers research questions over a company's historical, immutable fiscal-year financial filings (once filed, a given year's filing never changes), where analysts often need broad recall across many years of filings to answer comparative questions. Agent B answers operator questions about a live manufacturing line's current sensor readings (temperature, pressure, throughput), which update every few seconds and where only the single current value matters, not a broad set of historical candidates.\n\nWhich retrieval strategy should be applied to each agent, respectively?","options":["Both agents should use just-in-time agentic retrieval exclusively, since live tool calls are always preferable regardless of how static or dynamic the underlying data is.","Agent A should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval over a Databricks AI Search index built from the immutable filings corpus; Agent B should use just-in-time agentic retrieval via a tool call that queries the live sensor-reading table directly at inference time.","Agent A should use just-in-time retrieval, re-scanning all historical filings on every question; Agent B should use a periodically refreshed AI Search index rebuilt every few seconds to keep pace with sensor updates.","Both agents should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval, since embeddings are the standard approach for any RAG use case regardless of data volatility."],"correct":1,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"静的文書の検索と最新値の取得","concept":"ce-f2-q018","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Agent A should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval over a Databricks AI Search index built from the immutable filings corpus; Agent B should use just-in-time agentic retrieval via a tool call that queries the live sensor-reading table directly at inference time.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe correct retrieval strategy depends primarily on:\n\n- Data volatility\n\n- Required freshness\n\n- Data structure\n\n- Expected retrieval breadth\n\nFor Agent A, the fiscal-year filings are historical and immutable. Once indexed, their content does not change, and analysts frequently need relevant evidence from multiple years. Preprocessing, embedding, and indexing the filings once provides efficient semantic retrieval across the entire corpus.\n\nFor Agent B, sensor values change every few seconds, and the user needs only the value that is current when the question is asked. Querying the live table through a structured-data tool at inference time avoids returning stale readings from a periodically refreshed embedding index.\n\nTherefore, Agent A should use indexed retrieval, while Agent B should use a live tool call.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nFor Agent A, Databricks AI Search is designed to store and query embedded document content for RAG, semantic search, and similarity-based retrieval. A stable corpus of historical filings can be parsed, chunked, embedded, and indexed ahead of time, allowing the agent to retrieve relevant passages across many documents without rescanning the full corpus for every request.\n\nThis is well suited to questions such as:\n\nCompare the company’s stated liquidity risks across the last seven fiscal years.\n\nThe agent can retrieve relevant sections from multiple filings based on semantic relevance, with metadata filters such as fiscal year, filing type, or business segment where appropriate.\n\nFor Agent B, the sensor readings are structured, rapidly changing data. Databricks supports connecting agents to Unity Catalog tables through Genie, Databricks SQL, or deterministic Unity Catalog function tools. A direct inference-time query can retrieve the current value from the authoritative table when the question is asked.\n\nFor example, a tool could run logic equivalent to:\n\n```\nSELECT temperature, pressure, throughput, reading_timestampFROM manufacturing.current_line_readingsWHERE line_id = :line_idORDER BY reading_timestamp DESCLIMIT 1;\n```\n\nThis returns the latest available reading rather than a semantically similar but potentially outdated indexed record.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Both agents should use just-in-time agentic retrieval exclusively, since live tool calls are always preferable regardless of how static or dynamic the underlying data is.\n\nJust-in-time retrieval is appropriate for Agent B, but unnecessary and inefficient for Agent A.\n\nRescanning and processing many years of immutable filings for every research question would repeat work that can be performed once during ingestion. It would increase latency and compute cost while providing no freshness advantage because the historical filings do not change.\n\nC. Agent A should use just-in-time retrieval, re-scanning all historical filings on every question; Agent B should use a periodically refreshed AI Search index rebuilt every few seconds to keep pace with sensor updates.\n\nThis reverses the suitable strategies.\n\nAgent A’s static corpus is ideal for preprocessing and indexing. Agent B’s live values are better queried directly from the authoritative table.\n\nRebuilding or synchronizing an embedding index every few seconds would introduce unnecessary complexity and could still return a value that became outdated between refreshes. Embedding-based similarity search is also unnecessary when the required operation is a precise lookup for the latest sensor record.\n\nD. Both agents should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval, since embeddings are the standard approach for any RAG use case regardless of data volatility.\n\nEmbeddings are useful for semantic retrieval over unstructured or text-heavy corpora, but they are not the universal retrieval mechanism for every agent.\n\nFor Agent B, the user needs an exact, current structured value—not a semantically similar historical reading. Directly querying the current sensor table is more precise and timely.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is treating one retrieval pattern as universally preferable.\n\nUse pre-indexed retrieval when the knowledge corpus is relatively stable and questions require semantic discovery across many documents. Use just-in-time tools when the answer depends on rapidly changing, authoritative operational data.\n\nThe deciding factor is not whether both applications are called “RAG”; it is whether the required context should be prepared in advance or fetched at inference time.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production implementation could use:\n\nAgent A\n\nFiscal filings → parsing and chunking → Delta table with filing metadata → Databricks AI Search index → semantic or hybrid retrieval\n\nUseful metadata could include:\n\n- Fiscal year\n\n- Filing type\n\n- Section name\n\n- Page number\n\n- Business segment\n\n- Source document\n\nBroad comparative questions might require multiple searches, year-based metadata filtering, or retrieval from several filings rather than relying on a single top result.\n\nAgent B\n\nUser request → validated sensor or line identifier → governed SQL/tool call → latest table record → answer with reading timestamp\n\nThe response should include the observation time so operators can judge freshness. In safety-critical environments, the agent should also detect stale telemetry, missing readings, and abnormal sensor timestamps rather than presenting an old value as current.\n\nThe expected exam answer is B.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks AI Search\n[https://docs.datab](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search?utm_source=chatgpt.com)[ricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search)\n\n- Build an unstructured data pipeline for RAG\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag)\n\n- Connect agents to structured data\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools)\n\n- Connect agents to tools\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/agent-tool](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/agent-tool)","source_option_explanations":["A. Both agents should use just-in-time agentic retrieval exclusively, since live tool calls are always preferable regardless of how static or dynamic the underlying data is.\n\nJust-in-time retrieval is appropriate for Agent B, but unnecessary and inefficient for Agent A.\n\nRescanning and processing many years of immutable filings for every research question would repeat work that can be performed once during ingestion. It would increase latency and compute cost while providing no freshness advantage because the historical filings do not change.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Agent A should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval over a Databricks AI Search index built from the immutable filings corpus; Agent B should use just-in-time agentic retrieval via a tool call that queries the live sensor-reading table directly at inference time.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe correct retrieval strategy depends primarily on:\n\n- Data volatility\n\n- Required freshness\n\n- Data structure\n\n- Expected retrieval breadth\n\nFor Agent A, the fiscal-year filings are historical and immutable. Once indexed, their content does not change, and analysts frequently need relevant evidence from multiple years. Preprocessing, embedding, and indexing the filings once provides efficient semantic retrieval across the entire corpus.\n\nFor Agent B, sensor values change every few seconds, and the user needs only the value that is current when the question is asked. Querying the live table through a structured-data tool at inference time avoids returning stale readings from a periodically refreshed embedding index.\n\nTherefore, Agent A should use indexed retrieval, while Agent B should use a live tool call.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nFor Agent A, Databricks AI Search is designed to store and query embedded document content for RAG, semantic search, and similarity-based retrieval. A stable corpus of historical filings can be parsed, chunked, embedded, and indexed ahead of time, allowing the agent to retrieve relevant passages across many documents without rescanning the full corpus for every request.\n\nThis is well suited to questions such as:\n\nCompare the company’s stated liquidity risks across the last seven fiscal years.\n\nThe agent can retrieve relevant sections from multiple filings based on semantic relevance, with metadata filters such as fiscal year, filing type, or business segment where appropriate.\n\nFor Agent B, the sensor readings are structured, rapidly changing data. Databricks supports connecting agents to Unity Catalog tables through Genie, Databricks SQL, or deterministic Unity Catalog function tools. A direct inference-time query can retrieve the current value from the authoritative table when the question is asked.\n\nFor example, a tool could run logic equivalent to:\n\n```\nSELECT temperature, pressure, throughput, reading_timestampFROM manufacturing.current_line_readingsWHERE line_id = :line_idORDER BY reading_timestamp DESCLIMIT 1;\n```\n\nThis returns the latest available reading rather than a semantically similar but potentially outdated indexed record.","C. Agent A should use just-in-time retrieval, re-scanning all historical filings on every question; Agent B should use a periodically refreshed AI Search index rebuilt every few seconds to keep pace with sensor updates.\n\nThis reverses the suitable strategies.\n\nAgent A’s static corpus is ideal for preprocessing and indexing. Agent B’s live values are better queried directly from the authoritative table.\n\nRebuilding or synchronizing an embedding index every few seconds would introduce unnecessary complexity and could still return a value that became outdated between refreshes. Embedding-based similarity search is also unnecessary when the required operation is a precise lookup for the latest sensor record.","D. Both agents should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval, since embeddings are the standard approach for any RAG use case regardless of data volatility.\n\nEmbeddings are useful for semantic retrieval over unstructured or text-heavy corpora, but they are not the universal retrieval mechanism for every agent.\n\nFor Agent B, the user needs an exact, current structured value—not a semantically similar historical reading. Directly querying the current sensor table is more precise and timely."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search?utm_source=chatgpt.com","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/agent-tool"],"source_hash":"0c2242ca69a33e7499dfeebfcb168d8704f4b4643dc7df17b015575ddb2644d4","stem_ja":"チームが2つのエージェントの検索戦略を選んでいます。エージェントAは会社の過去の会計年度の財務開示文書を調査します。提出後に各年度の文書は変更されず、比較質問へ答えるには、複数年の文書から幅広く情報を拾う必要がよくあります。エージェントBは、稼働中の製造ラインの現在のセンサー値（温度、圧力、生産量）について作業者へ答えます。値は数秒ごとに変化し、幅広い過去の候補ではなく、現在の値1つだけが重要です。\n\nそれぞれに適用すべき検索戦略はどれですか。","options_ja":["基礎データが静的か動的かに関係なく、その場のツール呼び出しが常によいため、両方で必要時のエージェント検索だけを使う。","Aでは不変の開示文書コーパスから作ったDatabricks AI Searchインデックスに対する推論前の埋め込みベース検索を使い、Bでは推論時に最新センサー値のテーブルを直接問い合わせるツール呼び出しによる必要時検索を使う。","Aでは毎回の質問で過去の全開示文書を再走査する必要時検索を使い、Bではセンサー更新に追従するため数秒ごとに再構築するAI Searchインデックスを使う。","データの変動性に関係なく、どのRAGでも埋め込みが標準手法であるため、両方で推論前の埋め込みベース検索を使う。"],"explanation_ja":"不変の長文資料から関連情報を探す処理は、事前に作った埋め込み索引が適します。数秒で変わる現在値は、推論時に元の表を直接照会する方が鮮度と厳密さを保てます。覚え方は「静的な意味検索は索引、動く現在値はその場で照会」。常にどちらか1つが最良とは限りません。","distractors_ja":["静的で広い文書検索でも常に全件をその場で処理するのは、再利用可能な索引の利点を失います。","文書の不変性と比較検索、センサーの高頻度更新と厳密な現在値という違いに合っています。","静的文書の毎回の再走査も、現在値のための頻繁な索引再構築も、不要な負担が大きくなります。","意味的な候補探索は、頻繁に変わる現在の数値1件を正確に取得する用途の最適解とは限りません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-019","number":19,"form":2,"stem":"A context engineer investigates a multi-tenant support agent's declining context_recall scores in MLflow evaluation traces after a new Unity Catalog row-level security policy was applied to the shared support_tickets table, restricting each query to only the requesting tenant's rows. Cross-referencing the row filter function's definition, the engineer finds it checks current_user()'s email domain to determine tenant — but the agent authenticates as a single shared service principal for all tenants, not as an individual per-tenant user, so current_user() always resolves to the same identity regardless of which tenant's question is being asked, causing the filter to return either zero rows or one fixed tenant's rows for every request.\n\nWhich Unity Catalog governance action most directly resolves this failure?","options":["Remove the row-level security policy entirely, restoring unrestricted access to the support_tickets table for all tenants.","Rebuild the AI Search index from scratch to refresh stale embeddings for the support_tickets table.","Grant the shared service principal an exemption from the row filter policy so it can see all tenants' rows without restriction, without changing how the filter determines tenant identity.","Rewrite the row filter function to key off a tenant identifier explicitly passed and verified per request, rather than relying on current_user() against a shared service principal that cannot distinguish between tenants."],"correct":3,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"共有サービス主体とテナント識別の不一致","concept":"ce-f2-q019","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Rewrite the row filter function to key off a tenant identifier explicitly passed and verified per request, rather than relying on `current_user()` against a shared service principal that cannot distinguish between tenants.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure is caused by a mismatch between the row-level security policy’s identity assumption and the agent’s authentication model:\n\n- The row filter expects `current_user()` to identify the requesting tenant.\n\n- Every request is executed by the same shared service principal.\n\n- Therefore, `current_user()` returns the same service-principal identity for every tenant.\n\n- The policy cannot determine which tenant’s rows should be visible for a particular request.\n\nThis is not a retrieval-quality or stale-index problem. The policy is consistently applying the wrong tenant context before the agent receives the data.\n\nThe most direct correction is to scope access using a tenant identifier that is securely established and validated for each request, rather than attempting to infer the tenant from an execution identity that is deliberately shared.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nUnity Catalog row filters restrict rows at query time by evaluating a Boolean policy function. Identity-based functions work only when the executing identity meaningfully represents the user or tenant whose access should be enforced.\n\nIn this architecture, the shared service principal represents the application, not an individual tenant. It consequently provides no tenant-specific signal.\n\nA corrected design should follow this decision model:\n\n```\nAuthenticated request        ↓Verify requesting tenant        ↓Pass trusted tenant context to the governed query path        ↓Return only rows whose tenant_id matches that context\n```\n\nThe policy should compare the table’s `tenant_id` with a tenant identity established by trusted application logic. The value must not be accepted directly from unverified user input, because a caller could otherwise request another tenant’s identifier.\n\nThis restores:\n\n- Correct tenant-specific retrieval.\n\n- Data isolation between tenants.\n\n- Context recall for the requesting tenant.\n\n- Consistent policy enforcement across requests.\n\nAmong the available choices, D is the only option that repairs the defective tenant-resolution mechanism while preserving row-level isolation.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Remove the row-level security policy entirely, restoring unrestricted access to the `support_tickets` table for all tenants.\n\nThis might restore retrieval recall, but only by eliminating tenant isolation.\n\nThe shared service principal would then be able to retrieve tickets belonging to every tenant, potentially exposing one customer’s support data to another. Improving context recall does not justify bypassing a required security boundary.\n\nThe correct solution is to repair the policy’s tenant resolution, not remove the policy.\n\nB. Rebuild the AI Search index from scratch to refresh stale embeddings for the `support_tickets` table.\n\nThe scenario identifies no stale embeddings or synchronization issue.\n\nThe missing context is caused by the row filter returning the wrong rows before they reach the agent. Rebuilding an index would not change how `current_user()` resolves or how the row filter identifies the tenant.\n\nAdditionally, Unity Catalog tables with table-level row filters or column masks cannot directly serve as AI Search index source tables. The exact retrieval architecture therefore matters, but index rebuilding still does not address the stated identity-policy defect.\n\nC. Grant the shared service principal an exemption from the row filter policy so it can see all tenants' rows without restriction, without changing how the filter determines tenant identity.\n\nThis would bypass the policy rather than fix it.\n\nAlthough the application could theoretically filter tenant rows afterward, the foundational data-access layer would no longer enforce tenant isolation. A defect, prompt injection, malformed query, or application bug could expose cross-tenant records.\n\nExemptions are appropriate for narrowly controlled administrative or policy-management workflows—not as a substitute for correct tenant-aware authorization in a multi-tenant support agent.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is confusing poor `context_recall` with a search or embedding problem.\n\nMLflow exposes the symptom, but the root cause is governance-related: the row filter uses an identity signal that cannot vary by tenant.\n\nAnother trap is restoring recall by granting broader access. Correct retrieval must be achieved without weakening tenant isolation.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe tenant identifier must come from a trusted authentication or authorization mechanism, such as a validated token claim, tenant-to-session mapping, or application-controlled request context.\n\nA production design should not allow an LLM or end user to freely supply:\n\n```\ntenant_id = \"another-customer\"\n```\n\nand have the security policy trust it.\n\nDepending on the architecture, robust alternatives include:\n\n- Using distinct service principals or delegated identities per tenant.\n\n- Mapping authenticated identities to allowed tenants in a governed mapping table.\n\n- Exposing a secured dynamic view or Unity Catalog function that accepts only application-verified tenant context.\n\n- Restricting the shared service principal to a controlled query tool that always applies validated tenant filtering.\n\n- Testing negative cases to confirm that one tenant can never retrieve another tenant’s rows.\n\nThe expected exam answer is D, with the critical qualification that the tenant value must be established and verified outside the model rather than trusted merely because it appears in the generated SQL.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Row filters and column masks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/)\n\n- `current_user` function\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/current_user](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/current_user)\n\n- Manually apply row filters and column masks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/manually-apply](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/manually-apply)\n\n- Service principals\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/users-groups/service-principals](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/users-groups/service-principals)","source_option_explanations":["A. Remove the row-level security policy entirely, restoring unrestricted access to the `support_tickets` table for all tenants.\n\nThis might restore retrieval recall, but only by eliminating tenant isolation.\n\nThe shared service principal would then be able to retrieve tickets belonging to every tenant, potentially exposing one customer’s support data to another. Improving context recall does not justify bypassing a required security boundary.\n\nThe correct solution is to repair the policy’s tenant resolution, not remove the policy.","B. Rebuild the AI Search index from scratch to refresh stale embeddings for the `support_tickets` table.\n\nThe scenario identifies no stale embeddings or synchronization issue.\n\nThe missing context is caused by the row filter returning the wrong rows before they reach the agent. Rebuilding an index would not change how `current_user()` resolves or how the row filter identifies the tenant.\n\nAdditionally, Unity Catalog tables with table-level row filters or column masks cannot directly serve as AI Search index source tables. The exact retrieval architecture therefore matters, but index rebuilding still does not address the stated identity-policy defect.","C. Grant the shared service principal an exemption from the row filter policy so it can see all tenants' rows without restriction, without changing how the filter determines tenant identity.\n\nThis would bypass the policy rather than fix it.\n\nAlthough the application could theoretically filter tenant rows afterward, the foundational data-access layer would no longer enforce tenant isolation. A defect, prompt injection, malformed query, or application bug could expose cross-tenant records.\n\nExemptions are appropriate for narrowly controlled administrative or policy-management workflows—not as a substitute for correct tenant-aware authorization in a multi-tenant support agent.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Rewrite the row filter function to key off a tenant identifier explicitly passed and verified per request, rather than relying on `current_user()` against a shared service principal that cannot distinguish between tenants.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure is caused by a mismatch between the row-level security policy’s identity assumption and the agent’s authentication model:\n\n- The row filter expects `current_user()` to identify the requesting tenant.\n\n- Every request is executed by the same shared service principal.\n\n- Therefore, `current_user()` returns the same service-principal identity for every tenant.\n\n- The policy cannot determine which tenant’s rows should be visible for a particular request.\n\nThis is not a retrieval-quality or stale-index problem. The policy is consistently applying the wrong tenant context before the agent receives the data.\n\nThe most direct correction is to scope access using a tenant identifier that is securely established and validated for each request, rather than attempting to infer the tenant from an execution identity that is deliberately shared.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nUnity Catalog row filters restrict rows at query time by evaluating a Boolean policy function. Identity-based functions work only when the executing identity meaningfully represents the user or tenant whose access should be enforced.\n\nIn this architecture, the shared service principal represents the application, not an individual tenant. It consequently provides no tenant-specific signal.\n\nA corrected design should follow this decision model:\n\n```\nAuthenticated request        ↓Verify requesting tenant        ↓Pass trusted tenant context to the governed query path        ↓Return only rows whose tenant_id matches that context\n```\n\nThe policy should compare the table’s `tenant_id` with a tenant identity established by trusted application logic. The value must not be accepted directly from unverified user input, because a caller could otherwise request another tenant’s identifier.\n\nThis restores:\n\n- Correct tenant-specific retrieval.\n\n- Data isolation between tenants.\n\n- Context recall for the requesting tenant.\n\n- Consistent policy enforcement across requests.\n\nAmong the available choices, D is the only option that repairs the defective tenant-resolution mechanism while preserving row-level isolation."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/current_user","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/manually-apply","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/users-groups/service-principals"],"source_hash":"ab1a304b325ed5f486ef7e11f44521440c3f57f620d9ff43bfe8859a998f9dea","stem_ja":"複数テナント向けサポートエージェントで、共有support_ticketsテーブルへ新しいUnity Catalogの行レベルセキュリティポリシーを適用した後、MLflow評価トレースのcontext_recallが低下しました。ポリシーは各クエリを依頼元テナントの行だけに制限します。行フィルター関数を調べると、current_user()のメールドメインでテナントを判定しています。しかしエージェントは、テナントごとの個別ユーザーではなく、全テナント共通の1つのサービスプリンシパルで認証します。そのため、どのテナントからの質問でもcurrent_user()が同じ主体になり、すべてのリクエストで0行か、固定された1テナントの行だけを返します。\n\nこの障害を最も直接解決するUnity Catalogのガバナンス対応はどれですか。","options_ja":["行レベルセキュリティポリシーを完全に削除し、全テナントがsupport_ticketsへ制限なくアクセスできる状態に戻す。","support_ticketsの古い埋め込みを更新するため、AI Searchインデックスをゼロから作り直す。","テナント識別の仕組みは変えず、共有サービスプリンシパルを行フィルターの適用対象から除外し、全テナントの行を無制限で読めるようにする。","テナントを識別できない共有サービスプリンシパルのcurrent_user()に頼る代わりに、各リクエストで明示的に渡され、検証されたテナント識別子に基づいて判定するよう、行フィルター関数を書き直す。"],"explanation_ja":"検索対象が減った原因は索引ではなく、認証主体とテナント識別のずれです。共通サービスプリンシパルでは、current_user()から依頼元テナントを区別できません。リクエスト単位で信頼できるテナントIDを確定・検証し、アクセス制御へ反映します。ユーザーが自由に指定したIDを無検証で信用してはいけません。","distractors_ja":["隔離を外せば再現率が上がっても、テナント間の情報漏えいを招き、要件を満たしません。","失敗の原因はフィルターの識別ロジックなので、索引だけを再作成しても直りません。","共通主体を全面免除すると、必要なテナント分離が失われます。","共有主体とは別に、各依頼の検証済みテナントIDを使うことで、正しい行制限を維持できます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-020","number":20,"form":2,"stem":"Before deploying a clinical-decision-support agent, a team reviews the Unity Catalog environment it will retrieve from. It contains an official clinical_guidelines_certified catalog, maintained and version-controlled by a physician review board, and a large number of individual physicians' personal \"notes\" schemas containing informal draft guidance, personal shorthand, and in some cases outdated protocol versions that were formally superseded but never deleted from a physician's personal notes. The team wants the agent's retrieval space, before deployment, constrained to only the authoritative, board-reviewed sources.\n\nWhich governance strategy achieves this?","options":["Grant the agent's service principal broad SELECT access across both the certified catalog and all personal notes schemas, and add a system-prompt instruction telling it to prefer certified guidance if it notices a discrepancy.","Grant access only to the personal notes schemas, since individual physicians' notes are likely to reflect more current, real-world clinical practice than the periodically-updated certified catalog.","Register the agent's retrieval tools and AI Search index only against the clinical_guidelines_certified catalog, and scope the agent's service principal's SELECT privileges to that catalog alone, withholding access to any physician's personal notes schemas.","Leave Unity Catalog permissions unchanged, and instead rely on the AI Search index's relevance ranking to naturally surface the certified catalog's content ahead of personal notes in most queries."],"correct":2,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"権限と検索先で公式資料に限定","concept":"ce-f2-q020","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Register the agent's retrieval tools and AI Search index only against the `clinical_guidelines_certified` catalog, and scope the agent's service principal's `SELECT` privileges to that catalog alone, withholding access to any physician's personal notes schemas.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirement is to ensure that the agent can retrieve only authoritative, physician-board-reviewed guidance before deployment.\n\nThat requires enforcement at two complementary levels:\n\n- Retrieval configuration: Build the retrieval tools and AI Search index only from the certified catalog.\n\n- Unity Catalog authorization: Grant the agent’s service principal access only to the certified catalog and withhold access to personal notes schemas.\n\nThis design prevents informal, outdated, or superseded physician notes from becoming retrieval candidates at all. It applies governance before inference rather than asking the model or search ranker to distinguish authoritative content after untrusted content has already entered the retrieval scope.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption C applies the principles of least privilege, trusted-source curation, and defense in depth.\n\nThe AI Search index should contain only content from the governed `clinical_guidelines_certified` catalog. This prevents uncertified notes from being embedded or returned during retrieval.\n\nThe service principal should receive only the privileges required to access that approved source, such as:\n\n- `USE CATALOG` on `clinical_guidelines_certified`\n\n- `USE SCHEMA` on the required certified schemas\n\n- `SELECT` on the approved source tables, views, or AI Search index\n\nIt should not receive corresponding access to personal notes schemas.\n\nThis is stronger than prompt-level guidance because the agent cannot accidentally retrieve data it is not authorized to access. Even if the prompt is changed, a query is malformed, or retrieval behavior varies, Unity Catalog permissions continue enforcing the approved data boundary.\n\nFor a clinical-decision-support workload, excluding superseded and unreviewed sources before retrieval is substantially safer than attempting to resolve conflicting guidance during generation.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Grant the agent's service principal broad `SELECT` access across both the certified catalog and all personal notes schemas, and add a system-prompt instruction telling it to prefer certified guidance if it notices a discrepancy.\n\nThis exposes both authoritative and unreviewed content to the agent.\n\nA prompt instruction is not a reliable access-control boundary. The model could still retrieve, combine, quote, or prioritize an outdated personal note, particularly when the conflict is subtle or the note appears highly relevant to the query.\n\nThe correct approach is to prevent unauthorized or unapproved content from entering the retrieval scope rather than depending on the model to reject it afterward.\n\nB. Grant access only to the personal notes schemas, since individual physicians' notes are likely to reflect more current, real-world clinical practice than the periodically-updated certified catalog.\n\nThis directly contradicts the stated governance requirement.\n\nThe personal schemas contain:\n\n- Informal drafts\n\n- Personal shorthand\n\n- Unreviewed recommendations\n\n- Superseded protocol versions\n\n- Potentially inconsistent guidance\n\nRecency does not establish authority or clinical validity. The scenario explicitly identifies the physician-board-maintained catalog as the official source.\n\nD. Leave Unity Catalog permissions unchanged, and instead rely on the AI Search index's relevance ranking to naturally surface the certified catalog's content ahead of personal notes in most queries.\n\nRelevance ranking determines which content appears semantically or lexically related to a query. It does not determine whether that content is approved, current, or clinically authoritative.\n\nAn outdated personal note could rank above certified guidance because it closely matches the user’s wording. “Most queries” is also insufficient for a clinical workload where even an occasional retrieval of superseded guidance can be consequential.\n\nGovernance must be enforced through source selection and permissions, not ranking probabilities.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe primary trap is confusing retrieval relevance with source authority.\n\nA highly relevant document can still be outdated, unreviewed, or unsafe. Likewise, a system-prompt preference is not equivalent to restricting access.\n\nThe strongest design excludes untrusted content from both:\n\n- The agent’s permitted Unity Catalog scope\n\n- The AI Search corpus used for retrieval\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production clinical agent should maintain an explicit allowlist of approved sources rather than merely excluding known bad ones.\n\nA robust deployment would include:\n\n- An AI Search index sourced only from certified, current guideline assets.\n\n- Least-privilege permissions for the agent’s service principal.\n\n- Version, approval-status, effective-date, and supersession metadata.\n\n- A process that removes or invalidates superseded guideline chunks.\n\n- Retrieval evaluations that verify citations come only from approved sources.\n\n- Audit logging and lineage to identify which guideline version supported each answer.\n\n- Clinical escalation behavior when no approved guidance is available.\n\nThe certification answer is C because it enforces authority at the data-access and retrieval layers before the agent generates a response.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- What is Unity Catalog?\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/)\n\n- Unity Catalog permissions model concepts\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/permissions-concepts](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/permissions-concepts)\n\n- Add an AI Search index resource to a Databricks app\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/databricks-apps/vector-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/databricks-apps/vector-search)\n\n- Service principals\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/users-groups/service-principals](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/users-groups/service-principals)","source_option_explanations":["A. Grant the agent's service principal broad `SELECT` access across both the certified catalog and all personal notes schemas, and add a system-prompt instruction telling it to prefer certified guidance if it notices a discrepancy.\n\nThis exposes both authoritative and unreviewed content to the agent.\n\nA prompt instruction is not a reliable access-control boundary. The model could still retrieve, combine, quote, or prioritize an outdated personal note, particularly when the conflict is subtle or the note appears highly relevant to the query.\n\nThe correct approach is to prevent unauthorized or unapproved content from entering the retrieval scope rather than depending on the model to reject it afterward.","B. Grant access only to the personal notes schemas, since individual physicians' notes are likely to reflect more current, real-world clinical practice than the periodically-updated certified catalog.\n\nThis directly contradicts the stated governance requirement.\n\nThe personal schemas contain:\n\n- Informal drafts\n\n- Personal shorthand\n\n- Unreviewed recommendations\n\n- Superseded protocol versions\n\n- Potentially inconsistent guidance\n\nRecency does not establish authority or clinical validity. The scenario explicitly identifies the physician-board-maintained catalog as the official source.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Register the agent's retrieval tools and AI Search index only against the `clinical_guidelines_certified` catalog, and scope the agent's service principal's `SELECT` privileges to that catalog alone, withholding access to any physician's personal notes schemas.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirement is to ensure that the agent can retrieve only authoritative, physician-board-reviewed guidance before deployment.\n\nThat requires enforcement at two complementary levels:\n\n- Retrieval configuration: Build the retrieval tools and AI Search index only from the certified catalog.\n\n- Unity Catalog authorization: Grant the agent’s service principal access only to the certified catalog and withhold access to personal notes schemas.\n\nThis design prevents informal, outdated, or superseded physician notes from becoming retrieval candidates at all. It applies governance before inference rather than asking the model or search ranker to distinguish authoritative content after untrusted content has already entered the retrieval scope.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption C applies the principles of least privilege, trusted-source curation, and defense in depth.\n\nThe AI Search index should contain only content from the governed `clinical_guidelines_certified` catalog. This prevents uncertified notes from being embedded or returned during retrieval.\n\nThe service principal should receive only the privileges required to access that approved source, such as:\n\n- `USE CATALOG` on `clinical_guidelines_certified`\n\n- `USE SCHEMA` on the required certified schemas\n\n- `SELECT` on the approved source tables, views, or AI Search index\n\nIt should not receive corresponding access to personal notes schemas.\n\nThis is stronger than prompt-level guidance because the agent cannot accidentally retrieve data it is not authorized to access. Even if the prompt is changed, a query is malformed, or retrieval behavior varies, Unity Catalog permissions continue enforcing the approved data boundary.\n\nFor a clinical-decision-support workload, excluding superseded and unreviewed sources before retrieval is substantially safer than attempting to resolve conflicting guidance during generation.","D. Leave Unity Catalog permissions unchanged, and instead rely on the AI Search index's relevance ranking to naturally surface the certified catalog's content ahead of personal notes in most queries.\n\nRelevance ranking determines which content appears semantically or lexically related to a query. It does not determine whether that content is approved, current, or clinically authoritative.\n\nAn outdated personal note could rank above certified guidance because it closely matches the user’s wording. “Most queries” is also insufficient for a clinical workload where even an occasional retrieval of superseded guidance can be consequential.\n\nGovernance must be enforced through source selection and permissions, not ranking probabilities."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/permissions-concepts","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dev-tools/databricks-apps/vector-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/admin/users-groups/service-principals"],"source_hash":"9e31a9081b1af64f3645b9400d4f72c788a9ed66190b04c6b44b36bb22e7c936","stem_ja":"臨床判断支援エージェントのデプロイ前に、チームが検索対象のUnity Catalog環境を点検しています。医師の審査委員会が保守し版管理する公式のclinical_guidelines_certifiedカタログに加え、個々の医師の個人的なnotesスキーマが多数あります。個人用メモには非公式の指針案や独自の略記があり、正式に後継版へ置き換えられたのに削除されていない古いプロトコルも含まれます。チームは本番公開前に、エージェントの検索範囲を、正式で委員会審査済みの資料だけに制約したいと考えています。\n\nこれを実現するガバナンス戦略はどれですか。","options_ja":["認定済みカタログと全個人メモスキーマへ広いSELECT権限をサービスプリンシパルに付与し、矛盾に気づいたら認定済み指針を優先するようシステムプロンプトで指示する。","個人メモは定期更新の認定カタログより新しい現場診療を反映する可能性が高いため、個人メモスキーマだけへアクセスを付与する。","検索ツールとAI Searchインデックスの対象をclinical_guidelines_certifiedだけに登録し、エージェントのサービスプリンシパルのSELECT権限もそのカタログだけに限定して、医師の個人メモスキーマへのアクセスを与えない。","Unity Catalogの権限は変えず、多くの検索で個人メモより認定カタログの内容が自然に上位となるよう、AI Searchの関連度ランキングに頼る。"],"explanation_ja":"「公式資料だけ」を保証するには、モデルへのお願いや関連度順位ではなく、検索先と権限を制限します。認定カタログだけを索引・ツールの対象にし、実行主体もその範囲だけ読める設計にします。覚え方は「信頼できない情報は、順位を下げるより入口を閉じる」。矛盾を読んだ後の選別に頼りません。","distractors_ja":["非公式や古い資料も取得できてしまい、公式資料だけという制約を保証できません。","審査済みの正式資料を除き、非公式な情報を優先するため、要求と逆です。","検索設定とデータ権限の両方を正式な資料へ絞り、検索空間そのものを制約できます。","関連度は内容の近さであり、正式な権威やアクセス許可を強制する仕組みではありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-021","number":21,"form":2,"stem":"A coding assistant agent is helping a developer work on a specific project. Earlier in the current session, the developer said: \"for this project, use snake_case for all variable names.\" Several turns later, when the agent generates a new function, it uses camelCase instead. Investigating, the team finds the agent's harness queried its Lakebase-backed long-term memory store — which holds the developer's preferences from other, unrelated past projects — and retrieved a camelCase preference from a different project, applying it instead of the snake_case instruction the developer gave earlier in this very session.\n\nWhat is the mismatch, and what is the correct memory strategy?","options":["The agent is pulling from long-term/cross-session memory for a preference that was already explicitly stated in the current session's active context; it should prioritize the current session's in-context conversation history for facts stated in this session, reserving the Lakebase-backed long-term store for preferences the user hasn't restated in the current session.","The agent needs a larger context window; increasing it will let the current session's turns dominate over irrelevant long-term facts.","The agent is correctly using long-term memory; the fix is to ensure Lakebase syncs more frequently so cross-session facts stay fresher.","The agent should stop using Lakebase entirely and rely only on session-level context for all facts, including preferences that must be remembered across future sessions."],"correct":0,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"現在の指示を過去の好みより優先","concept":"ce-f2-q021","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. The agent is pulling from long-term/cross-session memory for a preference that was already explicitly stated in the current session's active context; it should prioritize the current session's in-context conversation history for facts stated in this session, reserving the Lakebase-backed long-term store for preferences the user hasn't restated in the current session.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe conflict is between two memory scopes:\n\n- Current-session context: “For this project, use `snake_case`.”\n\n- Long-term memory: The developer previously preferred `camelCase` in another project.\n\nThe current-session instruction is newer, more specific, and explicitly scoped to the project being discussed. The retrieved long-term preference belongs to an unrelated project and should not override it.\n\nThe appropriate precedence is:\n\nCurrent explicit project instruction → current-session context → project-scoped memory → general cross-session user preference\n\nTherefore, the agent should use `snake_case` for this project and consult long-term memory only when the active conversation has not already supplied a more specific instruction.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nDatabricks distinguishes between:\n\n- Short-term memory, which preserves context and follow-up information within one conversation.\n\n- Long-term memory, which stores preferences and accumulated information across conversations.\n\nThe developer’s `snake_case` instruction is active short-term/session context. The `camelCase` preference is cross-session long-term memory. Applying the older general preference over the current project-specific instruction is a memory-selection and precedence error.\n\nOption A corrects the mismatch by ensuring that retrieved memory supplements the active conversation rather than contradicting it. A long-term memory store is useful when the user has not specified a preference in the current session, but it should not displace a newer, directly applicable instruction.\n\nThe harness should also scope long-term memories appropriately. Databricks memory supports scopes, and its guidance notes that developers can deliberately partition memories by dimensions such as project or account. A coding-style preference from one project should therefore not be retrieved as an unqualified preference for every other project.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. The agent needs a larger context window; increasing it will let the current session's turns dominate over irrelevant long-term facts.\n\nThe current instruction is already present in the session, so the fundamental problem is not insufficient context capacity.\n\nA larger context window does not define which conflicting fact should take precedence. The agent could still receive both `snake_case` and `camelCase` preferences and select the wrong one unless the harness establishes recency, specificity, and scope rules.\n\nC. The agent is correctly using long-term memory; the fix is to ensure Lakebase syncs more frequently so cross-session facts stay fresher.\n\nThe long-term store may be operating correctly from a storage perspective, but the retrieved memory is inappropriate for the present project.\n\nSynchronizing more frequently would not resolve the semantic conflict. It could simply retrieve the unrelated `camelCase` preference faster.\n\nThe failure concerns memory relevance and precedence, not storage freshness.\n\nD. The agent should stop using Lakebase entirely and rely only on session-level context for all facts, including preferences that must be remembered across future sessions.\n\nThis would avoid the immediate conflict but would remove legitimate long-term personalization.\n\nLakebase-backed memory is useful for preferences, prior decisions, and accumulated information that should persist across conversations. The better solution is to scope and prioritize memory correctly, not eliminate persistent memory entirely.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is treating all remembered facts as equally authoritative.\n\nA retrieved long-term preference may be valid historically but still be wrong for the current task. The candidate must distinguish:\n\n- General preferences from project-specific instructions\n\n- Older memory from newer explicit guidance\n\n- Cross-session memory from active conversational state\n\nLong-term memory should augment current context, not overrule a more specific current instruction.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production memory policy should attach metadata to stored preferences, such as:\n\n```\nPreference: camelCaseScope: project_legacy_java_appSource session: 2026-05-14\n```\n\nThe current instruction could be represented as:\n\n```\nPreference: snake_caseScope: current_projectSource: active conversationPriority: explicit current-session instruction\n```\n\nBefore injecting retrieved memories, the harness should:\n\n- Detect whether the active session already contains an applicable instruction.\n\n- Filter long-term memories by user and project scope.\n\n- Prefer more specific instructions over general preferences.\n\n- Prefer current explicit instructions over older stored facts.\n\n- Suppress contradictory long-term memories unless clarification is necessary.\n\nThe exam answer is A. In a stronger implementation, the Lakebase memory store should also partition coding preferences by project rather than storing `camelCase` as an unrestricted user-wide rule.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent memory\n[https://d](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents?utm_source=chatgpt.com)[o](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)[cs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)\n\n- Self-managed agent memory (Lakebase)\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory)\n\n- Managed agent memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/managed-memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/managed-memory)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. The agent is pulling from long-term/cross-session memory for a preference that was already explicitly stated in the current session's active context; it should prioritize the current session's in-context conversation history for facts stated in this session, reserving the Lakebase-backed long-term store for preferences the user hasn't restated in the current session.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe conflict is between two memory scopes:\n\n- Current-session context: “For this project, use `snake_case`.”\n\n- Long-term memory: The developer previously preferred `camelCase` in another project.\n\nThe current-session instruction is newer, more specific, and explicitly scoped to the project being discussed. The retrieved long-term preference belongs to an unrelated project and should not override it.\n\nThe appropriate precedence is:\n\nCurrent explicit project instruction → current-session context → project-scoped memory → general cross-session user preference\n\nTherefore, the agent should use `snake_case` for this project and consult long-term memory only when the active conversation has not already supplied a more specific instruction.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nDatabricks distinguishes between:\n\n- Short-term memory, which preserves context and follow-up information within one conversation.\n\n- Long-term memory, which stores preferences and accumulated information across conversations.\n\nThe developer’s `snake_case` instruction is active short-term/session context. The `camelCase` preference is cross-session long-term memory. Applying the older general preference over the current project-specific instruction is a memory-selection and precedence error.\n\nOption A corrects the mismatch by ensuring that retrieved memory supplements the active conversation rather than contradicting it. A long-term memory store is useful when the user has not specified a preference in the current session, but it should not displace a newer, directly applicable instruction.\n\nThe harness should also scope long-term memories appropriately. Databricks memory supports scopes, and its guidance notes that developers can deliberately partition memories by dimensions such as project or account. A coding-style preference from one project should therefore not be retrieved as an unqualified preference for every other project.","B. The agent needs a larger context window; increasing it will let the current session's turns dominate over irrelevant long-term facts.\n\nThe current instruction is already present in the session, so the fundamental problem is not insufficient context capacity.\n\nA larger context window does not define which conflicting fact should take precedence. The agent could still receive both `snake_case` and `camelCase` preferences and select the wrong one unless the harness establishes recency, specificity, and scope rules.","C. The agent is correctly using long-term memory; the fix is to ensure Lakebase syncs more frequently so cross-session facts stay fresher.\n\nThe long-term store may be operating correctly from a storage perspective, but the retrieved memory is inappropriate for the present project.\n\nSynchronizing more frequently would not resolve the semantic conflict. It could simply retrieve the unrelated `camelCase` preference faster.\n\nThe failure concerns memory relevance and precedence, not storage freshness.","D. The agent should stop using Lakebase entirely and rely only on session-level context for all facts, including preferences that must be remembered across future sessions.\n\nThis would avoid the immediate conflict but would remove legitimate long-term personalization.\n\nLakebase-backed memory is useful for preferences, prior decisions, and accumulated information that should persist across conversations. The better solution is to scope and prioritize memory correctly, not eliminate persistent memory entirely."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents?utm_source=chatgpt.com","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/managed-memory"],"source_hash":"04675a7dc0fc09d130561e297360c0a151f47477eb0f601a92a8b575aa5da830","stem_ja":"コーディング支援エージェントが、あるプロジェクトを開発者と進めています。現在のセッションの前半で、開発者は「このプロジェクトではすべての変数名にsnake_caseを使う」と伝えました。数ターン後、エージェントは新しい関数にcamelCaseを使いました。調査すると、実行基盤はLakebaseの長期メモリを検索し、別の無関係な過去プロジェクトにおける開発者の好みであるcamelCaseを取得し、このセッションで伝えたsnake_caseの指示の代わりに適用していました。\n\nどのような不一致があり、正しいメモリ戦略は何ですか。","options_ja":["現在のセッションで明示された好みを、長期・セッション間メモリから取得しているのが不一致である。このセッションで述べた事実は現在の会話履歴を優先し、Lakebaseの長期保存は、ユーザーが現在のセッションで言い直していない好みに利用する。","エージェントにはもっと大きなコンテキストウィンドウが必要で、それを増やせば現在の会話が無関係な長期情報より優勢になる。","長期メモリの利用自体は正しく、セッション間の事実が新しくなるよう、Lakebaseの同期頻度を増やせばよい。","今後のセッションでも覚えておくべき好みを含む、すべての事実についてLakebaseの使用をやめ、セッション内のコンテキストだけに頼る。"],"explanation_ja":"明示された今回のプロジェクトの指示が、別案件の過去の好みより優先されます。長期メモリは現在の会話にない情報を補うもので、今の明確な指示を上書きするためのものではありません。覚え方は「今の明示指示が優先、過去の記憶は補助」。容量や同期頻度より、適用範囲と優先順位を直します。","distractors_ja":["必要な好みはすでに現在の会話にあり、その適用範囲も明示されています。現在の情報を優先するのが適切です。","窓を大きくしても、誤ったメモリ源を優先する設計はそのまま残ります。","別プロジェクトの好みが最新であっても、今回明示したプロジェクト規則に優先する理由にはなりません。","セッションをまたぐ好みの保持まで失うため、長期メモリを全面廃止する必要はありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-022","number":22,"form":2,"stem":"A multi-day competitive-intelligence research agent tracks findings across many parallel research threads, run by different team members over several days, investigating different competitors. The collective progress (what's been found, which sources have been checked, open questions) must be queryable by any team member's session at any time, must survive process restarts, and different worker processes may pick up different competitors' research at different times.\n\nWhich approach is required for tracking this collective progress?","options":["An in-context scratchpad is sufficient, since each team member's own session can regenerate any lost state by re-reading the original sources from scratch.","A Delta-backed state object is required, since the collective progress must be shared and queryable across multiple users and worker processes, survive restarts, and not depend on any single conversation's context window remaining intact.","Neither approach is needed; the task should rely entirely on the model's parametric knowledge to track collective progress without external state.","An in-context scratchpad is preferable because it keeps state changes visible to the model on every turn without requiring any additional read/write calls to external storage."],"correct":1,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"複数利用者で共有する永続的な進捗状態","concept":"ce-f2-q022","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. A Delta-backed state object is required, since the collective progress must be shared and queryable across multiple users and worker processes, survive restarts, and not depend on any single conversation's context window remaining intact.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scenario requires state with four properties:\n\n- Shared across different team members and sessions.\n\n- Accessible by multiple worker processes.\n\n- Durable across process restarts.\n\n- Queryable independently of any single conversation.\n\nAn in-context scratchpad is tied to a particular agent invocation or conversation thread. It is useful for temporary reasoning within that thread, but it cannot serve as the authoritative record for multi-day, multi-user research.\n\nA Delta-backed state object provides external, persistent storage for findings, checked sources, assignments, and unresolved questions. Any authorized worker or team member can read the latest collective progress and continue from it.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe competitive-intelligence work is shared workflow state, not merely conversational context.\n\nA Delta-backed object could store records such as:\n\n```\ncompetitor_idresearch_threadfindingsource_urlsource_statusopen_questionassigned_researcherprogress_statusupdated_atupdated_by\n```\n\nThis allows the system to:\n\n- Resume research after a worker restarts.\n\n- Let another worker continue a competitor’s thread.\n\n- Prevent unnecessary repetition of already-checked sources.\n\n- Query outstanding questions across all competitors.\n\n- Maintain an auditable history of findings.\n\n- Coordinate work without depending on a specific model context window.\n\nDelta Lake provides durable, transactional tables that support concurrent access without making one worker process the sole owner of the state. The core requirement is externalized, durable, shared state, which option B correctly identifies.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. An in-context scratchpad is sufficient, since each team member's own session can regenerate any lost state by re-reading the original sources from scratch.\n\nA scratchpad is limited to the context of the session in which it exists. Other users and workers cannot reliably query it, and it may be lost when the process or conversation ends.\n\nRe-reading every source after lost state would also:\n\n- Duplicate research work.\n\n- Increase model and retrieval costs.\n\n- Lose prior judgments and unresolved questions.\n\n- Make collaboration inconsistent.\n\n- Fail to preserve progress across workers.\n\nRegeneration is not a substitute for persistent shared state.\n\nC. Neither approach is needed; the task should rely entirely on the model's parametric knowledge to track collective progress without external state.\n\nA model’s parametric knowledge does not contain the team’s newly discovered findings, checked-source list, current assignments, or open questions.\n\nIt also cannot be updated reliably with each team member’s research progress during inference. Task-specific evolving state must be stored externally.\n\nD. An in-context scratchpad is preferable because it keeps state changes visible to the model on every turn without requiring any additional read/write calls to external storage.\n\nA scratchpad can be useful for temporary reasoning within one active thread, but it does not satisfy the cross-session and cross-process requirements.\n\nKeeping all accumulated research in every prompt would also eventually create:\n\n- Excessive token usage.\n\n- Context-window pressure.\n\n- Reduced relevance.\n\n- Conflicting copies of state across sessions.\n\n- No single authoritative version.\n\nThe agent can load the relevant portion of the durable state into context when needed; the complete state does not need to remain in every prompt.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is confusing working context with durable workflow state.\n\nAn in-context scratchpad is appropriate for temporary reasoning during one execution. It is not sufficient when information must be shared, queried, updated, and recovered across multiple sessions and processes.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA strong implementation can use both mechanisms:\n\n- In-context scratchpad: Temporary reasoning and planning for the current research step.\n\n- Persistent shared store: Authoritative findings, source status, assignments, open questions, and checkpoints.\n\nFor an analytically queryable research ledger, a Unity Catalog-managed Delta table is appropriate because it provides governance, durability, transactional writes, and shared SQL access.\n\nFor very frequent, low-latency, transactional agent checkpoints, Databricks also supports Lakebase as a durable operational state backend. That is an implementation consideration beyond the choices presented. The exam distinction remains that this workload requires external persistent shared state, making B the correct answer.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- What are ACID guarantees on Databricks?\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/acid](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/acid)\n\n- Agent state and memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)\n\n- Agent memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/stateful-agents](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/stateful-agents)\n\n- Self-managed agent memory with Lakebase\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory)","source_option_explanations":["A. An in-context scratchpad is sufficient, since each team member's own session can regenerate any lost state by re-reading the original sources from scratch.\n\nA scratchpad is limited to the context of the session in which it exists. Other users and workers cannot reliably query it, and it may be lost when the process or conversation ends.\n\nRe-reading every source after lost state would also:\n\n- Duplicate research work.\n\n- Increase model and retrieval costs.\n\n- Lose prior judgments and unresolved questions.\n\n- Make collaboration inconsistent.\n\n- Fail to preserve progress across workers.\n\nRegeneration is not a substitute for persistent shared state.","Correct Answer\n\nB. A Delta-backed state object is required, since the collective progress must be shared and queryable across multiple users and worker processes, survive restarts, and not depend on any single conversation's context window remaining intact.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scenario requires state with four properties:\n\n- Shared across different team members and sessions.\n\n- Accessible by multiple worker processes.\n\n- Durable across process restarts.\n\n- Queryable independently of any single conversation.\n\nAn in-context scratchpad is tied to a particular agent invocation or conversation thread. It is useful for temporary reasoning within that thread, but it cannot serve as the authoritative record for multi-day, multi-user research.\n\nA Delta-backed state object provides external, persistent storage for findings, checked sources, assignments, and unresolved questions. Any authorized worker or team member can read the latest collective progress and continue from it.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe competitive-intelligence work is shared workflow state, not merely conversational context.\n\nA Delta-backed object could store records such as:\n\n```\ncompetitor_idresearch_threadfindingsource_urlsource_statusopen_questionassigned_researcherprogress_statusupdated_atupdated_by\n```\n\nThis allows the system to:\n\n- Resume research after a worker restarts.\n\n- Let another worker continue a competitor’s thread.\n\n- Prevent unnecessary repetition of already-checked sources.\n\n- Query outstanding questions across all competitors.\n\n- Maintain an auditable history of findings.\n\n- Coordinate work without depending on a specific model context window.\n\nDelta Lake provides durable, transactional tables that support concurrent access without making one worker process the sole owner of the state. The core requirement is externalized, durable, shared state, which option B correctly identifies.","C. Neither approach is needed; the task should rely entirely on the model's parametric knowledge to track collective progress without external state.\n\nA model’s parametric knowledge does not contain the team’s newly discovered findings, checked-source list, current assignments, or open questions.\n\nIt also cannot be updated reliably with each team member’s research progress during inference. Task-specific evolving state must be stored externally.","D. An in-context scratchpad is preferable because it keeps state changes visible to the model on every turn without requiring any additional read/write calls to external storage.\n\nA scratchpad can be useful for temporary reasoning within one active thread, but it does not satisfy the cross-session and cross-process requirements.\n\nKeeping all accumulated research in every prompt would also eventually create:\n\n- Excessive token usage.\n\n- Context-window pressure.\n\n- Reduced relevance.\n\n- Conflicting copies of state across sessions.\n\n- No single authoritative version.\n\nThe agent can load the relevant portion of the durable state into context when needed; the complete state does not need to remain in every prompt."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/acid","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/stateful-agents","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory"],"source_hash":"a2639ceaf11390ce46abff11b27f030ede02ac4d07eb3cd5be993fd36738200c","stem_ja":"複数日にわたる競合調査エージェントが、異なるメンバーが数日かけて実行する、多数の並行した調査スレッドで、複数の競合に関する知見を追跡します。何を発見したか、どの情報源を確認したか、未解決の問いは何かという全体の進捗を、どのメンバーのセッションからもいつでも照会できる必要があります。プロセスの再起動後も保持され、別々のワーカープロセスが、異なる時点で各競合の調査を引き継ぐこともあります。\n\nこの全体進捗の追跡に必要な方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["各メンバーのセッションが元の情報源を最初から読み直して失われた状態を再生成できるため、コンテキスト内の作業メモで十分である。","全体の進捗は複数ユーザー・ワーカー間で共有・照会でき、再起動に耐え、1つの会話のコンテキストウィンドウに依存してはならないため、Deltaを基盤とする状態オブジェクトが必要である。","どちらも不要であり、外部状態を使わず、モデルが学習済みパラメーターに持つ知識だけで全体進捗を追跡すべきである。","外部ストレージへの追加の読み書き呼び出しなしに、すべてのターンで状態の変更をモデルへ見せられるため、コンテキスト内の作業メモの方がよい。"],"explanation_ja":"会話内の作業メモは、その会話が続く間の一時情報です。この問題では複数ユーザー・複数プロセスが進捗を引き継ぎ、再起動しても状態を失わない必要があるため、共有可能な永続ストレージが必要です。選択肢ではDelta-backed stateが適します。覚え方は「引き継ぐ進捗は、会話の外へ保存」です。","distractors_ja":["読み直しによる再生成は、常時共有や再開可能な進捗の保存という要件を満たしません。","会話やプロセスの寿命から独立した、共有・照会可能な進捗を保持できます。","学習済みパラメーターには、この実行で新たに得た進捗が自動的に書き込まれるわけではありません。","読み書き回数は減っても、別セッションからの参照や再起動後の復旧を保証できません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-023","number":23,"form":2,"stem":"An HR onboarding-assistant agent stores structured records (start date, department, manager, keyed by employee ID) and free-form notes summarizing past onboarding conversations (e.g., \"new hire expressed relocation stress about the move; HR partner recommended the relocation-assistance benefit\") in Lakebase. At query time, the agent needs to answer (a) \"what is this new hire's start date?\" and (b) \"has any new hire raised concerns similar to what this person is describing about relocation stress, and how were they addressed?\"\n\nWhich retrieval mechanism is appropriate for each query type?","options":["Both queries should use structured SQL queries only, since Lakebase is a relational Postgres store and does not support any form of semantic retrieval.","Query (a) should use Databricks AI Search since a start date is unstructured text; query (b) should use a structured SQL query since past concerns are stored as one row per employee.","Query (a) should use a structured SQL query against the Lakebase table by employee ID, since the answer is a single known field; query (b) should use Databricks AI Search / semantic retrieval over the free-form conversation notes, since it requires matching a described situation to conceptually related past notes rather than an exact key match.","Both queries should use Databricks AI Search over the Lakebase-persisted memory, since semantic search is universally more accurate than direct queries."],"correct":2,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"厳密な人事データと似た相談の検索","concept":"ce-f2-q023","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Query (a) should use a structured SQL query against the Lakebase table by employee ID, since the answer is a single known field; query (b) should use Databricks AI Search / semantic retrieval over the free-form conversation notes, since it requires matching a described situation to conceptually related past notes rather than an exact key match.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe two questions require different retrieval behaviors:\n\n- Query (a) requests an exact attribute for a known entity: the employee’s `start_date`, keyed by `employee_id`.\n\n- Query (b) asks for conceptually similar experiences across free-form narrative notes, even when those notes may use different wording.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- Use a deterministic structured query for the known field.\n\n- Use semantic retrieval for similarity matching over unstructured notes.\n\nThis is a hybrid retrieval design in which the retrieval mechanism is selected according to the structure of the data and the type of question.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nFor query (a), the agent knows both the target entity and the required column. A direct SQL query is precise and efficient:\n\n```\nSELECT start_dateFROM employee_onboardingWHERE employee_id = :employee_id;\n```\n\nSemantic search would add unnecessary approximation to an exact lookup. The agent does not need records that are conceptually similar to the employee; it needs one authoritative field from one identified row.\n\nFor query (b), exact key lookup is insufficient. The user is asking whether other employees described a similar situation, not whether their notes contain an exact phrase such as “relocation stress.”\n\nRelated notes might contain wording such as:\n\n- “Anxious about moving to a new city”\n\n- “Concerned about relocation expenses”\n\n- “Family is struggling with the transfer”\n\n- “Asked about moving support”\n\nSemantic retrieval can identify these conceptually related notes even when their vocabulary differs. The retrieved records can then show how HR addressed the concerns, such as recommending relocation assistance.\n\nThe overall pattern is:\n\nQueryData characteristicBest retrievalStart date for employee 123Exact structured field and known keySQL lookupSimilar relocation concernsFree-form language and conceptual similaritySemantic retrieval\n\nIf the notes remain directly in Lakebase, semantic retrieval can also be implemented using Lakebase Search where available. If Databricks AI Search is used, the notes must be made available through an appropriate indexed data pipeline. The key exam distinction remains exact structured retrieval versus semantic similarity retrieval.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Both queries should use structured SQL queries only, since Lakebase is a relational Postgres store and does not support any form of semantic retrieval.\n\nThe first part is appropriate for the start-date lookup, but the claim that Lakebase cannot support semantic retrieval is incorrect.\n\nMore importantly, ordinary SQL filtering alone is not the best mechanism for finding conceptually similar narratives when they use different words. Keyword conditions such as `LIKE '%relocation stress%'` could miss semantically related notes.\n\nB. Query (a) should use Databricks AI Search since a start date is unstructured text; query (b) should use a structured SQL query since past concerns are stored as one row per employee.\n\nThis reverses the appropriate mechanisms.\n\nA start date is a structured field, not unstructured text. It should be retrieved by employee ID through SQL.\n\nAlthough each note may be stored in a relational row, its contents are still free-form text. Physical storage in a table does not make conceptual similarity an exact structured lookup problem.\n\nD. Both queries should use Databricks AI Search over the Lakebase-persisted memory, since semantic search is universally more accurate than direct queries.\n\nSemantic retrieval is not universally more accurate.\n\nFor a known employee ID and a single structured field, SQL provides an exact answer. Vector similarity search could return approximate neighbors, introduce ranking behavior, and add unnecessary cost and complexity.\n\nSemantic retrieval should be used where meaning-based matching is required—not as a replacement for deterministic database queries.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is confusing the storage system with the retrieval method.\n\nFree-form notes can be stored in a relational table but still benefit from semantic retrieval. Conversely, a structured field such as `start_date` should not be retrieved semantically merely because an AI agent is making the request.\n\nChoose retrieval based on the question:\n\n- Exact key and field → structured query\n\n- Similar meaning across narrative text → semantic retrieval\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production agent can expose two narrowly scoped tools:\n\n```\nget_employee_start_date(employee_id)search_similar_onboarding_concerns(description, top_k)\n```\n\nThe structured tool should return only the requested employee fields. The semantic tool should return relevant notes with governed metadata such as:\n\n- Employee or anonymized case identifier\n\n- Concern category\n\n- Date\n\n- Resolution or benefit recommended\n\n- Similarity score\n\nBecause HR notes may contain sensitive personal information, semantic results should also enforce authorization, purpose limitation, tenant or department boundaries, and appropriate redaction. The LLM should never receive a broader set of employee notes than the requesting user is authorized to access.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Lakebase Postgres\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/)\n\n- Lakebase Search\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/lakebase-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/lakebase-search)\n\n- Databricks AI Search\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search)\n\n- Create AI agent tools using Unity Catalog functions\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/generative-ai/agent-framework/create-custom-tool](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/generative-ai/agent-framework/create-custom-tool)","source_option_explanations":["A. Both queries should use structured SQL queries only, since Lakebase is a relational Postgres store and does not support any form of semantic retrieval.\n\nThe first part is appropriate for the start-date lookup, but the claim that Lakebase cannot support semantic retrieval is incorrect.\n\nMore importantly, ordinary SQL filtering alone is not the best mechanism for finding conceptually similar narratives when they use different words. Keyword conditions such as `LIKE '%relocation stress%'` could miss semantically related notes.","B. Query (a) should use Databricks AI Search since a start date is unstructured text; query (b) should use a structured SQL query since past concerns are stored as one row per employee.\n\nThis reverses the appropriate mechanisms.\n\nA start date is a structured field, not unstructured text. It should be retrieved by employee ID through SQL.\n\nAlthough each note may be stored in a relational row, its contents are still free-form text. Physical storage in a table does not make conceptual similarity an exact structured lookup problem.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Query (a) should use a structured SQL query against the Lakebase table by employee ID, since the answer is a single known field; query (b) should use Databricks AI Search / semantic retrieval over the free-form conversation notes, since it requires matching a described situation to conceptually related past notes rather than an exact key match.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe two questions require different retrieval behaviors:\n\n- Query (a) requests an exact attribute for a known entity: the employee’s `start_date`, keyed by `employee_id`.\n\n- Query (b) asks for conceptually similar experiences across free-form narrative notes, even when those notes may use different wording.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- Use a deterministic structured query for the known field.\n\n- Use semantic retrieval for similarity matching over unstructured notes.\n\nThis is a hybrid retrieval design in which the retrieval mechanism is selected according to the structure of the data and the type of question.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nFor query (a), the agent knows both the target entity and the required column. A direct SQL query is precise and efficient:\n\n```\nSELECT start_dateFROM employee_onboardingWHERE employee_id = :employee_id;\n```\n\nSemantic search would add unnecessary approximation to an exact lookup. The agent does not need records that are conceptually similar to the employee; it needs one authoritative field from one identified row.\n\nFor query (b), exact key lookup is insufficient. The user is asking whether other employees described a similar situation, not whether their notes contain an exact phrase such as “relocation stress.”\n\nRelated notes might contain wording such as:\n\n- “Anxious about moving to a new city”\n\n- “Concerned about relocation expenses”\n\n- “Family is struggling with the transfer”\n\n- “Asked about moving support”\n\nSemantic retrieval can identify these conceptually related notes even when their vocabulary differs. The retrieved records can then show how HR addressed the concerns, such as recommending relocation assistance.\n\nThe overall pattern is:\n\nQueryData characteristicBest retrievalStart date for employee 123Exact structured field and known keySQL lookupSimilar relocation concernsFree-form language and conceptual similaritySemantic retrieval\n\nIf the notes remain directly in Lakebase, semantic retrieval can also be implemented using Lakebase Search where available. If Databricks AI Search is used, the notes must be made available through an appropriate indexed data pipeline. The key exam distinction remains exact structured retrieval versus semantic similarity retrieval.","D. Both queries should use Databricks AI Search over the Lakebase-persisted memory, since semantic search is universally more accurate than direct queries.\n\nSemantic retrieval is not universally more accurate.\n\nFor a known employee ID and a single structured field, SQL provides an exact answer. Vector similarity search could return approximate neighbors, introduce ranking behavior, and add unnecessary cost and complexity.\n\nSemantic retrieval should be used where meaning-based matching is required—not as a replacement for deterministic database queries."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/lakebase-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/generative-ai/agent-framework/create-custom-tool"],"source_hash":"8be2d7b15dda13f2ab063da694ed2d2e6ad5ba63eaed7ef65378ecf7a6890685","stem_ja":"人事の入社支援エージェントが、従業員IDをキーとする入社日・部署・上司などの構造化レコードと、過去の入社手続きの会話を要約した自由記述メモをLakebaseへ保存しています。メモには「新入社員が引っ越しのストレスを訴え、人事担当者が転居支援制度を勧めた」といった内容があります。質問時、エージェントは(a)「この新入社員の入社日はいつか」と、(b)「この人が話している転居ストレスに似た不安を訴えた新入社員は過去にいるか、どう対応したか」へ答える必要があります。\n\nそれぞれに適した取得方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["LakebaseはリレーショナルなPostgresストアで、意味検索を一切利用できないため、両方とも構造化SQLクエリだけを使う。","入社日は非構造テキストなので(a)にはDatabricks AI Searchを使い、過去の不安は従業員ごとに1行で保存されるため(b)には構造化SQLを使う。","(a)は既知の1フィールドが答えなので、従業員IDでLakebaseテーブルを構造化SQL検索する。(b)は正確なキー一致ではなく、説明された状況に概念的に関連する過去の相談を探すため、自由記述の会話メモに対するDatabricks AI Search／意味検索を使う。","意味検索は直接照会より普遍的に正確なので、両方ともLakebaseに永続化した記憶に対するDatabricks AI Searchを使う。"],"explanation_ja":"特定の従業員の入社日は、IDで1列を正確に取るSQLが適します。一方、「似た転居の不安」は表現が違っても意味が近い相談を探すため、意味検索が適します。覚え方は「正確なキーと値はSQL、似た内容は意味検索」。Lakebaseでの保存と、検索のための索引・仕組みは区別します。","distractors_ja":["構造化SQLだけでは、異なる表現を使った似た相談の概念的な照合に向きません。","入社日は構造化された既知のフィールドであり、両者に適した検索方法を逆にしています。","厳密なフィールド取得と、自然言語での類似状況探索を適切に使い分けています。","意味検索は近さを順位付けする方法で、既知のIDから日付を正確に取る照会より常に優れるわけではありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-024","number":24,"form":2,"stem":"A context engineer runs three configurations of a code-review agent in MLflow 3, varying which static-analysis tool outputs are included in context: linter output only; linter plus type-checker; linter plus type-checker plus test-coverage report. Each run logs precision, recall, and latency_score (higher = faster). The team wants the configuration that performs reliably across all three metrics, not one that excels on one while lagging on another.\n\nResults: Config 1 (linter only) has by far the best latency_score but noticeably the worst precision. Config 3 (all three tools) has the best precision but by far the worst latency_score, well below the other two. Config 2 (linter + type-checker) is not the top score on any single metric, but is close to the best on all three, with no metric far behind the others.\n\nWhich configuration should the engineer select as most reliable, and why?","options":["Config 1, since its latency advantage is the largest single-metric gap observed across all runs.","Config 3, since its precision advantage matters more than the other two metrics for a code-review use case.","Average the three raw metrics for each configuration and select whichever has the highest arithmetic mean.","Config 2, since comparing all three metrics together shows it has no metric significantly behind the others, while Configs 1 and 3 each lead on one metric only by sacrificing another."],"correct":3,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"精度・再現率・速さのバランス","concept":"ce-f2-q024","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Config 2, since comparing all three metrics together shows it has no metric significantly behind the others, while Configs 1 and 3 each lead on one metric only by sacrificing another.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe team wants the configuration that performs reliably across precision, recall, and latency, rather than maximizing one metric at the expense of another.\n\nThe results show:\n\n- Config 1 optimizes latency but has the weakest precision.\n\n- Config 3 optimizes precision but has substantially worse latency.\n\n- Config 2 remains close to the best result on all three metrics and has no major weakness.\n\nConfig 2 is therefore the most balanced and reliable configuration. It represents the best trade-off across the complete evaluation objective rather than winning a single isolated metric.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA reliable production configuration should meet acceptable performance thresholds across every metric that matters to the use case.\n\nConfig 2 provides:\n\n- Precision close to the strongest configuration.\n\n- Recall close to the strongest configuration.\n\n- Latency close to the fastest configuration.\n\n- No severe degradation in any evaluated dimension.\n\nBy contrast, Configs 1 and 3 are extreme configurations:\n\n- Config 1 gains speed by sacrificing review precision.\n\n- Config 3 gains precision by adding enough context to cause a major latency penalty.\n\nThe additional type-checker output in Config 2 appears to provide useful incremental context without the substantial overhead introduced by the test-coverage report. It therefore occupies the strongest balanced position among the three candidates.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Config 1, since its latency advantage is the largest single-metric gap observed across all runs.\n\nThis selects a configuration based on one metric even though the team explicitly wants reliable performance across all three.\n\nIts poor precision means the agent may generate more false-positive review findings or incorrectly identify code issues. The size of its latency advantage does not compensate for a significant weakness in another required metric unless the team has explicitly declared latency to be the overriding objective—which it has not.\n\nB. Config 3, since its precision advantage matters more than the other two metrics for a code-review use case.\n\nThe scenario does not state that precision has greater priority than recall and latency.\n\nConfig 3 has the strongest precision, but its latency score is far below the alternatives. Selecting it would impose a substantial performance penalty for an improvement in only one dimension.\n\nThis could be justified under a separately defined policy that prioritizes precision regardless of latency, but that is not the stated requirement.\n\nC. Average the three raw metrics for each configuration and select whichever has the highest arithmetic mean.\n\nA simple arithmetic mean is not automatically a valid way to combine different metrics.\n\nIt assumes that:\n\n- All metrics are on directly comparable scales.\n\n- Equal differences have equivalent practical value.\n\n- All three metrics should receive identical weights.\n\n- Strong performance in one metric may compensate for severe weakness in another.\n\nThose assumptions have not been established. An average could hide a major deficiency—for example, excellent precision offsetting unacceptable latency.\n\nBecause the team wants no metric to lag substantially, direct multi-metric comparison or explicit minimum thresholds is more appropriate than blindly averaging raw values.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is selecting the configuration with the highest individual score rather than evaluating the overall performance profile.\n\nA configuration can win one metric while still being unreliable because it performs poorly elsewhere. The question specifically directs attention to balanced performance across all three dimensions.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nIn production, the team should define acceptable thresholds before making the final selection, for example:\n\n- Precision must exceed the required minimum.\n\n- Recall must remain above the required coverage threshold.\n\n- Latency must remain within the interactive response objective.\n\nConfigurations that fail any mandatory threshold should be excluded. The remaining configurations can then be compared using business-defined priorities.\n\nA Pareto-style comparison is also useful:\n\n- Config 1 offers speed but sacrifices precision.\n\n- Config 3 offers precision but sacrifices speed.\n\n- Config 2 provides the most balanced operating point.\n\nThe team should also inspect trace-level results rather than relying only on aggregate means, especially for security-sensitive findings, complex code paths, and cases where the type checker or coverage report materially changes the review.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Tutorial: Evaluate and improve a GenAI application\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app)\n\n- Evaluate and monitor AI agents\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor)\n\n- MLflow 3 for GenAI\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/)\n\n- Building MLflow evaluation datasets\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset)","source_option_explanations":["A. Config 1, since its latency advantage is the largest single-metric gap observed across all runs.\n\nThis selects a configuration based on one metric even though the team explicitly wants reliable performance across all three.\n\nIts poor precision means the agent may generate more false-positive review findings or incorrectly identify code issues. The size of its latency advantage does not compensate for a significant weakness in another required metric unless the team has explicitly declared latency to be the overriding objective—which it has not.","B. Config 3, since its precision advantage matters more than the other two metrics for a code-review use case.\n\nThe scenario does not state that precision has greater priority than recall and latency.\n\nConfig 3 has the strongest precision, but its latency score is far below the alternatives. Selecting it would impose a substantial performance penalty for an improvement in only one dimension.\n\nThis could be justified under a separately defined policy that prioritizes precision regardless of latency, but that is not the stated requirement.","C. Average the three raw metrics for each configuration and select whichever has the highest arithmetic mean.\n\nA simple arithmetic mean is not automatically a valid way to combine different metrics.\n\nIt assumes that:\n\n- All metrics are on directly comparable scales.\n\n- Equal differences have equivalent practical value.\n\n- All three metrics should receive identical weights.\n\n- Strong performance in one metric may compensate for severe weakness in another.\n\nThose assumptions have not been established. An average could hide a major deficiency—for example, excellent precision offsetting unacceptable latency.\n\nBecause the team wants no metric to lag substantially, direct multi-metric comparison or explicit minimum thresholds is more appropriate than blindly averaging raw values.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Config 2, since comparing all three metrics together shows it has no metric significantly behind the others, while Configs 1 and 3 each lead on one metric only by sacrificing another.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe team wants the configuration that performs reliably across precision, recall, and latency, rather than maximizing one metric at the expense of another.\n\nThe results show:\n\n- Config 1 optimizes latency but has the weakest precision.\n\n- Config 3 optimizes precision but has substantially worse latency.\n\n- Config 2 remains close to the best result on all three metrics and has no major weakness.\n\nConfig 2 is therefore the most balanced and reliable configuration. It represents the best trade-off across the complete evaluation objective rather than winning a single isolated metric.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA reliable production configuration should meet acceptable performance thresholds across every metric that matters to the use case.\n\nConfig 2 provides:\n\n- Precision close to the strongest configuration.\n\n- Recall close to the strongest configuration.\n\n- Latency close to the fastest configuration.\n\n- No severe degradation in any evaluated dimension.\n\nBy contrast, Configs 1 and 3 are extreme configurations:\n\n- Config 1 gains speed by sacrificing review precision.\n\n- Config 3 gains precision by adding enough context to cause a major latency penalty.\n\nThe additional type-checker output in Config 2 appears to provide useful incremental context without the substantial overhead introduced by the test-coverage report. It therefore occupies the strongest balanced position among the three candidates."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset"],"source_hash":"ed08db4deffbd53925c05263d81dd8aada2737d0c47cad063d3c716e11051d44","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアがMLflow 3で、コードレビューエージェントの3構成を実行します。コンテキストに含める静的解析結果を、リンターのみ、リンター＋型チェッカー、リンター＋型チェッカー＋テストカバレッジ報告書と変えています。各実行ではprecision、recall、latency_score（高いほど高速）を記録します。チームは1指標で優れ、別の指標で劣るものではなく、3指標すべてで安定した構成を求めています。\n\n構成1（リンターのみ）はlatency_scoreが突出してよい一方、precisionが明らかに最悪です。構成3（全3ツール）はprecisionが最高ですが、latency_scoreが他の2つより大きく劣ります。構成2（リンター＋型チェッカー）はどの指標も単独首位ではありませんが、3つとも最高値に近く、極端に劣る指標がありません。\n\n最も信頼できる構成としてどれを選ぶべきですか。その理由も選んでください。","options_ja":["全実行の単一指標の差として、速さの優位性が最も大きいため、構成1。","コードレビューではprecisionが他の2指標より重要であるため、構成3。","各構成の3指標の生の値を平均し、算術平均が最も高いものを選ぶ。","3指標を一緒に比較すると、大きく劣るものがなく、構成1と3はそれぞれ別の指標を犠牲にして1指標で首位になっているため、構成2。"],"explanation_ja":"要件は「全指標で安定」であり、最速や最高precisionだけを求めていません。構成2には大きな弱点がないため、この条件で最適です。尺度や意味の違う生のスコアを無条件に平均することも避けます。覚え方は「要件がバランスなら、突出値より最低限を満たすかを見る」です。","distractors_ja":["速さだけを優先し、precisionが最悪という欠点を無視しているため、全指標で安定という要件に合いません。","この問題ではprecisionの最優先は指定されず、極端な遅さも避ける必要があります。","尺度や許容範囲が違う指標の生の平均では、大きな弱点が隠れる可能性があります。","3指標のどれも大きく劣らず、要求される信頼性のバランスに最も合っています。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-025","number":25,"form":2,"stem":"A compliance-checklist agent evaluates each submitted document against a fixed, small set of exactly 12 regulatory requirements stored in Lakebase. In virtually every session, all 12 requirements are checked against the submission — none are skipped — and the requirement set itself changes only a few times per year via a formal review process. The team is choosing between loading all 12 requirements once at the start of each session (static) versus querying Lakebase separately for each requirement as the agent evaluates it (dynamic).\n\nWhich retrieval approach is better suited to this scenario, and why?","options":["Static retrieval is better suited here, since virtually all 12 requirements are needed on every single call and the set rarely changes, so loading once avoids 12 repeated round-trips per submission with no meaningful freshness benefit from querying dynamically.","Dynamic retrieval is always better regardless of scenario specifics, since querying on demand is inherently more efficient than loading anything up front.","Static retrieval is better because Lakebase cannot support repeated per-field queries within a single session.","Dynamic retrieval is better suited here, since fetching each requirement individually lets the agent skip requirements it doesn't think are relevant."],"correct":0,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"毎回必要な小さな情報集合の静的取得","concept":"ce-f2-q025","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Static retrieval is better suited here, since virtually all 12 requirements are needed on every single call and the set rarely changes, so loading once avoids 12 repeated round-trips per submission with no meaningful freshness benefit from querying dynamically.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe choice between static and dynamic retrieval depends on how much of the source data is normally required and how frequently that data changes.\n\nThis scenario strongly favors static retrieval because:\n\n- The requirement set is small—exactly 12 items.\n\n- All 12 requirements are evaluated in virtually every session.\n\n- The requirements change only a few times per year.\n\n- Retrieving requirements individually would require repeated database calls without reducing the amount of context ultimately used.\n\nLoading the complete requirement set once at the beginning of the session provides the agent with all necessary compliance criteria while avoiding 12 separate Lakebase round-trips.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nStatic retrieval is appropriate when the context is:\n\n- Small enough to fit comfortably in the model’s context.\n\n- Predictably required for nearly every request.\n\n- Relatively stable.\n\n- More efficient to retrieve in one batch than through repeated calls.\n\nA single structured query could retrieve all active requirements:\n\n```\nSELECT    requirement_id,    requirement_text,    evaluation_criteria,    effective_versionFROM regulatory_requirementsWHERE active = TRUEORDER BY requirement_id;\n```\n\nThe result can then be placed into the session context once and reused as the agent checks the submitted document.\n\nDynamic retrieval is most valuable when only a small, unpredictable subset of a large or rapidly changing dataset is needed. Here, no meaningful selection is occurring because the agent almost always needs every requirement.\n\nThe team should still associate the retrieved set with a version or effective date. When a formal regulatory update occurs, subsequent sessions can load the newly approved version.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Dynamic retrieval is always better regardless of scenario specifics, since querying on demand is inherently more efficient than loading anything up front.\n\nThis is an invalid absolute rule.\n\nDynamic retrieval is not inherently more efficient. In this case, it would retrieve all 12 requirements anyway, but through 12 separate operations instead of one batched query.\n\nThat introduces:\n\n- Additional database round-trips.\n\n- Greater cumulative latency.\n\n- More tool-call overhead.\n\n- More opportunities for transient failures.\n\n- More complex traces and orchestration.\n\nRetrieval design must match the workload rather than defaulting universally to dynamic access.\n\nC. Static retrieval is better because Lakebase cannot support repeated per-field queries within a single session.\n\nThe conclusion is correct, but the justification is false.\n\nLakebase is a managed PostgreSQL-compatible operational database and can support repeated SQL queries. Nothing prevents the agent from querying individual requirements separately.\n\nThe issue is not platform capability. It is that repeated queries would be unnecessary and inefficient for this particular access pattern.\n\nD. Dynamic retrieval is better suited here, since fetching each requirement individually lets the agent skip requirements it doesn't think are relevant.\n\nThe scenario explicitly states that virtually all 12 requirements must be checked and none are normally skipped.\n\nAllowing the model to decide that a regulatory requirement is irrelevant could also undermine the completeness of the compliance review. The required checklist should generally be applied deterministically rather than allowing the agent to omit requirements based on its own judgment.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is assuming that dynamic retrieval is always more efficient because it retrieves information “only when needed.”\n\nThat reasoning fails when:\n\n- Nearly every item is always needed.\n\n- The dataset is small.\n\n- The data changes infrequently.\n\n- Dynamic access merely divides one batch retrieval into numerous smaller calls.\n\nThe retrieval strategy should minimize unnecessary context and unnecessary calls—not maximize dynamism.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production implementation should load the complete active checklist once per evaluation session and preserve its version alongside the result:\n\n```\nChecklist version: 2026.2Requirements evaluated: 12Effective date: 2026-07-01Result: 10 passed, 2 failed\n```\n\nThe system should also:\n\n- Retrieve only formally approved and active requirements.\n\n- Cache the checklist for the duration of the session or evaluation.\n\n- Invalidate the cache when a new approved version is published.\n\n- Record which checklist version was applied.\n\n- Require all 12 requirement IDs to have an outcome before marking the evaluation complete.\n\nDynamic retrieval would become more appropriate if the checklist grew to thousands of requirements and only a small subset applied based on jurisdiction, document type, industry, or effective date.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Lakebase Postgres\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/)\n\n- Create agent tools using Unity Catalog functions\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool)\n\n- RAG on Databricks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation)\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Static retrieval is better suited here, since virtually all 12 requirements are needed on every single call and the set rarely changes, so loading once avoids 12 repeated round-trips per submission with no meaningful freshness benefit from querying dynamically.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe choice between static and dynamic retrieval depends on how much of the source data is normally required and how frequently that data changes.\n\nThis scenario strongly favors static retrieval because:\n\n- The requirement set is small—exactly 12 items.\n\n- All 12 requirements are evaluated in virtually every session.\n\n- The requirements change only a few times per year.\n\n- Retrieving requirements individually would require repeated database calls without reducing the amount of context ultimately used.\n\nLoading the complete requirement set once at the beginning of the session provides the agent with all necessary compliance criteria while avoiding 12 separate Lakebase round-trips.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nStatic retrieval is appropriate when the context is:\n\n- Small enough to fit comfortably in the model’s context.\n\n- Predictably required for nearly every request.\n\n- Relatively stable.\n\n- More efficient to retrieve in one batch than through repeated calls.\n\nA single structured query could retrieve all active requirements:\n\n```\nSELECT    requirement_id,    requirement_text,    evaluation_criteria,    effective_versionFROM regulatory_requirementsWHERE active = TRUEORDER BY requirement_id;\n```\n\nThe result can then be placed into the session context once and reused as the agent checks the submitted document.\n\nDynamic retrieval is most valuable when only a small, unpredictable subset of a large or rapidly changing dataset is needed. Here, no meaningful selection is occurring because the agent almost always needs every requirement.\n\nThe team should still associate the retrieved set with a version or effective date. When a formal regulatory update occurs, subsequent sessions can load the newly approved version.","B. Dynamic retrieval is always better regardless of scenario specifics, since querying on demand is inherently more efficient than loading anything up front.\n\nThis is an invalid absolute rule.\n\nDynamic retrieval is not inherently more efficient. In this case, it would retrieve all 12 requirements anyway, but through 12 separate operations instead of one batched query.\n\nThat introduces:\n\n- Additional database round-trips.\n\n- Greater cumulative latency.\n\n- More tool-call overhead.\n\n- More opportunities for transient failures.\n\n- More complex traces and orchestration.\n\nRetrieval design must match the workload rather than defaulting universally to dynamic access.","C. Static retrieval is better because Lakebase cannot support repeated per-field queries within a single session.\n\nThe conclusion is correct, but the justification is false.\n\nLakebase is a managed PostgreSQL-compatible operational database and can support repeated SQL queries. Nothing prevents the agent from querying individual requirements separately.\n\nThe issue is not platform capability. It is that repeated queries would be unnecessary and inefficient for this particular access pattern.","D. Dynamic retrieval is better suited here, since fetching each requirement individually lets the agent skip requirements it doesn't think are relevant.\n\nThe scenario explicitly states that virtually all 12 requirements must be checked and none are normally skipped.\n\nAllowing the model to decide that a regulatory requirement is irrelevant could also undermine the completeness of the compliance review. The required checklist should generally be applied deterministically rather than allowing the agent to omit requirements based on its own judgment."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns"],"source_hash":"88d6c9bb5b50ee312efa30602ba838bad9d1a205a48571198a00c5ae97b29408","stem_ja":"コンプライアンス確認エージェントが、提出文書をLakebase内の固定された小さな12個の規制要件に照らして評価します。ほぼすべてのセッションで12個すべてを確認し、省略される要件はありません。要件自体の変更は、正式な審査を通じて年に数回だけです。チームは、各セッション開始時に12個を一度に読み込む静的取得と、評価するたびに各要件を個別にLakebaseへ問い合わせる動的取得を比較しています。\n\nこの状況により適した取得方法はどれですか。その理由も選んでください。","options_ja":["ほぼ毎回12個すべてが必要で、内容もまれにしか変わらないため、静的取得が適する。一度読み込めば、動的照会による実質的な鮮度上の利益なしに、文書ごとに12回往復する負担を避けられる。","必要時に照会する方が何かを先に読むより本質的に効率的なので、個別の状況にかかわらず動的取得が常によい。","Lakebaseは1セッション中にフィールド単位の照会を繰り返せないため、静的取得がよい。","各要件を個別に取得すれば、エージェントが無関係と判断した要件を省略できるため、動的取得が適する。"],"explanation_ja":"小さな集合をほぼ毎回すべて使い、変更もまれなら、最初にまとめて取得する方が合理的です。動的取得の利点は、不要な情報を読まずに済むことや鮮度ですが、この条件では利益が小さく、往復だけ増えます。覚え方は「少量・固定・全部必要なら一括」。必要時検索が常に最良ではありません。","distractors_ja":["必要な情報が常に全件で少量という条件に合い、個別照会の負担を減らします。","常に動的が効率的とは限らず、今回は全部が必要なので選別による節約がありません。","Lakebaseが反復照会できないという制約はなく、静的取得を選ぶ理由が誤っています。","問題は12要件を省略せず確認するため、任意に要件を飛ばす設計は適しません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-026","number":26,"form":2,"stem":"A medical-intake agent retrieves patient history to answer clinical questions. For a question about treating a sprained ankle, it retrieves and prominently references the patient's childhood tonsillectomy from 15 years ago — clearly irrelevant to the current issue — diluting the response with unrelated history. For a separate question about prescribing an antibiotic, it fails to surface a documented penicillin allergy from two years ago, because its retrieval only pulls data from the current visit's intake form.\n\nSelect the two risk patterns present in this memory system.","options":["Over-retrieval / context pollution — irrelevant history (the tonsillectomy) is retrieved and dilutes the response to an unrelated question.","Under-retrieval — relevant history (the penicillin allergy) exists but is missed because retrieval is scoped too narrowly to the current visit only.","Context poisoning — a hallucinated allergy was introduced into the patient's record.","Context clash — the current visit's intake form directly contradicts the childhood tonsillectomy record."],"correct":0,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"記憶の取り過ぎと取り漏れ","concept":"ce-f2-q026","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Over-retrieval / context pollution — irrelevant history (the tonsillectomy) is retrieved and dilutes the response to an unrelated question.\n\nB. Under-retrieval — relevant history (the penicillin allergy) exists but is missed because retrieval is scoped too narrowly to the current visit only.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scenario contains two opposite retrieval failures:\n\n- Too much irrelevant context: The childhood tonsillectomy is retrieved for a sprained-ankle question even though it has no bearing on the current clinical issue.\n\n- Too little relevant context: The documented penicillin allergy is omitted from an antibiotic-prescribing question because retrieval examines only the current visit.\n\nThe correct selections are therefore A and B. The first problem concerns retrieval precision and relevance; the second concerns retrieval coverage and sufficiency.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA — Over-retrieval/context pollution\n\nThe tonsillectomy is valid historical information, but it is irrelevant to treating a sprained ankle. Prominently injecting it into the model’s context:\n\n- Consumes context-window capacity.\n\n- Dilutes clinically useful information.\n\n- Can distract the model’s reasoning.\n\n- Makes the response less focused.\n\nDatabricks’ retrieval-quality guidance emphasizes filtering and relevance because reducing irrelevant candidates improves retrieval precision. MLflow’s `RetrievalRelevance` judge likewise evaluates whether each retrieved document is directly relevant to the user’s request.\n\nB — Under-retrieval\n\nThe penicillin allergy is highly relevant to an antibiotic decision, but the retriever fails to obtain it because its scope is limited to the current intake form.\n\nThis is under-retrieval because the necessary information:\n\n- Exists in the patient history.\n\n- Is relevant to the question.\n\n- Is absent from the supplied context.\n\n- Is missed due to an overly narrow retrieval boundary.\n\nRetrieval sufficiency and contextual-recall evaluations are intended to detect whether the retrieved context contains all information necessary to answer safely and correctly.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nC. Context poisoning — a hallucinated allergy was introduced into the patient's record.\n\nNo hallucinated or maliciously inserted allergy appears in the scenario.\n\nThe penicillin allergy is documented and genuine; the problem is that the system fails to retrieve it. That is an under-retrieval failure, not context poisoning.\n\nThe tonsillectomy is also genuine information. Its retrieval is inappropriate because it is irrelevant, not because it is false or poisoned.\n\nD. Context clash — the current visit's intake form directly contradicts the childhood tonsillectomy record.\n\nThe scenario provides no contradiction between two context elements.\n\nThe current intake form does not deny that the tonsillectomy occurred, nor does one source provide a conflicting allergy status. The issue is that the tonsillectomy is irrelevant and the allergy is absent from retrieval.\n\nA context clash would require incompatible facts or instructions, such as one record saying the patient has a penicillin allergy and another saying the patient has no known allergies.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is confusing irrelevant but accurate context with poisoned context.\n\nThe tonsillectomy is not false; it is simply irrelevant to the ankle question. Similarly, the allergy was not contradicted or fabricated—it was missed because retrieval was scoped too narrowly.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA medical retrieval system should balance precision and recall according to the clinical question.\n\nFor a musculoskeletal injury, retrieval might prioritize:\n\n- Relevant prior injuries.\n\n- Current medications.\n\n- Conditions affecting treatment.\n\n- Recent imaging or procedures.\n\nFor prescribing medication, it should always retrieve safety-critical information such as:\n\n- Drug allergies.\n\n- Current medications and interactions.\n\n- Renal or hepatic limitations.\n\n- Relevant contraindications.\n\nMetadata filtering can suppress unrelated historical procedures, while mandatory safety-context retrieval can ensure that allergies and medication risks are always included. MLflow evaluation should separately measure whether retrieved information is relevant and whether all required information is present.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- AI Search retrieval quality guide\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality)\n\n- Answer and context relevance judges\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/is_context_relevant](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/is_context_relevant)\n\n- RetrievalSufficiency judge\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/is_context_sufficient](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/is_context_sufficient)\n\n- Agent memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Over-retrieval / context pollution — irrelevant history (the tonsillectomy) is retrieved and dilutes the response to an unrelated question.\n\nB. Under-retrieval — relevant history (the penicillin allergy) exists but is missed because retrieval is scoped too narrowly to the current visit only.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scenario contains two opposite retrieval failures:\n\n- Too much irrelevant context: The childhood tonsillectomy is retrieved for a sprained-ankle question even though it has no bearing on the current clinical issue.\n\n- Too little relevant context: The documented penicillin allergy is omitted from an antibiotic-prescribing question because retrieval examines only the current visit.\n\nThe correct selections are therefore A and B. The first problem concerns retrieval precision and relevance; the second concerns retrieval coverage and sufficiency.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA — Over-retrieval/context pollution\n\nThe tonsillectomy is valid historical information, but it is irrelevant to treating a sprained ankle. Prominently injecting it into the model’s context:\n\n- Consumes context-window capacity.\n\n- Dilutes clinically useful information.\n\n- Can distract the model’s reasoning.\n\n- Makes the response less focused.\n\nDatabricks’ retrieval-quality guidance emphasizes filtering and relevance because reducing irrelevant candidates improves retrieval precision. MLflow’s `RetrievalRelevance` judge likewise evaluates whether each retrieved document is directly relevant to the user’s request.\n\nB — Under-retrieval\n\nThe penicillin allergy is highly relevant to an antibiotic decision, but the retriever fails to obtain it because its scope is limited to the current intake form.\n\nThis is under-retrieval because the necessary information:\n\n- Exists in the patient history.\n\n- Is relevant to the question.\n\n- Is absent from the supplied context.\n\n- Is missed due to an overly narrow retrieval boundary.\n\nRetrieval sufficiency and contextual-recall evaluations are intended to detect whether the retrieved context contains all information necessary to answer safely and correctly.","Correct Answer\n\nA. Over-retrieval / context pollution — irrelevant history (the tonsillectomy) is retrieved and dilutes the response to an unrelated question.\n\nB. Under-retrieval — relevant history (the penicillin allergy) exists but is missed because retrieval is scoped too narrowly to the current visit only.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scenario contains two opposite retrieval failures:\n\n- Too much irrelevant context: The childhood tonsillectomy is retrieved for a sprained-ankle question even though it has no bearing on the current clinical issue.\n\n- Too little relevant context: The documented penicillin allergy is omitted from an antibiotic-prescribing question because retrieval examines only the current visit.\n\nThe correct selections are therefore A and B. The first problem concerns retrieval precision and relevance; the second concerns retrieval coverage and sufficiency.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA — Over-retrieval/context pollution\n\nThe tonsillectomy is valid historical information, but it is irrelevant to treating a sprained ankle. Prominently injecting it into the model’s context:\n\n- Consumes context-window capacity.\n\n- Dilutes clinically useful information.\n\n- Can distract the model’s reasoning.\n\n- Makes the response less focused.\n\nDatabricks’ retrieval-quality guidance emphasizes filtering and relevance because reducing irrelevant candidates improves retrieval precision. MLflow’s `RetrievalRelevance` judge likewise evaluates whether each retrieved document is directly relevant to the user’s request.\n\nB — Under-retrieval\n\nThe penicillin allergy is highly relevant to an antibiotic decision, but the retriever fails to obtain it because its scope is limited to the current intake form.\n\nThis is under-retrieval because the necessary information:\n\n- Exists in the patient history.\n\n- Is relevant to the question.\n\n- Is absent from the supplied context.\n\n- Is missed due to an overly narrow retrieval boundary.\n\nRetrieval sufficiency and contextual-recall evaluations are intended to detect whether the retrieved context contains all information necessary to answer safely and correctly.","C. Context poisoning — a hallucinated allergy was introduced into the patient's record.\n\nNo hallucinated or maliciously inserted allergy appears in the scenario.\n\nThe penicillin allergy is documented and genuine; the problem is that the system fails to retrieve it. That is an under-retrieval failure, not context poisoning.\n\nThe tonsillectomy is also genuine information. Its retrieval is inappropriate because it is irrelevant, not because it is false or poisoned.","D. Context clash — the current visit's intake form directly contradicts the childhood tonsillectomy record.\n\nThe scenario provides no contradiction between two context elements.\n\nThe current intake form does not deny that the tonsillectomy occurred, nor does one source provide a conflicting allergy status. The issue is that the tonsillectomy is irrelevant and the allergy is absent from retrieval.\n\nA context clash would require incompatible facts or instructions, such as one record saying the patient has a penicillin allergy and another saying the patient has no known allergies."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/is_context_relevant","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/is_context_sufficient","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents"],"source_hash":"e8f5be1dbf27665d30a9fe1062131305e5730c5dfab0c68c80dea381931f474c","correct_many":[0,1],"stem_ja":"医療問診エージェントが、臨床上の質問に答えるため患者の履歴を取得します。足首の捻挫の治療について聞かれた際、15年前の小児期の扁桃摘出術を取得して大きく取り上げました。現在の問題に明らかに無関係で、不要な履歴が回答を薄めています。別の抗生物質の処方に関する質問では、取得対象を今回の受診の問診票だけに限定していたため、2年前に記録されたペニシリンアレルギーを見落としました。\n\nこのメモリシステムに存在するリスクのパターンを2つ選んでください。","options_ja":["過剰取得／コンテキストへの不要情報の混入。無関係な扁桃摘出術の履歴が取得され、別の質問への回答を薄めている。","取得不足。必要なペニシリンアレルギーの履歴は存在するが、今回の受診だけという狭すぎる検索範囲で見落としている。","コンテキスト汚染。幻覚による架空のアレルギーが患者の記録へ追加された。","コンテキストの衝突。今回の問診票が、小児期の扁桃摘出術の記録と直接矛盾している。"],"explanation_ja":"扁桃摘出術は「要らない情報を取り過ぎる」問題、ペニシリンアレルギーは「必要な情報を取り漏らす」問題です。対策は単に取得件数を増減することではなく、質問に合う関連性と検索範囲の設定です。覚え方は「無関係を入れる＝過剰、必要な既存情報を落とす＝不足」です。","distractors_ja":["捻挫と無関係な手術歴を回答へ持ち込み、重要な情報の比重を下げているパターンです。","過去の重要なアレルギーが検索範囲外となっており、必要な情報を取得できていません。","アレルギーは実際に記録されており、架空の情報を追加したという証拠はありません。","2つの記録が矛盾しているのではなく、検索する範囲と関連性が不適切です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-027","number":27,"form":2,"stem":"A team already has thread-level checkpointing working for single conversations in a personal-finance budgeting agent. They now want a user's budgeting preferences and category rules to be available and stay consistent no matter which surface — mobile app or web dashboard — the user is currently using, including cases where the user might have both open around the same time.\n\nWhich Lakebase design decision most directly ensures the user's preferences stay consistent and don't get silently overwritten across concurrent surfaces?","options":["Store preferences only in each surface's local session state, without any shared durable store, since each surface can independently track what the user told it.","Store user-level preferences in a Lakebase table keyed by user ID, and rely on Lakebase's ACID transactional guarantees so concurrent writes from different surfaces either succeed cleanly in sequence or are properly reconciled, rather than one surface silently overwriting the other's most recent change.","Sync preferences to a Unity Catalog Delta table on a daily batch schedule, since batch sync is sufficient for preference data.","Allow whichever surface writes last to silently overwrite the other's changes, since preference conflicts are rare enough not to require explicit handling."],"correct":1,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"複数画面で共有する利用者単位の記憶","concept":"ce-f2-q027","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Store user-level preferences in a Lakebase table keyed by user ID, and rely on Lakebase's ACID transactional guarantees so concurrent writes from different surfaces either succeed cleanly in sequence or are properly reconciled, rather than one surface silently overwriting the other's most recent change.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe existing thread-level checkpointing solves a different problem: preserving the state of one conversation. The new requirement concerns user-level state shared across multiple concurrent conversations and application surfaces.\n\nThe preferences must be:\n\n- Available to both mobile and web sessions.\n\n- Durable across restarts.\n\n- Associated with the user rather than one thread.\n\n- Updated safely when both surfaces write around the same time.\n\nA centralized Lakebase table keyed by user ID provides a single authoritative preference record. Transactional concurrency controls can then serialize, reject, or explicitly reconcile conflicting updates rather than allowing each surface to maintain an independent copy.\n\nTherefore, B is the best exam answer.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nLakebase is designed as a durable PostgreSQL-backed state store for agent state and memory. Storing preferences by `user_id` makes them available across sessions, devices, and worker processes rather than tying them to a single conversation checkpoint.\n\nA suitable design might include:\n\n```\nCREATE TABLE user_budget_preferences (    user_id        TEXT PRIMARY KEY,    preferences    JSONB NOT NULL,    version        BIGINT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,    updated_at     TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);\n```\n\nBoth mobile and web clients read and write the same authoritative row. Lakebase supports transactional application workloads and concurrent connections, making it appropriate for this shared operational state.\n\nThere is an important technical qualification: ACID transactions alone do not automatically merge two semantically conflicting preference changes. To fully satisfy “do not silently overwrite,” the application should use an explicit concurrency mechanism, such as:\n\n- Optimistic locking with a `version` column.\n\n- A conditional update comparing the previously read version.\n\n- `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` within a transaction.\n\n- Serializable or repeatable-read transactions with retry handling.\n\n- Field-level merge rules when two updates affect different preferences.\n\nPostgreSQL provides transaction-isolation and locking mechanisms for controlling concurrent modifications.\n\nThus, B is the intended answer, with “properly reconciled” understood to include appropriate transactional conflict-detection logic.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Store preferences only in each surface's local session state, without any shared durable store, since each surface can independently track what the user told it.\n\nThis would create separate and potentially conflicting versions of the user’s preferences.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Mobile could store a monthly dining limit of ₹8,000.\n\n- Web could still hold the previous limit of ₹10,000.\n\n- A process restart could erase one surface’s changes.\n\n- Neither surface would be an authoritative source.\n\nLocal session state is appropriate for temporary conversation information, not preferences that must remain consistent across surfaces.\n\nC. Sync preferences to a Unity Catalog Delta table on a daily batch schedule, since batch sync is sufficient for preference data.\n\nA daily batch would make changes stale for up to a day.\n\nIf the user changes a category rule on mobile and immediately opens the web dashboard, the web application might continue using the old value. This does not meet the requirement for concurrent, cross-surface consistency.\n\nDelta tables may be valuable for analytics, auditing, or historical processing, but the active preference store should be an online transactional system such as Lakebase.\n\nD. Allow whichever surface writes last to silently overwrite the other's changes, since preference conflicts are rare enough not to require explicit handling.\n\nThis is exactly the failure the team wants to prevent.\n\nA last-write-wins design can lose valid changes. For example:\n\n- Mobile and web both read version 5.\n\n- Mobile changes the food budget and writes version 6.\n\n- Web changes a savings rule based on its stale version 5 copy.\n\n- Web replaces the whole record, silently deleting the mobile change.\n\nConflicts may be infrequent, but silent loss of user preference data is still a correctness defect.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is assuming that thread-level checkpointing is sufficient for all memory.\n\nThread-level state belongs to one conversation. User preferences that must work across devices and concurrent sessions require a separate user-scoped, durable, shared state object.\n\nA second trap is assuming that merely using an ACID database automatically resolves the business meaning of concurrent updates. Transactions provide the foundation, but applications must still implement conflict detection or merging when overwrites are unacceptable.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production design should separate:\n\n- Thread state: Current conversation messages and intermediate workflow checkpoints, keyed by thread ID.\n\n- User memory: Budgeting preferences and category rules, keyed by user ID.\n\n- Audit history: Previous preference versions, timestamps, source surface, and actor.\n\nAn optimistic update could use:\n\n```\nUPDATE user_budget_preferencesSET preferences = :new_preferences,    version = version + 1,    updated_at = CURRENT_TIMESTAMPWHERE user_id = :user_id  AND version = :expected_version;\n```\n\nIf zero rows are updated, another surface changed the preferences first. The application can then reload the latest record and merge the changes or ask the user to resolve a genuine conflict.\n\nThe certification answer remains B, because it is the only option that establishes shared user-level persistence and supports safe concurrent updates.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent state and memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)\n\n- Self-managed agent memory with Lakebase\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory)\n\n- Lakebase Postgres\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/)\n\n- PostgreSQL transaction isolation\n[https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html)","source_option_explanations":["A. Store preferences only in each surface's local session state, without any shared durable store, since each surface can independently track what the user told it.\n\nThis would create separate and potentially conflicting versions of the user’s preferences.\n\nFor example:\n\n- Mobile could store a monthly dining limit of ₹8,000.\n\n- Web could still hold the previous limit of ₹10,000.\n\n- A process restart could erase one surface’s changes.\n\n- Neither surface would be an authoritative source.\n\nLocal session state is appropriate for temporary conversation information, not preferences that must remain consistent across surfaces.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Store user-level preferences in a Lakebase table keyed by user ID, and rely on Lakebase's ACID transactional guarantees so concurrent writes from different surfaces either succeed cleanly in sequence or are properly reconciled, rather than one surface silently overwriting the other's most recent change.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe existing thread-level checkpointing solves a different problem: preserving the state of one conversation. The new requirement concerns user-level state shared across multiple concurrent conversations and application surfaces.\n\nThe preferences must be:\n\n- Available to both mobile and web sessions.\n\n- Durable across restarts.\n\n- Associated with the user rather than one thread.\n\n- Updated safely when both surfaces write around the same time.\n\nA centralized Lakebase table keyed by user ID provides a single authoritative preference record. Transactional concurrency controls can then serialize, reject, or explicitly reconcile conflicting updates rather than allowing each surface to maintain an independent copy.\n\nTherefore, B is the best exam answer.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nLakebase is designed as a durable PostgreSQL-backed state store for agent state and memory. Storing preferences by `user_id` makes them available across sessions, devices, and worker processes rather than tying them to a single conversation checkpoint.\n\nA suitable design might include:\n\n```\nCREATE TABLE user_budget_preferences (    user_id        TEXT PRIMARY KEY,    preferences    JSONB NOT NULL,    version        BIGINT NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,    updated_at     TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP);\n```\n\nBoth mobile and web clients read and write the same authoritative row. Lakebase supports transactional application workloads and concurrent connections, making it appropriate for this shared operational state.\n\nThere is an important technical qualification: ACID transactions alone do not automatically merge two semantically conflicting preference changes. To fully satisfy “do not silently overwrite,” the application should use an explicit concurrency mechanism, such as:\n\n- Optimistic locking with a `version` column.\n\n- A conditional update comparing the previously read version.\n\n- `SELECT ... FOR UPDATE` within a transaction.\n\n- Serializable or repeatable-read transactions with retry handling.\n\n- Field-level merge rules when two updates affect different preferences.\n\nPostgreSQL provides transaction-isolation and locking mechanisms for controlling concurrent modifications.\n\nThus, B is the intended answer, with “properly reconciled” understood to include appropriate transactional conflict-detection logic.","C. Sync preferences to a Unity Catalog Delta table on a daily batch schedule, since batch sync is sufficient for preference data.\n\nA daily batch would make changes stale for up to a day.\n\nIf the user changes a category rule on mobile and immediately opens the web dashboard, the web application might continue using the old value. This does not meet the requirement for concurrent, cross-surface consistency.\n\nDelta tables may be valuable for analytics, auditing, or historical processing, but the active preference store should be an online transactional system such as Lakebase.","D. Allow whichever surface writes last to silently overwrite the other's changes, since preference conflicts are rare enough not to require explicit handling.\n\nThis is exactly the failure the team wants to prevent.\n\nA last-write-wins design can lose valid changes. For example:\n\n- Mobile and web both read version 5.\n\n- Mobile changes the food budget and writes version 6.\n\n- Web changes a savings rule based on its stale version 5 copy.\n\n- Web replaces the whole record, silently deleting the mobile change.\n\nConflicts may be infrequent, but silent loss of user preference data is still a correctness defect."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/self-managed-memory","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/","https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html"],"source_hash":"cffbb34869b02ca74bb6165b7f30baa8c2417b23c6852f832170d01af997de67","stem_ja":"個人向け家計管理エージェントで、チームは単一会話向けのスレッド単位チェックポイントをすでに動かしています。次に、モバイルアプリとWebダッシュボードのどちらを使っていても、利用者の予算の好みや費目分類のルールを参照でき、一貫して保てるようにしたいと考えています。両方の画面をほぼ同時に開く場合も含みます。\n\n複数画面の同時利用でも、利用者の好みの一貫性を保ち、黙って上書きされないようにするため、最も直接役立つLakebaseの設計判断はどれですか。","options_ja":["各画面がユーザーから聞いた内容を独立して記録できるため、共有の永続ストアを使わず、各画面のローカルセッション状態だけへ好みを保存する。","ユーザーIDをキーとするLakebaseテーブルへ利用者単位の好みを保存し、LakebaseのACIDトランザクション保証を利用する。異なる画面からの同時書き込みが順序立って成功するか適切に調整され、一方が他方の最新の変更を黙って上書きしないようにする。","好みのデータにはバッチ同期で十分なので、Unity CatalogのDeltaテーブルへ毎日バッチで同期する。","好みの競合はまれで明示的に扱う必要がないため、最後に書き込む画面が他方の変更を黙って上書きすることを許可する。"],"explanation_ja":"スレッドごとの状態とは別に、ユーザーIDで共有する永続データが必要です。選択肢の中ではLakebaseでトランザクションを使う案が最善です。ただしACIDだけで古い値による上書きが自動防止されるわけではありません。実装では版番号の比較、行ロック、適切な分離レベルと競合時の再試行なども設計します。\n\n補足：正解キーは原本どおり維持。ACIDを使う共有ストアという方向は最善だが、ACID保証だけでアプリケーションの古い値による更新消失を一律に防げるという原文の含意には補足が必要。PostgreSQLのRead Committedでは各文が異なるスナップショットを使い、更新方法次第で上書きが起こりうる。適切なロック、条件付き更新／楽観的競合制御、分離レベルと再試行を設計する。公式根拠：https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/transaction-iso.html","distractors_ja":["画面ごとに独立した状態しかないため、他方で更新された好みを参照できず、一貫性を保てません。","同じユーザーの共有ストアとトランザクションを使う方向が適切です。競合検出や更新制御の具体化も必要です。","日次同期では同時利用中の最新の変更が伝わらず、直近の更新を上書きする危険が残ります。","問題が避けたい黙った上書きをそのまま許しており、要求と反対です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-028","number":28,"form":2,"stem":"An internal IT-helpdesk agent retrieves accurate, correctly cited setup instructions, but its answers frequently address the wrong situation. A user asks \"how do I reset my VPN access\" without specifying which of the company's two VPN systems they use (different regions use different systems). The agent retrieves and confidently answers using one region's VPN reset steps — a real, accurately cited passage — when the user is actually on the other region's system. Retrieval ran before any attempt was made to establish which VPN system applies to this user.\n\nAt which pipeline stage should this mismatch be resolved, and what design change addresses it?","options":["This should be resolved during context compaction, by summarizing both VPN systems' instructions into a single unified summary before retrieval occurs.","This should be resolved during retrieval itself, by retrieving both VPN systems' instructions for every question so neither can be missed.","This should be resolved before retrieval, by having the agent first resolve which VPN system applies to this user — for example, from the user's known region on file, or a single clarifying question — and use that resolved intent to scope the retrieval query, rather than retrieving broadly and hoping the right passage surfaces.","This should be resolved after the response is generated, by adding a disclaimer noting that VPN steps vary by region."],"correct":2,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"検索前にユーザーの状況を確定","concept":"ce-f2-q028","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. This should be resolved before retrieval, by having the agent first resolve which VPN system applies to this user — for example, from the user's known region on file, or a single clarifying question — and use that resolved intent to scope the retrieval query, rather than retrieving broadly and hoping the right passage surfaces.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe retrieved instructions are factually correct and properly cited, but they apply to the wrong VPN system. This means the primary failure is not document accuracy, citation quality, or response generation. The retrieval request itself was underspecified.\n\nThe correct sequence is:\n\nUnderstand the user’s situation → determine the applicable VPN system → retrieve only that system’s instructions → generate the answer\n\nBecause retrieval occurred before determining the user’s region or VPN system, the retriever had insufficient information to select the correct source. The mismatch should therefore be corrected during query understanding or intent resolution before retrieval.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nDatabricks describes query understanding as analyzing the user’s request, identifying intent and relevant entities or filters, and transforming the request into a more specific retrieval query. Metadata extracted during this stage can then narrow retrieval to the appropriate subset of documents.\n\nFor this scenario, the agent can resolve the VPN system in one of two ways:\n\n- Retrieve the user’s known region from an authoritative employee profile.\n\n- Ask one focused clarification question, such as:\n“Are you using the Americas VPN or the Europe VPN?”\n\nOnce resolved, the agent can apply a filter such as:\n\n```\nvpn_system = \"EMEA\"\n```\n\nor:\n\n```\nregion = \"Europe\"\n```\n\nAI Search filters are specifically intended to narrow results using metadata values, preventing instructions for another region from entering the retrieved context.\n\nThis design addresses the mismatch at its source instead of expecting the model to infer the correct system from two competing instruction sets.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. This should be resolved during context compaction, by summarizing both VPN systems' instructions into a single unified summary before retrieval occurs.\n\nContext compaction addresses excessive or accumulated context, not unresolved user intent.\n\nIt also normally operates on information already present in the context. It cannot reliably merge two VPN procedures that are intentionally different without risking incorrect or unsafe instructions.\n\nThe agent first needs to determine which procedure applies—not compress incompatible procedures into one.\n\nB. This should be resolved during retrieval itself, by retrieving both VPN systems' instructions for every question so neither can be missed.\n\nRetrieving both systems increases recall but reduces precision.\n\nIt would place two competing sets of instructions into the prompt, potentially causing the agent to:\n\n- Combine steps from different VPN systems.\n\n- Select the wrong procedure again.\n\n- Present both procedures and burden the user with deciding.\n\n- Create a context clash between incompatible instructions.\n\nBroad retrieval is not a substitute for resolving a critical filter value. The correct system should be identified first, and retrieval should then be scoped accordingly.\n\nD. This should be resolved after the response is generated, by adding a disclaimer noting that VPN steps vary by region.\n\nA disclaimer does not correct the answer.\n\nThe user could still follow the wrong reset procedure, and the response would remain operationally unhelpful despite acknowledging uncertainty. Post-generation warnings are weaker than preventing the wrong context from being retrieved.\n\nThe problem must be resolved before the response is produced.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is treating an accurately retrieved and cited passage as automatically relevant.\n\nA passage can be:\n\n- Authentic\n\n- Accurate\n\n- Properly cited\n\n- Semantically related\n\nand still be wrong for the user’s specific situation.\n\nRetrieval quality depends not only on finding a real passage, but on resolving the user’s intent and applying the correct scope before searching.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production support agent should distinguish between:\n\n- Resolvable context: Region or VPN assignment already exists in the employee profile.\n\n- Unresolved ambiguity: No authoritative mapping is available, so clarification is required.\n\nA robust flow would be:\n\n- Identify the requesting employee.\n\n- Look up the employee’s assigned region or VPN system.\n\n- Ask one clarification question only when the lookup is unavailable or ambiguous.\n\n- Apply the resolved VPN system as an AI Search metadata filter.\n\n- Retrieve and cite only the applicable reset instructions.\n\n- Log the resolved system, filter, retrieved passages, and answer in MLflow traces.\n\nThe exam answer is C because the failure begins with incomplete query understanding and must be corrected before retrieval.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Improve RAG chain quality\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain)\n\n- AI Search filtering guide\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/filtering-guide](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/filtering-guide)\n\n- RAG on Databricks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation)\n\n- Query an AI Search index\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search)","source_option_explanations":["A. This should be resolved during context compaction, by summarizing both VPN systems' instructions into a single unified summary before retrieval occurs.\n\nContext compaction addresses excessive or accumulated context, not unresolved user intent.\n\nIt also normally operates on information already present in the context. It cannot reliably merge two VPN procedures that are intentionally different without risking incorrect or unsafe instructions.\n\nThe agent first needs to determine which procedure applies—not compress incompatible procedures into one.","B. This should be resolved during retrieval itself, by retrieving both VPN systems' instructions for every question so neither can be missed.\n\nRetrieving both systems increases recall but reduces precision.\n\nIt would place two competing sets of instructions into the prompt, potentially causing the agent to:\n\n- Combine steps from different VPN systems.\n\n- Select the wrong procedure again.\n\n- Present both procedures and burden the user with deciding.\n\n- Create a context clash between incompatible instructions.\n\nBroad retrieval is not a substitute for resolving a critical filter value. The correct system should be identified first, and retrieval should then be scoped accordingly.","Correct Answer\n\nC. This should be resolved before retrieval, by having the agent first resolve which VPN system applies to this user — for example, from the user's known region on file, or a single clarifying question — and use that resolved intent to scope the retrieval query, rather than retrieving broadly and hoping the right passage surfaces.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe retrieved instructions are factually correct and properly cited, but they apply to the wrong VPN system. This means the primary failure is not document accuracy, citation quality, or response generation. The retrieval request itself was underspecified.\n\nThe correct sequence is:\n\nUnderstand the user’s situation → determine the applicable VPN system → retrieve only that system’s instructions → generate the answer\n\nBecause retrieval occurred before determining the user’s region or VPN system, the retriever had insufficient information to select the correct source. The mismatch should therefore be corrected during query understanding or intent resolution before retrieval.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nDatabricks describes query understanding as analyzing the user’s request, identifying intent and relevant entities or filters, and transforming the request into a more specific retrieval query. Metadata extracted during this stage can then narrow retrieval to the appropriate subset of documents.\n\nFor this scenario, the agent can resolve the VPN system in one of two ways:\n\n- Retrieve the user’s known region from an authoritative employee profile.\n\n- Ask one focused clarification question, such as:\n“Are you using the Americas VPN or the Europe VPN?”\n\nOnce resolved, the agent can apply a filter such as:\n\n```\nvpn_system = \"EMEA\"\n```\n\nor:\n\n```\nregion = \"Europe\"\n```\n\nAI Search filters are specifically intended to narrow results using metadata values, preventing instructions for another region from entering the retrieved context.\n\nThis design addresses the mismatch at its source instead of expecting the model to infer the correct system from two competing instruction sets.","D. This should be resolved after the response is generated, by adding a disclaimer noting that VPN steps vary by region.\n\nA disclaimer does not correct the answer.\n\nThe user could still follow the wrong reset procedure, and the response would remain operationally unhelpful despite acknowledging uncertainty. Post-generation warnings are weaker than preventing the wrong context from being retrieved.\n\nThe problem must be resolved before the response is produced."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/filtering-guide","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/retrieval-augmented-generation","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search"],"source_hash":"4af917d770761214d1a57dd367472098542937db729d840fd7e8465c28fe70e9","stem_ja":"社内ITヘルプデスクのエージェントは、正確で出典も正しい設定手順を取得しますが、回答する状況をよく間違えます。会社には地域によって異なる2つのVPNシステムがあります。利用者がどちらを使うかを示さず「VPNアクセスをリセットする方法は」と聞くと、エージェントはある地域の実在する手順を正しく引用して自信を持って答えます。しかし利用者はもう一方の地域のシステムを使っています。利用者に適用すべきVPNの確認より先に、検索が行われていました。\n\nこの不一致はパイプラインのどの段階で解消すべきですか。また、どの設計変更で対処できますか。","options_ja":["検索前に両VPNの手順を1つの要約へまとめる、コンテキスト圧縮の段階で解消する。","どちらも取り漏らさないよう、すべての質問で両VPNの手順を取得する、検索そのものの段階で解消する。","検索前に、登録済みの利用者の地域情報や1つの確認質問などで、その利用者に適用するVPNを先に確定する。その確定した意図で検索範囲を絞り、広く検索して正しい箇所が出ることに期待しない。","回答を生成した後で、VPN手順は地域によって異なるという免責文を付けて解消する。"],"explanation_ja":"引用の正確さと、その利用者への適合性は別です。VPNの種類が未確定なら、検索する前に地域情報や確認質問で対象を絞ります。正しい資料を探しても、対象システムが違えば答えは役立ちません。覚え方は「何について答えるかを先に確定し、その条件で検索する」です。","distractors_ja":["異なるVPNの手順を混ぜると、どちらを使うかがさらに不明確になり、利用者の対象も確定できません。","両方を取っても、どちらが利用者に当てはまるかという未解決の問題が残ります。","対象VPNを先に確定し、その条件を検索へ渡すため、状況の取り違えを発生前に防げます。","誤った手順を出した後の但し書きでは、利用者に合う回答にはなりません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-029","number":29,"form":2,"stem":"An MCP server exposes 80 tools spanning a company's Kubernetes clusters, cloud infrastructure (AWS/GCP), monitoring systems, and ticketing system. The current agent harness loads the full JSON schema for all 80 tools into the system prompt on every turn, regardless of the task. Token analysis shows this static tool-schema block accounts for roughly 65% of total prompt tokens on typical turns, even though a given incident-response task usually only requires 3–5 tools.\n\nWhich progressive-disclosure design would most reduce token usage without limiting which tools the agent can ultimately access?","options":["Keep loading full schemas for all 80 tools on every turn, but compress them using a general-purpose text summarization pass before they enter the prompt.","Expose a lightweight tool-discovery mechanism — a searchable tool index or catalog — that returns only the names and short summaries of the small number of tools relevant to the current request, and load the full parameter schema only for the specific tool(s) the agent decides to invoke.","Reduce the number of registered tools from 80 down to the 10 most commonly used ones, permanently removing agent access to the other 70.","Move the full JSON schemas for all 80 tools into a single large few-shot example demonstrating correct usage, so the model learns the schemas implicitly rather than reading them directly."],"correct":1,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"ツールの段階的開示","concept":"ce-f2-q029","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Expose a lightweight tool-discovery mechanism — a searchable tool index or catalog — that returns only the names and short summaries of the small number of tools relevant to the current request, and load the full parameter schema only for the specific tool(s) the agent decides to invoke.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe primary problem is that the harness loads all 80 complete tool schemas on every turn, even though a typical incident requires only 3–5 tools. This causes irrelevant schemas to consume approximately 65% of the prompt.\n\nThe required solution must achieve both goals:\n\n- Substantially reduce tool-definition tokens.\n\n- Preserve the agent’s ability to access any of the 80 tools when relevant.\n\nOption B implements progressive tool disclosure:\n\n- Initially expose only a small discovery interface.\n\n- Search the catalog using the current incident’s requirements.\n\n- Return concise names and descriptions for relevant tools.\n\n- Load complete parameter schemas only for selected tools.\n\n- Invoke those tools using their exact schemas.\n\nThis reduces prompt overhead without permanently removing any capability.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nProgressive disclosure separates tool discovery from tool execution.\n\nFor an incident involving a failing Kubernetes workload, discovery might return only:\n\n- `k8s_get_pod_logs`\n\n- `k8s_describe_deployment`\n\n- `monitoring_get_alert`\n\n- `ticketing_create_incident`\n\nThe full input schemas for those tools are loaded only after the agent identifies them as relevant. Schemas for unrelated AWS billing, GCP networking, ticket search, or other Kubernetes operations remain outside the model context.\n\nA strong design uses three layers:\n\n- Catalog: Search tool names and short descriptions.\n\n- Inspect: Retrieve the complete schema for selected tools.\n\n- Execute: Invoke the selected tools with validated arguments.\n\nThis directly addresses the measured token problem while retaining eventual access to the entire MCP tool inventory.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Keep loading full schemas for all 80 tools on every turn, but compress them using a general-purpose text summarization pass before they enter the prompt.\n\nThis retains the inefficient all-tools-upfront architecture.\n\nGeneral-purpose summarization can also remove or distort schema details such as:\n\n- Required versus optional parameters\n\n- Allowed enumeration values\n\n- Nested object structures\n\n- Validation constraints\n\n- Side effects and safety requirements\n\nTool schemas are structured interfaces, not ordinary prose. The agent needs exact schemas for the tools it invokes, but it does not need every schema on every turn.\n\nC. Reduce the number of registered tools from 80 down to the 10 most commonly used ones, permanently removing agent access to the other 70.\n\nThis would reduce token usage, but it violates the requirement that the agent must retain access to all tools.\n\nLess frequently used tools may still be essential for unusual AWS, GCP, Kubernetes, monitoring, or ticketing incidents. Progressive disclosure removes irrelevant schemas from the active context without removing the underlying capabilities.\n\nD. Move the full JSON schemas for all 80 tools into a single large few-shot example demonstrating correct usage, so the model learns the schemas implicitly rather than reading them directly.\n\nThis does not meaningfully reduce context usage because the same large schema block would still be included in the prompt.\n\nIt would also be less reliable than providing formal tool definitions. The model could not safely infer exact parameter requirements from an example, especially for tools not demonstrated completely.\n\nFew-shot examples are useful for teaching behavioral patterns, not for replacing authoritative machine-readable interfaces.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming that reducing tool-schema tokens requires permanently reducing the agent’s capabilities.\n\nProgressive disclosure distinguishes between:\n\n- Tools the agent is authorized to access.\n\n- Tools currently relevant to the task.\n\n- Tool schemas that must be present in the active model context.\n\nAll 80 tools can remain available while only a few relevant definitions are disclosed for each incident.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production implementation should maintain a searchable catalog containing:\n\n- Tool name\n\n- One-line description\n\n- Source MCP server\n\n- Capability category\n\n- Read/write classification\n\n- Required permissions\n\n- Risk or approval level\n\nThe harness should cache schemas outside the model context, refresh the catalog when the MCP server reports tool-list changes, and inject only selected schemas into the current turn.\n\nDiscovery must not bypass governance. Unity Catalog permissions, MCP tool selection, service policies, and approval requirements should still determine which tools the requesting identity can discover and invoke.\n\nThe expected exam answer is B because it achieves the largest context reduction while preserving access to the complete governed tool collection.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Model Context Protocol — Client Best Practices: Progressive Tool Discovery\n[https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/clients/client-best-practices](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/clients/client-best-practices?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n\n- Connect agents to third-party tools with MCP Services\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/mcp/mcp-services](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/mcp/mcp-services)\n\n- Connect agents to tools\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/agent-tool](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/agent-tool)\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)","source_option_explanations":["A. Keep loading full schemas for all 80 tools on every turn, but compress them using a general-purpose text summarization pass before they enter the prompt.\n\nThis retains the inefficient all-tools-upfront architecture.\n\nGeneral-purpose summarization can also remove or distort schema details such as:\n\n- Required versus optional parameters\n\n- Allowed enumeration values\n\n- Nested object structures\n\n- Validation constraints\n\n- Side effects and safety requirements\n\nTool schemas are structured interfaces, not ordinary prose. The agent needs exact schemas for the tools it invokes, but it does not need every schema on every turn.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Expose a lightweight tool-discovery mechanism — a searchable tool index or catalog — that returns only the names and short summaries of the small number of tools relevant to the current request, and load the full parameter schema only for the specific tool(s) the agent decides to invoke.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe primary problem is that the harness loads all 80 complete tool schemas on every turn, even though a typical incident requires only 3–5 tools. This causes irrelevant schemas to consume approximately 65% of the prompt.\n\nThe required solution must achieve both goals:\n\n- Substantially reduce tool-definition tokens.\n\n- Preserve the agent’s ability to access any of the 80 tools when relevant.\n\nOption B implements progressive tool disclosure:\n\n- Initially expose only a small discovery interface.\n\n- Search the catalog using the current incident’s requirements.\n\n- Return concise names and descriptions for relevant tools.\n\n- Load complete parameter schemas only for selected tools.\n\n- Invoke those tools using their exact schemas.\n\nThis reduces prompt overhead without permanently removing any capability.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nProgressive disclosure separates tool discovery from tool execution.\n\nFor an incident involving a failing Kubernetes workload, discovery might return only:\n\n- `k8s_get_pod_logs`\n\n- `k8s_describe_deployment`\n\n- `monitoring_get_alert`\n\n- `ticketing_create_incident`\n\nThe full input schemas for those tools are loaded only after the agent identifies them as relevant. Schemas for unrelated AWS billing, GCP networking, ticket search, or other Kubernetes operations remain outside the model context.\n\nA strong design uses three layers:\n\n- Catalog: Search tool names and short descriptions.\n\n- Inspect: Retrieve the complete schema for selected tools.\n\n- Execute: Invoke the selected tools with validated arguments.\n\nThis directly addresses the measured token problem while retaining eventual access to the entire MCP tool inventory.","C. Reduce the number of registered tools from 80 down to the 10 most commonly used ones, permanently removing agent access to the other 70.\n\nThis would reduce token usage, but it violates the requirement that the agent must retain access to all tools.\n\nLess frequently used tools may still be essential for unusual AWS, GCP, Kubernetes, monitoring, or ticketing incidents. Progressive disclosure removes irrelevant schemas from the active context without removing the underlying capabilities.","D. Move the full JSON schemas for all 80 tools into a single large few-shot example demonstrating correct usage, so the model learns the schemas implicitly rather than reading them directly.\n\nThis does not meaningfully reduce context usage because the same large schema block would still be included in the prompt.\n\nIt would also be less reliable than providing formal tool definitions. The model could not safely infer exact parameter requirements from an example, especially for tools not demonstrated completely.\n\nFew-shot examples are useful for teaching behavioral patterns, not for replacing authoritative machine-readable interfaces."],"references":["https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/clients/client-best-practices?utm_source=chatgpt.com","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/mcp/mcp-services","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/agent-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns"],"source_hash":"0306005337f8f7d5e07c9d433cc5ee10211940cfa2f122d334f35d6f29a00c4a","stem_ja":"MCPサーバーが、社内のKubernetesクラスター、AWS／GCPのクラウド基盤、監視システム、チケット管理にまたがる80個のツールを公開しています。現在のエージェント実行基盤は、タスクに関係なく毎ターン80個すべての完全なJSONスキーマをシステムプロンプトへ読み込みます。トークン分析では、通常のターンのプロンプト全体の約65％をこの固定のツールスキーマ群が占めますが、1件の障害対応で必要なツールは通常3～5個だけです。\n\n最終的に利用できるツールを制限せず、トークン使用量を最も減らす段階的開示の設計はどれですか。","options_ja":["毎ターン80個すべての完全なスキーマを読み込み続けるが、プロンプトへ入る前に汎用的な文章要約で圧縮する。","検索可能なツール索引やカタログのような軽量な発見手段を公開する。現在の依頼に関係する少数のツール名と短い概要だけを返し、エージェントが呼び出すと決めた特定のツールだけについて、完全な引数スキーマを読み込む。","登録ツールを80個から最もよく使う10個へ減らし、残る70個へのアクセスを恒久的に除く。","80個すべての完全なJSONスキーマを、正しい利用法を示す1つの巨大なfew-shot例へ移し、モデルが直接読む代わりに暗黙的に学ぶようにする。"],"explanation_ja":"段階的開示では、最初に軽量な索引で候補を見つけ、使うツールの詳細だけを後から読み込みます。全ツールへのアクセスは維持し、毎ターンの不要なスキーマ投入を減らせます。覚え方は「概要で探し、必要な詳細だけ開く」。削除や文章要約ではなく、情報を渡すタイミングを変える設計です。","distractors_ja":["全件を常時投入する構造が残り、引数の正確な定義を汎用要約で壊す危険もあります。","概要検索と必要時の詳細取得を分けるため、全能力を残したままトークン負荷を減らせます。","不要な常時投入は減りますが、最終的に全ツールへアクセスできるという要件を満たしません。","巨大な例も入力トークンを消費し、必要なツールだけを選んで開示する仕組みにはなりません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-030","number":30,"form":2,"stem":"A support-operations agent has access to two registered tools:\n\ncreate_ticket(title: str, description: str, priority: str) — description: \"Creates a new support ticket with the given title, description, and priority.\"\n\nopen_ticket(summary: str, details: str, urgency: str) — description: \"Opens a new support ticket using the provided summary, details, and urgency level.\"\n\nWhen the agent needs to log a new issue, it inconsistently alternates between these two tools across otherwise identical requests, and reviewers report no clear pattern predicting which one gets chosen.\n\nWhat is causing the ambiguous tool selection, and which correction most directly resolves it?","options":["The agent's temperature setting is too high, causing random tool selection; lowering temperature to 0 will make tool selection deterministic and correct regardless of the description overlap.","Both tools should be renamed to more generic names like tool_1 and tool_2, since specific names are what's confusing the model into treating them as different capabilities.","The two tools have identical parameter counts, and identical parameter counts alone are sufficient to cause ambiguous selection regardless of the description text; adding a fourth parameter to one tool would resolve the issue without touching either description.","The two descriptions describe functionally identical capabilities in nearly interchangeable language, giving the model no distinguishing signal between them; the fix is to consolidate them into a single canonical ticket-creation tool, or redefine one tool's scope so their capabilities are clearly non-overlapping."],"correct":3,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"重複するツールの役割を明確化","concept":"ce-f2-q030","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. The two descriptions describe functionally identical capabilities in nearly interchangeable language, giving the model no distinguishing signal between them; the fix is to consolidate them into a single canonical ticket-creation tool, or redefine one tool's scope so their capabilities are clearly non-overlapping.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe agent sees two tools that appear semantically equivalent:\n\n- `create_ticket` and `open_ticket`\n\n- `title` and `summary`\n\n- `description` and `details`\n\n- `priority` and `urgency`\n\nBoth descriptions say that the tool creates a new support ticket. Nothing tells the model that one should be selected for a particular ticket type, system, workflow, or escalation condition.\n\nBecause the tools overlap completely, either choice appears valid. The inconsistent selection is therefore caused by ambiguous tool definitions, not necessarily random model behavior.\n\nThe most direct correction is to either:\n\n- Keep one canonical ticket-creation tool and remove the duplicate, or\n\n- Give each tool a distinct, non-overlapping purpose with explicit selection criteria.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nTool-calling models rely heavily on tool names, descriptions, parameter definitions, and the current request to decide which tool applies. Databricks recommends writing clear descriptions for a tool and its arguments so the model understands how and when to use it.\n\nHere, both tools express the same capability using synonyms. The schemas provide no decision boundary such as:\n\n- Internal versus customer-facing tickets\n\n- Incident versus service-request tickets\n\n- Jira versus ServiceNow\n\n- Standard versus emergency escalation\n\n- Drafting versus immediate creation\n\nThe strongest simplification would be to expose only one canonical tool:\n\n```\ncreate_support_ticket(    title: str,    description: str,    priority: str)\n```\n\nIf both tools must remain because they invoke different systems, their names and descriptions should state that distinction explicitly. For example:\n\n```\ncreate_servicenow_incident:Creates a ServiceNow incident for production outages and operationalservice degradation. Do not use for ordinary employee support requests.\n```\n\n```\ncreate_helpdesk_request:Creates an internal IT helpdesk request for access, hardware, software,or account-support issues. Do not use for production incidents.\n```\n\nThese definitions provide the model with observable routing criteria. Option D therefore resolves the root cause rather than attempting to mask the ambiguity.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. The agent's temperature setting is too high, causing random tool selection; lowering temperature to 0 will make tool selection deterministic and correct regardless of the description overlap.\n\nLowering temperature may reduce output variability, but it does not make the tool definitions semantically distinct.\n\nWhen two tools appear equally appropriate, a low-temperature model might choose one more consistently, but that does not establish that the selected tool is correct. Behavior could also change after a model, prompt, or tool-order update.\n\nDeterministic selection among ambiguous choices is still ambiguous design. The schemas themselves must provide a meaningful distinction.\n\nB. Both tools should be renamed to more generic names like `tool_1` and `tool_2`, since specific names are what's confusing the model into treating them as different capabilities.\n\nGeneric names would remove useful semantic information and make selection even harder.\n\nNames such as `create_ticket` at least communicate the tool’s purpose. Names such as `tool_1` and `tool_2` provide no indication of:\n\n- What operation each tool performs\n\n- When it should be used\n\n- What side effects it has\n\n- How the two tools differ\n\nEffective tool names should be specific, descriptive, and aligned with distinct capabilities.\n\nC. The two tools have identical parameter counts, and identical parameter counts alone are sufficient to cause ambiguous selection regardless of the description text; adding a fourth parameter to one tool would resolve the issue without touching either description.\n\nParameter count is not the governing issue.\n\nTwo tools can have the same number of parameters and still be selected reliably when their purposes are clearly different. Conversely, adding an irrelevant fourth parameter would not explain when the tool should be chosen.\n\nThe ambiguity comes from overlapping semantics, not schema length. Any additional parameter should represent a genuine functional distinction, not be added merely to make the schemas look different.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is blaming nondeterminism or temperature for a tool-design problem.\n\nModel settings can influence consistency, but they cannot supply a missing semantic boundary. When tools perform the same action and use interchangeable descriptions, the model has no principled basis for choosing between them.\n\nThe best correction is to remove redundant capabilities or define explicit, non-overlapping scopes.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nBefore registering multiple tools, create a tool-selection matrix:\n\nToolIntended useMust not be used for`create_incident`Production outage or degradationRoutine support requests`create_service_request`Access, hardware, or software requestProduction incidents\n\nFor reliable tool selection:\n\n- Use one canonical tool for one capability.\n\n- Use distinct names based on business purpose.\n\n- Explain both when to use and when not to use each tool.\n\n- Avoid synonym-only duplicates.\n\n- Use consistent parameter names for shared concepts.\n\n- Test tool selection with identical and borderline requests.\n\n- Review tool calls in MLflow traces after schema changes.\n\nThe exam answer is D because the observed inconsistency is caused by overlapping tool descriptions, and consolidation or clear scope separation directly removes that ambiguity.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Create agent tools using Unity Catalog functions\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool)\n\n- Connect agents to tools\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/agent-tool](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/agent-tool)\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- Extend Genie Code with agent skills — Best practices for clear names and focused scope\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/skills](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/skills?)","source_option_explanations":["A. The agent's temperature setting is too high, causing random tool selection; lowering temperature to 0 will make tool selection deterministic and correct regardless of the description overlap.\n\nLowering temperature may reduce output variability, but it does not make the tool definitions semantically distinct.\n\nWhen two tools appear equally appropriate, a low-temperature model might choose one more consistently, but that does not establish that the selected tool is correct. Behavior could also change after a model, prompt, or tool-order update.\n\nDeterministic selection among ambiguous choices is still ambiguous design. The schemas themselves must provide a meaningful distinction.","B. Both tools should be renamed to more generic names like `tool_1` and `tool_2`, since specific names are what's confusing the model into treating them as different capabilities.\n\nGeneric names would remove useful semantic information and make selection even harder.\n\nNames such as `create_ticket` at least communicate the tool’s purpose. Names such as `tool_1` and `tool_2` provide no indication of:\n\n- What operation each tool performs\n\n- When it should be used\n\n- What side effects it has\n\n- How the two tools differ\n\nEffective tool names should be specific, descriptive, and aligned with distinct capabilities.\\","C. The two tools have identical parameter counts, and identical parameter counts alone are sufficient to cause ambiguous selection regardless of the description text; adding a fourth parameter to one tool would resolve the issue without touching either description.\n\nParameter count is not the governing issue.\n\nTwo tools can have the same number of parameters and still be selected reliably when their purposes are clearly different. Conversely, adding an irrelevant fourth parameter would not explain when the tool should be chosen.\n\nThe ambiguity comes from overlapping semantics, not schema length. Any additional parameter should represent a genuine functional distinction, not be added merely to make the schemas look different.","Correct Answer\n\nD. The two descriptions describe functionally identical capabilities in nearly interchangeable language, giving the model no distinguishing signal between them; the fix is to consolidate them into a single canonical ticket-creation tool, or redefine one tool's scope so their capabilities are clearly non-overlapping.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe agent sees two tools that appear semantically equivalent:\n\n- `create_ticket` and `open_ticket`\n\n- `title` and `summary`\n\n- `description` and `details`\n\n- `priority` and `urgency`\n\nBoth descriptions say that the tool creates a new support ticket. Nothing tells the model that one should be selected for a particular ticket type, system, workflow, or escalation condition.\n\nBecause the tools overlap completely, either choice appears valid. The inconsistent selection is therefore caused by ambiguous tool definitions, not necessarily random model behavior.\n\nThe most direct correction is to either:\n\n- Keep one canonical ticket-creation tool and remove the duplicate, or\n\n- Give each tool a distinct, non-overlapping purpose with explicit selection criteria.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nTool-calling models rely heavily on tool names, descriptions, parameter definitions, and the current request to decide which tool applies. Databricks recommends writing clear descriptions for a tool and its arguments so the model understands how and when to use it.\n\nHere, both tools express the same capability using synonyms. The schemas provide no decision boundary such as:\n\n- Internal versus customer-facing tickets\n\n- Incident versus service-request tickets\n\n- Jira versus ServiceNow\n\n- Standard versus emergency escalation\n\n- Drafting versus immediate creation\n\nThe strongest simplification would be to expose only one canonical tool:\n\n```\ncreate_support_ticket(    title: str,    description: str,    priority: str)\n```\n\nIf both tools must remain because they invoke different systems, their names and descriptions should state that distinction explicitly. For example:\n\n```\ncreate_servicenow_incident:Creates a ServiceNow incident for production outages and operationalservice degradation. Do not use for ordinary employee support requests.\n```\n\n```\ncreate_helpdesk_request:Creates an internal IT helpdesk request for access, hardware, software,or account-support issues. Do not use for production incidents.\n```\n\nThese definitions provide the model with observable routing criteria. Option D therefore resolves the root cause rather than attempting to mask the ambiguity."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/agent-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/skills?"],"source_hash":"125d0e7b7eecea9b4bd17eed7993664dcb82feeb55c132062f884c9526bf3b37","stem_ja":"サポート運用エージェントには、2つの登録済みツールがあります。\n\ncreate_ticket(title: str, description: str, priority: str) — 説明：「指定された件名、説明、優先度で新しいサポートチケットを作成する。」\n\nopen_ticket(summary: str, details: str, urgency: str) — 説明：「与えられた要約、詳細、緊急度を使って新しいサポートチケットを開く。」\n\n新しい問題を記録する際、他の点では同じ依頼でも、エージェントはこの2ツールを一貫性なく交互に使い、どちらを選ぶか予測できる明確な傾向がないとレビュアーが報告しています。\n\n選択が曖昧になる原因は何ですか。また、最も直接解決する修正はどれですか。","options_ja":["temperatureが高すぎてツール選択がランダムになっている。説明の重なりに関係なく、temperatureを0にすれば、選択は決定的かつ正確になる。","具体的な名前が異なる機能とモデルに誤認させているため、両ツールをtool_1、tool_2のような一般的な名前へ変える。","引数の数が同じだけで、説明に関係なく選択が曖昧になる。一方に4つ目の引数を足せば、説明を変えずに解決する。","2つの説明がほぼ言い換え可能な言葉で同じ機能を表し、モデルに区別の手掛かりがない。チケット作成の標準ツール1つへ統合するか、一方の対象範囲を再定義して、機能が明確に重ならないようにする。"],"explanation_ja":"名前や引数名が少し違っても、説明上の機能が同じなら、選択の根拠はありません。重複能力は統合するか、対象システム・操作目的・利用条件を明確に分けます。覚え方は「選び分ける理由を説明に作る」。temperatureや引数数の変更では、役割の重複という原因を解消できません。","distractors_ja":["出力のばらつきを抑えても、どちらを選ぶべきかという意味上の区別は生まれません。","一般的な名前は役割の手掛かりをさらに減らし、重複を解消しません。","引数の数が同じこと自体は問題ではなく、機能と説明の重なりが選択を曖昧にしています。","同じ能力の重複を整理するか役割を分離することで、選択に必要な意味上の手掛かりを作れます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-031","number":31,"form":2,"stem":"A new engineer asks why a team's staged tool-discovery approach — where the agent first sees only tool names and one-line summaries, and full parameter schemas are loaded only for the specific tool selected on a given turn — uses meaningfully fewer tokens than loading full schemas for the entire catalog upfront, given that the total number of registered tools is identical either way and no tools are being removed from the catalog.\n\nWhich explanation is correct?","options":["Because full parameter schemas — with nested objects, enum lists, and descriptions — are typically far more token-heavy than a one-line name-and-summary entry, staging defers that heavier cost to only the few tools actually selected on a given turn, rather than paying it for every registered tool on every turn regardless of relevance.","Because the staged approach permanently deletes unused tools from the registry over time, so the catalog itself shrinks and there is simply less to load.","Because name-and-summary entries and full parameter schemas cost the same number of tokens, so the savings come entirely from using a smaller, cheaper model for the discovery stage.","Because staging compresses the full schemas using a lossy summarization pass before ever showing them to the model, trading completeness for token savings."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"段階的なツール発見で減るトークンの理由","concept":"ce-f2-q031","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Because full parameter schemas — with nested objects, enum lists, and descriptions — are typically far more token-heavy than a one-line name-and-summary entry, staging defers that heavier cost to only the few tools actually selected on a given turn, rather than paying it for every registered tool on every turn regardless of relevance.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe number of registered tools is not the main factor determining prompt cost. The important factor is how much information about those tools is placed in the model’s active context on each turn.\n\nA lightweight discovery catalog might expose only:\n\n- Tool name\n\n- One-line capability summary\n\n- Possibly a short category or risk label\n\nA complete tool definition can additionally contain:\n\n- Detailed descriptions\n\n- Required and optional parameters\n\n- Nested JSON objects\n\n- Enumeration values\n\n- Array definitions\n\n- Validation constraints\n\n- Parameter descriptions\n\n- Output schemas\n\nWith staged disclosure, the agent initially pays only the small catalog cost. It pays the much larger full-schema cost only for the few tools relevant to the current task.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAssume the registry contains 80 tools but a typical task needs only three.\n\nUnder full upfront loading, the prompt includes:\n\nFull schemas for all 80 tools on every relevant turn\n\nUnder progressive disclosure, the prompt includes:\n\nShort summaries for the catalog + full schemas for only the three selected tools\n\nThe other 77 tools remain registered and available. Their detailed schemas are simply kept outside the active model context until needed.\n\nMCP tools are schema-defined interfaces. Their definitions can include a name, description, input JSON Schema, annotations, and optional output schema. Complex JSON Schemas can be considerably larger than short catalog summaries.\n\nThe savings therefore come from deferring detailed context, not from deleting tools, summarizing schemas lossily, or changing models.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Because the staged approach permanently deletes unused tools from the registry over time, so the catalog itself shrinks and there is simply less to load.\n\nProgressive disclosure does not require deleting any tools.\n\nAll registered tools can remain discoverable and available. The design changes only which schemas are inserted into the active prompt for a particular request.\n\nPermanently deleting tools would reduce capabilities, which is not part of the described approach.\n\nC. Because name-and-summary entries and full parameter schemas cost the same number of tokens, so the savings come entirely from using a smaller, cheaper model for the discovery stage.\n\nA name and one-line summary are ordinarily much shorter than a complete parameter schema containing nested definitions, descriptions, constraints, and enumerations.\n\nA smaller discovery model could be an additional optimization, but it is not the reason the staged approach uses fewer prompt tokens. The token reduction occurs because substantially less schema text is supplied to the model.\n\nD. Because staging compresses the full schemas using a lossy summarization pass before ever showing them to the model, trading completeness for token savings.\n\nStaging does not require lossy compression.\n\nThe discovery stage uses intentionally concise metadata for selection. Once a tool is selected, the agent receives its complete, authoritative parameter schema before invocation.\n\nThis preserves exact details such as required parameters, enum values, nested structures, and validation constraints. Lossy schema summarization could remove critical invocation details and is therefore less reliable.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is confusing registered capability with active-context content.\n\nA tool can remain registered, authorized, and ultimately callable without its complete schema being included in every prompt. Progressive disclosure reduces the active token footprint while preserving the full tool catalog.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production implementation can use three stages:\n\n- Discover: Search concise tool names and summaries.\n\n- Inspect: Load the complete schema for selected candidate tools.\n\n- Invoke: Call the chosen tool with schema-validated arguments.\n\nThe harness can cache full schemas outside the model context and refresh them when the MCP server reports tool changes. It should also preserve authorization boundaries so the discovery mechanism returns only tools the agent is permitted to use.\n\nThe expected exam answer is A because the savings come from paying the detailed schema-token cost only for tools that are relevant on the current turn.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Model Context Protocol — Tools\n[https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/tools](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/tools)\n\n- Model Context Protocol — Understanding MCP servers\n[https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/server-concepts](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/server-concepts)\n\n- Model Context Protocol — Client Best Practices\n[https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/clients/client-best-practices](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/clients/client-best-practices)\n\n- Use MCP servers in agents — Databricks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/agents/mcp/use-mcp-in-agents](https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/agents/mcp/use-mcp-in-agents)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Because full parameter schemas — with nested objects, enum lists, and descriptions — are typically far more token-heavy than a one-line name-and-summary entry, staging defers that heavier cost to only the few tools actually selected on a given turn, rather than paying it for every registered tool on every turn regardless of relevance.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe number of registered tools is not the main factor determining prompt cost. The important factor is how much information about those tools is placed in the model’s active context on each turn.\n\nA lightweight discovery catalog might expose only:\n\n- Tool name\n\n- One-line capability summary\n\n- Possibly a short category or risk label\n\nA complete tool definition can additionally contain:\n\n- Detailed descriptions\n\n- Required and optional parameters\n\n- Nested JSON objects\n\n- Enumeration values\n\n- Array definitions\n\n- Validation constraints\n\n- Parameter descriptions\n\n- Output schemas\n\nWith staged disclosure, the agent initially pays only the small catalog cost. It pays the much larger full-schema cost only for the few tools relevant to the current task.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAssume the registry contains 80 tools but a typical task needs only three.\n\nUnder full upfront loading, the prompt includes:\n\nFull schemas for all 80 tools on every relevant turn\n\nUnder progressive disclosure, the prompt includes:\n\nShort summaries for the catalog + full schemas for only the three selected tools\n\nThe other 77 tools remain registered and available. Their detailed schemas are simply kept outside the active model context until needed.\n\nMCP tools are schema-defined interfaces. Their definitions can include a name, description, input JSON Schema, annotations, and optional output schema. Complex JSON Schemas can be considerably larger than short catalog summaries.\n\nThe savings therefore come from deferring detailed context, not from deleting tools, summarizing schemas lossily, or changing models.","B. Because the staged approach permanently deletes unused tools from the registry over time, so the catalog itself shrinks and there is simply less to load.\n\nProgressive disclosure does not require deleting any tools.\n\nAll registered tools can remain discoverable and available. The design changes only which schemas are inserted into the active prompt for a particular request.\n\nPermanently deleting tools would reduce capabilities, which is not part of the described approach.","C. Because name-and-summary entries and full parameter schemas cost the same number of tokens, so the savings come entirely from using a smaller, cheaper model for the discovery stage.\n\nA name and one-line summary are ordinarily much shorter than a complete parameter schema containing nested definitions, descriptions, constraints, and enumerations.\n\nA smaller discovery model could be an additional optimization, but it is not the reason the staged approach uses fewer prompt tokens. The token reduction occurs because substantially less schema text is supplied to the model.","D. Because staging compresses the full schemas using a lossy summarization pass before ever showing them to the model, trading completeness for token savings.\n\nStaging does not require lossy compression.\n\nThe discovery stage uses intentionally concise metadata for selection. Once a tool is selected, the agent receives its complete, authoritative parameter schema before invocation.\n\nThis preserves exact details such as required parameters, enum values, nested structures, and validation constraints. Lossy schema summarization could remove critical invocation details and is therefore less reliable."],"references":["https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-11-25/server/tools","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/server-concepts","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/develop/clients/client-best-practices","https://docs.databricks.com/gcp/en/agents/mcp/use-mcp-in-agents"],"source_hash":"2f08f9a950b98c080dd7a398511798473d300196801d00f3910cfcc2fcbf6546","stem_ja":"新しいエンジニアが、チームの段階的なツール発見の方式について質問しています。この方式では、エージェントは最初にツール名と1行の概要だけを見て、そのターンで選んだ特定ツールだけの完全な引数スキーマを読み込みます。カタログからツールは削除せず、登録されている総数も同じなのに、なぜカタログ全体の完全なスキーマを最初から読み込む方式より、トークンを大幅に節約できるのでしょうか。\n\n正しい説明はどれですか。","options_ja":["入れ子のオブジェクト、列挙値一覧、説明を含む完全な引数スキーマは、名前と1行概要より通常はるかに多くのトークンを使う。段階化すると、その大きな費用は実際に選んだ少数のツールでだけ発生し、関連性に関係なく毎ターン全登録ツール分を支払わずに済む。","段階的な方式では未使用ツールを徐々にレジストリから永久削除するため、カタログ自体が小さくなり、読み込む量が減る。","名前と概要も完全な引数スキーマも同じトークン数なので、節約はすべて、発見段階に小さく安いモデルを使うことによって生じる。","モデルへ見せる前に、非可逆な要約で完全なスキーマを圧縮し、完全性と引き換えにトークンを節約するためである。"],"explanation_ja":"登録数と、1回のモデル呼び出しに入れる情報量は別です。名前と概要は軽く、引数の詳細定義は重いため、使うものだけ詳細を読むと節約できます。情報を削除したり不正確に要約したりする必要はありません。覚え方は「全部持っていても、全部を毎回見せない」です。","distractors_ja":["重い詳細定義の投入を必要時に限定することが、登録数を変えずに節約できる直接の理由です。","問題ではカタログからツールを削除しないと明示されており、方式の説明と矛盾します。","概要と詳細スキーマのトークン量は通常異なり、小さいモデルへの切替が節約の前提ではありません。","段階的開示は必要な詳細を後で正確に読む設計であり、スキーマの非可逆圧縮が本質ではありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-032","number":32,"form":2,"stem":"A customer-support agent's message history is approaching the context window limit. The transcript includes: (1) a full raw CRM API response from early in the session, dozens of fields, from which the agent extracted and restated the customer's plan tier and renewal date in its own words immediately afterward; (2) the customer's currently-unresolved complaint, restated by the customer just two turns ago; (3) a full raw shipment-tracking API response from turn 3 showing an old, now-superseded shipping status, later fully superseded by a fresh tracking call in turn 40 that the agent is currently relying on; (4) the agent's final proposed resolution, offered in the most recent turn and not yet confirmed by the customer.\n\nSelect the two items that are safe to clear from context without affecting downstream task execution.","options":["The full raw CRM API response from item (1), since its relevant contents (plan tier, renewal date) were already extracted and restated elsewhere in the conversation.","The customer's currently-unresolved complaint from item (2), since it was already stated once and doesn't need to remain in context.","The full raw shipment-tracking response from item (3), since it has been fully superseded by the fresh tracking call in turn 40 that the agent is currently relying on.","The agent's final proposed resolution from item (4), since once offered it no longer serves any function in the ongoing trace."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"抽出済み・後続情報で置換済みのログを削る","concept":"ce-f2-q032","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. The full raw CRM API response from item (1), since its relevant contents (plan tier, renewal date) were already extracted and restated elsewhere in the conversation.\n\nC. The full raw shipment-tracking response from item (3), since it has been fully superseded by the fresh tracking call in turn 40 that the agent is currently relying on.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nSafe context clearing removes information that is redundant or obsolete while preserving information still required to continue the task.\n\nThe four items fall into two categories:\n\n- Safe to clear: Raw tool output whose useful facts have already been retained elsewhere, or output that has been completely superseded by newer authoritative data.\n\n- Must retain: Unresolved user intent and the agent’s latest unconfirmed proposal, because both still affect the next conversational action.\n\nTherefore, the safe choices are A and C.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA is safe to clear because the large CRM response is no longer the only representation of its relevant information. The agent has already extracted and restated the customer’s:\n\n- Plan tier\n\n- Renewal date\n\nKeeping dozens of unrelated raw CRM fields would consume context without adding useful information for the ongoing task. The compacted context should retain the extracted facts, not necessarily the full historical payload.\n\nC is safe to clear because the early shipment response is obsolete. A newer tracking call in turn 40 has fully superseded it and is the status currently driving the agent’s actions.\n\nKeeping both versions could:\n\n- Waste context tokens.\n\n- Introduce conflicting shipment states.\n\n- Cause the model to refer to an outdated status.\n\n- Reduce the prominence of the latest authoritative information.\n\nDatabricks describes context compaction as deciding which information remains active and which older material can be trimmed or summarized as conversations grow.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. The customer's currently-unresolved complaint from item (2), since it was already stated once and doesn't need to remain in context.\n\nThis should not be cleared.\n\nThe complaint is:\n\n- Still unresolved.\n\n- Central to the current support task.\n\n- Recently restated by the customer.\n\n- Necessary for judging whether the proposed resolution actually addresses the problem.\n\nRemoving it could cause the agent to lose the user’s objective, repeat earlier questions, or propose a resolution that addresses the wrong issue.\n\nThe fact that information has appeared before does not make it redundant. Its continuing relevance determines whether it should remain.\n\nD. The agent's final proposed resolution from item (4), since once offered it no longer serves any function in the ongoing trace.\n\nThis should also remain.\n\nThe customer has not yet confirmed, rejected, or modified the proposal. The agent needs the proposed resolution to interpret the customer’s next response, such as:\n\n- “Yes, that works.”\n\n- “No, I already tried that.”\n\n- “Can you change the renewal date instead?”\n\n- “Please proceed.”\n\nClearing the proposal could make those follow-up responses unintelligible or cause the agent to repeat the same recommendation.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming that old information can always be removed or that anything already stated is redundant.\n\nSafe clearing depends on task relevance:\n\n- Old raw output can be cleared when its useful facts are preserved.\n\n- Superseded facts can be cleared when a newer authoritative value exists.\n\n- Unresolved user needs and pending proposals must remain, regardless of repetition or message age.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production compaction policy should classify context by semantic role rather than blindly deleting the oldest messages.\n\nA useful policy is:\n\n- Retain: Current user objective, unresolved issues, commitments, pending decisions, latest authoritative tool results, and confirmed customer facts.\n\n- Summarize: Large tool responses whose relevant fields have been extracted.\n\n- Clear: Fully redundant raw payloads and obsolete results superseded by newer calls.\n\n- Archive outside active context: Complete raw tool outputs for audit and debugging.\n\nThe complete CRM and tracking payloads can remain available in MLflow traces or other durable logs even after they are removed from the model-facing context. This preserves observability without forcing the model to reread every historical API response.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- What is an AI Agent Harness? — Memory and context management\n[https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness](https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness)\n\n- MLflow Tracing — GenAI observability\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/)\n\n- Built-in LLM judges\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. The full raw CRM API response from item (1), since its relevant contents (plan tier, renewal date) were already extracted and restated elsewhere in the conversation.\n\nC. The full raw shipment-tracking response from item (3), since it has been fully superseded by the fresh tracking call in turn 40 that the agent is currently relying on.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nSafe context clearing removes information that is redundant or obsolete while preserving information still required to continue the task.\n\nThe four items fall into two categories:\n\n- Safe to clear: Raw tool output whose useful facts have already been retained elsewhere, or output that has been completely superseded by newer authoritative data.\n\n- Must retain: Unresolved user intent and the agent’s latest unconfirmed proposal, because both still affect the next conversational action.\n\nTherefore, the safe choices are A and C.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA is safe to clear because the large CRM response is no longer the only representation of its relevant information. The agent has already extracted and restated the customer’s:\n\n- Plan tier\n\n- Renewal date\n\nKeeping dozens of unrelated raw CRM fields would consume context without adding useful information for the ongoing task. The compacted context should retain the extracted facts, not necessarily the full historical payload.\n\nC is safe to clear because the early shipment response is obsolete. A newer tracking call in turn 40 has fully superseded it and is the status currently driving the agent’s actions.\n\nKeeping both versions could:\n\n- Waste context tokens.\n\n- Introduce conflicting shipment states.\n\n- Cause the model to refer to an outdated status.\n\n- Reduce the prominence of the latest authoritative information.\n\nDatabricks describes context compaction as deciding which information remains active and which older material can be trimmed or summarized as conversations grow.","B. The customer's currently-unresolved complaint from item (2), since it was already stated once and doesn't need to remain in context.\n\nThis should not be cleared.\n\nThe complaint is:\n\n- Still unresolved.\n\n- Central to the current support task.\n\n- Recently restated by the customer.\n\n- Necessary for judging whether the proposed resolution actually addresses the problem.\n\nRemoving it could cause the agent to lose the user’s objective, repeat earlier questions, or propose a resolution that addresses the wrong issue.\n\nThe fact that information has appeared before does not make it redundant. Its continuing relevance determines whether it should remain.","Correct Answer\n\nA. The full raw CRM API response from item (1), since its relevant contents (plan tier, renewal date) were already extracted and restated elsewhere in the conversation.\n\nC. The full raw shipment-tracking response from item (3), since it has been fully superseded by the fresh tracking call in turn 40 that the agent is currently relying on.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nSafe context clearing removes information that is redundant or obsolete while preserving information still required to continue the task.\n\nThe four items fall into two categories:\n\n- Safe to clear: Raw tool output whose useful facts have already been retained elsewhere, or output that has been completely superseded by newer authoritative data.\n\n- Must retain: Unresolved user intent and the agent’s latest unconfirmed proposal, because both still affect the next conversational action.\n\nTherefore, the safe choices are A and C.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA is safe to clear because the large CRM response is no longer the only representation of its relevant information. The agent has already extracted and restated the customer’s:\n\n- Plan tier\n\n- Renewal date\n\nKeeping dozens of unrelated raw CRM fields would consume context without adding useful information for the ongoing task. The compacted context should retain the extracted facts, not necessarily the full historical payload.\n\nC is safe to clear because the early shipment response is obsolete. A newer tracking call in turn 40 has fully superseded it and is the status currently driving the agent’s actions.\n\nKeeping both versions could:\n\n- Waste context tokens.\n\n- Introduce conflicting shipment states.\n\n- Cause the model to refer to an outdated status.\n\n- Reduce the prominence of the latest authoritative information.\n\nDatabricks describes context compaction as deciding which information remains active and which older material can be trimmed or summarized as conversations grow.","D. The agent's final proposed resolution from item (4), since once offered it no longer serves any function in the ongoing trace.\n\nThis should also remain.\n\nThe customer has not yet confirmed, rejected, or modified the proposal. The agent needs the proposed resolution to interpret the customer’s next response, such as:\n\n- “Yes, that works.”\n\n- “No, I already tried that.”\n\n- “Can you change the renewal date instead?”\n\n- “Please proceed.”\n\nClearing the proposal could make those follow-up responses unintelligible or cause the agent to repeat the same recommendation."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/"],"source_hash":"e699249709c2962a0e02a56ef3754f4ad6df28f73433b0a40ad1936cc3afe25e","correct_many":[0,2],"stem_ja":"顧客サポートエージェントのメッセージ履歴が、コンテキストウィンドウの上限に近づいています。履歴には、(1)セッション初期の多数のフィールドを含むCRM APIの生の応答全文があり、その直後にエージェントは必要な契約プランと更新日を抽出して自分の言葉で書き直しています。(2)顧客の未解決の苦情は、わずか2ターン前に顧客が言い直しています。(3)ターン3の配送追跡APIの生の応答全文は古い配送状態を示し、ターン40の新しい追跡結果によって完全に置き換わっています。エージェントが現在依存しているのも新しい結果です。(4)直近ターンには最終的な解決案がありますが、顧客はまだ承諾していません。\n\n後続タスクの実行に影響せず、コンテキストから安全に消せる項目を2つ選んでください。","options_ja":["必要な内容である契約プランと更新日が、すでに抽出され別の場所に言い直されているため、(1)のCRM APIの生の応答全文。","すでに一度述べられていて残す必要がないため、(2)の現在も未解決の顧客の苦情。","エージェントが現在依存するターン40の新しい追跡結果に完全に置き換わっているため、(3)の配送追跡の生の応答全文。","一度提示すれば進行中のトレースで役割を持たなくなるため、(4)のエージェントの最終解決案。"],"explanation_ja":"消しやすいのは「必要事項を別に保持した生データ」と「新しい結果で完全に置き換わった古いデータ」です。未解決の苦情と未承諾の解決案は、次のやり取りに必要なので残します。覚え方は「抽出済み・置換済みは削減候補、未完了の目的と提案は保持」です。","distractors_ja":["次の判断に必要なフィールドが別の発言に残るため、多数の不要フィールドを含む元応答は削減できます。","未解決の苦情は現在の目的そのもので、1回述べられたというだけでは削除できません。","新しい情報が完全に置き換えており、古い状態を残す方が判断を混乱させる可能性があります。","まだ顧客が受け入れていない提案は、次の確認や調整に必要な作業状態です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-033","number":33,"form":2,"stem":"An HR agent needs to answer: \"How many employees in the Sales department were hired in the last 6 months?\" The following Unity Catalog–registered tools are available:\n\n- get_employee_record(employee_id: STRING) — returns a single employee's profile fields. Accepts one employee ID at a time.\n\n- query_headcount_aggregate(department: STRING, start_date: DATE, end_date: DATE) — a registered SQL function returning aggregate headcount metrics (hire counts, total headcount) filtered by department and date range.\n\n- list_open_positions(department: STRING) — returns unfilled job openings for a department, not hires.\n\n- update_employee_department(employee_id: STRING, new_department: STRING) — a write function that updates an employee's assigned department.\n\nWhich tool is the most appropriate choice for this task, and why?","options":["get_employee_record, because department is a field on the employee record, and the agent can loop over every employee's record individually to compute the count manually.","list_open_positions, because it returns department-scoped results and \"positions\" is closely related in meaning to \"employees.\"","query_headcount_aggregate, because its parameters (department, start_date, end_date) directly match the task's required inputs, and it returns the exact aggregate metric needed as output, without requiring the agent to fetch and manually count individual records.","update_employee_department, because department is directly referenced in the task, so a department-related function is the most functionally relevant match."],"correct":2,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"入力と出力が目的に合うツールの選択","concept":"ce-f2-q033","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. `query_headcount_aggregate`, because its parameters (`department`, `start_date`, `end_date`) directly match the task's required inputs, and it returns the exact aggregate metric needed as output, without requiring the agent to fetch and manually count individual records.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe request is a structured aggregate question:\n\n- Metric: Number of employees hired\n\n- Filter: Sales department\n\n- Time range: Last six months\n\n- Expected output: A single count\n\nThe `query_headcount_aggregate` function directly accepts all required filters and returns the requested aggregate metric. It performs the calculation close to the governed data source instead of requiring the agent to retrieve many individual employee records and count them itself.\n\nThe correct tool-selection principle is to choose the tool whose declared purpose, inputs, and output most closely match the task.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n`query_headcount_aggregate` is specifically designed for headcount and hire-count aggregation. Its signature matches the information available in the user’s request:\n\n```\ndepartment = \"Sales\"start_date = beginning of the six-month periodend_date = current date\n```\n\nIt is preferable because it:\n\n- Applies the department and date filters directly.\n\n- Returns the exact aggregate needed.\n\n- Avoids retrieving unnecessary employee-level information.\n\n- Reduces tool calls, context usage, latency, and failure points.\n\n- Keeps the aggregation inside a governed SQL function.\n\n- Produces more deterministic results than model-side counting.\n\nUnity Catalog SQL functions are appropriate structured-retrieval tools when the required query is known in advance and the agent supplies well-defined parameters.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. `get_employee_record`, because department is a field on the employee record, and the agent can loop over every employee's record individually to compute the count manually.\n\nThis tool returns only one employee record at a time and requires a known employee ID.\n\nTo calculate the requested count, the agent would first need a complete list of employee IDs and then invoke the function repeatedly. This would introduce:\n\n- Numerous tool calls\n\n- Higher latency and cost\n\n- Additional context volume\n\n- Greater risk of skipped or duplicated records\n\n- Unnecessary exposure of employee-level details\n\nAlthough the tool can retrieve relevant fields for one employee, it is not designed for department-level aggregation.\n\nB. `list_open_positions`, because it returns department-scoped results and \"positions\" is closely related in meaning to \"employees.\"\n\nOpen positions are unfilled vacancies, not employees who have already been hired.\n\nThe fact that both concepts relate to staffing does not make them interchangeable. This tool would answer a different question: how many roles remain open in Sales.\n\nTool selection should be based on the tool’s actual operation and output—not merely on semantic similarity between words.\n\nD. `update_employee_department`, because department is directly referenced in the task, so a department-related function is the most functionally relevant match.\n\nThis is a write tool that modifies an employee’s department assignment. The user requested a read-only aggregate count.\n\nCalling it would:\n\n- Fail to answer the question.\n\n- Introduce an unnecessary data mutation.\n\n- Potentially alter authoritative HR records.\n\n- Violate the principle of selecting the least-powerful sufficient tool.\n\nA shared parameter or keyword such as `department` does not make a tool relevant when its underlying action is unrelated.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is selecting a tool based on a matching word—such as “department,” “employee,” or “positions”—rather than evaluating its complete contract.\n\nThe best tool is the one whose:\n\n- Inputs match the known filters.\n\n- Operation matches the requested action.\n\n- Output matches the required result.\n\n- Scope avoids unnecessary reads or writes.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nFor exact, structured aggregation, perform the computation in the data layer rather than retrieving individual rows into the model context.\n\nA production implementation should also:\n\n- Calculate the six-month date boundary deterministically outside the LLM when possible.\n\n- Give the agent read access only to the aggregate function.\n\n- Validate allowed department values.\n\n- Return the applied date range with the result.\n\n- Trace the function parameters and output in MLflow.\n\n- Avoid exposing employee-level records when only an aggregate is required.\n\nThe expected exam answer is C because it is the most direct, efficient, and least-privileged tool for the task.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Connect agents to structured data\n[https://doc](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/structured-retrieval-tools?utm_source=chatgpt.com)[s](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/structured-retrieval-tools)[.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framewo](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/structured-retrieval-tools?utm_source=chatgpt.com)[r](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/structured-retrieval-tools)[k/structured-retrieval-tools](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/structured-retrieval-tools?utm_source=chatgpt.com)\n\n- Create AI agent tools using Unity Catalog functions\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/create-custom-tool](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/create-custom-tool)\n\n- Connect agents to tools\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/agent-tool](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/agent-tool)\n\n- Function calling on Databricks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/model-serving/function-calling](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/model-serving/function-calling)","source_option_explanations":["A. `get_employee_record`, because department is a field on the employee record, and the agent can loop over every employee's record individually to compute the count manually.\n\nThis tool returns only one employee record at a time and requires a known employee ID.\n\nTo calculate the requested count, the agent would first need a complete list of employee IDs and then invoke the function repeatedly. This would introduce:\n\n- Numerous tool calls\n\n- Higher latency and cost\n\n- Additional context volume\n\n- Greater risk of skipped or duplicated records\n\n- Unnecessary exposure of employee-level details\n\nAlthough the tool can retrieve relevant fields for one employee, it is not designed for department-level aggregation.","B. `list_open_positions`, because it returns department-scoped results and \"positions\" is closely related in meaning to \"employees.\"\n\nOpen positions are unfilled vacancies, not employees who have already been hired.\n\nThe fact that both concepts relate to staffing does not make them interchangeable. This tool would answer a different question: how many roles remain open in Sales.\n\nTool selection should be based on the tool’s actual operation and output—not merely on semantic similarity between words.","Correct Answer\n\nC. `query_headcount_aggregate`, because its parameters (`department`, `start_date`, `end_date`) directly match the task's required inputs, and it returns the exact aggregate metric needed as output, without requiring the agent to fetch and manually count individual records.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe request is a structured aggregate question:\n\n- Metric: Number of employees hired\n\n- Filter: Sales department\n\n- Time range: Last six months\n\n- Expected output: A single count\n\nThe `query_headcount_aggregate` function directly accepts all required filters and returns the requested aggregate metric. It performs the calculation close to the governed data source instead of requiring the agent to retrieve many individual employee records and count them itself.\n\nThe correct tool-selection principle is to choose the tool whose declared purpose, inputs, and output most closely match the task.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n`query_headcount_aggregate` is specifically designed for headcount and hire-count aggregation. Its signature matches the information available in the user’s request:\n\n```\ndepartment = \"Sales\"start_date = beginning of the six-month periodend_date = current date\n```\n\nIt is preferable because it:\n\n- Applies the department and date filters directly.\n\n- Returns the exact aggregate needed.\n\n- Avoids retrieving unnecessary employee-level information.\n\n- Reduces tool calls, context usage, latency, and failure points.\n\n- Keeps the aggregation inside a governed SQL function.\n\n- Produces more deterministic results than model-side counting.\n\nUnity Catalog SQL functions are appropriate structured-retrieval tools when the required query is known in advance and the agent supplies well-defined parameters.","D. `update_employee_department`, because department is directly referenced in the task, so a department-related function is the most functionally relevant match.\n\nThis is a write tool that modifies an employee’s department assignment. The user requested a read-only aggregate count.\n\nCalling it would:\n\n- Fail to answer the question.\n\n- Introduce an unnecessary data mutation.\n\n- Potentially alter authoritative HR records.\n\n- Violate the principle of selecting the least-powerful sufficient tool.\n\nA shared parameter or keyword such as `department` does not make a tool relevant when its underlying action is unrelated."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/structured-retrieval-tools?utm_source=chatgpt.com","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/structured-retrieval-tools","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/create-custom-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/agent-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/model-serving/function-calling"],"source_hash":"20d06259af9220faf9db38669609047ad025bd23e7231e183493bf7833d87a1b","stem_ja":"人事エージェントが「過去6か月に採用された営業部門の従業員は何人か」へ答える必要があります。Unity Catalogに登録された次のツールを利用できます。\n\n- get_employee_record(employee_id: STRING)：従業員1人のプロフィール項目を返す。1回に1つの従業員IDを受け取る。\n- query_headcount_aggregate(department: STRING, start_date: DATE, end_date: DATE)：部門と期間で絞った採用人数・総人員などの集計値を返す、登録済みSQL関数。\n- list_open_positions(department: STRING)：部門の未充足の求人を返す。採用者ではない。\n- update_employee_department(employee_id: STRING, new_department: STRING)：従業員の所属部門を更新する書き込み関数。\n\nこのタスクで最も適切なツールはどれですか。その理由も選んでください。","options_ja":["departmentは従業員レコードの項目であり、全従業員を1人ずつ取得して手作業で数えられるため、get_employee_record。","部門で絞られた結果を返し、「求人」は「従業員」と意味が近いため、list_open_positions。","department、start_date、end_dateが必要な入力へ直接対応し、個別レコードを取得して手作業で数えず、必要な集計指標をそのまま返すため、query_headcount_aggregate。","タスクにdepartmentが直接登場するため、部門に関係する関数が機能上最も近いものとして、update_employee_department。"],"explanation_ja":"名前の単語が近いかではなく、「必要な入力を受け、求める出力を返すか」を確認します。部門と期間を渡して採用人数を返す集計関数なら、目的に直接合い、大量の個別レコードも不要です。覚え方は「入力条件と出力型で選ぶ」。集計質問に書き込みツールを使わないことも重要です。","distractors_ja":["1人分を取る機能で、必要な部門別・期間別の人数集計を直接返せず、多数の呼び出しが必要になります。","未充足求人の件数と、すでに採用された従業員数は異なる指標です。","質問の条件である部門と期間を受け、必要な採用人数をそのまま返せます。","質問は参照と集計であり、所属部門を変更する書き込み操作は不要です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-034","number":34,"form":2,"stem":"A Databricks infrastructure agent's system prompt has grown to include detailed instructions for 11 distinct capabilities. The 2 capabilities used in over 95% of requests — checking deployment status and restarting a service — are described concisely in a few lines each. The other 9, covering rare scenarios like initiating a full disaster-recovery failover, rotating root cloud credentials, and decommissioning a region, each include multiple paragraphs of detailed procedural instructions, edge cases, and required safety confirmations. Every single request pays the token cost of all 11 capabilities' instructions, even though the 9 rare ones are each invoked less than a few times per year.\n\nWhich loading strategy for these rarely-invoked capabilities minimizes baseline context cost without harming task success rate?","options":["Delete the instructions for the 9 rare capabilities entirely, since they are invoked so infrequently that supporting them well is not worth any token cost.","Keep all 11 capabilities' full instructions in the system prompt exactly as they are today, since removing any instructions risks the agent failing on a rare-but-important request.","Move all 11 capabilities, including the 2 frequently used ones, out of the system prompt and into on-demand Agent Skills, so the baseline system prompt contains no capability instructions at all.","Package each of the 9 rarely-invoked capabilities as a separate Agent Skill, loaded into context only when the agent's task recognition indicates that specific capability is needed, keeping only the 2 high-frequency capabilities' concise instructions in the always-loaded baseline system prompt."],"correct":3,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"低頻度の運用手順をAgent Skillsへ分離","concept":"ce-f2-q034","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Package each of the 9 rarely-invoked capabilities as a separate Agent Skill, loaded into context only when the agent's task recognition indicates that specific capability is needed, keeping only the 2 high-frequency capabilities' concise instructions in the always-loaded baseline system prompt.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe important pattern is the difference between usage frequency and instruction size:\n\n- Two capabilities are needed in more than 95% of requests and have concise instructions.\n\n- Nine capabilities are rarely used but have long, detailed procedures.\n\n- All requests currently pay the token cost of all eleven capabilities.\n\nThe best design keeps the compact, frequently needed instructions immediately available while moving the large, rarely needed procedures into on-demand Agent Skills.\n\nThis reduces the baseline prompt cost on normal requests without removing support for rare but critical operations.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAgent Skills are appropriate for detailed, task-specific guidance that does not need to be applied globally. The agent can identify the relevant skill through its name and description, then load its complete procedures, examples, edge cases, and safety requirements only when that capability is required.\n\nThe resulting design is:\n\n- Always loaded: Deployment-status checks and service restarts.\n\n- Loaded conditionally: Disaster-recovery failover, credential rotation, regional decommissioning, and the other rare workflows.\n\nThis provides the strongest balance of:\n\n- Lower baseline token usage.\n\n- Retained support for all eleven capabilities.\n\n- Full procedural context for rare tasks when needed.\n\n- Easier maintenance because each rare workflow is isolated in its own skill.\n\n- Reduced distraction from irrelevant high-risk procedures during routine requests.\n\nThe skill description must clearly state when the skill applies so the agent can recognize and load the correct capability.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Delete the instructions for the 9 rare capabilities entirely, since they are invoked so infrequently that supporting them well is not worth any token cost.\n\nThis reduces tokens by eliminating capabilities, not by loading them efficiently.\n\nThe rare operations are also consequential tasks such as disaster recovery, root-credential rotation, and regional decommissioning. Their low frequency does not make correct execution unimportant.\n\nDeleting their instructions would likely reduce task success precisely where detailed safety procedures are most necessary.\n\nB. Keep all 11 capabilities' full instructions in the system prompt exactly as they are today, since removing any instructions risks the agent failing on a rare-but-important request.\n\nThis preserves task coverage but does not address the excessive baseline token cost.\n\nEvery routine deployment-status check would continue carrying multiple paragraphs about unrelated disaster recovery, credential rotation, and regional decommissioning.\n\nOn-demand skills preserve those instructions without placing them in every request.\n\nC. Move all 11 capabilities, including the 2 frequently used ones, out of the system prompt and into on-demand Agent Skills, so the baseline system prompt contains no capability instructions at all.\n\nThis is technically possible, but it is not the best strategy for the described usage distribution.\n\nThe two compact capabilities are needed in more than 95% of requests. Moving them behind skill discovery would add a skill-selection and loading step to nearly every interaction while saving only a small number of baseline tokens.\n\nFrequently used, concise guidance belongs in the always-loaded baseline. Rare, verbose guidance benefits most from conditional loading.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is applying one loading strategy uniformly to every capability.\n\nThe optimal division depends on both:\n\n- How frequently a capability is used.\n\n- How much context its instructions consume.\n\nKeep compact, high-frequency guidance static. Load verbose, low-frequency guidance dynamically.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nEach rare skill should have a narrow scope and a distinctive description, for example:\n\n```\nSkill: initiate_disaster_recovery_failover Use only when the request explicitly requires failing production servicesover to the designated disaster-recovery region. Includes prerequisitevalidation, approval requirements, replication checks, execution steps,rollback criteria, and post-failover verification.\n```\n\nFor high-risk skills, on-demand loading should not replace authorization controls. The implementation should also enforce:\n\n- Identity and permission checks.\n\n- Required human confirmations.\n\n- Precondition validation.\n\n- Dry-run or impact-review steps.\n\n- Auditable tool calls.\n\n- Post-action verification.\n\nThe expected exam answer is D because it minimizes routine context cost while preserving complete instructions and task support for rare capabilities.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Extend Genie Code with agent skills\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/skills](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/skills)\n\n- Agent skills for AI coding assistants\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agent-skills/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agent-skills/)\n\n- Customize Genie Code with custom instructions\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/instructions](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/instructions)\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)","source_option_explanations":["A. Delete the instructions for the 9 rare capabilities entirely, since they are invoked so infrequently that supporting them well is not worth any token cost.\n\nThis reduces tokens by eliminating capabilities, not by loading them efficiently.\n\nThe rare operations are also consequential tasks such as disaster recovery, root-credential rotation, and regional decommissioning. Their low frequency does not make correct execution unimportant.\n\nDeleting their instructions would likely reduce task success precisely where detailed safety procedures are most necessary.","B. Keep all 11 capabilities' full instructions in the system prompt exactly as they are today, since removing any instructions risks the agent failing on a rare-but-important request.\n\nThis preserves task coverage but does not address the excessive baseline token cost.\n\nEvery routine deployment-status check would continue carrying multiple paragraphs about unrelated disaster recovery, credential rotation, and regional decommissioning.\n\nOn-demand skills preserve those instructions without placing them in every request.","C. Move all 11 capabilities, including the 2 frequently used ones, out of the system prompt and into on-demand Agent Skills, so the baseline system prompt contains no capability instructions at all.\n\nThis is technically possible, but it is not the best strategy for the described usage distribution.\n\nThe two compact capabilities are needed in more than 95% of requests. Moving them behind skill discovery would add a skill-selection and loading step to nearly every interaction while saving only a small number of baseline tokens.\n\nFrequently used, concise guidance belongs in the always-loaded baseline. Rare, verbose guidance benefits most from conditional loading.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Package each of the 9 rarely-invoked capabilities as a separate Agent Skill, loaded into context only when the agent's task recognition indicates that specific capability is needed, keeping only the 2 high-frequency capabilities' concise instructions in the always-loaded baseline system prompt.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe important pattern is the difference between usage frequency and instruction size:\n\n- Two capabilities are needed in more than 95% of requests and have concise instructions.\n\n- Nine capabilities are rarely used but have long, detailed procedures.\n\n- All requests currently pay the token cost of all eleven capabilities.\n\nThe best design keeps the compact, frequently needed instructions immediately available while moving the large, rarely needed procedures into on-demand Agent Skills.\n\nThis reduces the baseline prompt cost on normal requests without removing support for rare but critical operations.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAgent Skills are appropriate for detailed, task-specific guidance that does not need to be applied globally. The agent can identify the relevant skill through its name and description, then load its complete procedures, examples, edge cases, and safety requirements only when that capability is required.\n\nThe resulting design is:\n\n- Always loaded: Deployment-status checks and service restarts.\n\n- Loaded conditionally: Disaster-recovery failover, credential rotation, regional decommissioning, and the other rare workflows.\n\nThis provides the strongest balance of:\n\n- Lower baseline token usage.\n\n- Retained support for all eleven capabilities.\n\n- Full procedural context for rare tasks when needed.\n\n- Easier maintenance because each rare workflow is isolated in its own skill.\n\n- Reduced distraction from irrelevant high-risk procedures during routine requests.\n\nThe skill description must clearly state when the skill applies so the agent can recognize and load the correct capability."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/skills","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agent-skills/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/instructions","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns"],"source_hash":"2378e90a329d40e33868f3d28477698740b414626e5affb58e554359cc8dee18","stem_ja":"Databricksの基盤運用エージェントのシステムプロンプトに、11種類の機能の詳細な指示が蓄積しています。95％を超えるリクエストで使う2機能、デプロイ状態の確認とサービスの再起動は、それぞれ数行で簡潔に説明されています。残る9機能は、完全な災害復旧フェイルオーバーの開始、クラウドのルート認証情報のローテーション、リージョンの廃止などのまれな状況に対応し、複数段落の手順、例外条件、必要な安全確認が書かれています。9機能はそれぞれ年に数回未満しか呼ばれないのに、毎リクエストで11機能すべての指示のトークンコストがかかります。\n\n成功率を損なわずに、低頻度機能の指示による基本コンテキストの費用を最小化する読み込み戦略はどれですか。","options_ja":["利用が極めて少なく、適切に支援する価値がトークンコストに見合わないため、9機能の指示を完全に削除する。","指示を外すと、まれでも重要な依頼で失敗する危険があるため、11機能すべての完全な指示を今のままシステムプロンプトへ残す。","高頻度の2機能も含めて11機能すべてを必要時のAgent Skillsへ移し、基本システムプロンプトから機能の指示を完全になくす。","低頻度の9機能をそれぞれ独立したAgent Skillとしてまとめ、タスク認識で必要と判断したときだけ読み込む。常時読み込む基本システムプロンプトには、高頻度の2機能の簡潔な指示だけを残す。"],"explanation_ja":"まれに必要な詳細手順は削除せず、Agent Skillsとして必要時に読み込みます。普段の大半を占める2機能の短い指示は基本プロンプトに残せば、主な処理の成功率を保ちながら負荷を減らせます。覚え方は「よく使う短い指示は常駐、まれな長い手順は必要時」です。","distractors_ja":["低頻度でも災害復旧などは重要で、手順を削除すると必要時の成功率を損ないます。","まれな長文手順を全依頼へ投入する不要なコストが残り、最小化できません。","高頻度で短い2機能まで外すと、ほぼ毎回追加の読み込みが必要になり、今回の利用分布に合いません。","必要な能力と詳細手順を保持しながら、普段のプロンプトから低頻度の長文だけを除けます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-035","number":35,"form":2,"stem":"A context engineer oversees a long-running sales-outreach agent conducting a multi-touch prospecting sequence with a single prospect across dozens of turns. After a compaction event, the agent sends the prospect a follow-up offer with a discount code the prospect had already explicitly declined earlier in the same sequence. Inspection of the compacted context shows the compaction prompt collapsed the session into a single summary of the current best next action and the prospect's stated interests. The compaction prompt currently reads: \"Summarize the prospect's interests and the current best next action.\"\n\nWhich revised compaction prompt addresses the root cause of this failure without significantly increasing token cost?","options":["Summarize the prospect's interests and the current best next action, and include a deduplicated list of offers, discounts, or approaches the prospect has already explicitly declined.","Summarize the prospect's interests and the current best next action, and append the complete raw text of every message sent by the agent during the sequence.","Summarize the prospect's interests and the current best next action, and include a record of the agent's internal reasoning behind each prior outreach attempt.","Summarize the prospect's interests and the current best next action, and append verbatim copies of every message the prospect has sent."],"correct":0,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"圧縮後も拒否済みの提案を保持","concept":"ce-f2-q035","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Summarize the prospect's interests and the current best next action, and include a deduplicated list of offers, discounts, or approaches the prospect has already explicitly declined.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe compaction process preserved only:\n\n- The prospect’s interests\n\n- The recommended next action\n\nIt discarded a critical negative constraint: the prospect had already rejected the discount code.\n\nAs a result, the agent retained enough context to continue outreach, but not enough to avoid repeating a previously rejected approach. The revised prompt should explicitly preserve declined offers while keeping the representation compact.\n\nOption A addresses the failure directly by adding a short, deduplicated list rather than replaying the complete conversation.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAn effective compacted state must preserve information that affects future decisions, including:\n\n- Confirmed interests\n\n- Current objectives\n\n- Outstanding actions\n\n- Commitments already made\n\n- Approaches already attempted\n\n- Explicit user rejections and prohibitions\n\nA rejected offer is not incidental history. It is an active constraint on future outreach. Without it, the agent can repeat an inappropriate action even if its summary of the prospect’s interests is otherwise accurate.\n\nA suitable compacted summary could look like:\n\n```\nProspect interests:- Enterprise reporting- SSO integration Current best next action:- Offer a technical architecture session Explicitly declined:- 15% annual-plan discount- Free migration consultation\n```\n\nThe `deduplicated` requirement is important because the agent might have referenced the same rejected offer multiple times. Recording it once preserves the decision without unnecessarily increasing token usage.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Summarize the prospect's interests and the current best next action, and append the complete raw text of every message sent by the agent during the sequence.\n\nThis would preserve far more information than necessary and undermine the purpose of compaction.\n\nIt would:\n\n- Substantially increase context size\n\n- Preserve repetitive outreach messages\n\n- Include obsolete proposals\n\n- Make important rejections harder to identify\n\n- Eventually recreate the original context-window problem\n\nOnly the consequential state—such as attempted and rejected approaches—needs to be retained.\n\nC. Summarize the prospect's interests and the current best next action, and include a record of the agent's internal reasoning behind each prior outreach attempt.\n\nThe agent’s historical reasoning is not the missing information.\n\nThe failure occurred because the prospect’s explicit rejection was lost. Preserving internal rationale would add substantial context without reliably recording the externally confirmed constraint.\n\nFuture execution should be based on confirmed conversation state, not lengthy historical reasoning traces.\n\nD. Summarize the prospect's interests and the current best next action, and append verbatim copies of every message the prospect has sent.\n\nThis would likely preserve the rejection, but inefficiently.\n\nDozens of prospect messages could contain:\n\n- Greetings\n\n- Repeated details\n\n- Superseded information\n\n- Scheduling exchanges\n\n- Unrelated discussion\n\nA concise list of explicit rejections preserves the required decision state at much lower token cost.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is treating compaction as a generic summarization task that only preserves positive goals and the next action.\n\nLong-running agents must also retain negative state, including:\n\n- Rejected proposals\n\n- Prohibited actions\n\n- Failed approaches\n\n- Already-completed actions\n\n- User boundaries and constraints\n\nOtherwise, the compacted summary can appear coherent while causing the agent to repeat earlier mistakes.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production compaction prompt should explicitly preserve both positive and negative task state. For example:\n\nSummarize the prospect’s interests, confirmed facts, current best next action, unresolved questions, commitments, and a deduplicated list of offers or approaches explicitly declined or already attempted unsuccessfully. Exclude repetitive dialogue and obsolete intermediate reasoning.\n\nFor greater reliability, important rejections can also be maintained as structured state:\n\n```\n{  \"declined_offers\": [    {      \"offer\": \"15% annual-plan discount\",      \"status\": \"explicitly_declined\"    }  ]}\n```\n\nMLflow evaluations should include long-conversation test cases that verify the agent does not repeat declined offers after compaction.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- What is an AI Agent Harness? — Memory and context management\n[https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness](https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness)\n\n- Evaluate conversations\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations)\n\n- Agent memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Summarize the prospect's interests and the current best next action, and include a deduplicated list of offers, discounts, or approaches the prospect has already explicitly declined.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe compaction process preserved only:\n\n- The prospect’s interests\n\n- The recommended next action\n\nIt discarded a critical negative constraint: the prospect had already rejected the discount code.\n\nAs a result, the agent retained enough context to continue outreach, but not enough to avoid repeating a previously rejected approach. The revised prompt should explicitly preserve declined offers while keeping the representation compact.\n\nOption A addresses the failure directly by adding a short, deduplicated list rather than replaying the complete conversation.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAn effective compacted state must preserve information that affects future decisions, including:\n\n- Confirmed interests\n\n- Current objectives\n\n- Outstanding actions\n\n- Commitments already made\n\n- Approaches already attempted\n\n- Explicit user rejections and prohibitions\n\nA rejected offer is not incidental history. It is an active constraint on future outreach. Without it, the agent can repeat an inappropriate action even if its summary of the prospect’s interests is otherwise accurate.\n\nA suitable compacted summary could look like:\n\n```\nProspect interests:- Enterprise reporting- SSO integration Current best next action:- Offer a technical architecture session Explicitly declined:- 15% annual-plan discount- Free migration consultation\n```\n\nThe `deduplicated` requirement is important because the agent might have referenced the same rejected offer multiple times. Recording it once preserves the decision without unnecessarily increasing token usage.","B. Summarize the prospect's interests and the current best next action, and append the complete raw text of every message sent by the agent during the sequence.\n\nThis would preserve far more information than necessary and undermine the purpose of compaction.\n\nIt would:\n\n- Substantially increase context size\n\n- Preserve repetitive outreach messages\n\n- Include obsolete proposals\n\n- Make important rejections harder to identify\n\n- Eventually recreate the original context-window problem\n\nOnly the consequential state—such as attempted and rejected approaches—needs to be retained.","C. Summarize the prospect's interests and the current best next action, and include a record of the agent's internal reasoning behind each prior outreach attempt.\n\nThe agent’s historical reasoning is not the missing information.\n\nThe failure occurred because the prospect’s explicit rejection was lost. Preserving internal rationale would add substantial context without reliably recording the externally confirmed constraint.\n\nFuture execution should be based on confirmed conversation state, not lengthy historical reasoning traces.","D. Summarize the prospect's interests and the current best next action, and append verbatim copies of every message the prospect has sent.\n\nThis would likely preserve the rejection, but inefficiently.\n\nDozens of prospect messages could contain:\n\n- Greetings\n\n- Repeated details\n\n- Superseded information\n\n- Scheduling exchanges\n\n- Unrelated discussion\n\nA concise list of explicit rejections preserves the required decision state at much lower token cost."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents"],"source_hash":"ff255c511e5e046b4c4f208d0a688a10dd1832974252c971bcdee1b8aeb1f8b6","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、1人の見込み客に数十ターンかけて複数回の営業連絡を行う、長時間実行のエージェントを管理しています。圧縮の後、エージェントは、その一連の連絡の前半ですでに明示的に断られた割引コードを、追加提案として再送しました。圧縮後のコンテキストを調べると、見込み客の関心と現時点で最善の次の行動だけを1つの要約にしていました。現在の圧縮プロンプトは「見込み客の関心と、現時点で最善の次の行動を要約する」です。\n\nトークンコストを大きく増やさずに、原因に対処する修正版の圧縮プロンプトはどれですか。","options_ja":["見込み客の関心と、現時点で最善の次の行動を要約し、すでに明示的に断られた提案・割引・アプローチを重複を除いた一覧として含める。","見込み客の関心と、現時点で最善の次の行動を要約し、これまでエージェントが送った全メッセージの生の全文を追加する。","見込み客の関心と、現時点で最善の次の行動を要約し、過去の各営業連絡について、エージェントが内部で行った推論の記録を含める。","見込み客の関心と、現時点で最善の次の行動を要約し、見込み客が送った全メッセージを一字一句そのまま追加する。"],"explanation_ja":"要約から「もう勧めてはいけないもの」が抜けたため、同じ割引を再提案しています。拒否済みの提案を短い重複のない一覧で残せば、全文や内部推論を保存せずに再発を防げます。覚え方は「次にすることだけでなく、してはいけないことも残す」。後続判断を制約する過去の決定は重要です。","distractors_ja":["今回失われた拒否の履歴だけを簡潔に残し、同じ不適切な再提案を直接防げます。","必要なのは拒否の決定であり、エージェントの全送信文を残すと圧縮の節約効果を損ないます。","内部推論より、何を提案して何が拒否されたかという検証可能な決定・結果が必要です。","顧客の全文保存は過剰で、拒否済み項目を短く構造化する方が要件に合います。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-036","number":36,"form":2,"stem":"A team is tuning the compaction prompt for an engineering-incident-postmortem agent's long investigation traces. Their first version aggressively dropped all timestamps and specific error codes to save tokens. After deploying this version, the agent's root-cause reports became vague, unable to reference exact failure times or specific error codes needed to make the report actionable. The team wants to correct course using an appropriate tuning process, without immediately reverting to zero compaction.\n\nWhich iteration approach should the team follow to tune the compaction prompt effectively?","options":["Keep the original overly aggressive compaction prompt unchanged, and instead compensate by increasing the model's context window so any missed details matter less.","Skip iterative tuning entirely and disable compaction altogether, since any compaction prompt that has previously caused a quality drop can never be trusted going forward.","Immediately optimize for precision by trimming the current summary format even further, since a shorter summary is always a safe next step after any observed quality drop.","First revise the compaction prompt to maximize recall by capturing everything plausibly relevant to downstream tasks — including timestamps and error codes — even at the cost of some added length, and validate that report quality recovers; only then iterate to improve precision by removing content shown through testing to be genuinely superfluous."],"correct":3,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"圧縮は再現率を回復してから精度を改善","concept":"ce-f2-q036","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. First revise the compaction prompt to maximize recall by capturing everything plausibly relevant to downstream tasks — including timestamps and error codes — even at the cost of some added length, and validate that report quality recovers; only then iterate to improve precision by removing content shown through testing to be genuinely superfluous.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe first compaction prompt optimized too aggressively for brevity. It removed details that appeared small but were essential to the agent’s downstream task:\n\n- Exact incident timestamps establish event sequence and duration.\n\n- Specific error codes connect symptoms to systems, logs, and remediation steps.\n\n- Both make a postmortem actionable and verifiable.\n\nThe appropriate tuning sequence is therefore:\n\n- Restore high recall by preserving all information that might materially affect the final report.\n\n- Evaluate whether root-cause quality and actionability recover.\n\n- Remove only the content demonstrated through testing to be unnecessary.\n\nThis is safer than trying to create the shortest possible summary before confirming that it still preserves all task-critical evidence.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nContext compaction should reduce accumulated context without discarding information needed for later reasoning. Databricks describes compaction as the harness deciding what remains active and what can be summarized as a conversation or workflow grows.\n\nThe failed prompt treated timestamps and error codes as expendable details, but the downstream task specifically requires precise incident reconstruction. A revised prompt could state:\n\nSummarize the investigation while preserving exact timestamps, error codes, affected components, causal dependencies, confirmed findings, rejected hypotheses, remediation actions, and unresolved questions. Remove repetitive discussion and superseded intermediate observations.\n\nThe team should initially tolerate a somewhat longer summary to verify that the necessary evidence is retained. Once report quality has recovered, it can compare compaction-prompt versions against the same evaluation set and remove only information that does not reduce correctness, completeness, or actionability. MLflow evaluation datasets support testing fixes against known production failures, comparing application versions, and detecting regressions.\n\nThis reflects the correct tuning order:\n\nFirst prevent information loss; then optimize token efficiency.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Keep the original overly aggressive compaction prompt unchanged, and instead compensate by increasing the model's context window so any missed details matter less.\n\nA larger context window cannot recover details that the compaction process has already removed.\n\nOnce timestamps and error codes are omitted from the compacted state, the model no longer has access to them regardless of its maximum context capacity. This option increases available capacity without fixing the information-selection policy that caused the failure.\n\nB. Skip iterative tuning entirely and disable compaction altogether, since any compaction prompt that has previously caused a quality drop can never be trusted going forward.\n\nThe failure demonstrates that the current compaction prompt is poorly calibrated, not that compaction is inherently unsuitable.\n\nDisabling compaction would eventually reintroduce context-window pressure, higher token costs, and degradation across long investigation traces. The appropriate response is to revise, test, and validate the compaction policy rather than abandon it after one unsuccessful version.\n\nC. Immediately optimize for precision by trimming the current summary format even further, since a shorter summary is always a safe next step after any observed quality drop.\n\nThis moves in the wrong direction.\n\nThe observed failure is already caused by insufficient recall: downstream-critical details were discarded. Further trimming would probably remove additional evidence and make the reports even less specific.\n\nShorter context is useful only when the removed information is genuinely redundant or irrelevant. Brevity is not the primary objective; reliable downstream task execution is.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is treating the shortest compacted summary as the best one.\n\nCompaction must balance:\n\n- Recall: Retaining all details needed later\n\n- Precision: Excluding genuinely irrelevant or redundant material\n\n- Token efficiency: Keeping the retained representation concise\n\nWhen quality declines because necessary facts were omitted, restore recall first. Precision optimization comes only after testing confirms which content can safely be removed.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe team should build a regression evaluation set from incident traces that require exact details, including:\n\n- Multi-stage failures where event order matters\n\n- Repeated errors with different codes\n\n- Superseded hypotheses\n\n- Exact outage and recovery timestamps\n\n- Failures involving several dependent services\n\nFor every compaction-prompt version, evaluate whether the final postmortem retains:\n\n- The correct timeline\n\n- Exact error codes\n\n- Confirmed root cause\n\n- Supporting evidence\n\n- Remediation and prevention actions\n\n- Unresolved uncertainties\n\nMLflow supports session-level evaluation for long conversations, including knowledge-retention and conversation-completeness analysis, and it also supports custom judges for task-specific requirements.\n\nThe expected exam answer is D because it follows an evaluation-driven, recall-first tuning strategy rather than optimizing token reduction before preserving downstream utility.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- What is an AI Agent Harness?\n[https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness](https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness)\n\n- Tutorial: Evaluate and improve a GenAI application\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app)\n\n- Building MLflow evaluation datasets\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset)\n\n- Evaluate conversations\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations)","source_option_explanations":["A. Keep the original overly aggressive compaction prompt unchanged, and instead compensate by increasing the model's context window so any missed details matter less.\n\nA larger context window cannot recover details that the compaction process has already removed.\n\nOnce timestamps and error codes are omitted from the compacted state, the model no longer has access to them regardless of its maximum context capacity. This option increases available capacity without fixing the information-selection policy that caused the failure.","B. Skip iterative tuning entirely and disable compaction altogether, since any compaction prompt that has previously caused a quality drop can never be trusted going forward.\n\nThe failure demonstrates that the current compaction prompt is poorly calibrated, not that compaction is inherently unsuitable.\n\nDisabling compaction would eventually reintroduce context-window pressure, higher token costs, and degradation across long investigation traces. The appropriate response is to revise, test, and validate the compaction policy rather than abandon it after one unsuccessful version.","C. Immediately optimize for precision by trimming the current summary format even further, since a shorter summary is always a safe next step after any observed quality drop.\n\nThis moves in the wrong direction.\n\nThe observed failure is already caused by insufficient recall: downstream-critical details were discarded. Further trimming would probably remove additional evidence and make the reports even less specific.\n\nShorter context is useful only when the removed information is genuinely redundant or irrelevant. Brevity is not the primary objective; reliable downstream task execution is.","Correct Answer\n\nD. First revise the compaction prompt to maximize recall by capturing everything plausibly relevant to downstream tasks — including timestamps and error codes — even at the cost of some added length, and validate that report quality recovers; only then iterate to improve precision by removing content shown through testing to be genuinely superfluous.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe first compaction prompt optimized too aggressively for brevity. It removed details that appeared small but were essential to the agent’s downstream task:\n\n- Exact incident timestamps establish event sequence and duration.\n\n- Specific error codes connect symptoms to systems, logs, and remediation steps.\n\n- Both make a postmortem actionable and verifiable.\n\nThe appropriate tuning sequence is therefore:\n\n- Restore high recall by preserving all information that might materially affect the final report.\n\n- Evaluate whether root-cause quality and actionability recover.\n\n- Remove only the content demonstrated through testing to be unnecessary.\n\nThis is safer than trying to create the shortest possible summary before confirming that it still preserves all task-critical evidence.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nContext compaction should reduce accumulated context without discarding information needed for later reasoning. Databricks describes compaction as the harness deciding what remains active and what can be summarized as a conversation or workflow grows.\n\nThe failed prompt treated timestamps and error codes as expendable details, but the downstream task specifically requires precise incident reconstruction. A revised prompt could state:\n\nSummarize the investigation while preserving exact timestamps, error codes, affected components, causal dependencies, confirmed findings, rejected hypotheses, remediation actions, and unresolved questions. Remove repetitive discussion and superseded intermediate observations.\n\nThe team should initially tolerate a somewhat longer summary to verify that the necessary evidence is retained. Once report quality has recovered, it can compare compaction-prompt versions against the same evaluation set and remove only information that does not reduce correctness, completeness, or actionability. MLflow evaluation datasets support testing fixes against known production failures, comparing application versions, and detecting regressions.\n\nThis reflects the correct tuning order:"],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations"],"source_hash":"1f4b797b182a0ddbc1f87f3a7f92056f86409413dc8db78dafae429f02f0f896","stem_ja":"チームが、技術障害の振り返りエージェントの長い調査トレースを圧縮するプロンプトを調整しています。最初の版は、トークン節約のため、すべてのタイムスタンプと具体的なエラーコードを積極的に削除しました。導入後、根本原因報告は曖昧になり、対処に必要な正確な障害時刻やエラーコードへ言及できなくなりました。チームは、圧縮をただちに全廃するのではなく、適切な調整手順で方針を修正したいと考えています。\n\n圧縮プロンプトを有効に調整するため、どの反復方法を採るべきですか。","options_ja":["過剰に削る元のプロンプトを変えずに維持し、代わりにモデルのコンテキストウィンドウを増やして、失われた詳細の重要性を下げる。","一度品質を落とした圧縮プロンプトは今後も信頼できないため、反復調整をせず圧縮を完全に無効にする。","短い要約は品質低下の後でも常に安全な次の一歩なので、現状の要約をさらに削り、ただちに精度を最適化する。","まず長さが多少増えても、時刻やエラーコードを含む後続タスクに関係しそうな情報を広く残し、再現率を最大化するようプロンプトを修正して、報告品質の回復を検証する。その後、テストで本当に不要と判明した内容を除き、精度を改善する。"],"explanation_ja":"必要な詳細を落としている間は、まず保持すべき情報の取り漏れをなくします。時刻やエラーコードを復元して品質回復を確認した後、不要と検証できた情報だけを減らします。覚え方は「先に取り漏れを止め、後から余分を削る」。短さを先に最適化すると、同じ情報欠落を繰り返します。","distractors_ja":["大きな窓に変えても、圧縮プロンプトが削除した時刻やエラーコードは復元されません。","問題は調整可能な情報の落とし方であり、圧縮を全面廃止する前に改善を検証できます。","必要情報の欠落で品質が下がっているため、さらに削ると原因を悪化させます。","保持すべき情報の再現率を先に回復し、品質を確認しながら不要部分を減らす適切な順序です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-037","number":37,"form":2,"stem":"A customer-onboarding agent walks a new client through a 60-step platform setup checklist across a long session. At step 2, the client states a critical constraint: \"we operate under GDPR, all data must stay in EU region,\" which remains critical through step 58, where a region-selection decision must respect it. Steps 40–50 consist of the agent troubleshooting an unrelated login glitch that was fully resolved and is no longer relevant to anything downstream. The team is considering a simple, hard-coded trimming heuristic: drop the oldest N turns whenever context exceeds a fixed token threshold, keeping only the most recent messages.\n\nGiven this task's information relevance pattern, is the hard-coded recency-based trimming heuristic sufficient, or does the task require a more sophisticated compaction approach?","options":["The heuristic is sufficient, since recency is always the best proxy for relevance in any long-running agent task, regardless of when key constraints were established.","The heuristic is sufficient as long as the fixed token threshold is set high enough, since any sufficiently generous threshold will naturally avoid dropping anything important.","The heuristic is insufficient, because relevance here doesn't correlate with recency — the step-2 GDPR constraint remains critical at step 58, while some of the more recent steps 40–50 are low-value resolved troubleshooting; a more sophisticated, relevance-aware compaction approach that preserves key constraints regardless of position while discarding low-value recent content is needed instead.","The heuristic is insufficient, but the fix is simply to never drop any messages at all, regardless of token cost, rather than adopting any more sophisticated approach."],"correct":2,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"古いが重要な制約を残す圧縮","concept":"ce-f2-q037","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. The heuristic is insufficient, because relevance here doesn't correlate with recency — the step-2 GDPR constraint remains critical at step 58, while some of the more recent steps 40–50 are low-value resolved troubleshooting; a more sophisticated, relevance-aware compaction approach that preserves key constraints regardless of position while discarding low-value recent content is needed instead.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scenario demonstrates that message age is not a reliable measure of future relevance:\n\n- The GDPR and EU-data-residency requirement was established very early, at step 2.\n\n- That requirement remains essential when the agent reaches region selection at step 58.\n\n- The later login-troubleshooting messages are more recent but have been fully resolved and no longer influence downstream decisions.\n\nA fixed “drop the oldest N turns” policy would likely remove the critical GDPR constraint while preserving recent but obsolete troubleshooting details. The agent therefore requires relevance-aware compaction rather than mechanical recency-based trimming.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA long-running agent must preserve information according to its semantic and operational importance, not merely its position in the transcript.\n\nThe compaction policy should retain:\n\n- Confirmed constraints\n\n- Compliance and security requirements\n\n- User decisions\n\n- Unresolved tasks\n\n- Commitments and prohibitions\n\n- Facts required by later checklist steps\n\nIt should summarize or remove:\n\n- Resolved troubleshooting exchanges\n\n- Repeated explanations\n\n- Superseded intermediate observations\n\n- Raw tool output whose useful facts were already extracted\n\nDatabricks describes context compaction as the harness deciding what remains active and what should be summarized as conversations grow. It also identifies irrelevant retrieval and context degradation as common harness failures, reinforcing that context must be selectively curated rather than retained or deleted solely by age.\n\nFor this workflow, the compacted state should continue to contain a durable constraint such as:\n\nData residency: Customer operates under GDPR; all customer data and platform resources must remain in an approved EU region.\n\nThe resolved login episode can be reduced to a brief status entry or removed entirely:\n\nLogin issue: Resolved; no remaining action or downstream dependency.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. The heuristic is sufficient, since recency is always the best proxy for relevance in any long-running agent task, regardless of when key constraints were established.\n\nThis is incorrect because the scenario explicitly disproves the assumption.\n\nThe oldest relevant information—the GDPR constraint—is more important to the later region decision than the newer login-troubleshooting messages. Recency can be one signal, but it cannot be the sole criterion when early constraints remain active throughout a workflow.\n\nB. The heuristic is sufficient as long as the fixed token threshold is set high enough, since any sufficiently generous threshold will naturally avoid dropping anything important.\n\nA larger threshold may postpone the failure, but it does not correct the flawed selection rule.\n\nAs the 60-step session continues to grow, the system will eventually cross any finite threshold. When trimming occurs, it will still remove the oldest messages first, including potentially critical early requirements.\n\nThe threshold determines when information is dropped; it does not determine which information is safe to drop.\n\nD. The heuristic is insufficient, but the fix is simply to never drop any messages at all, regardless of token cost, rather than adopting any more sophisticated approach.\n\nKeeping everything indefinitely is not sustainable for a long-running agent.\n\nIt can produce:\n\n- Context-window exhaustion\n\n- Higher token costs\n\n- Increased latency\n\n- Greater distraction from obsolete material\n\n- Reduced reasoning quality as irrelevant history accumulates\n\nThe appropriate solution is selective retention and compaction, not unlimited transcript preservation.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming:\n\nOlder means less relevant, and newer means more relevant.\n\nThat may work for simple conversations where only recent turns matter, but it fails in long workflows containing enduring constraints, prior decisions, compliance requirements, and commitments.\n\nThe correct distinction is active versus resolved information, not simply old versus new information.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production onboarding agent should maintain important workflow state in a structured form alongside the conversational summary:\n\n```\nActive constraints:- GDPR applies- Data residency must remain in the EU- Approved deployment regions: eu-west-1, europe-west4 Current progress:- Checklist step: 52 of 60 Resolved issues:- Login problem resolved at step 50- No further action required\n```\n\nThe compaction prompt could state:\n\nPreserve all active compliance, security, residency, contractual, and user-confirmed constraints regardless of when they were introduced. Preserve unresolved actions and current checklist progress. Summarize or remove resolved troubleshooting, repeated discussion, and superseded details that have no downstream dependency.\n\nMLflow’s multi-turn evaluation can test whether an agent retains facts introduced early in a long conversation. In particular, the `KnowledgeRetention` judge evaluates whether information from earlier turns remains correctly reflected later in the session.\n\nThe expected exam answer is C because this task requires relevance-aware retention of durable constraints rather than fixed recency-based deletion.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- What is an AI Agent Harness?\n[https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness](https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness)\n\n- Evaluate conversations\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations)\n\n- Built-in LLM judges\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/)","source_option_explanations":["A. The heuristic is sufficient, since recency is always the best proxy for relevance in any long-running agent task, regardless of when key constraints were established.\n\nThis is incorrect because the scenario explicitly disproves the assumption.\n\nThe oldest relevant information—the GDPR constraint—is more important to the later region decision than the newer login-troubleshooting messages. Recency can be one signal, but it cannot be the sole criterion when early constraints remain active throughout a workflow.","B. The heuristic is sufficient as long as the fixed token threshold is set high enough, since any sufficiently generous threshold will naturally avoid dropping anything important.\n\nA larger threshold may postpone the failure, but it does not correct the flawed selection rule.\n\nAs the 60-step session continues to grow, the system will eventually cross any finite threshold. When trimming occurs, it will still remove the oldest messages first, including potentially critical early requirements.\n\nThe threshold determines when information is dropped; it does not determine which information is safe to drop.","Correct Answer\n\nC. The heuristic is insufficient, because relevance here doesn't correlate with recency — the step-2 GDPR constraint remains critical at step 58, while some of the more recent steps 40–50 are low-value resolved troubleshooting; a more sophisticated, relevance-aware compaction approach that preserves key constraints regardless of position while discarding low-value recent content is needed instead.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scenario demonstrates that message age is not a reliable measure of future relevance:\n\n- The GDPR and EU-data-residency requirement was established very early, at step 2.\n\n- That requirement remains essential when the agent reaches region selection at step 58.\n\n- The later login-troubleshooting messages are more recent but have been fully resolved and no longer influence downstream decisions.\n\nA fixed “drop the oldest N turns” policy would likely remove the critical GDPR constraint while preserving recent but obsolete troubleshooting details. The agent therefore requires relevance-aware compaction rather than mechanical recency-based trimming.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA long-running agent must preserve information according to its semantic and operational importance, not merely its position in the transcript.\n\nThe compaction policy should retain:\n\n- Confirmed constraints\n\n- Compliance and security requirements\n\n- User decisions\n\n- Unresolved tasks\n\n- Commitments and prohibitions\n\n- Facts required by later checklist steps\n\nIt should summarize or remove:\n\n- Resolved troubleshooting exchanges\n\n- Repeated explanations\n\n- Superseded intermediate observations\n\n- Raw tool output whose useful facts were already extracted\n\nDatabricks describes context compaction as the harness deciding what remains active and what should be summarized as conversations grow. It also identifies irrelevant retrieval and context degradation as common harness failures, reinforcing that context must be selectively curated rather than retained or deleted solely by age.\n\nFor this workflow, the compacted state should continue to contain a durable constraint such as:\n\nData residency: Customer operates under GDPR; all customer data and platform resources must remain in an approved EU region.\n\nThe resolved login episode can be reduced to a brief status entry or removed entirely:\n\nLogin issue: Resolved; no remaining action or downstream dependency.","D. The heuristic is insufficient, but the fix is simply to never drop any messages at all, regardless of token cost, rather than adopting any more sophisticated approach.\n\nKeeping everything indefinitely is not sustainable for a long-running agent.\n\nIt can produce:\n\n- Context-window exhaustion\n\n- Higher token costs\n\n- Increased latency\n\n- Greater distraction from obsolete material\n\n- Reduced reasoning quality as irrelevant history accumulates\n\nThe appropriate solution is selective retention and compaction, not unlimited transcript preservation."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/"],"source_hash":"3c3803c9514fc016ec9df5ae6d42166a073366a5da4b19f0bfcf69eab2c33cc6","stem_ja":"顧客導入エージェントが、長いセッションで60段階のプラットフォーム設定チェックリストを案内します。段階2で顧客は「当社はGDPRの対象で、すべてのデータをEUリージョン内に置く必要がある」と重要な制約を伝えました。この制約は、リージョンを選ぶ段階58でも重要です。段階40～50は無関係なログイン不具合の調査で、すでに完全に解決しており、後続処理に関係しません。チームは、コンテキストが固定トークン数を超えたら最も古いNターンを落とし、直近のメッセージだけを残す単純で固定的な切り詰めルールを検討しています。\n\nこのタスクの情報の関連性に照らすと、その新しさだけによる固定ルールで十分ですか。それとも、より高度な圧縮が必要ですか。","options_ja":["重要な制約がいつ確定したかに関係なく、新しさは長時間タスクで常に最良の関連性指標なので、ルールで十分である。","十分大きいトークン閾値なら自然に重要情報を落とさなくなるため、閾値を十分高くすればルールで十分である。","関連性と新しさが一致しないため不十分である。段階2のGDPR制約は58でも重要だが、より新しい段階40～50の解決済み不具合調査は価値が低い。位置に関係なく主要制約を保ち、価値の低い直近情報も除ける、関連性を考慮した高度な圧縮が必要である。","ルールは不十分だが、高度な手法を採るのではなく、トークンコストにかかわらず、どのメッセージも決して落とさないのが解決である。"],"explanation_ja":"新しい情報ほど重要とは限りません。最初のEU限定という制約が最終段階の選択を左右し、最近の解決済みログは不要です。意味と後続の依存関係で要約し、継続的な制約を保持する必要があります。覚え方は「古さではなく、次の判断に必要かで残す」。単なる先入れ先出しでは不十分です。","distractors_ja":["序盤の制約が終盤でも必須という具体的な反例があり、新しさを万能な基準にはできません。","閾値を上げても、いずれ古い重要制約を削る同じルールが適用されます。","古くても重要な制約を保ち、最近でも不要になった調査を削るため、実際の関連性に合います。","全文を永久に残すと有限のコンテキストを管理できず、必要情報を選ぶという課題を回避しています。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-038","number":38,"form":2,"stem":"A trip-planning agent's trace includes: (1) an early exchange where the agent misunderstood the destination city and the user corrected it, with the corrected city consistently used in every turn since; (2) the user's final confirmed travel dates, still actively referenced; (3) a restaurant recommendation the agent gave that the user explicitly said they didn't like and won't use; (4) the flight options the agent is currently comparing in its most recent turn.\n\nSelect the two items that are safe to remove during compaction without affecting downstream task execution.","options":["Item (1), the resolved destination-city misunderstanding, since the corrected city is what's actually used going forward and the confusion itself doesn't need to persist.","Item (2), the user's final confirmed travel dates, since dates stated early in a session are always safe to drop once compaction triggers.","Item (4), the flight options currently being compared, since flight options are always low-value content regardless of whether the agent is still using them.","Item (3), the explicitly rejected restaurant recommendation, since the user's decision not to use it has already been captured and the specific restaurant name isn't needed further."],"correct":0,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"解決済みの混乱と不要な提案を除く","concept":"ce-f2-q038","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Item (1), the resolved destination-city misunderstanding, since the corrected city is what's actually used going forward and the confusion itself doesn't need to persist.\n\nD. Item (3), the explicitly rejected restaurant recommendation, since the user's decision not to use it has already been captured and the specific restaurant name isn't needed further.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nCompaction should preserve information that still affects future decisions while removing resolved, redundant, or obsolete conversational detail.\n\nThe four items divide as follows:\n\n- Item 1: The original misunderstanding is resolved; only the corrected destination matters.\n\n- Item 2: The confirmed dates remain active constraints and must be retained.\n\n- Item 3: The recommendation is closed because the user rejected it; the full exchange can be removed once the rejection is represented compactly.\n\n- Item 4: The flight options are part of the agent’s current task and must remain available.\n\nTherefore, A and D are the safest items to remove.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA — Resolved destination misunderstanding\n\nThe incorrect destination is no longer useful. The corrected city has been consistently used throughout subsequent turns and represents the authoritative current state.\n\nKeeping the original misunderstanding could:\n\n- Waste context tokens.\n\n- Reintroduce an obsolete destination.\n\n- Distract the model with conflicting location names.\n\nThe compacted context should preserve only the confirmed city, not the historical confusion that led to it.\n\nD — Rejected restaurant recommendation\n\nThe restaurant recommendation is no longer an active candidate because the user explicitly rejected it. The detailed recommendation and surrounding dialogue can be removed after the decision is captured in compact form.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nRestaurant preference:- Prior recommendation rejected; do not reuse that option.\n```\n\nIn a robust implementation, retaining the restaurant name in a concise “rejected options” list would prevent the agent from recommending it again. What is safe to remove is the complete recommendation exchange, not necessarily the negative preference itself.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. Item (2), the user's final confirmed travel dates, since dates stated early in a session are always safe to drop once compaction triggers.\n\nThe confirmed travel dates remain active constraints. They affect:\n\n- Flight availability and pricing\n\n- Hotel reservations\n\n- Activity scheduling\n\n- Restaurant bookings\n\n- Itinerary feasibility\n\nInformation is not safe to remove simply because it appeared earlier in the conversation. Its ongoing relevance—not its age—determines whether it must remain.\n\nC. Item (4), the flight options currently being compared, since flight options are always low-value content regardless of whether the agent is still using them.\n\nThese flight options are part of the agent’s current working state.\n\nRemoving them could prevent the agent from:\n\n- Comparing prices and schedules.\n\n- Interpreting the user’s next response.\n\n- Remembering which options have already been evaluated.\n\n- Completing the current selection task.\n\nCurrent candidates and pending decisions should remain until the user selects, rejects, or replaces them.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming that compaction should remove information based only on message age or content category.\n\nThe correct distinction is:\n\n- Resolved or obsolete state: Safe to remove after preserving any useful conclusion.\n\n- Active constraints and pending decisions: Must remain regardless of when they were introduced.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA good compacted state would preserve:\n\n```\nConfirmed destination: ParisConfirmed travel dates: September 12–18Rejected restaurant: Restaurant XCurrent task: Comparing flight options A, B, and C\n```\n\nIt could remove:\n\n- The original incorrect destination.\n\n- The full correction dialogue.\n\n- The full rejected restaurant recommendation and discussion.\n\nThis preserves the minimum state required to continue the task while preventing repeated mistakes. MLflow multi-turn evaluation can then test whether the agent retains confirmed constraints and correctly interprets later follow-ups after compaction.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Evaluate conversations\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations)\n\n- Built-in LLM judges\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/)\n\n- Agent memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/stateful-agents](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/stateful-agents)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Item (1), the resolved destination-city misunderstanding, since the corrected city is what's actually used going forward and the confusion itself doesn't need to persist.\n\nD. Item (3), the explicitly rejected restaurant recommendation, since the user's decision not to use it has already been captured and the specific restaurant name isn't needed further.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nCompaction should preserve information that still affects future decisions while removing resolved, redundant, or obsolete conversational detail.\n\nThe four items divide as follows:\n\n- Item 1: The original misunderstanding is resolved; only the corrected destination matters.\n\n- Item 2: The confirmed dates remain active constraints and must be retained.\n\n- Item 3: The recommendation is closed because the user rejected it; the full exchange can be removed once the rejection is represented compactly.\n\n- Item 4: The flight options are part of the agent’s current task and must remain available.\n\nTherefore, A and D are the safest items to remove.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA — Resolved destination misunderstanding\n\nThe incorrect destination is no longer useful. The corrected city has been consistently used throughout subsequent turns and represents the authoritative current state.\n\nKeeping the original misunderstanding could:\n\n- Waste context tokens.\n\n- Reintroduce an obsolete destination.\n\n- Distract the model with conflicting location names.\n\nThe compacted context should preserve only the confirmed city, not the historical confusion that led to it.\n\nD — Rejected restaurant recommendation\n\nThe restaurant recommendation is no longer an active candidate because the user explicitly rejected it. The detailed recommendation and surrounding dialogue can be removed after the decision is captured in compact form.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nRestaurant preference:- Prior recommendation rejected; do not reuse that option.\n```\n\nIn a robust implementation, retaining the restaurant name in a concise “rejected options” list would prevent the agent from recommending it again. What is safe to remove is the complete recommendation exchange, not necessarily the negative preference itself.","B. Item (2), the user's final confirmed travel dates, since dates stated early in a session are always safe to drop once compaction triggers.\n\nThe confirmed travel dates remain active constraints. They affect:\n\n- Flight availability and pricing\n\n- Hotel reservations\n\n- Activity scheduling\n\n- Restaurant bookings\n\n- Itinerary feasibility\n\nInformation is not safe to remove simply because it appeared earlier in the conversation. Its ongoing relevance—not its age—determines whether it must remain.","C. Item (4), the flight options currently being compared, since flight options are always low-value content regardless of whether the agent is still using them.\n\nThese flight options are part of the agent’s current working state.\n\nRemoving them could prevent the agent from:\n\n- Comparing prices and schedules.\n\n- Interpreting the user’s next response.\n\n- Remembering which options have already been evaluated.\n\n- Completing the current selection task.\n\nCurrent candidates and pending decisions should remain until the user selects, rejects, or replaces them.","Correct Answer\n\nA. Item (1), the resolved destination-city misunderstanding, since the corrected city is what's actually used going forward and the confusion itself doesn't need to persist.\n\nD. Item (3), the explicitly rejected restaurant recommendation, since the user's decision not to use it has already been captured and the specific restaurant name isn't needed further.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nCompaction should preserve information that still affects future decisions while removing resolved, redundant, or obsolete conversational detail.\n\nThe four items divide as follows:\n\n- Item 1: The original misunderstanding is resolved; only the corrected destination matters.\n\n- Item 2: The confirmed dates remain active constraints and must be retained.\n\n- Item 3: The recommendation is closed because the user rejected it; the full exchange can be removed once the rejection is represented compactly.\n\n- Item 4: The flight options are part of the agent’s current task and must remain available.\n\nTherefore, A and D are the safest items to remove.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA — Resolved destination misunderstanding\n\nThe incorrect destination is no longer useful. The corrected city has been consistently used throughout subsequent turns and represents the authoritative current state.\n\nKeeping the original misunderstanding could:\n\n- Waste context tokens.\n\n- Reintroduce an obsolete destination.\n\n- Distract the model with conflicting location names.\n\nThe compacted context should preserve only the confirmed city, not the historical confusion that led to it.\n\nD — Rejected restaurant recommendation\n\nThe restaurant recommendation is no longer an active candidate because the user explicitly rejected it. The detailed recommendation and surrounding dialogue can be removed after the decision is captured in compact form.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\nRestaurant preference:- Prior recommendation rejected; do not reuse that option.\n```\n\nIn a robust implementation, retaining the restaurant name in a concise “rejected options” list would prevent the agent from recommending it again. What is safe to remove is the complete recommendation exchange, not necessarily the negative preference itself."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/concepts/judges/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/stateful-agents"],"source_hash":"932d6f37fb227cc5b08037bd98a764be19992609d4b81c330dcdfb15ada4e2ff","correct_many":[0,3],"stem_ja":"旅行計画エージェントのトレースには、(1)初期に目的の都市をエージェントが勘違いし、ユーザーが訂正したやり取りがあり、以後はすべてのターンで正しい都市を使っています。(2)最終確認済みの旅行日程は、今も参照されています。(3)勧めたレストランについて、ユーザーは好みでなく利用しないと明示しました。(4)直近ターンで、現在比較中の航空便の候補があります。\n\n圧縮時、後続のタスク実行に影響せずに安全に取り除ける項目を2つ選んでください。","options_ja":["以後使うのは訂正後の都市であり、混乱そのものを保持する必要はないため、(1)の解決済みの都市の取り違え。","セッション初期に述べられた日付は、圧縮が始まれば常に安全に落とせるため、(2)の最終確認済み旅行日程。","現在使っているかに関係なく、航空便候補は常に価値が低いため、(4)の比較中の便。","利用しないというユーザーの決定はすでに記録され、個別の店名は今後必要ないため、(3)の明示的に断られたレストラン推薦。"],"explanation_ja":"訂正後の都市や拒否の決定が保持され、元の誤解や具体的な店名が後続で不要なら、その詳細は削れます。一方、確定日程と比較中の便は、現在の判断に必要です。覚え方は「解決済みの経緯は削減、現在の制約と作業候補は保持」。拒否情報を無条件に全部捨てるという意味ではありません。","distractors_ja":["正しい都市が後続に残る前提で、すでに解決した勘違いの会話自体は不要です。","旅行日程は現在の便選択を制約する重要情報で、述べた時期だけでは削除できません。","今比較している便は未完了の作業状態であり、除くと次の判断ができなくなります。","選択肢が示すとおり拒否の決定が別に残り、店名が以後不要なら、推薦の詳細は削減できます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-039","number":39,"form":2,"stem":"A single internal finance agent handles two very different question types in the same deployment: routine, low-stakes questions about expense-report status (extremely high volume, no meaningful harm if a rare detail is lost to compaction), and occasional, high-stakes questions interpreting SOX financial-controls compliance requirements (rare, but where losing a subtle qualifying condition during compaction could produce a materially wrong compliance answer). The team currently applies one fixed, moderately aggressive compaction posture uniformly across every session, regardless of which question type is being handled.\n\nWhat compaction posture change best reflects the actual tradeoffs here?","options":["Apply the same moderately aggressive posture uniformly, since a single consistent policy is simpler to maintain regardless of what's actually at stake in a given conversation.","Vary the compaction posture by the type of question actually being handled in a given session — conservative, higher-fidelity compaction when the session involves SOX compliance interpretation, and more aggressive compaction for routine expense-status sessions — rather than applying one fixed posture regardless of stakes.","Apply aggressive compaction uniformly across both question types, since the high volume of expense-report questions means minimizing token cost should take priority overall.","Apply conservative, high-fidelity compaction uniformly across both question types, since the presence of any high-stakes compliance questions means every session should be treated with maximum caution regardless of cost."],"correct":1,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"判断の重大さに合わせた圧縮","concept":"ce-f2-q039","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Vary the compaction posture by the type of question actually being handled in a given session — conservative, higher-fidelity compaction when the session involves SOX compliance interpretation, and more aggressive compaction for routine expense-status sessions — rather than applying one fixed posture regardless of stakes.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe two workloads have materially different consequences if compaction removes a relevant detail:\n\n- Expense-status questions: High volume, low stakes, and tolerant of occasional loss of minor conversational detail.\n\n- SOX compliance interpretation: Low volume but high stakes, where losing an exception, qualifier, scope condition, or timing requirement could materially change the answer.\n\nA single fixed policy cannot optimize both workloads. The appropriate strategy is risk-adaptive compaction:\n\n- Use more aggressive summarization and trimming for routine operational questions.\n\n- Use conservative, high-recall compaction for compliance-related conversations.\n\nThis preserves fidelity where information loss is costly while controlling token usage where the consequences are minimal.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nCompaction is a trade-off among:\n\n- Context size and token cost\n\n- Information recall\n\n- Relevance and precision\n\n- Downstream task risk\n\nFor an expense-status session, the compacted state may need only:\n\n- Employee or report identifier\n\n- Current approval status\n\n- Outstanding action\n\n- Most recent authoritative tool result\n\nOlder dialogue and superseded status details can usually be removed aggressively.\n\nFor a SOX compliance session, compaction should preserve:\n\n- Exact control requirements\n\n- Qualifying conditions and exceptions\n\n- Applicable systems or entities\n\n- Effective dates and reporting periods\n\n- Evidence and source references\n\n- Unresolved interpretive questions\n\n- Prior conclusions and their supporting rationale\n\nThe question’s key phrase is “actual tradeoffs.” The cost of lost context is not uniform across the two task categories, so the compaction posture should not be uniform either.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Apply the same moderately aggressive posture uniformly, since a single consistent policy is simpler to maintain regardless of what's actually at stake in a given conversation.\n\nOperational simplicity does not justify applying an unsuitable risk posture.\n\nA moderately aggressive policy may be unnecessarily expensive for routine sessions while still being too lossy for SOX interpretation. Consistency is valuable only when the workloads have similar information-retention requirements.\n\nC. Apply aggressive compaction uniformly across both question types, since the high volume of expense-report questions means minimizing token cost should take priority overall.\n\nThis optimizes aggregate cost while ignoring the potentially material consequences of an incorrect compliance answer.\n\nThe high volume of low-risk traffic does not reduce the importance of preserving subtle conditions in the smaller number of SOX-related sessions. Cost optimization should be applied selectively rather than weakening high-stakes conversations.\n\nD. Apply conservative, high-fidelity compaction uniformly across both question types, since the presence of any high-stakes compliance questions means every session should be treated with maximum caution regardless of cost.\n\nThis would protect SOX conversations but impose unnecessary token cost and latency on the much larger routine workload.\n\nThe agent can identify the session’s task category and apply the appropriate policy. Treating every expense-status lookup as a compliance interpretation session would be overly conservative and inefficient.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming that one global compaction policy is inherently safer or easier to govern.\n\nThe appropriate retention level depends on the consequences of information loss. High-stakes tasks require higher recall and fidelity; routine, reversible tasks can tolerate more aggressive reduction.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production agent could define compaction tiers:\n\nSession typeCompaction posturePrimary objectiveRoutine expense statusAggressiveReduce tokens and latencySOX controls interpretationConservativePreserve exact conditions and evidenceMixed or uncertainConservative fallbackAvoid material information loss\n\nThe routing decision should be deterministic or validated, not left entirely to an unconstrained model. The team should evaluate each tier separately using representative MLflow datasets and multi-turn traces.\n\nFor SOX sessions, custom scorers should verify retention of exact exceptions, dates, applicability conditions, and citations. For expense sessions, testing should focus on status accuracy, latency, and token cost.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Evaluate conversations\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations)\n\n- Building MLflow evaluation datasets\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset)\n\n- Evaluate and monitor AI agents\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor)\n\n- What is an AI Agent Harness?\n[https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness](https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness)","source_option_explanations":["A. Apply the same moderately aggressive posture uniformly, since a single consistent policy is simpler to maintain regardless of what's actually at stake in a given conversation.\n\nOperational simplicity does not justify applying an unsuitable risk posture.\n\nA moderately aggressive policy may be unnecessarily expensive for routine sessions while still being too lossy for SOX interpretation. Consistency is valuable only when the workloads have similar information-retention requirements.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Vary the compaction posture by the type of question actually being handled in a given session — conservative, higher-fidelity compaction when the session involves SOX compliance interpretation, and more aggressive compaction for routine expense-status sessions — rather than applying one fixed posture regardless of stakes.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe two workloads have materially different consequences if compaction removes a relevant detail:\n\n- Expense-status questions: High volume, low stakes, and tolerant of occasional loss of minor conversational detail.\n\n- SOX compliance interpretation: Low volume but high stakes, where losing an exception, qualifier, scope condition, or timing requirement could materially change the answer.\n\nA single fixed policy cannot optimize both workloads. The appropriate strategy is risk-adaptive compaction:\n\n- Use more aggressive summarization and trimming for routine operational questions.\n\n- Use conservative, high-recall compaction for compliance-related conversations.\n\nThis preserves fidelity where information loss is costly while controlling token usage where the consequences are minimal.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nCompaction is a trade-off among:\n\n- Context size and token cost\n\n- Information recall\n\n- Relevance and precision\n\n- Downstream task risk\n\nFor an expense-status session, the compacted state may need only:\n\n- Employee or report identifier\n\n- Current approval status\n\n- Outstanding action\n\n- Most recent authoritative tool result\n\nOlder dialogue and superseded status details can usually be removed aggressively.\n\nFor a SOX compliance session, compaction should preserve:\n\n- Exact control requirements\n\n- Qualifying conditions and exceptions\n\n- Applicable systems or entities\n\n- Effective dates and reporting periods\n\n- Evidence and source references\n\n- Unresolved interpretive questions\n\n- Prior conclusions and their supporting rationale\n\nThe question’s key phrase is “actual tradeoffs.” The cost of lost context is not uniform across the two task categories, so the compaction posture should not be uniform either.","C. Apply aggressive compaction uniformly across both question types, since the high volume of expense-report questions means minimizing token cost should take priority overall.\n\nThis optimizes aggregate cost while ignoring the potentially material consequences of an incorrect compliance answer.\n\nThe high volume of low-risk traffic does not reduce the importance of preserving subtle conditions in the smaller number of SOX-related sessions. Cost optimization should be applied selectively rather than weakening high-stakes conversations.","D. Apply conservative, high-fidelity compaction uniformly across both question types, since the presence of any high-stakes compliance questions means every session should be treated with maximum caution regardless of cost.\n\nThis would protect SOX conversations but impose unnecessary token cost and latency on the much larger routine workload.\n\nThe agent can identify the session’s task category and apply the appropriate policy. Treating every expense-status lookup as a compliance interpretation session would be overly conservative and inefficient."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-conversations","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/build-eval-dataset","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor","https://www.databricks.com/blog/ai-harness"],"source_hash":"88878a834389b7584533d24a8e340c05b50edc1c225ca7f44af82756221b299e","stem_ja":"単一の社内財務エージェントが、同じデプロイで非常に異なる2種の質問を扱います。1つは経費精算の状態に関する日常的で影響の小さい質問で、件数が非常に多く、圧縮でまれな詳細を失っても実質的な害はありません。もう1つはSOX法の財務統制要件の解釈に関する、ときどきある重大な質問です。頻度は低いものの、微妙な限定条件を圧縮で失うと、重大に誤ったコンプライアンス回答になりえます。現在、チームは質問種別を問わず、すべてのセッションへ同じ中程度に積極的な圧縮方針を適用しています。\n\n実際のトレードオフを最もよく反映する圧縮方針の変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["会話ごとの重大さに関係なく、単一の方針の方が保守しやすいため、同じ中程度に積極的な圧縮を一律に使う。","重大さに関係なく固定方針を使うのではなく、実際に扱う質問に応じて変える。SOX解釈を含むセッションでは慎重で情報忠実度の高い圧縮、日常的な経費状態のセッションではより積極的な圧縮を使う。","経費精算の質問が大量にあるため、全体のトークン費用の最小化を優先し、両方へ積極的な圧縮を一律に使う。","重大なコンプライアンス質問が1つでもある以上、費用に関係なく全セッションで最大限慎重にすべきなので、両方へ情報忠実度の高い慎重な圧縮を一律に使う。"],"explanation_ja":"圧縮の強さは、失ってよい情報量と、失った場合の影響に合わせます。法的条件の微妙な違いが重要なSOX解釈は忠実度を優先し、低リスクで大量の状態確認は費用を抑えます。覚え方は「重大な判断ほど慎重に、低リスクの反復ほど軽く」。一律方針ではどちらかを犠牲にします。","distractors_ja":["保守の簡単さだけで、情報欠落による影響が大きく異なることを無視しています。","質問の重大さに応じて忠実度とトークン費用のバランスを変えられます。","大量の低リスク処理を理由に、SOXの重要条件まで削る危険を許してしまいます。","SOXには適切でも、影響の小さい大量の状態照会へ常に同じ高コストをかける必要はありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-040","number":40,"form":2,"stem":"A Resume-Screening Agent and an Interview-Scheduling Agent each independently track a candidate's current interview time rather than reading from the applicant-tracking system (ATS) as a single shared source of truth. After a recruiter reschedules an interview directly in the ATS, the Scheduling Agent's next message correctly reflects the new time. The Resume-Screening Agent, however, had cached the original time in its own local state days earlier and never re-checked it; it independently sends the candidate a separate confirmation for the old, now-cancelled time slot, creating two conflicting messages.\n\nWhich diagnosis and fix best applies?","options":["This is a failure caused by insufficient shared context — each agent maintained its own independent, unsynchronized copy of the interview time instead of reading from the ATS as a single authoritative source; the fix is to have both agents read current interview timing from that shared source rather than caching their own local copies.","This is an email-delivery defect unrelated to the agents' architecture; the fix is to add retry logic to the email-sending service.","This is expected behavior, since two independent agents will always occasionally disagree, and no architectural change can prevent it.","This is a scheduling-system bug in the ATS itself, since the ATS is what changed the appointment time in the first place."],"correct":0,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"共有の正となる最新状態を参照","concept":"ce-f2-q040","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. This is a failure caused by insufficient shared context — each agent maintained its own independent, unsynchronized copy of the interview time instead of reading from the ATS as a single authoritative source; the fix is to have both agents read current interview timing from that shared source rather than caching their own local copies.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe interview time is a shared, mutable operational fact. It can be changed by recruiters and must remain consistent across every agent interacting with the candidate.\n\nThe Scheduling Agent obtained the current time from the ATS, but the Resume-Screening Agent relied on a stale local copy. This created two competing versions of the same fact:\n\n- Authoritative ATS value: New interview time\n\n- Stale agent-local value: Canceled interview time\n\nThe mismatch is therefore a shared-state and source-of-truth failure. Both agents should retrieve the current interview schedule from the ATS whenever the timing affects an action or message.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe ATS should remain the authoritative system for mutable recruitment data such as:\n\n- Current interview time\n\n- Interview status\n\n- Assigned interviewer\n\n- Candidate stage\n\n- Rescheduling or cancellation state\n\nAgents should not independently maintain authoritative copies of rapidly changing business facts. Local conversation state can preserve temporary working context, but it should not override the current value in the operational system of record.\n\nDatabricks supports connecting agents to structured data sources through deterministic retrieval tools. Both agents can therefore call a shared ATS lookup tool before generating scheduling-related communications.\n\nA suitable flow would be:\n\nCandidate request or outbound communication → retrieve current ATS record → confirm active interview status and time → generate message\n\nThis ensures that both agents receive the same current value and prevents stale local memory from producing contradictory communications.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. This is an email-delivery defect unrelated to the agents' architecture; the fix is to add retry logic to the email-sending service.\n\nEmail delivery succeeded—the problem is that one email contained obsolete information.\n\nRetry logic would resend the same incorrect confirmation and could make the situation worse. The defect occurs before delivery, when the Resume-Screening Agent selects a stale interview time.\n\nC. This is expected behavior, since two independent agents will always occasionally disagree, and no architectural change can prevent it.\n\nIndependent agents do not have to disagree when they read mutable business facts from the same authoritative source.\n\nThe agents can have different roles and prompts while still sharing a governed retrieval tool for current ATS data. Multi-agent specialization does not require maintaining separate copies of operational truth.\n\nD. This is a scheduling-system bug in the ATS itself, since the ATS is what changed the appointment time in the first place.\n\nThe ATS behaved correctly: the recruiter updated the interview time, and the Scheduling Agent retrieved the new value successfully.\n\nThe error occurred because the Resume-Screening Agent did not re-query the ATS and instead trusted its stale cached state. The ATS update exposed the architectural weakness but did not cause it.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is confusing agent memory with an authoritative system of record.\n\nMemory is appropriate for conversational context, preferences, and past interactions. Mutable operational facts—such as current appointment times—should be refreshed from the authoritative business system before the agent acts on them.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nUse explicit precedence rules for shared operational facts:\n\nCurrent ATS record → shared durable state or cache with freshness controls → conversation-local copy\n\nA production implementation should:\n\n- Expose a shared `get_current_interview(candidate_id)` tool to both agents.\n\n- Re-query the ATS before sending confirmations or reminders.\n\n- Store the interview record’s version or `updated_at` timestamp.\n\n- Invalidate caches when ATS scheduling events occur.\n\n- Prevent non-scheduling agents from sending interview confirmations without checking the current record.\n\n- Trace the ATS value and message-generation decision in MLflow.\n\nShared persistent state can support coordination, but the system that owns the business process should remain authoritative. Databricks distinguishes durable agent state from external structured data that agents retrieve for current operational decisions.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Connect agents to structured data\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools)\n\n- Agent state and memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)\n\n- Agent memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/stateful-agents](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/stateful-agents)\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. This is a failure caused by insufficient shared context — each agent maintained its own independent, unsynchronized copy of the interview time instead of reading from the ATS as a single authoritative source; the fix is to have both agents read current interview timing from that shared source rather than caching their own local copies.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe interview time is a shared, mutable operational fact. It can be changed by recruiters and must remain consistent across every agent interacting with the candidate.\n\nThe Scheduling Agent obtained the current time from the ATS, but the Resume-Screening Agent relied on a stale local copy. This created two competing versions of the same fact:\n\n- Authoritative ATS value: New interview time\n\n- Stale agent-local value: Canceled interview time\n\nThe mismatch is therefore a shared-state and source-of-truth failure. Both agents should retrieve the current interview schedule from the ATS whenever the timing affects an action or message.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe ATS should remain the authoritative system for mutable recruitment data such as:\n\n- Current interview time\n\n- Interview status\n\n- Assigned interviewer\n\n- Candidate stage\n\n- Rescheduling or cancellation state\n\nAgents should not independently maintain authoritative copies of rapidly changing business facts. Local conversation state can preserve temporary working context, but it should not override the current value in the operational system of record.\n\nDatabricks supports connecting agents to structured data sources through deterministic retrieval tools. Both agents can therefore call a shared ATS lookup tool before generating scheduling-related communications.\n\nA suitable flow would be:\n\nCandidate request or outbound communication → retrieve current ATS record → confirm active interview status and time → generate message\n\nThis ensures that both agents receive the same current value and prevents stale local memory from producing contradictory communications.","B. This is an email-delivery defect unrelated to the agents' architecture; the fix is to add retry logic to the email-sending service.\n\nEmail delivery succeeded—the problem is that one email contained obsolete information.\n\nRetry logic would resend the same incorrect confirmation and could make the situation worse. The defect occurs before delivery, when the Resume-Screening Agent selects a stale interview time.","C. This is expected behavior, since two independent agents will always occasionally disagree, and no architectural change can prevent it.\n\nIndependent agents do not have to disagree when they read mutable business facts from the same authoritative source.\n\nThe agents can have different roles and prompts while still sharing a governed retrieval tool for current ATS data. Multi-agent specialization does not require maintaining separate copies of operational truth.","D. This is a scheduling-system bug in the ATS itself, since the ATS is what changed the appointment time in the first place.\n\nThe ATS behaved correctly: the recruiter updated the interview time, and the Scheduling Agent retrieved the new value successfully.\n\nThe error occurred because the Resume-Screening Agent did not re-query the ATS and instead trusted its stale cached state. The ATS update exposed the architectural weakness but did not cause it."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/stateful-agents","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns"],"source_hash":"4e1340d1f7f635800b56562fe3d3df699ff064ed14eca0d8d51fd799a223d6da","stem_ja":"履歴書審査エージェントと面接日程調整エージェントが、採用管理システム（ATS）を単一の共有された正の情報源として読む代わりに、候補者の面接時刻を独立に管理しています。採用担当者がATSで直接日程を変更すると、日程調整エージェントの次のメッセージは正しい新時刻を反映しました。一方、履歴書審査エージェントは数日前の元の時刻をローカル状態にキャッシュしたまま再確認せず、取り消された古い時間枠について別の確認を送り、矛盾する2つの連絡が発生しました。\n\n最も適切な診断と修正はどれですか。","options_ja":["共有コンテキストの不足による障害である。各エージェントがATSという単一の正の情報源を読む代わりに、同期されない独立した面接時刻を保持した。両方が独自のコピーをキャッシュするのではなく、共有情報源の現在の面接時刻を読むようにする。","エージェントの構成とは関係のないメール配送の不具合なので、メール送信サービスへ再試行処理を加える。","独立した2つのエージェントは必ず時々食い違い、設計では防げないので、想定どおりの動作である。","予定時刻を変更したATS自体のスケジュール機能の不具合である。"],"explanation_ja":"各エージェントが別々のコピーを正として扱うと、片方だけが古い状態になります。面接時刻はATSの現在値へ統一して参照し、各エージェントの独立した記憶を正式な予定として使わないようにします。覚え方は「共有事実には共有の正を1つ」。メールを再送しても、内容の不一致は直りません。","distractors_ja":["同期されないローカルコピーを排し、正式な共有データを読むことで、矛盾した時刻の連絡を防げます。","配送失敗ではなく、送信内容の元となる状態が古いという問題です。","正式な共有情報源と適切な更新・参照設計によって、この不一致は防止できます。","ATSの変更は正当であり、変更を読み直さなかったエージェント側の状態管理が原因です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-041","number":41,"form":2,"stem":"A coordinating agent reviews flagged social-media posts and dispatches each one to a specialist sub-agent (hate-speech, spam, misinformation), currently passing only the flagged post's raw text as the task message. A post is flagged specifically because the coordinator identified it as part of a coordinated pattern across several related posts it had already reviewed — but the dispatched sub-agent, seeing only the isolated post text with no visibility into that pattern reasoning, misclassifies it as benign in isolation, missing what made it violate policy specifically as part of a coordinated campaign.\n\nWhich configuration resolves this without expanding each sub-agent's context window?","options":["Give each sub-agent the coordinator's full raw multi-post review session trace in every dispatch, so no relevant detail is ever missed.","Attach a compact summary of the coordinator's relevant pattern-reasoning to each dispatched task message, so sub-agents inherit the necessary context without receiving the coordinator's entire raw session trace.","Have the coordinator stop dispatching to sub-agents entirely and classify every post itself, since the sub-agent architecture is fundamentally incompatible with pattern-level context.","Increase each sub-agent's model's context window so it can hold more historical posts, without changing what's included in the dispatch message itself."],"correct":1,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"委譲時に必要な判断の背景を渡す","concept":"ce-f2-q041","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Attach a compact summary of the coordinator's relevant pattern-reasoning to each dispatched task message, so sub-agents inherit the necessary context without receiving the coordinator's entire raw session trace.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe sub-agent is not failing because its model lacks enough context capacity. It is failing because the dispatched task omits the specific evidence that caused the coordinator to flag the post.\n\nThe post appears benign when examined alone, but violates policy when interpreted as part of a coordinated campaign. Therefore, the handoff must include a compact summary of the relevant cross-post pattern, such as:\n\n- Which related posts were identified\n\n- What coordinated behavior was observed\n\n- Why the current post contributes to that pattern\n\n- Which policy criterion might apply\n\nOption B transfers the minimum context necessary for correct classification without sending the coordinator’s entire multi-post trace.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nIn a multi-agent design, each specialist has its own context and does not automatically inherit the coordinator’s prior observations. The coordinator must explicitly include the task-relevant evidence in the handoff message.\n\nA useful dispatch could look like:\n\n```\nReview this post for coordinated misinformation. Post:[flagged post text] Relevant campaign context:- Five related accounts posted semantically equivalent claims within 12 minutes.- The posts share the same uncommon URL and phrasing.- Three accounts previously amplified one another in the same sequence.- Assess this post as part of that coordinated pattern, not only in isolation.\n```\n\nThis approach preserves the specialist architecture while keeping the transferred context focused. It also avoids increasing the sub-agent’s context-window size because the problem is solved by improving the content of the handoff, not expanding maximum capacity.\n\nThe coordinator should pass a concise evidence-based rationale rather than hidden internal chain-of-thought. The handoff needs observable facts, relevant conclusions, and policy signals—not a complete reasoning transcript.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Give each sub-agent the coordinator's full raw multi-post review session trace in every dispatch, so no relevant detail is ever missed.\n\nThis would probably include the necessary information, but it is unnecessarily expensive and noisy.\n\nThe full trace could contain:\n\n- Unrelated posts\n\n- Repeated observations\n\n- Superseded hypotheses\n\n- Tool outputs irrelevant to the assigned specialist\n\n- Sensitive information the specialist does not need\n\nSending everything increases token usage and can reduce attention to the evidence that actually matters. A compact, task-specific handoff is more precise.\n\nC. Have the coordinator stop dispatching to sub-agents entirely and classify every post itself, since the sub-agent architecture is fundamentally incompatible with pattern-level context.\n\nMulti-agent specialization is not inherently incompatible with cross-post analysis.\n\nThe failure is caused by an incomplete handoff, not by the existence of specialist agents. The coordinator can continue routing work as long as it passes the contextual evidence required for the specialist to perform its task correctly.\n\nEliminating sub-agents would discard their domain specialization without addressing the underlying context-transfer principle.\n\nD. Increase each sub-agent's model's context window so it can hold more historical posts, without changing what's included in the dispatch message itself.\n\nA larger context window cannot help when the required information is never included.\n\nThe sub-agent still receives only the isolated post text. Increasing its capacity does not give it access to the coordinator’s previously observed campaign pattern.\n\nThis is an information-transfer failure, not a context-capacity failure.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming that agents participating in the same workflow automatically share context.\n\nEach sub-agent only knows what is present in its own task message, memory, and accessible tools. Important upstream findings must be deliberately transferred during the handoff.\n\nAnother trap is responding to missing information by increasing context-window size. Capacity does not compensate for absent context.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nDefine an explicit handoff schema for coordinated-content reviews, for example:\n\n```\n{  \"post_text\": \"...\",  \"suspected_policy_area\": \"coordinated_misinformation\",  \"pattern_summary\": \"...\",  \"related_post_ids\": [\"p101\", \"p104\", \"p119\"],  \"observed_signals\": [    \"shared uncommon URL\",    \"near-identical language\",    \"synchronized posting\",    \"mutual amplification\"  ],  \"requested_assessment\": \"Evaluate this post in campaign context\"}\n```\n\nThe coordinator should include only evidence relevant to the selected specialist. MLflow traces and evaluation datasets should test both isolated and coordinated examples to ensure that the handoff preserves enough context for correct classification.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- Use Supervisor Agent to create a coordinated multi-agent system\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor)\n\n- Build a multi-agent system on Databricks Apps\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps)","source_option_explanations":["A. Give each sub-agent the coordinator's full raw multi-post review session trace in every dispatch, so no relevant detail is ever missed.\n\nThis would probably include the necessary information, but it is unnecessarily expensive and noisy.\n\nThe full trace could contain:\n\n- Unrelated posts\n\n- Repeated observations\n\n- Superseded hypotheses\n\n- Tool outputs irrelevant to the assigned specialist\n\n- Sensitive information the specialist does not need\n\nSending everything increases token usage and can reduce attention to the evidence that actually matters. A compact, task-specific handoff is more precise.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Attach a compact summary of the coordinator's relevant pattern-reasoning to each dispatched task message, so sub-agents inherit the necessary context without receiving the coordinator's entire raw session trace.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe sub-agent is not failing because its model lacks enough context capacity. It is failing because the dispatched task omits the specific evidence that caused the coordinator to flag the post.\n\nThe post appears benign when examined alone, but violates policy when interpreted as part of a coordinated campaign. Therefore, the handoff must include a compact summary of the relevant cross-post pattern, such as:\n\n- Which related posts were identified\n\n- What coordinated behavior was observed\n\n- Why the current post contributes to that pattern\n\n- Which policy criterion might apply\n\nOption B transfers the minimum context necessary for correct classification without sending the coordinator’s entire multi-post trace.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nIn a multi-agent design, each specialist has its own context and does not automatically inherit the coordinator’s prior observations. The coordinator must explicitly include the task-relevant evidence in the handoff message.\n\nA useful dispatch could look like:\n\n```\nReview this post for coordinated misinformation. Post:[flagged post text] Relevant campaign context:- Five related accounts posted semantically equivalent claims within 12 minutes.- The posts share the same uncommon URL and phrasing.- Three accounts previously amplified one another in the same sequence.- Assess this post as part of that coordinated pattern, not only in isolation.\n```\n\nThis approach preserves the specialist architecture while keeping the transferred context focused. It also avoids increasing the sub-agent’s context-window size because the problem is solved by improving the content of the handoff, not expanding maximum capacity.\n\nThe coordinator should pass a concise evidence-based rationale rather than hidden internal chain-of-thought. The handoff needs observable facts, relevant conclusions, and policy signals—not a complete reasoning transcript.","C. Have the coordinator stop dispatching to sub-agents entirely and classify every post itself, since the sub-agent architecture is fundamentally incompatible with pattern-level context.\n\nMulti-agent specialization is not inherently incompatible with cross-post analysis.\n\nThe failure is caused by an incomplete handoff, not by the existence of specialist agents. The coordinator can continue routing work as long as it passes the contextual evidence required for the specialist to perform its task correctly.\n\nEliminating sub-agents would discard their domain specialization without addressing the underlying context-transfer principle.","D. Increase each sub-agent's model's context window so it can hold more historical posts, without changing what's included in the dispatch message itself.\n\nA larger context window cannot help when the required information is never included.\n\nThe sub-agent still receives only the isolated post text. Increasing its capacity does not give it access to the coordinator’s previously observed campaign pattern.\n\nThis is an information-transfer failure, not a context-capacity failure."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps"],"source_hash":"911139ef63f26249f3479decb0aa22e0819c178be98bc8426e2492c1a264769f","stem_ja":"調整エージェントが、問題として検出したSNS投稿を審査し、ヘイトスピーチ・スパム・誤情報の専門サブエージェントへ振り分けます。現在はタスクメッセージとして、対象投稿の生テキストだけを渡しています。ある投稿は、調整役がすでに審査した複数の関連投稿との組織的なパターンの一部と判断したために検出されました。しかし委譲先はその背景を見ず、切り離された投稿文だけを読み、単独では無害と誤分類します。組織的キャンペーンの一部だからこそ規則違反となる要点を見逃しています。\n\n各サブエージェントのコンテキストウィンドウを拡張せずに解決する設定はどれですか。","options_ja":["必要な詳細を1つも落とさないよう、毎回の委譲で、複数投稿を審査した調整役の生のセッショントレース全体を各サブエージェントへ渡す。","調整役が見つけた関連パターンの判断の背景を短く要約し、各タスクメッセージに添付する。調整役の生の全履歴ではなく、必要なコンテキストをサブエージェントへ引き継ぐ。","パターン単位の背景はサブエージェント構成と根本的に両立しないため、委譲を完全にやめて、調整役がすべての投稿を自分で分類する。","渡すメッセージの内容は変えず、過去の投稿を多く保持できるよう、各サブエージェントのモデルのコンテキストウィンドウを増やす。"],"explanation_ja":"専門家に投稿だけを渡すと、なぜ問題として検出したかという関連性を失います。委譲時に必要なパターンと根拠を短く添えれば、全履歴を渡さずに文脈を引き継げます。覚え方は「対象物だけでなく、判断を左右する背景も渡す」。ここで必要なのは容量追加ではなく、引き渡す情報の設計です。","distractors_ja":["必要な背景は得られますが、関係のない全履歴まで投入するため、負荷を抑える設計として過剰です。","分類判断に必要なパターンをコンパクトに引き継ぎ、単独投稿としての誤解を防げます。","専門エージェントの構成自体ではなく、タスクへの背景情報不足を直せば対処できます。","渡す情報が同じなら背景は依然として欠落し、窓を広げても届かない情報は利用できません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-042","number":42,"form":2,"stem":"A Pricing Agent and an Inventory Agent both independently feed a Checkout-Summary Agent for the same order. The Pricing Agent validates a promotional discount code as still active and applies it. The Inventory Agent, unaware the Pricing Agent had just validated that specific code, independently checks its own separately-cached promo-validity list — which hasn't been refreshed as recently — and flags the same order as \"promo code likely expired.\" The Checkout-Summary Agent presents both conflicting statements to the customer in the same summary.\n\nWhich context propagation change would most likely prevent this conflict?","options":["Have the Pricing Agent stop validating promo codes entirely, and let the Inventory Agent be the sole source of promo-code status.","Have the Checkout-Summary Agent silently pick whichever statement appears first in its input, without surfacing the conflict to the customer.","Ensure both agents defer to one single, shared, authoritative validation of the promo code's status — computed once and propagated to whichever agent needs it — rather than each agent independently maintaining or checking its own separately-cached copy.","Have the Inventory Agent re-run its own independent promo-validity check more frequently, on a faster refresh schedule, while continuing to maintain it separately from the Pricing Agent's validation."],"correct":2,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"正式な検証結果を1回確定して伝播","concept":"ce-f2-q042","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Ensure both agents defer to one single, shared, authoritative validation of the promo code's status — computed once and propagated to whichever agent needs it — rather than each agent independently maintaining or checking its own separately-cached copy.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe conflict occurs because the two agents independently determine the same mutable fact using different cached data:\n\n- The Pricing Agent validates the promotion against current information.\n\n- The Inventory Agent uses an older, independently maintained cache.\n\n- The Checkout-Summary Agent receives two contradictory conclusions.\n\nPromo-code validity should be evaluated once by an authoritative source and propagated as shared order context. Both agents should consume the same validated result rather than independently recomputing it from potentially different data versions.\n\nThe correct pattern is:\n\nAuthoritative promotion validation → shared validated result → Pricing Agent, Inventory Agent, and Checkout-Summary Agent\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nPromo validity is a shared, mutable business fact. It should have one authoritative representation for the current order.\n\nFor example, the validation result could be propagated as structured context:\n\n```\n{  \"promo_code\": \"SAVE20\",  \"status\": \"ACTIVE\",  \"discount_percent\": 20,  \"validated_at\": \"2026-08-02T15:30:00Z\",  \"validation_source\": \"promotion_service\",  \"order_id\": \"ORD-10428\"}\n```\n\nThe Pricing Agent can apply the validated discount, while the Inventory Agent can use the result if promotion status affects reservation or eligibility logic. Neither agent should independently replace that result with a separately cached interpretation.\n\nDatabricks multi-agent patterns recognize that specialized agents have separate contexts and require deliberate orchestration and information exchange. A coordinator or shared retrieval layer should ensure that agents involved in the same workflow receive consistent authoritative inputs.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Have the Pricing Agent stop validating promo codes entirely, and let the Inventory Agent be the sole source of promo-code status.\n\nCentralizing validation in one component could be a valid architectural pattern, but this option assigns responsibility to the Inventory Agent even though its current information is stale.\n\nPromo-code validation is also more naturally associated with pricing or a dedicated promotion-validation service than inventory management. The important correction is not arbitrarily choosing the Inventory Agent; it is establishing one authoritative validation result that all agents share.\n\nB. Have the Checkout-Summary Agent silently pick whichever statement appears first in its input, without surfacing the conflict to the customer.\n\nInput order does not indicate which result is accurate or current.\n\nThis approach would:\n\n- Hide rather than resolve the disagreement.\n\n- Make correctness dependent on message ordering.\n\n- Potentially select the stale result.\n\n- Produce nondeterministic behavior if agent-output ordering changes.\n\nThe conflict must be prevented upstream by ensuring that both agents use the same authoritative fact.\n\nD. Have the Inventory Agent re-run its own independent promo-validity check more frequently, on a faster refresh schedule, while continuing to maintain it separately from the Pricing Agent's validation.\n\nA faster refresh might reduce the frequency of disagreement, but it would not eliminate the architectural problem.\n\nThe agents would still maintain two independent representations of the same fact. Conflicts could continue because of:\n\n- Refresh timing differences.\n\n- Cache invalidation delays.\n\n- Concurrent promotion updates.\n\n- Different query or validation logic.\n\n- Temporary service failures.\n\nThe appropriate solution is a shared authoritative value, not merely two caches that are refreshed more often.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is treating this as a cache-refresh-frequency problem.\n\nFresher independent caches can still disagree. The root issue is duplicated ownership of shared mutable context.\n\nAnother trap is allowing the summary agent to arbitrate conflicting facts based on presentation order rather than source authority, timestamp, or an explicit precedence rule.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production checkout workflow should validate the promotion once through a governed promotion service or deterministic tool. The resulting status should be attached to a shared order-state object and passed to every agent involved in the transaction.\n\nThe implementation should include:\n\n- A validation timestamp and result version.\n\n- A single authoritative validation source.\n\n- Order-scoped propagation of the result.\n\n- Cache invalidation when promotion rules change.\n\n- A rule preventing sub-agents from independently overriding validated shared state.\n\n- Trace logging showing which validation result each agent consumed.\n\nIf revalidation is required immediately before checkout, it should again occur once centrally, with the updated result propagated to all dependent agents.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- Use Supervisor Agent to create a coordinated multi-agent system\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor)\n\n- Connect agents to structured data\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools)\n\n- Agent state and memory\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)","source_option_explanations":["A. Have the Pricing Agent stop validating promo codes entirely, and let the Inventory Agent be the sole source of promo-code status.\n\nCentralizing validation in one component could be a valid architectural pattern, but this option assigns responsibility to the Inventory Agent even though its current information is stale.\n\nPromo-code validation is also more naturally associated with pricing or a dedicated promotion-validation service than inventory management. The important correction is not arbitrarily choosing the Inventory Agent; it is establishing one authoritative validation result that all agents share.","B. Have the Checkout-Summary Agent silently pick whichever statement appears first in its input, without surfacing the conflict to the customer.\n\nInput order does not indicate which result is accurate or current.\n\nThis approach would:\n\n- Hide rather than resolve the disagreement.\n\n- Make correctness dependent on message ordering.\n\n- Potentially select the stale result.\n\n- Produce nondeterministic behavior if agent-output ordering changes.\n\nThe conflict must be prevented upstream by ensuring that both agents use the same authoritative fact.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Ensure both agents defer to one single, shared, authoritative validation of the promo code's status — computed once and propagated to whichever agent needs it — rather than each agent independently maintaining or checking its own separately-cached copy.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe conflict occurs because the two agents independently determine the same mutable fact using different cached data:\n\n- The Pricing Agent validates the promotion against current information.\n\n- The Inventory Agent uses an older, independently maintained cache.\n\n- The Checkout-Summary Agent receives two contradictory conclusions.\n\nPromo-code validity should be evaluated once by an authoritative source and propagated as shared order context. Both agents should consume the same validated result rather than independently recomputing it from potentially different data versions.\n\nThe correct pattern is:\n\nAuthoritative promotion validation → shared validated result → Pricing Agent, Inventory Agent, and Checkout-Summary Agent\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nPromo validity is a shared, mutable business fact. It should have one authoritative representation for the current order.\n\nFor example, the validation result could be propagated as structured context:\n\n```\n{  \"promo_code\": \"SAVE20\",  \"status\": \"ACTIVE\",  \"discount_percent\": 20,  \"validated_at\": \"2026-08-02T15:30:00Z\",  \"validation_source\": \"promotion_service\",  \"order_id\": \"ORD-10428\"}\n```\n\nThe Pricing Agent can apply the validated discount, while the Inventory Agent can use the result if promotion status affects reservation or eligibility logic. Neither agent should independently replace that result with a separately cached interpretation.\n\nDatabricks multi-agent patterns recognize that specialized agents have separate contexts and require deliberate orchestration and information exchange. A coordinator or shared retrieval layer should ensure that agents involved in the same workflow receive consistent authoritative inputs.","D. Have the Inventory Agent re-run its own independent promo-validity check more frequently, on a faster refresh schedule, while continuing to maintain it separately from the Pricing Agent's validation.\n\nA faster refresh might reduce the frequency of disagreement, but it would not eliminate the architectural problem.\n\nThe agents would still maintain two independent representations of the same fact. Conflicts could continue because of:\n\n- Refresh timing differences.\n\n- Cache invalidation delays.\n\n- Concurrent promotion updates.\n\n- Different query or validation logic.\n\n- Temporary service failures.\n\nThe appropriate solution is a shared authoritative value, not merely two caches that are refreshed more often."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/structured-retrieval-tools","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management"],"source_hash":"3e198817890ff888f3099f5664b08694e929a8b4fdd891d2669746a4ea7c3d83","stem_ja":"同じ注文について、価格エージェントと在庫エージェントが独立して購入確認要約エージェントへ情報を送ります。価格エージェントはプロモーション割引コードがまだ有効と検証して適用します。在庫エージェントは、そのコードを価格側が直前に検証したことを知らず、更新がより古い独自キャッシュのプロモーション有効性一覧を確認し、同じ注文へ「割引コードは期限切れの可能性が高い」と付けます。要約エージェントは両方の矛盾した記述を、同じ顧客向け要約へ載せます。\n\nこの矛盾を最も防ぎやすい、コンテキスト伝播の変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["価格エージェントによる割引コード検証を完全にやめ、在庫エージェントだけをコード状態の情報源にする。","要約エージェントが入力に先に現れた記述を黙って選び、顧客へ矛盾を示さないようにする。","各エージェントが独立したキャッシュを管理・確認する代わりに、コード状態を1回だけ正式に検証し、その単一の共有結果を必要なエージェントへ伝播させ、両方が従うようにする。","価格側の検証とは別に管理する方式を維持したまま、在庫エージェント独自の確認と更新をより高頻度にする。"],"explanation_ja":"同じ事実を独立に判定し、鮮度の違うコピーを正としていることが原因です。正式な検証結果を1つ確定し、必要な各エージェントへ伝えれば、矛盾した再判定を避けられます。覚え方は「共通の事実は、一度確定して共有」。単に更新頻度を上げても、別々の正を持つ構造は残ります。","distractors_ja":["古い独自キャッシュを使う在庫側を単に唯一の正へしても、正式な検証と結果伝播の要件を満たしません。","入力順に根拠なく選ぶと、有効性の誤判断を隠すだけで正確さを保証できません。","共通の正式な判断結果を全員へ引き継ぎ、独立した異なる状態の適用を防げます。","古い状態を使う確率は下がっても、独立した検証結果が食い違う仕組み自体は残ります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-043","number":43,"form":2,"stem":"An orchestrator coordinates 6 sub-agents in a QA-testing pipeline, each running a different test suite (unit, integration, e2e, performance, security, accessibility). Each sub-agent currently reports back its full raw console and log output — thousands of lines per suite — rather than a structured summary, even though the orchestrator only needs to know which suites passed, which failed, and the specific details of any failures to decide its next action.\n\nWhich sub-agent output design change would most reduce orchestrator context load without compromising task coherence?","options":["Increase the orchestrator's context window so it can comfortably hold all 6 sub-agents' full raw logs without truncation.","Have sub-agents send their full raw logs to each other directly, bypassing the orchestrator entirely, so the orchestrator's context load is unaffected regardless of log size.","Have sub-agents return a concise, structured summary — pass/fail counts and specific failure details only — rather than full raw logs, reserving the full logs for separate storage the orchestrator doesn't need to load for its coordination decisions.","Remove sub-agents from the architecture and have the orchestrator run every test suite itself in a single unified context."],"correct":2,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"サブエージェントは判断に必要な構造化結果を返す","concept":"ce-f2-q043","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Have sub-agents return a concise, structured summary — pass/fail counts and specific failure details only — rather than full raw logs, reserving the full logs for separate storage the orchestrator doesn't need to load for its coordination decisions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe orchestrator’s responsibility is to coordinate the QA workflow, not to inspect every line produced by every test runner.\n\nTo decide the next action, it needs only:\n\n- Which test suites completed\n\n- Which suites passed or failed\n\n- The number of passed and failed tests\n\n- The specific failed tests and concise failure details\n\n- Whether retry, escalation, or remediation is required\n\nPassing thousands of raw log lines from six sub-agents adds substantial token overhead and irrelevant diagnostic noise. Option C preserves the information required for orchestration while keeping detailed logs available outside the model’s active context for troubleshooting.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nMulti-agent systems work best when each specialist returns a result shaped for the receiving agent’s decision. The orchestrator should receive a compact, predictable output contract rather than each sub-agent’s entire execution trace.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\n{  \"suite\": \"integration\",  \"status\": \"FAILED\",  \"tests_total\": 184,  \"tests_passed\": 181,  \"tests_failed\": 3,  \"failures\": [    {      \"test\": \"test_payment_timeout\",      \"error\": \"Expected HTTP 504, received HTTP 500\",      \"log_reference\": \"dbfs:/qa-runs/run-782/integration.log\"    }  ]}\n```\n\nThis design preserves task coherence because the orchestrator knows:\n\n- What failed\n\n- Why it failed\n\n- Which workflow decision to make\n\n- Where the complete logs can be retrieved if deeper investigation becomes necessary\n\nThe raw logs can remain in durable storage or MLflow traces rather than being repeatedly inserted into the orchestrator’s model context. MLflow Tracing records agent inputs, outputs, intermediate operations, and metadata, allowing detailed diagnostic information to remain observable without requiring every artifact to be included in the next model prompt.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. Increase the orchestrator's context window so it can comfortably hold all 6 sub-agents' full raw logs without truncation.\n\nA larger context window would increase capacity but would not improve the quality of the information passed to the orchestrator.\n\nThe orchestrator would still receive:\n\n- Thousands of irrelevant successful-test lines\n\n- Repeated setup and teardown messages\n\n- Dependency and environment output\n\n- Low-level debugging information unrelated to coordination\n\nThis increases token cost, latency, and distraction. It also postpones rather than fixes the underlying interface-design problem.\n\nB. Have sub-agents send their full raw logs to each other directly, bypassing the orchestrator entirely, so the orchestrator's context load is unaffected regardless of log size.\n\nThis would spread the unnecessary context burden among the sub-agents.\n\nThe test-suite agents have distinct responsibilities. The accessibility agent generally does not need the performance suite’s complete raw logs, nor does the unit-test agent need thousands of security-scanner messages.\n\nIt would also make coordination and debugging more complex by creating unnecessary peer-to-peer information flow.\n\nD. Remove sub-agents from the architecture and have the orchestrator run every test suite itself in a single unified context.\n\nThis eliminates useful specialization without solving the log-volume problem.\n\nThe orchestrator would still generate or receive all six suites’ raw logs, but now within one component. It would also become responsible for test execution, interpretation, coordination, and decision-making, increasing complexity and reducing modularity.\n\nDatabricks describes multi-agent architectures as useful for coordinating specialized agents across larger domains, although their interfaces and orchestration must be designed carefully.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe main trap is treating context-window capacity as a substitute for good information design.\n\nThe orchestrator does not need every available detail. It needs the minimum sufficient output required to coordinate the workflow. Full diagnostic artifacts should remain retrievable without being placed in active context by default.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nDefine a common output schema for all six test-suite agents, including:\n\n- Suite name\n\n- Execution status\n\n- Pass, fail, and skipped counts\n\n- Concise failure records\n\n- Severity or blocking status\n\n- Retry recommendation\n\n- Full-log reference\n\n- Execution or trace ID\n\nFor successful suites, the response can be extremely small. For failed suites, include only actionable failure details. The orchestrator can retrieve a specific raw log on demand when the summary is insufficient.\n\nThis creates progressive disclosure:\n\nSummary by default → targeted failure details when needed → complete logs only for deep diagnosis\n\nThe expected exam answer is C because it produces the largest reduction in orchestrator context while preserving every fact necessary for coordination and follow-up.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- Build a multi-agent system on Databricks Apps\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps)\n\n- MLflow Tracing – GenAI observability\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/)\n\n- Trace agents deployed on Databricks\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/prod-tracing](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/prod-tracing)","source_option_explanations":["A. Increase the orchestrator's context window so it can comfortably hold all 6 sub-agents' full raw logs without truncation.\n\nA larger context window would increase capacity but would not improve the quality of the information passed to the orchestrator.\n\nThe orchestrator would still receive:\n\n- Thousands of irrelevant successful-test lines\n\n- Repeated setup and teardown messages\n\n- Dependency and environment output\n\n- Low-level debugging information unrelated to coordination\n\nThis increases token cost, latency, and distraction. It also postpones rather than fixes the underlying interface-design problem.","B. Have sub-agents send their full raw logs to each other directly, bypassing the orchestrator entirely, so the orchestrator's context load is unaffected regardless of log size.\n\nThis would spread the unnecessary context burden among the sub-agents.\n\nThe test-suite agents have distinct responsibilities. The accessibility agent generally does not need the performance suite’s complete raw logs, nor does the unit-test agent need thousands of security-scanner messages.\n\nIt would also make coordination and debugging more complex by creating unnecessary peer-to-peer information flow.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Have sub-agents return a concise, structured summary — pass/fail counts and specific failure details only — rather than full raw logs, reserving the full logs for separate storage the orchestrator doesn't need to load for its coordination decisions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe orchestrator’s responsibility is to coordinate the QA workflow, not to inspect every line produced by every test runner.\n\nTo decide the next action, it needs only:\n\n- Which test suites completed\n\n- Which suites passed or failed\n\n- The number of passed and failed tests\n\n- The specific failed tests and concise failure details\n\n- Whether retry, escalation, or remediation is required\n\nPassing thousands of raw log lines from six sub-agents adds substantial token overhead and irrelevant diagnostic noise. Option C preserves the information required for orchestration while keeping detailed logs available outside the model’s active context for troubleshooting.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nMulti-agent systems work best when each specialist returns a result shaped for the receiving agent’s decision. The orchestrator should receive a compact, predictable output contract rather than each sub-agent’s entire execution trace.\n\nFor example:\n\n```\n{  \"suite\": \"integration\",  \"status\": \"FAILED\",  \"tests_total\": 184,  \"tests_passed\": 181,  \"tests_failed\": 3,  \"failures\": [    {      \"test\": \"test_payment_timeout\",      \"error\": \"Expected HTTP 504, received HTTP 500\",      \"log_reference\": \"dbfs:/qa-runs/run-782/integration.log\"    }  ]}\n```\n\nThis design preserves task coherence because the orchestrator knows:\n\n- What failed\n\n- Why it failed\n\n- Which workflow decision to make\n\n- Where the complete logs can be retrieved if deeper investigation becomes necessary\n\nThe raw logs can remain in durable storage or MLflow traces rather than being repeatedly inserted into the orchestrator’s model context. MLflow Tracing records agent inputs, outputs, intermediate operations, and metadata, allowing detailed diagnostic information to remain observable without requiring every artifact to be included in the next model prompt.","D. Remove sub-agents from the architecture and have the orchestrator run every test suite itself in a single unified context.\n\nThis eliminates useful specialization without solving the log-volume problem.\n\nThe orchestrator would still generate or receive all six suites’ raw logs, but now within one component. It would also become responsible for test execution, interpretation, coordination, and decision-making, increasing complexity and reducing modularity.\n\nDatabricks describes multi-agent architectures as useful for coordinating specialized agents across larger domains, although their interfaces and orchestration must be designed carefully."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/prod-tracing"],"source_hash":"635b01d161ab826687fadadc9d351fe11346d4a2ab916dde7bb9acdf2698e260","stem_ja":"オーケストレーターが、QAテストパイプラインの6つのサブエージェントを調整しています。各エージェントは、単体、結合、E2E、性能、セキュリティ、アクセシビリティという異なるテスト群を実行します。現在は構造化された要約の代わりに、各テスト群で数千行に及ぶコンソール出力とログ全文を返します。しかし、次の行動を決めるために必要なのは、どのテスト群が成功・失敗したかと、失敗の具体的な詳細だけです。\n\nタスクの一貫性を損なわず、オーケストレーターのコンテキスト負荷を最も減らす、出力設計の変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["6つのサブエージェントの生ログ全文が切り詰められず余裕を持って収まるよう、オーケストレーターのコンテキストウィンドウを増やす。","ログの大きさに関係なく調整役の負荷を変えないよう、オーケストレーターを迂回して、サブエージェント同士が生ログ全文を直接送り合う。","全文ログではなく、成功・失敗件数と失敗の具体的な内容だけの簡潔な構造化要約を返す。調整判断で読み込む必要のない全文ログは、別のストレージに保存する。","サブエージェントを構成から除き、オーケストレーター自身が、単一の統合コンテキストですべてのテスト群を実行する。"],"explanation_ja":"調整役が必要とするのは、全作業の経緯ではなく次の判断に使う結果です。成功・失敗と具体的な障害を構造化して返し、全文ログは別に保存して必要時だけ参照します。覚え方は「結果は短く構造化、詳細ログは外に」。判断材料を保ちつつ、各専門家の内部作業を抱え込まずに済みます。","distractors_ja":["容量を増やしても、判断に使わない大量の成功ログまで入力する不要な負荷は残ります。","他のサブエージェントまで無関係なログを抱え、調整役にも判断用の共通結果が届かない設計です。","次の判断に必要な情報だけを返し、調査用の全文は外部に残すため、負荷と一貫性を両立できます。","すべての実行結果を単一コンテキストへ集めるため、問題の負荷集中を悪化させます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-044","number":44,"form":2,"stem":"A team builds a single, monolithic \"full-stack-deployment\" agent that handles provisioning infrastructure, deploying application code, running database migrations, and monitoring post-deploy health, all within one continuous agent context for a given release. As deployments grow more complex, this single agent's context window fills with logs and output accumulated from every stage, and by the time it reaches the monitoring stage, it has begun losing track of early provisioning decisions — on one occasion misinterpreting normal post-deploy metrics as evidence of a provisioning-stage failure due to context rot within its own overloaded context.\n\nWhich boundary placement failure is occurring, and what's the general direction of the fix?","options":["The boundaries are drawn too coarse — a single agent is handling too many sequential responsibilities within one context, causing unmanageable context window growth; the fix is to split the work into a few more appropriately-scoped agents, such as separate provisioning, deployment, and monitoring agents.","The boundaries are drawn too fine — too many narrowly-scoped agents create excessive handoff overhead; the fix is to consolidate further into an even more monolithic single agent.","There is no boundary placement failure here; the misinterpretation is unrelated to the architecture and is simply a model-quality issue that a larger model would resolve regardless of context load.","The boundaries are correctly placed; the fix is to increase this single agent's context window, since a larger window always resolves context-rot-related misinterpretation regardless of how many responsibilities are packed into one context."],"correct":0,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"広すぎるエージェントの責任範囲","concept":"ce-f2-q044","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. The boundaries are drawn too coarse — a single agent is handling too many sequential responsibilities within one context, causing unmanageable context window growth; the fix is to split the work into a few more appropriately-scoped agents, such as separate provisioning, deployment, and monitoring agents.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe monolithic agent owns four distinct operational stages:\n\n- Infrastructure provisioning\n\n- Application deployment\n\n- Database migration\n\n- Post-deployment monitoring\n\nEach stage generates substantial logs, tool outputs, decisions, and intermediate state. Because all of this accumulates in one continuous context, information relevant to the current stage becomes mixed with large amounts of earlier-stage detail.\n\nBy the monitoring stage, the context is overloaded enough that the agent confuses normal health metrics with an earlier provisioning problem. This is a classic sign that the responsibility boundary is too coarse.\n\nThe general correction is to divide the workflow into a small number of coherent stages or specialized agents, with concise, structured handoffs between them.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nDatabricks recommends considering a multi-agent design when an application contains clearly distinct tasks, multiple conversational contexts, or a large set of tools. It also recommends providing each agent only the tools and context it requires rather than an unbounded amount of irrelevant context.\n\nA more appropriate architecture could be:\n\n- Provisioning agent\n\n- Creates infrastructure.\n\n- Validates resource configuration.\n\n- Returns resource identifiers, region, network decisions, and provisioning status.\n\n- Deployment and migration agent\n\n- Deploys application artifacts.\n\n- Executes governed database migrations.\n\n- Returns deployed version, migration status, and rollback information.\n\n- Monitoring agent\n\n- Receives the confirmed deployment baseline.\n\n- Evaluates post-deployment health.\n\n- Receives only the provisioning facts necessary to interpret current metrics.\n\nThe handoff should contain structured state such as:\n\n```\n{  \"release_id\": \"release-842\",  \"region\": \"eu-west-1\",  \"provisioning_status\": \"SUCCESS\",  \"application_version\": \"4.8.2\",  \"migration_status\": \"SUCCESS\",  \"expected_replica_count\": 6,  \"monitoring_baseline\": \"baseline-v14\"}\n```\n\nThe monitoring agent does not need thousands of raw provisioning and migration log lines. Those artifacts can remain in durable storage and be retrieved only when a specific diagnostic question requires them.\n\nThis reduces context growth while preserving the decisions that later stages depend on.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nB. The boundaries are drawn too fine — too many narrowly-scoped agents create excessive handoff overhead; the fix is to consolidate further into an even more monolithic single agent.\n\nThe scenario describes only one agent, so excessive fragmentation is not the current problem.\n\nFurther consolidation would increase:\n\n- Context accumulation\n\n- Tool-set size\n\n- Responsibility overlap\n\n- Difficulty isolating failures\n\n- Risk that information from one stage distracts reasoning in another\n\nAgent boundaries can certainly be drawn too narrowly in other designs, but this scenario shows the opposite failure.\n\nC. There is no boundary placement failure here; the misinterpretation is unrelated to the architecture and is simply a model-quality issue that a larger model would resolve regardless of context load.\n\nThe evidence explicitly connects the failure to the overloaded context:\n\n- Logs accumulate from every deployment stage.\n\n- Early decisions become difficult to track.\n\n- The agent confuses monitoring evidence with provisioning-stage failures.\n\nA stronger model might reduce the frequency of the error, but it would not correct the architectural cause. It would still receive an unnecessarily broad and noisy context covering several distinct responsibilities.\n\nD. The boundaries are correctly placed; the fix is to increase this single agent's context window, since a larger window always resolves context-rot-related misinterpretation regardless of how many responsibilities are packed into one context.\n\nA larger context window may postpone the limit, but it does not guarantee that the model will correctly prioritize information within an increasingly large and noisy trace.\n\nIt would also preserve:\n\n- Irrelevant successful-operation logs\n\n- Obsolete intermediate observations\n\n- Superseded hypotheses\n\n- Stage-specific outputs that later stages do not need\n\nThe threshold determines how much content can be retained; it does not make the underlying responsibility boundary appropriate.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming that context-window enlargement is the primary solution to every long-context failure.\n\nThe scenario is testing agent boundary placement. When one agent owns several clearly distinct, output-heavy responsibilities, the better response is to establish coherent execution boundaries and pass compact, task-relevant state between stages.\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nThe real-world solution does not necessarily require one autonomous LLM agent for every deployment step. A robust design may combine:\n\n- Deterministic orchestration for fixed deployment stages\n\n- Specialized agents for stages requiring judgment\n\n- Structured state shared between stages\n\n- Durable storage for raw logs\n\n- On-demand log retrieval for diagnostics\n\n- MLflow traces spanning the complete release workflow\n\nThe team should avoid overcorrecting into dozens of tiny agents. The goal is a few meaningful boundaries aligned with distinct responsibilities, tools, context requirements, and failure domains.\n\nThe expected exam answer is A because the current boundary is too coarse and is causing unrelated stage context to accumulate within one overloaded agent.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- Build a multi-agent system on Databricks Apps\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/generative-ai/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/generative-ai/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps)\n\n- Use Supervisor Agent to create a coordinated multi-agent system\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. The boundaries are drawn too coarse — a single agent is handling too many sequential responsibilities within one context, causing unmanageable context window growth; the fix is to split the work into a few more appropriately-scoped agents, such as separate provisioning, deployment, and monitoring agents.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe monolithic agent owns four distinct operational stages:\n\n- Infrastructure provisioning\n\n- Application deployment\n\n- Database migration\n\n- Post-deployment monitoring\n\nEach stage generates substantial logs, tool outputs, decisions, and intermediate state. Because all of this accumulates in one continuous context, information relevant to the current stage becomes mixed with large amounts of earlier-stage detail.\n\nBy the monitoring stage, the context is overloaded enough that the agent confuses normal health metrics with an earlier provisioning problem. This is a classic sign that the responsibility boundary is too coarse.\n\nThe general correction is to divide the workflow into a small number of coherent stages or specialized agents, with concise, structured handoffs between them.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nDatabricks recommends considering a multi-agent design when an application contains clearly distinct tasks, multiple conversational contexts, or a large set of tools. It also recommends providing each agent only the tools and context it requires rather than an unbounded amount of irrelevant context.\n\nA more appropriate architecture could be:\n\n- Provisioning agent\n\n- Creates infrastructure.\n\n- Validates resource configuration.\n\n- Returns resource identifiers, region, network decisions, and provisioning status.\n\n- Deployment and migration agent\n\n- Deploys application artifacts.\n\n- Executes governed database migrations.\n\n- Returns deployed version, migration status, and rollback information.\n\n- Monitoring agent\n\n- Receives the confirmed deployment baseline.\n\n- Evaluates post-deployment health.\n\n- Receives only the provisioning facts necessary to interpret current metrics.\n\nThe handoff should contain structured state such as:\n\n```\n{  \"release_id\": \"release-842\",  \"region\": \"eu-west-1\",  \"provisioning_status\": \"SUCCESS\",  \"application_version\": \"4.8.2\",  \"migration_status\": \"SUCCESS\",  \"expected_replica_count\": 6,  \"monitoring_baseline\": \"baseline-v14\"}\n```\n\nThe monitoring agent does not need thousands of raw provisioning and migration log lines. Those artifacts can remain in durable storage and be retrieved only when a specific diagnostic question requires them.\n\nThis reduces context growth while preserving the decisions that later stages depend on.","B. The boundaries are drawn too fine — too many narrowly-scoped agents create excessive handoff overhead; the fix is to consolidate further into an even more monolithic single agent.\n\nThe scenario describes only one agent, so excessive fragmentation is not the current problem.\n\nFurther consolidation would increase:\n\n- Context accumulation\n\n- Tool-set size\n\n- Responsibility overlap\n\n- Difficulty isolating failures\n\n- Risk that information from one stage distracts reasoning in another\n\nAgent boundaries can certainly be drawn too narrowly in other designs, but this scenario shows the opposite failure.","C. There is no boundary placement failure here; the misinterpretation is unrelated to the architecture and is simply a model-quality issue that a larger model would resolve regardless of context load.\n\nThe evidence explicitly connects the failure to the overloaded context:\n\n- Logs accumulate from every deployment stage.\n\n- Early decisions become difficult to track.\n\n- The agent confuses monitoring evidence with provisioning-stage failures.\n\nA stronger model might reduce the frequency of the error, but it would not correct the architectural cause. It would still receive an unnecessarily broad and noisy context covering several distinct responsibilities.","D. The boundaries are correctly placed; the fix is to increase this single agent's context window, since a larger window always resolves context-rot-related misinterpretation regardless of how many responsibilities are packed into one context.\n\nA larger context window may postpone the limit, but it does not guarantee that the model will correctly prioritize information within an increasingly large and noisy trace.\n\nIt would also preserve:\n\n- Irrelevant successful-operation logs\n\n- Obsolete intermediate observations\n\n- Superseded hypotheses\n\n- Stage-specific outputs that later stages do not need\n\nThe threshold determines how much content can be retained; it does not make the underlying responsibility boundary appropriate."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/generative-ai/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-bricks/multi-agent-supervisor"],"source_hash":"2eb13e1d71cc8e02802e11591824e21145ed369eb5487ce2ea1ef0271d531125","stem_ja":"チームが、リリースごとに1つの連続したコンテキストで、インフラの準備、アプリコードのデプロイ、データベース移行、デプロイ後の稼働監視まで扱う、一枚岩の「full-stack-deployment」エージェントを構築しました。デプロイが複雑になるにつれ、全段階のログと出力でコンテキストウィンドウが埋まり、監視段階に達する頃には、初期のインフラ準備で決めた事項を見失い始めます。あるときは、過負荷のコンテキストで情報を正しく扱えなくなり、正常なデプロイ後指標を、インフラ準備段階の障害の証拠と誤解しました。\n\nエージェントの境界設定にどのような失敗があり、修正の大まかな方向は何ですか。","options_ja":["境界が粗すぎる。1つのエージェントが同じコンテキストで多すぎる連続した責任を持ち、成長を管理できなくなっている。準備、デプロイ、監視など、適切な範囲の少数のエージェントに分ける。","境界が細かすぎる。狭い役割のエージェントが多すぎて引き渡し負担が増えているため、さらに一枚岩の単一エージェントへ統合する。","境界設定の失敗ではなく、構成と無関係なモデル品質の問題なので、コンテキスト負荷にかかわらず大きいモデルへ変えれば解決する。","境界は正しく、コンテキストが劣化する誤解は窓を広げれば常に解消するため、詰め込んだ責任数に関係なく、この1エージェントの窓を増やせばよい。"],"explanation_ja":"準備・移行・監視という異なる責任の詳細を、1つの作業コンテキストへ詰め込み過ぎています。意味のある少数の段階へ分け、確認済みの決定や状態だけを構造化して引き渡すのが適切です。覚え方は「1役に抱えさせ過ぎなら、責任の境界を分ける」。細かいエージェントを無数に作る必要はありません。","distractors_ja":["異なる責任とログの集中が原因なので、適切な段階分割で作業コンテキストを分ける方向が合います。","問題ではすでに単一のエージェントであり、細分化し過ぎが原因ではありません。","過負荷のコンテキストとの関連が明示され、モデルだけを変えても責任集中は残ります。","窓の拡大は容量を増やしますが、異なる段階の無関係なログが判断を妨げることを保証して防ぎません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B002-045","number":45,"form":2,"stem":"A document-translation pipeline translates the same source document into 8 independent target languages. None of the 8 translation sub-tasks depend on each other's output in any way — translating to French doesn't require waiting for the German translation to finish, or vice versa. Despite this, the team currently runs all 8 language translations strictly sequentially, one full agent run completing before the next begins, taking roughly 8 times longer than necessary with no coherence or quality benefit gained from the sequential ordering.\n\nWhich long-horizon strategy mismatch is occurring, and what should replace it?","options":["The mismatch is that a parallel/fan-out strategy is being used when a sequential strategy would be more coherent; the fix is to keep the current design, since sequential ordering is always safer regardless of dependency structure.","The mismatch is that a sequential execution strategy is being applied to sub-tasks that are genuinely independent and have no dependency on each other's output; the appropriate replacement is a parallel/fan-out execution strategy, dispatching all 8 language translations to run concurrently, since none depends on another's result.","The mismatch is unrelated to execution ordering; the real issue is translation quality, and the fix is to switch to a larger model while keeping the sequential design unchanged.","There is no mismatch; sequential execution is an unavoidable requirement of any multi-language translation pipeline regardless of whether the languages depend on each other."],"correct":1,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"独立したタスクを並列化","concept":"ce-f2-q045","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. The mismatch is that a sequential execution strategy is being applied to sub-tasks that are genuinely independent and have no dependency on each other's output; the appropriate replacement is a parallel/fan-out execution strategy, dispatching all 8 language translations to run concurrently, since none depends on another's result.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe determining factor is the dependency structure of the work:\n\n- All eight translations use the same source document.\n\n- Each produces an independent target-language output.\n\n- No translation consumes another translation’s result.\n\n- Sequential execution provides no quality or coherence advantage.\n\n- Sequential processing makes the overall duration approximately the sum of all eight translation times.\n\nBecause the translations have no inter-task dependencies, the orchestrator should fan the source document out to eight translation tasks concurrently and collect the results after they complete.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA sequential workflow is appropriate when one task requires the output of an earlier task. That dependency does not exist here.\n\nThe appropriate execution pattern is:\n\nSource document → fan out to eight language tasks → execute concurrently → fan in completed translations\n\nParallel execution can reduce total elapsed time from approximately:\n\nSum of all eight translation durations\n\nto approximately:\n\nDuration of the slowest translation, plus orchestration overhead\n\nThis change preserves translation quality because each worker still receives the complete source document, target language, terminology rules, and formatting requirements. It changes execution scheduling, not translation instructions.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\nA. The mismatch is that a parallel/fan-out strategy is being used when a sequential strategy would be more coherent; the fix is to keep the current design, since sequential ordering is always safer regardless of dependency structure.\n\nThe current implementation is sequential, not parallel.\n\nSequential ordering is not inherently safer. It is justified only when later tasks depend on earlier outputs, when shared resources require serialization, or when business rules impose ordering. None of those constraints appears here.\n\nC. The mismatch is unrelated to execution ordering; the real issue is translation quality, and the fix is to switch to a larger model while keeping the sequential design unchanged.\n\nThe scenario identifies no translation-quality problem.\n\nThe reported issue is that processing takes roughly eight times longer than necessary because independent tasks are run one after another. A larger model would not correct that scheduling inefficiency and might increase cost or latency further.\n\nD. There is no mismatch; sequential execution is an unavoidable requirement of any multi-language translation pipeline regardless of whether the languages depend on each other.\n\nMulti-language translation does not inherently require sequential execution.\n\nEach translation can operate independently when all workers receive the same authoritative source document and target-specific instructions. Sequential execution would be necessary only if one translation were derived from another, which is explicitly not the case.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is assuming that a multi-step or multi-agent workflow must always run sequentially.\n\nThe correct execution strategy depends on dependencies:\n\n- Dependent tasks: Sequential\n\n- Independent tasks: Parallel or fan-out\n\n- Independent tasks followed by synthesis: Fan-out, then fan-in\n\nReal-World Learning Point\n\nA production orchestrator should dispatch all eight translation jobs concurrently while applying:\n\n- Concurrency and rate limits\n\n- Independent retry handling per language\n\n- Target-language-specific terminology rules\n\n- Idempotent output paths\n\n- Partial-failure handling\n\n- A final fan-in step that verifies all expected translations arrived\n\nOne failed translation should generally be retried independently rather than forcing all successful translations to run again.\n\nThe expected exam answer is B because the current sequential strategy does not match the independent structure of the translation tasks.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent system design patterns\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns)\n\n- Build a multi-agent system on Databricks Apps\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps)\n\n- Configure task dependencies\n[https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-if](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-if)","source_option_explanations":["A. The mismatch is that a parallel/fan-out strategy is being used when a sequential strategy would be more coherent; the fix is to keep the current design, since sequential ordering is always safer regardless of dependency structure.\n\nThe current implementation is sequential, not parallel.\n\nSequential ordering is not inherently safer. It is justified only when later tasks depend on earlier outputs, when shared resources require serialization, or when business rules impose ordering. None of those constraints appears here.","Correct Answer\n\nB. The mismatch is that a sequential execution strategy is being applied to sub-tasks that are genuinely independent and have no dependency on each other's output; the appropriate replacement is a parallel/fan-out execution strategy, dispatching all 8 language translations to run concurrently, since none depends on another's result.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe determining factor is the dependency structure of the work:\n\n- All eight translations use the same source document.\n\n- Each produces an independent target-language output.\n\n- No translation consumes another translation’s result.\n\n- Sequential execution provides no quality or coherence advantage.\n\n- Sequential processing makes the overall duration approximately the sum of all eight translation times.\n\nBecause the translations have no inter-task dependencies, the orchestrator should fan the source document out to eight translation tasks concurrently and collect the results after they complete.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA sequential workflow is appropriate when one task requires the output of an earlier task. That dependency does not exist here.\n\nThe appropriate execution pattern is:\n\nSource document → fan out to eight language tasks → execute concurrently → fan in completed translations\n\nParallel execution can reduce total elapsed time from approximately:\n\nSum of all eight translation durations\n\nto approximately:\n\nDuration of the slowest translation, plus orchestration overhead\n\nThis change preserves translation quality because each worker still receives the complete source document, target language, terminology rules, and formatting requirements. It changes execution scheduling, not translation instructions.","C. The mismatch is unrelated to execution ordering; the real issue is translation quality, and the fix is to switch to a larger model while keeping the sequential design unchanged.\n\nThe scenario identifies no translation-quality problem.\n\nThe reported issue is that processing takes roughly eight times longer than necessary because independent tasks are run one after another. A larger model would not correct that scheduling inefficiency and might increase cost or latency further.","D. There is no mismatch; sequential execution is an unavoidable requirement of any multi-language translation pipeline regardless of whether the languages depend on each other.\n\nMulti-language translation does not inherently require sequential execution.\n\nEach translation can operate independently when all workers receive the same authoritative source document and target-specific instructions. Sequential execution would be necessary only if one translation were derived from another, which is explicitly not the case."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-system-design-patterns","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-framework/multi-agent-apps","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-if"],"source_hash":"b118ec3be35c9e1ab7b1c3ff2cf684aeae90a4815569e151b452d613fee4f20f","stem_ja":"文書翻訳パイプラインが、同じ原文を独立した8つの言語へ翻訳します。8つの翻訳タスクには互いの出力への依存がなく、フランス語翻訳はドイツ語の完了を待つ必要も、その逆もありません。それにもかかわらず、チームは1つのエージェント実行が完全に終わってから次を始める形で、8言語を厳密に順次処理しています。順番に行うことで一貫性や品質は向上せず、必要な時間のおよそ8倍かかっています。\n\n長時間タスクの戦略にどのような不一致があり、何に置き換えるべきですか。","options_ja":["並列／ファンアウトが使われているのに順次方式の方が一貫するという不一致である。依存関係にかかわらず順次方式が常に安全なので、今の設計を維持する。","互いの出力に依存しない、本当に独立した小タスクへ順次実行を適用しているのが不一致である。どれも他の結果を待つ必要がないため、8言語すべてを同時に起動する並列／ファンアウト方式へ置き換える。","実行順とは関係なく翻訳品質が本当の問題なので、順次設計は維持したまま大きいモデルへ変える。","言語間で依存しているかどうかに関係なく、複数言語の翻訳は必ず順次実行しなければならないので、不一致はない。"],"explanation_ja":"順次実行が必要なのは、後の処理が前の出力を必要とする場合です。今回は全タスクが同じ原文から独立して翻訳するので、並列に配り、最後に結果を集められます。覚え方は「依存ありは順次、独立なら並列」。実務では同時実行数やレート制限、言語ごとの再試行も管理します。","distractors_ja":["現在は順次実行であり、問題の状態を逆に説明しています。依存関係のない処理に順序の利益はありません。","タスク間に出力依存がないため、同時実行して待ち時間を削減する方式が合っています。","品質問題は示されておらず、不要な直列処理による遅さをモデル変更では解決できません。","同じ原文から独立に翻訳するなら、複数言語であることだけを理由に順次実行する必要はありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"}]},{"id":"CE-PRACTICE-3","title":"Context Engineer 模試3","minutes":90,"questions":[{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-001","number":1,"form":3,"stem":"A Databricks pipeline answers complex insurance-claims questions using three collaborating agents: routing, retrieval, and summarization. Each agent keeps separate session state, and communication occurs through a shared message bus. All three agents access the same up-to-date Databricks AI Search index.\n\nThe routing agent assigns a claim high priority, yet the final summary treats it as low priority. Claims adjusters have also observed conflicting recommendations, and the pipeline’s reliability scores have declined substantially over the past week.\n\nWhich underlying failure mode most likely explains the disagreement between agents?","options":["Different similarity thresholds and freshness settings cause the routing and summarization agents to retrieve disjoint or outdated document chunks for the same claim.","Agents independently assess claim severity using incomplete local context because authoritative shared context is insufficiently propagated, leaving the final decision without a consistent source of truth.","Out-of-order message delivery causes summarization to run before the routing classification arrives, so it proceeds with a null priority value.","Context truncation during prompt assembly prevents the routing agent’s priority classification from reaching the summarization agent’s input."],"correct":1,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"共有インデックスと共有タスク状態の違い","concept":"ce-f3-q001","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Agents independently assess claim severity using incomplete local context because authoritative shared context is insufficiently propagated, leaving the final decision without a consistent source of truth.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe required behavior is a coherent claim assessment across agents. The scenario establishes separate local states and contradictory decisions despite access to a common, current document index. B identifies the broader failure: relevant authoritative task context is not consistently shared or applied.\n\nA, C, and D propose specific implementation defects that could contribute to disagreement, but the scenario provides no retrieval comparisons, event-order traces, or truncation evidence establishing them.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Each agent keeps separate session state” — Technical signal: Decisions established in one agent’s session may be absent from another’s.\n\n- “Communication occurs through a shared message bus” — Technical signal: A communication channel exists, but its presence does not establish complete context propagation.\n\n- “The same up-to-date … index” — Technical signal: Shared source documents do not automatically convey the routing agent’s claim-specific classification.\n\n- “High priority” versus “low priority” — Technical signal: Agents are acting on incompatible interpretations of the same claim.\n\n- “Reliability scores have declined” — Technical signal: The coordination defect is affecting overall outcomes.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nEach agent can maintain a coherent local interpretation while lacking decisions established elsewhere. If the summarizer does not receive—or does not recognize as authoritative—the routing classification, it can independently derive a conflicting priority.\n\nB therefore best matches the reported failure pattern. Effective propagation must make relevant claim state available to downstream agents and establish how that state governs their work. Separate sessions and shared retrieval infrastructure do not provide this automatically.\n\nThis remains the best-supported diagnosis, rather than proof of the exact implementation defect.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Retrieval settings can influence which documents are returned, making different evidence a plausible explanation. However, no differing settings or stale results are reported. A current shared index does not guarantee identical results, but the scenario more directly supports inconsistent shared task context.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Premature processing could leave summarization without the classification. The message bus makes this tempting, but neither out-of-order delivery nor a null priority field is established. This explanation would fit if event traces confirmed that synthesis began before the required classification was available.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Trimming could remove an important classification and produce an inconsistent summary. However, no context-window pressure or prompt truncation is reported. This explanation would fit if prompt-assembly records showed that token-budget enforcement discarded the priority information.\n\nExam Trap\n\nA shared message bus can transport information without ensuring that every recipient receives, loads, and honors the same authoritative decisions. Likewise, a shared index supplies documents rather than automatically synchronizing workflow state.\n\nC or D could become the preferred diagnosis if traces established the corresponding timing or truncation mechanism.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nShared knowledge and shared task state serve different purposes. Retrieved documents supply evidence; task state captures the current claim’s classifications, findings, and decisions. Multi-agent consistency requires a clear authority and update policy for that state. A later agent may revise an earlier conclusion when justified, but that revision should be explicit and evidence-based.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nAn implementation could maintain a claim record containing its identifier, priority, classification version, and supporting evidence references. Before drafting, summarization would consume the applicable record and flag any proposed priority change for reconciliation. This could reduce contradictory recommendations while making decision changes traceable.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nShared documents support evidence; propagated authoritative state supports coordinated decisions.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen agents with separate local contexts disagree despite current shared retrieval, suspect insufficient shared task context. Select a specific transport, retrieval, or truncation defect only when the scenario provides evidence for it.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: The objective covering insufficient shared context as a cause of inconsistent outputs, conflicting decisions, and degraded reliability.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Databricks — Agent memory and sessions\n\nSupports: Session state contains interaction-specific messages, tool calls, and results that an agent must read to use as context.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)\n\n- Databricks — Query an AI Search index\n\nSupports: Retrieval queries support different search strategies, filters, and reranking; common index access alone does not establish identical retrieved context.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Retrieval settings can influence which documents are returned, making different evidence a plausible explanation. However, no differing settings or stale results are reported. A current shared index does not guarantee identical results, but the scenario more directly supports inconsistent shared task context.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Agents independently assess claim severity using incomplete local context because authoritative shared context is insufficiently propagated, leaving the final decision without a consistent source of truth.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe required behavior is a coherent claim assessment across agents. The scenario establishes separate local states and contradictory decisions despite access to a common, current document index. B identifies the broader failure: relevant authoritative task context is not consistently shared or applied.\n\nA, C, and D propose specific implementation defects that could contribute to disagreement, but the scenario provides no retrieval comparisons, event-order traces, or truncation evidence establishing them.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Each agent keeps separate session state” — Technical signal: Decisions established in one agent’s session may be absent from another’s.\n\n- “Communication occurs through a shared message bus” — Technical signal: A communication channel exists, but its presence does not establish complete context propagation.\n\n- “The same up-to-date … index” — Technical signal: Shared source documents do not automatically convey the routing agent’s claim-specific classification.\n\n- “High priority” versus “low priority” — Technical signal: Agents are acting on incompatible interpretations of the same claim.\n\n- “Reliability scores have declined” — Technical signal: The coordination defect is affecting overall outcomes.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nEach agent can maintain a coherent local interpretation while lacking decisions established elsewhere. If the summarizer does not receive—or does not recognize as authoritative—the routing classification, it can independently derive a conflicting priority.\n\nB therefore best matches the reported failure pattern. Effective propagation must make relevant claim state available to downstream agents and establish how that state governs their work. Separate sessions and shared retrieval infrastructure do not provide this automatically.\n\nThis remains the best-supported diagnosis, rather than proof of the exact implementation defect.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Premature processing could leave summarization without the classification. The message bus makes this tempting, but neither out-of-order delivery nor a null priority field is established. This explanation would fit if event traces confirmed that synthesis began before the required classification was available.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Trimming could remove an important classification and produce an inconsistent summary. However, no context-window pressure or prompt truncation is reported. This explanation would fit if prompt-assembly records showed that token-budget enforcement discarded the priority information."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search"],"source_hash":"4ee02601b565924dd173359d25c4214c0211402bf8d01a00185a6107064f8f62","stem_ja":"Databricksのパイプラインが、ルーティング・検索・要約という3つの協働エージェントを使って複雑な保険金請求の質問に回答しています。各エージェントは個別のセッション状態を持ち、共有メッセージバスを通じて通信します。3つとも同じ最新のDatabricks AI Searchインデックスにアクセスしています。\n\nルーティングエージェントはある請求を高優先度に分類しましたが、最終要約では低優先度として扱われました。査定担当者は相互に矛盾する推奨も確認しており、パイプラインの信頼性スコアは過去1週間で大幅に低下しました。\n\nエージェント間の不一致を最もよく説明する根本的な障害はどれですか。","options_ja":["類似度のしきい値と鮮度の設定が異なるため、同じ請求に対し、ルーティングエージェントと要約エージェントが重なりのない、または古い文書チャンクを取得している。","正本となる共有コンテキストが十分に伝播せず、各エージェントが不完全なローカルコンテキストで請求の重大度を独自に評価するため、最終判断の基準が統一されていない。","メッセージの配送順序が逆転し、ルーティングの分類が届く前に要約が実行されるため、優先度がnullのまま処理を進めている。","プロンプト組み立て時のコンテキスト切り詰めにより、ルーティングエージェントの優先度分類が要約エージェントの入力に届かなくなっている。"],"explanation_ja":"同じ検索インデックスを使っても、今回の請求に対する分類や判断まで自動的に共有されるわけではありません。別々の状態と矛盾する結論は、正本となるタスク状態の伝播不足を示します。配送順序や切り詰めは起こり得ますが、その証拠は設問にありません。「共通知識」と「今回の共有判断」を分けて考えます。","distractors_ja":["検索設定の違いで取得内容が変わる可能性はありますが、設定差や古い検索結果は示されておらず、診断の根拠が不足します。","別々のローカル状態に正本となる分類が届かなければ、個々には妥当な推論でも全体の判断が食い違います。これが最も直接的な説明です。","メッセージバスがあるだけでは順序逆転を証明できません。分類の到着前に要約が始まったことを示すイベント記録が必要です。","入力の切り詰めでも起こり得ますが、トークン圧迫や分類の削除を示す記録はありません。症状から具体的な実装不良を決めつけません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-002","number":2,"form":3,"stem":"A context engineer is troubleshooting a Databricks agent that manages a clinical trial data-processing pipeline spanning several months. The agent follows a rigid sequence of stages, executing them in the same order without accounting for work that has already completed successfully.\n\nWhen an upstream source is delayed or provides only part of its data, the agent discards completed stage outputs and restarts the entire sequence. Execution logs also show that stages unrelated to the unavailable source remain blocked. Repeated restarts after partial failures have made overall runtime unacceptable.\n\nWhich two changes should the engineer implement to address these failures? (Choose two.)","options":["Use dependency-graph-aware execution so independent stages can proceed without waiting for unrelated delayed stages.","Raise retry limits and timeout thresholds so the existing sequential workflow can wait longer for upstream data.","Replace the task planner with reactive, event-driven execution that starts each stage only when a new file arrives in the source directory.","Execute every stage simultaneously through parallel decomposition to eliminate delays imposed by sequential execution.","Introduce checkpointing that persists successful stage outputs and resumes execution from the last valid checkpoint instead of restarting the entire sequence."],"correct":0,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"依存関係に基づく実行とチェックポイント","concept":"ce-f3-q002","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Use dependency-graph-aware execution so independent stages can proceed without waiting for unrelated delayed stages.\n\nE. Introduce checkpointing that persists successful stage outputs and resumes execution from the last valid checkpoint instead of restarting the entire sequence.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scenario contains two distinct problems:\n\n- Unrelated stages are unnecessarily blocked, requiring scheduling based on actual dependencies.\n\n- Successful work is discarded after partial failures, requiring durable progress and recovery.\n\nA addresses the scheduling problem; E addresses the recovery problem. Together, they cover both observed failures. Longer waits, file-arrival triggers, and unrestricted parallelism do not provide that combination.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Stages unrelated to the unavailable source remain blocked” — Technical signal: The fixed sequence imposes unnecessary ordering constraints.\n\n- “Discards completed stage outputs” — Technical signal: Successful progress is not preserved for recovery.\n\n- “Restarts the entire sequence” — Technical signal: Recovery operates at an unnecessarily broad scope.\n\n- “Spanning several months” — Technical signal: Durable execution state matters across interruptions.\n\n- “Choose two” — Cardinality instruction: Select both dependency-aware scheduling and checkpoint-based recovery.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n- A respects actual prerequisites. A stage becomes eligible when its required inputs and predecessor stages are ready. Independent branches can advance while the affected branch waits. This preserves necessary ordering while removing unrelated blocking.\n\n- E preserves completed work. Checkpointing records execution state and successful outputs—or durable references to those outputs—so recovery can continue from a valid saved point. Completed work remains reusable rather than being discarded.\n\nThese changes are complementary: A improves progress during delays, while E limits repeated work after interruptions. Checkpoints must be durable, and the saved outputs must remain valid for the inputs being processed.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Longer timeouts and additional retries can accommodate temporary delays. However, they retain the unnecessary sequential blocking and do not preserve successful outputs after failure. This would fit better if premature timeout were the primary problem and dependency handling and recovery were already correct.\n\n- Option C — Different Use Case: File-arrival events can initiate processing when new data becomes available. However, an arrival event alone does not establish that every prerequisite is satisfied or preserve completed work. Event-driven triggers can complement dependency-aware execution, but this option does not resolve both reported defects.\n\n- Option D — Violates Requirement: Running independent work concurrently is appropriate, but executing every stage simultaneously ignores dependencies between stages. A downstream stage may require an upstream result that does not yet exist. This approach would fit only if all stages were genuinely independent.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is D: unnecessary sequential blocking makes unrestricted parallelism sound attractive. The decisive distinction is between independent stages and dependent stages. Parallelize eligible branches while retaining their prerequisite relationships; if every stage were independent, D would become defensible.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nReliable long-running workflows need two capabilities: dependency tracking determines what can run next, and persisted progress determines what must run again after failure. These capabilities solve different problems. Recovery should reuse valid completed results while rerunning unsuccessful work and any work whose prerequisites have changed.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA practical implementation could process laboratory and imaging data on separate branches, allowing imaging processing to continue while laboratory data is delayed. Persisting completed outputs would avoid repeating successful processing during recovery. Retried stages should also use idempotent writes so a partially completed attempt does not create duplicate records.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nDependencies control readiness; checkpoints preserve progress.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nUnrelated stages waiting unnecessarily point to dependency-aware scheduling. Successful stages repeatedly starting over point to checkpointing and resumable execution. Select both when the scenario reports both symptoms.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Configure task dependencies\n\nSupports: Dependency-based execution, upstream prerequisites, and parallel execution where dependencies permit.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-if)\n\n- LangChain — Checkpointers\n\nSupports: Persisted graph state, recovery from successful checkpoints, and preservation of successful node outputs during partial failures.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/checkpointers)\n\n- Databricks — Troubleshoot and repair job failures\n\nSupports: Selective recovery of unsuccessful tasks and the need for idempotent task logic when retries can repeat partial writes.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/repair-job-failures)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Use dependency-graph-aware execution so independent stages can proceed without waiting for unrelated delayed stages.\n\nE. Introduce checkpointing that persists successful stage outputs and resumes execution from the last valid checkpoint instead of restarting the entire sequence.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scenario contains two distinct problems:\n\n- Unrelated stages are unnecessarily blocked, requiring scheduling based on actual dependencies.\n\n- Successful work is discarded after partial failures, requiring durable progress and recovery.\n\nA addresses the scheduling problem; E addresses the recovery problem. Together, they cover both observed failures. Longer waits, file-arrival triggers, and unrestricted parallelism do not provide that combination.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Stages unrelated to the unavailable source remain blocked” — Technical signal: The fixed sequence imposes unnecessary ordering constraints.\n\n- “Discards completed stage outputs” — Technical signal: Successful progress is not preserved for recovery.\n\n- “Restarts the entire sequence” — Technical signal: Recovery operates at an unnecessarily broad scope.\n\n- “Spanning several months” — Technical signal: Durable execution state matters across interruptions.\n\n- “Choose two” — Cardinality instruction: Select both dependency-aware scheduling and checkpoint-based recovery.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n- A respects actual prerequisites. A stage becomes eligible when its required inputs and predecessor stages are ready. Independent branches can advance while the affected branch waits. This preserves necessary ordering while removing unrelated blocking.\n\n- E preserves completed work. Checkpointing records execution state and successful outputs—or durable references to those outputs—so recovery can continue from a valid saved point. Completed work remains reusable rather than being discarded.\n\nThese changes are complementary: A improves progress during delays, while E limits repeated work after interruptions. Checkpoints must be durable, and the saved outputs must remain valid for the inputs being processed.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Longer timeouts and additional retries can accommodate temporary delays. However, they retain the unnecessary sequential blocking and do not preserve successful outputs after failure. This would fit better if premature timeout were the primary problem and dependency handling and recovery were already correct.","Option C — Different Use Case: File-arrival events can initiate processing when new data becomes available. However, an arrival event alone does not establish that every prerequisite is satisfied or preserve completed work. Event-driven triggers can complement dependency-aware execution, but this option does not resolve both reported defects.","Option D — Violates Requirement: Running independent work concurrently is appropriate, but executing every stage simultaneously ignores dependencies between stages. A downstream stage may require an upstream result that does not yet exist. This approach would fit only if all stages were genuinely independent.","Correct Answer\n\nA. Use dependency-graph-aware execution so independent stages can proceed without waiting for unrelated delayed stages.\n\nE. Introduce checkpointing that persists successful stage outputs and resumes execution from the last valid checkpoint instead of restarting the entire sequence.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scenario contains two distinct problems:\n\n- Unrelated stages are unnecessarily blocked, requiring scheduling based on actual dependencies.\n\n- Successful work is discarded after partial failures, requiring durable progress and recovery.\n\nA addresses the scheduling problem; E addresses the recovery problem. Together, they cover both observed failures. Longer waits, file-arrival triggers, and unrestricted parallelism do not provide that combination.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Stages unrelated to the unavailable source remain blocked” — Technical signal: The fixed sequence imposes unnecessary ordering constraints.\n\n- “Discards completed stage outputs” — Technical signal: Successful progress is not preserved for recovery.\n\n- “Restarts the entire sequence” — Technical signal: Recovery operates at an unnecessarily broad scope.\n\n- “Spanning several months” — Technical signal: Durable execution state matters across interruptions.\n\n- “Choose two” — Cardinality instruction: Select both dependency-aware scheduling and checkpoint-based recovery.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n- A respects actual prerequisites. A stage becomes eligible when its required inputs and predecessor stages are ready. Independent branches can advance while the affected branch waits. This preserves necessary ordering while removing unrelated blocking.\n\n- E preserves completed work. Checkpointing records execution state and successful outputs—or durable references to those outputs—so recovery can continue from a valid saved point. Completed work remains reusable rather than being discarded.\n\nThese changes are complementary: A improves progress during delays, while E limits repeated work after interruptions. Checkpoints must be durable, and the saved outputs must remain valid for the inputs being processed."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-if","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/checkpointers","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/repair-job-failures"],"source_hash":"a085de61a0f079bf726edc9b714f2db6dc07881e5e656da3d48b5a9eedb58b78","correct_many":[0,4],"stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、数か月にわたる臨床試験データ処理パイプラインを管理するDatabricksエージェントの問題を調査しています。このエージェントは、すでに正常完了した作業を考慮せず、各段階を常に同じ順序で実行する固定的な手順に従っています。\n\n上流ソースが遅延したりデータの一部しか提供しなかったりすると、完了した段階の出力を破棄して全手順を最初からやり直します。実行ログでは、利用できないソースと無関係な段階まで待機しています。部分的な失敗のたびに再実行するため、全体の実行時間が許容できなくなりました。\n\nこれらの障害に対処するため、実装すべき変更を2つ選んでください。","options_ja":["依存関係グラフを考慮した実行により、独立した段階が、無関係な遅延中の段階を待たずに進めるようにする。","再試行回数の上限とタイムアウトのしきい値を増やし、既存の逐次ワークフローが上流データをより長く待てるようにする。","タスクプランナーを、ソースディレクトリへの新規ファイル到着時にのみ各段階を開始する、リアクティブなイベント駆動実行に置き換える。","並列分解によってすべての段階を同時実行し、逐次実行による遅延をなくす。","正常終了した段階の出力を永続化するチェックポイントを導入し、全手順の再開始ではなく、最後の有効なチェックポイントから再開する。"],"explanation_ja":"問題は「無関係な処理まで待たせること」と「完了済みの作業を失うこと」の2つです。前者には実際の依存関係に基づく実行、後者には永続チェックポイントによる再開が対応します。依存がある段階の順序は守り、独立した枝だけ並列に進めます。「依存関係は実行可能性、チェックポイントは進捗保存」と覚えます。","distractors_ja":["必要な先行処理が完了した段階だけを実行対象にすれば、依存する順序を守りつつ、無関係な枝の待ち時間を除けます。","待てる時間を延ばしても、不要な逐次制約と完了済み出力の破棄は残ります。今回の2つの原因を解消しません。","ファイル到着は開始のきっかけになりますが、前提条件の充足や完了出力の保存を保証しません。イベントだけでは不十分です。","独立した段階なら同時実行できますが、すべてを同時に動かすと、上流出力を必要とする後続処理が未準備で実行されます。","有効な出力と進捗を永続化しておけば、中断後に成功済みの作業を再利用できます。再試行される書き込みの冪等性も重要です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-003","number":3,"form":3,"stem":"A Databricks agent coordinates data quality remediation across a large enterprise dataset. Over dozens of steps, it evaluates validation rules, identifies violations, corrects records, and records the actions taken. Cluster preemption or network timeouts can interrupt execution.\n\nBusiness stakeholders require an auditable record of every correction and the ability to reverse changes. After an interruption, the agent must continue from saved progress without repeating corrections already committed to records.\n\nWhich state-management approach meets these requirements?","options":["Keep step outputs in a temporary Python dictionary shared between agent invocations during the same cluster session.","Append remediation actions to a plain text file on DBFS and reread that file before each step to reconstruct progress.","Maintain remediation history in an in-context scratchpad that is appended to the agent’s prompt at every step.","Persist intermediate state in a Delta table, using transaction history for traceability and checkpoints to resume from the last committed step."],"correct":3,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"監査可能で再開可能なDelta状態管理","concept":"ce-f3-q003","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Persist intermediate state in a Delta table, using transaction history for traceability and checkpoints to resume from the last committed step.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe hard requirements are durable progress, correction-level traceability, reversibility, and recovery without duplicate application. Temporary memory and prompt context cannot serve as reliable committed state across interruptions. A plain text log provides a record but lacks the transaction-aware recovery design required here.\n\nD supplies the appropriate transactional foundation. The implementation must connect checkpoint records to actual committed corrections and retain the information needed for reversal.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Cluster preemption or network timeouts” — Hard constraint: Progress must survive loss of the current execution environment or an uncertain response.\n\n- “An auditable record of every correction” — Hard constraint: Actions must be attributable to affected records and committed changes.\n\n- “The ability to reverse changes” — Hard constraint: Previous values or usable historical versions must remain available.\n\n- “Without repeating corrections already committed” — Hard constraint: Recovery must distinguish completed work from work requiring execution.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nDelta provides durable, transactional storage for workflow state. Saved completion records let the agent determine where to resume, while table history supports inspection of committed operations and versions.\n\nA complete implementation must also address three boundaries:\n\n- Traceability: Record workflow, step, record, and action identifiers, with references to the corresponding data changes.\n\n- Reversibility: Retain before-values or appropriate historical versions of the corrected dataset. History of the state table alone cannot undo changes to another table.\n\n- Safe recovery: Coordinate corrections and completion markers, or use idempotent application logic, so a correction committed just before a timeout is not applied again.\n\nHistorical recovery also depends on retaining the required logs and data files. D is the correct architecture; simply creating a Delta table does not automatically implement all these behaviors.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Violates Requirement: A Python dictionary is convenient for temporary working state. However, losing its process or cluster session loses that state, preventing reliable recovery and durable auditing. It fits disposable computation where restart recovery is unnecessary.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: A stored log can survive compute loss and support diagnostics. However, a plain text action log does not itself coordinate dataset commits, progress markers, and reversal information. Reconstructing intended actions does not necessarily establish which corrections actually committed. A robust log-based recovery system would require additional mechanisms absent from this option.\n\n- Option C — Violates Requirement: A scratchpad can help the agent track its current task, making it attractive for iterative work. It does not provide an authoritative, transactionally committed record of dataset changes or a durable reversal mechanism.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is B: persistent logging can resemble checkpointing. The distinction is whether the saved state reliably identifies committed effects and supports recovery.\n\nAlso avoid assuming Delta transactions automatically guarantee that an entire agent workflow runs exactly once. If the requirement were only diagnostic logging, B could be suitable; this scenario additionally requires commit-aware recovery and reversibility.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nWorking memory tracks what an agent is considering; durable execution state tracks what the system has committed. Reliable remediation requires recoverable state and controlled replay of effects. Auditing and reversal additionally require a link between each action and the affected data.\n\nFor terminology freshness, DBFS root and mounts are deprecated. The broader DBFS term does not establish the file’s location, and changing its storage location alone would not supply the missing recovery protocol.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nFor a correction to an invalid customer classification, an implementation could persist the record identifier, action identifier, old value, new value, and completion status. Recovery would recognize an already applied action, while reversal could use the recorded old value after checking for subsequent changes. This supports targeted recovery and auditability, with additional storage and coordination costs.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nPersist committed progress, preserve reversal evidence, and make retries safe.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen an agent must survive interruptions and audit or reverse data changes, choose durable transactional state. Verify that checkpoints correspond to committed corrections and that retained data supports recovery and undo.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: The objective distinguishing Delta-backed state from an in-context scratchpad for multi-step agent tasks.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Databricks — Work with table history\n\nSupports: Operation history, historical versions, restoration, and retention requirements.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/history)\n\n- Databricks — What are ACID guarantees on Databricks?\n\nSupports: Durable transactional commits and transaction boundaries that must be considered when coordinating data and state updates.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/acid)\n\n- Databricks — What is DBFS?\n\nSupports: Deprecation of DBFS root and mounts, and the distinction from the underlying filesystem technology.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dbfs/)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Violates Requirement: A Python dictionary is convenient for temporary working state. However, losing its process or cluster session loses that state, preventing reliable recovery and durable auditing. It fits disposable computation where restart recovery is unnecessary.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: A stored log can survive compute loss and support diagnostics. However, a plain text action log does not itself coordinate dataset commits, progress markers, and reversal information. Reconstructing intended actions does not necessarily establish which corrections actually committed. A robust log-based recovery system would require additional mechanisms absent from this option.","Option C — Violates Requirement: A scratchpad can help the agent track its current task, making it attractive for iterative work. It does not provide an authoritative, transactionally committed record of dataset changes or a durable reversal mechanism.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Persist intermediate state in a Delta table, using transaction history for traceability and checkpoints to resume from the last committed step.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe hard requirements are durable progress, correction-level traceability, reversibility, and recovery without duplicate application. Temporary memory and prompt context cannot serve as reliable committed state across interruptions. A plain text log provides a record but lacks the transaction-aware recovery design required here.\n\nD supplies the appropriate transactional foundation. The implementation must connect checkpoint records to actual committed corrections and retain the information needed for reversal.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Cluster preemption or network timeouts” — Hard constraint: Progress must survive loss of the current execution environment or an uncertain response.\n\n- “An auditable record of every correction” — Hard constraint: Actions must be attributable to affected records and committed changes.\n\n- “The ability to reverse changes” — Hard constraint: Previous values or usable historical versions must remain available.\n\n- “Without repeating corrections already committed” — Hard constraint: Recovery must distinguish completed work from work requiring execution.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nDelta provides durable, transactional storage for workflow state. Saved completion records let the agent determine where to resume, while table history supports inspection of committed operations and versions.\n\nA complete implementation must also address three boundaries:\n\n- Traceability: Record workflow, step, record, and action identifiers, with references to the corresponding data changes.\n\n- Reversibility: Retain before-values or appropriate historical versions of the corrected dataset. History of the state table alone cannot undo changes to another table.\n\n- Safe recovery: Coordinate corrections and completion markers, or use idempotent application logic, so a correction committed just before a timeout is not applied again.\n\nHistorical recovery also depends on retaining the required logs and data files. D is the correct architecture; simply creating a Delta table does not automatically implement all these behaviors."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/history","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/acid","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/dbfs/"],"source_hash":"c0c3fb7780f927e07fbdb4a5cac4d64604ced9b8aef26780ff7362437cf65836","stem_ja":"Databricksエージェントが、大規模な企業データセット全体のデータ品質修復を調整しています。数十段階にわたり、検証ルールを評価し、違反を検出し、レコードを修正して実施内容を記録します。クラスターのプリエンプションやネットワークのタイムアウトで実行が中断されることがあります。\n\n業務関係者は、すべての修正について監査可能な記録と、変更を元に戻せることを要求しています。中断後は保存済みの進捗から継続し、レコードにコミット済みの修正を重複して適用してはいけません。\n\nこれらの要件を満たす状態管理方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["同じクラスターセッション中のエージェント呼び出し間で共有する、一時的なPython辞書に各段階の出力を保持する。","DBFS上のプレーンテキストファイルに修復操作を追記し、各段階の前に読み直して進捗を再構築する。","エージェントのプロンプトに毎段階追加するコンテキスト内スクラッチパッドで、修復履歴を管理する。","中間状態をDeltaテーブルに永続化し、トランザクション履歴で追跡可能性を確保し、チェックポイントによって最後のコミット済み段階から再開する。"],"explanation_ja":"障害を越えて進捗と変更の証跡を残すには、永続的なトランザクション基盤が必要です。Deltaに中間状態と確定済み操作を記録し、チェックポイントから再開します。ただし状態テーブルの履歴だけで別テーブルの修正を取り消せるわけではありません。変更前の値や保持済みバージョン、冪等な適用・完了管理も設計します。","distractors_ja":["一時辞書はプロセスやクラスターの喪失に耐えません。永続的な監査記録、取り消し、障害後の安全な再開を満たせません。","テキストログは記録にはなりますが、修正のコミットと完了マーカーを整合させるトランザクション管理を提供しません。","スクラッチパッドは推論の作業領域です。コンテキスト切り詰めや実行環境の喪失を越える、正本の進捗台帳には向きません。","Deltaの永続状態と履歴を基盤に、修正の識別子・コミット・再開位置を結び付けます。重複防止や取り消しのための追加設計は必要です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-004","number":4,"form":3,"stem":"An AI engineer is developing a multimodal agent for a media company’s archive team. The archive contains thousands of titles collected over two decades, including complete episodes, raw interviews, and unedited B-roll. Each asset has been ingested as a single monolithic file.\n\nProducers ask visual questions, such as whether an asset includes a usable exterior shot of a particular landmark. To answer, the retrieval tool places the entire asset into the model’s context, although only a small segment is relevant. A few retrievals exhaust the context window.\n\nThe engineer must address this through proactive design rather than relying on compaction. Which strategy should the engineer apply?","options":["Have a vision language model generate a brief visual digest of each retrieved asset and pass only that digest into the agent’s context.","Add system-prompt instructions directing the agent to retrieve the smallest necessary portion of each asset and limit what enters context.","Search catalog metadata using text-only retrieval before accessing footage, allowing the agent to answer without loading media when the catalog is sufficient.","Preprocess each asset into scene-level metadata so the agent can locate the relevant segment and retrieve only that portion for visual examination."],"correct":3,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"マルチモーダル素材の区間単位取得","concept":"ce-f3-q004","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Preprocess each asset into scene-level metadata so the agent can locate the relevant segment and retrieve only that portion for visual examination.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe required outcome is an answer grounded in relevant visual footage while preventing unnecessary context growth. The underlying problem is retrieval granularity: a segment-level question triggers whole-asset loading.\n\nD changes what the retrieval system can locate and return. It enables selective examination before irrelevant footage enters context. A compresses an entire asset into a potentially lossy substitute; B supplies instructions without establishing segment access; C helps only when existing catalog information answers the question.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “A single monolithic file” — Technical signal: The retrieval unit is much larger than the information needed.\n\n- “A usable exterior shot” — Technical signal: The task concerns a specific visual moment and its suitability.\n\n- “Only a small segment is relevant” — Technical signal: Segment-level retrieval directly targets the excess context.\n\n- “Proactive design rather than … compaction” — Hard constraint: Reduce unnecessary loading before context becomes crowded.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nScene-level metadata provides a lightweight discovery layer over large media assets. Useful fields can include the asset identifier, scene description, visual annotations, and start/end timestamps. The agent searches that layer, identifies candidate segments, and loads selected footage for examination.\n\nThis combines metadata-guided discovery with scoped retrieval. The retrieval tool must support fetching the identified segment; metadata alone does not reduce context if the tool still returns the entire asset.\n\nMetadata also needs adequate coverage. A missing landmark annotation is not definitive evidence that the footage contains no relevant shot, and candidate footage should still be examined for usability.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: A separate vision-language summarization step can reduce the main agent’s context consumption. However, a brief whole-asset digest may omit a short landmark appearance or details needed to judge usability. It fits better when the requested result is a broad overview of the asset.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Instructions can encourage selective retrieval when tools already expose suitable segments. Here, the described retrieval behavior loads monolithic assets. Prompt instructions alone do not create scene boundaries, searchable visual annotations, or segment-fetching capability.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Catalog search can avoid unnecessary media retrieval when metadata already contains the answer. However, the scenario does not establish that the catalog describes individual visual segments. It does not directly solve whole-asset loading when footage must be examined.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is A: a short digest appears to solve context pressure. The distinction is whether the representation preserves the evidence needed for the question. D keeps the relevant footage available for direct inspection while avoiding unrelated material. If producers requested only a general visual overview, A would become more appropriate.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nSeparate discovery from examination. Lightweight metadata helps identify where relevant evidence resides; selective retrieval then brings that evidence into context. Effective proactive context management aligns retrieval granularity with the task instead of repeatedly loading oversized objects and compressing them afterward.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nAn implementation could index scene descriptions alongside asset identifiers and temporal boundaries. For a landmark query, the agent would retrieve candidate intervals and inspect those clips. This could lower per-query context usage and improve source attribution, with upfront processing costs and an ongoing need to evaluate annotation and retrieval quality.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nFind the scene through metadata, then load the footage needed to verify it.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen a small visual question causes whole-video retrieval, improve segmentation, metadata, and retrieval scope. Prefer targeted access to original evidence when a broad summary could discard the decisive detail.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: The objective covering proactive context management through just-in-time retrieval and tool-result scoping before compaction becomes necessary.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Databricks — Build an unstructured data pipeline for RAG\n\nSupports: Preprocessing, structural and content metadata, and smaller retrieval units. The scene-level design applies these principles to video.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag)\n\n- Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents\n\nSupports: Lightweight references, metadata-guided exploration, and loading relevant content on demand.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\n- Google Cloud — Shot change detection tutorial\n\nSupports: Video segmentation with start/end timestamps, illustrating how temporal boundaries can identify retrievable portions of footage.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.cloud.google.com/video-intelligence/docs/shot-detection)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: A separate vision-language summarization step can reduce the main agent’s context consumption. However, a brief whole-asset digest may omit a short landmark appearance or details needed to judge usability. It fits better when the requested result is a broad overview of the asset.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Instructions can encourage selective retrieval when tools already expose suitable segments. Here, the described retrieval behavior loads monolithic assets. Prompt instructions alone do not create scene boundaries, searchable visual annotations, or segment-fetching capability.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Catalog search can avoid unnecessary media retrieval when metadata already contains the answer. However, the scenario does not establish that the catalog describes individual visual segments. It does not directly solve whole-asset loading when footage must be examined.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Preprocess each asset into scene-level metadata so the agent can locate the relevant segment and retrieve only that portion for visual examination.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe required outcome is an answer grounded in relevant visual footage while preventing unnecessary context growth. The underlying problem is retrieval granularity: a segment-level question triggers whole-asset loading.\n\nD changes what the retrieval system can locate and return. It enables selective examination before irrelevant footage enters context. A compresses an entire asset into a potentially lossy substitute; B supplies instructions without establishing segment access; C helps only when existing catalog information answers the question.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “A single monolithic file” — Technical signal: The retrieval unit is much larger than the information needed.\n\n- “A usable exterior shot” — Technical signal: The task concerns a specific visual moment and its suitability.\n\n- “Only a small segment is relevant” — Technical signal: Segment-level retrieval directly targets the excess context.\n\n- “Proactive design rather than … compaction” — Hard constraint: Reduce unnecessary loading before context becomes crowded.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nScene-level metadata provides a lightweight discovery layer over large media assets. Useful fields can include the asset identifier, scene description, visual annotations, and start/end timestamps. The agent searches that layer, identifies candidate segments, and loads selected footage for examination.\n\nThis combines metadata-guided discovery with scoped retrieval. The retrieval tool must support fetching the identified segment; metadata alone does not reduce context if the tool still returns the entire asset.\n\nMetadata also needs adequate coverage. A missing landmark annotation is not definitive evidence that the footage contains no relevant shot, and candidate footage should still be examined for usability."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","https://docs.cloud.google.com/video-intelligence/docs/shot-detection"],"source_hash":"9b2ed59f080c9f4fe3f75e4c10b2cb44d8060984fbce7f0b1cf80ef017bbd97f","stem_ja":"AIエンジニアが、メディア企業のアーカイブ部門向けにマルチモーダルエージェントを開発しています。アーカイブには、完成した番組、未編集のインタビュー、未編集の補助映像（Bロール）など、20年間で集めた数千の作品があります。各素材は、分割されていない単一ファイルとして取り込まれています。\n\nプロデューサーは、特定のランドマークの使用可能な外観ショットがあるか、といった視覚的な質問をします。関連するのは小さな区間だけですが、検索ツールは回答のために素材全体をモデルのコンテキストへ渡します。数回検索するだけでコンテキストウィンドウが一杯になります。\n\n圧縮に頼らず、事前の設計によって対処する必要があります。採用すべき方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["検索した各素材について視覚言語モデルに短い映像ダイジェストを作らせ、そのダイジェストだけをエージェントのコンテキストへ渡す。","各素材の必要最小限の区間だけを取得し、コンテキストへ入れる内容を制限するよう、システムプロンプトに指示を追加する。","映像へアクセスする前にテキスト検索でカタログメタデータを調べ、カタログだけで十分なら映像を読み込まずに回答させる。","各素材をシーン単位のメタデータへ前処理し、関連区間を見つけて、その区間だけを取得して視覚的に確認できるようにする。"],"explanation_ja":"検索単位が素材全体なのに、質問は特定の映像区間を対象としています。シーンの説明・ランドマーク・開始終了時刻などを索引化し、候補区間だけを取得して実物を確認する設計が適切です。要約だけでは短いショットや使用可否の証拠を落とすおそれがあります。「メタデータで探し、必要な原映像で確かめる」と覚えます。","distractors_ja":["全体ダイジェストは軽量ですが、短いランドマークの登場や使用可否を判断する細部を省く可能性があります。原映像の必要区間を残す方が適切です。","必要な区間を取得できるツールや区間情報がなければ、指示だけで単一ファイルをシーン単位に取得できるようにはなりません。","カタログで回答できる場合には有効ですが、個別シーンの視覚情報が含まれるとは示されておらず、全体取得の原因を解決しません。","シーンの発見と映像の確認を分離します。検索粒度を質問に合わせることで、決定的な証拠を保ちながら不要な読み込みを減らせます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-005","number":5,"form":3,"stem":"A context engineer is refining a Databricks agent’s compaction prompt, which condenses customer support ticket histories for a downstream LLM. The source trace contains 40 turns, and the resulting summary must remain within the downstream model’s context window.\n\nInitial testing against a benchmark of escalated support cases shows that summaries repeatedly exclude earlier resolutions and important escalation events. The summaries nevertheless remain comfortably below the downstream context limit.\n\nWhich tuning action should the engineer take next?","options":["Introduce a token limit to produce shorter summaries and reduce downstream cost and latency, then evaluate whether important events survived.","Preserve recent turns verbatim while compressing older exchanges, assuming the latest interactions contain the most relevant information.","Expand retention instructions to capture all events, resolutions, and escalation signals, temporarily allowing a longer summary and refining verbosity in a later iteration.","Modify retention and pruning instructions together so coverage and relevance are optimized within the same iteration"],"correct":2,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"圧縮の調整は再現率から適合率へ","concept":"ce-f3-q005","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Expand retention instructions to capture all events, resolutions, and escalation signals, temporarily allowing a longer summary and refining verbosity in a later iteration.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe immediate requirement is to preserve information needed for escalated cases. The benchmark identifies missing facts, while the available context headroom permits a more complete summary. This makes recall the current bottleneck.\n\nC addresses those omissions first. Additional shortening, recency assumptions, or simultaneous pruning would be premature before confirming that required information survives.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Exclude earlier resolutions and important escalation events” — Technical signal: Compaction is losing required information.\n\n- “Comfortably below the downstream context limit” — Technical signal: Immediate shortening is unnecessary.\n\n- “Initial testing” — Sequence instruction: Establish adequate retention before optimizing away surplus content.\n\n- “Must remain within … context window” — Hard constraint: A longer summary must still respect the downstream budget.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nBroader retention instructions explicitly protect the categories that evaluation found missing. The engineer should rerun the benchmark to confirm that relevant resolutions and escalation signals are retained, then remove redundancy and unnecessary detail in a subsequent iteration.\n\n“All events” should mean all events relevant to downstream handling, rather than a verbatim reproduction of all 40 turns. The context-window limit remains binding throughout tuning.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: A token budget can control summary size, but further shortening risks worsening the demonstrated omissions. It becomes appropriate when adequate retention is established and output length needs tighter control.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Recency-based retention can preserve active conversational details. Here, however, earlier resolutions and escalations are explicitly important. Turn age alone is therefore an unsuitable relevance rule.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Joint tuning can change both coverage and concision, but pruning may counteract expanded retention and obscure which change caused an omission. Separate passes provide a clearer way to establish coverage before removing surplus material.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is D: optimizing both objectives together sounds efficient. The distinction is the tuning sequence—first establish that required facts survive, then refine what can be removed.\n\nIf summaries already retained all necessary information but exceeded the available budget, targeted pruning would become the appropriate next step.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nRecall concerns whether required source information survives compaction. Precision concerns how much retained information is relevant. A short, accurate summary can still have poor recall if it omits decisive facts. Compaction quality therefore requires evaluating information preservation as well as length.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA support team could use a retention checklist covering attempted fixes, outcomes, escalation reasons, and unresolved issues. Testing those fields against benchmark cases would reveal omissions before the summaries reach production. A temporarily longer summary consumes more tokens but can help prevent repeated troubleshooting or missed escalation context.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nRecover required facts first; remove redundancy second.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nMissing essential history despite available context space signals a recall problem. Broaden retention, verify coverage, and then tighten verbosity while preserving that coverage.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: The objective specifying recall-first compaction tuning followed by precision improvements that remove superfluous outputs.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents\n\nSupports: Compaction fidelity, risks of discarding subtle but important information, and tuning recall before pruning.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: A token budget can control summary size, but further shortening risks worsening the demonstrated omissions. It becomes appropriate when adequate retention is established and output length needs tighter control.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Recency-based retention can preserve active conversational details. Here, however, earlier resolutions and escalations are explicitly important. Turn age alone is therefore an unsuitable relevance rule.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Expand retention instructions to capture all events, resolutions, and escalation signals, temporarily allowing a longer summary and refining verbosity in a later iteration.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe immediate requirement is to preserve information needed for escalated cases. The benchmark identifies missing facts, while the available context headroom permits a more complete summary. This makes recall the current bottleneck.\n\nC addresses those omissions first. Additional shortening, recency assumptions, or simultaneous pruning would be premature before confirming that required information survives.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Exclude earlier resolutions and important escalation events” — Technical signal: Compaction is losing required information.\n\n- “Comfortably below the downstream context limit” — Technical signal: Immediate shortening is unnecessary.\n\n- “Initial testing” — Sequence instruction: Establish adequate retention before optimizing away surplus content.\n\n- “Must remain within … context window” — Hard constraint: A longer summary must still respect the downstream budget.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nBroader retention instructions explicitly protect the categories that evaluation found missing. The engineer should rerun the benchmark to confirm that relevant resolutions and escalation signals are retained, then remove redundancy and unnecessary detail in a subsequent iteration.\n\n“All events” should mean all events relevant to downstream handling, rather than a verbatim reproduction of all 40 turns. The context-window limit remains binding throughout tuning.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Joint tuning can change both coverage and concision, but pruning may counteract expanded retention and obscure which change caused an omission. Separate passes provide a clearer way to establish coverage before removing surplus material."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"0cb9d86771bc4c71f73e82da3f771e94e2d2c77b84af4c92fcf8d24fcb2ec11f","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、顧客サポートのチケット履歴を後段のLLM向けに要約するDatabricksエージェントの圧縮プロンプトを調整しています。元のトレースは40ターンあり、生成する要約は後段モデルのコンテキストウィンドウに収める必要があります。\n\nエスカレーションされたサポート事例のベンチマークで初期テストを行うと、以前の解決内容や重要なエスカレーションの出来事が繰り返し省かれていました。それでも、要約の長さは後段のコンテキスト上限を十分下回っています。\n\n次に行うべき調整はどれですか。","options_ja":["トークン上限を設けて要約をさらに短くし、後段のコストと遅延を減らしてから、重要な出来事が残ったか評価する。","最新のやり取りに最も重要な情報があると仮定し、最近のターンをそのまま残して古いやり取りを圧縮する。","保持する内容の指示を広げ、すべての出来事、解決内容、エスカレーションの兆候を捉えるようにし、一時的に長い要約を許して、後の反復で冗長さを調整する。","保持と削除の指示を同時に変更し、同じ反復の中で網羅性と関連性を最適化する。"],"explanation_ja":"現在のボトルネックは長さではなく、必要情報の取りこぼしです。余裕のあるコンテキスト予算内で保持対象を広げ、解決履歴やエスカレーションが残ることを評価します。その後で重複や不要な説明を削ります。すべての出来事とは後段処理に必要な出来事であり、40ターンの丸写しではありません。「再現率を先に、適合率を後に」が基本です。","distractors_ja":["短くすることを優先すると、すでに起きている重要情報の脱落を悪化させます。必要情報の保持を確認してから長さを最適化します。","古い情報でも、解決内容やエスカレーションには現在の判断を左右する価値があります。新しさだけで残す情報を決めてはいけません。","必要な出来事を明示して保持範囲を広げ、取りこぼしの改善を確かめる方法です。長くする場合もモデルの上限は守ります。","保持の拡張と削減を同時に変えると、どちらが脱落を引き起こしたか分かりにくくなります。まず網羅性を確立します。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-006","number":6,"form":3,"stem":"A Databricks document-analysis workflow uses a coordinating agent to divide a task among three specialist sub-agents. In production, each specialist produces a coherent result, but their results conflict with one another and omit conclusions established earlier in the overall workflow.\n\nAll three specialists can access the same retrieval index, and the embedding model has been confirmed to be current. When evaluated independently with the complete context supplied manually, each specialist produces accurate results.\n\nWhich explanation reasonably accounts for the downstream failures?","options":["An excessively high temperature setting causes the coordinator to vary its task decomposition and issue inconsistent subtask instructions.","Each specialist receives only its assigned task message, without the coordinator’s preceding analysis, tool results, or decisions.","A retrieval-index configuration error directs the specialists to a different document corpus from the one used by the coordinator.","The retrieval embedding model lacks training on the document domain, resulting in irrelevant retrieved passages and conflicting summaries."],"correct":1,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"委譲時に失われる前段の分析と判断","concept":"ce-f3-q006","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Each specialist receives only its assigned task message, without the coordinator’s preceding analysis, tool results, or decisions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe required outcome is agreement with the workflow’s established findings. The strongest diagnostic evidence is that the specialists perform correctly when given complete context manually. This points to information missing from production dispatches. B explains both the individually coherent answers and their collective inconsistency: each specialist solves a local task without the shared decisions needed to interpret it consistently.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Their results conflict with one another” — Technical signal: Individual coherence is insufficient for coordinated correctness.\n\n- “Omit conclusions established earlier” — Technical signal: Earlier workflow state is failing to reach downstream agents.\n\n- “The same retrieval index” — Technical signal: A different-corpus explanation has less support, although shared index access does not guarantee identical retrieval results.\n\n- “Complete context supplied manually” — Technical signal: Restoring context restores accurate behavior, making dispatch context the strongest suspect.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA task message identifies an assignment but may omit assumptions, resolved ambiguities, evidence, and decisions established by the coordinator. Specialists receiving incomplete dispatches can adopt different interpretations while producing locally reasonable answers.\n\nB is therefore the best-supported diagnosis. Inspect the actual dispatch inputs to confirm it. In production, the needed information can be conveyed through a structured handoff containing relevant conclusions, concise rationale, tool findings, constraints, and evidence references. This applies the documented principle that delegation needs explicit objectives and task boundaries.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Sampling variability could contribute to inconsistent decomposition, making temperature a tempting explanation. However, the scenario provides no evidence that dispatch instructions vary. Successful testing with complete context more directly supports a context-propagation failure. A would deserve greater attention if traces showed conflicting coordinator assignments across equivalent runs.\n\n- Option C — Violates Requirement: A different corpus could yield different evidence and incompatible conclusions. As written, however, this explanation conflicts with the stated shared-index setup and lacks evidence of divergent corpus routing. It would fit a scenario where traces confirmed different index endpoints or source collections.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Poor domain suitability could impair retrieval, and a current embedding model is not automatically suitable for every domain. Nevertheless, irrelevant retrieval is not reported here. The missing earlier conclusions and successful full-context tests make B the stronger explanation. D would become more plausible if retrieval evaluation demonstrated consistently irrelevant passages.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest misconception is that access to the same documents gives every agent the same understanding. Documents may contain evidence without containing the coordinator’s task-specific decisions. If dispatches already included all relevant shared context and retrieval tests instead showed poor relevance, the diagnosis could shift toward D.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nA multi-agent workflow contains both source knowledge and working state. Source knowledge provides facts; working state records the task’s scope, interpretations, findings, and decisions. Reliable coordination requires downstream agents to receive or retrieve the relevant working state. Persisting it somewhere is useful only when the consuming agent actually loads it.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nFor a contract-analysis workflow, the coordinator might establish which amendment governs the review. Including that decision and its supporting document references in every specialist’s dispatch would help prevent specialists from analyzing incompatible contract versions. Validate the change by checking cross-agent agreement and whether final conclusions honor the selected amendment.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nPass each specialist the assignment together with the decisions needed to interpret it.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen specialists succeed with complete context but disagree in production, inspect what crosses the dispatch boundary. Shared retrieval infrastructure alone does not establish shared task state.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: The registered objective covering downstream failures caused by dispatching individual task messages without the coordinator’s broader context.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Anthropic — How we built our multi-agent research system\n\nSupports: Explicit delegation objectives, task boundaries, context continuity, and coordination failures. The diagnosis above applies these principles to the supplied scenario.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/multi-agent-research-system)\n\n- Databricks — Agent memory and sessions\n\nSupports: Working state includes conversation messages, tool calls, and results; agents must read that state to use it as context.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Sampling variability could contribute to inconsistent decomposition, making temperature a tempting explanation. However, the scenario provides no evidence that dispatch instructions vary. Successful testing with complete context more directly supports a context-propagation failure. A would deserve greater attention if traces showed conflicting coordinator assignments across equivalent runs.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Each specialist receives only its assigned task message, without the coordinator’s preceding analysis, tool results, or decisions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe required outcome is agreement with the workflow’s established findings. The strongest diagnostic evidence is that the specialists perform correctly when given complete context manually. This points to information missing from production dispatches. B explains both the individually coherent answers and their collective inconsistency: each specialist solves a local task without the shared decisions needed to interpret it consistently.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Their results conflict with one another” — Technical signal: Individual coherence is insufficient for coordinated correctness.\n\n- “Omit conclusions established earlier” — Technical signal: Earlier workflow state is failing to reach downstream agents.\n\n- “The same retrieval index” — Technical signal: A different-corpus explanation has less support, although shared index access does not guarantee identical retrieval results.\n\n- “Complete context supplied manually” — Technical signal: Restoring context restores accurate behavior, making dispatch context the strongest suspect.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA task message identifies an assignment but may omit assumptions, resolved ambiguities, evidence, and decisions established by the coordinator. Specialists receiving incomplete dispatches can adopt different interpretations while producing locally reasonable answers.\n\nB is therefore the best-supported diagnosis. Inspect the actual dispatch inputs to confirm it. In production, the needed information can be conveyed through a structured handoff containing relevant conclusions, concise rationale, tool findings, constraints, and evidence references. This applies the documented principle that delegation needs explicit objectives and task boundaries.","Option C — Violates Requirement: A different corpus could yield different evidence and incompatible conclusions. As written, however, this explanation conflicts with the stated shared-index setup and lacks evidence of divergent corpus routing. It would fit a scenario where traces confirmed different index endpoints or source collections.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Poor domain suitability could impair retrieval, and a current embedding model is not automatically suitable for every domain. Nevertheless, irrelevant retrieval is not reported here. The missing earlier conclusions and successful full-context tests make B the stronger explanation. D would become more plausible if retrieval evaluation demonstrated consistently irrelevant passages."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://www.anthropic.com/research/multi-agent-research-system","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents"],"source_hash":"794147dbb455d5016771e750ec692e9229738f3447d456c3c6603dccc838187c","stem_ja":"Databricksの文書分析ワークフローで、調整役のエージェントがタスクを3つの専門サブエージェントに分担させています。本番では、各専門エージェントの結果はそれぞれ筋が通っていますが、相互に矛盾し、ワークフロー全体の前段で確定した結論も欠落しています。\n\n3つの専門エージェントは同じ検索インデックスにアクセスでき、埋め込みモデルも最新であると確認済みです。完全なコンテキストを手作業で与えて個別評価すると、どの専門エージェントも正確な結果を出します。\n\n後段の障害を合理的に説明するものはどれですか。","options_ja":["temperatureの設定が高すぎるため、調整役のタスク分解が変動し、一貫しないサブタスク指示が出されている。","各専門エージェントには担当タスクのメッセージだけが渡され、調整役のそれ以前の分析、ツール結果、判断が渡されていない。","検索インデックスの設定誤りにより、専門エージェントが調整役とは異なる文書コーパスへ向けられている。","検索用の埋め込みモデルが文書分野の学習を欠いており、無関係な文章の取得と矛盾する要約を引き起こしている。"],"explanation_ja":"完全なコンテキストを与えると正確になることが、最も強い診断材料です。本番の委譲で、前段の前提・証拠・判断が抜け落ちていると考えられます。タスク名と共通の検索先だけでは、各担当の解釈はそろいません。関連する結論や制約を構造化した引き継ぎに含めます。「資料へのアクセス」と「作業の文脈」を区別します。","distractors_ja":["高いtemperatureで指示が変動する可能性はありますが、その記録は示されていません。完全な文脈で改善するという証拠の方が直接的です。","前段の判断やツール結果がなければ、専門エージェントは別々の前提で担当作業を解釈します。個別には一貫しても全体は矛盾します。","全員が同じ検索インデックスを利用するという条件と合いません。異なるコーパスへ送られた証拠も示されていません。","最新モデルでも分野適合性は別途必要ですが、検索品質の低さは報告されていません。完全な文脈での改善は委譲入力の欠落を示します。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-007","number":7,"form":3,"stem":"A scientific-literature research workflow on Databricks uses a coordinator to launch two specialists in parallel: one retrieves citations, and the other evaluates research methodologies. Each specialist receives only the original user request and returns its findings to the coordinator.\n\nThe coordinator subsequently invokes a synthesis agent to prepare the final report. However, synthesis receives only the methodology findings and cannot access the retrieved citations. Its report consequently conflicts with the citation specialist’s findings.\n\nWhich two changes to context propagation would address this synthesis failure? (Choose two.)","options":["Have the coordinator combine both specialists’ outputs into one context payload and pass it to the synthesis agent.","Run methodology analysis before citation retrieval in a sequential workflow, while leaving the context forwarded to synthesis unchanged.","Pass only a brief methodology summary to synthesis and exclude citation-retrieval results to minimize context size.","Send every agent’s complete, unprocessed interaction history, including intermediate prompts and tool calls, to synthesis and let the model organize the information.","Extend the synthesis agent’s input schema to accept a structured context object with separate named fields for citation results and methodology results."],"correct":0,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"並列担当の成果を統合先へ完全に渡す","concept":"ce-f3-q007","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Have the coordinator combine both specialists’ outputs into one context payload and pass it to the synthesis agent.\n\nE. Extend the synthesis agent’s input schema to accept a structured context object with separate named fields for citation results and methodology results.\n\nThese are the intended best answers, with a wording limitation explained under Exam Trap.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe essential requirement is that synthesis can consider both specialists’ findings. The diagnosed failure is missing citation context at the final handoff. A repairs the coordinator’s outgoing payload; E provides an explicit receiving structure for both outputs. Changing execution order leaves the missing information unresolved, while forwarding every interaction introduces unnecessary material.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Synthesis receives only the methodology findings” — Technical signal: One upstream result is omitted at the handoff.\n\n- “Cannot access the retrieved citations” — Hard constraint: Synthesis needs citation evidence to reconcile its conclusions.\n\n- “Launch two specialists in parallel” — Technical signal: Their outputs must converge before final synthesis; parallel execution itself is not the identified defect.\n\n- “Choose two” — Cardinality instruction: Select the complementary propagation and input-contract changes.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n- A restores information delivery. Combining and forwarding both outputs makes citation findings available alongside methodology analysis. This addresses the explicit omission.\n\n- E makes the receiving contract explicit. Named fields distinguish the two result types and support checks that both are present. The coordinator must populate those fields, and synthesis must expose their contents to the model; declaring a schema alone does not deliver evidence.\n\nTogether, A and E implement collection, structured transfer, and consumption of both results. A can restore visibility independently when the existing receiver already accepts the combined payload; E complements that repair by formalizing its structure.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: Sequential execution changes scheduling. Because synthesis still receives the same incomplete context, the citation findings remain unavailable. Sequencing would help only if a dependency required one specialist to consume the other’s output and the relevant information was actually forwarded.\n\n- Option C — Violates Requirement: A compact summary can reduce context usage, but this option explicitly excludes the evidence synthesis needs. It preserves the diagnosed failure.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Complete histories could contain the missing citations, making this a plausible alternative. However, indiscriminate forwarding introduces redundant prompts, intermediate material, and additional context load. It provides a less controlled handoff than selected results with explicit structure. It cannot fairly be classified as inherently ineffective.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe item is not strictly unique as written: D could also restore citation visibility if the complete histories contain usable citation results and fit within the context window. Additionally, E requires actual population and consumption of its fields.\n\nA and E are the strongest intended engineering choices. A uniquely defensible version would explicitly ask for the two changes that provide complete, structured synthesis context while avoiding wholesale history forwarding. None of these changes guarantees contradiction-free generation; they address the stated missing-context cause.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nExecution flow determines when agents run; context propagation determines what they know. At a convergence point, the downstream agent needs the relevant outputs from every contributing branch. Structured inputs help preserve those dependencies, but the application must still transfer the values and include them in the model’s usable context.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA practical implementation could collect citation identifiers, supported findings, methodology assessments, and study associations before invoking synthesis. Checking that both result groups are present would catch incomplete handoffs early. Preserving study identifiers across the groups would also help reviewers verify that methodological judgments refer to the correct evidence.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nAt synthesis, collect every required branch result and preserve its identity.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen final synthesis contradicts an upstream specialist whose findings it never received, repair the handoff first. Combine the required outputs, structure them clearly, and confirm that synthesis actually consumes them.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- LangChain — Workflows and agents\n\nSupports: Parallel-result aggregation and orchestrator-worker synthesis using collected worker outputs.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/workflows-agents)\n\n- LangChain — Thinking in LangGraph\n\nSupports: Explicit shared-state fields and constructing downstream model inputs from the state each step requires.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/thinking-in-langgraph)\n\n- Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents\n\nSupports: Focused context, distilled sub-agent findings, and the drawbacks of unnecessary context accumulation.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Have the coordinator combine both specialists’ outputs into one context payload and pass it to the synthesis agent.\n\nE. Extend the synthesis agent’s input schema to accept a structured context object with separate named fields for citation results and methodology results.\n\nThese are the intended best answers, with a wording limitation explained under Exam Trap.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe essential requirement is that synthesis can consider both specialists’ findings. The diagnosed failure is missing citation context at the final handoff. A repairs the coordinator’s outgoing payload; E provides an explicit receiving structure for both outputs. Changing execution order leaves the missing information unresolved, while forwarding every interaction introduces unnecessary material.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Synthesis receives only the methodology findings” — Technical signal: One upstream result is omitted at the handoff.\n\n- “Cannot access the retrieved citations” — Hard constraint: Synthesis needs citation evidence to reconcile its conclusions.\n\n- “Launch two specialists in parallel” — Technical signal: Their outputs must converge before final synthesis; parallel execution itself is not the identified defect.\n\n- “Choose two” — Cardinality instruction: Select the complementary propagation and input-contract changes.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n- A restores information delivery. Combining and forwarding both outputs makes citation findings available alongside methodology analysis. This addresses the explicit omission.\n\n- E makes the receiving contract explicit. Named fields distinguish the two result types and support checks that both are present. The coordinator must populate those fields, and synthesis must expose their contents to the model; declaring a schema alone does not deliver evidence.\n\nTogether, A and E implement collection, structured transfer, and consumption of both results. A can restore visibility independently when the existing receiver already accepts the combined payload; E complements that repair by formalizing its structure.","Option B — Incorrect: Sequential execution changes scheduling. Because synthesis still receives the same incomplete context, the citation findings remain unavailable. Sequencing would help only if a dependency required one specialist to consume the other’s output and the relevant information was actually forwarded.","Option C — Violates Requirement: A compact summary can reduce context usage, but this option explicitly excludes the evidence synthesis needs. It preserves the diagnosed failure.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Complete histories could contain the missing citations, making this a plausible alternative. However, indiscriminate forwarding introduces redundant prompts, intermediate material, and additional context load. It provides a less controlled handoff than selected results with explicit structure. It cannot fairly be classified as inherently ineffective.","Correct Answer\n\nA. Have the coordinator combine both specialists’ outputs into one context payload and pass it to the synthesis agent.\n\nE. Extend the synthesis agent’s input schema to accept a structured context object with separate named fields for citation results and methodology results.\n\nThese are the intended best answers, with a wording limitation explained under Exam Trap.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe essential requirement is that synthesis can consider both specialists’ findings. The diagnosed failure is missing citation context at the final handoff. A repairs the coordinator’s outgoing payload; E provides an explicit receiving structure for both outputs. Changing execution order leaves the missing information unresolved, while forwarding every interaction introduces unnecessary material.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Synthesis receives only the methodology findings” — Technical signal: One upstream result is omitted at the handoff.\n\n- “Cannot access the retrieved citations” — Hard constraint: Synthesis needs citation evidence to reconcile its conclusions.\n\n- “Launch two specialists in parallel” — Technical signal: Their outputs must converge before final synthesis; parallel execution itself is not the identified defect.\n\n- “Choose two” — Cardinality instruction: Select the complementary propagation and input-contract changes.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n- A restores information delivery. Combining and forwarding both outputs makes citation findings available alongside methodology analysis. This addresses the explicit omission.\n\n- E makes the receiving contract explicit. Named fields distinguish the two result types and support checks that both are present. The coordinator must populate those fields, and synthesis must expose their contents to the model; declaring a schema alone does not deliver evidence.\n\nTogether, A and E implement collection, structured transfer, and consumption of both results. A can restore visibility independently when the existing receiver already accepts the combined payload; E complements that repair by formalizing its structure."],"references":["https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/workflows-agents","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/thinking-in-langgraph","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"15bce72c16b9aaf3a2467a1d4c5ec142a5c3ce9a4645904d5c743323ccb5de61","correct_many":[0,4],"stem_ja":"Databricks上の科学文献調査ワークフローで、調整役が2つの専門エージェントを並列に起動します。一方は引用文献を取得し、他方は研究方法を評価します。各専門エージェントには元のユーザー依頼だけが渡され、調査結果を調整役へ返します。\n\nその後、調整役が統合エージェントを呼び出して最終レポートを作成します。しかし統合側には研究方法の調査結果しか渡されず、取得済みの引用文献へアクセスできません。その結果、引用担当の調査結果と矛盾するレポートになります。\n\nこの統合の失敗に対処するコンテキスト伝播の変更を2つ選んでください。","options_ja":["調整役が両方の専門エージェントの出力を1つのコンテキストペイロードにまとめ、統合エージェントへ渡す。","研究方法の分析を引用文献の取得より前に逐次実行するが、統合側へ転送するコンテキストは変更しない。","コンテキストサイズを最小化するため、研究方法の短い要約だけを統合側へ渡し、引用文献の取得結果は除外する。","中間のプロンプトやツール呼び出しを含む、全エージェントの未処理のやり取り履歴を統合側へ送り、モデルに情報整理を任せる。","統合エージェントの入力スキーマを拡張し、引用結果と研究方法の結果を別々の名前付きフィールドに持つ構造化コンテキストオブジェクトを受け付けるようにする。"],"explanation_ja":"原因は並列処理ではなく、統合先に片方の成果が渡らないことです。両担当の結果をまとめて渡し、入力スキーマに引用と方法の項目を明示すれば、情報欠落を防げます。丸ごとの会話履歴は不要な過程まで持ち込むため過剰です。「必要な成果を両方、受け手が確実に扱える構造で渡す」と覚えます。","distractors_ja":["統合の根拠として両担当の成果が必要です。引用と方法の結果を同じ受信コンテキストへ含めることで、片方だけを見る障害を解消します。","実行順を変えても、転送する内容が片方だけなら引用情報は届きません。依存関係ではなく引き継ぎ内容の問題です。","不足している引用結果を意図的に除外してしまいます。小ささを優先して、最終統合に必要な証拠を失う選択です。","全履歴の転送はコンテキストを膨らませます。中間過程を丸ごと渡すより、必要な成果と参照を構造化する方が適切です。","名前付きの入力フィールドで、両成果の受け渡し契約を明確にします。渡す側の修正と受け取る側のスキーマ整備を組み合わせます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-008","number":8,"form":3,"stem":"A long-running Databricks orchestration agent manages multi-step data pipelines through registered tools named `run_job`, `query_catalog`, and `validate_schema`.\n\nFollowing compaction at turn 40, the agent repeats tool calls that previously succeeded and overlooks results required by subsequent steps. Examination of the compacted context shows that only each pipeline stage’s final state was retained.\n\nThe current compaction prompt reads:\n\n“Summarize the current pipeline state in a few sentences and include only what is necessary.”\n\nWhich prompt revision would restore continuity while limiting token consumption?","options":["Replace “include only what is necessary” with an instruction to preserve the reasoning chain and internal deliberation preceding every tool call.","Replace “include only what is necessary” with an instruction to preserve a compact record of every tool invocation, its key arguments, and its returned result.","Append an instruction to preserve every tool invocation’s complete input payload and all fields in its response.","Append an instruction to preserve the user’s initial task description and every clarification question from the session."],"correct":1,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"圧縮後も残すツール実行記録","concept":"ce-f3-q008","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Replace “include only what is necessary” with an instruction to preserve a compact record of every tool invocation, its key arguments, and its returned result.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe summary must preserve enough execution history to identify completed work and supply downstream dependencies. The observed failure begins after compaction removes that information.\n\nB directly restores the missing execution record with limited detail. A preserves deliberation, C retains excessive raw material, and D preserves task intent without recovering tool results.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Following compaction at turn 40” — Technical signal: The information-loss boundary is compaction.\n\n- “Repeats tool calls that previously succeeded” — Technical signal: The agent has lost its record of completed execution.\n\n- “Overlooks results required by subsequent steps” — Technical signal: Essential outputs and their associations must survive.\n\n- “Only each pipeline stage’s final state” — Technical signal: The summary omits operational details needed for continuation.\n\n- “Limiting token consumption” — Hard constraint: Preserve a compact execution record instead of complete payloads.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe tool name identifies the operation, key arguments identify its scope, and the result records what the operation actually returned. Preserving these together lets the agent recognize earlier work and reuse outputs appropriately.\n\nFor large responses, the compact record should retain essential values or resolvable references needed downstream. Preserve status and identifiers where material; a vague statement that a stage is “done” may omit the evidence needed to continue correctly.\n\nThis improves continuity without retaining all intermediate text.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: Deliberation can describe why a call was considered, but it does not reliably establish which invocation executed successfully or what result it returned. It therefore misses the information category implicated by the failures and can add substantial verbosity.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Full payloads would preserve execution details, making this tempting. However, retaining every response field carries unnecessary information into the compacted context and conflicts with the token-efficiency objective. It fits better in an external debugging trace than in a concise continuation summary.\n\n- Option D — Different Use Case: Original goals and clarification history can preserve intent. Here, the missing information concerns executed operations and their outputs. This revision would fit a failure caused by forgotten user requirements, but it does not repair the demonstrated execution-history loss.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is C: preserving everything appears safest. The distinction is between retaining decision-relevant execution facts and copying entire tool exchanges.\n\nIf the requirement were to retain a complete forensic trace outside the active context, full payload storage could be appropriate.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nA state snapshot describes where the workflow stands. An execution record identifies what ran, with which inputs, and what it produced. Compaction must preserve whichever representation supports the next action; end-state prose alone may lose identifiers and dependencies needed for continuation.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nAfter launching a pipeline job, a compact record could retain the job’s identifying arguments, returned execution identifier, and relevant observed status. Follow-up work could reference that execution instead of launching another. Large diagnostic responses could remain in external traces, reducing active-context load while preserving access to detail.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nPreserve the tool, its key inputs, and the outcome needed next.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nRepeated successful calls after compaction point to lost execution history. Retain compact invocation–argument–result records, including essential identifiers and downstream dependencies.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: Revising compaction prompts to preserve information whose removal causes downstream coherence failures without substantially increasing token cost.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents\n\nSupports: High-fidelity compaction that retains critical information while removing redundant tool output and unnecessary detail.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\n- Databricks — View traces in the Databricks MLflow UI\n\nSupports: Inspecting operation order, inputs, outputs, and errors to identify which execution facts the compacted context omitted.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces/ui-traces)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: Deliberation can describe why a call was considered, but it does not reliably establish which invocation executed successfully or what result it returned. It therefore misses the information category implicated by the failures and can add substantial verbosity.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Replace “include only what is necessary” with an instruction to preserve a compact record of every tool invocation, its key arguments, and its returned result.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe summary must preserve enough execution history to identify completed work and supply downstream dependencies. The observed failure begins after compaction removes that information.\n\nB directly restores the missing execution record with limited detail. A preserves deliberation, C retains excessive raw material, and D preserves task intent without recovering tool results.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Following compaction at turn 40” — Technical signal: The information-loss boundary is compaction.\n\n- “Repeats tool calls that previously succeeded” — Technical signal: The agent has lost its record of completed execution.\n\n- “Overlooks results required by subsequent steps” — Technical signal: Essential outputs and their associations must survive.\n\n- “Only each pipeline stage’s final state” — Technical signal: The summary omits operational details needed for continuation.\n\n- “Limiting token consumption” — Hard constraint: Preserve a compact execution record instead of complete payloads.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe tool name identifies the operation, key arguments identify its scope, and the result records what the operation actually returned. Preserving these together lets the agent recognize earlier work and reuse outputs appropriately.\n\nFor large responses, the compact record should retain essential values or resolvable references needed downstream. Preserve status and identifiers where material; a vague statement that a stage is “done” may omit the evidence needed to continue correctly.\n\nThis improves continuity without retaining all intermediate text.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Full payloads would preserve execution details, making this tempting. However, retaining every response field carries unnecessary information into the compacted context and conflicts with the token-efficiency objective. It fits better in an external debugging trace than in a concise continuation summary.","Option D — Different Use Case: Original goals and clarification history can preserve intent. Here, the missing information concerns executed operations and their outputs. This revision would fit a failure caused by forgotten user requirements, but it does not repair the demonstrated execution-history loss."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces/ui-traces"],"source_hash":"fcbf2dff3452f4329826153ee7eb41a7a47606f1a91c1421caf35261a223896f","stem_ja":"長時間動作するDatabricksのオーケストレーションエージェントが、`run_job`、`query_catalog`、`validate_schema`という登録済みツールを通じて多段階のデータパイプラインを管理しています。\n\n40ターン目に圧縮した後、以前成功したツール呼び出しを繰り返し、後続段階が必要とする結果を見落とすようになりました。圧縮後のコンテキストを調べると、パイプライン各段階の最終状態だけが残っています。\n\n現在の圧縮プロンプトは次のとおりです。\n\n「現在のパイプラインの状態を数文で要約し、必要なものだけを含めてください。」\n\nトークン消費を抑えながら作業の連続性を回復するプロンプトの修正はどれですか。","options_ja":["「必要なものだけを含めてください」を、各ツール呼び出しの前に行った推論の連鎖と内部検討を保持する指示に置き換える。","「必要なものだけを含めてください」を、各ツール呼び出し、主要な引数、返された結果の簡潔な記録を保持する指示に置き換える。","各ツール呼び出しの完全な入力ペイロードと、応答の全フィールドを保持する指示を追加する。","ユーザーの最初のタスク説明と、セッション中のすべての確認質問を保持する指示を追加する。"],"explanation_ja":"最終状態だけでは、何をどの引数で実施し、どの結果が得られたかを再利用できません。必要なのはツール実行の簡潔な操作記録です。呼び出し・主要引数・結果を残せば、成功済み処理の再実行や後続入力の欠落を防げます。内部の思考過程や全応答を残す必要はありません。「何を実行し、何を得たか」を圧縮後も追えるようにします。","distractors_ja":["内部検討の詳細は、確定したツール結果の代わりになりません。大量の推論過程を残しても、再開に必要な操作事実を確実に保存できません。","簡潔なツール記録なら、完了済み操作と後段の依存先を保持できます。連続性を回復しつつ不要なフィールドを省けます。","全入力・全応答の保存は圧縮の効果を弱めます。後続処理が必要とする主要引数と結果に絞るのが設問の要件です。","初期依頼や確認質問は有用でも、すでに実行した操作と結果の欠落を直接補えません。今回の繰り返しを防ぐ情報が不足します。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-009","number":9,"form":3,"stem":"A Databricks workflow uses an orchestrating agent to manage five sub-agents, each responsible for document analysis, entity extraction, and summarization. Before assigning follow-up work, the orchestrator appends every sub-agent’s unfiltered response—including intermediate reasoning chains, extracted entities, and progress messages—to its accumulated context.\n\nAt production document volumes, this context reaches capacity before the orchestrator has processed all results. Truncation errors occur, and the workflow loses coherence.\n\nWhich two changes to sub-agent output design would reduce the orchestrator’s context consumption while retaining access to the information needed for coordination? (Choose two.)","options":["Return a compact structured summary containing task status, key extracted entities, and a confidence score, excluding intermediate reasoning chains.","Decrease the number of sub-agents so fewer tokens reach the orchestrator before final synthesis.","Move the orchestrator to a foundation model with a larger context window so complete sub-agent outputs fit without truncation.","Consolidate the five sub-agents into one agent that sends a single combined response to the orchestrator for each workflow run.","Persist complete sub-agent outputs in a Lakebase table and return only the corresponding record identifier to the orchestrator."],"correct":0,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"調整役には要約、詳細は外部参照","concept":"ce-f3-q009","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Return a compact structured summary containing task status, key extracted entities, and a confidence score, excluding intermediate reasoning chains.\n\nE. Persist complete sub-agent outputs in a Lakebase table and return only the corresponding record identifier to the orchestrator.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nTwo requirements govern the choice: reduce information entering the active context and preserve what coordination requires. A reduces each response to decision-relevant metadata. E moves detailed results into external storage while retaining a reference through which they can be accessed.\n\nThese are the two output-design changes. B and D change agent organization; C increases context capacity without reducing the volume forwarded.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Every sub-agent’s unfiltered response” — Technical signal: Verbose output contracts directly contribute to context growth.\n\n- “Before assigning follow-up work” — Hard constraint: The orchestrator must retain enough task state to choose subsequent actions.\n\n- “Reaches capacity before … processed all results” — Technical signal: Results must have a smaller immediate context footprint.\n\n- “Changes to sub-agent output design” — Scope constraint: Select changes to returned information or its representation.\n\n- “Choose two” — Cardinality instruction: Both compact summaries and externally stored results are relevant patterns.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n- A reduces inline output size. A structured summary makes status and key entities available without forwarding intermediate reasoning or repetitive progress messages. Its fields must capture the facts actually needed for the next coordination decision.\n\n- E preserves detail outside active context. Lakebase is a managed Postgres database suitable for agent state. Returning a record identifier keeps the initial response small while retaining the full result externally. This applies the documented pattern of storing sub-agent artifacts and passing lightweight references.\n\nE requires a working retrieval path: the orchestrator or its tools must resolve the identifier and retrieve needed fields or portions. An identifier alone provides no understanding of the stored result, and immediately loading every complete record would recreate the original problem.\n\nA and E are independently useful output patterns. Neither requires the other to reduce immediate context load.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Different Use Case: Reducing agent count changes execution organization. It may reduce some overhead, but the same document workload can still produce the same total volume of raw results. It does not establish a compact output contract.\n\n- Option C — Different Use Case: A larger context window can provide additional headroom, making this tempting when truncation occurs. However, it leaves the forwarded output volume unchanged and does not answer the request to reduce context load through output design.\n\n- Option D — Different Use Case: Consolidating agents changes task boundaries. One combined response can contain as many tokens as five separate responses. Without summarization or externalization, fewer messages do not ensure a smaller context footprint.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is C: increasing capacity can postpone truncation, but the requested intervention concerns how much sub-agent output enters context. If the question instead asked how to accommodate an irreducible input that must remain fully available, a sufficiently large context window could be appropriate.\n\nAlso, returning references preserves usable information only when those references can be resolved.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nSeparate coordination state from detailed artifacts. Keep the information needed for the next decision readily available, and retain bulky results in accessible storage. Context efficiency depends on selecting useful information and retrieving detail when needed; neither message count nor agent count reliably measures token consumption.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA practical implementation could store full extraction results in Lakebase and return a brief status summary with a record identifier. The orchestrator could inspect relevant entities or unresolved items when assigning follow-up work. This reduces repeated transfer of large results while introducing database-query latency and a dependency on reliable record access.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nReturn the essentials, retain the details, and retrieve selectively.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen raw sub-agent responses overwhelm an orchestrator, look for compact structured summaries and external results returned by reference. Check that summaries preserve coordination facts and that references support selective retrieval.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Lakebase Postgres\n\nSupports: Lakebase’s managed Postgres storage capabilities and use as an agent state store.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects)\n\n- Anthropic — How we built our multi-agent research system\n\nSupports: Persisting sub-agent artifacts externally and returning lightweight references to reduce copying and token overhead.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/multi-agent-research-system)\n\n- Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents\n\nSupports: Distilled sub-agent summaries, selective retrieval through identifiers, and context-management limitations that remain with larger windows.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Return a compact structured summary containing task status, key extracted entities, and a confidence score, excluding intermediate reasoning chains.\n\nE. Persist complete sub-agent outputs in a Lakebase table and return only the corresponding record identifier to the orchestrator.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nTwo requirements govern the choice: reduce information entering the active context and preserve what coordination requires. A reduces each response to decision-relevant metadata. E moves detailed results into external storage while retaining a reference through which they can be accessed.\n\nThese are the two output-design changes. B and D change agent organization; C increases context capacity without reducing the volume forwarded.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Every sub-agent’s unfiltered response” — Technical signal: Verbose output contracts directly contribute to context growth.\n\n- “Before assigning follow-up work” — Hard constraint: The orchestrator must retain enough task state to choose subsequent actions.\n\n- “Reaches capacity before … processed all results” — Technical signal: Results must have a smaller immediate context footprint.\n\n- “Changes to sub-agent output design” — Scope constraint: Select changes to returned information or its representation.\n\n- “Choose two” — Cardinality instruction: Both compact summaries and externally stored results are relevant patterns.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n- A reduces inline output size. A structured summary makes status and key entities available without forwarding intermediate reasoning or repetitive progress messages. Its fields must capture the facts actually needed for the next coordination decision.\n\n- E preserves detail outside active context. Lakebase is a managed Postgres database suitable for agent state. Returning a record identifier keeps the initial response small while retaining the full result externally. This applies the documented pattern of storing sub-agent artifacts and passing lightweight references.\n\nE requires a working retrieval path: the orchestrator or its tools must resolve the identifier and retrieve needed fields or portions. An identifier alone provides no understanding of the stored result, and immediately loading every complete record would recreate the original problem.\n\nA and E are independently useful output patterns. Neither requires the other to reduce immediate context load.","Option B — Different Use Case: Reducing agent count changes execution organization. It may reduce some overhead, but the same document workload can still produce the same total volume of raw results. It does not establish a compact output contract.","Option C — Different Use Case: A larger context window can provide additional headroom, making this tempting when truncation occurs. However, it leaves the forwarded output volume unchanged and does not answer the request to reduce context load through output design.","Option D — Different Use Case: Consolidating agents changes task boundaries. One combined response can contain as many tokens as five separate responses. Without summarization or externalization, fewer messages do not ensure a smaller context footprint.","Correct Answer\n\nA. Return a compact structured summary containing task status, key extracted entities, and a confidence score, excluding intermediate reasoning chains.\n\nE. Persist complete sub-agent outputs in a Lakebase table and return only the corresponding record identifier to the orchestrator.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nTwo requirements govern the choice: reduce information entering the active context and preserve what coordination requires. A reduces each response to decision-relevant metadata. E moves detailed results into external storage while retaining a reference through which they can be accessed.\n\nThese are the two output-design changes. B and D change agent organization; C increases context capacity without reducing the volume forwarded.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Every sub-agent’s unfiltered response” — Technical signal: Verbose output contracts directly contribute to context growth.\n\n- “Before assigning follow-up work” — Hard constraint: The orchestrator must retain enough task state to choose subsequent actions.\n\n- “Reaches capacity before … processed all results” — Technical signal: Results must have a smaller immediate context footprint.\n\n- “Changes to sub-agent output design” — Scope constraint: Select changes to returned information or its representation.\n\n- “Choose two” — Cardinality instruction: Both compact summaries and externally stored results are relevant patterns.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n- A reduces inline output size. A structured summary makes status and key entities available without forwarding intermediate reasoning or repetitive progress messages. Its fields must capture the facts actually needed for the next coordination decision.\n\n- E preserves detail outside active context. Lakebase is a managed Postgres database suitable for agent state. Returning a record identifier keeps the initial response small while retaining the full result externally. This applies the documented pattern of storing sub-agent artifacts and passing lightweight references.\n\nE requires a working retrieval path: the orchestrator or its tools must resolve the identifier and retrieve needed fields or portions. An identifier alone provides no understanding of the stored result, and immediately loading every complete record would recreate the original problem.\n\nA and E are independently useful output patterns. Neither requires the other to reduce immediate context load."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects","https://www.anthropic.com/research/multi-agent-research-system","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"4fe154e616f03f522c8cef5e7e8d59e0f9bfb74723ce1f8c340cc671b7dfad97","correct_many":[0,4],"stem_ja":"Databricksのワークフローで、オーケストレーターが5つのサブエージェントを管理し、文書分析、エンティティ抽出、要約を担当させています。追加作業を割り当てる前に、オーケストレーターは各担当の未選別の応答を蓄積コンテキストへ追加します。中間の推論の連鎖、抽出したエンティティ、進捗メッセージもすべて含まれます。\n\n本番の文書量では、すべての結果を処理し終わる前にコンテキストが上限に達します。切り詰めエラーが発生し、ワークフローの一貫性が失われます。\n\n調整に必要な情報へのアクセスを維持しながら、オーケストレーターのコンテキスト消費を減らすため、サブエージェントの出力設計をどう変更すべきですか。2つ選んでください。","options_ja":["中間の推論の連鎖を除外し、タスクの状態、主要な抽出エンティティ、信頼度スコアを含む簡潔な構造化要約を返す。","サブエージェントの数を減らし、最終統合までにオーケストレーターへ届くトークン数を減らす。","より大きなコンテキストウィンドウを持つ基盤モデルへオーケストレーターを移し、サブエージェントの全出力が切り詰められず収まるようにする。","5つのサブエージェントを1つに統合し、ワークフロー実行ごとに1つの結合応答をオーケストレーターへ送るようにする。","サブエージェントの完全な出力をLakebaseテーブルへ永続化し、オーケストレーターには対応するレコード識別子だけを返す。"],"explanation_ja":"調整役が常時必要とするのは、担当の状態・要点・次の判断材料です。簡潔な構造化要約で渡し、詳細は外部に保存して識別子から必要時に読み出せるようにします。人数やモデル容量の変更より、受け渡す情報量の設計が直接の対策です。識別子だけを返す方式では、必要時に詳細を取得する手段も用意します。","distractors_ja":["進捗と主要な成果だけを構造化することで、調整に使わない中間過程を常時のコンテキストから除けます。","担当数を減らしても、未選別の大きな出力を渡す設計は残ります。必要な専門性や並列性を損なう可能性もあります。","上限を増やすだけでは、無関係な過程が注意を奪う問題を解消しません。出力設計を変えるという問いにも直接答えていません。","1つにまとめても応答が大きいままなら圧迫は続きます。分担構成を変えるより、返す情報を絞る方が直接的です。","完全な出力を失わず、通常時は軽量な参照だけを持てます。必要時のレコード取得を実装して、詳細へのアクセスを維持します。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-010","number":10,"form":3,"stem":"A pharmaceutical R&D team uses a research agent to prepare structured landscape memos for proposed drug targets. Discovery scientists expect each memo to cover prior research, competing programs, clinical development phases, and regulatory history.\n\nImplemented as a single custom agent on Agent Bricks, the system retrieves information from a Databricks AI Search index containing PubMed publications, ClinicalTrials.gov records, internal program memos, and FDA review documents. It completes each memo in one pass within a single context window.\n\nRequests limited to one target and one indication produce accurate results. For broader requests, however, the final memo omits competitor programs identified during the agent’s earlier analysis, associates quoted evidence with incorrect studies, and leaves some fields requiring cross-document reconciliation incomplete. MLflow traces show context utilization approaching the window limit before drafting begins. The entire workflow remains within one agent.\n\nWhich change to the agent boundaries best addresses this pattern?","options":["Assign a separate agent to each document source—PubMed, ClinicalTrials.gov, internal memos, and FDA reviews—and combine their source-specific outputs in an orchestrator.","Retain the single agent and reduce its compaction threshold so that context compression occurs before memo drafting begins.","Assign exploration and synthesis to separate sub-agents, passing condensed findings rather than raw evidence into the synthesis stage.","Assign parsing, retrieval, summarization, clustering, verification, citation, and drafting to individual single-step agents linked through sequential handoffs."],"correct":2,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"探索と統合を分けるエージェント境界","concept":"ce-f3-q010","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Assign exploration and synthesis to separate sub-agents, passing condensed findings rather than raw evidence into the synthesis stage.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe required outcome is a complete memo with correctly associated evidence. The decisive architectural signal is that broad exploration consumes the shared context before synthesis starts. The best boundary separates evidence-intensive investigation from final integration. C directly establishes that separation; A partitions by source, B preserves the existing boundary, and D fragments tightly connected work into many handoffs.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Requests limited to one target and one indication produce accurate results” — Technical signal: Performance deteriorates as research breadth increases.\n\n- “Omits competitor programs identified during the agent’s earlier analysis” — Technical signal: Relevant information was encountered but failed to survive into the final output.\n\n- “Context utilization approaching the window limit before drafting begins” — Technical signal: Exploration is consuming the capacity needed for synthesis.\n\n- “Change to the agent boundaries” — Selection instruction: Choose a decomposition strategy rather than a compaction setting.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nExploration sub-agents can investigate focused questions in separate context windows and return distilled findings. The synthesis agent then integrates those findings without inheriting the full search history. This follows the documented pattern of isolating detailed investigation while reserving the coordinating agent’s context for analysis and synthesis.\n\nFor this scenario, summaries must preserve program and study identifiers, clinical phase, regulatory findings, and citation references. “Condensed findings” must retain the relationships needed to produce an accurate memo; compression alone does not guarantee citation correctness.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Source specialists can simplify source-specific retrieval. However, a program’s publications, trial records, and regulatory documents may need to be reconciled together. Dividing strictly by repository can leave that integration burden with the orchestrator without explicitly controlling its synthesis context. This design fits better when source-specific expertise or access requirements drive decomposition.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Earlier compaction can reduce context pressure and is a reasonable intervention to evaluate. It is tempting because the trace shows a nearly full window. However, it changes context management inside the same agent rather than establishing the requested exploration–synthesis boundary.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: A staged workflow can provide clear checkpoints. Here, making every operation a separate agent introduces repeated transfers of study identity, evidence relationships, and task state. That increases coordination and information-loss risks without showing why each small operation needs an independent agent.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is B: approaching a context limit makes earlier compaction attractive. The distinction is the requested architectural boundary. If the requirement instead mandated retaining a single agent, B would become the strongest available intervention, subject to validating compaction fidelity.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nAn agent boundary determines which information stays together during reasoning and what must be communicated to another agent. Useful boundaries contain a coherent investigation and expose a compact result. Excessively broad boundaries overload context; excessively narrow boundaries require frequent handoffs. The appropriate balance depends on information dependencies.\n\nCurrent Databricks documentation describes custom agents built on Agent Bricks, with orchestration defined by the agent’s framework or harness.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA practical implementation could assign exploration by competitor program, allowing each investigator to reconcile publications, trial records, and regulatory documents. Each returns a structured summary with source references for synthesis. This would aim to preserve program coverage and evidence attribution while limiting drafting context, at the cost of additional agent execution and coordination. Validate the change against representative broad queries.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nKeep detailed investigation local, and pass complete, attributable findings across the synthesis boundary.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen broad research exhausts one agent’s context before drafting, look for a boundary separating exploration from synthesis. Prefer coherent research tasks with compact, traceable outputs, and avoid assuming that more agents automatically improve reliability.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents\n\nSupports: Separate exploration contexts, distilled findings for synthesis, compaction benefits, and compression risks.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\n- Anthropic — How we built our multi-agent research system\n\nSupports: Research decomposition, explicit delegation boundaries, coordination costs, and citation handling. The proposed pharmaceutical implementation is an application of these principles.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/multi-agent-research-system)\n\n- Databricks — What is Agent Bricks?\n\nSupports: Current custom-agent terminology, framework-controlled orchestration, AI Search integration, and MLflow tracing.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/concepts)\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: Section 7 objectives covering agent boundary placement, context growth, and handoff compression overhead.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Source specialists can simplify source-specific retrieval. However, a program’s publications, trial records, and regulatory documents may need to be reconciled together. Dividing strictly by repository can leave that integration burden with the orchestrator without explicitly controlling its synthesis context. This design fits better when source-specific expertise or access requirements drive decomposition.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Earlier compaction can reduce context pressure and is a reasonable intervention to evaluate. It is tempting because the trace shows a nearly full window. However, it changes context management inside the same agent rather than establishing the requested exploration–synthesis boundary.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Assign exploration and synthesis to separate sub-agents, passing condensed findings rather than raw evidence into the synthesis stage.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe required outcome is a complete memo with correctly associated evidence. The decisive architectural signal is that broad exploration consumes the shared context before synthesis starts. The best boundary separates evidence-intensive investigation from final integration. C directly establishes that separation; A partitions by source, B preserves the existing boundary, and D fragments tightly connected work into many handoffs.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Requests limited to one target and one indication produce accurate results” — Technical signal: Performance deteriorates as research breadth increases.\n\n- “Omits competitor programs identified during the agent’s earlier analysis” — Technical signal: Relevant information was encountered but failed to survive into the final output.\n\n- “Context utilization approaching the window limit before drafting begins” — Technical signal: Exploration is consuming the capacity needed for synthesis.\n\n- “Change to the agent boundaries” — Selection instruction: Choose a decomposition strategy rather than a compaction setting.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nExploration sub-agents can investigate focused questions in separate context windows and return distilled findings. The synthesis agent then integrates those findings without inheriting the full search history. This follows the documented pattern of isolating detailed investigation while reserving the coordinating agent’s context for analysis and synthesis.\n\nFor this scenario, summaries must preserve program and study identifiers, clinical phase, regulatory findings, and citation references. “Condensed findings” must retain the relationships needed to produce an accurate memo; compression alone does not guarantee citation correctness.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: A staged workflow can provide clear checkpoints. Here, making every operation a separate agent introduces repeated transfers of study identity, evidence relationships, and task state. That increases coordination and information-loss risks without showing why each small operation needs an independent agent."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","https://www.anthropic.com/research/multi-agent-research-system","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/concepts","https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf"],"source_hash":"86660e85143d035e3ec0de5e1228f8773cc3b4e06d5d90355291b2831ac6584f","stem_ja":"製薬企業の研究開発チームが、候補となる創薬標的について構造化した研究動向メモを作る調査エージェントを利用しています。探索研究者は、先行研究、競合プログラム、臨床開発段階、規制上の履歴を各メモに求めています。\n\nAgent Bricks上の単一カスタムエージェントとして実装され、PubMed論文、ClinicalTrials.govの記録、社内プログラムメモ、FDA審査文書を含むDatabricks AI Searchインデックスから情報を取得します。各メモを、1つのコンテキストウィンドウ内で一度の処理で完成させます。\n\n1つの標的・1つの適応症に限った依頼には正確に回答できます。しかし広い依頼では、前段の分析で見つけた競合プログラムを最終メモから落とし、引用証拠を誤った研究へ結び付け、複数文書の照合が必要な欄を未完成のまま残します。MLflowトレースでは、執筆開始前にコンテキスト使用量が上限へ近づいています。全ワークフローは1つのエージェント内にあります。\n\nこのパターンに最も適したエージェント境界の変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["PubMed、ClinicalTrials.gov、社内メモ、FDA審査という文書ソースごとに別のエージェントを割り当て、ソース別の出力をオーケストレーターで結合する。","単一エージェントを維持し、メモの執筆前にコンテキスト圧縮が起こるよう、圧縮のしきい値を下げる。","探索と統合を別々のサブエージェントへ割り当て、統合段階には生の証拠ではなく、要点をまとめた調査結果を渡す。","解析、検索、要約、クラスタリング、検証、引用、執筆をそれぞれ単一段階のエージェントへ割り当て、逐次の引き継ぎで接続する。"],"explanation_ja":"情報を広く集める探索と、根拠を整理して書く統合では必要なコンテキストが異なります。探索結果を出典付きの簡潔な成果にまとめて引き継げば、統合側が大量の作業履歴に埋もれるのを防げます。単に情報源ごと、または細かな操作ごとに分けると、分野横断の照合や引き継ぎが複雑になります。「探索と統合で窓を分ける」が要点です。","distractors_ja":["ソース別分割は取得の並列化には役立ちますが、探索と執筆が同じ窓で競合する問題や、ソース横断の統合負荷を直接整理しません。","圧縮の前倒しは補助策ですが、エージェント境界の変更ではありません。探索過程と統合作業が同じコンテキストを使う構造が残ります。","探索過程を統合から切り離し、必要な成果と出典を絞って受け渡せます。容量圧迫による欠落や引用の取り違えに直接対応します。","細分化しすぎると引き継ぎ回数が増え、段階間の情報欠落や調整負荷が増します。判断のまとまりを保った境界が重要です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-011","number":11,"form":3,"stem":"A manufacturing operations agent on Databricks has a 5,800-token system prompt. Much of it contains detailed procedures for parsing ISO certification documents, handling equipment warranty claims, and translating safety labels into multiple languages. Each capability is needed in fewer than 2% of sessions.\n\nReal-time production line status questions account for 94% of interactions. The engineering team needs to lower the context loaded by default while maintaining task success on this dominant workflow.\n\nHow should the engineer load instructions for the infrequently used capabilities?","options":["Summarize the specialized procedures and keep the shortened instructions permanently embedded in the system prompt.","Add a routing component before each agent invocation that identifies the request’s intent and inserts the corresponding capability instructions into the system prompt.","Encapsulate the specialized capabilities as Agent Skills and load their instructions dynamically when the request indicates that they are relevant.","Index the specialized instructions in a RAG knowledge base and use a lightweight intent classifier to retrieve applicable instructions at the beginning of every session."],"correct":2,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"低頻度の手順をAgent Skillsで遅延読み込み","concept":"ce-f3-q011","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Encapsulate the specialized capabilities as Agent Skills and load their instructions dynamically when the request indicates that they are relevant.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe objective is to reduce the instructions carried through ordinary production-status interactions while retaining access to specialized procedures.\n\nThe rarely used capabilities are distinct, reusable workflows, making them suitable for Agent Skills. C directly matches the exam objective of packaging rarely invoked capability instructions as skills and choosing a loading strategy that reduces baseline context cost. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “5,800-token system prompt” — Technical signal: Instructions create substantial baseline overhead.\n\n- “Fewer than 2% of sessions” — Usage signal: Detailed procedures are usually irrelevant to the current request.\n\n- “94% of interactions” — Workload signal: Production-status queries should remain the focus of the default context.\n\n- “Maintaining task success” — Hard constraint: Reducing tokens must preserve the instructions needed for successful execution.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAgent Skills use progressive disclosure:\n\n- Discovery: Load lightweight skill names and descriptions.\n\n- Activation: Load the full `SKILL.md` instructions when a task matches.\n\n- Execution: Load supporting resources as needed.\n\nThis preserves detailed procedures without placing them in every interaction. Skill metadata still consumes some context, and the runtime must support skill discovery and loading.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Summarization reduces the size of permanently loaded instructions, but every session still carries the specialized content. Compression can also remove procedural details needed for uncommon tasks.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Intent-based injection can achieve selective loading. It is a valid custom implementation, but C explicitly provides the reusable capability packaging and loading pattern targeted by this question. The stem does not establish that B necessarily uses more tokens or performs worse.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Instruction retrieval can work, but introduces a knowledge-base retrieval and instruction-assembly workflow for these procedures. C more directly expresses the intended capability-loading design. RAG is not inherently incapable of supplying instructions.\n\nExam Trap\n\nB is the strongest distractor because its loading behavior can overlap with C. The distinction is the explicit use of Agent Skills to package specialized procedures, rather than a general custom routing design.\n\nIf the runtime lacked skill support and the requirement were simply conditional prompt injection, B would be a reasonable choice. Do not assume that a separate routing layer always requires another LLM call.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nSeparate instructions that apply to every request from procedures needed only for particular tasks. Keep the common instructions available by default and make specialized procedures discoverable when needed.\n\nThis is proactive context management: avoid loading irrelevant material before it creates context pressure.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nCreate separate skills for ISO document parsing, warranty processing, and safety-label translation. Keep production-status guidance in the base prompt, then evaluate both common requests and rare capability requests after the change.\n\nThe operational benefit is lower routine context consumption; the main reliability check is whether relevant skills activate correctly.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nKeep common instructions ready; load specialized procedures when their tasks arise.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nA large system prompt containing rarely used, reusable procedures points to Agent Skills with dynamic loading. Select C here, while recognizing that custom intent-based injection can provide similar loading behavior.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent Skills — Agent Skills Overview\n\nSupports: Skill packaging, metadata discovery, conditional instruction loading, and progressive disclosure.\n\n[Official specification overview](https://agentskills.io/home)\n\n- Databricks — Add tools and skills\n\nSupports: Skills as workflow instructions, with local discovery or retrieval through MCP.\n\n[Official Databricks documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-gateway/coding-agent-mcp-skills)\n\n- Databricks — Certified Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: The explicit objective covering overloaded system prompts, rarely invoked capabilities, Agent Skills, and baseline context cost.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Summarization reduces the size of permanently loaded instructions, but every session still carries the specialized content. Compression can also remove procedural details needed for uncommon tasks.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Intent-based injection can achieve selective loading. It is a valid custom implementation, but C explicitly provides the reusable capability packaging and loading pattern targeted by this question. The stem does not establish that B necessarily uses more tokens or performs worse.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Encapsulate the specialized capabilities as Agent Skills and load their instructions dynamically when the request indicates that they are relevant.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe objective is to reduce the instructions carried through ordinary production-status interactions while retaining access to specialized procedures.\n\nThe rarely used capabilities are distinct, reusable workflows, making them suitable for Agent Skills. C directly matches the exam objective of packaging rarely invoked capability instructions as skills and choosing a loading strategy that reduces baseline context cost. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “5,800-token system prompt” — Technical signal: Instructions create substantial baseline overhead.\n\n- “Fewer than 2% of sessions” — Usage signal: Detailed procedures are usually irrelevant to the current request.\n\n- “94% of interactions” — Workload signal: Production-status queries should remain the focus of the default context.\n\n- “Maintaining task success” — Hard constraint: Reducing tokens must preserve the instructions needed for successful execution.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAgent Skills use progressive disclosure:\n\n- Discovery: Load lightweight skill names and descriptions.\n\n- Activation: Load the full `SKILL.md` instructions when a task matches.\n\n- Execution: Load supporting resources as needed.\n\nThis preserves detailed procedures without placing them in every interaction. Skill metadata still consumes some context, and the runtime must support skill discovery and loading.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Instruction retrieval can work, but introduces a knowledge-base retrieval and instruction-assembly workflow for these procedures. C more directly expresses the intended capability-loading design. RAG is not inherently incapable of supplying instructions."],"references":["https://agentskills.io/home","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-gateway/coding-agent-mcp-skills","https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf"],"source_hash":"723f8630014ff7bf9c62fd00e53a5be24046b065a70340a2a674740851a1b8c8","stem_ja":"Databricks上の製造オペレーションエージェントに、5,800トークンのシステムプロンプトがあります。その多くは、ISO認証文書の解析、機器の保証請求処理、安全ラベルの多言語翻訳についての詳細な手順です。各機能が必要になるのはセッションの2%未満です。\n\nリアルタイムの生産ラインの状態に関する質問が、やり取りの94%を占めます。エンジニアリングチームは、この主要ワークフローのタスク成功率を保ちながら、標準で読み込むコンテキストを減らす必要があります。\n\nまれに使う機能の指示を、どのように読み込むべきですか。","options_ja":["専門手順を要約し、短くした指示をシステムプロンプトへ常時埋め込んでおく。","エージェント呼び出しの前に要求の意図を識別し、対応する機能の指示をシステムプロンプトへ挿入するルーティングコンポーネントを追加する。","専門機能をAgent Skillsとしてまとめ、その機能が関連すると要求から分かった時だけ、指示を動的に読み込む。","専門指示をRAGナレッジベースに索引化し、軽量な意図分類器によって各セッション開始時に該当する指示を取得する。"],"explanation_ja":"利用頻度の低い詳細手順を毎回のシステムプロンプトに載せる必要はありません。Agent Skillsとして必要時に開示すれば、通常の生産状況照会を軽量に保ち、特殊作業では十分な指示を使えます。意図ルーティングでも似た仕組みは作れますが、この問いでは再利用可能な機能としての遅延読み込みを直接表すSkillsが最も適合します。","distractors_ja":["要約で多少は減らせますが、ほぼ使わない機能のコストを全要求が払い続けます。細かな手順の削除による品質低下にも注意が必要です。","必要な指示だけを挿入する考え方は有効です。ただし独自ルーターより、機能をSkillsとしてまとめる選択が設問の狙いを直接表します。","関連する作業が必要になってから詳細指示を読み込めます。主要な94%のやり取りの基礎コンテキストを小さく保つ方法です。","RAGによる取得でも実装可能ですが、セッション開始時の検索に依存します。専門機能の指示を必要時に開く設計としてSkillsがより直接的です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-012","number":12,"form":3,"stem":"A Databricks agent helps warehouse employees answer inventory questions. Its context includes a 120-token system prompt, 400 tokens of conversation history, and 10 tool definitions averaging 180 tokens each, for a combined tool footprint of 1,800 tokens. At runtime, its RAG pipeline also supplies a 1,600-token document chunk.\n\nOperational monitoring reports that requests frequently exceed the model’s 4,096-token context limit, resulting in truncated history and weaker continuity across turns. Sessions cover a narrow range of repetitive tasks and typically invoke 3–4 tools.\n\nWhich configuration change would most effectively reduce context consumption and improve the model’s focus?","options":["Decrease the retrieved RAG chunk from 1,600 to 800 tokens, using the recovered capacity to accommodate more tool definitions.","Shorten conversation history from 400 to 100 tokens, allocating the recovered space equally to tool definitions and retrieved content.","Configure a fixed, curated collection of the 4 most frequently used tools, lowering the tool-definition footprint to approximately 720 tokens per turn.","Introduce an intent-based routing layer that evaluates each user turn and supplies only the relevant tool definitions to the agent."],"correct":2,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"静的なツール選別と動的ルーティングの前提差","concept":"ce-f3-q012","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Configure a fixed, curated collection of the 4 most frequently used tools, lowering the tool-definition footprint to approximately 720 tokens per turn.\n\nC is the intended exam answer. However, the supplied facts do not establish that it uniquely outperforms D.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirement is to reduce context consumption while preserving useful conversation history and task capability. Tool definitions are the largest listed component, and the repetitive workload suggests that a smaller, stable tool collection may suffice.\n\nC provides a measurable reduction without shortening history or retrieved evidence. The important assumption is that the selected four tools cover the required workflows. Low session variety supports that assumption, but does not prove it.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “1,800 tokens” — Technical signal: Tool definitions occupy the largest listed share of context.\n\n- “Narrow range of repetitive tasks” — Workload signal: A fixed tool collection is a plausible fit.\n\n- “Typically invoke 3–4 tools” — Technical signal: Suggests excess tool exposure, although different sessions could use different subsets.\n\n- “Weaker continuity across turns” — Failure signal: Further reducing conversation history would aggravate the observed problem.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nUsing the supplied averages:\n\n- Existing tool definitions: `10 × 180 = 1,800 tokens`.\n\n- Curated tool definitions: `4 × 180 = 720 tokens`.\n\n- Reduction: `1,080 tokens`, or 60% of the tool-definition footprint.\n\n- Revised total for the listed components: `120 + 400 + 720 + 1,600 = 2,840 tokens`.\n\nFewer unnecessary definitions also reduce competing tool choices. Anthropic recommends a small collection of purposeful tools aligned with evaluated workflows and explains that excessive or overlapping tools can distract agents.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Reducing retrieval saves 800 tokens before any additional definitions are added. However, the scenario does not establish that half the retrieved content is dispensable, and adding more definitions could consume the recovered capacity.\n\n- Option B — Violates Requirement: Redistributing all 300 recovered tokens produces no net context reduction. It also removes history from an agent already suffering from poor conversational continuity.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best under the intended exam framing: Dynamic selection can reduce tool exposure and preserve access to less frequently needed capabilities. On-demand loading is an established context-saving approach. The repetitive workload makes static curation a reasonable first choice, but the question supplies no routing-cost or coverage measurements that conclusively make D inferior.\n\nExam Trap\n\nTwo limitations deserve attention:\n\n- The arithmetic does not demonstrate overflow. The listed components total `3,920 tokens`, leaving 176 tokens below the stated limit. Additional request content or variable runtime consumption could explain the reported overflow, but neither is quantified.\n\n- Tools used per session do not identify the required tool collection. Different sessions might each use four tools drawn from all ten.\n\nIf required tools vary substantially between sessions, D becomes more compelling. Do not assume dynamic routing inherently consumes more tokens; its implementation determines that trade-off.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nTool definitions consume context whenever they are supplied to the model, even when the corresponding tools are never called.\n\nStatic curation selects a fixed collection in advance. Dynamic routing selects definitions at runtime. Both reduce unnecessary context; workload coverage and measured performance determine which approach fits best.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nEvaluate the proposed four-tool collection against representative warehouse conversations, including uncommon requests. If it preserves task completion, deploying it recovers approximately 1,080 definition tokens per request under the supplied averages. If required capabilities disappear, retain a way to discover additional tools.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nUse a small fixed tool collection for stable workflows; select tools dynamically when required capabilities vary.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nA repetitive workload with many exposed tools points toward static tool curation, making C the intended answer. Confirm tool coverage before removing capabilities, and check the token arithmetic before accepting an overflow diagnosis.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Certified Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: Objective alignment for identifying disproportionate context consumption and applying proactive strategies such as minimal tool sets.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Anthropic — Writing effective tools for agents\n\nSupports: Purposeful tool selection, workflow-based evaluation, and the distraction caused by excessive tool choices.\n\n[Official engineering guidance](https://www.anthropic.com/research/writing-tools-for-agents)\n\n- Anthropic — Code execution with MCP: Building more efficient agents\n\nSupports: Loading relevant tool definitions on demand as a valid alternative to exposing every definition upfront.\n\n[Official architecture guidance](https://www.anthropic.com/research/code-execution-with-mcp)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Reducing retrieval saves 800 tokens before any additional definitions are added. However, the scenario does not establish that half the retrieved content is dispensable, and adding more definitions could consume the recovered capacity.","Option B — Violates Requirement: Redistributing all 300 recovered tokens produces no net context reduction. It also removes history from an agent already suffering from poor conversational continuity.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Configure a fixed, curated collection of the 4 most frequently used tools, lowering the tool-definition footprint to approximately 720 tokens per turn.\n\nC is the intended exam answer. However, the supplied facts do not establish that it uniquely outperforms D.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirement is to reduce context consumption while preserving useful conversation history and task capability. Tool definitions are the largest listed component, and the repetitive workload suggests that a smaller, stable tool collection may suffice.\n\nC provides a measurable reduction without shortening history or retrieved evidence. The important assumption is that the selected four tools cover the required workflows. Low session variety supports that assumption, but does not prove it.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “1,800 tokens” — Technical signal: Tool definitions occupy the largest listed share of context.\n\n- “Narrow range of repetitive tasks” — Workload signal: A fixed tool collection is a plausible fit.\n\n- “Typically invoke 3–4 tools” — Technical signal: Suggests excess tool exposure, although different sessions could use different subsets.\n\n- “Weaker continuity across turns” — Failure signal: Further reducing conversation history would aggravate the observed problem.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nUsing the supplied averages:\n\n- Existing tool definitions: `10 × 180 = 1,800 tokens`.\n\n- Curated tool definitions: `4 × 180 = 720 tokens`.\n\n- Reduction: `1,080 tokens`, or 60% of the tool-definition footprint.\n\n- Revised total for the listed components: `120 + 400 + 720 + 1,600 = 2,840 tokens`.\n\nFewer unnecessary definitions also reduce competing tool choices. Anthropic recommends a small collection of purposeful tools aligned with evaluated workflows and explains that excessive or overlapping tools can distract agents.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best under the intended exam framing: Dynamic selection can reduce tool exposure and preserve access to less frequently needed capabilities. On-demand loading is an established context-saving approach. The repetitive workload makes static curation a reasonable first choice, but the question supplies no routing-cost or coverage measurements that conclusively make D inferior."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://www.anthropic.com/research/writing-tools-for-agents","https://www.anthropic.com/research/code-execution-with-mcp"],"source_hash":"72f18a4ff65dc73b992091829a19659826a2986415a5bc9344065842db319160","stem_ja":"Databricksエージェントが倉庫の従業員の在庫照会を支援しています。コンテキストには、120トークンのシステムプロンプト、400トークンの会話履歴、平均180トークンのツール定義10個があり、ツール全体では1,800トークンになります。実行時にはRAGパイプラインから1,600トークンの文書チャンクも供給されます。\n\n運用監視では、要求がモデルの4,096トークンのコンテキスト上限を頻繁に超え、履歴の切り詰めとターン間の連続性低下が発生しています。セッションは種類の少ない反復的なタスクを扱い、通常3〜4個のツールを呼び出します。\n\nコンテキスト消費を減らしてモデルの集中を改善するうえで、最も効果的な設定変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["取得するRAGチャンクを1,600から800トークンへ減らし、空いた容量でさらに多くのツール定義を収める。","会話履歴を400から100トークンへ減らし、空いた容量をツール定義と取得コンテンツへ均等に配分する。","最もよく使う4個のツールを固定の選別済みセットとして設定し、ツール定義の量を1ターンあたり約720トークンへ減らす。","各ユーザーターンの意図を評価するルーティング層を導入し、関連するツール定義だけをエージェントへ供給する。"],"explanation_ja":"原資料の想定正答は、反復的な業務に合わせてツールを4個へ固定する方法です。定義は1,800から720トークンへ減り、履歴を削らず1,080トークン節約できます。ただし4個で全業務をカバーできるとは確定しておらず、動的ルーティングも有効です。また列挙された合計は3,920で上限未満です。静的・動的の優劣には前提不足がある設問として学びます。\n\n補足：原資料自身も想定正答は固定4ツールだが、動的ルーティングより一意に優れるとは証明されないと注記している。列挙トークンは120+400+1,800+1,600=3,920で、4,096未満。追加メッセージ等の未記載コストなら超過し得る。公式原則は最小の有用ツール集合と必要時の読み込みの双方を支持する：https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents 、https://www.anthropic.com/research/code-execution-with-mcp 。正解キーは原資料どおり維持。一意な採点を避けるためgrading_holdを推奨するが、最終判断はサイト側。","distractors_ja":["取得証拠を半分にしてよい根拠がなく、さらに定義を追加すると節約も失います。不要なツール定義を先に減らす方が設問に合います。","空いた300トークンをすべて再配分すれば総量は減りません。しかも連続性が低下している会話履歴をさらに削ってしまいます。","反復業務を必要な4個でカバーできるなら有効で、これが原資料の採点キーです。ただし、そのカバー範囲は設問だけでは証明されません。","必要ツールがターンごとに異なるなら合理的な対策です。ルーティングのコストや網羅性が未提示のため、固定セットより劣ると断定できません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。","grading_hold":"原資料の条件・選択肢では正解を一意に確定できないため、正答率の計算と期限・苦手復習から除外しています。原資料の想定正答は参考表示です。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-013","number":13,"form":3,"stem":"A context engineer is examining an MLflow trace from a Databricks compliance agent conducting a regional bank’s quarterly regulatory review. The workflow retrieves regulatory clauses, converts them into structured obligations with regional applicability metadata and citations, validates the obligations through a rules engine, and drafts an audit memo using the structured obligations and validation results.\n\nThe context window is nearing its compaction threshold, so compaction will occur before the workflow finishes. The trace contains these spans:\n\n- Span 1 — RETRIEVER, completed: Retrieved 22 regulatory clause excerpts from the compliance vector index. Its output contains 8,400 tokens of raw clause text, with source URI and section ID metadata.\n\n- Span 2 — LLM/CHAT_MODEL, completed: Generated a 410-token structured intermediate containing three obligations. Each includes a regional applicability tag explaining how state-level regulations modify the federal requirement, plus a citation to its originating clause.\n\n- Span 3 — TOOL, completed: Validated the three obligations through the rules engine. Its 280-token report contains an entry for each obligation, including its ID, pass/fail outcome, and a short explanation of the determining rule.\n\n- Span 4 — LLM/CHAT_MODEL, queued: Will produce the compliance memo for the bank’s audit team.\n\nWhich executed-span content can safely be summarized during compaction while preserving the inputs needed by span 4?","options":["Summarize span 1’s 22 raw excerpts because span 2 already carries the obligations, regional applicability metadata, and source citations used for memo synthesis.","Summarize span 2’s structured obligations because span 1 must remain verbatim for direct quotations and audit provenance, while span 3 already incorporates the obligation analysis into its validation outcomes.","Summarize all three completed spans because the default compaction digest preserves workflow state and findings at sufficient detail for the memo-generation step.","Summarize both span 1’s raw excerpts and span 2’s structured obligations because downstream processing makes earlier outputs redundant, leaving only span 3’s validation report necessary."],"correct":0,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"後続スパンの依存を保つ圧縮対象の選択","concept":"ce-f3-q013","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Summarize span 1’s 22 raw excerpts because span 2 already carries the obligations, regional applicability metadata, and source citations used for memo synthesis.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nStart with the queued step’s declared dependencies: structured obligations from span 2 and validation outcomes from span 3. Protect both, including their obligation-ID associations, regional interpretations, citations, and rule explanations.\n\nSpan 1’s bulky raw excerpts are the summarization candidate because the stated workflow has already transferred the information needed for synthesis into span 2. The decision follows downstream information requirements, rather than simply choosing the oldest or largest output.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Drafts … using the structured obligations and validation results” — Hard constraint: Spans 2 and 3 remain live inputs.\n\n- “Regional applicability tag” — Technical signal: Span 2 contains substantive interpretation that the pass/fail report does not reproduce.\n\n- “A citation to its originating clause” — Technical signal: Source provenance has been carried forward.\n\n- “ID, pass/fail outcome … explanation” — Technical signal: Span 3 supplies validation findings but does not replace the obligation descriptions.\n\n- “8,400 tokens” versus “410” and “280” — Technical signal: The raw excerpts dominate context consumption.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nUnder the declared architecture, span 4 needs to combine each obligation’s meaning, regional applicability, citation, and validation outcome. Those elements remain available through spans 2 and 3.\n\nTheir combined size is 410 + 280 = 690 tokens, excluding surrounding instructions and context. Summarizing the 8,400-token retrieval output therefore targets the largest redundant representation while protecting compact synthesis inputs.\n\nThis conclusion assumes the structured intermediate faithfully contains the information required by the stated memo task. Summarizing active-context excerpts does not require deleting the original source documents or audit records.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: It treats the structured obligations as disposable because validation has finished. However, span 3 contains outcomes keyed by obligation ID, not the complete obligation content, regional interpretation, and citations. The option also introduces a verbatim-quotation requirement absent from the scenario. Keeping raw clauses would not preserve the already extracted interpretation unless it were reconstructed.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: A generic digest is not evidence that every required field and association will survive. Summarizing all outputs indiscriminately risks losing regional tags, citations, or validation explanations. Broad compaction requires an explicit preservation contract and validation; the claimed default guarantee is unsupported.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Processing an output downstream does not establish that all its information has been transferred. The validation report does not replace span 2’s obligation descriptions and regional metadata. Retaining only pass/fail entries would leave the memo without essential explanatory content.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is D: “already processed” can sound equivalent to “no longer needed.” The determining question is whether the remaining representation preserves everything the next step requires.\n\nIf span 4 instead required exact regulatory quotations, the relevant original passages would need to remain available or be retrieved again; citations alone would not supply their wording.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nCompaction should follow information dependencies. An earlier output becomes redundant only when another retained representation preserves its downstream-relevant content and relationships. Preserve essential fields and links between records, even when the associated source material can be shortened.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA practical compactor could preserve the obligation objects and validation entries while replacing raw retrieval text with a short retrieval summary and source references. Keeping the original clauses accessible outside active context supports later inspection. Checking obligation-ID mappings would help prevent a rule outcome from being attached to the wrong obligation.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nSummarize redundant source text while preserving the next step’s information dependencies.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nIdentify the queued operation’s required inputs before selecting content for compaction. Summarize raw material when its needed meaning and provenance survive elsewhere; retain intermediate structures that still carry unique information.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: The objective covering identification of content that can be removed during compaction without affecting downstream task execution.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents\n\nSupports: Preserving critical information during compaction while removing redundant tool outputs; the dependency analysis above applies that principle to the supplied trace.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\n- Databricks — View traces in the Databricks MLflow UI\n\nSupports: Inspecting span relationships, execution order, inputs, and outputs to determine which information remains necessary.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces/ui-traces)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Summarize span 1’s 22 raw excerpts because span 2 already carries the obligations, regional applicability metadata, and source citations used for memo synthesis.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nStart with the queued step’s declared dependencies: structured obligations from span 2 and validation outcomes from span 3. Protect both, including their obligation-ID associations, regional interpretations, citations, and rule explanations.\n\nSpan 1’s bulky raw excerpts are the summarization candidate because the stated workflow has already transferred the information needed for synthesis into span 2. The decision follows downstream information requirements, rather than simply choosing the oldest or largest output.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Drafts … using the structured obligations and validation results” — Hard constraint: Spans 2 and 3 remain live inputs.\n\n- “Regional applicability tag” — Technical signal: Span 2 contains substantive interpretation that the pass/fail report does not reproduce.\n\n- “A citation to its originating clause” — Technical signal: Source provenance has been carried forward.\n\n- “ID, pass/fail outcome … explanation” — Technical signal: Span 3 supplies validation findings but does not replace the obligation descriptions.\n\n- “8,400 tokens” versus “410” and “280” — Technical signal: The raw excerpts dominate context consumption.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nUnder the declared architecture, span 4 needs to combine each obligation’s meaning, regional applicability, citation, and validation outcome. Those elements remain available through spans 2 and 3.\n\nTheir combined size is 410 + 280 = 690 tokens, excluding surrounding instructions and context. Summarizing the 8,400-token retrieval output therefore targets the largest redundant representation while protecting compact synthesis inputs.\n\nThis conclusion assumes the structured intermediate faithfully contains the information required by the stated memo task. Summarizing active-context excerpts does not require deleting the original source documents or audit records.","Option B — Incorrect: It treats the structured obligations as disposable because validation has finished. However, span 3 contains outcomes keyed by obligation ID, not the complete obligation content, regional interpretation, and citations. The option also introduces a verbatim-quotation requirement absent from the scenario. Keeping raw clauses would not preserve the already extracted interpretation unless it were reconstructed.","Option C — Incorrect: A generic digest is not evidence that every required field and association will survive. Summarizing all outputs indiscriminately risks losing regional tags, citations, or validation explanations. Broad compaction requires an explicit preservation contract and validation; the claimed default guarantee is unsupported.","Option D — Incorrect: Processing an output downstream does not establish that all its information has been transferred. The validation report does not replace span 2’s obligation descriptions and regional metadata. Retaining only pass/fail entries would leave the memo without essential explanatory content."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces/ui-traces"],"source_hash":"2626e53731b9cbc059b7409e20b3419e25d9f57b84331995a141647a8b6e725e","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、地域銀行の四半期規制レビューを行うDatabricksコンプライアンスエージェントのMLflowトレースを調べています。ワークフローは規制条項を取得し、地域ごとの適用情報と引用を持つ構造化された義務へ変換し、ルールエンジンで検証し、その義務と検証結果から監査メモを作成します。\n\nコンテキストが圧縮のしきい値に近づいており、作業完了前に圧縮されます。トレースには次のスパンがあります。\n\n- スパン1 — RETRIEVER、完了：コンプライアンスのベクトルインデックスから22件の規制条項の抜粋を取得。出力には、生の条項本文8,400トークンと、ソースURIおよび節IDのメタデータがある。\n- スパン2 — LLM/CHAT_MODEL、完了：3つの義務を含む410トークンの構造化中間結果を生成。各義務には、州の規制が連邦要件をどう修正するかを示す地域適用タグと、元の条項への引用がある。\n- スパン3 — TOOL、完了：3つの義務をルールエンジンで検証。280トークンの報告には、義務ごとのID、合否、判定を決めたルールの短い説明がある。\n- スパン4 — LLM/CHAT_MODEL、待機中：銀行の監査チーム向けにコンプライアンスメモを生成する。\n\nスパン4に必要な入力を保ったまま、圧縮で安全に要約できる実行済みスパンの内容はどれですか。","options_ja":["スパン2にメモ統合用の義務、地域適用メタデータ、出典引用がすでにあるため、スパン1の22件の生の抜粋を要約する。","直接引用と監査の出所確認のためスパン1は原文のまま残す必要があり、スパン3も検証結果に義務分析を組み込んでいるため、スパン2の構造化義務を要約する。","標準の圧縮要約がメモ生成に十分な詳しさで作業状態と知見を保存するので、完了済みの3スパンすべてを要約する。","後段の処理によって前段出力は不要になり、必要なのはスパン3の検証報告だけなので、スパン1の生の抜粋とスパン2の構造化義務を両方要約する。"],"explanation_ja":"後段のメモは、地域の修正条件を含む義務と、その検証結果を必要としています。スパン2・3を保持すれば、生の条項8,400トークンは要約して軽量化できます。重要なのは、処理済みかどうかではなく、後段へ必要情報が完全に引き継がれたかです。地域適用タグや出典まで含む中間結果は、単なる合否報告に置き換えないようにします。","distractors_ja":["生の条項から必要な義務・地域条件・出典がすでに中間結果へ移されています。その中間結果と検証報告を残して生本文を軽量化できます。","検証の合否だけでは、義務本文や州による修正条件を復元できません。後段が使う構造化中間結果を削る理由にはなりません。","標準要約が重要な地域条件まで保持する保証はありません。必要な構造化情報まで無差別に要約すると監査メモの入力を失います。","検証結果は義務の代わりではありません。後段は何を満たすべきかと、その検証結果の両方を必要とします。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-014","number":14,"form":3,"stem":"A Databricks research agent generates analyst reports whose claims must have verifiable citations. Reviewers must be able to connect every citation to the exact wording of its original source.\n\nDuring each step, a retrieval tool supplies several short source excerpts. The agent records an analysis explaining how those excerpts support its conclusions, then adds both the excerpts and the analysis to context used by later steps.\n\nAs execution continues, the growing context causes per-step token consumption to exceed the team’s budget. Which compaction strategy preserves citation traceability while reducing token usage?","options":["Snippet summarization: replace retrieved excerpts with brief paraphrases while preserving the complete reasoning traces.","Conservative compaction: preserve retrieved snippets verbatim while shortening reasoning traces and removing repetition, accepting a smaller token reduction to retain the exact evidence needed for citations.","Recency eviction: remove the oldest excerpts and reasoning as new material arrives, retaining only recent context within the token budget.","Aggressive compaction: replace each step’s excerpts and reasoning with a brief generated summary to maximize token recovery."],"correct":1,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"引用の原文を守る保守的な圧縮","concept":"ce-f3-q014","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Conservative compaction: preserve retrieved snippets verbatim while shortening reasoning traces and removing repetition, accepting a smaller token reduction to retain the exact evidence needed for citations.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nExact-source traceability is a hard constraint. Token reduction must therefore preserve the evidence supporting cited claims while shortening material that can tolerate compression.\n\nB explicitly protects original wording and reduces repetitive analysis. The other options replace or discard source evidence without specifying another mechanism through which reviewers can recover it.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Every citation” — Hard constraint: Traceability must hold across all cited claims.\n\n- “Exact wording of its original source” — Hard constraint: A meaning-preserving paraphrase alone is insufficient evidence.\n\n- “Both the excerpts and the analysis” — Technical signal: The context contains distinct evidence and interpretation layers that can be treated differently.\n\n- “Per-step token consumption … exceed[s] the team’s budget” — Hard constraint: Keeping the entire accumulated context unchanged is unsuitable.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nConservative compaction preserves the original snippets while compressing the agent’s recorded analysis into concise conclusions and essential claim-to-source associations. Removing repeated explanations reduces context consumption without rewriting the cited evidence.\n\nThe snippets should remain associated with their source identifiers and precise locators. Verbatim text alone does not establish where it originated.\n\nB provides the best balance among the choices, although the engineer must measure whether its token savings satisfy the actual budget.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Violates Requirement: Paraphrasing excerpts can save tokens, but the resulting text is not the original evidence. Keeping every reasoning trace also preserves a potentially verbose layer while compressing the layer needed for exact-text verification. It fits better when gist-level retention is sufficient or original evidence remains separately accessible.\n\n- Option C — Violates Requirement: Recency eviction bounds context size, but older excerpts may still support claims in the final report. Their age does not make them dispensable. This approach fits workflows where earlier material is genuinely irrelevant or reliably recoverable through another mechanism.\n\n- Option D — Violates Requirement: Aggressive summarization can reclaim substantial context, making it attractive under cost pressure. However, it replaces both evidence and interpretation with generated text and does not preserve access to exact source wording. It is better suited to tasks where retained meaning is sufficient.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is D: maximizing token savings appears to solve the immediate budget problem. The decisive distinction is whether compression preserves the evidence required to verify citations.\n\nIf the requirement allowed gist-only retention, aggressive summarization could become appropriate. A summary can also remain citable when an external evidence store preserves the original text and precise references, but that safeguard is absent from D.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nEvidence and interpretation have different preservation needs. Interpretation can often be condensed while retaining its conclusions; exact-text verification requires continued access to original evidence and provenance. That access may be maintained in active context or through a reliable external evidence store.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nAn implementation could retain each cited excerpt with its document identifier and passage location while replacing repeated analysis with a concise claim-to-source mapping. This would reduce repeated prompt content while preserving reviewer access to evidence. If more reduction were necessary, an external evidence store could support selective retrieval of the original passages.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nPreserve verifiable evidence and compress the explanation around it.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen exact source text must remain verifiable, favor compaction that protects evidence and provenance. Reduce repetition and analysis verbosity first; consider external evidence references when further context reduction is required.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: The objective comparing aggressive compaction’s lower token cost against conservative compaction’s higher information fidelity.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents\n\nSupports: Preserving critical information during compaction while removing redundant material; overly aggressive compression can discard important details.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\n- Anthropic — How we built our multi-agent research system\n\nSupports: Matching claims to specific source locations and retaining artifacts externally through lightweight references.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/multi-agent-research-system)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Violates Requirement: Paraphrasing excerpts can save tokens, but the resulting text is not the original evidence. Keeping every reasoning trace also preserves a potentially verbose layer while compressing the layer needed for exact-text verification. It fits better when gist-level retention is sufficient or original evidence remains separately accessible.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Conservative compaction: preserve retrieved snippets verbatim while shortening reasoning traces and removing repetition, accepting a smaller token reduction to retain the exact evidence needed for citations.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nExact-source traceability is a hard constraint. Token reduction must therefore preserve the evidence supporting cited claims while shortening material that can tolerate compression.\n\nB explicitly protects original wording and reduces repetitive analysis. The other options replace or discard source evidence without specifying another mechanism through which reviewers can recover it.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Every citation” — Hard constraint: Traceability must hold across all cited claims.\n\n- “Exact wording of its original source” — Hard constraint: A meaning-preserving paraphrase alone is insufficient evidence.\n\n- “Both the excerpts and the analysis” — Technical signal: The context contains distinct evidence and interpretation layers that can be treated differently.\n\n- “Per-step token consumption … exceed[s] the team’s budget” — Hard constraint: Keeping the entire accumulated context unchanged is unsuitable.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nConservative compaction preserves the original snippets while compressing the agent’s recorded analysis into concise conclusions and essential claim-to-source associations. Removing repeated explanations reduces context consumption without rewriting the cited evidence.\n\nThe snippets should remain associated with their source identifiers and precise locators. Verbatim text alone does not establish where it originated.\n\nB provides the best balance among the choices, although the engineer must measure whether its token savings satisfy the actual budget.","Option C — Violates Requirement: Recency eviction bounds context size, but older excerpts may still support claims in the final report. Their age does not make them dispensable. This approach fits workflows where earlier material is genuinely irrelevant or reliably recoverable through another mechanism.","Option D — Violates Requirement: Aggressive summarization can reclaim substantial context, making it attractive under cost pressure. However, it replaces both evidence and interpretation with generated text and does not preserve access to exact source wording. It is better suited to tasks where retained meaning is sufficient."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","https://www.anthropic.com/research/multi-agent-research-system"],"source_hash":"1c1e84ed57cc51f9c28600e36fc5021fb063462d057de5a5adc03ed763c893f9","stem_ja":"Databricksの調査エージェントが、検証可能な引用を必要とするアナリストレポートを生成しています。レビュー担当者は、各引用を元ソースの正確な表現に結び付けられなければなりません。\n\n各段階で、検索ツールが短いソース抜粋を複数返します。エージェントは、その抜粋が結論をどう裏付けるか分析を記録し、抜粋と分析の両方を後続段階のコンテキストへ追加します。\n\n処理が進むにつれコンテキストが増え、1段階あたりのトークン消費がチームの予算を超えるようになりました。トークン使用量を減らしながら、引用の追跡可能性を保持する圧縮方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["抜粋の要約：検索した抜粋を短い言い換えに置き換え、推論トレースは完全に保持する。","保守的な圧縮：検索した抜粋を原文のまま残し、推論トレースを短くして重複を削る。引用に必要な正確な証拠を残すため、トークン削減量が小さくなることを受け入れる。","新しさによる削除：新しい情報が入るたびに最も古い抜粋と推論を削り、予算内に収まる最近のコンテキストだけを保持する。","積極的な圧縮：各段階の抜粋と推論を短い生成要約に置き換え、回収するトークン数を最大化する。"],"explanation_ja":"この設問の強い条件は、引用を元ソースの正確な表現へ結び付けられることです。言い換えは意味が近くても引用証拠を置き換えられません。短い原文抜粋は保持し、重複した説明や推論の記述を圧縮します。最大の削減率より必要な証拠の保全を優先します。「証拠は原文、説明は簡潔に」と覚えると判断しやすくなります。","distractors_ja":["要約で原文の言い回しが失われます。推論過程を完全保存しても、元の表現に照合するという引用要件を満たしません。","必要な原文証拠を保持し、その周りの説明を減らします。削減効果は控えめでも、追跡可能性という必須条件を守れます。","古いことと不要であることは別です。今の結論を裏付ける古い抜粋を削ると、引用の検証ができなくなります。","大幅に縮められますが、抜粋まで生成要約にすると元の表現を失います。トークン削減より引用要件が優先されます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-015","number":15,"form":3,"stem":"A Databricks compliance agent is preparing a quarterly financial audit after 65 conversation turns. Its context window is 92% full, and several audit findings remain unresolved. An engineer wants to reclaim space by clearing selected raw `tool_result` blocks without summarizing the conversation, while minimizing disruption to ongoing work.\n\nThe engineer identifies four outputs:\n\n- Ledger reconciliation, turn 7: Returned 8,800 tokens of transaction rows from a read-only general ledger. The agent calculated taxable and non-taxable aggregates and supplied them to tax classification at turn 10. Subsequent replies and tool calls do not quote individual transaction rows.\n\n- Tax classification, turn 10: Returned 5,200 tokens describing bucket assignments for 312 transaction lines. The agent extracted bucket totals and supplied them to compliance flagging at turn 13. Subsequent replies and tool calls do not quote individual bucket assignments.\n\n- Compliance flagging, turn 13: Returned 95 tokens containing three flag codes and brief explanations. The agent explicitly references these codes and explanations at turns 24, 38, and 61 while developing its current audit findings.\n\n- Vendor master lookup, turn 19: Returned 3,400 tokens of supplier records and tax IDs from a read-only vendor master database. The agent cites a vendor name and tax ID from this output at turns 41 and 57.\n\nWhich outputs should the engineer clear?","options":["Clear the ledger reconciliation and tax classification outputs at turns 7 and 10 because the information needed by subsequent work has been carried forward into aggregates and the compliance flag summary.","Retain all three outputs at turns 7, 10, and 13 because unresolved findings might require quotations of individual transaction rows or bucket assignments.","Clear the outputs at turns 7, 10, and 19 because removing the three largest blocks recovers the most context space.","Clear only the tax classification output at turn 10 because the ledger output starts the dependency chain and must remain available for re-deriving challenged conclusions."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"ツール結果削除は後段の依存で判断する","concept":"ce-f3-q015","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Clear the ledger reconciliation and tax classification outputs at turns 7 and 10 because the information needed by subsequent work has been carried forward into aggregates and the compliance flag summary.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirements are to recover space without conversation summarization and minimize loss of information still supporting open findings. Follow the demonstrated dependencies: the large ledger and classification outputs supplied aggregates downstream, while later work directly references the compliance flags and vendor details.\n\nA therefore selects the strongest clearing candidates. Size helps prioritize savings after dependency assessment; it does not establish that an output is disposable.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Supplied them to … turn 10” and “turn 13” — Technical signal: Relevant aggregates have moved into downstream inputs.\n\n- “Do not quote individual transaction rows” or “bucket assignments” — Technical signal: The trace provides no demonstrated continuing use of those granular details.\n\n- “Explicitly references these codes and explanations” — Technical signal: The compliance flag output remains active evidence.\n\n- “Cites a vendor name and tax ID” — Technical signal: Vendor information also remains relevant.\n\n- “Without summarizing the conversation” — Scope constraint: Select raw-output clearing rather than broad compaction.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe selected outputs contain bulky detail whose demonstrated downstream contribution is represented by retained aggregates and flags. Clearing their raw bodies targets:\n\n8,800 + 5,200 = 14,000 tokens of raw output, before accounting for any replacement markers or message overhead.\n\nRetain the compliance flags and vendor lookup because the trace shows direct use of their contents in ongoing work.\n\nBefore clearing, confirm that required aggregates remain available in retained messages or tool-call inputs. Absence of later quotations supports the selection but is not universal proof that no future task could need the underlying records.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Retaining everything protects against hypothetical future requests. However, the supplied trace shows aggregate-based downstream use and no later quotations of the granular ledger or classification details. This option sacrifices the available space recovery without establishing that those raw outputs remain necessary.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: It maximizes immediate payload removal, but includes vendor data that the agent has referenced during the current workflow. It introduces more dependency risk than A. Large size alone does not justify clearing an actively used result.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Keeping the ledger could support re-derivation if that were required. However, being the earliest output in a chain does not itself make it a continuing dependency. The stated trace supports clearing both upstream raw blocks while preserving their relevant downstream information.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is C: the vendor lookup is large and read-only, so clearing it appears attractive. Its explicit downstream use is the decisive distinction.\n\nIf it no longer contained unique information needed for the open findings, clearing it could become defensible after checking the retained context and recovery path.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nContext pruning should follow remaining information dependencies. Neither an output’s age, size, read-only origin, nor position at the start of a chain determines whether it is still needed. Clear redundant raw detail while preserving the facts and associations that current work continues to consume.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nAn implementation could remove the two large raw payloads from active context while retaining derived totals, relevant source references, and external execution logs. This would reduce repeated prompt input without deleting the underlying audit evidence. Any later request for line-level investigation would require retrieving that detail again.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nClear redundant payloads after checking what ongoing work still uses.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nTrace information forward before clearing tool results. Prioritize large outputs whose needed facts survive downstream, and protect outputs still directly supporting unresolved work.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: The objective covering evaluation of raw tool outputs as clearing candidates when an agent’s message history approaches context capacity.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents\n\nSupports: Clearing redundant tool results and retaining necessary information or lightweight references.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\n- Databricks — View traces in the Databricks MLflow UI\n\nSupports: Inspecting operation order, span inputs, outputs, and message flow to assess continuing dependencies.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces/ui-traces)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Clear the ledger reconciliation and tax classification outputs at turns 7 and 10 because the information needed by subsequent work has been carried forward into aggregates and the compliance flag summary.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirements are to recover space without conversation summarization and minimize loss of information still supporting open findings. Follow the demonstrated dependencies: the large ledger and classification outputs supplied aggregates downstream, while later work directly references the compliance flags and vendor details.\n\nA therefore selects the strongest clearing candidates. Size helps prioritize savings after dependency assessment; it does not establish that an output is disposable.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Supplied them to … turn 10” and “turn 13” — Technical signal: Relevant aggregates have moved into downstream inputs.\n\n- “Do not quote individual transaction rows” or “bucket assignments” — Technical signal: The trace provides no demonstrated continuing use of those granular details.\n\n- “Explicitly references these codes and explanations” — Technical signal: The compliance flag output remains active evidence.\n\n- “Cites a vendor name and tax ID” — Technical signal: Vendor information also remains relevant.\n\n- “Without summarizing the conversation” — Scope constraint: Select raw-output clearing rather than broad compaction.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe selected outputs contain bulky detail whose demonstrated downstream contribution is represented by retained aggregates and flags. Clearing their raw bodies targets:\n\n8,800 + 5,200 = 14,000 tokens of raw output, before accounting for any replacement markers or message overhead.\n\nRetain the compliance flags and vendor lookup because the trace shows direct use of their contents in ongoing work.\n\nBefore clearing, confirm that required aggregates remain available in retained messages or tool-call inputs. Absence of later quotations supports the selection but is not universal proof that no future task could need the underlying records.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Retaining everything protects against hypothetical future requests. However, the supplied trace shows aggregate-based downstream use and no later quotations of the granular ledger or classification details. This option sacrifices the available space recovery without establishing that those raw outputs remain necessary.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: It maximizes immediate payload removal, but includes vendor data that the agent has referenced during the current workflow. It introduces more dependency risk than A. Large size alone does not justify clearing an actively used result.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Keeping the ledger could support re-derivation if that were required. However, being the earliest output in a chain does not itself make it a continuing dependency. The stated trace supports clearing both upstream raw blocks while preserving their relevant downstream information."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces/ui-traces"],"source_hash":"68ca2430fc6d2c83cbda91bffb0f0c6c541a3d95cf4758c13bae1d4d4a8ecbc8","stem_ja":"Databricksのコンプライアンスエージェントが、65ターンの会話を経て四半期財務監査を作成しています。コンテキストウィンドウは92%使用され、未解決の監査指摘が複数あります。エンジニアは会話を要約せず、一部の生の`tool_result`ブロックを削除して、作業への影響を抑えながら空きを増やしたいと考えています。\n\n4つの出力を確認しました。\n\n- 総勘定元帳の照合、7ターン目：読み取り専用の総勘定元帳から取引行8,800トークンを取得。エージェントは課税・非課税の集計を計算し、10ターン目の税分類へ渡した。その後の応答やツール呼び出しでは個別取引行を引用していない。\n- 税分類、10ターン目：312取引行の区分割り当てを説明する5,200トークンを返した。区分別合計を抽出し、13ターン目のコンプライアンスフラグ付けへ渡した。その後は個別の区分割り当てを引用していない。\n- コンプライアンスフラグ付け、13ターン目：3つのフラグコードと短い説明からなる95トークンを返した。現在の監査指摘を組み立てる24・38・61ターン目に、それらを明示的に参照している。\n- 仕入先マスター照会、19ターン目：読み取り専用の仕入先マスターデータベースから、仕入先レコードと納税者番号3,400トークンを返した。41・57ターン目に、この出力の仕入先名と納税者番号を引用している。\n\n削除すべき出力はどれですか。","options_ja":["後続作業に必要な情報が集計とコンプライアンスフラグ要約へ引き継がれているため、7・10ターン目の元帳照合と税分類の出力を削除する。","未解決の指摘で個別取引行や区分割り当ての引用が必要になる可能性があるため、7・10・13ターン目の3出力をすべて保持する。","最も大きい3ブロックを削除すると最大の空きを得られるため、7・10・19ターン目の出力を削除する。","元帳出力は依存関係の起点であり、異議が出た結論を再計算するために保持する必要があるので、10ターン目の税分類出力だけを削除する。"],"explanation_ja":"削除の判断は大きさや古さではなく、後段の情報依存で行います。7・10ターン目の生出力は必要な集計へ引き継がれ、個別行は再参照されていません。一方、13ターン目のフラグと19ターン目の仕入先情報は、後の会話から直接参照されています。「要点が引き継がれ、原出力への参照もないもの」を削除対象にします。","distractors_ja":["必要な数値が後段の集計やフラグへ残り、生の行はその後使われていないため、作業の依存を保ったまま大きな出力を減らせます。","未解決というだけで全生出力の保持は必要ありません。設問は個別行への再参照がないことと、必要情報の引き継ぎを明示しています。","仕入先名と納税者番号は後段で直接使われています。サイズだけで19ターン目を削ると、現在の判断の根拠を失います。","依存関係の起点であるだけでは、原出力を常駐させる理由になりません。必要な集計が引き継がれている点を重視します。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-016","number":16,"form":3,"stem":"A hospital network is developing a compound AI system using a custom agent on Agent Bricks to answer clinicians’ questions about individual patients’ medication histories.\n\nMedication records reside in a Delta table that receives continuous updates for prescriptions, refills, and discontinuations. Answers must reflect the current patient record when a clinician submits a request. The system’s privacy requirements restrict retrieval to the patient identified in that request, rather than a search across the patient population. End-to-end response latency must remain below two seconds.\n\nWhich retrieval strategy should the engineer implement?","options":["Create a Databricks AI Search Delta Sync index that updates from the source table, then retrieve semantically during inference.","Implement just-in-time agentic retrieval through a tool that executes a parameterized, patient-scoped query against the Delta table during inference.","Generate embeddings for every patient record nightly and supplement them with just-in-time retrieval of records changed during the day.","Build a Databricks AI Search index over all patient records during pipeline construction and perform embedding-based semantic retrieval during inference."],"correct":1,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"最新の患者情報はスコープを強制した必要時クエリ","concept":"ce-f3-q016","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Implement just-in-time agentic retrieval through a tool that executes a parameterized, patient-scoped query against the Delta table during inference.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe hard constraints are freshness, patient-specific scope, and a two-second response budget. This is a lookup of mutable structured records, so the retrieval mechanism should query the authoritative table using the patient identifier.\n\nB avoids dependence on an asynchronously refreshed embedding index and provides an explicit patient predicate. It is the strongest architectural fit, although meeting the latency requirement still requires measurement and optimization.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Continuous updates” — Technical signal: Precomputed representations can lag behind the source.\n\n- “Current patient record” — Hard constraint: Historical or asynchronously synchronized results are insufficient.\n\n- “Restrict retrieval to the patient identified” — Hard constraint: Scope must be enforced before records enter model context.\n\n- “Below two seconds” — Hard constraint: Evaluate the complete query-and-generation path, not retrieval time alone.\n\n- “Medication histories” in a “Delta table” — Technical signal: Structured, identifier-based retrieval directly matches the information need.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe tool can bind the requested patient identifier to a fixed query and retrieve that patient’s medication records directly from the committed Delta table snapshot. This avoids an additional index-synchronization boundary.\n\nPatient authorization must be checked separately: parameterization does not establish permission to access the supplied patient identifier.\n\nTwo boundaries matter:\n\n- Freshness: Direct querying provides a source-table snapshot at query execution. If “when submitted” requires an exact request-time snapshot, the implementation must explicitly select the applicable table version.\n\n- Latency: Just-in-time retrieval does not inherently guarantee a response below two seconds. Query execution, orchestration, and model generation must collectively satisfy that budget.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Violates Requirement: Delta Sync supports updating an index from source changes, making it attractive for changing data. However, synchronization is not instantaneous. Databricks documents seconds of latency for Continuous mode; Triggered mode requires synchronization to be initiated. Automatic updating therefore does not establish source-current answers for every request. Patient filtering would not eliminate this freshness gap.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: A nightly baseline plus live changes could work with careful reconciliation. However, it must correctly incorporate updates and discontinuations while avoiding stale baseline information. That introduces unnecessary reconciliation complexity for a query that can directly retrieve the patient’s current structured records.\n\n- Option D — Violates Requirement: A build-time index provides a precomputed representation, but this option specifies no ongoing synchronization mechanism. It therefore offers no assurance that subsequent prescriptions, refills, or discontinuations appear in answers. It fits better when source data changes infrequently or the permitted freshness interval accommodates rebuilding.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is A: “automatically synchronized” can sound equivalent to “current at request time.” Index freshness and query-response latency are different properties. Fast retrieval can still return an outdated indexed record.\n\nThe HIPAA premise also needs qualification. HHS states that the minimum-necessary standard does not apply to disclosures to, or requests by, healthcare providers for treatment purposes. Patient-scoped retrieval remains an explicit requirement of this scenario; it should not be presented as an unconditional HIPAA rule for every clinician query.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nChoose retrieval according to the question and its consistency requirements. Semantic search finds meaningfully related content. A parameterized source query selects structured records using explicit predicates. For frequently changing facts about a known patient, direct source retrieval avoids the freshness dependency introduced by a separate index.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA practical tool could validate access to the selected patient, query the relevant medication fields, and pass only those results to the agent. Representative-load testing would then establish whether database access plus model generation meets the two-second requirement. This design makes the retrieval boundary explicit without treating the strategy itself as a performance guarantee.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nFor current facts about a known patient, query that patient’s authorized source records at inference time.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nFresh structured data plus an exact entity identifier points toward just-in-time, scoped source queries. Verify authorization, snapshot semantics, and end-to-end latency separately.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Create AI Search endpoints and indexes\n\nSupports: Continuous and Triggered synchronization behavior, including the latency of Continuous synchronization.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search)\n\n- Databricks — What are ACID guarantees on Databricks?\n\nSupports: Committed table versions, snapshot reads, and transaction boundaries underlying direct-source retrieval.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/acid)\n\n- HHS — Minimum Necessary Requirement\n\nSupports: The minimum-necessary standard and its exception for specified treatment-purpose requests and disclosures.\n\n[Official guidance](https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/guidance/minimum-necessary-requirement/index.html)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Violates Requirement: Delta Sync supports updating an index from source changes, making it attractive for changing data. However, synchronization is not instantaneous. Databricks documents seconds of latency for Continuous mode; Triggered mode requires synchronization to be initiated. Automatic updating therefore does not establish source-current answers for every request. Patient filtering would not eliminate this freshness gap.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Implement just-in-time agentic retrieval through a tool that executes a parameterized, patient-scoped query against the Delta table during inference.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe hard constraints are freshness, patient-specific scope, and a two-second response budget. This is a lookup of mutable structured records, so the retrieval mechanism should query the authoritative table using the patient identifier.\n\nB avoids dependence on an asynchronously refreshed embedding index and provides an explicit patient predicate. It is the strongest architectural fit, although meeting the latency requirement still requires measurement and optimization.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Continuous updates” — Technical signal: Precomputed representations can lag behind the source.\n\n- “Current patient record” — Hard constraint: Historical or asynchronously synchronized results are insufficient.\n\n- “Restrict retrieval to the patient identified” — Hard constraint: Scope must be enforced before records enter model context.\n\n- “Below two seconds” — Hard constraint: Evaluate the complete query-and-generation path, not retrieval time alone.\n\n- “Medication histories” in a “Delta table” — Technical signal: Structured, identifier-based retrieval directly matches the information need.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe tool can bind the requested patient identifier to a fixed query and retrieve that patient’s medication records directly from the committed Delta table snapshot. This avoids an additional index-synchronization boundary.\n\nPatient authorization must be checked separately: parameterization does not establish permission to access the supplied patient identifier.\n\nTwo boundaries matter:\n\n- Freshness: Direct querying provides a source-table snapshot at query execution. If “when submitted” requires an exact request-time snapshot, the implementation must explicitly select the applicable table version.\n\n- Latency: Just-in-time retrieval does not inherently guarantee a response below two seconds. Query execution, orchestration, and model generation must collectively satisfy that budget.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: A nightly baseline plus live changes could work with careful reconciliation. However, it must correctly incorporate updates and discontinuations while avoiding stale baseline information. That introduces unnecessary reconciliation complexity for a query that can directly retrieve the patient’s current structured records.","Option D — Violates Requirement: A build-time index provides a precomputed representation, but this option specifies no ongoing synchronization mechanism. It therefore offers no assurance that subsequent prescriptions, refills, or discontinuations appear in answers. It fits better when source data changes infrequently or the permitted freshness interval accommodates rebuilding."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/acid","https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/privacy/guidance/minimum-necessary-requirement/index.html"],"source_hash":"227d28504ae2f041cf15e4996a21fa706a98bcfdb7a0a5f13916bcf017a2e0c7","stem_ja":"病院ネットワークが、Agent Bricks上のカスタムエージェントを使い、各患者の投薬履歴について臨床医の質問に答える複合AIシステムを開発しています。\n\n投薬記録はDeltaテーブルにあり、処方、再処方、中止について継続的に更新されます。臨床医が要求を送信した時点の最新の患者記録を回答へ反映する必要があります。プライバシー要件により、患者集団全体を検索するのではなく、要求で指定された患者だけを取得対象としなければなりません。エンドツーエンドの応答時間は2秒未満に保つ必要があります。\n\n実装すべき取得戦略はどれですか。","options_ja":["ソーステーブルから更新されるDatabricks AI SearchのDelta Syncインデックスを作り、推論時に意味検索で取得する。","推論時に、患者を限定したパラメーター付きクエリをDeltaテーブルへ実行するツールを使い、必要時のエージェント検索を実装する。","すべての患者記録の埋め込みを夜間に生成し、その日の変更レコードを必要時に取得して補完する。","パイプライン構築時に全患者記録のDatabricks AI Searchインデックスを作り、推論時に埋め込みベースの意味検索を行う。"],"explanation_ja":"必要なのは似た患者を探すことではなく、指定患者の最新の投薬記録です。要求時に患者IDで絞ったクエリを実行すれば、索引更新の遅延を避け、不要な患者の取得も防げます。クエリはパラメーター化し、アクセス権や患者スコープも強制します。2秒未満は実環境での測定が必要であり、必要時取得という方式だけで保証される値ではありません。","distractors_ja":["同期インデックスにはソース変更が反映されるまでの遅れがあり得ます。特定患者の最新記録を正確に取得する用途で意味類似度を使う必要もありません。","指定患者に対する現在のデータを要求時に取得できます。患者スコープの強制と構造化された正確な参照が、この条件に最も合います。","夜間の埋め込みは古くなり、当日変更との統合も複雑です。直接の患者限定クエリで満たせる要件に不要な処理を追加します。","全患者を対象とした静的な意味検索では、鮮度と指定患者への限定を直接満たせません。近さで順位付けするより正確なID参照が適切です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-017","number":17,"form":3,"stem":"A SaaS retail analytics platform provisions a separate Unity Catalog catalog, `retail_<customer>`, for each tenant. Schemas group tables by business domain, so ingestion-fed and SaaS-generated tables coexist:\n\n- `sales`: POS transactions and returns are written throughout the day by customer source systems. A nightly Lakeflow Jobs pipeline derives `daily_sales_by_store`, `hourly_revenue_by_category`, and `top_skus_weekly` from those tables.\n\n- `inventory`: Ingestion continuously updates stock levels and inbound shipments. SaaS ML pipelines produce `demand_forecast_7d` and `reorder_suggestions`.\n\n- `customer`: Customer master and contact tables are populated entirely through ingestion.\n\nThe platform deploys one agent per tenant through Model Serving, with a separate service principal for each agent.\n\nBefore registering an endpoint, governance must enforce two restrictions: the agent can retrieve data only from its tenant’s catalog, and within that catalog it can read only ingestion-fed tables. SaaS-generated summaries, forecasts, and other derived tables must remain inaccessible.\n\nWhich approach should the engineer implement?","options":["Replace tenant-specific identities with one shared service principal. Grant it `USE CATALOG` on every tenant catalog and table-level `SELECT` on ingestion-fed tables. Have retrieval code insert the requesting tenant’s catalog into every query.","Grant each tenant’s principal `USE CATALOG` on its catalog and `USE SCHEMA` plus schema-level `SELECT` on `sales`, `inventory`, and `customer`. Tag ingestion-fed tables `source=ingest` and make retrieval code query only tagged tables.","Grant each tenant’s principal `USE CATALOG` only on its catalog and `USE SCHEMA` plus schema-level `SELECT` on `sales`, `inventory`, and `customer`, relying on catalog isolation to restrict retrieval.","Grant each tenant’s principal `USE CATALOG` only on its catalog, `USE SCHEMA` on its domain schemas, and table-level `SELECT` only on ingestion-fed tables. Leave SaaS-generated summaries, forecasts, and other derived tables ungranted."],"correct":3,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"テナント分離とテーブル単位の最小権限","concept":"ce-f3-q017","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Grant each tenant’s principal `USE CATALOG` only on its catalog, `USE SCHEMA` on its domain schemas, and table-level `SELECT` only on ingestion-fed tables. Leave SaaS-generated summaries, forecasts, and other derived tables ungranted.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nBoth restrictions must hold at the authorization layer. Tenant-specific catalog access addresses cross-tenant retrieval. Because each domain schema mixes permitted and prohibited tables, read access must then be restricted at table level.\n\nD enforces both boundaries. A substitutes application routing for tenant-specific permissions; B and C grant read access too broadly within each tenant.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Separate … catalog … for each tenant” — Technical signal: Catalog boundaries align with tenant isolation.\n\n- “Ingestion-fed and SaaS-generated tables coexist” — Technical signal: Schema membership cannot distinguish permitted data origins.\n\n- “Separate service principal” — Technical signal: Each agent can receive independently scoped privileges.\n\n- “Must remain inaccessible” — Hard constraint: Retrieval preferences or application filters are insufficient.\n\n- “Before registering an endpoint” — Sequence instruction: Establish the restricted access configuration before exposing the agent.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nReading a table requires `SELECT` together with `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA` on its parents. Usage privileges alone do not grant permission to read every contained table.\n\nD therefore supplies the required parent privileges while selectively granting data access. Its table-level grants distinguish ingestion-fed tables from derived tables even when both occupy the same schema.\n\nCheck effective permissions, including group membership and inherited grants. Leaving a table directly ungranted does not block access supplied by a broader schema or catalog grant. The retrieval tool must also execute using the intended tenant-scoped identity.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Violates Requirement: A shared principal has a cross-tenant permission scope. Even if its required schema privileges were supplied, inserting a catalog name into queries would not restrict that identity’s underlying authorization to one tenant. Operational convenience does not satisfy the required isolation boundary.\n\n- Option B — Violates Requirement: Schema-level `SELECT` extends to current and future tables and views in those schemas, including SaaS-generated tables. The tag filter influences application behavior but does not remove those permissions. An explicitly enforced tag-based policy would be a different design from the retrieval-code filter described here.\n\n- Option C — Violates Requirement: It addresses the tenant boundary but fails the ingestion-only restriction. Schema-level `SELECT` permits reading the summaries and forecasts colocated with ingestion tables. It would fit if every table in those schemas were permitted.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is B: tagging tables appears to add governance precision. The distinction is whether the restriction is enforced by authorization or merely followed by retrieval code.\n\nIf enforced access policies were explicitly configured around governed tags, tags could participate in authorization. Applying a label and filtering queries is not that policy.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nMatch privilege granularity to the actual access boundary. A catalog may separate tenants while a schema separates business domains; neither necessarily separates data origins. When allowed and prohibited tables share a schema, table-level grants provide the required distinction.\n\nCurrent Databricks guidance recommends Agent Bricks CLI for new agents. The Model Serving context is retained here because changing deployment platforms is unnecessary to resolve the grant-design question.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nTenant provisioning could maintain an allowlist of ingestion-fed tables and apply grants to that tenant’s runtime principal. Before endpoint registration, verify that permitted reads succeed while same-tenant derived-table reads and cross-tenant reads fail. New ingestion tables would require deliberate authorization, adding maintenance work while avoiding automatic access to newly created SaaS outputs.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nCombine tenant-scoped catalog access with table-scoped permission to read approved data.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nMixed-origin schemas make schema-level `SELECT` too broad for ingestion-only access. Use separate tenant identities, necessary parent usage privileges, and explicit table grants, then verify effective runtime permissions.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Unity Catalog privileges reference\n\nSupports: Table-read prerequisites and inheritance of schema-level `SELECT` to current and future tables and views.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/privileges-reference)\n\n- Databricks — Unity Catalog permissions model concepts\n\nSupports: Container privileges, inherited authorization, and the distinction between explicit grants and policy-based enforcement.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/permissions-concepts)\n\n- Databricks — Authentication for agents (Model Serving)\n\nSupports: Runtime resource-access identities and the current deployment recommendation for new agents.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/model-serving/agent-authentication-model-serving)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Violates Requirement: A shared principal has a cross-tenant permission scope. Even if its required schema privileges were supplied, inserting a catalog name into queries would not restrict that identity’s underlying authorization to one tenant. Operational convenience does not satisfy the required isolation boundary.","Option B — Violates Requirement: Schema-level `SELECT` extends to current and future tables and views in those schemas, including SaaS-generated tables. The tag filter influences application behavior but does not remove those permissions. An explicitly enforced tag-based policy would be a different design from the retrieval-code filter described here.","Option C — Violates Requirement: It addresses the tenant boundary but fails the ingestion-only restriction. Schema-level `SELECT` permits reading the summaries and forecasts colocated with ingestion tables. It would fit if every table in those schemas were permitted.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Grant each tenant’s principal `USE CATALOG` only on its catalog, `USE SCHEMA` on its domain schemas, and table-level `SELECT` only on ingestion-fed tables. Leave SaaS-generated summaries, forecasts, and other derived tables ungranted.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nBoth restrictions must hold at the authorization layer. Tenant-specific catalog access addresses cross-tenant retrieval. Because each domain schema mixes permitted and prohibited tables, read access must then be restricted at table level.\n\nD enforces both boundaries. A substitutes application routing for tenant-specific permissions; B and C grant read access too broadly within each tenant.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Separate … catalog … for each tenant” — Technical signal: Catalog boundaries align with tenant isolation.\n\n- “Ingestion-fed and SaaS-generated tables coexist” — Technical signal: Schema membership cannot distinguish permitted data origins.\n\n- “Separate service principal” — Technical signal: Each agent can receive independently scoped privileges.\n\n- “Must remain inaccessible” — Hard constraint: Retrieval preferences or application filters are insufficient.\n\n- “Before registering an endpoint” — Sequence instruction: Establish the restricted access configuration before exposing the agent.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nReading a table requires `SELECT` together with `USE CATALOG` and `USE SCHEMA` on its parents. Usage privileges alone do not grant permission to read every contained table.\n\nD therefore supplies the required parent privileges while selectively granting data access. Its table-level grants distinguish ingestion-fed tables from derived tables even when both occupy the same schema.\n\nCheck effective permissions, including group membership and inherited grants. Leaving a table directly ungranted does not block access supplied by a broader schema or catalog grant. The retrieval tool must also execute using the intended tenant-scoped identity."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/privileges-reference","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control/permissions-concepts","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/model-serving/agent-authentication-model-serving"],"source_hash":"3f67ce4ce1635a2cdbe7c014da0707609c1557c705368edc965f58767c695c84","stem_ja":"SaaS型の小売分析プラットフォームが、テナントごとに別のUnity Catalogカタログ`retail_<customer>`を作成します。スキーマは業務分野別で、取り込みにより更新するテーブルとSaaSが生成するテーブルが混在しています。\n\n- `sales`：顧客ソースシステムがPOS取引と返品を日中書き込む。夜間のLakeflow Jobsパイプラインが、それらから`daily_sales_by_store`、`hourly_revenue_by_category`、`top_skus_weekly`を作る。\n- `inventory`：取り込み処理が在庫水準と入荷を継続更新する。SaaSのMLパイプラインが`demand_forecast_7d`と`reorder_suggestions`を作る。\n- `customer`：顧客マスターと連絡先テーブルは、すべて取り込み処理によって作成・更新する。\n\nプラットフォームはModel Servingを通じてテナントごとに1つのエージェントをデプロイし、各エージェントに別々のサービスプリンシパルを使用します。\n\nエンドポイントを登録する前に、ガバナンスで2つの制約を強制しなければなりません。エージェントが取得できるのは自身のテナントのカタログだけであり、その中でも取り込み由来のテーブルしか読み取れません。SaaSが生成する集計、予測、その他の派生テーブルはアクセス不可とします。\n\n実装すべき方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["テナントごとのIDを1つの共有サービスプリンシパルへ置き換える。全テナントカタログの`USE CATALOG`と、取り込み由来テーブルのテーブル単位`SELECT`を付与し、検索コードで要求元テナントのカタログをすべてのクエリへ挿入する。","各テナントのプリンシパルに自身のカタログの`USE CATALOG`と、`sales`・`inventory`・`customer`の`USE SCHEMA`およびスキーマ単位`SELECT`を付与する。取り込み由来テーブルに`source=ingest`タグを付け、検索コードがタグ付きテーブルだけを問い合わせるようにする。","各テナントのプリンシパルに、自身のカタログだけの`USE CATALOG`と、`sales`・`inventory`・`customer`の`USE SCHEMA`およびスキーマ単位`SELECT`を付与し、カタログの分離によって取得を制限する。","各テナントのプリンシパルに、自身のカタログだけの`USE CATALOG`、業務スキーマの`USE SCHEMA`、取り込み由来テーブルだけのテーブル単位`SELECT`を付与する。SaaS生成の集計、予測、その他の派生テーブルには権限を与えない。"],"explanation_ja":"テナント境界とテーブル種別の両方を権限で強制する必要があります。別々のIDを保ち、自身のカタログ・スキーマを利用できる権限と、許可した元テーブルだけのSELECTを与えます。スキーマに広くSELECTを付けると、同居する派生テーブルまで読めます。タグやコード上の絞り込みは、アクセス権による禁止の代わりにはなりません。","distractors_ja":["共有IDは他テナントのカタログにも権限を持ち、テナント分離をコードだけに依存させます。要求されたガバナンス境界を権限で強制できません。","スキーマ単位のSELECTで派生テーブルも読めてしまいます。タグを検索条件に使っても、付与済みの読み取り権限を取り消す効果はありません。","自身のカタログへの限定は満たしますが、その中の派生テーブルまで読み取れます。2つ目の制約が満たされません。","カタログ境界とテーブル単位の許可リストを重ね、両方の制約を権限で強制します。派生テーブルは未付与のままにします。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-018","number":18,"form":3,"stem":"A retail company is developing a custom agent on Agent Bricks with two registered tools:\n\n- A SQL tool that queries a live inventory database.\n\n- A vector-search tool that retrieves product descriptions, specifications, and categories from a static catalog index.\n\nBoth tools are correctly configured and return valid results when invoked.\n\nFor questions about whether a product is currently available, the agent repeatedly selects the catalog-search tool and generates a plausible stock answer without querying inventory. The catalog tool is not designed to provide current stock information.\n\nWhich context engineering issue best explains this behavior?","options":["The catalog index reflects a periodic snapshot and may therefore contain outdated product metadata.","The tool descriptions and routing instructions do not clearly distinguish live inventory questions from static product-catalog searches.","The inventory tool’s description omits the exact expressions “stock,” “in stock,” and “availability,” despite supporting real-time inventory queries.","The agent’s prompt omits a requirement to verify the authoritative source before answering, allowing plausible retrieved material to be treated as sufficient."],"correct":1,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"ライブ在庫と静的カタログのツール境界","concept":"ce-f3-q018","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. The tool descriptions and routing instructions do not clearly distinguish live inventory questions from static product-catalog searches.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question requires current inventory information. The appropriate capability exists and works, but the agent consistently chooses a tool serving another purpose. This points to insufficiently distinct selection guidance rather than a tool-execution defect.\n\nB captures that broader context-design issue. C and D propose specific wording omissions that could contribute, but the scenario does not establish either omission.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Both tools … return valid results” — Technical signal: Operational correctness does not explain the selection failure.\n\n- “Currently available” — Technical signal: The request requires live inventory information.\n\n- “Repeatedly selects the catalog-search tool” — Technical signal: The failure occurs when mapping user intent to tool capability.\n\n- “Not designed to provide current stock information” — Exclusion: Catalog results cannot establish live availability merely because they describe the product.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nTool descriptions and routing instructions provide context for deciding which capability matches a request. They should make the distinction explicit: inventory serves current stock questions, while catalog search serves descriptive product questions.\n\nWhen that distinction is unclear, an agent can select a semantically related tool whose output is valid but insufficient for the requested fact.\n\nB is the best-supported diagnosis. Inspect the actual descriptions, instructions, and selection traces to confirm it; the symptoms alone do not identify an exact missing phrase.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Different Use Case: Stale metadata can explain outdated descriptive answers. However, even freshly indexed product descriptions would not become an authoritative source for live stock. Index freshness does not explain why the available inventory capability is bypassed.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Omitting familiar availability terminology could weaken a description. Nevertheless, the scenario does not reveal its wording, and those exact expressions are not mandatory for successful semantic routing. C is a possible specific contributor to B, not an established cause.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: An authoritative-source instruction could improve behavior and prevent unsupported availability answers. However, the prompt is not shown, so its absence cannot be assumed. B more directly identifies the unclear boundary between the two tool purposes.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is C: its specific missing keywords make it sound diagnostic. Specificity is not evidence. The issue is whether the agent understands the tool’s purpose, not whether a fixed vocabulary appears.\n\nIf inspection confirmed that the inventory description failed to communicate its availability capability, C could identify the particular description defect.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nTool execution and tool selection are separate quality dimensions. A tool can return correct data for its own purpose while being inappropriate for the user’s question. Effective context makes capabilities, limitations, and intended selection conditions clear.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nThe engineer could explicitly describe inventory as the source for current stock and catalog search as the source for static product attributes. Evaluating varied availability questions alongside descriptive product questions would test whether routing improves without making every product query invoke inventory unnecessarily.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nA functioning tool still needs a clear purpose and selection boundary.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen working tools are consistently selected for the wrong intent, inspect descriptions and routing guidance. Treat missing keywords or prompt clauses as hypotheses until their actual wording is examined.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: Objectives covering confusing tool descriptions and selecting tools according to functional fit and task requirements.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Anthropic — Writing effective tools for agents — with agents\n\nSupports: Distinct tool purposes, ambiguity in tool selection, and descriptions that steer effective tool use.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/writing-tools-for-agents)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Different Use Case: Stale metadata can explain outdated descriptive answers. However, even freshly indexed product descriptions would not become an authoritative source for live stock. Index freshness does not explain why the available inventory capability is bypassed.","Correct Answer\n\nB. The tool descriptions and routing instructions do not clearly distinguish live inventory questions from static product-catalog searches.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question requires current inventory information. The appropriate capability exists and works, but the agent consistently chooses a tool serving another purpose. This points to insufficiently distinct selection guidance rather than a tool-execution defect.\n\nB captures that broader context-design issue. C and D propose specific wording omissions that could contribute, but the scenario does not establish either omission.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Both tools … return valid results” — Technical signal: Operational correctness does not explain the selection failure.\n\n- “Currently available” — Technical signal: The request requires live inventory information.\n\n- “Repeatedly selects the catalog-search tool” — Technical signal: The failure occurs when mapping user intent to tool capability.\n\n- “Not designed to provide current stock information” — Exclusion: Catalog results cannot establish live availability merely because they describe the product.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nTool descriptions and routing instructions provide context for deciding which capability matches a request. They should make the distinction explicit: inventory serves current stock questions, while catalog search serves descriptive product questions.\n\nWhen that distinction is unclear, an agent can select a semantically related tool whose output is valid but insufficient for the requested fact.\n\nB is the best-supported diagnosis. Inspect the actual descriptions, instructions, and selection traces to confirm it; the symptoms alone do not identify an exact missing phrase.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Omitting familiar availability terminology could weaken a description. Nevertheless, the scenario does not reveal its wording, and those exact expressions are not mandatory for successful semantic routing. C is a possible specific contributor to B, not an established cause.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: An authoritative-source instruction could improve behavior and prevent unsupported availability answers. However, the prompt is not shown, so its absence cannot be assumed. B more directly identifies the unclear boundary between the two tool purposes."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://www.anthropic.com/research/writing-tools-for-agents"],"source_hash":"3d702e4509c23710b95580b66a6fff0fc57c30f80e5b8153ae5520e96745b8e7","stem_ja":"小売企業が、Agent Bricks上で2つの登録済みツールを使うカスタムエージェントを開発しています。\n\n- 稼働中の在庫データベースを問い合わせるSQLツール。\n- 静的なカタログインデックスから商品説明、仕様、分類を取得するベクトル検索ツール。\n\nどちらも正しく設定されており、呼び出すと有効な結果を返します。\n\n商品の現在の在庫の有無を尋ねると、エージェントは繰り返しカタログ検索ツールを選び、在庫を照会せずにもっともらしい回答を作ります。カタログツールは現在の在庫情報を提供するためのものではありません。\n\nこの動作を最もよく説明するコンテキストエンジニアリング上の問題はどれですか。","options_ja":["カタログインデックスが定期的なスナップショットを反映しており、古い商品メタデータを含む可能性がある。","ツールの説明とルーティングの指示が、現在の在庫照会と静的な商品カタログ検索を明確に区別していない。","リアルタイムの在庫照会には対応しているが、在庫ツールの説明に「stock」「in stock」「availability」という正確な表現がない。","回答前に正本のソースを確認するという要件がプロンプトにないため、もっともらしい検索資料だけで十分だと扱ってしまう。"],"explanation_ja":"両ツールは動作しているのに、用途に合わない方が選ばれています。現在の在庫はライブSQL、商品仕様は静的な検索という意味上の境界を、ツール説明とルーティングに明示するのが重要です。特定の単語の欠落だけを原因と断定する必要はありません。「ツールが壊れた」のか「正しいツールを選べない」のかを切り分けます。","distractors_ja":["カタログが古いかどうか以前に、そもそも現在の在庫を提供する用途ではありません。選択すべきソースの誤りが直接の問題です。","ツールごとの担当範囲が曖昧だと、関連しそうな商品情報を在庫の根拠として使います。用途と正本の境界を明確にする診断です。","語句の補強は役立ちますが、完全一致のキーワードが必要とは限りません。より広い機能上の区別が不明確なことが根本です。","正本の確認指示は補助策になりますが、どのツールがどの問いに対応するかを示す必要があります。設問では用途の区別がより直接的です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-019","number":19,"form":3,"stem":"A Databricks customer support agent has two registered MCP tools with these descriptions:\n\n- One finds support tickets and active case records associated with a customer.\n\n- The other retrieves a customer’s issue history and details of unresolved cases using a customer ID.\n\nRepeated requests about outstanding customer issues cause the agent to alternate unpredictably between the tools. Requests about previously resolved issues also produce inconsistent tool choices. Tool-call logs confirm that the agent cannot reliably determine which tool applies.\n\nWhich overlap in the descriptions most directly explains this ambiguity?","options":["Neither description identifies its underlying system or data source, preventing the agent from distinguishing the backend databases during retrieval planning.","Both descriptions use semantically similar retrieval verbs, making their operations appear interchangeable to the agent.","Both descriptions present overlapping responsibilities: active or unresolved cases alongside broader ticket or issue histories, without establishing a clear functional boundary.","Both descriptions refer to a customer identifier as the lookup input, making their parameter signatures indistinguishable during tool selection."],"correct":2,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"MCPツール説明の機能的な重複","concept":"ce-f3-q019","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Both descriptions present overlapping responsibilities: active or unresolved cases alongside broader ticket or issue histories, without establishing a clear functional boundary.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question asks which described overlap explains unreliable selection. Compare the tools’ advertised responsibilities against both user intents: outstanding issues and resolved issue history.\n\nBoth descriptions appear applicable to current cases and broader customer issue records. Neither establishes a selection rule separating those responsibilities. C identifies this functional ambiguity; the other options focus on backend labels, verb choice, or inputs.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Active case records” and “unresolved cases” — Technical signal: Both descriptions advertise access to outstanding issues.\n\n- “Support tickets” and “issue history” — Technical signal: Both suggest broader customer support records without explicit status exclusions.\n\n- Inconsistency for both outstanding and resolved issues — Failure signal: The descriptions fail to distinguish the two request categories.\n\n- Tool-call logs confirm unreliable selection — Diagnostic evidence: The observed problem concerns choosing a tool.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA useful description explains when a tool applies and how its role differs from nearby alternatives. Here, the shared subject matter and case-status coverage leave both tools looking suitable.\n\nAnthropic’s tool-design guidance explicitly warns that overlapping functions or vague purposes can confuse tool selection and recommends giving each tool a distinct purpose.\n\nThe evidence establishes overlapping descriptions; it does not prove that the underlying implementations are identical.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: Backend names can help when source selection matters, but their omission does not inherently make tools ambiguous. Two tools querying the same database can still have clearly differentiated responsibilities.\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: Similar retrieval verbs are common across distinct tools. Replacing one verb would leave the overlapping case coverage unresolved.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Multiple tools can legitimately accept the same customer ID while serving different purposes. Furthermore, the descriptions alone do not establish identical parameter schemas.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distraction is the similar wording of the retrieval verbs. The decisive issue is what each tool claims to retrieve.\n\nIf the tools had clearly distinct responsibilities but queried different authoritative systems, missing source information could become important. That distinction is not established here.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nTool descriptions communicate purpose; input schemas specify accepted arguments. Sharing an input does not imply sharing a purpose. MCP represents descriptions and input schemas as separate tool fields. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nIf the implementations genuinely separate case states, describe one as retrieving currently unresolved cases and the other as retrieving resolved case history, with explicit exclusions. Then evaluate repeated requests for each intent.\n\nDescriptions must match actual behavior. If both tools provide equivalent results, consolidating them may be more maintainable than inventing an artificial distinction.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nDistinguish tools by their responsibilities and boundaries, not by their retrieval verbs.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen two descriptions appear to cover the same records and user intents, look for functional overlap. Clear scope and selection conditions address the ambiguity; shared identifiers and similar verbs are not sufficient explanations.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Anthropic — Writing effective tools for agents\n\nSupports: Distinct tool purposes and the selection problems caused by overlapping functionality.\n\n[Official engineering guidance](https://www.anthropic.com/research/writing-tools-for-agents)\n\n- Model Context Protocol — Tools\n\nSupports: The distinction between a tool’s functional description and its input schema.\n\n[Official protocol specification](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools)\n\n- Databricks — Certified Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: The objective covering ambiguous tool selection caused by overlapping MCP tool descriptions.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: Backend names can help when source selection matters, but their omission does not inherently make tools ambiguous. Two tools querying the same database can still have clearly differentiated responsibilities.","Option B — Incorrect: Similar retrieval verbs are common across distinct tools. Replacing one verb would leave the overlapping case coverage unresolved.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Both descriptions present overlapping responsibilities: active or unresolved cases alongside broader ticket or issue histories, without establishing a clear functional boundary.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe question asks which described overlap explains unreliable selection. Compare the tools’ advertised responsibilities against both user intents: outstanding issues and resolved issue history.\n\nBoth descriptions appear applicable to current cases and broader customer issue records. Neither establishes a selection rule separating those responsibilities. C identifies this functional ambiguity; the other options focus on backend labels, verb choice, or inputs.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Active case records” and “unresolved cases” — Technical signal: Both descriptions advertise access to outstanding issues.\n\n- “Support tickets” and “issue history” — Technical signal: Both suggest broader customer support records without explicit status exclusions.\n\n- Inconsistency for both outstanding and resolved issues — Failure signal: The descriptions fail to distinguish the two request categories.\n\n- Tool-call logs confirm unreliable selection — Diagnostic evidence: The observed problem concerns choosing a tool.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA useful description explains when a tool applies and how its role differs from nearby alternatives. Here, the shared subject matter and case-status coverage leave both tools looking suitable.\n\nAnthropic’s tool-design guidance explicitly warns that overlapping functions or vague purposes can confuse tool selection and recommends giving each tool a distinct purpose.\n\nThe evidence establishes overlapping descriptions; it does not prove that the underlying implementations are identical.","Option D — Incorrect: Multiple tools can legitimately accept the same customer ID while serving different purposes. Furthermore, the descriptions alone do not establish identical parameter schemas."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/writing-tools-for-agents","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools","https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf"],"source_hash":"1fec381fbd753c40914b071837683704ddf52ca140c1ce5615904706ed38b326","stem_ja":"Databricksのカスタマーサポートエージェントに、次の説明を持つ2つのMCPツールが登録されています。\n\n- 一方は、顧客に関連するサポートチケットと対応中の案件記録を検索する。\n- もう一方は、顧客IDを使って顧客の問題履歴と未解決案件の詳細を取得する。\n\n顧客の未処理の問題を繰り返し問い合わせると、エージェントは予測できない形で両ツールを使い分けます。以前解決した問題を尋ねても、ツール選択が安定しません。呼び出しログからも、どちらが該当するかを信頼して判断できていないと分かりました。\n\nこの曖昧さを最も直接的に説明する、ツール説明の重複はどれですか。","options_ja":["どちらの説明にも基盤システムやデータソースが書かれておらず、検索計画時にバックエンドのデータベースを区別できない。","どちらも意味の似た取得動詞を使っており、操作が互換であるように見える。","両方が対応中・未解決の案件と、より広いチケット・問題履歴を扱うと説明しており、機能上の境界が明示されていない。","どちらも顧客識別子を検索入力として示しており、ツール選択時にパラメータシグネチャを区別できない。"],"explanation_ja":"曖昧なのは単語や引数の見た目ではなく、どちらが未解決案件を担当し、どちらが履歴を担当するのかという責任範囲です。機能が重なる説明では、顧客IDが同じでも選択根拠が定まりません。対象案件の状態や使う場面、返す内容を明確に分けます。「名前を変えるより、担当範囲を分ける」が要点です。","distractors_ja":["バックエンド名は補助情報ですが、利用者の要求に対する機能上の違いが説明されなければ、システム名だけでは選択できません。","検索・取得のような動詞が似ていても、対象と役割が明確なら区別できます。今回の問題は責任範囲そのものが重なることです。","対応中と未解決、チケットと履歴が重なり、どちらを選ぶべきか決める境界がありません。動作に直結する重複です。","同じ顧客IDを使う複数ツールは自然です。入力が同じでも、機能と返却対象を明確にすれば選び分けられます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-020","number":20,"form":3,"stem":"A Databricks agent manages a pharmaceutical supply chain compliance workflow lasting several weeks and covering dozens of distribution centers. It decomposes the work into a flat list of atomic tasks, dispatching and tracking each task independently.\n\nSome tasks form tightly coupled groups. For example, the inspection records for all facilities in a region must be completed before the regional compliance certificate can be issued. The agent repeatedly mishandles these group dependencies: it loses intermediate group status, issues certificates while facility data remains incomplete, and struggles to recover when individual inspection tasks fail.\n\nWhich two changes should the engineer implement to address this failure pattern? (Choose two.)","options":["Run all facility inspections and certificate-generation tasks concurrently, using eventual consistency to combine partial results into the final compliance record.","Restart the entire flat task list whenever any facility inspection fails, discarding partial execution state between attempts.","Introduce hierarchical decomposition, placing related facility inspections beneath a parent node that tracks group completion before dependent work proceeds.","Enforce dependency gates at group boundaries so certificate generation begins only after every required facility inspection has a confirmed completed state.","Replace the workflow with a fixed sequential chain that processes every facility inspection individually before issuing any certificate, removing all concurrency."],"correct":2,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"階層的なタスク分解とグループ境界のゲート","concept":"ce-f3-q020","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Introduce hierarchical decomposition, placing related facility inspections beneath a parent node that tracks group completion before dependent work proceeds.\n\nD. Enforce dependency gates at group boundaries so certificate generation begins only after every required facility inspection has a confirmed completed state.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe essential requirement is that certificate generation respects the completion of its inspection group. Two defects prevent that: the agent lacks reliable aggregate group state, and downstream dispatch does not enforce the group’s prerequisites.\n\nC provides a structure for tracking related tasks together. D enforces the transition from inspection work to certificate generation. Together, they address both lost group state and premature dispatch while providing a scope for handling individual failures.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “A flat list of atomic tasks” — Technical signal: Individual task tracking does not currently represent the required group relationships.\n\n- “Tightly coupled groups” — Technical signal: Related tasks need an aggregate completion state.\n\n- “Must be completed before” — Hard constraint: Certificate generation has an explicit prerequisite.\n\n- “Loses intermediate group status” — Technical signal: The workflow needs persistent, inspectable group progress.\n\n- “Individual inspection tasks fail” — Technical signal: Recovery should identify affected work without treating every task as one undifferentiated list.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n- C creates an explicit coordination scope. A parent node can track required inspections and their pending, completed, or failed states. This makes an incomplete group visible and provides a place to coordinate recovery of its failed children.\n\n- D enforces readiness. The certificate task remains blocked until the required inspection results satisfy the group’s completion condition. A failed task must not count as complete merely because its execution terminated.\n\nThe selections work together: C represents group progress, and D uses that progress to control dispatch. For graceful recovery across interruptions, the implementation must also persist relevant state and configure retry or repair behavior; hierarchy alone does not automatically provide recovery.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Violates Requirement: Parallel execution is useful for independent inspections, but certificate generation depends on their completion. Launching certificates simultaneously bypasses that prerequisite. Merging results later cannot enforce a condition that should have been checked before issuance.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: A full restart may eventually complete after a transient failure. However, discarding partial state repeats successful work and leaves the missing group structure and dependency enforcement unresolved. It is poorly matched to localized failures in a long-running workflow.\n\n- Option E — Technically Possible but Not Best: A strictly enforced sequence could prevent some premature execution. However, serializing every facility does not introduce group-level status or targeted recovery. It also makes unrelated inspection work wait unnecessarily. It fits better when every task genuinely depends on the preceding task.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is E: removing concurrency can conceal coordination defects. The decisive requirement is correct dependency enforcement, which permits independent inspections to proceed concurrently.\n\nIf the entire workload were genuinely one dependency chain, sequential execution would be appropriate. Here, the important boundaries are groups of related inspections and their dependent certificates.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nHierarchical decomposition represents which tasks belong together; dependency gates determine when downstream work becomes eligible. These are complementary responsibilities.\n\nA flat task graph can also be correct when it explicitly models dependencies and aggregate state. The failure here is that the existing flat strategy does not reliably preserve or enforce those relationships.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nAn implementation could maintain one parent workflow per region, with child tasks for required facility inspections. If one inspection fails, that region remains incomplete while eligible work elsewhere continues. Persisted child results support selective repair, reducing repeated processing while keeping the certificate gate closed until the required results are available.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nTrack related work as a group, and release dependent work only when the group is ready.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nLost group progress points to hierarchical coordination; premature downstream execution points to explicit dependency gates. Select both when the workflow must track clustered work and enforce its completion boundary.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- LangChain — Subgraphs\n\nSupports: Representing a grouped workflow as a node within a parent graph, explicit parent–child communication, and configurable persistence.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/use-subgraphs)\n\n- Databricks — Configure task dependencies\n\nSupports: Enforcing upstream prerequisites while allowing eligible tasks to execute in parallel; distinguishing successful prerequisites from tasks that merely finished.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-if)\n\n- Databricks — Troubleshoot and repair job failures\n\nSupports: Selective recovery of unsuccessful tasks and affected dependent work instead of restarting all successful processing.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/repair-job-failures)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Violates Requirement: Parallel execution is useful for independent inspections, but certificate generation depends on their completion. Launching certificates simultaneously bypasses that prerequisite. Merging results later cannot enforce a condition that should have been checked before issuance.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: A full restart may eventually complete after a transient failure. However, discarding partial state repeats successful work and leaves the missing group structure and dependency enforcement unresolved. It is poorly matched to localized failures in a long-running workflow.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Introduce hierarchical decomposition, placing related facility inspections beneath a parent node that tracks group completion before dependent work proceeds.\n\nD. Enforce dependency gates at group boundaries so certificate generation begins only after every required facility inspection has a confirmed completed state.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe essential requirement is that certificate generation respects the completion of its inspection group. Two defects prevent that: the agent lacks reliable aggregate group state, and downstream dispatch does not enforce the group’s prerequisites.\n\nC provides a structure for tracking related tasks together. D enforces the transition from inspection work to certificate generation. Together, they address both lost group state and premature dispatch while providing a scope for handling individual failures.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “A flat list of atomic tasks” — Technical signal: Individual task tracking does not currently represent the required group relationships.\n\n- “Tightly coupled groups” — Technical signal: Related tasks need an aggregate completion state.\n\n- “Must be completed before” — Hard constraint: Certificate generation has an explicit prerequisite.\n\n- “Loses intermediate group status” — Technical signal: The workflow needs persistent, inspectable group progress.\n\n- “Individual inspection tasks fail” — Technical signal: Recovery should identify affected work without treating every task as one undifferentiated list.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n- C creates an explicit coordination scope. A parent node can track required inspections and their pending, completed, or failed states. This makes an incomplete group visible and provides a place to coordinate recovery of its failed children.\n\n- D enforces readiness. The certificate task remains blocked until the required inspection results satisfy the group’s completion condition. A failed task must not count as complete merely because its execution terminated.\n\nThe selections work together: C represents group progress, and D uses that progress to control dispatch. For graceful recovery across interruptions, the implementation must also persist relevant state and configure retry or repair behavior; hierarchy alone does not automatically provide recovery.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Introduce hierarchical decomposition, placing related facility inspections beneath a parent node that tracks group completion before dependent work proceeds.\n\nD. Enforce dependency gates at group boundaries so certificate generation begins only after every required facility inspection has a confirmed completed state.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe essential requirement is that certificate generation respects the completion of its inspection group. Two defects prevent that: the agent lacks reliable aggregate group state, and downstream dispatch does not enforce the group’s prerequisites.\n\nC provides a structure for tracking related tasks together. D enforces the transition from inspection work to certificate generation. Together, they address both lost group state and premature dispatch while providing a scope for handling individual failures.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “A flat list of atomic tasks” — Technical signal: Individual task tracking does not currently represent the required group relationships.\n\n- “Tightly coupled groups” — Technical signal: Related tasks need an aggregate completion state.\n\n- “Must be completed before” — Hard constraint: Certificate generation has an explicit prerequisite.\n\n- “Loses intermediate group status” — Technical signal: The workflow needs persistent, inspectable group progress.\n\n- “Individual inspection tasks fail” — Technical signal: Recovery should identify affected work without treating every task as one undifferentiated list.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n- C creates an explicit coordination scope. A parent node can track required inspections and their pending, completed, or failed states. This makes an incomplete group visible and provides a place to coordinate recovery of its failed children.\n\n- D enforces readiness. The certificate task remains blocked until the required inspection results satisfy the group’s completion condition. A failed task must not count as complete merely because its execution terminated.\n\nThe selections work together: C represents group progress, and D uses that progress to control dispatch. For graceful recovery across interruptions, the implementation must also persist relevant state and configure retry or repair behavior; hierarchy alone does not automatically provide recovery.","Option E — Technically Possible but Not Best: A strictly enforced sequence could prevent some premature execution. However, serializing every facility does not introduce group-level status or targeted recovery. It also makes unrelated inspection work wait unnecessarily. It fits better when every task genuinely depends on the preceding task."],"references":["https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/use-subgraphs","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/run-if","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/jobs/repair-job-failures"],"source_hash":"d937e34e118b5b308c49cd40c81a50b80b8cd805842c279d8004b4c7614d3fcd","correct_many":[2,3],"stem_ja":"Databricksエージェントが、数週間にわたり数十の配送センターを対象とする医薬品サプライチェーンのコンプライアンス業務を管理しています。作業を原子的なタスクの平坦なリストへ分解し、各タスクを独立に割り当てて追跡します。\n\n一部のタスクは強く結び付いたグループを形成します。例えば地域の適合証明書を発行する前に、その地域の全施設の検査記録が完了していなければなりません。エージェントはこのグループ依存を繰り返し誤って扱い、中間のグループ状態を失い、施設データが未完成なのに証明書を出し、個々の検査タスクの失敗からの回復にも苦労しています。\n\nこの障害パターンに対処する変更を2つ選んでください。","options_ja":["施設検査と証明書生成の全タスクを同時に実行し、結果整合性によって部分結果を最終的な適合記録へまとめる。","施設検査が1つでも失敗するたびに平坦なタスクリスト全体を再開始し、試行の間で部分実行の状態を破棄する。","階層的な分解を導入し、関連する施設検査を親ノードの下へ置き、依存する作業へ進む前にグループの完了を追跡する。","グループ境界に依存関係のゲートを設け、必要なすべての施設検査で完了状態が確認された後にだけ証明書生成を開始する。","ワークフローを固定の逐次チェーンへ置き換え、全施設の検査を1つずつ処理してから証明書を発行し、すべての並列実行をなくす。"],"explanation_ja":"施設ごとの作業だけを平坦に管理すると、「地域全体が完了したか」を見失います。親ノードで関連検査の状態をまとめ、全必須検査の成功を証明書発行の条件にします。階層化はグループ状態、ゲートは開始条件を担当します。独立した地域や施設は並列化できるので、全工程を逐次にする必要はありません。","distractors_ja":["証明書発行には全検査の完了という前提条件があります。後から整合することに任せると、未完了の状態で発行してしまいます。","成功済みの進捗を捨てるため再実行が増えます。グループの依存や回復を改善する方法にはなりません。","関連タスクを親の下へまとめることで、地域単位の進捗を明示できます。失敗した子タスクの回復も追いやすくなります。","グループ内の必須検査がすべて完了したことを確認してから証明書を出すため、報告された早すぎる発行を防げます。","順序は守れますが独立作業の並列性まで失います。必要なのは全体の直列化ではなく、実際の依存に沿った制御です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-021","number":21,"form":3,"stem":"An engineer is configuring a Databricks agent for automotive service center employees. Staff provide a vehicle identification number (VIN) and a reported symptom. The agent must summarize the vehicle’s operating profile, determine the applicable SOP procedure code, and retrieve the procedure’s narrative service steps.\n\nThe available sources are:\n\n- Unity Catalog Delta table `vehicle_profiles`, containing `vin`, `engine_type`, and `operating_spec`.\n\n- Unity Catalog Delta table `symptom_sop_map`, containing `symptom_code` and `sop_procedure_code`.\n\n- Unity Catalog Delta table `service_history`, containing `vin`, `service_date`, and `technician_id`.\n\n- A Databricks AI Search index built from scanned OEM service manuals containing narrative SOP instructions and safety warnings.\n\nWhich combination of context elements best supports the required workflow?","options":["Define the agent’s automotive service role in the system prompt; explicitly direct operating-profile lookups by VIN to `service_history` and symptom-to-procedure lookups to `symptom_sop_map`; use AI Search for narrative OEM instructions.","Define the agent’s automotive service role in the system prompt; explicitly direct operating-profile lookups by VIN to `vehicle_profiles` and symptom-to-procedure lookups to `symptom_sop_map`; use AI Search for narrative OEM instructions.","Define the agent’s automotive service role in the system prompt; generally instruct it to choose among the available Delta tables for structured lookups using supplied identifiers; use AI Search for narrative OEM instructions.","Define the agent’s automotive service role in the system prompt; explicitly direct operating-profile lookups by VIN to `vehicle_profiles`; use semantic retrieval from AI Search for both procedure-code identification and narrative OEM instructions."],"correct":1,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"識別子による構造化参照と手順本文の検索","concept":"ce-f3-q021","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Define the agent’s automotive service role in the system prompt; explicitly direct operating-profile lookups by VIN to `vehicle_profiles` and symptom-to-procedure lookups to `symptom_sop_map`; use AI Search for narrative OEM instructions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nMatch each requested output to the source whose schema or content directly supports it:\n\n- Vehicle operating profile → `vehicle_profiles`.\n\n- Symptom-to-procedure relationship → `symptom_sop_map`.\n\n- Narrative service steps → the OEM manual index.\n\nB supplies these mappings explicitly. The automotive role establishes task scope, while the tool-use instructions establish where each required fact should come from.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- `engine_type` and `operating_spec` — Schema evidence: These fields support the operating-profile summary.\n\n- `symptom_code` and `sop_procedure_code` — Relationship evidence: The table directly represents symptom-to-procedure resolution.\n\n- `service_date` and `technician_id` — Exclusion signal: These describe service events, not operating specifications.\n\n- “Narrative service steps” — Content signal: The manuals contain the procedural text the agent must retrieve.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nB gives the agent a source-specific retrieval plan. Parameterized table queries can obtain the vehicle attributes and procedure code; that code then guides retrieval of the corresponding manual content.\n\nDatabricks supports predefined query logic through Unity Catalog function tools. AI Search supports semantic, hybrid, and full-text retrieval for finding relevant indexed content. \n\nOne implementation prerequisite remains: if the reported symptom is free text, it must be resolved to a valid `symptom_code` before querying the mapping table. The supplied schema does not define that normalization step.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: `service_history` contains service dates and technician identifiers. Sharing the `vin` key does not give it the operating attributes needed for the requested profile.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: An agent could infer the appropriate tables from sufficiently detailed schemas. However, generic lookup guidance leaves source selection implicit when the required mappings are already known.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Search might find procedure codes in manual text, but this bypasses the dedicated symptom-to-procedure mapping. Semantic relevance alone does not establish the required mapping as directly as the structured relationship does.\n\nExam Trap\n\nA table containing the correct identifier is not automatically the correct source. Both `vehicle_profiles` and `service_history` contain `vin`; only the former includes the requested operating attributes.\n\nIf the task instead requested previous service dates and technician assignments, `service_history` would become relevant.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nChoose sources using both lookup keys and requested attributes. A key identifies the record; the remaining fields determine which questions that record can answer.\n\nExplicit context should also preserve relationships between retrieval stages—for example, carrying the resolved procedure code into the manual search.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nConfigure separate tools for VIN-based profile lookup and symptom-code resolution. Pass the returned procedure code into the manual retrieval request, retaining relevant service steps, safety warnings, and source references.\n\nThis makes incorrect source choices easier to detect and helps prevent instructions for an unrelated procedure from entering the answer.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nMatch the requested fields first, resolve the procedure code, then retrieve its instructions.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nSelect B: `vehicle_profiles` supplies operating attributes, `symptom_sop_map` supplies the procedure relationship, and AI Search supplies narrative instructions. Explicit source mappings are stronger than generic lookup guidance.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Create agent tools using Unity Catalog functions\n\nSupports: Predefined, parameterized queries for structured lookups.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool)\n\n- Databricks — Query an AI Search index\n\nSupports: Retrieval of relevant indexed content using semantic, hybrid, or full-text queries.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: `service_history` contains service dates and technician identifiers. Sharing the `vin` key does not give it the operating attributes needed for the requested profile.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Define the agent’s automotive service role in the system prompt; explicitly direct operating-profile lookups by VIN to `vehicle_profiles` and symptom-to-procedure lookups to `symptom_sop_map`; use AI Search for narrative OEM instructions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nMatch each requested output to the source whose schema or content directly supports it:\n\n- Vehicle operating profile → `vehicle_profiles`.\n\n- Symptom-to-procedure relationship → `symptom_sop_map`.\n\n- Narrative service steps → the OEM manual index.\n\nB supplies these mappings explicitly. The automotive role establishes task scope, while the tool-use instructions establish where each required fact should come from.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- `engine_type` and `operating_spec` — Schema evidence: These fields support the operating-profile summary.\n\n- `symptom_code` and `sop_procedure_code` — Relationship evidence: The table directly represents symptom-to-procedure resolution.\n\n- `service_date` and `technician_id` — Exclusion signal: These describe service events, not operating specifications.\n\n- “Narrative service steps” — Content signal: The manuals contain the procedural text the agent must retrieve.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nB gives the agent a source-specific retrieval plan. Parameterized table queries can obtain the vehicle attributes and procedure code; that code then guides retrieval of the corresponding manual content.\n\nDatabricks supports predefined query logic through Unity Catalog function tools. AI Search supports semantic, hybrid, and full-text retrieval for finding relevant indexed content. \n\nOne implementation prerequisite remains: if the reported symptom is free text, it must be resolved to a valid `symptom_code` before querying the mapping table. The supplied schema does not define that normalization step.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: An agent could infer the appropriate tables from sufficiently detailed schemas. However, generic lookup guidance leaves source selection implicit when the required mappings are already known.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Search might find procedure codes in manual text, but this bypasses the dedicated symptom-to-procedure mapping. Semantic relevance alone does not establish the required mapping as directly as the structured relationship does."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search"],"source_hash":"b69c8231a4ac712750c5a3fa0f0f3b477d7cc55acbb69a0226c8ccc5437939c7","stem_ja":"エンジニアが、自動車サービスセンターの従業員向けにDatabricksエージェントを設定しています。従業員は車両識別番号（VIN）と申告された症状を入力します。エージェントは車両の運転仕様を要約し、該当するSOP手順コードを特定し、その手順の文章による整備手順を取得しなければなりません。\n\n利用可能なソースは次のとおりです。\n\n- Unity CatalogのDeltaテーブル`vehicle_profiles`：`vin`、`engine_type`、`operating_spec`を含む。\n- Unity CatalogのDeltaテーブル`symptom_sop_map`：`symptom_code`、`sop_procedure_code`を含む。\n- Unity CatalogのDeltaテーブル`service_history`：`vin`、`service_date`、`technician_id`を含む。\n- 文章のSOP指示と安全警告を含む、スキャン済みOEM整備マニュアルから構築したDatabricks AI Searchインデックス。\n\n必要なワークフローを最もよく支えるコンテキスト要素の組み合わせはどれですか。","options_ja":["システムプロンプトで自動車整備の役割を定義し、VINによる運転仕様の参照先を`service_history`、症状から手順への参照先を`symptom_sop_map`と明示し、OEMの文章による指示にはAI Searchを使う。","システムプロンプトで自動車整備の役割を定義し、VINによる運転仕様の参照先を`vehicle_profiles`、症状から手順への参照先を`symptom_sop_map`と明示し、OEMの文章による指示にはAI Searchを使う。","システムプロンプトで自動車整備の役割を定義し、提供された識別子による構造化参照では利用可能なDeltaテーブルから選ぶよう一般的に指示し、OEMの文章による指示にはAI Searchを使う。","システムプロンプトで自動車整備の役割を定義し、VINによる運転仕様の参照先を`vehicle_profiles`と明示し、手順コードの特定とOEMの文章による指示の両方にAI Searchの意味検索を使う。"],"explanation_ja":"役割だけでなく、各判断に使う正本ソースを指定します。VINから仕様はvehicle_profiles、症状から手順コードはsymptom_sop_mapという正確な構造化参照を使い、そのコードに対応する手順本文をAI Searchで取得します。service_historyには仕様の列がなく、正確な対応表を意味検索へ置き換える必要もありません。","distractors_ja":["service_historyの列は整備日や技術者であり、求めるエンジンや運転仕様がありません。参照先テーブルを取り違えています。","必要な情報を持つテーブルとキーを明示し、文章の手順だけを検索へ任せます。構造化データと非構造化文書を適切に使い分けています。","一般的な役割指示だけでは、どのテーブルを使うかをモデルの推測に委ねます。明示できる対応関係を具体的に指定する方が確実です。","正確な症状と手順コードの対応表があるのに意味検索へ任せています。構造化された決定的な参照を先に使う方が適切です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-022","number":22,"form":3,"stem":"A Databricks agent autonomously coordinates incident response over several days. Its inputs arrive continuously as structured logs, tool results, and brief status messages. Decisions usually depend on recent information, and newer updates frequently supersede earlier messages.\n\nThe engineer is choosing between two approaches: a fixed sliding window that removes messages beyond a predetermined turn count, and semantic compaction that summarizes older context before removing it.\n\nWhich information-relevance characteristic supports choosing the fixed sliding window?","options":["Individually minor status updates collectively describe a progression of state changes that a fixed cutoff would preserve.","Important dependencies extend across distant turns, and semantic compaction would discard their detail sooner than fixed trimming.","Required decision context consistently resides in a predictable recent portion of the history, making a fixed window sufficient without semantic summarization.","Fixed trimming is a standard context-management technique whose suitability does not depend on the workflow’s particular relevance pattern."],"correct":2,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"固定窓が適する情報の寿命","concept":"ce-f3-q022","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Required decision context consistently resides in a predictable recent portion of the history, making a fixed window sufficient without semantic summarization.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe deciding requirement is retaining the information needed for subsequent decisions. A fixed window selects messages by age rather than meaning, so it is justified when age reliably predicts relevance.\n\nC establishes that relationship. The other options either describe dependencies that could cross the cutoff or claim that workload-specific relevance does not matter.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Decisions usually depend on recent information” — Technical signal: Recency may serve as a useful relevance proxy.\n\n- “Newer updates frequently supersede earlier messages” — Technical signal: Older messages can become redundant.\n\n- “Predetermined turn count” — Technical signal: The heuristic cannot independently recognize an old but important fact.\n\n- “Which information-relevance characteristic” — Selection instruction: Choose the condition that makes trimming appropriate, rather than its popularity or simplicity.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nWhen information becomes obsolete predictably, a validated recent-history window can retain necessary decision context while removing superseded messages. Semantic summarization adds less value when older material contains no unique information needed later.\n\nThe engineer should validate the window against representative workflows. Turn count also needs checking against the token budget because differently sized messages can consume different amounts of context.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: Collectively meaningful updates may require retaining a sequence that extends beyond the cutoff. Their individual brevity does not establish that fixed trimming preserves the necessary progression. The option assumes the preservation property it needs to demonstrate.\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: Long-range dependencies make age-based deletion risky. A carefully designed semantic summary can retain important older facts after their original messages leave context; fixed trimming simply discards messages outside its window. There is no basis for assuming trimming preserves such dependencies better.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Being a common technique does not establish suitability. The choice depends on whether discarded messages still contain unique constraints, decisions, or unresolved information required by the workflow.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest misconception is treating “mostly recent” as sufficient evidence that everything older is disposable. The relevant distinction is predictable loss of relevance.\n\nIf older messages continue to contain unique constraints or unresolved decisions, semantic compaction—or separately retained state—becomes more appropriate.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nSliding-window trimming uses message age as a proxy for usefulness. Semantic compaction evaluates content to preserve important information across a longer history. The simpler heuristic fits when its age-based rule reliably matches the workload’s information needs.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nFor incident monitoring, a current service-health update may supersede earlier health snapshots. A validated window could remove obsolete snapshots while retaining recent tool outcomes, avoiding repeated summarization work. Any unresolved recovery action would still need to remain available rather than disappearing solely because it was recorded earlier.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nUse age-based trimming when age reliably predicts irrelevance.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nPredictable relevance decay supports a fixed sliding window. Persistent facts and long-range dependencies call for content-aware preservation instead.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: The objective evaluating whether trimming heuristics are sufficient for a task’s information-relevance pattern or whether more sophisticated compaction is required.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- LangChain — Short-term memory\n\nSupports: Message trimming, token-based context limits, information-loss risks from removal, and summarization as an alternative.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/short-term-memory)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: Collectively meaningful updates may require retaining a sequence that extends beyond the cutoff. Their individual brevity does not establish that fixed trimming preserves the necessary progression. The option assumes the preservation property it needs to demonstrate.","Option B — Incorrect: Long-range dependencies make age-based deletion risky. A carefully designed semantic summary can retain important older facts after their original messages leave context; fixed trimming simply discards messages outside its window. There is no basis for assuming trimming preserves such dependencies better.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Required decision context consistently resides in a predictable recent portion of the history, making a fixed window sufficient without semantic summarization.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe deciding requirement is retaining the information needed for subsequent decisions. A fixed window selects messages by age rather than meaning, so it is justified when age reliably predicts relevance.\n\nC establishes that relationship. The other options either describe dependencies that could cross the cutoff or claim that workload-specific relevance does not matter.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Decisions usually depend on recent information” — Technical signal: Recency may serve as a useful relevance proxy.\n\n- “Newer updates frequently supersede earlier messages” — Technical signal: Older messages can become redundant.\n\n- “Predetermined turn count” — Technical signal: The heuristic cannot independently recognize an old but important fact.\n\n- “Which information-relevance characteristic” — Selection instruction: Choose the condition that makes trimming appropriate, rather than its popularity or simplicity.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nWhen information becomes obsolete predictably, a validated recent-history window can retain necessary decision context while removing superseded messages. Semantic summarization adds less value when older material contains no unique information needed later.\n\nThe engineer should validate the window against representative workflows. Turn count also needs checking against the token budget because differently sized messages can consume different amounts of context.","Option D — Incorrect: Being a common technique does not establish suitability. The choice depends on whether discarded messages still contain unique constraints, decisions, or unresolved information required by the workflow."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/short-term-memory"],"source_hash":"9edb04824ffe0751fec03df3afc7900d805611c99902b2435bf8de0e5f476092","stem_ja":"Databricksエージェントが、数日間にわたりインシデント対応を自律的に調整します。入力は構造化ログ、ツール結果、短い状態メッセージとして継続的に届きます。判断は通常最近の情報に依存し、新しい更新が以前のメッセージを置き換えることも頻繁にあります。\n\nエンジニアは、所定のターン数より古いメッセージを取り除く固定のスライディングウィンドウと、古いコンテキストを要約してから取り除く意味的な圧縮を比較しています。\n\n固定のスライディングウィンドウを選ぶ根拠となる、情報の関連性の特徴はどれですか。","options_ja":["個々には小さな状態更新が、全体として状態変化の進行を表しており、固定の打ち切りでもその進行を保持できる。","重要な依存関係が遠く離れたターンをまたぎ、固定の切り詰めよりも意味的な圧縮の方が、その詳細を早く失ってしまう。","判断に必要なコンテキストが、履歴のうち予測可能な最近の範囲に一貫して収まるため、意味的な要約なしでも固定ウィンドウで十分である。","固定の切り詰めは標準的なコンテキスト管理手法なので、適合性はそのワークフロー固有の関連性のパターンに左右されない。"],"explanation_ja":"固定窓が向くのは、必要な情報が常に最近の一定範囲へ収まり、古い内容が新しい更新で置き換わる場合です。この条件なら、要約モデルを呼ぶことなく古いメッセージを落とせます。遠い過去の決定や、更新の積み重ねが現在の判断に必要なら、固定窓だけでは危険です。「古いか」ではなく「古くても必要か」で選びます。","distractors_ja":["小さな更新の積み重ね自体が重要なら、古い更新の削除で必要な経緯を失う可能性があります。固定窓が保つとは限りません。","離れたターンへの依存があるなら、固定の年齢による削除はむしろ危険です。意味的な保持の必要性を検討する条件です。","関連情報が予測可能な最近の範囲だけにあるなら、固定窓で必要十分な文脈を保持できます。設問の更新パターンに合います。","どの方法も普遍的には適しません。必要な情報の寿命と依存関係によって、切り詰めと要約を使い分けます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-023","number":23,"form":3,"stem":"A legal document review agent on Databricks connects to three MCP servers. The first provides document ingestion and clause extraction tools; the second provides e-signature routing and redlining tools; and the third provides billing, HR lookup, and IT ticketing tools.\n\nFor every request, the agent receives all 20 registered tool schemas from these servers in its model context. Requests to extract indemnification clauses consume excessive tool-selection tokens, and repeated identical requests produce inconsistent tool choices.\n\nWhich change should the engineer make to address both problems?","options":["Introduce intent-aware tool discovery across the three MCP servers, loading only the schemas for servers and tools relevant to the current request.","Consolidate the three servers’ schemas into one registry with a unified interface and include that registry in the model context for every request.","Rank the MCP servers by historical request frequency and supply all their tools in that order for every request.","Publish concise tool manifests from each MCP server and include both the manifests and all complete tool schemas in the model context."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"MCPの意図に応じた段階的なツール発見","concept":"ce-f3-q023","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Introduce intent-aware tool discovery across the three MCP servers, loading only the schemas for servers and tools relevant to the current request.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe intervention must reduce schema tokens and help the agent focus on applicable tools. The request concerns clause extraction, yet the context includes unrelated administrative and signing capabilities.\n\nA changes which schemas enter the context. B and C retain the full collection, while D adds more material. Selective discovery therefore directly addresses the stated configuration problem.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “All 20 registered tool schemas” — Technical signal: Complete definitions are loaded without considering relevance.\n\n- “For every request” — Loading-pattern signal: Unnecessary schema overhead recurs.\n\n- “Extract indemnification clauses” — Intent signal: The request points toward document-processing capabilities.\n\n- “Excessive tool-selection tokens” and “inconsistent tool choices” — Failure signals: The change must address both context consumption and selection focus.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nProgressive disclosure lets an agent discover relevant capabilities through lightweight information, then obtain detailed definitions when needed. Anthropic documents loading tool definitions on demand and searching for relevant definitions instead of presenting the entire collection upfront.\n\nFor this request, the discovery layer would expose the required clause-extraction tools and any necessary document-ingestion tools. Unrelated schemas would stay outside the active context.\n\nThis reduces unnecessary choices; it does not guarantee perfect selection. Discovery accuracy and clear tool descriptions still matter.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: A unified registry can simplify integration. However, including the complete registry on every request preserves the schema footprint and irrelevant choices. Consolidation alone does not implement selective loading.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: Frequency-based ordering changes presentation, not the number of schemas supplied. Historical popularity also does not establish relevance to the current request.\n\n- Option D — Violates Requirement: Adding manifests alongside every complete schema increases context consumption. Lightweight manifests help when they enable selective access to detailed definitions.\n\nExam Trap\n\nA unified registry or concise manifest can sound like a context optimization. Check what ultimately enters the model’s prompt.\n\nB would become a strong alternative if the registry were searched externally and returned only relevant definitions. As written, it supplies the whole registry every time.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nDistinguish available tools from tools exposed in the current model context. An agent can retain access to a broad catalog while receiving only the definitions needed for its current task.\n\nMCP provides tool discovery and invocation mechanisms; the client or orchestration layer controls how discovered information is presented to the model. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nMaintain a searchable catalog of tool names, descriptions, and server associations. For an indemnification request, retrieve the relevant document-processing definitions, validate their parameters, and invoke the appropriate tool.\n\nEvaluate schema-token consumption and tool-selection accuracy together. An overly restrictive discovery step could hide a required capability.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nKeep the catalog discoverable; load detailed schemas according to the task.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen every request receives every MCP schema, choose intent-aware discovery with selective schema loading. Reordering or consolidating the same complete definitions does not remove their context cost.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Anthropic — Code execution with MCP: Building more efficient agents\n\nSupports: Progressive disclosure, tool discovery, and loading relevant definitions on demand.\n\n[Official architecture guidance](https://www.anthropic.com/research/code-execution-with-mcp)\n\n- Model Context Protocol — Tools\n\nSupports: Tool discovery, schemas, and the distinction between protocol capabilities and application presentation.\n\n[Official protocol specification](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools)\n\n- Databricks — Certified Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: Progressive disclosure for MCP tool access and reducing context consumption compared with loading full schemas upfront.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Introduce intent-aware tool discovery across the three MCP servers, loading only the schemas for servers and tools relevant to the current request.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe intervention must reduce schema tokens and help the agent focus on applicable tools. The request concerns clause extraction, yet the context includes unrelated administrative and signing capabilities.\n\nA changes which schemas enter the context. B and C retain the full collection, while D adds more material. Selective discovery therefore directly addresses the stated configuration problem.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “All 20 registered tool schemas” — Technical signal: Complete definitions are loaded without considering relevance.\n\n- “For every request” — Loading-pattern signal: Unnecessary schema overhead recurs.\n\n- “Extract indemnification clauses” — Intent signal: The request points toward document-processing capabilities.\n\n- “Excessive tool-selection tokens” and “inconsistent tool choices” — Failure signals: The change must address both context consumption and selection focus.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nProgressive disclosure lets an agent discover relevant capabilities through lightweight information, then obtain detailed definitions when needed. Anthropic documents loading tool definitions on demand and searching for relevant definitions instead of presenting the entire collection upfront.\n\nFor this request, the discovery layer would expose the required clause-extraction tools and any necessary document-ingestion tools. Unrelated schemas would stay outside the active context.\n\nThis reduces unnecessary choices; it does not guarantee perfect selection. Discovery accuracy and clear tool descriptions still matter.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: A unified registry can simplify integration. However, including the complete registry on every request preserves the schema footprint and irrelevant choices. Consolidation alone does not implement selective loading.","Option C — Incorrect: Frequency-based ordering changes presentation, not the number of schemas supplied. Historical popularity also does not establish relevance to the current request.","Option D — Violates Requirement: Adding manifests alongside every complete schema increases context consumption. Lightweight manifests help when they enable selective access to detailed definitions."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/code-execution-with-mcp","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools","https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf"],"source_hash":"2b248bb2e6004b4b7b4bf1bde03f3a715ec20920ad9730976676d4b0e7b45f16","stem_ja":"Databricks上の法務文書レビューエージェントが、3つのMCPサーバーへ接続しています。1つ目は文書取り込みと条項抽出、2つ目は電子署名の回付と修正履歴の作成、3つ目は請求、HR照会、ITチケット処理のツールを提供します。\n\nエージェントは要求のたびに、これらのサーバーの登録済みツールスキーマ20個すべてをモデルのコンテキストへ受け取ります。補償条項を抽出する要求ではツール選択のトークンが過剰に消費され、同じ要求を繰り返してもツール選択が一貫しません。\n\n両方の問題に対処する変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["3つのMCPサーバーをまたぐ意図に応じたツール発見を導入し、現在の要求に関連するサーバーとツールのスキーマだけを読み込む。","3つのサーバーのスキーマを統一インターフェースの1つのレジストリへまとめ、そのレジストリを毎回モデルのコンテキストへ含める。","過去の要求頻度でMCPサーバーを順位付けし、その順序で全ツールを毎回供給する。","各MCPサーバーから簡潔なツールマニフェストを公開し、マニフェストとすべての完全なツールスキーマを両方コンテキストへ含める。"],"explanation_ja":"補償条項の抽出に、請求や電子署名などの全ツールは不要です。要求の意図に応じて必要なサーバー・ツールを発見し、選んだ定義だけを読み込めば、トークン量と競合する選択肢の両方を減らせます。並べ替えやレジストリ統合だけでは定義の総量は変わりません。「探す情報は軽く、詳しい定義は必要分だけ」が基本です。","distractors_ja":["不要な定義を読み込まず、該当する機能だけに選択肢を絞れます。コンテキスト消費と選択の曖昧さに同時に対応します。","管理場所をまとめても20個全部のスキーマを毎回渡すなら、トークン量と不要な選択肢は残ります。","順序を変えるだけで読み込む全量は変わりません。今回の意図と無関係なツールも選択候補として残ります。","軽量マニフェストに完全スキーマまで加えると総量が増えます。段階的開示では、詳細を選択後まで読み込まないことが重要です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-024","number":24,"form":3,"stem":"A customer support agent on Databricks initializes a session context object before executing its first turn handler. Every subsequent turn handler receives that same session context.\n\nThe initialization process must obtain the user’s plan tier, region, and several entitlement flags from the Lakebase `customer_profile` table. These attributes remain unchanged throughout a session, although they may differ when the user starts another session.\n\nWhich retrieval strategy satisfies the initialization requirement while minimizing Lakebase requests and their contribution to turn-response latency?","options":["Retrieve the attributes once into a cache shared across sessions, then initialize each session from that cache.","Retrieve the attributes from Lakebase during session initialization, place them in the per-session context object, and reuse them throughout that session.","Retrieve the attributes from Lakebase whenever a turn needs them, obtaining current table values on each such turn.","Retrieve the attributes during session initialization and refresh them from Lakebase every five minutes."],"correct":1,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"セッション内で不変な属性の一度だけの取得","concept":"ce-f3-q024","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Retrieve the attributes from Lakebase during session initialization, place them in the per-session context object, and reuse them throughout that session.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nTwo requirements determine the answer:\n\n- The attributes must already exist before the first turn handler executes.\n\n- They remain valid for the entire session.\n\nB places retrieval at the required initialization boundary and avoids repeated profile queries during later turns. A introduces cross-session freshness concerns, C postpones retrieval until turn execution, and D adds unnecessary refreshes.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Before executing its first turn handler” — Hard timing constraint: Lazy retrieval during a turn is too late.\n\n- “Every subsequent turn handler receives that same session context” — Architecture signal: The application already provides a reuse mechanism.\n\n- “Remain unchanged throughout a session” — Freshness signal: Repeated reads provide no required benefit.\n\n- “May differ…another session” — Scope signal: Reloading at each new session matches the attributes’ validity period.\n\n- “Minimizing Lakebase requests” — Optimization requirement: Avoid recurring profile lookups.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe initialization routine queries `customer_profile` for the relevant user, stores the required fields, and completes before turn processing begins. Later handlers read those fields from session context.\n\nFor these attributes, the normal pattern becomes one initialization lookup per session, rather than repeated lookups across turns. A new session performs a new lookup.\n\nDatabricks distinguishes state maintained within an interaction from information retained across separate conversations. That distinction supports keeping this session’s loaded profile values scoped to its context. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: A shared cache can reduce database traffic, but the described approach provides no invalidation or refresh mechanism. It could initialize a new session with outdated attributes after the profile changes between sessions.\n\n- Option C — Violates Requirement: Fetching values inside a turn does not populate the required context before the first handler starts. It also adds repeated database latency for attributes that remain stable during the session.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: It meets the initial loading requirement, but five-minute refreshes add round-trips without a stated need. The interval is unrelated to the supplied session-validity rule.\n\nExam Trap\n\n“Could change across sessions” does not mean “must refresh every turn.” Match the retrieval boundary to the period during which the values remain valid.\n\nIf updates had to become visible during an active session, dynamic retrieval or an explicit refresh mechanism would become appropriate.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nChoose a retrieval strategy using three questions: When must the data first be available? How long does it remain valid? Where can it be reused?\n\nHere, the answers are before turn execution, for one session, and in the existing session context. This is session-scoped preloading.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nHave session creation await the profile lookup before accepting the first turn. Store only the required attributes in that session’s context and let subsequent handlers reuse them.\n\nThis moves the profile-query latency into initialization and removes repeated `customer_profile` reads from ordinary turn handling. It does not imply that the agent performs no other database operations.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nLoad before first use, and reuse for the data’s stated validity period.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nRequired before turn 1 plus stable throughout the session means preload once into per-session context. Refresh at the next session boundary when values may have changed.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Agent memory and sessions\n\nSupports: The distinction between interaction-scoped state and information retained across conversations.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)\n\n- Databricks — Certified Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: Evaluating static versus dynamic Lakebase retrieval and matching memory access to the information need.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: A shared cache can reduce database traffic, but the described approach provides no invalidation or refresh mechanism. It could initialize a new session with outdated attributes after the profile changes between sessions.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Retrieve the attributes from Lakebase during session initialization, place them in the per-session context object, and reuse them throughout that session.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nTwo requirements determine the answer:\n\n- The attributes must already exist before the first turn handler executes.\n\n- They remain valid for the entire session.\n\nB places retrieval at the required initialization boundary and avoids repeated profile queries during later turns. A introduces cross-session freshness concerns, C postpones retrieval until turn execution, and D adds unnecessary refreshes.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Before executing its first turn handler” — Hard timing constraint: Lazy retrieval during a turn is too late.\n\n- “Every subsequent turn handler receives that same session context” — Architecture signal: The application already provides a reuse mechanism.\n\n- “Remain unchanged throughout a session” — Freshness signal: Repeated reads provide no required benefit.\n\n- “May differ…another session” — Scope signal: Reloading at each new session matches the attributes’ validity period.\n\n- “Minimizing Lakebase requests” — Optimization requirement: Avoid recurring profile lookups.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe initialization routine queries `customer_profile` for the relevant user, stores the required fields, and completes before turn processing begins. Later handlers read those fields from session context.\n\nFor these attributes, the normal pattern becomes one initialization lookup per session, rather than repeated lookups across turns. A new session performs a new lookup.\n\nDatabricks distinguishes state maintained within an interaction from information retained across separate conversations. That distinction supports keeping this session’s loaded profile values scoped to its context.","Option C — Violates Requirement: Fetching values inside a turn does not populate the required context before the first handler starts. It also adds repeated database latency for attributes that remain stable during the session.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: It meets the initial loading requirement, but five-minute refreshes add round-trips without a stated need. The interval is unrelated to the supplied session-validity rule."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents","https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf"],"source_hash":"b85ac554ba850b4cba49847836d2b07ab7354f95918fad45416e4e55158b4f0e","stem_ja":"Databricksのカスタマーサポートエージェントが、最初のターン処理を実行する前にセッションコンテキストオブジェクトを初期化します。その後の各ターン処理は、同じセッションコンテキストを受け取ります。\n\n初期化では、Lakebaseの`customer_profile`テーブルからユーザーのプラン階層、地域、いくつかの利用資格フラグを取得する必要があります。これらの属性はセッション中には変わりませんが、別のセッションを開始するときには異なる可能性があります。\n\n初期化の要件を満たし、Lakebaseへの要求数と、それが各ターンの応答遅延へ与える影響を最小化する取得戦略はどれですか。","options_ja":["属性を一度だけセッション間で共有するキャッシュへ取得し、各セッションをそのキャッシュから初期化する。","セッション初期化時に属性をLakebaseから取得し、セッション単位のコンテキストオブジェクトへ格納して、そのセッション中は再利用する。","ターンが属性を必要とするたびにLakebaseから取得し、その都度テーブルの最新値を得る。","セッション初期化時に属性を取得し、5分おきにLakebaseから更新する。"],"explanation_ja":"属性の変化の境界がセッションに一致しています。開始時に1回だけ現在値を取得し、そのセッションのコンテキストで再利用すれば、要件を満たしてターンごとの問い合わせを省けます。セッションをまたぐキャッシュは変更を見落とし、毎回取得や定期更新は不要な呼び出しを増やします。「値が変わる境界に取得タイミングを合わせる」と覚えます。","distractors_ja":["新しいセッションでは属性が変わる可能性があります。更新条件のない共通キャッシュでは、開始時点の正しい値を保証できません。","セッション開始時の現在値を確保し、その後は不変という条件を利用できます。必要な取得を1回に抑える方法です。","毎ターン最新値を取得できますが、セッション内で変わらない属性には過剰です。要求数と応答遅延が増えます。","5分ごとの更新には根拠がありません。セッション内不変という条件なら余分であり、開始時の1回取得で十分です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-025","number":25,"form":3,"stem":"A Databricks agent supports a financial services compliance team. A nightly job checks each account’s recorded transactions against the bank’s approved control thresholds and writes its findings to a Delta table shared with the regulator through Delta Sharing.\n\nA compliance officer now needs an immediate, independent evaluation of one account, followed by reconciliation against that account’s nightly findings. The audit must identify every transaction meeting the specified criteria, and the finished report must conform exactly to a predefined schema.\n\nThe Unity Catalog tool registry contains these five tools:\n\n- `delta_account_reader`: Queries the governed Delta tables used by the nightly job and returns an initial transaction set matching the specified audit criteria.\n\n- `semantic_tx_retriever`: Searches a vector index for an account’s transaction records that are semantically relevant to the specified audit criteria.\n\n- `threshold_evaluator`: Takes one account’s transaction records, applies the bank’s approved control logic, and produces structured evaluation results.\n\n- `acct_ctrl_dispatcher`: Takes an account identifier and evaluation results as a JSON payload, retrieves the nightly findings, performs reconciliation internally, and returns the comparison result for the agent’s finished report.\n\n- `recon_delta_probe`: Takes an account identifier and returns its nightly findings, leaving the discrepancy comparison and report production to the agent.\n\nWhich three-tool sequence best satisfies these requirements?","options":["`semantic_tx_retriever` → `threshold_evaluator` → `recon_delta_probe`","`delta_account_reader` → `threshold_evaluator` → `recon_delta_probe`","`delta_account_reader` → `threshold_evaluator` → `acct_ctrl_dispatcher`","`semantic_tx_retriever` → `threshold_evaluator` → `acct_ctrl_dispatcher`"],"correct":2,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"監査で必要な正確な取得とツール入出力の連鎖","concept":"ce-f3-q025","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. `delta_account_reader` → `threshold_evaluator` → `acct_ctrl_dispatcher`\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nApply two selection tests:\n\n- Record coverage: The audit requires all qualifying transactions. Choose the governed table query over semantic relevance retrieval.\n\n- Operation placement: The registry provides a tool that accepts the new evaluation and performs the required reconciliation. Prefer that contract over returning batch data for the agent to compare itself.\n\nThese choices identify C. The order also respects dependencies: obtain transactions, evaluate them, then reconcile the evaluation against the nightly findings.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Every transaction meeting the specified criteria” — Hard constraint: Retrieval must support exhaustive criteria-based coverage.\n\n- “Approved control thresholds” — Hard constraint: Use the supplied control evaluator.\n\n- “Independent evaluation” — Sequence requirement: Produce new findings before comparing them with the batch findings.\n\n- “Conform exactly to a predefined schema” — Hard constraint: Preserve structured results and enforce the report contract.\n\n- “Performs reconciliation internally” — Functional signal: `acct_ctrl_dispatcher` performs the required comparison rather than merely retrieving its inputs.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe three tools form a compatible processing chain:\n\n- `delta_account_reader` obtains the account’s criteria-matching transactions from governed tables.\n\n- `threshold_evaluator` consumes those records and returns the new structured findings.\n\n- `acct_ctrl_dispatcher` consumes the account identifier and those findings, retrieves the batch baseline, and performs the comparison.\n\nDatabricks documents Unity Catalog function tools as a way to execute predefined logic, including parameterized SQL queries. This supports placing specified data operations in tools. \n\nImportant boundary: The sequence alone does not guarantee schema compliance. Its implementation must retrieve the complete qualifying record set and validate the final report against the required schema. Accepting JSON does not itself establish an output-schema guarantee.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Violates Requirement: Semantic relevance retrieval does not establish that every transaction satisfying exact audit criteria has been returned. It also leaves reconciliation to the agent. Databricks describes ANN retrieval as finding semantically similar records, which serves a different selection purpose from exhaustive predicate matching.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: It selects the appropriate reader and evaluator, but `recon_delta_probe` only supplies batch findings. The agent must then implement the comparison despite an available tool whose contract already performs it. Schema-constrained generation could help format a report, but formatting alone does not establish comparison correctness.\n\n- Option D — Violates Requirement: It uses the appropriate reconciliation tool, but the semantic retrieval step still lacks a completeness guarantee. Correct downstream processing cannot recover qualifying transactions omitted upstream.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDo not infer behavior from tool names. Despite its name, `acct_ctrl_dispatcher` performs the comparison; `recon_delta_probe` only retrieves the baseline.\n\nB would become more appropriate if the agent needed a comparison method unsupported by the dispatcher, provided the agent had a reliable comparison implementation and schema enforcement.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nEvaluate a tool chain along three dimensions: complete inputs, compatible handoffs, and required operations.\n\nSchema correctness is a separate dimension from factual and computational correctness. Structured output controls can constrain a response’s format, while the retrieval and evaluation logic determine whether its contents are complete and accurate. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nPass the reader’s complete results into the evaluator, then forward the structured evaluation to the reconciliation tool without rewriting it into prose. Validate the returned report before release.\n\nRecording the data cutoff and control version also helps reviewers distinguish discrepancies caused by newly recorded transactions from differences in evaluation behavior.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nFor this audit: exact records → approved evaluation → tool-performed reconciliation.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\n“All qualifying records” favors criteria-based table retrieval over semantic similarity. When a supplied tool accepts the evaluation and performs the required comparison, choose it over a lookup that leaves that operation to the agent.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Create agent tools using Unity Catalog functions\n\nSupports: Tools executing predefined logic and parameterized queries.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool)\n\n- Databricks — Query an AI Search index\n\nSupports: Semantic similarity retrieval and its distinction from exhaustive audit selection.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search)\n\n- Databricks — Structured outputs on Databricks\n\nSupports: Explicit schema-constrained generation and its implementation boundaries.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/model-serving/structured-outputs)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Violates Requirement: Semantic relevance retrieval does not establish that every transaction satisfying exact audit criteria has been returned. It also leaves reconciliation to the agent. Databricks describes ANN retrieval as finding semantically similar records, which serves a different selection purpose from exhaustive predicate matching.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: It selects the appropriate reader and evaluator, but `recon_delta_probe` only supplies batch findings. The agent must then implement the comparison despite an available tool whose contract already performs it. Schema-constrained generation could help format a report, but formatting alone does not establish comparison correctness.","Correct Answer\n\nC. `delta_account_reader` → `threshold_evaluator` → `acct_ctrl_dispatcher`\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nApply two selection tests:\n\n- Record coverage: The audit requires all qualifying transactions. Choose the governed table query over semantic relevance retrieval.\n\n- Operation placement: The registry provides a tool that accepts the new evaluation and performs the required reconciliation. Prefer that contract over returning batch data for the agent to compare itself.\n\nThese choices identify C. The order also respects dependencies: obtain transactions, evaluate them, then reconcile the evaluation against the nightly findings.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Every transaction meeting the specified criteria” — Hard constraint: Retrieval must support exhaustive criteria-based coverage.\n\n- “Approved control thresholds” — Hard constraint: Use the supplied control evaluator.\n\n- “Independent evaluation” — Sequence requirement: Produce new findings before comparing them with the batch findings.\n\n- “Conform exactly to a predefined schema” — Hard constraint: Preserve structured results and enforce the report contract.\n\n- “Performs reconciliation internally” — Functional signal: `acct_ctrl_dispatcher` performs the required comparison rather than merely retrieving its inputs.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe three tools form a compatible processing chain:\n\n- `delta_account_reader` obtains the account’s criteria-matching transactions from governed tables.\n\n- `threshold_evaluator` consumes those records and returns the new structured findings.\n\n- `acct_ctrl_dispatcher` consumes the account identifier and those findings, retrieves the batch baseline, and performs the comparison.\n\nDatabricks documents Unity Catalog function tools as a way to execute predefined logic, including parameterized SQL queries. This supports placing specified data operations in tools. \n\nImportant boundary: The sequence alone does not guarantee schema compliance. Its implementation must retrieve the complete qualifying record set and validate the final report against the required schema. Accepting JSON does not itself establish an output-schema guarantee.","Option D — Violates Requirement: It uses the appropriate reconciliation tool, but the semantic retrieval step still lacks a completeness guarantee. Correct downstream processing cannot recover qualifying transactions omitted upstream."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/model-serving/structured-outputs"],"source_hash":"6367977cde59aa059d93752a862c1c19f6e942bc78adf3f3bccefcd773c15fb1","stem_ja":"Databricksエージェントが金融サービスのコンプライアンスチームを支援しています。夜間ジョブが、各口座の記録済み取引を銀行承認済みの管理しきい値に照らして確認し、規制当局へDelta Sharingで共有するDeltaテーブルへ結果を書き込みます。\n\n担当者は今、1つの口座を即時に独立評価し、その後、その口座の夜間結果と照合する必要があります。監査は指定条件を満たすすべての取引を識別し、完成した報告は所定のスキーマへ厳密に従わなければなりません。\n\nUnity Catalogのツールレジストリには次の5つがあります。\n\n- `delta_account_reader`：夜間ジョブが使用する管理対象Deltaテーブルを問い合わせ、監査条件に一致する最初の取引集合を返す。\n- `semantic_tx_retriever`：ベクトルインデックスから、指定監査条件と意味的に関連する口座取引を検索する。\n- `threshold_evaluator`：1口座の取引記録を受け取り、銀行承認済みの管理ロジックを適用して構造化評価結果を作る。\n- `acct_ctrl_dispatcher`：口座識別子と評価結果をJSONペイロードとして受け取り、夜間結果の取得と照合を内部実行し、完成報告用の比較結果を返す。\n- `recon_delta_probe`：口座識別子を受け取り夜間結果を返すが、差異比較と報告作成はエージェントへ任せる。\n\n要件を最もよく満たす3ツールの実行順序はどれですか。","options_ja":["`semantic_tx_retriever` → `threshold_evaluator` → `recon_delta_probe`","`delta_account_reader` → `threshold_evaluator` → `recon_delta_probe`","`delta_account_reader` → `threshold_evaluator` → `acct_ctrl_dispatcher`","`semantic_tx_retriever` → `threshold_evaluator` → `acct_ctrl_dispatcher`"],"explanation_ja":"条件に該当する全取引を漏れなく扱うので、意味類似度の上位取得ではなく、管理対象Deltaへの正確なクエリを選びます。次に承認済みロジックで独立評価し、照合を内部実行するツールへ構造化結果を渡します。似た名前でなく、入力・処理・出力の契約を読みます。最終スキーマの厳密性は、比較ツールに加えて出力検証も必要です。","distractors_ja":["意味検索では条件に合う全取引の網羅を保証できません。さらに最後のツールは夜間結果を返すだけで照合を実行しません。","正確な取引取得と独立評価は適切ですが、最後は夜間結果の取得だけです。明示的に照合を内部実行するツールの方が要件に合います。","正確な全件取得、承認済みロジックの評価、構造化結果を受けた内部照合の順です。ツールの処理内容と入出力がつながっています。","内部照合は適切でも、最初の意味検索では条件に合う取引の取りこぼしがあり得ます。網羅性の要件に合いません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-026","number":26,"form":3,"stem":"A healthcare scheduling agent on Databricks has a 5,100-token system prompt containing detailed procedures for three capabilities, each used in fewer than 2% of sessions:\n\n- Insurance eligibility checks involving a third-party payer API with variable response times.\n\n- Rescheduling fee calculations with complex branches across dozens of insurance plans and provider tiers.\n\n- Medical record release consent processes involving conditional steps and regulatory references.\n\nAppointment availability questions account for 95% of traffic. The team must reduce per-session context cost while preserving success on this primary workflow. The solution must operate within the existing Databricks agent framework without adding hosted components to administer.\n\nHow should the engineer load the three infrequently used capabilities?","options":["Summarize their instruction blocks and keep the condensed procedures permanently in the system prompt for every session.","Cache the complete system prompt server-side so subsequent sessions can reuse the capability blocks at a reduced effective billing cost.","Package the capabilities as separate Agent Skills and load each instruction block only when the user’s intent makes that skill relevant, excluding the full blocks from the baseline prompt.","Store the capability instructions in a retrieval-augmented index and insert the highest-ranked block into the user message at query time."],"correct":2,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"低頻度の専門手順とAgent Skills","concept":"ce-f3-q026","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Package the capabilities as separate Agent Skills and load each instruction block only when the user’s intent makes that skill relevant, excluding the full blocks from the baseline prompt.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe dominant workflow rarely needs these lengthy procedures. The strongest loading strategy therefore keeps them available without including their full instructions in ordinary availability requests.\n\nC directly addresses this requirement through conditional loading. It also matches the exam objective covering rarely invoked capabilities, Agent Skills, and reduced baseline context cost. \n\nThe existing runtime must support skill discovery and loading. Packaging instructions alone does not activate that behavior.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “5,100-token system prompt” — Technical signal: Detailed instructions create baseline context overhead.\n\n- “Fewer than 2% of sessions” — Usage signal: Full procedures are unnecessary in almost every session.\n\n- “95% of traffic” — Workload signal: Availability queries should receive focused context.\n\n- “Without adding hosted components” — Hard constraint: Prefer capability loading within the existing runtime.\n\n- Complex branches and conditional steps — Reliability signal: Retain detailed procedures for relevant requests rather than relying on lossy summaries.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAgent Skills use progressive disclosure: the agent initially sees lightweight names and descriptions, then loads a matching skill’s full `SKILL.md` instructions when needed. Supporting resources can also be loaded during execution. \n\nSkills can be available as local files, allowing a compatible runtime to load them without a separately hosted retrieval index. Databricks documents both local skill discovery and skill retrieval through \n\nThe saving concerns instruction tokens. Skills do not eliminate payer API latency or automatically make complex fee calculations correct.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Shorter blocks reduce tokens, but every session still receives all three procedures. Summarization may also omit exceptions or conditional steps needed when those capabilities are invoked.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Where supported, cache hits can reduce billed input cost and processing time. However, cached instructions still occupy the context window; caching does not selectively remove the rarely needed blocks.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Retrieval can supply instructions dynamically, but adds an index dependency and relies on ranking to select the appropriate procedure. Loading the highest-ranked block alone does not establish that all required workflow instructions are present. It is less direct than the skill-loading pattern targeted here.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDo not confuse reduced billing for repeated tokens with reduced baseline context. Caching can complement selective loading, but these mechanisms address different costs.\n\nIf the requirement were to reduce billing for a stable prompt that genuinely needed every instruction on every request, B could be a strong choice.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nA capability can remain available without its entire procedure being present in every request. Discoverability requires a small description; execution requires the relevant detailed instructions.\n\nComplexity and external API calls do not disqualify a capability from skill packaging. They affect how the activated workflow executes.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nKeep appointment-availability guidance in the base prompt and package the three specialized workflows separately. For fee calculations, the activated skill can direct the agent to tested calculation code or existing tools.\n\nEvaluate ordinary availability requests alongside rare-intent and mixed-intent requests. This verifies that lower baseline context consumption preserves task success and that skills activate when required.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nKeep rare procedures discoverable, and load their details when the task needs them.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nA prompt overloaded with rarely used procedures points to Agent Skills with conditional loading. Caching can lower reuse costs, while dynamic skill loading reduces the instructions carried by default.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Agent Skills — Agent Skills Overview\n\nSupports: Skill packaging, discovery metadata, and conditional loading through progressive disclosure.\n\n[Official overview](https://agentskills.io/home)\n\n- Databricks — Add tools and skills\n\nSupports: Local skill discovery and loading instructions through MCP.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-gateway/coding-agent-mcp-skills)\n\n- Anthropic — Context windows\n\nSupports: Cached prompt tokens continue to occupy context-window capacity.\n\n[Official documentation](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-windows)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Shorter blocks reduce tokens, but every session still receives all three procedures. Summarization may also omit exceptions or conditional steps needed when those capabilities are invoked.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Where supported, cache hits can reduce billed input cost and processing time. However, cached instructions still occupy the context window; caching does not selectively remove the rarely needed blocks.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Package the capabilities as separate Agent Skills and load each instruction block only when the user’s intent makes that skill relevant, excluding the full blocks from the baseline prompt.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe dominant workflow rarely needs these lengthy procedures. The strongest loading strategy therefore keeps them available without including their full instructions in ordinary availability requests.\n\nC directly addresses this requirement through conditional loading. It also matches the exam objective covering rarely invoked capabilities, Agent Skills, and reduced baseline context cost. \n\nThe existing runtime must support skill discovery and loading. Packaging instructions alone does not activate that behavior.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “5,100-token system prompt” — Technical signal: Detailed instructions create baseline context overhead.\n\n- “Fewer than 2% of sessions” — Usage signal: Full procedures are unnecessary in almost every session.\n\n- “95% of traffic” — Workload signal: Availability queries should receive focused context.\n\n- “Without adding hosted components” — Hard constraint: Prefer capability loading within the existing runtime.\n\n- Complex branches and conditional steps — Reliability signal: Retain detailed procedures for relevant requests rather than relying on lossy summaries.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAgent Skills use progressive disclosure: the agent initially sees lightweight names and descriptions, then loads a matching skill’s full `SKILL.md` instructions when needed. Supporting resources can also be loaded during execution. \n\nSkills can be available as local files, allowing a compatible runtime to load them without a separately hosted retrieval index. Databricks documents both local skill discovery and skill retrieval through \n\nThe saving concerns instruction tokens. Skills do not eliminate payer API latency or automatically make complex fee calculations correct.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Retrieval can supply instructions dynamically, but adds an index dependency and relies on ranking to select the appropriate procedure. Loading the highest-ranked block alone does not establish that all required workflow instructions are present. It is less direct than the skill-loading pattern targeted here."],"references":["https://agentskills.io/home","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-gateway/coding-agent-mcp-skills","https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-windows"],"source_hash":"e858e65d2715fc272ed5feb2a64d3644b1845035968f982ba4dc671f0b234dd9","stem_ja":"Databricks上の医療予約エージェントに、3つの機能についての詳細手順を含む5,100トークンのシステムプロンプトがあります。各機能はセッションの2%未満でしか使われません。\n\n- 応答時間が変動する外部保険支払者APIを使った保険資格確認。\n- 数十の保険プランと医療提供者の階層をまたぐ複雑な分岐を持つ、予約変更手数料の計算。\n- 条件付きの段階と規制参照を含む、診療記録開示の同意手続き。\n\n予約空き状況の質問がトラフィックの95%を占めます。チームはこの主要ワークフローの成功を保ちながら、セッションごとのコンテキストコストを減らす必要があります。既存のDatabricksエージェントフレームワーク内で動作し、管理するホスト型コンポーネントを追加してはいけません。\n\nまれに使う3機能をどのように読み込むべきですか。","options_ja":["指示ブロックを要約し、短くした手順をすべてのセッションのシステムプロンプトに常時保持する。","完全なシステムプロンプトをサーバー側でキャッシュし、後続セッションが機能ブロックを実効的に低い課金コストで再利用できるようにする。","各機能を別々のAgent Skillsにまとめ、ユーザーの意図から該当するときだけ指示ブロックを読み込み、基礎プロンプトから完全なブロックを除外する。","機能の指示を検索拡張用インデックスに格納し、問い合わせ時に最上位のブロックをユーザーメッセージへ挿入する。"],"explanation_ja":"まれな機能の詳細を、主要な予約照会のたびに払う必要はありません。別々のAgent Skillsにして、関連するときだけ詳細を開けば、通常のコンテキストを小さく保てます。キャッシュは課金面を改善してもモデルが読む量を減らしません。外部の検索基盤を追加せず、既存フレームワークで必要時に機能を読み込む点も要件に合います。","distractors_ja":["短くしても全セッションで不要な指示を読み込みます。複雑な規制条件を要約で落とすおそれもあり、必要時読み込みがより直接的です。","キャッシュによる料金や遅延の改善と、モデルのコンテキスト消費は別です。全指示が注意を使い続ける問題が残ります。","関連機能の詳細だけを必要時に使い、95%の通常業務の基礎コンテキストを軽量に保てます。追加の検索基盤も不要です。","検索索引の運用と検索品質への依存を追加します。最高順位の1ブロックが必要手順を確実に満たすとは限らず、Skillsがより適合します。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-027","number":27,"form":3,"stem":"A financial services research agent on Databricks develops competitor profiles over several days and multiple sessions. Conversation compaction successfully manages context within each session.\n\nHowever, MLflow traces reveal that starting a new session for the same research task causes the agent to repeat completed company analyses and reconstruct its research framework. The team requires the workload to span sessions rather than placing the entire multi-day history in one context window.\n\nWhich approach should preserve coherent progress across these session boundaries?","options":["Increase the compaction trigger threshold so more conversation accumulates before each session is summarized and reinitialized.","Insert the complete message history from previous sessions into the next session’s system prompt before resuming work.","Persist structured progress notes to durable external memory when each session ends, then load those notes into the context at the beginning of the next session.","Index complete session transcripts in Databricks AI Search and retrieve every previous transcript chunk into each new session’s context."],"correct":2,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"複数セッションをまたぐ構造化進捗メモ","concept":"ce-f3-q027","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Persist structured progress notes to durable external memory when each session ends, then load those notes into the context at the beginning of the next session.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure occurs at the session boundary, while within-session compaction already works. The missing capability is durable transfer of task progress into the next session.\n\nC provides both required operations: persist the useful state and reload it before continuing. It also respects the context constraint by carrying forward structured progress rather than the complete conversation.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Several days and multiple sessions” — Persistence requirement: Task state must outlive an individual session.\n\n- “Compaction successfully manages context” — Exclusion signal: Retuning compaction does not directly address the observed failure.\n\n- Repeated completed analyses — Failure signal: Completion status is missing from the new session.\n\n- Reconstructed research framework — Failure signal: Prior methodological decisions are also missing.\n\n- “Rather than…entire multi-day history” — Hard constraint: Full-history replay is unsuitable.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nStructured notes can preserve the information needed to resume:\n\n- The research objective and agreed framework.\n\n- Companies already analyzed and their completion status.\n\n- Key findings with references to supporting evidence.\n\n- Unresolved questions and the next planned actions.\n\nPersisting these notes makes them available after the session ends; loading them makes them usable in the next session. Anthropic describes this pattern as structured note-taking outside the context window, with notes retrieved later to preserve progress. \n\nKeep the continuation notes concise and current. Detailed evidence can remain externally accessible through references.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: Changing when compaction triggers does not establish durable storage or restoration across sessions. A higher threshold also does not inherently improve which information the summary retains.\n\n- Option B — Violates Requirement: Replaying every prior message recreates the growing multi-day workload inside the new context window. Changing its placement to the system prompt does not reduce its size.\n\n- Option D — Violates Requirement: External transcript storage is feasible, but retrieving every chunk defeats the context constraint. The problem is the exhaustive reload, not the use of AI Search itself.\n\nExam Trap\n\nExternal storage alone does not solve context growth. Examine what the agent loads back into its active window.\n\nD would become more appropriate if it retrieved only relevant evidence on demand while a compact progress record established what had already been completed.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nContext management requires both persistence and selection. Persistence keeps information available after a session ends; selection determines which information enters the next context window.\n\nDatabricks distinguishes interaction-scoped session state from durable information carried across conversations. Lakebase can support persistent agent state and memory. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nStore a progress record keyed by the research task in Lakebase. Update completed-company status, framework decisions, evidence references, and next actions before ending the session. Initialize the next session from that record.\n\nFor workflows subject to unexpected interruption, saving at meaningful milestones also reduces dependence on a successful session-end write.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nPersist the progress needed to continue, then reload it before resuming.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nSuccessful compaction plus forgetting between sessions points to missing cross-session persistence and restoration. Choose durable structured progress notes, with a bounded reload into each new session.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Agent memory and sessions\n\nSupports: The distinction between session state and information retained across conversations.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)\n\n- Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents\n\nSupports: Structured note-taking outside the context window and restoring notes after context resets.\n\n[Official engineering guidance](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: Changing when compaction triggers does not establish durable storage or restoration across sessions. A higher threshold also does not inherently improve which information the summary retains.","Option B — Violates Requirement: Replaying every prior message recreates the growing multi-day workload inside the new context window. Changing its placement to the system prompt does not reduce its size.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Persist structured progress notes to durable external memory when each session ends, then load those notes into the context at the beginning of the next session.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure occurs at the session boundary, while within-session compaction already works. The missing capability is durable transfer of task progress into the next session.\n\nC provides both required operations: persist the useful state and reload it before continuing. It also respects the context constraint by carrying forward structured progress rather than the complete conversation.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Several days and multiple sessions” — Persistence requirement: Task state must outlive an individual session.\n\n- “Compaction successfully manages context” — Exclusion signal: Retuning compaction does not directly address the observed failure.\n\n- Repeated completed analyses — Failure signal: Completion status is missing from the new session.\n\n- Reconstructed research framework — Failure signal: Prior methodological decisions are also missing.\n\n- “Rather than…entire multi-day history” — Hard constraint: Full-history replay is unsuitable.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nStructured notes can preserve the information needed to resume:\n\n- The research objective and agreed framework.\n\n- Companies already analyzed and their completion status.\n\n- Key findings with references to supporting evidence.\n\n- Unresolved questions and the next planned actions.\n\nPersisting these notes makes them available after the session ends; loading them makes them usable in the next session. Anthropic describes this pattern as structured note-taking outside the context window, with notes retrieved later to preserve progress. \n\nKeep the continuation notes concise and current. Detailed evidence can remain externally accessible through references.","Option D — Violates Requirement: External transcript storage is feasible, but retrieving every chunk defeats the context constraint. The problem is the exhaustive reload, not the use of AI Search itself."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"70df484eb0f9fd08e0df701d3ebef35b791726d25a665967e47f622517e5554d","stem_ja":"Databricks上の金融サービス調査エージェントが、数日間・複数セッションにわたり競合企業のプロフィールを作成しています。会話の圧縮は各セッション内のコンテキストを適切に管理しています。\n\nしかしMLflowトレースを見ると、同じ調査タスクでも新しいセッションを始めるたびに、完了した企業分析をやり直し、調査の枠組みも再構築しています。チームは、数日分の履歴全体を1つのコンテキストウィンドウに入れるのではなく、セッションをまたいで作業することを求めています。\n\nこれらのセッション境界を越えて、一貫した進捗を保持する方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["圧縮の開始しきい値を上げ、各セッションを要約・再初期化する前に、より多くの会話が蓄積するようにする。","作業再開前に、過去のセッションの完全なメッセージ履歴を次のセッションのシステムプロンプトへ挿入する。","各セッション終了時に構造化した進捗メモを永続的な外部メモリへ保存し、次のセッションの開始時にそのメモをコンテキストへ読み込む。","完全なセッション記録をDatabricks AI Searchへ索引化し、新しいセッションのたびに過去の全記録チャンクをコンテキストへ取得する。"],"explanation_ja":"セッション内の圧縮が成功しても、その成果が次のセッションへ自動で渡るとは限りません。外部へ構造化した進捗・完了済み分析・次の作業を保存し、再開時に読み戻す必要があります。全履歴の投入は、窓を分けたいという要件にも反します。「圧縮は今の窓、永続メモリは次の窓」を区別します。","distractors_ja":["しきい値は同じセッション内の圧縮タイミングを変えるだけです。終了後に進捗を保存して読み戻す仕組みにはなりません。","全履歴を毎回投入するとコンテキストが増え続けます。セッションをまたいで小さな作業状態を引き継ぐという要件に合いません。","確定した進捗を外部に残し、次回の開始時に読み戻すことで、完了作業と研究方針を引き継ぎ、やり直しを減らせます。","記録の索引化だけでなく、結局すべてを読み込むのでコンテキスト問題が再発します。必要な構造化進捗を選んで渡す方が適切です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-028","number":28,"form":3,"stem":"A memory-augmented Databricks agent stores session memories in a Lakebase OLTP Postgres table. Records include `user_id`, `session_timestamp`, `preference_category`, and `confirmed_preference_value`.\n\nThe agent must return every confirmed preference recorded for one specified user during the last 30 days. An additional Databricks AI Search index contains conversation-summary embeddings derived from a Delta table synchronized from Lakebase.\n\nWhich retrieval mechanism best fits this request?","options":["Combine AI Search similarity ranking with structured post-filtering, because hybrid retrieval improves recall for time-bounded Lakebase queries.","Query the Lakebase Postgres table directly using an exact `user_id` predicate and a bounded `session_timestamp` range, without similarity ranking.","Search the memory embeddings through AI Search with a date-range metadata filter, because prefiltered semantic search is the preferred mechanism for time-bounded memory retrieval.","Query Lakebase using exact predicates on `user_id`, `session_id`, and `preference_category`, because combining these attributes provides the most precise user-memory lookup."],"correct":1,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"Lakebaseの正確な日時範囲検索","concept":"ce-f3-q028","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Query the Lakebase Postgres table directly using an exact `user_id` predicate and a bounded `session_timestamp` range, without similarity ranking.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe request defines membership through structured conditions: a specific user, confirmed preferences, and a 30-day interval. It asks for every matching record, with no semantic relevance criterion.\n\nA direct structured query matches that requirement. The availability of embeddings does not create a need to use them.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Every confirmed preference” — Completeness requirement: Return the qualifying set rather than a relevance-ranked subset.\n\n- “One specified user” — Exact-match constraint: Filter on `user_id`.\n\n- “Last 30 days” — Range constraint: Filter `session_timestamp` using the required time boundaries.\n\n- Named relational columns — Technical signal: The selection criteria are already represented as structured data.\n\n- Additional embeddings index — Distractor signal: An available source is not automatically the appropriate source.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nSQL predicates can select records by user identity and timestamp range directly. PostgreSQL’s `WHERE` clause retains rows satisfying the specified conditions. \n\nThe implementation should:\n\n- Bind the specified `user_id`.\n\n- Apply the 30-day timestamp interval.\n\n- Enforce the table’s actual confirmation criterion.\n\n- Return the complete result set, handling pagination if necessary.\n\nThe confirmation representation is not fully defined in the question. Do not assume that a non-null `confirmed_preference_value` proves confirmation unless the schema establishes that rule.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Different Use Case: Similarity ranking is useful when meaning determines relevance. Here, post-filtering a ranked candidate set can discard records without recovering qualifying records that never entered that set. Adding similarity does not inherently improve completeness for exact predicates.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: AI Search supports metadata filters, but a date filter alone does not establish the required user and confirmation conditions. Semantic ranking also adds no requested selection capability. AI Search supports filter-only queries too; the issue is choosing the simplest mechanism that directly satisfies this task.\n\n- Option D — Violates Requirement: Restricting retrieval to a session or category could exclude preferences from other sessions or categories within the requested period. It also omits the required timestamp range. Additional predicates do not improve correctness when they change the requested set.\n\nExam Trap\n\n“More precise” does not mean “more filters.” Correct filtering includes the requested constraints without introducing restrictions that remove valid results.\n\nIf the user instead asked for memories conceptually related to a shopping interest, semantic retrieval—combined with appropriate user and date filters—would become relevant.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nDistinguish eligibility from relevance. Structured predicates determine whether a record belongs in an exactly defined set. Similarity ranking determines how closely content matches a meaning-based query.\n\nThis request defines eligibility completely enough to choose structured retrieval.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nExpose a parameterized Lakebase query tool accepting the user identifier and time boundaries. Return all qualifying confirmed preferences across sessions and categories.\n\nKeep the embeddings index available for requests that require semantic discovery, such as recalling earlier discussions about comfortable travel clothing.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nExact identity plus a date range calls for predicates; meaning-based relevance calls for search.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\n“All records for this user within this period” points to structured query retrieval. Choose B, apply the confirmation criterion, and avoid unrequested session or category restrictions.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- PostgreSQL — Table Expressions\n\nSupports: Selecting rows through explicit `WHERE` predicates.\n\n[Official documentation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/queries-table-expressions.html)\n\n- Databricks — Query an AI Search index\n\nSupports: Similarity retrieval, metadata filtering, filter-only queries, and result limits.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Different Use Case: Similarity ranking is useful when meaning determines relevance. Here, post-filtering a ranked candidate set can discard records without recovering qualifying records that never entered that set. Adding similarity does not inherently improve completeness for exact predicates.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Query the Lakebase Postgres table directly using an exact `user_id` predicate and a bounded `session_timestamp` range, without similarity ranking.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe request defines membership through structured conditions: a specific user, confirmed preferences, and a 30-day interval. It asks for every matching record, with no semantic relevance criterion.\n\nA direct structured query matches that requirement. The availability of embeddings does not create a need to use them.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Every confirmed preference” — Completeness requirement: Return the qualifying set rather than a relevance-ranked subset.\n\n- “One specified user” — Exact-match constraint: Filter on `user_id`.\n\n- “Last 30 days” — Range constraint: Filter `session_timestamp` using the required time boundaries.\n\n- Named relational columns — Technical signal: The selection criteria are already represented as structured data.\n\n- Additional embeddings index — Distractor signal: An available source is not automatically the appropriate source.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nSQL predicates can select records by user identity and timestamp range directly. PostgreSQL’s `WHERE` clause retains rows satisfying the specified conditions. \n\nThe implementation should:\n\n- Bind the specified `user_id`.\n\n- Apply the 30-day timestamp interval.\n\n- Enforce the table’s actual confirmation criterion.\n\n- Return the complete result set, handling pagination if necessary.\n\nThe confirmation representation is not fully defined in the question. Do not assume that a non-null `confirmed_preference_value` proves confirmation unless the schema establishes that rule.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: AI Search supports metadata filters, but a date filter alone does not establish the required user and confirmation conditions. Semantic ranking also adds no requested selection capability. AI Search supports filter-only queries too; the issue is choosing the simplest mechanism that directly satisfies this task.","Option D — Violates Requirement: Restricting retrieval to a session or category could exclude preferences from other sessions or categories within the requested period. It also omits the required timestamp range. Additional predicates do not improve correctness when they change the requested set."],"references":["https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/queries-table-expressions.html","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search"],"source_hash":"6c5c4a59a6711553c178a11176df67c4d4e428e2c9c9051a68b4073d19426a18","stem_ja":"メモリ拡張型のDatabricksエージェントが、LakebaseのOLTP Postgresテーブルへセッションメモリを保存しています。レコードには`user_id`、`session_timestamp`、`preference_category`、`confirmed_preference_value`があります。\n\nエージェントは、指定された1人のユーザーについて、直近30日間に記録された確定済みの好みをすべて返す必要があります。追加のDatabricks AI Searchインデックスには、Lakebaseから同期したDeltaテーブルに由来する会話要約の埋め込みが入っています。\n\nこの要求に最も適した取得方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["時間範囲付きのLakebaseクエリではハイブリッド取得が再現率を改善するため、AI Searchの類似度順位付けと構造化された事後フィルタリングを組み合わせる。","Lakebase Postgresテーブルへ、正確な`user_id`の条件と範囲を限定した`session_timestamp`の条件を直接問い合わせ、類似度による順位付けは行わない。","時間範囲付きメモリ取得では事前フィルター付き意味検索が推奨されるため、AI Searchでメモリ埋め込みを検索し、日付範囲のメタデータフィルターを使う。","これらの属性を組み合わせると最も精密なユーザーメモリ照会になるため、`user_id`、`session_id`、`preference_category`の完全一致条件でLakebaseへ問い合わせる。"],"explanation_ja":"誰の、いつの記録かが厳密に決まっており、条件に合う全件が必要です。LakebaseへユーザーIDと日時範囲で直接クエリするのが適切で、意味的に近い順に上位だけを返す必要はありません。指定されていないセッションやカテゴリで絞ると必要な記録を落とします。「正確な条件で全件ならSQL、似た意味を探すなら検索」と覚えます。","distractors_ja":["意味的な順位付けは要求されていません。上位候補と事後フィルターを挟むと、条件に合う全件を返す処理がかえって複雑になります。","ユーザーIDと直近30日の範囲が、そのまま構造化クエリの条件になります。正確な集合の取得に類似度計算は不要です。","日付で絞れても、意味検索は類似性による取得です。今回のような全件を要求する正確な日時範囲検索に優先すべき理由はありません。","要求は1つのセッションやカテゴリに限定されていません。不要な条件を加えると、直近30日の別セッションや別カテゴリを取りこぼします。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-029","number":29,"form":3,"stem":"A context engineer is developing an MCP server that exposes 30 tools to a Databricks agent. To satisfy a strict token budget, the architecture uses progressive disclosure: at startup, the agent receives only tool names and one-sentence descriptions.\n\nA user asks the agent to query a particular Unity Catalog table. After examining the lightweight catalog, the agent identifies one likely tool and proceeds to the inspect layer.\n\nWhat should the agent do at this layer?","options":["Invoke the selected tool immediately, inferring its arguments from the brief description because catalog discovery has already identified the tool.","Retrieve the complete schemas and documentation for the selected tool, then use its schema to assemble a valid invocation.","Retrieve an expanded description and usage examples for the selected tool, leaving parameter types to be loaded during execution.","Retrieve complete schemas for all 30 tools and compare their parameter definitions before constructing the selected tool’s invocation."],"correct":1,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"MCPの発見・検査・実行の段階","concept":"ce-f3-q029","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Retrieve the complete schemas and documentation for the selected tool, then use its schema to assemble a valid invocation.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe agent has identified a likely capability, but it has not yet obtained the information needed to construct valid arguments. The next step must provide that detail while preserving selective loading.\n\nB satisfies both requirements: inspect the selected tool’s contract, then prepare the call. A skips inspection, C postpones essential schema information, and D loads unrelated definitions.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Only tool names and one-sentence descriptions” — Technical signal: Discovery metadata does not establish parameter names, types, or requirements.\n\n- “One likely tool” — Technical signal: Detailed inspection can be limited to that candidate.\n\n- “Inspect layer” — Sequence instruction: Understand the invocation contract before execution.\n\n- “Strict token budget” — Hard constraint: Avoid loading every tool’s complete definition unnecessarily.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe selected tool’s input schema identifies the arguments it accepts, including required fields, types, and applicable constraints. Its documentation clarifies intended usage and helps confirm that it fits the request.\n\nLoading these details for one candidate gives the agent enough information to construct a schema-conforming call without placing all 30 definitions into context. Execution and server-side validation remain subsequent responsibilities; reading a schema does not itself execute the tool or establish authorization.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: Identifying a tool does not establish its calling convention. A short description may indicate its purpose while omitting required arguments or accepted formats. Guessing bypasses the inspection needed to construct a valid request.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: Examples and richer descriptions can help explain usage, but they do not replace authoritative parameter definitions. Deferring types until execution leaves the agent constructing arguments without the complete contract.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Loading every schema could expose the selected tool’s definition, but it defeats the intended selective-disclosure pattern. Other tools’ parameters do not determine whether this tool’s arguments are valid. Broader inspection is warranted only if candidate selection remains unresolved.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is C: additional examples appear to provide inspection detail. The decisive distinction is whether the agent obtains the actual invocation schema before constructing the call.\n\nExamples can supplement a schema; they should not substitute for required parameter definitions.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nProgressive disclosure separates capability discovery, interface inspection, and execution. Each stage reveals the detail needed for its immediate decision.\n\nThese are architectural stages rather than mandatory MCP method names. MCP tool definitions include schemas; the mechanism exposing selective inspection is implementation-specific.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nFor a table-query request, inspection could establish whether the chosen tool expects a fully qualified table identifier, SQL text, or another input structure. Constructing the request from that contract would reduce avoidable argument errors while loading detailed context for only the relevant tool.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nDiscover the capability, inspect its contract, then construct the call.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nNames and brief descriptions support tool selection; schemas support valid invocation. At inspection, load the selected tool’s detailed definition before preparing execution.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: Progressive disclosure through staged tool discovery and context-efficient execution.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Anthropic — Code execution with MCP for efficient agents\n\nSupports: Loading tool definitions on demand and exposing different detail levels, including full schemas.\n\n[Official article](https://www.anthropic.com/research/code-execution-with-mcp)\n\n- Model Context Protocol — Tools\n\nSupports: Tool definitions, input schemas, discovery, and invocation with an arguments object.\n\n[Official specification](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: Identifying a tool does not establish its calling convention. A short description may indicate its purpose while omitting required arguments or accepted formats. Guessing bypasses the inspection needed to construct a valid request.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Retrieve the complete schemas and documentation for the selected tool, then use its schema to assemble a valid invocation.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe agent has identified a likely capability, but it has not yet obtained the information needed to construct valid arguments. The next step must provide that detail while preserving selective loading.\n\nB satisfies both requirements: inspect the selected tool’s contract, then prepare the call. A skips inspection, C postpones essential schema information, and D loads unrelated definitions.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Only tool names and one-sentence descriptions” — Technical signal: Discovery metadata does not establish parameter names, types, or requirements.\n\n- “One likely tool” — Technical signal: Detailed inspection can be limited to that candidate.\n\n- “Inspect layer” — Sequence instruction: Understand the invocation contract before execution.\n\n- “Strict token budget” — Hard constraint: Avoid loading every tool’s complete definition unnecessarily.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe selected tool’s input schema identifies the arguments it accepts, including required fields, types, and applicable constraints. Its documentation clarifies intended usage and helps confirm that it fits the request.\n\nLoading these details for one candidate gives the agent enough information to construct a schema-conforming call without placing all 30 definitions into context. Execution and server-side validation remain subsequent responsibilities; reading a schema does not itself execute the tool or establish authorization.","Option C — Incorrect: Examples and richer descriptions can help explain usage, but they do not replace authoritative parameter definitions. Deferring types until execution leaves the agent constructing arguments without the complete contract.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Loading every schema could expose the selected tool’s definition, but it defeats the intended selective-disclosure pattern. Other tools’ parameters do not determine whether this tool’s arguments are valid. Broader inspection is warranted only if candidate selection remains unresolved."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://www.anthropic.com/research/code-execution-with-mcp","https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools"],"source_hash":"cb5db0a5be7930544a28d16eb5cced393fb1d03aa4c47c1e54cb05d8a57aa5d4","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、Databricksエージェントへ30個のツールを公開するMCPサーバーを開発しています。厳しいトークン予算を満たすため、段階的開示を採用し、起動時にはツール名と1文の説明だけをエージェントへ渡します。\n\nユーザーが特定のUnity Catalogテーブルを問い合わせるよう依頼しました。エージェントは軽量なカタログを調べて有望なツールを1つ見つけ、inspect層へ進みます。\n\nこの層で行うべき処理はどれですか。","options_ja":["カタログの発見でツールが特定できたので、短い説明から引数を推測し、選んだツールを直ちに呼び出す。","選んだツールの完全なスキーマと文書を取得し、そのスキーマを使って有効な呼び出しを組み立てる。","選んだツールの詳しい説明と使用例を取得し、パラメーターの型は実行時に読み込む。","選んだツールの呼び出しを組み立てる前に、30個すべての完全なスキーマを取得してパラメーター定義を比較する。"],"explanation_ja":"段階的開示は、最初に軽い情報で候補を探し、選んだ候補だけを詳しく調べてから実行する流れです。inspect層では、引数名・型・必須項目などの完全な契約を読みます。実行時まで型を後回しにしたり、短い説明から推測したりしません。「発見は名前、検査はスキーマ、実行は検証した引数」と整理します。","distractors_ja":["ツール名の発見と、有効な引数を組み立てる準備は別です。推測した引数では型や必須条件を満たさない可能性があります。","選択した候補だけの完全な定義を取得するので、トークンを抑えつつ正しい引数を準備できます。inspectの役割に一致します。","使用例だけでは必須項目や型の全契約を保証できません。呼び出しを作る前に必要なスキーマを読んでおくべきです。","候補を1つに絞った後で全30個の定義を読むのは、段階的開示による節約を失わせます。必要な候補だけ詳しく読みます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-030","number":30,"form":3,"stem":"An engineer is comparing four agent configurations in an MLflow 3 experiment to identify a reliable candidate for production question answering. The run metadata reveals several evaluation differences:\n\n- Run 1 used a 200-question benchmark. Runs 2–4 used an expanded 350-question benchmark containing additional edge cases.\n\n- `context_recall` was measured with `k=3` for Runs 1–2 and `k=10` for Runs 3–4.\n\n- Each run used different LLM-judge prompts for `answer_correctness` and `faithfulness` as the evaluation rubrics evolved.\n\nThe recorded scores differ substantially. What should the engineer do before choosing a configuration?","options":["Rank the configurations using only `context_recall`, because retrieval metrics are unaffected by changes to the LLM-judge prompts.","Evaluate all four configurations again on the 350-question benchmark, retaining each configuration’s previous evaluation cutoff and judge prompts.","Evaluate all four configurations again on one shared benchmark, using the same `context_recall` cutoff and the same judge-prompt versions across runs, then compare the results.","Normalize each metric to a common numerical range, such as through min-max scaling, and rank the configurations using the transformed scores."],"correct":2,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"MLflow実験の公平な比較条件","concept":"ce-f3-q030","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Evaluate all four configurations again on one shared benchmark, using the same `context_recall` cutoff and the same judge-prompt versions across runs, then compare the results.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scores reflect changes to both the agent configurations and the evaluation process. Consequently, the engineer cannot reliably attribute score differences to configuration quality.\n\nC standardizes all three identified measurement differences: benchmark composition, retrieval evaluation cutoff, and judge criteria. Databricks explicitly recommends comparing application versions using the same evaluation dataset and judges. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- 200 versus 350 questions — Evaluation inconsistency: The runs face different question distributions and edge-case coverage.\n\n- `k=3` versus `k=10` — Metric-definition inconsistency: Retrieval coverage is assessed at different cutoffs.\n\n- Different judge prompts — Rubric inconsistency: Identical answers could receive different assessments.\n\n- “Before choosing a configuration” — Sequence requirement: Establish comparability before ranking candidates.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA controlled comparison changes the candidate configuration while keeping the measurement process consistent.\n\nFor the new evaluations:\n\n- Use one fixed benchmark and its expected answers.\n\n- Apply one shared evaluation cutoff for `context_recall`.\n\n- Use an unchanged prompt version for each judge metric across all four configurations.\n\nThe correctness judge and faithfulness judge can retain different purposes; each metric’s rubric must remain consistent between runs. MLflow supports custom judge prompts and version tracking as evaluation criteria evolve. \n\nThe 350-question benchmark is a sensible shared choice because it includes the added edge cases, although C’s essential requirement is a common evaluation protocol.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: Avoiding judge-based metrics removes only one inconsistency. `context_recall` still uses different cutoffs and, for Run 1, a different benchmark. Retrieval coverage alone also does not establish answer correctness or faithfulness.\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: Standardizing the dataset is necessary, but retaining different cutoffs and judge prompts leaves two measurement differences unresolved. Nothing establishes that dataset size is the largest source of variation.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Normalization changes the numerical scale, not the questions, retrieval cutoff, or assessment criteria. It cannot make measurements from different protocols equivalent.\n\nExam Trap\n\nA shared metric name does not guarantee a shared measurement. Check its parameters, evaluated records, and scorer version.\n\nNormalization would be useful when combining comparable metrics with different numerical scales. It cannot repair inconsistent evaluation conditions.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nAn evaluation protocol is part of the measurement instrument. Its dataset, metric parameters, and grading rubric determine what a score means.\n\nKeep evaluation settings distinct from the agent configuration being tested. Otherwise, a score improvement could reflect a changed test rather than a better agent.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nRecord the benchmark version, retrieval evaluation cutoff, judge-prompt versions, and judge model alongside each MLflow evaluation run. Re-evaluate every candidate under that fixed protocol and inspect both aggregate results and edge-case failures.\n\nThis creates a comparison that reviewers can reproduce and interpret.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nSame questions, same cutoff, same rubrics—then compare configurations.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen evaluation datasets, metric parameters, or judge prompts differ, the logged scores are not directly comparable. Standardize the evaluation protocol and rerun all candidates before selecting a winner.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Tutorial: Evaluate and improve an agent\n\nSupports: Comparing application versions with the same dataset and judges.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app)\n\n- MLflow — LLM Judges and Scorers\n\nSupports: Configurable evaluation criteria, custom judge prompts, and judge version tracking.\n\n[Official documentation](https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/genai/eval-monitor/scorers/)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: Avoiding judge-based metrics removes only one inconsistency. `context_recall` still uses different cutoffs and, for Run 1, a different benchmark. Retrieval coverage alone also does not establish answer correctness or faithfulness.","Option B — Incorrect: Standardizing the dataset is necessary, but retaining different cutoffs and judge prompts leaves two measurement differences unresolved. Nothing establishes that dataset size is the largest source of variation.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Evaluate all four configurations again on one shared benchmark, using the same `context_recall` cutoff and the same judge-prompt versions across runs, then compare the results.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe scores reflect changes to both the agent configurations and the evaluation process. Consequently, the engineer cannot reliably attribute score differences to configuration quality.\n\nC standardizes all three identified measurement differences: benchmark composition, retrieval evaluation cutoff, and judge criteria. Databricks explicitly recommends comparing application versions using the same evaluation dataset and judges. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- 200 versus 350 questions — Evaluation inconsistency: The runs face different question distributions and edge-case coverage.\n\n- `k=3` versus `k=10` — Metric-definition inconsistency: Retrieval coverage is assessed at different cutoffs.\n\n- Different judge prompts — Rubric inconsistency: Identical answers could receive different assessments.\n\n- “Before choosing a configuration” — Sequence requirement: Establish comparability before ranking candidates.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA controlled comparison changes the candidate configuration while keeping the measurement process consistent.\n\nFor the new evaluations:\n\n- Use one fixed benchmark and its expected answers.\n\n- Apply one shared evaluation cutoff for `context_recall`.\n\n- Use an unchanged prompt version for each judge metric across all four configurations.\n\nThe correctness judge and faithfulness judge can retain different purposes; each metric’s rubric must remain consistent between runs. MLflow supports custom judge prompts and version tracking as evaluation criteria evolve. \n\nThe 350-question benchmark is a sensible shared choice because it includes the added edge cases, although C’s essential requirement is a common evaluation protocol.","Option D — Incorrect: Normalization changes the numerical scale, not the questions, retrieval cutoff, or assessment criteria. It cannot make measurements from different protocols equivalent."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/eval-monitor/evaluate-app","https://mlflow.org/docs/latest/genai/eval-monitor/scorers/"],"source_hash":"8b319844c727d21f50712216aaa8c8cc675e1c3cc5de462a596c5b7522093ce3","stem_ja":"エンジニアが、本番の質問応答に信頼できる候補を選ぶため、MLflow 3の実験で4つのエージェント設定を比較しています。実行メタデータから、評価条件に次の違いが見つかりました。\n\n- Run 1は200問のベンチマークを使用。Run 2〜4は、追加の境界事例を含む350問へ拡張したベンチマークを使用。\n- `context_recall`は、Run 1〜2で`k=3`、Run 3〜4で`k=10`として測定。\n- 評価基準の更新に伴い、`answer_correctness`と`faithfulness`のLLM評価者のプロンプトが各Runで異なる。\n\n記録されたスコアには大きな差があります。設定を選ぶ前に何をすべきですか。","options_ja":["検索指標はLLM評価者のプロンプトの変更に影響されないため、`context_recall`だけを使って設定を順位付けする。","350問のベンチマークで4設定を再評価するが、評価の打ち切り値と評価者プロンプトは各設定の以前のものを維持する。","1つの共通ベンチマークで4設定を再評価し、`context_recall`の打ち切り値と評価者プロンプトのバージョンも全Runで統一してから比較する。","min-maxスケーリングなどで各指標を共通の数値範囲へ正規化し、変換したスコアで設定を順位付けする。"],"explanation_ja":"問題集合、検索のk、評価者の採点基準が違えば、点数差が設定差か評価条件差か分かりません。共通のデータと同じ測定手順で再評価し、初めて設定を公平に比較できます。数値のスケールをそろえるだけでは、測っている対象や基準の違いは消えません。「同じものさし・同じ問題で比べる」が要点です。","distractors_ja":["LLM評価者と別の指標でも、検索のkと問題集合が異なります。検索指標だけに絞っても比較条件は統一されません。","問題集合だけ統一しても、検索の打ち切り値と評価者が違うままです。残った交絡要因で順位が変わる可能性があります。","ベンチマーク、検索条件、評価者を固定することで、スコアの違いを設定の違いとして評価しやすくなります。","範囲を変換しても、異なるデータや採点基準で得た数値の意味はそろいません。測定条件から統一する必要があります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-031","number":31,"form":3,"stem":"A Databricks customer support agent keeps a running summary of conversations from all users and sessions in one vector index. On every turn, it uses semantic search over that index to reconstruct conversational context.\n\nUsers report that the agent forgets information supplied earlier in their current conversation and sometimes introduces unrelated information from other users’ previous sessions. Response latency has also increased as the conversation collection grows.\n\nWhich memory configuration best corrects the underlying mismatch?","options":["Partition the vector index by user ID and session ID, restricting retrieval to the current session while continuing to store users’ long-term facts in the index.","Maintain a session-specific short-term buffer containing the conversation’s turns, and use the vector store for durable user facts that should be recalled across sessions through semantic search.","Move the data into a relational table partitioned by session ID and fetch every row for the current session on each turn to serve both short-term and long-term memory requirements.","Maintain a session-specific buffer for current-turn context and write a complete conversation summary to the vector index after every turn so semantic retrieval remains current across active sessions."],"correct":1,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"短期セッション状態と長期ユーザーメモリの分離","concept":"ce-f3-q031","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Maintain a session-specific short-term buffer containing the conversation’s turns, and use the vector store for durable user facts that should be recalled across sessions through semantic search.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe agent is using semantic retrieval as its primary mechanism for remembering an ongoing conversation. That can omit earlier session facts and introduce unrelated material.\n\nB assigns each memory type its appropriate role: session state maintains conversational continuity, while long-term retrieval supplies relevant facts from previous sessions. Databricks explicitly distinguishes these two scopes. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Earlier in their current conversation” — Memory-scope signal: The missing information belongs in short-term session state.\n\n- “All users and sessions in one vector index” — Isolation signal: Retrieval needs appropriate user and session boundaries.\n\n- “Semantic search…on every turn” — Architecture signal: Relevance ranking is substituting for conversation-state retention.\n\n- Increasing latency — Performance signal: The design repeatedly searches accumulated history to recover current conversational context.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA session buffer preserves the conversation’s ordered turns without requiring each earlier fact to win a semantic similarity search. Long-term retrieval can then contribute selected, durable facts when relevant.\n\nLangChain documents short-term memory as thread-level state, with separation between conversations and persistence through a checkpointer. \n\nNecessary boundary: Separating memory types does not automatically prevent cross-user disclosure. Both session access and long-term retrieval must be scoped to the authorized user.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: User and session filtering can reduce unrelated retrieval. However, the current conversation still depends on semantic search, which can omit necessary turns. Filtering alone does not correct that memory-role mismatch.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: A relational database can validly persist session history. The weakness is treating a current-session query as the solution for both memory scopes and loading every row on every turn. It does not provide the proposed selective recall of durable cross-session facts.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Retaining current-turn context does not establish continuity across turns. Repeated summary writes also leave the agent dependent on semantic retrieval and do not establish user isolation.\n\nExam Trap\n\n“Short-term” describes the memory’s conversational scope, not a requirement to store it only in RAM. A database-backed session history can still be short-term memory.\n\nLikewise, retaining session turns does not require placing an indefinitely growing transcript verbatim into every model request. Long sessions may still need context trimming or compaction.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nChoose memory by purpose:\n\n- Session state: What has happened in this conversation?\n\n- Long-term memory: What previously learned information matters to this user now?\n\nStorage technology alone does not determine the memory type. Scope, retention, and retrieval behavior determine its role.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nUse trusted application identity to select the user and session, maintain that session’s conversation state, and retrieve long-term facts only within the user’s authorized scope. Databricks advises deriving session ownership from trusted application context. \n\nThis avoids making current-session continuity depend on a search over every user’s accumulated conversations.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nKeep the current conversation in session state; selectively recall durable facts across sessions.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nForgetting current-session facts while retrieving unrelated historical details indicates a memory-scope mismatch. Choose session-scoped short-term memory plus selective, user-scoped long-term retrieval.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Agent memory and sessions\n\nSupports: Separate roles for session state and durable cross-session memory.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)\n\n- LangChain — Short-term memory\n\nSupports: Thread-scoped conversation state, persistence, and context-length management.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/short-term-memory)\n\n- Databricks — Agent sessions\n\nSupports: Session ownership derived from trusted identity. This managed feature is currently Beta; the answer does not require adopting it.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/managed-sessions)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: User and session filtering can reduce unrelated retrieval. However, the current conversation still depends on semantic search, which can omit necessary turns. Filtering alone does not correct that memory-role mismatch.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Maintain a session-specific short-term buffer containing the conversation’s turns, and use the vector store for durable user facts that should be recalled across sessions through semantic search.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe agent is using semantic retrieval as its primary mechanism for remembering an ongoing conversation. That can omit earlier session facts and introduce unrelated material.\n\nB assigns each memory type its appropriate role: session state maintains conversational continuity, while long-term retrieval supplies relevant facts from previous sessions. Databricks explicitly distinguishes these two scopes. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Earlier in their current conversation” — Memory-scope signal: The missing information belongs in short-term session state.\n\n- “All users and sessions in one vector index” — Isolation signal: Retrieval needs appropriate user and session boundaries.\n\n- “Semantic search…on every turn” — Architecture signal: Relevance ranking is substituting for conversation-state retention.\n\n- Increasing latency — Performance signal: The design repeatedly searches accumulated history to recover current conversational context.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA session buffer preserves the conversation’s ordered turns without requiring each earlier fact to win a semantic similarity search. Long-term retrieval can then contribute selected, durable facts when relevant.\n\nLangChain documents short-term memory as thread-level state, with separation between conversations and persistence through a checkpointer. \n\nNecessary boundary: Separating memory types does not automatically prevent cross-user disclosure. Both session access and long-term retrieval must be scoped to the authorized user.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: A relational database can validly persist session history. The weakness is treating a current-session query as the solution for both memory scopes and loading every row on every turn. It does not provide the proposed selective recall of durable cross-session facts.","Option D — Incorrect: Retaining current-turn context does not establish continuity across turns. Repeated summary writes also leave the agent dependent on semantic retrieval and do not establish user isolation."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/short-term-memory","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/agent-memory/managed-sessions"],"source_hash":"7f03f1030ac29c234d8a31517b34ed01046393d33e940e4e7fbc152b1aa3d68f","stem_ja":"Databricksのカスタマーサポートエージェントが、全ユーザー・全セッションの会話を継続的に要約し、1つのベクトルインデックスへ保存しています。各ターンでそのインデックスを意味検索し、会話コンテキストを再構築します。\n\nユーザーから、現在の会話で先ほど伝えた情報を忘れ、ときには他ユーザーの以前のセッションから無関係な情報を持ち込むと報告されています。会話の蓄積が増えるにつれ、応答遅延も増えています。\n\n根本的な不適合を最も適切に修正するメモリ構成はどれですか。","options_ja":["ベクトルインデックスをユーザーIDとセッションIDで分割し、取得を現在のセッションへ限定する一方、ユーザーの長期的な事実はそのインデックスへ保存し続ける。","会話のターンを含むセッション専用の短期バッファを保持し、ベクトルストアはセッションをまたいで意味検索で思い出すべき、永続的なユーザーの事実に使う。","データをセッションIDで分割したリレーショナルテーブルへ移し、各ターンで現在のセッションの全行を取得して、短期・長期両方のメモリ要件を満たす。","現在のターンのコンテキスト用にセッション専用バッファを保持し、各ターンの後に完全な会話要約をベクトルインデックスへ書いて、活動中の全セッションで意味検索の内容を最新に保つ。"],"explanation_ja":"現在の会話を、過去の全会話から類似度検索で組み立て直していることが原因です。短期の会話状態はセッション専用バッファへ、セッションを越えて残すユーザーの事実は長期ストアへ分けます。長期取得にも利用者のスコープ制御が必要です。「いま話したことは短期状態、次回も要ることは長期記憶」と役割を分けます。","distractors_ja":["他人の混入は減らせますが、現在の会話の連続性を毎回の意味検索に依存させる構造は残ります。短期状態の保持を分けるべきです。","現在の会話を直接保持して取りこぼしを防ぎ、長期の事実だけを必要に応じて検索できます。異なる寿命の情報に適した構成です。","現在のセッションの全行だけでは、セッションを越えたユーザーの事実を扱えません。短期と長期を1つの取得方式で代用しています。","短期バッファは改善ですが、毎ターン全要約を共通索引へ保存しても、何を誰の長期記憶として選ぶかという境界が不明確です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-032","number":32,"form":3,"stem":"A context engineer is reviewing a Databricks legal document analysis agent that must answer exclusively from retrieved contract clauses stored in a Unity Catalog volume.\n\nThe execution trace confirms retrieval of Clauses 4.2, 7.1, and 9.3 from the active contract. However, the model’s context also includes the complete conversation from an earlier session about a software licensing agreement.\n\nThe response accurately references Clause 4.2, then introduces indemnification and intellectual property ownership provisions absent from the retrieved clauses. Those additions closely resemble the earlier licensing discussion.\n\nWhich context failure mode best characterizes this behavior?","options":["Context distraction, because the lengthy prior-session material causes the model to focus excessively on accumulated context while neglecting its training knowledge.","Context confusion, because the agent fails to distinguish the relevant retrieved evidence from unnecessary contextual material and incorporates that material into an inaccurate response.","Context poisoning, because the previous session introduces factually incorrect information that corrupts the grounding instructions and is repeatedly reused.","Context clash, because the system instructions and retrieved clauses provide contradictory directions about which contractual obligations apply."],"correct":1,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"関連証拠と別件履歴を混同するcontext confusion","concept":"ce-f3-q032","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Context confusion, because the agent fails to distinguish the relevant retrieved evidence from unnecessary contextual material and incorporates that material into an inaccurate response.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe hard requirement is to answer exclusively from the current contract’s retrieved clauses. Retrieval succeeds, but the response incorporates unrelated session history. Therefore, the strongest diagnosis is confusion caused by superfluous context, rather than missing evidence or conflicting instructions.\n\nOfficial vendor guidance describes context confusion as irrelevant contextual content influencing the model’s response. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Hard constraint — “exclusively from retrieved contract clauses”: Prior-session discussion is outside the permitted evidence.\n\n- Technical signal — successful retrieval: The required clauses reached the context.\n\n- Technical signal — additions resemble the earlier discussion: This identifies the source of the unsupported material.\n\n- Exclusion — provisions absent from retrieved clauses: The final answer exceeds its grounding boundary.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe agent combines an appropriate citation with unrelated contractual terms. Correctly citing Clause 4.2 does not support the additional assertions.\n\nThe licensing content could be accurate for the earlier agreement. Its factual accuracy there would not make it relevant evidence for the current contract.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: The trace does not establish that context length caused the failure or that the model neglected training knowledge. Training knowledge is also outside the permitted evidence for this task.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: The previous discussion is not shown to contain false information, and repeated propagation of an embedded error is not demonstrated.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: No contradictory instructions appear between the system prompt and retrieved clauses. The agent instead introduces material from an unrelated session.\n\nExam Trap\n\nUnsupported content does not automatically mean context poisoning. Here, the evidence identifies irrelevant information being applied to the current task.\n\nPoisoning would be a stronger diagnosis if the agent recorded an erroneous contractual claim as established fact and repeatedly relied on it afterward.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nRelevance and truth are separate properties: information can be correct in its original setting yet inappropriate for another task. Diagnosing these context failure modes is an explicit exam objective. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nScope loaded history to the active session and contract, then verify each contractual assertion against its supporting clause. This reduces unsupported carryover between document reviews.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nRelevant evidence mixed with unrelated context points to context confusion.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose B when retrieval succeeds but irrelevant contextual material enters the answer. Look for demonstrated false-fact propagation before selecting poisoning, or actual contradictions before selecting clash.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Elastic — Managing agentic memory with Elasticsearch\nSupports: Context confusion and its distinction from poisoning, distraction, and clash.\n[Official vendor guidance](https://www.elastic.co/search-labs/blog/agentic-memory-management-elasticsearch)\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\nSupports: Alignment with the objective covering diagnosis of context failure modes from agent traces.\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: The trace does not establish that context length caused the failure or that the model neglected training knowledge. Training knowledge is also outside the permitted evidence for this task.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Context confusion, because the agent fails to distinguish the relevant retrieved evidence from unnecessary contextual material and incorporates that material into an inaccurate response.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe hard requirement is to answer exclusively from the current contract’s retrieved clauses. Retrieval succeeds, but the response incorporates unrelated session history. Therefore, the strongest diagnosis is confusion caused by superfluous context, rather than missing evidence or conflicting instructions.\n\nOfficial vendor guidance describes context confusion as irrelevant contextual content influencing the model’s response. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Hard constraint — “exclusively from retrieved contract clauses”: Prior-session discussion is outside the permitted evidence.\n\n- Technical signal — successful retrieval: The required clauses reached the context.\n\n- Technical signal — additions resemble the earlier discussion: This identifies the source of the unsupported material.\n\n- Exclusion — provisions absent from retrieved clauses: The final answer exceeds its grounding boundary.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe agent combines an appropriate citation with unrelated contractual terms. Correctly citing Clause 4.2 does not support the additional assertions.\n\nThe licensing content could be accurate for the earlier agreement. Its factual accuracy there would not make it relevant evidence for the current contract.","Option C — Incorrect: The previous discussion is not shown to contain false information, and repeated propagation of an embedded error is not demonstrated.","Option D — Incorrect: No contradictory instructions appear between the system prompt and retrieved clauses. The agent instead introduces material from an unrelated session."],"references":["https://www.elastic.co/search-labs/blog/agentic-memory-management-elasticsearch","https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf"],"source_hash":"f47088f98b9912be9c2ea09dc5c4caa7278dfe4d1a1de07dc0aef5468d0bd5af","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、Unity Catalogボリュームに保存された取得済み契約条項だけを根拠に回答しなければならない、Databricksの法務文書分析エージェントを調べています。\n\n実行トレースでは、現在の契約の第4.2条・第7.1条・第9.3条を取得したことが確認できます。しかしモデルのコンテキストには、ソフトウェアライセンス契約を話し合った以前のセッションの会話全体も含まれています。\n\n回答は第4.2条を正確に参照した後、取得条項にはない補償と知的財産権の帰属についての規定を持ち込みます。その追加内容は、以前のライセンスの議論によく似ています。\n\nこの動作を最も適切に表すコンテキスト障害はどれですか。","options_ja":["コンテキストの注意逸脱。長い以前のセッション資料によって、学習済み知識を無視し、蓄積コンテキストへ過度に集中するため。","コンテキストの混同。関連する取得証拠と不要な文脈資料を区別できず、その資料を不正確な回答へ組み込むため。","コンテキストの汚染。以前のセッションが事実として誤った情報を持ち込み、根拠に従う指示を壊し、繰り返し再利用するため。","コンテキストの衝突。適用すべき契約義務について、システム指示と取得条項が相反する指示を与えているため。"],"explanation_ja":"以前のライセンス契約の内容が正しいかどうかではなく、今回の証拠ではない情報を回答へ混ぜたことが問題です。関連資料と無関係な履歴を取り違えるため、context confusionに当たります。誤情報の反復はpoisoning、相反する指示はclashです。「別件の正しい情報でも、混ぜれば今回の誤答になる」と覚えます。","distractors_ja":["注意の配分が問題になる場合はありますが、この回答では別件の規定を今回の証拠として使っています。学習知識への依存の問題ではありません。","以前の議論と現在の取得証拠の境界を失い、別件の条項を混入させています。関連性の取り違えが直接の特徴です。","以前の議論自体が誤情報であるとは示されていません。無関係な資料の混入と、虚偽情報による汚染を区別します。","システム指示と取得条項が相反するとは示されていません。指示衝突ではなく、不要な過去資料の取り違えです。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-033","number":33,"form":3,"stem":"A personalized shopping agent on Databricks serves returning customers who expect it to remember product preferences and purchase topics discussed in earlier conversations. After several weeks of operation, users report that each new session starts without recognizing those preferences or prior discussions.\n\nWhich configuration best addresses this limitation?","options":["Connect a Lakebase-backed durable store and configure the agent to retain important user preferences and interaction summaries across sessions.","Configure `AsyncPostgresSaver` with one permanent thread ID per user, preserving conversation state by reusing that thread across separate conversations.","Configure a user-scoped `InMemorySaver` in the compiled LangGraph graph and export each session’s final checkpoint to a vector index for retrieval across sessions.","Enable Delta Lake change data feed on the agent state table and have the runtime reload its latest snapshot when each session begins."],"correct":0,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"リピーターを支えるユーザー単位の永続メモリ","concept":"ce-f3-q033","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Connect a Lakebase-backed durable store and configure the agent to retain important user preferences and interaction summaries across sessions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe required information must survive separate conversations and remain associated with the returning user. This calls for durable user memory with persistence and recall across sessions.\n\nA directly provides that architecture. Databricks distinguishes session state from durable facts and preferences recalled in later conversations, with Lakebase-backed storage supporting these capabilities. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Returning customers” — Identity signal: Memories must remain associated with the same user.\n\n- “Earlier conversations” — Persistence requirement: Information must cross session boundaries.\n\n- “Product preferences and purchase topics” — Memory-content signal: Useful facts and summaries should be retained.\n\n- “Each new session” — Failure-boundary signal: The limitation concerns cross-session recall rather than continuity within one conversation.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA durable store can retain user-specific preferences and concise interaction summaries independently of an individual conversation’s state. New sessions can retrieve the relevant records and supply them to the agent.\n\nThe implementation needs both directions:\n\n- Write: Persist useful preferences and summaries under the appropriate user identity.\n\n- Read: Retrieve relevant memories during subsequent sessions.\n\nAttaching storage alone does not create recall; the agent must actually load and use the saved information.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: `AsyncPostgresSaver` provides durable thread checkpoints. Reusing one thread can preserve history, but treats separate conversations as one continuing thread. A user memory store supports recall across independent threads more directly.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Custom checkpoint export and retrieval could preserve information, but adds a separate memory pipeline around an in-memory checkpointer. A final graph checkpoint also differs from curated preferences and summaries. `InMemorySaver` itself does not provide durable persistence.\n\n- Option D — Different Use Case: Change data feed tracks table-row changes for incremental processing and replication. It does not itself define preference extraction, user-memory organization, or recall. Delta tables could support a custom memory implementation, but enabling CDF is not the missing memory capability.\n\nExam Trap\n\nA persistent checkpointer is genuinely durable. The distinction is its scope: checkpoints preserve a thread’s state, while a store can preserve user information across threads.\n\nB would be appropriate when the requirement is to resume the same ongoing conversation. Here, the requirement is personalization across separate conversations.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nDurability and memory scope are separate properties. Database-backed checkpoints can survive restarts while remaining thread-scoped.\n\nLangGraph distinguishes checkpointers for conversation state from stores for cross-session user or application data.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nGive each conversation its own thread ID while storing durable preferences under the returning customer’s identity. For example, retain a stated preference for fragrance-free products and retrieve it when a later session concerns skincare.\n\nThis preserves personalization without requiring every new conversation to replay the customer’s complete transcript.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nCheckpoint the conversation; store the preferences for future conversations.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nRemembering preferences across new sessions requires durable user memory plus retrieval, making A the best fit. A database checkpointer preserves thread state, but does not automatically provide cross-thread personalization.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Agent memory and sessions\n\nSupports: Lakebase-backed memory and the distinction between session state and durable cross-session knowledge.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)\n\n- LangGraph — Memory\n\nSupports: `AsyncPostgresSaver`, `InMemorySaver`, and the distinction between thread checkpoints and cross-session stores.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/add-memory)\n\n- Databricks — Use change data feed on Databricks\n\nSupports: CDF’s role in tracking row changes and supporting incremental processing.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/features/change-data-feed)\n\n- Databricks — Certified Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\n\nSupports: Configuring persistent agent memory across sessions using a Lakebase-backed durable store.\n\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Connect a Lakebase-backed durable store and configure the agent to retain important user preferences and interaction summaries across sessions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe required information must survive separate conversations and remain associated with the returning user. This calls for durable user memory with persistence and recall across sessions.\n\nA directly provides that architecture. Databricks distinguishes session state from durable facts and preferences recalled in later conversations, with Lakebase-backed storage supporting these capabilities. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Returning customers” — Identity signal: Memories must remain associated with the same user.\n\n- “Earlier conversations” — Persistence requirement: Information must cross session boundaries.\n\n- “Product preferences and purchase topics” — Memory-content signal: Useful facts and summaries should be retained.\n\n- “Each new session” — Failure-boundary signal: The limitation concerns cross-session recall rather than continuity within one conversation.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA durable store can retain user-specific preferences and concise interaction summaries independently of an individual conversation’s state. New sessions can retrieve the relevant records and supply them to the agent.\n\nThe implementation needs both directions:\n\n- Write: Persist useful preferences and summaries under the appropriate user identity.\n\n- Read: Retrieve relevant memories during subsequent sessions.\n\nAttaching storage alone does not create recall; the agent must actually load and use the saved information.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: `AsyncPostgresSaver` provides durable thread checkpoints. Reusing one thread can preserve history, but treats separate conversations as one continuing thread. A user memory store supports recall across independent threads more directly.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Custom checkpoint export and retrieval could preserve information, but adds a separate memory pipeline around an in-memory checkpointer. A final graph checkpoint also differs from curated preferences and summaries. `InMemorySaver` itself does not provide durable persistence.","Option D — Different Use Case: Change data feed tracks table-row changes for incremental processing and replication. It does not itself define preference extraction, user-memory organization, or recall. Delta tables could support a custom memory implementation, but enabling CDF is not the missing memory capability."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/add-memory","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/tables/features/change-data-feed","https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf"],"source_hash":"7420d1499c986b17d322f045384d052700df6a4b154a028fd4716a90137da82b","stem_ja":"Databricks上のパーソナライズされた買い物エージェントが、以前の会話で話した商品の好みや購入に関する話題を覚えていることを期待するリピーターを支援しています。数週間運用したところ、新しいセッションのたびに、それらの好みや過去の話を認識しない状態で始まると報告されました。\n\nこの制約に最も適切に対処する設定はどれですか。","options_ja":["Lakebaseを基盤とする永続ストアへ接続し、重要なユーザーの好みとやり取りの要約をセッション間で保持するよう設定する。","ユーザーごとに1つの恒久的なスレッドIDを使う`AsyncPostgresSaver`を設定し、別々の会話でも同じスレッドを再利用して会話状態を保持する。","コンパイル済みLangGraphグラフにユーザースコープの`InMemorySaver`を設定し、各セッションの最終チェックポイントを、セッション間で取得するためベクトルインデックスへ書き出す。","エージェント状態テーブルでDelta Lakeの変更データフィードを有効にし、各セッション開始時に最新スナップショットを再読み込みするようランタイムを設定する。"],"explanation_ja":"求められるのは1つの会話の再開ではなく、別の会話でも使うユーザー単位の永続メモリです。Lakebase-backed storeへ重要な好みや要約をユーザーID付きで保存し、新しいセッションで読み戻します。スレッドのチェックポイント、プロセス内保存、変更通知はそれぞれ役割が違います。「会話を続ける状態」と「次の会話でも覚える事実」を分けます。","distractors_ja":["別セッションでも再利用する事実を永続保存する方式です。安定したユーザー識別子と読み書きの仕組みを組み合わせます。","チェックポインターは基本的にスレッド状態の保存です。全会話を1つの恒久スレッドへまとめるのは、ユーザー単位の選択的な長期記憶とは異なります。","InMemorySaverだけでは実行環境を越える永続性がありません。チェックポイントの索引化も、必要なユーザーの事実を適切に保存する設計の代わりではありません。","変更データフィードはテーブル変更の記録です。何をユーザーの長期記憶として保存し、次回どの情報を読み込むかを自動で実装しません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-034","number":34,"form":3,"stem":"An engineer is developing a Databricks AI application that records each LLM prompt, the retrieved context supplied with it, and the generated response. Each inference must also be associated with token consumption, latency, and user feedback scores.\n\nThe team needs an integrated platform that versions prompt templates alongside model artifacts, associates evaluation results with specific runs, and provides programmatic queries for dashboards comparing experiment iterations. They want to avoid implementing a custom logging backend.\n\nWhich product best meets these requirements?","options":["Unity Catalog, for registering prompt assets, governing inference-log access, and tracking lineage between datasets and generated outputs.","Lakebase, for storing inference records transactionally, representing prompt versions as database rows, and querying latency through SQL.","MLflow 3, for recording inference traces, versioning prompt templates alongside model artifacts, and querying evaluation results across experiment runs.","MCP, for publishing the inference endpoint and prompt templates through standardized interfaces that agent frameworks can discover and invoke."],"correct":2,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"MLflow 3によるトレース・プロンプト・評価の管理","concept":"ce-f3-q034","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. MLflow 3, for recording inference traces, versioning prompt templates alongside model artifacts, and querying evaluation results across experiment runs.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirements combine observability, experiment tracking, versioning, and evaluation analysis. The decisive constraint is obtaining these capabilities without building a logging backend.\n\nMLflow 3 provides the integrated tracking and evaluation layer. Unity Catalog contributes governance, Lakebase provides database storage, and MCP provides interoperability, but none independently supplies the complete requested workflow.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Prompts, retrieved context, and responses — Observability requirement: Capture execution details through traces and spans.\n\n- Token consumption and latency — Measurement requirement: Associate operational metrics with inference execution.\n\n- User feedback and evaluation results — Quality requirement: Link assessments to the corresponding traces and runs.\n\n- Prompt versions and experiment iterations — Lifecycle requirement: Preserve configuration identity for comparisons.\n\n- “Avoid implementing a custom logging backend” — Hard constraint: Select an integrated tracking platform.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nMLflow Tracing captures component inputs, outputs, latency, and token usage across LLM and retrieval operations. Databricks provides the managed tracking infrastructure. \n\nPrompt Registry versions templates and links them to experiments and evaluation results. \n\nProgrammatic trace queries can retrieve spans, assessments, token usage, and associated run or model information. Unity Catalog-backed trace tables also support direct SQL analysis.\n\nCapturing every required component still requires appropriate instrumentation, and user feedback must be collected and logged.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Different Use Case: Unity Catalog provides governance and storage integration. Those capabilities complement MLflow, but do not independently replace its tracing, experiment comparison, and evaluation functionality.\n\n- Option B — Violates Requirement: Lakebase could store the records, but the team would need to implement logging schemas, version relationships, feedback associations, and analysis logic. That recreates the custom backend the question excludes.\n\n- Option D — Different Use Case: MCP standardizes how applications expose and access capabilities. It does not itself provide the required experiment history, prompt version management, or evaluation tracking.\n\nExam Trap\n\nPrompt versions and model artifacts are distinct entities. The technically precise wording in C uses Prompt Registry for prompt-template versions, alongside model tracking; prompts should not be described as registered models.\n\nDatabricks currently labels Prompt Registry Beta.\n\nIf the task were principally access control and asset governance, Unity Catalog would become the stronger product choice.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nAn observability platform preserves relationships between what ran, which configuration it used, what it produced, and how that execution was assessed.\n\nStoring individual records is only part of the requirement. Reliable comparisons also require consistent links among versions, runs, traces, and feedback.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nInstrument retrieval and LLM calls, associate execution with the application and prompt versions, and attach feedback to the corresponding trace. A dashboard can then compare answer quality, latency, and token usage across iterations.\n\nThis lets reviewers connect a performance change to the configuration that produced it.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nFor traces, versions, evaluations, and experiment comparisons together, choose MLflow.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nA requirement combining inference observability, prompt versioning, evaluation linkage, and queryable experiment history points to MLflow 3. Governance, storage, and tool protocols support that workflow but do not replace its tracking layer.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Tracing overview\n\nSupports: Managed tracing of inputs, outputs, latency, token usage, and execution flow.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/overview)\n\n- Databricks — Prompt Registry\n\nSupports: Prompt versioning, experiment and evaluation lineage, and current Beta status.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry)\n\n- Databricks — Programmatic access to traces\n\nSupports: Querying traces, assessments, run associations, and Unity Catalog-backed trace data.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces/query-via-sdk)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Different Use Case: Unity Catalog provides governance and storage integration. Those capabilities complement MLflow, but do not independently replace its tracing, experiment comparison, and evaluation functionality.","Option B — Violates Requirement: Lakebase could store the records, but the team would need to implement logging schemas, version relationships, feedback associations, and analysis logic. That recreates the custom backend the question excludes.","Correct Answer\n\nC. MLflow 3, for recording inference traces, versioning prompt templates alongside model artifacts, and querying evaluation results across experiment runs.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirements combine observability, experiment tracking, versioning, and evaluation analysis. The decisive constraint is obtaining these capabilities without building a logging backend.\n\nMLflow 3 provides the integrated tracking and evaluation layer. Unity Catalog contributes governance, Lakebase provides database storage, and MCP provides interoperability, but none independently supplies the complete requested workflow.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Prompts, retrieved context, and responses — Observability requirement: Capture execution details through traces and spans.\n\n- Token consumption and latency — Measurement requirement: Associate operational metrics with inference execution.\n\n- User feedback and evaluation results — Quality requirement: Link assessments to the corresponding traces and runs.\n\n- Prompt versions and experiment iterations — Lifecycle requirement: Preserve configuration identity for comparisons.\n\n- “Avoid implementing a custom logging backend” — Hard constraint: Select an integrated tracking platform.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nMLflow Tracing captures component inputs, outputs, latency, and token usage across LLM and retrieval operations. Databricks provides the managed tracking infrastructure. \n\nPrompt Registry versions templates and links them to experiments and evaluation results. \n\nProgrammatic trace queries can retrieve spans, assessments, token usage, and associated run or model information. Unity Catalog-backed trace tables also support direct SQL analysis.\n\nCapturing every required component still requires appropriate instrumentation, and user feedback must be collected and logged.","Option D — Different Use Case: MCP standardizes how applications expose and access capabilities. It does not itself provide the required experiment history, prompt version management, or evaluation tracking."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/overview","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/tracing/observe-with-traces/query-via-sdk"],"source_hash":"76349eebedc87b23fbba86d155a98b6504e67522301484b6fb8cf2e9c15e8709","stem_ja":"エンジニアが、各LLMプロンプト、同時に渡す取得コンテキスト、生成応答を記録するDatabricksのAIアプリケーションを開発しています。各推論には、トークン消費、遅延、ユーザーフィードバックのスコアも関連付ける必要があります。\n\nチームは、モデル成果物と併せてプロンプトテンプレートをバージョン管理し、評価結果を特定の実行へ結び付け、実験の反復を比較するダッシュボード用にプログラムから問い合わせられる統合基盤を必要としています。独自のログ保存バックエンドを実装することは避けたいと考えています。\n\nこれらの要件を最もよく満たす製品はどれですか。","options_ja":["Unity Catalog。プロンプト資産の登録、推論ログへのアクセス管理、データセットと生成出力のリネージ追跡を行う。","Lakebase。推論記録をトランザクションとして保存し、プロンプトの版をデータベース行で表現し、SQLで遅延を問い合わせる。","MLflow 3。推論トレースを記録し、モデル成果物と併せてプロンプトテンプレートをバージョン管理し、実験実行を横断して評価結果を問い合わせる。","MCP。推論エンドポイントとプロンプトテンプレートを、エージェントフレームワークが発見・呼び出せる標準インターフェースとして公開する。"],"explanation_ja":"推論のトレース、評価、実行間比較、プロンプトのバージョン管理をまとめて扱うのがMLflow 3です。Unity Catalogはガバナンス、Lakebaseはトランザクション保存、MCPはツール接続が主な役割であり、この観測・実験管理の一式を自作なしで提供する答えにはなりません。プロンプトはPrompt Registryで管理でき、モデル成果物とは役割を区別します。","distractors_ja":["権限やリネージを管理できますが、ここで必要な推論トレース・評価・実験比較の統合機能を主に担う製品ではありません。","任意のログを格納できますが、トレース収集や評価との連携を自作することになります。独自バックエンドを避ける要件に合いません。","トレース、メトリクス、評価結果、プロンプトの版を実験と結び付けて管理できます。今回必要な観測と比較の機能に一致します。","MCPは呼び出し可能なツール等の接続契約です。推論の実験記録や評価結果の比較基盤そのものではありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-035","number":35,"form":3,"stem":"A long-running research agent analyzes regulatory compliance documents through dozens of sequential tool calls. After conversation compaction, its recommendations begin contradicting decisions established earlier in the session.\n\nInspection reveals that the compactor retained only factual findings. Its current instruction asks it to summarize the principal facts and discoveries made so far.\n\nWhich revised instruction best restores continuity without substantially increasing token usage?","options":["Summarize the findings and append a structured record of every earlier user message, retaining each message’s original wording.","Summarize the findings and retain the complete outputs of every tool call performed during the session.","Summarize the findings and preserve the agent’s internal chain-of-thought reasoning from every preceding step.","Summarize the findings and retain a deduplicated list of user-stated constraints, exclusions, and preferences."],"correct":3,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"圧縮後も維持するユーザーの制約と除外事項","concept":"ce-f3-q035","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Summarize the findings and retain a deduplicated list of user-stated constraints, exclusions, and preferences.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure begins after compaction, and the summary preserves findings while omitting guidance governing acceptable recommendations. The revision must retain that guidance without restoring the full transcript.\n\nOption D targets the missing information directly. Deduplication avoids repeatedly carrying the same requirement forward.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Sequence signal — “after conversation compaction”: Investigate information lost during summarization.\n\n- Technical signal — only factual findings retained: Facts alone do not preserve the task’s governing requirements.\n\n- Technical signal — contradictory recommendations: Earlier restrictions or preferences may no longer influence decisions.\n\n- Hard constraint — limited token increase: Preserve compact requirements rather than complete histories.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nConstraints determine what recommendations must satisfy. Exclusions identify prohibited approaches, while preferences guide choices among acceptable alternatives. Retaining these alongside findings preserves both the evidence and the user’s intended direction.\n\nCompaction guidance emphasizes preserving critical information and tuning summaries for recall before removing unnecessary detail. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Original user messages would preserve requirements, but also retain repetition and incidental conversation. A concise requirements list meets the token constraint more efficiently.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Tool outputs preserve evidence, but do not necessarily contain user instructions. Keeping every output also undermines compression.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: Detailed internal reasoning is not a substitute for explicitly retaining user requirements. The necessary continuation state can be represented directly without preserving step-by-step internal deliberation.\n\nExam Trap\n\nA factual summary can be accurate yet insufficient for continuing the task correctly. Remembering what the agent discovered does not ensure it remembers what the user permits.\n\nIf the observed failure instead involved losing specific tool results needed downstream, a compact record of those results would become the appropriate addition.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nCompaction should preserve task state, not merely document content. Distinguish hard constraints from preferences, and deduplicate repeated instructions without erasing exceptions or later corrections.\n\nThe exam guide explicitly covers identifying information discarded during compaction and revising the prompt with minimal additional token cost. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nStructure the summary with separate findings and user-requirements fields. Evaluate whether recommendations after compaction still obey earlier exclusions; this checks continuity rather than summary fluency alone.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nCompact the conversation while preserving the requirements that govern its next steps.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose D when a findings-only summary loses user intent. Retain deduplicated constraints, exclusions, and preferences rather than restoring all messages, tool outputs, or internal reasoning.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Anthropic — Effective Context Engineering for AI Agents\nSupports: High-fidelity compaction, retention of critical information, and recall-first tuning.\n[Official guidance](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\nSupports: Diagnosing post-compaction coherence failures and revising retention instructions efficiently.\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Original user messages would preserve requirements, but also retain repetition and incidental conversation. A concise requirements list meets the token constraint more efficiently.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Tool outputs preserve evidence, but do not necessarily contain user instructions. Keeping every output also undermines compression.","Option C — Incorrect: Detailed internal reasoning is not a substitute for explicitly retaining user requirements. The necessary continuation state can be represented directly without preserving step-by-step internal deliberation.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Summarize the findings and retain a deduplicated list of user-stated constraints, exclusions, and preferences.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure begins after compaction, and the summary preserves findings while omitting guidance governing acceptable recommendations. The revision must retain that guidance without restoring the full transcript.\n\nOption D targets the missing information directly. Deduplication avoids repeatedly carrying the same requirement forward.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Sequence signal — “after conversation compaction”: Investigate information lost during summarization.\n\n- Technical signal — only factual findings retained: Facts alone do not preserve the task’s governing requirements.\n\n- Technical signal — contradictory recommendations: Earlier restrictions or preferences may no longer influence decisions.\n\n- Hard constraint — limited token increase: Preserve compact requirements rather than complete histories.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nConstraints determine what recommendations must satisfy. Exclusions identify prohibited approaches, while preferences guide choices among acceptable alternatives. Retaining these alongside findings preserves both the evidence and the user’s intended direction.\n\nCompaction guidance emphasizes preserving critical information and tuning summaries for recall before removing unnecessary detail."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf"],"source_hash":"4c507edb38ee4c1b0f1661c31db70741642afd29e7b548221218f0b92e9a64c6","stem_ja":"長時間動作する調査エージェントが、数十回の逐次ツール呼び出しで規制コンプライアンス文書を分析しています。会話圧縮の後、そのセッションの前段で確定した判断と矛盾する推奨を出すようになりました。\n\n調査すると、圧縮処理は事実としての知見しか保持していません。現在の指示は、それまでに判明した主要な事実と発見を要約するよう求めています。\n\nトークン使用量を大幅に増やさずに、作業の連続性を最もよく回復する修正指示はどれですか。","options_ja":["知見を要約し、以前のユーザーメッセージをすべて原文のまま構造化記録として追加する。","知見を要約し、セッションで実施したすべてのツール呼び出しの完全な出力を保持する。","知見を要約し、それ以前の各段階の内部の思考の連鎖を保持する。","知見を要約し、ユーザーが示した制約、除外事項、好みを重複のない一覧として保持する。"],"explanation_ja":"事実だけが残っても、ユーザーが禁止したことや選んだ条件を失うと、以前の判断と食い違います。制約・除外・好みを明示した重複のない一覧を保持すれば、全会話や全ツール結果を戻さずに連続性を補えます。保存すべきなのは内部思考の丸写しではなく、今後の判断を拘束する確定事項です。","distractors_ja":["全メッセージを原文で残すと、不要な挨拶や繰り返しまで保持し、圧縮の効果を大きく損ないます。必要な制約を抽出します。","ツール出力だけではユーザーの制約を確実に保持できず、全量保存は大量のトークンを使います。今回の欠落を直接補いません。","内部思考の完全保存は不要で、トークンも増大します。今後の行動に必要な決定・根拠・制約を短く引き継げば十分です。","事実要約に欠けている行動条件を、重複を除いて補えます。簡潔さと前段の判断への整合性を両立する方法です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-036","number":36,"form":3,"stem":"A fraud investigation assistant on Databricks supports investigations that continue across multiple sessions for weeks or months. Two or three analysts typically share each investigation, while each analyst also works on several other cases.\n\nThe assistant uses Agent Bricks Custom Agents and one retrieval tool querying a Databricks AI Search index. The index contains incident reports, suspect dossiers, transaction logs, and exhibits, synchronized nightly from Delta tables maintained by the case management system. Retrieval uses `num_results=50`. The application has operated for nine months.\n\nOn Friday, analyst A and the assistant rule out two suspects using alibi evidence, identify a third as the likely actor, and establish two accounts as the funds-transfer pathway. On Monday, analyst B opens the same investigation to prepare another subpoena request. The assistant asks which suspect, eliminations, and accounts are relevant.\n\nThe engineer reproduces this behavior across investigations: reasoning is effective within a session, but subsequent sessions lose the accumulated conclusions regardless of which analyst returns.\n\nWhich change best addresses this pattern?","options":["Create persistent case memory in a Unity Catalog Delta table keyed by `case_id`. Add tools that save structured session conclusions—including eliminations, confirmations, entities, and decisions—and retrieve the active case’s records during initialization, before the analyst’s first message.","Create a Unity Catalog Delta table keyed by analyst user ID, append completed conversation transcripts, and load the analyst’s recent transcripts into each new session.","Increase retrieval from `num_results=50` to `num_results=150` and apply a client-side similarity-score threshold of `0.25` to surface more relevant case context.","Replace nightly index synchronization with Lakeflow Connect streaming ingestion so recent investigation activity becomes searchable within minutes and is available when sessions begin."],"correct":0,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"担当者を越えて引き継ぐ案件単位の永続メモリ","concept":"ce-f3-q036","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Create persistent case memory in a Unity Catalog Delta table keyed by `case_id`. Add tools that save structured session conclusions—including eliminations, confirmations, entities, and decisions—and retrieve the active case’s records during initialization, before the analyst’s first message.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe assistant needs to preserve conclusions across sessions and analyst handoffs. The investigation is the stable unit of work, so memory must be associated with the case.\n\nA supplies all three missing elements: durable storage, the correct `case_id` scope, and explicit save/load behavior. The retrieval corpus supplies evidence, but the scenario describes no mechanism that persists the assistant’s accumulated investigative conclusions.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Weeks or months across sessions — Persistence requirement: Conclusions must outlive individual conversations.\n\n- Several analysts share each investigation — Ownership signal: Memory must follow the case.\n\n- Each analyst handles several investigations — Isolation signal: Analyst identity alone cannot distinguish active case context.\n\n- Effective reasoning within sessions — Diagnostic signal: The observed loss occurs at session boundaries.\n\n- Missing eliminations, confirmations, and account scope — Content signal: The missing information is accumulated task state.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA records the investigation’s current conclusions independently of the conversation that produced them. The next session retrieves those records using the active `case_id`, allowing another analyst to continue from the established state.\n\nDatabricks distinguishes interaction-scoped session state from durable facts and decisions retained across conversations. Its documentation explicitly includes ongoing projects as subjects for persistent memory. \n\nThe initialization path must actually execute the read and place the results into context. Registering a memory tool without arranging its use would not satisfy the timing requirement.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: Analyst-keyed history does not align with shared investigations. Analyst B would not automatically receive analyst A’s conclusions, while an analyst’s recent transcripts could contain several unrelated cases. Full transcripts also introduce unnecessary context.\n\n- Option C — Different Use Case: Retrieval-count and score-threshold tuning can change which indexed evidence is returned. Neither setting creates a durable record of session conclusions or guarantees that those conclusions enter the next session.\n\n- Option D — Different Use Case: Faster ingestion addresses source-data freshness. It does not establish a write/read path for conclusions developed during conversations. Lakeflow Connect ingestion and AI Search index synchronization are also distinct pipeline concerns.\n\nExam Trap\n\nPersonalization examples often use user-scoped memory, but this task belongs to a shared investigation. Choose the identifier that represents the continuing work.\n\nPer-analyst memory would fit individual preferences or personal workflow settings. It would not replace shared case conclusions.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nPersistent task memory needs three design decisions:\n\n- Scope: Which entity owns the state?\n\n- Contents: Which conclusions and decisions must survive?\n\n- Lifecycle: When are records written and restored?\n\nHere, those decisions are case scope, structured investigative progress, and session-boundary persistence.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nStore case conclusions with supporting evidence references, timestamps, and status. Monday’s initialization can then restore Friday’s findings while retaining the distinction between a likely actor and a confirmed fact.\n\nAs evidence changes, update or supersede earlier conclusions so the assistant does not treat outdated findings as current.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nFor shared, continuing work, persist memory under the work item’s identity.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nGood within-session reasoning followed by repeated cold starts indicates missing persistent task memory. With rotating analysts and multiple simultaneous investigations, choose case-scoped save/load behavior, making A the best fit.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Agent memory and sessions\n\nSupports: Durable facts and decisions across conversations, including project-scoped knowledge.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)\n\n- Databricks — Create AI Search endpoints and indexes\n\nSupports: Index synchronization as a retrieval-data pipeline concern.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search)\n\n- Databricks — Lakeflow Connect connector concepts\n\nSupports: Ingestion pipelines that keep downstream source data current.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/lakeflow-connect/)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Create persistent case memory in a Unity Catalog Delta table keyed by `case_id`. Add tools that save structured session conclusions—including eliminations, confirmations, entities, and decisions—and retrieve the active case’s records during initialization, before the analyst’s first message.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe assistant needs to preserve conclusions across sessions and analyst handoffs. The investigation is the stable unit of work, so memory must be associated with the case.\n\nA supplies all three missing elements: durable storage, the correct `case_id` scope, and explicit save/load behavior. The retrieval corpus supplies evidence, but the scenario describes no mechanism that persists the assistant’s accumulated investigative conclusions.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Weeks or months across sessions — Persistence requirement: Conclusions must outlive individual conversations.\n\n- Several analysts share each investigation — Ownership signal: Memory must follow the case.\n\n- Each analyst handles several investigations — Isolation signal: Analyst identity alone cannot distinguish active case context.\n\n- Effective reasoning within sessions — Diagnostic signal: The observed loss occurs at session boundaries.\n\n- Missing eliminations, confirmations, and account scope — Content signal: The missing information is accumulated task state.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA records the investigation’s current conclusions independently of the conversation that produced them. The next session retrieves those records using the active `case_id`, allowing another analyst to continue from the established state.\n\nDatabricks distinguishes interaction-scoped session state from durable facts and decisions retained across conversations. Its documentation explicitly includes ongoing projects as subjects for persistent memory. \n\nThe initialization path must actually execute the read and place the results into context. Registering a memory tool without arranging its use would not satisfy the timing requirement.","Option B — Incorrect: Analyst-keyed history does not align with shared investigations. Analyst B would not automatically receive analyst A’s conclusions, while an analyst’s recent transcripts could contain several unrelated cases. Full transcripts also introduce unnecessary context.","Option C — Different Use Case: Retrieval-count and score-threshold tuning can change which indexed evidence is returned. Neither setting creates a durable record of session conclusions or guarantees that those conclusions enter the next session.","Option D — Different Use Case: Faster ingestion addresses source-data freshness. It does not establish a write/read path for conclusions developed during conversations. Lakeflow Connect ingestion and AI Search index synchronization are also distinct pipeline concerns."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ingestion/lakeflow-connect/"],"source_hash":"4bfddd5f00b474c593b7501a39be0237913b81c7458b45ecc6d5a2600895d078","stem_ja":"Databricks上の不正調査アシスタントが、数週間から数か月、複数セッションにわたる調査を支援しています。通常1件を2〜3人の分析担当者が共有し、各担当者は他の複数案件も扱っています。\n\nアシスタントはAgent Bricks Custom Agentsと、Databricks AI Searchインデックスを問い合わせる1つの検索ツールを使います。索引には、案件管理システムのDeltaテーブルから毎晩同期する事故報告、容疑者資料、取引ログ、証拠物があります。検索設定は`num_results=50`です。アプリケーションは9か月運用されています。\n\n金曜日、担当者Aとアシスタントはアリバイの証拠で容疑者2人を除外し、3人目が有力と判断し、2口座を資金移動の経路と特定しました。月曜日、担当者Bが同じ案件で次の召喚状請求を準備しようとすると、アシスタントは対象の容疑者、除外した人、口座を質問します。\n\n他の案件でも再現でき、セッション内の推論は有効ですが、どの担当者が戻っても後のセッションでは蓄積した結論を失います。\n\nこのパターンに最も適切に対処する変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["`case_id`をキーとするUnity CatalogのDeltaテーブルに永続的な案件メモリを作る。除外・確認・エンティティ・判断などの構造化したセッション結論を保存するツールと、担当者の最初のメッセージ前の初期化時に対象案件の記録を取得するツールを追加する。","担当者のユーザーIDをキーとするUnity CatalogのDeltaテーブルを作り、完了した会話記録を追記し、新しいセッションへ担当者の最近の会話記録を読み込む。","`num_results=50`から`num_results=150`へ取得件数を増やし、クライアント側で類似度スコアのしきい値`0.25`を適用して、関連する案件の文脈をより多く取得する。","夜間の索引同期をLakeflow Connectのストリーミング取り込みへ置き換え、最近の調査活動を数分以内に検索可能にして、セッション開始時に利用できるようにする。"],"explanation_ja":"保存すべきなのは文書だけでなく、調査で確定した除外・容疑・口座・判断です。しかも共有単位は担当者ではなく案件です。case_idをキーに結論を永続保存し、次回開始時に読み戻せば、担当者が交代しても作業を引き継げます。検索件数や同期頻度の変更だけでは、保存されていないセッションの結論は復活しません。","distractors_ja":["案件単位で結論を保存し、次の開始時にコンテキストへ入れることで、担当者とセッションの両方の境界を越えて調査を継続できます。","担当者ごとの保存では、担当者Aの結論を担当者Bが読み戻せません。1人が複数案件を扱うため、別案件の混入も防ぐ必要があります。","検索対象の証拠を増やしても、前回の会話で確定した判断が索引に保存されていなければ得られません。メモリの欠落が原因です。","元文書の鮮度は改善できますが、会話内の結論を案件メモリとして記録する仕組みにはなりません。同期遅延の証拠もありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-037","number":37,"form":3,"stem":"A Databricks RAG agent will answer detailed questions about a large contract collection. Each contract is a structured PDF averaging 40 pages, with recognizable sections including “Definitions,” “Obligations,” and “Termination Clauses.”\n\nThe embedding model accepts at most 512 tokens. Queries focus on individual clauses—for example, identifying termination conditions within an indemnification clause—so retrieval must locate precise provisions rather than document-wide summaries.\n\nWhich chunking strategy is most appropriate?","options":["Split documents using section boundaries, then subdivide sections exceeding 400 tokens into smaller chunks that fit the embedding model’s input limit.","Treat every paragraph as a separate chunk throughout the collection to maximize granularity for clause-specific searches.","Create fixed 512-token chunks with 50-token overlap, disregarding section boundaries while respecting the embedding input limit.","Embed each complete section as one chunk, retaining its full context even when it exceeds 512 tokens."],"correct":0,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"契約の構造を守り入力上限にも収めるチャンク分割","concept":"ce-f3-q037","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Split documents using section boundaries, then subdivide sections exceeding 400 tokens into smaller chunks that fit the embedding model’s input limit.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe strategy must satisfy two requirements: preserve meaningful contract structure and stay within the embedding model’s capacity.\n\nA combines both. Section boundaries provide a useful starting point, while further subdivision prevents long sections from exceeding the limit. Paragraph-only splitting does not guarantee suitable size or sufficient clause context; fixed windows discard useful structure; unrestricted sections violate the model limit.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Recognizable contract sections — Structure signal: Natural boundaries are available and should guide splitting.\n\n- 512-token embedding limit — Hard constraint: Each embedding input must fit.\n\n- Clause-focused questions — Retrieval requirement: Chunks should concentrate on specific provisions.\n\n- 40-page contracts — Scale signal: Whole-document representations would be too broad for this task.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nStructure-aware splitting keeps related provisions together where possible. Long sections can then be divided at subsection, paragraph, or sentence boundaries while enforcing the token limit.\n\nDatabricks recommends considering document structure, semantic coherence, chunk size, and embedding-model limits when preparing RAG data. \n\nUse the embedding model’s tokenizer to check the complete input. Any headings or other text added before embedding must also fit within 512 tokens.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Paragraph boundaries can preserve local meaning. However, a paragraph may exceed 512 tokens, and a clause may span several paragraphs. Splitting every paragraph separately does not address both constraints.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Fixed-size chunks provide predictable lengths, and overlap can reduce boundary losses. However, the strategy explicitly ignores available contract structure and may divide a clause or combine unrelated provisions.\n\n- Option D — Violates Requirement: Preserving a whole section does not override the embedding limit. Oversized inputs risk truncation or rejection, depending on the serving implementation, potentially excluding the provision needed for retrieval.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe smallest chunk is not automatically the best chunk. Granularity helps only when the retrieved text retains enough meaning to answer the question.\n\nThe 400-token threshold is appropriate to this scenario; it is not a universal optimum. If documents lacked reliable structural boundaries, a token-bounded recursive splitting strategy would become more attractive.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nChunking balances semantic completeness against retrieval specificity, subject to a hard input-size limit.\n\nA useful chunk contains a coherent unit that can be matched to a question. When that unit is too large, preserve its structural identity while dividing its content.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nParse headings and clause identifiers from each PDF, subdivide oversized sections, and retain document ID, section heading, clause identifier, and page references as metadata.\n\nThis helps retrieve the relevant provision and trace it back to its location. Databricks recommends preserving document structure and maintaining semantic boundaries during chunking. \n\nMemory Hook\n\nFollow meaningful boundaries, then enforce the embedding token limit.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nClear document structure plus targeted queries favors structure-aware chunking. Add token-bounded subdivision whenever sections can exceed the embedding window—making A the best fit.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Build an unstructured data pipeline for RAG\n\nSupports: Chunking strategies, semantic coherence, metadata, and embedding input limits.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag)\n\n- Databricks — AI Search retrieval quality guide\n\nSupports: Preserving document structure, semantic boundaries, and useful chunk metadata.\n\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Split documents using section boundaries, then subdivide sections exceeding 400 tokens into smaller chunks that fit the embedding model’s input limit.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe strategy must satisfy two requirements: preserve meaningful contract structure and stay within the embedding model’s capacity.\n\nA combines both. Section boundaries provide a useful starting point, while further subdivision prevents long sections from exceeding the limit. Paragraph-only splitting does not guarantee suitable size or sufficient clause context; fixed windows discard useful structure; unrestricted sections violate the model limit.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Recognizable contract sections — Structure signal: Natural boundaries are available and should guide splitting.\n\n- 512-token embedding limit — Hard constraint: Each embedding input must fit.\n\n- Clause-focused questions — Retrieval requirement: Chunks should concentrate on specific provisions.\n\n- 40-page contracts — Scale signal: Whole-document representations would be too broad for this task.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nStructure-aware splitting keeps related provisions together where possible. Long sections can then be divided at subsection, paragraph, or sentence boundaries while enforcing the token limit.\n\nDatabricks recommends considering document structure, semantic coherence, chunk size, and embedding-model limits when preparing RAG data. \n\nUse the embedding model’s tokenizer to check the complete input. Any headings or other text added before embedding must also fit within 512 tokens.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Paragraph boundaries can preserve local meaning. However, a paragraph may exceed 512 tokens, and a clause may span several paragraphs. Splitting every paragraph separately does not address both constraints.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Fixed-size chunks provide predictable lengths, and overlap can reduce boundary losses. However, the strategy explicitly ignores available contract structure and may divide a clause or combine unrelated provisions.","Option D — Violates Requirement: Preserving a whole section does not override the embedding limit. Oversized inputs risk truncation or rejection, depending on the serving implementation, potentially excluding the provision needed for retrieval."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality"],"source_hash":"ae9dfa2c29d78dec5716b74b8344f6da4586cc8d9d12159af7ac18a275a181ab","stem_ja":"DatabricksのRAGエージェントが、大量の契約文書について詳しい質問に答える予定です。各契約は平均40ページの構造化PDFで、「定義」「義務」「解除条項」などの分かりやすい節を持ちます。\n\n埋め込みモデルの入力上限は512トークンです。質問は、例えば補償条項内の解除条件を特定するといった個別条項に集中します。そのため文書全体の要約ではなく、正確な規定を特定できる検索が必要です。\n\n最も適切なチャンク分割方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["節の境界で文書を分割し、400トークンを超える節はさらに小さく分けて、埋め込みモデルの入力上限に収める。","条項ごとの検索で粒度を最大化するため、全文書で各段落を別々のチャンクとして扱う。","節の境界は無視し、埋め込みの入力上限を守りながら、512トークンの固定チャンクを50トークン重複させて作る。","512トークンを超える場合でも、節の文脈を完全に保持するため、各節全体を1つのチャンクとして埋め込む。"],"explanation_ja":"契約の節や条項は、義務と例外を解釈する意味のまとまりです。構造を優先して分け、長い節だけ入力上限に合うよう再分割します。400トークンは512の上限に余裕を残す設定であり、すべての用途の万能値ではありません。細かさだけを最大化したり、固定長で機械的に切ったりせず、「意味の境界とモデル上限を両立する」と覚えます。","distractors_ja":["節の意味を保ちながら、大きすぎる部分だけを再分割できます。条項単位の精密な検索と埋め込み入力制限の両方に対応します。","段落が法的な意味の単位と一致するとは限りません。条件と例外を別々にすると、粒度は上がっても正しい条項の解釈を損ないます。","長さは制御できますが、節や条項の途中を切りやすく、構造化された契約という特徴を十分利用できません。","入力上限を超える節は切り詰めや処理失敗につながります。全文脈を守る意図でも、モデルが読めないサイズにはできません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-038","number":38,"form":3,"stem":"A customer support agent conducts extended conversations involving more than 100 tool calls per session. MLflow traces reveal that, as context usage approaches approximately 75% of the model’s window, the agent begins attributing earlier tool results incorrectly, recalling the wrong previously retrieved policy, and overlooking constraints established in earlier turns.\n\nThe model has not reached its hard token limit. The team requires each conversation to remain one uninterrupted session while preserving previously retrieved facts and maintaining retrieval capability.\n\nWhich configuration best addresses this deterioration?","options":["Switch to a model with a larger context window, allowing more conversation turns before attention becomes constrained and restoring accurate recall of earlier tool results.","Apply sliding-window truncation that removes the oldest messages whenever context usage exceeds 75%, keeping the active history within a smaller operating range.","Stop retrieval tool calls after a predetermined number of turns, preventing further context growth while accepting that additional policy lookups become unavailable.","Enable conversation compaction that condenses message history into a high-fidelity summary and continues the conversation using that summary alongside recently used context items."],"correct":3,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"ハード上限前の品質低下と会話圧縮","concept":"ce-f3-q038","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Enable conversation compaction that condenses message history into a high-fidelity summary and continues the conversation using that summary alongside recently used context items.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirements are continuity, retention of retrieved facts, and continued retrieval access. Compaction reduces the active history while carrying its important information forward. Truncation drops earlier evidence, disabling retrieval violates a requirement, and a larger window alone does not establish reliable recall.\n\nThe exam guide specifically covers recognizing context-length degradation and selecting an intervention that restores performance. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Technical signal — degradation before the hard limit: Capacity and reliable context use are different concerns.\n\n- Hard constraint — one uninterrupted session: The solution must preserve conversational continuity.\n\n- Hard constraint — preserve retrieved facts: Simply deleting old messages is unsuitable.\n\n- Hard constraint — maintain retrieval capability: Tool access must remain available.\n\n- Technical signal — errors near 75% usage: This is an observed intervention point for this workload, not a universal threshold.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nCompaction replaces accumulated history with a smaller representation that preserves information needed for continued work. Recent context can remain available in detail while earlier facts are carried through the summary. This supports an ongoing conversation without retaining every historical message in the active prompt. \n\nFor this agent, the retention rules should explicitly preserve policy facts and source identifiers, tool-result associations, user constraints, decisions, and unresolved issues.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: A larger window provides additional capacity, but does not guarantee accurate use of earlier information. It leaves the accumulated-history problem unresolved.\n\n- Option B — Violates Requirement: Age-based deletion can remove facts and constraints that remain necessary later.\n\n- Option C — Violates Requirement: Disabling retrieval prevents new policy lookups, directly reducing the required capability.\n\nExam Trap\n\nA context window is a capacity limit, not a guarantee of consistent recall throughout that capacity.\n\nSliding-window trimming would become more suitable if older information predictably became irrelevant and could safely be discarded. This scenario explicitly requires retaining earlier facts.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nA single user session can continue across changes to the model’s active context. Session continuity does not require sending the complete transcript on every invocation.\n\nCompaction is not guaranteed to be lossless. Its retention quality must be evaluated; exact evidence that must remain reproducible should also be preserved outside the summary. Anthropic recommends tuning compaction for recall before removing unnecessary detail. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nTrigger compaction before the observed degradation region, then evaluate whether the agent still associates policies with the correct sources and obeys earlier constraints. Preserve original tool results for verification when exact quotations are required.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nWhen history must remain useful but cannot remain fully active, compact it with explicit retention rules.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose D when long-context performance deteriorates before overflow and the agent must retain earlier information while continuing tool use. Treat the observed 75% point as workload evidence, not a universal configuration value.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Anthropic — Compaction Overview\nSupports: Replacing older conversation history with a summary while retaining recent context.\n[Official documentation](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/compaction)\n\n- Anthropic — Effective Context Engineering for AI Agents\nSupports: Long-context degradation, compaction, and retention-quality tradeoffs.\n[Official guidance](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: A larger window provides additional capacity, but does not guarantee accurate use of earlier information. It leaves the accumulated-history problem unresolved.","Option B — Violates Requirement: Age-based deletion can remove facts and constraints that remain necessary later.","Option C — Violates Requirement: Disabling retrieval prevents new policy lookups, directly reducing the required capability.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Enable conversation compaction that condenses message history into a high-fidelity summary and continues the conversation using that summary alongside recently used context items.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirements are continuity, retention of retrieved facts, and continued retrieval access. Compaction reduces the active history while carrying its important information forward. Truncation drops earlier evidence, disabling retrieval violates a requirement, and a larger window alone does not establish reliable recall.\n\nThe exam guide specifically covers recognizing context-length degradation and selecting an intervention that restores performance. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Technical signal — degradation before the hard limit: Capacity and reliable context use are different concerns.\n\n- Hard constraint — one uninterrupted session: The solution must preserve conversational continuity.\n\n- Hard constraint — preserve retrieved facts: Simply deleting old messages is unsuitable.\n\n- Hard constraint — maintain retrieval capability: Tool access must remain available.\n\n- Technical signal — errors near 75% usage: This is an observed intervention point for this workload, not a universal threshold.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nCompaction replaces accumulated history with a smaller representation that preserves information needed for continued work. Recent context can remain available in detail while earlier facts are carried through the summary. This supports an ongoing conversation without retaining every historical message in the active prompt. \n\nFor this agent, the retention rules should explicitly preserve policy facts and source identifiers, tool-result associations, user constraints, decisions, and unresolved issues."],"references":["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/compaction","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"6fb202010e0ef5dcf8bcb05fc82cf39cc9eadd914c22509d0d2c2bc62361db43","stem_ja":"カスタマーサポートエージェントが、1セッションで100回を超えるツール呼び出しを含む長い会話を行います。MLflowトレースによると、コンテキスト使用率がモデルのウィンドウの約75%へ近づくと、以前のツール結果を誤って別のものに結び付け、以前取得した別のポリシーを思い出し、前のターンで確定した制約を見落とし始めます。\n\nモデルは厳密なトークン上限には達していません。会話を中断のない1つのセッションとして保ち、過去に取得した事実と検索機能も維持する必要があります。\n\nこの品質低下に最も適した設定はどれですか。","options_ja":["より大きなコンテキストウィンドウを持つモデルへ切り替え、注意が制約されるまでに保持できる会話を増やし、以前のツール結果を正確に思い出せるようにする。","使用率が75%を超えるたびに古いメッセージを削除するスライディングウィンドウの切り詰めを適用し、アクティブな履歴をより小さな範囲に保つ。","所定のターン数を超えたら検索ツールの呼び出しを止め、追加のポリシー照会ができなくなることを受け入れてコンテキスト増大を防ぐ。","会話履歴を忠実な要約へ圧縮し、その要約と最近使ったコンテキスト項目を併用して会話を継続する、会話圧縮を有効にする。"],"explanation_ja":"コンテキストは上限に達する前でも、長さと不要情報によって記憶の対応付けや注意の精度が落ちます。必要な事実・制約・判断を高忠実度の要約へ残し、最近の情報と合わせて同じ会話を続けます。古い順に削るだけでは必要な証拠が失われ、検索停止は機能要件に反します。「容量内でも品質は下がる」がポイントです。","distractors_ja":["大きい窓は余裕を増やしますが、長文の中で情報を誤って使う現象を必ず解消するわけではありません。必要情報の整理が必要です。","古い情報にも現在の制約や根拠が含まれます。単純な削除では、保持しなければならない過去の事実を失う可能性があります。","検索を続けられることが必須なので、追加照会を禁止する方法は条件違反です。成長を止めるために必要機能を捨ててはいけません。","必要な事実と制約を残しつつ履歴を軽量化できます。会話と検索機能を保ちながら、注意を使う情報を整理する直接的な対策です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-039","number":39,"form":3,"stem":"A data analyst has configured a Genie Agent, formerly called a Genie Space, for a corporate treasury team. The configuration includes accurate table and column descriptions, trusted SQL assets for regulatory reporting, and SQL expressions for important metrics.\n\nValidation shows that quantitative calculations are accurate, but treasury terminology is interpreted incorrectly:\n\n- “The rates desk” prompts Genie to look for a `rates_desk` column instead of applying the appropriate `product_type` or `business_unit` filter.\n\n- “bps” is not recognized as “basis points.”\n\n- “Last fixing” is not understood to mean the most recent ECB reference-rate date.\n\nWhich configuration change should the engineer make?","options":["Register trusted example SQL queries for frequently asked treasury questions, using the team’s terminology so matching requests reuse vetted queries.","Define “rates desk,” “bps,” and “last fixing” through knowledge-store SQL filters and measures so Genie applies their associated logic when interpreting questions.","Add general instructions explaining the treasury team’s specialized vocabulary so Genie can interpret analysts’ questions using the correct business context.","Add sample questions containing the team’s preferred terminology so Genie can infer the intended meanings from their phrasing."],"correct":2,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"Genieの一般指示で業務語彙を定義する","concept":"ce-f3-q039","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Add general instructions explaining the treasury team’s specialized vocabulary so Genie can interpret analysts’ questions using the correct business context.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nC is the intended answer: the scenario emphasizes missing vocabulary definitions rather than incorrect calculations. Explicit definitions directly explain how the team uses its terminology.\n\nHowever, the item is not completely exclusive under current documentation. Option B is also defensible for concepts expressible as reusable SQL. Databricks recommends structured SQL definitions over text instructions when they can address the requirement. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Technical signal — accurate quantitative answers: Existing calculation logic is working.\n\n- Technical signal — misinterpreted terminology: The observed gap concerns business meaning.\n\n- Technical signal — three different vocabulary failures: The agent needs explicit explanations spanning organizational shorthand, an abbreviation, and a date convention.\n\n- Scope signal — treasury-team terminology: Definitions must reflect this team’s usage.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nGeneral instructions can explain that “rates desk” refers to an organizational or product grouping, “bps” means basis points, and “last fixing” has the team-specific date meaning given in the scenario.\n\nDatabricks documents text instructions as a way to supply domain jargon and concepts that apply broadly. It also limits their recommended use to context that does not fit better into structured formats. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Example SQL can teach terminology through concrete question-to-query mappings. It is useful for recurring query patterns, but does not directly provide the shared glossary emphasized by the question.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: SQL filters, measures, and synonyms can encode concepts such as desk membership and latest-date selection. This is a strong alternative under current guidance; it should not be dismissed as incapable of teaching terminology.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Questions containing unexplained jargon do not establish what that jargon means. They provide phrasing without the missing definitions or corresponding SQL.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDo not conclude that terminology always belongs in general instructions. Current Genie supports business semantics and synonyms in SQL expressions.\n\nIf the requirement explicitly prioritized consistent execution of a reusable desk filter or latest-fixing calculation, B would become the stronger choice. The present wording points toward C but leaves meaningful overlap.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nSeparate vocabulary explanations from executable business definitions. Text explains meaning; structured expressions encode reusable logic; example SQL demonstrates complete query patterns. These mechanisms can complement each other.\n\nThe exam objective explicitly covers selecting instructions, sample questions, and trusted SQL assets for Genie configuration. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nDocument the treasury glossary, encode suitable filters and calculations as structured definitions, and test alternative phrasings. This reduces interpretation errors without relying solely on natural-language instructions.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nExplain unfamiliar language explicitly, and encode reusable SQL logic where it fits.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose C for the intended vocabulary-gap diagnosis. In production, prefer structured definitions for executable semantics and use general instructions for remaining global terminology.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Tune Genie Agent Quality\nSupports: Domain instructions, example queries, SQL expressions, synonyms, and current naming.\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality)\n\n- Databricks — Curate an Effective Genie Agent\nSupports: Preference for structured SQL definitions when they satisfy the requirement.\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Example SQL can teach terminology through concrete question-to-query mappings. It is useful for recurring query patterns, but does not directly provide the shared glossary emphasized by the question.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: SQL filters, measures, and synonyms can encode concepts such as desk membership and latest-date selection. This is a strong alternative under current guidance; it should not be dismissed as incapable of teaching terminology.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Add general instructions explaining the treasury team’s specialized vocabulary so Genie can interpret analysts’ questions using the correct business context.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nC is the intended answer: the scenario emphasizes missing vocabulary definitions rather than incorrect calculations. Explicit definitions directly explain how the team uses its terminology.\n\nHowever, the item is not completely exclusive under current documentation. Option B is also defensible for concepts expressible as reusable SQL. Databricks recommends structured SQL definitions over text instructions when they can address the requirement. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Technical signal — accurate quantitative answers: Existing calculation logic is working.\n\n- Technical signal — misinterpreted terminology: The observed gap concerns business meaning.\n\n- Technical signal — three different vocabulary failures: The agent needs explicit explanations spanning organizational shorthand, an abbreviation, and a date convention.\n\n- Scope signal — treasury-team terminology: Definitions must reflect this team’s usage.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nGeneral instructions can explain that “rates desk” refers to an organizational or product grouping, “bps” means basis points, and “last fixing” has the team-specific date meaning given in the scenario.\n\nDatabricks documents text instructions as a way to supply domain jargon and concepts that apply broadly. It also limits their recommended use to context that does not fit better into structured formats.","Option D — Incorrect: Questions containing unexplained jargon do not establish what that jargon means. They provide phrasing without the missing definitions or corresponding SQL."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices"],"source_hash":"aac92a8d936d918b2ad9d102197f9d89edb817fbdfe5e0de6645db7926283349","stem_ja":"データアナリストが、企業の財務部門向けにGenie Agent（旧称Genie Space）を設定しました。正確なテーブル・列の説明、規制報告用の信頼済みSQL資産、重要指標のSQL式が含まれています。\n\n検証では数値計算は正しいものの、財務部門の用語が誤って解釈されます。\n\n- 「the rates desk」で、適切な`product_type`または`business_unit`のフィルターを使わず、`rates_desk`という列を探す。\n- 「bps」を「basis points（ベーシスポイント）」と認識しない。\n- 「last fixing」がECB参照レートの最新日を意味すると理解しない。\n\n行うべき設定変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["頻出する財務の質問について、部門の用語を使った信頼済みのSQL例を登録し、一致する依頼で検証済みクエリを再利用させる。","「rates desk」「bps」「last fixing」をナレッジストアのSQLフィルターとメジャーで定義し、質問解釈時に対応ロジックを適用させる。","財務部門固有の語彙を説明する一般指示を追加し、アナリストの質問を適切な業務文脈で解釈できるようにする。","部門が使う用語を含むサンプル質問を追加し、その言い回しから意図する意味を推測できるようにする。"],"explanation_ja":"数式やテーブル説明は正しいので、足りないのは利用者の言葉を業務上の意味へ対応付ける説明です。略語、部署内の呼び方、日付の定義を一般指示で明示します。SQL例は特定の質問パターンには役立ちますが、語彙全体の意味を教える代わりではありません。「計算の定義」と「利用者の語彙の定義」を分けます。","distractors_ja":["頻出パターンを再利用するには有効ですが、未知の言い回しも含めた語彙の解釈問題を、個別クエリだけで埋める方法ではありません。","実行ロジックの定義は役立つ場合がありますが、今回必要なのは部門語彙の意味です。全用語をSQL要素で代用する必要はありません。","利用者の表現と正しい業務概念を直接対応付けます。列名の誤解や略語・日付表現の解釈に対する最も直接的な修正です。","質問例に単語があるだけでは、その単語の意味は確定しません。推測へ任せず、一般指示で明示する方が確実です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-040","number":40,"form":3,"stem":"A context engineer is developing a multi-turn research assistant with LangGraph on Databricks. The agent executes in an asynchronous Python environment and must persist accumulated conversation state in Lakebase-backed PostgreSQL so interrupted threads can resume.\n\nA valid asynchronous database connection pool is already available. The engineer must select and initialize the checkpointer to register when compiling the LangGraph graph.\n\nWhich approach is correct?","options":["Construct `AsyncPostgresSaver` with the async pool, execute `checkpointer.setup()` synchronously because initialization requires synchronous execution, and register it using `graph.compile(checkpointer=checkpointer)`.","Construct `AsyncCheckpointer` with the async pool, execute `await checkpointer.setup()`, set its `connection_string` parameter to the Lakebase PostgreSQL endpoint, and pass it to `graph.compile()`.","Construct `AsyncPostgresSaver` with the async pool, execute `await checkpointer.setup()` to initialize the database schema, and register it using `graph.compile(checkpointer=checkpointer)`.","Construct `AsyncCheckpointer` with the async pool and register it using `graph.compile(checkpointer=checkpointer)`, allowing automatic migration on the first write to initialize the schema."],"correct":2,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"非同期Postgresチェックポインターの初期化","concept":"ce-f3-q040","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Construct `AsyncPostgresSaver` with the async pool, execute `await checkpointer.setup()` to initialize the database schema, and register it using `graph.compile(checkpointer=checkpointer)`.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe implementation must match both the PostgreSQL backend and the asynchronous runtime. `AsyncPostgresSaver` provides that combination. Its database initialization is asynchronous and must be awaited before use; the initialized instance is then supplied through the graph’s `checkpointer` argument. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Hard constraint — asynchronous Python: Database initialization must follow the async execution model.\n\n- Hard constraint — persistent thread state: The agent needs a durable checkpointer.\n\n- Technical signal — PostgreSQL pool already available: Use the PostgreSQL saver with the existing compatible pool.\n\n- Sequence constraint — initialize before use: Schema setup precedes checkpoint operations.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe essential initialization sequence, inside the existing async environment, is:\n\n```\nfrom langgraph.checkpoint.postgres.aio import AsyncPostgresSaver checkpointer = AsyncPostgresSaver(pool)await checkpointer.setup()compiled_graph = graph.compile(checkpointer=checkpointer)\n```\n\n`setup()` creates the required checkpoint tables and runs migrations. The implementation explicitly requires initial setup; constructing the saver alone does not perform it. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: It selects the correct class but omits `await`. Calling this asynchronous method without awaiting it does not complete initialization.\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: `AsyncCheckpointer` is not the documented concrete PostgreSQL saver. The proposed generic class and `connection_string` configuration do not match the required implementation.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: It selects the wrong class and incorrectly assumes first-write schema migration replaces explicit setup.\n\nExam Trap\n\nAn async runtime does not mean every operation needs `await`: await `setup()`, but call `compile()` normally.\n\nA synchronous PostgreSQL implementation would instead use `PostgresSaver` and synchronous setup. That is a different runtime configuration.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nA checkpointer persists execution state associated with a conversation thread. It differs from a cross-thread memory store used for reusable user facts or preferences.\n\nThe pool must be compatible with the saver’s supported PostgreSQL connection interface. Initialize the schema on first use or during deployment setup, rather than treating setup as a per-turn operation.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nKeep the pool available while the compiled graph runs and reuse the same `thread_id` when continuing an interrupted conversation. This allows the agent to locate the correct persisted thread state. Lakebase provides the managed PostgreSQL backend for that persistence. \n\nMemory Hook\n\nAsync PostgreSQL checkpoints use `AsyncPostgresSaver`: await setup, then compile.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose C. Match the saver to the database and execution model, explicitly initialize its schema, and register it through `checkpointer=`.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- LangGraph — Memory\nSupports: Async PostgreSQL checkpointing, setup, compilation, and thread-scoped state.\n[Official documentation](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/add-memory)\n\n- LangGraph — AsyncPostgresSaver Implementation\nSupports: Connection-pool handling and asynchronous schema initialization.\n[Official source code](https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/blob/main/libs/checkpoint-postgres/langgraph/checkpoint/postgres/aio.py)\n\n- Databricks — Agent State and Memory\nSupports: Lakebase as a persistent PostgreSQL backend for agent state.\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: It selects the correct class but omits `await`. Calling this asynchronous method without awaiting it does not complete initialization.","Option B — Incorrect: `AsyncCheckpointer` is not the documented concrete PostgreSQL saver. The proposed generic class and `connection_string` configuration do not match the required implementation.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Construct `AsyncPostgresSaver` with the async pool, execute `await checkpointer.setup()` to initialize the database schema, and register it using `graph.compile(checkpointer=checkpointer)`.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe implementation must match both the PostgreSQL backend and the asynchronous runtime. `AsyncPostgresSaver` provides that combination. Its database initialization is asynchronous and must be awaited before use; the initialized instance is then supplied through the graph’s `checkpointer` argument. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Hard constraint — asynchronous Python: Database initialization must follow the async execution model.\n\n- Hard constraint — persistent thread state: The agent needs a durable checkpointer.\n\n- Technical signal — PostgreSQL pool already available: Use the PostgreSQL saver with the existing compatible pool.\n\n- Sequence constraint — initialize before use: Schema setup precedes checkpoint operations.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe essential initialization sequence, inside the existing async environment, is:\n\n```\nfrom langgraph.checkpoint.postgres.aio import AsyncPostgresSaver checkpointer = AsyncPostgresSaver(pool)await checkpointer.setup()compiled_graph = graph.compile(checkpointer=checkpointer)\n```\n\n`setup()` creates the required checkpoint tables and runs migrations. The implementation explicitly requires initial setup; constructing the saver alone does not perform it.","Option D — Incorrect: It selects the wrong class and incorrectly assumes first-write schema migration replaces explicit setup."],"references":["https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langgraph/add-memory","https://github.com/langchain-ai/langgraph/blob/main/libs/checkpoint-postgres/langgraph/checkpoint/postgres/aio.py","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management"],"source_hash":"e8d9a7b2e9449343758c9c5c2207443d8ed42980459912d7b80d575108ef84e5","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、Databricks上でLangGraphを使った複数ターンの調査アシスタントを開発しています。エージェントは非同期Python環境で動き、中断したスレッドを再開できるよう、蓄積した会話状態をLakebaseを基盤とするPostgreSQLへ永続化する必要があります。\n\n有効な非同期データベース接続プールはすでに利用できます。LangGraphグラフのコンパイル時に登録するチェックポインターを選び、初期化する必要があります。\n\n正しい方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["非同期プールで`AsyncPostgresSaver`を作り、初期化は同期実行が必要なので`checkpointer.setup()`を同期的に実行し、`graph.compile(checkpointer=checkpointer)`で登録する。","非同期プールで`AsyncCheckpointer`を作り、`await checkpointer.setup()`を実行し、その`connection_string`をLakebase PostgreSQLエンドポイントへ設定して、`graph.compile()`へ渡す。","非同期プールで`AsyncPostgresSaver`を作り、`await checkpointer.setup()`でデータベーススキーマを初期化し、`graph.compile(checkpointer=checkpointer)`で登録する。","非同期プールで`AsyncCheckpointer`を作り、`graph.compile(checkpointer=checkpointer)`で登録し、最初の書き込み時の自動マイグレーションにスキーマ初期化を任せる。"],"explanation_ja":"PostgreSQLの非同期チェックポインターはAsyncPostgresSaverです。初回の保存スキーマをawait付きのsetupで初期化し、グラフのcompileにcheckpointerとして渡します。非同期のsetupを呼ぶだけでawaitしないと、必要な処理が実行完了しません。チェックポイントはスレッド内の進捗であり、別会話のユーザー事実を扱う長期ストアとは区別します。","distractors_ja":["非同期setupはawaitして完了させる必要があります。同期関数のように呼ぶだけでは、スキーマ初期化を正しく実行できません。","ここで使う具体的なPostgres実装はAsyncPostgresSaverです。提示されたAsyncCheckpointerの初期化手順は適切なAPIではありません。","非同期プール、非同期setup、コンパイル時のcheckpointer登録という手順が一致します。中断したスレッドを永続状態から再開できます。","適切な具体クラスを使わず、必要な初期化を自動の初回書き込みに任せています。設問の正しいPostgres初期化手順ではありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-041","number":41,"form":3,"stem":"A context engineer is developing a Databricks customer support agent that uses progressive disclosure to access MCP tools. The server exposes 60 tools, including four with similar names and overlapping descriptions:\n\n- `fetch_order_status`\n\n- `get_order_status`\n\n- `retrieve_order_info`\n\n- `lookup_order_details`\n\nWhen a customer asks about a delayed shipment, discovery supplies all four schemas to the model. The agent spends excessive tokens comparing redundant candidates and selects different tools across otherwise identical requests.\n\nWhich change should the engineer implement?","options":["Add distinct capability descriptions to each tool’s registry metadata, then match those descriptions against user intent during discovery and expose only the best-matching tool schema.","Cluster the four tools by embedding similarity during discovery, then expose the schema nearest the centroid of the highest-scoring cluster.","Replace the four registry entries with one canonical tool schema containing a lookup-variant parameter, ensuring that order requests expose only one schema.","Load all four schemas into context, then use a lightweight intent classifier to rank them and forward only the highest-ranked schema for execution."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"ツールの機能説明と詳細ロード前の絞り込み","concept":"ce-f3-q041","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Add distinct capability descriptions to each tool’s registry metadata, then match those descriptions against user intent during discovery and expose only the best-matching tool schema.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe solution must address both ambiguous selection and unnecessary schema loading. Option A makes the capabilities distinguishable and filters candidates before their full schemas enter context.\n\nClear tool descriptions support appropriate selection, while progressive disclosure limits detailed definitions to those needed for the request. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Technical signal — overlapping descriptions: Discovery lacks meaningful capability distinctions.\n\n- Technical signal — four schemas surfaced: Filtering occurs too broadly.\n\n- Technical signal — inconsistent selections: Similar names do not provide reliable routing criteria.\n\n- Technical signal — progressive disclosure: Detailed schemas should be loaded selectively.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption A combines two complementary changes:\n\n- Describe each tool’s actual purpose, supported inputs, returned information, and appropriate usage conditions.\n\n- Use those distinctions to identify the relevant capability before loading its complete schema.\n\nThe descriptions must reflect genuine implementation differences. Renaming identical capabilities or inventing distinctions would not improve routing.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Clustering can reduce redundant candidates, but similarity to a cluster’s center does not establish suitability for a delayed-shipment request. It also leaves the descriptions ambiguous.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Consolidation may suit genuinely interchangeable implementations. However, it requires an interface redesign and can move the unresolved choice into the lookup-variant parameter.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Ranking may improve the final selection, but loading all four schemas first has already incurred the context cost. It also leaves the underlying metadata ambiguity unresolved.\n\nExam Trap\n\nSelecting one tool for execution does not mean only one schema entered context. Filtering must precede full-schema loading to address the token problem.\n\nIf the four tools were confirmed to be interchangeable duplicates, consolidating them into one canonical implementation could make C appropriate.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nProgressive disclosure separates lightweight capability discovery from detailed schema inspection. Discovery identifies what is relevant; inspection supplies the parameters needed to construct a valid call.\n\nReturning one schema is appropriate for this specific lookup request. More complex requests may legitimately require several tools.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nEvaluate delayed-shipment requests and paraphrases after updating metadata and discovery. Compare selection accuracy and schema-token usage to confirm that routing improves while context overhead falls.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nDistinguish capabilities first, filter during discovery, and load schemas afterward.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose A when overlapping metadata causes both selection ambiguity and excess schema tokens. Ranking after loading is too late to recover the context already consumed.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Anthropic — Writing Effective Tools for Agents\nSupports: Clear capability boundaries, descriptions, inputs, and outputs.\n[Official guidance](https://www.anthropic.com/research/writing-tools-for-agents)\n\n- Anthropic — Code Execution with MCP\nSupports: Progressive disclosure and loading tool definitions on demand.\n[Official guidance](https://www.anthropic.com/research/code-execution-with-mcp)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Add distinct capability descriptions to each tool’s registry metadata, then match those descriptions against user intent during discovery and expose only the best-matching tool schema.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe solution must address both ambiguous selection and unnecessary schema loading. Option A makes the capabilities distinguishable and filters candidates before their full schemas enter context.\n\nClear tool descriptions support appropriate selection, while progressive disclosure limits detailed definitions to those needed for the request. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Technical signal — overlapping descriptions: Discovery lacks meaningful capability distinctions.\n\n- Technical signal — four schemas surfaced: Filtering occurs too broadly.\n\n- Technical signal — inconsistent selections: Similar names do not provide reliable routing criteria.\n\n- Technical signal — progressive disclosure: Detailed schemas should be loaded selectively.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption A combines two complementary changes:\n\n- Describe each tool’s actual purpose, supported inputs, returned information, and appropriate usage conditions.\n\n- Use those distinctions to identify the relevant capability before loading its complete schema.\n\nThe descriptions must reflect genuine implementation differences. Renaming identical capabilities or inventing distinctions would not improve routing.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Clustering can reduce redundant candidates, but similarity to a cluster’s center does not establish suitability for a delayed-shipment request. It also leaves the descriptions ambiguous.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Consolidation may suit genuinely interchangeable implementations. However, it requires an interface redesign and can move the unresolved choice into the lookup-variant parameter.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Ranking may improve the final selection, but loading all four schemas first has already incurred the context cost. It also leaves the underlying metadata ambiguity unresolved."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/writing-tools-for-agents","https://www.anthropic.com/research/code-execution-with-mcp"],"source_hash":"08021f65bc75f419d52de2536c1fa0e1f3c1612240b85e134d9d85ff0903ad70","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、MCPツールへ段階的開示でアクセスするDatabricksカスタマーサポートエージェントを開発しています。サーバーは60個のツールを公開し、その中に名前が似て説明も重なる4つがあります。\n\n- `fetch_order_status`\n- `get_order_status`\n- `retrieve_order_info`\n- `lookup_order_details`\n\n顧客が配送の遅延を問い合わせると、発見段階で4つすべてのスキーマがモデルへ渡されます。エージェントは重複候補の比較に過剰なトークンを使い、ほぼ同じ依頼でも異なるツールを選びます。\n\n実装すべき変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["各ツールのレジストリメタデータに、区別できる機能説明を追加する。発見時にユーザーの意図とその説明を照合し、最も合うツールのスキーマだけを公開する。","発見段階で4ツールを埋め込み類似度でクラスタリングし、最高得点クラスターの重心に最も近いスキーマを公開する。","4つのレジストリエントリを、検索方式を指定するパラメーターを持つ1つの正規ツールスキーマに置き換え、注文の依頼には常に1つのスキーマだけを公開する。","4つすべてのスキーマをコンテキストへ読み込み、その後で軽量な意図分類器で順位付けし、最高順位のスキーマだけを実行へ渡す。"],"explanation_ja":"段階的開示の入口で、機能の違いを読み分けられることが重要です。説明を明確化し、意図に合う候補だけの詳細スキーマを読み込めば、比較トークンと選択の揺れを同時に減らせます。似た名前の代表を数学的に選ぶだけでは用途への適合を保証できません。「違いをメタデータに書き、詳細を読む前に絞る」が要点です。","distractors_ja":["実際の機能境界を発見時の軽量情報へ反映し、必要な定義だけを読み込みます。意味の曖昧さとコンテキスト消費の両方を改善します。","クラスターの重心に近いことは、配送遅延への対応に適することを意味しません。機能の違いを明示する必要があります。","統合できる実装もありますが、1つのツールへまとめるだけでは各方式の用途が明確になりません。公開前の機能照合がより直接的です。","全スキーマを読み込んだ時点でトークンコストは発生しています。実行候補だけ後から絞っても、モデルのコンテキストは軽くなりません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-042","number":42,"form":3,"stem":"A Databricks multi-agent pipeline forecasts supply-chain demand. A coordinator divides the work among five sub-agents, each responsible for a product category.\n\nFollowing ingestion of a major inventory update, the sub-agents generate forecasts that agree with one another but are systematically lower than expected. The coordinator’s reasoning log shows that it used the updated figures. However, the sub-agents’ retrieved material still contains supplier lead times and stock quantities from before the update. Neither the embedding model version nor the schema has changed.\n\nWhich diagnosis and corrective action best fit these observations?","options":["Dispatch messages omit the coordinator’s updated reasoning, leaving sub-agents unaware of revised figures; include a compressed summary of the coordinator’s trace with each assignment.","The Databricks AI Search index has not incorporated the inventory update, causing retrieval of outdated documents; synchronize the index with the updated Delta table.","The embedding model cannot adequately represent changes in numerical magnitude, making retrieved documents semantically outdated; fine-tune the model on current inventory records and rebuild the index.","Ingestion wrote the update to a new Delta table while sub-agents remain connected to the previous table; change their source configurations to reference the new table."],"correct":1,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"ソース更新と検索索引の鮮度のずれ","concept":"ce-f3-q042","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. The Databricks AI Search index has not incorporated the inventory update, causing retrieval of outdated documents; synchronize the index with the updated Delta table.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe decisive evidence is the content of the retrieved passages: they still contain pre-update values. That points most directly to stale indexed data.\n\nThe coordinator’s awareness of the update does not refresh the sub-agents’ retrieval source. Synchronizing the index addresses the outdated evidence that the sub-agents actually receive. Databricks documents synchronization as the mechanism for incorporating source-table changes into Delta Sync indexes.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Technical signal — retrieved material contains pre-update figures: The freshness problem appears in retrieval output.\n\n- Technical signal — coordinator uses current figures: Coordinator awareness and retrieval freshness differ.\n\n- Technical signal — forecasts agree but are systematically wrong: Agreement does not establish that shared evidence is current.\n\n- Exclusion — unchanged embedding version and schema: Neither change is identified as the trigger.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAn index can serve older content until source changes are incorporated. Updating that index makes the amended records available to subsequent searches.\n\nSynchronization behavior depends on configuration: continuous Delta Sync indexes update automatically, while triggered indexes require an explicit synchronization request. The workflow should verify successful completion before relying on refreshed results. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Different Use Case: Passing relevant coordinator context can resolve handoff omissions. However, it does not repair the demonstrated problem of retrieval returning outdated records.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: Embedding quality affects similarity matching, but does not itself rewrite stored document text into older values. The trace does not establish a need for fine-tuning.\n\n- Option D — Different Use Case: Reconfiguring sources would fit an actual table migration. The scenario provides no evidence that ingestion created a new table.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDo not diagnose every multi-agent failure as inadequate context propagation. Inspect whether sub-agents receive current retrieval results before changing dispatch messages.\n\nIf retrieval returned current figures but dispatch omitted an essential coordinator decision, A would become the stronger explanation.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nData freshness, retrieval relevance, and context propagation are separate concerns. Diagnose the earliest demonstrated mismatch.\n\nThe exam objective most directly applicable here covers identifying AI Search configuration problems that degrade retrieved context.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nAfter ingestion and index synchronization, retrieve several known updated inventory records and compare their values with the source table. This checks that forecasting agents receive the intended data revision.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nOld values in retrieved passages point first to retrieval-source freshness.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose B when source updates exist but retrieved evidence remains old. Mutually consistent agents can still share the same stale information.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Create AI Search Endpoints and Indexes\nSupports: Delta Sync index refresh behavior and synchronization options.\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search)\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\nSupports: Diagnosing AI Search configuration problems affecting retrieved context.\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Different Use Case: Passing relevant coordinator context can resolve handoff omissions. However, it does not repair the demonstrated problem of retrieval returning outdated records.","Correct Answer\n\nB. The Databricks AI Search index has not incorporated the inventory update, causing retrieval of outdated documents; synchronize the index with the updated Delta table.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe decisive evidence is the content of the retrieved passages: they still contain pre-update values. That points most directly to stale indexed data.\n\nThe coordinator’s awareness of the update does not refresh the sub-agents’ retrieval source. Synchronizing the index addresses the outdated evidence that the sub-agents actually receive. Databricks documents synchronization as the mechanism for incorporating source-table changes into Delta Sync indexes.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Technical signal — retrieved material contains pre-update figures: The freshness problem appears in retrieval output.\n\n- Technical signal — coordinator uses current figures: Coordinator awareness and retrieval freshness differ.\n\n- Technical signal — forecasts agree but are systematically wrong: Agreement does not establish that shared evidence is current.\n\n- Exclusion — unchanged embedding version and schema: Neither change is identified as the trigger.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAn index can serve older content until source changes are incorporated. Updating that index makes the amended records available to subsequent searches.\n\nSynchronization behavior depends on configuration: continuous Delta Sync indexes update automatically, while triggered indexes require an explicit synchronization request. The workflow should verify successful completion before relying on refreshed results.","Option C — Incorrect: Embedding quality affects similarity matching, but does not itself rewrite stored document text into older values. The trace does not establish a need for fine-tuning.","Option D — Different Use Case: Reconfiguring sources would fit an actual table migration. The scenario provides no evidence that ingestion created a new table."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search","https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf"],"source_hash":"7de5f01a65082dbaf33ee3e57d487c4a5bfcae45e3dfb9a4c9efdbbed353c560","stem_ja":"Databricksのマルチエージェントパイプラインが、サプライチェーンの需要を予測します。調整役は作業を5つのサブエージェントへ分け、それぞれが商品カテゴリを担当します。\n\n大きな在庫更新を取り込んだ後、サブエージェントは互いには一致するものの、期待より系統的に低い予測を出します。調整役の推論ログでは更新済みの数値を使っていますが、サブエージェントが取得した資料には更新前の仕入先リードタイムや在庫数量が残っています。埋め込みモデルの版もスキーマも変更されていません。\n\nこれらの観察に最も合う診断と対策はどれですか。","options_ja":["委譲メッセージに調整役の更新後の推論が含まれず、新しい数値をサブエージェントが認識できていない。割り当てごとに調整役トレースの圧縮要約を含める。","Databricks AI Searchインデックスに在庫更新が取り込まれておらず、古い文書が取得されている。更新済みDeltaテーブルとインデックスを同期する。","埋め込みモデルが数値の大きさの変化を十分表現できず、取得文書が意味的に古くなっている。最新在庫レコードでモデルを微調整し、インデックスを再構築する。","取り込みが新しいDeltaテーブルへ更新を書いた一方、サブエージェントは以前のテーブルへ接続したままである。ソース設定を新しいテーブルへ変更する。"],"explanation_ja":"決め手は、サブエージェントの検索結果自体が更新前の値を含むことです。共有判断の不足より、索引の鮮度をまず疑い、更新済みDeltaと同期します。モデルやスキーマが変わらないことは、数値の更新が検索索引へ届いている保証にはなりません。「ソースは新しい、検索結果は古い」なら同期経路を確認します。","distractors_ja":["引き継ぎの改善が必要な場合もありますが、今回は検索結果の本文が古いという直接証拠があります。まず索引の更新を直すべきです。","ソース更新が索引へ反映されていないことが、全担当の同じ方向への誤りを説明します。最新テーブルとの同期が直接の修正です。","数値表現の能力ではなく、取得文書に旧値が残る問題です。モデルの微調整は同期不足の代わりになりません。","別テーブルへ書いたという証拠はありません。提示された事実から、未同期の索引という診断の方が直接的です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-043","number":43,"form":3,"stem":"A corporate legal team uses a Databricks agent to search regulatory opinions and contract clauses through a DELTA_SYNC Databricks AI Search index configured with `pipeline_type=\"TRIGGERED\"`.\n\nAn automated pipeline refreshes the source Delta table every morning. During a compliance review, attorneys discover that the agent cites the original wording of a clause amended three weeks earlier. The source table already contains the amendment, but the retrieved passage still contains the superseded text.\n\nWhich change most cost-effectively corrects this freshness problem?","options":["Schedule a Databricks job to invoke index synchronization after the daily ingestion pipeline finishes, bringing the index up to date with each daily refresh.","Change pipeline_type from TRIGGERED to CONTINUOUS, keeping the index synchronized with source changes at seconds-level latency.","Replace the DELTA_SYNC index with a DIRECT_ACCESS index and implement programmatic upserts in the ingestion workflow.","Delete and recreate the Databricks AI Search index from the current source-table snapshot to incorporate outstanding amendments."],"correct":0,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"TRIGGERED索引は取り込み後に明示的に同期する","concept":"ce-f3-q043","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Schedule a Databricks job to invoke index synchronization after the daily ingestion pipeline finishes, bringing the index up to date with each daily refresh.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe source contains the correct text, so the discrepancy lies between the source table and the searchable index. A triggered index requires an explicit synchronization operation.\n\nBecause updates arrive daily and cost-effectiveness is the deciding criterion, synchronize after ingestion completes. Continuous synchronization provides greater freshness than the stated update cadence requires. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Technical signal — `pipeline_type=\"TRIGGERED\"`: Synchronization must be explicitly initiated.\n\n- Technical signal — current source, outdated retrieved text: The index has not incorporated the amendment.\n\n- Sequence constraint — daily ingestion: Synchronization should follow successful ingestion.\n\n- Preference — “most cost-effectively”: Match synchronization activity to the update cadence.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption A retains the existing index and automates the missing refresh step. The SDK’s `index.sync()` operation synchronizes a triggered Delta Sync index with its source Delta table. \n\nMaking synchronization dependent on successful ingestion avoids refreshing before the amended data is committed. The workflow should also monitor synchronization completion before treating the index as current.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Continuous synchronization addresses freshness on supported endpoints, but Databricks documents higher associated compute cost. Daily updates do not establish a need for seconds-level synchronization.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Direct Vector Access supports application-managed updates, but replacing the index introduces unnecessary migration and update-management work.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Rebuilding can incorporate current data once, but adds avoidable operational work and leaves future daily synchronization unaddressed.\n\nExam Trap\n\n`DELTA_SYNC` identifies the index’s source-synchronization model; it does not mean every configuration synchronizes continuously.\n\nIf source updates arrived throughout the day and the requirement demanded seconds-level freshness, B would become more appropriate—provided the endpoint supports continuous synchronization. Storage-optimized endpoints support triggered synchronization only. \n\nFoundation Concept\n\nSource freshness and index freshness are separate properties. Correct source data cannot improve retrieval until the index incorporates it.\n\nThis question maps to the exam objective covering diagnosis and remediation of AI Search retrieval configuration problems. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nAdd synchronization as a downstream task in the morning workflow and alert on failures. This makes index freshness part of the ingestion process rather than a separate manual responsibility.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nTriggered index plus scheduled ingestion means explicitly sync after ingestion.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose A when the source is current, a triggered index is stale, and updates arrive on a predictable schedule. Reserve continuous synchronization for requirements that justify its additional cost.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Create AI Search Endpoints and Indexes\nSupports: Triggered versus continuous synchronization, cost differences, and endpoint restrictions.\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search)\n\n- Databricks — AI Search Python SDK\nSupports: Using `sync()` with a triggered Delta Sync index.\n[Official API reference](https://api-docs.databricks.com/python/ai-search/databricks.ai_search.html)","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Schedule a Databricks job to invoke index synchronization after the daily ingestion pipeline finishes, bringing the index up to date with each daily refresh.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe source contains the correct text, so the discrepancy lies between the source table and the searchable index. A triggered index requires an explicit synchronization operation.\n\nBecause updates arrive daily and cost-effectiveness is the deciding criterion, synchronize after ingestion completes. Continuous synchronization provides greater freshness than the stated update cadence requires. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Technical signal — `pipeline_type=\"TRIGGERED\"`: Synchronization must be explicitly initiated.\n\n- Technical signal — current source, outdated retrieved text: The index has not incorporated the amendment.\n\n- Sequence constraint — daily ingestion: Synchronization should follow successful ingestion.\n\n- Preference — “most cost-effectively”: Match synchronization activity to the update cadence.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption A retains the existing index and automates the missing refresh step. The SDK’s `index.sync()` operation synchronizes a triggered Delta Sync index with its source Delta table. \n\nMaking synchronization dependent on successful ingestion avoids refreshing before the amended data is committed. The workflow should also monitor synchronization completion before treating the index as current.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Continuous synchronization addresses freshness on supported endpoints, but Databricks documents higher associated compute cost. Daily updates do not establish a need for seconds-level synchronization.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Direct Vector Access supports application-managed updates, but replacing the index introduces unnecessary migration and update-management work.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Rebuilding can incorporate current data once, but adds avoidable operational work and leaves future daily synchronization unaddressed."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/create-ai-search","https://api-docs.databricks.com/python/ai-search/databricks.ai_search.html"],"source_hash":"79c5a63a6f0cd0492a6f23b1c32735cb2828ab0c8d31881bd42a24d3750dfbbb","stem_ja":"企業の法務チームが、`pipeline_type=\"TRIGGERED\"`に設定されたDELTA_SYNCのDatabricks AI Searchインデックスを通じて、規制意見書と契約条項を検索するエージェントを使っています。\n\n自動パイプラインが毎朝ソースのDeltaテーブルを更新します。コンプライアンスレビュー中、弁護士は3週間前に修正した条項の旧文言をエージェントが引用していると気付きました。ソーステーブルには修正内容がありますが、検索結果は廃止された本文のままです。\n\nこの鮮度の問題を最も費用対効果よく修正する変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["日次取り込みパイプラインの終了後に、Databricksジョブからインデックス同期を呼び出すようスケジュールし、日次更新ごとに索引を最新にする。","pipeline_typeをTRIGGEREDからCONTINUOUSへ変更し、秒単位の遅延でソースの変更を索引へ同期し続ける。","DELTA_SYNCインデックスをDIRECT_ACCESSへ置き換え、取り込みワークフローにプログラムによるupsertを実装する。","未反映の修正を取り込むため、現在のソーステーブルのスナップショットからDatabricks AI Searchインデックスを削除・再作成する。"],"explanation_ja":"TRIGGEREDではソースが変わっただけで同期が走るとは限りません。日次更新が要件なら、取り込み完了後に明示的な同期を連結すれば十分です。CONTINUOUSへの変更や自前upsert、毎回の作り直しは、この頻度に対して運用やコストが過剰です。「更新の頻度に同期の頻度を合わせる」と考えます。","distractors_ja":["取り込みと同期を順番につなぎ、更新済みデータが索引へ確実に届くようにします。日次更新という頻度に合った対策です。","より低遅延の同期には使えますが、毎朝更新するソースに対して常時同期は過剰になり得ます。必要な鮮度に合わせて選びます。","自前の書き込み・削除管理が増えます。既存Delta Syncの同期呼び出しで解決できる問題に、別方式への移行は不要です。","一度は最新化できますが、次の変更でも同じ問題が起こります。再作成ではなく、継続的に適切な同期を実行する仕組みが必要です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-044","number":44,"form":3,"stem":"A context engineer is developing a Databricks-hosted RAG agent for a large collection of internal wiki articles. Documents range from brief 200-word FAQs to technical guides containing more than 3,000 words, and their headings do not follow a consistent structure.\n\nThe embedding model accepts up to 8,192 tokens. Users ask both broad explanatory questions, such as “How does our deployment pipeline work?”, and precise questions, such as “What is the retry limit for the job scheduler?”\n\nWhich chunking approach best supports this workload?","options":["Divide documents into fixed 512-token chunks without overlap, relying on the embedding model’s large context capacity to compensate for splits within sentences.","Embed each article as one chunk, because the 8,192-token input capacity is sufficient to accommodate complete articles without truncation.","Store every sentence as an individual chunk, ensuring that precise lookup questions always retrieve an exact match.","Use recursive character-based splitting with a target size of 512–1,024 tokens and 10–15% overlap, preserving surrounding context for both broad and precise questions."],"correct":3,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"不統一なWikiに適した再帰的分割と重複","concept":"ce-f3-q044","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Use recursive character-based splitting with a target size of 512–1,024 tokens and 10–15% overlap, preserving surrounding context for both broad and precise questions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe strategy must accommodate inconsistent document structure and both query types. Whole articles can be too broad for precise retrieval, while individual sentences can omit context needed for explanations. Recursive splitting provides a practical middle ground, with overlap reducing boundary-related information loss.\n\nDatabricks recommends considering chunk size, semantic coherence, overlap, and the nature of the documents together. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Technical signal — widely varying article lengths: One article should not automatically equal one retrieval chunk.\n\n- Technical signal — inconsistent headings: A heading-dependent strategy would be unreliable.\n\n- Technical signal — broad and narrow questions: Chunks need both local precision and surrounding context.\n\n- Hard constraint — 8,192-token embedding limit: Inputs must fit, but that limit does not determine the optimal retrieval size.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nRecursive splitting attempts larger text boundaries first, such as paragraph breaks, before falling back to smaller separators. It therefore works without requiring standardized headings. Overlap helps preserve information spanning adjacent chunks. \n\nShort FAQs can remain intact when they fit; longer guides are subdivided. The proposed sizes and overlap are starting settings to evaluate, rather than universally optimal values.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: A larger embedding input capacity cannot restore surrounding text omitted from an individual chunk. Arbitrary boundaries without overlap can separate related information.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Whole-document chunks may suit short, focused articles. Longer guides can combine many topics, reducing lookup precision. The stated word counts also do not establish that every article fits the token limit.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: Sentence-sized chunks may lose definitions, qualifiers, or antecedents supplied nearby. Granularity does not guarantee an exact retrieval match.\n\nExam Trap\n\nAn embedding model’s maximum input size is a ceiling, not a recommended chunk size.\n\nIf every document were short, self-contained, and focused on one topic, whole-document chunking could be appropriate. That condition does not describe this mixed corpus.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nChunking balances retrieval precision against contextual completeness. Smaller chunks isolate details; larger chunks preserve relationships but may include unrelated material.\n\nAlso distinguish splitting boundaries from size measurement: recursive character splitters ordinarily measure characters. To enforce token-based targets, configure token-aware measurement using the embedding model’s tokenizer. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nEvaluate the configuration against both deployment-overview questions and scheduler-limit lookups. Retain article identifiers with chunks so retrieved evidence remains traceable. Adjust size and overlap based on answer coverage and retrieval quality.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nFor irregular documents and mixed queries, preserve natural boundaries with moderately sized, overlapping chunks.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose D. Recursive splitting accommodates inconsistent structure, while moderate chunk sizes and overlap balance precise retrieval with context preservation.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Build an Unstructured Data Pipeline for RAG\nSupports: Chunk-size, overlap, coherence, and retrieval tradeoffs.\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag)\n\n- LangChain — Splitting Recursively\nSupports: Recursive separators and overlap behavior.\n[Official documentation](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/integrations/splitters/recursive_text_splitter)\n\n- LangChain — Splitting by Token\nSupports: Token-aware sizing and tokenizer selection.\n[Official documentation](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/integrations/splitters/split_by_token)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: A larger embedding input capacity cannot restore surrounding text omitted from an individual chunk. Arbitrary boundaries without overlap can separate related information.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Whole-document chunks may suit short, focused articles. Longer guides can combine many topics, reducing lookup precision. The stated word counts also do not establish that every article fits the token limit.","Option C — Incorrect: Sentence-sized chunks may lose definitions, qualifiers, or antecedents supplied nearby. Granularity does not guarantee an exact retrieval match.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Use recursive character-based splitting with a target size of 512–1,024 tokens and 10–15% overlap, preserving surrounding context for both broad and precise questions.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe strategy must accommodate inconsistent document structure and both query types. Whole articles can be too broad for precise retrieval, while individual sentences can omit context needed for explanations. Recursive splitting provides a practical middle ground, with overlap reducing boundary-related information loss.\n\nDatabricks recommends considering chunk size, semantic coherence, overlap, and the nature of the documents together. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Technical signal — widely varying article lengths: One article should not automatically equal one retrieval chunk.\n\n- Technical signal — inconsistent headings: A heading-dependent strategy would be unreliable.\n\n- Technical signal — broad and narrow questions: Chunks need both local precision and surrounding context.\n\n- Hard constraint — 8,192-token embedding limit: Inputs must fit, but that limit does not determine the optimal retrieval size.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nRecursive splitting attempts larger text boundaries first, such as paragraph breaks, before falling back to smaller separators. It therefore works without requiring standardized headings. Overlap helps preserve information spanning adjacent chunks. \n\nShort FAQs can remain intact when they fit; longer guides are subdivided. The proposed sizes and overlap are starting settings to evaluate, rather than universally optimal values."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/integrations/splitters/recursive_text_splitter","https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/integrations/splitters/split_by_token"],"source_hash":"ecca837b10123b7f9d81e3e119ca721e23c92f68c26a3d360e78fd3c163d260d","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、大量の社内Wiki記事を対象に、DatabricksでホストするRAGエージェントを開発しています。文書には200語の短いFAQから3,000語を超える技術ガイドまであり、見出し構造は統一されていません。\n\n埋め込みモデルは最大8,192トークンを受け付けます。ユーザーは「デプロイパイプラインはどう動くか」といった広い説明と、「ジョブスケジューラーの再試行上限は何か」といった正確な質問の両方をします。\n\nこの業務に最も適したチャンク分割方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["文の途中で分かれる影響はモデルの大きなコンテキスト容量で補えると考え、重複なしの512トークン固定チャンクへ分割する。","8,192トークンの入力容量なら記事全体を切り詰めずに収められるため、各記事を1つのチャンクとして埋め込む。","精密な照会で必ず完全一致が得られるように、各文を独立したチャンクとして保存する。","512〜1,024トークンを目標に、10〜15%の重複を持つ再帰的な文字ベース分割を使い、広い質問と精密な質問の両方に周辺の文脈を残す。"],"explanation_ja":"構造が不統一で長さも多様なWikiには、境界を探しながら適度な長さへ分ける再帰的分割が向きます。少し重複させれば境界付近の情報を失いにくく、細部と周辺説明の両方を取得できます。モデルに全記事が入ることと、検索単位として適切なことは別です。提示サイズと重複率は出発点で、実データで評価します。","distractors_ja":["埋め込みモデルの容量が大きくても、別チャンクへ切れた文章の周辺関係が自動で回復するわけではありません。重複なしは境界の損失を招きます。","全記事を埋め込めても、長い記事全体では局所的な質問の信号が薄まります。語数とトークン数も一致するとは限りません。","文だけでは条件や背景を分断し、広い説明に必要なまとまりを失います。細かくすれば必ず完全一致で取得できるわけでもありません。","不統一な構造に適応しつつ、必要な周辺文脈を重複で保てます。幅広い質問と細かな照会のバランスを取りやすい方法です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B003-045","number":45,"form":3,"stem":"A context engineer evaluates a multi-turn question-answering agent using four context configurations within one MLflow 3 experiment. The configurations differ in retrieval chunk size, the number of documents retrieved, and whether a system prompt summarizing retrieved context is included.\n\nEvery run records `answer_correctness`, `context_recall`, and `faithfulness`. The engineer defines a reliable configuration as one that performs well across all three metrics, without a weak result on any individual metric.\n\nWhich comparison method best identifies that configuration?","options":["Calculate the arithmetic mean of the three metrics for each configuration and select the configuration with the highest average.","Display the runs in an MLflow parallel coordinates chart and select the configuration that maintains high scores across all three metric axes.","Calculate the range or standard deviation of each configuration’s three scores and select the configuration with the smallest spread.","Identify the highest individual metric score across all runs and select the configuration that produced it."],"correct":1,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"平行座標グラフで全指標の弱点を確認する","concept":"ce-f3-q045","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Display the runs in an MLflow parallel coordinates chart and select the configuration that maintains high scores across all three metric axes.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirement is strong performance on every metric, so the comparison must keep individual weaknesses visible. A parallel coordinates chart supports simultaneous inspection of the metrics without collapsing them into one score.\n\nMLflow’s chart lets engineers select parameters and metrics to investigate relationships between configuration settings and performance. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Hard constraint — “across all three metrics”: No metric can be ignored.\n\n- Hard constraint — “without a weak result”: Strong scores cannot compensate for a poor score elsewhere.\n\n- Technical signal — four configurations in MLflow 3: The runs can be compared using the experiment’s visualization tools.\n\n- Technical signal — configuration parameters vary: Include those parameters alongside metrics to associate results with their settings.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nEach run forms a connected profile across the selected axes. A configuration that scores well throughout remains visibly strong across all three metrics; a weak metric appears as a drop on its corresponding axis.\n\nThe chart supports inspection rather than automatically declaring a winner. Check actual values and axis scales before selecting a configuration.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: An average permits compensation between metrics. For example, hypothetical scores of `1.0, 1.0, 0.4` average `0.8`, concealing the weak third score. This conflicts with the requirement.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: Small spread establishes similarity, not quality. Scores of `0.2, 0.2, 0.2` have zero spread but are uniformly poor.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: One exceptional score says nothing about the other two metrics. It can select a configuration with substantial weaknesses.\n\nExam Trap\n\n“Balanced” does not mean merely “similar.” The scores must be high and balanced.\n\nIf the objective instead explicitly allowed tradeoffs and defined equal metric weights, ranking by arithmetic mean could be appropriate.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nMulti-metric evaluation distinguishes absolute performance, balance, and tradeoffs. A visualization preserves these distinctions; an aggregate score can hide them.\n\nComparisons also require consistent evaluation data and scoring definitions across runs. Sharing an experiment alone does not establish that consistency.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nSet minimum acceptable values for each metric, inspect the four profiles, and exclude configurations that fail any minimum. This prevents a strong overall impression from masking a retrieval or grounding weakness.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nWhen every metric must be strong, inspect every metric rather than only an average or a peak.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose B when the requirement is simultaneous strength across multiple metrics. Averages can hide weak scores, while low spread can describe uniformly poor performance.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Compare MLflow Runs and Models Using Graphs and Charts\nSupports: Parallel coordinates charts for comparing configuration parameters and performance metrics.\n[Official documentation](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow/visualize-runs)\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\nSupports: Selecting reliable context configurations using MLflow 3 experiment results.\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: An average permits compensation between metrics. For example, hypothetical scores of `1.0, 1.0, 0.4` average `0.8`, concealing the weak third score. This conflicts with the requirement.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Display the runs in an MLflow parallel coordinates chart and select the configuration that maintains high scores across all three metric axes.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirement is strong performance on every metric, so the comparison must keep individual weaknesses visible. A parallel coordinates chart supports simultaneous inspection of the metrics without collapsing them into one score.\n\nMLflow’s chart lets engineers select parameters and metrics to investigate relationships between configuration settings and performance. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Hard constraint — “across all three metrics”: No metric can be ignored.\n\n- Hard constraint — “without a weak result”: Strong scores cannot compensate for a poor score elsewhere.\n\n- Technical signal — four configurations in MLflow 3: The runs can be compared using the experiment’s visualization tools.\n\n- Technical signal — configuration parameters vary: Include those parameters alongside metrics to associate results with their settings.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nEach run forms a connected profile across the selected axes. A configuration that scores well throughout remains visibly strong across all three metrics; a weak metric appears as a drop on its corresponding axis.\n\nThe chart supports inspection rather than automatically declaring a winner. Check actual values and axis scales before selecting a configuration.","Option C — Incorrect: Small spread establishes similarity, not quality. Scores of `0.2, 0.2, 0.2` have zero spread but are uniformly poor.","Option D — Incorrect: One exceptional score says nothing about the other two metrics. It can select a configuration with substantial weaknesses."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow/visualize-runs","https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf"],"source_hash":"d61c41d13c1fabe049c9c9c4d9625326f974b147e6d4511c666b5a5f4e8df9b6","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、1つのMLflow 3実験で4種類のコンテキスト設定を使い、複数ターンの質問応答エージェントを評価しています。設定は、取得チャンクのサイズ、取得文書数、取得内容を要約するシステムプロンプトを含めるかどうかが異なります。\n\n各実行には`answer_correctness`、`context_recall`、`faithfulness`を記録します。エンジニアは、どの個別指標にも弱い結果がなく、3指標すべてで良い成績の設定を信頼できると定義しています。\n\nその設定を最もよく見つける比較方法はどれですか。","options_ja":["各設定の3指標の算術平均を計算し、平均が最も高い設定を選ぶ。","MLflowの平行座標グラフに実行を表示し、3つの指標軸すべてで高得点を維持する設定を選ぶ。","各設定の3得点の範囲または標準偏差を計算し、ばらつきが最も小さい設定を選ぶ。","全実行の中で最も高い個別指標の得点を見つけ、それを出した設定を選ぶ。"],"explanation_ja":"求めているのは平均の最大化ではなく、どの軸にも弱点がない設定です。平行座標グラフなら、各設定が3指標のどこで落ちるかを同時に見られます。平均は低い軸を高い軸で隠し、ばらつきの小ささは全指標が低い設定でも達成できます。「全部高い」と「平均が高い」「同じくらい」を区別します。","distractors_ja":["1指標が弱くても、他の高得点で平均が上がる場合があります。個別の弱点を認めないという選定条件を直接満たしません。","各軸に沿った性能を同時に確認でき、どの指標にも低い点がない設定を選べます。設問の信頼性の定義に一致します。","全指標が低くても数値がそろえばばらつきは小さくなります。均一さだけでは高い品質を示しません。","1つだけ高い指標があっても、残りが弱ければ要件を満たしません。3指標を同時に見る必要があります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"}]},{"id":"CE-PRACTICE-4","title":"Context Engineer 模試4","minutes":90,"questions":[{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-001","number":1,"form":4,"stem":"A Databricks agent assists legal teams reviewing pharmaceutical trial protocols. The documents combine narrative background, nested dosage tables with qualifying footnotes, cross-referenced section identifiers, and captions explaining study-design figures.\n\nThe agent must identify which section governs a dosage threshold and which footnote conditions qualify it. Available sources include:\n\n- Original PDF binaries in Unity Catalog Volumes.\n\n- A Delta table of extracted text chunks, indexed in Databricks AI Search and tagged with document identifiers and page numbers.\n\n- A page-level Delta table containing table summaries, figure captions, and section hierarchy, accessible through Unity Catalog functions.\n\nWhich context combination best supports these questions?","options":["Include the entire structural metadata table in the prompt alongside AI Search results so the model can inspect the complete document hierarchy.","Use the original PDFs and structural metadata, locating files by document identifier and extracting table content during query processing.","Retrieve only text chunks through AI Search, relying on semantic similarity to recover the necessary table and footnote relationships.","Retrieve relevant text passages through AI Search, then use their document and page references to fetch scoped structural metadata for the corresponding tables, figures, and sections."],"correct":3,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"本文検索と構造メタデータの組み合わせ","concept":"ce-f4-q001","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Retrieve relevant text passages through AI Search, then use their document and page references to fetch scoped structural metadata for the corresponding tables, figures, and sections.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe task requires both relevant content and the relationships that explain it. Text retrieval locates the dosage discussion; structural metadata helps establish its section hierarchy and surrounding table or figure context.\n\nD combines these sources using the identifiers already attached to each chunk. It supplies targeted supporting context without loading the complete metadata collection or routinely reprocessing original PDFs.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Nested tables and qualifying footnotes — Structural requirement: A nearby number or sentence alone may not establish the applicable condition.\n\n- Cross-referenced sections — Relationship requirement: The agent must understand how passages belong to the protocol hierarchy.\n\n- Document identifiers and page numbers — Linking mechanism: Retrieved chunks can identify the relevant metadata records.\n\n- Metadata accessible through Unity Catalog functions — Tool signal: Scoped structured lookups are already available.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe agent can:\n\n- Retrieve passages relevant to the threshold.\n\n- Read their document and page references.\n\n- Query the corresponding structural metadata.\n\n- Combine the source text with the section, table, and figure context when answering.\n\nDatabricks recommends retaining structural metadata such as section headings, page numbers, and semantic boundaries alongside document chunks. \n\nUnity Catalog function tools can execute predefined, parameterized queries for the metadata lookup. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Complete metadata could provide the needed relationships, but loading the whole collection adds irrelevant material. The chunk identifiers allow a more focused lookup.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Parsing original PDFs can recover detailed content. However, making it the routine query path repeats extraction work despite the available indexed passages and linked metadata. It remains useful when extracted evidence is incomplete.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: Similarity can locate relevant wording, but does not reliably establish relationships among nested table entries, footnotes, and governing sections. It ignores the available source designed to supply that structure.\n\nExam Trap\n\nStructural metadata helps interpret evidence; a table summary does not replace the exact wording of a qualifying footnote.\n\nIf extraction omitted the qualifier, inspect or reparse the relevant original PDF pages. Databricks document parsing supports structured elements including tables, section headers, captions, and footnotes. \n\nFoundation Concept\n\nUse semantic retrieval to locate relevant passages and structured lookup to recover their relationships.\n\nThe connecting identifiers matter: a page number alone is insufficient across a collection. Pair it with the document identifier, and follow explicit cross-references when additional pages are needed.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nAfter retrieving a threshold passage, fetch metadata for that document and page to identify the enclosing section and table context. Preserve the exact qualifier text and source references in the evidence supplied for generation.\n\nThis reduces the chance of attaching a condition to the wrong threshold or citing a similarly numbered section from another protocol.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nRetrieve the passage, then recover its structure through scoped identifiers.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nQuestions about tables, footnotes, and section relationships need more than text similarity. Choose D: combine relevant passages with scoped structural metadata, while preserving the actual qualifying source text.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Build an unstructured data pipeline for RAG](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag)\n\nSupports: Structural metadata and identifiers that connect chunks to document context.\n\n- [Databricks — Create agent tools using Unity Catalog functions](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool)\n\nSupports: Parameterized tools for targeted structured-data lookups.\n\n- [Databricks — ai_parse_document function](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_parse_document)\n\nSupports: Extracting document structure, including tables, captions, section headers, and footnotes.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Complete metadata could provide the needed relationships, but loading the whole collection adds irrelevant material. The chunk identifiers allow a more focused lookup.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Parsing original PDFs can recover detailed content. However, making it the routine query path repeats extraction work despite the available indexed passages and linked metadata. It remains useful when extracted evidence is incomplete.","Option C — Incorrect: Similarity can locate relevant wording, but does not reliably establish relationships among nested table entries, footnotes, and governing sections. It ignores the available source designed to supply that structure.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Retrieve relevant text passages through AI Search, then use their document and page references to fetch scoped structural metadata for the corresponding tables, figures, and sections.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe task requires both relevant content and the relationships that explain it. Text retrieval locates the dosage discussion; structural metadata helps establish its section hierarchy and surrounding table or figure context.\n\nD combines these sources using the identifiers already attached to each chunk. It supplies targeted supporting context without loading the complete metadata collection or routinely reprocessing original PDFs.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Nested tables and qualifying footnotes — Structural requirement: A nearby number or sentence alone may not establish the applicable condition.\n\n- Cross-referenced sections — Relationship requirement: The agent must understand how passages belong to the protocol hierarchy.\n\n- Document identifiers and page numbers — Linking mechanism: Retrieved chunks can identify the relevant metadata records.\n\n- Metadata accessible through Unity Catalog functions — Tool signal: Scoped structured lookups are already available.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe agent can:\n\n- Retrieve passages relevant to the threshold.\n\n- Read their document and page references.\n\n- Query the corresponding structural metadata.\n\n- Combine the source text with the section, table, and figure context when answering.\n\nDatabricks recommends retaining structural metadata such as section headings, page numbers, and semantic boundaries alongside document chunks. \n\nUnity Catalog function tools can execute predefined, parameterized queries for the metadata lookup."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-data-pipeline-rag","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/create-custom-tool","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/functions/ai_parse_document"],"source_hash":"a10ff96187d4985fe13523de1dec73b49bd0fd55bbea41b775d00889c9e10042","stem_ja":"Databricksエージェントが、医薬品の臨床試験実施計画書を確認する法務チームを支援しています。文書には背景の説明文、条件を補足する脚注付きの入れ子状の用量表、相互参照される節の識別子、試験デザインの図を説明するキャプションが混在しています。\n\nエージェントは、用量の閾値を規定する節と、その閾値に条件を付ける脚注を特定する必要があります。利用可能な情報源は次のとおりです。\n\n- Unity Catalog Volumes内の元のPDFバイナリ。\n- 抽出したテキストチャンクを格納したDeltaテーブル。Databricks AI Searchで索引化され、文書識別子とページ番号が付与されています。\n- 表の要約、図のキャプション、節の階層を含むページ単位のDeltaテーブル。Unity Catalog関数から参照できます。\n\nこれらの質問を最も適切に支援するコンテキストの組み合わせはどれですか。","options_ja":["モデルが文書階層全体を調べられるよう、構造メタデータテーブル全体をAI Searchの結果とともにプロンプトへ含めます。","元のPDFと構造メタデータを使用し、文書識別子でファイルを見つけ、クエリ処理中に表の内容を抽出します。","AI Searchでテキストチャンクだけを取得し、必要な表と脚注の関係の復元を意味的類似度に任せます。","AI Searchで関連する本文を取得し、その文書・ページ参照を使って、該当する表、図、節に範囲を絞った構造メタデータを取得します。"],"explanation_ja":"本文検索は該当箇所を見つけ、構造メタデータは「どの節・表・脚注に属するか」を補います。文書IDとページ番号で両者を結び、必要な範囲だけ渡す方法が最適です。表の要約だけで正確な脚注の文言を置き換えない点も重要です。覚え方は「本文を探し、IDで構造を補う」です。","distractors_ja":["構造は得られますが、全件を渡すと無関係な情報が増えます。文書IDとページ番号があるため対象範囲に絞れます。","元PDFの再解析が必要な場合はありますが、既存の索引と構造情報があるのに毎回抽出し直す方法は非効率です。","意味的な近さだけでは、入れ子の表と脚注、上位節の適用関係までは確実に復元できません。","関連本文と対応する構造情報を必要な範囲だけ結合でき、用量と適用条件の取り違えを防げます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-002","number":2,"form":4,"stem":"A pharmaceutical RAG application uses clinical-trial summary chunks stored in a Unity Catalog-governed Delta table with precomputed embeddings. Vector similarity search retrieves conceptually relevant passages but misses some passages containing specific compound names and dosage identifiers that the embeddings represent poorly.\n\nThe engineer needs to recover those keyword-sensitive results while retaining semantic retrieval, without provisioning a separate keyword-search index or implementing application-side result merging.\n\nWhich configuration best meets these requirements?","options":["Combine ANN search with a `LIKE` filter so the filtered vector ranking recovers passages containing the required compound and dosage terms.","Run full-text search, then use the Databricks reranker with ANN to produce a hybrid result ranking.","Use hybrid search to execute ANN and full-text retrieval in one request and return a fused ranking.","Run ANN search first, then retain only returned passages containing the specified compound and dosage terms."],"correct":2,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"固有識別子を補うハイブリッド検索","concept":"ce-f4-q002","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Use hybrid search to execute ANN and full-text retrieval in one request and return a fused ranking.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe existing semantic retrieval works, so replacing it would discard useful behavior. The missing capability is keyword-based candidate retrieval for identifiers that embeddings overlook.\n\nC adds that complementary retrieval path and lets Databricks perform the fusion. It satisfies both operational constraints: no separately managed keyword index and no downstream merge implementation.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Conceptually relevant passages are retrieved — Preservation requirement: Keep semantic retrieval.\n\n- Specific compound names and dosage identifiers are missed — Retrieval signal: Add keyword-sensitive retrieval.\n\n- Poor embedding representation — Diagnostic signal: Filtering or reordering the same candidates does not directly add the missing retrieval path.\n\n- No separate index or application-side merging — Architecture constraint: Use the integrated hybrid query mode.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nDatabricks hybrid search runs ANN and full-text searches in parallel and combines their results using Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF). Semantic matches and keyword matches can therefore contribute to one returned ranking. \n\nConfigure the search request with `query_type=\"HYBRID\"`. The index must contain the text needed for keyword retrieval alongside its embeddings.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: A supported text filter can restrict eligible records and change the ANN search space. However, it does not combine independent keyword and semantic rankings. It may also exclude useful semantic matches that lack the literal terms.\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: A reranker reorders retrieved candidates; it does not turn full-text results into a separate ANN retrieval branch. Native hybrid search performs the required candidate retrieval and fusion. The SDK documents reranking as reordering initial results.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Post-filtering can remove unwanted ANN results, but cannot recover passages absent from the ANN candidate set. It therefore leaves the demonstrated recall gap unresolved.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDistinguish finding additional candidates from filtering or reordering existing candidates.\n\nIf the necessary passages were already retrieved but ranked too low, reranking could be appropriate. Here, the missing passages need an additional retrieval signal.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nSemantic retrieval captures conceptual similarity. Keyword retrieval captures lexical evidence. Hybrid retrieval combines their strengths when a workload needs both.\n\nA filter answers whether a record is eligible; a reranker reassesses candidates already retrieved.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nFor application-generated query embeddings, send the query text and a compatible query vector with the hybrid request. The text supports keyword matching, while the vector supports semantic matching.\n\nEvaluate identifier-heavy questions alongside paraphrased questions to confirm that the change improves the former while retaining performance on the latter.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nMissing keyword candidates calls for hybrid retrieval; misplaced existing candidates calls for reranking.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen semantic search works but misses precise names or identifiers, choose integrated hybrid search. C adds full-text retrieval and returns a service-fused ranking.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Query an AI Search index](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search)\n\nSupports: ANN, full-text retrieval, hybrid search, and RRF fusion.\n\n- [Databricks — AI Search Python SDK reference](https://api-docs.databricks.com/python/ai-search/databricks.ai_search.html)\n\nSupports: Hybrid query configuration, query text/vector inputs, and reranker behavior.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: A supported text filter can restrict eligible records and change the ANN search space. However, it does not combine independent keyword and semantic rankings. It may also exclude useful semantic matches that lack the literal terms.","Option B — Incorrect: A reranker reorders retrieved candidates; it does not turn full-text results into a separate ANN retrieval branch. Native hybrid search performs the required candidate retrieval and fusion. The SDK documents reranking as reordering initial results.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Use hybrid search to execute ANN and full-text retrieval in one request and return a fused ranking.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe existing semantic retrieval works, so replacing it would discard useful behavior. The missing capability is keyword-based candidate retrieval for identifiers that embeddings overlook.\n\nC adds that complementary retrieval path and lets Databricks perform the fusion. It satisfies both operational constraints: no separately managed keyword index and no downstream merge implementation.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Conceptually relevant passages are retrieved — Preservation requirement: Keep semantic retrieval.\n\n- Specific compound names and dosage identifiers are missed — Retrieval signal: Add keyword-sensitive retrieval.\n\n- Poor embedding representation — Diagnostic signal: Filtering or reordering the same candidates does not directly add the missing retrieval path.\n\n- No separate index or application-side merging — Architecture constraint: Use the integrated hybrid query mode.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nDatabricks hybrid search runs ANN and full-text searches in parallel and combines their results using Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF). Semantic matches and keyword matches can therefore contribute to one returned ranking. \n\nConfigure the search request with `query_type=\"HYBRID\"`. The index must contain the text needed for keyword retrieval alongside its embeddings.","Option D — Incorrect: Post-filtering can remove unwanted ANN results, but cannot recover passages absent from the ANN candidate set. It therefore leaves the demonstrated recall gap unresolved."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search","https://api-docs.databricks.com/python/ai-search/databricks.ai_search.html"],"source_hash":"ac0cee8c012c8e5ed3fb9edc2197fd099e73525c88992dc23d14c0bd08c65bbe","stem_ja":"医薬品向けRAGアプリケーションが、事前計算した埋め込みを持ち、Unity Catalogで管理されるDeltaテーブルに臨床試験の要約チャンクを保存しています。ベクトル類似検索は概念的に関連する本文を取得しますが、埋め込みで十分表現できない特定の化合物名や用量識別子を含む本文を取り逃がしています。\n\nエンジニアは、意味検索を維持しながら、別のキーワード検索索引を用意したり、アプリケーション側で検索結果の統合を実装したりせずに、キーワードに依存するこれらの結果も取得する必要があります。\n\nこの要件を最もよく満たす設定はどれですか。","options_ja":["ANN検索と`LIKE`フィルタを組み合わせ、フィルタ後のベクトル順位で必要な化合物名と用量の語を含む本文を回収します。","全文検索を実行した後、DatabricksのリランカーをANNと組み合わせてハイブリッドな結果順位を生成します。","ハイブリッド検索を使い、1回のリクエストでANN検索と全文検索を実行して、融合された順位を返します。","まずANN検索を実行し、その返却本文のうち指定の化合物名と用量の語を含むものだけを残します。"],"explanation_ja":"必要なのは、ベクトル検索が見落とした語句一致の候補を追加することです。ハイブリッド検索ならANNと全文検索を実行し、サービス側で順位を融合できます。フィルタやリランキングは候補の絞り込み・並べ替えであり、欠けたキーワード検索経路の代わりにはなりません。","distractors_ja":["フィルタは対象を限定しますが、語句検索と意味検索の独立した候補や順位を融合する仕組みではありません。","リランカーは取得済み候補の順位を評価し直す機能で、全文検索とANNの候補取得を自動的に構成するものではありません。","意味による一致と正確な名称による一致を両立し、別索引の管理やアプリ側の結果統合も不要にできます。","ANNが返さなかった本文は、返却後のフィルタでは追加できません。今回の取り逃がしが残ります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-003","number":3,"form":4,"stem":"A finance team uses a Genie Agent, formerly called a Genie Space, to ask recurring questions about regional open pipeline. Requests vary only by fiscal period—for example, FY26 Q1, FY26 Q2, or the last quarter.\n\nThe approved SQL uses the same `JOIN`, `GROUP BY`, and aggregation for every request; only the fiscal-period filter changes. The team requires these answers to receive the chat UI’s trusted-answer designation.\n\nWhich asset should the engineer configure to reuse the vetted query with the requested period?","options":["A parameterized example SQL query with a fiscal-period parameter typed as Date or String, allowing the same query structure to accept different period values.","A Unity Catalog SQL function defining the `open_pipeline` measure, with Genie generating the surrounding regional grouping and fiscal-period filtering for each request.","General text instructions describing the required `SELECT`, `JOIN`, `WHERE`, and `GROUP BY` pattern for Genie to regenerate each time.","Separate static example SQL queries for FY26 Q1, FY26 Q2, and the last quarter, allowing Genie to match requests to individual examples."],"correct":0,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"可変条件付きの承認済みSQL","concept":"ce-f4-q003","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. A parameterized example SQL query with a fiscal-period parameter typed as Date or String, allowing the same query structure to accept different period values.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirement is to reuse one complete, approved query pattern while changing a value. Parameterization directly represents that distinction: retain the SQL structure and supply the fiscal period at runtime.\n\nA also supports the required trusted-answer behavior. B preserves only a measure definition, C requires SQL regeneration, and D duplicates the pattern across examples.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Identical joins, grouping, and aggregation — Reuse signal: One query template already covers the recurring workload.\n\n- Only the fiscal-period filter changes — Parameterization signal: The variable belongs in a query parameter.\n\n- User-supplied period — Input requirement: Values must be supplied at runtime.\n\n- Trusted-answer designation — Hard requirement: Use a supported trusted asset rather than text guidance alone.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nGenie supports typed parameters in example SQL queries. These allow it to reuse the registered query structure with values extracted from user requests.\n\nDatabricks states that, in chat mode, using the exact text of a parameterized example query produces a verified answer. Correct parameter substitution preserves that vetted structure. \n\nChoose Date or String according to how the fiscal period is represented in the underlying query—not simply according to the wording of the question.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: SQL functions can also be trusted assets. However, this option encapsulates only `open_pipeline`; Genie still constructs the surrounding query. It does not preserve the complete approved pattern as directly as A.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: Text instructions guide generation but do not register the vetted query for direct reuse. They cannot, by themselves, satisfy the trusted-asset requirement.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Static examples can cover known questions, but duplicate the same logic and require further examples for additional periods. A provides the requested reusable pattern with a runtime value.\n\nExam Trap\n\nA query inspired by an example is different from direct reuse of that registered query. Registration alone does not guarantee that every related question receives a verified answer.\n\nCurrent Databricks documentation calls the qualifying responses “verified answers” and the supporting queries and functions “trusted assets.”\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nParameterization separates stable query logic from variable inputs. Use it when question variations change values while leaving the approved computation intact.\n\nA reusable measure is a component; a parameterized query can preserve the complete retrieval and aggregation pattern.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nReplace the fixed period value with a parameter such as `:fiscal_period`, configure its type, and add usage guidance for recurring regional-pipeline questions. Resolve “last quarter” using the team’s fiscal calendar.\n\nTest several periods and inspect whether Genie reuses the registered query and receives the expected verified-answer designation.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nSame vetted SQL, different filter value: parameterize the example query.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nAn unchanged query pattern with variable user inputs points to a parameterized example SQL query. Choose A when the complete vetted pattern must be reused and support trusted-answer behavior.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Tune Genie Agent quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality)\n\nSupports: Typed parameters, trusted assets, exact query reuse, verified answers, and current Genie terminology.\n\n- [Databricks — Curate an effective Genie Agent](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices)\n\nSupports: Choosing structured SQL examples and definitions over text-only generation guidance.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. A parameterized example SQL query with a fiscal-period parameter typed as Date or String, allowing the same query structure to accept different period values.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirement is to reuse one complete, approved query pattern while changing a value. Parameterization directly represents that distinction: retain the SQL structure and supply the fiscal period at runtime.\n\nA also supports the required trusted-answer behavior. B preserves only a measure definition, C requires SQL regeneration, and D duplicates the pattern across examples.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Identical joins, grouping, and aggregation — Reuse signal: One query template already covers the recurring workload.\n\n- Only the fiscal-period filter changes — Parameterization signal: The variable belongs in a query parameter.\n\n- User-supplied period — Input requirement: Values must be supplied at runtime.\n\n- Trusted-answer designation — Hard requirement: Use a supported trusted asset rather than text guidance alone.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nGenie supports typed parameters in example SQL queries. These allow it to reuse the registered query structure with values extracted from user requests.\n\nDatabricks states that, in chat mode, using the exact text of a parameterized example query produces a verified answer. Correct parameter substitution preserves that vetted structure. \n\nChoose Date or String according to how the fiscal period is represented in the underlying query—not simply according to the wording of the question.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: SQL functions can also be trusted assets. However, this option encapsulates only `open_pipeline`; Genie still constructs the surrounding query. It does not preserve the complete approved pattern as directly as A.","Option C — Incorrect: Text instructions guide generation but do not register the vetted query for direct reuse. They cannot, by themselves, satisfy the trusted-asset requirement.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Static examples can cover known questions, but duplicate the same logic and require further examples for additional periods. A provides the requested reusable pattern with a runtime value."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices"],"source_hash":"b9cb551b3f6487506e1dc23448030e0f2643d4e223b92ee7517b3504e7d51ec7","stem_ja":"財務チームが、以前はGenie Spaceと呼ばれていたGenie Agentを使って、地域別の未成約案件パイプラインについて繰り返し質問しています。リクエストの違いは会計期間だけで、例えばFY26 Q1、FY26 Q2、前四半期です。\n\n承認済みSQLは、すべてのリクエストで同じ`JOIN`、`GROUP BY`、集計を使い、会計期間のフィルタだけが変わります。チームは、これらの回答にチャットUIの信頼済み回答の表示が付くことを要求しています。\n\n指定された期間で検証済みクエリを再利用するため、どのアセットを設定すべきですか。","options_ja":["会計期間パラメータをDate型またはString型にした、パラメータ付きのサンプルSQLクエリ。同じクエリ構造に異なる期間値を渡せるようにします。","`open_pipeline`指標を定義するUnity Catalog SQL関数。地域別グループ化と会計期間フィルタを含む周辺SQLは、リクエストごとにGenieが生成します。","必要な`SELECT`、`JOIN`、`WHERE`、`GROUP BY`のパターンを説明し、Genieが毎回再生成するための一般的な文章指示。","FY26 Q1、FY26 Q2、前四半期それぞれについて別個の静的サンプルSQLクエリを用意し、リクエストを各サンプルに対応付けます。"],"explanation_ja":"SQLの構造は固定で、変わるのは期間の値だけです。パラメータ付きサンプルクエリなら、承認済みの結合・集計を維持したまま入力値を差し替えられます。信頼済みアセットを登録するだけでなく、実際にそのクエリが再利用されたか確認します。覚え方は「構造が同じ、値だけ違うならパラメータ」です。","distractors_ja":["完全な承認済みクエリを再利用しつつ、期間だけを実行時に差し替えられるため、要件に最も直接的です。","指標の定義は共通化できますが、周辺の結合・グループ化・期間条件は再生成され、承認済みSQL全体を固定できません。","文章は生成を誘導しますが、検証済みクエリそのものを信頼済みアセットとして再利用する仕組みではありません。","既知の期間には対応できますが、同じロジックが重複し、新しい期間のたびにサンプルを追加する必要があります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-004","number":4,"form":4,"stem":"A hospital operations team uses a Genie Agent, formerly called a Genie Space, to analyze emergency department waiting times, discharge rates, and nurse-to-patient ratios. The analyst has configured the relevant tables, general domain instructions, and sample questions.\n\nStaffing-ratio metrics must follow the compliance team’s approved calculation. For discharge and waiting-time questions not covered by a trusted asset, Genie must remain able to generate ad hoc SQL.\n\nWhich configuration best satisfies both requirements?","options":["Describe the approved staffing formula in general text instructions and let Genie implement it when generating SQL.","Register trusted example queries for all three metric categories so every operational answer receives a trusted-answer label.","Provide a sample staffing question and its expected answer so Genie can imitate the calculation and response format.","Register the approved staffing-ratio SQL query as a trusted asset, while leaving discharge and waiting-time questions available for ad hoc SQL generation."],"correct":3,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"信頼済みSQLとアドホック生成の両立","concept":"ce-f4-q004","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Register the approved staffing-ratio SQL query as a trusted asset, while leaving discharge and waiting-time questions available for ad hoc SQL generation.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirements distinguish a calculation that must follow approved logic from questions that need exploratory flexibility.\n\nD supplies executable, vetted SQL for the staffing calculation without requiring every possible operational question to have a registered query. Text instructions or expected answers alone do not provide that executable asset.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Compliance-approved staffing calculation — Hard requirement: Preserve the official computation in vetted SQL.\n\n- Free-form discharge and waiting-time questions — Flexibility requirement: Retain SQL generation for uncovered requests.\n\n- General instructions and sample questions already exist — Configuration signal: The missing element is the approved executable query.\n\n- “Both requirements” — Selection criterion: Neither text-only guidance nor a promise of universal trusted coverage is sufficient.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nTrusted assets provide approved SQL that Genie can use for anticipated questions. Databricks supports example SQL queries and Unity Catalog SQL functions as trusted assets, and describes their qualifying responses as verified answers. \n\nThe approved staffing query supplies the governed calculation. Other questions can still use generated SQL when no trusted asset applies.\n\nAdd clear usage guidance and validate that staffing requests actually reuse the approved calculation. Registering an asset alone does not prove that every related question follows it.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Text can explain the formula, but Genie must translate that description into executable SQL. This provides weaker control over the calculation than a vetted query.\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: Registering queries across all categories does not guarantee that every free-form response will use a trusted asset or receive a verified designation. Additional trusted queries can be useful, but universal coverage is not established.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: A sample question and expected answer illustrate desired behavior. They do not register the executable staffing calculation as a trusted asset.\n\nDatabricks recommends structured SQL definitions and examples over text instructions when those mechanisms can express the requirement. \n\nExam Trap\n\nTrusted assets do not require every question to be pre-registered. They can support selected calculations within a broader exploratory experience.\n\nAlso distinguish an expected answer from executable SQL: demonstrating a correct result does not establish how future results will be calculated.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nMatch the control to the requirement. Approved computations benefit from reusable executable logic; open-ended analysis benefits from contextual guidance and SQL generation.\n\nA verified-answer designation applies to qualifying asset use, not automatically to every answer produced by the agent.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nValidate the staffing query against compliance-approved examples, register it, and test varied staffing questions to confirm correct selection and calculation reuse. Separately test an uncovered waiting-time question to confirm that ad hoc analysis remains available.\n\nThis checks both controlled computation and exploratory behavior.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nProvide vetted SQL for mandatory calculations while preserving exploration for uncovered questions.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen one metric requires approved logic and other topics require ad hoc analysis, curate the trusted asset selectively. Choose D; instructions and sample answers alone do not provide the executable calculation.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Tune Genie Agent quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality)\n\nSupports: Trusted assets, SQL examples, usage guidance, verified answers, and current Genie terminology.\n\n- [Databricks — Curate an effective Genie Agent](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices)\n\nSupports: Selecting structured SQL guidance rather than relying solely on text instructions.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Text can explain the formula, but Genie must translate that description into executable SQL. This provides weaker control over the calculation than a vetted query.","Option B — Incorrect: Registering queries across all categories does not guarantee that every free-form response will use a trusted asset or receive a verified designation. Additional trusted queries can be useful, but universal coverage is not established.","Option C — Incorrect: A sample question and expected answer illustrate desired behavior. They do not register the executable staffing calculation as a trusted asset.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Register the approved staffing-ratio SQL query as a trusted asset, while leaving discharge and waiting-time questions available for ad hoc SQL generation.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirements distinguish a calculation that must follow approved logic from questions that need exploratory flexibility.\n\nD supplies executable, vetted SQL for the staffing calculation without requiring every possible operational question to have a registered query. Text instructions or expected answers alone do not provide that executable asset.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Compliance-approved staffing calculation — Hard requirement: Preserve the official computation in vetted SQL.\n\n- Free-form discharge and waiting-time questions — Flexibility requirement: Retain SQL generation for uncovered requests.\n\n- General instructions and sample questions already exist — Configuration signal: The missing element is the approved executable query.\n\n- “Both requirements” — Selection criterion: Neither text-only guidance nor a promise of universal trusted coverage is sufficient.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nTrusted assets provide approved SQL that Genie can use for anticipated questions. Databricks supports example SQL queries and Unity Catalog SQL functions as trusted assets, and describes their qualifying responses as verified answers. \n\nThe approved staffing query supplies the governed calculation. Other questions can still use generated SQL when no trusted asset applies.\n\nAdd clear usage guidance and validate that staffing requests actually reuse the approved calculation. Registering an asset alone does not prove that every related question follows it."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices"],"source_hash":"d6aaa397519d3c62e372b7414703bad148f1373e1b65790ba404148474a936fb","stem_ja":"病院運営チームが、以前はGenie Spaceと呼ばれていたGenie Agentを使い、救急部門の待ち時間、退院率、看護師対患者比率を分析しています。アナリストは関連テーブル、一般的な業務領域の指示、サンプル質問を設定済みです。\n\n人員配置比率の指標は、コンプライアンスチームが承認した計算に従う必要があります。信頼済みアセットが対応していない退院や待ち時間の質問については、Genieが引き続きアドホックSQLを生成できなければなりません。\n\n両方の要件を最もよく満たす設定はどれですか。","options_ja":["承認済みの人員配置計算式を一般的な文章指示に記載し、SQL生成時の実装をGenieに任せます。","3種類の指標すべてに信頼済みのサンプルクエリを登録し、あらゆる運営上の回答に信頼済み回答のラベルが付くようにします。","人員配置についてのサンプル質問と期待する回答を与え、計算と回答形式をGenieに模倣させます。","承認済みの人員配置比率SQLクエリを信頼済みアセットとして登録し、退院と待ち時間の質問ではアドホックSQL生成を利用可能なままにします。"],"explanation_ja":"厳密に固定したいのは人員配置比率の計算です。そのSQLを信頼済みアセットにし、それ以外では通常のSQL生成を残せば、統制と柔軟性を両立できます。「一部の計算を固定する」ことと「すべての回答を検証済みにする」ことを混同しないのがポイントです。","distractors_ja":["文章だけでは、承認済みの計算式が毎回そのまま実行されることを直接確保できません。","全指標の固定は求められていません。また、サンプル登録だけであらゆる回答に信頼済みラベルが付くわけではありません。","質問と期待回答は参考例にすぎず、承認済みの計算ロジックをSQLアセットとして固定するものではありません。","統制が必要な計算だけを登録し、未対応の質問のアドホック生成を保つことで、二つの条件を満たします。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-005","number":5,"form":4,"stem":"A Databricks field-service agent retrieves equipment troubleshooting instructions through a `DELTA_SYNC` AI Search index. Guides for models CX-400, CX-410, and CX-420 use similar language but contain different replacement parts, torque specifications, and calibration steps.\n\nThe pipeline uses `databricks-gte-large-en` embeddings with `chunk_size=512` tokens and `chunk_overlap=50` tokens. Evaluation shows that requests for a particular model—for example, replacing the belt tensioner on a CX-400—often retrieve another model’s procedure. The passages match the general task but contain specifications for the wrong equipment.\n\nWhich configuration change most directly addresses this problem?","options":["Set `query_type=\"HYBRID\"` to combine semantic and keyword retrieval, merging their rankings through Reciprocal Rank Fusion.","Replace the embedding model with `databricks-bge-large-en` and apply its recommended query instruction to improve query-to-passage alignment.","Reduce `chunk_size` from 512 to 128 tokens so model-specific identifiers occupy a larger proportion of each embedded passage.","Configure the built-in reranker with `columns_to_rerank` to place the most model-specific passages higher in the initial results."],"correct":0,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"機種名の正確な一致と意味検索","concept":"ce-f4-q005","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Set `query_type=\"HYBRID\"` to combine semantic and keyword retrieval, merging their rankings through Reciprocal Rank Fusion.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nSemantic matching recognizes the shared maintenance procedure but does not reliably distinguish closely related model identifiers. The immediate need is an additional lexical signal for those identifiers while retaining useful semantic matching.\n\nA supplies that signal directly. Changing embeddings or chunk sizes could affect results, and reranking could improve ordering, but hybrid retrieval explicitly addresses the demonstrated keyword-sensitive gap.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Closely related models with similar procedures — Similarity signal: Semantic relevance can favor the wrong model.\n\n- CX-400 versus CX-410 and CX-420 — Identifier signal: Small textual differences determine applicability.\n\n- Relevant task, incorrect specifications — Failure signal: General topical relevance is insufficient.\n\n- No demonstrated truncation or chunk-boundary failure — Exclusion signal: The evidence does not specifically support smaller chunks.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nHybrid search runs ANN and full-text retrieval in parallel, then combines their rankings using Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF). The keyword component provides evidence for the requested model code, while ANN retains conceptual matching for the procedure.\n\nDatabricks identifies specific identifiers and technical terms as suitable for keyword retrieval and documents hybrid search as combining that capability with semantic search. \n\nThe model identifiers must be present in the indexed text or searchable metadata.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Another embedding model might improve performance, but the change does not directly introduce keyword retrieval. Databricks notes that BGE’s recommended query instruction has domain-dependent effects.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Smaller passages can improve localization, but may separate model identifiers from procedural details. The question supplies no evidence that 512-token chunks are the cause.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Reranking can help when the correct procedure is already among the candidates. It primarily changes candidate ordering; hybrid search directly adds the missing lexical retrieval signal.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDo not confuse topical similarity with equipment applicability. Procedures can sound nearly identical while specifying different parts and settings.\n\nHybrid search improves ranking but does not enforce an exact-model restriction. Where model metadata is available and applicability must be strict, an appropriate metadata filter can complement retrieval.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nRetrieval needs signals that represent the distinctions important to the task. Semantic similarity captures shared meaning; lexical matching helps distinguish names, codes, and identifiers.\n\nChoose the intervention that addresses the observed distinction before changing unrelated pipeline components.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nEnable hybrid queries and evaluate matched questions for CX-400, CX-410, and CX-420. Check whether the retrieved passages belong to the requested model, rather than judging success only by whether they discuss the correct maintenance task.\n\nRetain model identifiers with each passage so both retrieval and validation can use them.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nSimilar procedures plus distinct model codes call for semantic and keyword retrieval together.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen vector search retrieves the right topic for the wrong product identifier, choose hybrid search. A adds lexical discrimination while preserving semantic retrieval.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Query an AI Search index](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search)\n\nSupports: Hybrid retrieval, RRF fusion, identifier-sensitive search, filtering, and reranking.\n\n- [Databricks — Supported Foundation Model APIs models](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/foundation-model-apis/supported-models)\n\nSupports: The named embedding endpoints and BGE query-instruction guidance.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Set `query_type=\"HYBRID\"` to combine semantic and keyword retrieval, merging their rankings through Reciprocal Rank Fusion.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nSemantic matching recognizes the shared maintenance procedure but does not reliably distinguish closely related model identifiers. The immediate need is an additional lexical signal for those identifiers while retaining useful semantic matching.\n\nA supplies that signal directly. Changing embeddings or chunk sizes could affect results, and reranking could improve ordering, but hybrid retrieval explicitly addresses the demonstrated keyword-sensitive gap.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Closely related models with similar procedures — Similarity signal: Semantic relevance can favor the wrong model.\n\n- CX-400 versus CX-410 and CX-420 — Identifier signal: Small textual differences determine applicability.\n\n- Relevant task, incorrect specifications — Failure signal: General topical relevance is insufficient.\n\n- No demonstrated truncation or chunk-boundary failure — Exclusion signal: The evidence does not specifically support smaller chunks.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nHybrid search runs ANN and full-text retrieval in parallel, then combines their rankings using Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF). The keyword component provides evidence for the requested model code, while ANN retains conceptual matching for the procedure.\n\nDatabricks identifies specific identifiers and technical terms as suitable for keyword retrieval and documents hybrid search as combining that capability with semantic search. \n\nThe model identifiers must be present in the indexed text or searchable metadata.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Another embedding model might improve performance, but the change does not directly introduce keyword retrieval. Databricks notes that BGE’s recommended query instruction has domain-dependent effects.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Smaller passages can improve localization, but may separate model identifiers from procedural details. The question supplies no evidence that 512-token chunks are the cause.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Reranking can help when the correct procedure is already among the candidates. It primarily changes candidate ordering; hybrid search directly adds the missing lexical retrieval signal."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/foundation-model-apis/supported-models"],"source_hash":"6ac0958b89e5983de4b4de07feef4379c4272b6bf5e3265bd8e7a7b9bfdeed64","stem_ja":"Databricksのフィールドサービス用エージェントが、`DELTA_SYNC`型AI Search索引を使って機器のトラブルシューティング手順を取得しています。CX-400、CX-410、CX-420のガイドは似た文章ですが、交換部品、トルク仕様、校正手順は異なります。\n\nパイプラインは`databricks-gte-large-en`埋め込みを使い、`chunk_size=512`トークン、`chunk_overlap=50`トークンです。評価では、例えばCX-400のベルトテンショナー交換など、特定機種への要求で別機種の手順がよく取得されます。本文は一般的な作業には合っていますが、機器の仕様が違っています。\n\nこの問題に最も直接対処する設定変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["`query_type=\"HYBRID\"`を設定し、意味検索とキーワード検索を組み合わせ、Reciprocal Rank Fusionで順位を統合します。","埋め込みモデルを`databricks-bge-large-en`に置き換え、推奨のクエリ指示を適用して、クエリと本文の対応を改善します。","`chunk_size`を512から128トークンへ縮小し、各埋め込み本文に占める機種識別子の割合を高めます。","組み込みリランカーの`columns_to_rerank`を設定し、初期結果の中で最も機種に特化した本文を上位へ移します。"],"explanation_ja":"作業内容の意味は合っているのに機種名が違うため、機種識別子の語句一致を検索に加えるのが直接的です。ハイブリッド検索は意味とキーワードの両方を使えます。候補に正しい機種が入っていて順位だけが悪い場合はリランキングですが、今回は識別子を拾う検索信号の不足が中心です。","distractors_ja":["類似した作業説明の意味検索を残しつつ、CX-400などの正確な識別子に基づく検索信号を加えられます。","モデル変更が有効な可能性はありますが、正確な機種識別子の一致を追加する方法ほど問題に直接対応しません。","細分化だけでは識別子の一致は保証されず、仕様や手順を理解するための関連文脈を分断するおそれもあります。","取得済み候補の順位改善には有効ですが、正しい機種の候補を回収するためのキーワード検索を追加するものではありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-006","number":6,"form":4,"stem":"A Databricks financial research agent answers analyst questions using vector retrieval over earnings reports. Its context contains a 180-token system prompt, 250 tokens of conversation history, and 8 retrieved chunks of 550 tokens each.\n\nThe retrieved material consumes 4,400 tokens, leaving 3,362 tokens of the model’s 8,192-token window for the analyst’s question and response. Profiling identifies repeated regulatory boilerplate and duplicate table headers that seldom support the final answers.\n\nWhich two changes most directly reduce retrieved-context tokens while preserving useful financial evidence? Choose two.","options":["Return 3 reranked chunks instead of 8, selecting the most relevant evidence and reducing injected chunk tokens by more than 60%.","Replace plain cosine-based selection with MMR to increase result diversity, without changing chunk lengths or removing boilerplate.","Rebuild chunks at 150–200 tokens using semantic boundaries, removing repetitive regulatory boilerplate and duplicate headers before indexing.","Re-index with an embedding model offering finer-grained representations, while retaining the existing chunk boundaries and metadata.","Increase conversation history from 250 to 900 tokens to improve conversational continuity and compensate for retrieved-context pressure."],"correct":0,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"取得件数とチャンク設計によるトークン削減","concept":"ce-f4-q006","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Return 3 reranked chunks instead of 8, selecting the most relevant evidence and reducing injected chunk tokens by more than 60%.\n\nC. Rebuild chunks at 150–200 tokens using semantic boundaries, removing repetitive regulatory boilerplate and duplicate headers before indexing.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nRetrieved context is the dominant identified token cost. The two direct controls are how many chunks reach the model and how much unnecessary text each chunk contains.\n\nA reduces the final chunk count while using relevance ranking to guide selection. C reduces chunk size and removes the specific redundancy found during profiling. They work independently and can be combined.\n\nB and D may change retrieval quality without reducing the supplied token volume. E increases context consumption.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- 8 × 550 = 4,400 tokens — Budget evidence: Retrieved material dominates the listed inputs.\n\n- Repeated boilerplate and headers — Diagnostic evidence: Preprocessing can remove identifiable redundancy.\n\n- Preserve useful financial evidence — Hard constraint: Token reduction must retain meaning and answer coverage.\n\n- Choose two — Cardinality requirement: Select both fewer relevant chunks and cleaner, smaller chunks.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA reduces the final result count. At the original chunk size:\n\n- Baseline: `8 × 550 = 4,400 tokens`.\n\n- Three chunks: `3 × 550 = 1,650 tokens`.\n\n- Saving: `2,750 tokens`, or 62.5%.\n\nKeep an adequate candidate pool for reranking, then return the best three to the model. Databricks distinguishes the final `num_results` from the number of candidates used by its reranker.\n\nC reduces unnecessary text per chunk. Semantic boundaries help preserve coherent financial passages while repeated material is removed. Databricks recommends removing boilerplate while preserving document structure and semantic boundaries. .\n\nCombined, three 150–200-token chunks contain approximately 450–600 tokens, before additional formatting or metadata. These targets require evaluation: reranking does not guarantee that three chunks contain everything needed for every question.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: MMR can reduce informational redundancy by selecting diverse results. However, eight approximately 550-token chunks still consume approximately 4,400 tokens if count and length remain unchanged.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Better embeddings may improve relevance. Keeping the same chunk sizes and result count does not directly remove the profiled token waste.\n\n- Option E — Violates Requirement: Increasing history adds 650 tokens and leaves retrieved-context consumption unchanged. It reduces the remaining window rather than relieving pressure.\n\nExam Trap\n\nLess repetitive information does not necessarily mean fewer tokens. Check whether an option actually changes the text volume supplied to the model.\n\nAlso, “rarely cited” does not prove that content is dispensable. Preserve necessary column labels, currencies, units, reporting periods, and qualifying notes when removing duplicated headers or boilerplate.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nApproximate retrieved-context cost is:\n\n`number of injected chunks × average tokens per chunk`\n\nReduce either factor while checking evidence coverage. Retrieval quality improvements alone do not establish token savings.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nClean repeated material during ingestion, preserve meaningful table context, and re-index coherent chunks. At query time, rerank candidates before selecting the final passages.\n\nCompare answer correctness and evidence coverage on the same financial benchmark before accepting the smaller context configuration.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nFewer relevant chunks and less redundant text directly reduce retrieval tokens.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen profiling identifies oversized, repetitive retrieved context, choose A and C. Reduce the final result count and clean the chunks, then verify that the smaller context still supports the answers.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Query an AI Search index](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search)\n\nSupports: Reranking and the distinction between candidate processing and final result count.\n\n- [Databricks — AI Search retrieval quality guide](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality)\n\nSupports: Boilerplate removal, semantic boundaries, structure preservation, and evaluation.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Return 3 reranked chunks instead of 8, selecting the most relevant evidence and reducing injected chunk tokens by more than 60%.\n\nC. Rebuild chunks at 150–200 tokens using semantic boundaries, removing repetitive regulatory boilerplate and duplicate headers before indexing.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nRetrieved context is the dominant identified token cost. The two direct controls are how many chunks reach the model and how much unnecessary text each chunk contains.\n\nA reduces the final chunk count while using relevance ranking to guide selection. C reduces chunk size and removes the specific redundancy found during profiling. They work independently and can be combined.\n\nB and D may change retrieval quality without reducing the supplied token volume. E increases context consumption.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- 8 × 550 = 4,400 tokens — Budget evidence: Retrieved material dominates the listed inputs.\n\n- Repeated boilerplate and headers — Diagnostic evidence: Preprocessing can remove identifiable redundancy.\n\n- Preserve useful financial evidence — Hard constraint: Token reduction must retain meaning and answer coverage.\n\n- Choose two — Cardinality requirement: Select both fewer relevant chunks and cleaner, smaller chunks.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA reduces the final result count. At the original chunk size:\n\n- Baseline: `8 × 550 = 4,400 tokens`.\n\n- Three chunks: `3 × 550 = 1,650 tokens`.\n\n- Saving: `2,750 tokens`, or 62.5%.\n\nKeep an adequate candidate pool for reranking, then return the best three to the model. Databricks distinguishes the final `num_results` from the number of candidates used by its reranker.\n\nC reduces unnecessary text per chunk. Semantic boundaries help preserve coherent financial passages while repeated material is removed. Databricks recommends removing boilerplate while preserving document structure and semantic boundaries. .\n\nCombined, three 150–200-token chunks contain approximately 450–600 tokens, before additional formatting or metadata. These targets require evaluation: reranking does not guarantee that three chunks contain everything needed for every question.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: MMR can reduce informational redundancy by selecting diverse results. However, eight approximately 550-token chunks still consume approximately 4,400 tokens if count and length remain unchanged.","Correct Answer\n\nA. Return 3 reranked chunks instead of 8, selecting the most relevant evidence and reducing injected chunk tokens by more than 60%.\n\nC. Rebuild chunks at 150–200 tokens using semantic boundaries, removing repetitive regulatory boilerplate and duplicate headers before indexing.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nRetrieved context is the dominant identified token cost. The two direct controls are how many chunks reach the model and how much unnecessary text each chunk contains.\n\nA reduces the final chunk count while using relevance ranking to guide selection. C reduces chunk size and removes the specific redundancy found during profiling. They work independently and can be combined.\n\nB and D may change retrieval quality without reducing the supplied token volume. E increases context consumption.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- 8 × 550 = 4,400 tokens — Budget evidence: Retrieved material dominates the listed inputs.\n\n- Repeated boilerplate and headers — Diagnostic evidence: Preprocessing can remove identifiable redundancy.\n\n- Preserve useful financial evidence — Hard constraint: Token reduction must retain meaning and answer coverage.\n\n- Choose two — Cardinality requirement: Select both fewer relevant chunks and cleaner, smaller chunks.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA reduces the final result count. At the original chunk size:\n\n- Baseline: `8 × 550 = 4,400 tokens`.\n\n- Three chunks: `3 × 550 = 1,650 tokens`.\n\n- Saving: `2,750 tokens`, or 62.5%.\n\nKeep an adequate candidate pool for reranking, then return the best three to the model. Databricks distinguishes the final `num_results` from the number of candidates used by its reranker.\n\nC reduces unnecessary text per chunk. Semantic boundaries help preserve coherent financial passages while repeated material is removed. Databricks recommends removing boilerplate while preserving document structure and semantic boundaries. .\n\nCombined, three 150–200-token chunks contain approximately 450–600 tokens, before additional formatting or metadata. These targets require evaluation: reranking does not guarantee that three chunks contain everything needed for every question.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Better embeddings may improve relevance. Keeping the same chunk sizes and result count does not directly remove the profiled token waste.","Option E — Violates Requirement: Increasing history adds 650 tokens and leaves retrieved-context consumption unchanged. It reduces the remaining window rather than relieving pressure."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality"],"source_hash":"dbc541227449dfb6c714be6b94931776199a0e7be5a19b7a54f2e346f39f34cf","correct_many":[0,2],"stem_ja":"Databricksの財務調査エージェントが、決算報告書をベクトル検索してアナリストの質問に回答しています。コンテキストには180トークンのシステムプロンプト、250トークンの会話履歴、各550トークンの取得チャンク8件が含まれます。\n\n取得資料は4,400トークンを消費し、モデルの8,192トークンのウィンドウには、アナリストの質問と応答用に3,362トークンが残ります。プロファイリングでは、規制に関する定型文と重複した表ヘッダーが繰り返し現れ、最終回答を支えることはほとんどありません。\n\n有用な財務上の根拠を維持しながら、取得コンテキストのトークン数を最も直接削減する変更を2つ選んでください。","options_ja":["8件の代わりにリランキング済みの3件を返し、最も関連性の高い根拠を選んで、注入するチャンクのトークン数を60％超削減します。","チャンク長を変えたり定型文を削除したりせずに、通常のコサイン類似度による選択をMMRに置き換えて結果の多様性を高めます。","意味的な境界に沿って150～200トークンのチャンクへ再構成し、索引作成前に反復する規制定型文と重複ヘッダーを取り除きます。","既存のチャンク境界とメタデータを維持したまま、より細かな表現が可能な埋め込みモデルで再索引化します。","会話の連続性を改善し、取得コンテキストの圧迫を補うため、会話履歴を250から900トークンへ増やします。"],"explanation_ja":"コンテキストへ渡す量は「件数×1件の長さ」で減らせます。関連性の高い3件への選別と、不要な定型文を除いた短い意味単位への再構成が直接的です。8件から3件なら62.5％減です。多様化や埋め込み精度の改善だけでは、注入するテキスト量そのものは減りません。","distractors_ja":["高い関連性を持つ3件に絞れば、同じ長さでも8件から62.5％減らせます。必要な根拠が残るか評価します。","MMRは多様性を改善しますが、件数やチャンク長を変えなければ渡すトークン数を直接削減しません。","意味を保つ短い単位にし、不要な反復文を索引前に除くことで、有用な情報の密度を高められます。","埋め込みの表現力を上げても、同じ件数・長さのテキストを渡す限り、コンテキストの消費量は変わりません。","履歴を650トークン増やすため、取得コンテキストの圧迫を軽減するどころか、残り容量を減らします。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-007","number":7,"form":4,"stem":"A finance Q&A agent on Databricks calls a Genie Agent, formerly called a Genie Space, using tables in `finance.silver`. Genie relies on Unity Catalog table and column metadata to select a source.\n\nMLflow evaluations using the `Correctness` scorer reveal repeated source-selection errors: net-revenue questions use `revenue_gross`, while gross-revenue questions use `revenue`.\n\nBoth tables remain valid production sources and must stay available. The definitions distinguishing them must also be reusable by other Genie Agents and BI consumers accessing the same schema.\n\nWhich intervention best meets these requirements?","options":["Add Unity Catalog `COMMENT` descriptions to both tables and their relevant columns, explaining their business meaning, units, and calculation basis.","Mark `revenue` as certified and `revenue_gross` as deprecated so consumers favor `revenue` as the authoritative source.","Add instructions to this Genie Agent specifying which table answers net-revenue questions and which answers gross-revenue questions.","Apply custom tags identifying one table as the primary revenue source and the other as secondary, encouraging Genie to prefer the primary source."],"correct":0,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"共有する業務定義はUnity Catalogメタデータへ","concept":"ce-f4-q007","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Add Unity Catalog `COMMENT` descriptions to both tables and their relevant columns, explaining their business meaning, units, and calculation basis.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure concerns ambiguous business meaning in metadata that Genie already uses. The additional requirement is to make the distinction available beyond one agent.\n\nA places the definitions on the shared data assets. C could improve this agent’s behavior, but its instructions would not establish reusable catalog definitions. B and D substitute source preference for the required net-versus-gross distinction.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Genie reads Unity Catalog metadata — Intervention signal: Improve the information used for source selection.\n\n- Net and gross sources are reversed — Semantic failure: Explain what each table represents.\n\n- Both tables remain valid — Hard constraint: Neither should be dismissed merely to simplify selection.\n\n- Other Genie Agents and BI consumers need the definitions — Scope requirement: Store the clarification in shared catalog metadata.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nTable comments can identify each dataset’s purpose and calculation basis. Column comments can clarify the associated amounts, units, and adjustments.\n\nDatabricks supports comments on tables and columns through `COMMENT ON`. \n\nGenie’s default table descriptions draw on Unity Catalog metadata, while agent-level knowledge-store changes remain local to that agent. Catalog comments therefore match the required reuse scope. \n\nThese descriptions guide selection; they do not enforce query routing mechanically. Validate their effect using representative questions.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: Certification and deprecation communicate trust or lifecycle status. They do not define net versus gross revenue, and the scenario provides no basis for deprecating the valid gross-revenue source. Deprecation also does not itself revoke access.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Local instructions could resolve selection within this agent. However, other agents and BI consumers would not inherit those instructions, leaving the shared-definition requirement unmet.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Both tables answer legitimate but different questions. Primary/secondary labels obscure that distinction and do not themselves define an intent-to-table mapping.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe question is about which metric a source represents, not which source deserves greater trust.\n\nIf an interpretation applied only within one Genie Agent, local instructions could be appropriate. Here, multiple consumers require the same definitions.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nPlace semantic definitions at the scope where they must be reused. Shared asset meaning belongs in catalog metadata; application-specific interpretation can live in local instructions.\n\nTrust labels and business definitions serve different purposes.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nHave the finance owner confirm the exact net/gross definitions, included adjustments, units, and reporting basis. Record those distinctions on both tables and relevant columns, then rerun paired net- and gross-revenue questions.\n\nCheck generated SQL as well as answer scores to confirm that source selection improved.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nShared data meaning belongs on the shared catalog asset.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen ambiguous table meaning affects several consumers, improve Unity Catalog comments. Choose A; local instructions have narrower reach, and preference tags do not explain metric semantics.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — COMMENT ON](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/sql-ref-syntax-ddl-comment)\n\nSupports: Adding descriptions to shared tables and columns.\n\n- [Databricks — Tune Genie Agent quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality)\n\nSupports: Genie’s use of catalog descriptions and the local scope of agent-level metadata.\n\n- [Databricks — Flag data as certified or deprecated](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/certify-deprecate-data)\n\nSupports: Certification and deprecation as trust and lifecycle signals.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Add Unity Catalog `COMMENT` descriptions to both tables and their relevant columns, explaining their business meaning, units, and calculation basis.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure concerns ambiguous business meaning in metadata that Genie already uses. The additional requirement is to make the distinction available beyond one agent.\n\nA places the definitions on the shared data assets. C could improve this agent’s behavior, but its instructions would not establish reusable catalog definitions. B and D substitute source preference for the required net-versus-gross distinction.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Genie reads Unity Catalog metadata — Intervention signal: Improve the information used for source selection.\n\n- Net and gross sources are reversed — Semantic failure: Explain what each table represents.\n\n- Both tables remain valid — Hard constraint: Neither should be dismissed merely to simplify selection.\n\n- Other Genie Agents and BI consumers need the definitions — Scope requirement: Store the clarification in shared catalog metadata.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nTable comments can identify each dataset’s purpose and calculation basis. Column comments can clarify the associated amounts, units, and adjustments.\n\nDatabricks supports comments on tables and columns through `COMMENT ON`. \n\nGenie’s default table descriptions draw on Unity Catalog metadata, while agent-level knowledge-store changes remain local to that agent. Catalog comments therefore match the required reuse scope. \n\nThese descriptions guide selection; they do not enforce query routing mechanically. Validate their effect using representative questions.","Option B — Incorrect: Certification and deprecation communicate trust or lifecycle status. They do not define net versus gross revenue, and the scenario provides no basis for deprecating the valid gross-revenue source. Deprecation also does not itself revoke access.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Local instructions could resolve selection within this agent. However, other agents and BI consumers would not inherit those instructions, leaving the shared-definition requirement unmet.","Option D — Incorrect: Both tables answer legitimate but different questions. Primary/secondary labels obscure that distinction and do not themselves define an intent-to-table mapping."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/sql/language-manual/sql-ref-syntax-ddl-comment","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/certify-deprecate-data"],"source_hash":"39e24db1104aba493f2be787285545202e7da0af23e6d1c14b1e03844fd565d7","stem_ja":"Databricks上の財務Q&Aエージェントが、`finance.silver`のテーブルを使うGenie Agent（以前のGenie Space）を呼び出しています。GenieはUnity Catalogのテーブル・列メタデータを頼りに情報源を選びます。\n\nMLflowの`Correctness`スコアラーによる評価では、情報源の選択ミスが繰り返し発生しています。純売上高の質問で`revenue_gross`を使い、総売上高の質問で`revenue`を使っています。\n\n両テーブルは有効な本番情報源であり、引き続き利用可能でなければなりません。また、二つを区別する定義は、同じスキーマを利用するほかのGenie AgentやBI利用者でも再利用できる必要があります。\n\nこれらの要件を最もよく満たす対策はどれですか。","options_ja":["両テーブルと関連列にUnity Catalogの`COMMENT`を追加し、業務上の意味、単位、計算の基準を説明します。","`revenue`を認定済み、`revenue_gross`を非推奨とし、利用者が`revenue`を正当な情報源として優先するようにします。","このGenie Agentに、純売上高にはどのテーブルを、総売上高にはどのテーブルを使うかの指示を追加します。","一方のテーブルを主要な売上情報源、もう一方を補助情報源とするカスタムタグを付け、主要情報源をGenieに優先させます。"],"explanation_ja":"両方が正しい情報源なので、必要なのは優劣付けではなく意味の明確化です。Unity Catalogのテーブル・列コメントなら、業務定義、単位、計算基準を複数のGenieやBIで共有できます。単一エージェントの指示だけを直すと、ほかの利用者へ定義が伝わりません。","distractors_ja":["業務上の意味を共有メタデータへ記録するため、両情報源を残したまま複数の利用者による再利用を支援できます。","どちらも有効な本番情報源なので、片方を非推奨にするのは事実と要件に反し、総売上高の利用を妨げます。","このエージェントの改善にはなり得ますが、ほかのGenieやBIで再利用するという条件を満たしません。","問題は主要・補助の順位ではなく、純売上高と総売上高の定義です。タグの優先順位ではこの意味の差を説明できません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-008","number":8,"form":4,"stem":"A Databricks agent supports an internal IT help desk. Its retrieval pipeline searches a continuously maintained knowledge base and supplies the relevant passages on every request.\n\nThe system prompt contains a 400-token persona introduction, a 600-token supported-software list, and a 200-token closing instruction section. None establishes whether retrieved knowledge or pretrained knowledge takes precedence.\n\nThe agent understands requests correctly and receives the appropriate KB evidence. Nevertheless, it answers version-specific questions from pretrained knowledge and recommends releases identified as deprecated in the KB.\n\nThe team permits a targeted prompt edit, but no complete prompt rewrite or retrieval-infrastructure changes. Which revision best addresses the problem?","options":["Expand the persona introduction with the model’s training-data cutoff date to explain possible limitations in version knowledge.","Add a rule to the closing section stating that retrieved KB passages take precedence whenever they conflict with pretrained knowledge.","Place the 600-token software list after the closing instructions so it appears last in the prompt.","Require the closing section to specify a consistent version-number format, such as `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`."],"correct":1,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"取得した根拠と事前学習知識の優先順位","concept":"ce-f4-q008","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Add a rule to the closing section stating that retrieved KB passages take precedence whenever they conflict with pretrained knowledge.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nIntent recognition and retrieval already work. The missing instruction concerns how the agent should resolve conflicting information.\n\nB supplies that decision rule through a small permitted edit. It directly addresses the use of outdated knowledge without replacing the prompt or changing retrieval.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Continuously maintained KB — Authority signal: Current software guidance is available.\n\n- Correct passages already present — Exclusion signal: Missing retrieval evidence is not the demonstrated failure.\n\n- Answers follow pretrained knowledge — Diagnostic signal: The agent lacks a clear source-precedence rule.\n\n- Targeted edit only — Hard constraint: Add the missing rule within the existing prompt.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAn explicit rule tells the agent which evidence governs version and support-status claims. For example:\n\nFor software versions and support status, prioritize the retrieved internal KB whenever it conflicts with your pretrained knowledge.\n\nThis converts an implicit expectation into an actionable instruction. Anthropic documents explicitly restricting answers to supplied documents as a grounding technique. \n\nThe rule fits this scenario because the KB is described as current and the retrieved passages are relevant.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Different Use Case: A cutoff disclosure communicates limitations. It does not tell the agent to follow the available current evidence when answering.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: Moving the software list changes ordering but adds no authority rule. The list identifies supported titles; it does not resolve conflicting version information.\n\n- Option D — Different Use Case: Version formatting affects presentation. A consistently formatted deprecated release remains an incorrect recommendation.\n\nExam Trap\n\nHaving the correct evidence in context does not guarantee that the model will use it appropriately. The prompt must explain its role when sources disagree.\n\nIf the relevant passages were absent or stale, a precedence sentence alone would not resolve the underlying retrieval problem.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nGrounding requires both access to evidence and instructions for using it. Retrieval supplies information; the prompt establishes how that information should influence the answer.\n\nDatabricks describes prompt augmentation as combining retrieved information with instructions that guide grounded generation. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nAdd the precedence sentence, then evaluate version questions where the approved KB contradicts older product knowledge. Check that recommendations follow the KB’s current support status.\n\nThis tests the specific behavior the revision is intended to change while keeping the existing retrieval pipeline.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nWhen current evidence is present but ignored, define which source takes precedence.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nCorrect intent plus correct retrieved passages points toward an evidence-use instruction. Choose B: explicitly prioritize the current KB over conflicting pretrained knowledge.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Anthropic — Reduce hallucinations](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations)\n\nSupports: Explicitly grounding responses in supplied documents rather than general knowledge.\n\n- [Databricks — Improve RAG chain quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain)\n\nSupports: Using prompt instructions to guide generation from retrieved evidence.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Different Use Case: A cutoff disclosure communicates limitations. It does not tell the agent to follow the available current evidence when answering.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Add a rule to the closing section stating that retrieved KB passages take precedence whenever they conflict with pretrained knowledge.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nIntent recognition and retrieval already work. The missing instruction concerns how the agent should resolve conflicting information.\n\nB supplies that decision rule through a small permitted edit. It directly addresses the use of outdated knowledge without replacing the prompt or changing retrieval.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Continuously maintained KB — Authority signal: Current software guidance is available.\n\n- Correct passages already present — Exclusion signal: Missing retrieval evidence is not the demonstrated failure.\n\n- Answers follow pretrained knowledge — Diagnostic signal: The agent lacks a clear source-precedence rule.\n\n- Targeted edit only — Hard constraint: Add the missing rule within the existing prompt.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAn explicit rule tells the agent which evidence governs version and support-status claims. For example:\n\nFor software versions and support status, prioritize the retrieved internal KB whenever it conflicts with your pretrained knowledge.\n\nThis converts an implicit expectation into an actionable instruction. Anthropic documents explicitly restricting answers to supplied documents as a grounding technique. \n\nThe rule fits this scenario because the KB is described as current and the retrieved passages are relevant.","Option C — Incorrect: Moving the software list changes ordering but adds no authority rule. The list identifies supported titles; it does not resolve conflicting version information.","Option D — Different Use Case: Version formatting affects presentation. A consistently formatted deprecated release remains an incorrect recommendation."],"references":["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain"],"source_hash":"6d1cc77f61ba3e531b4647aff931650050bec9da7ab54adda150c217209ce8d4","stem_ja":"Databricksエージェントが社内ITヘルプデスクを支援しています。検索パイプラインは継続的に保守されるナレッジベースを検索し、すべてのリクエストで関連本文を提供します。\n\nシステムプロンプトは400トークンのペルソナ紹介、600トークンの対応ソフトウェア一覧、200トークンの末尾の指示で構成されています。取得した知識と事前学習知識のどちらを優先するかは、どこにも定めていません。\n\nエージェントは要求を正しく理解し、適切なKBの根拠も受け取っています。それでもバージョンに関する質問に事前学習知識で回答し、KBでは非推奨とされるリリースを勧めてしまいます。\n\nチームは限定的なプロンプト編集を認めていますが、全面的な書き直しや検索基盤の変更は認めていません。最も適切な修正はどれですか。","options_ja":["ペルソナ紹介にモデルの学習データのカットオフ日を追加し、バージョン知識の制約を説明します。","取得したKBの本文と事前学習知識が矛盾する場合は、必ずKBを優先するという規則を末尾に追加します。","600トークンのソフトウェア一覧がプロンプトの最後に来るよう、末尾の指示より後ろへ移動します。","末尾の指示で、`MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`など一貫したバージョン番号形式を指定するようにします。"],"explanation_ja":"検索や意図理解は成功しているので、不足しているのは根拠の優先順位です。「KBと事前学習知識が食い違えばKBを使う」という短い規則を追加すれば、全面改修せずに原因へ直接対処できます。形式や並び順ではなく、どの情報を正とするかを明示します。","distractors_ja":["知識が古い可能性を知らせても、KBとの矛盾時にどちらを使うかの規則は定まりません。","すでに正しい根拠が取得されているため、その根拠を優先する明確な規則が最小の変更で原因を直します。","一覧を後ろへ動かすだけでは、取得したKBと事前学習知識の優先順位は明確になりません。","表記の統一は読みやすさに役立ちますが、非推奨リリースを勧める知識選択の誤りは修正しません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-009","number":9,"form":4,"stem":"A Databricks inventory agent has a 4,096-token context window. System instructions and tool descriptions occupy 1,800 tokens, and 1,000 tokens are reserved for the user’s request and model response. Each few-shot example costs approximately 250 tokens, allowing five examples.\n\nThe agent uses `inventory_lookup`, `reorder_check`, and `supplier_lead_time`. Six examples are available:\n\n- Example 1: A warehouse manager asks about reordering a depleted SKU. The agent uses `inventory_lookup`, verifies depletion, calls `reorder_check`, and returns a binary reorder decision.\n\n- Example 2: A procurement officer asks when a low-stock item can be replenished. The agent calls `reorder_check` followed by `supplier_lead_time`, then recommends replenishment.\n\n- Example 3: A store associate requests one product’s stock count. The agent calls `inventory_lookup` and returns a scalar quantity.\n\n- Example 4: A store associate requests one product’s stock count. The agent calls `inventory_lookup` and returns a structured object containing quantity and a restocking flag.\n\n- Example 5: A store associate asks whether a forecasted shortage requires action. The agent calls `inventory_lookup`, checks minimum levels, calls `reorder_check`, and returns a binary decision.\n\n- Example 6: A logistics coordinator requests a supplier’s fulfillment time. The agent calls `supplier_lead_time` and returns a scalar lead-time value.\n\nWhich example should be removed to fit the budget while preserving the broadest behavioral coverage?","options":["Example 1, because Examples 2 and 5 already demonstrate its tools, making its decision path redundant.","Example 6, because Example 2 already calls `supplier_lead_time`, so the standalone lookup introduces no additional tool.","Example 3, because Example 4 demonstrates the same user role and tool path while providing a richer response structure.","Example 5, because its threshold comparison repeats Example 1’s decision pattern and its user role appears elsewhere."],"correct":2,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"few-shotの重複と行動カバレッジ","concept":"ce-f4-q009","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Example 3, because Example 4 demonstrates the same user role and tool path while providing a richer response structure.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe budget requires removing one example:\n\n- Available example budget: `4,096 − 1,800 − 1,000 = 1,296 tokens`.\n\n- Five examples: approximately `1,250 tokens`.\n\n- Six examples: approximately `1,500 tokens`.\n\nChoose the example with the smallest additional contribution. Example 4 retains the standalone inventory lookup and demonstrates structured output. Example 6 still demonstrates a scalar response, making Example 3 the intended coverage-based exclusion.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Five-example limit — Hard constraint: One candidate must be excluded.\n\n- Examples 3 and 4 share a request, role, and tool path — Redundancy signal: They overlap substantially.\n\n- Example 4 includes quantity and a restocking flag — Output signal: It demonstrates an additional response structure.\n\n- Example 6 returns a scalar — Coverage signal: Removing Example 3 does not eliminate scalar outputs from the set.\n\n- “Behavioral coverage” — Selection criterion: Consider conditions, tool sequences, and responses—not merely tool names.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nRemoving Example 3 retains:\n\n- Both multi-tool workflows.\n\n- Standalone inventory and supplier lookups.\n\n- Depleted-stock and forecasted-shortfall scenarios.\n\n- Binary decisions, recommendations, structured objects, and scalar responses.\n\nThis follows the principle of selecting complementary demonstrations rather than spending limited context on overlapping examples. Anthropic recommends a compact set of diverse, representative examples that demonstrate expected behavior. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: The tools overlap with other examples, but Example 1 demonstrates an actual depletion scenario. Tool coverage alone does not establish that this condition is redundant.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Example 2 embeds supplier lookup within a replenishment workflow. Example 6 separately demonstrates when to invoke that tool directly and return its scalar result.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Forecasted shortage and minimum-level comparison differ from confirmed depletion. Sharing a user role and binary result does not erase that decision-condition coverage.\n\nExam Trap\n\n“Richer output” is not automatically better. C is strongest under the question’s coverage heuristic because other retained examples preserve the overlapping behaviors.\n\nIf inventory requests specifically required scalar-only responses, Example 3 would be important and the selection would need reconsideration.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nAn example’s value depends on what it adds to the retained set. Evaluate its marginal contribution across user intent, decision conditions, tool sequence, and output contract.\n\nRepeated tool names can still demonstrate distinct workflows; different wording can still demonstrate essentially the same behavior.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nRetain Examples 1, 2, 4, 5, and 6, then evaluate stock-count, depletion, forecast-shortfall, replenishment, and standalone lead-time requests.\n\nMeasure actual prompt tokens: the estimated five-example configuration leaves only 46 tokens beyond the stated reservations.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nRemove the demonstration that adds the least behavior to the remaining set.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose C: Example 3 overlaps with Example 4’s inventory path, while scalar output remains represented by Example 6. Judge examples by their contribution to the set, not by tool names alone.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\nSupports: Curating diverse, representative examples within limited context.\n\n- [Databricks — Certified Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\nSupports: Selecting few-shot examples by marginal contribution, tool-path coverage, output structure, and token budget.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: The tools overlap with other examples, but Example 1 demonstrates an actual depletion scenario. Tool coverage alone does not establish that this condition is redundant.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Example 2 embeds supplier lookup within a replenishment workflow. Example 6 separately demonstrates when to invoke that tool directly and return its scalar result.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Example 3, because Example 4 demonstrates the same user role and tool path while providing a richer response structure.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe budget requires removing one example:\n\n- Available example budget: `4,096 − 1,800 − 1,000 = 1,296 tokens`.\n\n- Five examples: approximately `1,250 tokens`.\n\n- Six examples: approximately `1,500 tokens`.\n\nChoose the example with the smallest additional contribution. Example 4 retains the standalone inventory lookup and demonstrates structured output. Example 6 still demonstrates a scalar response, making Example 3 the intended coverage-based exclusion.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Five-example limit — Hard constraint: One candidate must be excluded.\n\n- Examples 3 and 4 share a request, role, and tool path — Redundancy signal: They overlap substantially.\n\n- Example 4 includes quantity and a restocking flag — Output signal: It demonstrates an additional response structure.\n\n- Example 6 returns a scalar — Coverage signal: Removing Example 3 does not eliminate scalar outputs from the set.\n\n- “Behavioral coverage” — Selection criterion: Consider conditions, tool sequences, and responses—not merely tool names.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nRemoving Example 3 retains:\n\n- Both multi-tool workflows.\n\n- Standalone inventory and supplier lookups.\n\n- Depleted-stock and forecasted-shortfall scenarios.\n\n- Binary decisions, recommendations, structured objects, and scalar responses.\n\nThis follows the principle of selecting complementary demonstrations rather than spending limited context on overlapping examples. Anthropic recommends a compact set of diverse, representative examples that demonstrate expected behavior.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Forecasted shortage and minimum-level comparison differ from confirmed depletion. Sharing a user role and binary result does not erase that decision-condition coverage."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf"],"source_hash":"2bcd8ab26a79246548a0e0adf95a1bfa08a3d3eeeb6288362729a5a0955c671d","stem_ja":"Databricksの在庫エージェントは4,096トークンのコンテキストウィンドウを持ちます。システム指示とツール説明が1,800トークンを占め、利用者の要求とモデル応答用に1,000トークンを確保します。few-shot例は1件約250トークンで、5件を含められます。\n\nエージェントは`inventory_lookup`、`reorder_check`、`supplier_lead_time`を使います。候補は6件です。\n\n- 例1：倉庫管理者が欠品したSKUの再発注を質問します。`inventory_lookup`で欠品を確認し、`reorder_check`を呼んで二値の再発注判断を返します。\n- 例2：購買担当者が在庫の少ない商品の補充時期を質問します。`reorder_check`、`supplier_lead_time`の順に呼び、補充を推奨します。\n- 例3：店舗担当者が1商品の在庫数を要求します。`inventory_lookup`を呼び、単一の数量を返します。\n- 例4：店舗担当者が1商品の在庫数を要求します。`inventory_lookup`を呼び、数量と補充フラグを含む構造化オブジェクトを返します。\n- 例5：店舗担当者が、予測される品不足に対応が必要か質問します。`inventory_lookup`で最低水準を確認し、`reorder_check`を呼んで二値判断を返します。\n- 例6：物流担当者が仕入先の履行所要時間を要求します。`supplier_lead_time`を呼び、単一のリードタイム値を返します。\n\n最も広い行動カバレッジを保ちつつ予算に収めるには、どの例を除くべきですか。","options_ja":["例1。例2と例5がすでに同じツールを示しており、その判断経路は重複しているため。","例6。例2がすでに`supplier_lead_time`を呼んでおり、単独参照では新しいツールを追加しないため。","例3。例4が同じ利用者の役割とツール経路を示し、さらに豊かな応答構造を提供しているため。","例5。閾値比較が例1の判断パターンを繰り返し、利用者の役割もほかの例に現れるため。"],"explanation_ja":"few-shotはツール名の数だけでなく、判断経路と応答構造の違いで選びます。原問の意図では、同じ役割・同じ単独ツール経路を持つ例3を外し、構造化応答を示す例4を残します。例1と例5は欠品確認と予測閾値判断が異なり、例6は単独のリードタイム参照を示しています。","distractors_ja":["ツールが重なっていても、確認済みの欠品から再発注判断へ進む経路には独自の条件があります。単純なツール重複だけで除きません。","複合的な補充推奨と、リードタイムだけを直接返す経路は異なります。ツール名が同じだけで無価値とはいえません。","同一の役割と単独参照経路が例4にもあるため、原問では最も重複が大きい例として扱います。","予測不足と最低水準の比較は、すでに欠品しているかの確認とは異なる判断条件を含みます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-010","number":10,"form":4,"stem":"A context engineer is developing a Databricks agent that coordinates three tools: a freight rate calculator, a shipment status tracker, and a carrier booking submitter.\n\nThe model has an 8,192-token context window. The system prompt requires 1,900 tokens, and 5,100 tokens are reserved for the user’s request and the model’s response. This leaves 1,192 tokens for few-shot examples. At approximately 595 tokens per example, only two examples can be included.\n\nFive examples are available:\n\n- Example 1: A freight rate tool returns several rate options, which the agent reproduces without modification.\n\n- Example 2: A freight rate tool returns an empty list, which the agent repeats without explanation or additional handling.\n\n- Example 3: A shipment status tool returns structured JSON, from which the agent selects and summarizes two fields.\n\n- Example 4: A carrier booking tool returns a confirmation identifier, and the agent responds with a single acknowledgment sentence.\n\n- Example 5: A carrier booking tool returns a conflict code, and the agent produces a structured explanation together with a recommended next action.\n\nWhich pair should the engineer select?","options":["Examples 3 and 4, because they demonstrate different tools and output structures, cover retrieval and booking confirmation, and avoid overlap with the available freight rate examples.","Examples 1 and 4, because they demonstrate two tools, cover list retrieval and booking confirmation, and represent the two most common successful response patterns expected from the agent.","Examples 3 and 5, because Example 3 uniquely demonstrates shipment status retrieval with JSON field extraction, while Example 5 uniquely demonstrates interpreting a conflict code and producing a multipart response.","Examples 2 and 3, because they cover an empty freight rate result and JSON field extraction, demonstrating two tools and two different response structures."],"correct":2,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"few-shotの限界効用","concept":"ce-f4-q010","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Examples 3 and 5, because Example 3 uniquely demonstrates shipment status retrieval with JSON field extraction, while Example 5 uniquely demonstrates interpreting a conflict code and producing a multipart response.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nSelect examples by the additional behavior each teaches within the available budget. Example 3 demonstrates transforming structured tool data into a focused summary. Example 5 demonstrates interpreting an exceptional result and translating it into an explanation plus an actionable recommendation.\n\nTogether, they teach more distinct response behavior than copying a list or acknowledging a confirmation. This matches the exam objective of evaluating examples by their marginal contribution, including tool paths and output structures. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Hard constraint: Two examples consume approximately 1,190 of the available 1,192 tokens.\n\n- Technical signal: Example 3 requires selective extraction and summarization from JSON.\n\n- Technical signal: Example 5 requires conflict interpretation and a structured next action.\n\n- Distractor clue: Example 2 encounters an empty result but demonstrates no meaningful handling of it.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nBoth selected examples demonstrate a transformation from tool output to a useful response:\n\n- Example 3: Structured JSON → relevant fields → concise summary.\n\n- Example 5: Conflict code → explanation → recommended action.\n\nThey also cover different tools and different response structures. Diverse examples that demonstrate intended behavior are recommended over a lengthy collection of repetitive cases. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- A — Technically Possible but Not Best: This pair covers retrieval and a successful write acknowledgment. However, Example 4 demonstrates a simpler transformation than Example 5. Avoiding overlap with examples that are not selected provides no additional benefit.\n\n- B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Both examples demonstrate valid successful outcomes, but their responses require limited interpretation. The stem also provides no evidence that these are the most common request patterns.\n\n- D — Technically Possible but Not Best: An empty result can be important, but Example 2 merely repeats it. It does not teach clarification, explanation, or a recovery action.\n\nExam Trap\n\nAn example containing an error or empty result is valuable only when it demonstrates the desired response to that condition.\n\nIf production evaluations instead showed frequent failures acknowledging successful bookings, Example 4 could deserve priority. Complexity alone does not determine example value; demonstrated needs matter.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nFew-shot examples teach the relationship between inputs, tool results, and expected outputs. Under a fixed budget, favor examples that add distinct, relevant behavior. Official prompting guidance recommends examples that are relevant, diverse, and clearly structured. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nEvaluate the selected pair against held-out shipment and booking requests. Check whether the agent extracts the correct status fields and recommends appropriate actions for booking conflicts. Change the examples when observed failures justify a different allocation.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nChoose examples that teach distinct decisions and transformations per token.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nSelect Examples 3 and 5 — option C. They demonstrate JSON extraction and actionable conflict handling, providing the strongest behavioral coverage within the two-example budget.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\nSupports selecting few-shot examples by marginal contribution under a token budget.\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Anthropic — Effective Context Engineering for AI Agents\nSupports curating diverse examples that demonstrate expected agent behavior.\n[Official guidance](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\n- Anthropic — Prompting Best Practices\nSupports relevant, diverse, and structured few-shot examples.\n[Official documentation](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/claude-prompting-best-practices)","source_option_explanations":["A — Technically Possible but Not Best: This pair covers retrieval and a successful write acknowledgment. However, Example 4 demonstrates a simpler transformation than Example 5. Avoiding overlap with examples that are not selected provides no additional benefit.","B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Both examples demonstrate valid successful outcomes, but their responses require limited interpretation. The stem also provides no evidence that these are the most common request patterns.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Examples 3 and 5, because Example 3 uniquely demonstrates shipment status retrieval with JSON field extraction, while Example 5 uniquely demonstrates interpreting a conflict code and producing a multipart response.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nSelect examples by the additional behavior each teaches within the available budget. Example 3 demonstrates transforming structured tool data into a focused summary. Example 5 demonstrates interpreting an exceptional result and translating it into an explanation plus an actionable recommendation.\n\nTogether, they teach more distinct response behavior than copying a list or acknowledging a confirmation. This matches the exam objective of evaluating examples by their marginal contribution, including tool paths and output structures. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Hard constraint: Two examples consume approximately 1,190 of the available 1,192 tokens.\n\n- Technical signal: Example 3 requires selective extraction and summarization from JSON.\n\n- Technical signal: Example 5 requires conflict interpretation and a structured next action.\n\n- Distractor clue: Example 2 encounters an empty result but demonstrates no meaningful handling of it.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nBoth selected examples demonstrate a transformation from tool output to a useful response:\n\n- Example 3: Structured JSON → relevant fields → concise summary.\n\n- Example 5: Conflict code → explanation → recommended action.\n\nThey also cover different tools and different response structures. Diverse examples that demonstrate intended behavior are recommended over a lengthy collection of repetitive cases.","D — Technically Possible but Not Best: An empty result can be important, but Example 2 merely repeats it. It does not teach clarification, explanation, or a recovery action."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/claude-prompting-best-practices"],"source_hash":"58326bd77a9298014031e919d46559aaf46c21096be3708c3a0c6fbba86e4129","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、運賃計算、配送状況追跡、運送会社への予約送信という3つのツールを連携するDatabricksエージェントを開発しています。\n\nモデルのコンテキストウィンドウは8,192トークンです。システムプロンプトが1,900トークンを必要とし、利用者の要求とモデル応答用に5,100トークンを確保するため、few-shot例には1,192トークンが残ります。1件約595トークンなので、含められるのは2件だけです。\n\n5つの例が利用できます。\n\n- 例1：運賃ツールが複数の運賃候補を返し、エージェントは変更せずに再掲します。\n- 例2：運賃ツールが空リストを返し、エージェントは説明や追加処理をせずにそのまま返します。\n- 例3：配送状況ツールが構造化JSONを返し、エージェントは2項目を選んで要約します。\n- 例4：予約ツールが確認識別子を返し、エージェントは1文の確認メッセージを返します。\n- 例5：予約ツールが競合コードを返し、エージェントは構造化した説明と推奨する次の対応を返します。\n\nどの組み合わせを選ぶべきですか。","options_ja":["例3と例4。異なるツールと出力構造を示し、取得と予約確認を網羅し、運賃の例との重複も避けられるため。","例1と例4。2つのツール、リスト取得と予約確認を示し、想定される最も一般的な2つの成功応答パターンを表すため。","例3と例5。例3は配送状況取得とJSON項目抽出を、例5は競合コードの解釈と複数部分からなる応答を、それぞれ独自に示すため。","例2と例3。空の運賃結果とJSON項目抽出を網羅し、2つのツールと2種類の応答構造を示すため。"],"explanation_ja":"限られた例では、結果の単純な再掲より、モデルに判断が必要な独自の処理を教えます。JSONから必要項目を選ぶ例3と、競合を解釈して次の行動まで説明する例5が最も補完的です。2件で1,190トークンとなり予算にも収まります。覚え方は「例の数より、教える新しい判断」です。","distractors_ja":["取得と確認を示しますが、単純な確認文よりも競合解釈を教える例を加える方が独自の判断を広くカバーできます。","成功時の再掲と確認が中心で、JSON抽出や競合対応といった、より学習価値の高い変換・判断が不足します。","異なるツールに加え、情報抽出と例外解釈という別種の判断を示し、限られた予算の価値を高めます。","空リストをそのまま返す例は、空結果の意味や次の行動を教えておらず、競合対応の例ほど有益ではありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-011","number":11,"form":4,"stem":"A context engineer is investigating a Databricks customer support agent that manages complex billing escalations. Its system instructions require strict adherence to the official refund policy, designate Billing-Policies tool results as authoritative, and classify FAQ excerpts and previous conversations as non-binding guidance.\n\nAn execution trace shows the following:\n\n- Billing-Policies returns the official rule: chargebacks filed more than 90 days after purchase are not eligible for refunds.\n\n- Customer-FAQ-Search returns a community discussion containing a highly rated but outdated answer. It incorrectly claims that chargebacks are fully refundable regardless of filing date. The agent incorporates this claim into its running summary as a confirmed fact.\n\n- A customer subsequently raises a dispute involving a chargeback from 18 months ago. The agent approves a full refund based on its summary, overlooking the official exclusion even though the policy remains in context.\n\n- In later comparable cases, the agent continues approving refunds and cites its earlier summary to justify those decisions.\n\nWhich context failure mode best describes this trace?","options":["Context distraction, because excessive policy, FAQ, and conversation content causes the agent to overlook the 90-day restriction and follow irrelevant retrieved information.","Context clash, because two contradictory authoritative sources cause the agent to produce an inconsistent decision that partially follows each policy.","Context confusion, because the agent applies a policy section intended for another transaction category to the chargeback dispute.","Context poisoning, because an incorrect refund-eligibility statement becomes accepted context and is repeatedly reused, overriding the correct policy that remains available."],"correct":3,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"誤情報が記憶に固定されるcontext poisoning","concept":"ce-f4-q011","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Context poisoning, because an incorrect refund-eligibility statement becomes accepted context and is repeatedly reused, overriding the correct policy that remains available.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe defining sequence is incorrect information → accepted summary fact → repeated downstream reuse. The agent transforms an unreliable community statement into apparent established knowledge, then repeatedly relies on that contaminated context.\n\nContext poisoning describes an error entering context and being referenced repeatedly. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Authority constraint: Only Billing-Policies provides authoritative policy.\n\n- Entry point: The unofficial claim is summarized as an established fact.\n\n- Propagation signal: Subsequent decisions repeatedly cite that summary.\n\n- Diagnostic distinction: The correct policy remains in context.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nStep 2 introduces the false premise into the agent’s working context. Steps 3 and 4 demonstrate its continuing influence.\n\nThe agent’s earlier statement supplies no independent evidence, but the agent treats it as justification. The failure therefore involves persistent contamination of the information used for later decisions.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- A — Incorrect: The trace does not establish excessive context length or attention overload. It identifies a specific false statement that repeatedly drives decisions.\n\n- B — Incorrect: The sources are not both authoritative, and the agent does not partially apply each policy. Although contradictory content exists, repeated reliance on the embedded error makes poisoning the stronger diagnosis.\n\n- C — Incorrect: No transaction-category mismatch appears. The agent relies on a false claim about the same chargeback category.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDo not select context clash merely because two statements disagree. Here, the strongest clue is that the agent preserves and repeatedly cites an incorrect summary.\n\nClash would be a stronger answer if competing instructions or facts drove inconsistent decisions without this repeated propagation of an embedded error. Clash itself does not require two authoritative sources.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nContext poisoning does not require malicious intent. An inaccurate retrieved statement or faulty summary can become a persistent false premise.\n\nDiagnosing poisoning, distraction, confusion, and clash is an explicit certification objective. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nInvalidate the incorrect summary and preserve source authority when generating replacement summaries. Verify refund decisions against Billing-Policies rather than treating prior agent conclusions as independent evidence.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nA false fact that enters context and keeps driving later decisions signals context poisoning.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose D. The agent promotes an unofficial error into accepted context and repeatedly uses it to override the available official policy.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\nSupports diagnosing context failure modes from agent traces.\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Drew Breunig — How Long Contexts Fail\nDefines context poisoning and distinguishes distraction, confusion, and clash.\n[Original taxonomy](https://www.dbreunig.com/2025/06/22/how-contexts-fail-and-how-to-fix-them.html)","source_option_explanations":["A — Incorrect: The trace does not establish excessive context length or attention overload. It identifies a specific false statement that repeatedly drives decisions.","B — Incorrect: The sources are not both authoritative, and the agent does not partially apply each policy. Although contradictory content exists, repeated reliance on the embedded error makes poisoning the stronger diagnosis.","C — Incorrect: No transaction-category mismatch appears. The agent relies on a false claim about the same chargeback category.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Context poisoning, because an incorrect refund-eligibility statement becomes accepted context and is repeatedly reused, overriding the correct policy that remains available.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe defining sequence is incorrect information → accepted summary fact → repeated downstream reuse. The agent transforms an unreliable community statement into apparent established knowledge, then repeatedly relies on that contaminated context.\n\nContext poisoning describes an error entering context and being referenced repeatedly. \n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Authority constraint: Only Billing-Policies provides authoritative policy.\n\n- Entry point: The unofficial claim is summarized as an established fact.\n\n- Propagation signal: Subsequent decisions repeatedly cite that summary.\n\n- Diagnostic distinction: The correct policy remains in context.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nStep 2 introduces the false premise into the agent’s working context. Steps 3 and 4 demonstrate its continuing influence.\n\nThe agent’s earlier statement supplies no independent evidence, but the agent treats it as justification. The failure therefore involves persistent contamination of the information used for later decisions."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://www.dbreunig.com/2025/06/22/how-contexts-fail-and-how-to-fix-them.html"],"source_hash":"0448acd2f524b48fe93d40431eee8f310902432673b30e3c1a652d885cb3db79","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、複雑な請求エスカレーションを処理するDatabricks顧客サポートエージェントを調査しています。システム指示は公式返金ポリシーの厳守を求め、Billing-Policiesツールの結果を正当な情報源、FAQ抜粋と過去の会話を拘束力のない参考情報としています。\n\n実行トレースは次を示しています。\n\n- Billing-Policiesが公式ルールを返します。購入後90日を超えて申し立てたチャージバックは返金対象外です。\n- Customer-FAQ-Searchが、高評価ではあるものの古い回答を含むコミュニティ議論を返します。その回答は、申立日によらずチャージバックは全額返金できると誤って主張しています。エージェントはこの主張を確定した事実として実行中の要約に取り込みます。\n- その後、顧客が18か月前のチャージバックに関する異議を申し立てます。エージェントは、公式の除外条件がコンテキスト内に残っているにもかかわらず見落とし、要約に基づいて全額返金を承認します。\n- 後の同様の案件でも、エージェントは返金承認を続け、以前の要約を判断の根拠として引用します。\n\nこのトレースを最もよく表すコンテキスト障害はどれですか。","options_ja":["Context distraction。過剰なポリシー、FAQ、会話が原因で90日の制限を見落とし、無関係な取得情報に従っているため。","Context clash。矛盾する二つの正当な情報源によって、それぞれのポリシーに部分的に従う不整合な判断が生じたため。","Context confusion。チャージバックの異議申立てに、別の取引種別向けのポリシー節を適用しているため。","Context poisoning。誤った返金資格の記述が信頼されたコンテキストになり、利用可能な正しいポリシーを上書きする形で繰り返し再利用されているため。"],"explanation_ja":"誤情報が一度の誤答で終わらず、要約で確定事実として保存され、後続判断の根拠になり続けています。これはcontext poisoningです。正しいポリシーも存在するため、単なる情報不足ではありません。覚え方は「誤った内容が記憶に定着し、誤りを再生産する」です。","distractors_ja":["情報量による注意散漫より、誤情報が要約へ確定事実として取り込まれ、継続利用される点が決定的です。","両方が正当な情報源ではありません。FAQは参考と明示され、公式ポリシーより下位です。","別の取引種別のルールを誤適用した証拠はありません。誤った返金ルール自体が記憶されています。","誤情報が永続的な根拠として扱われ、以後も正しい公式ルールより優先されるという障害を直接説明します。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-012","number":12,"form":4,"stem":"A contract review assistant on Databricks receives ambiguous requests such as “Can I back out?” and “What falls under this?” Its vector retrieval returns well-formed contract clauses, but the resulting answers repeatedly address the wrong contractual issue.\n\nTraces show no execution errors in vector search, prompt assembly, or response generation. The pipeline consists of input ingestion, pre-retrieval enrichment, context retrieval, prompt construction, and response synthesis.\n\nWhere should the engineer intervene so retrieval receives a grounded interpretation of the user’s request?","options":["Pre-retrieval enrichment, where ambiguous requests are translated into contract-specific representations before producing the query embedding used for retrieval.","Context retrieval, where a dispatcher chooses among contract-domain indexes by comparing query-embedding similarity scores.","Response synthesis, where system instructions constrain the answer to the contractual topic most relevant to the session history.","Prompt construction, where retrieved clauses are organized and formatted within a structured prompt template."],"correct":0,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"検索前のユーザー意図の具体化","concept":"ce-f4-q012","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Pre-retrieval enrichment, where ambiguous requests are translated into contract-specific representations before producing the query embedding used for retrieval.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe intervention must affect what retrieval searches for. Ambiguous references leave the intended contract and issue unresolved, even when every component executes successfully.\n\nPre-retrieval enrichment can resolve those references and express the intended issue using contract terminology. Changes applied after retrieval cannot, by themselves, correct the query that selected the clauses.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Ambiguous requests — Intent signal: References such as “this” lack an independently meaningful retrieval target.\n\n- Well-formed clauses addressing the wrong issue — Failure signal: Successful retrieval execution does not establish relevance.\n\n- No execution errors — Diagnostic signal: Investigate semantic interpretation rather than treating the symptoms as an API failure.\n\n- “So retrieval receives” — Timing constraint: Grounding must occur before the search.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nPre-retrieval enrichment can identify the intended topic, resolve references using available conversation or contract context, and rewrite the request into a more explicit search query.\n\nDatabricks describes query understanding as transforming user requests before retrieval to represent intent, extract entities, and improve document selection.\n\nThe retriever must consume that enriched query. Rewriting text elsewhere without changing the retrieval input would not address the problem.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Different Use Case: Routing helps select among distinct corpora when the intended domain is identifiable. Comparing embeddings of an unresolved request does not establish what its ambiguous references mean.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Synthesis constraints can improve answer discipline, but they operate after clause selection. Without another retrieval step, they cannot supply relevant evidence omitted by the original search.\n\n- Option D — Different Use Case: Structured formatting helps the model interpret retrieved material. It does not resolve the user’s intent before that material is selected.\n\nExam Trap\n\nAn error-free trace can still describe an unsuccessful answer. Operational validity and semantic relevance are separate checks.\n\nIf the correct clauses were consistently retrieved but misunderstood during generation, intervention at prompt construction or synthesis would become more appropriate.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nQuery enrichment establishes the retrieval target; retrieval selects evidence; prompt construction presents that evidence; synthesis produces the answer.\n\nDiagnose the earliest stage where the intended meaning is missing. Databricks also identifies query reformulation as an approach for ambiguous searches.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nSuppose earlier conversation establishes a particular agreement and a question about early termination. The enrichment stage can carry those references into a standalone retrieval query about that agreement’s termination rights and conditions.\n\nIf the available context does not identify the agreement or intended issue, obtain clarification before inventing a specific interpretation.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nGround the request before using it to select evidence.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nAmbiguous questions followed by valid but contextually wrong retrieval point to pre-retrieval enrichment. Choose A: resolve intent and references before embedding and searching.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Improve RAG chain quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain)\n\nSupports: Query understanding, intent extraction, and rewriting before retrieval.\n\n- [Databricks — AI Search retrieval quality guide](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality)\n\nSupports: Reformulating ambiguous queries to improve retrieval.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Pre-retrieval enrichment, where ambiguous requests are translated into contract-specific representations before producing the query embedding used for retrieval.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe intervention must affect what retrieval searches for. Ambiguous references leave the intended contract and issue unresolved, even when every component executes successfully.\n\nPre-retrieval enrichment can resolve those references and express the intended issue using contract terminology. Changes applied after retrieval cannot, by themselves, correct the query that selected the clauses.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Ambiguous requests — Intent signal: References such as “this” lack an independently meaningful retrieval target.\n\n- Well-formed clauses addressing the wrong issue — Failure signal: Successful retrieval execution does not establish relevance.\n\n- No execution errors — Diagnostic signal: Investigate semantic interpretation rather than treating the symptoms as an API failure.\n\n- “So retrieval receives” — Timing constraint: Grounding must occur before the search.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nPre-retrieval enrichment can identify the intended topic, resolve references using available conversation or contract context, and rewrite the request into a more explicit search query.\n\nDatabricks describes query understanding as transforming user requests before retrieval to represent intent, extract entities, and improve document selection.\n\nThe retriever must consume that enriched query. Rewriting text elsewhere without changing the retrieval input would not address the problem.","Option B — Different Use Case: Routing helps select among distinct corpora when the intended domain is identifiable. Comparing embeddings of an unresolved request does not establish what its ambiguous references mean.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Synthesis constraints can improve answer discipline, but they operate after clause selection. Without another retrieval step, they cannot supply relevant evidence omitted by the original search.","Option D — Different Use Case: Structured formatting helps the model interpret retrieved material. It does not resolve the user’s intent before that material is selected."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/tutorials/ai-cookbook/quality-rag-chain","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/retrieval-quality"],"source_hash":"f621d1710310fe461b6e85ba183837f9fb0b7c79901106bb7b98b6100a1ec3fd","stem_ja":"Databricksの契約確認アシスタントは、「撤回できますか」「これは何の対象ですか」といった曖昧な要求を受けます。ベクトル検索は形式の整った契約条項を返しますが、回答は繰り返し別の契約上の論点に答えてしまいます。\n\nトレースでは、ベクトル検索、プロンプト組み立て、応答生成の実行エラーはありません。パイプラインは入力取り込み、検索前の情報補完、コンテキスト検索、プロンプト構築、応答合成で構成されています。\n\n検索が利用者の要求の根拠ある解釈を受け取れるよう、どこで介入すべきですか。","options_ja":["検索前の情報補完。検索に使うクエリ埋め込みを生成する前に、曖昧な要求を契約に即した表現へ変換します。","コンテキスト検索。クエリ埋め込みの類似度スコアを比較して、ディスパッチャーが契約領域別の索引を選びます。","応答合成。システム指示により、セッション履歴と最も関係する契約上の論点へ回答を制約します。","プロンプト構築。取得した条項を構造化プロンプトテンプレート内で整理・整形します。"],"explanation_ja":"実行は正常でも、曖昧な質問をそのまま検索すれば、違う論点の正しい条項を取得してしまいます。クエリ埋め込みを作る前に、対象契約や撤回・適用範囲などの意図を具体化します。取得後の整形や回答制約より先に、検索へ渡す意味を直すのがポイントです。","distractors_ja":["利用者の意味を検索前に具体化するため、誤った解釈のまま埋め込み・検索へ進む原因を修正できます。","曖昧な要求の埋め込みを比較しても、そもそもの意図が不明なままで、誤った領域へルーティングされ得ます。","回答生成で論点を絞っても、必要な条項がすでに検索されていなければ、適切な根拠を供給できません。","整形は取得済み条項の見せ方を変えますが、間違った論点に基づく検索自体は修正しません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-013","number":13,"form":4,"stem":"A context engineer is developing a Databricks agent to triage support tickets for a B2B software platform. Each ticket contains approximately 200 tokens. The agent must:\n\n- Assign P0, P1, P2, or P3 severity according to whether the issue completely blocks work, impairs core functionality, affects a non-critical feature, or provides information only. Classification requires interpreting the ticket body rather than detecting keywords.\n\n- Select one of eight engineering teams based on the ticket’s implied technical domain.\n\n- Produce a concise acknowledgment tailored to the reported issue.\n\nThe pipeline must handle up to 500 tickets per minute, complete each ticket in under 2 seconds, and remain within a 2,048-token budget per invocation. Inference costs must also remain minimal.\n\nWhich reasoning configuration best fits these requirements?","options":["Use standard mode with extended thinking disabled, because eliminating all reasoning overhead minimizes latency and inference cost at 500 tickets per minute.","Enable extended thinking and set `max_tokens` to 1,024, because restricting response length lowers cost while retaining unrestricted reasoning depth.","Enable extended thinking with its default thinking budget, because four severity categories and eight routing destinations require deep reasoning to maximize accuracy.","Use reduced thinking, because the task requires modest structured judgment, a small reasoning allocation fits the 2,048-token budget, and latency and cost remain manageable at 500 tickets per minute."],"correct":3,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"小さな推論予算と高スループット","concept":"ce-f4-q013","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Use reduced thinking, because the task requires modest structured judgment, a small reasoning allocation fits the 2,048-token budget, and latency and cost remain manageable at 500 tickets per minute.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe intended balance is limited reasoning for a bounded classification and routing task. The agent must interpret meaning, but the workflow does not involve extensive investigation or complex planning. Reduced thinking is therefore the best fit among the choices.\n\nThe exam guide explicitly includes choosing between standard, extended, and reduced thinking based on task requirements and token constraints.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Technical signal: Severity depends on interpreting business impact rather than matching keywords.\n\n- Scope signal: Four severity categories and eight teams create a bounded decision space.\n\n- Hard constraint: Each invocation has only 2,048 tokens.\n\n- Performance constraint: Under 2 seconds per ticket at up to 500 tickets per minute.\n\n- Cost constraint: Minimal inference spending is a stated priority.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nReduced thinking allocates some reasoning capacity without choosing an expansive default thinking budget. This matches a short workflow involving impact assessment, technical classification, and a brief response.\n\nModel-provider guidance supports lower reasoning effort for straightforward, latency-sensitive workloads, with evaluation to confirm that quality remains acceptable.\n\nHowever, the mode alone cannot guarantee the latency or throughput targets. Those require measurement using the selected model and serving configuration.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Standard mode can interpret tickets and may perform adequately. Its explanation is misleading: disabling extended thinking does not eliminate all reasoning or inference computation. The question’s intended choice preserves a modest reasoning allocation.\n\n- B — Incorrect: An output cap does not preserve unrestricted reasoning depth. Token-limit behavior depends on the model; in supported manual extended-thinking configurations, thinking tokens share the output allowance with the final response.\n\n- C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Deep reasoning could help unusually difficult cases, but the number of labels alone does not justify its additional token and latency cost under these constraints.\n\nExam Trap\n\n“Requires judgment” does not automatically mean “requires extended thinking.” Standard generation can also perform semantic classification.\n\nIf evaluations showed that standard mode met the same quality requirements at lower cost, it could be the better production choice. D is the intended architectural tradeoff, rather than an empirically proven optimum.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nReasoning effort, output length, and total invocation budget are separate controls. A short visible answer can still consume substantial reasoning tokens. Allocate the budget across instructions, ticket content, reasoning where applicable, and the final response.\n\n“Reduced thinking” describes the exam’s strategy; its implementation and supported settings depend on the chosen model.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nCompare standard and reduced-effort configurations on representative tickets, including ambiguous impact descriptions. Measure severity accuracy, routing accuracy, acknowledgment quality, token cost, and end-to-end latency under the expected load.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nUse the smallest reasoning allocation that preserves the required decision quality.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose D: reduced thinking for modest semantic judgment under tight token, latency, and cost limits. Validate the operational targets through evaluation and load testing.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide\nSupports selecting reasoning modes according to performance and token constraints.\n[Official exam guide](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\n- Anthropic — Effort\nExplains reasoning effort tradeoffs involving quality, latency, and cost.\n[Official documentation](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/effort)\n\n- Anthropic — Extended Thinking\nExplains model-specific thinking budgets and their relationship to output limits.\n[Official documentation](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/extended-thinking)","source_option_explanations":["A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Standard mode can interpret tickets and may perform adequately. Its explanation is misleading: disabling extended thinking does not eliminate all reasoning or inference computation. The question’s intended choice preserves a modest reasoning allocation.","B — Incorrect: An output cap does not preserve unrestricted reasoning depth. Token-limit behavior depends on the model; in supported manual extended-thinking configurations, thinking tokens share the output allowance with the final response.","C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Deep reasoning could help unusually difficult cases, but the number of labels alone does not justify its additional token and latency cost under these constraints.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Use reduced thinking, because the task requires modest structured judgment, a small reasoning allocation fits the 2,048-token budget, and latency and cost remain manageable at 500 tickets per minute.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe intended balance is limited reasoning for a bounded classification and routing task. The agent must interpret meaning, but the workflow does not involve extensive investigation or complex planning. Reduced thinking is therefore the best fit among the choices.\n\nThe exam guide explicitly includes choosing between standard, extended, and reduced thinking based on task requirements and token constraints.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Technical signal: Severity depends on interpreting business impact rather than matching keywords.\n\n- Scope signal: Four severity categories and eight teams create a bounded decision space.\n\n- Hard constraint: Each invocation has only 2,048 tokens.\n\n- Performance constraint: Under 2 seconds per ticket at up to 500 tickets per minute.\n\n- Cost constraint: Minimal inference spending is a stated priority.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nReduced thinking allocates some reasoning capacity without choosing an expansive default thinking budget. This matches a short workflow involving impact assessment, technical classification, and a brief response.\n\nModel-provider guidance supports lower reasoning effort for straightforward, latency-sensitive workloads, with evaluation to confirm that quality remains acceptable.\n\nHowever, the mode alone cannot guarantee the latency or throughput targets. Those require measurement using the selected model and serving configuration."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/effort","https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/extended-thinking"],"source_hash":"51ecd0d5f17a3cc6fcd8d1327837d5a21f24925f65d2552998bbe791f2314067","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、B2Bソフトウェア基盤のサポートチケットを振り分けるDatabricksエージェントを開発しています。各チケットは約200トークンです。エージェントには次の処理が必要です。\n\n- 業務の完全停止、主要機能の支障、非重要機能への影響、情報提供のみ、という状況に応じてP0、P1、P2、P3を割り当てます。キーワード検出ではなく本文の解釈が必要です。\n- チケットが暗に示す技術領域に基づき、8つのエンジニアリングチームから1つを選びます。\n- 報告された問題に即した簡潔な受付メッセージを生成します。\n\nパイプラインは毎分最大500件を処理し、1件を2秒未満で完了させ、呼び出しごとに2,048トークン以内に収める必要があります。推論コストも最小限でなければなりません。\n\n最も適した推論設定はどれですか。","options_ja":["標準モードを使い拡張思考を無効にします。毎分500件では推論の追加負担をすべて除くことで、遅延と推論費用を最小化できるためです。","拡張思考を有効にし、`max_tokens`を1,024に設定します。応答長の制限でコストを抑えながら、無制限の推論の深さを維持できるためです。","拡張思考を既定の思考予算で有効にします。4つの重大度と8つの送信先で精度を最大化するには深い推論が必要なためです。","縮小思考を使います。適度な構造化判断が必要であり、小さな推論割り当てなら2,048トークンに収まり、毎分500件でも遅延と費用を管理できるためです。"],"explanation_ja":"単なる文字列一致ではなく、重大度・領域・応答の軽い判断が必要です。一方、深い多段推論に大きな予算を使う余裕はありません。原問では、小さい推論割り当てで品質と速度を両立する縮小思考が最適です。実際のパラメータ名や上限は選ぶモデルで異なるため、遅延・精度を測定します。","distractors_ja":["軽い構造化判断まで不要とはいえません。推論を全面的に外すことが、必要な品質との最良の均衡だとは示されていません。","`max_tokens`だけで出力と内部推論の両方を望みどおり管理できるとは限りません。無制限の思考は厳しい予算と整合しません。","既定の拡張予算では、単純な分類に比べ遅延と費用が増える可能性があり、毎分500件・2秒未満の制約に不向きです。","軽量な判断の余地を残しつつ思考量を抑えるため、本文解釈と高頻度・低遅延・低費用という要件を両立しやすくなります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-014","number":14,"form":4,"stem":"A Databricks agent for customer support has 40 tools registered, covering billing, shipping, returns, and account management. All 40 tool schemas are loaded into context on every turn regardless of relevance. For a conversation about a shipping delay, the agent is now consistently slow to respond and occasionally calls a billing tool when asked about shipping status.\n\nWhich context management technique would most directly address this failure?","options":["Increase the model's context window size so all 40 tool schemas can be processed without truncation.","Route each incoming conversation to a pre-filtered, intent-specific subset of tools (for example, shipping-only tools for shipping queries) instead of exposing the full 40-tool registry on every turn.","Append a static instruction to the system prompt telling the agent to ignore irrelevant tools.","Compact the conversation history after every turn to free up space for the full tool registry."],"correct":1,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"意図別ツール絞り込み","concept":"ce-f4-q014","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Route each incoming conversation to a pre-filtered, intent-specific subset of tools (for example, shipping-only tools for shipping queries) instead of exposing the full 40-tool registry on every turn.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe solution must address both unnecessary context load and incorrect tool selection. Intent-based filtering directly removes irrelevant schemas before the model selects a tool. Increasing capacity, adding instructions, or summarizing history leaves the underlying oversized tool set exposed.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “All 40 tool schemas are loaded into context on every turn regardless of relevance” — Technical signal: The avoidable context load comes from tool exposure.\n\n- “a conversation about a shipping delay” — Technical signal: The identified intent supports a narrower tool set.\n\n- “consistently slow to respond and occasionally calls a billing tool” — Technical signal: Both efficiency and tool-selection accuracy need improvement.\n\n- “most directly” — Selection instruction: Address the source of the failure.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nFiltering the available tools before inference reduces schema tokens and removes irrelevant choices from the model’s immediate selection set. Shipping questions receive shipping capabilities without competing billing schemas.\n\nThe subset must still contain every capability needed for the task. In production, reassess routing when intent changes or spans multiple categories; filtering should not permanently strand a conversation in its initial category.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: A larger context window accommodates more input, making it tempting when context seems overloaded. However, the scenario does not establish truncation, and greater capacity leaves irrelevant schemas and competing tool choices present. It would fit a task whose necessary context exceeds the existing window.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: An instruction can guide selection, but all 40 schemas still consume context. It asks the model to disregard distractions that routing could remove. Prompt clarification is useful when relevant tools remain ambiguous, but it does not directly reduce unnecessary tool exposure.\n\n- Option D — Different Use Case: Compaction summarizes accumulated conversation history. It is tempting because it can reduce context size, but the stated source of excess is the tool registry reloaded every turn. Compaction would fit a long conversation whose history is causing context pressure; applying it every turn also introduces unnecessary processing.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is A: confusing context capacity with context relevance. Fitting all tools into the window does not make them useful or easy to distinguish. A larger window becomes defensible if all schemas are genuinely necessary and the actual failure is insufficient capacity.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nContext engineering includes deciding what information and capabilities enter the model’s context. A focused tool set reduces avoidable competition during selection. Tool filtering complements authorization controls; it does not replace permissions or execution-time checks.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA support application can classify a request as shipping-related and provide shipment tracking and delivery-investigation tools. This reduces irrelevant schema input and opportunities for billing-tool mistakes. Measure latency and routing accuracy, and allow escalation to additional tools when a delivery issue becomes a refund request.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nExpose the tools needed for the current intent, and expand the set when the task requires it.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen an agent receives many irrelevant tools and selects the wrong category, filter tools before inference. Use compaction for excessive history and larger windows for necessary context that genuinely cannot fit.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide:](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\nSupports: Domain alignment through Section 1’s objective on proactive context management, including minimal tool sets before compaction becomes necessary.\n\n- [Databricks — Govern an MCP:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-gateway/govern-mcp-service)\n\nSupports: Selecting exposed tools and distinguishing removal from the tool list from policies that restrict execution. Intent-based routing is application logic, not an automatic feature implied by this documentation.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: A larger context window accommodates more input, making it tempting when context seems overloaded. However, the scenario does not establish truncation, and greater capacity leaves irrelevant schemas and competing tool choices present. It would fit a task whose necessary context exceeds the existing window.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Route each incoming conversation to a pre-filtered, intent-specific subset of tools (for example, shipping-only tools for shipping queries) instead of exposing the full 40-tool registry on every turn.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe solution must address both unnecessary context load and incorrect tool selection. Intent-based filtering directly removes irrelevant schemas before the model selects a tool. Increasing capacity, adding instructions, or summarizing history leaves the underlying oversized tool set exposed.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “All 40 tool schemas are loaded into context on every turn regardless of relevance” — Technical signal: The avoidable context load comes from tool exposure.\n\n- “a conversation about a shipping delay” — Technical signal: The identified intent supports a narrower tool set.\n\n- “consistently slow to respond and occasionally calls a billing tool” — Technical signal: Both efficiency and tool-selection accuracy need improvement.\n\n- “most directly” — Selection instruction: Address the source of the failure.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nFiltering the available tools before inference reduces schema tokens and removes irrelevant choices from the model’s immediate selection set. Shipping questions receive shipping capabilities without competing billing schemas.\n\nThe subset must still contain every capability needed for the task. In production, reassess routing when intent changes or spans multiple categories; filtering should not permanently strand a conversation in its initial category.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: An instruction can guide selection, but all 40 schemas still consume context. It asks the model to disregard distractions that routing could remove. Prompt clarification is useful when relevant tools remain ambiguous, but it does not directly reduce unnecessary tool exposure.","Option D — Different Use Case: Compaction summarizes accumulated conversation history. It is tempting because it can reduce context size, but the stated source of excess is the tool registry reloaded every turn. Compaction would fit a long conversation whose history is causing context pressure; applying it every turn also introduces unnecessary processing."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-gateway/govern-mcp-service"],"source_hash":"651a1ec362e6dac7f02139b34223a47c5134ba5ab9d073aa29fc5633150788ca","stem_ja":"Databricksの顧客サポートエージェントに、請求、配送、返品、アカウント管理を扱う40個のツールが登録されています。関連性に関係なく、毎ターン40個すべてのツールスキーマがコンテキストへ読み込まれます。配送遅延の会話で応答が常に遅くなり、配送状況を尋ねられた際に請求ツールを呼ぶこともあります。\n\nこの障害に最も直接対処するコンテキスト管理手法はどれですか。","options_ja":["モデルのコンテキストウィンドウを大きくし、40個のツールスキーマを切り捨てずに処理できるようにします。","各会話を、例えば配送質問なら配送ツールだけ、というように意図別に事前選別したツール部分集合へ振り分け、毎ターン40個すべてを公開しないようにします。","無関係なツールを無視するよう、システムプロンプトに静的な指示を追加します。","すべてのツール一覧のために空間を空けるよう、毎ターン会話履歴を圧縮します。"],"explanation_ja":"不要なツール定義は、トークン消費だけでなく誤ったツール選択の原因にもなります。配送の会話には配送に関係するツールだけを提示すれば、両方の問題へ直接対処できます。大きなウィンドウや履歴圧縮で「全部を入れる」のではなく、必要な候補を選ぶことが先です。","distractors_ja":["容量が増えても40個の無関係な定義を読み込む負担や、請求ツールを誤選択する原因は残ります。","要求の意図に関連するツールだけに絞るため、無駄なコンテキストと選択候補の混乱を同時に減らせます。","指示を増やすだけでは、無関係な40個の定義の読み込み負担や紛らわしい候補自体を除去できません。","履歴を圧縮しても、原因である全ツールの毎ターン読み込みは続き、会話の重要情報を失う危険も増えます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-015","number":15,"form":4,"stem":"A context engineer is designing a new Databricks agent for an internal engineering-documentation assistant. The agent will have a code-search tool that can return full file contents (sometimes 2,000+ lines) and a ticket-lookup tool that returns complete ticket histories. The engineer wants to minimize context window pressure from the start, before relying on compaction to recover space later in long sessions.\n\nWhich combination of strategies should the engineer apply at design time?","options":["Allow both tools to return their full, unfiltered output, and rely on the agent's compaction step to summarize the history once the context window approaches capacity.","Pre-load the full contents of the most frequently accessed files and tickets into the system prompt at the start of every session so the agent never needs to call the tools.","Restrict both tools to returning only a boolean \"found/not found\" result, requiring the agent to ask a human for full details whenever needed.","Configure the code-search and ticket-lookup tools to return only the fields or snippets relevant to the query (for example, matching line ranges or open-ticket summary fields) rather than full dumps, and retrieve ticket details only when the conversation actually needs them."],"correct":3,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"ツール結果のスコープと必要時取得","concept":"ce-f4-q015","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Configure the code-search and ticket-lookup tools to return only the fields or snippets relevant to the query (for example, matching line ranges or open-ticket summary fields) rather than full dumps, and retrieve ticket details only when the conversation actually needs them.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirement is to prevent unnecessary context growth while preserving the assistant’s ability to answer engineering questions. D combines tool-result scoping with just-in-time retrieval. A postpones the problem, B introduces large amounts of content upfront, and C removes information the assistant needs.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “full file contents (sometimes 2,000+ lines)” — Technical signal: Code-search results can introduce substantial irrelevant content.\n\n- “complete ticket histories” — Technical signal: Ticket responses need selective fields or targeted history retrieval.\n\n- “from the start, before relying on compaction” — Hard constraint: The solution must reduce incoming context proactively.\n\n- “combination of strategies” — Selection instruction: Choose the option that controls both response size and retrieval timing.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe two strategies address different sources of growth:\n\n- Tool-result scoping limits how much information each call introduces.\n\n- Just-in-time retrieval avoids introducing details before they become relevant.\n\nTogether, they preserve useful evidence while reducing unnecessary input. Tools should retain source identifiers and support follow-up retrieval so the agent can expand snippets or inspect ticket history when the initial result is insufficient.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Violates Requirement: Full outputs preserve all available details, making this superficially convenient. However, waiting for compaction explicitly conflicts with preventing pressure from the start. Compaction remains useful for accumulated history after proactive controls are in place.\n\n- Option B — Violates Requirement: Preloading can avoid some retrieval calls, but placing complete files and tickets in every session consumes context before their relevance is established. A small, consistently needed reference may justify preloading; frequent access alone does not justify loading full records.\n\n- Option C — Different Use Case: Boolean results minimize response size but cannot explain code behavior or ticket decisions. Requiring human assistance for every substantive detail undermines the documentation assistant. Boolean output fits an existence-check task where presence or absence is the complete answer.\n\nExam Trap\n\nA is the strongest distractor because compaction is a valid context-management technique. The decisive distinction is preventing unnecessary content from entering context versus summarizing it afterward. Compaction becomes appropriate when the problem is preserving essential information from an already accumulated conversation.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nOptimize for sufficient relevant information, not simply the fewest tokens. Scoping controls how much enters context; retrieval timing controls when it enters. Excessive filtering can omit dependencies or important historical decisions, so the agent needs a path to request more evidence.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nFor a question about retry behavior, return the matching function with surrounding lines and a relevant ticket’s status and summary. Fetch discussion history only if the reason for the behavior remains unclear. This reduces input volume, although follow-up calls can add latency; evaluate answer completeness alongside token savings.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nRetrieve only what is needed now, with a path to obtain more.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nLarge tool outputs plus a requirement to prevent context pressure point to scoped results and just-in-time retrieval. Preserve access to detail; use compaction later when accumulated history warrants it.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Anthropic — Writing effective tools for agents — with agents:](https://www.anthropic.com/research/writing-tools-for-agents)\n\nSupports: Meaningful tool outputs, selective fields, range selection, filtering, and adjustable response detail.\n\n- [Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents:](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\nSupports: Just-in-time retrieval, lightweight references, progressive access to detail, and retrieval trade-offs.\n\n- [Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide:](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\nSupports: Section 1 explicitly covers proactive strategies including just-in-time retrieval and tool-result scoping before compaction becomes necessary.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Violates Requirement: Full outputs preserve all available details, making this superficially convenient. However, waiting for compaction explicitly conflicts with preventing pressure from the start. Compaction remains useful for accumulated history after proactive controls are in place.","Option B — Violates Requirement: Preloading can avoid some retrieval calls, but placing complete files and tickets in every session consumes context before their relevance is established. A small, consistently needed reference may justify preloading; frequent access alone does not justify loading full records.","Option C — Different Use Case: Boolean results minimize response size but cannot explain code behavior or ticket decisions. Requiring human assistance for every substantive detail undermines the documentation assistant. Boolean output fits an existence-check task where presence or absence is the complete answer.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Configure the code-search and ticket-lookup tools to return only the fields or snippets relevant to the query (for example, matching line ranges or open-ticket summary fields) rather than full dumps, and retrieve ticket details only when the conversation actually needs them.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirement is to prevent unnecessary context growth while preserving the assistant’s ability to answer engineering questions. D combines tool-result scoping with just-in-time retrieval. A postpones the problem, B introduces large amounts of content upfront, and C removes information the assistant needs.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “full file contents (sometimes 2,000+ lines)” — Technical signal: Code-search results can introduce substantial irrelevant content.\n\n- “complete ticket histories” — Technical signal: Ticket responses need selective fields or targeted history retrieval.\n\n- “from the start, before relying on compaction” — Hard constraint: The solution must reduce incoming context proactively.\n\n- “combination of strategies” — Selection instruction: Choose the option that controls both response size and retrieval timing.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe two strategies address different sources of growth:\n\n- Tool-result scoping limits how much information each call introduces.\n\n- Just-in-time retrieval avoids introducing details before they become relevant.\n\nTogether, they preserve useful evidence while reducing unnecessary input. Tools should retain source identifiers and support follow-up retrieval so the agent can expand snippets or inspect ticket history when the initial result is insufficient."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/writing-tools-for-agents","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf"],"source_hash":"7c680e3cadeed52f6d052c705f05a72cba56c55a8f2bcf4345a541f6526eaa6c","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、社内エンジニアリング文書アシスタント用の新しいDatabricksエージェントを設計しています。コード検索ツールはファイル全体（2,000行を超える場合もあります）を返せ、チケット参照ツールは完全なチケット履歴を返します。エンジニアは、長いセッションの後半で圧縮して空間を回復する方法に頼る前に、最初からコンテキストウィンドウへの圧迫を最小限にしたいと考えています。\n\n設計時にどの戦略の組み合わせを適用すべきですか。","options_ja":["両ツールが未加工の完全な出力を返すことを許可し、ウィンドウが容量に近づいたら圧縮ステップで履歴を要約させます。","頻繁に使うファイルとチケットの全内容を毎セッション開始時にシステムプロンプトへ事前ロードし、ツール呼び出しを不要にします。","両ツールを「見つかった／見つからない」という真偽値だけを返すよう制限し、詳細が必要なときは必ず人へ問い合わせます。","コード検索とチケット参照が、全件のダンプではなく、該当行の範囲や未完了チケットの要約項目など、クエリに関係する項目・抜粋だけを返すよう設定し、会話で本当に必要になったときだけチケットの詳細を取得します。"],"explanation_ja":"先に不要な情報を入れない設計が、後からの圧縮より効果的です。ツールの返却範囲をクエリに関係する項目・行へ絞り、詳細は必要時に追加取得します。ただし真偽値だけでは仕事に必要な根拠が不足します。「少なければよい」ではなく「必要十分」が判断基準です。","distractors_ja":["不要な情報を大量投入してから圧縮する事後対処であり、設計段階で負担を減らすという目的に反します。","頻繁に参照する資料でも、毎回の全量事前読み込みは基礎トークン負担を増やし、無関係な内容も含めます。","情報を減らしすぎて、コードやチケットを理解し解決する能力まで失います。必要な抜粋を返すことが重要です。","関連項目だけを返し、詳しい情報は必要時に取得するため、判断材料を保ちながら初期の圧迫を防げます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-016","number":16,"form":4,"stem":"A context engineer is troubleshooting a sales operations Genie Agent, formerly called a Genie Space. Identical revenue questions asked in separate sessions—for example, a request for Q3 EMEA revenue—produce different totals.\n\nThe agent uses a Unity Catalog managed table containing individual transactions. Its configuration includes:\n\n- Comprehensive table and column comments.\n\n- Text instructions covering daily FX-rate conversion, accrual timing, and intercompany eliminations.\n\n- Sample questions addressing quarterly and regional revenue.\n\nQuery history shows that Genie generates fresh SQL in each session, implementing currency conversion and intercompany eliminations differently.\n\nWhich change would most reliably stabilize these revenue answers?","options":["Implement the approved revenue calculation in a parameterized Unity Catalog SQL function and attach it to the Genie Agent as a trusted asset.","Add a complete example SQL query implementing the revenue calculation so Genie can learn its structure.","Extend the text instructions with detailed pseudocode describing each revenue-calculation step.","Add a reusable revenue SQL expression to the agent’s knowledge store."],"correct":0,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"承認済み計算の信頼済みSQL関数化","concept":"ce-f4-q016","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Implement the approved revenue calculation in a parameterized Unity Catalog SQL function and attach it to the Genie Agent as a trusted asset.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe metadata and business instructions are already present. The observed failure is variation in the executable calculation: Genie reconstructs important accounting steps differently between sessions.\n\nA moves the complete approved calculation into an executable asset. Genie can supply parameters while the function preserves the calculation’s implementation. The other choices provide guidance or reusable components without enclosing the entire calculation as directly.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Comprehensive comments — Exclusion signal: Missing basic metadata is not the demonstrated problem.\n\n- Detailed revenue instructions already exist — Diagnostic signal: Explaining the rules has not stabilized implementation.\n\n- Daily FX conversion, accrual timing, and eliminations — Complexity signal: Correctness depends on several coordinated operations.\n\n- Different SQL implementations across sessions — Decisive evidence: The calculation logic itself needs to remain consistent.\n\n- “Most reliably” — Selection criterion: Prefer an executable canonical implementation over additional generation guidance.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA parameterized function can encapsulate the FX lookup, accrual rules, elimination logic, and aggregation. Parameters allow reuse across periods and regions without asking Genie to recreate those operations.\n\nDatabricks documents Unity Catalog SQL functions as trusted assets and states that Genie cannot view or modify the SQL inside the function. \n\nThe function still needs correct implementation, suitable usage guidance, appropriate permissions, and validation that Genie selects it with the intended parameters.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Complete SQL examples can substantially improve answers. However, Genie may learn from and adapt an example for related questions. This option does not explicitly establish a parameterized trusted execution path.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Pseudocode could clarify ambiguous rules, but Genie must still translate those rules into SQL. It leaves the observed source of variation in place.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: SQL expressions are valid, reusable definitions for metrics and are more precise than prose. However, defining a revenue expression alone does not necessarily fix the surrounding FX joins, accrual processing, and elimination operations. The function can encapsulate the complete calculation.\n\nDatabricks distinguishes SQL expressions for business concepts from example queries that teach broader query patterns, and recommends structured SQL guidance over text when possible. \n\nExam Trap\n\nA SQL expression is a strong option for a reusable metric formula. Here, the failure involves a complete calculation pipeline spanning several business rules.\n\nIf the data already contained correctly converted, accrued, and eliminated amounts, a reusable aggregation expression could be sufficient. Also, a stable function does not freeze results: changed transactions or FX data can legitimately change the answer.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nDistinguish a definition that guides query generation from an executable implementation that owns the calculation.\n\nFor complex logic that must remain consistent, reduce the amount of business logic the model must reconstruct on each request.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nValidate a revenue function against finance-approved results, attach it as a trusted asset, and test representative period-and-region questions. Inspect generated calls to confirm that Genie uses the function and resolves parameters correctly.\n\nThis centralizes changes to the revenue implementation and makes discrepancies easier to investigate.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nWhen complex SQL keeps changing, encapsulate the approved calculation in a trusted function.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nGood metadata plus detailed instructions will not prevent every variation in generated SQL. When the complete business calculation must remain consistent across requests, choose a parameterized trusted function—A.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Tune Genie Agent quality](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality)\n\nSupports: Current Genie terminology, trusted SQL functions, parameterization, and protected function implementation.\n\n- [Databricks — Curate an effective Genie Agent](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices)\n\nSupports: Choosing SQL expressions, example queries, and text instructions according to their roles.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Implement the approved revenue calculation in a parameterized Unity Catalog SQL function and attach it to the Genie Agent as a trusted asset.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe metadata and business instructions are already present. The observed failure is variation in the executable calculation: Genie reconstructs important accounting steps differently between sessions.\n\nA moves the complete approved calculation into an executable asset. Genie can supply parameters while the function preserves the calculation’s implementation. The other choices provide guidance or reusable components without enclosing the entire calculation as directly.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Comprehensive comments — Exclusion signal: Missing basic metadata is not the demonstrated problem.\n\n- Detailed revenue instructions already exist — Diagnostic signal: Explaining the rules has not stabilized implementation.\n\n- Daily FX conversion, accrual timing, and eliminations — Complexity signal: Correctness depends on several coordinated operations.\n\n- Different SQL implementations across sessions — Decisive evidence: The calculation logic itself needs to remain consistent.\n\n- “Most reliably” — Selection criterion: Prefer an executable canonical implementation over additional generation guidance.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA parameterized function can encapsulate the FX lookup, accrual rules, elimination logic, and aggregation. Parameters allow reuse across periods and regions without asking Genie to recreate those operations.\n\nDatabricks documents Unity Catalog SQL functions as trusted assets and states that Genie cannot view or modify the SQL inside the function. \n\nThe function still needs correct implementation, suitable usage guidance, appropriate permissions, and validation that Genie selects it with the intended parameters.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Complete SQL examples can substantially improve answers. However, Genie may learn from and adapt an example for related questions. This option does not explicitly establish a parameterized trusted execution path.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Pseudocode could clarify ambiguous rules, but Genie must still translate those rules into SQL. It leaves the observed source of variation in place.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: SQL expressions are valid, reusable definitions for metrics and are more precise than prose. However, defining a revenue expression alone does not necessarily fix the surrounding FX joins, accrual processing, and elimination operations. The function can encapsulate the complete calculation."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices"],"source_hash":"b0e2270576a7210bf8f231f8cbb7fe77a45e9a9586aa620a783adff6b47e029f","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、以前はGenie Spaceと呼ばれていた営業業務向けGenie Agentを調査しています。例えばQ3のEMEA売上高という同じ質問でも、別セッションでは異なる合計になります。\n\nエージェントは個々の取引を含むUnity Catalog管理テーブルを使用しています。設定は次のとおりです。\n\n- 包括的なテーブル・列コメント。\n- 日次為替換算、発生主義による計上時点、グループ内取引の消去に関する文章指示。\n- 四半期別・地域別の売上高に関するサンプル質問。\n\nクエリ履歴では、Genieがセッションごとに新しいSQLを生成し、為替換算とグループ内取引の消去を異なる方法で実装しています。\n\n売上高の回答を最も確実に安定させる変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["承認済みの売上高計算をパラメータ付きUnity Catalog SQL関数として実装し、信頼済みアセットとしてGenie Agentへ接続します。","売上高計算を実装した完全なサンプルSQLクエリを追加し、その構造をGenieに学習させます。","各売上高計算手順を説明する詳細な疑似コードを、文章指示へ追加します。","再利用可能な売上高SQL式を、エージェントの知識ストアへ追加します。"],"explanation_ja":"メタデータや指示はあるのに、生成される計算手順が揺れています。承認済み計算をパラメータ付きSQL関数へ閉じ込め、信頼済みアセットとして使えば、地域や期間を変えつつ同じロジックを実行できます。計算の説明を増やすより、実行する計算そのものを固定するのが有効です。","distractors_ja":["為替換算や消去のロジックを実行可能な共通関数に固定し、同じ入力条件に一貫した計算を適用できます。","構造の参考にはなりますが、Genieに毎回再実装させる前提では、承認済みロジックの安定した直接実行ほど確実ではありません。","文章を詳しくしても解釈とSQL生成の余地が残り、観測された計算の揺れを直接排除できません。","単一の式は部分的な再利用に向きますが、為替・計上・消去を含む計算全体をパラメータ付き関数として固定する方が適切です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-017","number":17,"form":4,"stem":"An engineer compares two prompt configurations for a Databricks contract-review agent using MLflow experiment results.\n\nConfiguration P contains a role declaration, detailed task instructions, and prose guidance for ambiguous or overlapping clause categories. It averages 205 tokens per call, achieves 81% classification accuracy, and takes 290 ms.\n\nConfiguration Q retains the role declaration, replaces the detailed task instructions with a concise directive, removes the prose edge-case section, and adds three examples demonstrating different edge-case classifications. It averages 340 tokens per call, achieves 93% accuracy, and takes 380 ms.\n\nQ therefore adds 135 tokens per call. Which conclusion is supported by these results?","options":["The 12-percentage-point accuracy improvement justifies the additional tokens, and shortening the task instruction caused the improvement.","The 12-percentage-point accuracy improvement justifies the additional tokens, and the few-shot edge-case examples caused the improvement.","The additional tokens are unjustified because replacing prose guidance with examples creates ambiguity that outweighs the reported accuracy improvement.","The additional tokens are justified, but the improvement comes from the larger token allowance because the prose guidance and replacement examples contribute equivalent instructional value."],"correct":1,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"プロンプト比較と因果推論の限界","concept":"ce-f4-q017","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. The 12-percentage-point accuracy improvement justifies the additional tokens, and the few-shot edge-case examples caused the improvement.\n\nB is the intended exam answer. Strictly, no option is fully established by the supplied evidence: the experiment does not isolate the examples’ effect or specify criteria for accepting the cost and latency increases.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe directly supported finding is that Q achieves higher measured accuracy while consuming more tokens and taking longer:\n\n- Accuracy: +12 percentage points.\n\n- Tokens: +135 per call.\n\n- Latency: +90 ms.\n\nThe examples are a plausible explanation, but Q also changes the task instruction and removes prose guidance. These simultaneous edits prevent attributing the improvement uniquely to examples.\n\nWhether the trade-off is justified depends on application requirements that the question does not provide.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Three distinct edge-case examples — Mechanism hypothesis: Demonstrations could clarify difficult classification boundaries.\n\n- Several prompt elements change together — Attribution limitation: The experiment compares bundles of edits.\n\n- 81% to 93% — Observed outcome: Q improves measured accuracy by 12 percentage points.\n\n- Higher tokens and latency — Trade-off evidence: Quality improves alongside resource consumption.\n\n- No acceptance thresholds — Missing decision criterion: Economic or operational justification remains conditional.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nB reflects the intended teaching point: relevant examples can demonstrate desired classifications more concretely than prose alone. Anthropic recommends relevant, diverse examples covering edge cases to improve consistency.\n\nThat supports the proposed mechanism, but does not prove it caused this particular result. B should therefore be treated as the closest intended answer, not a rigorous causal conclusion.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: The experiment does not isolate instruction shortening. It cannot establish that this edit caused the gain.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: The results do not demonstrate increased ambiguity or show that it outweighs the accuracy improvement. Rejecting the trade-off also requires cost and latency criteria.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: More tokens do not inherently produce better classifications. The configurations contain different instructional content, and equivalent instructional value is not established.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDo not confuse a successful configuration change with proof about one component.\n\nAlso, 12 percentage points is an absolute accuracy difference, not a 12% relative improvement. A larger measured gain can still be unacceptable if it violates latency or cost requirements.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nA comparison between two prompt bundles measures their overall performance difference. Identifying the contribution of an individual edit requires controlled comparisons that isolate that edit.\n\nCost justification is a separate decision: compare the measured benefit against the application’s acceptance criteria.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nRun an ablation comparing the concise instruction with and without the examples while holding other prompt content constant. Separately test the prose section’s contribution. Use the same benchmark and scorers, then assess accuracy, tokens, and latency against agreed targets.\n\nDatabricks recommends consistent evaluation datasets, explicit prompt-version tracking, and edge-case testing. \n\nMemory Hook\n\nMeasure bundle improvements; isolate edits before naming their cause.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nFor the intended exam key, select B. The defensible experimental conclusion is that Q gains 12 percentage points at an additional 135 tokens and 90 ms; causal attribution and trade-off acceptance require further evidence.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Evaluate and compare prompt versions](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts)\n\nSupports: Consistent evaluation, prompt-version tracking, and edge-case testing.\n\n- [Anthropic — Prompting best practices](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/claude-prompting-best-practices)\n\nSupports: Relevant, diverse few-shot exam","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: The experiment does not isolate instruction shortening. It cannot establish that this edit caused the gain.","Correct Answer\n\nB. The 12-percentage-point accuracy improvement justifies the additional tokens, and the few-shot edge-case examples caused the improvement.\n\nB is the intended exam answer. Strictly, no option is fully established by the supplied evidence: the experiment does not isolate the examples’ effect or specify criteria for accepting the cost and latency increases.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe directly supported finding is that Q achieves higher measured accuracy while consuming more tokens and taking longer:\n\n- Accuracy: +12 percentage points.\n\n- Tokens: +135 per call.\n\n- Latency: +90 ms.\n\nThe examples are a plausible explanation, but Q also changes the task instruction and removes prose guidance. These simultaneous edits prevent attributing the improvement uniquely to examples.\n\nWhether the trade-off is justified depends on application requirements that the question does not provide.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Three distinct edge-case examples — Mechanism hypothesis: Demonstrations could clarify difficult classification boundaries.\n\n- Several prompt elements change together — Attribution limitation: The experiment compares bundles of edits.\n\n- 81% to 93% — Observed outcome: Q improves measured accuracy by 12 percentage points.\n\n- Higher tokens and latency — Trade-off evidence: Quality improves alongside resource consumption.\n\n- No acceptance thresholds — Missing decision criterion: Economic or operational justification remains conditional.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nB reflects the intended teaching point: relevant examples can demonstrate desired classifications more concretely than prose alone. Anthropic recommends relevant, diverse examples covering edge cases to improve consistency.\n\nThat supports the proposed mechanism, but does not prove it caused this particular result. B should therefore be treated as the closest intended answer, not a rigorous causal conclusion.","Option C — Incorrect: The results do not demonstrate increased ambiguity or show that it outweighs the accuracy improvement. Rejecting the trade-off also requires cost and latency criteria.","Option D — Incorrect: More tokens do not inherently produce better classifications. The configurations contain different instructional content, and equivalent instructional value is not established."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts","https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/claude-prompting-best-practices"],"source_hash":"4c1a27f762256317b3dd4da64e2260b04193f56734a90700aa0acb2037cb29cb","stem_ja":"エンジニアがMLflowの実験結果を使い、Databricksの契約確認エージェントの2つのプロンプト設定を比較しています。\n\n設定Pには役割宣言、詳細なタスク指示、曖昧または重複する条項分類についての文章の手引きが含まれます。1回平均205トークン、分類精度81％、所要時間290msです。\n\n設定Qは役割宣言を残し、詳細なタスク指示を簡潔な指示に置き換え、文章による例外ケースの節を削除し、異なる例外分類を示す3つの例を追加しています。1回平均340トークン、精度93％、所要時間380msです。\n\nしたがってQでは1回につき135トークン増えます。これらの結果により支持される結論はどれですか。","options_ja":["精度の12パーセントポイント向上は追加トークンを正当化し、その改善はタスク指示を短縮したことが原因です。","精度の12パーセントポイント向上は追加トークンを正当化し、その改善は例外ケースのfew-shot例が原因です。","文章の手引きを例へ置き換えると曖昧さが生じ、報告された精度向上を上回る悪影響があるため、追加トークンは正当化されません。","追加トークンは正当化されますが、文章の手引きと代替の例は同等の指示価値を持つため、改善はトークンの割り当てが増えたことによるものです。"],"explanation_ja":"原資料の意図はfew-shot例を挙げる選択肢です。ただし厳密には正答が不十分です。Qは複数箇所を同時に変えており、例だけが改善の原因とは断定できません。確実に言えるのは精度が12ポイント上がり、135トークン・90ms増えたことです。原因を知るには変更を分離した比較、採用判断には費用・遅延の許容基準が必要です。\n\n補足：原資料が明示する正解キーはBだが、原解説自身も『どの選択肢も厳密には立証されない』と認める。複数プロンプト要素が同時変更され因果帰属不可、費用許容基準も不明。キーは保持し注意付き解説を作成。公式比較手順：https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/prompt-version-mgmt/prompt-registry/evaluate-prompts","distractors_ja":["指示短縮と例の追加などが同時に起きているため、短縮だけを原因とは特定できません。追加費用の許容基準も不明です。","原資料の意図する答えですが、few-shotが原因との断定は実験で分離されていません。効果の仮説と因果の証明を区別します。","実測精度は向上しており、曖昧さがその効果を上回るという証拠はありません。費用対効果も要件次第です。","トークン数が増えたこと自体が改善の原因とはいえず、文章と例が同等の指示価値という前提も実証されていません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。","grading_hold":"原資料の条件・選択肢では正解を一意に確定できないため、正答率の計算と期限・苦手復習から除外しています。原資料の想定正答は参考表示です。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-018","number":18,"form":4,"stem":"An agent handling multi-step data-pipeline troubleshooting has a system prompt containing a 1,200-token block of verbose, example-heavy instructions covering edge cases that occur in fewer than 2% of conversations, alongside 300 tokens of core routing logic the agent needs for the other 98% of cases. The team observes the agent frequently losing track of the core routing logic and misapplying the rare-edge-case instructions to common situations. The team wants to fix this by editing the system prompt itself, without introducing any new on-demand loading mechanism.\n\nWhich change most directly improves the agent's focus?","options":["Reorder the system prompt so the edge-case instructions appear first and the core routing logic appears last, since models weight earlier content more heavily.","Duplicate the core routing logic three times within the system prompt so it receives proportionally more attention relative to the edge-case block.","Rewrite the 1,200-token edge-case block into a short, explicitly-scoped decision rule that only applies when specific trigger conditions are detected, reducing its footprint and making clear it is secondary to the core routing logic.","Leave the system prompt structure unchanged, but lower the model's sampling temperature so it follows instructions more literally."],"correct":2,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"稀な例外指示の明確な適用条件","concept":"ce-f4-q018","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Rewrite the 1,200-token edge-case block into a short, explicitly-scoped decision rule that only applies when specific trigger conditions are detected, reducing its footprint and making clear it is secondary to the core routing logic.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe change must improve routing through a system-prompt edit without adding on-demand loading. C addresses both observed problems: excessive exception content and unclear applicability. It reduces the distracting material while explicitly defining when the exception should influence routing.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “verbose, example-heavy instructions” — Technical signal: The exception block contains candidates for simplification.\n\n- “edge cases that occur in fewer than 2% of conversations” — Technical signal: Rare scenarios dominate the prompt despite limited applicability.\n\n- “misapplying the rare-edge-case instructions to common situations” — Technical signal: The exception’s activation conditions need clarification.\n\n- “editing the system prompt itself” — Hard constraint: The intervention must change prompt content.\n\n- “without introducing any new on-demand loading mechanism” — Exclusion: Moving instructions into dynamically loaded skills is outside scope.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nC combines conciseness, explicit conditions, and clear precedence. The model receives less irrelevant example material and a clearer distinction between normal routing and exceptional handling.\n\nThe rewrite must preserve necessary exception behavior. Shortening instructions helps only if the trigger conditions and required actions remain sufficient to handle genuine edge cases.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: Reordering is possible, but its justification is internally inconsistent: placing edge cases first would emphasize them under its own claimed earlier-content preference. Position alone also leaves the verbose block and unclear applicability unchanged; there is no universal rule that earlier text always receives greater weight.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Repetition can emphasize an instruction, making this tempting. However, duplication adds tokens and maintenance burden without clarifying when exceptions apply. Repeating text three times does not guarantee proportionally greater attention.\n\n- Option D — Violates Requirement: Lower temperature adjusts sampling randomness where supported; it does not edit the system prompt or resolve ambiguous instruction scope. It may make behavior more consistent without making the routing decision correct.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe trap is treating prompt length or repetition as a direct control over attention. The decisive improvement is to clarify when an exception applies, while removing unnecessary elaboration. Lower temperature would become relevant if the problem were unwanted output variability under an already clear prompt, rather than systematic exception misapplication.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nA useful instruction distinguishes the default behavior, the condition that activates an exception, and the action required when that condition holds. Frequency alone does not determine importance: rare but consequential cases still need adequate coverage.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA troubleshooting assistant could retain its normal diagnostic sequence and activate a specialized recovery branch only when a defined error signature appears. Test the revision against both routine incidents and genuine exceptions: improved common-case routing should not conceal a regression in rare-case handling.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nKeep the default clear and the exception conditional.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen verbose exception instructions overshadow normal behavior, condense them into precise trigger-and-action rules. Under a prompt-only constraint, explicit scope addresses the failure more directly than reordering, duplication, or sampling changes.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents:](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\nSupports: Concise, sufficient system instructions and avoiding excessive edge-case examples.\n\n- [Databricks — Foundation model REST API reference:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/foundation-model-apis/api-reference)\n\nSupports: Temperature controls sampling randomness, distinguishing it from prompt revision.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: Reordering is possible, but its justification is internally inconsistent: placing edge cases first would emphasize them under its own claimed earlier-content preference. Position alone also leaves the verbose block and unclear applicability unchanged; there is no universal rule that earlier text always receives greater weight.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Repetition can emphasize an instruction, making this tempting. However, duplication adds tokens and maintenance burden without clarifying when exceptions apply. Repeating text three times does not guarantee proportionally greater attention.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Rewrite the 1,200-token edge-case block into a short, explicitly-scoped decision rule that only applies when specific trigger conditions are detected, reducing its footprint and making clear it is secondary to the core routing logic.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe change must improve routing through a system-prompt edit without adding on-demand loading. C addresses both observed problems: excessive exception content and unclear applicability. It reduces the distracting material while explicitly defining when the exception should influence routing.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “verbose, example-heavy instructions” — Technical signal: The exception block contains candidates for simplification.\n\n- “edge cases that occur in fewer than 2% of conversations” — Technical signal: Rare scenarios dominate the prompt despite limited applicability.\n\n- “misapplying the rare-edge-case instructions to common situations” — Technical signal: The exception’s activation conditions need clarification.\n\n- “editing the system prompt itself” — Hard constraint: The intervention must change prompt content.\n\n- “without introducing any new on-demand loading mechanism” — Exclusion: Moving instructions into dynamically loaded skills is outside scope.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nC combines conciseness, explicit conditions, and clear precedence. The model receives less irrelevant example material and a clearer distinction between normal routing and exceptional handling.\n\nThe rewrite must preserve necessary exception behavior. Shortening instructions helps only if the trigger conditions and required actions remain sufficient to handle genuine edge cases.","Option D — Violates Requirement: Lower temperature adjusts sampling randomness where supported; it does not edit the system prompt or resolve ambiguous instruction scope. It may make behavior more consistent without making the routing decision correct."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/foundation-model-apis/api-reference"],"source_hash":"11def15efb0073502fa52e98ac0f96f43151df5fefa0fa550605262a75c2da96","stem_ja":"多段階のデータパイプライン調査を行うエージェントのシステムプロンプトには、会話の2％未満でしか発生しない例外に関する、例を多く含む冗長な1,200トークンの指示と、残る98％で必要な300トークンの中核ルーティングロジックがあります。チームは、中核ロジックを見失い、稀な例外の指示を普通の状況へ誤適用することが頻繁にあると観測しています。新しい必要時読み込み機構を導入せず、システムプロンプト自体の編集で解決したいと考えています。\n\nエージェントの注意を最も直接改善する変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["例外指示を先頭、中核ルーティングを末尾に移します。モデルは前方の内容をより重く扱うためです。","中核ルーティングロジックをプロンプト内で3回複製し、例外ブロックに対する注意の割合を増やします。","1,200トークンの例外ブロックを、特定の発動条件が検出されたときだけ適用する短い明示的な判断規則に書き直し、量を減らすとともに中核ルーティングより従属的であることを明確にします。","プロンプト構造は変えず、モデルのサンプリング温度を下げて、指示へより字義どおり従わせます。"],"explanation_ja":"条件は「プロンプト編集のみ」です。例外を短くまとめ、発動条件と中核ルールへの従属関係を明示すれば、量と誤適用の両方を改善できます。単純な並べ替えや反復では、例外がいつ適用されるのかという曖昧さが残ります。","distractors_ja":["配置を変えるだけでは例外の適用条件が明確になりません。また、前方を重視するという理由と中核を末尾にする操作が噛み合いません。","中核を反復するとトークンが増えます。例外が適用される条件の曖昧さを解く方が直接的です。","例外の範囲を条件で限定し、短い規則へ圧縮するため、基礎ロジックへ注意を戻しつつ例外対応を残せます。","温度を下げても、曖昧な指示の意味や優先順位は修正されず、同じ誤適用を安定して繰り返す可能性があります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-019","number":19,"form":4,"stem":"A Databricks agent performs a single, well-defined task: classify an inbound support ticket into one of six fixed categories using only the ticket subject and body, with no multi-step tool use and no retrieval. The team runs this classification step on a very high volume of tickets per hour and needs to minimize latency and token cost per call while keeping accuracy acceptable for a well-structured task.\n\nWhich reasoning-mode choice is most appropriate, and why?","options":["Reduced thinking, because the task is a simple, single-step classification with a small fixed label set, so the added token cost and latency of extended reasoning would not meaningfully improve accuracy at this volume.","Extended thinking, because any task performed at high volume should use the most thorough reasoning mode available to minimize the risk of misclassification.","Standard thinking, because classification tasks always require a moderate reasoning budget regardless of their structure or volume.","Extended thinking, because a larger reasoning budget always reduces the agent's effective context window pressure by compressing the input before responding."],"correct":0,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"単純分類の推論コスト","concept":"ce-f4-q019","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Reduced thinking, because the task is a simple, single-step classification with a small fixed label set, so the added token cost and latency of extended reasoning would not meaningfully improve accuracy at this volume.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe objective is to minimize latency and token cost while meeting an acceptable accuracy threshold. The task is well structured, uses only supplied text, and requires no multi-step investigation. Reduced thinking is therefore the most appropriate starting choice; additional reasoning needs a demonstrated accuracy benefit to justify its cost.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “single, well-defined task” — Technical signal: Limited task complexity favors a smaller reasoning budget.\n\n- “six fixed categories” — Technical signal: The output space is constrained.\n\n- “no multi-step tool use and no retrieval” — Exclusion: No investigation or orchestration requires additional reasoning.\n\n- “very high volume” — Technical signal: Small per-call costs accumulate across many requests.\n\n- “while keeping accuracy acceptable” — Hard constraint: Efficiency gains must preserve the required quality.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nReduced thinking limits reasoning effort, which can lower generated-token usage and response latency. This fits a straightforward classification step where the ticket already contains the needed evidence.\n\nThe choice remains conditional on evaluation: test representative tickets, including ambiguous examples and less frequent categories. Use the lowest supported reasoning setting that meets the accuracy target.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Extended thinking may help difficult classifications, but high volume alone does not justify it. Applying greater reasoning effort to every ticket adds overhead without an established quality benefit. It would fit if evaluations showed that complex tickets required deeper reasoning to meet the accuracy threshold.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: Standard thinking is a possible configuration, but classification does not always require a moderate budget. Task difficulty and measured performance determine the appropriate effort. Standard thinking becomes reasonable if reduced thinking fails the quality target and a moderate setting meets it.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: A larger reasoning budget does not automatically compress the input or reduce context pressure. Reasoning and compaction are different mechanisms. Additional reasoning can consume more tokens and increase latency; context accounting varies by model.\n\nExam Trap\n\nA is the best answer, but its accuracy claim should not be treated as a universal guarantee. Task simplicity, rather than volume itself, supports expecting limited benefit from extended reasoning. Volume amplifies the cost difference. If measured accuracy improves enough to justify that difference, a higher reasoning setting may become appropriate.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nReasoning effort is a quality–cost–latency trade-off. “Reduced,” “standard,” and “extended” describe conceptual choices here; actual controls and supported values depend on the deployed model and endpoint. A small label set also does not guarantee easy classification if category boundaries are ambiguous.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA support system can evaluate reduced thinking against its existing classifier using labeled tickets, measuring per-category accuracy, latency, and token usage. If quality remains acceptable, deploying the lower-effort setting reduces processing overhead across the ticket stream. Inspecting per-category results helps prevent aggregate accuracy from hiding poor performance on rare categories.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nUse the least reasoning effort that passes the task’s quality checks.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nSimple, self-contained classification with strict cost and latency goals favors reduced thinking. Increase the budget when evaluation shows that additional reasoning delivers a necessary quality improvement.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Query reasoning models:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/model-serving/query-reason-models)\n\nSupports: Model-specific reasoning controls and the trade-off between greater reasoning effort, latency, and token usage.\n\n- [Anthropic — Effort:](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/effort)\n\nSupports: Lower effort for simple or latency-sensitive workloads and evaluating quality before deployment.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Reduced thinking, because the task is a simple, single-step classification with a small fixed label set, so the added token cost and latency of extended reasoning would not meaningfully improve accuracy at this volume.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe objective is to minimize latency and token cost while meeting an acceptable accuracy threshold. The task is well structured, uses only supplied text, and requires no multi-step investigation. Reduced thinking is therefore the most appropriate starting choice; additional reasoning needs a demonstrated accuracy benefit to justify its cost.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “single, well-defined task” — Technical signal: Limited task complexity favors a smaller reasoning budget.\n\n- “six fixed categories” — Technical signal: The output space is constrained.\n\n- “no multi-step tool use and no retrieval” — Exclusion: No investigation or orchestration requires additional reasoning.\n\n- “very high volume” — Technical signal: Small per-call costs accumulate across many requests.\n\n- “while keeping accuracy acceptable” — Hard constraint: Efficiency gains must preserve the required quality.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nReduced thinking limits reasoning effort, which can lower generated-token usage and response latency. This fits a straightforward classification step where the ticket already contains the needed evidence.\n\nThe choice remains conditional on evaluation: test representative tickets, including ambiguous examples and less frequent categories. Use the lowest supported reasoning setting that meets the accuracy target.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Extended thinking may help difficult classifications, but high volume alone does not justify it. Applying greater reasoning effort to every ticket adds overhead without an established quality benefit. It would fit if evaluations showed that complex tickets required deeper reasoning to meet the accuracy threshold.","Option C — Incorrect: Standard thinking is a possible configuration, but classification does not always require a moderate budget. Task difficulty and measured performance determine the appropriate effort. Standard thinking becomes reasonable if reduced thinking fails the quality target and a moderate setting meets it.","Option D — Incorrect: A larger reasoning budget does not automatically compress the input or reduce context pressure. Reasoning and compaction are different mechanisms. Additional reasoning can consume more tokens and increase latency; context accounting varies by model."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/machine-learning/model-serving/query-reason-models","https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/effort"],"source_hash":"908f5030456658913d93b2a4e9203ec8d7f954cad86dfe4c2ede9c8ba721440e","stem_ja":"Databricksエージェントが、明確に定義された単一タスクを実行します。受信したサポートチケットを件名と本文だけから6つの固定カテゴリのいずれかに分類し、多段階のツール利用や検索はありません。チームは毎時非常に大量のチケットでこの分類を実行し、構造化されたタスクで許容できる精度を保ちながら、1回の遅延とトークン費用を最小限にする必要があります。\n\n最も適切な推論モードと理由はどれですか。","options_ja":["縮小思考。少数の固定ラベルへの単純な1段階分類なので、この処理量で拡張推論の追加トークンと遅延に見合う精度向上は期待しにくいためです。","拡張思考。大量実行するタスクは、誤分類の危険を最小限にするため、常に最も徹底した推論モードを使うべきだからです。","標準思考。分類タスクは、構造や処理量に関係なく、常に中程度の推論予算を必要とするためです。","拡張思考。推論予算を増やせば、応答前に入力が圧縮され、エージェントの実質的なコンテキスト負担が常に減るためです。"],"explanation_ja":"固定6分類、検索なし、多段処理なしという軽いタスクなので、小さな推論割り当てが適します。処理量が多いほど1回の不要な思考コストが累積します。拡張思考を使うのは、複雑な判断で実測品質の改善がその追加コストを上回る場合です。","distractors_ja":["単純な1段階分類では縮小思考から始め、許容精度を確認することで、速度と費用の要件を満たしやすくなります。","高頻度であること自体は深い推論の必要性を意味しません。むしろ余分な1回の費用と遅延が増幅されます。","分類は常に中程度の予算が必要という規則はありません。必要な推論量はタスクの複雑さと実測品質で判断します。","拡張思考が入力を自動圧縮して負担を減らすわけではありません。通常は追加の推論トークンや時間を使います。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-020","number":20,"form":4,"stem":"A context engineer is tuning a Databricks agent's system prompt and has a fixed token budget for few-shot examples. Four candidate examples are available: (1) a common case the agent already handles correctly without any examples; (2) a case demonstrating a previously-untested tool-call path the agent has never been observed using correctly; (3) a case with a highly unusual output format that will never be requested in production; and (4) a case showing how to handle an ambiguous user request by asking a clarifying question, which the agent currently handles inconsistently. The budget allows only two of the four examples.\n\nWhich two should be kept?","options":["The common, well-handled case and the untested tool-call path, since showing one strong baseline example alongside one novel path builds a balanced prompt.","The example of the untested tool-call path and the example of handling ambiguous requests with a clarifying question, since both address behaviors the agent does not currently perform reliably.","The unusual output-format case and the ambiguous-request case, since together they cover the widest variety of distinct response styles.","The common, well-handled case and the unusual output-format case, since both are self-contained and require no additional context to understand."],"correct":1,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"未習得行動を教えるfew-shot選択","concept":"ce-f4-q020","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. The example of the untested tool-call path and the example of handling ambiguous requests with a clarifying question, since both address behaviors the agent does not currently perform reliably.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nWith only two example slots, prioritize useful behaviors where demonstrations have the greatest expected incremental benefit. Examples (2) and (4) cover an unvalidated tool path and inconsistent ambiguity handling. Example (1) reinforces an already successful behavior; example (3) demonstrates a format irrelevant to production.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “budget allows only two” — Cardinality instruction: Select the answer containing the strongest pair.\n\n- “already handles correctly without any examples” — Technical signal: The common-case example has limited demonstrated incremental value.\n\n- “previously-untested tool-call path” — Technical signal: A relevant capability lacks validated coverage.\n\n- “will never be requested in production” — Exclusion: Output-format novelty provides no stated production benefit.\n\n- “currently handles inconsistently” — Technical signal: Clarification behavior is an observed reliability gap.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nExample (2) can demonstrate when and how to use the tool-call path. Example (4) demonstrates when missing information warrants clarification instead of an unsupported assumption.\n\nThey cover distinct behaviors and jointly use both available slots. Their benefit should still be validated on separate test cases; including an example does not guarantee reliable generalization.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: A baseline example can establish a useful pattern, and the tool-path example adds coverage. Here, however, baseline performance is already adequate. This pair leaves the known clarification problem unaddressed.\n\n- Option C — Different Use Case: The clarification example is valuable, but the unusual format is explicitly outside production requirements. Variety alone does not justify spending scarce tokens on it. That format would become relevant if production required it.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Self-contained examples are easier to interpret, but that property does not establish usefulness. This pair reinforces an existing success and an irrelevant format while omitting both opportunities for improvement.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is A’s appeal to a “balanced prompt.” Balance matters only when it improves relevant behavior. If common-case performance were unreliable while clarification was already reliable, the baseline-plus-tool-path pair could become preferable.\n\nAlso, an untested path is not a proven failure: it represents an evidence gap. B remains the strongest pair under the supplied facts.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nSelect few-shot examples by their expected contribution to task performance under the token budget. Favor relevant, complementary demonstrations over redundancy or novelty for its own sake.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA support agent might receive one example showing a correct diagnostic-tool call and another showing a targeted question when the affected environment is unspecified. Evaluate tool selection and clarification on unseen requests, while checking that ordinary requests still receive direct answers without unnecessary questioning.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nSpend example tokens on relevant behavior gaps.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nUnder a tight few-shot budget, keep examples that add useful coverage or address inconsistent behavior. Already-mastered cases and never-needed formats generally offer less value.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Context Engineer Associate Exam Guide:](https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf)\n\nSupports: Section 2’s objective on selecting examples by their marginal contribution, including untested tool paths and ambiguous inputs.\n\n- [Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents:](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\nSupports: Curating representative examples of desired behavior and adding examples based on observed performance gaps.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: A baseline example can establish a useful pattern, and the tool-path example adds coverage. Here, however, baseline performance is already adequate. This pair leaves the known clarification problem unaddressed.","Correct Answer\n\nB. The example of the untested tool-call path and the example of handling ambiguous requests with a clarifying question, since both address behaviors the agent does not currently perform reliably.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nWith only two example slots, prioritize useful behaviors where demonstrations have the greatest expected incremental benefit. Examples (2) and (4) cover an unvalidated tool path and inconsistent ambiguity handling. Example (1) reinforces an already successful behavior; example (3) demonstrates a format irrelevant to production.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “budget allows only two” — Cardinality instruction: Select the answer containing the strongest pair.\n\n- “already handles correctly without any examples” — Technical signal: The common-case example has limited demonstrated incremental value.\n\n- “previously-untested tool-call path” — Technical signal: A relevant capability lacks validated coverage.\n\n- “will never be requested in production” — Exclusion: Output-format novelty provides no stated production benefit.\n\n- “currently handles inconsistently” — Technical signal: Clarification behavior is an observed reliability gap.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nExample (2) can demonstrate when and how to use the tool-call path. Example (4) demonstrates when missing information warrants clarification instead of an unsupported assumption.\n\nThey cover distinct behaviors and jointly use both available slots. Their benefit should still be validated on separate test cases; including an example does not guarantee reliable generalization.","Option C — Different Use Case: The clarification example is valuable, but the unusual format is explicitly outside production requirements. Variety alone does not justify spending scarce tokens on it. That format would become relevant if production required it.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Self-contained examples are easier to interpret, but that property does not establish usefulness. This pair reinforces an existing success and an irrelevant format while omitting both opportunities for improvement."],"references":["https://www.databricks.com/sites/default/files/2026-10/Databricks-Certified-Context-Engineer-Associate-Exam-Guide-August-2026.pdf","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"0c5de614779bc8cb68f868a678f8b9dbd4c168fbbe0436e5cd5ccad0be23ac3b","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアがDatabricksエージェントのシステムプロンプトを調整しており、few-shot例のトークン予算は固定です。候補は、（1）例なしでもすでに正しく扱う一般的なケース、（2）正しく使えたことがまだ観測されていない未検証のツール呼び出し経路を示すケース、（3）本番では決して要求されない非常に特殊な出力形式のケース、（4）曖昧な要求に確認質問で対応する例で、現在は対応が不安定なケース、の4つです。予算上、残せるのは2つだけです。\n\nどの2つを残すべきですか。","options_ja":["すでに得意な一般ケースと未検証のツール経路。強い基本例と新しい経路を1つずつ示せば、バランスのよいプロンプトになるためです。","未検証のツール経路の例と、曖昧な要求へ確認質問で対応する例。どちらもエージェントがまだ安定して実行できない行動を改善するためです。","特殊な出力形式の例と曖昧な要求の例。異なる応答スタイルを最も広くカバーできるためです。","すでに得意な一般ケースと特殊な出力形式の例。どちらも自己完結し、理解に追加の文脈を必要としないためです。"],"explanation_ja":"例は「追加すると何が改善するか」で選びます。未検証のツール経路と不安定な確認質問は、実際の能力の穴を埋めます。すでにできることの反復や本番で使わない形式へ予算を使うより、改善の限界効用が大きくなります。","distractors_ja":["得意な基本例を再度教える価値は小さく、現に不安定な曖昧要求への対応が残ってしまいます。","実用上必要で、現在不安定な二つの行動を直接補うため、限られた例の予算を最も有効に使えます。","本番で決して要求されない形式を教えても、実際のタスク成功率へ寄与しません。多様性だけが目的ではありません。","理解しやすいかどうかだけではなく、実運用で不足する行動を改善できるかが選択基準です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-021","number":21,"form":4,"stem":"A Databricks agent's system prompt instructs it to \"always provide a thorough, detailed response.\" In production, the agent pads simple yes/no answers with unnecessary multi-paragraph explanations, increasing both response latency and user complaints about verbosity. The team wants to fix this with the smallest possible increase in token cost and ongoing maintenance.\n\nWhich revision strategy best resolves the failure?","options":["Add a separate explicit rule for every anticipated question type (yes/no, multi-part, open-ended, and so on) specifying the expected response length for each.","Leave the instruction unchanged, and instead fine-tune the underlying model on a dataset of concise responses to override the system prompt's effect.","Remove all response-length guidance from the system prompt entirely, relying on the model's default behavior to naturally calibrate length.","Replace the blanket \"always thorough\" instruction with a single conditional guideline that ties response depth to the complexity of the user's question, so simple questions get brief answers and complex ones get detail."],"correct":3,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"一律の詳細回答指示を条件付きにする","concept":"ce-f4-q021","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Replace the blanket \"always thorough\" instruction with a single conditional guideline that ties response depth to the complexity of the user's question, so simple questions get brief answers and complex ones get detail.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure comes from an unconditional instruction that encourages detail even when unnecessary. The solution must correct that behavior with minimal prompt overhead and maintenance. D directly replaces the problematic instruction with one general rule that preserves detail when warranted.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “always provide a thorough, detailed response” — Technical signal: The instruction applies indiscriminately to every question.\n\n- “pads simple yes/no answers” — Technical signal: Response depth is poorly matched to the task.\n\n- “increasing both response latency and user complaints” — Technical signal: Excess output has operational and usability costs.\n\n- “smallest possible increase in token cost and ongoing maintenance” — Hard constraint: Prefer a compact, reusable revision over extensive rules or model training.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA conditional guideline gives the agent a clear criterion for choosing response depth: question complexity. It removes the pressure to elaborate on every answer while retaining permission to explain complex topics.\n\nReplacing the original instruction also avoids leaving contradictory guidance in the prompt. Brief answers should still include qualifications necessary for correctness.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Detailed rules can specify response lengths, but enumerating every question type adds prompt tokens and maintenance. This approach fits applications requiring rigid formats for a small, stable set of request types.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Fine-tuning can influence response style, but it introduces dataset preparation, training, and evaluation work while retaining the instruction causing the problem. It does not guarantee that concise behavior will override that instruction.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Removing the instruction eliminates the explicit verbosity demand and reduces prompt length. However, it leaves the desired relationship between complexity and depth unspecified. D communicates that relationship with little additional overhead.\n\nExam Trap\n\nC is tempting because deleting guidance uses the fewest prompt tokens. The requirement is the smallest effective intervention, not simply the shortest prompt. C could be sufficient if evaluation established that the model’s default behavior already calibrated response depth reliably.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nPrompt instructions should express a useful decision criterion without prescribing every possible case. Here, conditional guidance balances clarity with flexibility: provide enough detail to answer the question accurately, and expand when complexity requires it.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA support assistant can answer a straightforward feature-availability question briefly while giving a fuller explanation for a migration question involving dependencies. Test both request types after the revision, checking output length, completeness, and latency so shorter answers do not omit necessary information.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nMatch response depth to the question’s needs.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen an absolute instruction causes systematic overproduction, replace it with a concise conditional guideline. Correct the instruction driving the failure before adding extensive rules or changing the model.\n\nOfficial References\n\n[Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents:](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\nSupports: Clear, minimal instructions that balance specificity with flexibility, avoiding both brittle rule lists and insufficient guidance.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Detailed rules can specify response lengths, but enumerating every question type adds prompt tokens and maintenance. This approach fits applications requiring rigid formats for a small, stable set of request types.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Fine-tuning can influence response style, but it introduces dataset preparation, training, and evaluation work while retaining the instruction causing the problem. It does not guarantee that concise behavior will override that instruction.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Removing the instruction eliminates the explicit verbosity demand and reduces prompt length. However, it leaves the desired relationship between complexity and depth unspecified. D communicates that relationship with little additional overhead.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Replace the blanket \"always thorough\" instruction with a single conditional guideline that ties response depth to the complexity of the user's question, so simple questions get brief answers and complex ones get detail.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure comes from an unconditional instruction that encourages detail even when unnecessary. The solution must correct that behavior with minimal prompt overhead and maintenance. D directly replaces the problematic instruction with one general rule that preserves detail when warranted.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “always provide a thorough, detailed response” — Technical signal: The instruction applies indiscriminately to every question.\n\n- “pads simple yes/no answers” — Technical signal: Response depth is poorly matched to the task.\n\n- “increasing both response latency and user complaints” — Technical signal: Excess output has operational and usability costs.\n\n- “smallest possible increase in token cost and ongoing maintenance” — Hard constraint: Prefer a compact, reusable revision over extensive rules or model training.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nA conditional guideline gives the agent a clear criterion for choosing response depth: question complexity. It removes the pressure to elaborate on every answer while retaining permission to explain complex topics.\n\nReplacing the original instruction also avoids leaving contradictory guidance in the prompt. Brief answers should still include qualifications necessary for correctness."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"92e18f09a1685ca37e31b99fe723fc8fdc2e39f9069bd39fa662e8432f57a7dd","stem_ja":"Databricksエージェントのシステムプロンプトは、「常に徹底的で詳細な回答をする」と指示しています。本番では単純なはい・いいえの回答にも不要な複数段落の説明を付け、応答遅延と冗長さへの利用者の不満を増やしています。チームは、トークン費用と継続的保守の増加を最小限にして直したいと考えています。\n\nこの障害を最もよく解決する修正方針はどれですか。","options_ja":["はい・いいえ、複数項目、自由回答など、予想される質問の種類ごとに別の明示的な規則を追加し、それぞれの期待回答長を指定します。","指示は変えず、簡潔な回答のデータセットで基盤モデルをファインチューニングし、システムプロンプトの効果を上書きします。","回答長に関する指示をすべてシステムプロンプトから除き、モデルの既定動作が自然に適切な長さへ調整することに任せます。","一律の「常に徹底的に」を、利用者の質問の複雑さに回答の深さを合わせる単一の条件付き指針へ置き換え、単純な質問は短く、複雑な質問は詳しくします。"],"explanation_ja":"原因は「常に詳細」という無条件の指示です。複雑さに応じて深さを変える一つの規則へ置き換えれば、短い質問と難しい質問の両方に対応でき、例外規則の増殖も防げます。誤った一般指示には、まず狙いを絞った条件付けを行います。","distractors_ja":["質問種別が増えるほど規則と保守が増えます。共通の条件付き原則なら少ないトークンで広く適用できます。","プロンプトの原因を残したまま、学習データやモデルの保守まで増やすため、最小変更という要件に合いません。","原因の指示は消せますが、望ましい長さの基準まで失います。条件付きの簡潔な指針の方が行動を安定させます。","冗長さを生む一律指示を直接修正し、必要なときの詳細さも残すため、費用と保守の増加を抑えられます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-022","number":22,"form":4,"stem":"A context engineer compares two system prompt versions for a document-summarization agent using tracked experiment runs. Version 1 (800 tokens) achieves an 82% task-success rate. Version 2 (1,600 tokens, due to an added block of six detailed formatting examples) achieves an 84% task-success rate. All other factors — model, retrieval, tools — are held constant between the two runs.\n\nBased on this result, what should the engineer conclude, and what is the most defensible next step?","options":["Version 2 should be adopted unchanged, since any measured improvement in task-success rate justifies additional token cost regardless of its size.","The result shows token count has no real effect on task-success rate, since a 2x increase produced only a 2-point gain, so prompt length should be minimized going forward without further testing.","The formatting-examples block is driving the token increase, and a 2-percentage-point gain for a doubling of prompt tokens is unlikely to justify the full cost; the engineer should test a trimmed version of that block, such as one or two examples, before adopting Version 2 as-is.","The comparison is inconclusive and cannot be used to evaluate the formatting-examples block, since experiment tracking only measures token counts, not which prompt element drove a given outcome."],"correct":2,"domain":"System Prompt and Instruction Design","topic":"プロンプト長と実測効果の費用対効果","concept":"ce-f4-q022","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. The formatting-examples block is driving the token increase, and a 2-percentage-point gain for a doubling of prompt tokens is unlikely to justify the full cost; the engineer should test a trimmed version of that block, such as one or two examples, before adopting Version 2 as-is.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe added examples increase prompt size substantially while producing a modest observed improvement:\n\n- Success-rate change: 84% − 82% = 2 percentage points.\n\n- Additional prompt tokens: 1,600 − 800 = 800 tokens.\n\n- Prompt-size increase: (800 ÷ 800) × 100 = 100%.\n\nC offers the most defensible next step: test whether fewer examples preserve the benefit. Neither automatic adoption nor blanket rejection follows from these results.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “due to an added block of six detailed formatting examples” — Technical signal: The source of the extra prompt tokens is identified.\n\n- “82%” versus “84%” — Technical signal: The observed improvement is two percentage points.\n\n- “All other factors … held constant” — Experimental constraint: The comparison isolates the prompt revision more effectively than an uncontrolled comparison.\n\n- “most defensible next step” — Selection instruction: Favor further targeted evaluation over an unsupported final conclusion.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nTesting one or two representative examples assesses whether the full six-example block is necessary. Compare the trimmed prompt with both existing versions using the same evaluation cases and scoring criteria, measuring success, token usage, and latency.\n\nThis tests the examples’ incremental value instead of assuming that all six contribute equally or that their benefit scales with length.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: It treats every measured gain as sufficient justification, ignoring uncertainty and cost. Version 2 could be worthwhile if the improvement is reproducible and valuable enough, but the result does not justify automatic adoption.\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: A small observed improvement does not prove prompt length has no effect. The experiment changes both content and length; it does not establish a general relationship between token count and success.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Experiment tracking can capture outcomes, prompt versions, latency, and token usage—not just token counts. The controlled change supports investigating the added block, although tracking alone cannot establish which individual example caused an improvement.\n\nExam Trap\n\nC’s next step is stronger than its economic judgment. Without sample size, uncertainty estimates, pricing, and the value of successful summaries, “unlikely to justify” remains provisional. A two-point gain could be valuable.\n\nAlso, doubling system-prompt tokens does not necessarily double total request cost: document input, output tokens, and caching can affect the total.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nEvaluate a prompt addition by its incremental benefit relative to its incremental cost. Removing or reducing a component and retesting—an ablation test—helps determine whether that component earns its place. Observed improvement, statistical reliability, and business value are separate questions.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nFor a summarization service, compare zero, two, and six formatting examples on representative documents. If two examples retain the quality improvement with fewer tokens, they provide a more efficient configuration. If six consistently prevent costly formatting failures, the longer prompt may be justified.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nMake added examples earn their tokens through evaluation.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nA modest quality gain from a much larger prompt calls for a targeted ablation test. Keep the smallest example set that reliably delivers the required quality, rather than assuming longer or shorter is inherently better.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — What is agent observability and quality?:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/agent-observability-and-quality)\n\nSupports: Tracking execution outcomes, token usage, latency, and cost, then evaluating revisions against datasets.\n\n- [Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents:](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\nSupports: Starting with minimal sufficient prompts and adding representative examples based on evaluated behavior.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: It treats every measured gain as sufficient justification, ignoring uncertainty and cost. Version 2 could be worthwhile if the improvement is reproducible and valuable enough, but the result does not justify automatic adoption.","Option B — Incorrect: A small observed improvement does not prove prompt length has no effect. The experiment changes both content and length; it does not establish a general relationship between token count and success.","Correct Answer\n\nC. The formatting-examples block is driving the token increase, and a 2-percentage-point gain for a doubling of prompt tokens is unlikely to justify the full cost; the engineer should test a trimmed version of that block, such as one or two examples, before adopting Version 2 as-is.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe added examples increase prompt size substantially while producing a modest observed improvement:\n\n- Success-rate change: 84% − 82% = 2 percentage points.\n\n- Additional prompt tokens: 1,600 − 800 = 800 tokens.\n\n- Prompt-size increase: (800 ÷ 800) × 100 = 100%.\n\nC offers the most defensible next step: test whether fewer examples preserve the benefit. Neither automatic adoption nor blanket rejection follows from these results.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “due to an added block of six detailed formatting examples” — Technical signal: The source of the extra prompt tokens is identified.\n\n- “82%” versus “84%” — Technical signal: The observed improvement is two percentage points.\n\n- “All other factors … held constant” — Experimental constraint: The comparison isolates the prompt revision more effectively than an uncontrolled comparison.\n\n- “most defensible next step” — Selection instruction: Favor further targeted evaluation over an unsupported final conclusion.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nTesting one or two representative examples assesses whether the full six-example block is necessary. Compare the trimmed prompt with both existing versions using the same evaluation cases and scoring criteria, measuring success, token usage, and latency.\n\nThis tests the examples’ incremental value instead of assuming that all six contribute equally or that their benefit scales with length.","Option D — Incorrect: Experiment tracking can capture outcomes, prompt versions, latency, and token usage—not just token counts. The controlled change supports investigating the added block, although tracking alone cannot establish which individual example caused an improvement."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/agent-observability-and-quality","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"257e52fccb22560550f3398fd7ff6fbf4f8fb656def3d191c3f5302bfcdd9f6c","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、記録された実験実行を使って文書要約エージェントのシステムプロンプト2版を比較しています。版1は800トークンでタスク成功率82％です。版2は詳細な形式例6件のブロック追加により1,600トークンとなり、成功率84％です。モデル、検索、ツールというほかの要因は2回の実行で一定に保たれています。\n\nこの結果から何を結論し、次に何をするのが最も妥当ですか。","options_ja":["成功率の測定上の改善は、どれほど小さくても追加トークン費用を正当化するため、版2を変更せず採用します。","トークン2倍で2ポイントしか改善しなかったので、トークン数は成功率へ実質影響しないと考え、今後は追加試験せずにプロンプト長を最小化します。","形式例のブロックがトークン増加を生んでおり、プロンプト2倍に対する2パーセントポイントの改善は全費用を正当化しにくいため、版2をそのまま採用する前に1～2例などへ削った版を試します。","実験追跡はトークン数しか測らず、どのプロンプト要素が結果を生んだかは測れないため、この比較では形式例ブロックを評価できず結論不能です。"],"explanation_ja":"成功率は2ポイント改善していますが、プロンプトは2倍です。追加ブロックを1～2例へ削った版を同じ条件で評価し、品質を保てる最小構成を探します。小さな改善を無条件に採用せず、逆にトークンが無意味とも断定しません。統計的なばらつきや費用の許容条件も確認します。","distractors_ja":["改善があっても、その大きさ、費用、遅延、業務上の価値を比較する必要があります。すべての改善が採用理由にはなりません。","この比較だけでプロンプト長が無意味とはいえません。情報内容と効果を見ながら、縮小版の追加実験が必要です。","増加要因が明確なので、そのブロックを小さくした構成を試し、成功率とトークン費用の均衡を検証できます。","実験追跡では成功率などの評価結果も比較できます。条件を固定した構成比較には意味があります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-023","number":23,"form":4,"stem":"A Unity Catalog environment used by a new agent contains both an authoritative, governance-reviewed `finance.gold.quarterly_revenue` table and several derived, ad-hoc tables created by individual analysts for one-off analyses, none of which have been reviewed or certified. Before deploying the agent, the team wants to ensure it can only retrieve from the authoritative, certified assets.\n\nWhich governance strategy most directly achieves this before deployment?","options":["Add a system-prompt instruction telling the agent to prefer the finance.gold schema over any other schema when answering revenue questions.","Rename all ad-hoc analyst tables with a \"DRAFT_\" prefix so the agent can recognize and avoid them by naming convention alone.","Grant the agent read access to all tables in the environment, and rely on the agent to use its judgment about which source is most authoritative for each question.","Tag the authoritative tables as certified in Unity Catalog, and scope the agent's retrieval index and data-access grants to include only those certified assets, excluding the ungoverned derived tables from the agent's retrieval surface."],"correct":3,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"権威ある情報源だけを検索可能にする","concept":"ce-f4-q023","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Tag the authoritative tables as certified in Unity Catalog, and scope the agent's retrieval index and data-access grants to include only those certified assets, excluding the ungoverned derived tables from the agent's retrieval surface.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirement is exclusive access to approved sources before deployment. D combines identification of approved assets with enforced restrictions on retrieval and data access. The other options leave unreviewed sources accessible and rely on the model to avoid them.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “authoritative, governance-reviewed” — Technical signal: An approved source has already been identified.\n\n- “none … reviewed or certified” — Exclusion: The analyst-created tables do not meet the approval requirement.\n\n- “can only retrieve” — Hard constraint: A preference is insufficient; access must be constrained.\n\n- “before deploying” — Sequence instruction: Configure and verify the restrictions before production use.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nCertification records which assets meet organizational standards. Unity Catalog supports the governed tag `system.certification_status` with the value `certified`. The tag is a trust signal; it does not independently prohibit access to other tables. \n\nThe enforcement comes from restricting the index’s contents and the effective permissions used by the agent’s retrieval tools. Verify that broader inherited or group-based access does not leave excluded sources reachable. Unity Catalog grants and policies provide the access controls. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Violates Requirement: A prompt preference may influence source selection, but “prefer” permits alternatives. It neither blocks access nor guarantees that every asset in the preferred schema is certified.\n\n- Option B — Violates Requirement: A naming convention helps people recognize drafts, but it does not enforce authorization. The agent could still retrieve those tables.\n\n- Option C — Violates Requirement: Broad read access directly conflicts with restricting retrieval to certified assets. Model judgment cannot substitute for the required access boundary.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe decisive part of D is scoping retrieval and grants, not merely adding a certification label. Tagging without enforcement would still allow unreviewed data to enter context.\n\nA prompt preference could be appropriate if the requirement were merely to rank preferred sources while allowing other approved sources; that is weaker than the restriction here.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nCertification identifies trusted assets; authorization determines accessible assets; retrieval configuration determines which accessible content can be returned. These controls must agree. A restricted source list is ineffective if another tool still provides unrestricted access.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nBefore launch, configure the agent to retrieve from `finance.gold.quarterly_revenue` and test that attempts to query analyst tables fail under its runtime identity. Also verify that the retrieval index contains no copied content from excluded tables. This prevents unreviewed figures from entering answers through either direct queries or indexed results.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nLabel what is trusted, then enforce what is reachable.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\n“Only authoritative sources” requires restrictions on retrieval and permissions before inference. Prompts, names, and certification labels support governance but do not replace enforcement.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Flag data as certified or deprecated:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/certify-deprecate-data)\n\nSupports: Certification status, its meaning, and controlled assignment.\n\n- [Databricks — Access control in Unity Catalog:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control)\n\nSupports: Enforcing access through privileges and policies.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Violates Requirement: A prompt preference may influence source selection, but “prefer” permits alternatives. It neither blocks access nor guarantees that every asset in the preferred schema is certified.","Option B — Violates Requirement: A naming convention helps people recognize drafts, but it does not enforce authorization. The agent could still retrieve those tables.","Option C — Violates Requirement: Broad read access directly conflicts with restricting retrieval to certified assets. Model judgment cannot substitute for the required access boundary.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Tag the authoritative tables as certified in Unity Catalog, and scope the agent's retrieval index and data-access grants to include only those certified assets, excluding the ungoverned derived tables from the agent's retrieval surface.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe requirement is exclusive access to approved sources before deployment. D combines identification of approved assets with enforced restrictions on retrieval and data access. The other options leave unreviewed sources accessible and rely on the model to avoid them.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “authoritative, governance-reviewed” — Technical signal: An approved source has already been identified.\n\n- “none … reviewed or certified” — Exclusion: The analyst-created tables do not meet the approval requirement.\n\n- “can only retrieve” — Hard constraint: A preference is insufficient; access must be constrained.\n\n- “before deploying” — Sequence instruction: Configure and verify the restrictions before production use.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nCertification records which assets meet organizational standards. Unity Catalog supports the governed tag `system.certification_status` with the value `certified`. The tag is a trust signal; it does not independently prohibit access to other tables. \n\nThe enforcement comes from restricting the index’s contents and the effective permissions used by the agent’s retrieval tools. Verify that broader inherited or group-based access does not leave excluded sources reachable. Unity Catalog grants and policies provide the access controls."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/certify-deprecate-data","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/access-control"],"source_hash":"02bcbc2c1a3650d4641c1c3e7c4ef180f9adf86ae28d9a33016a7b0324bab1c9","stem_ja":"新しいエージェントが利用するUnity Catalog環境には、正当な情報源としてガバナンス審査を受けた`finance.gold.quarterly_revenue`テーブルと、個々のアナリストが一度限りの分析用に作成した派生・アドホックテーブルが複数あります。派生テーブルはどれも審査や認定を受けていません。チームはデプロイ前に、エージェントが正当な認定済みアセットだけを取得できるようにしたいと考えています。\n\nデプロイ前に最も直接これを実現するガバナンス方針はどれですか。","options_ja":["売上高の質問ではほかのスキーマより`finance.gold`を優先するよう、システムプロンプトへ指示を追加します。","アナリストのアドホックテーブル名すべてに`DRAFT_`を付け、命名規則だけでエージェントが識別して避けられるようにします。","環境内のすべてのテーブルに読み取り権限を与え、質問ごとに最も正当な情報源を選ぶ判断をエージェントへ任せます。","正当なテーブルをUnity Catalogで認定済みとタグ付けし、エージェントの検索索引とデータアクセス権限をその認定済みアセットだけに限定して、統制されていない派生テーブルを検索範囲から除外します。"],"explanation_ja":"「優先してほしい」ではなく「認定済み以外を取得できない」が要件です。認定の識別に加え、索引の収録範囲とアクセス権限そのものを限定します。プロンプトや命名は参考情報であり、アクセス境界にはなりません。覚え方は「タグで見分け、権限と索引で制限」です。","distractors_ja":["優先するという指示だけでは、未審査の派生テーブルを選んだり読んだりできる状態が残ります。","名前は意味の手掛かりですが、読み取りを防ぐ技術的な制限ではなく、命名に従わない表もあり得ます。","すべてを読める権限は、認定済みだけを取得可能にする要件と反対です。モデルの判断へ統制を任せません。","認定の情報を、検索対象と権限の実際の制限へ反映するため、デプロイ前に取得範囲を確実に狭められます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-024","number":24,"form":4,"stem":"A context engineer needs to (1) govern which columns of a customer table an agent is allowed to see based on the caller's role, and (2) give the agent a durable, queryable place to persist structured conversation state between sessions that can be joined against other transactional data.\n\nWhich pairing of Databricks components addresses these two needs, respectively?","options":["Unity Catalog for the role-based column governance, and Lakebase for the durable, queryable conversation state.","MLflow 3 for the role-based column governance, and Unity Catalog for the durable, queryable conversation state.","MCP for the role-based column governance, and MLflow 3 for the durable, queryable conversation state.","Lakebase for the role-based column governance, and MCP for the durable, queryable conversation state."],"correct":0,"domain":"Foundations of Context Engineering","topic":"ガバナンスと永続的会話状態の役割分担","concept":"ce-f4-q024","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Unity Catalog for the role-based column governance, and Lakebase for the durable, queryable conversation state.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nMatch each requirement to its architectural responsibility:\n\n- Caller-dependent access to customer columns requires data governance: Unity Catalog.\n\n- Persistent structured state with relational querying requires database storage: Lakebase.\n\nA satisfies both requirements in the specified order. The other options substitute observability or connectivity components for governance or storage.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “which columns … based on the caller's role” — Hard constraint: Access must depend on the caller’s identity or group membership.\n\n- “durable” and “between sessions” — Hard constraint: State must survive beyond the current interaction.\n\n- “structured … joined against other transactional data” — Technical signal: A relational database fits the required querying.\n\n- “respectively” — Selection instruction: The first component must provide governance; the second must provide persistence.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nUnity Catalog supports column masks and dynamic views that control the data returned to a querying identity. Masks redact column values; curated views can expose selected columns. Caller-specific enforcement requires the query to execute with the appropriate identity—using a shared privileged identity does not automatically enforce each caller’s restrictions.\n\nLakebase provides a managed PostgreSQL backend for persistent agent state. Structured state stored in relational tables can be queried and joined with accessible transactional tables. This requires an appropriate schema and data access; it does not imply automatic joins to every external data source.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: MLflow provides tracing, evaluation, and observability, making it relevant to agent operations, but it does not enforce customer-table column policies. Unity Catalog can govern tables containing state, but it is not itself the transactional database engine requested for the second requirement.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: MCP connects agents to tools and services; those services may enforce governance, but the protocol itself does not define the customer table’s column policies. MLflow can retain execution records, but logging traces is different from maintaining application-owned transactional conversation state.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Lakebase has PostgreSQL access controls, so associating it with permissions is understandable. However, Unity Catalog is the intended component for the stated Databricks table-governance requirement. More decisively, MCP supplies connectivity, not durable database storage.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDo not confuse persisted traces with operational state. MLflow records what happened during agent execution; Lakebase can hold the structured state the application reads and updates to continue working. If the requirement changed to evaluating previous executions, MLflow would become relevant—but neither B nor C would become correct without also fixing its governance component.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nSeparate four responsibilities: governance determines permitted access, storage preserves application data, connectivity exposes capabilities, and observability records behavior. These components can work together, but their primary responsibilities are not interchangeable.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA support agent retrieves customer information through a Unity Catalog view that redacts sensitive values for unauthorized staff. It stores a conversation’s customer ID, pending action, and workflow status in Lakebase, then joins that state with an orders table in the same database when the user returns. Persisted state must also have appropriate access controls so it does not become an alternate route to restricted information.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nUnity Catalog governs access; Lakebase persists state; MCP connects tools; MLflow observes and evaluates.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nCaller-dependent column access points to Unity Catalog. Durable structured state requiring SQL queries and joins points to Lakebase.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Row filters and column masks:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/)\n\nSupports: Query-time column masking and identity-aware dynamic views.\n\n- [Databricks — Agent state and memory:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)\n\nSupports: Lakebase as a managed PostgreSQL backend for persistent agent state and memory.\n\n- [Databricks — What is agent observability and quality?:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/agent-observability-and-quality)\n\nSupports: MLflow’s tracing and evaluation role, distinguishing execution records from application state.\n\n- [Databricks — MCPs:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/mcp-tools)\n\nSupports: MCP connectivity and the use of separate governance controls.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Unity Catalog for the role-based column governance, and Lakebase for the durable, queryable conversation state.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nMatch each requirement to its architectural responsibility:\n\n- Caller-dependent access to customer columns requires data governance: Unity Catalog.\n\n- Persistent structured state with relational querying requires database storage: Lakebase.\n\nA satisfies both requirements in the specified order. The other options substitute observability or connectivity components for governance or storage.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “which columns … based on the caller's role” — Hard constraint: Access must depend on the caller’s identity or group membership.\n\n- “durable” and “between sessions” — Hard constraint: State must survive beyond the current interaction.\n\n- “structured … joined against other transactional data” — Technical signal: A relational database fits the required querying.\n\n- “respectively” — Selection instruction: The first component must provide governance; the second must provide persistence.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nUnity Catalog supports column masks and dynamic views that control the data returned to a querying identity. Masks redact column values; curated views can expose selected columns. Caller-specific enforcement requires the query to execute with the appropriate identity—using a shared privileged identity does not automatically enforce each caller’s restrictions.\n\nLakebase provides a managed PostgreSQL backend for persistent agent state. Structured state stored in relational tables can be queried and joined with accessible transactional tables. This requires an appropriate schema and data access; it does not imply automatic joins to every external data source.","Option B — Incorrect: MLflow provides tracing, evaluation, and observability, making it relevant to agent operations, but it does not enforce customer-table column policies. Unity Catalog can govern tables containing state, but it is not itself the transactional database engine requested for the second requirement.","Option C — Incorrect: MCP connects agents to tools and services; those services may enforce governance, but the protocol itself does not define the customer table’s column policies. MLflow can retain execution records, but logging traces is different from maintaining application-owned transactional conversation state.","Option D — Incorrect: Lakebase has PostgreSQL access controls, so associating it with permissions is understandable. However, Unity Catalog is the intended component for the stated Databricks table-governance requirement. More decisively, MCP supplies connectivity, not durable database storage."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/data-governance/unity-catalog/filters-and-masks/","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/mlflow3/genai/agent-observability-and-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/mcp-tools"],"source_hash":"641243a3453e88d9b96293e2ff89ff8c9fcfea11dbdd9df3e052626af6ca3569","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアには、（1）呼び出し元の役割に基づき、顧客テーブルのどの列をエージェントが参照してよいか統制すること、（2）セッション間で構造化会話状態を永続保存し、ほかのトランザクションデータとも結合できる、クエリ可能な場所をエージェントへ提供すること、が必要です。\n\nそれぞれの要件に対応するDatabricksコンポーネントの組み合わせはどれですか。","options_ja":["役割に基づく列の統制にUnity Catalog、永続的でクエリ可能な会話状態にLakebase。","役割に基づく列の統制にMLflow 3、永続的でクエリ可能な会話状態にUnity Catalog。","役割に基づく列の統制にMCP、永続的でクエリ可能な会話状態にMLflow 3。","役割に基づく列の統制にLakebase、永続的でクエリ可能な会話状態にMCP。"],"explanation_ja":"Unity Catalogはデータアクセスの統制、Lakebaseは低遅延で読み書きできる永続的なリレーショナル状態の保存を担います。MLflowは追跡・評価、MCPはツール接続のプロトコルです。製品名を丸暗記するより、「誰が見られるか」と「何を持続して覚えるか」を分けて考えます。","distractors_ja":["列へのアクセス統制と、セッションをまたぐ構造化状態の保存を、それぞれ適切なコンポーネントへ割り当てています。","MLflowは実験やトレースを管理しますが、顧客テーブルの役割別列統制を担う製品ではありません。","MCPはツールの公開・呼び出しの仕組みであり列権限の管理層ではなく、MLflowも会話用OLTP状態の主保存先ではありません。","LakebaseとMCPの役割を取り違えています。MCPというプロトコル自体は、永続的なクエリ可能データベースではありません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-025","number":25,"form":4,"stem":"A Genie-based analytics agent frequently fails to answer questions about \"net revenue\" correctly, instead returning raw `gross_revenue` column values. Unity Catalog shows the `gross_revenue` and `net_revenue` columns exist, but neither has a column comment, and no certified SQL expression or metric view defines how \"net revenue\" should be computed from the underlying columns.\n\nWhat is the highest-impact configuration change?","options":["Add descriptive column comments to both columns and define a certified SQL expression, or metric view, for \"net revenue\" in Unity Catalog, giving Genie an authoritative, reusable definition of the business term.","Instruct Genie's end users to always type \"gross revenue minus returns and discounts\" instead of \"net revenue\" when asking questions.","Grant the Genie service principal broader read access to additional finance tables, in case the correct revenue figure exists elsewhere.","Increase the number of sample questions in the Genie space that mention \"revenue,\" without adding any new column metadata."],"correct":0,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"業務用語の共有定義","concept":"ce-f4-q025","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Add descriptive column comments to both columns and define a certified SQL expression, or metric view, for \"net revenue\" in Unity Catalog, giving Genie an authoritative, reusable definition of the business term.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe evidence points to missing business meaning: the columns exist, but their distinctions and calculation rules are undocumented. A directly addresses that gap through descriptive metadata and a reusable metric definition. More permissions or repeated question wording does not establish the correct calculation.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “returning raw `gross_revenue` column values” — Technical signal: Genie is mapping the requested business metric to the wrong data.\n\n- “columns exist” — Technical signal: Missing columns are not the stated problem.\n\n- “neither has a column comment” — Technical signal: Genie lacks descriptive context distinguishing the fields.\n\n- “no certified SQL expression or metric view defines” — Technical signal: The business term lacks an authoritative calculation.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nColumn comments clarify what each field represents, including whether `net_revenue` is already calculated and how it should be aggregated. A validated metric definition supplies the business logic Genie should reuse.\n\nUse the organization’s approved definition and make the resulting expression or metric view available to Genie. Do not assume that returns and discounts must be subtracted again from an already-net value.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Explicit wording may help an individual query, but it shifts responsibility to every user and leaves the shared definition missing. The proposed formula also needs business validation; it is not established by the stem.\n\n- Option C — Different Use Case: Additional access can help when required data is genuinely unavailable. Here, the relevant columns exist and the identified gap is semantic metadata. Broader access does not explain their meaning or calculation.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Sample questions can demonstrate useful ways to ask about data, but merely repeating “revenue” supplies no authoritative calculation. A validated example SQL query would add meaningful logic; that is more than D proposes.\n\nExam Trap\n\nA is the best answer, but its terminology needs qualification. Genie knowledge-store SQL expressions are scoped to the Genie Agent; they are not automatically Unity Catalog objects. Metric views are the appropriate Unity Catalog alternative offered in A. “Certified SQL expression” should not be confused with Genie’s specific trusted assets feature, which covers example SQL queries and SQL functions.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nBusiness analytics requires both descriptive meaning and executable logic. Metadata explains what a field means; a metric definition establishes how to calculate and aggregate it. Current documentation calls Genie Spaces Genie Agents; this naming change does not alter the underlying need for curated business context.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA finance owner validates the net-revenue definition, documents both columns, and publishes a metric view used by Genie. Testing totals across periods and business units helps catch double subtraction or incorrect aggregation before users rely on the answers.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nDefine the metric once, and give Genie both its meaning and its calculation.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen the data exists but Genie confuses business metrics, improve column metadata and supply validated metric logic. More access or more mentions of the term will not resolve an undefined business concept.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Curate an effective Genie Agent:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices)\n\nSupports: Using Unity Catalog column descriptions and structured metric definitions to improve accuracy.\n\n- [Databricks — Tune Genie Agent quality:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality)\n\nSupports: SQL expressions, agent-scoped knowledge-store configuration, trusted assets, and the naming change from Genie Spaces.\n\n- [Databricks — Query metric views:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/uc-semantics/metric-views/query)\n\nSupports: Reusable metric calculations and consumption through Genie Agents.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Add descriptive column comments to both columns and define a certified SQL expression, or metric view, for \"net revenue\" in Unity Catalog, giving Genie an authoritative, reusable definition of the business term.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe evidence points to missing business meaning: the columns exist, but their distinctions and calculation rules are undocumented. A directly addresses that gap through descriptive metadata and a reusable metric definition. More permissions or repeated question wording does not establish the correct calculation.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “returning raw `gross_revenue` column values” — Technical signal: Genie is mapping the requested business metric to the wrong data.\n\n- “columns exist” — Technical signal: Missing columns are not the stated problem.\n\n- “neither has a column comment” — Technical signal: Genie lacks descriptive context distinguishing the fields.\n\n- “no certified SQL expression or metric view defines” — Technical signal: The business term lacks an authoritative calculation.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nColumn comments clarify what each field represents, including whether `net_revenue` is already calculated and how it should be aggregated. A validated metric definition supplies the business logic Genie should reuse.\n\nUse the organization’s approved definition and make the resulting expression or metric view available to Genie. Do not assume that returns and discounts must be subtracted again from an already-net value.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Explicit wording may help an individual query, but it shifts responsibility to every user and leaves the shared definition missing. The proposed formula also needs business validation; it is not established by the stem.","Option C — Different Use Case: Additional access can help when required data is genuinely unavailable. Here, the relevant columns exist and the identified gap is semantic metadata. Broader access does not explain their meaning or calculation.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Sample questions can demonstrate useful ways to ask about data, but merely repeating “revenue” supplies no authoritative calculation. A validated example SQL query would add meaningful logic; that is more than D proposes."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/uc-semantics/metric-views/query"],"source_hash":"7cbf235e11ca420ee38e8fb6d4cb0158d85d959a45f6918d72521f70a3de69c8","stem_ja":"Genieを使う分析エージェントが「純売上高」の質問へ正しく答えられず、生の`gross_revenue`列の値を返すことが頻繁にあります。Unity Catalogには`gross_revenue`と`net_revenue`の両列がありますが、どちらにも列コメントがなく、元の列から「純売上高」をどう計算するかを定義する認定済みSQL式やメトリクスビューもありません。\n\n最も効果の大きい設定変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["両列に説明的なコメントを追加し、Unity Catalogで「純売上高」の認定済みSQL式またはメトリクスビューを定義して、業務用語の正当で再利用可能な定義をGenieへ与えます。","Genieの利用者へ、質問では「純売上高」でなく必ず「総売上高から返品と値引きを引いたもの」と入力するよう指示します。","正しい売上高がほかにある場合に備え、追加の財務テーブルへの広い読み取り権限をGenieのサービスプリンシパルへ与えます。","新しい列メタデータは加えず、Genie Spaceで「売上高」に言及するサンプル質問を増やします。"],"explanation_ja":"業務用語とデータの意味の対応が不足しています。コメントで列を区別し、承認された計算式やメトリクスビューで純売上高の定義を固定すると、Genieが一貫した根拠を再利用できます。アクセス範囲や曖昧な質問例を増やしても、欠けた意味定義は埋まりません。","distractors_ja":["列の意味と計算の共通定義を同時に補い、誤った列の選択と用語解釈の揺れへ直接対処できます。","利用者へ言い換えを強制する方法では共有の業務定義が残らず、別の表現で再び同じ誤りが起きます。","必要な列はすでに存在しています。意味の問題を権限拡大で直すことはできず、無関係な候補が増えます。","「売上高」という語の例を増やすだけでは、総額と純額の意味や計算方法の違いを明確にできません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-026","number":26,"form":4,"stem":"A context engineer is building a Genie space for a retail operations team. Available Unity Catalog assets include: a wide raw ingestion table with 200 undocumented columns updated hourly; a curated, documented `store_daily_sales` view with business-friendly column names; a parameterized query `top_products_by_region(region, start_date, end_date)`; and a one-off SQL script, written by a single analyst eight months ago, containing hard-coded warehouse IDs that are no longer in use.\n\nWhich combination of assets should the engineer curate into the Genie space?","options":["The raw ingestion table and the curated view, since more columns give Genie more data to draw on when answering questions.","The parameterized query and the stale hard-coded SQL script, since both were written to answer specific business questions.","All four assets, so that Genie has the maximum possible coverage of the retail operations domain.","The curated `store_daily_sales` view and the `top_products_by_region` parameterized query, since both are documented, business-friendly, and current."],"correct":3,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"Genieへ渡すアセットのキュレーション","concept":"ce-f4-q026","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. The curated `store_daily_sales` view and the `top_products_by_region` parameterized query, since both are documented, business-friendly, and current.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nSelect assets that provide understandable data and reusable business logic. The curated view supplies clear sales context; the parameterized query supports a relevant analytical task across regions and dates. The undocumented raw table adds ambiguity, while the stale script contains known-invalid references.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “200 undocumented columns” — Technical signal: Broad coverage comes without the metadata needed for reliable interpretation.\n\n- “curated, documented … business-friendly column names” — Technical signal: The view is explicitly prepared for business consumption.\n\n- “parameterized query” — Technical signal: Region and date inputs support reuse instead of fixed assumptions.\n\n- “warehouse IDs that are no longer in use” — Exclusion: The script requires correction before use.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe selected assets complement each other:\n\n- `store_daily_sales` provides a documented data source for sales questions.\n\n- `top_products_by_region` provides a reusable query pattern whose inputs can vary with the user’s request.\n\nAdd the view as a data source and curate the query as a parameterized example, validating its SQL, parameter meanings, and dependencies first. Parameterization enables reuse; it does not itself establish correctness.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Raw data may contain useful details, but more columns do not automatically improve answers. This combination introduces undocumented fields and omits the reusable query. Raw data would become relevant if required questions needed details unavailable in the curated assets, after appropriate documentation and validation.\n\n- Option B — Outdated: The parameterized query is useful, but the script’s obsolete IDs make it unsuitable as supplied. Having once answered a business question does not establish current validity. It could be reconsidered after repair and testing.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Including everything maximizes exposure, but also introduces ambiguity and stale logic. Domain coverage should expand through validated assets that meet identified needs, rather than indiscriminate inclusion.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest trap is equating more data with better context. Hourly updates establish refresh frequency, not semantic clarity or suitability.\n\nD also slightly overstates the evidence: the stem explicitly documents the view, but does not establish that the parameterized query is documented or current. D remains the best selection, with query validation required before production use.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nCuration selects relevant, understandable, validated assets. A data source supplies facts; an example query supplies a pattern for using those facts. Current documentation calls Genie Spaces Genie Agents; the same curation principles apply.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA retail manager can explore daily store performance through the view and request top products for a selected region and period through the parameterized query. Testing representative regions and date boundaries helps prevent incorrect results while keeping obsolete operational assumptions out of the agent’s context.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nCurate clear data and reusable logic; validate both before use.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nPrefer documented business views and validated parameterized queries. Add raw or legacy assets only when a demonstrated need justifies them and their quality issues have been resolved.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Curate an effective Genie Agent:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices)\n\nSupports: Focused datasets, documented columns, and relevant example SQL instead of unnecessary data exposure.\n\n- [Databricks — Tune Genie Agent quality:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality)\n\nSupports: Parameterized example queries, parameter documentation, trusted assets, and the Genie Spaces naming change.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Raw data may contain useful details, but more columns do not automatically improve answers. This combination introduces undocumented fields and omits the reusable query. Raw data would become relevant if required questions needed details unavailable in the curated assets, after appropriate documentation and validation.","Option B — Outdated: The parameterized query is useful, but the script’s obsolete IDs make it unsuitable as supplied. Having once answered a business question does not establish current validity. It could be reconsidered after repair and testing.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Including everything maximizes exposure, but also introduces ambiguity and stale logic. Domain coverage should expand through validated assets that meet identified needs, rather than indiscriminate inclusion.","Correct Answer\n\nD. The curated `store_daily_sales` view and the `top_products_by_region` parameterized query, since both are documented, business-friendly, and current.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nSelect assets that provide understandable data and reusable business logic. The curated view supplies clear sales context; the parameterized query supports a relevant analytical task across regions and dates. The undocumented raw table adds ambiguity, while the stale script contains known-invalid references.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “200 undocumented columns” — Technical signal: Broad coverage comes without the metadata needed for reliable interpretation.\n\n- “curated, documented … business-friendly column names” — Technical signal: The view is explicitly prepared for business consumption.\n\n- “parameterized query” — Technical signal: Region and date inputs support reuse instead of fixed assumptions.\n\n- “warehouse IDs that are no longer in use” — Exclusion: The script requires correction before use.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe selected assets complement each other:\n\n- `store_daily_sales` provides a documented data source for sales questions.\n\n- `top_products_by_region` provides a reusable query pattern whose inputs can vary with the user’s request.\n\nAdd the view as a data source and curate the query as a parameterized example, validating its SQL, parameter meanings, and dependencies first. Parameterization enables reuse; it does not itself establish correctness."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/tune-quality"],"source_hash":"4b60d0d5de254015f52262100f58f8386dad860afc4a31895d373b5937ad1716","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが小売運営チーム向けのGenie Spaceを構築しています。Unity Catalogの利用可能アセットは、説明のない200列を持ち毎時更新される幅広い生取り込みテーブル、業務で理解しやすい列名を持つ整理・文書化済みの`store_daily_sales`ビュー、パラメータ付きクエリ`top_products_by_region(region, start_date, end_date)`、1人のアナリストが8か月前に書き、現在は使われていない倉庫IDをハードコードした一度限りのSQLスクリプトです。\n\nどの組み合わせをGenie Spaceへ選んで登録すべきですか。","options_ja":["生取り込みテーブルと整理済みビュー。列を増やせば、Genieが質問に答えるためのデータが増えるためです。","パラメータ付きクエリと古いハードコードのSQLスクリプト。どちらも特定の業務質問へ答えるために作られたためです。","4つすべて。小売運営領域の網羅性を最大にするためです。","整理済みの`store_daily_sales`ビューと`top_products_by_region`パラメータ付きクエリ。どちらも文書化され、業務で扱いやすく、現行のためです。"],"explanation_ja":"Genieへ渡すのは量よりも、意味が明確で現在使える情報です。整理済みビューとパラメータ付きクエリは業務の意図に沿い、説明不足の生データや失効した固定条件を避けられます。キュレーションとは、すべて見せることではなく、答えに使うべきアセットを選ぶことです。","distractors_ja":["生テーブルの説明のない200列を足すと、誤った列選択や意味の混乱が増える可能性があります。","業務目的で作られていても、失効した倉庫IDを固定したスクリプトは現在の質問に適しません。","網羅性を理由に不明瞭な生データや古いSQLも渡すと、正当な情報源の選択を難しくします。","業務上の意味が明確で、現在の条件へ適応できるビューとパラメータ付きクエリを選べます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-027","number":27,"form":4,"stem":"A context engineer needs to design a RAG pipeline for an internal policy-document assistant. The documents live as files in a Unity Catalog volume, access to which is restricted by Unity Catalog permissions per department. The pipeline must ensure that: (1) a user only ever sees retrieved content from documents their Unity Catalog permissions allow, and (2) the retrieved chunks actually injected into the agent's context are chunks, not entire source documents.\n\nWhich pipeline design satisfies both requirements?","options":["Index the full text of every document regardless of department, and add a system-prompt instruction telling the agent not to disclose content from departments the user does not have access to.","Chunk and index the Unity Catalog-governed documents into a Databricks AI Search index that enforces the caller's Unity Catalog permissions at query time, and inject only the matching chunks, not full documents, into the agent's context.","Chunk and index the documents without any permission awareness, then filter the final agent response after generation to remove any sentences that originated from restricted documents.","Skip indexing entirely and inject full source documents directly into context for every query, relying on Unity Catalog's volume-level permissions to prevent the agent from reading unauthorized files at inference time."],"correct":1,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"検索時の認可とチャンク単位注入","concept":"ce-f4-q027","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Chunk and index the Unity Catalog-governed documents into a Databricks AI Search index that enforces the caller's Unity Catalog permissions at query time, and inject only the matching chunks, not full documents, into the agent's context.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nB is the intended answer because it combines authorization during retrieval with chunk-only context injection. However, it is correct only if caller-aware authorization is explicitly implemented; source-volume permissions do not automatically become document-level permissions inside a shared AI Search index.\n\nA and C rely on controlling generated output after unrestricted retrieval. D explicitly injects full documents, violating the second requirement.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “their Unity Catalog permissions allow” — Hard constraint: Authorization must reflect the actual caller.\n\n- “restricted … per department” — Technical signal: Retrieval must respect departmental access boundaries.\n\n- “chunks, not entire source documents” — Hard constraint: Chunking must carry through to context assembly.\n\n- “both requirements” — Selection instruction: Neither access control nor chunking alone is sufficient.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAn implementation of B must:\n\n- Split documents into chunks while retaining source identifiers and authorization metadata.\n\n- Authenticate the caller and determine their current permitted sources.\n\n- Enforce those permissions through trusted retrieval logic, such as mandatory ACL filters or appropriately permissioned separate indexes.\n\n- Inject only authorized matching chunk text and relevant source metadata.\n\nCurrent Databricks documentation states that AI Search does not support native row- and column-level permissions, but application-level ACLs can be implemented using its filter API. Index access and source-document access must therefore be distinguished.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Violates Requirement: A prompt instruction is not an authorization boundary. It asks the model to suppress restricted information after making that information available, and it does not establish chunk-only injection.\n\n- Option C — Violates Requirement: Post-generation filtering attempts to remove restricted information too late. Restricted content can already influence the answer, and paraphrasing or combining information makes sentence-level removal unreliable.\n\n- Option D — Violates Requirement: Caller-scoped volume reads can enforce file access, but this option injects entire documents. It therefore fails the explicit chunk-only requirement even if authentication is implemented correctly.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDo not equate a Unity Catalog-governed index with automatic inheritance of every source document’s permissions. AI Search checks privileges on the index; fine-grained access within a shared index requires additional enforcement.\n\nAlso, volume privileges apply at the volume level. If departments need different file-access boundaries, placing their files in different folders of one volume does not itself establish those boundaries.\n\nIf B means automatic source-permission propagation with no additional implementation, none of the options is fully correct as written.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nAuthorization and relevance are independent filters: a chunk must be both permitted and useful before entering the model’s context. Chunking controls information granularity; it does not create access control. Copying governed content into an index requires preserving its access restrictions explicitly.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA policy assistant can store source-volume identifiers alongside chunks and use a backend to derive mandatory search filters from the authenticated user’s current access. Users must not be able to bypass that backend and query the shared index unrestricted. Permission changes must also be reflected before subsequent retrievals.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nAuthorize the source, retrieve the chunk, then build the context.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose permission-aware retrieval followed by chunk-only injection. For implementation, verify that source permissions are explicitly enforced rather than assuming an index inherits them automatically.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Databricks AI Search:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search)\n\nSupports: Lack of native row- and column-level permissions and application-level ACLs through filtering.\n\n- [Databricks — Query an AI Search index:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search)\n\nSupports: Index-level Unity Catalog privileges, filtered retrieval, and selecting returned columns.\n\n- [Databricks — Privileges for Unity Catalog volumes:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/volumes/privileges)\n\nSupports: Volume-level file access and the privileges required to read files.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Violates Requirement: A prompt instruction is not an authorization boundary. It asks the model to suppress restricted information after making that information available, and it does not establish chunk-only injection.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Chunk and index the Unity Catalog-governed documents into a Databricks AI Search index that enforces the caller's Unity Catalog permissions at query time, and inject only the matching chunks, not full documents, into the agent's context.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nB is the intended answer because it combines authorization during retrieval with chunk-only context injection. However, it is correct only if caller-aware authorization is explicitly implemented; source-volume permissions do not automatically become document-level permissions inside a shared AI Search index.\n\nA and C rely on controlling generated output after unrestricted retrieval. D explicitly injects full documents, violating the second requirement.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “their Unity Catalog permissions allow” — Hard constraint: Authorization must reflect the actual caller.\n\n- “restricted … per department” — Technical signal: Retrieval must respect departmental access boundaries.\n\n- “chunks, not entire source documents” — Hard constraint: Chunking must carry through to context assembly.\n\n- “both requirements” — Selection instruction: Neither access control nor chunking alone is sufficient.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAn implementation of B must:\n\n- Split documents into chunks while retaining source identifiers and authorization metadata.\n\n- Authenticate the caller and determine their current permitted sources.\n\n- Enforce those permissions through trusted retrieval logic, such as mandatory ACL filters or appropriately permissioned separate indexes.\n\n- Inject only authorized matching chunk text and relevant source metadata.\n\nCurrent Databricks documentation states that AI Search does not support native row- and column-level permissions, but application-level ACLs can be implemented using its filter API. Index access and source-document access must therefore be distinguished.","Option C — Violates Requirement: Post-generation filtering attempts to remove restricted information too late. Restricted content can already influence the answer, and paraphrasing or combining information makes sentence-level removal unreliable.","Option D — Violates Requirement: Caller-scoped volume reads can enforce file access, but this option injects entire documents. It therefore fails the explicit chunk-only requirement even if authentication is implemented correctly."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/query-ai-search","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/volumes/privileges"],"source_hash":"4ed90bfa4252baa8527f65d0aa5583b3aa6e44339356babc7ca5be02cd36423e","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが社内ポリシー文書アシスタントのRAGパイプラインを設計する必要があります。文書はUnity Catalog Volume内のファイルとして保存され、アクセスは部門ごとのUnity Catalog権限で制限されています。パイプラインは、（1）利用者が自分のUnity Catalog権限で許可された文書由来の内容だけを見ること、（2）エージェントのコンテキストへ実際に注入される取得結果が、元文書全体ではなくチャンクであること、を保証しなければなりません。\n\n両方の要件を満たす設計はどれですか。","options_ja":["部門によらずすべての文書全文を索引化し、利用者にアクセス権のない部門の情報を開示しないよう、システムプロンプトに指示します。","Unity Catalogで統制された文書をチャンク化して、クエリ時に呼び出し元のUnity Catalog権限を強制するDatabricks AI Search索引へ登録し、文書全体でなく一致したチャンクだけをコンテキストへ注入します。","権限を考慮せず文書をチャンク化・索引化し、生成後の最終回答から制限文書由来の文を除去します。","索引化を省き、各クエリで元文書全体をコンテキストへ直接入れます。推論時の未許可ファイルの読み取りはUnity CatalogのVolume単位の権限に防がせます。"],"explanation_ja":"原問の意図は、検索時の認可と一致チャンクだけの注入を組み合わせる設計です。ただしAI Search索引が元文書の権限を自動継承するわけではありません。認証済み利用者に基づく必須ACLフィルタや索引分離など、明示的な認可実装が前提です。プロンプトや生成後フィルタへアクセス制御を任せません。\n\n補足：正解キーBを保持。ただし原文の『呼出者のUC権限をクエリ時に強制する索引』は必須ACLフィルタや索引分離等を実装した場合の条件付き。AI Searchの索引権限と元Volume/文書権限の自動継承は異なる。原解説にも同じ注記あり。公式：https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search 、https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/volumes/privileges","distractors_ja":["制限情報を取得した後にモデルへ隠すよう頼んでも認可境界にはならず、未許可情報が回答へ影響し得ます。","呼び出し元の認可を明示的に実装することが条件です。索引アクセス権だけで元文書の細粒度権限が自動反映されるとは考えません。","制限情報を取得・利用した後の出力除去は遅すぎます。言い換えや情報の結合による漏えいも防ぎきれません。","ファイルの読み取り権限を守れても、文書全体を注入するため、チャンクだけを渡すという明示要件に反します。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-028","number":28,"form":4,"stem":"A context engineer is designing two separate agents. Agent A answers general product-FAQ questions from a mostly-static knowledge base that changes a few times per year. Agent B answers questions about a customer's current account balance and recent transactions, which change continuously throughout the day.\n\nWhich retrieval strategy should each agent use, respectively?","options":["Agent A should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval against a periodically-updated index; Agent B should use just-in-time agentic retrieval that queries the live account and transaction data via tool calls at inference time.","Agent A should use just-in-time agentic retrieval so its answers always reflect the latest product updates; Agent B should use pre-inference retrieval so account balances are embedded in advance for faster responses.","Both agents should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval, since embedding-based search is always faster than live tool calls regardless of how frequently the underlying data changes.","Both agents should use just-in-time agentic retrieval, since dynamically querying the source system at inference time is always more accurate than any indexed approach."],"correct":0,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"情報更新頻度に合わせる検索方式","concept":"ce-f4-q028","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Agent A should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval against a periodically-updated index; Agent B should use just-in-time agentic retrieval that queries the live account and transaction data via tool calls at inference time.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nMatch retrieval to the information’s structure and freshness requirements. Agent A needs relevant passages from relatively stable text. Agent B needs exact, current customer records. A provides semantic retrieval for the FAQ and live, targeted queries for account data.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “general product-FAQ questions” — Technical signal: Semantic matching can connect varied wording to relevant knowledge-base passages.\n\n- “changes a few times per year” — Technical signal: An index refreshed when content changes is a reasonable fit.\n\n- “current account balance and recent transactions” — Hard constraint: Answers require customer-specific, up-to-date records.\n\n- “change continuously throughout the day” — Technical signal: Previously indexed account values may become stale.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nFor Agent A, retrieve relevant indexed passages before the answer-generation call. Maintain freshness by updating the index when product content changes.\n\nFor Agent B, call an authorized account API or structured query tool when the information is needed. Query by the authenticated customer’s identity and return the relevant balance and transactions. Freshness still depends on the source’s update and consistency guarantees.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Agentic retrieval can serve FAQs, but embedding account balances in advance introduces avoidable freshness risk. The pairing prioritizes live access for the less dynamic source while weakening the current-data requirement.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: Embedding-based retrieval is not always faster than live queries. Performance depends on the implementation, and speed alone does not satisfy Agent B’s freshness requirement.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Both agents could use tool-driven retrieval, but dynamic querying is not universally more accurate. Retrieval quality also depends on source correctness and query execution. Agent A’s straightforward task does not establish a need for additional agentic orchestration.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDo not confuse precomputed embeddings with answers computed in advance: Agent A still searches the index for each question.\n\nAlso, retrieval timing and source freshness are separate design choices. An agentic tool can query an index, and a deterministic pre-inference step can query a live database. A is the best pairing offered, not a rule that all live data requires agentic retrieval.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nChoose retrieval using three questions: What information is needed, how fresh must it be, and how precisely must it be selected? Stable narrative knowledge often suits semantic search; changing numerical records usually require exact queries against an authoritative source.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA support application can retrieve warranty explanations from an indexed knowledge base while querying a customer’s latest balance through an account tool. This preserves reusable FAQ retrieval while avoiding stale balance snapshots; the account path must handle source failures without presenting old values as current.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nSearch stable knowledge; query current state.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nMostly static FAQs favor indexed retrieval with appropriate refreshes. Continuously changing account records favor live, authorized queries—without assuming either approach is universally faster or more accurate.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Databricks AI Search:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search)\n\nSupports: Embedding-based retrieval and index synchronization with changing source data.\n\n- [Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents:](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agent)\n\nSupports: Pre-inference retrieval, just-in-time tool-based retrieval, and their trade-offs.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Agent A should use pre-inference, embedding-based retrieval against a periodically-updated index; Agent B should use just-in-time agentic retrieval that queries the live account and transaction data via tool calls at inference time.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nMatch retrieval to the information’s structure and freshness requirements. Agent A needs relevant passages from relatively stable text. Agent B needs exact, current customer records. A provides semantic retrieval for the FAQ and live, targeted queries for account data.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “general product-FAQ questions” — Technical signal: Semantic matching can connect varied wording to relevant knowledge-base passages.\n\n- “changes a few times per year” — Technical signal: An index refreshed when content changes is a reasonable fit.\n\n- “current account balance and recent transactions” — Hard constraint: Answers require customer-specific, up-to-date records.\n\n- “change continuously throughout the day” — Technical signal: Previously indexed account values may become stale.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nFor Agent A, retrieve relevant indexed passages before the answer-generation call. Maintain freshness by updating the index when product content changes.\n\nFor Agent B, call an authorized account API or structured query tool when the information is needed. Query by the authenticated customer’s identity and return the relevant balance and transactions. Freshness still depends on the source’s update and consistency guarantees.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: Agentic retrieval can serve FAQs, but embedding account balances in advance introduces avoidable freshness risk. The pairing prioritizes live access for the less dynamic source while weakening the current-data requirement.","Option C — Incorrect: Embedding-based retrieval is not always faster than live queries. Performance depends on the implementation, and speed alone does not satisfy Agent B’s freshness requirement.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Both agents could use tool-driven retrieval, but dynamic querying is not universally more accurate. Retrieval quality also depends on source correctness and query execution. Agent A’s straightforward task does not establish a need for additional agentic orchestration."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/ai-search/ai-search","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agent"],"source_hash":"98faf34e97560f8393a7ea68a8012f73ab3f99e894c274c8777586965e48813a","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが別々の2つのエージェントを設計しています。エージェントAは年に数回だけ変更される、ほぼ静的なナレッジベースから一般的な製品FAQへ回答します。エージェントBは、日中継続的に変化する顧客の現在残高と直近取引について回答します。\n\nそれぞれにどの検索戦略を使うべきですか。","options_ja":["Aは定期更新された索引に対する推論前の埋め込み検索、Bは推論時にツールを呼び出してライブの口座・取引データを問い合わせる必要時のエージェント検索を使います。","Aは常に最新の製品更新を反映するよう必要時のエージェント検索、Bは応答を高速化するため残高を事前に埋め込む推論前検索を使います。","両方とも推論前の埋め込み検索を使います。元データの変更頻度に関係なく、埋め込み検索はライブツール呼び出しより常に高速なためです。","両方とも必要時のエージェント検索を使います。推論時に元システムへ動的に問い合わせる方が、どんな索引方式よりも常に正確なためです。"],"explanation_ja":"ほぼ静的なFAQは、定期更新の埋め込み索引で十分な鮮度を得やすくなります。現在残高のように常時変わる値は、回答時に元システムへ問い合わせます。検索方式の優劣を一律に決めるのではなく、必要な鮮度、遅延、費用を情報の性質に合わせます。","distractors_ja":["変更の少ない知識には索引検索、現在値にはライブ取得と使い分け、鮮度と実行負担を適切に合わせています。","更新の少ないFAQと現在残高への戦略が逆です。残高の事前埋め込みは回答時点の値から遅れるおそれがあります。","埋め込み検索が常に高速とは限らず、速くても残高が古ければ要件を満たしません。","ライブ検索がすべての用途で常に優れるとはいえません。安定したFAQでは索引方式も有効で費用対効果を得られます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-029","number":29,"form":4,"stem":"A Databricks agent helps returning customers plan meals across multiple separate chat sessions on different days. In session 1, a user states a permanent, severe shellfish allergy during a recurring meal-planning conversation. In session 2, a new chat on a later day, the user asks for recipe recommendations and the agent suggests a shrimp dish, because session 1's content is gone and the agent has no cross-session memory for this user — the allergy was only captured in that session's transient conversation history, never written to any durable store.\n\nWhich mismatch does this exhibit, and what is the correct fix?","options":["The agent relied on a cross-session memory store for information that only exists in the current session; the fix is to stop checking long-term storage and rely solely on the active conversation window.","The agent's context window is too small to hold the session 1 conversation, so the fix is to upgrade to a model with a larger context window that can retain all prior sessions simultaneously.","The agent relied on transient, session-only history for information that needs to persist across sessions; the fix is to write durable facts like the stated allergy into a cross-session memory store, such as a Lakebase-backed user profile, that every future session retrieves from.","The agent should re-ask the dietary-restriction question at the start of every single session regardless of memory architecture, since allergy information is too sensitive to persist automatically."],"correct":2,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"セッション履歴と利用者単位の長期記憶","concept":"ce-f4-q029","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. The agent relied on transient, session-only history for information that needs to persist across sessions; the fix is to write durable facts like the stated allergy into a cross-session memory store, such as a Lakebase-backed user profile, that every future session retrieves from.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe fact must remain available across separate conversations, but it was stored only in transient history. C corrects both parts of the failure: persist the fact beyond the original session and retrieve it when subsequent sessions need it. Increasing context capacity does not recover information that was never persisted.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “multiple separate chat sessions on different days” — Hard constraint: Continuity must cross session boundaries.\n\n- “permanent, severe shellfish allergy” — Technical signal: This is an enduring constraint relevant to future meal recommendations.\n\n- “only captured in that session's transient conversation history” — Technical signal: The storage lifetime does not match the information’s required lifetime.\n\n- “never written to any durable store” — Technical signal: Persistence is missing, not merely retrieval from an existing memory.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nLakebase provides a managed PostgreSQL backend for persistent agent state and memory. Store the user-stated constraint under a stable user identifier, then retrieve the correct user’s profile before generating later meal recommendations. \n\nWriting alone is insufficient: the application must also load and apply the constraint. For this scenario, a required profile lookup is more dependable than leaving retrieval of the critical fact to an optional semantic search.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: It reverses the mismatch. The agent has no cross-session store containing this fact; relying exclusively on the active window would preserve the failure.\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: A larger context window can accommodate more supplied information, but it does not automatically retain or reconnect separate conversations. It would help with capacity problems within available context, not absent durable storage.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Asking again can recover missing information, but mandatory repetition does not repair cross-session memory. Sensitivity calls for appropriate handling and user controls; it does not establish a universal prohibition on persistence. Reconfirmation is useful when memory is unavailable, uncertain, or being updated.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDo not confuse context capacity, session persistence, and cross-session user memory. Even a durably saved conversation does not automatically become a user profile available to every new chat.\n\nThe phrase “every future session” means consistently retrieving relevant constraints in this recurring meal-planning workflow—not loading every stored personal fact into every unrelated conversation.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nMatch memory scope to information scope. Session state supports one interaction; user-scoped memory preserves facts relevant across interactions. Databricks explicitly separates these stores and describes extracting durable facts from sessions into memory. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA meal-planning application can save the stated allergy in an access-controlled user profile and require that profile to load before recommending recipes. If the lookup fails, it should obtain the missing restrictions rather than interpret unavailable memory as “no restrictions.” This prevents a storage or retrieval failure from silently removing the user’s constraint.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nA fact needed next session must be saved beyond this session and retrieved again.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen an enduring user fact disappears between chats, diagnose a memory-lifetime mismatch. Persist it under the user’s identity and make retrieval part of the future workflow; a larger context window is not the fix.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Agent state and memory:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)\n\nSupports: Lakebase-backed persistence and the distinction between within-session and cross-session memory.\n\n- [Databricks — Agent memory and sessions:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)\n\nSupports: Separate session and memory stores, user-relevant durable facts, and transferring information from sessions into memory.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: It reverses the mismatch. The agent has no cross-session store containing this fact; relying exclusively on the active window would preserve the failure.","Option B — Incorrect: A larger context window can accommodate more supplied information, but it does not automatically retain or reconnect separate conversations. It would help with capacity problems within available context, not absent durable storage.","Correct Answer\n\nC. The agent relied on transient, session-only history for information that needs to persist across sessions; the fix is to write durable facts like the stated allergy into a cross-session memory store, such as a Lakebase-backed user profile, that every future session retrieves from.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe fact must remain available across separate conversations, but it was stored only in transient history. C corrects both parts of the failure: persist the fact beyond the original session and retrieve it when subsequent sessions need it. Increasing context capacity does not recover information that was never persisted.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “multiple separate chat sessions on different days” — Hard constraint: Continuity must cross session boundaries.\n\n- “permanent, severe shellfish allergy” — Technical signal: This is an enduring constraint relevant to future meal recommendations.\n\n- “only captured in that session's transient conversation history” — Technical signal: The storage lifetime does not match the information’s required lifetime.\n\n- “never written to any durable store” — Technical signal: Persistence is missing, not merely retrieval from an existing memory.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nLakebase provides a managed PostgreSQL backend for persistent agent state and memory. Store the user-stated constraint under a stable user identifier, then retrieve the correct user’s profile before generating later meal recommendations. \n\nWriting alone is insufficient: the application must also load and apply the constraint. For this scenario, a required profile lookup is more dependable than leaving retrieval of the critical fact to an optional semantic search.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Asking again can recover missing information, but mandatory repetition does not repair cross-session memory. Sensitivity calls for appropriate handling and user controls; it does not establish a universal prohibition on persistence. Reconfirmation is useful when memory is unavailable, uncertain, or being updated."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents"],"source_hash":"bbc422a560597239ff4458f993f343f277dabb71ccd42fcb3ee2d9c8b82831e6","stem_ja":"Databricksエージェントが、日をまたぐ別々のチャットセッションで、再訪顧客の食事計画を支援しています。セッション1で、利用者は繰り返し行う食事計画の会話中に、永続的で重度の甲殻類アレルギーがあると伝えました。後日の新しいチャットであるセッション2では、レシピの提案を求められたエージェントがエビ料理を勧めます。セッション1の内容は消え、利用者のセッション間記憶もないためです。アレルギーはそのセッションの一時的な会話履歴にだけ入り、永続ストアには一度も書かれていませんでした。\n\nどの不一致があり、どう修正すべきですか。","options_ja":["現在のセッションにしかない情報に対してセッション間ストアを参照している不一致です。長期ストレージの参照をやめ、現在の会話ウィンドウだけへ依存します。","セッション1の会話を保持するにはウィンドウが小さすぎる不一致です。過去の全セッションを同時に保持できる大きなコンテキストモデルへ変更します。","セッション間で持続すべき情報を一時的なセッション履歴だけへ依存した不一致です。アレルギーのような持続的事実を、Lakebase利用者プロファイルなど将来の各セッションが取得するセッション間ストアへ書き込みます。","アレルギーは自動保存するには機微性が高すぎるため、記憶設計によらず毎セッション開始時に必ず食事制限を聞き直すべきです。"],"explanation_ja":"次の会話でも必要な事実を、一時的な会話履歴だけに保存したことが原因です。安定した利用者IDに結び付けた長期ストアへ保存し、各セッションで関連事実を取得します。永続保存するだけでなく、次回の回答前に読み込むところまでが解決策です。","distractors_ja":["説明が逆です。今回は長期ストアに事実がなく、一時履歴にしか保存されていないことが問題です。","ウィンドウを大きくしても、そもそも渡されない別セッションの履歴を自動復元することはできません。","情報の必要期間と保存先を合わせ、将来のセッションでも利用者固有の制約を回答へ反映できます。","機微情報の扱いには適切な統制が必要ですが、設問の記憶欠落を毎回の聞き直しだけで解決するのは意図と異なります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-030","number":30,"form":4,"stem":"An agent executes a multi-step data-validation workflow that can take several hours and may be interrupted by infrastructure restarts, deployment rollouts, or the user closing their session mid-task. The workflow must resume exactly where it left off, including which validation steps have completed and their results, even after a full process restart, not just within one continuous model context.\n\nShould this state be held in an in-context scratchpad or a Delta-backed state object?","options":["An in-context scratchpad, because scratchpads are faster to update on every step and the task's long duration makes minimizing write latency the top priority.","A Delta-backed state object, because an in-context scratchpad exists only within a single running context or session and does not survive a process restart or a closed session, while the task explicitly requires resuming after exactly those events.","An in-context scratchpad, because the task is sequential and scratchpads are designed specifically for tracking ordered, multi-step progress within a conversation.","Either approach is equivalent here, since both can represent the same validation-step state in structured form."],"correct":1,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"再起動に耐えるワークフロー状態","concept":"ce-f4-q030","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. A Delta-backed state object, because an in-context scratchpad exists only within a single running context or session and does not survive a process restart or a closed session, while the task explicitly requires resuming after exactly those events.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe decisive requirement is recovery after the running process disappears. A scratchpad can represent progress, but without external persistence it cannot restore that progress after a restart. A Delta-backed state object provides durable storage that a replacement process can read.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “may be interrupted by infrastructure restarts, deployment rollouts” — Hard constraint: State must survive beyond the executing process.\n\n- “which validation steps have completed and their results” — Hard constraint: Recovery requires structured progress and outputs.\n\n- “even after a full process restart” — Technical signal: Durable persistence is essential.\n\n- “not just within one continuous model context” — Exclusion: Context-only tracking is insufficient.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nPersist a workflow identifier, completed-step statuses, results or result references, and the next pending step. After restart, the workflow reloads that record and continues from its committed checkpoint.\n\nDelta Lake provides atomic commits and durable storage of committed changes, making it suitable for this state record. The application must explicitly write checkpoints and implement recovery logic; choosing Delta alone does not create resumability. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Violates Requirement: Fast scratchpad updates may reduce overhead, but write latency is not the overriding requirement. Losing completed work after a restart directly violates the recovery requirement.\n\n- Option C — Violates Requirement: A scratchpad can track ordered steps during an active conversation. Sequential execution does not make that state durable, so it cannot independently support the required recovery.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Equivalent data structures do not imply equivalent persistence. Both approaches can represent completed steps, but only the externally persisted state remains available independently of the running context.\n\nExam Trap\n\n“Resume exactly where it left off” requires more than selecting a durable store. Recovery normally starts from the last successfully committed checkpoint. If a step finishes but its checkpoint is not committed before a crash, recovery may need to repeat or reconcile that step.\n\nUse atomic state updates where applicable and retry-safe operations. A context scratchpad alone does not provide these guarantees; persisting and restoring it would introduce an external persistence mechanism.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nState representation answers what is recorded; durability answers whether it survives failure; recovery logic answers how execution continues. A reliable workflow needs all three.\n\nA scratchpad would suffice if the requirement were only to track progress within an uninterrupted context and restarting the task were acceptable.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nAfter each validation step, store its outcome and completion status under the workflow ID. Following a deployment restart, the new worker reads the checkpoint and skips committed completed steps. Recording the input-data version also helps prevent resumed validation from silently mixing results from different dataset versions.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nProgress that must survive a restart must be committed outside the running context.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nRestart-safe workflow progress requires durable state, so choose the Delta-backed object. Checkpointing and recovery logic determine how precisely execution resumes.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — What are ACID guarantees on Databricks?:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/acid)\n\nSupports: Atomic commits, durability, and the distinction between committed and uncommitted changes.\n\n- [Databricks — Agent memory and sessions:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents)\n\nSupports: Persisting session state so an interaction can survive restarts and be resumed.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Violates Requirement: Fast scratchpad updates may reduce overhead, but write latency is not the overriding requirement. Losing completed work after a restart directly violates the recovery requirement.","Correct Answer\n\nB. A Delta-backed state object, because an in-context scratchpad exists only within a single running context or session and does not survive a process restart or a closed session, while the task explicitly requires resuming after exactly those events.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe decisive requirement is recovery after the running process disappears. A scratchpad can represent progress, but without external persistence it cannot restore that progress after a restart. A Delta-backed state object provides durable storage that a replacement process can read.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “may be interrupted by infrastructure restarts, deployment rollouts” — Hard constraint: State must survive beyond the executing process.\n\n- “which validation steps have completed and their results” — Hard constraint: Recovery requires structured progress and outputs.\n\n- “even after a full process restart” — Technical signal: Durable persistence is essential.\n\n- “not just within one continuous model context” — Exclusion: Context-only tracking is insufficient.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nPersist a workflow identifier, completed-step statuses, results or result references, and the next pending step. After restart, the workflow reloads that record and continues from its committed checkpoint.\n\nDelta Lake provides atomic commits and durable storage of committed changes, making it suitable for this state record. The application must explicitly write checkpoints and implement recovery logic; choosing Delta alone does not create resumability.","Option C — Violates Requirement: A scratchpad can track ordered steps during an active conversation. Sequential execution does not make that state durable, so it cannot independently support the required recovery.","Option D — Incorrect: Equivalent data structures do not imply equivalent persistence. Both approaches can represent completed steps, but only the externally persisted state remains available independently of the running context."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/lakehouse/acid","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents"],"source_hash":"5ed8071d5038b18b2b9f088a753a8a34f7cb9454c59386de84d7895cded9eb2c","stem_ja":"エージェントが、数時間かかる場合のある多段階データ検証ワークフローを実行しています。基盤の再起動、デプロイの切り替え、利用者による作業途中のセッション終了で中断される可能性があります。ワークフローは、単一の継続中モデルコンテキスト内だけでなく、プロセスを完全に再起動した後でも、どの検証が完了したかとその結果を含め、中断箇所から正確に再開する必要があります。\n\n状態はコンテキスト内のスクラッチパッドと、Deltaを基盤にした状態オブジェクトのどちらに持たせるべきですか。","options_ja":["コンテキスト内スクラッチパッド。各ステップで高速に更新でき、長時間のタスクでは書き込み遅延の最小化が最優先だからです。","Deltaを基盤にした状態オブジェクト。コンテキスト内スクラッチパッドは単一の実行中コンテキストまたはセッションにしか存在せず、設問が明示的に耐えることを求めるプロセス再起動やセッション終了をまたいで残らないためです。","コンテキスト内スクラッチパッド。タスクが逐次処理で、スクラッチパッドは会話内の順序付き多段進捗の追跡専用に設計されているためです。","どちらも同等。どちらも同じ検証ステップの状態を構造化形式で表現できるためです。"],"explanation_ja":"必要なのは状態を表現できることだけでなく、実行プロセスが消えても残ることです。完了ステップと結果を外部のDelta状態へ永続化すれば再開できます。スクラッチパッドは実行中の作業メモには適しても、それだけでは障害後の回復要件を満たしません。","distractors_ja":["速さよりも再起動後の再開が明示要件です。一時的な文脈だけでは、プロセス消失時に進捗も失われます。","完了ステップと結果を実行外へ永続化でき、プロセス再起動やセッション終了にまたがる再開を支えられます。","逐次の進捗を記述できても、保存先が一時的であれば再起動に耐えるわけではありません。","同じ構造を表現できても、耐久性と障害後の復元性が異なります。この違いが設問の中心です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-031","number":31,"form":4,"stem":"An agent architecture persists user preference profiles in Lakebase. The team is deciding between (a) loading each user's full preference profile into the system prompt once at session start, or (b) having the agent issue a live query against Lakebase for only the specific preference fields relevant to the current turn. Preference profiles are small, under 200 tokens, and rarely change mid-session, but the agent handles a high volume of short, single-turn interactions where added latency directly hurts user experience.\n\nGiven these characteristics, which approach is the better fit, and why?","options":["Dynamic retrieval on every turn, because querying Lakebase live always produces more accurate results than loading data once at session start.","Dynamic retrieval on every turn, because single-turn interactions have no prior context to build on, so each turn must independently re-fetch all available user data regardless of size or volatility.","Static retrieval at session start, because Lakebase cannot support live queries during an active agent session.","Static retrieval at session start, because the small, rarely-changing profile poses little staleness risk, while the high-volume, single-turn, latency-sensitive workload makes a per-turn live query to Lakebase an unnecessary added cost."],"correct":3,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"小さく安定したプロファイルの事前読み込み","concept":"ce-f4-q031","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Static retrieval at session start, because the small, rarely-changing profile poses little staleness risk, while the high-volume, single-turn, latency-sensitive workload makes a per-turn live query to Lakebase an unnecessary added cost.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe profile is small enough to load with little context overhead and stable enough to reuse during the session. Loading it before generation avoids making the agent decide to fetch individual fields through an additional tool interaction. D is the best fit, although its latency advantage depends on implementation.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “under 200 tokens” — Technical signal: Loading the complete profile adds little context.\n\n- “rarely change mid-session” — Technical signal: Refreshing fields during the interaction offers limited freshness benefit.\n\n- “short, single-turn interactions” — Technical signal: Additional agent-tool orchestration can be disproportionate to the task.\n\n- “added latency directly hurts user experience” — Hard constraint: Avoid unnecessary steps on the response path.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nLoad the authenticated user’s profile during request or session initialization, then provide it to the model before it generates an answer. This makes the relevant preferences immediately available without an agent-initiated retrieval cycle.\n\nThe architectural inference is that a small, stable profile favors upfront loading over runtime exploration. Official context-engineering guidance recognizes this trade-off and supports combining upfront context with dynamic retrieval when needed. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: Live retrieval can improve freshness, but it does not always improve accuracy. For stable preferences, it may return the same information while adding orchestration overhead. It becomes more valuable when changes during the session must be reflected immediately.\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: A new interaction needs relevant context, but it does not require an agent-driven query or retrieval of all available user data. Initialization can supply the small profile directly.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: Its retrieval choice is reasonable, but its explanation is false. Lakebase is a managed PostgreSQL backend for agent state and memory; it is not restricted to access before a session starts.\n\nExam Trap\n\nFor a truly single-turn session, both approaches may perform one database read. Static loading does not automatically reduce query count or eliminate database latency.\n\nD’s strongest justification is avoiding an additional model-driven tool-call cycle, not amortizing one read across many turns. If both implementations perform equally efficient reads before the same generation call, a latency advantage is not established by the stem alone.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nCompare context size, freshness requirements, and retrieval overhead together. “Static retrieval” here means loading a session snapshot from durable storage—not permanently freezing the profile. Large profiles or rapidly changing preferences can justify selective dynamic retrieval instead.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA short-answer assistant can load language, units, and response-style preferences alongside authentication, then generate its answer in one model call. Measure end-to-end latency and ensure that user-requested preference changes update the stored profile and any active snapshot.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nSmall and stable favors preload; large or changing favors selective retrieval.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose D for this small, stable, latency-sensitive profile. Treat the expected benefit as reduced orchestration overhead, and verify it rather than assuming fewer database reads.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Databricks — Agent state and memory:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management)\n\nSupports: Lakebase as persistent PostgreSQL storage for agent state and cross-session information.\n\n- [Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents:](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\nSupports: Upfront versus just-in-time retrieval trade-offs and hybrid approaches.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: Live retrieval can improve freshness, but it does not always improve accuracy. For stable preferences, it may return the same information while adding orchestration overhead. It becomes more valuable when changes during the session must be reflected immediately.","Option B — Incorrect: A new interaction needs relevant context, but it does not require an agent-driven query or retrieval of all available user data. Initialization can supply the small profile directly.","Option C — Incorrect: Its retrieval choice is reasonable, but its explanation is false. Lakebase is a managed PostgreSQL backend for agent state and memory; it is not restricted to access before a session starts.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Static retrieval at session start, because the small, rarely-changing profile poses little staleness risk, while the high-volume, single-turn, latency-sensitive workload makes a per-turn live query to Lakebase an unnecessary added cost.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe profile is small enough to load with little context overhead and stable enough to reuse during the session. Loading it before generation avoids making the agent decide to fetch individual fields through an additional tool interaction. D is the best fit, although its latency advantage depends on implementation.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “under 200 tokens” — Technical signal: Loading the complete profile adds little context.\n\n- “rarely change mid-session” — Technical signal: Refreshing fields during the interaction offers limited freshness benefit.\n\n- “short, single-turn interactions” — Technical signal: Additional agent-tool orchestration can be disproportionate to the task.\n\n- “added latency directly hurts user experience” — Hard constraint: Avoid unnecessary steps on the response path.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nLoad the authenticated user’s profile during request or session initialization, then provide it to the model before it generates an answer. This makes the relevant preferences immediately available without an agent-initiated retrieval cycle.\n\nThe architectural inference is that a small, stable profile favors upfront loading over runtime exploration. Official context-engineering guidance recognizes this trade-off and supports combining upfront context with dynamic retrieval when needed."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/oltp/projects/state-management","https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"8a2fb7dd5cc487ccc0ba6eac68e8495eae38676356a2202042a67d0c70050375","stem_ja":"エージェント設計では、利用者の好みのプロファイルをLakebaseへ永続保存しています。チームは、（a）セッション開始時に各利用者の完全なプロファイルを1回だけシステムプロンプトへ読み込む方法と、（b）毎ターン、現在のやり取りに関係する特定の好みの項目だけをエージェントがLakebaseへライブ問い合わせする方法、を比較しています。プロファイルは200トークン未満と小さく、セッション中の変更も稀です。一方、エージェントは短い1ターンのやり取りを大量に処理し、追加の遅延が利用者体験へ直接悪影響を及ぼします。\n\nこの特徴を踏まえ、どちらが適し、理由は何ですか。","options_ja":["毎ターンの動的取得。Lakebaseへのライブ問い合わせは、セッション開始時の1回の読み込みより常に正確なためです。","毎ターンの動的取得。1ターンのやり取りには積み上げる過去コンテキストがないため、各ターンでデータ量や変更頻度によらず全利用者データを独立に取り直す必要があるためです。","セッション開始時の静的取得。Lakebaseは実行中のエージェントセッション内のライブ問い合わせを支援できないためです。","セッション開始時の静的取得。小さく変更が稀なプロファイルは陳腐化の危険が小さく、大量・1ターン・低遅延重視の処理で毎ターンのライブ問い合わせは不要な追加負担になるためです。"],"explanation_ja":"動的取得が常に最適ではありません。200トークン未満で変化の少ない情報なら、開始時にまとめて読み込んでも負担と鮮度リスクは小さくなります。今回は短い会話を大量処理し遅延が重要なので、毎ターンの追加ツール往復を避ける静的取得が適します。","distractors_ja":["最新値を得る利点はありますが、変更の少ない情報では毎ターンの問い合わせが常に有利とはいえません。","1ターン会話であることは、サイズや変更頻度を無視して毎回すべて取り直す理由にはなりません。","静的取得という選択は適切でも、理由が誤りです。Lakebaseはセッション中でもライブ問い合わせできます。","小さいプロファイルの事前読み込みで必要な情報を低負担に供給し、毎ターンの追加取得による遅延を避けられます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-032","number":32,"form":4,"stem":"A long-running customer-support agent retrieves prior conversation history to resolve a user's new ticket. In one deployment, the agent retrieves the user's entire support history going back three years on every new ticket, including many resolved, unrelated issues, and complaints describe increasingly generic, unfocused responses. In another deployment, the team retrieves nothing from history by default, restarting from a blank context on every new ticket, which causes the agent to repeatedly ask customers for information they have already provided.\n\nWhich risk does each configuration illustrate, and what middle-ground fix addresses both?","options":["Both configurations illustrate the same risk, under-retrieval, because in both cases the agent fails to use all of the user's available historical data to its full potential.","The first configuration illustrates under-retrieval, since including more history should always improve a support agent's ability to help; the second illustrates over-retrieval, since starting from blank context is overly conservative.","The first configuration illustrates over-retrieval, or context pollution, from loading three years of mostly-irrelevant history, and the second illustrates under-retrieval that loses needed recent context; the fix is to retrieve a relevance-scoped subset, such as recent or topically-related tickets, rather than either extreme.","Both configurations are equally effective trade-offs, and the complaints described are unrelated to how much prior history is retrieved."],"correct":2,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"履歴の過剰取得と不足取得","concept":"ce-f4-q032","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. The first configuration illustrates over-retrieval, or context pollution, from loading three years of mostly-irrelevant history, and the second illustrates under-retrieval that loses needed recent context; the fix is to retrieve a relevance-scoped subset, such as recent or topically-related tickets, rather than either extreme.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe first deployment supplies excessive irrelevant history; the second omits useful prior information. The fix must preserve continuity while limiting distraction. C addresses both through selective retrieval based on the current ticket’s needs.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “entire support history going back three years” — Technical signal: Retrieval is indiscriminate.\n\n- “resolved, unrelated issues” — Technical signal: Much of the retrieved material does not help the current task.\n\n- “generic, unfocused responses” — Technical signal: The symptoms are consistent with context pollution.\n\n- “retrieves nothing from history” — Technical signal: Relevant historical information is excluded.\n\n- “information they have already provided” — Technical signal: Missing context causes avoidable repetition.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nRetrieve relevant prior tickets and durable customer facts, using topic, recency, and unresolved status as selection signals. Return concise relevant details and fetch additional history when needed.\n\nThis preserves useful continuity without loading the entire archive. Databricks distinguishes raw session records from durable facts recalled across conversations when relevant. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: It treats maximum historical-data usage as the goal. The first deployment already retrieves too much; adding more would not resolve the stated relevance problem.\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: It reverses the definitions. Loading irrelevant history is over-retrieval; omitting needed history is under-retrieval. More history does not always improve answers.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Both configurations can be implemented, but the described failures show that neither meets this workflow’s needs. The retrieval policies directly explain the stated risks, although production diagnosis should still verify causality through evaluation.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe middle ground is relevance, not an arbitrary quantity or date cutoff. A recent ticket can be irrelevant, while an older unresolved issue or enduring customer constraint can remain essential.\n\nRetrieving no history could be appropriate for a genuinely independent task that needs no prior information. That condition does not hold here.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nOver-retrieval reduces the proportion of useful context; under-retrieval removes information needed to answer correctly. Effective context engineering seeks sufficient relevant evidence within a bounded context, rather than maximizing or minimizing retrieval indiscriminately. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nFor a recurring login problem, retrieve the earlier related troubleshooting steps and unresolved findings, while excluding an unrelated resolved billing dispute. The agent can avoid repeating completed diagnostics without being distracted by irrelevant complaints. Evaluate both repeated-question frequency and resolution quality when tuning retrieval.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nRemember what helps this ticket, not everything—or nothing.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nUnrelated history flooding context indicates over-retrieval; repeated requests for known information indicate under-retrieval. Retrieve a task-relevant subset, with recency as one signal rather than the sole rule.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- Databricks — Agent memory and sessions:\n\nSupports: Separating session history from durable, relevant cross-conversation memory.\n\n```\nhttps://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/agents/custom-agents/stateful-agents\n```\n\n- Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents:\n\nSupports: Context pollution risks and selective retrieval of relevant information.\n\n```\nhttps://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents\n```","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: It treats maximum historical-data usage as the goal. The first deployment already retrieves too much; adding more would not resolve the stated relevance problem.","Option B — Incorrect: It reverses the definitions. Loading irrelevant history is over-retrieval; omitting needed history is under-retrieval. More history does not always improve answers.","Correct Answer\n\nC. The first configuration illustrates over-retrieval, or context pollution, from loading three years of mostly-irrelevant history, and the second illustrates under-retrieval that loses needed recent context; the fix is to retrieve a relevance-scoped subset, such as recent or topically-related tickets, rather than either extreme.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe first deployment supplies excessive irrelevant history; the second omits useful prior information. The fix must preserve continuity while limiting distraction. C addresses both through selective retrieval based on the current ticket’s needs.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “entire support history going back three years” — Technical signal: Retrieval is indiscriminate.\n\n- “resolved, unrelated issues” — Technical signal: Much of the retrieved material does not help the current task.\n\n- “generic, unfocused responses” — Technical signal: The symptoms are consistent with context pollution.\n\n- “retrieves nothing from history” — Technical signal: Relevant historical information is excluded.\n\n- “information they have already provided” — Technical signal: Missing context causes avoidable repetition.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nRetrieve relevant prior tickets and durable customer facts, using topic, recency, and unresolved status as selection signals. Return concise relevant details and fetch additional history when needed.\n\nThis preserves useful continuity without loading the entire archive. Databricks distinguishes raw session records from durable facts recalled across conversations when relevant.","Option D — Incorrect: Both configurations can be implemented, but the described failures show that neither meets this workflow’s needs. The retrieval policies directly explain the stated risks, although production diagnosis should still verify causality through evaluation."],"references":[],"source_hash":"cd448278d3c9b240ff6d74e670a60d2da8858010695e13b8ae38e57b5392ae65","stem_ja":"長時間動作する顧客サポートエージェントが、新しいチケットを解決するために過去の会話履歴を取得します。ある配備では、新規チケットごとに利用者の3年前までのサポート履歴全体を取得し、解決済みで無関係な問題も多く含めています。その結果、一般的で焦点の定まらない回答が増えたという苦情があります。別の配備では既定で履歴を一切取得せず、新規チケットごとに空のコンテキストから再開するため、顧客がすでに伝えた情報を何度も聞き直します。\n\nそれぞれどの危険を示し、両方に対応する中間的な改善策は何ですか。","options_ja":["両方とも不足取得です。どちらも利用可能な過去の利用者データを最大限活用できていないためです。","最初は不足取得です。履歴を多く入れれば常に支援能力が向上するためです。後者は、空で始めることが過度に保守的なので過剰取得です。","最初は3年分の主に無関係な履歴を入れる過剰取得、つまりコンテキスト汚染で、後者は必要な最近の文脈を失う不足取得です。両極端でなく、最近または同じ論点のチケットなど、関連性で範囲を絞った部分集合を取得します。","両方とも同等に有効なトレードオフであり、苦情は取得する過去履歴の量と無関係です。"],"explanation_ja":"履歴は多すぎても少なすぎても失敗します。全履歴は現在の論点を埋もれさせ、ゼロ取得は必要な既知情報を失わせます。現在のチケットに関連する最近の履歴などへ絞り、連続性を保つ最小限の情報を取得します。覚え方は「全部でもゼロでもなく、関連する分だけ」です。","distractors_ja":["最初はすでに多すぎる履歴が焦点を損なっています。利用可能な全履歴を使うこと自体が目的ではありません。","分類が逆です。過剰な無関係履歴は過剰取得、必要な履歴を全く渡さない構成は不足取得です。","観測された両方の症状を説明し、必要な連続性とコンテキストの集中を両立する改善になっています。","回答の一般化と繰り返しの質問は、それぞれ過剰・不足取得に対応する具体的な失敗として記述されています。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-033","number":33,"form":4,"stem":"An agent is asked to answer: \"What was our Q3 on-time delivery rate for the Northeast region, and how does it compare to Q2?\" Available data sources include a `shipments` Delta table (region, ship_date, delivery_date, promised_date), a `customer_feedback` table (free-text reviews), and a `warehouse_staffing` table (headcount by location).\n\nWhich context elements does the agent actually need to correctly scope and execute this task?","options":["The shipments table and the customer_feedback table, since customer sentiment about delivery speed should be factored into the on-time rate calculation.","The shipments table and the warehouse_staffing table, since staffing levels in the Northeast region directly determine the correct on-time rate figure.","Only the shipments table, filtered to the Northeast region and the Q2/Q3 date ranges, aggregated to compute the on-time delivery rate for each quarter.","All three tables, to ensure the agent has complete organizational context before answering a delivery-performance question."],"correct":2,"domain":"Knowledge Retrieval and Genie Configuration","topic":"業務質問に必要なデータだけを選ぶ","concept":"ce-f4-q033","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Only the shipments table, filtered to the Northeast region and the Q2/Q3 date ranges, aggregated to compute the on-time delivery rate for each quarter.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe task requires a delivery-performance metric, a regional filter, and a quarterly comparison. The `shipments` table contains the relevant region and dates. Customer reviews and staffing levels are unnecessary for calculating the requested rate.\n\nC supplies the appropriate data scope, assuming the reporting period and business definition of on-time delivery are established.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “on-time delivery rate” — Technical signal: Compare actual delivery with the promised date.\n\n- “Northeast region” — Hard constraint: Restrict the shipment population geographically.\n\n- “Q3 … compare to Q2” — Hard constraint: Calculate separate quarterly rates and compare them.\n\n- `delivery_date`, `promised_date` — Technical signal: The shipment records contain the fields needed to determine timeliness.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nUnder the usual definition, a delivered shipment is on time when `delivery_date <= promised_date`. For each quarter:\n\nOn-time delivery rate (%) = 100 × on-time eligible shipments ÷ all eligible shipments\n\nQuarterly change (percentage points) = Q3 rate (%) − Q2 rate (%)\n\nApply the same eligibility and quarter-assignment rules to both periods. No numeric result can be calculated because shipment counts are not supplied.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Different Use Case: Reviews describe customer perceptions, which may be useful for satisfaction analysis. They do not determine whether a shipment met its promised delivery date. Including sentiment would change the requested metric.\n\n- Option B — Different Use Case: Staffing could help investigate potential causes of delivery performance, but it does not determine the rate’s numerator or denominator. It becomes relevant to a separate question about why performance changed.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Broader context may support exploratory analysis, but this task has a specific metric and scope. Adding unrelated tables introduces unnecessary complexity without supplying required calculation inputs.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDistinguish measuring performance from explaining performance. Staffing and feedback might inform an investigation, but they are not needed to calculate the delivery rate.\n\n“Only the shipments table” does not mean “no additional definitions.” The stem omits the year, fiscal versus calendar quarters, which date assigns shipments to quarters, and treatment of undelivered shipments or missing dates. Resolve those through approved metric definitions or clarification before executing.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nSelect data from the metric’s dependencies: the population, qualifying condition, filters, and aggregation. Extra tables are justified only when they supply a required dependency or answer an additional question.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA logistics dashboard can calculate both rates from a governed shipment metric and pass only the quarterly counts, rates, and comparison to the agent. Consistent quarter and eligibility rules prevent misleading comparisons; a quarter with no eligible shipments should be reported as undefined rather than automatically as 0%.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nCalculate the metric from its facts; investigate its causes separately.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nFor a scoped KPI comparison, retrieve the table containing the measurement fields and apply consistent filters and definitions. Add explanatory datasets only when the user asks for explanatory analysis.\n\nOfficial References\n\n[Databricks — Curate an effective Genie Agent:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices)\n\nSupports: Selecting only necessary datasets, defining business metrics, and clarifying missing scope before analysis.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Different Use Case: Reviews describe customer perceptions, which may be useful for satisfaction analysis. They do not determine whether a shipment met its promised delivery date. Including sentiment would change the requested metric.","Option B — Different Use Case: Staffing could help investigate potential causes of delivery performance, but it does not determine the rate’s numerator or denominator. It becomes relevant to a separate question about why performance changed.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Only the shipments table, filtered to the Northeast region and the Q2/Q3 date ranges, aggregated to compute the on-time delivery rate for each quarter.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe task requires a delivery-performance metric, a regional filter, and a quarterly comparison. The `shipments` table contains the relevant region and dates. Customer reviews and staffing levels are unnecessary for calculating the requested rate.\n\nC supplies the appropriate data scope, assuming the reporting period and business definition of on-time delivery are established.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “on-time delivery rate” — Technical signal: Compare actual delivery with the promised date.\n\n- “Northeast region” — Hard constraint: Restrict the shipment population geographically.\n\n- “Q3 … compare to Q2” — Hard constraint: Calculate separate quarterly rates and compare them.\n\n- `delivery_date`, `promised_date` — Technical signal: The shipment records contain the fields needed to determine timeliness.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nUnder the usual definition, a delivered shipment is on time when `delivery_date <= promised_date`. For each quarter:\n\nOn-time delivery rate (%) = 100 × on-time eligible shipments ÷ all eligible shipments\n\nQuarterly change (percentage points) = Q3 rate (%) − Q2 rate (%)\n\nApply the same eligibility and quarter-assignment rules to both periods. No numeric result can be calculated because shipment counts are not supplied.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Broader context may support exploratory analysis, but this task has a specific metric and scope. Adding unrelated tables introduces unnecessary complexity without supplying required calculation inputs."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices"],"source_hash":"07b4650c8d7d2631e8960313740f9e6e75833640617b19038666e8b16071d4d7","stem_ja":"エージェントが「北東地域のQ3の期限内配送率はいくらで、Q2と比べてどうですか」と尋ねられました。利用可能な情報源には、`shipments` Deltaテーブル（region、ship_date、delivery_date、promised_date）、`customer_feedback`テーブル（自由記述レビュー）、`warehouse_staffing`テーブル（拠点別人員数）があります。\n\nタスクの範囲を正しく絞り、実行するために実際に必要なコンテキスト要素はどれですか。","options_ja":["`shipments`と`customer_feedback`。配送速度に関する顧客感情を期限内率の計算へ反映すべきだからです。","`shipments`と`warehouse_staffing`。北東地域の人員数が正しい期限内率の数値を直接決めるためです。","`shipments`のみ。北東地域とQ2・Q3の日付範囲に絞り、各四半期の期限内配送率を集計します。","3つすべて。配送実績への回答前に、組織全体の完全な文脈を確保するためです。"],"explanation_ja":"求められているのは率の算出と四半期比較であり、原因分析や顧客感情ではありません。配送日、約束日、地域、対象期間があるshipmentsから計算できます。関連しそうな情報を全部加えるより、求める指標を計算するための列・条件だけを選びます。","distractors_ja":["顧客の感想はサービス評価には役立っても、配送日と約束日から算出する期限内率の分子・分母ではありません。","人員配置は原因分析の候補ですが、期限内に配達した件数の実測割合を直接計算するためには不要です。","地域と期間を限定した配送実績から、期限内件数と全配送件数を集計し、2四半期の率を比較できます。","完全な組織情報は不要です。無関係な表を加えると、結合の誤りやコンテキストの無駄が増えます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-034","number":34,"form":4,"stem":"A user asks an internal analytics agent, \"How are we doing against the plan this quarter?\" The agent retrieves and correctly summarizes data from a `quarterly_revenue_plan` table — technically accurate output. However, the user works on the support team and meant \"the plan\" to refer to their team's quarterly support-ticket resolution targets, not the company's revenue plan. The agent's retrieval and summarization logic both performed correctly on the table it was given.\n\nAt which pipeline stage should this mismatch actually be resolved?","options":["At the summarization stage, by instructing the agent to summarize quarterly_revenue_plan data more concisely so it is easier for any user to interpret regardless of their department.","At the retrieval stage, by adding more columns from quarterly_revenue_plan to the retrieved context so the agent has more detail to draw on.","At the data-ingestion stage, by merging quarterly_revenue_plan and the support-ticket targets table into a single combined table so only one source exists.","At the intent-interpretation stage, before retrieval begins — the agent needs to disambiguate what \"the plan\" refers to, for example using the user's role or department, so it selects the correct data source, since retrieval and summarization performed correctly on the wrong input."],"correct":3,"domain":"Memory Architecture with Lakebase and MLflow","topic":"検索前の曖昧な業務用語の解消","concept":"ce-f4-q034","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. At the intent-interpretation stage, before retrieval begins — the agent needs to disambiguate what \"the plan\" refers to, for example using the user's role or department, so it selects the correct data source, since retrieval and summarization performed correctly on the wrong input.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure occurred when the agent interpreted “the plan” as the revenue plan. Retrieval and summarization then correctly executed that mistaken interpretation. Resolve the intended business meaning before selecting the source; modifying downstream processing does not correct the original mismatch.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “the plan” — Technical signal: The phrase has multiple plausible business meanings.\n\n- “user works on the support team” — Technical signal: Department context helps interpret the request.\n\n- “meant … support-ticket resolution targets” — Hard constraint: The answer must address support performance.\n\n- “retrieval and summarization … performed correctly” — Exclusion: The stem explicitly locates the problem upstream of those operations.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nIntent interpretation should establish the relevant business domain, metric, and reporting period before retrieving task data. Use available conversation context and reliable user-role information; if multiple interpretations remain plausible, ask a targeted clarification.\n\nDatabricks recommends explicitly configuring clarification behavior when business questions omit necessary scope or metrics.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Different Use Case: Concise summarization can improve readability, but a shorter revenue answer still does not answer the support-target question. It fits a presentation problem, not an intent mismatch.\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: More columns from the wrong table add detail without supplying the intended support metrics. Additional retrieval would help if the correct source had been chosen but necessary fields were missing.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: A combined data model may support cross-domain reporting, but it does not establish which plan the user means. The agent would still need to distinguish revenue targets from support targets.\n\nExam Trap\n\nFactually accurate does not necessarily mean responsive to the user’s intent. The agent can faithfully summarize a valid source and still answer the wrong question.\n\nDepartment is evidence, not proof: a support employee might legitimately ask about revenue. Clarify when the available context does not resolve the ambiguity.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nA reliable pipeline follows this sequence: establish intent and scope, select the source, retrieve evidence, then formulate the answer. Diagnose the earliest incorrect decision rather than changing a later stage that worked correctly.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nBefore querying, an internal assistant could confirm: “Do you mean the support team’s ticket-resolution targets?” Once confirmed, it retrieves the applicable targets and actual results. This avoids unnecessary revenue queries and reduces confidently irrelevant answers.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nResolve what the user means before deciding what data to fetch.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen retrieval and summarization are correct but the answer addresses the wrong business topic, inspect intent interpretation. Resolve ambiguous terms using context or clarification before task-data retrieval.\n\nOfficial References\n\n[Databricks — Curate an effective Genie Agent:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices)\n\nSupports: Business-context interpretation and explicit clarification rules for underspecified questions.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Different Use Case: Concise summarization can improve readability, but a shorter revenue answer still does not answer the support-target question. It fits a presentation problem, not an intent mismatch.","Option B — Incorrect: More columns from the wrong table add detail without supplying the intended support metrics. Additional retrieval would help if the correct source had been chosen but necessary fields were missing.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: A combined data model may support cross-domain reporting, but it does not establish which plan the user means. The agent would still need to distinguish revenue targets from support targets.","Correct Answer\n\nD. At the intent-interpretation stage, before retrieval begins — the agent needs to disambiguate what \"the plan\" refers to, for example using the user's role or department, so it selects the correct data source, since retrieval and summarization performed correctly on the wrong input.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure occurred when the agent interpreted “the plan” as the revenue plan. Retrieval and summarization then correctly executed that mistaken interpretation. Resolve the intended business meaning before selecting the source; modifying downstream processing does not correct the original mismatch.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “the plan” — Technical signal: The phrase has multiple plausible business meanings.\n\n- “user works on the support team” — Technical signal: Department context helps interpret the request.\n\n- “meant … support-ticket resolution targets” — Hard constraint: The answer must address support performance.\n\n- “retrieval and summarization … performed correctly” — Exclusion: The stem explicitly locates the problem upstream of those operations.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nIntent interpretation should establish the relevant business domain, metric, and reporting period before retrieving task data. Use available conversation context and reliable user-role information; if multiple interpretations remain plausible, ask a targeted clarification.\n\nDatabricks recommends explicitly configuring clarification behavior when business questions omit necessary scope or metrics."],"references":["https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-agents/best-practices"],"source_hash":"07dbd34688240ea9edf75837086479e74fafb4ce1a22c0f543dfb4c6ad17af3f","stem_ja":"利用者が社内分析エージェントに「今四半期の計画に対して、進捗はどうですか」と尋ねました。エージェントは`quarterly_revenue_plan`テーブルのデータを取得して正しく要約し、技術的には正確な出力を返しました。しかし利用者はサポートチーム所属で、「計画」は全社売上計画でなく、自分のチームの四半期サポートチケット解決目標の意味でした。取得ロジックと要約ロジックは、与えられたテーブルに対しては正しく動作しています。\n\nこの不一致は、実際にはパイプラインのどの段階で解消すべきですか。","options_ja":["要約段階。部門を問わず理解しやすいよう、`quarterly_revenue_plan`データをさらに簡潔に要約させます。","検索段階。`quarterly_revenue_plan`の列をさらに取得し、エージェントが使える詳細を増やします。","データ取り込み段階。`quarterly_revenue_plan`とサポートチケット目標を1つの結合テーブルへ統合し、情報源を1つにします。","検索開始前の意図解釈段階。検索と要約は誤った入力に対して正常だったため、利用者の役割や部門などから「計画」の意味を明確にし、正しい情報源を選ぶ必要があります。"],"explanation_ja":"「正しいデータを正しく要約した」ことと「利用者が欲しい答え」は別です。計画という曖昧語の意味を、役割・部門や必要な確認で検索前に特定します。売上データを詳しく・短くしても、サポート目標を聞いている利用者の意図との不一致は直りません。","distractors_ja":["文章を短くしても、売上計画という対象の取り違えは残ります。要約品質ではなく意図の問題です。","間違った対象の列を増やしても、サポートチームの解決目標についての必要な根拠は得られません。","情報源を統合するだけでは、「計画」のどの意味を利用者が求めたかという判断は解消されません。","利用者の意味を先に確定すれば、正常な検索と要約を正しい対象へ適用でき、根本の取り違えを防げます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-035","number":35,"form":4,"stem":"A multi-agent system has a \"pricing agent\" and an \"inventory agent\" that each independently respond to parts of a customer's question about whether a discounted bundle is available. The pricing agent, unaware that the inventory agent's context includes a note that one bundle item is out of stock, confirms the discount is available. The inventory agent, unaware of the specific discount terms the pricing agent is applying, reports the item unavailable without mentioning the bundle discount at all. The two responses are combined and shown to the customer, and they contradict each other.\n\nWhich context-propagation change would most likely prevent this conflict?","options":["Run the two agents sequentially instead of independently, with the second agent's full raw conversation transcript appended in its entirety to the first agent's already-long context, regardless of relevance.","Add a post-processing step that averages or blends the two agents' final text responses into a single combined answer after both have already been generated.","Propagate the relevant shared facts — the item's stock status and the applicable discount terms — into both agents' context before each formulates its response, so neither agent reasons from an incomplete, disjoint view of the situation.","Instruct each agent's system prompt to state its conclusions more confidently, so contradictions are less noticeable to the customer."],"correct":2,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"複数エージェントへの共有事実の伝播","concept":"ce-f4-q035","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. Propagate the relevant shared facts — the item's stock status and the applicable discount terms — into both agents' context before each formulates its response, so neither agent reasons from an incomplete, disjoint view of the situation.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure comes from missing shared context. Each agent knows facts that affect the other agent’s conclusion, but neither receives those facts before responding. Sharing stock status and discount terms lets both distinguish between a valid promotion and a bundle that can actually be fulfilled.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Unaware” of the other agent’s information identifies a context-propagation gap.\n\n- Stock status and discount terms jointly determine the answer.\n\n- Responses are combined after generation means the agents have already reasoned from incomplete inputs.\n\n- “Before each formulates its response” addresses the failure at its source.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nBoth agents need the relevant cross-domain facts to answer consistently. The pricing agent can qualify its discount statement based on stock availability, while the inventory agent can explain how the missing item affects the discounted bundle.\n\nThis fits official guidance that agents operate with isolated contexts and require deliberate orchestration and communication of task information. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Sequential execution can help when dependencies require it, but copying an entire transcript regardless of relevance adds unnecessary context. Execution order alone does not ensure both agents receive the facts they need.\n\n- B — Incorrect: Blending final text does not resolve the underlying factual dependency. It could obscure the contradiction without establishing whether the discounted bundle is available.\n\n- D — Incorrect: Confidence does not repair missing information. It would make unsupported conclusions sound more certain.\n\nExam Trap\n\nParallel execution is not inherently the problem; parallel reasoning with incomplete shared facts is.\n\nAlso, “the discount applies” and “the item is out of stock” can both be true. The customer-facing failure is presenting them without explaining how they jointly affect bundle availability.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nContext propagation transfers relevant facts, constraints, and dependencies between agents. Specialized agents can retain separate responsibilities while sharing the information necessary for compatible conclusions.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nAn orchestrator could provide both agents with a compact record containing bundle items, current stock status, discount conditions, and any substitution rules. Their responses could then support a coherent answer such as: “The promotion applies, but the bundle is currently unavailable because one required item is out of stock.”\n\nMemory Hook\n\nShare the facts that connect the tasks before the agents draw conclusions.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose C: propagate relevant shared facts before response generation. Neither raw transcript dumping nor polished final wording fixes reasoning based on missing dependencies.\n\nOfficial References\n\n[Anthropic — Multi-agent orchestration](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/managed-agents/multiagent-orchestration)\nSupports: Agents have isolated conversation contexts and require explicit coordination.\n\n[Anthropic — How we built our multi-agent research system](https://www.anthropic.com/research/multi-agent-research-system)\nSupports: Clear delegation, focused agent contexts, and careful handoffs in multi-agent workflows.","source_option_explanations":["A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Sequential execution can help when dependencies require it, but copying an entire transcript regardless of relevance adds unnecessary context. Execution order alone does not ensure both agents receive the facts they need.","B — Incorrect: Blending final text does not resolve the underlying factual dependency. It could obscure the contradiction without establishing whether the discounted bundle is available.","Correct Answer\n\nC. Propagate the relevant shared facts — the item's stock status and the applicable discount terms — into both agents' context before each formulates its response, so neither agent reasons from an incomplete, disjoint view of the situation.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe failure comes from missing shared context. Each agent knows facts that affect the other agent’s conclusion, but neither receives those facts before responding. Sharing stock status and discount terms lets both distinguish between a valid promotion and a bundle that can actually be fulfilled.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Unaware” of the other agent’s information identifies a context-propagation gap.\n\n- Stock status and discount terms jointly determine the answer.\n\n- Responses are combined after generation means the agents have already reasoned from incomplete inputs.\n\n- “Before each formulates its response” addresses the failure at its source.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nBoth agents need the relevant cross-domain facts to answer consistently. The pricing agent can qualify its discount statement based on stock availability, while the inventory agent can explain how the missing item affects the discounted bundle.\n\nThis fits official guidance that agents operate with isolated contexts and require deliberate orchestration and communication of task information.","D — Incorrect: Confidence does not repair missing information. It would make unsupported conclusions sound more certain."],"references":["https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/managed-agents/multiagent-orchestration","https://www.anthropic.com/research/multi-agent-research-system"],"source_hash":"28b95be52e8bdfeada8a295ea0908aa960e97e78277f01a4e490a02a977c2092","stem_ja":"マルチエージェントシステムに価格エージェントと在庫エージェントがあり、値引きされたセット商品が購入可能かという顧客の質問の各部分へ独立に回答します。価格エージェントは、在庫エージェントのコンテキストにセットの1商品が在庫切れという情報があることを知らず、割引を利用可能と確認します。在庫エージェントは価格側が適用する具体的な割引条件を知らず、セット割引には触れずに商品が入手不可と報告します。顧客へ示された二つの回答は矛盾しています。\n\nこの衝突を防ぐ可能性が最も高いコンテキスト伝播の変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["独立実行ではなく逐次実行にし、関連性によらず第2エージェントの完全な生会話履歴を、第1エージェントのすでに長いコンテキストへすべて追加します。","両方の回答が生成された後、最終テキストを平均化・混合して1つの回答にする後処理を追加します。","それぞれが回答を作る前に、商品の在庫状態と適用する割引条件という関連する共有事実を両方のコンテキストへ伝え、不完全で分離した状況理解から推論しないようにします。","各エージェントのシステムプロンプトに、より自信を持って結論を述べるよう指示し、矛盾を顧客に目立たせないようにします。"],"explanation_ja":"担当ごとの情報が分断されているため、局所的には整合していても全体では矛盾します。両者に在庫と割引条件を先に共有し、同じ事実に基づいて判断させます。必要なのは全履歴の転送でも文面の合成でもなく、結論を制約する共有事実の伝播です。","distractors_ja":["順序を変えたり生履歴を丸ごと追加したりするだけでは、必要な共有事実を適切に伝える設計にならず負担も増えます。","矛盾した根拠からできた文章を混ぜても、価格条件と在庫条件を整合させた判断にはなりません。","両エージェントが同じ在庫状態と割引条件を踏まえて結論を作るため、情報の分断による衝突を予防できます。","自信のある表現は事実の不足を補いません。矛盾を見えにくくするだけでは、顧客へ誤った結論を返します。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-036","number":36,"form":4,"stem":"An agent's MCP tool registry includes `update_customer_record(customer_id, fields)`, described as \"Updates fields on an existing customer record,\" and `create_customer_record(customer_id, fields)`, described as \"Updates a customer's record with the given fields, creating it if needed.\" The agent frequently calls `create_customer_record` for routine updates to existing customers, sometimes overwriting fields unexpectedly.\n\nWhat is causing the ambiguous tool selection?","options":["The tool names are too similar in length, which causes the model's tokenizer to confuse the two function names at the embedding level.","The two descriptions overlap: `create_customer_record`'s description frames it as performing the same \"update... fields\" behavior as `update_customer_record`, giving the agent no clear signal for when upsert or creation behavior is actually appropriate versus a pure update.","The agent is selecting `create_customer_record` because it was registered later in the MCP server's tool list, and agents default to the most recently added tool.","The parameter schemas for both tools are identical, which is the direct cause of the model's confusion regardless of how each tool is described."],"correct":1,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"ツール説明の機能重複","concept":"ce-f4-q036","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. The two descriptions overlap: `create_customer_record`'s description frames it as performing the same \"update... fields\" behavior as `update_customer_record`, giving the agent no clear signal for when upsert or creation behavior is actually appropriate versus a pure update.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nBoth descriptions make their tools appear suitable for updating an existing customer. The agent lacks a clear selection boundary between routine updates and create-if-missing behavior. B identifies this semantic overlap directly; the other options assert unsupported causes.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Updates fields on an existing customer record” — Technical signal: The first tool explicitly targets existing records.\n\n- “Updates … creating it if needed” — Technical signal: The second tool also advertises updates, overlapping the first.\n\n- “routine updates to existing customers” — Technical signal: The request fits both descriptions as written.\n\n- “overwriting fields unexpectedly” — Technical signal: Mutation behavior needs clearer documentation and implementation checks.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe name `create_customer_record` suggests creation, but its description advertises upsert behavior: updating existing records or creating missing ones. Without explicit usage conditions, either tool appears applicable to a routine update.\n\nTool descriptions should distinguish intended use, preconditions, and effects. Official tool-design guidance identifies overlapping functionality and vague purposes as sources of selection confusion. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: Similar name length does not establish tokenizer or embedding confusion. The scenario provides direct evidence of overlapping meanings, not a tokenization defect.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: No registration order is supplied, and there is no general MCP rule that agents choose the most recently registered tool. This explanation invents a cause absent from the scenario.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Identical parameters can serve different operations. Schemas describe accepted inputs; descriptions can distinguish when each operation is appropriate. Shared parameter names do not make confusion inevitable regardless of descriptions.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is D: identical inputs can make tools look similar, but input compatibility is not operational equivalence.\n\nAlso, unexpected overwrites are not inherent to upserts. The stem does not establish whether omitted fields are preserved, cleared, or replaced. Investigate the implementation separately rather than assuming that description changes alone fix mutation behavior.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nA tool contract needs to explain both how to call it and when to choose it. Names, descriptions, schemas, and actual behavior should agree. Distinct names cannot compensate for descriptions that advertise the same purpose.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nFor a customer phone-number change, document which operation updates an existing record and what happens to unspecified fields. Separately document when creating a missing record is permitted. Test both tool selection and field preservation so a correct-looking call does not conceal unintended data changes.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nSame inputs can be valid; overlapping purposes need explicit boundaries.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen two tools advertise the same action, inspect their descriptions and usage conditions first. Clarify update versus create-if-missing behavior, then verify that each implementation matches its contract.\n\nOfficial References\n\n[Anthropic — Writing effective tools for agents — with agents:](https://www.anthropic.com/research/writing-tools-for-agents)\n\nSupports: Distinct tool purposes, avoiding semantic overlap, and clear descriptions of inputs and behavior.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: Similar name length does not establish tokenizer or embedding confusion. The scenario provides direct evidence of overlapping meanings, not a tokenization defect.","Correct Answer\n\nB. The two descriptions overlap: `create_customer_record`'s description frames it as performing the same \"update... fields\" behavior as `update_customer_record`, giving the agent no clear signal for when upsert or creation behavior is actually appropriate versus a pure update.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nBoth descriptions make their tools appear suitable for updating an existing customer. The agent lacks a clear selection boundary between routine updates and create-if-missing behavior. B identifies this semantic overlap directly; the other options assert unsupported causes.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Updates fields on an existing customer record” — Technical signal: The first tool explicitly targets existing records.\n\n- “Updates … creating it if needed” — Technical signal: The second tool also advertises updates, overlapping the first.\n\n- “routine updates to existing customers” — Technical signal: The request fits both descriptions as written.\n\n- “overwriting fields unexpectedly” — Technical signal: Mutation behavior needs clearer documentation and implementation checks.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe name `create_customer_record` suggests creation, but its description advertises upsert behavior: updating existing records or creating missing ones. Without explicit usage conditions, either tool appears applicable to a routine update.\n\nTool descriptions should distinguish intended use, preconditions, and effects. Official tool-design guidance identifies overlapping functionality and vague purposes as sources of selection confusion.","Option C — Incorrect: No registration order is supplied, and there is no general MCP rule that agents choose the most recently registered tool. This explanation invents a cause absent from the scenario.","Option D — Incorrect: Identical parameters can serve different operations. Schemas describe accepted inputs; descriptions can distinguish when each operation is appropriate. Shared parameter names do not make confusion inevitable regardless of descriptions."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/writing-tools-for-agents"],"source_hash":"44b4c6b6d1e096a2d234f811989d316d95cad4dadead39397862e6b2b2a8e8c9","stem_ja":"MCPのツール一覧に、`update_customer_record(customer_id, fields)`（説明：「既存の顧客レコードの項目を更新する」）と、`create_customer_record(customer_id, fields)`（説明：「指定項目で顧客レコードを更新し、必要なら新規作成する」）があります。エージェントは既存顧客の通常更新で`create_customer_record`を頻繁に呼び、項目を予期せず上書きすることがあります。\n\nツール選択が曖昧になる原因はどれですか。","options_ja":["名前の長さが似すぎており、モデルのトークナイザーが埋め込みレベルで二つの関数名を混同するためです。","説明が重複しているためです。`create_customer_record`の説明が`update_customer_record`と同じ「項目を更新」という動作を前面に出し、単純更新とupsert・新規作成を使い分ける明確な手掛かりを与えていません。","`create_customer_record`はMCPサーバーの一覧へ後から登録され、エージェントは既定で最後に追加されたツールを選ぶためです。","両ツールのパラメータスキーマが同一であり、各ツールの説明内容に関係なく、それが直接の混乱の原因となっているためです。"],"explanation_ja":"パラメータが同じでも、説明で操作の目的と使う条件が明確なら区別できます。今回は作成ツールも「更新する」と説明され、通常更新との機能境界が曖昧です。既存更新・新規作成・upsertの条件や副作用を説明し分けることが、適切な選択へつながります。","distractors_ja":["文字数が似ていることを直接の原因とする証拠はありません。入力には、機能説明の明確な重複が示されています。","二つとも更新の用途に見える説明が、どちらを使うべきかの判断基準を曖昧にしています。","登録が新しいツールへ必ず優先するという一般規則はなく、設問にもそのような観測はありません。","同じ引数を取るツールでも、説明・機能・副作用が違えば選び分けられます。スキーマだけが原因とはいえません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-037","number":37,"form":4,"stem":"A long-running research agent's message history is approaching context capacity after dozens of tool calls. The history includes: (1) a raw 3,000-token API response from a web-search tool call made 40 turns ago, whose key facts have already been extracted and restated in the agent's own subsequent summary message; (2) a tool result from 2 turns ago that the agent is actively reasoning about in its next response; and (3) a raw database query result from 35 turns ago containing figures that have not been restated anywhere since and may still be needed for a final report the user requested.\n\nWhich raw tool output is the best candidate for clearing from context?","options":["The tool result from 2 turns ago, because older information is always a safer candidate for removal than recently retrieved information.","The 35-turn-old database query result, because any tool output that has not been referenced again since it was retrieved is automatically safe to discard.","The 40-turn-old web-search result, because its key facts were already extracted and restated in a later summary message, making the raw original redundant.","All three outputs, because clearing the maximum amount of raw tool output is always preferable once context approaches capacity."],"correct":2,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"要約済みの重複ツール結果を削る","concept":"ce-f4-q037","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nC. The 40-turn-old web-search result, because its key facts were already extracted and restated in a later summary message, making the raw original redundant.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nClear output whose useful information remains available elsewhere. The web-search response has already been summarized; the recent result is actively needed, and the database result contains potentially necessary figures preserved nowhere else. C releases space with the least indicated information loss.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “key facts … extracted and restated” — Technical signal: The web-search output has a retained replacement.\n\n- “actively reasoning about” — Exclusion: The recent result supports the immediate next response.\n\n- “figures … not been restated anywhere” — Technical signal: The database output contains unique information.\n\n- “may still be needed for a final report” — Hard constraint: Preserve evidence supporting the unfinished deliverable.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe later summary retains the web-search result’s useful content, so removing the redundant raw response reduces context pressure. This matches guidance to preserve critical information while discarding redundant tool outputs. \n\nBefore clearing it, verify that the summary preserves any exact values, qualifications, and source references required for the remaining work.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: It selects a recent, actively needed result while offering a rationale about older information. More importantly, age alone does not establish whether removal is safe.\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: Lack of recent reference does not imply lack of future value. The database result contains unique figures potentially required for the final report. It becomes a candidate only after necessary information is preserved elsewhere or confirmed unnecessary.\n\n- Option D — Violates Requirement: Maximum clearing sacrifices both active evidence and potentially essential report data. The objective is sufficient space without undermining task completion.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe decisive clue is redundancy, not “40 turns ago.” An old result may remain essential, while a recent result may already be redundant. If the web-search summary omitted details needed later, clearing the original would require preserving those details first.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nJudge removable context by information value, duplication, and future dependencies. A summary is a replacement only to the extent that it faithfully preserves what the remaining task needs.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA research workflow can retain a concise findings summary with source identifiers while keeping raw responses in an external archive. Clearing the duplicate response from active context reduces input size; the archive allows later verification without keeping every raw result in each model call.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nClear duplicated evidence before unique or actively used evidence.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nPrefer clearing raw outputs whose necessary information is already preserved. Protect active results and unique facts needed for unfinished work, regardless of their age.\n\nOfficial References\n\n[Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents:](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\nSupports: Tool-result clearing, removal of redundant outputs, and preservation of critical information during compaction.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: It selects a recent, actively needed result while offering a rationale about older information. More importantly, age alone does not establish whether removal is safe.","Option B — Incorrect: Lack of recent reference does not imply lack of future value. The database result contains unique figures potentially required for the final report. It becomes a candidate only after necessary information is preserved elsewhere or confirmed unnecessary.","Correct Answer\n\nC. The 40-turn-old web-search result, because its key facts were already extracted and restated in a later summary message, making the raw original redundant.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nClear output whose useful information remains available elsewhere. The web-search response has already been summarized; the recent result is actively needed, and the database result contains potentially necessary figures preserved nowhere else. C releases space with the least indicated information loss.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “key facts … extracted and restated” — Technical signal: The web-search output has a retained replacement.\n\n- “actively reasoning about” — Exclusion: The recent result supports the immediate next response.\n\n- “figures … not been restated anywhere” — Technical signal: The database output contains unique information.\n\n- “may still be needed for a final report” — Hard constraint: Preserve evidence supporting the unfinished deliverable.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe later summary retains the web-search result’s useful content, so removing the redundant raw response reduces context pressure. This matches guidance to preserve critical information while discarding redundant tool outputs. \n\nBefore clearing it, verify that the summary preserves any exact values, qualifications, and source references required for the remaining work.","Option D — Violates Requirement: Maximum clearing sacrifices both active evidence and potentially essential report data. The objective is sufficient space without undermining task completion."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"6cbcd0c22b46decc1e2dfd6b792689fa865733f0bf29b3b33cfc50f4ec42e58a","stem_ja":"長時間動作する調査エージェントのメッセージ履歴が、数十回のツール呼び出し後にコンテキスト容量へ近づいています。履歴には、（1）40ターン前のWeb検索ツールの生API応答3,000トークンで、重要事実はすでに抽出され、後のエージェント自身の要約で再記述済みのもの、（2）2ターン前のツール結果で、次の応答に向け現在推論中のもの、（3）35ターン前の生データベース結果で、以来どこにも再記述されておらず、利用者が求める最終報告にまだ必要かもしれない数値、が含まれます。\n\nコンテキストから消去する最適な生ツール出力はどれですか。","options_ja":["2ターン前のツール結果。古い情報は新しく取得した情報より常に安全に削除できるためです。","35ターン前のデータベース結果。取得後に再参照されなかったツール結果は常に安全に捨てられるためです。","40ターン前のWeb検索結果。重要事実は後の要約ですでに抽出・再記述され、生の元応答が重複しているためです。","3つすべて。容量へ近づいたら、生ツール出力を最大限削る方が常に望ましいためです。"],"explanation_ja":"削る判断は古さだけでなく、必要な情報が別に残っているかで行います。40ターン前の検索結果は重要事実が後の要約にあるため、生出力は重複です。直近で推論中の結果と、唯一の数値記録は残します。覚え方は「古いから消すのではなく、必要情報が残るから消す」です。","distractors_ja":["次の応答で使用中の根拠を消すと推論が破綻します。また、理由は古い情報に言及しており選択対象とも矛盾します。","再参照されていなくても、最終報告に必要な唯一の数値かもしれません。未要約の根拠を自動削除しません。","重要事実が後の要約に保持されているため、元の3,000トークンを除いても必要な情報を失いにくい候補です。","削減量の最大化だけでは、現在の推論や最終報告に必要な情報まで失います。情報の依存関係を確認します。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-038","number":38,"form":4,"stem":"A general-purpose internal assistant's system prompt includes: (1) core instructions used in virtually every conversation, covering tone, escalation policy, and the core tool list; (2) a 600-token block of detailed instructions for handling an end-of-quarter reporting process that occurs roughly four times a year; and (3) a 400-token block of instructions for a rare compliance-audit workflow invoked a handful of times annually.\n\nWhich capabilities are good candidates for packaging as on-demand-loaded skills, and what loading strategy minimizes baseline context cost?","options":["Package the core instructions as a skill loaded only when a conversation begins, since core instructions are the largest single block and offer the most savings if removed from the base prompt.","Combine all three blocks into a single skill that loads in its entirety whenever any one of the three capabilities is needed, to simplify maintenance into one file.","Leave all three blocks in the base system prompt, and instead reduce baseline context cost by shortening the core instructions, since they are used in every conversation.","Package the quarterly-reporting and compliance-audit instruction blocks as separate skills that are loaded only when their specific task is actually invoked, keeping only the core, always-needed instructions permanently in the base system prompt."],"correct":3,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"低頻度能力を別Skillとして必要時に読み込む","concept":"ce-f4-q038","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Package the quarterly-reporting and compliance-audit instruction blocks as separate skills that are loaded only when their specific task is actually invoked, keeping only the core, always-needed instructions permanently in the base system prompt.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe core instructions apply almost universally, while the two specialized workflows are rarely needed. Separate skills let ordinary conversations avoid both detailed blocks and let each specialized task load only its own instructions. D reduces baseline context without removing routinely required guidance.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “used in virtually every conversation” — Technical signal: Core guidance belongs in the baseline.\n\n- “600-token block … roughly four times a year” — Technical signal: Reporting instructions are substantial but infrequently relevant.\n\n- “400-token block … a handful of times annually” — Technical signal: Audit instructions are another independent candidate for deferred loading.\n\n- “only when their specific task is actually invoked” — Technical signal: Task-specific activation avoids loading unrelated procedures.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nUse progressive disclosure: retain concise skill names and descriptions for discovery, then load the relevant skill’s full instructions when its task is identified. Referenced resources can load later as needed. This is the loading pattern defined by the Agent Skills standard. \n\nThe detailed baseline reduction is 600 + 400 = 1,000 tokens, before accounting for the smaller discovery metadata and loading guidance. Those instruction tokens are deferred, not eliminated from sessions that need them.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Loading core instructions at every conversation start still places them in virtually every active context. Moving them to a file does not create meaningful baseline savings if they must always be loaded.\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: A combined skill simplifies packaging, but any need for core guidance triggers all three blocks. Even reporting-only tasks would receive audit instructions unnecessarily.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Concise core instructions can help, but this leaves all 1,000 tokens of rarely relevant procedures permanently loaded. It misses the more direct opportunity to defer specialized content.\n\nExam Trap\n\nPackaging instructions as a skill does not save context if the full skill is loaded every time. Loading behavior, not file organization alone, determines the savings.\n\nAlso, defer the detailed audit workflow—not any organization-wide compliance rule that must govern ordinary conversations. Such universal rules remain core guidance.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nSeparate capabilities by when they are needed. Databricks recommends focused skills with clear descriptions so the agent can recognize the relevant workflow and load it selectively. \n\nReal-World Connection\n\nAn ordinary support request receives the core instructions and compact skill metadata. A quarterly-reporting request additionally loads the reporting procedure, while audit instructions remain unloaded. Test activation on representative requests so context savings do not come at the expense of missing required guidance.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nKeep universal rules present; load specialized procedures when needed.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nRare, distinct workflows are strong candidates for separate on-demand skills. Keep core instructions available and use concise discovery metadata to activate only the relevant procedure.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Agent Skills — Overview:](https://agentskills.io/home)\n\nSupports: Discovery metadata, task-triggered activation, and progressive loading of supporting resources.\n\n- [Databricks — Extend Genie Code with agent skills:](https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/skills)\n\nSupports: Focused skills, clear activation descriptions, and loading specialized instructions only in relevant contexts.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Technically Possible but Not Best: Loading core instructions at every conversation start still places them in virtually every active context. Moving them to a file does not create meaningful baseline savings if they must always be loaded.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: A combined skill simplifies packaging, but any need for core guidance triggers all three blocks. Even reporting-only tasks would receive audit instructions unnecessarily.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Concise core instructions can help, but this leaves all 1,000 tokens of rarely relevant procedures permanently loaded. It misses the more direct opportunity to defer specialized content.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Package the quarterly-reporting and compliance-audit instruction blocks as separate skills that are loaded only when their specific task is actually invoked, keeping only the core, always-needed instructions permanently in the base system prompt.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe core instructions apply almost universally, while the two specialized workflows are rarely needed. Separate skills let ordinary conversations avoid both detailed blocks and let each specialized task load only its own instructions. D reduces baseline context without removing routinely required guidance.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “used in virtually every conversation” — Technical signal: Core guidance belongs in the baseline.\n\n- “600-token block … roughly four times a year” — Technical signal: Reporting instructions are substantial but infrequently relevant.\n\n- “400-token block … a handful of times annually” — Technical signal: Audit instructions are another independent candidate for deferred loading.\n\n- “only when their specific task is actually invoked” — Technical signal: Task-specific activation avoids loading unrelated procedures.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nUse progressive disclosure: retain concise skill names and descriptions for discovery, then load the relevant skill’s full instructions when its task is identified. Referenced resources can load later as needed. This is the loading pattern defined by the Agent Skills standard. \n\nThe detailed baseline reduction is 600 + 400 = 1,000 tokens, before accounting for the smaller discovery metadata and loading guidance. Those instruction tokens are deferred, not eliminated from sessions that need them."],"references":["https://agentskills.io/home","https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie-code/skills"],"source_hash":"dd7785cc504fcb9680e4be438e2426185cb0362d72548492b76e572af029dbf9","stem_ja":"汎用社内アシスタントのシステムプロンプトには、（1）文体、エスカレーション方針、中核ツール一覧など、ほぼすべての会話で使う中核指示、（2）年に約4回発生する四半期末報告の詳細指示600トークン、（3）年に数回だけ使うコンプライアンス監査の指示400トークン、があります。\n\nどの能力が必要時に読み込むSkillの候補に適し、どの読み込み戦略が基礎コンテキスト費用を最小化しますか。","options_ja":["中核指示を会話開始時だけ読み込むSkillにします。最大のブロックなので、基礎プロンプトから外した際の削減幅が最も大きいためです。","3つすべてを単一Skillにまとめ、どれか1つでも必要なときは全体を読み込み、1ファイルで保守を単純化します。","3つとも基礎システムプロンプトへ残し、毎会話で使う中核指示を短くして基礎費用を減らします。","四半期報告とコンプライアンス監査の指示を別々のSkillにし、それぞれのタスクが実際に呼び出されたときだけ読み込み、常に必要な中核指示だけを基礎システムプロンプトへ常駐させます。"],"explanation_ja":"利用頻度と役割で分けます。常時必要な文体や統制は基礎プロンプトに残し、低頻度の報告・監査手順は独立したSkillとして必要時だけ読み込みます。別Skillにすることで、報告のために監査指示まで読み込む無駄も避けられます。","distractors_ja":["ほぼ毎会話で必要な指示を外すと、毎回また読み込むことになり、低頻度能力を外すほどの継続的削減にはなりません。","1つ必要なだけで全能力を読み込むため、低頻度手順を基礎負担から切り離す効果が小さくなります。","中核の短縮は可能でも、年間数回のために1,000トークンを毎回持つ主な無駄は残ります。","常時必要な基礎指示を保ち、報告・監査の詳細は各タスク発生時だけ追加できるため、普段の負担を減らせます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-039","number":39,"form":4,"stem":"A context engineer initially manages context window pressure for a multi-day research agent using a simple hard-coded heuristic: always keep the most recent 20 messages and discard everything older. Over time, the team observes that important facts stated early in the research process, such as a scope-defining constraint set on day one, are being discarded once the conversation exceeds 20 messages, even though that early constraint remains relevant through the entire multi-day task.\n\nIs the hard-coded recency-based heuristic sufficient here, and if not, what should replace it?","options":["No, the heuristic is insufficient — it assumes relevance correlates with recency, but this task has long-lived early constraints that stay relevant regardless of age; a content-aware compaction approach that identifies and preserves durable facts, not just recent ones, is needed instead.","Yes, the heuristic is sufficient, because keeping a fixed recent-message window is the simplest possible way to bound a growing context, and simplicity should always be preferred over more complex alternatives.","No, the heuristic is insufficient, so the fix is to increase the fixed window from 20 messages to 100 messages, which will reliably preserve all early constraints regardless of total conversation length.","Yes, the heuristic is sufficient, because the scope-defining constraint from day one was already acted upon, so it no longer needs to remain in context for the rest of the task."],"correct":0,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"新しさだけの履歴削除の限界","concept":"ce-f4-q039","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. No, the heuristic is insufficient — it assumes relevance correlates with recency, but this task has long-lived early constraints that stay relevant regardless of age; a content-aware compaction approach that identifies and preserves durable facts, not just recent ones, is needed instead.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe context policy must preserve information needed throughout the task. The observed failure shows that message age does not reliably indicate relevance. A addresses this mismatch by selecting what to preserve based on meaning and future dependencies rather than a fixed position cutoff.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “discard everything older” — Technical signal: Removal depends solely on age.\n\n- “scope-defining constraint set on day one” — Technical signal: Early information governs later decisions.\n\n- “remains relevant through the entire multi-day task” — Hard constraint: The constraint must survive repeated context reductions.\n\n- “important facts … are being discarded” — Technical signal: The existing heuristic has already failed its information-preservation requirement.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nContent-aware compaction carries forward active constraints, decisions, essential findings, and unfinished work while removing redundant detail. The agent can continue with that summary plus recent messages. Official guidance describes preserving critical information during compaction and tuning for recall before trimming unnecessary content. \n\nValidate that the day-one constraint remains available and governs behavior after multiple compaction cycles. A summary is useful only if it preserves the required meaning.\n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Incorrect: Simplicity is valuable when a method meets the requirements. Here, the fixed window demonstrably removes information the agent still needs.\n\n- Option C — Incorrect: Increasing the window to 100 messages delays the same failure. Once an early constraint falls outside that larger window, it is still discarded; preservation is not guaranteed regardless of conversation length.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Acting on a constraint once does not exhaust its purpose. A scope restriction can govern every subsequent research decision until it is explicitly changed or no longer applicable.\n\nExam Trap\n\nA larger recent-message window changes when information disappears, not whether relevant information is preserved.\n\nRecency-only trimming could suffice if older content had no remaining dependencies or necessary facts were reliably retained elsewhere. The scenario explicitly establishes the opposite.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nRecency measures when information appeared; relevance measures whether the remaining task needs it. Long-running workflows require retention based on continuing relevance, including updates when earlier constraints are superseded.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA research agent instructed on day one to exclude pre-2020 studies should carry that restriction in its compacted task summary. It can discard repetitive search results while preserving the restriction, avoiding an out-of-scope final report without retaining the entire conversation.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nKeep what still governs the task, regardless of when it was said.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nWhen early constraints remain important, a fixed recent-message window is insufficient. Use content-aware compaction that preserves active requirements and test that they survive successive reductions.\n\nOfficial References\n\n[Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents:](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\nSupports: Preserving critical information during compaction and prioritizing recall before removing superfluous content.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. No, the heuristic is insufficient — it assumes relevance correlates with recency, but this task has long-lived early constraints that stay relevant regardless of age; a content-aware compaction approach that identifies and preserves durable facts, not just recent ones, is needed instead.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe context policy must preserve information needed throughout the task. The observed failure shows that message age does not reliably indicate relevance. A addresses this mismatch by selecting what to preserve based on meaning and future dependencies rather than a fixed position cutoff.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “discard everything older” — Technical signal: Removal depends solely on age.\n\n- “scope-defining constraint set on day one” — Technical signal: Early information governs later decisions.\n\n- “remains relevant through the entire multi-day task” — Hard constraint: The constraint must survive repeated context reductions.\n\n- “important facts … are being discarded” — Technical signal: The existing heuristic has already failed its information-preservation requirement.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nContent-aware compaction carries forward active constraints, decisions, essential findings, and unfinished work while removing redundant detail. The agent can continue with that summary plus recent messages. Official guidance describes preserving critical information during compaction and tuning for recall before trimming unnecessary content. \n\nValidate that the day-one constraint remains available and governs behavior after multiple compaction cycles. A summary is useful only if it preserves the required meaning.","Option B — Incorrect: Simplicity is valuable when a method meets the requirements. Here, the fixed window demonstrably removes information the agent still needs.","Option C — Incorrect: Increasing the window to 100 messages delays the same failure. Once an early constraint falls outside that larger window, it is still discarded; preservation is not guaranteed regardless of conversation length.","Option D — Incorrect: Acting on a constraint once does not exhaust its purpose. A scope restriction can govern every subsequent research decision until it is explicitly changed or no longer applicable."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"8ebf9c588d44609df723372b354cbeda338ab7383cf30d24188fe336b778a654","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアは、数日間の調査エージェントのコンテキスト圧迫を、常に直近20メッセージを残し、それ以前をすべて捨てる単純な固定ヒューリスティックで管理していました。やがてチームは、初日に定めた調査範囲を規定する制約など、初期に伝えた重要事実が、会話が20メッセージを超えると捨てられていることに気付きます。その制約は数日間のタスク全体を通じて有効です。\n\nこの固定された新しさ基準のヒューリスティックで十分ですか。不十分なら何へ置き換えるべきですか。","options_ja":["不十分です。この手法は関連性が新しさに比例すると仮定しますが、このタスクでは古くても有効な初期制約があります。新しい事実だけでなく持続的な事実を識別・保持する、内容を考慮した圧縮へ置き換えるべきです。","十分です。固定の最近メッセージ範囲を残すことが、増え続けるコンテキストを制限する最も単純な方法であり、単純さは常に複雑な方法より優先すべきだからです。","不十分なので、固定範囲を20から100メッセージへ増やします。これにより総会話長に関係なく、すべての初期制約を確実に残せます。","十分です。初日に決めた範囲制約にはすでに対応したので、その後のタスクではもうコンテキストに残す必要がないためです。"],"explanation_ja":"関連性と新しさは同じではありません。初日に決めた制約が今後の全行動を制限するなら、古くても残す必要があります。固定件数の切り捨てから、制約・決定・未完了事項などの持続的な意味を保持する圧縮へ変えます。件数を100へ増やしても、さらに長くなれば同じ問題です。","distractors_ja":["長く有効な制約を内容に基づいて保つため、時間だけで捨てる手法が失った継続性を回復できます。","単純でも必要な制約を消してタスクを誤らせるなら不十分です。単純さが正しさより常に優先するわけではありません。","100を超える会話では再び初期制約を失います。上限を増やすだけでは、新しさを関連性と同一視する原因が残ります。","一度使った制約でも、後続の判断を引き続き制限するなら必要です。過去に対応済みという理由だけで削除しません。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-040","number":40,"form":4,"stem":"A context engineer is choosing a compaction strategy for two different agents. Agent X is a high-volume, low-stakes FAQ bot where an occasional minor loss of conversational nuance has little consequence, and cost efficiency at scale matters a great deal. Agent Y is a low-volume, high-stakes legal-document review agent where losing a single subtle qualifying clause during compaction could materially change the agent's conclusions, and the team runs it at a low enough volume that token cost is a minor concern.\n\nWhich compaction approach fits each agent, and why?","options":["Agent X should use conservative compaction so that even low-stakes conversations preserve full fidelity; Agent Y should use aggressive compaction to keep legal-review costs as low as possible.","Both agents should use the same aggressive compaction strategy, since cost reduction should be standardized across all agents in a Databricks deployment regardless of their risk profile.","Both agents should avoid compaction entirely, since any compaction strategy introduces some risk of losing subtle context, and legal and FAQ use cases cannot tolerate that risk differently.","Agent X should use aggressive compaction, since cost efficiency matters most and losing minor nuance has little consequence at its scale; Agent Y should use conservative compaction, since fidelity is critical and token cost is a minor concern for its workload."],"correct":3,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"失う情報のリスクに合わせた圧縮強度","concept":"ce-f4-q040","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nD. Agent X should use aggressive compaction, since cost efficiency matters most and losing minor nuance has little consequence at its scale; Agent Y should use conservative compaction, since fidelity is critical and token cost is a minor concern for its workload.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nMatch compaction strength to the consequences of information loss and the workload’s cost priorities. Agent X can tolerate minor nuance loss and benefits from token savings across many interactions. Agent Y needs to preserve subtle qualifications because losing them could change its conclusions.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Agent X: high volume, low stakes — token savings matter at scale.\n\n- Minor nuance loss has little consequence — stronger compression is acceptable.\n\n- Agent Y: subtle clauses materially affect conclusions — fidelity takes priority.\n\n- Low volume and minor cost concerns — retaining more context is justified.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAggressive compaction prioritizes a smaller retained context; conservative compaction preserves more detail at greater token cost. These approaches fit the different requirements stated for the two agents.\n\nAnthropic’s context-engineering guidance specifically warns that overly aggressive compaction can lose subtle but critical information whose importance becomes apparent later. That risk directly matches Agent Y’s scenario. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- A — Incorrect: Reverses the priorities. It spends more tokens where nuance loss is tolerable and sacrifices detail where that loss could materially affect conclusions.\n\n- B — Violates requirements: Applies one cost-driven strategy despite explicitly different risk profiles. Standardization does not justify ignoring fidelity requirements.\n\n- C — Incorrect: Treats any information-loss risk as a reason to reject compaction universally. The question explicitly establishes that the agents have different tolerances for that risk.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDo not equate conservative compaction with guaranteed losslessness. Important qualifications, exceptions, and negations still need preservation checks. Likewise, aggressive compaction should preserve essential facts and instructions even when minor conversational nuance can be dropped.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nCompaction involves a tradeoff between context size and information fidelity. Choose the strategy according to the consequences of losing information, then weigh token cost and workload volume.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nThe FAQ bot could retain a short summary of the user’s question and unresolved issue while removing redundant exchanges. The document-review agent could retain exact qualifying clauses and source references, with access to the original documents to verify consequential conclusions.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nCompress more when nuance is cheap to lose; preserve more when nuance changes the answer.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose aggressive compaction for Agent X and conservative compaction for Agent Y. The deciding factor is the cost of information loss relative to the benefit of token savings.\n\nOfficial References\n\n[Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\nSupports: Compaction tradeoffs and the danger of losing subtle, critical context through overly aggressive compression.","source_option_explanations":["A — Incorrect: Reverses the priorities. It spends more tokens where nuance loss is tolerable and sacrifices detail where that loss could materially affect conclusions.","B — Violates requirements: Applies one cost-driven strategy despite explicitly different risk profiles. Standardization does not justify ignoring fidelity requirements.","C — Incorrect: Treats any information-loss risk as a reason to reject compaction universally. The question explicitly establishes that the agents have different tolerances for that risk.","Correct Answer\n\nD. Agent X should use aggressive compaction, since cost efficiency matters most and losing minor nuance has little consequence at its scale; Agent Y should use conservative compaction, since fidelity is critical and token cost is a minor concern for its workload.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nMatch compaction strength to the consequences of information loss and the workload’s cost priorities. Agent X can tolerate minor nuance loss and benefits from token savings across many interactions. Agent Y needs to preserve subtle qualifications because losing them could change its conclusions.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- Agent X: high volume, low stakes — token savings matter at scale.\n\n- Minor nuance loss has little consequence — stronger compression is acceptable.\n\n- Agent Y: subtle clauses materially affect conclusions — fidelity takes priority.\n\n- Low volume and minor cost concerns — retaining more context is justified.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nAggressive compaction prioritizes a smaller retained context; conservative compaction preserves more detail at greater token cost. These approaches fit the different requirements stated for the two agents.\n\nAnthropic’s context-engineering guidance specifically warns that overly aggressive compaction can lose subtle but critical information whose importance becomes apparent later. That risk directly matches Agent Y’s scenario."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"b608e3116dd28d0ca11848772fa1e13599aa706f559286d9e2b81b34a31b2ef2","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが、異なる2つのエージェントの圧縮方針を選んでいます。Xは大量処理する低リスクのFAQボットで、会話の細かなニュアンスが時々少し失われても影響は小さく、規模が大きいため費用効率が非常に重要です。Yは少量処理する高リスクの法務文書確認エージェントで、圧縮中に微妙な限定条項を1つ失うだけで結論が実質的に変わる可能性があります。処理量が十分少なく、トークン費用の重要性は低い状況です。\n\nそれぞれに適した圧縮方針と理由はどれですか。","options_ja":["Xは低リスクでも忠実性を完全に保つよう保守的に圧縮し、Yは法務確認費用を最小にするため強く圧縮します。","Databricks配備ではリスクに関係なく費用削減を統一すべきなので、両方とも同じ強い圧縮を使います。","どんな圧縮にも微妙な文脈を失う危険があり、法務とFAQで許容度を変えることはできないため、両方とも圧縮を一切使いません。","Xは規模上の費用効率が最重要で、微妙なニュアンスの喪失の影響が小さいため強く圧縮します。Yは忠実性が重要でトークン費用の懸念が小さいため、保守的に圧縮します。"],"explanation_ja":"圧縮は一律の設定ではなく、情報を失った場合の影響と費用のバランスで選びます。大量・低リスクのFAQは削減を優先し、少量・高リスクの法務は条件や例外の保持を優先します。どちらでも品質評価は必要ですが、許容する情報損失の基準は異なります。","distractors_ja":["優先順位が逆です。費用重視のFAQでは削減余地が大きく、条項を失えない法務では忠実性を守る必要があります。","リスクと費用の条件が違うため、同じ削減強度を強制すると法務で重大な情報を失うおそれがあります。","圧縮の危険はありますが、用途ごとに許容度を変えられます。すべての場面で放棄する必要はありません。","設問が示す情報損失の影響と費用の重要度に合わせ、FAQでは削減、法務では保持を優先しています。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-041","number":41,"form":4,"stem":"In a multi-agent document-processing workflow, an orchestrating agent dispatches five sub-agents, each of which processes one section of a long contract and returns its full raw reasoning trace — including intermediate drafts, discarded alternative interpretations, and tool-call logs — back to the orchestrator. The orchestrator's context is now saturated trying to synthesize a final summary from all five full traces, and is beginning to miss key findings buried in the verbose returns.\n\nWhich change to sub-agent output design reduces the orchestrator's context load without compromising task coherence?","options":["Have each sub-agent return a structured, concise summary of its key findings instead of its full raw reasoning trace, so the orchestrator synthesizes from five focused summaries rather than five verbose traces.","Reduce the number of sub-agents from five to one, having a single agent process the entire contract sequentially, so the orchestrator only needs to synthesize from one trace instead of five.","Have each sub-agent send its full raw trace directly to the end user instead of to the orchestrator, removing the synthesis step from the orchestrator's responsibilities entirely.","Keep the full raw traces, but instruct the orchestrator's system prompt to \"focus only on the most important parts\" of each trace when synthesizing."],"correct":0,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"サブエージェントの構造化された返却","concept":"ce-f4-q041","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. Have each sub-agent return a structured, concise summary of its key findings instead of its full raw reasoning trace, so the orchestrator synthesizes from five focused summaries rather than five verbose traces.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe bottleneck is the volume and relevance of the sub-agent returns. Intermediate drafts, discarded interpretations, and tool logs bury the findings the orchestrator needs. Structured summaries preserve useful results while reducing the material it must process.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Full raw reasoning trace” — excessive detail crosses the agent boundary.\n\n- “Context is now saturated” — returned content overwhelms the orchestrator.\n\n- “Missing key findings” — important results are buried in noise.\n\n- “Without compromising task coherence” — preserve findings and dependencies needed for synthesis.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption A directly changes the sub-agent output design while preserving the orchestrator’s synthesis role. Anthropic describes this architecture explicitly: sub-agents perform extensive work in their own contexts and return condensed summaries, allowing the lead agent to concentrate on synthesizing results. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- B — Technically Possible but Not Best: A single agent might process the contract, but returning one enormous trace can still overwhelm the orchestrator. It changes task allocation without directly fixing verbose outputs.\n\n- C — Violates Requirement: Sending separate traces to the user removes the coordinated final synthesis and shifts the integration burden onto the user.\n\n- D — Incorrect: Asking the orchestrator to focus does not reduce the tokens entering its context. The saturation problem remains.\n\nExam Trap\n\n“Concise” does not mean stripping away qualifications or unresolved ambiguities. For this contract workflow, summaries should retain material exceptions, relevant clause references, and dependencies between sections.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nA sub-agent’s handoff should contain the information needed for the next stage: findings, supporting evidence, uncertainties, and relevant dependencies. The orchestrator needs an actionable result from each specialist.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nEach contract-section agent could return a consistent structure containing: section identifier, key obligations, exceptions, clause references, unresolved questions, and cross-section dependencies. This makes findings easier to compare and preserves a route back to the original text when verification is necessary.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nKeep exploration local; pass findings forward.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose A: use structured, concise sub-agent summaries. Preserve the evidence and dependencies required for synthesis while removing unnecessary intermediate material.\n\nOfficial References\n\n[Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\nSupports: Sub-agent architectures that isolate detailed work and return condensed summaries to the coordinating agent.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. Have each sub-agent return a structured, concise summary of its key findings instead of its full raw reasoning trace, so the orchestrator synthesizes from five focused summaries rather than five verbose traces.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe bottleneck is the volume and relevance of the sub-agent returns. Intermediate drafts, discarded interpretations, and tool logs bury the findings the orchestrator needs. Structured summaries preserve useful results while reducing the material it must process.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Full raw reasoning trace” — excessive detail crosses the agent boundary.\n\n- “Context is now saturated” — returned content overwhelms the orchestrator.\n\n- “Missing key findings” — important results are buried in noise.\n\n- “Without compromising task coherence” — preserve findings and dependencies needed for synthesis.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption A directly changes the sub-agent output design while preserving the orchestrator’s synthesis role. Anthropic describes this architecture explicitly: sub-agents perform extensive work in their own contexts and return condensed summaries, allowing the lead agent to concentrate on synthesizing results.","B — Technically Possible but Not Best: A single agent might process the contract, but returning one enormous trace can still overwhelm the orchestrator. It changes task allocation without directly fixing verbose outputs.","C — Violates Requirement: Sending separate traces to the user removes the coordinated final synthesis and shifts the integration burden onto the user.","D — Incorrect: Asking the orchestrator to focus does not reduce the tokens entering its context. The saturation problem remains."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"e21067e9523134158c071debdc6d6b79648b167bd7b8dc457286f11c5aff352c","stem_ja":"複数エージェントによる文書処理で、オーケストレーターが5つのサブエージェントを派遣し、それぞれ長い契約書の1節を処理します。各サブエージェントは中間草稿、不採用の代替解釈、ツール呼び出しログを含む完全な生の推論トレースを返します。オーケストレーターは5つの完全なトレースから最終要約を作ろうとしてコンテキストが飽和し、冗長な返却内容に埋もれた主要な発見を見落とし始めています。\n\nタスクの一貫性を損なわず、オーケストレーターの負担を減らすサブエージェント出力の変更はどれですか。","options_ja":["完全な生推論トレースでなく、主要な発見の構造化された簡潔な要約を各サブエージェントに返させ、5つの冗長なトレースでなく、5つの焦点の定まった要約から統合させます。","5つを1つへ減らし、1エージェントに契約全体を順に処理させ、オーケストレーターが統合するトレースを5つから1つへ減らします。","サブエージェントが完全な生トレースをオーケストレーターでなく利用者へ直接送り、統合をオーケストレーターの責任から完全に外します。","完全な生トレースは維持し、オーケストレーターのシステムプロンプトに、統合時には各トレースの最重要部分だけへ注意するよう指示します。"],"explanation_ja":"委任の利点は、内部作業を分離し、親には必要な結論だけを戻せることです。各サブエージェントから、発見、根拠、制約、未解決点を簡潔に構造化して返せば統合しやすくなります。生履歴を全部戻すと、分割したはずのコンテキストが親へ再集中します。","distractors_ja":["内部作業と統合に必要な成果を分離し、主要な発見を保持したまま、親のコンテキスト消費を減らせます。","構成全体を変更して1つへ負担を集中させる方法で、返却内容の冗長さという原因を直接改善しません。","利用者へ生トレースを渡しても、一貫した最終要約を作るタスクを満たせず、統合責任を放棄しています。","注意するよう指示しても、生トレースのトークン負担はそのままで、必要な発見が埋もれる原因が残ります。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-042","number":42,"form":4,"stem":"A multi-agent customer-onboarding workflow has been split into 12 extremely narrow sub-agents, one for each individual form field validated. Each handoff between sub-agents requires compressing and re-summarizing the entire onboarding context so far, and the team finds that the cumulative token cost of these 12 repeated compressions now exceeds what a single, moderately-scoped agent would have cost to run the whole workflow directly.\n\nWhich boundary-placement failure does this illustrate?","options":["Unmanageable context-window growth within individual agents — each of the 12 narrow sub-agents is independently accumulating too much context on its own, causing each one's context window to approach capacity.","Excessive handoff-compression overhead — the boundaries were drawn too narrowly, creating many handoffs, and the cumulative cost of repeatedly compressing and re-summarizing context at each one now exceeds the cost of running the task with fewer, more broadly-scoped agents.","Both failure modes simultaneously, since any multi-agent system with more than ten sub-agents will necessarily exhibit both excessive handoff overhead and unmanageable per-agent context growth.","Neither failure mode — the scenario describes a tooling cost issue unrelated to how the agent boundaries were drawn."],"correct":1,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"細分化しすぎたエージェント境界","concept":"ce-f4-q042","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Excessive handoff-compression overhead — the boundaries were drawn too narrowly, creating many handoffs, and the cumulative cost of repeatedly compressing and re-summarizing context at each one now exceeds the cost of running the task with fewer, more broadly-scoped agents.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe workflow is over-fragmented: each agent handles only one field, but every transition requires processing the accumulated onboarding context again. The stated problem is the cumulative cost of these handoffs, not excessive context growth inside individual agents.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “12 extremely narrow sub-agents” — responsibilities are split too finely.\n\n- “Each handoff requires compressing and re-summarizing” — coordination repeatedly consumes tokens.\n\n- “Exceeds what a single…agent would have cost” — delegation overhead outweighs its benefit.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption B identifies both the architectural cause—overly narrow boundaries—and its observed consequence—repeated compression costs.\n\nAnthropic’s guidance identifies context duplication, coordination messages, and handoff summaries as sources of multi-agent overhead. It recommends dividing work along context boundaries so closely related tasks do not require constant context transfers. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- A — Incorrect: The scenario provides no evidence that individual agents are approaching their context limits. Total workflow token consumption and per-agent context occupancy are different measurements.\n\n- C — Incorrect: There is no universal ten-agent threshold that necessarily produces both failures. The scenario establishes handoff overhead only.\n\n- D — Incorrect: The cost is directly linked to the chosen agent boundaries: splitting validation into tiny tasks creates repeated summarization work.\n\nExam Trap\n\nDo not confuse high total token cost across agents with an overflowing context window within an agent. Many small contexts can still produce an expensive workflow through repeated processing.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nAgent boundaries should balance specialization and context isolation against coordination costs. Tasks that depend on substantially the same information are often better grouped within one agent.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nThe team could group related field validations into a few coherent stages—or retain one agent for the whole form. Passing structured validation results between stages would also avoid repeatedly summarizing the entire onboarding history.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nWhen transferring context costs more than doing the work, the boundaries are too narrow.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose B: excessive handoff-compression overhead. Consolidate closely related tasks so the workflow spends fewer tokens repeatedly transferring the same context.\n\nOfficial References\n\n[Anthropic — Building multi-agent systems: When and how to use them](https://claude.com/resources/articles/building-multi-agent-systems-when-and-how-to-use-them)\nSupports: Coordination overhead, handoff summarization costs, and choosing agent boundaries around shared context.","source_option_explanations":["A — Incorrect: The scenario provides no evidence that individual agents are approaching their context limits. Total workflow token consumption and per-agent context occupancy are different measurements.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Excessive handoff-compression overhead — the boundaries were drawn too narrowly, creating many handoffs, and the cumulative cost of repeatedly compressing and re-summarizing context at each one now exceeds the cost of running the task with fewer, more broadly-scoped agents.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe workflow is over-fragmented: each agent handles only one field, but every transition requires processing the accumulated onboarding context again. The stated problem is the cumulative cost of these handoffs, not excessive context growth inside individual agents.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “12 extremely narrow sub-agents” — responsibilities are split too finely.\n\n- “Each handoff requires compressing and re-summarizing” — coordination repeatedly consumes tokens.\n\n- “Exceeds what a single…agent would have cost” — delegation overhead outweighs its benefit.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption B identifies both the architectural cause—overly narrow boundaries—and its observed consequence—repeated compression costs.\n\nAnthropic’s guidance identifies context duplication, coordination messages, and handoff summaries as sources of multi-agent overhead. It recommends dividing work along context boundaries so closely related tasks do not require constant context transfers.","C — Incorrect: There is no universal ten-agent threshold that necessarily produces both failures. The scenario establishes handoff overhead only.","D — Incorrect: The cost is directly linked to the chosen agent boundaries: splitting validation into tiny tasks creates repeated summarization work."],"references":["https://claude.com/resources/articles/building-multi-agent-systems-when-and-how-to-use-them"],"source_hash":"716f894b9d2f9c81477c8ea0cac79aa84e236c5ac618066d7b180c881e5eb528","stem_ja":"マルチエージェントの顧客オンボーディング処理が、検証するフォームの各項目ごとに1つ、計12個の極端に狭いサブエージェントへ分割されています。各引き継ぎで、それまでのオンボーディングのコンテキスト全体を圧縮・再要約する必要があります。チームは、12回の反復圧縮の累積トークン費用が、適度な範囲を担当する1つのエージェントで処理全体を直接実行する費用を超えたと気付きました。\n\nどの境界設定の障害を示していますか。","options_ja":["個々のエージェント内の制御不能なウィンドウ増大。12個それぞれが独立にコンテキストを蓄積しすぎ、各ウィンドウが容量へ近づいています。","過剰な引き継ぎ圧縮負担。境界が狭すぎて引き継ぎが増え、繰り返す圧縮と再要約の累積費用が、少数のより広い範囲のエージェントで実行する費用を超えています。","両方同時。10個を超えるサブエージェントのシステムは、必ず過剰な引き継ぎ負担と管理不能な各コンテキストの増大を同時に起こすためです。","どちらでもありません。ツール費用の問題であり、エージェントの境界設定とは無関係です。"],"explanation_ja":"フォーム1項目ごとに分けたことで、仕事より文脈の受け渡しに費用を使っています。これは個々のウィンドウ容量ではなく、引き継ぎの累積負担です。同じ情報を多く共有する処理をまとめ、必要な構造化結果だけを渡します。覚え方は「渡す費用が仕事を超えたら、境界が細かすぎる」です。","distractors_ja":["個々のエージェントの容量超過は記述されていません。問題は全体で繰り返す圧縮の費用です。","細かすぎる分割が何度もの再要約を生み、委任の利点より調整の負担が大きくなったことを説明します。","10個という普遍的な閾値はありません。設問が示すのは引き継ぎ負担で、各エージェントの容量超過ではありません。","再要約は選んだ境界の数と共有情報の多さに直接関係しているため、境界設定が原因です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-043","number":43,"form":4,"stem":"A long-running agent is managing a multi-week software migration project where later steps depend heavily on decisions made in earlier steps — for example, a schema choice made in week 1 constrains how every subsequent table migration must be written. The team's current strategy treats each week's work as an independent, isolated sub-task with no passed-forward context from prior weeks, chosen originally because it was the simplest way to keep each week's context small. The team is now seeing migration scripts in week 3 that directly conflict with the schema decisions made in week 1.\n\nWhich long-horizon strategy mismatch is occurring, and what should replace it?","options":["The task has strong cross-week dependencies, but the current strategy treats weeks as fully independent; the mismatch is applying an independent-subtask strategy to a dependency-heavy task, and it should be replaced with one that explicitly carries forward prior decisions, such as a structured decision log, rather than treating each week in isolation.","The task has no real dependencies between weeks, so the current independent-subtask strategy is actually correct, and the schema conflicts are an unrelated data-entry error that does not reflect a strategy problem.","The mismatch is that each week's context is too small; the fix is to carry forward every prior week's complete raw conversation history into each new week's context rather than any decisions log.","The mismatch is that the task was split by week instead of by migration script; the fix is to restructure the task into one sub-task per script, with no other change to how context is carried forward between them."],"correct":0,"domain":"Multi-Agent and Long-Horizon Task Design","topic":"長期タスクの決定ログと依存関係","concept":"ce-f4-q043","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. The task has strong cross-week dependencies, but the current strategy treats weeks as fully independent; the mismatch is applying an independent-subtask strategy to a dependency-heavy task, and it should be replaced with one that explicitly carries forward prior decisions, such as a structured decision log, rather than treating each week in isolation.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe migration’s later steps depend on earlier architectural decisions. Resetting context each week removes constraints that remain binding throughout the project. A persistent decision log lets each new phase retrieve those constraints without loading every previous conversation.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Later steps depend heavily on decisions made in earlier steps” — the work is not independent.\n\n- “Schema choice made in week 1 constrains…every subsequent table migration” — early decisions have lasting relevance.\n\n- “No passed-forward context” — the strategy breaks continuity.\n\n- “Week 3…conflict[s] with…week 1” — missing dependencies produce inconsistent results.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption A identifies both the mismatch and the appropriate remedy: preserve relevant decisions across task boundaries.\n\nOfficial context-engineering guidance describes structured note-taking as persisting information outside the context window and retrieving it later to maintain critical context and dependencies during extended work. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- B — Incorrect: Directly contradicts the stated cross-week dependencies. The schema conflicts are evidence of broken context continuity.\n\n- C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Carrying complete histories could preserve decisions, but adds unnecessary material and growing context pressure. The requirement is to retain relevant constraints, not every prior exchange.\n\n- D — Incorrect: Changing the unit of work from weeks to scripts does not fix missing context propagation. Each script still needs the earlier schema decisions.\n\nExam Trap\n\nA small context is useful only if it contains the information needed for the task. Calendar boundaries do not make dependent work independent.\n\nAlso, writing a decision log is insufficient unless subsequent phases actually retrieve and apply it.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nLong-horizon strategy must match the task’s dependency structure. Independent tasks can operate in isolation; dependent tasks need durable state that carries decisions, constraints, progress, and unresolved issues across phases.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA migration decision log could record the approved schema, naming conventions, key mappings, affected tables, and any superseded decisions. Before generating each migration script, the agent retrieves the applicable entries and checks the script against them.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nReset the working context, but carry forward the decisions that still govern the work.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nChoose A: an independent-subtask strategy is being applied to dependency-heavy work. Replace isolated weekly contexts with explicit continuity through a persistent, retrieved decision log.\n\nOfficial References\n\n[Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\nSupports: Structured note-taking and persistent memory for preserving dependencies and continuity across long-running tasks.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. The task has strong cross-week dependencies, but the current strategy treats weeks as fully independent; the mismatch is applying an independent-subtask strategy to a dependency-heavy task, and it should be replaced with one that explicitly carries forward prior decisions, such as a structured decision log, rather than treating each week in isolation.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe migration’s later steps depend on earlier architectural decisions. Resetting context each week removes constraints that remain binding throughout the project. A persistent decision log lets each new phase retrieve those constraints without loading every previous conversation.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “Later steps depend heavily on decisions made in earlier steps” — the work is not independent.\n\n- “Schema choice made in week 1 constrains…every subsequent table migration” — early decisions have lasting relevance.\n\n- “No passed-forward context” — the strategy breaks continuity.\n\n- “Week 3…conflict[s] with…week 1” — missing dependencies produce inconsistent results.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nOption A identifies both the mismatch and the appropriate remedy: preserve relevant decisions across task boundaries.\n\nOfficial context-engineering guidance describes structured note-taking as persisting information outside the context window and retrieving it later to maintain critical context and dependencies during extended work.","B — Incorrect: Directly contradicts the stated cross-week dependencies. The schema conflicts are evidence of broken context continuity.","C — Technically Possible but Not Best: Carrying complete histories could preserve decisions, but adds unnecessary material and growing context pressure. The requirement is to retain relevant constraints, not every prior exchange.","D — Incorrect: Changing the unit of work from weeks to scripts does not fix missing context propagation. Each script still needs the earlier schema decisions."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"84a82bd1919863b936d00a471819a43e4f439b0e0bfffcd8e156584cdee9aeea","stem_ja":"長時間動作するエージェントが、数週間のソフトウェア移行を管理しています。後の手順は前の手順の決定へ強く依存します。例えば第1週のスキーマ選択が、その後すべてのテーブル移行の書き方を制約します。現在は各週の仕事を独立・分離したサブタスクとし、過去の週からコンテキストを引き継いでいません。当初は各週のコンテキストを小さく保つ最も単純な方法として選びました。現在、第3週の移行スクリプトが第1週のスキーマ決定と直接矛盾しています。\n\nどの長期戦略の不一致があり、何へ置き換えるべきですか。","options_ja":["週をまたぐ依存が強いのに各週を完全独立と扱っています。依存の多い仕事へ独立サブタスク戦略を適用した不一致であり、各週を分離せず、構造化した決定ログなどで以前の決定を明示的に引き継ぐ方式へ置き換えます。","週の間には実際の依存がないため、独立サブタスク戦略は正しく、スキーマの矛盾は無関係な入力ミスで戦略の問題ではありません。","各週のコンテキストが小さすぎることが不一致です。決定ログではなく、過去すべての週の完全な生会話履歴を各新しい週へ引き継ぎます。","週ごとでなく移行スクリプトごとに分けるべきことが不一致です。文脈の引き継ぎ方は変えず、1スクリプト1サブタスクへ再構成します。"],"explanation_ja":"時間で区切っても、仕事の依存関係は消えません。第1週のスキーマ決定が後続を制約するなら、決定・制約・進捗を永続ログに残し、各段階で関連項目を取得して適用します。全履歴を持ち越す必要はありませんが、有効な決定まで毎週リセットしてはいけません。","distractors_ja":["独立でない仕事を分断した原因を正しく捉え、後続段階へ必要な決定を持ち越す解決策を示しています。","後の移行が前のスキーマに強く依存すると明示されています。矛盾は連続性を失った戦略の結果です。","生履歴全体なら情報が残る可能性はありますが、不要な内容と容量が増えます。必要な決定を選んで引き継ぎます。","分割単位を変えても、以前のスキーマ選択が引き継がれなければ、各スクリプトで同じ矛盾が起きます。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-044","number":44,"form":4,"stem":"An agent needs to look up a customer's current loyalty-points balance given their customer_id, as a single value it will insert into a response. Three tools are registered in Unity Catalog: `get_customer_summary(customer_id)`, which returns a large JSON object with roughly 40 fields including loyalty points, order history, and support tickets; `get_loyalty_points_balance(customer_id)`, which returns a single integer; and `update_loyalty_points(customer_id, delta)`, which modifies the balance by a given amount and returns the new total.\n\nWhich tool should the agent select for this task, and why?","options":["`get_loyalty_points_balance`, because it is a read-only lookup that returns exactly the single value needed, with no unnecessary fields and no side effects on the customer's data.","`get_customer_summary`, because returning more fields than strictly necessary gives the agent extra context in case a follow-up question requires other customer details.","`update_loyalty_points`, because passing a delta of zero will return the current total without actually changing the balance, achieving the same result as a read-only lookup.","Either `get_customer_summary` or `get_loyalty_points_balance` would be equally appropriate, since both tools include the loyalty-points figure in their output."],"correct":0,"domain":"Tool Design, MCP, and Agent Context","topic":"必要な値だけを返す読み取り専用ツール","concept":"ce-f4-q044","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nA. `get_loyalty_points_balance`, because it is a read-only lookup that returns exactly the single value needed, with no unnecessary fields and no side effects on the customer's data.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe task requires one current value using an available `customer_id`, with no requested modification. The dedicated balance lookup matches the input, output, and operation precisely. The summary retrieves unnecessary data; the update tool introduces mutation semantics without a task requirement.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “look up” — Technical signal: The requested operation is a read.\n\n- “given their customer_id” — Technical signal: The dedicated lookup’s required input is available.\n\n- “a single value” — Hard constraint: An integer directly matches the response requirement.\n\n- “modifies the balance” — Exclusion: The update tool performs an operation the task does not require.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n`get_loyalty_points_balance(customer_id)` returns the needed integer directly, avoiding extraction from a large JSON response and leaving the customer’s balance unchanged. This follows guidance to return relevant information and minimize unnecessary tool-response content. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: The summary contains the balance, so it could answer the question. However, roughly 39 additional fields consume context without serving the current request. It would fit a broader task requiring several customer attributes.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: If zero is accepted, it might return an unchanged numerical balance. However, the stem does not guarantee that a zero-delta update has no other effects. Depending on implementation, it could still trigger writes, timestamps, events, or validation. The dedicated read avoids that uncertainty.\n\n- Option D — Incorrect: Both read tools may contain the answer, but they are not equally well matched. The dedicated lookup returns exactly what the task needs with less irrelevant output.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe strongest distractor is C: no numerical change does not necessarily mean no side effects. A mutation API is not interchangeable with a read API merely because its arguments appear neutral.\n\nThe update tool becomes appropriate when the user actually requests an authorized points adjustment.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nSelect tools by functional fit, input compatibility, output relevance, and effects on state. A tool that can produce the answer is not automatically the best tool for the task.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA customer-service assistant can fetch the balance and insert the returned integer directly into its reply. This avoids loading unrelated orders and support tickets and avoids invoking the points-adjustment workflow for a simple inquiry.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nFor one value and no change, choose the focused read.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nPrefer the read-only tool that directly returns the required value. Broader output and mutation-based workarounds need a concrete task justification.\n\nOfficial References\n\n- [Anthropic — Writing effective tools for agents — with agents:](https://www.anthropic.com/research/writing-tools-for-agents)\n\nSupports: Focused tool purposes, relevant outputs, and token-efficient responses.","source_option_explanations":["Correct Answer\n\nA. `get_loyalty_points_balance`, because it is a read-only lookup that returns exactly the single value needed, with no unnecessary fields and no side effects on the customer's data.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nThe task requires one current value using an available `customer_id`, with no requested modification. The dedicated balance lookup matches the input, output, and operation precisely. The summary retrieves unnecessary data; the update tool introduces mutation semantics without a task requirement.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “look up” — Technical signal: The requested operation is a read.\n\n- “given their customer_id” — Technical signal: The dedicated lookup’s required input is available.\n\n- “a single value” — Hard constraint: An integer directly matches the response requirement.\n\n- “modifies the balance” — Exclusion: The update tool performs an operation the task does not require.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\n`get_loyalty_points_balance(customer_id)` returns the needed integer directly, avoiding extraction from a large JSON response and leaving the customer’s balance unchanged. This follows guidance to return relevant information and minimize unnecessary tool-response content.","Option B — Technically Possible but Not Best: The summary contains the balance, so it could answer the question. However, roughly 39 additional fields consume context without serving the current request. It would fit a broader task requiring several customer attributes.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: If zero is accepted, it might return an unchanged numerical balance. However, the stem does not guarantee that a zero-delta update has no other effects. Depending on implementation, it could still trigger writes, timestamps, events, or validation. The dedicated read avoids that uncertainty.","Option D — Incorrect: Both read tools may contain the answer, but they are not equally well matched. The dedicated lookup returns exactly what the task needs with less irrelevant output."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/writing-tools-for-agents"],"source_hash":"983bc05f7a19ddf3a583321a1b6817fbde2f400bdb825512671080f049647bee","stem_ja":"エージェントは、与えられた`customer_id`から現在の顧客ポイント残高を調べ、応答へ挿入する単一の値として取得する必要があります。Unity Catalogには3つのツールがあります。`get_customer_summary(customer_id)`はポイント、注文履歴、サポートチケットなど約40項目の大きなJSONを返します。`get_loyalty_points_balance(customer_id)`は整数1つを返します。`update_loyalty_points(customer_id, delta)`は指定量だけ残高を変更し、新しい合計を返します。\n\nどのツールを選ぶべきで、理由は何ですか。","options_ja":["`get_loyalty_points_balance`。必要な単一の値をそのまま返す読み取り専用参照であり、余計な項目も顧客データへの副作用もないためです。","`get_customer_summary`。追加質問でほかの顧客情報が必要になった場合に備え、必要以上の項目が追加コンテキストを提供するためです。","`update_loyalty_points`。差分をゼロにすれば、実際に残高を変えずに現在の合計を返し、読み取り専用参照と同じ結果になるためです。","`get_customer_summary`と`get_loyalty_points_balance`のどちらでも同等に適切です。どちらの出力にもポイント残高が含まれるためです。"],"explanation_ja":"入力IDがあり、必要なのは残高1値、更新要求はありません。したがって専用の読み取りツールが最も適合します。広い概要は不要な39項目を増やし、差分ゼロの更新でもタイムスタンプやイベントなどの副作用がないとは限りません。「値が出る」だけでなく操作と出力量を合わせます。","distractors_ja":["必要な入力と出力に正確に合い、余計なデータ取得や変更系APIの副作用を避けられます。","将来必要かもしれない情報を先に全部取得すると、現在の要求に不要なコンテキストと処理が増えます。","数値が変わらなくても、更新時刻、イベント、検証などの副作用がないとは保証されません。参照には専用の読み取りを使います。","どちらも答えを含んでも同等ではありません。単一値の取得には、不要な項目を返さない専用参照がより適しています。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"},{"id":"CE-20261126-B004-045","number":45,"form":4,"stem":"A context engineer is tuning a new compaction prompt for a long-running agent. On the first iteration, the prompt is tuned aggressively for brevity and token savings, and testing reveals the compacted summaries omit several facts that turn out to be needed later in the session, causing downstream failures.\n\nFollowing the recommended tuning methodology, what should the engineer do next, and in what order?","options":["Continue tuning for brevity, since the token-cost problem the engineer originally set out to solve should remain the top priority even if some facts are occasionally dropped.","Re-tune the prompt to first maximize recall — capturing all potentially relevant information even at the cost of some verbosity — validate that downstream failures stop, and only then iterate to improve precision by trimming content that recall-testing shows to be genuinely superfluous.","Skip prompt tuning entirely and switch to a model with a larger context window, so compaction becomes unnecessary for this agent.","Revert to no compaction at all, since any summarization step risks dropping information that might later be needed."],"correct":1,"domain":"Context Compression and Compaction","topic":"圧縮は再現率から適合率へ調整する","concept":"ce-f4-q045","source_kind":"provided","source_url":"https://www.udemy.com/course/databricks-context-engineer-associate-practice-exams/","source_explanation":"Correct Answer\n\nB. Re-tune the prompt to first maximize recall — capturing all potentially relevant information even at the cost of some verbosity — validate that downstream failures stop, and only then iterate to improve precision by trimming content that recall-testing shows to be genuinely superfluous.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nFirst establish that compaction preserves the information required to continue the task. Then reduce unnecessary content while checking that task performance remains intact. B follows this sequence; the alternatives prioritize brevity prematurely or abandon compaction without establishing that it is unnecessary.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “recommended tuning methodology” — Technical signal: The question tests the established recall-first approach to compaction.\n\n- “next, and in what order” — Sequence instruction: Both the actions and their ordering matter.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe recommended sequence is:\n\n- Preserve relevant facts, constraints, decisions, and unfinished work.\n\n- Test whether the compacted context supports correct downstream execution.\n\n- Remove redundant or irrelevant content incrementally.\n\n- Repeat the tests after each reduction.\n\nOfficial guidance explicitly recommends maximizing recall before improving precision by eliminating superfluous content. \n\nWhy the Other Options Are Not the Best Fit\n\n- Option A — Incorrect: Lower token cost is useful, but accepting loss of necessary facts undermines the purpose of compaction. Brevity becomes the next optimization only after adequate information preservation is established.\n\n- Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: A larger context window may delay compaction, but it does not repair a lossy compaction prompt or guarantee that long-running tasks never need context management.\n\n- Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Disabling compaction avoids summarization loss but allows history to continue growing. It could fit a bounded task that comfortably fits the context window, which the supplied text does not establish.\n\nExam Trap\n\nThe scenario preceding the question is missing. B is the most defensible answer to the methodology question, assuming the omitted scenario concerns compaction dropping needed information and causing downstream failures.\n\nPassing tests supports the revision; it does not prove that no future information loss is possible. Use representative continuations, including cases that depend on earlier details.\n\nFoundation Concept\n\nIn compaction, recall concerns retaining needed information; precision concerns excluding unnecessary information. A short summary is successful only if it preserves enough context for the remaining task.\n\nReal-World Connection\n\nA research agent’s compacted summary might omit an exclusion the user specified earlier. First revise the summary instructions to preserve exclusions and verify that subsequent recommendations respect them. Then shorten duplicate findings while retaining that behavior.\n\nMemory Hook\n\nPreserve first, validate second, trim third.\n\n30-Second Exam Takeaway\n\nTune compaction for recall before precision. Establish downstream correctness, then reduce tokens without reintroducing information-loss failures.\n\nOfficial References\n\n[Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents:](https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents)\n\nSupports: Recall-first compaction tuning followed by removal of superfluous content.","source_option_explanations":["Option A — Incorrect: Lower token cost is useful, but accepting loss of necessary facts undermines the purpose of compaction. Brevity becomes the next optimization only after adequate information preservation is established.","Correct Answer\n\nB. Re-tune the prompt to first maximize recall — capturing all potentially relevant information even at the cost of some verbosity — validate that downstream failures stop, and only then iterate to improve precision by trimming content that recall-testing shows to be genuinely superfluous.\n\nExam Reasoning Explanation\n\nFirst establish that compaction preserves the information required to continue the task. Then reduce unnecessary content while checking that task performance remains intact. B follows this sequence; the alternatives prioritize brevity prematurely or abandon compaction without establishing that it is unnecessary.\n\nKey Exam Clues\n\n- “recommended tuning methodology” — Technical signal: The question tests the established recall-first approach to compaction.\n\n- “next, and in what order” — Sequence instruction: Both the actions and their ordering matter.\n\nWhy This Is Correct\n\nThe recommended sequence is:\n\n- Preserve relevant facts, constraints, decisions, and unfinished work.\n\n- Test whether the compacted context supports correct downstream execution.\n\n- Remove redundant or irrelevant content incrementally.\n\n- Repeat the tests after each reduction.\n\nOfficial guidance explicitly recommends maximizing recall before improving precision by eliminating superfluous content.","Option C — Technically Possible but Not Best: A larger context window may delay compaction, but it does not repair a lossy compaction prompt or guarantee that long-running tasks never need context management.","Option D — Technically Possible but Not Best: Disabling compaction avoids summarization loss but allows history to continue growing. It could fit a bounded task that comfortably fits the context window, which the supplied text does not establish."],"references":["https://www.anthropic.com/research/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"source_hash":"f1256b810486f91f95cbf103d0420733c4e09da0d262039b722ca08e1d95deb5","stem_ja":"コンテキストエンジニアが長時間動作するエージェントの新しい圧縮プロンプトを調整しています。初回では簡潔さとトークン節約を強く優先しましたが、試験で、要約がセッション後半に必要になる複数の事実を省き、下流処理を失敗させると分かりました。\n\n推奨される調整手順に従うと、次に何を、どの順序で行うべきですか。","options_ja":["一部の事実が時々落ちても、最初の目的であるトークン費用の削減を最優先すべきなので、簡潔さをさらに調整します。","最初に再現率を最大化するよう再調整し、多少長くても関連する可能性のある情報をすべて捉えます。下流の失敗が止まることを検証した後で、再現率試験により本当に不要と分かった内容を削り、適合率を改善します。","プロンプト調整をやめ、大きなコンテキストウィンドウのモデルへ切り替え、このエージェントには圧縮が不要になるようにします。","どんな要約も後で必要な情報を落とす危険があるため、圧縮を一切しない方式へ戻します。"],"explanation_ja":"まず後続処理に必要な事実・制約・決定が残るようにし、継続タスクの成功を検証します。その後で重複や不要情報を少しずつ削り、再評価します。圧縮の目的は最短文ではなく、タスクを続けられる小さな文脈です。覚え方は「保持する、確かめる、削る」の順です。","distractors_ja":["必要な事実が失われている間に短さを優先すると、タスク失敗を維持します。まず情報の保持を回復します。","再現率で必要情報を残したうえで、検証しながら余計な内容を除くという、推奨される順序です。","大きいウィンドウは圧縮の必要を遅らせる可能性がありますが、長期タスクで不要になる保証はなく、損失の多い圧縮も直りません。","要約の損失を避けても履歴は増え続けます。代表的な継続タスクで保持を検証し、適切な圧縮へ調整する方が適切です。"],"validation":"提供された模試の原文と正解キーを保持。日本語訳・日本語解説を追加。"}]}]};
